var e=[];e.push({id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin`,title:`Pyrexia of unknown origin: classic, nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Pyrexia of unknown origin (PUO)** is fever of **38.3 C or more on several occasions for at least 3 weeks** that stays undiagnosed after a **defined set of obligatory investigations** (Durack-Street: 3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital); it is split into **classic, nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated** PUO, and in India it is most often **tuberculosis, enteric fever, lymphoma or adult-onset Still disease** - an uncommon disease presenting typically far more often than a common disease presenting atypically.`,keywords:[`PUO`,`FUO`,`fever of unknown origin`,`Petersdorf-Beeson`,`Durack-Street`,`nosocomial PUO`,`neutropenic fever`,`HIV-associated PUO`,`inflammation of unknown origin`,`potentially diagnostic clues`,`FDG PET-CT`,`adult-onset Still disease`,`Yamaguchi criteria`,`naproxen test`,`drug fever`,`factitious fever`,`disseminated tuberculosis`,`Kikuchi disease`,`giant cell arteritis`,`bone marrow biopsy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Petersdorf and Beeson (1961)** first defined PUO as temperature **over 38.3 C (101 F) on several occasions**, illness lasting **more than 3 weeks**, and no diagnosis after **1 week of inpatient study**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Durack and Street (1991)** replaced the 1 week in hospital with **3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital** (or 1 week of intelligent and invasive outpatient investigation) and added **nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated** categories. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Harrison 22e** uses a **qualitative definition**: fever 38.3 C or more on at least two occasions, illness of 3 weeks or more, **no known immunocompromised state**, and no diagnosis after a **specified list of obligatory investigations** - the quality of the work-up matters, not the days spent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammation of unknown origin (IUO)** is the same problem with raised CRP/ESR but temperature under 38.3 C - it is worked up identically and has a similar spectrum, with more vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`In Western series, the share of PUO caused by **infection has fallen to about 20-25%**, non-infectious inflammatory disease (NIID) is 20-30%, neoplasm 10-15%, and **up to half stay undiagnosed** - better imaging diagnoses many causes before they become PUO. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **Indian series** (AIIMS, PGIMER, CMC Vellore cohorts), **infection still causes 40-60%**, and **tuberculosis is the commonest single cause** (most extrapulmonary or disseminated), followed by enteric fever, infective endocarditis, kala-azar, brucellosis and melioidosis; undiagnosed is usually 10-20%. [API 2019]`,`**Neoplasms** cause 10-20% of Indian PUO - **lymphoma** (Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin) leads, then leukaemia, renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma and metastatic disease. [API 2019]`,`**NIID** cause 10-20% in India - **adult-onset Still disease (AOSD)** in young adults, SLE, vasculitis (Takayasu, polyarteritis), and **giant cell arteritis or polymyalgia rheumatica** in those over 50. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Age changes the spectrum**: under 50, infection and AOSD dominate; over 65, **GCA/PMR, malignancy and TB** - so ESR, temporal arteries and a cancer search matter more in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infections (India)**: disseminated, miliary and extrapulmonary **TB** (lymph node, abdominal, spinal, genitourinary), enteric fever (often partially treated), **culture-negative infective endocarditis**, liver or splenic abscess, **visceral leishmaniasis** (Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal), brucellosis, melioidosis, chronic malaria, HIV and its opportunistic infections. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Hidden pyogenic foci**: subphrenic, pelvic, psoas, perinephric and dental abscess, prostatitis, cholangitis, sinusitis, vertebral osteomyelitis and **infected vascular grafts or devices**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neoplastic**: lymphoma (B symptoms, LDH rise), acute leukaemia and myelodysplasia, **renal cell carcinoma** (classic paraneoplastic fever), hepatocellular carcinoma, colon cancer, atrial myxoma and **Castleman disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NIID**: AOSD, SLE, **giant cell arteritis/PMR**, Takayasu arteritis, polyarteritis nodosa, ANCA vasculitis, sarcoidosis, inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn), reactive arthritis and **Kikuchi-Fujimoto disease** (necrotising lymphadenitis in young women). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Miscellaneous**: **drug fever**, thyroiditis (subacute), **recurrent pulmonary emboli or DVT**, haematoma, alcoholic hepatitis, adrenal insufficiency, pheochromocytoma, periodic fever syndromes (FMF), **factitious fever** and habitual hyperthermia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest drugs causing fever**: beta-lactams, sulphonamides (cotrimoxazole), anticonvulsants (phenytoin, carbamazepine), **antitubercular drugs**, allopurinol, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa, quinidine and heparin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Risk factors that reorder the list**: diabetes (melioidosis, TB, abscess), HIV risk behaviour, prosthetic valve or pacemaker (endocarditis), raw milk and animal contact (brucellosis), residence in a kala-azar block, **rice-field work in Odisha or coastal Karnataka** (melioidosis), and recent travel. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Partial antibiotic treatment** is a peculiarly Indian cause - over-the-counter quinolones and cephalosporins turn enteric fever, endocarditis and abscess into **culture-negative PUO**. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why fever persists`,points:[`**Fever** is a raised hypothalamic set-point: **IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha and interferons** induce endothelial **COX-2** in the OVLT, and **PGE2 acting on EP3 receptors** raises the set-point - so any process that keeps cytokines flowing keeps the fever going. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intracellular pathogens** (Mycobacterium, Salmonella, Brucella, Leishmania, Coxiella) survive inside macrophages, evade blood culture and drive **granuloma formation** - the reason liver and marrow biopsy find them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tumour fever** comes from cytokines released by tumour cells or by tumour necrosis (lymphoma, RCC, hepatoma, liver metastases) - classically responds to **naproxen**, unlike most infective fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AOSD** is an **autoinflammatory** disease of innate immunity with an **IL-1 and IL-18 storm** and macrophage activation - which explains quotidian spiking fever, neutrophilia, **very high ferritin** and the dramatic response to IL-1 blockade. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Hyperferritinaemia** reflects activated macrophages; ferritin over 3000-5000 ng/mL in PUO points to **AOSD, HLH or disseminated infection**, and a **glycosylated ferritin under 20%** is specific for AOSD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug fever** is mostly a **type III or IV hypersensitivity** (some are pyrogenic contamination or altered thermoregulation), so it starts after sensitisation - typically **7-10 days** after the drug - and settles **48-72 hours** after stopping it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative bradycardia** (pulse rising under 10 beats per degree C) in PUO points to drug fever, enteric fever, brucellosis, leptospirosis, CNS lesions or **factitious fever**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why PUO is mostly common diseases**: most cases are **atypical presentations of common diseases** (TB, abscess, lymphoma) rather than rare ones - so the work-up starts with the common and treatable. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - Durack-Street categories`,points:[`**Classic PUO**: fever over 38.3 C on several occasions, **over 3 weeks**, undiagnosed after **3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital** - causes are infection, neoplasm, NIID and miscellaneous. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Nosocomial (healthcare-associated) PUO**: fever over 38.3 C in a hospitalised patient, **not present or incubating at admission**, undiagnosed after **3 days including at least 2 days of culture incubation** - think line infection, C. difficile, drug fever, DVT/PE, sinusitis, acalculous cholecystitis, pancreatitis. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Neutropenic PUO**: fever over 38.3 C with **ANC under 500/microlitre** (or expected to fall below it in 1-2 days), undiagnosed after **3 days including 2 days of cultures** - think invasive fungal infection (Aspergillus, Candida), perianal infection, typhlitis; managed under febrile neutropenia protocols (see the fever in the immunocompromised topic). [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**HIV-associated PUO**: fever over 38.3 C in confirmed HIV, lasting **over 4 weeks as an outpatient or over 3 days in hospital**, undiagnosed after 3 days with 2 days of culture - in India **TB is the leading cause**, then MAC, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis, **Talaromyces marneffei (Manipur)**, CMV, leishmaniasis, lymphoma and drug fever. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Category by cause (classic PUO)**: infection, neoplasm, NIID, miscellaneous, and **undiagnosed** - the five-way split examiners expect in a long answer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Category by pattern**: continuous (enteric, lobar pneumonia), remittent, intermittent (malaria, abscess, lymphoma), **quotidian double spike** (AOSD, gonococcal endocarditis, kala-azar), **Pel-Ebstein** cyclical fever (Hodgkin) - patterns are suggestive only, and are blunted by antipyretics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrent (episodic) PUO**: febrile episodes separated by fever-free intervals of at least 2 weeks - think periodic fever syndromes, Hodgkin, Crohn, recurrent cholangitis, malaria relapse and factitious fever; it carries a good prognosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Document the fever** first: ask for a **temperature chart** with 4-6 hourly readings off antipyretics, and witness a recording - 10-15% of referred PUO have no true fever or have habitual hyperthermia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constitutional symptoms**: drenching night sweats, **weight loss over 10% in 6 months** and pruritus suggest lymphoma or TB; rigors suggest abscess, cholangitis, endocarditis or pyelonephritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Joint pain, sore throat and an evanescent salmon rash** appearing with the fever spike in a young adult - **AOSD**; arthralgia, photosensitivity, oral ulcers and alopecia - SLE. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Headache, jaw claudication, visual loss and shoulder-girdle stiffness** in a person over 50 - **GCA/PMR**; it is rarer in Indians but is missed because nobody asks. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Exposure history**: TB contact, raw milk or goat/cattle contact (**brucellosis**), soil and water exposure in diabetes (**melioidosis**), residence in a **kala-azar** block, sexual and injection history (HIV), travel and pets. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Drug history**: every drug started in the last 6 weeks including **ATT, anticonvulsants and ayurvedic preparations**, and all antibiotics already taken - partial treatment explains negative cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Past history**: valve disease or prosthetic valve, dental procedures, recent surgery, **previous TB treatment**, splenectomy, malignancy and immunosuppressants including steroids and biologics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clues to factitious fever**: a health-care worker, rapid defervescence without sweating, **temperature over 41 C with normal pulse**, absence of diurnal variation and discordance between oral and urine temperature. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Repeat the full examination every few days** - new murmurs, nodes, rashes and organomegaly appear over time; Harrison calls these **potentially diagnostic clues (PDCs)** that steer the next test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulse-temperature relation**: relative bradycardia (drug fever, enteric, brucella, factitious); disproportionate tachycardia (thyrotoxicosis, PE, abscess). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: salmon-pink evanescent rash (AOSD), splinter haemorrhages, Janeway lesions and Osler nodes (endocarditis), erythema nodosum (TB, sarcoid, IBD), livedo reticularis (PAN), **hyperpigmentation of kala-azar**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eyes**: fundal **Roth spots** (endocarditis), choroid tubercles (miliary TB - examine every PUO fundus), uveitis (sarcoid, Behcet), conjunctival suffusion (leptospirosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymph nodes**: matted cervical nodes (TB), rubbery painless nodes (lymphoma), tender posterior cervical nodes in young women (**Kikuchi**), generalised nodes (HIV, SLE, AOSD) - a palpable node is the easiest biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdomen**: **massive splenomegaly** (kala-azar, chronic malaria, lymphoma, CML), tender liver (abscess, hepatoma), ballotable kidney (RCC), and **per rectal and pelvic examination** for perirectal or prostatic abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: a new or changing regurgitant murmur (endocarditis), **unequal pulses or bruits** (Takayasu), tender thickened temporal artery with reduced pulsation (GCA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spine and joints**: percussion tenderness over vertebrae (TB spine, brucellosis, pyogenic spondylodiscitis), sacroiliac tenderness (brucellosis), synovitis (AOSD, SLE, reactive arthritis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - the discriminating clue`,points:[`**Disseminated TB versus lymphoma**: both give night sweats, weight loss and nodes; necrotising granuloma on biopsy, **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** or culture confirms TB, while LDH rise and Reed-Sternberg or monoclonal cells confirm lymphoma - **always biopsy before ATT** when a node is available. [WHO TB 2024]`,`**AOSD versus sepsis**: both have high spiking fever and neutrophilia; AOSD has **sore throat, salmon rash, arthritis, ferritin over 3000** with low glycosylated fraction and sterile cultures - and is a **diagnosis of exclusion**. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Infective endocarditis (culture-negative)**: prior antibiotics, Bartonella, Coxiella, Brucella or HACEK - send **3 sets of blood cultures before any antibiotic**, serology and **echocardiography (TOE)**; modified Duke-ISCVID 2023 criteria apply. [ESC 2023]`,`**Visceral leishmaniasis versus lymphoma or CML**: fever with massive spleen and pancytopenia in Bihar - **rK39 rapid test** and splenic or marrow aspirate for LD bodies separate them. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Drug fever versus infection**: patient looks well relative to the fever, relative bradycardia, eosinophilia (about 20%) and rash (about 20%), and **fever falls within 72 hours of stopping the drug**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GCA versus PMR alone**: PMR has proximal stiffness with raised ESR; headache, jaw claudication, visual symptoms or **halo sign on temporal artery ultrasound** mean GCA and need urgent high-dose steroid. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**HLH**: PUO with cytopenias, splenomegaly, ferritin over 500 (often over 10000), triglycerides 265 mg/dL or more and fibrinogen 150 mg/dL or less - **HLH-2004 criteria** (5 of 8) and HScore; see the HLH topic. [HLH-2004]`,`**Factitious and habitual hyperthermia**: no inflammatory markers rise - a **normal CRP and ESR with 'fever'** should make you witness the reading before ordering a PET-CT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line (obligatory stage)`,points:[`**Harrison obligatory tests**: ESR and **CRP**, CBC with differential and smear, electrolytes, creatinine, total protein and **protein electrophoresis**, ALP, AST, ALT, **LDH**, CK, **ferritin**, ANA, RF, urinalysis, **3 sets of blood cultures**, urine culture, chest X-ray, abdominal ultrasound and an **IGRA or tuberculin test**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India adds**: malaria smear and RDT, **HIV serology**, dengue/scrub IgM where fever began acutely, **Widal is not used**, rK39 in kala-azar areas, and **Brucella serology (SAT titre 1:160 or more)** with exposure. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Blood cultures**: 3 sets from separate sites, **20 mL per set** in adults, taken **before any antibiotic** and incubated for at least 5 days; ask for prolonged incubation and Brucella or HACEK alert - yield falls by half after a single antibiotic dose. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CRP and ESR**: a **normal CRP and ESR** make an organic cause unlikely and lower the yield of PET-CT; ESR over 100 mm/h suggests TB, endocarditis, GCA, myeloma, lymphoma or abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smear clues**: pancytopenia (kala-azar, TB marrow, HLH, lymphoma, SLE), atypical lymphocytes (EBV, CMV), monocytosis (TB, endocarditis, brucellosis), eosinophilia (drug fever, parasites, lymphoma, vasculitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine**: microscopic haematuria (endocarditis, RCC, PAN), **sterile pyuria** (genitourinary TB - send 3 early-morning urine samples for Xpert and culture). [NTEP 2025]`,`**Imaging first line**: chest X-ray may show miliary mottling or nodes; **ultrasound abdomen** finds abscess, nodes, splenic lesions and renal mass; **echocardiography** if a murmur or blood culture positive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Do not order** a panel of every serology at once - false positives multiply; let **PDCs direct** the second-stage tests. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tissue diagnosis`,points:[`**CECT chest-abdomen-pelvis** is the usual second-stage test in India - it finds abdominal nodes, abscesses, renal and pancreatic masses and splenic lesions. [API 2019]`,`**18F-FDG PET-CT** is the most useful imaging when obligatory tests fail and **CRP or ESR is raised** - it contributes to the final diagnosis in about **40-60%**, localises a biopsy target, and a **normal PET-CT predicts a good prognosis** and spontaneous remission. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PET-CT pitfalls**: FDG is taken up by infection, tumour and inflammation alike (it does not separate TB from lymphoma); recent **steroids reduce uptake**, high glucose degrades images, and the brain, heart and urinary tract are poorly seen. [EANM/SNMMI 2013]`,`**Biopsy the most accessible abnormal tissue**: **excision biopsy of a whole node** (not FNAC alone) with histology, AFB stain, **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** and mycobacterial culture - FNAC misses lymphoma architecture. [WHO TB 2024]`,`**Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy** (with culture) helps when there is **cytopenia or a raised LDH**: it finds lymphoma, leukaemia, TB granulomas, leishmania, brucella, histoplasma and haemophagocytosis; yield without cytopenia is low. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver biopsy** finds granulomas (TB, sarcoid, brucella) in a minority and is reserved for **raised ALP or hepatomegaly**; **temporal artery ultrasound then biopsy** for those over 50 with high ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mycobacterial tools**: **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra or Truenat** on any tissue or fluid, **MGIT liquid culture**, ADA in pleural/peritoneal fluid (over 40 U/L), urine **LF-LAM in advanced HIV** (CD4 under 200 or seriously ill). [NTEP 2025]`,`**Advanced microbiology**: 16S rRNA PCR on valve or tissue, **plasma metagenomic NGS** where available at tertiary centres, Coxiella and Bartonella serology for culture-negative endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AOSD labs**: ferritin often over 3000 ng/mL, **glycosylated ferritin under 20%**, neutrophil leucocytosis, raised transaminases, negative ANA and RF; **Yamaguchi criteria** need 5 features including 2 major, after exclusions. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Rule one - stop and think, do not treat blindly**: in a stable patient, **withhold empirical antibiotics and steroids** until tissue and cultures are taken - they cure nothing and erase the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stop all non-essential drugs** (or switch to unrelated classes) for 72 hours when drug fever is possible - fever that resolves confirms it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Symptomatic relief**: **paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly (max 4 g/day)** only when fever is distressing, and record the temperature before each dose so the chart stays useful. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Naproxen test** (naproxen **375 mg twice daily for 3 days**): rapid sustained defervescence favours tumour fever over infection - it is suggestive only, not a diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empirical ATT** is justified only in a deteriorating patient with strong TB clues (miliary pattern, granuloma on biopsy, high ADA) after samples are sent - give **HRZE by NTEP weight band for 2 months then HRE for 4 months**; response is usually seen in **2-3 weeks** and lack of response mandates re-evaluation. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Suspected GCA with visual symptoms**: start **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day** immediately (IV methylprednisolone if visual loss) and arrange biopsy or ultrasound within 1-2 weeks - steroids must not wait. [EULAR 2023]`,`**Suspected culture-negative endocarditis**: after 3 blood culture sets, empirical **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus gentamicin or vancomycin** per the endocarditis protocol (see the infective endocarditis topic). [ESC 2023]`,`**Counsel and follow up**: explain that PUO is a process, keep a temperature diary, review every **1-2 weeks**, and refer to a tertiary centre after 3 weeks if stage 1 is negative. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** when the patient is unstable, has **weight loss with cytopenias**, needs invasive biopsy, has HIV with low CD4, or is neutropenic, elderly or pregnant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neutropenic fever** (ANC under 500 and temperature 38.3 C once or 38.0 C for an hour) is an emergency: cultures and **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly (or cefepime or meropenem)** within 1 hour - this is not a PUO to watch. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Nosocomial PUO**: remove or change lines older than 72 hours with signs, culture catheter tip and peripheral blood, test **stool for C. difficile toxin**, Doppler the legs, check sinuses in intubated patients, and stop suspect drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV-associated PUO**: CD4 count, viral load, **urine LF-LAM, Xpert on sputum and other samples**, serum cryptococcal antigen, mycobacterial blood culture, and marrow biopsy for MAC, histoplasma, talaromyces and leishmania. [NACO 2021]`,`**Clinically deteriorating, still undiagnosed**: after all samples, a time-limited empirical trial - **ATT**, or **broad-spectrum antibiotics** in sepsis, or **steroids** only after lymphoma and TB are excluded - each trial with predefined stop rules. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AOSD confirmed**: **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day**; refractory or steroid-dependent disease - **anakinra 100 mg SC daily**, canakinumab or **tocilizumab 8 mg/kg IV 4-weekly**; screen daily for **macrophage activation syndrome** (falling ESR with rising ferritin). [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Kala-azar confirmed**: **liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg single IV infusion** (Indian programme regimen) - see the visceral leishmaniasis topic. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Monitor** a daily temperature chart, weight, CBC, LFT and ferritin, and repeat imaging or biopsy if a new PDC appears. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: GCA/PMR, TB, malignancy, endocarditis and drug fever dominate; fever may be blunted, so **CRP/ESR and delirium** carry more weight than the thermometer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: avoid ionising imaging where possible - **MRI and ultrasound** first; listeriosis, pyelonephritis, TB and SLE; ATT first line (HRZE) is safe; avoid streptomycin. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: melioidosis (multiple liver and splenic abscesses, prostatic abscess), TB, emphysematous pyelonephritis and mucormycosis - **CT abdomen** early, culture on Ashdown medium for Burkholderia. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: line infection, endocarditis, TB (peritoneal and pericardial) and drug fever; dose all drugs for GFR (see the antimicrobial pharmacology topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: infections (enteric, TB, UTI, malaria) cause most PUO, then **systemic JIA (Still disease)**, Kawasaki and leukaemia - a marrow examination precedes any steroid. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**Steroid or biologic users**: TB reactivation (anti-TNF), nocardia, fungal and CMV infection; **screen for latent TB before biologics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a careful repeat examination, smear, CBC, ESR, chest X-ray, ultrasound, Xpert/Truenat on any sample and **node biopsy** solve most Indian PUO before PET-CT is needed. [API 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Prognosis depends on the cause**: malignancy-related PUO carries the highest mortality; infection and NIID do well when treated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Undiagnosed PUO has a good prognosis** - most resolve spontaneously within weeks to months, and mortality in long-term follow-up is low when the initial work-up was complete. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Harm from blind therapy**: empirical antibiotics mask endocarditis and abscess; empirical steroids accelerate TB, mask lymphoma (and make the biopsy uninterpretable) and precipitate **strongyloides hyperinfection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complications of the causes**: valve destruction and emboli (endocarditis), visual loss (GCA), **MAS/HLH** (AOSD, lymphoma, TB, EBV), and dissemination (TB, fungal) - each with high mortality if missed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complications of the work-up**: radiation dose from repeated CT, bleeding after liver biopsy, and the cost of a long admission - hence the stepwise approach. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a tertiary centre** when stage 1 tests are negative at 3 weeks, a PET-CT or invasive biopsy is needed, or the patient is HIV-positive, neutropenic or deteriorating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Follow up stable undiagnosed patients** every 2-4 weeks with history, examination, CBC, CRP/ESR - repeat the obligatory tests if a new clue appears. [Harrison 22e]`,`**On empirical ATT**: review at 2 weeks for fever and weight, LFT at baseline and if symptomatic, and **register on Ni-kshay**; persisting fever at 4-6 weeks means re-evaluate rather than add drugs. [NTEP 2025]`,`**On steroids for AOSD or GCA**: monitor glucose, BP, bone protection (calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate), and taper by the guideline schedule. [EULAR 2023]`,`**Write a fever summary** for each referral: chart, every test with date, antibiotics already given and the PDCs found - it prevents a costly repeat of the same work-up. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Harrison 22e (2025)** keeps the quality-based definition (obligatory investigations rather than days in hospital) and places **FDG PET-CT** as the next step when CRP/ESR is raised and stage 1 is negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EULAR/PReS 2024 Still disease recommendations** treat **systemic JIA and AOSD as one disease (Still disease)**, advise early **IL-1 or IL-6 blockade** to spare steroids, aim for steroid-free remission by 6 months, and screen for MAS. [EULAR/PReS 2024]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 GCA classification criteria** (age 50 or more as entry, then weighted clinical, imaging and biopsy items) and **EULAR 2023 large-vessel vasculitis recommendations** favour **ultrasound of temporal and axillary arteries** as the first test. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline** adopts updated Duke criteria with **FDG PET-CT and cardiac CT as major imaging criteria** - relevant to culture-negative endocarditis presenting as PUO. [ESC 2023]`,`**WHO TB diagnosis guidelines (2024 consolidated update)** endorse molecular WHO-recommended rapid diagnostics (Xpert Ultra, Truenat) as initial tests on extrapulmonary samples, and targeted NGS for resistance - adopted by **NTEP**. [WHO TB 2024]`,`**India's kala-azar programme** has kept every endemic block below **1 case per 10,000** since 2023, so a PUO with splenomegaly outside the old belt is now less likely to be VL - but rK39 is still sent in Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and UP. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: define PUO and give the **four Durack-Street categories** with their time criteria - write 3 weeks, 3 visits/3 days, 2 days of culture, and 4 weeks for HIV outpatients. [Durack-Street 1991]`,`**Commonest cause of PUO in India**: **tuberculosis** (extrapulmonary/disseminated); commonest neoplastic cause: **lymphoma**; commonest NIID in young adults: **AOSD**; in the elderly: **GCA**. [API 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: starting ceftriaxone plus steroids on day 1 - write that empirical therapy is reserved for unstable patients, culture-negative endocarditis, suspected miliary TB and suspected GCA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Factitious fever clues** and the **naproxen test** are favourite short-answer items. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PET-CT question**: say it localises but does not characterise, has 40-60% diagnostic contribution, needs raised inflammatory markers to be useful, and a negative scan is reassuring. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Draw** the staged work-up flow chart (obligatory tests, PDC-guided tests, PET-CT, biopsy, watchful waiting) - it earns the most marks in a 10-mark answer. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Durack-Street categories of PUO`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Duration of undiagnosed work-up`,`Leading causes in India`],rows:[[`Classic`,`Over 38.3 C on several occasions for over 3 weeks`,`3 OPD visits or 3 days in hospital (or 1 week of intelligent outpatient study)`,`TB, enteric fever, endocarditis, lymphoma, AOSD, kala-azar`],[`Nosocomial`,`Over 38.3 C in an inpatient, not present or incubating at admission`,`3 days including 2 days of culture incubation`,`Line infection, C. difficile, drug fever, DVT/PE, sinusitis, acalculous cholecystitis`],[`Neutropenic`,`Over 38.3 C with ANC under 500 or expected to fall below 500 in 1-2 days`,`3 days including 2 days of culture incubation`,`Invasive candidiasis and aspergillosis, perianal infection, typhlitis, resistant Gram-negatives`],[`HIV-associated`,`Over 38.3 C in confirmed HIV infection`,`Over 4 weeks as outpatient or over 3 days in hospital, with 2 days of culture`,`TB, MAC, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis, talaromycosis, CMV, lymphoma, drug fever`]]},{heading:`Causes of classic PUO and their clues`,columns:[`Group`,`Main causes`,`Clinical clue`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Infection`,`Extrapulmonary or miliary TB, enteric fever, endocarditis, abscess, brucellosis, kala-azar, melioidosis`,`Night sweats, nodes, murmur, massive spleen, raw milk, diabetes`,`Xpert/culture on tissue, 3 blood cultures, echo, rK39, Brucella SAT, CT`],[`Neoplasm`,`Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, leukaemia, RCC, hepatoma, metastases`,`Weight loss, pruritus, rubbery nodes, raised LDH, haematuria`,`Excision node biopsy, marrow biopsy, CT, PET-CT`],[`Non-infectious inflammatory`,`AOSD, SLE, GCA/PMR, Takayasu, PAN, sarcoidosis, Crohn, Kikuchi`,`Salmon rash and sore throat, jaw claudication, unequal pulses, oral ulcers`,`Ferritin and glycosylated ferritin, ANA, temporal artery ultrasound/biopsy, CT angiography`],[`Miscellaneous`,`Drug fever, thyroiditis, PE/DVT, haematoma, adrenal insufficiency, factitious fever`,`Well-looking patient, relative bradycardia, eosinophilia, normal CRP`,`Drug withdrawal for 72 h, TSH, Doppler, CTPA, witnessed temperature`],[`Undiagnosed`,`10-20% in Indian series, up to 50% in Western series`,`Normal CRP and ESR, normal PET-CT`,`Watchful waiting with review - good prognosis`]]},{heading:`Staged work-up of classic PUO`,columns:[`Stage`,`Tests`,`Interpretation / cut-off`,`Next step if negative`],rows:[[`1. Confirm fever`,`Witnessed 4-6 hourly chart off antipyretics`,`38.3 C on at least 2 occasions; CRP rise confirms organic fever`,`No fever - habitual hyperthermia or factitious; stop work-up`],[`2. Obligatory tests`,`CBC, smear, ESR, CRP, LFT, LDH, CK, ferritin, electrolytes, creatinine, electrophoresis, ANA, RF, urine, 3 blood cultures, urine culture, HIV, IGRA, chest X-ray, ultrasound abdomen`,`ESR over 100 - TB, endocarditis, GCA, myeloma; ferritin over 3000 - AOSD, HLH`,`Look for potentially diagnostic clues`],[`3. PDC-directed tests`,`Echo, CECT chest-abdomen-pelvis, rK39, Brucella SAT, Xpert on fluids, temporal artery ultrasound`,`Brucella SAT 1:160 or more; ADA over 40 U/L in effusion`,`FDG PET-CT if CRP/ESR raised`],[`4. FDG PET-CT`,`Whole-body PET-CT`,`Localises a biopsy target in 40-60%; normal scan - good prognosis`,`Biopsy the hottest accessible lesion`],[`5. Tissue`,`Excision node biopsy, marrow aspirate and biopsy with culture, liver biopsy, temporal artery biopsy`,`Granuloma, Reed-Sternberg cells, LD bodies, haemophagocytosis`,`Watchful waiting or a time-limited therapeutic trial`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in PUO (diagnostic tests and targeted therapy)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day (2-3 g in liver disease)`,`Only for distress; record temperature before each dose`,`Hepatotoxicity in overdose, alcohol use, malnutrition`],[`Naproxen (naproxen test)`,`375 mg oral twice daily`,`3 days`,`GI bleeding, AKI; suggestive of tumour fever only, not diagnostic`],[`HRZE fixed-dose combination (NTEP)`,`H75 R150 Z400 E275 tablets by weight band: 25-34 kg 2, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6`,`2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE (longer for CNS and spinal TB)`,`Hepatitis, rash, optic neuritis (E), gout (Z); rifampicin interactions`],[`Prednisolone (GCA, AOSD)`,`GCA 40-60 mg/day oral (IV methylprednisolone 0.25-1 g/day x 3 if visual loss); AOSD 0.5-1 mg/kg/day`,`Taper over 12-18 months in GCA; aim steroid-free by 6 months in Still disease`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, TB reactivation - exclude TB and lymphoma first`],[`Anakinra (Still disease)`,`100 mg SC once daily`,`Long-term, taper when in remission`,`Injection-site reactions, neutropenia, infection; screen latent TB`],[`Tocilizumab (Still disease, GCA)`,`8 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks or 162 mg SC weekly`,`As maintenance, steroid-sparing`,`Masks CRP and fever, raised lipids and LFT, GI perforation, TB reactivation`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B (kala-azar)`,`10 mg/kg single IV infusion`,`Single dose`,`Infusion reactions, hypokalaemia, nephrotoxicity (less than conventional)`],[`Doxycycline plus rifampicin or gentamicin (brucellosis)`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD oral plus rifampicin 600-900 mg OD, or gentamicin 5 mg/kg IV OD for 7-14 days`,`6 weeks (12 weeks or more for spondylitis)`,`Photosensitivity, oesophagitis; avoid doxycycline in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`PUO clues to the diagnosis (differential table)`,columns:[`Clue`,`Think of`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Relative bradycardia`,`Drug fever, enteric fever, brucellosis, factitious fever`,`Drug withdrawal, blood/marrow culture, SAT`],[`Quotidian spike with salmon rash and sore throat`,`Adult-onset Still disease`,`Ferritin over 3000, glycosylated ferritin under 20%`],[`Massive splenomegaly with pancytopenia`,`Kala-azar, lymphoma, chronic malaria, CML`,`rK39, marrow or splenic aspirate, smear`],[`New regurgitant murmur`,`Infective endocarditis`,`3 blood cultures, TOE, Duke-ISCVID criteria`],[`Jaw claudication, age over 50, ESR over 50`,`Giant cell arteritis`,`Temporal artery ultrasound (halo) or biopsy`],[`Raised CRP with no clue at all`,`Lymphoma, occult abscess, vasculitis`,`FDG PET-CT then biopsy`],[`Normal CRP and ESR`,`Factitious or habitual hyperthermia`,`Witnessed temperature, paired urine temperature`]]}],redFlags:[`Neutropenia (ANC under 500) with fever - treat as febrile neutropenia within 1 hour, not as PUO.`,`Haemodynamic instability, rising lactate or new organ dysfunction - sepsis pathway now.`,`New visual symptoms or jaw claudication in a patient over 50 - same-day steroids for GCA.`,`Pancytopenia with splenomegaly and ferritin over 10000 - suspected HLH; urgent haematology.`,`New or changing murmur with emboli - suspected endocarditis; admit for cultures and echo.`,`HIV with CD4 under 200 and fever - admit for TB, cryptococcal and fungal work-up.`,`Rapid weight loss with nodes and drenching sweats - urgent excision biopsy for lymphoma.`],pearls:[`PUO is usually an **uncommon presentation of a common disease**, not a rare disease.`,`In India, think **TB first**, then enteric fever, endocarditis, lymphoma and AOSD.`,`**Biopsy before ATT or steroids** - an empirical trial erases the diagnosis.`,`**Normal CRP and ESR** with fever: witness the thermometer before ordering PET-CT.`,`Durack-Street numbers: **3 weeks, 3 visits or 3 days; 2 days of culture; HIV 4 weeks OPD**.`,`**Ferritin over 3000** with low glycosylated ferritin (under 20%) - AOSD; ferritin over 10000 with cytopenias - HLH.`,`**PET-CT localises, it does not characterise** - always follow with biopsy.`,`Undiagnosed PUO after a complete work-up has a **good prognosis** - watchful waiting is a valid plan.`,`Examine the **fundus for choroid tubercles and Roth spots** in every PUO.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q1`,stem:`According to Durack and Street, which of the following best defines nosocomial PUO?`,options:[`Fever over 38.3 C for 3 weeks in an outpatient after 3 clinic visits`,`Fever over 38.3 C in a hospitalised patient, not present at admission, undiagnosed after 3 days including 2 days of culture incubation`,`Fever over 38.3 C with ANC under 500, undiagnosed after 3 days`,`Fever over 38.3 C in HIV lasting more than 4 weeks as an outpatient`],answer:1,explanation:`Nosocomial PUO is fever arising in hospital that was not present or incubating at admission and stays undiagnosed after 3 days of work-up including at least 2 days of culture incubation. The 3-week, 3-visit definition is classic PUO, the ANC under 500 definition is neutropenic PUO, and the 4-week outpatient rule belongs to HIV-associated PUO.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q2`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman has 5 weeks of daily evening fever spikes to 39.5 C, a salmon-pink rash that appears with the fever, sore throat and wrist arthritis. WBC is 18,000 with 88% neutrophils, ferritin 9,500 ng/mL, ANA and RF negative, and cultures sterile. Which test most supports the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Anti-dsDNA antibody`,`Antistreptolysin O titre`,`Glycosylated ferritin fraction under 20%`,`Rheumatoid factor IgA`],answer:2,explanation:`Quotidian fever, evanescent salmon rash, sore throat, arthritis, neutrophilia and very high ferritin with negative ANA and RF fit adult-onset Still disease, and a glycosylated ferritin fraction under 20% is the most specific supporting test. Anti-dsDNA points to SLE, which causes leucopenia rather than neutrophilia; ASO supports rheumatic fever, which lacks ferritin this high; RF is already negative and not part of Still diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q3`,stem:`In a stable patient with classic PUO, stage 1 obligatory tests are unremarkable but CRP is 85 mg/L. What is the most appropriate next investigation according to current practice?`,options:[`Empirical four-drug antitubercular therapy for 4 weeks`,`Whole-body 18F-FDG PET-CT to localise a biopsy target`,`Empirical ceftriaxone and doxycycline for 2 weeks`,`Blind liver biopsy`],answer:1,explanation:`When obligatory tests are negative and inflammatory markers are raised, FDG PET-CT is the most useful next test because it localises lesions for biopsy and contributes to the diagnosis in about half of cases. Empirical ATT and empirical antibiotics in a stable patient erase the diagnosis, and a blind liver biopsy has low yield unless ALP is raised or the liver is enlarged.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man started phenytoin 10 days ago and now has fever of 39 C with a pulse of 84/min, a mild maculopapular rash and eosinophils of 12%. He looks well. What is the best initial step?`,options:[`Start IV piperacillin-tazobactam after blood cultures`,`Start oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg`,`Order an FDG PET-CT`,`Stop or substitute phenytoin and watch for defervescence within 72 hours`],answer:3,explanation:`Fever 7-10 days after a new drug, relative bradycardia, eosinophilia and rash in a well-looking patient suggest drug fever, so the diagnostic and therapeutic step is to stop or substitute the drug, expecting fever to settle within 48-72 hours. Broad-spectrum antibiotics are not indicated in a stable patient without sepsis, steroids are not first-line and would mask other diagnoses, and PET-CT is premature.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-q5`,stem:`In which of the following situations is an empirical therapeutic trial most clearly justified in PUO before a tissue diagnosis?`,options:[`Suspected giant cell arteritis with visual symptoms in a 70-year-old`,`A stable young man with 4 weeks of fever and normal CRP`,`Suspected lymphoma with a palpable cervical node`,`Undiagnosed fever after negative PET-CT in a well patient`],answer:0,explanation:`Suspected GCA with visual symptoms needs immediate high-dose steroids because blindness can follow within days, and a biopsy remains positive for up to 2 weeks after starting steroids. A palpable node in suspected lymphoma should be excised first, since steroids destroy the histology. A stable patient with normal CRP, or with a negative PET-CT, needs watchful waiting rather than a blind trial.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define pyrexia of unknown origin and classify it. Discuss the aetiology of classic PUO in India and outline a stepwise approach to its investigation and management.`,openingLines:[`Pyrexia of unknown origin is fever of 38.3 C or more on several occasions for more than 3 weeks that remains undiagnosed after 3 outpatient visits or 3 days in hospital (Durack-Street 1991), or after a defined set of obligatory investigations (Harrison 22e).`,`Durack and Street divide it into classic, nosocomial, neutropenic and HIV-associated PUO; in India tuberculosis is the commonest single cause.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`Petersdorf-Beeson 1961, Durack-Street 1991, Harrison qualitative definition.`,`Four categories with their time criteria (draw the table).`,`Inflammation of unknown origin - same approach with raised CRP but temperature under 38.3 C.`]},{heading:`Aetiology in India`,points:[`Infection 40-60%: TB (extrapulmonary/disseminated), enteric fever, endocarditis, abscess, kala-azar, brucellosis, melioidosis, HIV.`,`Neoplasm 10-20%: lymphoma, leukaemia, RCC, hepatoma.`,`NIID 10-20%: AOSD, SLE, GCA/PMR, Takayasu, sarcoidosis, Kikuchi.`,`Miscellaneous: drug fever, thyroiditis, PE, factitious fever; undiagnosed 10-20%.`]},{heading:`History and examination - potentially diagnostic clues`,points:[`Confirm fever with a chart; night sweats, weight loss, exposures, drugs, past TB.`,`Repeat examination: fundus, nodes, murmurs, spleen, temporal arteries, spine, rectal examination.`]},{heading:`Stepwise investigation`,points:[`Stage 1 obligatory tests: CBC, ESR, CRP, LFT, LDH, ferritin, ANA, RF, urine, 3 blood cultures, HIV, IGRA, chest X-ray, ultrasound.`,`Stage 2 PDC-directed: echo, CECT, rK39, Brucella SAT, Xpert on fluids.`,`Stage 3: FDG PET-CT if CRP/ESR raised; Stage 4: excision node, marrow, liver, temporal artery biopsy.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Withhold empirical antibiotics and steroids in a stable patient; stop non-essential drugs.`,`Empirical trials only for unstable patients, culture-negative endocarditis, suspected miliary TB, suspected GCA.`,`Treat the cause: ATT by NTEP weight band, steroids/IL-1 blockade for AOSD, L-AmB for kala-azar.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`Undiagnosed PUO after a full work-up has a good prognosis; review every 2-4 weeks.`,`Malignancy carries the worst outcome; avoid harm from blind therapy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of the four Durack-Street categories`,`Flow chart: confirm fever - obligatory tests - PDC-directed tests - PET-CT - biopsy - watchful waiting`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Aetiology in India`,marks:2},{part:`Stepwise investigation with flow chart`,marks:4},{part:`Management and prognosis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Durack-Street`,`obligatory investigations`,`PDC`,`PET-CT`,`tuberculosis`,`AOSD`,`biopsy before treatment`]},{id:`infectious-fever-pyrexia-unknown-origin-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Short note: Role of FDG PET-CT in pyrexia of unknown origin.`,openingLines:[`18F-FDG PET-CT images glucose uptake by activated leucocytes and tumour cells, so it localises infection, inflammation and malignancy in one whole-body scan.`,`It is the preferred next test in classic PUO when obligatory investigations are negative and CRP or ESR is raised.`],answer:[{heading:`Principle`,points:[`FDG is taken up through GLUT transporters by neutrophils, macrophages and tumour cells and trapped after phosphorylation.`]},{heading:`Indications and yield`,points:[`Stage 1 negative with raised CRP/ESR; contributes to the diagnosis in about 40-60%.`,`Identifies a biopsy target (node, bone, vessel wall in large-vessel vasculitis).`,`A normal scan predicts spontaneous resolution and a good prognosis.`]},{heading:`Limitations`,points:[`Does not separate infection, tumour and inflammation - biopsy is mandatory.`,`Reduced sensitivity after steroids and with hyperglycaemia; physiological uptake in brain, heart and urinary tract.`,`Cost and availability in India; CECT is often done first.`]}],mustDraw:[`Position of PET-CT in the staged PUO work-up`],markSplit:[{part:`Principle`,marks:1},{part:`Indications and yield`,marks:2},{part:`Limitations and Indian context`,marks:2}],keywords:[`FDG`,`GLUT`,`biopsy target`,`negative predictive value`,`steroids reduce uptake`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Fever of Unknown Origin chapter`,`Durack DT, Street AC. Fever of unknown origin - re-examined and redefined. Curr Clin Top Infect Dis, 1991`,`Fautrel B et al. EULAR/PReS recommendations for the diagnosis and management of Still's disease, Ann Rheum Dis, 2024`,`ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria for giant cell arteritis; EULAR 2023 recommendations for large-vessel vasculitis`,`ESC 2023 Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 3: Diagnosis, 2024 update; NTEP Training Modules, MoHFW`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 11th edition, 2019`]}),e.push({id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock`,title:`Sepsis and septic shock: recognition and the first hour`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Sepsis** is **life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection** (an acute rise in **SOFA score of 2 or more**), and **septic shock** is sepsis needing **vasopressors to keep MAP 65 mmHg or more with lactate over 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid** (Sepsis-3, 2016); the first hour decides survival - **lactate, blood cultures, broad-spectrum antibiotics, 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid and noradrenaline** (SSC hour-1 bundle).`,keywords:[`Sepsis-3`,`septic shock`,`SOFA`,`qSOFA`,`NEWS2`,`SIRS`,`hour-1 bundle`,`Surviving Sepsis Campaign`,`lactate`,`noradrenaline`,`norepinephrine`,`vasopressin`,`hydrocortisone`,`Ringer lactate`,`balanced crystalloid`,`capillary refill time`,`source control`,`Phoenix criteria`,`MAP 65`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Sepsis-3 (JAMA 2016)** defines sepsis as **life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection**, operationalised as an acute **SOFA rise of 2 points or more** - this predicts in-hospital mortality of about 10%. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**Septic shock** is sepsis with circulatory and cellular-metabolic abnormality: **vasopressor needed to maintain MAP 65 mmHg or more and serum lactate over 2 mmol/L** despite adequate fluid - hospital mortality **over 40%**. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`The old terms **SIRS-based sepsis and 'severe sepsis'** were dropped in 2016 because SIRS is present in many uninfected inpatients and misses a minority with organ failure. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**Global burden (GBD, Lancet 2020)**: about **49 million cases and 11 million sepsis-related deaths in 2017** - roughly **1 in 5 deaths worldwide**, with the highest burden in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia. [Rudd, Lancet 2020]`,`**India**: in the **INDICAPS** multicentre ICU study, about **28% of ICU admissions had sepsis**, and sepsis carried the highest ICU mortality; India also carries one of the largest absolute numbers of sepsis deaths in GBD estimates, with **neonatal sepsis** a major share. [INDICAPS 2016]`,`**WHO resolution WHA 70.7 (2017)** made sepsis a global health priority, asking member states to improve prevention, recognition and management. [WHO 2017]`,`In India, the **commonest sources** are lung (pneumonia), urinary tract, abdomen and skin, and the tropical fevers - **scrub typhus, leptospirosis, falciparum malaria, melioidosis and dengue** - mimic or cause septic shock. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Resistant organisms** shape empirical choice: Indian ICU Gram-negatives show high ESBL and rising **carbapenem resistance in Klebsiella and Acinetobacter** in ICMR AMRSN surveillance. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Organisms**: Gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter) now cause more sepsis than Gram-positives (S. aureus, streptococci) in Indian ICUs; fungi (Candida) in long ICU stays. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Host risk**: age over 65 or infancy, **diabetes**, CKD, cirrhosis, malignancy, HIV, malnutrition, alcohol use, pregnancy and the puerperium, splenectomy, steroids and chemotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Healthcare risk**: urinary catheter, central line, mechanical ventilation, recent surgery, recent hospitalisation and prior broad-spectrum antibiotics (predict MDR organisms). [SSC 2021]`,`**Tropical sepsis**: scrub typhus and leptospirosis cause **vasculitic shock**, falciparum malaria causes severe malaria shock (algid malaria), and **melioidosis** causes septic shock in diabetics. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Toxin-mediated shock**: **staphylococcal and streptococcal toxic shock syndrome** (tampons, wounds, necrotising fasciitis) - superantigens cause massive cytokine release. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstetric sepsis**: septic abortion, puerperal endometritis, chorioamnionitis and pyelonephritis remain leading causes of maternal death in India. [WHO 2017]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the why behind the signs`,points:[`**Recognition**: PAMPs (LPS, lipoteichoic acid) and DAMPs from injured tissue bind **Toll-like receptors** and NOD-like receptors on innate cells, activating **NF-kappaB** and releasing **TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6** - the fever, tachycardia and acute-phase response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dysregulation**: the response is both **hyperinflammatory and immunosuppressive** at once - lymphocyte apoptosis and monocyte deactivation explain secondary infections and late deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vasodilatation**: inducible **nitric oxide synthase**, vasopressin deficiency and K-ATP channel opening cause **low systemic vascular resistance** - the warm, bounding, wide pulse pressure of early septic shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endothelial injury**: damage to the **glycocalyx** causes capillary leak, tissue oedema and intravascular hypovolaemia - why fluids help early but overload harms later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coagulation**: tissue factor expression, depletion of protein C and antithrombin, and impaired fibrinolysis cause **microvascular thrombosis and DIC** - low platelets are part of SOFA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lactate** rises mainly from **adrenergic-driven aerobic glycolysis** and reduced hepatic clearance, not only from tissue hypoxia - so it is a marker of severity rather than a pure oxygen-debt measure. [SSC 2021]`,`**Septic cardiomyopathy**: reversible biventricular dysfunction from cytokines and NO occurs in up to half - explains shock that does not respond to fluid and the role of **dobutamine**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Organ failure**: mitochondrial dysfunction and microcirculatory shunting cause AKI, ARDS, encephalopathy, ileus, cholestasis and adrenal insufficiency (**critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency**) - the basis for steroid therapy in shock. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and scoring - SOFA, qSOFA, NEWS2`,points:[`**SOFA** scores 6 organs from 0 to 4: **respiration (PaO2/FiO2), coagulation (platelets), liver (bilirubin), cardiovascular (MAP, vasopressors), CNS (GCS), renal (creatinine or urine output)** - baseline assumed 0 unless known dysfunction. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**qSOFA** has 3 items, 1 point each: **respiratory rate 22/min or more, altered mentation (GCS under 15), systolic BP 100 mmHg or less** - a score of 2 or more outside the ICU flags a high risk of death. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**SSC 2021 recommends against qSOFA as a single screening tool** compared with SIRS, NEWS or MEWS - qSOFA is specific but **insensitive**, so a score under 2 does not exclude sepsis. [SSC 2021]`,`**NEWS2 (RCP 2017)** scores RR, SpO2, supplemental oxygen, temperature, systolic BP, heart rate and consciousness (including new confusion): **5 or more - urgent review**, **7 or more - emergency response**, and a single parameter scoring 3 needs urgent review. [RCP NEWS2 2017]`,`**SIRS criteria** (2 or more of temperature over 38 or under 36 C, HR over 90, RR over 20 or PaCO2 under 32, WBC over 12,000 or under 4,000 or over 10% bands) remain a sensitive screen but are **not part of the sepsis definition**. [SSC 2021]`,`**Septic shock** in Sepsis-3 needs **both** vasopressor-dependent hypotension and lactate over 2 mmol/L after adequate fluid; hypotension with normal lactate is 'sepsis with hypotension'. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**Children**: the **Phoenix Sepsis Criteria (JAMA 2024)** define paediatric sepsis as suspected infection with a **Phoenix Sepsis Score of 2 or more** (respiratory, cardiovascular, coagulation, neurological), and septic shock as sepsis with at least 1 cardiovascular point. [Phoenix 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Source symptoms**: cough and breathlessness (pneumonia), dysuria, loin pain and rigors (urosepsis), abdominal pain and vomiting (cholangitis, perforation), spreading painful skin (cellulitis, necrotising fasciitis), headache and neck stiffness (meningitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early warning symptoms** families notice: **new confusion or drowsiness**, breathlessness, reduced urine, mottled cold skin, 'feeling like I might die' - the UK Sepsis Trust and WHO use these in public messaging. [WHO 2017]`,`**Atypical presentation in the elderly**: no fever (or hypothermia), falls, delirium, reduced intake and new incontinence - **hypothermia under 36 C carries a worse prognosis** than fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetics and immunosuppressed** may have blunted fever and pain, so a 'silent' source (emphysematous pyelonephritis, liver abscess, melioidosis) is common. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Exposure history for tropical shock**: farm work and grass (scrub typhus), floodwater (leptospirosis), travel to a malarious area, soil exposure in diabetes (melioidosis). [ICMR 2022]`,`**MDR risk history**: hospitalisation or antibiotics in the last 90 days, dialysis, nursing-home residence and known colonisation with ESBL or CRE - each pushes the empirical choice up the ladder. [SSC 2021]`,`**Allergy and medication history**: beta-lactam allergy, anticoagulants, steroids, SGLT2 inhibitors (euglycaemic ketoacidosis) and metformin (lactate). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Vital signs first**: temperature, HR, RR (the most sensitive and most often missed sign), BP with **MAP = (SBP + 2 x DBP)/3**, SpO2, GCS and capillary glucose. [SSC 2021]`,`**Warm shock**: flushed warm peripheries, bounding pulse, wide pulse pressure and low diastolic pressure - **a diastolic under 40 mmHg** signals profound vasodilatation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cold shock**: cool mottled skin, weak pulse and narrow pulse pressure - suggests hypovolaemia or septic cardiomyopathy, common late and in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Capillary refill time over 3 seconds** and a **mottling score** spreading from the knees correlate with mortality; CRT normalisation is a useful resuscitation target. [ANDROMEDA-SHOCK 2019]`,`**Hunt the source**: chest crackles, costovertebral tenderness, Murphy sign, guarding, cellulitis or crepitus, **pain out of proportion** (necrotising fasciitis), **eschar** (scrub typhus), conjunctival suffusion (leptospirosis), purpura (meningococcaemia), lines and catheters. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Organ dysfunction signs**: confusion, oliguria under 0.5 mL/kg/h, jaundice, bleeding, ileus, tachypnoea with low SpO2 - each adds to SOFA. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**Fluid responsiveness at the bedside**: **passive leg raise** (a 10% or greater rise in stroke volume or pulse pressure), IVC collapsibility on ultrasound, and lung ultrasound B-lines warning of overload. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - shock that is not sepsis`,points:[`**Hypovolaemic shock** (GI bleed, burns, diarrhoea): cold, collapsed veins, low JVP, haemoglobin falls or haemoconcentration - no infective source. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiogenic shock** (MI, myocarditis): raised JVP, crackles, gallop, ECG changes, poor LV on echo - fluids worsen it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstructive shock** (massive PE, tamponade, tension pneumothorax): raised JVP with clear lungs, RV dilatation or effusion on POCUS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaphylaxis**: sudden onset after an exposure, urticaria, angioedema and bronchospasm - IM adrenaline 0.5 mg is the treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dengue shock syndrome**: plasma leak at defervescence with **rising haematocrit**, narrow pulse pressure (20 mmHg or less) and low platelets - needs titrated fluid boluses, not the 30 mL/kg sepsis block; see the dengue topic. [WHO 2025]`,`**Adrenal crisis, thyroid storm, pancreatitis, DKA and heat stroke** mimic septic shock with fever or hypotension; check glucose, sodium, potassium and history of steroid use. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe falciparum malaria**: may present as shock with acidosis - a **malaria RDT and smear in every febrile shock** in India; artesunate is the treatment. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Blood cultures**: at least **2 sets (aerobic and anaerobic) from separate sites**, 20 mL per set in adults, **before antibiotics**, but never delay antibiotics more than 45 minutes to get them. [SSC 2021]`,`**Serum lactate**: over 2 mmol/L abnormal, **4 mmol/L or more** triggers the 30 mL/kg fluid bolus; **repeat within 2-4 hours** if raised - a falling lactate guides resuscitation. [SSC 2021]`,`**Bedside panel**: CBC, creatinine, urea, electrolytes, bilirubin, ALT, glucose, **arterial blood gas** (pH, bicarbonate, PaO2/FiO2), coagulation (PT/INR, fibrinogen) - each maps to a SOFA component. [Sepsis-3 2016]`,`**India-specific tests in febrile shock**: malaria RDT and smear, dengue NS1/IgM, scrub typhus IgM ELISA, leptospira IgM, and urine and site cultures - they change the drug (doxycycline, artesunate). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Source imaging**: chest X-ray, **POCUS** (lungs, gallbladder, kidneys, abdomen, heart), then CT abdomen or other imaging within hours when a drainable source is possible. [SSC 2021]`,`**Procalcitonin**: SSC 2021 recommends **against using PCT to decide whether to start antibiotics**, but suggests PCT plus clinical assessment to help **decide when to stop**. [SSC 2021]`,`**Advanced**: echocardiography for cardiac function, cortisol is not needed before steroids, multiplex PCR panels for rapid organism and resistance gene identification where available. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Hour-1 bundle and first-hour management`,points:[`**Hour-1 bundle (SSC 2018, retained in 2021)**: measure lactate (re-measure if over 2), obtain blood cultures before antibiotics, give **broad-spectrum antibiotics**, give **30 mL/kg crystalloid** for hypotension or lactate 4 or more, and start **vasopressors** if hypotensive during or after fluids to keep **MAP 65 mmHg or more**. [SSC 2021]`,`**Antibiotic timing**: within **1 hour** for possible septic shock or high likelihood of sepsis; when sepsis is only possible and there is no shock, a **rapid assessment** and antibiotics **within 3 hours** if concern persists. [SSC 2021]`,`**Fluids**: at least **30 mL/kg IV crystalloid within the first 3 hours** (about 2 L for 70 kg) given as 500 mL boluses with reassessment; **balanced crystalloids (Ringer lactate) are suggested over 0.9% saline**. [SSC 2021]`,`**Albumin**: SSC 2021 suggested albumin when large volumes of crystalloid have been given; library-wide updates note **SSC 2026 now suggests crystalloid alone over adding albumin** - gelatin and **starches are not used** (HES increases AKI and death). [SSC 2021]`,`**Noradrenaline** is the **first-line vasopressor**: start **0.05-0.1 microgram/kg/min**, titrate to **MAP 65 mmHg**; it may be started through a **large peripheral vein** (proximal, checked hourly) rather than delaying for central access. [SSC 2021]`,`**Add vasopressin 0.03 units/min** when noradrenaline reaches **0.25-0.5 microgram/kg/min**; add **dobutamine** (or switch to adrenaline) for cardiac dysfunction with persistent hypoperfusion; **dopamine** only in selected bradycardic patients. [SSC 2021]`,`**Hydrocortisone 200 mg/day** (50 mg IV 6-hourly or infusion) is suggested for septic shock with **ongoing noradrenaline 0.25 microgram/kg/min or more for at least 4 hours**; fludrocortisone 50 microgram daily is optional (APROCCHSS). [SSC 2021]`,`**Source control** within **6-12 hours**: drain abscesses, relieve obstructed urinary tract or bile duct, debride necrotising infection, remove infected lines after new access. [SSC 2021]`,`**Oxygen** to SpO2 94-98% (88-92% if hypercapnic risk); **high-flow nasal oxygen** over non-invasive ventilation for hypoxaemic respiratory failure; lung-protective ventilation **6 mL/kg** predicted body weight with plateau under 30 cmH2O if intubated. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Empirical antibiotics in sepsis (Indian protocol)`,points:[`**Community-acquired sepsis, no MDR risk**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily** (or 2 g 12-hourly for meningitis) - add **doxycycline 100 mg 12-hourly (IV or oral)** or azithromycin when scrub typhus or leptospirosis is possible. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Healthcare-associated or MDR risk**: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly** or **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly** (2 g in meningitis); add **vancomycin** (loading dose 25 mg/kg) or teicoplanin if MRSA risk (lines, skin source, known carriage). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Carbapenem-resistant risk** (known CRE colonisation, ICU outbreak): **ceftazidime-avibactam with or without aztreonam**, or polymyxin-based combinations - only with ID or microbiology input (see the MDR Gram-negative topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Double Gram-negative cover** (adding amikacin 15-20 mg/kg once daily) is suggested only with **high MDR risk**, and stopped once susceptibilities are known. [SSC 2021]`,`**Antifungal** empirical therapy (an echinocandin) only in high-risk patients - prolonged ICU stay, TPN, multifocal Candida colonisation, abdominal surgery with leak. [SSC 2021]`,`**Beta-lactam dosing**: give a full **loading dose immediately**, then maintenance as **prolonged (extended or continuous) infusion** over bolus - the BLING III trial and 2024 meta-analysis supported a mortality benefit. [SSC 2021]`,`**Severe malaria with shock**: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg** at 0, 12 and 24 hours then daily, plus antibiotics because bacterial co-infection is common. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Reassess daily for de-escalation** and stop at **7 days or less** for most infections with adequate source control; see the empirical antibiotics topic. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`After the first hour - IPD and ICU care`,points:[`**Admission criteria**: any hypotension, lactate 2 or more, new organ dysfunction (SOFA rise 2), NEWS2 7 or more, need for oxygen, or vulnerable host - **ICU or HDU** for vasopressors. [SSC 2021]`,`**Monitoring**: HR, BP, MAP, RR, SpO2 and GCS every **15 minutes** in the first hour, then hourly; **urine output target 0.5 mL/kg/h or more** by catheter; arterial line once on vasopressors. [SSC 2021]`,`**Guide further fluid** by dynamic measures (passive leg raise, stroke volume variation) and **capillary refill time** rather than CVP alone; stop fluid when unresponsive or overloaded. [SSC 2021]`,`**Restrictive fluids** after initial resuscitation are safe (CLASSIC 2022, CLOVERS 2023 showed no mortality difference); plan **de-resuscitation** with diuretics once shock resolves and overload develops. [CLOVERS 2023]`,`**Blood transfusion** when **haemoglobin under 7 g/dL** (restrictive strategy); platelets only if under 10,000 (or under 20,000 with bleeding risk or under 50,000 for procedures). [SSC 2021]`,`**Glucose**: start insulin at **180 mg/dL or more**, target **144-180 mg/dL**; **VTE prophylaxis** with LMWH unless contraindicated; **stress ulcer prophylaxis** in those with GI bleeding risk. [SSC 2021]`,`**Sodium bicarbonate** only for **pH 7.2 or less with AKI stage 2-3**; **vitamin C, IVIG and polymyxin B haemoperfusion are not recommended**. [SSC 2021]`,`**Goals of care**: discuss prognosis and goals with family **within 72 hours** of ICU admission and integrate palliative care principles where appropriate. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`District hospital protocol (resource-limited setting)`,points:[`**Triage and screen** every febrile or unwell patient with NEWS2 (or SIRS plus mental status), and keep a **sepsis trolley** with cultures, cannulae, Ringer lactate, ceftriaxone, doxycycline, artesunate and noradrenaline. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Minutes 0-15**: oxygen, **two 18G cannulae**, blood cultures (or at least one set), point-of-care lactate or ABG, glucose, malaria RDT; **minutes 15-60**: first antibiotic dose and 30 mL/kg Ringer lactate in 500 mL boluses. [SSC 2021]`,`**Noradrenaline infusion through a peripheral line**: 4 mg in 50 mL 5% dextrose (80 microgram/mL), start at **0.05-0.1 microgram/kg/min** in a large proximal vein, check the site hourly; if unavailable, **adrenaline** is the alternative, and **dopamine** only if neither is available. [SSC 2021]`,`**When lactate is unavailable**, use **capillary refill time, mental status and urine output** as perfusion markers - the ANDROMEDA-SHOCK trial supports CRT-targeted resuscitation. [ANDROMEDA-SHOCK 2019]`,`**Refer early** (after the first dose of antibiotic and with fluids running) to an ICU when vasopressors, ventilation, dialysis or surgical source control are needed - send cultures, a written resuscitation chart and the drugs given. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Severe fluid caution** in malnutrition, severe anaemia, heart failure and in **children without shock** - the FEAST trial showed bolus harm in African children with severe febrile illness without hypotension. [FEAST 2011]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: use the **obstetric early warning score (MEOWS)** and the omqSOFA; left lateral tilt after 20 weeks, avoid tetracyclines and aminoglycosides where possible, and involve obstetrics for source control (evacuation of retained products). [WHO 2017]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, lower MAP target may be acceptable (**60-65 mmHg in those aged 65 or more**, per the 65 trial and later SSC advice), and careful fluid in heart failure. [SSC 2021]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: give a **full loading dose** of antibiotics regardless of renal function, then adjust maintenance for GFR; consider line infection in dialysis catheters. [SSC 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis**: lower baseline BP, relative adrenal insufficiency, high risk of AKI-HRS - albumin has a role in SBP-related sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neutropenic sepsis**: antipseudomonal beta-lactam (**piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime or meropenem**) within 1 hour; add vancomycin for line infection, skin source or haemodynamic instability. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Children**: recognise using Phoenix criteria and the **SSC Children 2020** guideline - in settings without ICU, give fluid boluses only with hypotension, up to **40 mL/kg in the first hour** in 10-20 mL/kg aliquots where ICU is available, with frequent reassessment. [SSC Children 2020]`,`**Diabetes**: consider melioidosis (add **meropenem or ceftazidime** in endemic areas), emphysematous pyelonephritis and necrotising fasciitis; keep glucose 144-180 mg/dL. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Mortality**: about **10% for sepsis and over 40% for septic shock**; every hour of antibiotic delay in septic shock raises mortality (by about 4-8% per hour in observational data). [SSC 2021]`,`**Organ complications**: ARDS, AKI (up to half need renal support in shock), DIC, acute liver failure, septic encephalopathy, ileus and critical illness polyneuromyopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-sepsis syndrome**: about half of survivors have **physical, cognitive or psychological impairment**, and readmission within 90 days is common - often for recurrent infection. [SSC 2021]`,`**Poor prognostic markers**: **lactate 4 or more**, persistent need for high-dose vasopressors, hypothermia, high SOFA, MDR organism, delayed source control and older age. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic harm**: fluid overload (positive balance predicts death), C. difficile and resistant superinfection after prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics, line infections and pressure injuries. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**After discharge**, SSC 2021 recommends **screening for physical, cognitive and emotional problems**, referral to post-critical illness follow-up, and medication reconciliation. [SSC 2021]`,`**Prevention**: vaccination (**pneumococcal, influenza, Hib and typhoid conjugate**), hand hygiene, catheter and line bundles, early UTI and cellulitis treatment, diabetes control, and clean delivery practices. [WHO 2017]`,`**Public awareness**: teach families the warning signs (confusion, breathlessness, mottled skin, no urine for 12 hours) and to seek care urgently. [WHO 2017]`,`**Antimicrobial stewardship** after recovery: document organism, susceptibility and duration in the discharge summary so the next prescriber does not escalate blindly. [ICMR 2022]`,`**National link**: IDSP reporting of clustered sepsis-causing tropical fevers and the **Kayakalp** and infection-control programme in public hospitals target HAI reduction. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**SSC 2021** (still the core reference): against qSOFA alone for screening; antibiotics in 1 hour (shock/probable) versus 3 hours (possible); balanced crystalloids; peripheral noradrenaline start; CRT as adjunct; hydrocortisone for ongoing vasopressor need; prolonged beta-lactam infusion; against vitamin C. [SSC 2021]`,`**SSC adult update (2026)** as reflected across this library: NEWS2/MEWS/SIRS over qSOFA, crystalloid alone over adding albumin, **MAP 60-65 mmHg suggested in those aged 65 or more**, peripheral vasopressor start, and active fluid removal after resuscitation. [SSC 2026]`,`**Phoenix Sepsis Criteria (JAMA 2024)** replaced the 2005 paediatric SIRS-based definitions. [Phoenix 2024]`,`**BLING III (JAMA 2024)** and its meta-analysis supported **continuous or prolonged beta-lactam infusion**; **BALANCE (NEJM 2025)** showed **7 days was non-inferior to 14 days** for uncomplicated bloodstream infection. [BALANCE 2025]`,`**Viva classics**: define sepsis and septic shock (Sepsis-3); list qSOFA and why SSC rejects it as a screen; the 5 hour-1 elements; first-line vasopressor and when to add vasopressin and steroids; lactate thresholds 2 and 4. [SSC 2021]`,`**Common mistakes**: waiting for cultures before antibiotics, giving 30 mL/kg to dengue shock, using dopamine first, starting steroids in sepsis without shock, and forgetting doxycycline for scrub typhus in Indian febrile shock. [ICMR 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Definitions and scores`,columns:[`Term`,`Criteria`,`Use`,`Note`],rows:[[`Sepsis (Sepsis-3)`,`Suspected infection plus acute SOFA rise of 2 or more`,`Definition`,`In-hospital mortality about 10%`],[`Septic shock`,`Vasopressor for MAP 65 or more and lactate over 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid`,`Definition`,`Mortality over 40%`],[`qSOFA`,`RR 22 or more, altered mentation, SBP 100 or less (2 of 3)`,`Prognosis outside ICU`,`SSC 2021: not a single screening tool`],[`NEWS2`,`RR, SpO2, oxygen, temperature, SBP, HR, consciousness`,`Screening and escalation`,`5 or more urgent; 7 or more emergency`],[`SIRS`,`2 of temperature, HR over 90, RR over 20, WBC abnormal`,`Sensitive screen`,`Not part of the definition`],[`Phoenix (children)`,`Phoenix Sepsis Score 2 or more with suspected infection`,`Paediatric definition (2024)`,`Shock: 1 or more cardiovascular point`]]},{heading:`SOFA score (simplified)`,columns:[`Organ`,`Measure`,`Score 1-2`,`Score 3-4`],rows:[[`Respiration`,`PaO2/FiO2 (mmHg)`,`Under 400 (1), under 300 (2)`,`Under 200 (3), under 100 (4) with respiratory support`],[`Coagulation`,`Platelets (per microlitre)`,`Under 150,000 (1), under 100,000 (2)`,`Under 50,000 (3), under 20,000 (4)`],[`Liver`,`Bilirubin (mg/dL)`,`1.2-1.9 (1), 2.0-5.9 (2)`,`6.0-11.9 (3), 12 or more (4)`],[`Cardiovascular`,`MAP and vasopressors`,`MAP under 70 (1), dopamine 5 or less or any dobutamine (2)`,`Noradrenaline 0.1 or less (3), over 0.1 microgram/kg/min (4)`],[`CNS`,`Glasgow coma scale`,`13-14 (1), 10-12 (2)`,`6-9 (3), under 6 (4)`],[`Renal`,`Creatinine (mg/dL) or urine output`,`1.2-1.9 (1), 2.0-3.4 (2)`,`3.5-4.9 or under 500 mL/day (3), 5 or more or under 200 mL/day (4)`]]},{heading:`Drugs in the first hour of septic shock`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ringer lactate (balanced crystalloid)`,`30 mL/kg IV in 500 mL boluses`,`Within 3 hours, then by dynamic measures`,`Overload, pulmonary oedema; caution in heart failure, severe anaemia`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.05-0.1 microgram/kg/min IV start, titrate to MAP 65 (usual up to 0.5-1)`,`Titrate every 5-15 min; wean once MAP stable`,`Extravasation necrosis, arrhythmia, digital ischaemia`],[`Vasopressin`,`0.03 units/min IV fixed`,`Add when noradrenaline 0.25-0.5 microgram/kg/min`,`Digital and mesenteric ischaemia; not titrated upward`],[`Adrenaline`,`0.05-0.3 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Alternative or add-on vasopressor`,`Tachyarrhythmia, raised lactate (beta-2 effect)`],[`Dobutamine`,`2.5-20 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Septic cardiomyopathy with hypoperfusion despite MAP 65`,`Tachycardia, hypotension (vasodilatation)`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`50 mg IV 6-hourly (200 mg/day) plus optional fludrocortisone 50 microgram oral daily`,`Start if noradrenaline 0.25 or more for 4 h; taper when off vasopressors`,`Hyperglycaemia, hypernatraemia, superinfection; not for sepsis without shock`],[`Ceftriaxone plus doxycycline`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD; doxycycline 100 mg IV/oral 12-hourly`,`Community sepsis; review at 48-72 h`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; avoid doxycycline in pregnancy`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly (extended infusion) / 1 g IV 8-hourly`,`HAI or MDR risk; de-escalate on culture`,`Seizures (high-dose beta-lactam in AKI), C. difficile`]]},{heading:`Shock types - differential`,columns:[`Shock type`,`Skin and JVP`,`Key bedside clue`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Septic (distributive)`,`Warm early, cold late; JVP low-normal`,`Fever source, wide pulse pressure, lactate rise`,`Cultures, antibiotics, crystalloid, noradrenaline`],[`Hypovolaemic`,`Cold, collapsed veins, low JVP`,`Bleeding or fluid loss`,`Volume or blood`],[`Cardiogenic`,`Cold, raised JVP, crackles`,`Chest pain, ECG changes, poor LV on echo`,`Inotrope, revascularisation; avoid fluid load`],[`Obstructive`,`Raised JVP, clear lungs`,`PE, tamponade or tension pneumothorax on POCUS`,`Thrombolysis, pericardiocentesis, decompression`],[`Anaphylactic`,`Flushed, urticaria, angioedema`,`Exposure minutes before`,`IM adrenaline 0.5 mg`],[`Dengue shock`,`Cold, narrow pulse pressure 20 or less`,`Rising haematocrit at defervescence`,`Titrated crystalloid 5-10 mL/kg/h, not 30 mL/kg block`]]},{heading:`Lactate and perfusion targets`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Threshold`,`Meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`Lactate`,`Over 2 mmol/L`,`Hypoperfusion or stress; defines shock with vasopressor`,`Repeat in 2-4 h`],[`Lactate`,`4 mmol/L or more`,`High risk of death`,`30 mL/kg crystalloid, urgent senior review`],[`MAP`,`65 mmHg (60-65 if aged 65 or over)`,`Organ perfusion threshold`,`Noradrenaline if below after fluid`],[`Capillary refill time`,`Over 3 seconds`,`Poor peripheral perfusion`,`Reassess fluid responsiveness`],[`Urine output`,`Under 0.5 mL/kg/h`,`Renal hypoperfusion or AKI`,`Catheter, review fluid and MAP`],[`Haemoglobin`,`Under 7 g/dL`,`Transfusion threshold`,`Transfuse packed cells`]]}],redFlags:[`Systolic BP under 90 mmHg or MAP under 65 mmHg after a fluid bolus - septic shock.`,`Lactate 4 mmol/L or more - high-risk sepsis needing immediate resuscitation.`,`New confusion, GCS fall or agitation with fever - organ dysfunction.`,`Respiratory rate 25/min or more or SpO2 under 92% despite oxygen.`,`Urine output under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 2 hours or no urine for 12 hours.`,`Mottled skin, capillary refill over 3 seconds or purpuric rash.`,`NEWS2 of 7 or more, or neutropenia with fever.`,`Pain out of proportion with skin changes - possible necrotising fasciitis needing surgery.`],pearls:[`Sepsis-3: **infection plus SOFA rise of 2**; shock: **vasopressor for MAP 65 plus lactate over 2**.`,`**qSOFA predicts death but misses sepsis** - screen with NEWS2 or SIRS.`,`Hour-1 bundle mnemonic **'LCAFV'**: Lactate, Cultures, Antibiotics, Fluids, Vasopressors.`,`**Respiratory rate** is the most sensitive and most neglected vital sign in sepsis.`,`**Noradrenaline first**, vasopressin second, steroids third (at 0.25 microgram/kg/min for 4 hours).`,`Give the **full loading dose** of antibiotic even in AKI; adjust only the maintenance.`,`In India add **doxycycline** to ceftriaxone in febrile shock - scrub typhus is a common cause.`,`Dengue shock is not septic shock - **no 30 mL/kg block**; titrate to haematocrit.`,`Lactate is a **severity marker** (adrenergic glycolysis), not purely an oxygen-debt measure.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-q1`,stem:`According to Sepsis-3, septic shock is identified clinically by which combination?`,options:[`SIRS criteria with systolic BP under 90 mmHg`,`qSOFA of 2 or more with lactate over 4 mmol/L`,`Vasopressor required to maintain MAP 65 mmHg or more and lactate over 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid`,`Hypotension responsive to a 30 mL/kg fluid bolus`],answer:2,explanation:`Sepsis-3 defines septic shock as sepsis needing a vasopressor to keep MAP at 65 mmHg or more with lactate over 2 mmol/L after adequate fluid resuscitation. SIRS criteria were removed from the definitions, qSOFA is a prognostic prompt rather than a shock definition, and fluid-responsive hypotension without vasopressor need does not meet the shock criteria.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-q2`,stem:`A 60-year-old diabetic man with urosepsis remains hypotensive (MAP 58 mmHg) after 2 litres of Ringer lactate. Central venous access will take 2 hours. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start noradrenaline through a large proximal peripheral vein and titrate to MAP 65 mmHg`,`Give another 3 litres of crystalloid before any vasopressor`,`Start dopamine at 10 microgram/kg/min`,`Wait for the central line before starting any vasopressor`],answer:0,explanation:`SSC guidance makes noradrenaline the first-line vasopressor and suggests starting it peripherally rather than delaying for central access, with hourly site checks. Large further fluid volumes without a dynamic assessment risk overload, dopamine causes more arrhythmias and is reserved for selected bradycardic patients, and waiting 2 hours with a MAP under 65 worsens organ perfusion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-q3`,stem:`In septic shock, when does the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021 suggest adding IV hydrocortisone?`,options:[`In every patient with sepsis at diagnosis`,`Only when a random cortisol is under 10 microgram/dL`,`When lactate exceeds 4 mmol/L`,`When noradrenaline of 0.25 microgram/kg/min or more is needed for at least 4 hours`],answer:3,explanation:`SSC 2021 suggests hydrocortisone 200 mg/day for septic shock with ongoing vasopressor need, defined as noradrenaline 0.25 microgram/kg/min or more for at least 4 hours. Steroids are not given in sepsis without shock, a random cortisol is not required before starting them, and lactate level alone is not a trigger.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-q4`,stem:`Why does the Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommend against qSOFA as a single screening tool for sepsis?`,options:[`It requires laboratory values not available at triage`,`It is specific but insensitive, so many patients with sepsis score under 2`,`It overestimates mortality in young patients`,`It was validated only in children`],answer:1,explanation:`qSOFA uses only bedside items (RR 22 or more, altered mentation, SBP 100 or less) and predicts death well, but it is insensitive, missing many patients early in sepsis, so SIRS, NEWS2 or MEWS are preferred for screening. It needs no laboratory values, it was derived in adults, and over-estimating mortality is not the reason for the recommendation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-q5`,stem:`A 35-year-old farmer from Tamil Nadu presents in November with 6 days of fever, hypotension, SpO2 90% and a black crusted lesion in the groin. Which empirical regimen is most appropriate in the first hour after cultures?`,options:[`Meropenem plus colistin`,`Ceftriaxone alone`,`Ceftriaxone plus doxycycline (or azithromycin)`,`Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate`],answer:2,explanation:`The eschar, season and farm exposure suggest scrub typhus with septic shock, so ceftriaxone for undifferentiated bacterial sepsis plus doxycycline or azithromycin for Orientia is the right empirical start. Ceftriaxone alone has no activity against Orientia, meropenem plus colistin is unjustified Reserve-level therapy for community sepsis, and an oral agent is inappropriate in shock.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define sepsis and septic shock as per Sepsis-3. Describe the pathophysiology and outline the management of septic shock in the first hour in a district hospital.`,openingLines:[`Sepsis is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection, identified by an acute rise in SOFA score of 2 or more (Sepsis-3, 2016).`,`Septic shock is sepsis needing vasopressors to maintain MAP 65 mmHg or more with lactate over 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid, with mortality over 40%.`],answer:[{heading:`Definitions and screening`,points:[`Sepsis-3 definitions; SOFA components; qSOFA and why SSC 2021 rejects it as a single screen; NEWS2 5 and 7 thresholds.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`PAMPs/DAMPs, TLRs, NF-kappaB, cytokines; vasodilatation via iNOS; glycocalyx injury and leak; DIC; septic cardiomyopathy; lactate from adrenergic glycolysis; immunoparalysis.`]},{heading:`Hour-1 bundle`,points:[`Lactate, 2 blood cultures, broad-spectrum antibiotic, 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in 3 h, noradrenaline for MAP 65.`,`Antibiotics within 1 h for shock or probable sepsis; within 3 h if only possible.`]},{heading:`District hospital protocol`,points:[`Minutes 0-15: oxygen, two cannulae, cultures, POC lactate, glucose, malaria RDT; 15-60: ceftriaxone plus doxycycline, Ringer lactate boluses.`,`Peripheral noradrenaline (adrenaline if unavailable); CRT and urine output when lactate unavailable.`]},{heading:`Beyond the first hour`,points:[`Vasopressin, hydrocortisone, source control in 6-12 h, monitoring targets (MAP, urine, CRT, lactate), Hb 7, glucose 144-180, VTE prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Referral and prognosis`,points:[`Refer for ICU support after the first dose; mortality 10% sepsis, over 40% shock; post-sepsis syndrome.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of the hour-1 bundle with timings`,`Vasopressor ladder: noradrenaline, vasopressin, hydrocortisone, dobutamine`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`First-hour management with flow chart`,marks:4},{part:`Further care and referral`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Sepsis-3`,`SOFA`,`qSOFA`,`hour-1 bundle`,`noradrenaline`,`lactate`,`Ringer lactate`,`source control`]},{id:`infectious-fever-sepsis-septic-shock-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate qSOFA, SIRS and NEWS2 as tools in the recognition of sepsis.`,openingLines:[`qSOFA, SIRS and NEWS2 are bedside tools; only SOFA forms part of the Sepsis-3 definition.`,`SSC 2021 recommends against qSOFA as a single screening tool, preferring the more sensitive SIRS, NEWS or MEWS.`],answer:[{heading:`Components`,points:[`qSOFA: RR 22 or more, altered mentation, SBP 100 or less.`,`SIRS: temperature, HR over 90, RR over 20, WBC abnormal.`,`NEWS2: RR, SpO2, oxygen, temperature, SBP, HR, consciousness.`]},{heading:`Performance`,points:[`qSOFA specific, insensitive - predicts death; SIRS sensitive, non-specific; NEWS2 balanced and tracks deterioration.`]},{heading:`Use`,points:[`qSOFA 2 or more - prompt to look for organ dysfunction; NEWS2 5 urgent, 7 emergency; SIRS - screen, not definition.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table of qSOFA, SIRS and NEWS2`],markSplit:[{part:`Components`,marks:2},{part:`Performance and use`,marks:3}],keywords:[`qSOFA`,`SIRS`,`NEWS2`,`sensitivity`,`screening`]}],references:[`Singer M et al. The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3), JAMA, 2016`,`Evans L et al. Surviving Sepsis Campaign: International Guidelines for Management of Sepsis and Septic Shock 2021, Crit Care Med / Intensive Care Med, 2021`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Sepsis and Septic Shock chapter`,`Schlapbach LJ et al. International Consensus Criteria for Pediatric Sepsis and Septic Shock (Phoenix), JAMA, 2024`,`Royal College of Physicians. National Early Warning Score (NEWS) 2, 2017`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022; ICMR Standard Treatment Workflow - Sepsis`,`Rudd KE et al. Global, regional and national sepsis incidence and mortality, 1990-2017, Lancet, 2020`]}),e.push({id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr`,title:`Empirical antibiotic therapy: ICMR Treatment Guidelines and the AWaRe classification`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Empirical antibiotic therapy** is the choice of an antibiotic **before the organism is known**, based on the **syndrome, likely pathogens, local antibiogram, host risk and severity**; in India it follows the **ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes (2019, updated 2022)** and the **WHO AWaRe classification** (Access, Watch, Reserve), with **cultures first, the narrowest effective Access drug, review at 48-72 hours and the shortest effective course**.`,keywords:[`ICMR treatment guidelines`,`AWaRe`,`Access Watch Reserve`,`WHO AWaRe antibiotic book`,`empirical therapy`,`definitive therapy`,`de-escalation`,`antibiotic time-out`,`short-course therapy`,`antimicrobial stewardship`,`Schedule H1`,`Red Line campaign`,`NAP-AMR`,`antibiogram`,`ceftriaxone`,`amoxicillin`,`nitrofurantoin`,`doxycycline`,`piperacillin-tazobactam`,`carbapenem`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology of antibiotic use`,points:[`**Empirical therapy** treats the likely pathogens of a syndrome before culture results; **definitive (targeted) therapy** follows identification and susceptibility; **prophylaxis** prevents infection in an uninfected patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India is the largest consumer of antibiotics** in absolute terms; in a national private-sector sales analysis (Koya et al, Lancet Reg Health SE Asia 2022), **Watch antibiotics made up about 55%** of consumption, **Access only about 27%**, and **azithromycin** was the single most consumed molecule. [Koya 2022]`,`The same analysis found a large share of antibiotic formulations sold were **unapproved by the central regulator** or were **fixed-dose combinations** without a rationale. [Koya 2022]`,`**Global AMR burden (GRAM, Lancet 2022)**: in 2019 bacterial AMR was directly responsible for **1.27 million deaths** and associated with **4.95 million**; GRAM 2024 forecasts **1.91 million attributable deaths a year by 2050**. [GRAM 2024]`,`**ICMR AMRSN** surveillance shows high ESBL rates in E. coli and Klebsiella, rising **carbapenem resistance** in Klebsiella and Acinetobacter, and about **40% MRSA** among S. aureus - the evidence behind ICMR empirical choices. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**WHO target**: at least **60% of national antibiotic use from the Access group** (GPW13, 2019), raised to **70% by 2030** in the **UN General Assembly political declaration on AMR (2024)**. [UNGA 2024]`,`An estimated **50% of antibiotic prescriptions** in primary care are unnecessary - viral URTI, acute diarrhoea and undifferentiated fever are the main drivers. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Principles of empirical prescribing`,points:[`**Decide if an antibiotic is needed at all**: viral URTI, most acute watery diarrhoea, acute bronchitis and asymptomatic bacteriuria (except pregnancy and before urological procedures) need **no antibiotic**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Send cultures before the first dose** in moderate-severe infection: 2 blood culture sets, urine, sputum, pus or CSF as indicated - culture after antibiotics often fails. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Choose by the syndrome**: site (lung, urine, skin, abdomen, CNS), setting (community versus hospital), host (diabetes, immunosuppression, pregnancy, renal function) and severity (sepsis or not). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Use local data**: the hospital **antibiogram** (cumulative susceptibility, updated yearly) overrides a national guideline when local resistance differs. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Start narrow in stable patients, broad in septic ones**: the sicker the patient, the broader the empirical cover - then **de-escalate** once results return. [SSC 2021]`,`**Right dose, route and timing**: full loading dose, oral where absorption is reliable (fluoroquinolones, linezolid, doxycycline, metronidazole have near-complete bioavailability), IV for sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Document** the indication, the planned duration and a review date on every antibiotic order - the core of the ICMR AMSP and WHO policy guidance. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`]},{heading:`Why it matters - pharmacology and resistance mechanism`,points:[`**Selection pressure**: every antibiotic course kills susceptible flora and selects resistant gut organisms (ESBL, CRE) - **broad-spectrum Watch drugs select resistance most**, which is why they are flagged. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**ESBL enzymes** hydrolyse third-generation cephalosporins (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime) - this is why ceftriaxone fails for hospital-acquired Gram-negative infection in India. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Carbapenemases (NDM-1, OXA-48)** are widespread in India and inactivate meropenem - each unnecessary carbapenem course accelerates them, so carbapenems are reserved for ESBL or septic patients. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Collateral damage**: cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones and clindamycin carry the highest risk of **C. difficile infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intracellular organisms** (Rickettsia, Orientia, Leptospira early, Chlamydia, Mycoplasma) are not covered by beta-lactams - hence doxycycline or azithromycin in Indian febrile syndromes. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Short courses** reduce selection pressure without loss of efficacy in most infections, because bacterial burden falls rapidly after the first days and host defences clear the rest. [Spellberg, JAMA IM 2016]`]},{heading:`WHO AWaRe classification (2023 update)`,points:[`**WHO AWaRe** (first 2017, updated 2019, 2021, 2023 and **2025 with the EML**) classifies antibiotics into **Access, Watch and Reserve** to guide selection and monitor use; the 2025 list classifies about **268 antibiotics**. [WHO AWaRe 2025]`,`**Access**: first- or second-choice drugs for common infections with **lower resistance potential** - **amoxicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, ampicillin, benzylpenicillin, cloxacillin, cefalexin, cefazolin, doxycycline, gentamicin, amikacin, metronidazole, nitrofurantoin, cotrimoxazole, clindamycin, chloramphenicol**. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**Watch**: higher resistance potential, priority stewardship targets - **third- and fourth-generation cephalosporins** (ceftriaxone, cefixime, cefotaxime, ceftazidime, cefepime), **macrolides** (azithromycin, clarithromycin), **fluoroquinolones**, **piperacillin-tazobactam, carbapenems**, cefuroxime, **vancomycin and teicoplanin**. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**Reserve**: last-resort drugs for confirmed or suspected **MDR infection** - **colistin, polymyxin B, ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam, cefiderocol, linezolid, tigecycline, daptomycin, IV fosfomycin, aztreonam** and newer agents. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**Not recommended**: fixed-dose combinations of multiple broad-spectrum antibiotics with no evidence (common in India, e.g. cefixime-ofloxacin, ofloxacin-ornidazole) - WHO lists such combinations as **not recommended** for use. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**WHO AWaRe Antibiotic Book (2022)** gives syndrome-wise first and second choices with **short durations** for 35 common infections in primary care and hospital - the companion to the classification. [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`,`**India**: the **NLEM** lists core Access and Watch antibiotics; the **Red Line campaign (2016)** marks prescription-only antibiotics with a red vertical line, and **Schedule H1 (2014)** requires a separate sale register for third/fourth-generation cephalosporins, carbapenems, newer quinolones and anti-TB drugs. [MoHFW 2016]`]},{heading:`ICMR syndromic regimens - respiratory and ENT`,points:[`**Acute pharyngitis**: most are viral; treat probable streptococcal pharyngitis (Centor/McIsaac 3 or more) with **amoxicillin 500 mg 8-hourly (or 1 g 12-hourly) for 10 days** or **benzathine penicillin 1.2 MU IM single dose**; azithromycin only for penicillin allergy. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute rhinosinusitis**: antibiotics only for symptoms beyond 10 days, severe symptoms or 'double worsening' - **amoxicillin (or amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 8-hourly) for 5-7 days**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute otitis media**: watchful waiting in mild cases over 2 years; **amoxicillin 80-90 mg/kg/day in children for 5 days** (10 days under 2 years). [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`,`**CAP in OPD, no comorbidity**: **amoxicillin 1 g 8-hourly** or **doxycycline 100 mg 12-hourly** (azithromycin 500 mg daily where atypicals likely) for **5 days**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CAP in OPD with comorbidity**: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 8-hourly (or 1 g 12-hourly) plus doxycycline or azithromycin** for 5 days. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CAP admitted, non-ICU**: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily (or amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV 8-hourly) plus azithromycin 500 mg daily or doxycycline**; ICU: add **piperacillin-tazobactam** only with Pseudomonas risk; total **5-7 days** if clinically stable. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Influenza season**: add **oseltamivir 75 mg 12-hourly for 5 days** to severe CAP; see the pneumonia and influenza topics. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute exacerbation of COPD**: antibiotic only when **sputum purulence** is present with increased dyspnoea or volume - **amoxicillin, doxycycline or azithromycin for 5 days**. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`ICMR syndromic regimens - urine, gut and fever`,points:[`**Acute uncomplicated cystitis (women)**: **nitrofurantoin 100 mg 12-hourly for 5 days** or **fosfomycin 3 g single oral dose**; avoid fluoroquinolones and cefixime empirically because Indian E. coli resistance is high. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute pyelonephritis / complicated UTI**: culture first, then **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily or amikacin 15 mg/kg IV once daily**; **piperacillin-tazobactam or ertapenem/meropenem** for ESBL risk or sepsis; total **7 days** if improving (IDSA 2025 cUTI guidance). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria**: treat only in **pregnancy** and before urological procedures with mucosal breach. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute watery diarrhoea**: **ORS and zinc, no antibiotic**; **cholera** - **doxycycline 300 mg single dose** or azithromycin 1 g single dose (child 20 mg/kg). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Dysentery**: **azithromycin** (adult 500 mg daily for 3 days, child 10 mg/kg daily for 3 days) or **ceftriaxone** if severe - ciprofloxacin is no longer reliable for Shigella in India. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Enteric fever**: OPD - **azithromycin 500 mg 12-hourly (1 g/day) for 7 days** or **cefixime 20 mg/kg/day for 14 days**; IPD - **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily (child 75 mg/kg/day) for 10-14 days**; avoid fluoroquinolones empirically. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute undifferentiated fever beyond day 3-5** with suspected scrub typhus or leptospirosis: **doxycycline 100 mg 12-hourly for 7 days** (azithromycin in pregnancy); see the AUF topic. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Intra-abdominal infection (community)**: **ceftriaxone plus metronidazole** or **amoxicillin-clavulanate**; **piperacillin-tazobactam** for severe or healthcare-associated; **4 days after source control** (STOP-IT). [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`ICMR syndromic regimens - skin, CNS, sepsis and special hosts`,points:[`**Cellulitis (non-purulent)**: **cefalexin 500 mg 6-hourly** or **cloxacillin**, IV **cefazolin 1-2 g 8-hourly** if systemic; **clindamycin** in penicillin allergy; **5-7 days**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Abscess**: **incision and drainage** is the treatment; add an anti-MRSA agent (cotrimoxazole, doxycycline or clindamycin) only for large abscess, systemic signs or immunosuppression. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Necrotising fasciitis**: emergency surgery plus **piperacillin-tazobactam (or meropenem) plus clindamycin**, with vancomycin or linezolid if MRSA risk. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Acute bacterial meningitis**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly** plus **vancomycin** where pneumococcal resistance is a concern, plus **ampicillin 2 g 4-hourly** if over 50, pregnant or immunocompromised (Listeria); **dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg 6-hourly for 4 days** before or with the first dose. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Sepsis with no focus (community)**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV plus doxycycline**; healthcare-associated: **piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem** with or without vancomycin - see the sepsis topic. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Febrile neutropenia**: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly** or cefoperazone-sulbactam per ICMR, or **cefepime**; meropenem if prior ESBL colonisation or septic. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Diabetic foot infection**: mild - **amoxicillin-clavulanate or cefalexin**; moderate-severe - **piperacillin-tazobactam** with or without MRSA cover; 1-2 weeks for soft tissue (IWGDF/IDSA 2023). [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia**: **piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime or meropenem** based on unit antibiogram, add **vancomycin or linezolid** if MRSA risk; **7 days** total. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations that make empiricism safe`,points:[`**Gram stain** of pus, sputum, CSF or urine gives an answer in an hour and narrows the choice (Gram-positive cocci in clusters - staphylococci; Gram-negative diplococci - meningococcus or gonococcus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood cultures**: 2 sets, 20 mL each; positivity within 24-48 h; **rapid identification (MALDI-TOF) and molecular resistance markers** (mecA, CTX-M, KPC, NDM) shorten time to targeted therapy. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Urine culture** with colony count is essential in pyelonephritis, complicated UTI, pregnancy and recurrence - but **never culture asymptomatic urine** in catheterised patients. [ICMR 2022]`,`**CRP and procalcitonin**: a **procalcitonin under 0.25 ng/mL** in lower respiratory infection favours stopping or withholding antibiotics; in sepsis PCT helps **stop**, not start. [SSC 2021]`,`**Hospital antibiogram**: published yearly by microbiology (CLSI M39 method), stratified by ward and ICU - use it to choose the empirical drug for hospital-onset infections. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Point-of-care tests** that prevent antibiotics: malaria RDT, dengue NS1, influenza and COVID antigen tests, **CRP point-of-care** in primary care for respiratory infections. [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`]},{heading:`De-escalation, IV-to-oral switch and the 48-72 hour time-out`,points:[`**Antibiotic time-out at 48-72 hours**: review diagnosis, cultures, clinical response and ask - stop, continue, narrow, switch to oral, or set the stop date. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**De-escalation** means narrowing the spectrum (e.g. meropenem to ceftriaxone for a susceptible E. coli), stopping redundant cover (anaerobic double cover, MRSA cover when cultures are negative) or stopping altogether. [SSC 2021]`,`**IV-to-oral switch criteria**: afebrile for 24-48 hours, haemodynamically stable, improving markers, able to swallow and absorb, and no deep-seated infection needing IV therapy (endocarditis, meningitis, undrained abscess). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Highly bioavailable oral drugs** (over 90%): **doxycycline, fluoroquinolones, linezolid, metronidazole, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole** - switching early saves cannula infections and hospital days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Negative cultures in an improving patient** do not mean 'continue broad cover' - re-examine the diagnosis and stop or narrow. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Stop rules**: stop antibiotics at 3 days when bacterial infection has been ruled out, and avoid 'tail' courses after discharge without a defined end date. [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`]},{heading:`Duration shortening - shorter is better`,points:[`**CAP**: **5 days** (minimum, afebrile 48 h and stable); a 3-day course was non-inferior in stable adults in the PTC trial (Lancet 2021). [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Cystitis**: nitrofurantoin **5 days**, fosfomycin single dose, cotrimoxazole 3 days where susceptible; **pyelonephritis**: 7 days (fluoroquinolone) to 10-14 days (beta-lactam), with IDSA 2025 favouring **7 days** when improving. [IDSA 2025]`,`**Gram-negative bacteraemia, uncomplicated**: **7 days** is non-inferior to 14 days (Yahav 2019; **BALANCE, NEJM 2025** across bloodstream infections excluding S. aureus). [BALANCE 2025]`,`**Intra-abdominal infection with source control**: **4 days** (STOP-IT, NEJM 2015). [STOP-IT 2015]`,`**Cellulitis**: **5-6 days** if improving; **HAP/VAP**: 7 days; **acute exacerbation of COPD**: 5 days; **streptococcal pharyngitis**: 10 days of amoxicillin remains standard. [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`,`**Exceptions needing long courses**: S. aureus bacteraemia (14 days minimum uncomplicated), endocarditis (4-6 weeks), osteomyelitis (6 weeks), TB (6 months), brain abscess and melioidosis eradication phase. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: penicillins, cephalosporins, azithromycin, nitrofurantoin (avoid at term) and fosfomycin are preferred; **avoid doxycycline, fluoroquinolones, aminoglycosides and cotrimoxazole near term** - see the antimicrobial pharmacology topic. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, more C. difficile, renal dose adjustment, avoid nitrofurantoin if CrCl under 30 mL/min (Beers 2023), fluoroquinolone tendon and aortic risks. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Children**: weight-based dosing; **amoxicillin is the backbone** of paediatric AWaRe therapy (dispersible tablets); avoid fluoroquinolones unless no alternative. [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`,`**CKD**: give full loading doses, adjust maintenance; avoid nitrofurantoin at low GFR, prefer once-daily dosing intervals for aminoglycosides with level monitoring. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Penicillin allergy label**: over 90% of labelled patients are not truly allergic; a low-risk history allows **direct oral amoxicillin challenge** - delabelling cuts Watch and Reserve use (see the antibiotic allergy topic). [AAAAI 2022]`,`**Primary care without a laboratory**: syndrome-based Access drugs, delayed ('back-up') prescriptions for URTI and safety-netting, and point-of-care CRP where available. [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications of inappropriate empiricism`,points:[`**Treatment failure** from wrong cover: ceftriaxone for scrub typhus, ciprofloxacin for enteric fever, cefixime for ESBL cystitis. [ICMR 2022]`,`**C. difficile infection** after broad-spectrum courses, especially cephalosporins, clindamycin and fluoroquinolones, in the elderly and those on PPIs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adverse drug events**: about **1 in 5 hospitalised patients** on antibiotics has an adverse drug event, and many are on antibiotics they did not need (Tamma, JAMA IM 2017). [Tamma 2017]`,`**Masked diagnosis**: antibiotics before cultures in endocarditis, enteric fever or meningitis convert them to culture-negative disease. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Community resistance**: national surveillance links high Watch use to rising ESBL and carbapenemase carriage in healthy Indians. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`]},{heading:`Stewardship, follow-up and national programme link`,points:[`**ICMR AMSP (2018)** guidance: hospital stewardship committee, prescribing policy, **prior authorisation for Reserve drugs**, prospective audit and feedback, and antibiograms. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**NAP-AMR (2017-2021)** set India's One Health strategy; **NAP-AMR 2.0 (2025-2029)** was launched in November 2025 with stronger surveillance, stewardship and private-sector engagement. [MoHFW 2025]`,`**Regulatory steps**: Schedule H1 (2014), Red Line campaign (2016), ban on **colistin as a growth promoter in animals (2019)** and periodic bans on irrational FDCs. [MoHFW 2019]`,`**Kerala's KARSAP** (2018) was the first state AMR action plan, with campaigns against over-the-counter antibiotic sales. [KARSAP 2018]`,`**Counsel patients**: complete the prescribed short course, do not save or share antibiotics, do not demand them for colds, and return if not better in 48-72 hours. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Audit indicators**: percentage of Access use, days of therapy per 1000 patient-days, time to de-escalation and culture-before-antibiotic rate. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO AWaRe 2023** updated the classification alongside the 2023 EML; **AWaRe 2025** (with the 2025 EML) expanded it to about 268 antibiotics. [WHO AWaRe 2025]`,`**UNGA political declaration on AMR (2024)**: reduce AMR deaths by **10% by 2030** and reach **70% Access use** in human health. [UNGA 2024]`,`**ICMR Treatment Guidelines 2nd edition (2022)** retained syndrome-wise first choices with an Access-first approach and doxycycline for Indian febrile syndromes. [ICMR 2022]`,`**IDSA 2025 complicated UTI guideline**: shorter courses (**7 days** for most improving cUTI and urinary Gram-negative bacteraemia). [IDSA 2025]`,`**BALANCE trial (NEJM 2025)**: 7 days non-inferior to 14 days for bloodstream infections in hospitalised patients, supporting shorter courses. [BALANCE 2025]`,`**India NAP-AMR 2.0 (November 2025)** - second national action plan with One Health governance. [MoHFW 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Define AWaRe** and give 3 examples of each group - amoxicillin (Access), ceftriaxone/azithromycin (Watch), colistin/linezolid (Reserve). [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**Trap**: amikacin and gentamicin are **Access**, while azithromycin and cefixime are **Watch** - examiners like this inversion of instinct. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**Write the 5 Ds of stewardship**: right Drug, Dose, De-escalation, Duration and Diagnosis (some add Drug route). [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Common mistakes**: cefixime-ofloxacin for fever, antibiotics for viral URTI, culture after antibiotics, 14-day courses for uncomplicated infections, carbapenem for community sepsis. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Draw** an empirical decision flow: is it infection - which syndrome - which setting and host - culture - Access drug - review at 48-72 h - de-escalate - stop. [ICMR 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`WHO AWaRe groups with examples`,columns:[`Group`,`Definition`,`Examples`,`Use`],rows:[[`Access`,`First or second choice for common infections; lower resistance potential`,`Amoxicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, cefalexin, cefazolin, cloxacillin, doxycycline, gentamicin, amikacin, metronidazole, nitrofurantoin, cotrimoxazole, clindamycin`,`Widely available; target 70% of use by 2030`],[`Watch`,`Higher resistance potential; stewardship priority`,`Ceftriaxone, cefixime, cefotaxime, ceftazidime, cefepime, cefuroxime, azithromycin, clarithromycin, ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, piperacillin-tazobactam, meropenem, vancomycin, teicoplanin`,`Specific indications; monitor use`],[`Reserve`,`Last resort for confirmed or suspected MDR infection`,`Colistin, polymyxin B, ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam, cefiderocol, linezolid, tigecycline, daptomycin, IV fosfomycin, aztreonam`,`Prior authorisation; protected`],[`Not recommended`,`Irrational fixed-dose combinations`,`Multiple broad-spectrum FDCs such as cefixime-ofloxacin, ofloxacin-ornidazole`,`Should not be used`]]},{heading:`ICMR empirical regimens by syndrome`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`First choice`,`Alternative / severe`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Streptococcal pharyngitis`,`Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS oral`,`Benzathine penicillin 1.2 MU IM once; azithromycin if allergic`,`10 days`],[`CAP - OPD, no comorbidity`,`Amoxicillin 1 g TDS or doxycycline 100 mg BD`,`Azithromycin 500 mg OD if atypical likely`,`5 days`],[`CAP - admitted`,`Ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV OD + azithromycin or doxycycline`,`Pip-taz if Pseudomonas risk; oseltamivir in flu season`,`5-7 days`],[`Uncomplicated cystitis`,`Nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD`,`Fosfomycin 3 g single dose`,`5 days / single dose`],[`Pyelonephritis / cUTI`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD or amikacin 15 mg/kg OD`,`Pip-taz, ertapenem or meropenem if ESBL/sepsis`,`7 days if improving`],[`Enteric fever`,`Azithromycin 1 g/day oral (OPD)`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD (IPD); cefixime 20 mg/kg/day`,`7 days (azithro); 10-14 days (others)`],[`Cellulitis`,`Cefalexin 500 mg QID or cloxacillin`,`Cefazolin IV; clindamycin if allergic`,`5-7 days`],[`Meningitis`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV BD + vancomycin`,`Add ampicillin if over 50, pregnant or immunocompromised; dexamethasone`,`7-14 days by organism`],[`Community sepsis, no focus`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV + doxycycline 100 mg BD`,`Pip-taz or meropenem if HAI/MDR risk`,`Review at 48-72 h; usually 7 days`]]},{heading:`Common empirical antibiotics - doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin`,`500 mg-1 g oral 8-hourly; child 40-90 mg/kg/day`,`5-10 days by syndrome`,`Rash (EBV), diarrhoea; penicillin allergy`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 8-hourly; 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Cholestatic hepatitis (commonest antibiotic DILI), diarrhoea`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral/IV 12-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`100 mg oral 12-hourly (modified release)`,`5 days`,`Avoid CrCl under 30, at term and in G6PD deficiency; pulmonary fibrosis long term`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV/IM daily; 2 g 12-hourly in meningitis`,`Review at 48-72 h`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; no calcium co-infusion in neonates`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg oral daily (1 g/day in enteric fever)`,`3-7 days`,`QT prolongation, diarrhoea; Watch group - overused in India`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly, extended infusion`,`By culture; de-escalate`,`Hypokalaemia, thrombocytopenia, AKI with vancomycin`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly (2 g in meningitis)`,`By culture; de-escalate`,`Seizures, lowers valproate levels, selects CRE`]]},{heading:`Evidence for short courses`,columns:[`Infection`,`Short course`,`Key trial / guideline`,`Exception`],rows:[[`Community-acquired pneumonia`,`5 days (3 days if stable)`,`PTC trial 2021; ATS/IDSA 2019`,`Empyema, S. aureus, Legionella severe`],[`Cystitis`,`5 days nitrofurantoin; single-dose fosfomycin`,`ICMR 2022; WHO AWaRe Book`,`Men, pregnancy (7 days)`],[`Pyelonephritis / cUTI`,`7 days`,`IDSA 2025`,`Abscess, obstruction undrained`],[`Gram-negative bacteraemia`,`7 days`,`Yahav 2019; BALANCE 2025`,`Source not controlled, immunosuppressed`],[`Intra-abdominal infection`,`4 days after source control`,`STOP-IT 2015`,`No source control`],[`Cellulitis`,`5-6 days`,`WHO AWaRe Book 2022`,`Abscess undrained, necrotising infection`]]},{heading:`Differential - when NOT to give an antibiotic`,columns:[`Presentation`,`Usual cause`,`What to do instead`,`Antibiotic only if`],rows:[[`Common cold, URTI`,`Viral`,`Symptomatic care, safety-net`,`Bacterial complication (sinusitis over 10 days)`],[`Acute bronchitis`,`Viral`,`Reassure; cough lasts 3 weeks`,`Pneumonia on examination or X-ray`],[`Acute watery diarrhoea`,`Viral or toxin`,`ORS and zinc`,`Cholera with dehydration, dysentery`],[`Asymptomatic bacteriuria`,`Colonisation`,`No treatment`,`Pregnancy or urological procedure`],[`Fever day 1-3, no focus, well`,`Viral, dengue`,`Paracetamol, tests, review in 48 h`,`Day 3-5 suspected scrub/lepto, or sepsis`]]}],redFlags:[`Sepsis or septic shock - broad empirical therapy within 1 hour, not Access-only OPD choices.`,`Meningitis signs (neck stiffness, altered sensorium, purpura) - IV ceftriaxone immediately and admit.`,`Necrotising soft-tissue infection - emergency surgery plus broad-spectrum cover.`,`Febrile neutropenia - antipseudomonal beta-lactam within 1 hour.`,`No clinical response at 48-72 hours - re-culture, image for a collection, reconsider diagnosis.`,`Severe diarrhoea after antibiotics with leucocytosis - suspect C. difficile.`,`Anaphylaxis or severe cutaneous reaction to an antibiotic - stop and treat urgently.`],pearls:[`AWaRe: **Access first, Watch with care, Reserve as last resort**.`,`**Amikacin and gentamicin are Access; azithromycin and cefixime are Watch** - a favourite trap.`,`**Culture before the first dose; review at 48-72 hours; write the stop date**.`,`In India, **doxycycline** covers what ceftriaxone misses: scrub typhus, rickettsia, leptospira.`,`**Shorter is better**: CAP 5, cystitis 5, cUTI 7, Gram-negative bacteraemia 7, intra-abdominal 4 days after source control.`,`**Avoid fluoroquinolones empirically** for enteric fever, dysentery and cystitis in India - resistance is high.`,`WHO target: **70% of antibiotic use from Access by 2030** (UNGA 2024).`,`Five Ds of stewardship: **Diagnosis, Drug, Dose, De-escalation, Duration**.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-q1`,stem:`Which of the following antibiotics is classified in the WHO AWaRe Access group?`,options:[`Azithromycin`,`Ceftriaxone`,`Amikacin`,`Levofloxacin`],answer:2,explanation:`Amikacin, like gentamicin, sits in the Access group because its resistance potential is relatively low and it is a key second-choice agent. Azithromycin, ceftriaxone and levofloxacin are all Watch antibiotics with high resistance potential and are the main targets of stewardship monitoring.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old non-pregnant woman has dysuria and frequency for 2 days with no fever or loin pain. What is the preferred empirical therapy per ICMR guidance?`,options:[`Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days`,`Ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days`,`Cefixime 200 mg twice daily for 7 days`,`Ceftriaxone 1 g IM daily for 3 days`],answer:0,explanation:`Nitrofurantoin for 5 days (or single-dose fosfomycin) is first choice for uncomplicated cystitis because it concentrates in urine, retains activity against most Indian E. coli including many ESBL producers, and is an Access drug. Ciprofloxacin and cefixime face high resistance in India and are Watch drugs, and injectable ceftriaxone is unnecessary for lower UTI.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-q3`,stem:`A man recently discharged from hospital was started on meropenem for suspected ESBL pyelonephritis. On day 3 he is afebrile, and urine and blood cultures grow an ESBL-negative E. coli susceptible to ceftriaxone and amikacin. What is the most appropriate stewardship action?`,options:[`Continue meropenem for 14 days because the first report said ESBL`,`De-escalate to ceftriaxone (or an oral susceptible agent) and complete about 7 days in total`,`Switch to oral nitrofurantoin for 14 days`,`Add colistin to prevent resistance`],answer:1,explanation:`With clinical improvement and a ceftriaxone-susceptible isolate, the 48-72 hour time-out calls for de-escalation to the narrowest effective agent and about 7 days total for an improving complicated UTI. Continuing meropenem selects carbapenem resistance, nitrofurantoin does not reach adequate renal tissue levels for pyelonephritis, and adding colistin is unjustified Reserve use.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-q4`,stem:`According to the STOP-IT trial, what is the appropriate antibiotic duration for complicated intra-abdominal infection after adequate source control?`,options:[`About 4 days`,`10 days`,`14 days`,`Until the white cell count normalises`],answer:0,explanation:`STOP-IT showed that a fixed course of about 4 days after adequate source control gave outcomes similar to treating until resolution of fever, leucocytosis and ileus. Longer fixed courses of 10-14 days add adverse effects and resistance without benefit, and continuing until the white count normalises was the control strategy that offered no advantage.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-q5`,stem:`The UN General Assembly political declaration on AMR (2024) set which target for Access-group antibiotics?`,options:[`At least 40% of human antibiotic use by 2030`,`At least 50% of human antibiotic use by 2025`,`Elimination of Watch antibiotics from primary care by 2030`,`At least 70% of human antibiotic use by 2030`],answer:3,explanation:`The 2024 UNGA declaration raised the earlier WHO target of 60% Access use to at least 70% of human antibiotic use from the Access group by 2030, alongside a 10% reduction in AMR deaths. The 40% and 50% figures are below even the earlier WHO target, and Watch drugs remain necessary for specific indications so they are not to be eliminated.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the principles of empirical antibiotic therapy. Describe the WHO AWaRe classification and outline ICMR-recommended empirical regimens for common syndromes in primary care.`,openingLines:[`Empirical antibiotic therapy is treatment chosen before the pathogen is known, guided by the syndrome, likely organisms, local resistance, host factors and severity.`,`The WHO AWaRe classification groups antibiotics into Access, Watch and Reserve to guide selection and monitor use, with a target of 70% Access use by 2030.`],answer:[{heading:`Principles`,points:[`Is an antibiotic needed; culture first; syndrome, setting, host, severity; local antibiogram; right dose and route; document indication and stop date.`]},{heading:`AWaRe classification`,points:[`Access, Watch, Reserve and Not recommended - definitions and examples (table).`,`AWaRe Antibiotic Book 2022; AWaRe 2023/2025 updates; UNGA 2024 target.`]},{heading:`ICMR syndromic regimens`,points:[`Pharyngitis, CAP, cystitis, pyelonephritis, diarrhoea/dysentery, enteric fever, cellulitis, AUF, community sepsis - first choice, alternative, duration.`]},{heading:`Review and de-escalation`,points:[`48-72 hour time-out, IV-to-oral switch criteria, de-escalation examples.`]},{heading:`Duration shortening`,points:[`CAP 5, cystitis 5, cUTI 7, Gram-negative bacteraemia 7, intra-abdominal 4 days - trials.`]},{heading:`Stewardship and policy`,points:[`ICMR AMSP 2018, NAP-AMR, Schedule H1, Red Line campaign, colistin ban in animals.`]}],mustDraw:[`AWaRe table with definitions and examples`,`Flow chart of empirical prescribing with the 48-72 hour time-out`],markSplit:[{part:`Principles`,marks:2},{part:`AWaRe classification`,marks:2},{part:`ICMR syndromic regimens`,marks:4},{part:`De-escalation, duration and stewardship`,marks:2}],keywords:[`AWaRe`,`ICMR 2022`,`empirical`,`de-escalation`,`time-out`,`short course`,`antibiogram`]},{id:`infectious-fever-empirical-antibiotics-icmr-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Short note: De-escalation and duration shortening of antibiotic therapy.`,openingLines:[`De-escalation is narrowing or stopping empirical antibiotics once culture results and clinical response are known, usually at the 48-72 hour antibiotic time-out.`,`Randomised trials show shorter courses are as effective as traditional long courses for most common infections.`],answer:[{heading:`De-escalation`,points:[`Time-out at 48-72 h; narrow spectrum, stop redundant cover, IV-to-oral switch, stop if infection excluded.`]},{heading:`Short-course evidence`,points:[`CAP 5 days (3 if stable), cystitis 5, cUTI 7, Gram-negative bacteraemia 7 (BALANCE 2025), intra-abdominal 4 (STOP-IT).`]},{heading:`Benefits and exceptions`,points:[`Less resistance, C. difficile and adverse events; exceptions: S. aureus bacteraemia, endocarditis, osteomyelitis, TB.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of infection versus short-course duration with trial`],markSplit:[{part:`De-escalation`,marks:2},{part:`Short-course evidence`,marks:2},{part:`Benefits and exceptions`,marks:1}],keywords:[`de-escalation`,`time-out`,`STOP-IT`,`BALANCE`,`IV-to-oral switch`]}],references:[`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`ICMR Antimicrobial Stewardship Program Guideline, 2018`,`WHO AWaRe classification of antibiotics, 2023 and 2025 updates; WHO Model List of Essential Medicines, 2025`,`The WHO AWaRe (Access, Watch, Reserve) Antibiotic Book, 2022`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Treatment and Prophylaxis of Bacterial Infections`,`ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Research and Surveillance Network (AMRSN) Annual Report, 2023`,`Political Declaration of the High-level Meeting on Antimicrobial Resistance, UN General Assembly, 2024`,`National Action Plan on AMR (NAP-AMR), MoHFW, 2017; NAP-AMR 2.0, 2025`]}),e.push({id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology`,title:`Antimicrobial pharmacology for clinicians: PK/PD, dosing in renal/hepatic failure and drug interactions`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Antimicrobial pharmacology** links **pharmacokinetics** (what the body does to the drug - absorption, volume of distribution, clearance) with **pharmacodynamics** (what the drug does to the bug - **time above MIC** for beta-lactams, **peak/MIC** for aminoglycosides, **AUC/MIC** for vancomycin and fluoroquinolones), so that clinicians give a **full loading dose**, adjust **maintenance** for renal or hepatic failure, use **therapeutic drug monitoring** for narrow-therapeutic-index drugs, and avoid **interactions, QT prolongation and teratogenic exposure**.`,keywords:[`PK/PD`,`time-dependent killing`,`concentration-dependent killing`,`fT>MIC`,`AUC/MIC`,`Cmax/MIC`,`post-antibiotic effect`,`loading dose`,`volume of distribution`,`augmented renal clearance`,`therapeutic drug monitoring`,`vancomycin AUC`,`extended-interval aminoglycoside`,`Cockcroft-Gault`,`renal dose adjustment`,`CYP3A4`,`rifampicin induction`,`QT prolongation`,`pregnancy safety`,`prolonged infusion`],sections:[{heading:`Definitions and why it matters`,points:[`**Pharmacokinetics (PK)** describes absorption, distribution (**volume of distribution, Vd**), metabolism and elimination (**clearance, half-life**); **pharmacodynamics (PD)** relates concentration to antimicrobial effect against the **minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**MIC** is the lowest concentration that inhibits visible growth in vitro; **breakpoints** (CLSI, EUCAST) translate MIC into susceptible, intermediate (or 'susceptible, increased exposure') and resistant using PK/PD targets. [CLSI M100 2025]`,`**Mutant prevention concentration** and the **mutant selection window** (between MIC and MPC) explain why under-dosing selects resistance - especially with fluoroquinolones. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Inadequate exposure is common in the critically ill**: up to 1 in 5 ICU patients on standard beta-lactam doses miss PK/PD targets (DALI study), with worse outcomes. [DALI 2014]`,`**Adverse drug events** from antibiotics cause about **1 in 5 emergency visits for drug harms** in some series; nephrotoxicity, QT prolongation and interactions are the commonest preventable ones. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India-specific issues**: high prevalence of **CKD and diabetes**, widespread rifampicin use in TB, self-medication with azithromycin and fluoroquinolones, and limited TDM availability outside tertiary centres. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Pharmacokinetic principles`,points:[`**Bioavailability**: near 100% oral for **doxycycline, fluoroquinolones, linezolid, metronidazole, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole, rifampicin** - oral equals IV when the gut works; low for oral vancomycin (useful only for C. difficile) and cefuroxime axetil. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Chelation**: fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines bind **calcium, magnesium, aluminium, iron and zinc** - give them **2 hours before or 4-6 hours after** antacids, iron, calcium or dairy. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Volume of distribution**: hydrophilic drugs (**beta-lactams, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, colistin**) have low Vd that **expands in sepsis, oedema, ascites and fluid resuscitation** - so the loading dose must be bigger, not smaller. [Roberts, Lancet ID 2014]`,`**Lipophilic drugs** (fluoroquinolones, macrolides, tetracyclines, linezolid, rifampicin) have large Vd, penetrate cells and tissues, and are less affected by fluid shifts. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Protein binding**: highly bound drugs (**ceftriaxone, ertapenem, teicoplanin, flucloxacillin**) have more free drug and faster clearance in **hypoalbuminaemia** - consider more frequent dosing in the critically ill. [Roberts, Lancet ID 2014]`,`**Augmented renal clearance (CrCl over 130 mL/min)** occurs in young trauma, burns, sepsis and pregnancy, causing sub-therapeutic levels of renally cleared drugs - standard doses may fail. [Roberts, Lancet ID 2014]`,`**Tissue penetration**: CSF penetration needs inflamed meninges and high doses (ceftriaxone 2 g 12-hourly, meropenem 2 g 8-hourly); **daptomycin is inactivated by surfactant** (not for pneumonia); **tigecycline** gives low blood levels (poor for bacteraemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Half-life and steady state**: steady state takes about **4-5 half-lives** - the reason a loading dose is needed for long half-life drugs (vancomycin, teicoplanin, colistin) and in renal failure. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Pharmacodynamic patterns - the classification examiners ask`,points:[`**Time-dependent killing (fT>MIC)**: **beta-lactams** - penicillins need about **50%**, cephalosporins **60-70%**, carbapenems **40%** of the dosing interval above MIC; critically ill patients may benefit from **100% fT>MIC**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Concentration-dependent killing (Cmax/MIC)**: **aminoglycosides** target **Cmax/MIC of 8-10 or more**, with a prolonged **post-antibiotic effect** - the rationale for once-daily (extended-interval) dosing. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Concentration-dependent with time (AUC24/MIC)**: **vancomycin** (AUC/MIC **400-600** for MRSA with MIC 1), **fluoroquinolones** (AUC/MIC **over 125 for Gram-negatives**, about 30-50 for pneumococcus), linezolid, azithromycin, tetracyclines and daptomycin. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Post-antibiotic effect (PAE)**: persistent suppression after levels fall below MIC - long for aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones against Gram-negatives, minimal for beta-lactams against Gram-negatives (except carbapenems). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Bactericidal versus bacteriostatic** matters mainly in **endocarditis, meningitis and neutropenia**; for most infections bacteriostatic agents (doxycycline, linezolid) are equally effective. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Synergy and antagonism**: beta-lactam plus aminoglycoside is synergistic for enterococcal endocarditis (cell-wall damage lets the aminoglycoside enter); bacteriostatic plus bactericidal combinations may antagonise in meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inoculum effect**: beta-lactams lose activity at high bacterial density (abscess, endocarditis vegetation) - one reason drainage matters. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Applying PK/PD at the bedside - loading doses and infusions`,points:[`**Loading dose = target concentration x Vd** - it depends on Vd, **not on renal function**; give a **full first dose** of every antibiotic in sepsis and in AKI, and adjust only the maintenance. [SSC 2021]`,`**Prolonged or continuous beta-lactam infusion**: piperacillin-tazobactam **4.5 g over 3-4 hours 8-hourly** (or continuous) or meropenem **1-2 g over 3 hours 8-hourly** after a loading bolus maximises fT>MIC; SSC 2021 suggests it for maintenance in sepsis. [SSC 2021]`,`**BLING III (JAMA 2024)** found no significant 90-day mortality difference alone, but the accompanying **individual-patient meta-analysis** showed **lower mortality with continuous infusion** - supporting prolonged infusion in the critically ill. [BLING III 2024]`,`**Extended-interval aminoglycoside**: **gentamicin 5-7 mg/kg** or **amikacin 15-20 mg/kg** IV once daily (amikacin 25-30 mg/kg loading in critically ill), with interval guided by a level - **less nephrotoxicity** than 8-hourly dosing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vancomycin loading dose**: **20-35 mg/kg (actual body weight, max 3 g)** in serious MRSA infection, then **15-20 mg/kg 8-12-hourly**, adjusted to AUC. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Colistin (as CMS)**: loading **9 million IU**, then **4.5 million IU 12-hourly** with renal adjustment; **polymyxin B** 2-2.5 mg/kg loading then 1.25-1.5 mg/kg 12-hourly, **no renal adjustment** - preferred for systemic infection. [Polymyxin consensus 2019]`,`**Obesity**: hydrophilic drug loading doses use **actual body weight** (vancomycin) or **adjusted body weight** (aminoglycosides); maintenance by levels. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)`,points:[`**Vancomycin (ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP 2020)**: target **AUC24 400-600 mg.h/L** (MIC 1) by **Bayesian software or two levels** (peak and trough); **trough-only monitoring (15-20 mg/L) is no longer recommended** for serious MRSA because it causes more nephrotoxicity. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Where AUC tools are unavailable** (common in Indian hospitals), a **trough of 10-15 mg/L** with clinical response and creatinine monitoring is a pragmatic compromise; troughs over 15-20 mg/L raise AKI risk. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Aminoglycosides (once daily)**: a random level **6-14 hours after the dose** plotted on the **Hartford nomogram** sets the interval (24, 36 or 48 h); gentamicin trough should be **under 1 mg/L**; peak monitoring for endocarditis synergy dosing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Teicoplanin**: loading **6-12 mg/kg 12-hourly for 3-5 doses**, then daily; trough **at least 15-20 mg/L** (higher for bone infection and endocarditis). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Voriconazole**: trough **1-5.5 mg/L** (CYP2C19 polymorphism causes wide variation); **posaconazole** trough over 0.7 mg/L for prophylaxis and over 1 mg/L for treatment. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Beta-lactam TDM** in ICU (where available) helps augmented renal clearance, obesity, ECMO and CRRT; **linezolid trough** monitoring (2-7 mg/L) reduces thrombocytopenia in renal failure and long courses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Timing rule**: draw **troughs just before** the next dose (within 30 minutes), **peaks 30-60 minutes after** the end of infusion, and always at steady state unless loading has been given. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Dosing in renal failure`,points:[`**Estimate renal function** with **Cockcroft-Gault CrCl** = (140 - age) x weight (kg) / (72 x serum creatinine mg/dL), x 0.85 for women - most drug labels use it; eGFR (CKD-EPI) is acceptable for most drugs when indexed to body surface. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**No adjustment needed**: **ceftriaxone, doxycycline, azithromycin, clindamycin, linezolid, moxifloxacin, metronidazole (largely), rifampicin, cloxacillin, polymyxin B, tigecycline** - biliary or hepatic clearance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adjust maintenance**: penicillins and cephalosporins (except ceftriaxone), **carbapenems**, ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin, **vancomycin, aminoglycosides, colistin (CMS)**, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole, acyclovir, ganciclovir, ethambutol and pyrazinamide. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Meropenem**: CrCl over 50 - 1 g 8-hourly; **26-50 - 1 g 12-hourly; 10-25 - 500 mg 12-hourly; under 10 - 500 mg daily**. [Meropenem SmPC]`,`**Piperacillin-tazobactam**: CrCl over 40 - 4.5 g 6-8-hourly; **20-40 - 4.5 g 8-hourly (or 2.25 g 6-hourly)**; **under 20 - 4.5 g 12-hourly (or 2.25 g 8-hourly)**; extra dose after haemodialysis. [Pip-taz SmPC]`,`**Cefepime and high-dose penicillins/carbapenems** accumulate in AKI and cause **neurotoxicity** (encephalopathy, myoclonus, non-convulsive status) - suspect it in a confused patient with renal failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB drugs in CKD (CrCl under 30 or dialysis)**: isoniazid and rifampicin unchanged; **ethambutol and pyrazinamide thrice weekly** after dialysis. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Nitrofurantoin**: avoid if **CrCl under 30 mL/min** (Beers 2023; older threshold was 60) - ineffective urine levels and toxicity. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Haemodialysis**: dose **after dialysis** on dialysis days for dialysable drugs (aminoglycosides, most beta-lactams, vancomycin with high-flux membranes); vancomycin **loading 25 mg/kg then 500-1000 mg after each session** guided by pre-dialysis levels. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Dosing in hepatic failure`,points:[`**Liver dysfunction** has no creatinine-equivalent marker; use **Child-Pugh class** and drug labels - Child-Pugh C usually means avoid or halve hepatically cleared drugs. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Hepatotoxic antimicrobials**: **isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide**, amoxicillin-clavulanate (cholestatic, commonest antibiotic cause of DILI), flucloxacillin, nitrofurantoin (chronic hepatitis), ketoconazole, tetracyclines at high IV doses, erythromycin estolate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ATT in liver disease**: in decompensated cirrhosis use regimens with fewer hepatotoxic drugs (e.g. **avoid pyrazinamide**; levofloxacin, ethambutol, linezolid or streptomycin-based combinations per NTEP), with LFT every 2-4 weeks. [NTEP 2025]`,`**ATT-induced hepatitis**: stop all hepatotoxic drugs if **ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or over 5 times without**, or bilirubin rise; reintroduce sequentially (rifampicin, then isoniazid; pyrazinamide last or omitted) once ALT under 2 times ULN. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Metronidazole**: halve the dose in severe hepatic impairment; **tigecycline**: reduce maintenance in Child-Pugh C; **voriconazole**: halve maintenance in Child-Pugh A-B. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ceftriaxone** at high doses causes **biliary pseudolithiasis** (sludge), reversible on stopping - in children and with prolonged courses. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Drug interactions that matter`,points:[`**Rifampicin** is a potent **CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and P-glycoprotein inducer**: it lowers levels of oral contraceptives, warfarin, **DOACs (avoid)**, dolutegravir (give **50 mg twice daily**), protease inhibitors (avoid), tacrolimus, cyclosporine, azoles, methadone, sulfonylureas and steroids; induction lasts about **2 weeks after stopping**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Macrolides (clarithromycin, erythromycin) inhibit CYP3A4**: rhabdomyolysis with simvastatin and atorvastatin, colchicine toxicity, raised DOAC and calcium-channel blocker levels; **azithromycin has minimal CYP effect**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Azoles**: fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9 and 3A4 (warfarin, phenytoin, sulfonylureas - hypoglycaemia); voriconazole and itraconazole strongly inhibit CYP3A4 (tacrolimus, statins, vincristine). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Linezolid** is a weak **MAO inhibitor**: serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, SNRIs, tramadol and pethidine; also causes **myelosuppression after 2 weeks**, lactic acidosis, peripheral and optic neuropathy. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Cotrimoxazole**: **hyperkalaemia** with ACE inhibitors, ARBs and spironolactone (trimethoprim blocks ENaC like amiloride), raised INR with warfarin, and marrow toxicity with **methotrexate**; trimethoprim also raises creatinine without changing GFR. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Carbapenems lower valproate levels** by 60-90% within a day - seizures; avoid the combination or use an alternative antiepileptic. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2**: theophylline and tizanidine toxicity; **metronidazole** raises INR with warfarin (CYP2C9). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Nephrotoxic combinations**: **vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam** has a higher AKI rate than vancomycin with cefepime or meropenem; aminoglycosides plus loop diuretics, amphotericin, NSAIDs or contrast compound risk. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`QT prolongation and other class toxicities`,points:[`**QT-prolonging antimicrobials**: macrolides (erythromycin over clarithromycin over azithromycin), **fluoroquinolones** (moxifloxacin highest), azoles (fluconazole, voriconazole), **bedaquiline, delamanid, clofazimine**, antimalarials (quinine, chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine, lumefantrine), pentamidine and foscarnet. [CredibleMeds 2025]`,`**Risk factors for torsades**: QTc over 500 ms or rise over 60 ms, **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia**, bradycardia, female sex, age over 65, structural heart disease, congenital long QT and combinations of QT drugs. [AHA 2010]`,`**Practical rule**: baseline ECG and K/Mg before combining two QT drugs (e.g. bedaquiline plus clofazimine plus moxifloxacin in DR-TB); stop if QTc exceeds **500 ms**. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Fluoroquinolone warnings**: tendinitis and **Achilles rupture** (higher with steroids and age over 60), peripheral neuropathy, CNS effects, **dysglycaemia**, **aortic aneurysm and dissection** (FDA 2018), and EMA/MHRA restrictions to infections where other antibiotics are unsuitable (MHRA 2024). [MHRA 2024]`,`**Aminoglycosides**: nephrotoxicity (proximal tubule, non-oliguric, usually reversible) and **ototoxicity** (vestibular with gentamicin and streptomycin, cochlear with amikacin; irreversible; mitochondrial **m.1555A>G** variant raises risk). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Beta-lactams**: hypersensitivity (true IgE allergy under 1% of labelled patients' reactions on retesting), **neurotoxicity** in renal failure, C. difficile; **ceftriaxone with calcium** precipitates in neonates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vancomycin**: **infusion reaction ('vancomycin flushing reaction', formerly red man syndrome)** - histamine release with rapid infusion, prevented by infusing at **no faster than 1 g per hour**; not an allergy. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Pregnancy and lactation safety`,points:[`**FDA PLLR (2015)** replaced letter categories A, B, C, D, X with narrative risk summaries - examiners may still ask the old letters, but write the current approach. [FDA PLLR 2015]`,`**Generally safe**: **penicillins, cephalosporins, azithromycin and erythromycin base**, clindamycin, metronidazole (including first trimester on current data), **nitrofurantoin (avoid at 36 weeks or more)**, fosfomycin, vancomycin, meropenem and first-line ATT (HRZE). [WHO AWaRe Book 2022]`,`**Avoid tetracyclines including doxycycline** after the first trimester (teeth discolouration, bone growth, maternal hepatotoxicity with high IV doses) - azithromycin replaces doxycycline for scrub typhus in pregnancy. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Avoid aminoglycosides**, especially **streptomycin** (fetal eighth-nerve damage) - use only for life-threatening infection with level monitoring. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Cotrimoxazole**: folate antagonism in the **first trimester** (neural tube risk - give high-dose folic acid) and **kernicterus near term**; still used when benefit is clear (PCP prophylaxis in HIV). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Fluoroquinolones**: avoided due to animal cartilage data, though human data are reassuring - used when no alternative exists (e.g. DR-TB). **Chloramphenicol** near term causes **grey baby syndrome**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Antifungals**: avoid **high-dose fluconazole (400-800 mg daily) in the first trimester** (craniofacial and skeletal defects); single-dose 150 mg is generally considered acceptable; avoid voriconazole. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Lactation**: most antibiotics are compatible; watch for infant diarrhoea and thrush; avoid chloramphenicol, and prefer short courses of fluoroquinolones or tetracyclines only if no alternative. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Special populations - children, elderly, critically ill`,points:[`**Children**: weight-based dosing; **doxycycline is acceptable for courses of 21 days or less at any age** (AAP 2021); **fluoroquinolones** reserved for specific indications; **sulphonamides and ceftriaxone** avoided in neonatal jaundice (bilirubin displacement). [AAP Red Book 2021]`,`**Neonates**: immature glucuronidation causes **grey baby syndrome** with chloramphenicol; ceftriaxone with calcium-containing IV fluids is contraindicated. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Elderly (Beers 2023)**: avoid nitrofurantoin if CrCl under 30 or long-term; caution with **fluoroquinolones** (delirium, tendon, aortic, hypoglycaemia), **cotrimoxazole with ACE inhibitors/ARBs** (hyperkalaemia) and with warfarin. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Critically ill**: expanded Vd, augmented renal clearance or AKI, hypoalbuminaemia, CRRT and ECMO alter levels - use **high loading doses, prolonged infusions and TDM**. [Roberts, Lancet ID 2014]`,`**CRRT**: drug clearance depends on effluent rate; most beta-lactams need doses close to normal renal function at standard effluent rates (20-25 mL/kg/h) - under-dosing is commoner than toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obesity and malnutrition**: dose by appropriate weight; malnutrition reduces albumin and lean mass (creatinine overestimates GFR). [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Monitoring during therapy`,points:[`**Aminoglycosides**: creatinine every 2-3 days, levels per nomogram, audiometry in courses over 7-10 days, and stop by day 5-7 where possible. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vancomycin**: creatinine at least every 2-3 days (daily in ICU), AUC or trough at steady state (after the 4th dose), weekly CBC for neutropenia in long courses. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Linezolid**: **weekly CBC** (thrombocytopenia after 10-14 days), visual acuity and colour vision in long courses (DR-TB), lactate if symptomatic. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Colistin/polymyxin B**: daily creatinine and urine output (AKI in 30-60%), and watch for neurotoxicity; polymyxin B causes skin hyperpigmentation. [Polymyxin consensus 2019]`,`**ATT**: baseline LFT in high-risk patients (alcohol, HBV/HCV, HIV, elderly, pregnancy) and if symptoms; colour vision for ethambutol; ECG for QT drugs in DR-TB. [NTEP 2025]`,`**OPAT and long courses**: weekly CBC, creatinine and LFT for beta-lactams (neutropenia, hepatitis), and line care checks. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**AGS Beers Criteria 2023**: nitrofurantoin avoided if CrCl under 30 mL/min; caution with fluoroquinolones and with cotrimoxazole plus renin-angiotensin blockers in older adults. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**BLING III and IPD meta-analysis (JAMA 2024)**: continuous beta-lactam infusion associated with lower 90-day mortality in sepsis on pooled data. [BLING III 2024]`,`**MHRA (January 2024)** restricted systemic fluoroquinolones to situations where other recommended antibiotics are inappropriate, reinforcing the EMA 2018-2019 restrictions. [MHRA 2024]`,`**ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP 2020 vancomycin consensus** remains current: AUC-guided dosing (400-600), loading 20-35 mg/kg, no trough-only targets. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**KDIGO 2024 CKD guideline** supports eGFR (creatinine with cystatin C where available) for drug dosing decisions when accuracy matters. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**WHO AWaRe 2025 and EML 2025** - choose the Access option first even when renal or hepatic issues tempt broad agents; see the empirical antibiotics topic. [WHO AWaRe 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classify antibiotics by PK/PD** - time-dependent (beta-lactams, also vancomycin partially), concentration-dependent (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, daptomycin, metronidazole), AUC-dependent (vancomycin, linezolid, azithromycin, tetracyclines). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Why once-daily aminoglycosides?** High Cmax/MIC, post-antibiotic effect, and saturable renal cortical uptake (less nephrotoxicity), plus less adaptive resistance. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Why loading doses in AKI?** Loading depends on Vd, not clearance - under-dosing the first dose is a common and lethal error. [SSC 2021]`,`**Interaction viva**: rifampicin with OCP, warfarin, dolutegravir, DOACs; linezolid with SSRIs; carbapenem with valproate; clarithromycin with simvastatin; cotrimoxazole with ACE inhibitors. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Pregnancy viva**: name 4 safe (penicillins, cephalosporins, azithromycin, nitrofurantoin before term) and 4 avoided (tetracyclines, aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, cotrimoxazole near term) antibiotics. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`PK/PD classification of antimicrobials`,columns:[`Killing pattern`,`PK/PD target`,`Drugs`,`Dosing strategy`],rows:[[`Time-dependent`,`fT>MIC: penicillins 50%, cephalosporins 60-70%, carbapenems 40%`,`Beta-lactams (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, monobactams)`,`Frequent doses, prolonged or continuous infusion`],[`Concentration-dependent`,`Cmax/MIC 8-10 or more`,`Aminoglycosides, daptomycin, metronidazole`,`Large once-daily dose`],[`AUC-dependent`,`AUC24/MIC: vancomycin 400-600; quinolones over 125 (Gram-negative)`,`Vancomycin, fluoroquinolones, linezolid, azithromycin, tetracyclines, colistin`,`Optimise total daily exposure; TDM for vancomycin`],[`Post-antibiotic effect - long`,`Suppression after levels fall below MIC`,`Aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, carbapenems`,`Allows extended intervals`]]},{heading:`Renal dose adjustment of common antimicrobials`,columns:[`Drug`,`Normal dose`,`CrCl 10-50 mL/min`,`CrCl under 10 / haemodialysis`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV daily`,`No change`,`No change (max 2 g/day)`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`1 g 12-hourly (26-50); 500 mg 12-hourly (10-25)`,`500 mg daily; dose after HD`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`4.5 g 8-hourly (20-40)`,`4.5 g 12-hourly; extra dose after HD`],[`Vancomycin`,`Load 20-35 mg/kg, then 15-20 mg/kg 8-12-hourly`,`Full load, then extend interval by AUC/levels`,`Load 25 mg/kg, then 500-1000 mg after each HD`],[`Gentamicin`,`5-7 mg/kg once daily`,`Same dose, interval 36-48 h by level`,`Dose after HD by level`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`500-750 mg oral 12-hourly`,`250-500 mg 12-hourly (CrCl 30-50)`,`250-500 mg daily`],[`Cotrimoxazole`,`960 mg 12-hourly`,`50% dose if CrCl 15-30`,`Avoid if under 15 unless on HD`],[`Fluconazole`,`200-400 mg daily`,`50% maintenance (CrCl 50 or less)`,`Full dose after each HD`],[`Doxycycline, azithromycin, linezolid, clindamycin, metronidazole`,`Usual`,`No change`,`No change`]]},{heading:`TDM targets`,columns:[`Drug`,`When to sample`,`Target`,`Toxicity / action`],rows:[[`Vancomycin`,`Steady state; Bayesian 1-2 levels or peak plus trough`,`AUC24 400-600 mg.h/L (trough 10-15 if AUC not available)`,`AKI above AUC 600 or trough over 15-20`],[`Gentamicin (once daily)`,`6-14 h after dose (Hartford nomogram)`,`Trough under 1 mg/L`,`Extend interval; nephro- and ototoxicity`],[`Amikacin (once daily)`,`Peak 30-60 min after infusion; random level by nomogram`,`Peak about 8-10 x MIC (often 50-64 mg/L in critically ill)`,`Ototoxicity (cochlear), nephrotoxicity`],[`Teicoplanin`,`Trough before dose, day 3-5`,`At least 15-20 mg/L`,`Higher for bone and endocarditis`],[`Voriconazole`,`Trough day 5`,`1-5.5 mg/L`,`Visual effects, hepatotoxicity, encephalopathy above range`],[`Linezolid`,`Trough in long courses or CKD`,`2-7 mg/L`,`Thrombocytopenia above 7-8 mg/L`]]},{heading:`Key interactions and safety cautions - drug table`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Rifampicin`,`10 mg/kg oral daily (450-600 mg)`,`As per TB or other regimen`,`CYP3A4/P-gp inducer: OCP, warfarin, DOACs, dolutegravir (double), PIs; hepatitis, orange secretions`],[`Clarithromycin`,`500 mg oral 12-hourly`,`5-14 days`,`CYP3A4 inhibitor: statins, colchicine, DOACs; QT prolongation`],[`Linezolid`,`600 mg oral/IV 12-hourly (600 mg daily in DR-TB)`,`Watch beyond 14 days`,`Serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, thrombocytopenia, optic and peripheral neuropathy`],[`Cotrimoxazole`,`960 mg oral 12-hourly (higher for PCP)`,`By indication`,`Hyperkalaemia with ACEi/ARB, warfarin and methotrexate interaction, SJS, first trimester and term caution`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`By culture`,`Drops valproate levels - seizures; neurotoxicity in AKI`],[`Levofloxacin / moxifloxacin`,`750 mg / 400 mg oral daily`,`5-7 days (longer in TB)`,`QT, tendon rupture, aortic aneurysm, dysglycaemia, chelation with antacids and iron`],[`Gentamicin`,`5-7 mg/kg IV once daily`,`Level-guided; ideally 5-7 days or less`,`Nephrotoxicity, irreversible ototoxicity; avoid in pregnancy and myasthenia`],[`Vancomycin`,`Load 20-35 mg/kg, then 15-20 mg/kg 8-12-hourly`,`AUC-guided`,`AKI (worse with pip-taz), infusion flushing reaction if faster than 1 g/h`]]},{heading:`Antimicrobials in pregnancy`,columns:[`Generally safe`,`Use with caution`,`Avoid`,`Reason to avoid`],rows:[[`Penicillins, cephalosporins`,`Nitrofurantoin (not at 36 weeks or more)`,`Tetracyclines, doxycycline`,`Teeth and bone; maternal hepatotoxicity`],[`Azithromycin, erythromycin base`,`Cotrimoxazole (first trimester, term)`,`Aminoglycosides, streptomycin`,`Fetal ototoxicity`],[`Clindamycin, metronidazole`,`Fluoroquinolones (if no alternative)`,`Chloramphenicol near term`,`Grey baby syndrome`],[`Fosfomycin, vancomycin, meropenem`,`Single-dose fluconazole 150 mg`,`High-dose fluconazole first trimester, voriconazole`,`Craniofacial and skeletal defects`],[`HRZE (first-line ATT)`,`Linezolid (limited data)`,`Ribavirin, griseofulvin`,`Teratogenic`]]}],redFlags:[`Rising creatinine on vancomycin, aminoglycoside or colistin - check levels and stop or adjust today.`,`New tinnitus, vertigo or hearing loss on an aminoglycoside - stop the drug.`,`QTc over 500 ms or syncope on QT-prolonging antimicrobials - stop and correct K and Mg.`,`Confusion, myoclonus or seizures on cefepime or carbapenem in renal failure - neurotoxicity.`,`Jaundice or ALT over 5 times ULN on ATT - stop hepatotoxic drugs.`,`Fever, agitation, clonus on linezolid with an SSRI - serotonin syndrome.`,`Platelets falling below 100,000 on linezolid - stop or reduce dose.`,`Seizure in a patient on valproate started on meropenem - interaction; change one drug.`],pearls:[`**Beta-lactams: time; aminoglycosides: peak; vancomycin and quinolones: AUC**.`,`**Loading dose depends on Vd, not kidneys** - give the full first dose even in AKI.`,`Vancomycin: **AUC 400-600**, not trough 15-20 (ASHP/IDSA 2020).`,`**Ceftriaxone, doxycycline, azithromycin, linezolid, clindamycin, metronidazole** - no renal adjustment.`,`Rifampicin **halves** most co-drugs; **double dolutegravir** to 50 mg twice daily.`,`**Carbapenem plus valproate** - valproate levels crash within a day.`,`**Linezolid plus SSRI** - serotonin syndrome; linezolid beyond 2 weeks - check platelets.`,`Fluoroquinolones: **2 hours before or 6 hours after** antacids, iron or calcium.`,`Pregnancy: avoid the **'TACo'** drugs - Tetracyclines, Aminoglycosides, Chloramphenicol, cotrimoxazole near term (and quinolones if possible).`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-q1`,stem:`Which pharmacodynamic index best predicts the efficacy of meropenem?`,options:[`Peak concentration to MIC ratio`,`24-hour AUC to MIC ratio`,`Percentage of the dosing interval that free drug stays above the MIC`,`Duration of post-antibiotic effect`],answer:2,explanation:`Carbapenems, like all beta-lactams, show time-dependent killing, so the percentage of the dosing interval with free drug above the MIC (about 40% for carbapenems) predicts efficacy, which is why prolonged infusions help. Peak/MIC governs aminoglycosides, AUC/MIC governs vancomycin and fluoroquinolones, and post-antibiotic effect is a supporting property rather than the efficacy index.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-q2`,stem:`A 70 kg man with septic shock and AKI (creatinine 3.2 mg/dL) needs vancomycin for suspected MRSA bacteraemia. What is the correct first dose?`,options:[`500 mg because of renal failure`,`15 mg/kg every 48 hours from the start`,`No vancomycin until a trough level is available`,`A full loading dose of 20-35 mg/kg (about 1.5-2.5 g), then adjust maintenance by levels`],answer:3,explanation:`The loading dose depends on volume of distribution, which expands in sepsis, not on renal clearance, so a full 20-35 mg/kg load is needed even in AKI, with maintenance then guided by levels or AUC. A reduced first dose leaves the patient sub-therapeutic for days, and no level can be measured before any drug has been given.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-q3`,stem:`A woman on rifampicin-containing ATT asks about contraception. She currently uses a combined oral contraceptive pill. What is the best advice?`,options:[`Continue the pill; rifampicin has no effect on hormones`,`Switch to a copper IUCD or DMPA injection, because rifampicin induces CYP3A4 and reduces pill efficacy`,`Take the pill twice daily`,`Stop contraception because ATT is safe in pregnancy`],answer:1,explanation:`Rifampicin strongly induces CYP3A4, lowering ethinylestradiol and progestogen levels and causing contraceptive failure, so a method unaffected by enzyme induction such as a copper IUCD or DMPA is advised, with induction persisting for about 2 weeks after rifampicin stops. Doubling the pill is unreliable, and ATT safety in pregnancy does not justify an unplanned pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-q4`,stem:`Which antibiotic does NOT require dose adjustment in a patient with CrCl of 15 mL/min?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone`,`Meropenem`,`Levofloxacin`,`Cotrimoxazole`],answer:0,explanation:`Ceftriaxone is cleared by both biliary and renal routes, so no adjustment is needed in renal failure (a ceiling of 2 g daily is often advised when hepatic and renal failure coexist). Meropenem, levofloxacin and cotrimoxazole are mainly renally excreted and accumulate at low clearance, causing neurotoxicity, QT problems or hyperkalaemia unless the dose or interval is changed.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-q5`,stem:`According to the 2020 ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP consensus, what is the preferred monitoring strategy for vancomycin in serious MRSA infection?`,options:[`Trough levels of 15-20 mg/L for all patients`,`Peak levels of 30-40 mg/L`,`AUC24 of 400-600 mg.h/L estimated by Bayesian software or two levels`,`No monitoring if creatinine is normal`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2020 consensus moved from trough-only targets to AUC-guided dosing with a target AUC24 of 400-600 mg.h/L (assuming MIC 1), estimated by Bayesian software or a peak and trough, because troughs of 15-20 mg/L caused more AKI without better outcomes. Peak targets apply to aminoglycosides, and omitting monitoring risks both failure and nephrotoxicity.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Explain the pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic principles of antimicrobial dosing. How are these applied in critically ill patients and in renal failure? Add a note on therapeutic drug monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to the drug (absorption, distribution, clearance) and pharmacodynamics what the drug does to the organism relative to the MIC.`,`Antimicrobials kill in a time-dependent (beta-lactams), concentration-dependent (aminoglycosides) or AUC-dependent (vancomycin, fluoroquinolones) manner, and dosing follows that pattern.`],answer:[{heading:`PK principles`,points:[`Bioavailability (oral equals IV for quinolones, doxycycline, linezolid, metronidazole), Vd hydrophilic versus lipophilic, protein binding, clearance, half-life and steady state.`]},{heading:`PD patterns`,points:[`Time-dependent fT>MIC targets; concentration-dependent Cmax/MIC 8-10; AUC/MIC 400-600 vancomycin, over 125 quinolones; post-antibiotic effect.`]},{heading:`Critically ill`,points:[`Expanded Vd, augmented renal clearance, hypoalbuminaemia, CRRT/ECMO; full loading doses, prolonged beta-lactam infusion (BLING III), once-daily aminoglycosides.`]},{heading:`Renal failure`,points:[`Cockcroft-Gault; loading dose unchanged; adjust maintenance by dose or interval; drugs needing no change; neurotoxicity of cefepime and carbapenems; dosing after HD.`]},{heading:`TDM`,points:[`Vancomycin AUC 400-600 (2020 consensus); gentamicin Hartford nomogram, trough under 1; teicoplanin, voriconazole, linezolid targets; sampling times.`]}],mustDraw:[`Concentration-time curve showing Cmax/MIC, AUC/MIC and fT>MIC`,`Table of PK/PD class with examples and dosing strategy`],markSplit:[{part:`PK principles`,marks:2},{part:`PD patterns with curve`,marks:3},{part:`Critically ill and renal failure`,marks:3},{part:`TDM`,marks:2}],keywords:[`fT>MIC`,`Cmax/MIC`,`AUC/MIC`,`loading dose`,`Vd`,`augmented renal clearance`,`TDM`,`Cockcroft-Gault`]},{id:`infectious-fever-antimicrobial-pharmacology-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Short note: Clinically important antimicrobial drug interactions.`,openingLines:[`Antimicrobial interactions are mostly pharmacokinetic through CYP450 induction or inhibition, chelation and P-glycoprotein, or pharmacodynamic through additive QT, serotonergic or nephrotoxic effects.`,`Rifampicin, macrolides, azoles, linezolid, cotrimoxazole and carbapenems account for most clinically important interactions.`],answer:[{heading:`Enzyme inducers`,points:[`Rifampicin: OCP, warfarin, DOACs, dolutegravir (50 mg BD), PIs, tacrolimus, azoles, steroids; lasts 2 weeks after stopping.`]},{heading:`Enzyme inhibitors`,points:[`Clarithromycin and erythromycin: statins, colchicine; fluconazole: warfarin, sulfonylureas; ciprofloxacin: theophylline, tizanidine.`]},{heading:`Pharmacodynamic and other`,points:[`Linezolid with SSRIs; cotrimoxazole with ACEi (hyperkalaemia) and methotrexate; carbapenem with valproate; QT drug combinations; quinolone chelation; vancomycin with pip-taz (AKI).`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: antimicrobial - interacting drug - mechanism - consequence`],markSplit:[{part:`Inducers`,marks:2},{part:`Inhibitors`,marks:1},{part:`Pharmacodynamic interactions`,marks:2}],keywords:[`CYP3A4`,`rifampicin`,`linezolid`,`valproate`,`QT`,`chelation`]}],references:[`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Treatment and Prophylaxis of Bacterial Infections`,`Rybak MJ et al. Therapeutic monitoring of vancomycin for serious MRSA infections: revised consensus guideline (ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP), Am J Health Syst Pharm, 2020`,`Tsuji BT et al. International consensus guidelines for the optimal use of the polymyxins, Pharmacotherapy, 2019`,`American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated Beers Criteria, J Am Geriatr Soc, 2023`,`Dulhunty JM et al. Continuous vs intermittent beta-lactam infusion in critically ill patients with sepsis (BLING III), JAMA, 2024`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) guidelines and Guidelines for PMDT in India, MoHFW`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`]});var t=[];t.push({id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis`,title:`Infective endocarditis`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Infective endocarditis (IE)** is microbial infection of the **endocardial surface - native or prosthetic valve, or an intracardiac device** - forming a **vegetation**; it is diagnosed by the **2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria** (blood cultures plus imaging), treated with **prolonged bactericidal IV antibiotics** counted from the first negative culture, and needs **early surgery** for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or embolic risk.`,keywords:[`IE`,`bacterial endocarditis`,`subacute bacterial endocarditis`,`SABE`,`prosthetic valve endocarditis`,`PVE`,`modified Duke criteria`,`Duke-ISCVID 2023`,`vegetation`,`Osler nodes`,`Janeway lesions`,`Roth spots`,`culture-negative endocarditis`,`HACEK`,`Streptococcus gallolyticus`,`Enterococcus faecalis`,`endocarditis team`,`POET trial`,`CIED infection`,`dental prophylaxis amoxicillin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Infective endocarditis** is infection of the endocardium, usually a **heart valve**, with a **vegetation** of platelets, fibrin, microcolonies and inflammatory cells as its hallmark lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Global incidence is about **3-10 per 100 000 person-years** and rising, driven by **older age, prosthetic valves, intracardiac devices and healthcare contact**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In high-income countries **Staphylococcus aureus** is now the commonest cause (about 30%), followed by oral streptococci and enterococci; **healthcare-associated IE** makes up about a quarter to a third of cases. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In **India** the patient is younger (mean age in the 30s-40s in most tertiary series) and **rheumatic heart disease** remains the commonest underlying lesion, though congenital, degenerative and prosthetic valve substrates are rising. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Indian series report **culture-negative IE in about 30-50%**, chiefly from **antibiotics given before blood cultures** - the single most avoidable failure in Indian practice. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE)** forms 10-30% of all IE; **early PVE** (under 1 year after surgery) is mostly staphylococcal and nosocomial, late PVE resembles native valve IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In-hospital mortality is **15-30%** and 1-year mortality about **30-40%**, highest with S. aureus, PVE, heart failure, stroke and perivalvular abscess. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Right-sided IE** (5-10%) is mainly tricuspid, in **people who inject drugs**, with indwelling lines or pacemaker leads, and is usually S. aureus. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Viridans (oral) streptococci** cause most community native-valve IE in India, with a **subacute** course; they enter through dental and gingival bacteraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Staphylococcus aureus** causes **acute, destructive IE** that can infect a **normal valve**, with rapid abscess formation and metastatic infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enterococcus faecalis** affects elderly men after genitourinary or bowel procedures; **Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis)** IE is linked to **colorectal neoplasia** and mandates colonoscopy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Coagulase-negative staphylococci** cause early PVE and device infection; **HACEK organisms** (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) cause slow-growing culture-positive IE with large vegetations. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Culture-negative causes**: prior antibiotics (commonest), **Coxiella burnetii, Bartonella, Brucella** (important in India), Tropheryma whipplei, fungi (Candida, Aspergillus) and Legionella. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**High-risk substrates**: previous IE, **prosthetic valve or valve repair material**, untreated cyanotic congenital heart disease and congenital repairs with prosthetic material. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Intermediate-risk substrates**: **rheumatic heart disease**, degenerative valve disease, **bicuspid aortic valve**, mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation, HCM and cardiac implantable electronic devices (CIED). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Host factors**: injection drug use, haemodialysis, indwelling central lines, diabetes, poor dental hygiene, immunosuppression and recent invasive procedures. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Endothelial injury** from turbulent jets (regurgitant or stenotic lesions, shunts) exposes subendothelial matrix, on which platelets and fibrin form a sterile **non-bacterial thrombotic vegetation (NBTE)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`During a **transient bacteraemia** (chewing, brushing, dental work), organisms with **adhesins** - dextran and FimA in streptococci, **MSCRAMMs** such as clumping factor and fibronectin-binding protein in S. aureus - stick to the NBTE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S. aureus can invade intact endothelium**, which is why it infects normal valves and why a structurally normal heart does not exclude IE in SAB. [Harrison 22e]`,`Vegetations sit on the **low-pressure side of the jet**: the **atrial surface of the mitral valve** and the **ventricular surface of the aortic valve** (Venturi effect). [Harrison 22e]`,`Organisms inside a vegetation are at very high density (**10^9-10^10 per gram**), in a **slow-growing, biofilm-like state** shielded from phagocytes - hence the need for **bactericidal drugs in high doses for weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Local destruction** causes leaflet perforation, chordal rupture and acute regurgitation (heart failure); extension into the annulus causes **abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula and heart block** (aortic root near the AV node). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolisation** of friable vegetation fragments causes stroke, splenic and renal infarcts, mycotic aneurysms and (right-sided) **septic pulmonary emboli**; risk is highest with vegetations **over 10 mm** and in the first 2 weeks of therapy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Immune-complex deposition** from sustained antigenaemia explains **Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis**, low complement and a positive rheumatoid factor in subacute IE. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By **course**: **acute IE** (days, toxic, destructive - usually S. aureus) versus **subacute IE** (weeks to months, low-grade fever, weight loss, immune signs - usually viridans streptococci). [Harrison 22e]`,`By **site and substrate**: **native valve IE (NVE)**, **prosthetic valve IE (PVE)** - early under 1 year, late after 1 year - **CIED-related IE**, and **right-sided IE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`By **acquisition**: community-acquired, **healthcare-associated** (nosocomial, or non-nosocomial with dialysis or lines) and **injection-drug-use** IE - this drives the empirical regimen. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`By **microbiology**: culture-positive, **culture-negative** (prior antibiotics or fastidious organisms) and **non-infective** (marantic NBTE in cancer, **Libman-Sacks** in SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`The diagnostic classification is the **2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria**: **definite** (pathology, or 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor), **possible** (1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor) and **rejected**. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**Rejected** IE: firm alternative diagnosis, resolution with **4 days or less** of antibiotics, no pathological evidence at surgery or autopsy after 4 days or less of antibiotics, or failure to meet possible criteria. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Fever** is present in about **90%**, often with chills, anorexia and weight loss; it may be absent in the elderly, in renal failure and after partial antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`,`The classic Indian OPD story is **prolonged low-grade fever for weeks in a young adult with known RHD**, already treated with several short antibiotic courses - think IE before calling it PUO. [API Textbook 12e]`,`Ask for **recent dental work, procedures, catheters, haemodialysis, injection drug use** and a **prosthetic valve or pacemaker**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic presentations** may be the first sign: **stroke, sudden visual loss, acute limb ischaemia, flank pain with haematuria (renal infarct) or left upper quadrant pain (splenic infarct)**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Breathlessness, orthopnoea and oedema** signal valve destruction with heart failure - the commonest complication and the commonest reason for surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Non-specific features - **back pain, arthralgia, myalgia** - are common and can mimic rheumatic fever, vertebral osteomyelitis or a connective tissue disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right-sided IE** presents with fever and **cough, pleuritic chest pain and haemoptysis** from septic pulmonary emboli, often misread as pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`A **new or changing regurgitant murmur** is found in about **85%**; a murmur may be absent in right-sided and mural IE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splinter haemorrhages** - linear dark-red streaks in the nail bed - are common but non-specific (also trauma, manual work). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osler nodes** are **tender**, pea-sized nodules in the **finger and toe pulps** (immune-complex vasculitis); **Janeway lesions** are **painless** macular haemorrhages on **palms and soles** (septic microemboli). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Roth spots** are retinal haemorrhages with **pale centres** on fundoscopy; **conjunctival petechiae** are seen on everting the lower lid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** (about 30% in subacute IE) and **clubbing** (long-standing subacute IE) favour a subacute course. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **signs of heart failure** (basal crackles, raised JVP) and **neurological deficits** - each changes the surgical decision. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Examine the **teeth and gums**, skin injection sites and line or pacemaker pocket for the portal of entry. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Peripheral stigmata are now seen in **under 10-15%** of cases because diagnosis is earlier - their absence never excludes IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute rheumatic fever** with carditis: young patient with RHD and fever, but **migratory polyarthritis, raised ASO, prolonged PR, negative cultures** and no vegetation on echo. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**Enteric fever, brucellosis and TB**: prolonged fever with splenomegaly; blood culture and echo separate them - brucella can itself cause IE. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**SLE with Libman-Sacks endocarditis** and **antiphospholipid syndrome**: sterile vegetations, positive ANA/anti-dsDNA or antiphospholipid antibodies, low C3 and C4. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Marantic (NBTE) endocarditis** in adenocarcinoma: embolic strokes with sterile vegetations and no fever response pattern. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atrial myxoma**: fever, emboli, raised ESR and a 'tumour plop'; echo shows a pedunculated mass attached to the **interatrial septum**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Vasculitis** (polyarteritis, ANCA vasculitis): fever, purpura, GN and mononeuritis, but negative cultures and no valve lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uncomplicated S. aureus bacteraemia** from a line or skin focus: separate by TOE and by whether bacteraemia clears within 72 hours - see the Bloodstream infection topic. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line to advanced`,points:[`**Three sets of blood cultures** (aerobic and anaerobic bottles, about **10 mL per bottle** in adults) from **separate venepunctures at least 30 minutes apart**, all **before antibiotics**; bacteraemia is continuous, so timing to fever spikes is unnecessary. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In a stable patient who has had antibiotics, **stop them and repeat cultures** after a wash-out where safe; in a toxic patient, take cultures and start empirical treatment within an hour. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Transthoracic echo (TTE)** is the first imaging test; sensitivity is about **70% for native valves** and about **50% for prosthetic valves**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Transoesophageal echo (TOE)** (sensitivity over **90%**) is recommended when TTE is negative with persisting suspicion, in **prosthetic valve or device**, and even after a positive TTE to define complications (except isolated good-quality right-sided NVE). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`If the first echo is negative but suspicion remains, **repeat TTE/TOE in 5-7 days**; repeat at once with any new complication (murmur, heart block, embolism). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Supporting tests: **normocytic anaemia, raised ESR and CRP, microscopic haematuria**, positive rheumatoid factor and low complement; a **new PR prolongation or AV block** on serial ECG suggests an aortic root abscess. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Cardiac CT angiography** detects abscess, pseudoaneurysm and fistula as well as or better than TOE; **brain MRI and abdominal imaging** find silent emboli that add a minor criterion. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**18F-FDG PET/CT** is a **major imaging criterion** in PVE more than 3 months after implantation and helps diagnose device infection; within 3 months uptake is only a minor criterion. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`For culture-negative IE send **serology for Coxiella burnetii (phase I IgG 1:800 or more is a major criterion), Bartonella and Brucella**; send excised **valve tissue for culture, histology and 16S rRNA PCR / sequencing**. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`Do **ANA, anti-dsDNA and antiphospholipid antibodies** when cultures and serology are negative, to exclude non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Management - principles and empirical therapy`,points:[`IE is managed in a **reference centre by an Endocarditis Team** (cardiologist, cardiac surgeon, infectious diseases physician, microbiologist, imaging specialist) - a Class I recommendation. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Use **bactericidal** drugs at **high IV doses for prolonged periods**; duration is counted from the **first day of negative blood cultures** (or from surgery if the excised valve is culture-positive). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Empirical - community NVE or late PVE**: **ampicillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses plus (flu)cloxacillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day IV once daily**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Empirical - penicillin allergy**: **vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day IV in 2 doses plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Empirical - early PVE (under 12 months) or healthcare-associated IE**: **vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day IV plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day plus rifampicin 900-1200 mg/day** in 2-3 doses. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Narrow therapy as soon as the organism and **MIC** are known; **repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours** until negative. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Do not start aspirin or other antiplatelets** to prevent embolism; continue anticoagulation for a mechanical valve but **switch to heparin** in the acute phase and **stop it after an intracranial haemorrhage**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Remove the source**: dental extraction of infected teeth, drainage of abscesses and **complete removal of an infected CIED system** (generator and all leads). [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Targeted antibiotic regimens`,points:[`**Penicillin-susceptible oral streptococci / S. gallolyticus (MIC 0.125 mg/L or less), NVE**: **benzylpenicillin 12-18 million U/day IV** in 4-6 doses, or **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily**, for **4 weeks** (6 weeks in PVE). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Two-week regimen** for uncomplicated streptococcal NVE with normal renal function: penicillin or ceftriaxone **plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg once daily for 2 weeks**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Penicillin-resistant streptococci (MIC above 0.125)**: higher-dose penicillin (24 million U/day) or amoxicillin **plus gentamicin for the first 2 weeks**, total 4 weeks NVE, 6 weeks PVE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**MSSA NVE**: **(flu)cloxacillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses** or **cefazolin 6 g/day IV in 3 doses** for **4-6 weeks**; **gentamicin is not recommended** in staphylococcal NVE (nephrotoxic, no benefit). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**MRSA NVE**: **vancomycin** targeted to an **AUC of 400-600 mg.h/L** (or trough 15-20 mg/L where AUC is not available) or **daptomycin 10 mg/kg once daily**, for 4-6 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Staphylococcal PVE**: anti-staphylococcal drug **plus rifampicin 900 mg/day for 6 weeks or more plus gentamicin for 2 weeks**; add rifampicin only after **3-5 days** of effective therapy once bacteraemia clears. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Enterococcus faecalis**: **ampicillin/amoxicillin 200 mg/kg/day IV plus ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly for 6 weeks** (preferred, and the choice for high-level aminoglycoside resistance), or ampicillin plus gentamicin for 2 weeks within a 4-6-week course. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**HACEK**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily** for 4 weeks (NVE) or 6 weeks (PVE); **fungal IE** needs surgery plus amphotericin B-based therapy and long-term azole suppression. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Partial oral step-down** (POET trial) is endorsed for **stable left-sided IE** from streptococci, E. faecalis, S. aureus or CoNS after **at least 10 days IV** (7 days after surgery), with no abscess on TOE. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Surgery - indications and timing`,points:[`Surgery is needed in about **half of left-sided IE**; timing is **emergency (within 24 hours)**, **urgent (within 3-5 days)** or **non-urgent (same admission)**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Heart failure**: **emergency surgery** for refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock from severe regurgitation, obstruction or fistula; urgent surgery for severe regurgitation with heart failure symptoms. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Uncontrolled infection**: **abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula, enlarging vegetation, prosthetic dehiscence or new AV block** - urgent surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Difficult organisms**: fungal or multidrug-resistant IE, and **blood cultures still positive after 1 week** of appropriate therapy with metastatic foci controlled - urgent surgery; PVE due to **S. aureus or non-HACEK Gram-negatives** should be considered for surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic prevention**: urgent surgery for a **vegetation 10 mm or more with an embolic event** on therapy, or 10 mm or more with severe valve disease and low operative risk; **30 mm or more** alone may justify surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`After an **ischaemic stroke or TIA without haemorrhage or coma**, indicated surgery **should not be delayed**; after **intracranial haemorrhage** surgery is usually deferred for about **4 weeks**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Right-sided IE**: surgery for right heart failure with severe TR unresponsive to diuretics, persistent bacteraemia, large (over 20 mm) tricuspid vegetations with recurrent pulmonary emboli, or difficult organisms. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: IE is rare but carries high maternal and fetal mortality; penicillins, cephalosporins and vancomycin are usable, **aminoglycosides only when essential**; decide delivery timing with the Endocarditis Team. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Elderly**: atypical, afebrile or confusional presentation; enterococcal and S. gallolyticus IE are more common - **colonoscopy is recommended** for S. gallolyticus. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: dose vancomycin and gentamicin by **levels**; dialysis catheters are a major source of S. aureus IE; prefer **ampicillin-ceftriaxone** over gentamicin for E. faecalis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**People who inject drugs**: tricuspid S. aureus IE with septic emboli; a **shorter 2-week cloxacillin course** is acceptable in uncomplicated right-sided MSSA IE; offer **opioid agonist therapy** and harm reduction. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**CIED infection**: **complete hardware removal** (generator and all leads) plus 4-6 weeks of antibiotics; reimplant at a new site only after cultures clear. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: three good-volume cultures before antibiotics and a TTE are achievable at most district hospitals; refer for TOE, surgery and serology rather than stacking empirical antibiotics. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Children with congenital heart disease**: S. aureus and viridans streptococci dominate; dosing by weight, and post-repair residual shunts at a prosthetic patch remain high-risk lifelong. [AHA 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Heart failure** (about 40%) is the commonest complication and the strongest predictor of death; aortic regurgitation decompensates fastest. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Perivalvular extension** (abscess, fistula, heart block) is commoner in **aortic and prosthetic valve IE** and usually needs surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Systemic embolism** occurs in **20-50%**; the brain is involved in about two-thirds, and embolic risk falls sharply after the **first 2 weeks** of effective antibiotics. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Neurological complications** (15-30%): ischaemic stroke, haemorrhage, **mycotic (infectious) aneurysm**, abscess and meningitis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Renal**: immune-complex GN, infarcts, and **drug-induced AKI** (aminoglycosides, vancomycin) - a reason to keep aminoglycoside courses short. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Splenic infarct or abscess** - persistent fever with left upper quadrant pain after valve control suggests abscess needing drainage or splenectomy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Relapse** (same organism within 6 months) suggests inadequate duration or an uncontrolled focus; **recurrence risk** after one episode is several-fold, so prophylaxis is lifelong. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Any fever with a **murmur, prosthetic valve, device or S. aureus bacteraemia** needs blood cultures and **same-week echo**; refer to a centre with cardiac surgery if IE is likely. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`During treatment: **daily clinical review, blood cultures until clear, weekly CBC, creatinine and CRP**, drug levels (vancomycin, gentamicin) and **ECG** for conduction block. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`At the end of therapy: **baseline TTE** for future comparison, dental review, and teaching the patient to **report fever and get cultures before any antibiotic**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Follow-up at **1, 3, 6 and 12 months** for relapse, heart failure and valve dysfunction. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Dental hygiene and twice-yearly dental review** in all at-risk patients is the main preventive measure - daily bacteraemia from chewing exceeds that from a single extraction. [AHA 2021 prevention]`,`**Antibiotic prophylaxis** is given only to **high-risk patients** before **dental procedures involving gingival or periapical manipulation or perforation of oral mucosa**: **amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before** (children 50 mg/kg). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`For penicillin allergy use **azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg** or **doxycycline 100 mg**; **clindamycin is no longer recommended** because of C. difficile risk. [AHA 2021 prevention]`,`**RHD in India**: **benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis** prevents rheumatic recurrences but **does not cover IE** - these patients may carry penicillin-resistant oral streptococci, so use a non-penicillin agent for dental cover when prophylaxis is chosen. [AHA 2021 prevention]`,`Strict **asepsis for central lines, dialysis access and device implantation** prevents healthcare-associated IE, now a quarter or more of cases. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Duke-ISCVID 2023** (International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases) updated the 2000 modified Duke criteria: **cardiac CT and FDG PET/CT** became major imaging criteria and **surgical inspection** became a major criterion. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`Duke-ISCVID widened **'typical' organisms** to include **S. lugdunensis, E. faecalis (any setting), all streptococci except S. pneumoniae and S. pyogenes, Granulicatella, Abiotrophia and Gemella**, and added **PCR / sequencing** and **Bartonella serology**. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`Duke-ISCVID added new minor predispositions (**transcatheter valves, CIED, HOCM, prior valve repair**) and dropped the timing rules for culture positivity. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**ESC 2023 IE** made the **Endocarditis Team** Class I, defined **urgent surgery as within 3-5 days** (previously up to 7), and endorsed **partial oral step-down** after the POET trial. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`ESC 2023 gave **cefazolin** equal standing with (flu)cloxacillin for MSSA, removed **gentamicin from staphylococcal NVE**, and recommended **TOE even after a positive TTE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`ESC 2023 extended **high-risk prophylaxis to transcatheter valve recipients** and to patients with **ventricular assist devices**, and removed clindamycin for penicillin allergy (in line with **AHA 2021**). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Guideline difference**: ESC 2023 and AHA 2021 do not give routine prophylaxis to **RHD without a prosthesis**, while many Indian cardiologists still do; the high-risk list is the examinable answer. [AHA 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Commonest viva: **'Name the modified Duke criteria'** - give the 2023 Duke-ISCVID version, starting with the **two major criteria (micro and imaging)** and the new **surgical** major criterion. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**Osler is Ouch** (tender, pulps), **Janeway is painless** (palms and soles) - a favourite one-mark trap. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest mistake** in India: starting antibiotics in a febrile RHD patient **before three blood cultures**, which converts a curable streptococcal IE into culture-negative IE. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Vegetation site**: atrial side of the mitral, ventricular side of the aortic - the low-pressure side of the jet. [Harrison 22e]`,`**New PR prolongation** in aortic valve IE means a **root abscess** - an urgent surgical indication. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**S. gallolyticus means colonoscopy**; **S. aureus bacteraemia means echo**; **prosthetic valve means TOE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Duration is counted from the **first negative blood culture**, not from admission. [ESC 2023 IE]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antibiotic regimens in infective endocarditis (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Benzylpenicillin (streptococci, MIC 0.125 or less)`,`12-18 million U/day IV in 4-6 doses or continuous infusion`,`4 weeks NVE; 6 weeks PVE; 2 weeks if combined with gentamicin in uncomplicated NVE`,`Hypersensitivity; seizures at high dose in renal failure; sodium/potassium load`],[`Ceftriaxone (streptococci, HACEK; with ampicillin for E. faecalis)`,`2 g IV once daily (streptococci, HACEK); 2 g IV 12-hourly with ampicillin for E. faecalis`,`4 weeks NVE, 6 weeks PVE; 6 weeks for E. faecalis`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; avoid with calcium infusions in neonates`],[`(Flu)cloxacillin (MSSA)`,`12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses`,`4-6 weeks NVE; 6 weeks or more PVE (with rifampicin + gentamicin)`,`Phlebitis, cholestatic hepatitis, neutropenia with long courses`],[`Cefazolin (MSSA, alternative)`,`6 g/day IV in 3 doses`,`4-6 weeks`,`Usable in non-severe penicillin allergy; rash, C. difficile`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA, penicillin allergy, empirical PVE)`,`Loading 25-30 mg/kg, then 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly`,`AUC 400-600 mg.h/L (or trough 15-20 mg/L); 4-6 weeks`,`Nephrotoxicity (worse with gentamicin), infusion reaction - infuse over at least 1 hour, ototoxicity`],[`Daptomycin (MRSA, alternative)`,`10 mg/kg IV once daily`,`4-6 weeks; weekly CPK`,`Myopathy (CPK), eosinophilic pneumonia; useless in pneumonia (inactivated by surfactant)`],[`Gentamicin (synergy)`,`3 mg/kg IV once daily`,`2 weeks only (streptococci, enterococci, staphylococcal PVE); trough under 1 mg/L`,`Nephrotoxicity and irreversible vestibular/cochlear toxicity; not for staphylococcal NVE`],[`Rifampicin (prosthetic material)`,`900 mg/day IV or oral in 2-3 doses`,`Start after 3-5 days of effective therapy; 6 weeks or more`,`Hepatotoxicity, many drug interactions (warfarin, DOACs, ART), orange secretions; never alone`]]},{heading:`2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria for infective endocarditis`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Includes`,`Note`],rows:[[`Major - microbiological`,`Typical organism in 2 or more separate blood culture sets; non-typical organism in 3 or more sets (with intracardiac prosthesis for skin commensals); positive PCR/sequencing for Coxiella, Bartonella or T. whipplei; Coxiella phase I IgG 1:800 or more; Bartonella IgG 1:800 or more`,`Typical now includes S. aureus, S. lugdunensis, E. faecalis, most streptococci, HACEK, Granulicatella, Abiotrophia, Gemella`],[`Major - imaging`,`Echo or cardiac CT: vegetation, perforation, aneurysm, abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula; new significant regurgitation; new prosthetic dehiscence; FDG PET/CT uptake at valve, graft or leads`,`PET is major only if more than 3 months after implantation`],[`Major - surgical`,`IE seen on direct inspection at heart surgery`,`New in 2023; applies when imaging and microbiology fall short`],[`Minor - predisposition`,`Previous IE, prosthetic valve, prior valve repair, congenital heart disease, more than mild regurgitation or stenosis, CIED, HOCM, injection drug use`,`Transcatheter valves and devices included`],[`Minor - fever`,`Temperature above 38.0 C`,`-`],[`Minor - vascular`,`Arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, cerebral or splenic abscess, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial haemorrhage, conjunctival haemorrhage, Janeway lesions, purulent purpura`,`Includes silent emboli found on imaging`],[`Minor - immunological`,`Positive rheumatoid factor, Osler nodes, Roth spots, immune-complex glomerulonephritis`,`-`],[`Minor - microbiological / imaging / exam`,`Culture positive short of major; PET uptake within 3 months of implant; new regurgitant murmur on auscultation if echo unavailable`,`-`],[`Classification`,`Definite: pathology, or 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor; Possible: 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor`,`Rejected if alternative diagnosis or resolution with 4 days or less of antibiotics`]]},{heading:`Indications and timing of surgery (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Indication`,`Example`,`Timing`],rows:[[`Heart failure - shock or refractory pulmonary oedema`,`Acute severe aortic or mitral regurgitation, obstruction, fistula`,`Emergency (within 24 hours)`],[`Heart failure - symptomatic severe regurgitation`,`Severe AR/MR with heart failure symptoms, haemodynamically stable`,`Urgent (3-5 days)`],[`Locally uncontrolled infection`,`Abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula, enlarging vegetation, prosthetic dehiscence, new AV block`,`Urgent`],[`Difficult organism`,`Fungi, multidrug-resistant organisms, S. aureus or non-HACEK Gram-negative PVE`,`Urgent or non-urgent`],[`Persistent bacteraemia`,`Blood cultures positive after 1 week of appropriate therapy, metastatic foci controlled`,`Urgent`],[`Embolic risk`,`Vegetation 10 mm or more with embolic event on therapy; 10 mm or more with severe valve disease and low operative risk; 30 mm or more`,`Urgent`],[`After stroke`,`Ischaemic stroke or TIA without haemorrhage or coma`,`Do not delay`],[`After intracranial haemorrhage`,`Haemorrhagic stroke`,`Usually defer about 4 weeks`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever with a murmur`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Infective endocarditis`,`Persistent bacteraemia, vegetation, embolic and immune signs`,`3 blood cultures + TTE/TOE`],[`Acute rheumatic fever (carditis)`,`Migratory polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, prolonged PR`,`ASO/anti-DNase B, ESR/CRP, echo (no vegetation)`],[`Libman-Sacks endocarditis (SLE/APS)`,`Multisystem lupus features, sterile vegetations`,`ANA, anti-dsDNA, antiphospholipid antibodies, C3/C4`],[`Marantic endocarditis (NBTE)`,`Known or occult adenocarcinoma, embolic strokes, no fever pattern`,`Negative cultures; search for cancer`],[`Atrial myxoma`,`Positional symptoms, tumour plop, raised ESR`,`Echo: pedunculated mass on interatrial septum`],[`Enteric fever / brucellosis with flow murmur`,`Hepatosplenomegaly, relative bradycardia, raw milk exposure`,`Blood culture, brucella serology, normal echo`],[`Anaemia or thyrotoxicosis with flow murmur`,`Soft ejection systolic murmur, no regurgitation`,`Haemoglobin, TSH, normal echo`]]},{heading:`Echocardiography and imaging in IE`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`What it shows / cut-off`],rows:[[`Transthoracic echo (TTE)`,`First test in all suspected IE`,`Sensitivity about 70% NVE, 50% PVE; baseline LV function`],[`Transoesophageal echo (TOE)`,`Negative TTE with suspicion; prosthetic valve or CIED; positive TTE to define complications`,`Sensitivity over 90%; abscess, perforation, vegetation size (10 mm and 30 mm cut-offs)`],[`Repeat TTE/TOE`,`5-7 days after a negative study if suspicion persists; new complication`,`Evolving vegetation or abscess`],[`Cardiac CT angiography`,`Suspected perivalvular extension, pre-operative coronary assessment`,`Abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula`],[`18F-FDG PET/CT`,`Possible PVE or CIED infection`,`Major criterion if more than 3 months after implant; also finds distant foci`],[`Brain MRI / CT`,`Neurological symptoms; pre-operative screening`,`Emboli, haemorrhage, mycotic aneurysm`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with a murmur and **new breathlessness or pulmonary oedema** - acute valve destruction, needs emergency surgery assessment.`,`**New PR prolongation or heart block** on ECG in suspected aortic valve IE - perivalvular abscess.`,`**Focal neurological deficit, sudden visual loss or severe headache** in a patient with a murmur and fever - embolic stroke or mycotic aneurysm.`,`**Fever in a patient with a prosthetic valve or pacemaker** - blood cultures and TOE, never empirical oral antibiotics.`,`**S. aureus in any blood culture** - assume IE until TOE excludes it.`,`**Blood cultures still positive after 7 days** of appropriate therapy - uncontrolled infection, refer for surgery.`,`Acute limb ischaemia, flank pain with haematuria or left upper quadrant pain with fever - **systemic embolism**.`],pearls:[`**Three sets of blood cultures before the first antibiotic** - the single most important step in IE.`,`**Osler - Ouch** (tender pulps, immune); **Janeway - painless** (palms and soles, embolic).`,`**FROM JANE** for IE features: Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anaemia, Nail-bed (splinter) haemorrhages, Emboli.`,`Vegetations sit on the **low-pressure side of the jet** - atrial surface of mitral, ventricular surface of aortic.`,`**S. gallolyticus means colonoscopy** - linked to colorectal cancer.`,`Duration counts from the **first negative blood culture**, or from surgery if the valve culture is positive.`,`**Gentamicin is out for staphylococcal native-valve IE** (ESC 2023) - nephrotoxic without benefit.`,`**Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone** for E. faecalis - works by saturating complementary penicillin-binding proteins and avoids aminoglycoside toxicity.`,`Dental prophylaxis: **amoxicillin 2 g, 30-60 minutes before**, high-risk patients only; **clindamycin is out**.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve implanted 2 years ago has fever for 3 weeks. TOE is equivocal. Under the 2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria, which finding counts as a MAJOR criterion?`,options:[`Positive rheumatoid factor`,`Abnormal FDG uptake around the prosthetic valve on PET/CT`,`Temperature of 38.5 C`,`Splinter haemorrhages in three nail beds`],answer:1,explanation:`Duke-ISCVID 2023 made 18F-FDG PET/CT uptake at a prosthetic valve a major imaging criterion when the valve was implanted more than 3 months earlier, as here. A positive rheumatoid factor is an immunological minor criterion, fever above 38.0 C is a minor criterion, and splinter haemorrhages are non-specific and do not even appear as a named vascular criterion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q2`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve, allergic to penicillin (urticaria), needs a dental extraction. Which prophylaxis follows ESC 2023 and AHA 2021?`,options:[`Clindamycin 600 mg orally 1 hour before`,`No prophylaxis because she is on warfarin`,`Amoxicillin 2 g orally 1 hour before`,`Azithromycin 500 mg orally 30-60 minutes before`],answer:3,explanation:`A prosthetic valve is high risk and an extraction manipulates the gingiva, so prophylaxis is indicated; with penicillin allergy azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg (or doxycycline 100 mg) is used. Clindamycin was removed by AHA 2021 and ESC 2023 because of C. difficile risk, amoxicillin is unsafe in her allergy, and anticoagulation is not a reason to withhold prophylaxis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q3`,stem:`A 70-year-old man on haemodialysis has native mitral valve IE caused by Enterococcus faecalis with high-level gentamicin resistance. What is the preferred regimen?`,options:[`Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone IV for 6 weeks`,`Vancomycin plus gentamicin IV for 4 weeks`,`Ceftriaxone alone IV for 4 weeks`,`Cloxacillin plus rifampicin IV for 6 weeks`],answer:0,explanation:`Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone saturates complementary penicillin-binding proteins, is synergistic against E. faecalis and avoids aminoglycosides - ideal with high-level gentamicin resistance and renal failure. Gentamicin adds toxicity without synergy when resistance is high-level, ceftriaxone alone is inactive because enterococci are intrinsically cephalosporin-resistant, and cloxacillin-rifampicin is a staphylococcal PVE regimen.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q4`,stem:`A 68-year-old man is treated for aortic valve IE due to Streptococcus gallolyticus. Which additional investigation is specifically recommended?`,options:[`Bone marrow biopsy`,`Colonoscopy`,`Brucella serology`,`Upper GI endoscopy only`],answer:1,explanation:`S. gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis biotype I) bacteraemia and IE are strongly associated with colorectal adenoma and carcinoma, so ESC 2023 recommends colonoscopy. Upper GI endoscopy alone misses the colon, bone marrow biopsy has no role with a known organism, and brucella serology is for culture-negative IE.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-q5`,stem:`On day 4 of appropriate antibiotics for MSSA aortic valve IE, a haemodynamically stable patient develops a new PR interval of 280 ms. What does this most likely indicate, and what is the next step?`,options:[`Drug-induced conduction delay; switch cloxacillin to cefazolin`,`Rheumatic carditis; add aspirin`,`Perivalvular aortic root abscess; urgent TOE and surgery within 3-5 days`,`Vagal tone from fever; repeat ECG in a week`],answer:2,explanation:`A new PR prolongation or AV block in aortic valve IE signals extension into the aortic root near the AV node - an abscess, which is locally uncontrolled infection and an urgent (3-5 day) surgical indication under ESC 2023. Beta-lactams do not cause heart block, aspirin is not recommended in IE, and waiting a week risks complete heart block and fistula.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`A 28-year-old man with known rheumatic mitral regurgitation presents with fever for 4 weeks. Discuss the diagnostic criteria, investigations and management of infective endocarditis, including indications for surgery.`,openingLines:[`Infective endocarditis is microbial infection of the endocardium, usually a valve, with a vegetation as its hallmark; in India rheumatic heart disease remains the commonest substrate and viridans streptococci the commonest organism.`,`It is diagnosed by the 2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria, combining blood cultures and imaging, and treated with prolonged bactericidal IV antibiotics with early surgery for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or embolic risk.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical assessment`,points:[`Fever, weight loss, previous antibiotic courses, dental work; embolic and heart failure symptoms.`,`Signs: changing murmur, splinter haemorrhages, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, Roth spots, splenomegaly, clubbing.`,`Look for portal of entry (teeth) and complications (heart failure, neurological deficit).`]},{heading:`Duke-ISCVID 2023 criteria`,points:[`Major: microbiology (typical organism in 2 sets, serology/PCR for Coxiella and Bartonella); imaging (echo, CT, PET/CT); surgical inspection.`,`Minor: predisposition, fever above 38 C, vascular phenomena, immunological phenomena, sub-major microbiology.`,`Definite: 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor; possible: 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Three blood culture sets from separate sites 30 minutes apart before antibiotics.`,`TTE first, TOE if negative with suspicion or to define complications; repeat in 5-7 days.`,`CBC, ESR/CRP, urine (haematuria), creatinine, RF, complement, ECG (PR interval).`,`Culture-negative: Coxiella, Bartonella, Brucella serology; valve PCR; cardiac CT, PET/CT, brain MRI as needed.`]},{heading:`Antibiotic therapy`,points:[`Empirical: ampicillin + (flu)cloxacillin + gentamicin; vancomycin + gentamicin if penicillin-allergic.`,`Viridans streptococci (MIC 0.125 or less): penicillin G 12-18 MU/day or ceftriaxone 2 g daily for 4 weeks; 2 weeks with gentamicin if uncomplicated.`,`MSSA: cloxacillin 12 g/day or cefazolin 6 g/day 4-6 weeks; MRSA: vancomycin (AUC 400-600) or daptomycin.`,`E. faecalis: ampicillin + ceftriaxone 6 weeks; HACEK: ceftriaxone 4 weeks.`,`Duration from first negative culture; partial oral step-down after 10 days in stable patients (POET).`]},{heading:`Surgery`,points:[`Emergency: refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock.`,`Urgent (3-5 days): abscess, fistula, AV block, enlarging vegetation, fungal/MDR organism, persistent bacteraemia, vegetation 10 mm or more with embolism.`,`Ischaemic stroke without haemorrhage does not delay surgery; haemorrhage defers it about 4 weeks.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Endocarditis Team; serial cultures, drug levels, ECG; end-of-treatment echo.`,`Dental hygiene; prophylaxis only for high-risk groups: amoxicillin 2 g before dental procedures.`,`Continue benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis for RHD; it does not prevent IE.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected IE - blood cultures - TTE - TOE - Duke-ISCVID classification - treatment`,`Table of Duke-ISCVID major and minor criteria`,`Diagram of vegetation sites on mitral and aortic valves`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Duke-ISCVID criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Antibiotic therapy`,marks:2},{part:`Surgery, follow-up and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Duke-ISCVID`,`blood cultures`,`TOE`,`vegetation`,`endocarditis team`,`surgery timing`,`RHD`]},{id:`infectious-fever-infective-endocarditis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on antibiotic prophylaxis of infective endocarditis.`,openingLines:[`Antibiotic prophylaxis of IE is a single pre-procedure dose given only to patients at the highest risk of IE or of a poor outcome from it, before dental procedures that manipulate gingiva or periapical tissue.`,`Current guidance (ESC 2023, AHA 2021) restricts prophylaxis because daily bacteraemia from chewing and brushing far exceeds that from a single procedure; oral hygiene is the main prevention.`],answer:[{heading:`Who gets prophylaxis (high risk)`,points:[`Previous IE.`,`Surgically or transcatheter implanted prosthetic valve, and any material used for valve repair.`,`Untreated cyanotic congenital heart disease; repaired CHD with prosthetic material for 6 months, or lifelong with residual shunt or regurgitation.`,`Ventricular assist device (ESC 2023).`]},{heading:`Which procedures`,points:[`Dental extraction, oral surgery, scaling and procedures manipulating gingiva or periapical region or perforating oral mucosa.`,`Not routinely for respiratory, GI or genitourinary procedures, or for local anaesthetic injection in non-infected tissue.`]},{heading:`Regimens`,points:[`Amoxicillin 2 g orally (children 50 mg/kg) 30-60 minutes before; ampicillin or cefazolin/ceftriaxone IV if oral not possible.`,`Penicillin allergy: azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg, or doxycycline 100 mg orally.`,`Clindamycin is no longer recommended.`]},{heading:`Indian context`,points:[`RHD without a prosthesis is intermediate risk; prophylaxis is not routine in ESC/AHA, though often given in India.`,`Benzathine penicillin for rheumatic fever does not prevent IE; use a non-penicillin agent if prophylaxis is chosen.`,`Twice-yearly dental review and oral hygiene for all with valve disease.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: high-risk group, procedure, drug and dose`],markSplit:[{part:`Rationale and high-risk groups`,marks:2},{part:`Procedures and regimens`,marks:2},{part:`Indian context and oral hygiene`,marks:1}],keywords:[`amoxicillin 2 g`,`high-risk cardiac conditions`,`dental procedures`,`clindamycin removed`,`RHD`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infective endocarditis`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Infective endocarditis`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis (Delgado V et al, Eur Heart J 2023)`,`The 2023 Duke-International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases Criteria for Infective Endocarditis (Fowler VG et al, Clin Infect Dis 2023)`,`Prevention of Viridans Group Streptococcal Infective Endocarditis: AHA Scientific Statement (Wilson WR et al, Circulation 2021)`,`Iversen K et al. Partial Oral versus Intravenous Antibiotic Treatment of Endocarditis (POET), N Engl J Med 2019`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Infective endocarditis`]}),t.push({id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections`,title:`Bloodstream infection, S. aureus bacteraemia and catheter-related infections`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Bloodstream infection (BSI)** is a positive blood culture with a true pathogen; **S. aureus bacteraemia (SAB)** is never a contaminant and is managed by a **bundle - ID input, repeat cultures, echo, source control, a beta-lactam for MSSA and adequate duration** - while **catheter-related BSI (CRBSI)** is proved by **differential time to positivity of 2 hours or more** or matching tip and blood cultures and usually needs **line removal**.`,keywords:[`BSI`,`bacteraemia`,`SAB`,`Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia`,`MRSA`,`MSSA`,`CRBSI`,`CLABSI`,`central line bundle`,`differential time to positivity`,`antibiotic lock therapy`,`Candida auris`,`candidaemia`,`cefazolin`,`vancomycin AUC`,`daptomycin`,`teicoplanin`,`echinocandin`,`blood culture contamination`,`ICMR AMRSN`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Bacteraemia** is viable bacteria in blood; **bloodstream infection** is bacteraemia or fungaemia with clinical significance - a true pathogen, or a skin commensal in 2 or more sets with a compatible illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary BSI** has no other site of infection (often line-related); **secondary BSI** seeds from a focus such as the urinary tract, abdomen, lung or skin. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**S. aureus bacteraemia** has an incidence of about **20-50 per 100 000 per year** and a **30-day mortality of 20-30%**, higher than most other bacteraemias. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CLABSI** (central line-associated BSI) is a surveillance term: laboratory-confirmed BSI with a central line in place for **more than 2 calendar days** and no other source; **CRBSI** is the stricter clinical diagnosis proving the line is the source. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`In the **ICMR-AIIMS HAI surveillance network** of Indian ICUs, the CLABSI rate was about **9 per 1000 central-line days**, several times the rate in high-income ICUs, with **Klebsiella and Acinetobacter** leading. [Mathur et al, Lancet Glob Health 2022]`,`**ICMR AMRSN** data show **MRSA in roughly 40% of S. aureus isolates** in Indian tertiary hospitals, with high rates in ICU and bloodstream isolates. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`In Indian ICUs, Gram-negative organisms dominate BSI; **candidaemia** is common, with **C. tropicalis** the leading species and **Candida auris** in about **5%** of ICU candidaemia in a 27-centre study. [Chakrabarti et al, Intensive Care Med 2015]`,`**Healthcare-associated SAB** (lines, dialysis, surgery) now exceeds community-onset SAB in most series. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Central venous catheters** cause most primary BSI; risk is highest with **non-tunnelled femoral lines**, multiple lumens, TPN and long dwell time. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`Line organisms: **coagulase-negative staphylococci** (commonest), **S. aureus**, enterococci, **Gram-negative bacilli** (Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas - commoner in India) and **Candida**. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`SAB risk factors: **haemodialysis** (especially via catheter), injection drug use, diabetes, prosthetic material, skin disease, recent surgery and **nasal S. aureus carriage**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Candidaemia risk factors: **broad-spectrum antibiotics, central lines, TPN, abdominal surgery, pancreatitis, neutropenia** and long ICU stay. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`**Candida auris** spreads between patients via **colonised skin and contaminated surfaces and equipment**, unlike most candidaemia, which is endogenous from the gut. [CDC 2024 C. auris]`,`**Community MRSA** (often **PVL-positive**) causes recurrent boils, necrotising pneumonia and bacteraemia in young people without healthcare contact. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Catheters are colonised **extraluminally** from the skin along the tract (dominant in the **first 1-2 weeks**) or **intraluminally** from hub contamination (dominant in **long-term lines**). [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`Organisms form a **biofilm** on the catheter surface - a matrix that blocks antibiotic penetration and phagocytes, which is why most CRBSI needs **removal, not just antibiotics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`S. aureus adheres through **MSCRAMMs** (clumping factor, fibronectin-binding proteins), invades endothelium, survives inside cells and seeds **metastatic foci** (spine, joints, valves, kidney). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Methicillin resistance** comes from the **mecA gene** on SCCmec, encoding **PBP2a** with low affinity for beta-lactams - all beta-lactams except ceftaroline and ceftobiprole fail. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vancomycin** penetrates and kills slowly; MSSA treated with vancomycin rather than a beta-lactam has **higher failure and mortality** - why MSSA must be switched to cefazolin or cloxacillin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**C. auris** carries efflux pumps and ERG11 mutations: about **90% are fluconazole-resistant**, up to a third amphotericin-resistant, and a few pan-resistant. [CDC 2024 C. auris]`,`**Persistent bacteraemia** (cultures positive beyond 72 hours on therapy) means an undrained focus, an intravascular source (IE, septic thrombophlebitis, infected graft) or inadequate therapy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By **source**: primary (including line-related) versus secondary to a focus; by **acquisition**: community-onset, healthcare-associated community-onset and hospital-onset (after 48 hours). [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**Uncomplicated SAB**: IE excluded by echo, **no implanted prosthesis**, follow-up blood cultures **negative within 2-4 days**, **defervescence within 72 hours** of effective therapy and **no metastatic focus**. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`**Complicated SAB**: anything else - endocarditis, metastatic foci, prosthesis, persistent bacteraemia or fever - needing **4-6 weeks** of therapy. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`**Catheter infections**: **exit-site infection** (erythema or pus within 2 cm), **tunnel infection** (tenderness more than 2 cm along the tunnel), **pocket infection** (port) and **CRBSI**. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**Short-term (non-tunnelled) versus long-term (tunnelled cuffed, ports)** catheters - removal is routine for the first and selective for the second. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**True pathogen versus contaminant**: S. aureus, Gram-negatives, enterococci, pneumococci and Candida are always true; CoNS, Corynebacterium, Bacillus, Cutibacterium and micrococci in **one of two sets** usually mean contamination. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**Fever with rigors**, often during or soon after **line flushing or dialysis**, in a patient with a central line and no other source suggests CRBSI. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`Local signs at the exit site are **absent in most CRBSI** - their absence does not clear the line. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`SAB may present as **sepsis, back pain (vertebral osteomyelitis, epidural abscess), joint pain, new murmur or skin pustules**; ask about recent boils, lines and dialysis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Examine for **new murmur, spinal tenderness, septic joints, psoas signs, Janeway lesions** and the **line or port site** for erythema, tenderness and pus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Suppurative thrombophlebitis** shows as a tender, palpable cord with persistent bacteraemia after line removal. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**Candidaemia** may cause **endophthalmitis** (blurred vision, floaters, white retinal lesions) and painless nodular skin lesions; many patients have no specific signs. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`In the elderly and immunosuppressed, BSI may show only **confusion, hypotension, tachypnoea or hypothermia**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Blood culture contamination**: one of two sets with a skin commensal, long time to positivity, well patient - repeat cultures, do not treat blindly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary BSI** from UTI, cholangitis, pneumonia or intra-abdominal abscess - same organism at the focus; treat the focus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infective endocarditis** - persistent bacteraemia, murmur, emboli; TOE separates it - see the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Non-infective fever in ICU**: drug fever, thrombophlebitis without infection, transfusion reaction, DVT/PE, acalculous cholecystitis, pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Septic thrombophlebitis or infected graft**: persistent positive cultures despite line removal - Doppler or CT. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Take **2-3 sets of blood cultures** (each set **20 mL** split between aerobic and anaerobic bottles) **before antibiotics**; total volume is the main driver of yield. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`For suspected CRBSI, take **one set through the catheter and one peripherally** at the same time; **differential time to positivity (DTP) of 2 hours or more** (catheter positive first) indicates CRBSI. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`When a catheter is removed, send the **tip (distal 5 cm)** for culture: **15 CFU or more** by semi-quantitative roll plate (or **more than 10^2 CFU** quantitative) with the same organism in blood confirms CRBSI. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`Do not send catheter tips routinely when there is no suspicion of infection - colonisation without bacteraemia misleads. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`In SAB, **repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours until negative**; the date of the first negative culture sets the duration clock. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`**Echo in all SAB**: TTE for everyone and **TOE** for prosthetic valve or device, persistent bacteraemia over 72 hours, injection drug use, community-onset SAB without a clear source, or vertebral osteomyelitis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Rapid tests on positive bottles - **MALDI-TOF identification, PCR for mecA**, multiplex panels - give species and MRSA status hours to a day earlier and speed de-escalation. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**C. auris** is **misidentified as C. haemulonii** (or other species) by VITEK-2 and API systems; **MALDI-TOF or sequencing** identifies it - alert the lab when C. haemulonii is reported. [CDC 2024 C. auris]`,`In candidaemia, arrange a **dilated fundus examination** within the first week, and echo if cultures persist. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`Blood culture **contamination rate** should be **under 3%** (CLSI target; under 1% is the newer aspiration) - audit it in every hospital. [CLSI M47 2022]`]},{heading:`Management - S. aureus bacteraemia bundle`,points:[`**Infectious diseases consultation** in SAB reduces mortality and is the core of the bundle; where no ID physician exists, a senior physician follows a written SAB checklist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MSSA**: switch to **cefazolin 2 g IV 8-hourly** or **cloxacillin 2 g IV 4-hourly** - both outperform vancomycin; cefazolin is better tolerated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MRSA**: **vancomycin** dosed to an **AUC of 400-600 mg.h/L** (loading 25-30 mg/kg) or **daptomycin 8-10 mg/kg IV once daily**; use daptomycin when vancomycin MIC is 2 mg/L or more or AKI develops. [ASHP/IDSA vancomycin 2020]`,`Until susceptibility is known, start **vancomycin** (or teicoplanin) in a sick patient with Gram-positive cocci in clusters; switch to a beta-lactam as soon as MSSA is reported. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`**Adjunctive rifampicin does not improve outcome** in SAB (ARREST trial), and adding a beta-lactam to vancomycin for MRSA increased AKI without benefit (CAMERA2). [Thwaites et al, Lancet 2018]`,`**Source control**: remove infected lines, drain abscesses, debride bone and remove infected prostheses where feasible - within **72 hours**. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`Duration: **uncomplicated SAB at least 14 days IV** from first negative culture; **complicated SAB 4-6 weeks**. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`**Early oral step-down** (after 5-7 days IV) in carefully selected low-risk SAB was non-inferior in the **SABATO trial**; it is an option, not yet routine. [Kaasch et al, Lancet Infect Dis 2024]`,`**Linezolid** is not a first-line drug for SAB (bacteriostatic); it remains useful for MRSA skin infection and pneumonia. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`]},{heading:`Management - catheter-related BSI and candidaemia`,points:[`**Remove the catheter** in CRBSI from **S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Candida, mycobacteria or fungi**, and in sepsis with shock, tunnel or pocket infection, endocarditis, suppurative thrombophlebitis or bacteraemia beyond 72 hours. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**Short-term non-tunnelled catheters** are removed in almost all CRBSI; insert a new line at a **different site**, not over a guidewire. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**CoNS CRBSI**: remove the line and treat **5-7 days**, or retain a long-term line with **antibiotic lock plus systemic therapy for 10-14 days**. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**Enterococcal and Gram-negative CRBSI**: **7-14 days**; salvage of a long-term line with antibiotic lock is possible if the patient is stable and cultures clear. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**Uncomplicated Gram-negative BSI** responding to therapy needs only **7 days**, non-inferior to 14 days (BALANCE trial). [BALANCE, N Engl J Med 2025]`,`**Candidaemia**: start an **echinocandin** - **caspofungin 70 mg loading then 50 mg IV daily**, or **micafungin 100 mg IV daily** - remove the central line, and treat **14 days after the first negative culture** and symptom resolution. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`Step down to **fluconazole 400 mg daily** (after 800 mg loading) in a stable patient with a susceptible isolate and cleared cultures after **5-7 days**; **never use fluconazole empirically for C. auris**. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`**Antibiotic lock therapy**: high-concentration antibiotic (with heparin) dwells in the lumen for hours to days to sterilise intraluminal biofilm; always combined with systemic therapy. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Haemodialysis catheters**: CRBSI can often be treated through a **guidewire exchange or lock** for CoNS and Gram-negatives, but **S. aureus, Pseudomonas and Candida** require removal; dose vancomycin after dialysis by levels. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**Pregnancy**: beta-lactams and vancomycin are preferred; data on daptomycin are limited, echinocandins are used when necessary, and **fluconazole is avoided in the first trimester**. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`**Neonates and children**: CoNS and Candida dominate neonatal line sepsis; candidaemia in neonates needs **lumbar puncture and eye examination** because of CNS involvement. [IDSA 2016 Candida]`,`**Elderly and diabetics**: higher rates of metastatic SAB (spine, joints) - examine the back and joints daily and image early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prosthetic valves, grafts and joints**: SAB in their presence is complicated by definition - TOE and at least 4-6 weeks of therapy. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`**Resource-limited hospital**: two good-volume cultures, a TTE and a peripheral line instead of a central one where possible prevent more CRBSI than any new antibiotic. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`SAB seeds **endocarditis in about 10-15%** and metastatic foci (vertebral osteomyelitis, epidural abscess, septic arthritis, psoas, renal abscess) in up to a third. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Predictors of poor outcome** in SAB: persistent bacteraemia, community onset without a source, prosthesis, delayed source control and **vancomycin for MSSA**. [Harrison 22e]`,`CRBSI complications: **septic thrombophlebitis**, endocarditis, osteomyelitis and septic shock. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`Candidaemia carries **30-40% mortality**; **C. auris** outbreaks have mortality around **30-60%**, largely from underlying illness. [CDC 2024 C. auris]`,`**SAB relapse** within 3 months suggests an undrained focus or inadequate duration. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Any **S. aureus** or **Candida** in blood is a reason for admission and specialist review - never treat as an outpatient contaminant. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`Monitor **vancomycin AUC (or trough) and creatinine** at least twice weekly, **CPK weekly** on daptomycin, and **LFT** on azoles. [ASHP/IDSA vancomycin 2020]`,`Repeat **blood cultures** until negative and **echo** if they persist beyond 72 hours; image the spine with MRI for back pain. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`,`On OPAT, check **CBC and creatinine weekly** and review the line for exit-site infection - see the IV to oral switch and OPAT topic. [IDSA 2018 OPAT]`,`Follow up SAB at **4 and 12 weeks** after therapy for relapse and new back or joint pain. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Prevention - CLABSI bundle and C. auris control`,points:[`**Insertion bundle**: hand hygiene, **maximal sterile barrier precautions** (cap, mask, sterile gown, gloves, full-body drape), **chlorhexidine more than 0.5% in alcohol** skin preparation allowed to dry, and a checklist with authority to stop. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Site and technique**: avoid the **femoral vein** in adults, use **ultrasound guidance** for jugular lines, and use the fewest lumens needed. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Maintenance bundle**: **daily review of line need** and prompt removal, **scrub the hub** before every access, chlorhexidine-impregnated dressings, and change transparent dressings **every 7 days** (gauze every 2 days) or when soiled. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Daily chlorhexidine bathing** of ICU patients reduces CLABSI and MRSA acquisition. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`Peripheral cannulas are **replaced when clinically indicated**, not routinely every 72-96 hours, with daily site inspection (VIP score). [CDC/HICPAC]`,`**MRSA control**: contact precautions, screening of high-risk admissions and **decolonisation (mupirocin nasal plus chlorhexidine)** before cardiac and orthopaedic surgery. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**C. auris**: single room with **contact precautions**, screen contacts (axilla and groin swabs), and clean with **chlorine-based or hydrogen peroxide disinfectants** - quaternary ammonium compounds do not work. [CDC 2024 C. auris]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**SHEA/IDSA/APIC Compendium 2022** update on CLABSI prevention made chlorhexidine bathing and chlorhexidine dressings essential practices and kept femoral avoidance in adults. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**BALANCE trial** (published 2025): 7 days was non-inferior to 14 days for bloodstream infection in hospitalised patients, supporting short courses for uncomplicated Gram-negative BSI. [BALANCE, N Engl J Med 2025]`,`**SABATO trial** (2024): early oral switch after 5-7 days IV was non-inferior in low-risk uncomplicated SAB. [Kaasch et al, Lancet Infect Dis 2024]`,`**Duke-ISCVID 2023** made S. aureus a typical IE organism in any setting, keeping echo mandatory in SAB. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**ASHP/IDSA 2020 vancomycin consensus** replaced trough-only monitoring with **AUC-guided dosing (400-600)** to cut nephrotoxicity. [ASHP/IDSA vancomycin 2020]`,`**WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List 2022** placed **C. auris** in the critical group; ICMR and NCDC advise lab alerts and contact precautions. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`**Guideline difference**: IDSA 2011 still says 14 days minimum for uncomplicated SAB; trial evidence (SABATO) supports earlier oral switch in selected cases - write the 14-day rule in the exam and mention the trial. [IDSA 2011 MRSA]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**S. aureus in blood is never a contaminant** - the most rewarded single line. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MSSA gets cefazolin or cloxacillin, not vancomycin** - a classic viva and MCQ. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DTP of 2 hours or more** and **15 CFU on roll plate** are the two numbers for CRBSI. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`Lines that **must come out**: **S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Candida**, fungi and mycobacteria. [IDSA 2009 CRBSI]`,`**C. auris**: misidentified as C. haemulonii, fluconazole-resistant, spreads on surfaces, killed by chlorine not quaternary ammonium. [CDC 2024 C. auris]`,`Common mistake: treating a **single CoNS-positive bottle** in a well patient for 2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for bloodstream and catheter infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cefazolin (MSSA)`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`14 days uncomplicated; 4-6 weeks complicated`,`Rash, C. difficile; usable in non-severe penicillin allergy`],[`Cloxacillin (MSSA)`,`2 g IV 4-hourly (12 g/day)`,`As above`,`Phlebitis, cholestatic hepatitis, neutropenia`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA, empirical)`,`Loading 25-30 mg/kg, then 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly`,`AUC 400-600 mg.h/L (trough 15-20 if AUC unavailable)`,`AKI (worse with piperacillin-tazobactam, aminoglycosides), infusion reaction; slow infusion`],[`Teicoplanin (MRSA, alternative)`,`Loading 6-12 mg/kg IV 12-hourly for 3 doses, then once daily`,`Trough above 15-20 mg/L for serious infection`,`Less nephrotoxic than vancomycin; rash, thrombocytopenia`],[`Daptomycin (MRSA, vancomycin MIC 2 or more)`,`8-10 mg/kg IV once daily`,`Weekly CPK`,`Myopathy; stop statin; not for pneumonia`],[`Linezolid (MRSA skin, pneumonia)`,`600 mg oral/IV 12-hourly`,`Under 14 days preferred`,`Thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, neuropathy; bacteriostatic - not first-line for SAB`],[`Caspofungin / micafungin (candidaemia, C. auris)`,`Caspofungin 70 mg then 50 mg IV daily; micafungin 100 mg IV daily`,`14 days after first negative culture`,`Hepatotoxicity (mild), infusion reactions; poor urine and eye penetration`],[`Fluconazole (step-down, susceptible Candida)`,`800 mg load then 400 mg daily oral/IV`,`Complete 14 days after clearance`,`QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity, interactions; inactive against C. krusei and most C. auris`]]},{heading:`Diagnosing catheter-related BSI`,columns:[`Method`,`Positive criterion`,`Practical point`],rows:[[`Differential time to positivity`,`Catheter set positive 2 hours or more before peripheral set`,`Equal volumes, drawn at the same time; line stays in`],[`Semi-quantitative tip culture (roll plate)`,`15 CFU or more with same organism in peripheral blood`,`Send distal 5 cm only when CRBSI is suspected`],[`Quantitative tip culture`,`More than 10^2 CFU with same organism in blood`,`Sonication or vortex method`],[`Quantitative paired blood cultures`,`Catheter count 3 times or more the peripheral count`,`Accurate but laborious; few Indian labs`],[`Exit-site swab`,`Pus or organism at exit site`,`Supports exit-site infection; does not prove CRBSI`]]},{heading:`Uncomplicated versus complicated S. aureus bacteraemia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Uncomplicated SAB`,`Complicated SAB`],rows:[[`Endocarditis`,`Excluded by echo`,`Present or not excluded`],[`Prosthesis`,`None`,`Prosthetic valve, joint, graft or device`],[`Follow-up cultures`,`Negative within 2-4 days`,`Positive beyond 72 hours`],[`Fever`,`Settles within 72 hours`,`Persists`],[`Metastatic focus`,`None`,`Spine, joint, abscess, lung emboli`],[`Duration`,`At least 14 days IV from first negative culture`,`4-6 weeks`]]},{heading:`Catheter management by organism`,columns:[`Organism`,`Short-term catheter`,`Long-term catheter`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Coagulase-negative staphylococci`,`Remove`,`May retain with lock + systemic therapy`,`5-7 days if removed; 10-14 days if retained`],[`S. aureus`,`Remove`,`Remove`,`14 days minimum; 4-6 weeks if complicated`],[`Enterococcus`,`Remove`,`May retain with lock if stable`,`7-14 days`],[`Gram-negative bacilli (non-Pseudomonas)`,`Remove`,`May retain with lock if stable`,`7-14 days`],[`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Remove`,`Remove`,`7-14 days`],[`Candida (including C. auris)`,`Remove`,`Remove`,`14 days after first negative culture`]]},{heading:`True bacteraemia versus contamination`,columns:[`Clue`,`Favours true infection`,`Favours contamination`],rows:[[`Organism`,`S. aureus, Gram-negatives, enterococci, pneumococci, Candida`,`CoNS, Corynebacterium, Bacillus, Cutibacterium, micrococci`],[`Number of sets positive`,`2 or more sets`,`1 of 2 or more sets`],[`Time to positivity`,`Short (under 24 hours)`,`Long (over 2-3 days)`],[`Clinical picture`,`Sepsis, prosthesis, line, focus`,`Well patient, no device`]]}],redFlags:[`**Any S. aureus** in a blood culture - admit, repeat cultures, echo, find the source.`,`**Candida in blood** - remove lines, start an echinocandin, examine the eyes.`,`Fever with **hypotension or rigors during line flushing or dialysis** - CRBSI with sepsis, pull the line.`,`**Back pain or new neurological deficit** in SAB - epidural abscess or vertebral osteomyelitis, urgent MRI.`,`**Blood cultures still positive after 72 hours** of appropriate therapy - endocarditis, thrombophlebitis or undrained focus.`,`**Tunnel or pocket infection** with pus or spreading erythema - remove the device.`,`Laboratory report of **C. haemulonii** or unidentified Candida in an ICU patient - consider C. auris, isolate now.`],pearls:[`**S. aureus in blood is never a contaminant.**`,`**MSSA: beta-lactam beats vancomycin** - cefazolin 2 g 8-hourly or cloxacillin 2 g 4-hourly.`,`SAB bundle - **ECHO-SID**: Echo, Cultures repeated, Hardware/source out, Optimal beta-lactam, Sufficient duration, ID consult.`,`**DTP 2 hours or more** = catheter-related.`,`Lines that must come out: **SPC** - S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Candida.`,`Duration starts from the **first negative blood culture**, not the first dose.`,`**C. auris**: wrong ID (haemulonii), wrong drug (fluconazole), wrong disinfectant (quaternary ammonium).`,`CLABSI bundle: **hand hygiene, full barrier, chlorhexidine, avoid femoral, remove early**.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old diabetic man has 2 of 2 blood culture sets positive for methicillin-susceptible S. aureus after starting empirical vancomycin. He has no penicillin allergy. What is the best definitive therapy?`,options:[`Continue vancomycin for 14 days`,`Switch to oral linezolid`,`Switch to cefazolin 2 g IV 8-hourly`,`Add rifampicin to vancomycin`],answer:2,explanation:`For MSSA bacteraemia, an anti-staphylococcal beta-lactam (cefazolin or cloxacillin) gives lower failure and mortality than vancomycin, which kills slowly. Continuing vancomycin is the classic error, linezolid is bacteriostatic and not first-line for SAB, and adjunctive rifampicin showed no benefit in the ARREST trial.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-q2`,stem:`A patient on TPN through a central line has fever. Paired blood cultures are drawn simultaneously. Which result most strongly supports catheter-related bloodstream infection?`,options:[`Catheter set positive 3 hours before the peripheral set with the same organism`,`Peripheral set positive 3 hours before the catheter set`,`Exit-site swab grows coagulase-negative staphylococci`,`Catheter tip grows 5 CFU on roll plate with negative blood cultures`],answer:0,explanation:`A differential time to positivity of 2 hours or more, with the catheter-drawn set turning positive first, indicates a higher organism load in the line and confirms CRBSI. The reverse pattern points away from the line, an exit-site swab shows only local colonisation, and 5 CFU on roll plate with negative blood cultures is below the 15 CFU threshold and is not bloodstream infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-q3`,stem:`In which of the following catheter-related bloodstream infections can a long-term tunnelled catheter reasonably be retained with antibiotic lock therapy?`,options:[`Staphylococcus aureus`,`Candida parapsilosis`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Coagulase-negative staphylococci in a stable patient`],answer:3,explanation:`IDSA allows salvage of a long-term catheter infected by coagulase-negative staphylococci in a stable patient, using antibiotic lock plus 10-14 days of systemic therapy. S. aureus, Pseudomonas and Candida form tenacious biofilms and carry high relapse and metastatic risk, so the catheter must be removed in all three.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-q4`,stem:`An ICU patient's blood culture yeast is reported by VITEK-2 as Candida haemulonii. MALDI-TOF later identifies Candida auris. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Fluconazole is the drug of choice as most isolates are susceptible`,`An echinocandin is first-line and the patient needs contact precautions`,`Quaternary ammonium disinfectants reliably clear it from surfaces`,`Spread is only endogenous from the patient's gut, so isolation is unnecessary`],answer:1,explanation:`C. auris is commonly misidentified as C. haemulonii, about 90% of isolates resist fluconazole, and echinocandins are first-line. It colonises skin and survives on surfaces, spreading between patients, so contact precautions and chlorine or hydrogen peroxide disinfectants are needed; quaternary ammonium compounds do not reliably kill it.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-q5`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman has E. coli bacteraemia from pyelonephritis. She is afebrile by 48 hours, the source is controlled and she is on a susceptible oral agent. What total antibiotic duration is supported by current trial evidence?`,options:[`3 days`,`21 days`,`6 weeks`,`7 days`],answer:3,explanation:`Randomised trials, most recently BALANCE, show 7 days is non-inferior to 14 days for uncomplicated Gram-negative bloodstream infection with a controlled source and clinical response. Three days is untested and too short, and 21 days or 6 weeks are reserved for endovascular infection, undrained abscess or osteomyelitis and only add resistance and adverse effects.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the evaluation and management of Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia. Add a note on the differences between methicillin-susceptible and methicillin-resistant S. aureus treatment.`,openingLines:[`S. aureus bacteraemia is the isolation of S. aureus from blood; it is never a contaminant, carries a 20-30% 30-day mortality and seeds endocarditis in 10-15% and metastatic foci in up to a third.`,`It is managed by a bundle: infectious diseases input, repeat blood cultures, echocardiography, source control, an anti-staphylococcal beta-lactam for MSSA, and duration set by whether the bacteraemia is uncomplicated or complicated.`],answer:[{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History: lines, dialysis, injection drug use, prosthesis, skin infection, back or joint pain.`,`Examination: murmur, spine, joints, line site, skin, fundus.`,`Repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours until negative.`,`TTE in all; TOE for prosthesis, device, persistent bacteraemia, community onset without source, drug use, vertebral osteomyelitis.`,`Imaging of symptomatic sites (MRI spine, ultrasound abdomen).`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Uncomplicated: no IE, no prosthesis, cultures negative by 2-4 days, fever settles within 72 hours, no metastatic focus.`,`Complicated: any other situation.`]},{heading:`Antibiotic therapy`,points:[`Empirical vancomycin until susceptibility known.`,`MSSA: cefazolin 2 g IV 8-hourly or cloxacillin 2 g IV 4-hourly.`,`MRSA: vancomycin to AUC 400-600 or daptomycin 8-10 mg/kg daily; teicoplanin an Indian alternative.`,`No routine rifampicin (ARREST) or beta-lactam add-on for MRSA (CAMERA2).`]},{heading:`Source control and duration`,points:[`Remove infected catheters, drain abscesses, remove infected hardware within 72 hours.`,`Uncomplicated: at least 14 days IV from first negative culture; complicated: 4-6 weeks.`,`Selected low-risk cases: early oral switch (SABATO 2024).`]},{heading:`MSSA versus MRSA`,points:[`Mechanism: mecA encodes PBP2a, so beta-lactams fail against MRSA.`,`MSSA: beta-lactam superior to vancomycin; MRSA: glycopeptide or daptomycin.`,`MRSA is about 40% of Indian isolates (ICMR AMRSN); contact precautions and decolonisation.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`CLABSI insertion and maintenance bundles, chlorhexidine bathing, avoid femoral lines.`,`Pre-operative decolonisation for cardiac and orthopaedic surgery.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of the SAB bundle: cultures - echo - source - drug - duration`,`Table: uncomplicated versus complicated SAB`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Classification and duration`,marks:2},{part:`Antibiotic therapy and MSSA versus MRSA`,marks:3},{part:`Source control and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`SAB bundle`,`cefazolin`,`vancomycin AUC`,`TOE`,`uncomplicated SAB`,`source control`,`MRSA`]},{id:`infectious-fever-bloodstream-catheter-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI) prevention bundle.`,openingLines:[`CLABSI is a laboratory-confirmed bloodstream infection in a patient with a central line in place for more than 2 days, with no other source; it is largely preventable.`,`Prevention rests on an insertion bundle and a maintenance bundle, audited by CLABSI rate per 1000 central-line days, which is about 9 in Indian ICU surveillance.`],answer:[{heading:`Insertion bundle`,points:[`Hand hygiene before insertion.`,`Maximal sterile barrier: cap, mask, sterile gown and gloves, full-body drape.`,`Chlorhexidine more than 0.5% in alcohol, allowed to dry.`,`Avoid femoral site in adults; ultrasound guidance; fewest lumens; checklist with a trained observer.`]},{heading:`Maintenance bundle`,points:[`Daily review of need and prompt removal.`,`Scrub the hub before each access; aseptic handling.`,`Chlorhexidine dressing; transparent dressing changed every 7 days, gauze every 2 days.`,`Daily chlorhexidine bathing in ICU.`]},{heading:`Surveillance and feedback`,points:[`CLABSI rate = CLABSI episodes / central-line days x 1000.`,`Monthly feedback to ICU teams; compliance audit of each bundle element.`]}],mustDraw:[`Box diagram: insertion bundle and maintenance bundle`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Insertion bundle`,marks:2},{part:`Maintenance bundle and surveillance`,marks:2}],keywords:[`CLABSI`,`maximal barrier`,`chlorhexidine`,`femoral avoidance`,`scrub the hub`,`line days`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Staphylococcal infections; Infections acquired in health care facilities`,`IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Intravascular Catheter-Related Infection, 2009 (Mermel LA et al)`,`IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Treatment of MRSA Infections, 2011 (Liu C et al)`,`IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Candidiasis, 2016 update (Pappas PG et al)`,`SHEA/IDSA/APIC Compendium: Strategies to prevent CLABSI in acute-care hospitals, 2022 update`,`ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP Consensus: Therapeutic monitoring of vancomycin for serious MRSA infections, 2020`,`ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Research and Surveillance Network (AMRSN) Annual Report, 2023`,`National Guidelines for Infection Prevention and Control in Healthcare Facilities, NCDC/MoHFW, 2020`]}),t.push({id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative`,title:`Multidrug-resistant Gram-negative infections: ESBL, CRE, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Multidrug-resistant Gram-negative infections** are caused by Enterobacterales, Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas carrying **ESBLs, AmpC or carbapenemases**; therapy is chosen by **mechanism** - a **carbapenem for serious ESBL infection**, **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam (or cefiderocol) for the NDM metallo-beta-lactamases that dominate India**, **sulbactam-based combinations for CRAB** and newer beta-lactam combinations for DTR-Pseudomonas - with **polymyxins as last resort** and strict infection control.`,keywords:[`MDR GNB`,`ESBL`,`AmpC`,`CRE`,`carbapenemase`,`NDM-1`,`OXA-48`,`KPC`,`metallo-beta-lactamase`,`CRAB`,`DTR Pseudomonas`,`ceftazidime-avibactam`,`aztreonam`,`cefiderocol`,`colistin`,`polymyxin B`,`sulbactam-durlobactam`,`ceftolozane-tazobactam`,`MERINO trial`,`IDSA AMR guidance 2024`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**MDR** is non-susceptibility to at least one agent in **3 or more antimicrobial classes**; **XDR** is susceptibility to only 1-2 classes; **PDR** is resistance to all agents tested. [ECDC/CDC 2012 definitions]`,`**DTR (difficult-to-treat resistance)** in Pseudomonas means non-susceptibility to **all first-line beta-lactams (piperacillin-tazobactam, ceftazidime, cefepime, aztreonam, meropenem, imipenem) and fluoroquinolones**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**WHO Bacterial Priority Pathogens List 2024** places **carbapenem-resistant A. baumannii, carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales and third-generation cephalosporin-resistant Enterobacterales** in the **critical** group; carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas moved to **high**. [WHO BPPL 2024]`,`India has among the **highest Gram-negative resistance rates** in the world: in ICMR AMRSN data, **more than half of K. pneumoniae** and roughly **85-90% of A. baumannii** isolates are carbapenem-resistant. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**ESBL production** is found in most E. coli and K. pneumoniae from Indian hospitals and in a large share of community E. coli UTI - so third-generation cephalosporins fail commonly even outside hospital. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**NDM-1** (New Delhi metallo-beta-lactamase) was first described in 2008 in a patient treated in India; **NDM and OXA-48-like (OXA-181, OXA-232)** are the dominant Indian carbapenemases, often **co-produced** in K. pneumoniae, while KPC is rare. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`Neonatal sepsis in Indian NICUs is driven by **MDR Klebsiella and Acinetobacter** (DeNIS study), with high mortality. [DeNIS, Lancet Glob Health 2016]`,`Mortality of carbapenem-resistant bloodstream infection is roughly **30-50%**, about double that of susceptible infection. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Prior antibiotics** - especially carbapenems, cephalosporins and fluoroquinolones - are the strongest risk factor for MDR GNB colonisation and infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ICU stay, mechanical ventilation, central lines, urinary catheters, surgery and long hospitalisation** add risk; most MDR infection follows colonisation of gut, skin or airway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Healthcare contact**: dialysis, nursing homes, transfer from another hospital and **hospitalisation abroad or in a high-prevalence region** in the last 12 months. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Community drivers in India**: over-the-counter antibiotics, antibiotic use in poultry and aquaculture, and **sewage contamination of water** spread ESBL and NDM genes. [NAP-AMR 2017]`,`**Acinetobacter baumannii** survives for weeks on dry surfaces and equipment (ventilators, bed rails) and causes **VAP, CLABSI and wound infection** in ICUs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudomonas aeruginosa** prefers moist environments (sinks, respiratory equipment) and infects ventilated, neutropenic, burn and **bronchiectasis/cystic fibrosis** patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stenotrophomonas maltophilia** emerges after carbapenem exposure because it carries an intrinsic metallo-beta-lactamase (L1). [IDSA AMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - mechanisms of resistance`,points:[`**ESBLs** (now mostly **CTX-M-15**) hydrolyse penicillins, all cephalosporins up to the fourth generation and aztreonam, are inhibited by clavulanate and tazobactam in vitro, but spare **carbapenems** and cephamycins. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AmpC** beta-lactamases are **chromosomal and inducible** in Enterobacter cloacae, Klebsiella aerogenes and Citrobacter freundii; third-generation cephalosporins select derepressed mutants, while **cefepime** is a weak inducer and stable. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Carbapenemases** by Ambler class: **A - KPC** (serine; inhibited by avibactam, vaborbactam, relebactam), **B - metallo-beta-lactamases NDM, VIM, IMP** (zinc-dependent; not inhibited by any marketed serine inhibitor), **D - OXA-48-like** (weak carbapenemase, inhibited by avibactam). [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**MBLs cannot hydrolyse aztreonam**, but NDM strains almost always also carry ESBL/AmpC that do; **avibactam protects aztreonam** from these - the logic of **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Cefiderocol** is a siderophore cephalosporin that rides the bacterial **iron-uptake channels** into the periplasm (a 'Trojan horse') and resists most carbapenemases including MBLs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-enzymatic mechanisms**: porin loss (OprD loss gives imipenem resistance in Pseudomonas), **efflux pumps** (MexAB-OprM), target changes (gyrA mutations to fluoroquinolones, 16S rRNA methylases to all aminoglycosides). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Polymyxin resistance** arises from lipid A modification (**mgrB** mutation in Klebsiella, plasmid **mcr-1** genes), which removes the negative charge colistin binds to. [Harrison 22e]`,`Resistance genes travel on **plasmids and transposons**, so they spread between species and from gut flora to pathogens - explaining hospital outbreaks and community spread. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification by organism and mechanism`,points:[`**ESBL-producing Enterobacterales (ESBL-E)**: E. coli, K. pneumoniae, Proteus - resistant to ceftriaxone and cefepime, carbapenem-susceptible. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**AmpC-E at moderate to high risk of clinically significant induction**: Enterobacter cloacae, K. aerogenes, Citrobacter freundii - can turn ceftriaxone-resistant on therapy. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**CRE**: Enterobacterales resistant to at least one carbapenem; subgroup by **carbapenemase type** (KPC, NDM, OXA-48-like) because the drug choice differs. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**CRAB**: carbapenem-resistant A. baumannii - usually OXA-23 carbapenemase; often susceptible only to polymyxins, tigecycline/minocycline, sulbactam (variable) and cefiderocol. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**DTR-P. aeruginosa**: resistant to all first-line beta-lactams and fluoroquinolones; some carry MBLs (VIM, NDM), more often porin loss and efflux. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**S. maltophilia**: intrinsically resistant to carbapenems; TMP-SMX is the backbone. [IDSA AMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - presentation and colonisation`,points:[`MDR GNB cause the same syndromes as susceptible organisms - **UTI, VAP/HAP, bloodstream, intra-abdominal, surgical site and line infection** - but **fail empirical therapy**, with persistent fever or shock at 48-72 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspect MDR GNB in **hospital-onset sepsis, prior antibiotics in 90 days, known colonisation, ICU transfer or recent hospitalisation**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Colonisation is not infection**: Acinetobacter in an endotracheal aspirate without new infiltrate, or Klebsiella in catheter urine without symptoms, should not be treated. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria** with MDR organisms is treated only in pregnancy and before urological procedures with mucosal breach. [IDSA ASB 2019]`,`**Community ESBL UTI** presents as an ordinary cystitis or pyelonephritis that fails cefixime or ceftriaxone - see the UTI topic for cystitis choices. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`Examine for the **source**: line sites, surgical wounds, chest, abdomen and catheter; drainable collections are commoner than true drug failure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of 'treatment failure'`,points:[`**Undrained focus** (abscess, empyema, obstructed urinary tract, infected line) - imaging and source control, not escalation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wrong dose or delivery**: underdosed beta-lactam, no loading dose, augmented renal clearance in young ICU patients, or poor tissue penetration (tigecycline in blood, polymyxin in lung). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-bacterial cause**: candidaemia, drug fever, CMV, DVT/PE, transfusion reaction, pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contaminant or colonist** treated as pathogen, such as Acinetobacter in tracheal aspirate. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**C. difficile infection** after broad-spectrum antibiotics - diarrhoea, leucocytosis, fever. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Send **cultures from the suspected site and blood before changing antibiotics**; reports with **MIC values** (not only S/I/R) guide dosing. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Phenotypic carbapenemase tests**: **mCIM** detects a carbapenemase and **eCIM** (with EDTA) identifies an **MBL**; Carba NP is a rapid colorimetric test. [CLSI M100 2025]`,`**Immunochromatographic lateral-flow tests** (for example detecting KPC, OXA-48, VIM, IMP, NDM) and **PCR** (Xpert Carba-R) name the carbapenemase in 15 minutes to an hour - essential for choosing ceftazidime-avibactam alone versus with aztreonam. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Synergy testing** of ceftazidime-avibactam with aztreonam (disc stacking, strip crossing or broth disc elution) is available in many Indian labs for NDM isolates. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Colistin susceptibility** must be tested by **broth microdilution** - disc diffusion and gradient strips are unreliable. [CLSI M100 2025]`,`**Rectal swab screening** for CRE in ICU admissions and outbreak contacts identifies carriers for contact precautions. [WHO CRE guidance 2017]`,`Supportive: **procalcitonin** trends to stop antibiotics, renal function and drug levels (aminoglycosides, vancomycin co-therapy). [ICMR AMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management - ESBL and AmpC Enterobacterales`,points:[`**ESBL-E outside the urinary tract** (bacteraemia, pneumonia, intra-abdominal): a **carbapenem** - **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly**, imipenem-cilastatin 500 mg 6-hourly or **ertapenem 1 g once daily**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`Use **meropenem or imipenem, not ertapenem, in critical illness or hypoalbuminaemia** (ertapenem is highly protein-bound and levels fall). [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Piperacillin-tazobactam is not recommended for ESBL-E bacteraemia even when reported susceptible** - the MERINO trial showed higher 30-day mortality than meropenem (12.3% vs 3.7%). [Harris et al, JAMA 2018]`,`**ESBL-E cystitis**: **nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days** or **TMP-SMX** if susceptible; fosfomycin 3 g single dose for E. coli; a single aminoglycoside dose is an alternative. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**ESBL-E pyelonephritis or complicated UTI**: TMP-SMX, ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin if susceptible; otherwise ertapenem, meropenem or an aminoglycoside. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Oral step-down** to TMP-SMX or a fluoroquinolone is reasonable once the patient is stable, source controlled and the isolate susceptible. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**AmpC-E (E. cloacae, K. aerogenes, C. freundii) serious infection**: **cefepime 2 g IV 8-hourly** if cefepime MIC is 2 mg/L or less; use a carbapenem if the MIC is 4-8 (likely ESBL co-production). Avoid ceftriaxone except for cystitis. [IDSA AMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Management - CRE by carbapenemase`,points:[`**KPC-producing CRE**: **ceftazidime-avibactam 2.5 g IV 8-hourly**, meropenem-vaborbactam or imipenem-relebactam; cefiderocol is an alternative. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**NDM (and other MBL)-producing CRE - the usual Indian situation**: **ceftazidime-avibactam 2.5 g IV 8-hourly plus aztreonam 2 g IV 8-hourly, infused at the same time**, or **cefiderocol 2 g IV 8-hourly over 3 hours**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**OXA-48-like CRE**: **ceftazidime-avibactam** is preferred; cefiderocol an alternative; meropenem-vaborbactam and imipenem-relebactam are inactive. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**NDM plus OXA-48 co-producers** (common in Indian K. pneumoniae) are treated as NDM: **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam**. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Aztreonam-avibactam** in a single product (approved in Europe in 2024 and the USA in 2025) delivers the same logic in one drug. [EMA 2024]`,`**Polymyxins are not preferred for CRE** - higher mortality and nephrotoxicity than newer beta-lactam combinations; keep them for when newer agents are unavailable or the isolate is resistant. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**CRE cystitis**: a **single dose of an aminoglycoside** (amikacin 15 mg/kg), **colistin** (renally concentrated), ciprofloxacin or TMP-SMX if susceptible. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Combination therapy is not routinely needed** once an active newer agent is used; tigecycline is never used alone for bacteraemia or UTI because serum and urine levels are low. [IDSA AMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Management - CRAB, DTR-Pseudomonas and Stenotrophomonas`,points:[`**CRAB**: **sulbactam-durlobactam with imipenem-cilastatin** is preferred (IDSA 2024); where unavailable, **high-dose ampicillin-sulbactam (sulbactam 6-9 g/day)** combined with at least one other active agent. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`Partner agents for CRAB: **polymyxin B, minocycline, tigecycline (high dose) or cefiderocol**; **combination therapy is recommended** for moderate to severe CRAB. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`Adding **meropenem to colistin did not improve CRAB outcomes** (AIDA and OVERCOME trials); avoid rifampicin and nebulised antibiotics as routine add-ons. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**DTR-P. aeruginosa**: **ceftolozane-tazobactam, ceftazidime-avibactam or imipenem-relebactam** are preferred; **cefiderocol** for MBL producers or when the others are inactive. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`For Pseudomonas susceptible to traditional agents, use **extended infusion** of a beta-lactam (meropenem 2 g 8-hourly over 3 hours in severe infection); routine double coverage is not needed once susceptibility is known. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**S. maltophilia**: two agents - **TMP-SMX (8-12 mg/kg/day trimethoprim)** plus minocycline, levofloxacin or cefiderocol, or **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Polymyxin B is preferred over colistin for systemic infection** (it is given as the active drug, reaches levels faster and is less nephrotoxic); **colistin is preferred for lower UTI** because colistimethate is renally cleared. [International polymyxin consensus 2019]`,`**Colistin dosing**: loading **9 million IU** (300 mg colistin base activity), then **4.5 million IU 12-hourly** with normal renal function; **polymyxin B**: loading 2-2.5 mg/kg then **1.25-1.5 mg/kg 12-hourly**. [International polymyxin consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / ICU - principles`,points:[`**Empirical choice by local antibiogram and risk**: hospital-onset sepsis in an Indian ICU with known CRE colonisation needs early cover active against it; otherwise start a carbapenem or piperacillin-tazobactam per unit policy and de-escalate. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Loading doses** of beta-lactams, polymyxins and aminoglycosides are given in full regardless of renal function; maintenance doses are then adjusted. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Prolonged or continuous infusion** of beta-lactams raises the time above MIC and improves cure in critically ill patients (BLING III, 2024). [Dulhunty et al, JAMA 2024]`,`**Source control within 6-12 hours** where possible - drainage, debridement, device removal - matters more than the choice between two active drugs. [SSC 2021]`,`**De-escalate at 48-72 hours** to the narrowest active agent once cultures return, and stop at a defined duration: **7 days** for most bacteraemia with source control, VAP and pyelonephritis. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Reserve newer agents** (ceftazidime-avibactam, cefiderocol) through a **stewardship authorisation** process - they are **Reserve** antibiotics in the WHO AWaRe list. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`Monitor **creatinine daily** on polymyxins and aminoglycosides, and watch for **neuromuscular blockade** (apnoea) with polymyxins. [International polymyxin consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: carbapenems (meropenem) and beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitors are usable; avoid aminoglycosides (ototoxicity), tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones where possible; polymyxins only if no option. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: reduce maintenance doses of carbapenems, ceftazidime-avibactam, cefiderocol and colistin by creatinine clearance; polymyxin B needs no renal adjustment. [International polymyxin consensus 2019]`,`**Neonates**: MDR Klebsiella and Acinetobacter sepsis; colistin and meropenem by weight; strict NICU hand hygiene prevents most. [DeNIS, Lancet Glob Health 2016]`,`**Neutropenic fever** in a known MDR carrier: tailor empirical therapy to the carriage organism - see the Fever in the immunocompromised topic. [ECIL 2013]`,`**Elderly and long-term care**: high ESBL carriage; avoid treating asymptomatic bacteriuria, which drives resistance. [IDSA ASB 2019]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where newer agents are unaffordable, colistin or polymyxin B plus a second active drug (high-dose meropenem if MIC 8 or less, tigecycline, aminoglycoside) is used - prevention and stewardship give more benefit per rupee. [ICMR AMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`MDR infections double **mortality**, prolong ICU stay and multiply cost; inadequate empirical therapy is the main driver of excess death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Polymyxin nephrotoxicity** occurs in about **30-60%** (dose-dependent, usually reversible); **neurotoxicity** causes paraesthesia and, rarely, respiratory muscle paralysis. [International polymyxin consensus 2019]`,`**Emergent resistance on therapy**: ceftazidime-avibactam resistance can arise in KPC (blaKPC mutations); cefiderocol resistance in NDM producers. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**C. difficile infection and candidaemia** follow prolonged broad-spectrum therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Colonisation with CRE persists for **months**; previous carriers should be flagged on readmission. [WHO CRE guidance 2017]`]},{heading:`Prevention and infection control`,points:[`**WHO core components for CRE, CRAB and CRPA**: multimodal IPC strategy, **hand hygiene**, **active surveillance**, **contact precautions**, **patient isolation or cohorting**, **environmental cleaning** and monitoring. [WHO CRE guidance 2017]`,`**Contact precautions**: gown and gloves for all contact with the patient and environment, dedicated equipment (stethoscope, BP cuff), and a single room or cohort ward. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Antimicrobial stewardship** - pre-authorisation of reserve drugs, 48-72-hour review, defined durations - reduces CRE rates; see the AMR and stewardship topic. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Device bundles** for CLABSI, VAP and CAUTI remove the main portals - see the Healthcare-associated infections topic. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Chlorhexidine bathing** in ICU lowers carriage of Gram-positive organisms and some Gram-negatives; cleaning sinks and drains controls Pseudomonas and CRE reservoirs. [WHO CRE guidance 2017]`,`**National linkage**: the **National Action Plan on AMR (NAP-AMR 2017)**, **ICMR AMRSN** and **NCDC's NARS-Net** surveillance, and the **Red Line campaign** (antibiotics marked with a red line are prescription-only). [NAP-AMR 2017]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**IDSA AMR Guidance 2024** (version 4.0): **sulbactam-durlobactam** became preferred for CRAB; **cefiderocol** and **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam** remain preferred for MBL-producing CRE. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`IDSA 2024 kept **carbapenems for ESBL-E infections outside the urinary tract**, discouraged piperacillin-tazobactam and cefepime for them, and advised **cefepime for AmpC-E** when MIC is 2 or less. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**WHO BPPL 2024** kept CRAB and CRE critical and moved carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas to the **high** priority group. [WHO BPPL 2024]`,`**WHO AWaRe 2023** lists ceftazidime-avibactam, cefiderocol, polymyxins and meropenem-vaborbactam as **Reserve** antibiotics. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`,`**Aztreonam-avibactam** was approved in Europe (2024) and the USA (2025) for infections with limited options, including MBL producers. [EMA 2024]`,`**BLING III (2024)**: continuous beta-lactam infusion showed a trend to lower 90-day mortality in sepsis, supporting prolonged infusion. [Dulhunty et al, JAMA 2024]`,`**Guideline difference**: IDSA 2024 favours newer beta-lactams over polymyxins, while many Indian hospitals still rely on colistin because of cost and access; write the IDSA choice and add the Indian practical alternative. [ICMR AMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**MERINO**: meropenem beats piperacillin-tazobactam for ESBL bacteraemia - a favourite MCQ. [Harris et al, JAMA 2018]`,`**Why add aztreonam to ceftazidime-avibactam**: MBLs spare aztreonam, avibactam protects it from co-produced ESBL and AmpC. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Ambler classes**: A (KPC, ESBL), B (MBL - NDM, VIM, IMP; zinc), C (AmpC), D (OXA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**SPICE / HECK-Yes** organisms carry inducible AmpC - use cefepime, not ceftriaxone, for serious infection. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**Colistin for urine, polymyxin B for blood** - a high-yield distinction. [International polymyxin consensus 2019]`,`Common mistakes: treating **colonisation**, using **tigecycline for bacteraemia**, and giving **ertapenem in shock with low albumin**. [IDSA AMR 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for MDR Gram-negative infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly; 2 g 8-hourly over 3 hours for severe or less susceptible infection`,`7 days for most bacteraemia with source control`,`Seizures (less than imipenem), C. difficile; lowers valproate levels sharply`],[`Ertapenem`,`1 g IV once daily`,`Non-critical ESBL infection, OPAT`,`Avoid in shock or hypoalbuminaemia; no Pseudomonas or Acinetobacter activity`],[`Cefepime (AmpC-E)`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`If cefepime MIC 2 mg/L or less`,`Neurotoxicity (encephalopathy) in renal failure`],[`Ceftazidime-avibactam`,`2.5 g IV 8-hourly over 2 hours`,`KPC and OXA-48 alone; with aztreonam for NDM`,`Renal dose adjustment; resistance emerging in KPC`],[`Aztreonam (with ceftazidime-avibactam)`,`2 g IV 8-hourly, infused at the same time`,`NDM/MBL CRE, S. maltophilia`,`Safe in penicillin allergy except ceftazidime cross-reaction; never alone for NDM`],[`Cefiderocol`,`2 g IV 8-hourly over 3 hours`,`MBL CRE, DTR-Pseudomonas, CRAB (in combination)`,`Higher mortality signal in CRAB (CREDIBLE-CR); renal adjustment; costly`],[`Ceftolozane-tazobactam`,`1.5 g IV 8-hourly (3 g 8-hourly for pneumonia)`,`DTR-Pseudomonas`,`No activity against carbapenemase producers or Acinetobacter`],[`Ampicillin-sulbactam (CRAB)`,`9 g IV 8-hourly or equivalent giving sulbactam 6-9 g/day`,`With a second active agent`,`Diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity; large sodium load`],[`Polymyxin B`,`Loading 2-2.5 mg/kg, then 1.25-1.5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly`,`Preferred polymyxin for systemic infection; no renal adjustment`,`Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, skin hyperpigmentation`],[`Colistin (colistimethate)`,`Loading 9 million IU, then 4.5 million IU IV 12-hourly`,`Preferred for lower UTI; adjust to creatinine clearance`,`Nephrotoxicity 30-60%, neuromuscular blockade; test MIC by broth microdilution`],[`Tigecycline / minocycline`,`Tigecycline 100 mg then 50 mg 12-hourly (high dose 200 then 100); minocycline 200 mg 12-hourly`,`CRAB partner drug`,`Low serum and urine levels - not for bacteraemia or UTI alone; nausea, pancreatitis, tooth staining`]]},{heading:`Beta-lactamase classes and what works`,columns:[`Ambler class / enzyme`,`Typical organisms`,`Hydrolyses`,`Drug of choice`],rows:[[`A - ESBL (CTX-M)`,`E. coli, K. pneumoniae`,`Penicillins, cephalosporins, aztreonam`,`Carbapenem (serious); nitrofurantoin or TMP-SMX (cystitis)`],[`A - KPC`,`K. pneumoniae (rare in India)`,`All beta-lactams including carbapenems`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam, imipenem-relebactam`],[`B - MBL (NDM, VIM, IMP)`,`K. pneumoniae, E. coli, Pseudomonas (common in India)`,`All beta-lactams except aztreonam; zinc-dependent`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam + aztreonam, or cefiderocol`],[`C - AmpC`,`E. cloacae, K. aerogenes, C. freundii`,`Penicillins, cephamycins, third-generation cephalosporins`,`Cefepime (MIC 2 or less), else carbapenem`],[`D - OXA-48-like (OXA-181, OXA-232)`,`K. pneumoniae (common in India)`,`Penicillins, weakly carbapenems; spares ceftazidime`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam`],[`D - OXA-23`,`A. baumannii`,`Carbapenems`,`Sulbactam-durlobactam or high-dose sulbactam combination`]]},{heading:`Laboratory tests for resistance mechanisms`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`mCIM`,`Presence of a carbapenemase`,`Positive = carbapenemase producer`],[`eCIM (with EDTA)`,`Metallo-beta-lactamase`,`Restored zone with EDTA = MBL (NDM, VIM, IMP)`],[`Lateral-flow immunoassay`,`KPC, OXA-48, NDM, VIM, IMP in 15 minutes`,`Directs ceftazidime-avibactam alone versus with aztreonam`],[`PCR (Xpert Carba-R)`,`Carbapenemase genes`,`Rapid; also used on rectal swabs`],[`Ceftazidime-avibactam + aztreonam synergy`,`Combined activity on NDM isolates`,`Synergy supports the combination`],[`Colistin broth microdilution`,`Colistin MIC`,`2 mg/L or less = wild type (intermediate category in CLSI)`]]},{heading:`Infection versus colonisation`,columns:[`Situation`,`Usually colonisation`,`Suggests infection`],rows:[[`Endotracheal aspirate`,`Organism grown, no new infiltrate, stable oxygenation`,`New infiltrate, fever, purulent secretions, worsening oxygenation`],[`Catheter urine`,`Bacteriuria without fever or symptoms`,`Fever, rigors, flank pain, no other source`],[`Wound swab`,`Superficial growth, clean wound`,`Cellulitis, pus, wound breakdown`],[`Rectal swab`,`CRE carriage`,`Never treated - used for isolation decisions`]]}],redFlags:[`**Hospital-onset sepsis with shock** in a known CRE or CRAB carrier - start cover active against the carriage organism within 1 hour.`,`**Fever persisting at 72 hours** on a carbapenem - look for an undrained focus and request carbapenemase testing.`,`**NDM-producing isolate** reported - ceftazidime-avibactam alone will fail; aztreonam must be added.`,`**Rising creatinine or paraesthesia/weakness** on colistin - nephro- or neurotoxicity; reassess dose and need.`,`**Cluster of CRE, CRAB or C. auris** in one unit - outbreak; inform the infection control team the same day.`,`**Neonate with sepsis in a NICU** with known MDR Klebsiella or Acinetobacter - urgent escalation per unit antibiogram.`],pearls:[`**MERINO**: meropenem, not piperacillin-tazobactam, for ESBL bacteraemia.`,`**NDM - add aztreonam** to ceftazidime-avibactam, infused together.`,`**MBLs spare aztreonam**; avibactam shields it from ESBL and AmpC.`,`**SPICE/HECK-Yes** = inducible AmpC: cefepime, not ceftriaxone.`,`**Colistin for urine, polymyxin B for blood.**`,`**Ertapenem: not in shock or low albumin**; no Pseudomonas or Acinetobacter cover.`,`**Tigecycline never alone** for bacteraemia or UTI - low levels.`,`**Do not treat colonisation** - Acinetobacter in tracheal aspirate is not VAP by itself.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-q1`,stem:`A 64-year-old woman has bacteraemia with ceftriaxone-resistant E. coli (ESBL producer) from pyelonephritis. The isolate is reported susceptible to piperacillin-tazobactam and meropenem. Which definitive therapy is supported by the MERINO trial?`,options:[`Meropenem`,`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`Cefepime`,`Tigecycline`],answer:0,explanation:`MERINO randomised ESBL E. coli and Klebsiella bacteraemia to meropenem or piperacillin-tazobactam and found higher 30-day mortality with piperacillin-tazobactam despite in-vitro susceptibility, so IDSA 2024 prefers a carbapenem. Cefepime is hydrolysed by ESBLs and is not recommended, and tigecycline reaches very low serum levels and is unsuitable for bacteraemia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-q2`,stem:`A ventilated ICU patient in Delhi has K. pneumoniae bacteraemia. eCIM is positive and a lateral-flow test shows NDM. Which regimen is most appropriate?`,options:[`Meropenem-vaborbactam`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam alone`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam given together`,`Imipenem-relebactam`],answer:2,explanation:`NDM is a zinc metallo-beta-lactamase not inhibited by avibactam, vaborbactam or relebactam, so all three newer combinations fail on their own. MBLs cannot hydrolyse aztreonam, and avibactam protects aztreonam from the co-produced ESBL and AmpC enzymes - hence ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam (or cefiderocol) for NDM producers.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-q3`,stem:`Enterobacter cloacae is isolated from blood; it is reported susceptible to ceftriaxone and cefepime (MIC 1 mg/L). Which agent does IDSA 2024 suggest for this serious infection?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone, as the isolate is susceptible`,`Cefepime`,`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`Cefuroxime`],answer:1,explanation:`E. cloacae carries a chromosomal inducible AmpC; ceftriaxone selects derepressed mutants and can fail during therapy despite initial susceptibility. Cefepime is a weak inducer and stable to AmpC, so it is suggested when its MIC is 2 mg/L or less. Amoxicillin-clavulanate and cefuroxime are hydrolysed by AmpC and are intrinsically inactive.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-q4`,stem:`A patient with carbapenem-resistant K. pneumoniae lower urinary tract infection (cystitis) has no access to newer beta-lactams. Which polymyxin is preferred and why?`,options:[`Polymyxin B, because it is excreted unchanged in urine`,`Neither, as polymyxins do not reach urine`,`Polymyxin B, because it needs renal dose adjustment`,`Colistin, because its prodrug colistimethate is renally cleared and concentrates in urine`],answer:3,explanation:`Colistin is given as colistimethate, which is largely cleared by the kidney and converted to active colistin in urine, giving high urinary levels - so colistin is preferred for lower UTI. Polymyxin B is cleared non-renally, reaches low urinary concentrations and needs no renal adjustment, which is why it is preferred for bloodstream infection instead.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-q5`,stem:`A 50-year-old man in septic shock with serum albumin 2.1 g/dL has an ESBL-producing K. pneumoniae intra-abdominal infection. Which statement about carbapenem choice is correct?`,options:[`Meropenem is preferred over ertapenem because ertapenem levels fall with hypoalbuminaemia`,`Ertapenem is preferred because once-daily dosing gives better levels in shock`,`Any carbapenem is equivalent, as all are minimally protein-bound`,`Imipenem should be avoided because it does not cover ESBL producers`],answer:0,explanation:`Ertapenem is about 85-95% protein-bound; in hypoalbuminaemia its free fraction is cleared fast and levels fall, so IDSA 2024 suggests meropenem or imipenem in critical illness or low albumin. Meropenem is minimally protein-bound, unlike ertapenem, and imipenem is fully active against ESBL producers.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the mechanisms of resistance and the management of carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales infections in India. Add a note on infection control measures.`,openingLines:[`Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales are Enterobacterales resistant to at least one carbapenem, usually through carbapenemase production; WHO lists them as critical priority pathogens.`,`In India, NDM metallo-beta-lactamase and OXA-48-like enzymes dominate, often together, so the choice of ceftazidime-avibactam with aztreonam or cefiderocol depends on identifying the carbapenemase.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden`,points:[`More than half of K. pneumoniae carbapenem-resistant in ICMR AMRSN data.`,`Mortality of CRE bacteraemia 30-50%.`,`Risk factors: prior antibiotics, ICU, devices, long stay, transfers.`]},{heading:`Mechanisms`,points:[`Class A KPC (serine), class B MBL (NDM, VIM, IMP; zinc), class D OXA-48-like.`,`Porin loss plus ESBL/AmpC; efflux pumps.`,`Plasmid spread explains outbreaks and community spread.`]},{heading:`Laboratory diagnosis`,points:[`mCIM and eCIM, Carba NP, lateral-flow assay, Xpert Carba-R.`,`Colistin MIC by broth microdilution; synergy testing for ceftazidime-avibactam with aztreonam.`]},{heading:`Treatment by enzyme`,points:[`KPC: ceftazidime-avibactam, meropenem-vaborbactam, imipenem-relebactam.`,`NDM: ceftazidime-avibactam 2.5 g plus aztreonam 2 g IV 8-hourly together, or cefiderocol 2 g 8-hourly.`,`OXA-48: ceftazidime-avibactam.`,`Cystitis: single-dose aminoglycoside or colistin.`,`Polymyxins when newer agents unavailable; polymyxin B for blood, colistin for urine.`,`Source control, loading doses, extended infusion, de-escalation, 7-day courses.`]},{heading:`Infection control`,points:[`Hand hygiene, contact precautions, single room or cohorting.`,`Active surveillance with rectal swabs; environmental cleaning.`,`Antimicrobial stewardship with reserve-drug authorisation; device bundles.`,`NAP-AMR, ICMR AMRSN surveillance, Red Line campaign.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: Ambler class, enzyme, drug of choice`,`Flow chart: CRE isolate - carbapenemase test - drug selection`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Mechanisms`,marks:2},{part:`Laboratory diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:3},{part:`Infection control`,marks:2}],keywords:[`CRE`,`NDM`,`OXA-48`,`ceftazidime-avibactam`,`aztreonam`,`cefiderocol`,`polymyxin`,`contact precautions`]},{id:`infectious-fever-mdr-gram-negative-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between colistin and polymyxin B.`,openingLines:[`Colistin (polymyxin E) and polymyxin B are cationic polypeptide antibiotics that disrupt the Gram-negative outer membrane by binding lipid A; both are last-resort Reserve drugs for MDR Gram-negative infection.`,`They differ chiefly in pharmacokinetics: colistin is given as an inactive prodrug cleared by the kidney, polymyxin B as the active drug cleared non-renally.`],answer:[{heading:`Pharmacology`,points:[`Colistin: given as colistimethate sodium (prodrug), slowly converted; polymyxin B: given as active drug.`,`Colistin: renal clearance, high urine levels, dose adjusted to creatinine clearance; polymyxin B: non-renal clearance, low urine levels, no renal adjustment.`,`Colistin reaches target levels slowly - large loading dose essential; polymyxin B reaches levels faster.`]},{heading:`Clinical use`,points:[`Colistin preferred for lower UTI; polymyxin B preferred for bloodstream and other systemic infection.`,`Colistin: loading 9 million IU then 4.5 million IU 12-hourly; polymyxin B: 2-2.5 mg/kg load then 1.25-1.5 mg/kg 12-hourly.`]},{heading:`Toxicity and testing`,points:[`Nephrotoxicity commoner with colistin; both cause neurotoxicity and neuromuscular blockade.`,`Polymyxin B causes skin hyperpigmentation.`,`Susceptibility for both by broth microdilution only.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: colistin versus polymyxin B`],markSplit:[{part:`Pharmacokinetics`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical use and dosing`,marks:2},{part:`Toxicity and testing`,marks:1}],keywords:[`colistimethate`,`polymyxin B`,`renal clearance`,`loading dose`,`broth microdilution`,`nephrotoxicity`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections due to Gram-negative bacilli; Antibacterial resistance`,`Infectious Diseases Society of America 2024 Guidance on the Treatment of Antimicrobial-Resistant Gram-Negative Infections (Tamma PD et al, Clin Infect Dis 2024)`,`International Consensus Guidelines for the Optimal Use of the Polymyxins (Tsuji BT et al, Pharmacotherapy 2019)`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Research and Surveillance Network (AMRSN) Annual Report, 2023`,`WHO Bacterial Priority Pathogens List, 2024`,`WHO Guidelines for the prevention and control of carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae, A. baumannii and P. aeruginosa in health care facilities, 2017`,`Harris PNA et al. Effect of piperacillin-tazobactam vs meropenem on 30-day mortality for ESBL bloodstream infection (MERINO), JAMA 2018`]}),t.push({id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection`,title:`Healthcare-associated infections and infection prevention: hand hygiene, isolation, biomedical waste`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`A **healthcare-associated infection (HAI)** is an infection **not present or incubating at admission**, appearing **48 hours or more after admission** (or within 30 days of surgery, 90 with an implant); most are **device- or procedure-related (CLABSI, CAUTI, VAP, SSI)** and preventable by **standard precautions, hand hygiene at the WHO 5 moments, transmission-based isolation, care bundles and safe biomedical waste handling under the BMW Rules 2016 (amended 2018)**, organised in India through the **NCDC IPC guidelines 2020 and Kayakalp**.`,keywords:[`HAI`,`nosocomial infection`,`hospital infection control committee`,`standard precautions`,`WHO 5 moments`,`hand hygiene`,`contact precautions`,`droplet precautions`,`airborne precautions`,`CAUTI`,`SSI`,`VAP`,`Kayakalp`,`NCDC IPC guidelines 2020`,`Biomedical Waste Management Rules 2016`,`colour coding`,`needle-stick injury`,`post-exposure prophylaxis`,`Spaulding classification`,`surgical antibiotic prophylaxis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**HAI** is an infection that was **not present or incubating at admission** and appears on **hospital day 3 or later** (48 hours or more), or after discharge within the window for that infection - 30 days after surgery, **90 days with an implant**. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`WHO estimates that **7 of every 100 patients in high-income** and **15 of every 100 in low- and middle-income countries** acquire at least one HAI during an acute hospital stay. [WHO Global IPC Report 2022]`,`In **ICUs**, HAI affects up to **30% of patients**, and device-associated infection rates in LMIC ICUs are **2-3 times or more** those in high-income ICUs. [WHO Global IPC Report 2022]`,`The **ICMR-AIIMS HAI surveillance network** across Indian tertiary ICUs found a **CLABSI rate of about 9 per 1000 central-line days**, mostly from **Klebsiella, Acinetobacter and Candida**, with high carbapenem resistance. [Mathur et al, Lancet Glob Health 2022]`,`The **big four device/procedure HAIs** are **CLABSI, CAUTI, VAP and SSI**; together with **C. difficile infection** and hospital-onset MDR organisms they form the surveillance targets. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`At least **50-70% of HAIs are preventable** with existing evidence-based practices. [WHO Global IPC Report 2022]`,`HAI raises **mortality, length of stay and cost**, and is the main engine of antimicrobial resistance spread in hospitals - see the MDR Gram-negative topic. [WHO Global IPC Report 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Agent**: endogenous flora (gut, skin) and exogenous organisms from the hands of staff, equipment and environment - **S. aureus, Enterobacterales, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, enterococci, Candida, C. difficile**, influenza, norovirus and respiratory viruses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Host**: extremes of age, ICU admission, diabetes, malnutrition, immunosuppression, neutropenia, burns, long stay and **prior antibiotics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Devices** are the biggest modifiable risk: each day of urinary catheterisation adds about **3-7% risk of bacteriuria**; ventilation and central lines carry similar daily risks. [CDC HICPAC CAUTI 2009]`,`**Procedures**: surgery (wound class, duration, hypothermia, hyperglycaemia), dialysis, endoscopy with poorly reprocessed scopes, and injections. [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**System factors**: overcrowding, **understaffing** (low nurse-to-patient ratio), lack of running water and alcohol rub, poor sterilisation and absent IPC staff. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Unsafe injection practice** - reuse of syringes or multidose vials - spreads **hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV** in outpatient and informal settings in India. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - chain of infection`,points:[`The **chain of infection** has six links: **infectious agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host** - every IPC measure breaks one link. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Contact transmission** (commonest in hospitals): direct via **hands of healthcare workers**, or indirect via fomites - stethoscopes, bed rails, BP cuffs, mobile phones. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Droplet transmission**: particles larger than **5 micrometres** travel about **1-2 metres** and fall - influenza, pertussis, meningococcus, diphtheria, mumps. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`**Airborne transmission**: droplet nuclei **5 micrometres or smaller** stay suspended and travel far - **TB, measles, varicella, disseminated zoster**, and SARS-CoV-2 during aerosol-generating procedures. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`**Devices bypass host defences**: catheters and endotracheal tubes breach mucosa, carry a **biofilm** and give organisms a direct route to bladder, lung or blood. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antibiotic pressure** removes normal flora, allowing colonisation by resistant organisms and **C. difficile**, which then spread to other patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hands are the main vehicle**: transient flora picked up from patients survive minutes to hours on skin and are removed by **alcohol rub in 20-30 seconds**. [WHO Hand Hygiene 2009]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By **site**: CLABSI, CAUTI, **VAP/HAP**, **SSI**, C. difficile infection, and others (skin, gastrointestinal, viral outbreaks). [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**SSI by depth**: **superficial incisional** (skin and subcutaneous), **deep incisional** (fascia and muscle), **organ/space** (any part opened or manipulated). [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**Surgical wound class**: **clean** (about 1-2% SSI), **clean-contaminated** (respiratory, GI or GU tract entered under control), **contaminated** (gross spillage, fresh trauma) and **dirty** (pus, perforated viscus). [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**Precaution tiers**: **standard precautions for all patients**, plus **contact, droplet or airborne precautions** based on the suspected organism. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`**Spaulding classification** of equipment: **critical** (enters sterile tissue - sterilise), **semi-critical** (contacts mucosa, such as endoscopes - high-level disinfection), **non-critical** (intact skin - low-level disinfection). [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**HAI versus community-onset**: infections apparent on admission day 1-2 are community-onset; from day 3 they count as hospital-onset. [CDC NHSN 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical recognition and surveillance definitions`,points:[`**CAUTI**: catheter in place **more than 2 days**, fever over 38 C, suprapubic or costovertebral tenderness, and urine culture with **10^5 CFU/mL or more** of no more than two organisms. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`Do **not** diagnose CAUTI from **cloudy or smelly urine** or a positive culture without symptoms - **asymptomatic bacteriuria** is not treated except in pregnancy and before urological surgery. [IDSA ASB 2019]`,`**SSI**: pus from the wound, organism from aseptically obtained fluid, or wound opened by the surgeon with pain, swelling, redness or heat, within 30 days (90 with implant). [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**VAP**: pneumonia after **48 hours of mechanical ventilation** - new infiltrate plus fever, purulent secretions and worsening oxygenation; NHSN tracks **ventilator-associated events (VAE)** for objectivity. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**C. difficile infection**: **3 or more unformed stools in 24 hours** after antibiotics with a positive toxin or PCR test; test only unformed stool. [IDSA/SHEA CDI 2021]`,`**Outbreak recognition**: a cluster of the same organism or syndrome in one unit, or more cases than the baseline - inform the infection control team the same day. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`Standard precautions and hand hygiene`,points:[`**Standard precautions** apply to **every patient**: hand hygiene, PPE by anticipated exposure, respiratory hygiene, safe injection practice, sharps safety, environmental cleaning, linen and waste handling, and reprocessing of equipment. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**WHO 5 moments**: **before touching a patient, before a clean/aseptic procedure, after body fluid exposure risk, after touching a patient, after touching patient surroundings**. [WHO Hand Hygiene 2009]`,`**Alcohol-based hand rub** (60-80% alcohol) for **20-30 seconds** is the default; use **soap and water for 40-60 seconds** when hands are visibly soiled and after caring for **C. difficile or norovirus** patients (alcohol does not kill spores). [WHO Hand Hygiene 2009]`,`The **WHO multimodal strategy**: system change (rub at the point of care), training, monitoring and feedback of compliance, reminders, and an institutional safety climate. [WHO Hand Hygiene 2009]`,`**Gloves do not replace hand hygiene** - perform it before donning and after removing gloves; change gloves between patients. [WHO Hand Hygiene 2009]`,`**Respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette**: masks for coughing patients in OPD waiting areas, tissues, and separation of at least **1 metre** - cheap and effective in Indian OPDs. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Safe injection practice**: one needle, one syringe, one time; **auto-disable syringes** for immunisation; single-dose vials preferred; never recap needles by hand. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`Transmission-based precautions and isolation`,points:[`**Contact precautions** (MRSA, CRE, CRAB, **C. auris**, C. difficile, scabies, norovirus): single room or cohort, **gown and gloves** on entry, dedicated equipment and enhanced cleaning. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`**Droplet precautions** (influenza, **meningococcus** for the first 24 hours of therapy, pertussis, diphtheria, mumps, rubella): single room or spacing of more than 1 metre, **surgical mask within 1-2 metres**, patient masked during transport. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`**Airborne precautions** (**pulmonary TB, measles, varicella**, disseminated zoster): **airborne infection isolation room** with negative pressure and **12 or more air changes per hour** (6 in older rooms), and **N95 or higher respirators** for staff. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`Where negative-pressure rooms are unavailable, use a **well-ventilated single room with windows open** and exhaust fans, away from immunocompromised patients - NCDC's practical alternative. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Donning order**: hand hygiene, gown, mask/respirator, goggles or face shield, gloves; **doffing order**: gloves, goggles, gown, mask (outside the room), hand hygiene. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Protective isolation** (reverse barrier) for severe neutropenia and stem-cell transplant: HEPA-filtered positive-pressure rooms; avoid live plants and uncooked food. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`Isolation is **ended** by organism-specific rules: meningococcus after **24 hours of effective therapy**; pulmonary TB after effective treatment with clinical improvement; measles 4 days after rash onset; varicella when all lesions are crusted. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`]},{heading:`Investigations - surveillance and outbreak work-up`,points:[`**Device-associated rate** = number of infections / device-days x 1000 (for example CLABSI per 1000 central-line days); **device utilisation ratio** = device-days / patient-days. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**SSI rate** = SSIs / operations in that category x 100, stratified by wound class and procedure, with **post-discharge follow-up to 30 or 90 days**. [CDC NHSN 2025]`,`**Hospital antibiogram** each year from the microbiology lab guides empirical therapy and tracks resistance trends. [ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Outbreak investigation steps**: confirm the outbreak and diagnosis, define cases, find cases, describe by time-place-person (epidemic curve), hypothesise, test (case-control or cohort), control, and communicate. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Typing** of isolates (antibiogram pattern, PCR, whole-genome sequencing) shows whether cases are linked. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Environmental sampling is not routine**; it is done only in outbreak investigation, for water in dialysis, and for operating-theatre air after construction or commissioning. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Sterilisation monitoring**: physical (time, temperature, pressure), **chemical indicators** in every pack (Bowie-Dick test daily for prevacuum autoclaves) and **biological indicators** (Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores) at least weekly. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`CAUTI - prevention and management`,points:[`**Avoid unnecessary catheters**: valid indications are acute retention or obstruction, accurate output monitoring in the critically ill, peri-operative use for selected surgery, healing of sacral wounds in incontinence, and end-of-life comfort. [CDC HICPAC CAUTI 2009]`,`**Insert aseptically** with sterile gloves, drape and a single-use lubricant, using the **smallest suitable catheter**; secure it to prevent movement. [CDC HICPAC CAUTI 2009]`,`**Maintain a closed drainage system**, keep the **bag below bladder level and off the floor**, empty with a clean container per patient, and do not disconnect for sampling (use the sampling port). [CDC HICPAC CAUTI 2009]`,`**Review need daily** and remove early using **nurse-driven removal protocols** and reminders; consider intermittent catheterisation or condom catheters instead. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Do not** routinely change catheters, screen for bacteriuria, irrigate the bladder with antimicrobials or give prophylactic antibiotics at removal. [CDC HICPAC CAUTI 2009]`,`**Treat CAUTI**: send urine from a **freshly replaced catheter** if the old one has been in more than 2 weeks; treat **7 days** if prompt response, **10-14 days** if delayed response. [IDSA CAUTI 2009]`]},{heading:`SSI and VAP - prevention bundles`,points:[`**Pre-operative**: bathe with soap, **do not remove hair** (or clip, never shave), treat remote infections, control glucose, and decolonise **S. aureus carriers (mupirocin nasal plus chlorhexidine body wash)** before cardiac and orthopaedic surgery. [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**Surgical antibiotic prophylaxis**: usually **cefazolin 2 g IV** (3 g if 120 kg or more) **within 120 minutes before incision** (60 minutes in ASHP/US guidance); redose cefazolin if surgery lasts **over 4 hours** or blood loss exceeds 1500 mL. [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**Do not continue prophylaxis after the operation** - WHO recommends against post-operative prolongation, a common Indian practice that drives resistance. [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**Intra-operative**: **alcohol-based chlorhexidine skin preparation**, surgical hand antisepsis, normothermia, glucose control, minimising theatre traffic, and triclosan-coated sutures. [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**Colorectal surgery**: mechanical bowel preparation **combined with oral antibiotics** reduces SSI. [WHO SSI 2018]`,`**VAP bundle**: **head of bed 30-45 degrees**, daily sedation interruption and **spontaneous breathing trials**, early mobilisation, subglottic secretion drainage and avoiding unnecessary reintubation. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Chlorhexidine oral care is no longer recommended** for VAP prevention (possible harm); toothbrushing is advised instead. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`]},{heading:`Occupational exposure: needle-stick injury and PEP`,points:[`**Immediate first aid**: wash the wound with **soap and running water**, let it bleed freely, **do not squeeze, scrub or apply bleach**; irrigate splashes to eyes or mouth with water or saline. [NACO 2021]`,`**Report** to the designated officer at once and assess the **exposure** (percutaneous, mucous membrane, non-intact skin; volume; device) and the **source** (HIV, HBV, HCV status - test with consent). [NACO 2021]`,`**Transmission risk** after percutaneous exposure: **HIV about 0.3%** (mucosal 0.09%), **HCV about 1.8%** (lower in recent data), and **HBV 6-30%** depending on source HBeAg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV PEP**: **TLD - tenofovir 300 mg + lamivudine 300 mg + dolutegravir 50 mg once daily for 28 days**, started **as early as possible, ideally within 2 hours and not beyond 72 hours**. [NACO 2021]`,`**HIV testing of the exposed worker** at baseline and at follow-up (NACO advises testing at 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months); counsel on safe sex and no blood donation until cleared. [NACO 2021]`,`**HBV**: no action if previously vaccinated with **anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL or more**; if unvaccinated, give **HBIG 0.06 mL/kg** plus the first vaccine dose (within 24 hours, up to 7 days); known non-responders get HBIG twice or HBIG plus revaccination. [CDC 2013 HBV PEP]`,`**HCV**: no PEP exists; test anti-HCV and ALT at baseline, **HCV RNA at 3 weeks or later**, and anti-HCV at 6 months; treat acute infection with direct-acting antivirals. [CDC 2020 HCV HCP]`,`Prevent sharps injury with **no recapping, immediate disposal in puncture-proof containers at the point of use, safety-engineered devices** and **hepatitis B vaccination for all healthcare workers**. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`Biomedical waste management (BMW Rules 2016, amended 2018)`,points:[`The **Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016** (MoEFCC) replaced the 1998 rules, cut categories to **four colour codes**, and apply to every facility generating waste, including camps and clinics. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**Yellow** (non-chlorinated bags): **anatomical waste, soiled waste (dressings, plaster casts, blood bags), expired or discarded medicines, chemical waste, discarded linen** and pre-treated microbiology/lab waste - **incineration, plasma pyrolysis or deep burial** (rural, with approval). [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**Red** (non-chlorinated bags or containers): **contaminated recyclable plastics** - tubing, IV sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes without needles, gloves - **autoclaving or microwaving, then shredding** and recycling; never incinerated. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**White (translucent)** puncture-, leak- and tamper-proof containers: **sharps** - needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels, blades - autoclaving or dry-heat sterilisation, then **shredding, mutilation or encapsulation**. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**Blue** (cardboard boxes with blue marking or containers): **glassware** (vials, ampoules, broken glass) and **metallic body implants** - disinfection or autoclaving, then recycling. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**2018 amendment**: **phase-out of chlorinated plastic bags and gloves** (blood bags excepted) by March 2019, **bar-code tracking** of bags and containers, and on-site pre-treatment of lab and highly infectious waste. [BMW Amendment 2018]`,`**Rules of storage and transport**: untreated waste must reach treatment **within 48 hours**; segregation **at the point of generation**; liquid waste pre-treated before discharge; records kept **5 years** and annual reports filed with the State Pollution Control Board. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`Waste handlers need **training at induction and annually**, PPE, and **immunisation against hepatitis B and tetanus** with health checks. [BMW Rules 2016]`]},{heading:`IPC programme in India: NCDC 2020 and Kayakalp`,points:[`The **National Guidelines for IPC in Healthcare Facilities (NCDC, MoHFW, January 2020)** set the structure: a **Hospital Infection Control Committee (HICC)** chaired by the head of the institution, an **infection control team** (IPC officer, microbiologist, **infection control nurses**) and written policies. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`HICC responsibilities: policies and SOPs, surveillance of HAI and device rates, outbreak management, training, antibiotic policy with the stewardship team, staff health (vaccination, PEP) and audits. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`NCDC 2020 aligns with the **WHO core components of IPC** - programme, guidelines, education, surveillance, multimodal strategies, monitoring and feedback, workload and staffing, built environment. [WHO IPC Core Components 2016]`,`**Kayakalp** was launched by MoHFW on **15 May 2015** under **Swachh Bharat Abhiyan** to promote cleanliness, hygiene and infection control in public health facilities, with **cash awards** at district hospital, sub-divisional, CHC, PHC and **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** levels. [MoHFW Kayakalp 2015]`,`Kayakalp assessment runs in three stages - **internal, peer and external assessment** - scoring **hospital upkeep, sanitation and hygiene, waste management, infection control, support services, hygiene promotion** and eco-friendly practices beyond the boundary. [MoHFW Kayakalp 2015]`,`**NQAS (National Quality Assurance Standards)** and **NABH** accreditation include IPC as a core area, so Kayakalp, NQAS and NABH reinforce one another. [MoHFW NQAS 2020]`,`**Mera Aspataal** patient feedback and **Swachh Swasth Sarvatra** link cleanliness of facilities to the surrounding community (open-defecation-free blocks). [MoHFW Kayakalp 2015]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**OPD and primary care**: triage coughing patients, respiratory hygiene, alcohol rub at every desk, single-use syringes, sterilised instruments and colour-coded bins - most HAI prevention in India happens here. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Dialysis units**: dedicated machines and staff for **HBV-positive** patients, regular HBV/HCV screening, and water quality testing. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Labour rooms and newborn units**: clean birth practices, hand hygiene, **kangaroo mother care** and minimal handling prevent neonatal sepsis, the commonest HAI in Indian newborns. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Pregnant healthcare workers**: avoid caring for patients with varicella, rubella or CMV-shedding infants if non-immune; ensure vaccination before pregnancy. [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`**Endoscopy and dental clinics**: semi-critical items need **high-level disinfection** (2% glutaraldehyde or orthophthalaldehyde, or automated reprocessors) and drying; dental handpieces are heat-sterilised. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Resource-limited facilities**: 1% sodium hypochlorite for blood spills, boiling or autoclaving instruments, open-window ventilation for airborne isolation, and **hand rub made locally** to the WHO formula. [NCDC IPC 2020]`,`**Healthcare worker vaccination**: hepatitis B for all, annual influenza, MMR and varicella for non-immune staff, and Td/Tdap. [NCDC IPC 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**SHEA/IDSA/APIC Compendium 2022** updated prevention of CLABSI, CAUTI, SSI, VAP/VAE, MRSA and C. difficile, and **dropped chlorhexidine oral care** for VAP prevention. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**WHO Global Strategy on IPC (2023)** and the **Global Action Plan and Monitoring Framework 2024-2030** set targets for every country to have a functioning national IPC programme. [WHO Global IPC Strategy 2023]`,`**WHO Global Report on IPC 2024** (second report) found progress since COVID-19 but persistent gaps in staffing, surveillance and WASH in LMIC facilities. [WHO Global IPC Report 2024]`,`**NACO 2021** switched HIV PEP to **dolutegravir-based TLD for 28 days**. [NACO 2021]`,`**BMW (Amendment) Rules 2018/2019** added bar-coding, phase-out of chlorinated bags and the requirement for common treatment facilities. [BMW Amendment 2018]`,`**IDSA/SHEA 2021 C. difficile update** made **fidaxomicin** preferred over oral vancomycin for initial episodes where available. [IDSA/SHEA CDI 2021]`,`**Guideline difference**: WHO advises prophylaxis **within 120 minutes before incision**, ASHP/US guidance **within 60 minutes**; both agree it ends with the operation. [WHO SSI 2018]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Biomedical waste colour coding'** - draw the four-colour table with examples and treatment. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**Red bag = recyclable plastics** (tubing, catheters, gloves); **yellow = anatomical and soiled**; **white = sharps**; **blue = glass and metal implants**. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**C. difficile and norovirus need soap and water**, not alcohol rub. [WHO Hand Hygiene 2009]`,`**Airborne = TB, measles, varicella** (N95, negative pressure); **droplet = influenza, meningococcus, pertussis** (surgical mask). [CDC/HICPAC Isolation 2007]`,`HIV PEP: **within 72 hours, ideally 2 hours, TLD for 28 days**; HBV PEP: **HBIG plus vaccine** if unvaccinated; HCV: **no PEP**. [NACO 2021]`,`Common mistakes: **shaving the surgical site**, **continuing prophylactic antibiotics for days after surgery**, and **treating asymptomatic catheter bacteriuria**. [WHO SSI 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and biologicals in infection prevention`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir (TLD) - HIV PEP`,`300 mg + 300 mg + 50 mg, one tablet orally once daily`,`28 days, start within 72 hours (ideally 2 hours)`,`Nausea, headache, insomnia; dolutegravir interacts with antacids/iron - separate doses`],[`Hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG)`,`0.06 mL/kg IM`,`Single dose within 24 hours (up to 7 days); repeat at 1 month in non-responders`,`Local pain; give at a different site from vaccine`],[`Hepatitis B vaccine`,`1 mL (20 micrograms) IM deltoid`,`0, 1 and 6 months; check anti-HBs 1-2 months after`,`Local soreness; anti-HBs under 10 mIU/mL means non-responder`],[`Cefazolin - surgical prophylaxis`,`2 g IV (3 g if 120 kg or more)`,`Within 60-120 minutes before incision; redose after 4 hours; stop at end of surgery`,`Allergy; use clindamycin or vancomycin in severe beta-lactam allergy`],[`Mupirocin 2% nasal + chlorhexidine body wash - S. aureus decolonisation`,`Nasal ointment twice daily; 2-4% chlorhexidine wash daily`,`5 days before cardiac or orthopaedic surgery`,`Mupirocin resistance with overuse; chlorhexidine skin irritation`],[`Alcohol-based hand rub`,`3 mL covering all hand surfaces`,`20-30 seconds per episode`,`Dryness (use emollient formulations); ineffective against spores`],[`Sodium hypochlorite`,`1% (10 000 ppm) for blood and body-fluid spills; lower strengths for routine surfaces`,`Contact time about 10 minutes; prepare fresh daily`,`Corrodes metal, irritant; inactivated by organic matter - wipe gross spill first`]]},{heading:`Biomedical waste colour coding (BMW Rules 2016, amended 2018)`,columns:[`Category`,`Container`,`Examples`,`Treatment and disposal`],rows:[[`Yellow`,`Non-chlorinated yellow bags`,`Anatomical waste, soiled dressings and plaster, expired medicines, chemical waste, discarded linen, pre-treated lab waste`,`Incineration, plasma pyrolysis or deep burial (rural, with approval)`],[`Red`,`Non-chlorinated red bags or containers`,`Tubing, IV sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes without needles, gloves`,`Autoclaving or microwaving, then shredding and recycling - no incineration`],[`White (translucent)`,`Puncture-, leak- and tamper-proof container`,`Needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels, blades`,`Autoclaving or dry-heat sterilisation, then shredding, mutilation or encapsulation`],[`Blue`,`Cardboard box with blue marking or blue container`,`Glass vials, ampoules, broken glass, metallic implants`,`Disinfection or autoclaving, then recycling`]]},{heading:`Transmission-based precautions`,columns:[`Type`,`Particle / route`,`Examples`,`Key measures`],rows:[[`Contact`,`Direct touch or fomites`,`MRSA, CRE, CRAB, C. auris, C. difficile, scabies, norovirus`,`Single room or cohort, gown and gloves, dedicated equipment, enhanced cleaning`],[`Droplet`,`Particles over 5 micrometres, about 1-2 metres`,`Influenza, meningococcus, pertussis, diphtheria, mumps, rubella`,`Surgical mask within 1-2 metres, spacing over 1 metre, mask patient in transport`],[`Airborne`,`Droplet nuclei 5 micrometres or less, long range`,`Pulmonary TB, measles, varicella, disseminated zoster`,`Negative-pressure room (12 or more air changes per hour) or ventilated room, N95 respirator`],[`Protective`,`Protects the patient`,`Severe neutropenia, stem-cell transplant`,`HEPA, positive pressure, restricted visitors`]]},{heading:`Spaulding classification of patient-care items`,columns:[`Category`,`Contact`,`Examples`,`Processing`],rows:[[`Critical`,`Sterile tissue or vascular system`,`Surgical instruments, implants, urinary catheters, needles`,`Sterilisation (steam autoclave 121 C for 15-30 minutes or 134 C for 3-5 minutes; ETO or plasma for heat-sensitive items)`],[`Semi-critical`,`Mucous membranes or non-intact skin`,`Endoscopes, laryngoscope blades, vaginal probes`,`High-level disinfection (2% glutaraldehyde, orthophthalaldehyde)`],[`Non-critical`,`Intact skin`,`BP cuffs, stethoscopes, bed rails`,`Low-level disinfection (alcohol, detergent, hypochlorite)`]]},{heading:`Post-exposure management after a needle-stick injury`,columns:[`Virus`,`Risk (percutaneous)`,`Action`,`Follow-up`],rows:[[`HIV`,`About 0.3%`,`TLD for 28 days, within 72 hours (ideally 2 hours) if source positive or unknown high-risk`,`HIV test at baseline, 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months (NACO)`],[`Hepatitis B`,`6-30% (higher if source HBeAg positive)`,`Vaccinated responder: nothing; unvaccinated: HBIG + vaccine; non-responder: HBIG x 2 or HBIG + revaccination`,`Anti-HBs after vaccination; HBsAg if exposed unprotected`],[`Hepatitis C`,`About 1.8% (lower in recent series)`,`No PEP`,`Anti-HCV and ALT baseline; HCV RNA at 3 weeks or later; anti-HCV at 6 months; treat with DAAs if infected`]]}],redFlags:[`**Cluster of the same organism or syndrome** in a ward or ICU - suspected outbreak; inform the infection control team the same day.`,`**Needle-stick or splash from a known or suspected HIV-positive source** - start PEP within hours, not days.`,`Patient with **suspected pulmonary TB, measles or varicella** in a general ward or OPD queue - move to airborne isolation now.`,`**Fever with purpuric rash** in a patient - droplet precautions until 24 hours of effective therapy (meningococcus).`,`**Sterilisation failure** (positive biological indicator) - recall all loads since the last negative test.`,`**CRE, CRAB or C. auris** reported from a new patient - contact precautions and screening of contacts.`,`**Mixed or unsegregated biomedical waste**, or untreated waste stored beyond 48 hours - a legal and safety breach.`],pearls:[`**WHO 5 moments**: 2 before (patient, aseptic task), 3 after (body fluid, patient, surroundings).`,`**Rub 20-30 seconds, wash 40-60 seconds**; soap and water for C. difficile and norovirus.`,`BMW colours: **Yellow - burn; Red - recycle plastic; White - sharps; Blue - glass and metal.**`,`Airborne: **'My Chicken has TB'** - Measles, Chickenpox, TB.`,`**Chain of infection** has six links; every IPC measure breaks one.`,`Surgical prophylaxis: **right drug (cefazolin), right time (before incision), right stop (end of surgery)**.`,`**No PEP for hepatitis C** - only follow-up testing and early DAA treatment.`,`**Kayakalp** launched 15 May 2015 under Swachh Bharat; **NCDC IPC guidelines** January 2020.`,`**Clip, never shave** the surgical site.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-q1`,stem:`Under the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 (amended 2018), a used urinary catheter, IV tubing and disposable gloves should be discarded into which category?`,options:[`Yellow bag for incineration`,`Red bag for autoclaving, shredding and recycling`,`White translucent puncture-proof container`,`Blue cardboard box`],answer:1,explanation:`Contaminated recyclable plastics such as tubing, catheters, urine bags, syringes without needles and gloves go into the red category, which is autoclaved or microwaved, shredded and recycled - never incinerated. The yellow category is for anatomical and soiled waste, white containers are for sharps, and blue boxes are for glassware and metallic implants.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-q2`,stem:`A nurse has just cared for a patient with Clostridioides difficile diarrhoea. Which hand hygiene method is appropriate after removing her gloves?`,options:[`Alcohol-based hand rub for 20-30 seconds`,`No hand hygiene is needed because gloves were worn`,`Chlorhexidine-alcohol rub for 60 seconds`,`Soap and running water for 40-60 seconds`],answer:3,explanation:`C. difficile spores are not killed by alcohol, so they must be mechanically removed by washing with soap and running water for 40-60 seconds. Alcohol rub, with or without chlorhexidine, is the default for most moments but fails against spores, and gloves never replace hand hygiene because hands are contaminated during removal.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-q3`,stem:`Which patient requires airborne precautions with an N95 respirator and a negative-pressure (or well-ventilated single) room?`,options:[`A child with suspected measles`,`A young adult with meningococcal meningitis`,`A patient with influenza A`,`A patient colonised with carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella`],answer:0,explanation:`Measles spreads by droplet nuclei of 5 micrometres or less that remain suspended, so it needs airborne precautions, like pulmonary TB and varicella. Meningococcus and influenza spread by large droplets over 1-2 metres and need droplet precautions with a surgical mask, while carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella spreads by contact and needs gown and gloves.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-q4`,stem:`A resident sustains a needle-stick from an HBsAg-positive patient. She completed three doses of hepatitis B vaccine and had a documented anti-HBs of 110 mIU/mL. What is the correct hepatitis B post-exposure management?`,options:[`HBIG plus a booster vaccine dose`,`Two doses of HBIG one month apart`,`No hepatitis B prophylaxis is needed`,`Start tenofovir for 28 days`],answer:2,explanation:`A documented responder (anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL or more after vaccination) is protected and needs no HBIG or booster, even when the source is HBsAg-positive. HBIG with vaccine is for unvaccinated workers, two HBIG doses are for known non-responders, and tenofovir is not used as hepatitis B post-exposure prophylaxis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-q5`,stem:`For an elective open hernia repair with mesh, which surgical antibiotic prophylaxis practice is correct according to WHO SSI guidelines?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone 1 g IV twice daily for 5 days after surgery`,`Cefazolin 2 g IV within 120 minutes before incision, not continued after surgery`,`Cefazolin 2 g IV at skin closure, continued for 24 hours`,`Oral amoxicillin started the evening before surgery and continued for 7 days`],answer:1,explanation:`Prophylaxis must achieve tissue levels at incision, so cefazolin is given before incision (within 120 minutes per WHO; 60 minutes in US guidance) and WHO recommends against post-operative prolongation. Multi-day ceftriaxone or amoxicillin adds resistance and C. difficile risk without lowering SSI, and a dose at skin closure is too late to protect the wound.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define healthcare-associated infections. Discuss their types, the chain of transmission and the measures for their prevention and control in a district hospital in India.`,openingLines:[`A healthcare-associated infection is an infection not present or incubating at admission that appears 48 hours or more after admission, or within 30 days of surgery (90 days with an implant).`,`Most are device- or procedure-related - CLABSI, CAUTI, VAP and SSI - and at least half are preventable by standard precautions, hand hygiene, transmission-based isolation, care bundles and a functioning infection control programme as laid down in the NCDC IPC guidelines 2020.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden`,points:[`WHO: 7% of hospital patients in high-income and 15% in LMIC acquire an HAI; up to 30% in ICUs.`,`India: CLABSI about 9 per 1000 line-days in ICMR-AIIMS network; high carbapenem resistance.`]},{heading:`Types`,points:[`CLABSI, CAUTI, VAP/HAP, SSI (superficial, deep, organ/space), C. difficile, viral outbreaks.`,`Risk factors: agent, host, devices, procedures, overcrowding, understaffing.`]},{heading:`Chain of infection`,points:[`Agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission (contact, droplet, airborne), portal of entry, susceptible host.`,`Hands of healthcare workers are the main vehicle.`]},{heading:`Prevention - patient level`,points:[`Standard precautions for all; WHO 5 moments; alcohol rub 20-30 seconds.`,`Contact, droplet and airborne precautions by organism.`,`Bundles: CLABSI, CAUTI (avoid, aseptic insertion, closed system, daily review), VAP (head up, sedation breaks), SSI (no shaving, cefazolin before incision, stop after surgery).`,`Safe injections, sterilisation by Spaulding category, environmental cleaning.`]},{heading:`Prevention - system level`,points:[`HICC and infection control team; policies; surveillance of device rates and SSI; antibiogram.`,`Antimicrobial stewardship; outbreak investigation.`,`Staff health: hepatitis B vaccination, needle-stick PEP (TLD 28 days).`,`Biomedical waste segregation by BMW Rules 2016/2018 colour codes.`,`Kayakalp, NQAS and NABH as quality drivers.`]}],mustDraw:[`Chain of infection diagram with the measure that breaks each link`,`Table of transmission-based precautions`,`Biomedical waste colour-coding table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Types and risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Chain of infection`,marks:2},{part:`Patient-level prevention`,marks:3},{part:`System-level prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`HAI`,`chain of infection`,`standard precautions`,`hand hygiene`,`bundles`,`HICC`,`Kayakalp`,`BMW Rules`]},{id:`infectious-fever-hospital-acquired-infection-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Write a short note on the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 and their 2018 amendment.`,openingLines:[`The Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016, notified by the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, replaced the 1998 rules and reduced waste into four colour-coded categories segregated at the point of generation.`,`The 2018 amendment phased out chlorinated plastic bags and gloves, introduced bar-code tracking and mandated on-site pre-treatment of laboratory and highly infectious waste.`],answer:[{heading:`Colour categories`,points:[`Yellow: anatomical, soiled, expired medicines, chemical, linen - incineration, plasma pyrolysis or deep burial.`,`Red: recyclable contaminated plastics - autoclave or microwave, shred, recycle.`,`White translucent: sharps - autoclave or dry heat, shred or encapsulate.`,`Blue: glassware and metallic implants - disinfect, recycle.`]},{heading:`Key duties of the occupier`,points:[`Segregate at source; treat or hand over within 48 hours.`,`Pre-treat lab and microbiology waste on site; treat liquid waste.`,`Train staff at induction and annually; immunise handlers against hepatitis B and tetanus.`,`Maintain records for 5 years; file an annual report with the State Pollution Control Board.`]},{heading:`2018 amendment`,points:[`Chlorinated bags and gloves phased out (except blood bags).`,`Bar-coding of bags and containers; GPS tracking of transport by common facilities.`]}],mustDraw:[`Colour-coding table: category, examples, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Background and principles`,marks:1},{part:`Colour categories`,marks:2},{part:`Duties and 2018 amendment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`BMW Rules 2016`,`colour coding`,`segregation at source`,`48 hours`,`bar-coding`,`chlorinated plastics`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections acquired in health care facilities`,`National Guidelines for Infection Prevention and Control in Healthcare Facilities, NCDC, MoHFW, Government of India, January 2020`,`Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 and (Amendment) Rules 2018, Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, Government of India`,`Kayakalp: Award to Public Health Facilities - Guidelines, MoHFW, 2015`,`WHO Guidelines on Hand Hygiene in Health Care, 2009; WHO Global Guidelines for the Prevention of Surgical Site Infection, 2nd edition, 2018`,`SHEA/IDSA/APIC Compendium of Strategies to Prevent Healthcare-Associated Infections in Acute-Care Hospitals, 2022 updates`,`National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment (PEP), NACO, 2021`,`CDC/HICPAC Guideline for Isolation Precautions, 2007 (updated 2023) and Guideline for Prevention of CAUTI, 2009`]});var n=[];n.push({id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic`,title:`Systemic fungal infections: candidiasis, aspergillosis, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Invasive (systemic) fungal infections** are deep-tissue or bloodstream infections by yeasts (**Candida, Cryptococcus**), moulds (**Aspergillus**) or dimorphic fungi (**Histoplasma, Talaromyces**) in a host with broken barriers or depressed immunity; treatment is organism-led - an **echinocandin for candidaemia**, **voriconazole or isavuconazole for invasive aspergillosis**, and the **WHO 2022 single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine and fluconazole** regimen for HIV cryptococcal meningitis.`,keywords:[`invasive fungal infection`,`IFI`,`candidaemia`,`invasive candidiasis`,`Candida auris`,`invasive aspergillosis`,`chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`galactomannan`,`beta-D-glucan`,`cryptococcal meningitis`,`CrAg`,`histoplasmosis`,`talaromycosis`,`echinocandin`,`caspofungin`,`micafungin`,`voriconazole`,`isavuconazole`,`liposomal amphotericin B`,`flucytosine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Invasive fungal infection (IFI)** means fungus in sterile tissue or blood with tissue damage; it is graded **proven, probable or possible** by the **EORTC/MSGERC 2020** definitions, which need host factors, clinical signs and mycological evidence. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`Globally fungal disease kills an estimated **3.8 million people a year**, about 2.5 million directly attributable - more than malaria; the **WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List 2022** puts **Cryptococcus neoformans, Candida auris, Aspergillus fumigatus and Candida albicans** in the critical group. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`India carries one of the largest fungal burdens: an estimated **over 57 million people** have a serious fungal disease, with **chronic pulmonary aspergillosis (CPA) around 1.7 million** (mostly after treated TB) and the world's highest rate of **mucormycosis**. [Ray et al., Open Forum Infect Dis 2022]`,`The Indian ICU candidaemia study (27 ICUs) found an incidence of about **6.5 per 1000 ICU admissions**, with **C. tropicalis (about 42%)** the commonest species and over 30 species overall, and **C. auris** in about 5%. [Chakrabarti, Intensive Care Med 2015]`,`**C. auris** was first characterised as a nosocomial pathogen in **Indian ICUs** (South Asian clade I); it spreads on hands and surfaces, colonises skin, and is often **fluconazole-resistant (over 90%)**. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`Cryptococcal meningitis causes about **15-19% of AIDS-related deaths** worldwide and is the leading cause of adult meningitis where HIV is common; Indian HIV cohorts report **CrAg positivity of 3-8%** at CD4 under 100. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis** is endemic in the **Gangetic plains (West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar)** and Assam; **talaromycosis (Talaromyces marneffei)** is endemic in **Manipur** and the North-East. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Candidaemia risk**: central venous catheter, broad-spectrum antibiotics, ICU stay over 7 days, TPN, abdominal surgery or anastomotic leak, necrotising pancreatitis, haemodialysis, neutropenia and **colonisation at several sites**. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis risk**: **neutrophils under 500/mm3 for over 10 days**, allogeneic stem-cell transplant and GVHD, high-dose steroids (prednisolone 0.3 mg/kg/day or more for over 3 weeks), ibrutinib and other BTK inhibitors, and severe influenza or COVID in ICU (IAPA, CAPA). [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis** affects the **immunocompetent with a lung cavity** - post-tuberculosis cavities (commonest in India), sarcoidosis, COPD and bullae. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Cryptococcus neoformans** (pigeon droppings, decaying wood) causes disease mainly at **CD4 under 100 cells/mm3**; **C. gattii** (eucalyptus) can infect immunocompetent hosts and forms brain cryptococcomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other cryptococcal hosts: solid-organ transplant, long-term steroids, **anti-TNF and fingolimod**, cirrhosis, sarcoidosis and idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Histoplasma capsulatum** is inhaled from bat and bird guano (caves, poultry, old buildings); dissemination follows **HIV at CD4 under 150**, steroids or TNF blockers, but adrenal disease occurs in apparently healthy Indian men. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uncontrolled diabetes** raises risk of candidiasis, mucormycosis and cryptococcosis - see the Mucormycosis topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`**Neutrophils** kill hyphae and **T cells (Th1, Th17) with macrophages** control yeasts and dimorphs - so **neutropenia predisposes to moulds and candidaemia**, while **CD4 lymphopenia predisposes to Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Talaromyces and Pneumocystis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Candida** reaches blood from the **gut (translocation)** or a **colonised catheter biofilm**; biofilms resist azoles, which is why the **catheter must be removed**. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Aspergillus** conidia (2-3 micrometres) reach alveoli, germinate into hyphae and **invade blood vessels**, causing infarction - the **halo sign** (haemorrhage around a nodule) early and the **air-crescent sign** when neutrophils recover. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcus** is inhaled, disseminates and enters the CNS; its **polysaccharide capsule (glucuronoxylomannan)** blocks phagocytosis and **clogs arachnoid villi**, raising intracranial pressure - the cause of headache, papilloedema, cranial nerve palsies and early death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug targets**: **azoles** inhibit lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51) and ergosterol synthesis; **echinocandins** inhibit **beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase** in the cell wall; **polyenes (amphotericin)** bind ergosterol and punch membrane pores; **flucytosine** is converted to 5-fluorouracil inside the fungus. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Why some drugs fail**: Cryptococcus and Mucorales have little beta-glucan, so **echinocandins do not work** against them; fluconazole has no mould activity; **C. krusei is intrinsically fluconazole-resistant** and C. glabrata often dose-dependent. [Harrison 22e]`,`Echinocandins and fluconazole cross poorly into the eye and CSF - **endophthalmitis or meningitis needs fluconazole or amphotericin**, not an echinocandin alone. [IDSA 2016]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020**: **proven** = organism on histology or culture from sterile tissue; **probable** = host factor + clinical/radiological feature + mycological evidence (galactomannan, beta-D-glucan, culture, PCR); **possible** = host and clinical criteria without mycology. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Candidiasis spectrum**: mucocutaneous (oral, oesophageal, vulvovaginal) versus **invasive** - candidaemia, deep-seated (intra-abdominal abscess, endocarditis, osteomyelitis), **chronic disseminated (hepatosplenic)** after neutropenia, and endophthalmitis. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Aspergillus spectrum by immunity**: **invasive aspergillosis** (immunosuppressed), **chronic pulmonary aspergillosis** (simple aspergilloma, chronic cavitary, chronic fibrosing - over 3 months), and **ABPA** (hypersensitivity in asthma - see the Asthma topic). [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Cryptococcosis**: asymptomatic **cryptococcal antigenaemia** (CrAg positive, CSF negative), pulmonary, **meningitis/meningoencephalitis**, and disseminated (skin papules mimicking molluscum). [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis**: acute pulmonary, chronic cavitary pulmonary (TB mimic), **progressive disseminated (PDH)** and **adrenal** histoplasmosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Candidaemia**: an ICU patient on broad antibiotics with a central line who develops **new fever not responding to antibiotics** after day 5-7, often with no localising sign. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: neutropenic fever persisting **over 96 hours despite antibiotics**, pleuritic chest pain, cough and **haemoptysis**; sinus pain and epistaxis in sinus disease. [IDSA 2016]`,`**CPA**: cured TB patient with months of **cough, weight loss, fatigue and haemoptysis** and a 'sputum-negative TB relapse' label - the classic Indian misdiagnosis. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis**: **subacute headache over 1-3 weeks**, fever, vomiting, blurred or double vision and confusion; neck stiffness is **absent in about 70%** because inflammation is minimal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Disseminated histoplasmosis**: prolonged fever, weight loss, diarrhoea, **painful oral or tongue ulcers**, and features of **adrenal insufficiency** (fatigue, postural giddiness, pigmentation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Talaromycosis**: fever, anaemia and **umbilicated skin papules on face and trunk** in advanced HIV from Manipur or South-East Asia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Fundoscopy** in every candidaemia - fluffy white **chorioretinal exudates** (Candida endophthalmitis) change duration to at least 4-6 weeks. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Skin**: tender erythematous nodules in candidaemia (often C. tropicalis); **necrotic eschar** in mould infection; **molluscum-like umbilicated papules** in Cryptococcus, Histoplasma and Talaromyces. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CNS signs in cryptococcosis**: papilloedema, **sixth nerve palsy** (raised ICP), reduced vision, low GCS - a GCS under 15 predicts higher mortality. [WHO 2022]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy** with pancytopenia suggest disseminated histoplasmosis, talaromycosis or leishmaniasis in the right context. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral adrenal enlargement** with **postural hypotension and hyperpigmentation** in a middle-aged man suggests adrenal histoplasmosis (or TB). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing and coarse crackles** over an old TB cavity with haemoptysis suggest an **aspergilloma** within CPA. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Persistent ICU fever**: candidaemia versus resistant Gram-negative sepsis, Clostridioides difficile, drug fever and line infection by bacteria - **beta-D-glucan and blood culture** separate them. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Nodules with halo in a neutropenic patient**: invasive aspergillosis versus **mucormycosis** (reverse halo, over 10 nodules, sinusitis, voriconazole breakthrough) - galactomannan is negative in mucormycosis. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**CPA versus TB relapse**: both cavitate and bleed; CPA has **negative sputum NAAT, a positive Aspergillus IgG** (over 20 mg/L or kit cut-off) and a fungal ball or pericavitary thickening. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Chronic meningitis in HIV**: cryptococcal versus **TB meningitis** (high protein, low glucose, lymphocytes, basal exudates) - **CSF CrAg** is near 100% sensitive for cryptococcus. [WHO 2022]`,`**Disseminated histoplasmosis** mimics **disseminated TB, kala-azar and lymphoma** - bone marrow aspirate with fungal stains, urine antigen and culture separate them. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Blood culture** (2-3 sets, 20 mL each, automated system) is positive in only **about 50% of invasive candidiasis**; identify to species with **MALDI-TOF** - routine biochemical kits misidentify C. auris as C. haemulonii. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Serum (1,3)-beta-D-glucan**: positive at **80 pg/mL or more** (Fungitell), negative predictive value about 95% for invasive candidiasis; false positives with albumin, IVIG, haemodialysis, gauze and piperacillin batches; negative in Cryptococcus and Mucorales. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Galactomannan (Aspergillus)**: positive at an optical density index of **1.0 or more in serum or BAL**, or serum 0.7 plus BAL 0.8 or more (EORTC/MSGERC 2020); sensitivity falls on mould-active prophylaxis and in non-neutropenic patients. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**HRCT chest** in neutropenic fever over 3-5 days: nodules, **halo sign**, cavitation, **air-crescent sign**; CPA shows a cavity with fungal ball, pleural thickening and progression over 3 months. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Cryptococcal antigen (CrAg) lateral flow assay** on serum, plasma or CSF: sensitivity and specificity **over 95%**; India ink is cheap but misses about 20-30%; CSF opening pressure **25 cm H2O or more** is raised. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasma**: **urine and serum antigen** (sensitivity about 90% in disseminated disease), bone marrow or ulcer biopsy with **GMS/PAS stain** showing small intracellular yeasts, and culture (slow, 2-4 weeks). [WHO/PAHO 2020]`,`Always do **antifungal susceptibility testing** for invasive Candida, and **therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)** for voriconazole, posaconazole, itraconazole and flucytosine. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`]},{heading:`Management - candidiasis`,points:[`**Mucosal**: oral thrush - **clotrimazole troche 10 mg five times daily** or fluconazole 100-200 mg/day for 7-14 days; **oesophageal - fluconazole 200-400 mg/day for 14-21 days**. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Candidaemia first line is an echinocandin**: **caspofungin 70 mg IV loading then 50 mg daily**, **micafungin 100 mg IV daily** or **anidulafungin 200 mg loading then 100 mg daily**. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Remove or change all central venous catheters** as soon as possible; take **repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours** until negative. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Step down to oral fluconazole 400 mg (6 mg/kg) daily** after **5-7 days** if the isolate is susceptible, the patient is stable and repeat cultures are negative; voriconazole step-down for C. krusei. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Duration: 14 days after the first negative blood culture** and resolution of symptoms, provided there is no metastatic focus; endophthalmitis, endocarditis and osteomyelitis need weeks to months. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Fluconazole** (800 mg loading, then 400 mg daily; halve after loading if CrCl under 50 mL/min) is acceptable in a stable, non-critically ill patient with low risk of resistance; **liposomal amphotericin 3-5 mg/kg/day** if echinocandins are unavailable or not tolerated. [IDSA 2016]`,`**C. auris**: echinocandin first line, **contact isolation, chlorhexidine or chlorine-based cleaning** (quaternary ammonium compounds fail), and screening of contacts (axilla-groin swab). [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`]},{heading:`Management - aspergillosis, cryptococcosis, histoplasmosis`,points:[`**Invasive aspergillosis**: **voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x 2 doses then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly** (oral 200-300 mg 12-hourly), trough **1-5.5 mg/L**; or **isavuconazole 200 mg 8-hourly for 6 doses then 200 mg daily** - equal efficacy, fewer side effects (SECURE trial). [IDSA 2016]`,`Aspergillosis alternatives: **liposomal amphotericin 3 mg/kg/day**; posaconazole; treat for at least **6-12 weeks** and until immunosuppression is reversed. [IDSA 2016]`,`**CPA**: **itraconazole 200 mg twice daily** or voriconazole 150-200 mg twice daily for **at least 6 months**; **bronchial artery embolisation** for haemoptysis; surgery for a single aspergilloma. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**HIV cryptococcal meningitis (WHO 2022)**: **single dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** + **flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses for 14 days** + **fluconazole 1200 mg/day for 14 days** (AMBITION-cm). [WHO 2022]`,`Then **consolidation fluconazole 800 mg/day for 8 weeks** and **maintenance 200 mg/day** until CD4 is 200 or more (or 100-200 with suppressed viral load) after at least 1 year. [WHO 2022]`,`If liposomal amphotericin is unavailable: **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day + flucytosine for 7 days**, then fluconazole 1200 mg/day for days 8-14; without amphotericin, **fluconazole 1200 mg + flucytosine for 14 days**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Raised ICP**: **therapeutic lumbar puncture** to drain CSF until pressure is under 20 cm H2O or halved, repeated daily; **no steroids, acetazolamide or mannitol** (CryptoDex harm). [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis (WHO/PAHO 2020)**: severe disease - **liposomal amphotericin 3 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks**, then **itraconazole 200 mg thrice daily for 3 days then twice daily for at least 12 months**; mild-moderate - itraconazole alone. [WHO/PAHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and ICU care`,points:[`**Admit** all invasive fungal infections: candidaemia, febrile neutropenia with lung nodules, suspected cryptococcal meningitis and disseminated histoplasmosis. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Amphotericin safety bundle**: **1 L normal saline before each dose**, give over 2 hours (deoxycholate over 4-6 hours), **daily potassium and magnesium supplements** (K 1-2 tablets of 8 mEq twice daily, Mg 1-2 tablets), creatinine and electrolytes **twice weekly**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Empirical antifungal** in persistent neutropenic fever after 4-7 days of antibiotics: caspofungin or liposomal amphotericin; prefer a **pre-emptive (biomarker and CT-driven)** strategy where tests are available. [IDSA 2016]`,`Echinocandin **empirical** therapy may be used in septic ICU patients with risk factors and multisite colonisation; **stop after 4-5 days** if beta-D-glucan and cultures are negative. [IDSA 2016]`,`Monitor **flucytosine** for marrow suppression (CBC twice weekly) and adjust dose to creatinine clearance; check **voriconazole trough on day 5**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Source control**: drain intra-abdominal abscesses, remove infected prostheses, and early valve surgery for Candida endocarditis. [IDSA 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **amphotericin B (liposomal) is the drug of choice**; avoid high-dose fluconazole (craniofacial defects in first trimester) and voriconazole; flucytosine is teratogenic in animals. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Neonates**: candidaemia in preterm infants - **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day** or fluconazole 12 mg/kg/day; always do LP and fundoscopy (high CNS spread). [IDSA 2016]`,`**CKD**: echinocandins, voriconazole oral and isavuconazole need **no renal dose change**; avoid IV voriconazole at CrCl under 50 (cyclodextrin vehicle accumulates); reduce fluconazole and flucytosine. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Liver disease**: reduce **caspofungin to 35 mg daily** in Child-Pugh B; voriconazole halves the maintenance dose in mild-moderate cirrhosis. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Drug interactions**: azoles inhibit **CYP3A4** - raise tacrolimus, cyclosporine, statins, warfarin and vincristine levels; **rifampicin cuts voriconazole levels by about 95%** (contraindicated). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where liposomal amphotericin or flucytosine is unaffordable, use **amphotericin deoxycholate with saline loading** and the oral fluconazole-flucytosine regimen; CrAg lateral-flow tests are cheap and need no laboratory. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Candidaemia mortality is 30-60%**; each day of delay in antifungal therapy raises mortality - early source control and echinocandin cut it. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`Candidaemia metastasises to **eye (up to 16%)**, heart valves, bone and kidney; hepatosplenic candidiasis presents as fever when neutrophils recover. [IDSA 2016]`,`Invasive aspergillosis carries **30-60% mortality** and up to 90% in cerebral disease; CPA has 5-year mortality around **40-50%** and causes life-threatening haemoptysis. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`Untreated cryptococcal meningitis is **almost always fatal**; with the WHO 2022 regimen **10-week mortality is about 25%**; blindness, deafness and hydrocephalus follow uncontrolled ICP. [WHO 2022]`,`**Cryptococcal IRIS** occurs in 10-30% after ART - hence the **4-6 week delay** before starting ART. [WHO 2022]`,`Drug toxicity: amphotericin - nephrotoxicity, **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia**, infusion reactions, anaemia; voriconazole - **visual disturbance (30%)**, hallucinations, photosensitivity, skin cancer, periostitis and hepatitis; flucytosine - neutropenia. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and the programme link`,points:[`**Refer** to a tertiary centre with mycology, ICU and TDM for C. auris, aspergillosis, cryptococcal meningitis needing repeated LPs, and Candida endocarditis. [ICMR AMRSN 2024]`,`**Follow-up**: CT chest every 2-4 weeks in aspergillosis; LFT and voriconazole levels; **visual acuity and fundus** in cryptococcal and Candida eye disease; CPA reviewed at 3 and 6 months with CT and Aspergillus IgG. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Antifungal prophylaxis**: **posaconazole delayed-release 300 mg daily** in AML/MDS induction and GVHD; fluconazole in allogeneic transplant; avoid blanket fluconazole in ICUs (selects C. auris and C. glabrata). [IDSA 2016]`,`**CrAg screening** of all PLHIV with **CD4 under 100 (consider under 200)** before ART is part of the WHO advanced HIV disease package and NACO care - see the Opportunistic infections in HIV topic. [WHO 2022]`,`**Infection prevention**: line-care bundles, chlorhexidine bathing, hand hygiene and **HEPA-filtered rooms** during construction near haematology wards. [IDSA 2016]`,`India's **ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance Network (AMRSN)** includes fungal surveillance; report C. auris clusters to the hospital infection control committee. [ICMR AMRSN 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ECMM/ISHAM/ASM global guideline on candidiasis (2025)** confirms **echinocandin first line** for candidaemia, adds **rezafungin 400 mg then 200 mg once weekly** as an option, and stresses early catheter removal and step-down at 5 days. [ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025]`,`**Rezafungin**, a once-weekly echinocandin, was approved by the FDA and EMA in **2023** (ReSTORE trial non-inferior to caspofungin). [FDA 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 cryptococcal guideline** replaced 7-day amphotericin regimens with **single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** plus 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List (2022)** - the first such list - names 19 fungi; critical priority: C. neoformans, C. auris, A. fumigatus, C. albicans. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020** revised galactomannan thresholds (serum or BAL 1.0) and added PCR as mycological evidence for probable aspergillosis. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**ISHAM 2024 revised ABPA criteria** use total IgE over 500 IU/mL and Aspergillus-specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more as obligatory criteria; managed under the Asthma topic. [ISHAM 2024]`,`Agents in late trials: **olorofim** (dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibitor, mould-active) and **fosmanogepix** (Gwt1 inhibitor) - not yet licensed in India. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Why not fluconazole for aspergillosis?'** - no mould activity; **'Why not an echinocandin for cryptococcus?'** - the capsule-bearing yeast lacks a beta-glucan-rich wall target. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: treating candidaemia without **removing the line, repeating cultures and doing fundoscopy** - examiners call these the three bundle steps. [IDSA 2016]`,`Common mistake: calling Candida in sputum or urine of a catheterised ICU patient an infection - it is usually **colonisation**; treat candiduria only if symptomatic, neutropenic, infant or before urological surgery. [IDSA 2016]`,`Know the **WHO 2022 crypto regimen** by heart: **10 mg/kg L-AmB once + flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day + fluconazole 1200 mg/day for 14 days, then 800 mg for 8 weeks, then 200 mg**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Galactomannan for Aspergillus, beta-D-glucan for Candida (and Pneumocystis), CrAg for Cryptococcus, urine antigen for Histoplasma** - and none of these for Mucorales. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Halo sign = early angioinvasive aspergillosis; air-crescent = recovery phase; reverse halo = think mucormycosis**. [IDSA 2016]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antifungal drugs: dose, duration and toxicity`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Fluconazole`,`Candidaemia 800 mg loading then 400 mg (6 mg/kg) PO/IV daily; crypto 1200 mg induction, 800 mg consolidation, 200 mg maintenance`,`Halve after loading if CrCl under 50; candidaemia 14 days after negative culture`,`QT prolongation, hepatitis, CYP3A4 interactions; no mould activity; C. krusei resistant; avoid high dose in pregnancy`],[`Caspofungin`,`70 mg IV loading then 50 mg IV daily`,`Step down to fluconazole after 5-7 days if stable and susceptible; 35 mg in Child-Pugh B`,`Infusion reaction, raised transaminases; poor CSF, eye and urine penetration; no activity against Cryptococcus or Mucorales`],[`Micafungin / anidulafungin`,`Micafungin 100 mg IV daily; anidulafungin 200 mg then 100 mg IV daily`,`As caspofungin; no renal adjustment`,`Hepatotoxicity (micafungin); same spectrum gaps as caspofungin`],[`Voriconazole`,`6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x 2 then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly; oral 200-300 mg 12-hourly`,`Trough 1-5.5 mg/L on day 5; at least 6-12 weeks in IA`,`Visual disturbance, hallucinations, photosensitivity, skin cancer, periostitis (fluoride), hepatitis, QT; avoid with rifampicin; IV vehicle accumulates if CrCl under 50`],[`Isavuconazole`,`200 mg IV/PO 8-hourly x 6 doses then 200 mg daily`,`No renal adjustment; TDM optional`,`Shortens QT (contraindicated in familial short QT), fewer visual and liver effects than voriconazole`],[`Itraconazole`,`200 mg PO twice daily (CPA, histoplasmosis maintenance); 200 mg thrice daily x 3 days loading in histoplasmosis`,`6 months in CPA; 12 months in disseminated histoplasmosis; trough over 0.5-1 mg/L`,`Negative inotrope - avoid in heart failure; hepatitis; erratic absorption (capsules with food and acid)`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`3-5 mg/kg/day IV (single 10 mg/kg for HIV crypto)`,`Histoplasmosis 2 weeks; IA salvage; neutropenic empirical`,`Less nephrotoxic than deoxycholate; hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions; safe in pregnancy`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`0.7-1 mg/kg/day IV over 4-6 h after 1 L saline`,`7 days in WHO alternative crypto regimen`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, rigors, anaemia, phlebitis - monitor creatinine and K twice weekly`],[`Flucytosine`,`100 mg/kg/day PO in 4 divided doses`,`14 days in cryptococcal induction; adjust to CrCl`,`Marrow suppression, hepatitis, diarrhoea; never use alone (rapid resistance); limited availability in India`]]},{heading:`Classification and organism-host matching`,columns:[`Organism`,`Typical host`,`Key syndrome`,`Diagnostic test of choice`],rows:[[`Candida spp.`,`ICU, central line, TPN, abdominal surgery, neutropenia`,`Candidaemia, intra-abdominal candidiasis, endophthalmitis`,`Blood culture with MALDI-TOF ID; beta-D-glucan 80 pg/mL or more`],[`Candida auris`,`ICU, long stay, ventilated, prior antifungals`,`Outbreak candidaemia, often fluconazole-resistant`,`MALDI-TOF (not VITEK-2 older databases); susceptibility testing`],[`Aspergillus fumigatus / flavus`,`Neutropenia over 10 days, HSCT, steroids; post-TB cavity (CPA)`,`Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis; CPA; aspergilloma`,`CT halo sign, galactomannan ODI 1.0 or more; Aspergillus IgG for CPA`],[`Cryptococcus neoformans`,`HIV with CD4 under 100, transplant, steroids`,`Subacute meningitis with raised ICP`,`CrAg lateral flow (serum, CSF); India ink; CSF culture`],[`Histoplasma capsulatum`,`HIV CD4 under 150; healthy adults in Gangetic plains`,`Disseminated disease, oral ulcers, adrenal masses`,`Urine antigen; marrow or adrenal biopsy (GMS)`],[`Talaromyces marneffei`,`Advanced HIV, Manipur / North-East`,`Fever, anaemia, umbilicated papules`,`Skin scraping or marrow - septate yeasts; culture (red pigment)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fungal look-alikes`,columns:[`Presentation`,`Fungal cause`,`Main mimic`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Persistent ICU fever on antibiotics`,`Candidaemia`,`MDR Gram-negative sepsis`,`Beta-D-glucan positive, Candida in blood culture; procalcitonin often lower`],[`Neutropenic fever with lung nodules`,`Invasive aspergillosis`,`Mucormycosis`,`Galactomannan positive; mucor - reverse halo, sinus disease, voriconazole breakthrough`],[`Cavity with haemoptysis after TB`,`Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`TB relapse`,`NAAT negative, Aspergillus IgG positive, fungal ball, pericavitary thickening`],[`Subacute meningitis in HIV`,`Cryptococcal meningitis`,`TB meningitis`,`CSF CrAg positive, opening pressure high, low cell count; TBM - high protein, basal exudate`],[`Fever, pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly`,`Disseminated histoplasmosis`,`Kala-azar, disseminated TB, lymphoma`,`Urine Histoplasma antigen, intracellular yeasts on marrow (GMS)`],[`Bilateral adrenal masses with Addisonian features`,`Adrenal histoplasmosis`,`Adrenal TB, metastases`,`Biopsy with fungal stain; low-attenuation enlarged glands`]]},{heading:`Fungal biomarkers and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Positive cut-off`,`Use`,`Pitfalls`],rows:[[`(1,3)-beta-D-glucan (Fungitell)`,`80 pg/mL or more (60-79 indeterminate)`,`Invasive candidiasis, aspergillosis, Pneumocystis; high NPV`,`False positive with albumin, IVIG, haemodialysis, gauze, Gram-negative sepsis; negative in Cryptococcus and Mucorales`],[`Galactomannan (serum)`,`ODI 1.0 or more single, or 0.7 with BAL 0.8 (EORTC/MSGERC 2020)`,`Invasive aspergillosis in neutropenia; twice-weekly screening`,`Low sensitivity on mould-active prophylaxis and in non-neutropenic hosts`],[`Galactomannan (BAL)`,`ODI 1.0 or more`,`Probable IA, including ICU and CAPA`,`Contamination from airway colonisation`],[`Cryptococcal antigen LFA`,`Any positive (titre guides prognosis)`,`Screening at CD4 under 100-200; diagnosis on CSF`,`Prozone at very high titres (rare); does not guide cure - stays positive`],[`CSF opening pressure`,`25 cm H2O or more raised`,`Decides therapeutic LP in crypto meningitis`,`Must be measured lying lateral, relaxed`],[`Histoplasma urine antigen`,`Kit-specific positive`,`Disseminated histoplasmosis (sensitivity about 90%)`,`Cross-reacts with Talaromyces, Blastomyces`],[`Aspergillus-specific IgG`,`Kit-specific (about 20-40 mg/L)`,`Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`Low in immunosuppressed; not for invasive disease`]]},{heading:`WHO 2022 cryptococcal meningitis regimens (HIV)`,columns:[`Phase`,`Preferred`,`Alternative`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Induction (2 weeks)`,`L-AmB 10 mg/kg single dose + flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day + fluconazole 1200 mg/day x 14 days`,`AmB deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day + flucytosine x 7 days, then fluconazole 1200 mg days 8-14; or fluconazole 1200 mg + flucytosine x 14 days`,`Therapeutic LP if opening pressure 25 cm H2O or more; saline loading and K/Mg`],[`Consolidation (8 weeks)`,`Fluconazole 800 mg/day`,`-`,`Children: 6-12 mg/kg/day (max 800 mg)`],[`Maintenance`,`Fluconazole 200 mg/day`,`-`,`Stop at CD4 200 or more, or 100-200 with suppressed VL, after at least 1 year`],[`ART timing`,`Start 4-6 weeks after antifungal start`,`-`,`Earlier ART raises mortality (COAT trial)`],[`Asymptomatic CrAg positive (CSF negative)`,`Fluconazole 800 mg x 2 weeks, 400 mg x 8 weeks, then 200 mg`,`Single L-AmB + 2 weeks fluconazole-flucytosine if available (ACACIA)`,`LP before treatment to exclude meningitis`]]}],redFlags:[`Candidaemia with persisting positive blood cultures beyond 72 hours, new murmur or eye findings (endocarditis, endophthalmitis)`,`Neutropenic fever over 96 hours with haemoptysis or lung nodules`,`HIV patient with headache, vomiting, visual blurring or confusion - suspect cryptococcal meningitis and do LP with opening pressure`,`CSF opening pressure 25 cm H2O or more, papilloedema or sixth nerve palsy`,`Massive haemoptysis (over 200-600 mL/24 h) from an aspergilloma`,`Postural hypotension, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia with adrenal masses (adrenal crisis from histoplasmosis)`,`Candida auris isolated anywhere - immediate contact isolation and infection-control alert`],pearls:[`**Candida bundle**: echinocandin early, pull the line, repeat cultures till negative, fundoscopy, 14 days after first negative culture.`,`**C. tropicalis** is India's commonest ICU candidaemia species; **C. auris** was first recognised in Indian hospitals.`,`**Echinocandins miss Cryptococcus and Mucorales**; fluconazole misses all moulds and C. krusei.`,`**WHO 2022 crypto: 10 mg/kg L-AmB once + 14 days 5FC + fluconazole 1200 mg**, then 800 mg 8 weeks, then 200 mg; ART at 4-6 weeks.`,`**No steroids, no acetazolamide for crypto ICP** - drain CSF by daily therapeutic LP.`,`Post-TB patient with haemoptysis and negative sputum NAAT - think **chronic pulmonary aspergillosis**, check Aspergillus IgG.`,`**Voriconazole** - TDM 1-5.5 mg/L, visual flashes, photosensitivity, never with rifampicin.`,`Biomarker match: **GM - Aspergillus, BDG - Candida/PCP, CrAg - Cryptococcus, urine Ag - Histoplasma**.`,`Bilateral adrenal masses in a healthy Bengali man: **adrenal histoplasmosis** until proven otherwise.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old man on day 9 of ICU stay after bowel surgery has a central line, TPN and is on meropenem. He develops new fever and hypotension. Blood culture grows yeast. What is the most appropriate initial antifungal?`,options:[`Oral fluconazole 200 mg daily`,`Intravenous caspofungin 70 mg loading then 50 mg daily`,`Intravenous voriconazole 6 mg/kg twice on day 1`,`Oral flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day`],answer:1,explanation:`Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin) are first line for candidaemia, especially in shock and in India where C. auris and C. glabrata resistance to azoles is common. Low-dose fluconazole is under-dosed and unsafe in a septic patient; voriconazole is a mould drug used for step-down only for C. krusei; flucytosine must never be used alone because resistance develops rapidly.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q2`,stem:`In a neutropenic patient with acute myeloid leukaemia, a CT chest shows a nodule surrounded by ground-glass attenuation. Which serum test best supports probable invasive aspergillosis by EORTC/MSGERC 2020 criteria?`,options:[`Cryptococcal antigen lateral flow assay`,`Histoplasma urine antigen`,`Galactomannan optical density index of 1.0 or more`,`Anti-Candida mannan antibody`],answer:2,explanation:`The halo sign in a neutropenic host plus a serum galactomannan ODI of 1.0 or more (or BAL 1.0) fulfils probable invasive aspergillosis under EORTC/MSGERC 2020. CrAg detects the cryptococcal capsule, urine antigen detects Histoplasma, and Candida mannan serology plays no part in mould diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q3`,stem:`A 32-year-old man with HIV (CD4 42 cells/mm3, not on ART) has 2 weeks of headache. CSF CrAg is positive and opening pressure is 34 cm H2O. Per WHO 2022, which induction regimen is preferred?`,options:[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate 0.7 mg/kg/day alone for 14 days with dexamethasone`,`Fluconazole 400 mg daily for 8 weeks and start ART the same week`,`Caspofungin 70 mg loading then 50 mg daily with acetazolamide`,`Single liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg with 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole 1200 mg/day`],answer:3,explanation:`WHO 2022, based on the AMBITION-cm trial, prefers single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg plus 14 days of flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day and fluconazole 1200 mg/day, with daily therapeutic LP for the raised pressure. Dexamethasone and acetazolamide cause harm, fluconazole 400 mg alone is inadequate induction, ART must be delayed 4-6 weeks, and echinocandins have no activity against Cryptococcus.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man treated for pulmonary TB 4 years ago presents with 5 months of cough, weight loss and recurrent haemoptysis. Sputum CBNAAT is negative. CT shows an upper-lobe cavity with an intracavitary mass and pleural thickening. The most useful next test is:`,options:[`Aspergillus-specific IgG`,`Serum galactomannan`,`Repeat Widal test`,`Serum (1,3)-beta-D-glucan`],answer:0,explanation:`Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis in a post-TB cavity is diagnosed by more than 3 months of symptoms, a cavity with fungal ball or pericavitary thickening, and a raised Aspergillus-specific IgG. Serum galactomannan and beta-D-glucan are insensitive in the immunocompetent host with chronic disease, and Widal has no role.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-q5`,stem:`A renal transplant recipient on tacrolimus is started on voriconazole for invasive aspergillosis. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Voriconazole should be combined with rifampicin to prevent TB reactivation`,`Voriconazole will lower tacrolimus levels, so the tacrolimus dose should be increased`,`Voriconazole will raise tacrolimus levels; reduce the tacrolimus dose and monitor troughs`,`Isavuconazole has no CYP3A4 interaction, so it can be swapped with no monitoring`],answer:2,explanation:`Voriconazole inhibits CYP3A4 and raises tacrolimus levels, so the tacrolimus dose is cut (often to about one-third) with close trough monitoring. Rifampicin induces CYP enzymes and lowers voriconazole levels by about 95%, so the pair is contraindicated. Isavuconazole is a milder CYP3A4 inhibitor but still raises tacrolimus and needs monitoring.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, clinical features, diagnosis and management of invasive candidiasis in the intensive care unit. Add a note on Candida auris.`,openingLines:[`Invasive candidiasis is infection of the bloodstream (candidaemia) or deep tissues by Candida species, most often in ICU patients with central lines, broad-spectrum antibiotics, TPN and abdominal surgery.`,`It carries 30-60% mortality; the ECMM/ISHAM/ASM 2025 guideline recommends an echinocandin first, early catheter removal, repeat cultures and fundoscopy, with step-down to fluconazole after 5-7 days.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and species`,points:[`Indian ICU incidence about 6.5 per 1000 admissions; **C. tropicalis** commonest; C. auris about 5% (Chakrabarti 2015).`,`Fourth commonest cause of hospital bloodstream infection worldwide.`]},{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Central venous catheter, broad-spectrum antibiotics, TPN, ICU stay over 7 days.`,`Abdominal surgery, anastomotic leak, necrotising pancreatitis, haemodialysis, neutropenia, steroids.`,`Multisite colonisation (Candida colonisation index over 0.5; Candida score 3 or more).`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fever not responding to antibiotics; septic shock; skin nodules.`,`Endophthalmitis (fluffy chorioretinal exudates), endocarditis, osteomyelitis; hepatosplenic candidiasis after neutrophil recovery.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Blood cultures (positive in about 50%); species by MALDI-TOF; antifungal susceptibility.`,`Serum beta-D-glucan 80 pg/mL or more; high NPV to stop empirical therapy.`,`Echocardiography if persistent candidaemia; fundoscopy within the first week.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`**Caspofungin 70 mg then 50 mg daily** (or micafungin 100 mg, anidulafungin 200/100 mg).`,`Remove all central lines; repeat blood cultures every 24-48 hours.`,`Step down to **fluconazole 400 mg daily** at 5-7 days if stable, susceptible, cultures negative.`,`Duration **14 days after first negative culture**; longer for metastatic foci.`,`Liposomal amphotericin 3-5 mg/kg/day if echinocandins fail or unavailable; source control of abscesses.`]},{heading:`Candida auris`,points:[`Multidrug-resistant, persists on surfaces, colonises skin; first recognised in Indian ICUs.`,`Echinocandin first line; contact isolation, chlorine-based disinfection, contact screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected candidaemia - cultures, echinocandin, line removal, fundoscopy, step-down, duration`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors and epidemiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Management with doses`,marks:4},{part:`Candida auris`,marks:1}],keywords:[`candidaemia`,`echinocandin`,`catheter removal`,`beta-D-glucan`,`Candida auris`,`fluconazole step-down`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fungal-infections-systemic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on cryptococcal meningitis in HIV, including CrAg screening and the WHO 2022 treatment regimen.`,openingLines:[`Cryptococcal meningitis is a subacute meningoencephalitis caused by Cryptococcus neoformans in people with advanced HIV (CD4 under 100), and causes about 15-19% of AIDS deaths.`,`WHO 2022 recommends CrAg screening before ART at CD4 under 100 and a single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B induction regimen.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`Headache over 1-3 weeks, fever, vomiting, visual loss; neck stiffness often absent.`,`LP with opening pressure; CSF CrAg (over 95% sensitive), India ink, culture.`]},{heading:`Treatment (WHO 2022)`,points:[`Induction: L-AmB 10 mg/kg once + flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day + fluconazole 1200 mg/day for 14 days.`,`Consolidation fluconazole 800 mg/day x 8 weeks; maintenance 200 mg/day until CD4 200 or more.`,`Therapeutic LP for opening pressure 25 cm H2O or more; no steroids or acetazolamide.`,`Start ART 4-6 weeks after antifungal therapy begins (COAT trial).`]},{heading:`CrAg screening`,points:[`Serum CrAg for all with CD4 under 100 (consider under 200) before ART.`,`Positive: LP; if CSF negative, pre-emptive fluconazole 800 mg x 2 weeks, 400 mg x 8 weeks, then 200 mg.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: induction, consolidation and maintenance phases with doses`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment regimen`,marks:2.5},{part:`CrAg screening`,marks:1}],keywords:[`cryptococcal meningitis`,`CrAg`,`AMBITION`,`liposomal amphotericin`,`flucytosine`,`therapeutic LP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on candidiasis, aspergillosis, cryptococcosis and histoplasmosis`,`Cornely OA et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of candidiasis: an initiative of ECMM, ISHAM and ASM, Lancet Infectious Diseases, 2025`,`Pappas PG et al. Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Candidiasis: IDSA, 2016`,`Patterson TF et al. Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Aspergillosis: IDSA, 2016; and Denning DW et al. ERS/ESCMID Chronic Pulmonary Aspergillosis guideline, 2016`,`Donnelly JP et al. Revision and update of the consensus definitions of invasive fungal disease (EORTC/MSGERC), Clinical Infectious Diseases, 2020`,`WHO Guidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV, 2022`,`WHO/PAHO Guidelines for diagnosing and managing disseminated histoplasmosis among people living with HIV, 2020; WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List, 2022`,`Chakrabarti A et al. Incidence, characteristics and outcome of ICU-acquired candidemia in India, Intensive Care Medicine, 2015`]}),n.push({id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis`,title:`Mucormycosis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Mucormycosis** is an acute, **angioinvasive infection by moulds of the order Mucorales** (Rhizopus, Mucor, Lichtheimia, Apophysomyces) that causes **thrombosis and black necrotic tissue**, chiefly **rhino-orbito-cerebral (ROCM)** disease in **uncontrolled diabetes, ketoacidosis and steroid use**; cure rests on three simultaneous steps - **early liposomal amphotericin B 5-10 mg/kg/day, urgent surgical debridement and reversal of the risk factor** - per the **ECMM/MSG ERC 2019** global guideline.`,keywords:[`black fungus`,`zygomycosis`,`Mucorales`,`Rhizopus arrhizus`,`Apophysomyces`,`ROCM`,`rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis`,`COVID-associated mucormycosis`,`CAM`,`black turbinate sign`,`reverse halo sign`,`liposomal amphotericin B`,`posaconazole`,`isavuconazole`,`debridement`,`orbital exenteration`,`TRAMB`,`GRP78`,`Honavar staging`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Mucormycosis** is invasive infection by fungi of the order **Mucorales** (class Mucoromycetes, formerly Zygomycetes), characterised by **angioinvasion, thrombosis and tissue infarction**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Global incidence is estimated at about **0.2 per 100,000** in developed countries; in India the pre-COVID estimated prevalence was about **14 per 100,000 - roughly 70-80 times higher**. [Prakash and Chakrabarti, J Fungi 2019]`,`During the **2021 COVID-19 second wave**, India reported an epidemic of **COVID-associated mucormycosis (CAM)** - **over 45,000 cases** reported to MoHFW by mid-2021 - and states were asked to make it **notifiable under the Epidemic Diseases Act**. [MoHFW 2021]`,`The **ICMR multicentre study** (Patel et al.) found CAM in 65% of 287 mucormycosis patients in 2020; **diabetes was present in about 74%** and **inappropriate glucocorticoid use in about 21%** of CAM cases. [Patel, Emerg Infect Dis 2021]`,`In India **ROCM forms about two-thirds** of cases, followed by pulmonary, cutaneous, renal (isolated renal mucormycosis is reported almost only from India) and gastrointestinal disease. [Prakash and Chakrabarti, J Fungi 2019]`,`**Rhizopus arrhizus** causes about 50-60% of Indian cases; **Apophysomyces variabilis** (cutaneous, trauma, immunocompetent) and **Rhizopus microsporus** are next; Mucor and Lichtheimia are less common. [Prakash and Chakrabarti, J Fungi 2019]`,`The **WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List 2022** places **Mucorales in the high-priority group**. [WHO FPPL 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus**, especially **diabetic ketoacidosis**, is the dominant risk in India (**over 70%**), unlike the West where haematological malignancy dominates. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Systemic corticosteroids** - high dose, long duration or started too early in COVID-19 - impair macrophage and neutrophil function and cause steroid-induced hyperglycaemia. [ICMR 2021]`,`**COVID-19 itself** contributes through endothelial damage, lymphopenia, hyperferritinaemia and raised free iron, plus tocilizumab, prolonged ICU stay and new-onset diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haematological malignancy, neutropenia, stem-cell and solid-organ transplant** remain key risks; mucormycosis can break through **voriconazole prophylaxis**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Iron overload and deferoxamine therapy** (e.g. dialysis patients) - Rhizopus uses deferoxamine as a xenosiderophore to take up iron. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trauma, burns, contaminated dressings and injections** cause cutaneous mucormycosis, often in immunocompetent people (Apophysomyces, Saksenaea). [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Other risks: **CKD, cirrhosis, malnutrition, IV drug use** (cerebral disease) and **neonatal prematurity** (gastrointestinal). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why it is so destructive`,points:[`Sporangiospores are inhaled into the **nose and paranasal sinuses** or lungs; healthy **neutrophils and macrophages** kill them, so disease needs impaired phagocytes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acidosis frees iron**: low pH dissociates iron from transferrin, and **free serum iron** is a growth factor for Rhizopus - the core reason DKA predisposes. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hyphae bind endothelial **GRP78** via the spore-coat protein **CotH**; **hyperglycaemia, iron and ketone bodies up-regulate GRP78 and CotH**, so invasion is faster in DKA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Angioinvasion** causes vessel thrombosis and **infarction** - hence the **black eschar**, painless necrosis and poor drug delivery to dead tissue, which is why **surgery is essential**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Spread is **contiguous and perineural/perivascular**: nasal mucosa to sinuses, then orbit via the lamina papyracea and nasolacrimal duct, then **orbital apex, cavernous sinus and brain**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mucorales are **intrinsically resistant to voriconazole, fluconazole and echinocandins**; only **amphotericin B, posaconazole and isavuconazole** are reliably active. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Mucorales cell walls lack galactomannan in detectable amounts and contain little beta-D-glucan - so **galactomannan and beta-D-glucan tests are negative**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Classification and ROCM staging`,points:[`**Clinical forms**: rhino-orbito-cerebral (ROCM), pulmonary, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, renal and **disseminated** (two or more non-contiguous sites). [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Honavar staging (2021) for ROCM**: **Stage 1 - nasal mucosa**; **Stage 2 - paranasal sinuses**; **Stage 3 - orbit**; **Stage 4 - CNS**; each has sub-stages a-d by extent. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`Stage 1a limited to the middle turbinate, 1b involving the inferior turbinate or nasolacrimal duct ostium, 1c the nasal septum, 1d bilateral nasal mucosa. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`Stage 2a one sinus, 2b two ipsilateral sinuses, 2c more than two ipsilateral sinuses and/or **palate or oral cavity**, 2d bilateral sinuses or zygoma and masticator space. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`Stage 3a nasolacrimal duct or medial orbit with vision intact, 3b diffuse orbital involvement, **3c central retinal artery occlusion, orbital apex or superior orbital fissure syndrome with vision loss**, 3d bilateral orbit. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`Stage 4a focal cavernous sinus or cribriform plate, 4b diffuse cavernous sinus thrombosis, 4c beyond the cavernous sinus - skull base, **internal carotid occlusion, brain infarction**, 4d multifocal or diffuse CNS disease. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`**Diagnostic certainty (EORTC/MSGERC 2020)**: proven needs histology showing tissue invasion by broad hyphae or culture from a sterile site; probable needs host, clinical-radiological and mycological criteria. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Typical patient: an Indian adult with **uncontrolled diabetes**, often on steroids for COVID-19, presenting **2-3 weeks** after the illness (median about 14-18 days). [Patel, Emerg Infect Dis 2021]`,`Early ROCM: **unilateral facial pain or numbness**, nasal stuffiness, **blood-stained or black nasal discharge**, and headache. [ICMR 2021]`,`Orbital spread: **periorbital swelling, drooping eyelid, double vision**, eye pain and **sudden loss of vision**. [ICMR 2021]`,`Oral involvement: **toothache, loosening of teeth** and a black area on the palate. [ICMR 2021]`,`CNS spread: **altered sensorium, hemiparesis, seizures** and cranial nerve palsies from cavernous sinus thrombosis or carotid occlusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary mucormycosis**: fever, cough, pleuritic chest pain and **haemoptysis** (may be massive) in a neutropenic or diabetic patient on voriconazole. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Cutaneous**: a wound that turns **black and spreads rapidly** despite antibiotics after trauma, burns or injection. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Nasal endoscopy** shows pale (ischaemic) then **black necrotic turbinate or septal mucosa** that does not bleed when touched - the earliest sign. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Palatal eschar** (black ulcer on the hard palate) indicates stage 2c - the hard palate is supplied by vessels that thrombose. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`Facial signs: **periorbital and cheek swelling**, skin discoloration, and loss of sensation over V2 (maxillary nerve). [Harrison 22e]`,`Orbital apex syndrome: **proptosis, ophthalmoplegia (III, IV, VI), ptosis, loss of vision and V1 sensory loss**; a fixed dilated pupil with no perception of light suggests central retinal artery occlusion. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`**Bilateral signs** or contralateral cranial nerve palsy suggest cavernous sinus thrombosis (stage 4b). [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`Look for **DKA signs** - Kussmaul breathing, dehydration, ketotic breath - and **check capillary glucose and ketones** at the bedside. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Invasive aspergillosis of the sinuses**: similar picture but slower, galactomannan positive, narrow septate hyphae branching at 45 degrees, and responds to voriconazole. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Bacterial orbital cellulitis**: fever, painful proptosis and sinusitis but **no black necrosis**, and response to IV antibiotics within 48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cavernous sinus thrombosis from bacteria** (S. aureus, facial infection): high fever, bilateral chemosis; MRI and endoscopy separate it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, NK/T-cell lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma** cause destructive midline lesions - biopsy with fungal stain and immunohistochemistry decides. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary mucormycosis versus aspergillosis**: suspect mucor with **reverse halo sign, over 10 nodules, pleural effusion, sinusitis** and breakthrough on voriconazole. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Ecthyma gangrenosum** (Pseudomonas) can cause black skin lesions in neutropenia - culture and biopsy distinguish. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Direct microscopy (KOH with calcofluor white)** of nasal scraping, endoscopic biopsy or debrided tissue: **broad (6-25 micrometres), ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae with wide-angle (up to 90 degree) branching** - a same-day diagnosis. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Histopathology** (H&E, PAS, GMS) shows **tissue and vessel invasion** and infarction - needed for proven disease; tissue (not swab) is the right specimen. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Culture** on Sabouraud dextrose agar (without cycloheximide) grows fluffy 'lid-lifter' colonies in 24-48 hours, but is positive in **only about 50%** because grinding destroys aseptate hyphae - mince, do not homogenise. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Contrast MRI of paranasal sinuses, orbit and brain** is the imaging of choice; the **'black turbinate' sign** (non-enhancing devitalised mucosa) is an early finding; MRI defines orbital apex, cavernous sinus and brain extent. [ICMR 2021]`,`**CT of sinuses** shows mucosal thickening and bone erosion; **HRCT chest** may show the **reverse halo sign**, multiple nodules, cavitation and effusion. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Galactomannan and beta-D-glucan are negative** - a positive result suggests aspergillosis or co-infection; **Mucorales PCR** on blood or tissue is used in specialist laboratories. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Baseline workup: **blood glucose, HbA1c, ketones, ABG, electrolytes (K, Mg), creatinine, CBC, ferritin and HIV test** - before and during amphotericin. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Mucormycosis is **never an OPD-managed disease** - the family physician's job is **early suspicion and same-day referral** to a centre with ENT, ophthalmology, neurosurgery, mycology and amphotericin. [ICMR 2021]`,`Suspect ROCM in any diabetic or post-COVID patient with **unilateral facial pain, nasal black discharge, toothache, eyelid swelling or diplopia** - follow the ICMR 'do not miss warning signs' advisory. [ICMR 2021]`,`Before transfer: **check capillary glucose and ketones**, start IV fluids and insulin if in DKA, and **stop or taper non-essential steroids**. [ICMR 2021]`,`Do not start **empirical voriconazole** (inactive, and selects Mucorales) and do not give antibiotics as a substitute for urgent referral. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**After discharge**: supervise oral **posaconazole or isavuconazole** step-down, glycaemic control (HbA1c target under 7%), dental and prosthetic rehabilitation, and psychological support after facial or orbital surgery. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - the three pillars`,points:[`**First-line antifungal**: **liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day IV**, increased to **10 mg/kg/day for CNS involvement**; start on suspicion, **without waiting for culture**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Alternatives: **amphotericin B lipid complex 5 mg/kg/day**; **isavuconazole 200 mg 8-hourly x 6 doses then 200 mg daily** or **posaconazole IV/delayed-release tablet 300 mg 12-hourly on day 1 then 300 mg daily** as first-line options when amphotericin is contraindicated. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Amphotericin B deoxycholate 1-1.5 mg/kg/day** is used only when lipid formulations are unavailable (as during the 2021 shortage); it is strongly discouraged by ECMM/MSG because of nephrotoxicity. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Surgery: early and repeated debridement** of all necrotic tissue - endoscopic sinus surgery, maxillectomy; **orbital exenteration** for a blind, frozen orbit or progressive orbital disease. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Reverse the risk factor**: correct DKA with insulin infusion, **target glucose 140-180 mg/dL**, taper steroids, stop deferoxamine, and recover neutrophils (G-CSF where neutropenic). [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Transcutaneous retrobulbar amphotericin B (TRAMB)** - liposomal amphotericin 3.5 mg in 1 mL injected retrobulbar for 3-5 doses - was used in Indian centres for stage 3a-3b orbital disease to save the eye; evidence is observational only. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`**Step-down** to oral **posaconazole delayed-release 300 mg daily** or **isavuconazole 200 mg daily** once stable, imaging improving and no further debridement needed, usually after **3-6 weeks** of IV therapy. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Total duration**: until clinical and radiological resolution and reversal of immunosuppression - **typically 3-6 months**; posaconazole trough target **over 1 mg/L**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Monitoring and supportive care in hospital`,points:[`**Amphotericin monitoring**: creatinine, potassium and magnesium **daily for the first week then twice weekly**; preload **500-1000 mL normal saline** before each dose; supplement K and Mg routinely. [ICMR 2021]`,`A **creatinine rise of over 2-fold** on liposomal amphotericin calls for hydration review and dose reduction or a switch to isavuconazole, not abrupt cessation. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Repeat MRI** at 2-4 weeks or on clinical worsening; **repeat nasal endoscopy** with debridement of fresh necrosis. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Combination therapy** (amphotericin plus posaconazole or isavuconazole) is used as salvage in refractory disease but is not proven superior. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Iron chelation with deferasirox** is **not recommended** - the DEFEAT Mucor trial showed higher mortality. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Hyperbaric oxygen** is an optional adjunct in some centres; evidence is weak. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **liposomal amphotericin** is preferred; posaconazole and isavuconazole are avoided (teratogenic in animals). [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**CKD and transplant**: liposomal amphotericin needs no dose change but monitor creatinine; isavuconazole is the least nephrotoxic option; **azoles raise tacrolimus and cyclosporine levels**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Children**: same drugs - liposomal amphotericin 5-10 mg/kg/day; neonatal **gastrointestinal mucormycosis** mimics necrotising enterocolitis. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Haematological malignancy**: **posaconazole prophylaxis** in AML/MDS induction and GVHD reduces mould infections; breakthrough on voriconazole should prompt a search for Mucorales. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where liposomal amphotericin is unaffordable, conventional amphotericin with saline loading, early surgery and strict glycaemic control still save lives; government schemes (PM-JAY) covered CAM treatment in 2021. [ICMR 2021]`,`**COVID-19 or other illness needing steroids**: use **dexamethasone 6 mg/day for up to 10 days only in hypoxic patients** - steroid stewardship is the main preventive lever. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Overall mortality 40-80%** depending on site and host: ROCM about **30-50%**, pulmonary about **50-70%**, disseminated **over 90%**. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Indian CAM cohorts report mortality around **30-45%**; mortality rises steeply with **CNS involvement (stage 4)** and delayed surgery. [Patel, Emerg Infect Dis 2021]`,`**Each 6-day delay** in starting amphotericin roughly doubles mortality (from about 35% to over 80% in a key cohort). [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Sequelae: **blindness, orbital exenteration, maxillectomy defects**, facial disfigurement, cranial nerve palsies, stroke and depression. [ICMR 2021]`,`Complications of therapy: **amphotericin nephrotoxicity and hypokalaemia**; posaconazole hepatotoxicity and QT prolongation. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Relapse** occurs if therapy stops before immune recovery or residual necrotic tissue remains. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Refer immediately** any suspected case - mucormycosis is a **surgical-medical emergency**; transfer with glucose control started. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Follow-up**: monthly visits on step-down azole with **LFT, creatinine, posaconazole levels**, glycaemic review, nasal endoscopy and MRI at 1 and 3 months or as advised. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**Prevention**: tight **glycaemic control** in all diabetics and during steroid therapy (monitor glucose 4 times daily while on steroids), **judicious steroid and tocilizumab use**, and early diabetes detection in COVID-19. [ICMR 2021]`,`Hospital measures: clean **humidifier water** (sterile or boiled-cooled water), HEPA filtration in transplant units, and prompt wound care. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Counselling**: explain that it is **not contagious**, that 'black, white, yellow fungus' labels are media terms, and that the long course and surgery are needed for cure. [MoHFW 2021]`,`Link to **NP-NCD**: opportunistic screening for diabetes at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs detects the commonest risk factor early. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`The **ECMM/MSG ERC global guideline (2019)** remains the reference: strong recommendation for **early complete surgery** and **liposomal amphotericin 5-10 mg/kg/day** first line; isavuconazole and posaconazole (delayed-release tablet or IV) as alternatives and step-down. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`**ICMR/MoHFW evidence-based advisories (2021)** on screening, diagnosis and management of CAM and steroid stewardship guide Indian practice. [ICMR 2021]`,`The **Honavar ROCM staging (2021)** from the Collaborative OPAI-IJO Study on Mucormycosis in COVID-19 (COSMIC) is now widely used in India. [Honavar, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021]`,`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020** definitions apply to mucormycosis; Mucorales PCR is gaining acceptance as mycological evidence. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**WHO FPPL 2022** lists Mucorales as high priority, driving surveillance and drug development. [WHO FPPL 2022]`,`New agents: **fosmanogepix** shows activity against Mucorales in trials; **olorofim has no activity** against Mucorales. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Why does DKA predispose to mucormycosis?'** - acidosis frees iron from transferrin, hyperglycaemia up-regulates GRP78 and CotH, and phagocytes fail. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Three pillars**: antifungal (L-AmB), surgery (debridement), and reversal of risk (glucose, steroids, neutropenia) - examiners expect all three. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Common mistake: prescribing **voriconazole** for a sinus mould in a diabetic - it is inactive against Mucorales. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Common mistake: sending a **swab or galactomannan** - send tissue for KOH, histology and culture; galactomannan is negative. [ECMM/MSG 2019]`,`Microscopy viva: **mucor - broad aseptate ribbon-like hyphae, right-angle branching; aspergillus - narrow septate hyphae, acute-angle (45 degree) dichotomous branching**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Imaging viva: **black turbinate sign** on MRI (sinonasal) and **reverse halo sign** on CT chest (pulmonary). [ECMM/MSG 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for mucormycosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Liposomal amphotericin B (first line)`,`5 mg/kg/day IV; 10 mg/kg/day if CNS involved`,`Usually 3-6 weeks IV until stable, then oral step-down`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions; preload saline; preferred in pregnancy`],[`Amphotericin B lipid complex`,`5 mg/kg/day IV`,`As above`,`Similar to liposomal; avoid in CNS disease where liposomal is preferred`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`1-1.5 mg/kg/day IV over 4-6 h`,`Only if lipid forms unavailable`,`Marked nephrotoxicity, rigors, hypokalaemia, anaemia; discouraged by ECMM/MSG`],[`Isavuconazole`,`200 mg IV/PO 8-hourly x 6 doses, then 200 mg daily`,`First-line alternative or step-down; months`,`Shortens QT (avoid in familial short QT); fewer hepatic and visual effects; CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Posaconazole (delayed-release tablet or IV)`,`300 mg 12-hourly day 1, then 300 mg daily`,`Step-down; trough over 1 mg/L`,`Hepatotoxicity, QT prolongation, hypokalaemia, raises tacrolimus; avoid in pregnancy; suspension poorly absorbed`],[`Insulin (risk reversal)`,`IV infusion in DKA, then basal-bolus`,`Glucose 140-180 mg/dL in hospital`,`Hypoglycaemia, hypokalaemia - monitor K especially with amphotericin`]]},{heading:`Honavar staging of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis (2021)`,columns:[`Stage`,`Site`,`Sub-stages (a-d)`,`Usual management`],rows:[[`1`,`Nasal mucosa`,`a middle turbinate; b inferior turbinate or NLD ostium; c septum; d bilateral`,`L-AmB + endoscopic debridement`],[`2`,`Paranasal sinuses`,`a one sinus; b two ipsilateral; c more than two and/or palate/oral cavity; d bilateral or zygoma/masticator space`,`L-AmB + FESS / maxillectomy for palate`],[`3`,`Orbit`,`a NLD or medial orbit, vision intact; b diffuse orbital; c CRAO, apex or SOF syndrome, vision loss; d bilateral`,`3a-3b: debridement +/- TRAMB; 3c-3d: consider exenteration`],[`4`,`CNS`,`a focal cavernous sinus or cribriform plate; b diffuse cavernous sinus thrombosis; c skull base, ICA occlusion, infarction; d multifocal/diffuse CNS`,`L-AmB 10 mg/kg/day; neurosurgical opinion; poor prognosis`]]},{heading:`Mucormycosis versus look-alikes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mucormycosis`,`Invasive aspergillosis`,`Bacterial orbital cellulitis`],rows:[[`Host`,`DKA, steroids, COVID, neutropenia`,`Neutropenia, transplant, steroids`,`Any age, sinusitis, children`],[`Tissue`,`Black necrosis, palatal eschar`,`Necrosis less striking`,`Red, swollen, no necrosis`],[`Microscopy`,`Broad aseptate ribbons, wide-angle branching`,`Narrow septate hyphae, 45 degree branching`,`No hyphae`],[`Biomarkers`,`Galactomannan and BDG negative`,`Galactomannan positive`,`Raised CRP, procalcitonin`],[`Imaging`,`Black turbinate (MRI); reverse halo (chest)`,`Halo sign, air-crescent`,`Subperiosteal abscess, fat stranding`],[`Drug of choice`,`Liposomal amphotericin; posaconazole/isavuconazole`,`Voriconazole or isavuconazole`,`IV ceftriaxone + clindamycin or similar`],[`Voriconazole`,`Inactive - may select Mucorales`,`First line`,`Not relevant`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding in mucormycosis`,`Practical point`],rows:[[`KOH / calcofluor of tissue`,`Broad (6-25 micrometres) pauci-septate hyphae, right-angle branching`,`Same-day; send tissue, not swab`],[`Histopathology (H&E, PAS, GMS)`,`Angioinvasion, infarction, broad hyphae`,`Needed for proven diagnosis`],[`Culture (SDA, no cycloheximide)`,`Fluffy rapid-growing colonies in 24-48 h`,`Positive in only about 50%; mince tissue, do not grind`],[`Contrast MRI sinus-orbit-brain`,`Black turbinate sign; orbital apex, cavernous sinus extent`,`Imaging of choice; repeat at 2-4 weeks`],[`CT chest`,`Reverse halo sign, over 10 nodules, effusion`,`Points to mucor over aspergillus`],[`Galactomannan / beta-D-glucan`,`Negative`,`Positive suggests aspergillosis or co-infection`],[`Glucose, ketones, ABG, HbA1c`,`Hyperglycaemia, DKA`,`Correct urgently - core of therapy`],[`Creatinine, K, Mg`,`Baseline before amphotericin`,`Daily in week 1, then twice weekly`]]}],redFlags:[`Diabetic or post-COVID patient with unilateral facial pain, numbness or swelling`,`Black or blood-stained nasal discharge, black nasal or palatal eschar`,`Eyelid swelling, proptosis, diplopia, ptosis or sudden vision loss`,`Toothache or loosening of teeth with sinus symptoms in uncontrolled diabetes`,`Altered sensorium, hemiparesis or cranial nerve palsies (cavernous sinus or brain spread)`,`Haemoptysis with lung nodules in a neutropenic or diabetic patient on voriconazole`,`Rapidly spreading black necrosis of a wound after trauma or injection`],pearls:[`**Three pillars**: liposomal amphotericin, aggressive surgery, reverse the risk (DKA, steroids, neutropenia).`,`**Acidosis frees iron** and up-regulates **GRP78/CotH** - why DKA and Rhizopus go together.`,`Mucor hyphae: **broad, ribbon-like, aseptate, right-angle branching**; Aspergillus: narrow, septate, 45 degrees.`,`**Voriconazole and echinocandins do not work**; only amphotericin, posaconazole, isavuconazole.`,`**Galactomannan and BDG are negative** in mucormycosis.`,`MRI: **black turbinate sign**; CT chest: **reverse halo sign**.`,`**Deferoxamine** feeds Rhizopus; deferasirox adjunct increased mortality (DEFEAT Mucor).`,`Honavar staging: **1 nose, 2 sinus, 3 orbit, 4 brain** - each with a-d.`,`India has the world's highest burden - about **70-80 times** the global rate.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man with uncontrolled diabetes, treated with dexamethasone for COVID-19 three weeks ago, has left facial pain, black nasal crusts and periorbital swelling. Nasal biopsy shows broad aseptate hyphae with right-angle branching. Which is the most appropriate antifungal to start?`,options:[`Voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly`,`Caspofungin 70 mg IV loading then 50 mg daily`,`Fluconazole 800 mg IV daily`,`Liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day IV`],answer:3,explanation:`Broad aseptate hyphae with wide-angle branching in a diabetic post-steroid patient mean mucormycosis; ECMM/MSG 2019 recommends liposomal amphotericin B 5-10 mg/kg/day first line with urgent debridement. Voriconazole, caspofungin and fluconazole have no reliable activity against Mucorales, and voriconazole may even select them.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-q2`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains why diabetic ketoacidosis predisposes to mucormycosis?`,options:[`Acidosis releases iron from transferrin and hyperglycaemia up-regulates endothelial GRP78`,`Ketones directly stimulate fungal ergosterol synthesis`,`Insulin deficiency increases galactomannan production by the fungus`,`Hyperosmolality enhances T-cell recognition of fungal antigens`],answer:0,explanation:`Low pH dissociates iron from transferrin, and free iron drives Rhizopus growth; hyperglycaemia, iron and ketones also up-regulate endothelial GRP78 and fungal CotH, allowing angioinvasion, while neutrophil function falls. Ergosterol synthesis and galactomannan are unrelated to DKA, and hyperosmolality impairs rather than enhances immunity.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-q3`,stem:`In the Honavar staging of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis, involvement of more than two ipsilateral paranasal sinuses with a black eschar on the hard palate is staged as:`,options:[`Stage 1d`,`Stage 2c`,`Stage 3a`,`Stage 4a`],answer:1,explanation:`Stage 2 is paranasal sinus disease; 2c is more than two ipsilateral sinuses and/or palate or oral cavity involvement. Stage 1d is bilateral nasal mucosal disease only, stage 3a is limited orbital disease with intact vision, and stage 4a is focal cavernous sinus or cribriform plate involvement.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-q4`,stem:`A patient with ROCM has received liposomal amphotericin for 4 weeks after two debridements. He is clinically stable, MRI shows regression and glucose is controlled. The best next step is:`,options:[`Stop all antifungals and review in 3 months`,`Switch to oral voriconazole 200 mg twice daily`,`Step down to oral posaconazole delayed-release 300 mg daily or isavuconazole 200 mg daily`,`Add deferasirox to chelate iron`],answer:2,explanation:`ECMM/MSG 2019 recommends oral step-down to posaconazole delayed-release tablets or isavuconazole after a stable response, continued until clinical and radiological resolution and immune recovery, usually for months in total. Stopping at 4 weeks risks relapse, voriconazole is inactive against Mucorales, and deferasirox increased mortality in the DEFEAT Mucor trial.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-q5`,stem:`A neutropenic AML patient on voriconazole prophylaxis develops fever and haemoptysis. CT chest shows a ground-glass area surrounded by a ring of consolidation and a small effusion. Serum galactomannan is negative. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Invasive aspergillosis breaking through prophylaxis`,`Pulmonary mucormycosis`,`Pneumocystis pneumonia`,`Candida pneumonia`],answer:1,explanation:`The reverse halo sign (central ground glass with a consolidation rim), pleural effusion, negative galactomannan and breakthrough on voriconazole all point to pulmonary mucormycosis. Aspergillosis would usually be galactomannan positive and voriconazole-sensitive, Pneumocystis gives diffuse ground glass, and Candida pneumonia is exceedingly rare.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, pathogenesis, clinical staging, diagnosis and management of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis. Add a note on its prevention in India.`,openingLines:[`Mucormycosis is an acute angioinvasive infection by moulds of the order Mucorales, causing thrombosis and tissue necrosis, most often rhino-orbito-cerebral disease in uncontrolled diabetes and steroid use.`,`India has about 70-80 times the global burden and saw an epidemic of COVID-associated mucormycosis in 2021; treatment rests on liposomal amphotericin, surgical debridement and reversal of risk factors (ECMM/MSG 2019).`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Uncontrolled diabetes and DKA (over 70% in India); corticosteroids; COVID-19.`,`Haematological malignancy, transplant, neutropenia; deferoxamine; trauma and burns.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Inhaled spores - impaired phagocytes - germination in nose and sinuses.`,`Acidosis frees iron; hyperglycaemia up-regulates GRP78 and CotH - angioinvasion, thrombosis, infarction.`,`Spread to orbit, cavernous sinus and brain; necrotic tissue blocks drug delivery.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and staging`,points:[`Facial pain, black nasal discharge, palatal eschar, periorbital swelling, diplopia, vision loss, altered sensorium.`,`Honavar 2021: stage 1 nose, 2 sinuses, 3 orbit, 4 CNS, with sub-stages a-d.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Tissue KOH/calcofluor: broad aseptate hyphae, right-angle branching; histopathology for angioinvasion; culture.`,`Contrast MRI sinus-orbit-brain (black turbinate sign); galactomannan negative.`,`Glucose, ketones, ABG, creatinine, K, Mg.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day (10 mg/kg/day for CNS).`,`Early and repeated surgical debridement; exenteration for advanced orbital disease; TRAMB for selected stage 3.`,`Control glucose, correct DKA, taper steroids.`,`Step down to posaconazole 300 mg daily or isavuconazole 200 mg daily; total 3-6 months.`,`Monitor creatinine, potassium, magnesium; avoid deferasirox.`]},{heading:`Prevention in India`,points:[`Glycaemic control and monitoring during steroids; steroid stewardship in COVID-19.`,`Early diabetes screening (NP-NCD), clean humidifier water, awareness of warning signs, early referral.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of spread: nose - sinuses - orbit - cavernous sinus - brain with Honavar stages`,`Flow chart of the three pillars of management`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and staging`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Mucorales`,`ROCM`,`DKA`,`GRP78`,`liposomal amphotericin`,`debridement`,`posaconazole`,`isavuconazole`,`Honavar staging`]},{id:`infectious-fever-mucormycosis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between mucormycosis and invasive aspergillosis.`,openingLines:[`Both are angioinvasive mould infections of immunocompromised hosts, but they differ in host, microscopy, biomarkers, imaging and drug of choice.`,`Distinguishing them matters because voriconazole, first line for aspergillosis, is inactive against Mucorales.`],answer:[{heading:`Host and site`,points:[`Mucor: DKA, steroids, COVID; rhino-orbito-cerebral commonest in India.`,`Aspergillus: prolonged neutropenia, transplant; pulmonary commonest.`]},{heading:`Laboratory`,points:[`Mucor: broad aseptate ribbon-like hyphae, right-angle branching; galactomannan and BDG negative.`,`Aspergillus: narrow septate hyphae, acute-angle branching; galactomannan and BDG positive.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`Mucor: reverse halo, over 10 nodules, effusion, black turbinate on MRI.`,`Aspergillus: halo sign, air-crescent sign.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Mucor: liposomal amphotericin 5-10 mg/kg/day, surgery, posaconazole/isavuconazole step-down.`,`Aspergillus: voriconazole or isavuconazole; surgery rarely needed.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Host and site`,marks:1},{part:`Microscopy and biomarkers`,marks:2},{part:`Imaging`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`mucormycosis`,`aspergillosis`,`aseptate hyphae`,`galactomannan`,`reverse halo`,`voriconazole`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on mucormycosis`,`Cornely OA et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of mucormycosis: an initiative of the ECMM in cooperation with the Mycoses Study Group Education and Research Consortium, Lancet Infectious Diseases, 2019`,`ICMR / MoHFW Evidence-based advisory on screening, diagnosis and management of mucormycosis in the era of COVID-19, 2021`,`Honavar SG. Code Mucor: guidelines for the diagnosis, staging and management of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis in the setting of COVID-19, Indian Journal of Ophthalmology, 2021`,`Patel A et al. Multicenter epidemiologic study of coronavirus disease-associated mucormycosis, India, Emerging Infectious Diseases, 2021`,`Prakash H, Chakrabarti A. Global epidemiology of mucormycosis, Journal of Fungi, 2019`,`Donnelly JP et al. EORTC/MSGERC consensus definitions of invasive fungal disease, Clinical Infectious Diseases, 2020`]}),n.push({id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv`,title:`Opportunistic infections in HIV: prophylaxis and treatment`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Opportunistic infections (OIs)** are infections that cause disease, or unusually severe disease, because of **HIV-induced CD4 T-cell depletion**; each appears below a predictable **CD4 threshold** (TB at any count, **PCP under 200, toxoplasma and cryptococcus under 100, CMV and MAC under 50**), and they are prevented by **cotrimoxazole, TB preventive therapy, CrAg screening and early ART** - started within 2 weeks of most OIs but **delayed 4-6 weeks in cryptococcal and TB meningitis** to avoid fatal IRIS (NACO 2021, WHO 2021-2022).`,keywords:[`OI`,`advanced HIV disease`,`AHD package`,`CD4 count`,`cotrimoxazole prophylaxis`,`CPT`,`Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia`,`PCP`,`cerebral toxoplasmosis`,`cryptococcal meningitis`,`CMV retinitis`,`TB-HIV`,`TB-LAM`,`TB preventive treatment`,`3HP`,`IRIS`,`MAC`,`PML`,`oesophageal candidiasis`,`NACO 2021`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`An **opportunistic infection** is caused by an organism that rarely causes disease in an intact host but does so when **CD4 T cells fall**; AIDS-defining OIs mark **WHO clinical stage 4**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Advanced HIV disease (AHD)** is **CD4 under 200 cells/mm3 or WHO stage 3 or 4** in adults and adolescents, and **all children under 5 years** with HIV - this group has the highest OI death risk. [WHO 2021]`,`India has about **25 lakh people living with HIV** (adult prevalence about **0.2%**), the second or third largest number in the world; about **one-third present late with CD4 under 200**. [NACO India HIV Estimates 2023]`,`**Tuberculosis is the commonest OI and the leading cause of death in PLHIV** - globally about 1 in 3 AIDS deaths; India accounts for a large share of global HIV-associated TB. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`Indian OI spectrum (NACO cohorts): **TB (about 50-70% of OIs)**, oral and oesophageal candidiasis, PCP, cryptococcal meningitis, toxoplasmosis, CMV retinitis, chronic diarrhoea (Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora) and herpes zoster. [NACO 2021]`,`Since **Treat All (2017)** and dolutegravir-based ART (2020), OI incidence has fallen, but late presenters and treatment failures still drive OI admissions. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and the CD4 ladder`,points:[`**Any CD4 count**: TB, bacterial pneumonia (S. pneumoniae), herpes zoster, oral candidiasis, Kaposi sarcoma, lymphoma - risk rises as CD4 falls. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**CD4 under 200**: **Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)**, oesophageal candidiasis, histoplasmosis (under 150), and talaromycosis in the North-East (under 100). [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**CD4 under 100**: **cerebral toxoplasmosis** (if Toxoplasma IgG positive), **cryptococcal meningitis**, cryptosporidiosis, microsporidiosis and **PML** (JC virus). [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**CD4 under 50**: **CMV retinitis and colitis**, **disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC)**, primary CNS lymphoma (EBV). [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Cystoisospora belli** (chronic watery diarrhoea) is common in India and responds to cotrimoxazole - which also prevents it. [NACO 2021]`,`Other contributors: **malnutrition, IV drug use** (Manipur, Mizoram, Punjab), **hepatitis B and C co-infection** and **unrecognised ART failure**. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the CD4 count predicts the OI`,points:[`HIV binds **CD4 and CCR5/CXCR4**, destroys CD4 T cells (especially gut **memory Th17 cells** early), and causes chronic immune activation and **microbial translocation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CD4 T cells orchestrate macrophage activation (IFN-gamma)** against intracellular pathogens - Mycobacteria, Toxoplasma, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma - so these reactivate as CD4 falls. [Harrison 22e]`,`Most OIs are **reactivation of latent infection** (TB, toxoplasma tissue cysts, CMV, JC virus, VZV) - which is why **serology (Toxoplasma IgG) and TB screening** predict risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cotrimoxazole** blocks sequential steps of folate synthesis (sulfamethoxazole - dihydropteroate synthase; trimethoprim - dihydrofolate reductase), covering **Pneumocystis, Toxoplasma, Cystoisospora, many bacteria and malaria**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`In PCP, the **inflammatory response to dying organisms** worsens hypoxia in the first days of therapy - why **adjunctive prednisolone** reduces mortality in moderate-severe PCP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IRIS** happens when ART restores pathogen-specific T cells that attack residual antigen - **paradoxical** (worsening of a treated OI) or **unmasking** (a hidden OI declares itself), mostly in the **first 3 months**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**WHO clinical staging** (stage 1 asymptomatic to stage 4 AIDS-defining) is used with CD4 to define AHD and decide prophylaxis. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO stage 3** examples: unexplained weight loss over 10%, chronic diarrhoea over 1 month, persistent fever over 1 month, oral candidiasis, oral hairy leukoplakia, **pulmonary TB**, severe bacterial infections. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO stage 4** examples: HIV wasting, **PCP, extrapulmonary TB, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcal meningitis, CMV retinitis, oesophageal candidiasis**, Kaposi sarcoma, PML, HIV encephalopathy, disseminated MAC or histoplasmosis. [WHO 2021]`,`**CDC CD4 categories**: 500 or more, 200-499, and **under 200 (AIDS)**; children use age-specific CD4 percentages. [CDC 2014]`,`**IRIS classification**: paradoxical versus unmasking; INSHI criteria require a temporal link to ART, a fall in viral load and exclusion of drug toxicity, resistance or a new infection. [INSHI 2008]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**PCP**: **subacute (2-4 weeks) progressive exertional dyspnoea**, dry cough and low-grade fever - a patient who desaturates on walking with a near-normal chest X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral toxoplasmosis**: headache, fever, confusion, **focal deficits (hemiparesis) and seizures** over days to weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis**: subacute headache, vomiting, blurred vision; neck stiffness often absent - see the Systemic fungal infections topic. [WHO 2022]`,`**CMV retinitis**: **floaters, flashing lights, scotomata or painless loss of vision**, often in one eye first; colitis causes bloody diarrhoea and pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV-TB**: the **4-symptom screen - current cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats** - any one positive needs evaluation; extrapulmonary and disseminated TB are common at low CD4. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Oesophageal candidiasis**: **odynophagia and retrosternal pain** on swallowing, usually with oral thrush. [NACO 2021]`,`**Chronic diarrhoea** (over 1 month) with weight loss suggests Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Microsporidia, CMV or MAC. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Oral cavity**: thrush (pseudomembranous), **oral hairy leukoplakia** (EBV, lateral tongue, non-scrapable), angular cheilitis, aphthous ulcers - all point to immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Desaturation on exertion** (fall of SpO2 by 3% or more on a 1-minute sit-to-stand or walk) with few crackles suggests **PCP**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fundoscopy** in every PLHIV with CD4 under 100: CMV retinitis gives **'pizza-pie' or 'cottage cheese and ketchup'** - perivascular white necrosis with haemorrhage. [NACO 2021]`,`**Focal neurological signs** with fever point to toxoplasmosis, tuberculoma or lymphoma; **no fever and slowly progressive deficits without mass effect** suggest PML. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: umbilicated papules (Cryptococcus, Talaromyces, Histoplasma), multidermatomal **herpes zoster**, Kaposi sarcoma, seborrhoeic dermatitis and pruritic papular eruption. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymphadenopathy with fever** - matted nodes suggest TB; rapidly enlarging asymmetric nodes suggest lymphoma. [NACO 2021]`,`**Wasting** (BMI under 18.5) and generalised lymphadenopathy with hepatosplenomegaly suggest disseminated TB, MAC or histoplasmosis. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Diffuse pulmonary infiltrates**: PCP (bilateral perihilar ground glass, high LDH, no effusion) versus **TB** (any pattern at low CD4, effusion, nodes) versus bacterial pneumonia (lobar, acute) versus CMV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ring-enhancing brain lesions**: **toxoplasmosis** (multiple, basal ganglia and corticomedullary, IgG positive) versus **tuberculoma** versus **primary CNS lymphoma** (single, periventricular, EBV DNA in CSF) versus neurocysticercosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**White matter lesions without enhancement or mass effect**: PML (JC virus PCR in CSF) versus HIV encephalopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic meningitis**: cryptococcal (CrAg positive, high opening pressure) versus TB meningitis (high protein, low glucose, basal exudates). [WHO 2022]`,`**Fever without focus at CD4 under 100**: disseminated TB, MAC, histoplasmosis, talaromycosis, lymphoma, and bacteraemia (non-typhoidal Salmonella). [DHHS OI 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Baseline for every PLHIV**: CD4 count, **viral load** (NACO: at 6 and 12 months then yearly), CBC, LFT, creatinine, HBsAg, anti-HCV, VDRL, urine, chest X-ray and TB symptom screen. [NACO 2021]`,`**AHD screening (CD4 under 200)**: **serum CrAg** (CD4 under 100, consider under 200), **urine TB-LAM** (inpatients with TB symptoms or CD4 under 200; outpatients with symptoms and CD4 under 100), and Toxoplasma IgG. [WHO 2021]`,`**TB diagnosis**: **CBNAAT/Truenat** on sputum or any sample as the first test, plus **TB-LAM** - LAM performs best at low CD4 and reduces mortality when used in inpatients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**PCP**: induced sputum or BAL for **immunofluorescence or PCR**; **LDH over 500 U/L**, **beta-D-glucan positive**, ABG with **PaO2 under 70 mmHg or A-a gradient 35 or more** marks moderate-severe disease. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Brain lesions**: **contrast MRI** is better than CT for toxoplasmosis (multiple lesions); LP only after excluding mass effect; CSF EBV PCR for lymphoma, JC virus PCR for PML. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**CMV**: diagnosis of retinitis is **clinical by dilated fundoscopy**; blood CMV PCR does not prove end-organ disease; colitis needs biopsy with inclusion bodies. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Diarrhoea**: stool **modified acid-fast stain** (Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Cyclospora), microsporidia stain, culture; colonoscopy and biopsy if negative. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Prophylaxis - primary and secondary`,points:[`**Cotrimoxazole preventive therapy (CPT)**: **960 mg (one DS tablet) once daily** for all adults with **CD4 350 or less or WHO stage 3-4**, and all HIV-exposed infants from 6 weeks; it prevents PCP, toxoplasma, Cystoisospora, bacterial infections and malaria. [NACO 2021]`,`**Stopping CPT (NACO)**: when on ART with **CD4 over 350 on two occasions 6 months apart** and viral suppression; WHO suggests continuing where malaria and severe bacterial infection are common. [NACO 2021]`,`**Cotrimoxazole allergy**: desensitise if non-severe, or use **dapsone 100 mg daily** (check G6PD) for PCP; add pyrimethamine-leucovorin if toxoplasma IgG positive. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**TB preventive treatment (TPT)** for all PLHIV after excluding active TB: **3HP (isoniazid 900 mg + rifapentine 900 mg weekly for 12 weeks)** or **6H (isoniazid 300 mg daily for 6 months)** with pyridoxine; 3HP can be given with dolutegravir. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Cryptococcal**: CrAg-positive without meningitis - **fluconazole 800 mg x 2 weeks, 400 mg x 8 weeks, then 200 mg** until immune recovery; routine primary fluconazole prophylaxis is not recommended where CrAg screening exists. [WHO 2022]`,`**MAC prophylaxis** (azithromycin 1200 mg weekly) is **not needed if ART is started immediately** (DHHS 2024); consider only if ART is delayed with CD4 under 50. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Secondary prophylaxis (maintenance)** after PCP, toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis or CMV continues until **CD4 over 200 for at least 3-6 months** on ART with suppressed viral load. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Vaccines**: hepatitis B, pneumococcal (PCV then PPSV23), influenza yearly, HPV; **avoid live vaccines** (MMR, varicella, yellow fever) at CD4 under 200. [DHHS OI 2024]`]},{heading:`Treatment of the major OIs`,points:[`**PCP**: **cotrimoxazole 15-20 mg/kg/day of trimethoprim** in 3-4 divided doses (about 2 DS tablets 8-hourly for 60 kg) for **21 days**, then secondary prophylaxis. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**PCP steroids** if PaO2 under 70 mmHg or A-a gradient 35 or more: **prednisolone 40 mg twice daily days 1-5, 40 mg daily days 6-10, 20 mg daily days 11-21**, started within 72 hours. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`PCP alternatives: **primaquine 30 mg daily + clindamycin 600 mg IV 8-hourly** (G6PD check), or atovaquone for mild disease. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Toxoplasmosis**: **pyrimethamine 200 mg once then 50-75 mg daily + sulfadiazine 1-1.5 g 6-hourly + folinic acid 10-25 mg daily** for **6 weeks**; in India, **cotrimoxazole 5 mg/kg TMP twice daily** is equally effective and widely used. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Empirical toxoplasma therapy** for ring-enhancing lesions with positive IgG - expect clinical response in **7-14 days**; no response means brain biopsy (lymphoma, TB). [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**CMV retinitis**: **valganciclovir 900 mg twice daily for 14-21 days, then 900 mg daily**, plus **intravitreal ganciclovir 2 mg** for sight-threatening (zone 1) lesions; IV ganciclovir 5 mg/kg 12-hourly if oral not possible. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Oesophageal candidiasis**: **fluconazole 200-400 mg daily for 14-21 days**; oral thrush 100 mg daily for 7-14 days. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Cystoisospora**: cotrimoxazole DS twice daily for 10 days; **cryptosporidiosis**: ART is the cure, with rehydration and nitazoxanide as an adjunct. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Disseminated MAC**: **clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily + ethambutol 15 mg/kg daily** for at least 12 months. [DHHS OI 2024]`]},{heading:`TB-HIV, timing of ART and IRIS`,points:[`**HIV-TB treatment** follows the standard NTEP regimen (2HRZE/4HRE); start ATT first and **ART within 2 weeks** irrespective of CD4, except TB meningitis. [WHO 2021]`,`**TB meningitis**: delay ART **4-8 weeks** after ATT (earlier ART increased severe adverse events in trials). [WHO 2021]`,`**Rifampicin-dolutegravir interaction**: give **dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily** during rifampicin and for 2 weeks after; rifabutin replaces rifampicin with boosted PIs. [NACO 2021]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis**: delay ART **4-6 weeks** after antifungal start (COAT trial excess mortality with early ART). [WHO 2022]`,`**Other OIs (PCP, toxoplasma, CMV)**: start ART **within 2 weeks** (ACTG A5164 showed fewer deaths and AIDS progression). [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Paradoxical TB-IRIS** (fever, enlarging nodes, effusions) in 10-20%: continue ATT and ART, give **prednisolone 1.5 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks then 0.75 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks**; prophylactic prednisone (40 mg x 2 weeks then 20 mg x 2 weeks) at CD4 100 or less cut IRIS in the PredART trial. [Meintjes, NEJM 2018]`,`**Do not stop ART for IRIS** unless life-threatening (e.g. cryptococcal IRIS with raised ICP or TB meningitis IRIS). [DHHS OI 2024]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency care`,points:[`**Admit**: SpO2 under 94% or respiratory distress, altered sensorium, focal deficit or seizure, severe headache with vomiting, sudden vision loss, severe dehydration, CD4 under 200 with fever and danger signs. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO danger signs** in PLHIV: respiratory rate over 30, heart rate over 120, unable to walk unaided, temperature 39 C or more - triage for rapid TB-LAM, CrAg and empirical therapy. [WHO 2021]`,`In a **seriously ill PLHIV with suspected TB**, start empirical ATT if tests are unavailable or negative and there is no response to antibiotics. [WHO 2021]`,`**PCP with respiratory failure**: oxygen, high-flow or non-invasive ventilation, IV cotrimoxazole and steroids; watch for **pneumothorax**. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Brain lesion with mass effect**: dexamethasone only for midline shift or clinical deterioration, and taper quickly (masks lymphoma response). [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Monitor cotrimoxazole toxicity**: rash (stop at Stevens-Johnson features), **hyperkalaemia, cytopenias, hepatitis** and creatinine rise at treatment doses. [DHHS OI 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: CPT continues (benefit outweighs first-trimester folate risk); treat PCP with cotrimoxazole; avoid valganciclovir if possible; TPT with isoniazid is safe (3HP data limited). [NACO 2021]`,`**Children**: CPT for all HIV-exposed infants from 6 weeks until HIV excluded; children with HIV continue until 5 years or as per CD4; PCP peaks at 3-6 months of age. [NACO 2021]`,`**CKD**: reduce cotrimoxazole treatment doses at CrCl under 30 and watch potassium; adjust ganciclovir to creatinine clearance. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**HBV co-infection**: ART must contain **TDF plus lamivudine**; stopping them can cause hepatitis flare. [NACO 2021]`,`**Elderly PLHIV**: more drug interactions (statins, antiplatelets), cardiovascular risk and atypical OI presentations. [NACO 2021]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where CD4 testing is unavailable, use WHO staging; give CPT, TPT and ART on clinical grounds; use cotrimoxazole for toxoplasmosis when pyrimethamine is unavailable. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**PCP mortality** is 10-20% overall and **over 50% when ventilation is needed**; toxoplasmosis and cryptococcal meningitis carry 20-30% mortality even with treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`CMV retinitis causes **retinal detachment** and blindness; immune recovery uveitis may follow ART. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`**Follow-up**: monthly visits in the first 6 months, adherence counselling, CD4 at 6 months, **viral load at 6 and 12 months then yearly**, and fundoscopy every 3 months while CD4 under 100. [NACO 2021]`,`**An OI after 6 months on ART** suggests treatment failure - check viral load; two values over 1000 copies/mL 3 months apart with adherence support confirm failure. [NACO 2021]`,`**Refer** to the ART Centre or Centre of Excellence for CD4 under 100 with OIs, suspected IRIS, treatment failure and complex drug interactions. [NACO 2021]`,`**Programme link**: the **National AIDS and STD Control Programme (NACP Phase V, 2021-2026)** provides free CD4, viral load, CPT, ART and links with NTEP through single-window TB-HIV services. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2021 consolidated HIV guidelines**: rapid ART (same day, within 7 days), **ART within 2 weeks of TB treatment**, and the **AHD package** (CrAg, TB-LAM, CPT, TPT, rapid ART, adherence support). [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO 2022 cryptococcal guideline**: single high-dose liposomal amphotericin regimen and CrAg screening at CD4 under 100. [WHO 2022]`,`**NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment 2021**: TLD (tenofovir-lamivudine-dolutegravir) first line, CPT at CD4 350 or less, AHD package, viral load-based monitoring. [NACO 2021]`,`**NTEP TPT guidelines 2021** expanded **3HP and 1HP** for PLHIV and household contacts. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**WHO 2024 TB consolidated guidelines (Module 6)** recommend **TB-LAM-supported diagnosis** and rapid molecular testing for all PLHIV with symptoms. [WHO 2024]`,`**DHHS/NIH/IDSA adult OI guidelines (living, updated 2024)**: no routine MAC prophylaxis if ART started immediately; ART within 2 weeks for most OIs. [DHHS OI 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Learn the **CD4 ladder**: any - TB, zoster; **under 200 - PCP, candida oesophagitis; under 100 - toxo, crypto; under 50 - CMV, MAC**. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`Classic viva: **'When do you start ART after an OI?'** - within 2 weeks for most, including TB; **4-6 weeks for cryptococcal**, 4-8 weeks for TB meningitis. [WHO 2021]`,`PCP steroid trigger: **PaO2 under 70 mmHg or A-a gradient 35 or more** - prednisolone 40 BD x5, 40 OD x5, 20 OD x11. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`Common mistake: forgetting to **double dolutegravir (50 mg BD) with rifampicin**. [NACO 2021]`,`Common mistake: **stopping ART for IRIS** - continue both ART and OI therapy, and add steroids for paradoxical TB-IRIS. [DHHS OI 2024]`,`Cotrimoxazole covers **PCP, Toxo, Isospora, bacteria and malaria** - one tablet, five infections. [NACO 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for prophylaxis and treatment of major OIs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cotrimoxazole (prophylaxis)`,`960 mg (1 DS) PO once daily; infants 6 weeks onward by weight`,`Until CD4 over 350 x2 on ART with VL suppressed (NACO)`,`Rash, SJS/TEN, cytopenias, hyperkalaemia, hepatitis; G6PD-independent`],[`Cotrimoxazole (PCP treatment)`,`TMP 15-20 mg/kg/day PO/IV in 3-4 doses`,`21 days, then prophylaxis`,`As above, more frequent at high dose; adjust if CrCl under 30`],[`Prednisolone (PCP)`,`40 mg BD d1-5, 40 mg OD d6-10, 20 mg OD d11-21`,`If PaO2 under 70 or A-a gradient 35 or more`,`Hyperglycaemia, may unmask TB or CMV`],[`Pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid`,`200 mg load then 50-75 mg daily; sulfadiazine 1-1.5 g 6-hourly; folinic acid 10-25 mg daily`,`6 weeks, then maintenance`,`Marrow suppression, crystalluria, rash; pyrimethamine scarce in India`],[`Valganciclovir`,`900 mg PO BD induction, then 900 mg OD`,`14-21 days induction; maintenance till CD4 over 100-150 for 3-6 months`,`Neutropenia, anaemia, thrombocytopenia; renal dose adjustment; teratogenic`],[`Fluconazole`,`Oesophageal 200-400 mg daily; crypto pre-emptive 800 mg then 400 mg then 200 mg`,`14-21 days for oesophagitis`,`Hepatitis, QT, interactions`],[`Isoniazid + rifapentine (3HP)`,`Isoniazid 900 mg + rifapentine 900 mg PO weekly (adult over 30 kg)`,`12 weekly doses`,`Flu-like reaction, hepatitis; compatible with DTG; not with PIs`],[`Isoniazid (6H)`,`300 mg PO daily + pyridoxine 10-25 mg`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy`],[`Clarithromycin + ethambutol (MAC)`,`500 mg BD + 15 mg/kg daily`,`At least 12 months`,`GI upset, QT; optic neuritis with ethambutol`]]},{heading:`CD4 thresholds and the OI they predict`,columns:[`CD4 (cells/mm3)`,`Typical OIs`,`Prophylaxis / screening`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Any count`,`TB, bacterial pneumonia, zoster, oral candida, Kaposi`,`TPT (3HP/6H) after excluding TB; vaccines`,`4-symptom screen, CBNAAT, CXR`],[`Under 350`,`Bacterial infections, malaria, isosporiasis`,`Cotrimoxazole 960 mg daily (NACO)`,`-`],[`Under 200`,`PCP, oesophageal candida, histoplasmosis`,`Cotrimoxazole; AHD package`,`Induced sputum IF/PCR, LDH, BDG; TB-LAM`],[`Under 100`,`Toxoplasmosis, cryptococcus, cryptosporidium, PML`,`CrAg screen; cotrimoxazole covers toxo`,`Serum CrAg, toxo IgG, MRI brain`],[`Under 50`,`CMV retinitis/colitis, disseminated MAC, CNS lymphoma`,`Fundoscopy every 3 months; ART (no routine MAC prophylaxis)`,`Dilated fundoscopy, AFB blood culture`]]},{heading:`Ring-enhancing brain lesions in HIV`,columns:[`Feature`,`Toxoplasmosis`,`Primary CNS lymphoma`,`Tuberculoma`],rows:[[`CD4`,`Under 100`,`Under 50`,`Any`],[`Number and site`,`Multiple, basal ganglia, corticomedullary junction`,`Usually single, periventricular, crosses corpus callosum`,`Multiple or single, often with basal meningitis`],[`Fever`,`Common`,`Uncommon`,`Common`],[`Supportive test`,`Toxoplasma IgG positive (negative almost excludes)`,`CSF EBV DNA PCR; SPECT/PET hot lesion`,`CSF TB features, CBNAAT, chest TB`],[`Response test`,`Clinical and radiological response to therapy in 7-14 days`,`No response to toxo therapy; biopsy`,`Response to ATT over weeks`]]},{heading:`Timing of ART after an opportunistic infection`,columns:[`OI`,`When to start ART`,`Evidence / reason`,`IRIS management`],rows:[[`Pulmonary or extrapulmonary TB (not CNS)`,`Within 2 weeks of ATT, any CD4`,`SAPIT, CAMELIA, STRIDE - lower mortality at low CD4`,`Prednisolone 1.5 mg/kg/day x2 weeks then 0.75 x2 weeks`],[`TB meningitis`,`4-8 weeks after ATT`,`Early ART increased severe adverse events`,`Steroids part of TBM therapy; careful monitoring`],[`Cryptococcal meningitis`,`4-6 weeks after antifungal start`,`COAT trial - early ART raised mortality`,`Therapeutic LP; steroids only if life-threatening`],[`PCP, toxoplasmosis, bacterial infections`,`Within 2 weeks`,`ACTG A5164 - fewer deaths/AIDS progression`,`Continue ART; supportive care`],[`CMV retinitis`,`Within 2 weeks`,`Intravitreal therapy protects the eye`,`Immune recovery uveitis - topical/periocular steroids`],[`Cryptosporidiosis, PML, Kaposi`,`Immediately`,`ART is the only effective therapy`,`Continue ART`]]},{heading:`Key investigation cut-offs in HIV OIs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / finding`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`CD4 count`,`Under 200 or WHO stage 3-4`,`Advanced HIV disease - AHD package`],[`PaO2 / A-a gradient`,`Under 70 mmHg / 35 or more`,`Moderate-severe PCP - add steroids`],[`LDH`,`Over 500 U/L`,`Supports PCP; very high suggests poor prognosis`],[`Viral load`,`Under 1000 copies/mL suppressed; over 1000 twice 3 months apart = failure`,`NACO 2021 monitoring`],[`CSF opening pressure`,`25 cm H2O or more`,`Therapeutic LP in cryptococcal meningitis`],[`Toxoplasma IgG`,`Negative`,`Toxoplasmosis very unlikely; think lymphoma or TB`]]}],redFlags:[`Breathlessness with SpO2 under 94% or desaturation on exertion (PCP)`,`Headache with vomiting, blurred vision or confusion (cryptococcal meningitis)`,`Focal neurological deficit or seizure (toxoplasmosis, lymphoma, tuberculoma)`,`Floaters or sudden painless loss of vision (CMV retinitis)`,`WHO danger signs: RR over 30, HR over 120, unable to walk unaided, temperature 39 C or more`,`Worsening fever, nodes or neurological signs in the first 3 months of ART (IRIS)`,`Severe dehydration from chronic diarrhoea, or odynophagia preventing oral intake`],pearls:[`**CD4 ladder**: any - TB; under 200 - PCP; under 100 - toxo, crypto; under 50 - CMV, MAC.`,`**Cotrimoxazole 960 mg daily** at CD4 350 or less covers PCP, toxo, Isospora, bacteria, malaria.`,`**ART timing**: 2 weeks for most OIs including TB; **4-6 weeks crypto**, 4-8 weeks TB meningitis.`,`**PCP steroids** if PaO2 under 70 or A-a gradient 35 or more - 40 BD, 40 OD, 20 OD over 21 days.`,`**DTG 50 mg twice daily with rifampicin** - and for 2 weeks after stopping it.`,`**Toxo IgG negative**: think lymphoma or TB, not toxoplasmosis.`,`CMV retinitis: **pizza-pie fundus**; valganciclovir 900 mg BD then OD.`,`**AHD package**: CrAg, TB-LAM, CPT, TPT, rapid ART.`,`Never stop ART for IRIS unless it threatens life.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old newly diagnosed HIV-positive man has CD4 64 cells/mm3, no symptoms and a normal chest X-ray. Which set of interventions best fits the WHO advanced HIV disease package?`,options:[`Start ART only after CD4 rises above 100`,`Fluconazole 200 mg daily prophylaxis and delay ART for 3 months`,`Serum CrAg, urine TB-LAM if symptomatic, cotrimoxazole, TB preventive treatment and rapid ART`,`Weekly azithromycin and isoniazid only`],answer:2,explanation:`The AHD package for CD4 under 200 includes CrAg screening (under 100), TB-LAM where indicated, cotrimoxazole, TPT once active TB is excluded and rapid ART initiation with adherence support. Waiting for CD4 to rise is wrong because ART is what raises it; universal fluconazole prophylaxis is replaced by CrAg screening; MAC prophylaxis is unnecessary when ART starts immediately.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-q2`,stem:`A PLHIV with CD4 90 has 3 weeks of progressive exertional dyspnoea and dry cough. SpO2 is 89%, PaO2 62 mmHg, LDH 680 U/L and the chest X-ray shows bilateral perihilar haziness. Along with high-dose cotrimoxazole, which is correct?`,options:[`Prednisolone 40 mg twice daily for 5 days, then tapered over 21 days`,`Avoid steroids because they worsen PCP`,`Add amphotericin B for possible cryptococcal pneumonia`,`Start ART on the same day and defer cotrimoxazole`],answer:0,explanation:`This is moderate-severe PCP (PaO2 under 70 mmHg); adjunctive prednisolone 40 mg BD days 1-5, 40 mg OD days 6-10 and 20 mg OD days 11-21, started within 72 hours, reduces mortality and respiratory failure. Steroids do not worsen PCP, amphotericin is not indicated, and cotrimoxazole is the treatment, not something to defer; ART follows within 2 weeks.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-q3`,stem:`A PLHIV with CD4 40 has fever, right hemiparesis and seizures. MRI shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia. Toxoplasma IgG is positive. What is the most appropriate step?`,options:[`Immediate stereotactic brain biopsy`,`Empirical anti-toxoplasma therapy and repeat assessment in 10-14 days`,`Whole-brain radiotherapy for lymphoma`,`Start ART today and give no specific therapy`],answer:1,explanation:`Multiple basal ganglia ring-enhancing lesions with positive Toxoplasma IgG at CD4 under 100 justify empirical pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine-folinic acid or high-dose cotrimoxazole, with clinical response expected in 7-14 days. Biopsy is reserved for non-response, a single lesion with negative IgG or suspected lymphoma; radiotherapy is premature; ART follows within 2 weeks alongside specific treatment.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-q4`,stem:`A man with HIV (CD4 120) and newly diagnosed pulmonary TB started on rifampicin-based ATT. When and how should ART (TLD) be started?`,options:[`After completing 6 months of ATT, with standard dolutegravir dose`,`Within 2 weeks of ATT, with dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily during rifampicin`,`After 8 weeks of ATT, replacing rifampicin with streptomycin`,`Immediately with efavirenz because dolutegravir cannot be used with rifampicin`],answer:1,explanation:`WHO 2021 and NACO 2021 advise starting ART within 2 weeks of ATT regardless of CD4 (except TB meningitis), and rifampicin induction requires dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily during rifampicin and for 2 weeks after. Waiting until ATT finishes raises mortality, streptomycin substitution is obsolete, and dolutegravir is usable with rifampicin once the dose is doubled.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-q5`,stem:`Three weeks after starting ART, a patient on ATT for TB lymphadenitis develops high fever and rapidly enlarging, tender cervical nodes. Viral load has fallen 2 log. Sputum shows no rifampicin resistance. The best management is:`,options:[`Stop ART and ATT and restart after 4 weeks`,`Switch to second-line ART for treatment failure`,`Change ATT to an MDR-TB regimen`,`Continue ART and ATT; give prednisolone 1.5 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks then 0.75 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks`],answer:3,explanation:`This is paradoxical TB-IRIS: worsening soon after ART with a good virological response and no drug resistance. Both ART and ATT continue, prednisolone reduces symptoms and hospital stay (Meintjes regimen) and nodes may need aspiration. Stopping therapy is dangerous, a falling viral load excludes ART failure, and there is no evidence of MDR-TB.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Enumerate the opportunistic infections in HIV according to CD4 count. Discuss the prophylaxis of opportunistic infections and the management of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. Add a note on the timing of ART after an opportunistic infection.`,openingLines:[`Opportunistic infections are infections that cause disease, or unusually severe disease, because of HIV-induced CD4 depletion; each appears below a predictable CD4 threshold.`,`They are prevented by cotrimoxazole, TB preventive treatment, CrAg screening and early ART (NACO 2021, WHO AHD package), and ART follows most OIs within 2 weeks.`],answer:[{heading:`OIs by CD4 count`,points:[`Any: TB, bacterial pneumonia, zoster, oral candidiasis, Kaposi sarcoma.`,`Under 200: PCP, oesophageal candidiasis, histoplasmosis.`,`Under 100: toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, cryptosporidiosis, PML.`,`Under 50: CMV retinitis, disseminated MAC, primary CNS lymphoma.`]},{heading:`Prophylaxis`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole 960 mg daily at CD4 350 or less or WHO stage 3-4; stop at CD4 over 350 twice on ART (NACO).`,`TPT: 3HP weekly x 12 or 6H daily after excluding TB.`,`CrAg screen at CD4 under 100; pre-emptive fluconazole if positive and CSF negative.`,`Vaccines; avoid live vaccines at CD4 under 200; secondary prophylaxis until CD4 over 200.`]},{heading:`PCP - diagnosis`,points:[`Subacute exertional dyspnoea, dry cough, exertional desaturation; bilateral perihilar ground glass.`,`Induced sputum or BAL IF/PCR; LDH over 500; beta-D-glucan; ABG.`]},{heading:`PCP - treatment`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole TMP 15-20 mg/kg/day in 3-4 doses for 21 days.`,`Prednisolone if PaO2 under 70 or A-a gradient 35 or more: 40 BD x5, 40 OD x5, 20 OD x11 days.`,`Alternative primaquine + clindamycin; then secondary prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Timing of ART`,points:[`Within 2 weeks for most OIs including TB (any CD4).`,`4-6 weeks after antifungal start in cryptococcal meningitis; 4-8 weeks in TB meningitis.`,`Watch for IRIS in the first 3 months; continue ART.`]}],mustDraw:[`CD4 ladder diagram with the OI at each threshold`,`Table: ART timing by OI`],markSplit:[{part:`OIs by CD4`,marks:2},{part:`Prophylaxis`,marks:3},{part:`PCP diagnosis and treatment`,marks:3},{part:`Timing of ART`,marks:2}],keywords:[`CD4`,`cotrimoxazole`,`PCP`,`prednisolone`,`TPT`,`CrAg`,`IRIS`,`ART timing`]},{id:`infectious-fever-opportunistic-infections-hiv-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS).`,openingLines:[`IRIS is a paradoxical clinical worsening of a known infection, or the unmasking of a hidden one, after starting ART, caused by recovering pathogen-specific immunity.`,`It occurs mostly in the first 3 months of ART, especially when baseline CD4 is under 50-100 and ART starts soon after an OI.`],answer:[{heading:`Types and common pathogens`,points:[`Paradoxical (treated OI worsens) and unmasking (subclinical OI appears).`,`TB, cryptococcus, CMV (immune recovery uveitis), MAC, hepatitis B, PML, Kaposi sarcoma.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Temporal link to ART, viral load falling, CD4 rising.`,`Exclude drug resistance, new infection, drug toxicity and non-adherence.`]},{heading:`Management and prevention`,points:[`Continue ART and OI treatment; NSAIDs for mild cases.`,`TB-IRIS: prednisolone 1.5 mg/kg/day x 2 weeks then 0.75 mg/kg/day x 2 weeks.`,`Prevent: screen for TB and CrAg before ART; delay ART 4-6 weeks in cryptococcal meningitis; prophylactic prednisone (PredART) at CD4 100 or less with TB.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: worsening on ART - exclude failure, resistance, new OI - diagnose IRIS - manage`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and types`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`IRIS`,`paradoxical`,`unmasking`,`prednisolone`,`PredART`,`cryptococcal`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on HIV disease: AIDS and related disorders`,`NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India, 2021`,`WHO Consolidated guidelines on HIV prevention, testing, treatment, service delivery and monitoring, 2021`,`WHO Guidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV, 2022`,`Panel on Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Opportunistic Infections in Adults and Adolescents with HIV (NIH, CDC, HIVMA/IDSA), living guideline updated 2024`,`NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of Tuberculosis Preventive Treatment in India, 2021`,`Meintjes G et al. Prednisone for the prevention of paradoxical tuberculosis-associated IRIS (PredART), New England Journal of Medicine, 2018`,`NACO India HIV Estimates 2023: Technical Report`]}),n.push({id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations`,title:`HIV special situations: PEP, PrEP, PPTCT, paediatric HIV and HIV-2`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`HIV prevention and special-population care rest on a few fixed rules: **post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP)** with **TDF + 3TC + DTG for 28 days started within 72 hours (ideally 2 hours)**; **pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP)** with daily **TDF/FTC** or long-acting injectables for people at substantial risk; **prevention of parent-to-child transmission (PPTCT)** by universal antenatal **HIV and syphilis testing**, **lifelong dolutegravir-based ART**, infant **nevirapine (or dual) prophylaxis**, **early infant diagnosis by DNA PCR from 6 weeks** and supported breastfeeding; and recognising **HIV-2**, which is **intrinsically resistant to NNRTIs** - all underpinned by **U=U** (an undetectable viral load does not transmit HIV sexually).`,keywords:[`PEP`,`post-exposure prophylaxis`,`needlestick injury`,`PrEP`,`pre-exposure prophylaxis`,`TDF/FTC`,`cabotegravir`,`lenacapavir`,`PPTCT`,`EMTCT`,`Option B+`,`dolutegravir in pregnancy`,`infant nevirapine prophylaxis`,`early infant diagnosis`,`EID`,`DNA PCR`,`paediatric HIV`,`HIV-2`,`U=U`,`viral load monitoring`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**PEP** is short-term ART taken after a potential HIV exposure to prevent infection; **PrEP** is ART taken by HIV-negative people **before** exposure; **PPTCT/EMTCT** is the package that prevents transmission from a mother living with HIV to her child. [WHO 2021]`,`India has about **25 lakh PLHIV**, **about 66,000-68,000 new infections a year** and about 20,000 pregnant women with HIV needing PPTCT each year; prevalence is highest in **Mizoram, Nagaland and Manipur**. [NACO India HIV Estimates 2023]`,`Without any intervention, **mother-to-child transmission is 15-45%**: about 5-10% in pregnancy, 10-20% in labour and 5-20% through breastfeeding; with suppressive ART it falls to **under 1%**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Occupational transmission risk** after a percutaneous injury from an HIV-positive source is about **0.3%**, after mucous membrane exposure about **0.09%** - versus about 6-30% for hepatitis B and 1.8% for hepatitis C. [CDC 2013]`,`Per-act sexual risk: **receptive anal intercourse about 1.4%**, receptive vaginal about 0.08%; sharing injecting equipment about 0.6%. [CDC 2014]`,`**HIV-2** is concentrated in **West Africa**; in India it is uncommon, reported mainly from **western India (Maharashtra, Goa)**, often as **dual HIV-1/HIV-2 infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`India's goals under **NACP Phase V (2021-2026)** are the **UNAIDS 95-95-95 targets** and **elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis (EMTCT)**. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Occupational exposure**: needlestick (especially **hollow-bore needles with visible blood** or from an artery or vein), scalpel injuries, splashes to eyes or mouth, and contact with non-intact skin. [NACO 2021]`,`**Infectious fluids**: blood, semen, vaginal secretions, CSF, pleural, peritoneal, pericardial, synovial and amniotic fluid; **saliva, urine, sweat, tears and vomit are not infectious unless visibly bloody**. [NACO 2021]`,`**Non-occupational exposure**: unprotected sex, sexual assault, condom breakage, shared needles - key populations are **sex workers, MSM, transgender people, people who inject drugs and their partners**. [NACO 2021]`,`**MTCT risk factors**: **high maternal viral load** (the single strongest), low CD4, **new infection in pregnancy or breastfeeding**, no or late ART, prolonged rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, invasive procedures, mixed feeding and mastitis. [WHO 2021]`,`**Paediatric HIV** is almost entirely **vertically acquired**; adolescents may also acquire it sexually or through injecting drug use. [NACO 2021]`,`**HIV-2 risk** follows travel or partners from West Africa and areas of western India; it is less transmissible sexually and vertically than HIV-1. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the window that PEP and PrEP exploit`,points:[`After mucosal or percutaneous exposure, HIV first infects local **dendritic cells, macrophages and CD4 T cells**, reaches regional lymph nodes in **24-72 hours** and becomes systemic within days - PEP works by blocking this early local replication. [Harrison 22e]`,`Animal studies show PEP efficacy falls sharply when started after **24-36 hours** and is negligible after **72 hours** - hence 'as early as possible, ideally within 2 hours, never after 72'. [WHO 2024]`,`**PrEP** puts active drug (**tenofovir diphosphate**) in genital and rectal tissue before exposure; rectal tissue reaches protective levels faster than cervicovaginal tissue, which is the basis for **event-driven 2-1-1 dosing only in cisgender men**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Dolutegravir** is an integrase inhibitor with a **high genetic barrier**, rapid viral load decline (useful late in pregnancy) and once-daily dosing; it is the anchor of PEP, PPTCT and first-line ART. [NACO 2021]`,`**Breastfeeding transmission** depends on **breast-milk viral load** and gut integrity - **mixed feeding** damages the infant gut mucosa and raises transmission more than exclusive breastfeeding. [WHO 2016]`,`**Infant antibodies are maternal IgG** that cross the placenta and persist **up to 18 months** - so infant diagnosis before 18 months needs a **virological test (DNA PCR)**, not an antibody test. [NACO 2021]`,`**HIV-2** differs in reverse transcriptase structure (**Y181I, Y188L**), making it **intrinsically resistant to efavirenz and nevirapine**; it has lower viral loads and slower CD4 decline. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - exposure grading and infant risk`,points:[`**NACO exposure grading**: **mild** - mucous membrane or non-intact skin with small volume, or superficial scratch with a solid needle; **moderate** - large volume or prolonged contact, or moderate injury with a solid needle; **severe** - **deep injury with a hollow-bore needle, visible blood on the device, or needle used in an artery or vein**. [NACO 2021]`,`**Source status**: known HIV positive (with viral load if known), HIV negative, or unknown - PEP is started if the source is positive or unknown and stopped if a rapid test on the source is negative (unless in the window period with high risk). [NACO 2021]`,`**Infant risk (WHO/NACO)**: **high-risk** if the mother received **under 4 weeks of ART at delivery**, has **viral load over 1000 copies/mL** in the 4 weeks before delivery, acquired HIV in pregnancy or breastfeeding, or was diagnosed only postpartum; all others are **low-risk**. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO viral load categories (2023)**: **undetectable (not detected)** - zero risk of sexual transmission; **suppressed (under 1000 copies/mL)** - almost zero risk; **unsuppressed (1000 or more)** - transmission possible and treatment failure likely. [WHO 2023]`,`**WHO paediatric clinical staging** (stages 1-4) applies to children, with **all children under 5 years counted as advanced HIV disease**. [WHO 2021]`,`**HIV-2 serology**: rapid tests and line immunoassays differentiate **HIV-1, HIV-2 and dual reactivity**; HIV-1 viral load assays **do not measure HIV-2**. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history and presentation`,points:[`**PEP history**: time since exposure (hours), type of device and depth, fluid, glove use, source HIV/HBV/HCV status and viral load, and the exposed person's **hepatitis B vaccination and antibody status**. [NACO 2021]`,`**Acute HIV (seroconversion)** 2-4 weeks after exposure: fever, sore throat, rash, lymphadenopathy, mucosal ulcers - ask at every PEP or PrEP visit, because starting PrEP during acute HIV breeds resistance. [WHO 2022]`,`**Antenatal history**: HIV and syphilis test result, ART start date and adherence, last viral load, partner's status, previous children's status, and feeding plans. [NACO 2021]`,`**Paediatric HIV presentation**: **failure to thrive**, recurrent or persistent diarrhoea, persistent oral thrush after 6-8 weeks, recurrent pneumonia, **PCP at 3-6 months**, developmental delay and encephalopathy. [WHO 2021]`,`**Older children and adolescents**: chronic otitis or parotid swelling, chronic lung disease, stunting, delayed puberty, skin disease, school difficulties and disclosure-related distress. [NACO 2021]`,`**HIV-2**: long asymptomatic period; may present with OIs at low CD4 even with an 'undetectable' HIV-1 viral load - a classic clue. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Exposure site**: depth of wound, bleeding, contamination; after sexual assault - injuries documented as per the **MoHFW medico-legal guidelines (2014)**. [MoHFW 2014]`,`**Pregnant woman with HIV**: weight, BMI, oral thrush, lymphadenopathy, signs of TB, genital ulcers (syphilis, herpes) and WHO staging. [NACO 2021]`,`**Infant**: weight-for-age and length on growth charts, oral thrush, hepatosplenomegaly, generalised lymphadenopathy, **parotid enlargement**, developmental milestones. [WHO 2021]`,`**Child with HIV**: **digital clubbing and chronic cough** (lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis - LIP, or bronchiectasis), molluscum, chronic scabies, recurrent herpes zoster. [WHO 2021]`,`**Signs of congenital syphilis** in HIV-exposed infants: snuffles, hepatosplenomegaly, desquamating rash on palms and soles, pseudoparalysis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PrEP visit**: weight, blood pressure, signs of STIs (urethral discharge, genital ulcers), and features of acute HIV. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Febrile illness after PEP or PrEP start**: acute HIV seroconversion versus drug reaction versus intercurrent viral illness - an **HIV antigen/antibody test plus viral load** separates them. [WHO 2022]`,`**Failure to thrive in an infant**: HIV versus malnutrition, TB, congenital infections (TORCH), cow's milk protein allergy and congenital heart disease. [WHO 2021]`,`**LIP versus miliary TB** in a child with HIV: LIP has **clubbing, parotid enlargement, no fever** and a reticulonodular pattern; miliary TB is febrile and acute. [WHO 2021]`,`**Persistent low CD4 with undetectable HIV-1 viral load**: HIV-2 infection, poor CD4 recovery (immunological non-responder), or lab error. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indeterminate infant results**: true infection versus contamination or low-level maternal DNA - repeat DNA PCR on a new sample before starting lifelong ART. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**HIV diagnosis (NACO)**: adults - a **three-test strategy** with three different rapid kits; children under 18 months - **DNA PCR on dried blood spot (DBS)**. [NACO 2021]`,`**PEP baseline**: HIV test of exposed person (and source if possible), **HBsAg and anti-HBs**, anti-HCV, creatinine, pregnancy test; **follow-up HIV test at 3 months** (WHO 2024 simplified schedule; older NACO schedules also tested at 6 weeks and 6 months). [WHO 2024]`,`**PrEP baseline and follow-up**: HIV test (exclude acute HIV), **creatinine (eGFR 60 mL/min or more for TDF)**, HBsAg, STI screen and pregnancy test; repeat HIV test **every 3 months**, creatinine every 6-12 months. [WHO 2022]`,`**Antenatal**: universal **dual HIV/syphilis rapid test** at first ANC visit, HBsAg (triple elimination); repeat HIV test in the third trimester for women at high risk. [NACO 2021]`,`**Pregnant women on ART**: viral load at **6 months on ART** and in the **late third trimester (around 32-36 weeks)** to classify infant risk; if over 1000, adherence support and repeat. [NACO 2021]`,`**Early infant diagnosis (EID)**: **DBS DNA PCR at 6 weeks** (or first contact after), repeated at **6 months and 12 months** or 6 weeks after breastfeeding stops; **confirm with antibody tests at 18 months**; any positive PCR is confirmed on a second sample. [NACO 2021]`,`**Viral load monitoring on ART (NACO)**: at **6 months, 12 months, then every 12 months**; **1000 copies/mL or more** - enhanced adherence counselling and repeat after 3 months; persistent - treatment failure. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`PEP - management in OPD and casualty`,points:[`**First aid**: wash the wound with **soap and running water**, let it bleed freely, **do not squeeze, scrub, suck or apply bleach**; flush eyes and mouth with water or saline. [NACO 2021]`,`**Regimen (NACO 2021)**: **tenofovir 300 mg + lamivudine 300 mg + dolutegravir 50 mg (TLD), one tablet once daily for 28 days**; start **as early as possible (ideally within 2 hours), not after 72 hours**. [NACO 2021]`,`**Alternatives** if DTG is unavailable or not tolerated: TDF + 3TC with **atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir**; TAF or FTC can replace TDF or 3TC. [WHO 2024]`,`**Children**: weight-based ABC or AZT + 3TC + DTG (paediatric DTG) for 28 days. [WHO 2024]`,`**Hepatitis B**: if unvaccinated or non-responder and source HBsAg positive or unknown - **HBIG 0.06 mL/kg IM + start hepatitis B vaccination**; vaccinated responders (anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL or more) need nothing. [NACO 2021]`,`**Sexual assault survivors**: HIV PEP, **emergency contraception** (levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 hours), presumptive STI treatment, hepatitis B vaccine, counselling and medico-legal care. [MoHFW 2014]`,`**Counselling**: adherence for all 28 days, condoms and no blood donation until the 3-month test is negative, and report symptoms of acute HIV. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`PrEP`,points:[`**Indication**: HIV-negative people at **substantial risk** - sex workers, MSM, transgender people, people who inject drugs, and **serodiscordant partners** whose partner is not yet virally suppressed. [WHO 2022]`,`**Oral PrEP**: **TDF 300 mg + FTC 200 mg (or TDF + 3TC) once daily**; protective levels after about **7 days**; efficacy **over 90%** with good adherence. [WHO 2022]`,`**Event-driven (2-1-1) PrEP** for cisgender men: **2 tablets 2-24 hours before sex, 1 tablet 24 hours later and 1 tablet 48 hours after the first dose**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Long-acting cabotegravir (CAB-LA)**: **600 mg IM, two injections 4 weeks apart, then every 8 weeks** - recommended by WHO in 2022 (HPTN 083 and 084 superior to oral PrEP). [WHO 2022]`,`**Lenacapavir**: subcutaneous injection **twice yearly**; PURPOSE 1 and 2 trials (2024) showed near-complete protection; FDA approved and **WHO recommended in 2025**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Dapivirine vaginal ring** (monthly) is a WHO-recommended option for women who cannot use oral PrEP. [WHO 2021]`,`In India, **NACO offers oral PrEP** to key populations through targeted intervention sites; long-acting agents are not yet in the programme. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`PPTCT - pregnancy, delivery and infant`,points:[`**Universal testing**: every pregnant woman is offered **HIV and syphilis testing at the first ANC visit**; HIV-positive women start **lifelong TLD the same day or as soon as possible, irrespective of CD4** (Option B+ since 2014). [NACO 2021]`,`**Dolutegravir is safe in pregnancy and at conception**: the 2018 Tsepamo neural-tube-defect signal fell to **about 0.1%, no different from other ART**, and WHO recommends DTG for all women including those planning pregnancy; give folic acid. [WHO 2021]`,`**Delivery**: vaginal delivery is preferred when viral load is suppressed; **caesarean section for obstetric indications** (and considered if viral load over 1000 near term); avoid prolonged rupture, artificial rupture, episiotomy and instrumental delivery where possible. [NACO 2021]`,`**Low-risk infant prophylaxis**: **nevirapine syrup once daily for 6 weeks** (10 mg if birth weight 2-2.5 kg, 15 mg if over 2.5 kg). [NACO 2021]`,`**High-risk infant prophylaxis**: **dual zidovudine + nevirapine for 6 weeks**, continued (nevirapine with or without zidovudine) for a further 6 weeks - **12 weeks in all if breastfed**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Infant feeding**: **exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months** with the mother on ART, then complementary foods with continued breastfeeding to at least 12 months (WHO up to 24 months); **avoid mixed feeding** in the first 6 months. [WHO 2016]`,`**HIV-exposed infant**: **cotrimoxazole from 6 weeks** until HIV is excluded after breastfeeding ends; routine UIP vaccines including **BCG** (defer if infant is confirmed HIV-positive and symptomatic). [NACO 2021]`,`**Syphilis in pregnancy**: **benzathine penicillin 2.4 million units IM** (single dose for early syphilis; weekly x3 for late or unknown duration) given at least 30 days before delivery prevents congenital syphilis. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Paediatric HIV - treatment`,points:[`**Treat all**: every child with confirmed HIV starts ART **as soon as possible**, regardless of CD4 or stage. [NACO 2021]`,`**Preferred regimen**: **abacavir + lamivudine + dolutegravir**; **paediatric DTG 10 mg dispersible tablets** for children aged 4 weeks or more and weighing 3 kg or more, and the **50 mg adult tablet from 20 kg**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Neonates (under 4 weeks)**: AZT + 3TC with **raltegravir** or nevirapine, switching to DTG at 4 weeks and 3 kg. [WHO 2021]`,`**Lopinavir/ritonavir** remains an alternative (pellets or granules) where DTG is not possible. [WHO 2021]`,`**Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis** for all children with HIV (up to 5 years in NACO, then as per CD4); **TB screening and TPT**, growth monitoring and **age-appropriate disclosure**. [NACO 2021]`,`**Adolescents**: transition to adult services, sexual and reproductive health counselling, adherence support and mental health screening. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`HIV-2 and special adult situations`,points:[`**HIV-2 is intrinsically resistant to NNRTIs (efavirenz, nevirapine) and enfuvirtide**, and less susceptible to some PIs (atazanavir); **integrase inhibitors (dolutegravir), NRTIs and boosted darunavir or lopinavir** are active - so **TLD suits HIV-2 and dual infection**. [NACO 2021]`,`**HIV-2 monitoring** needs HIV-2-specific viral load assays (not widely available) - otherwise monitor **CD4 trends and clinical response**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV-2 natural history**: slower progression, lower viral load, **lower sexual and vertical transmission (MTCT about 0-4%)** - but it does cause AIDS and must be treated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serodiscordant couples**: the positive partner on ART with sustained suppression does not transmit sexually (**U=U**); PrEP for the negative partner until suppression is confirmed; **safer conception** planning. [WHO 2023]`,`**Women of reproductive age**: DTG-based ART with contraception counselling; **rifampicin and efavirenz reduce hormonal contraceptive efficacy** - DMPA or IUCD preferred. [NACO 2021]`,`**Healthcare workers with HIV** on suppressive ART need not stop clinical work but avoid exposure-prone procedures if viraemic, per institutional policy. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**PEP side effects** with TLD are mild (headache, nausea, insomnia); completion rates are higher than with older AZT or LPV/r regimens. [WHO 2024]`,`**PrEP risks**: small reversible **creatinine rise** and bone-density loss with TDF; **resistance if PrEP is started during undiagnosed acute HIV**; **hepatitis B flare** if TDF/FTC is stopped in HBsAg-positive users. [WHO 2022]`,`**Untreated paediatric HIV** kills about **one-third of infants by 1 year and half by 2 years**; early ART (CHER trial) cut early mortality by about 75%. [WHO 2021]`,`**Follow-up of HIV-exposed infants**: monthly visits to 6 months, then 3-monthly till 18 months for growth, prophylaxis, feeding and EID testing. [NACO 2021]`,`**Women after PPTCT**: continue lifelong ART, viral load monitoring, cervical cancer screening and family planning. [NACO 2021]`,`**Refer** to the ART centre: all positive tests, high-risk infants, indeterminate EID results, suspected HIV-2 or dual infection and adolescents with treatment failure. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO PEP guideline 2024**: TDF + 3TC/FTC + **DTG preferred** for 28 days; dispense the full course at the first visit; simplified testing (baseline and 3 months); PEP via community and pharmacy delivery. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2025** recommended **twice-yearly injectable lenacapavir** as an additional PrEP option; **CAB-LA every 2 months** recommended since 2022. [WHO 2025]`,`**WHO 2023 policy brief on viral load and transmission**: undetectable = zero sexual transmission; under 1000 copies/mL = almost zero - formal endorsement of **U=U**. [WHO 2023]`,`**NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment 2021**: TLD for adults, adolescents and pregnant women, paediatric DTG, viral load monitoring at 6 and 12 months then yearly. [NACO 2021]`,`**WHO 2021**: **dolutegravir for all**, including women of childbearing potential and pregnant women, after the neural-tube-defect signal resolved. [WHO 2021]`,`**Triple elimination (HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B)** of vertical transmission is now the WHO framework; India's programme uses **dual HIV/syphilis RDTs** in ANC. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`PEP in one line: **TLD once daily x 28 days, start within 2 hours, not after 72 hours; test at baseline and 3 months**. [NACO 2021]`,`Classic question: **'Why DNA PCR and not ELISA in an infant?'** - maternal IgG persists up to 18 months. [NACO 2021]`,`Classic mistake: **mixed feeding** - examiners want 'exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months with the mother on ART'. [WHO 2016]`,`Classic mistake: giving **efavirenz or nevirapine for HIV-2** - intrinsically resistant. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic mistake: **starting PrEP without excluding acute HIV** - leads to resistance. [WHO 2022]`,`**U=U**: viral load undetectable for 6 months or more = no sexual transmission; it does not apply fully to breastfeeding, where risk is low but not zero. [WHO 2023]`,`High-risk infant = **mother on ART under 4 weeks, VL over 1000, or new infection** - gets dual prophylaxis for 12 weeks if breastfed. [WHO 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for PEP, PrEP and PPTCT`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`TLD (tenofovir 300 + lamivudine 300 + dolutegravir 50)`,`1 tablet PO once daily`,`PEP 28 days; lifelong for treatment and PPTCT`,`Nausea, headache, insomnia, weight gain; TDF renal toxicity; DTG 50 mg BD with rifampicin`],[`TDF 300 mg + FTC 200 mg (oral PrEP)`,`1 tablet daily; or 2-1-1 event-driven (cisgender men)`,`While at risk; HIV test every 3 months`,`Creatinine rise, bone loss; avoid if eGFR under 60; HBV flare on stopping`],[`Cabotegravir long-acting`,`600 mg IM gluteal; 2 doses 4 weeks apart, then every 8 weeks`,`While at risk`,`Injection-site reactions; long pharmacokinetic tail - resistance risk if HIV acquired`],[`Lenacapavir`,`Subcutaneous injection twice yearly (with oral loading)`,`While at risk`,`Injection-site nodules; not yet available in India`],[`Nevirapine (infant, low-risk)`,`10 mg PO OD (2-2.5 kg) or 15 mg OD (over 2.5 kg)`,`6 weeks from birth`,`Rash, hepatotoxicity (rare in infants)`],[`Zidovudine + nevirapine (infant, high-risk)`,`AZT 4 mg/kg PO BD + nevirapine as above`,`6 weeks dual, then 6 more weeks if breastfed (12 weeks)`,`Anaemia, neutropenia (AZT)`],[`Paediatric dolutegravir`,`10 mg dispersible tablets by weight band (4 weeks and 3 kg or more); 50 mg from 20 kg`,`Lifelong with ABC + 3TC`,`Insomnia, headache; check interactions with rifampicin`],[`Benzathine penicillin (syphilis in pregnancy)`,`2.4 million units IM`,`Single dose early; weekly x 3 late/unknown`,`Anaphylaxis (rare), Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction`],[`HBIG + hepatitis B vaccine (PEP)`,`HBIG 0.06 mL/kg IM + vaccine 0, 1, 6 months`,`Within 24 hours ideally, up to 7 days`,`Local pain; give at separate sites`]]},{heading:`NACO grading of occupational exposure`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Source HIV positive / unknown`,`Source HIV negative`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Mucous membrane or non-intact skin, small volume; superficial scratch with solid or low-calibre needle`,`Start PEP (TLD 28 days)`,`No PEP`],[`Moderate`,`Mucous membrane or non-intact skin, large volume or long contact; moderate injury with solid needle`,`Start PEP`,`No PEP`],[`Severe`,`Deep injury, hollow-bore needle, visible blood on device, needle from artery or vein`,`Start PEP urgently`,`No PEP (unless source in window period with high risk)`],[`Intact skin`,`Blood on intact skin only`,`No PEP`,`No PEP`]]},{heading:`Infant prophylaxis and testing by risk`,columns:[`Situation`,`Infant ARV prophylaxis`,`Testing`,`Feeding`],rows:[[`Low-risk: mother on ART over 4 weeks with suppressed VL`,`Nevirapine once daily x 6 weeks`,`DBS DNA PCR at 6 weeks, 6 and 12 months; antibody at 18 months`,`Exclusive breastfeeding 6 months, then continued with complementary feeds`],[`High-risk: ART under 4 weeks, VL over 1000, new maternal infection`,`AZT + nevirapine x 6 weeks, then nevirapine +/- AZT 6 more weeks if breastfed`,`Consider birth PCR; PCR at 6 weeks and as above`,`Breastfeed with maternal ART and adherence support`],[`Mother diagnosed during breastfeeding`,`Start maternal ART now; infant dual prophylaxis for 12 weeks`,`Infant PCR immediately and repeat`,`Continue breastfeeding`],[`Positive infant PCR`,`Stop prophylaxis; start ABC + 3TC + DTG (or neonatal regimen)`,`Confirm on second sample`,`Continue breastfeeding`]]},{heading:`HIV-1 versus HIV-2`,columns:[`Feature`,`HIV-1`,`HIV-2`],rows:[[`Distribution`,`Worldwide; most Indian infections`,`West Africa; western India, often dual`],[`Progression`,`Faster; median 8-10 years to AIDS untreated`,`Slower; many long-term non-progressors`],[`Viral load`,`High`,`Low; HIV-1 assays do not detect it`],[`Transmission`,`Higher sexual and vertical (15-45% untreated MTCT)`,`Lower (MTCT about 0-4%)`],[`NNRTI (efavirenz, nevirapine)`,`Active`,`Intrinsically resistant`],[`Integrase inhibitors and NRTIs`,`Active`,`Active - TLD suitable`],[`Diagnosis`,`Rapid tests, ELISA, HIV-1 VL`,`Differentiating rapid test or line immunoassay; HIV-2 VL where available`]]},{heading:`Monitoring cut-offs and schedules`,columns:[`Situation`,`Test`,`Schedule / cut-off`],rows:[[`PEP`,`HIV test`,`Baseline and 3 months (WHO 2024)`],[`PrEP`,`HIV test, creatinine`,`HIV every 3 months; creatinine every 6-12 months; eGFR 60 or more for TDF`],[`Adult on ART (NACO)`,`Viral load`,`6 months, 12 months, then yearly; failure = 1000 or more twice, 3 months apart`],[`Pregnant woman on ART`,`Viral load`,`Late third trimester (about 32-36 weeks) to grade infant risk`],[`HIV-exposed infant`,`DBS DNA PCR`,`6 weeks, 6 months, 12 months or 6 weeks after weaning; antibody at 18 months`],[`U=U`,`Viral load`,`Undetectable sustained 6 months or more = no sexual transmission`]]}],redFlags:[`High-risk exposure (hollow-bore needle with visible blood, sexual assault) - start PEP immediately, do not wait for source results`,`Presentation for PEP between 48 and 72 hours - start at once; beyond 72 hours PEP is not recommended`,`Fever, rash and sore throat 2-4 weeks after exposure or while on PrEP - suspect acute HIV`,`Pregnant woman newly diagnosed in late pregnancy or labour - start ART now and treat infant as high-risk`,`HIV-exposed infant with failure to thrive, persistent thrush or severe pneumonia (possible PCP)`,`Positive infant DNA PCR - start ART the same week after a confirmatory sample`,`Low CD4 or OI with undetectable HIV-1 viral load - think HIV-2`],pearls:[`**PEP**: TLD x 28 days; within 2 hours ideal, 72 hours maximum.`,`**Wash, do not squeeze** a needlestick wound; no bleach.`,`**Infant ELISA is useless before 18 months** - maternal IgG; use DNA PCR from 6 weeks.`,`**Exclusive breastfeeding** with maternal ART beats mixed feeding.`,`**High-risk infant** = mother on ART under 4 weeks, VL over 1000, or new infection - dual AZT + NVP.`,`**HIV-2 laughs at NNRTIs** - use dolutegravir-based ART.`,`**U=U**: undetectable equals untransmittable (sexually).`,`**2-1-1 PrEP** only for cisgender men.`,`**Dual HIV/syphilis test** at the first antenatal visit; benzathine penicillin 30 days before delivery.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-q1`,stem:`A nursing officer sustains a deep needlestick injury with a hollow-bore needle used to draw blood from a patient with HIV whose viral load is unknown. She presents 90 minutes later. What is the correct PEP regimen?`,options:[`Zidovudine plus lamivudine for 14 days`,`Tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir once daily for 28 days`,`Single-dose nevirapine`,`Wait for her baseline HIV result before deciding`],answer:1,explanation:`NACO 2021 and WHO 2024 recommend TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir) once daily for 28 days, started as early as possible and within 72 hours, for a severe exposure from a positive source. Two-drug zidovudine regimens and 14-day courses are outdated, single-dose nevirapine has no role in PEP, and waiting for her own result delays protection - the baseline test is drawn but PEP starts at once.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-q2`,stem:`An infant born to a mother living with HIV is 8 weeks old and breastfed. Which is the appropriate test to diagnose HIV infection in this infant?`,options:[`HIV-1/2 antibody ELISA`,`Rapid antibody test on maternal blood`,`CD4 count of the infant`,`HIV DNA PCR on a dried blood spot`],answer:3,explanation:`Maternal IgG antibodies cross the placenta and persist up to 18 months, so antibody tests cannot diagnose infant infection; NACO EID uses DNA PCR on a dried blood spot from 6 weeks. A maternal test only confirms exposure, and the infant CD4 count is age-dependent and does not diagnose infection.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-q3`,stem:`A woman tested HIV-positive for the first time in labour at 38 weeks and was started on TLD. She plans to breastfeed. Which infant prophylaxis is recommended?`,options:[`Nevirapine once daily for 6 weeks only`,`No prophylaxis because the mother has started ART`,`Dual zidovudine plus nevirapine for 6 weeks, continued for a total of 12 weeks while breastfeeding`,`Cotrimoxazole alone from birth`],answer:2,explanation:`The infant is high-risk because the mother received under 4 weeks of ART before delivery, so WHO/NACO advise dual zidovudine plus nevirapine for 6 weeks, extended to 12 weeks in breastfed infants. Nevirapine alone for 6 weeks is for low-risk infants, maternal ART started in labour has not suppressed viral load, and cotrimoxazole starts at 6 weeks to prevent PCP but is not antiretroviral prophylaxis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-q4`,stem:`A 40-year-old man from Goa with a positive HIV screening test has CD4 180 cells/mm3 but an HIV-1 viral load below the detection limit and is not on ART. The line immunoassay shows HIV-2 reactivity only. Which ART regimen should be avoided?`,options:[`Tenofovir + lamivudine + efavirenz`,`Tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir`,`Abacavir + lamivudine + dolutegravir`,`Tenofovir + lamivudine + boosted lopinavir`],answer:0,explanation:`HIV-2 is intrinsically resistant to NNRTIs such as efavirenz and nevirapine because of its reverse transcriptase structure, so an efavirenz regimen would fail. Dolutegravir-based regimens (with tenofovir or abacavir) and boosted lopinavir regimens are active against HIV-2. The undetectable HIV-1 viral load with a low CD4 is the classic clue to HIV-2.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-q5`,stem:`An HIV-negative man whose male partner has HIV with a viral load of 15,000 copies/mL wants PrEP. He reports fever, sore throat and a rash for 5 days, and had condomless sex 3 weeks ago. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start daily TDF/FTC today and test for HIV at 3 months`,`Start event-driven 2-1-1 PrEP immediately`,`Defer PrEP, test for acute HIV with a 4th-generation antigen/antibody test or viral load, and offer PEP only if exposure is under 72 hours`,`Reassure him that PrEP is not needed because his partner is on ART`],answer:2,explanation:`Fever, sore throat and rash 3 weeks after a high-risk exposure suggest acute HIV; PrEP must be deferred until acute infection is excluded with an antigen/antibody test or viral load, because two-drug PrEP during acute HIV selects resistance. Starting daily or event-driven PrEP today ignores that risk, and his partner is not suppressed (15,000 copies/mL), so U=U does not yet apply.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the prevention of parent-to-child transmission of HIV in India. Describe the antenatal, intrapartum and postnatal interventions, infant prophylaxis, infant feeding and early infant diagnosis.`,openingLines:[`Parent-to-child transmission of HIV occurs in pregnancy, labour or breastfeeding, and without intervention affects 15-45% of exposed infants; with suppressive maternal ART it falls below 1%.`,`Under NACP Phase V, NACO offers universal antenatal HIV and syphilis testing, lifelong dolutegravir-based ART for every pregnant woman with HIV, risk-based infant prophylaxis, early infant diagnosis by DNA PCR and supported breastfeeding.`],answer:[{heading:`Timing and risk factors`,points:[`In utero 5-10%, intrapartum 10-20%, breastfeeding 5-20%.`,`High maternal viral load, new infection, late ART, prolonged rupture of membranes, mixed feeding, mastitis.`]},{heading:`Antenatal`,points:[`Universal dual HIV/syphilis RDT at first ANC visit; HBsAg; retest high-risk women in third trimester.`,`Lifelong TLD irrespective of CD4, same day if possible; folic acid; DTG safe.`,`Viral load late in third trimester; partner testing; cotrimoxazole if CD4 350 or less; TB screen and TPT.`,`Benzathine penicillin for syphilis at least 30 days before delivery.`]},{heading:`Intrapartum`,points:[`Vaginal delivery if suppressed; caesarean for obstetric indications (consider if VL over 1000).`,`Avoid prolonged rupture, ARM, episiotomy, instrumental delivery; universal precautions.`]},{heading:`Infant prophylaxis`,points:[`Low-risk: nevirapine daily for 6 weeks (10 or 15 mg by weight).`,`High-risk (ART under 4 weeks, VL over 1000, new infection): AZT + NVP 6 weeks, extended to 12 weeks if breastfed.`,`Cotrimoxazole from 6 weeks until HIV excluded.`]},{heading:`Feeding`,points:[`Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months with maternal ART; then complementary feeds with continued breastfeeding to at least 12 months.`,`Avoid mixed feeding; replacement feeding only if AFASS criteria met.`]},{heading:`Early infant diagnosis`,points:[`DBS DNA PCR at 6 weeks, 6 months, 12 months or 6 weeks after weaning; antibody at 18 months.`,`Positive PCR confirmed on second sample; start ABC + 3TC + DTG.`]},{heading:`EMTCT targets`,points:[`ANC coverage, HIV and syphilis testing and maternal ART each 95% or more; MTCT under 5% (breastfeeding) or under 2%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: ANC test - positive - TLD - VL in third trimester - delivery - risk-based infant prophylaxis - EID schedule`],markSplit:[{part:`Burden and timing of transmission`,marks:1},{part:`Antenatal and intrapartum interventions`,marks:3},{part:`Infant prophylaxis and feeding`,marks:3},{part:`Early infant diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`EMTCT targets`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PPTCT`,`EMTCT`,`dolutegravir`,`nevirapine prophylaxis`,`breastfeeding`,`DNA PCR`,`early infant diagnosis`,`syphilis`]},{id:`infectious-fever-hiv-special-situations-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the management of occupational exposure to HIV (post-exposure prophylaxis).`,openingLines:[`Post-exposure prophylaxis is a 28-day course of three antiretrovirals started within 72 hours, ideally within 2 hours, of an exposure to a potentially HIV-infected body fluid.`,`NACO 2021 recommends TLD once daily; the decision depends on the type of exposure and the source's HIV status.`],answer:[{heading:`Immediate steps`,points:[`Wash with soap and water; let it bleed; do not squeeze, scrub or use bleach; flush mucosa.`,`Report to the PEP officer; document the exposure.`]},{heading:`Assess`,points:[`Exposure grade: mild, moderate, severe; fluid type; time since exposure.`,`Source: HIV, HBV, HCV status (rapid test with consent).`,`Exposed person: baseline HIV, HBsAg, anti-HBs, anti-HCV, creatinine, pregnancy test.`]},{heading:`Decide and treat`,points:[`Source positive or unknown with mild-severe exposure: TLD once daily for 28 days.`,`Source negative: no PEP (unless window-period risk); intact skin: no PEP.`,`Hepatitis B: HBIG plus vaccine if non-immune and source positive or unknown.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Adherence and side-effect review; HIV test at 3 months; safe sex and no blood donation until then.`]}],mustDraw:[`PEP flow chart: first aid - grade exposure - source status - TLD 28 days - follow-up testing`],markSplit:[{part:`First aid and reporting`,marks:1},{part:`Assessment of exposure and source`,marks:1.5},{part:`PEP regimen and HBV`,marks:1.5},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PEP`,`needlestick`,`TLD`,`exposure grading`,`HBIG`,`72 hours`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on HIV disease and HIV-2`,`NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India, 2021`,`WHO Guidelines for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis, 2024`,`WHO Guidelines on long-acting injectable cabotegravir for HIV prevention, 2022; WHO recommendation on lenacapavir for HIV prevention, 2025`,`WHO Consolidated guidelines on HIV prevention, testing, treatment, service delivery and monitoring, 2021`,`WHO The role of HIV viral suppression in improving individual health and reducing transmission: policy brief, 2023`,`MoHFW Guidelines and Protocols: Medico-legal care for survivors/victims of sexual violence, 2014`,`NACO India HIV Estimates 2023: Technical Report`]});var r=[];r.push({id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail`,title:`Leptospirosis in depth: Weil disease, pulmonary haemorrhage and prophylaxis`,oneLiner:`**Leptospirosis** is a **zoonotic spirochaetal vasculitis** caught from **rat-urine-contaminated water or soil** through abraded skin or mucosa; most cases are a self-limiting biphasic fever, but 5-10% progress to **Weil disease (jaundice, AKI, bleeding)** or **severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome** - diagnosed by **IgM ELISA or MAT**, treated with **doxycycline (mild) or penicillin/ceftriaxone (severe)**, and prevented in the flood season with **doxycycline 200 mg once weekly**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`leptospirosis`,`Weil disease`,`Weil's syndrome`,`Leptospira interrogans`,`rat fever`,`field fever`,`Andaman haemorrhagic fever`,`severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome`,`SPHS`,`MAT`,`microscopic agglutination test`,`IgM ELISA`,`modified Faine's criteria`,`conjunctival suffusion`,`doxycycline prophylaxis`,`benzyl penicillin`,`ceftriaxone`,`NPPCL`,`flood fever`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Leptospirosis** is a systemic zoonosis caused by pathogenic spirochaetes of the genus **Leptospira** (mainly L. interrogans), with more than **250 pathogenic serovars** grouped into about 24 serogroups. [Harrison 22e]`,`The global burden is estimated at about **1 million cases and 59,000 deaths a year**, concentrated in tropical, low-income, flood-prone settings - more deaths than many better-known tropical diseases. [Costa et al, PLoS NTD 2015]`,`In India it is **endemic along the western and southern coast** - **Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Gujarat** - and in the **Andaman and Nicobar Islands**, where a pulmonary-haemorrhagic outbreak in 1988 was first called 'Andaman haemorrhagic fever'. [NCDC Leptospirosis Guidelines 2015]`,`Kerala consistently reports the **largest number of notified cases and deaths** in India, with surges after the **2018 and 2019 floods**. [NCDC 2024]`,`Cases rise sharply **2-4 weeks after heavy rainfall or flooding** (incubation plus exposure lag), peaking **July to November** in the south-west monsoon belt and later in Tamil Nadu with the north-east monsoon. [NCDC 2015]`,`It is a **notifiable disease** in India, reported through **IDSP on the IHIP portal**, and has a dedicated **Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis** under NCDC in endemic states. [NCDC 2024]`,`Leptospirosis accounts for an estimated **5-15% of acute undifferentiated fever** in endemic coastal districts during the monsoon - the reason it sits in every AUF panel there (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`Most infections are **mild or subclinical** (90% anicteric), but **5-10% develop severe disease** with a case fatality of **5-15%** for Weil disease and **over 50%** for severe pulmonary haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`The **maintenance host is the rat** (Rattus norvegicus, R. rattus), which carries leptospires lifelong in its **renal tubules** and sheds them in urine without illness; serogroup **Icterohaemorrhagiae** from rats causes most severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other reservoirs are **cattle (Hardjo, Pomona), pigs (Pomona), dogs (Canicola) and bandicoots** - livestock exposure explains rural cases away from rats. [Harrison 22e]`,`Humans are **accidental, dead-end hosts**; there is effectively **no person-to-person spread**, so isolation is unnecessary and standard precautions suffice. [Harrison 22e]`,`Leptospires survive for **weeks in warm, moist, neutral-pH soil and fresh water**, which is why paddy fields and stagnant floodwater stay infective long after the rain. [WHO 2003]`,`Entry is through **cuts and abrasions, softened (water-logged) skin, conjunctiva and oral or nasal mucosa**; ingestion of contaminated water is a minor route. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Occupational risk groups**: paddy and sugarcane farmers, **MGNREGS workers** clearing canals, sewage and sanitation workers, fishermen, veterinarians, abattoir and dairy workers, and miners. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Recreational and disaster risk**: wading or swimming in floodwater, rescue and relief work after floods, adventure water sports, and living in slums with **rodent infestation and open drains**. [NCDC 2015]`,`Walking **barefoot**, working with **open wounds**, and not using boots or gloves multiply the risk; this is where counselling and chemoprophylaxis are targeted. [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Leptospires are **highly motile corkscrew spirochaetes** that penetrate skin and mucosa, enter the blood within hours and seed **every organ during the leptospiraemic phase** (first 7-10 days). [Harrison 22e]`,`The core lesion is a **systemic vasculitis with endothelial damage and capillary leak**, explaining haemorrhages, conjunctival suffusion, pulmonary bleeding and hypotension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Leptospiral **LPS is weakly endotoxic** and signals through **TLR2 rather than TLR4** in humans; outer-membrane lipoproteins such as **LipL32** drive the cytokine storm and tubular injury. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kidney**: acute **tubulo-interstitial nephritis** with proximal tubular dysfunction - impaired sodium reabsorption increases distal delivery and **potassium wasting**, giving the characteristic **non-oliguric, hypokalaemic AKI**; oliguria is a late, severe sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver**: jaundice comes from **disruption of hepatocyte intercellular junctions and cholestasis** plus haemolysis, not necrosis - so **bilirubin is high (often above 20 mg/dL) while transaminases rise only modestly** (usually under 5 times normal) and liver failure is rare. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung**: endothelial injury of alveolar capillaries with **linear deposition of IgG and complement on the alveolar basement membrane** causes the **severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (SPHS)**, often without thrombocytopenia severe enough to explain it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Muscle**: direct invasion causes focal necrosis - hence **calf and lumbar myalgia and a raised CPK**, which helps separate leptospirosis from viral hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **immune phase** (after day 7) is driven by antibody and immune complexes: leptospires vanish from blood but persist in **kidney (urine), CSF and aqueous humour**, explaining aseptic meningitis and late uveitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why the drugs**: leptospires are **extracellular** and highly susceptible to **penicillins, third-generation cephalosporins, doxycycline and macrolides**; killing them can release antigen and provoke a **Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** in the first hours of treatment. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and clinical phases`,points:[`Incubation is **2-30 days, usually 5-14 days** - ask about exposure in the last month, not the last week. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospiraemic (acute) phase**, about 3-10 days: abrupt fever, rigors, severe headache, myalgia and conjunctival suffusion; organisms are in **blood and CSF** - the window for **PCR and blood culture**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immune phase**, day 7 to 30: antibodies appear, organisms are shed in **urine**; the patient may defervesce for 1-3 days and then relapse with **aseptic meningitis**, uveitis or rash - the classic **biphasic course** is often blurred in severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anicteric leptospirosis (about 90%)**: influenza-like, self-limiting fever, often with aseptic meningitis; mortality is very low. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Icteric leptospirosis - Weil disease (5-10%)**: the triad of **jaundice, acute kidney injury and haemorrhage**, often with myocarditis and altered sensorium; the phases merge into one continuous severe illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (SPHS)**: haemoptysis, breathlessness and bilateral alveolar infiltrates progressing to ARDS, sometimes **without jaundice**; it is now the leading cause of leptospirosis death in many Indian series. [Harrison 22e]`,`A practical bedside grading used in Indian protocols: **mild** (ambulant, no organ failure), **moderate** (needs admission - vomiting, jaundice, rising creatinine) and **severe** (shock, oliguria, pulmonary haemorrhage, altered sensorium, bleeding). [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Abrupt high fever with rigors**, intense **frontal or retro-orbital headache** and prostration are the commonest presenting complaints (over 90%). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe myalgia, especially of the calves and lumbar muscles**, is a key discriminating symptom - patients often cannot walk. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gastrointestinal symptoms - **nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhoea** - are common and can mimic acute abdomen, cholecystitis or pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cough, haemoptysis or breathlessness** in a febrile patient from a flood area is pulmonary leptospirosis until proved otherwise - haemoptysis may be the first sign of SPHS. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **reduced or dark urine, yellow eyes and bleeding** (epistaxis, gum bleeding, melaena) - the Weil disease symptoms. [NCDC 2015]`,`Take a **4-week exposure history**: floodwater, paddy, drains, sewage, animals, rodents at home, barefoot work, cuts on feet - this earns the epidemiological score in Faine's criteria. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**Atypical presentations**: aseptic meningitis, isolated myocarditis with arrhythmia, acalculous cholecystitis, acute pancreatitis, and **late anterior uveitis** weeks to months after the fever. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Conjunctival suffusion** - redness of the bulbar conjunctiva **without purulent discharge**, most marked on the palpebral fissure, appearing on day 3-4 - is the most specific bedside sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subconjunctival haemorrhage** with suffusion strongly suggests leptospirosis in the right setting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calf muscle tenderness** on squeezing, out of proportion to the fever, supports the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice** - often deep, with an orange tint (jaundice plus suffusion) - and a mildly **tender hepatomegaly**; splenomegaly is less common. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative bradycardia (Faget sign)** may be seen early; **tachycardia, hypotension, irregular pulse (AF, AV block)** indicate myocarditis or shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **petechiae, purpura and bleeding**; in the chest, **crackles and tachypnoea** may be the only clue before haemoptysis. [NCDC 2015]`,`Check **urine output and neck stiffness**; a transient maculopapular or petechial rash and lymphadenopathy occur in a minority. [Harrison 22e]`,`The absence of an **eschar** and the presence of **calf tenderness plus suffusion** help separate it from scrub typhus at the bedside. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Scrub typhus**: same season and AKI/ARDS, but look for an **eschar**, lymphadenopathy and bilirubin rarely above 5 mg/dL; both respond to doxycycline (see the Scrub typhus topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Falciparum malaria**: jaundice, AKI and ARDS also occur - **smear or RDT** is mandatory in every case; co-infection exists. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Dengue**: leucopenia and haemoconcentration rather than **neutrophilic leucocytosis**, and AKI or deep jaundice is unusual. [WHO 2025]`,`**Acute viral hepatitis**: transaminases in the **thousands**, little fever by the time of jaundice, no AKI or myalgia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hantavirus (HFRS)**: fever, AKI and haemorrhage after rodent exposure - rare in India but in the same differential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis with cholangitis, enteric fever and severe influenza or Goodpasture syndrome** for the pulmonary-renal picture - leptospirosis is the commonest infectious pulmonary-renal syndrome in the tropics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**CBC**: **neutrophilic leucocytosis** (often 10,000-30,000/uL) with **thrombocytopenia** in about half; a high ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine**: proteinuria, haematuria, granular casts and pyuria in the first week - an abnormal urine with fever is an early clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal**: raised creatinine with **low or normal potassium** (hypokalaemia in up to 40-45%), raised urea, hyponatraemia; **hyperkalaemia signals oliguric, late AKI**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LFT**: conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, ALP raised, **AST/ALT usually under 200-250 U/L**; **CPK raised** in about half; amylase may be raised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IgM ELISA** turns positive from **day 5-7** and is the practical test in district hospitals (NCDC sentinel labs); a negative test before day 5 does not exclude disease. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Microscopic agglutination test (MAT)** is the **reference serological test**: a **fourfold rise in paired sera**, or a single titre of **1:400 or more in endemic areas** (1:100-1:200 in non-endemic settings), is diagnostic; it needs live cultures and is done in reference labs (RMRC Port Blair, NCDC). [WHO 2003]`,`**PCR of blood (first 7-10 days) and urine (after the first week)** gives an early confirmed diagnosis before antibodies; **culture in EMJH medium** is slow (up to 13 weeks) and insensitive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dark-field microscopy is not recommended** - artefacts (fibrin threads) give false positives. [WHO 2003]`,`In severe disease: **chest X-ray** (patchy or diffuse alveolar shadows), ABG, ECG and troponin (myocarditis), coagulation profile, and a malaria smear in every case. [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Do not wait for serology** - start treatment on clinical and epidemiological suspicion; antibiotics are most effective in the **first 5 days**. [NCDC 2015]`,`Mild disease (adult): **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days** - also covers scrub typhus, the main look-alike. [NCDC 2015]`,`Alternatives: **amoxicillin 500 mg orally three times daily for 7 days** (pregnancy, children under 8 if preferred), or **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 3 days**. [NCDC 2015]`,`Children: **amoxicillin 25-50 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses** or **doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg twice daily** (short courses do not stain teeth), or azithromycin 10 mg/kg once daily. [IAP 2022]`,`Symptomatic care: **paracetamol only** (no NSAIDs - nephrotoxic and bleeding risk), oral fluids, and avoid IM injections. [ICMR 2022]`,`Review in **24-48 hours** with repeat creatinine, potassium, bilirubin and platelets; give written red flags (low urine, cough with blood, yellow eyes, drowsiness). [NCDC 2015]`,`Warn about a **Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** - fever, rigors and hypotension within hours of the first dose - which is usually mild and needs only fluids and antipyretics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for jaundice, creatinine above 1.5 mg/dL or oliguria, hypotension, breathlessness or haemoptysis, bleeding, altered sensorium, persistent vomiting, platelets under 50,000, pregnancy or myocarditis. [NCDC 2015]`,`Severe disease: **benzyl penicillin (crystalline penicillin G) 1.5 million units IV 6-hourly for 7 days**, or **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily for 7 days** (equivalent in trials), or cefotaxime 1 g IV 6-hourly. [Harrison 22e]`,`Where scrub typhus cannot be excluded, **add doxycycline 100 mg IV or orally twice daily** (or azithromycin) to the beta-lactam - beta-lactams do not cover Orientia. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fluids**: correct hypovolaemia cautiously with balanced crystalloid and replace **potassium** in polyuric AKI; avoid overload, which worsens pulmonary haemorrhage. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Dialysis early**: indications are oliguria not responding to fluids, hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload, uraemic encephalopathy or pulmonary haemorrhage; **early and daily dialysis** improved survival in a randomised trial. [Andrade et al, CJASN 2007]`,`**SPHS**: early ICU transfer, **lung-protective ventilation (tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight, plateau under 30 cmH2O, higher PEEP)**, prone position for severe ARDS, platelet and FFP support for bleeding. [SSC 2026]`,`**Corticosteroids (pulse methylprednisolone) and plasma exchange** have been used for pulmonary haemorrhage, but trials are small and conflicting - not routine; consider only in specialist ICUs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shock**: cultures, at least **30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid** guided by dynamic measures, and **noradrenaline for MAP 65 mmHg**; ECG monitoring for myocarditis-related arrhythmias. [SSC 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: leptospirosis can cause **miscarriage, fetal death and congenital infection**; treat with **amoxicillin or IV penicillin/ceftriaxone**; azithromycin is an alternative; avoid doxycycline for treatment courses. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Children**: usually milder, with less jaundice and AKI, but myocarditis, acalculous cholecystitis and pancreatitis are reported; doxycycline is acceptable for short courses at any age. [IAP 2022]`,`**Elderly and diabetics**: higher risk of AKI, myocarditis and death; lower threshold for admission and early nephrology input. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: no dose change for ceftriaxone or doxycycline; **penicillin G dose must be reduced** in severe renal impairment to avoid seizures. [BNF 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: start doxycycline or IV penicillin on clinical grounds, refer with a note of urine output and creatinine; do not delay for MAT or ELISA. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Post-flood clusters**: set up **fever camps**, line-list cases on IHIP, and offer chemoprophylaxis to relief workers and the exposed population. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Acute kidney injury** in up to 40-60% of severe cases; renal recovery is usually complete, though a few have persistent tubular defects. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary haemorrhage / ARDS** is the leading cause of death, with mortality **above 50%** once ventilation is needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myocarditis**: ST-T changes, AF, AV block and cardiogenic shock; ECG abnormalities are common in severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemorrhage and DIC**, thrombocytopenia, gastrointestinal bleeding and **haemolysis** add to the jaundice. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological**: aseptic meningitis (common, benign), encephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and **late uveitis** in up to 2-10% weeks to months later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognostic markers**: age over 40, **oliguria, hyperkalaemia, hypotension, pulmonary infiltrates or haemoptysis, altered sensorium, arrhythmia** and thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Overall case fatality in Indian hospital series ranges from **5% to 20%**, and is highest with combined pulmonary-renal involvement. [NCDC 2015]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`In hospital: **urine output hourly**, creatinine and potassium **daily** (twice daily in AKI), bilirubin and platelets every 24-48 hours, SpO2 and respiratory rate continuously in the first 72 hours. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Refer** to a centre with **dialysis and ventilation** at the first sign of oliguria, rising creatinine, haemoptysis, SpO2 under 94% or hypotension - SPHS can kill within 24-72 hours. [NCDC 2015]`,`Expect defervescence in **2-4 days**; jaundice can persist for weeks without meaning treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`After discharge, check **creatinine and urine at 2-4 weeks** and ask about **eye pain, redness or blurred vision** for 6 months (late uveitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Send **paired sera 10-14 days apart** for MAT when the first sample is negative or equivocal - a fourfold rise confirms the case for surveillance. [WHO 2003]`,`Notify every suspected and confirmed case on **IHIP**; a cluster triggers a rodent and environmental investigation. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, chemoprophylaxis and national programme`,points:[`**Pre-exposure chemoprophylaxis**: **doxycycline 200 mg orally once a week**, started at the beginning of exposure and continued throughout the period of exposure, for high-risk groups (farmers in paddy season, flood relief workers, sanitation workers). [NCDC 2015]`,`Doxycycline prophylaxis reduces **clinical disease and death** more than it reduces seroconversion; the evidence base is modest (small trials, Cochrane 2009), so it adds to, not replaces, protective measures. [Cochrane 2009]`,`In **pregnancy and young children**, where weekly doxycycline is avoided, **amoxicillin or azithromycin** is used under state protocols; follow the local state guideline for doses. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Personal protection**: gumboots, gloves, waterproof dressings over cuts, avoid wading or swimming in floodwater, and wash and dry feet after exposure. [NCDC 2015]`,`**Environmental**: rodent control, safe disposal of garbage, drainage, and **vaccination of cattle and dogs**; there is **no licensed human vaccine in India**. [NCDC 2015]`,`The NCDC **Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis** supports surveillance, lab strengthening (IgM ELISA at sentinel sites), case-management training and IEC in endemic states, with a One Health approach linking veterinary services. [NCDC 2024]`,`Kerala runs pre-monsoon **'doxycycline campaigns'** for MGNREGS workers, farmers and fishermen, distributing weekly doxycycline through primary health centres and **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs**. [Kerala DHS 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows (2022-23 updates)** place leptospirosis inside the **AUF syndromic algorithm** - empirical **doxycycline** for outpatients after day 3-5 of undiagnosed fever and **ceftriaxone plus doxycycline** for severe febrile illness. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**NCDC flood and monsoon advisories (2023-2025)** reiterate **doxycycline 200 mg weekly** for flood-exposed populations and mandatory reporting of AUF clusters on the **IHIP** digital platform. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** guidance applies to septic leptospirosis: screening with NEWS2/MEWS rather than qSOFA alone, **balanced crystalloids**, dynamic fluid assessment and MAP 65 mmHg. [SSC 2026]`,`Molecular diagnosis (**real-time PCR targeting lipL32**) is increasingly available in Indian VRDL network labs and is preferred over waiting for MAT in the first week. [ICMR VRDL 2024]`,`There is still **no change in first-line antibiotics** - penicillin, ceftriaxone and doxycycline remain equivalent; recent meta-analyses do not support routine steroids in pulmonary haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Weil disease - aetiology, clinical features, diagnosis and management'** - always draw the biphasic curve with the test windows (blood PCR/culture early; IgM, MAT and urine later). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Why is the AKI hypokalaemic and non-oliguric?' - **proximal tubular sodium wasting with increased distal delivery** causes potassium loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Bilirubin 25 mg/dL with ALT 120 - what fever?' - **leptospirosis** (cholestasis plus haemolysis, not necrosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **dismissing a negative IgM on day 3** - it is too early; repeat after day 5-7 or send PCR. [NCDC 2015]`,`Common mistake: giving **NSAIDs or aminoglycosides** in a febrile patient with rising creatinine. [ICMR 2022]`,`Know the **modified Faine's criteria** (WHO SEARO 2009/2012): clinical (A), epidemiological (B) and laboratory (C) scores; **A or A+B of 26 or more, or A+B+C of 25 or more**, is presumptive leptospirosis. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`Know the prophylaxis dose cold: **doxycycline 200 mg once weekly** - not 100 mg daily, which is the treatment dose. [NCDC 2015]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in leptospirosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (mild)`,`100 mg oral BD; child 2.2 mg/kg BD`,`7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis (take upright with water), photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Amoxicillin (mild)`,`500 mg oral TDS; child 25-50 mg/kg/day in 3 doses`,`7 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea; safe in pregnancy; J-H reaction`],[`Azithromycin (mild)`,`500 mg oral OD; child 10 mg/kg OD`,`3 days`,`GI upset, QT prolongation; pregnancy alternative`],[`Benzyl penicillin G (severe)`,`1.5 million units IV 6-hourly`,`7 days`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, anaphylaxis; reduce dose in severe renal failure (seizures)`],[`Ceftriaxone (severe)`,`1-2 g IV OD; child 50-75 mg/kg/day`,`7 days`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; add doxycycline if scrub possible`],[`Cefotaxime (severe)`,`1 g IV 6-hourly`,`7 days`,`As ceftriaxone; useful in neonates`],[`Doxycycline (prophylaxis)`,`200 mg oral once weekly`,`From start of exposure until it ends`,`Take with food and water; not for pregnancy or young children`]]},{heading:`Clinical forms and phases of leptospirosis`,columns:[`Form / phase`,`Timing`,`Features`,`Best test in this window`],rows:[[`Leptospiraemic phase`,`Days 1-7 (up to 10)`,`Fever, rigors, headache, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion`,`Blood PCR, blood culture`],[`Immune phase`,`Day 7 to 30`,`Relapse of fever, aseptic meningitis, uveitis, leptospiruria`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, urine PCR`],[`Anicteric (90%)`,`Biphasic`,`Influenza-like, self-limiting`,`IgM ELISA from day 5`],[`Weil disease (5-10%)`,`Continuous severe illness`,`Jaundice, AKI, bleeding, myocarditis`,`IgM ELISA/MAT; PCR if early`],[`SPHS`,`Often day 4-6`,`Haemoptysis, ARDS, alveolar infiltrates`,`PCR; MAT/ELISA`]]},{heading:`Modified Faine's criteria (WHO SEARO) - key items`,columns:[`Part`,`Item`,`Score`],rows:[[`A - clinical`,`Headache; fever; temperature above 39 C`,`2 each`],[`A - clinical`,`Conjunctival suffusion (bilateral); meningism; muscle pain (calf)`,`4 each`],[`A - clinical`,`Suffusion plus meningism plus myalgia together`,`10`],[`A - clinical`,`Jaundice 1; albuminuria or nitrogen retention 2`,`1-2`],[`B - epidemiological`,`Rainfall 5; contact with contaminated environment 4; animal contact 1`,`1-5`],[`C - laboratory`,`IgM ELISA, SAT or single high MAT titre 15; rising MAT titre or PCR 25; culture isolation`,`15-25; culture = certain`],[`Interpretation`,`A or A+B 26 or more, or A+B+C 25 or more`,`Presumptive leptospirosis`]]},{heading:`Leptospirosis versus look-alikes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Leptospirosis`,`Scrub typhus`,`Falciparum malaria`,`Dengue`,`Viral hepatitis`],rows:[[`Key bedside sign`,`Conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness`,`Eschar, nodes`,`Pallor, splenomegaly`,`Flush, rash, tourniquet test`,`Jaundice after fever settles`],[`WBC`,`Neutrophilic leucocytosis`,`Normal or high`,`Normal or low`,`Leucopenia`,`Normal or low`],[`Bilirubin vs ALT`,`Bilirubin very high, ALT modest`,`Both modest`,`High bilirubin (haemolysis)`,`ALT raised, little jaundice`,`ALT in thousands`],[`Kidney`,`Non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI`,`AKI possible`,`Oliguric AKI`,`Rare`,`Rare`],[`Lung`,`Pulmonary haemorrhage`,`ARDS, pneumonitis`,`ARDS`,`Effusion from leak`,`None`],[`Confirm`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, PCR`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`,`Smear, RDT`,`NS1, IgM`,`IgM HAV/HEV, HBsAg`]]},{heading:`Laboratory cut-offs and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding that supports leptospirosis`,`Caveat`],rows:[[`MAT`,`Fourfold rise in paired sera, or single titre 1:400 or more (endemic)`,`Reference lab only; cross-reacts between serogroups`],[`IgM ELISA`,`Positive from day 5-7`,`False negative early; may persist months`],[`PCR`,`Blood positive days 1-7/10; urine after week 1`,`Turns negative after antibiotics`],[`Serum potassium`,`Low or normal despite AKI`,`Hyperkalaemia means oliguric late AKI - dialysis`],[`Bilirubin and ALT`,`Bilirubin often above 20 mg/dL, ALT under 250 U/L`,`ALT in thousands suggests hepatitis`],[`CPK`,`Raised in about half`,`Also raised in rhabdomyolysis of any cause`]]}],redFlags:[`**Haemoptysis, breathlessness or SpO2 under 94%** - pulmonary haemorrhage; ICU now`,`**Oliguria (under 0.5 mL/kg/h)** or creatinine rising, or **hyperkalaemia** - dialysis centre`,`**Hypotension or shock** (SBP under 90 mmHg) despite fluids`,`**Deep jaundice with bleeding** (epistaxis, melaena, haematuria) or platelets under 50,000`,`**Altered sensorium or seizures**`,`**Irregular pulse, ECG changes or chest pain** - myocarditis`,`**Pregnancy** with suspected leptospirosis`],pearls:[`**Suffusion, calves, kidneys, jaundice** - think rat urine.`,`Leptospirosis **AKI is non-oliguric and hypokalaemic**; hyperkalaemia means late, severe disease.`,`**Bilirubin out of proportion to transaminases** - leptospirosis or falciparum, not hepatitis.`,`**Blood PCR in week 1, urine PCR and serology after week 1** - the test follows the phase.`,`**MAT is the reference test**: fourfold rise, or single titre 1:400 or more in endemic areas.`,`Treat on suspicion: **doxycycline for mild, penicillin G or ceftriaxone for severe** - equally effective.`,`**Prophylaxis is doxycycline 200 mg once weekly** throughout exposure.`,`Death comes from the **lung** (SPHS) more than the kidney now - watch respiratory rate and SpO2.`,`Leptospirosis is the commonest **infectious pulmonary-renal syndrome** in the tropics.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q1`,stem:`A 35-year-old sewage worker from Mumbai presents on day 6 of fever with calf pain, deep jaundice and reduced urine. Creatinine 3.2 mg/dL, potassium 3.1 mmol/L, bilirubin 18 mg/dL, ALT 110 U/L, WBC 18,000/uL. Which treatment is most appropriate?`,options:[`Oral doxycycline 200 mg once weekly`,`IV benzyl penicillin 1.5 million units 6-hourly for 7 days`,`IV gentamicin with metronidazole`,`Oral cefixime 200 mg twice daily for 14 days`],answer:1,explanation:`This is Weil disease (jaundice, AKI with hypokalaemia, leucocytosis, bilirubin far above ALT) and needs IV therapy: benzyl penicillin 1.5 MU 6-hourly or ceftriaxone for 7 days. Weekly doxycycline 200 mg is the prophylaxis dose, not treatment. Gentamicin is nephrotoxic and has no place in leptospiral AKI. Oral cefixime is an enteric-fever regimen and is inadequate for severe leptospirosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q2`,stem:`A farmer with suspected leptospirosis is seen on day 3 of illness. Which test is most likely to confirm the diagnosis at this point?`,options:[`Leptospira IgM ELISA`,`Microscopic agglutination test on a single sample`,`Real-time PCR on blood`,`Dark-field microscopy of urine`],answer:2,explanation:`In the leptospiraemic phase (first 7-10 days) organisms are in blood, so blood PCR is the test of choice on day 3. IgM ELISA turns positive only from day 5-7, and MAT needs antibodies (ideally paired sera), so both may be negative this early. Dark-field microscopy is unreliable because fibrin threads mimic spirochaetes, and urine shedding starts in the immune phase.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q3`,stem:`What is the recommended chemoprophylaxis for an adult flood-relief volunteer in Kerala?`,options:[`Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily throughout exposure`,`Azithromycin 1 g single dose after exposure`,`Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 7 days`,`Doxycycline 200 mg once weekly from the start of exposure until it ends`],answer:3,explanation:`Indian national guidelines advise doxycycline 200 mg once a week, starting at the beginning of exposure and continuing throughout it, for high-risk groups such as flood-relief workers. Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily is the treatment dose for mild disease, not prophylaxis. Amoxicillin for 7 days is a treatment course, and a single post-exposure azithromycin dose is not the standard regimen.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q4`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains the hypokalaemia seen in leptospiral acute kidney injury?`,options:[`Impaired proximal tubular sodium reabsorption increasing distal sodium delivery and potassium secretion`,`Hyperaldosteronism from adrenal invasion`,`Gastrointestinal potassium loss from diarrhoea alone`,`Intracellular shift from insulin release`],answer:0,explanation:`Leptospiral outer-membrane proteins injure the proximal tubule, so sodium reabsorption fails. More sodium reaches the distal nephron, where it is exchanged for potassium, giving non-oliguric, potassium-wasting AKI. Adrenal invasion is not a feature. Diarrhoea can add to the loss but does not explain the renal wasting, and an insulin-driven shift plays no part.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-q5`,stem:`A patient with leptospirosis develops haemoptysis and SpO2 of 86% on room air on day 5. Chest X-ray shows bilateral alveolar shadows. What is the most important next step?`,options:[`Switch the antibiotic to meropenem`,`Transfer to ICU for lung-protective ventilation and organ support`,`Give oral prednisolone and continue ward care`,`Transfuse platelets to a target above 1 lakh and observe`],answer:1,explanation:`This is severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome, the leading cause of death in leptospirosis, and needs immediate ICU care with lung-protective ventilation (6 mL/kg), PEEP and dialysis if needed. Leptospires stay sensitive to penicillin and ceftriaxone, so switching to meropenem adds nothing. Steroids are unproven and not routine. Platelets help bleeding but do not treat the alveolar capillary injury, and observing on the ward is unsafe.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the epidemiology, pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of leptospirosis. Add a note on Weil disease and chemoprophylaxis.`,openingLines:[`Leptospirosis is a zoonotic spirochaetal infection by pathogenic Leptospira, acquired through abraded skin or mucosa from water or soil contaminated with the urine of rats and other animals.`,`It is usually a self-limiting biphasic fever, but 5-10% develop Weil disease (jaundice, AKI, haemorrhage) or pulmonary haemorrhage, with high mortality.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`About 1 million cases and 59,000 deaths a year worldwide.`,`India: Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Andaman; post-monsoon and post-flood peaks.`,`Reservoir rats (renal carriage); cattle, pigs, dogs; occupational - farmers, sewage workers, MGNREGS.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Entry through skin and mucosa, leptospiraemia, systemic vasculitis with capillary leak.`,`Kidney: tubulo-interstitial nephritis, proximal Na wasting, non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI.`,`Liver: cholestasis, junction disruption - high bilirubin, modest ALT; lung: alveolar haemorrhage.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 5-14 days; leptospiraemic phase (fever, headache, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion).`,`Immune phase: aseptic meningitis, uveitis.`,`Weil disease: jaundice, AKI, bleeding, myocarditis; SPHS: haemoptysis, ARDS.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`CBC neutrophilia, thrombocytopenia; urine casts; low K with raised creatinine; high CPK.`,`Blood PCR (week 1), IgM ELISA from day 5-7, MAT fourfold rise or 1:400 or more; urine PCR later.`,`Modified Faine's criteria for presumptive diagnosis.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Mild: doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days, or amoxicillin, or azithromycin.`,`Severe: penicillin G 1.5 MU IV 6-hourly or ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV OD x 7 days; add doxycycline if scrub possible.`,`Supportive: K replacement, early daily dialysis, lung-protective ventilation for SPHS; no NSAIDs.`]},{heading:`Prevention and chemoprophylaxis`,points:[`Doxycycline 200 mg once weekly throughout exposure for high-risk groups.`,`Boots, gloves, wound cover, rodent control, animal vaccination; no human vaccine in India.`,`Notify on IHIP; NCDC Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Biphasic course with fever curve, leptospiraemia, antibodies and the test windows`,`Table of mild versus severe treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and transmission`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and Weil disease`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Chemoprophylaxis and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Weil disease`,`MAT`,`IgM ELISA`,`doxycycline 200 mg weekly`,`penicillin G`,`SPHS`,`hypokalaemic AKI`]},{id:`infectious-fever-leptospirosis-detail-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on pulmonary haemorrhage in leptospirosis.`,openingLines:[`Severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome (SPHS) is alveolar haemorrhage from leptospiral injury of alveolar capillaries, often presenting with haemoptysis and ARDS around day 4-6.`,`It is now the leading cause of death in leptospirosis, with mortality above 50% once ventilation is required.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Endothelial damage with linear IgG and complement on the alveolar basement membrane.`,`Thrombocytopenia contributes but does not fully explain the bleeding.`]},{heading:`Recognition`,points:[`Cough, haemoptysis, tachypnoea, falling SpO2, bilateral alveolar infiltrates.`,`May occur without jaundice; often with AKI (pulmonary-renal syndrome).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`ICU, lung-protective ventilation 6 mL/kg, PEEP, prone position; avoid fluid overload.`,`IV penicillin G or ceftriaxone; early dialysis; platelet and FFP support.`,`Steroids and plasma exchange unproven - not routine.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Poor markers: oliguria, hyperkalaemia, hypotension, altered sensorium; mortality over 50% if ventilated.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: haemoptysis in febrile patient - SpO2, CXR - ICU - ventilation and dialysis`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical recognition`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Prognosis`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`SPHS`,`alveolar haemorrhage`,`ARDS`,`lung-protective ventilation`,`pulmonary-renal syndrome`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Leptospirosis chapter`,`National Guidelines: Diagnosis, Case Management, Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis, NCDC, MoHFW, 2015`,`WHO/ILS. Human leptospirosis: guidance for diagnosis, surveillance and control, 2003`,`WHO SEARO. Informal expert consultation on surveillance, diagnosis and risk reduction of leptospirosis (modified Faine's criteria), 2009`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India - Acute Undifferentiated Fever, 2022-2023`,`Costa F et al. Global morbidity and mortality of leptospirosis: a systematic review. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2015`,`Andrade L et al. Daily versus alternate-day haemodialysis in leptospirosis-associated AKI. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol 2007`,`Surviving Sepsis Campaign: International Guidelines for Management of Sepsis and Septic Shock, 2026`]}),r.push({id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral`,title:`Chikungunya, Zika and other arboviral fevers`,oneLiner:`**Arboviral fevers** are arthropod-borne viral infections; in India the ones beyond dengue and JE are **chikungunya** (Aedes, alphavirus - fever with **disabling polyarthritis** that becomes **chronic in a third**), **Zika** (Aedes flavivirus - mild rash illness that causes **congenital Zika syndrome** in pregnancy), tick-borne **Kyasanur Forest disease** and **CCHF**, Culex-borne **West Nile**, and the bat-borne zoonosis **Nipah** in Kerala - all managed supportively with **paracetamol, no NSAIDs until dengue is excluded**, and controlled by vector and infection-prevention measures.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`chikungunya`,`CHIKV`,`chronic chikungunya arthritis`,`Zika`,`congenital Zika syndrome`,`microcephaly`,`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`KFD`,`monkey fever`,`CCHF`,`Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever`,`West Nile virus`,`Nipah`,`Chandipura`,`Aedes albopictus`,`Haemaphysalis spinigera`,`Hyalomma`,`hydroxychloroquine`,`methotrexate`,`ribavirin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Arboviruses** are viruses maintained in nature by **blood-feeding arthropods** (mosquitoes, ticks, sandflies); the human syndromes are **fever-arthralgia-rash, haemorrhagic fever and encephalitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chikungunya** (Makonde for 'that which bends up') re-emerged in India in **2005-06** after 32 years, with about **1.4 million suspected cases in 2006**, driven by the **E1-A226V mutation** that adapted the virus to **Aedes albopictus**. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`India now reports chikungunya every year under **NCVBDC**, with the largest numbers from **Karnataka, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Delhi**, mostly July-November with dengue. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Zika** was first confirmed in India in **Ahmedabad (2016-17)**, then caused outbreaks in **Jaipur (2018)**, **Kerala (2021)** and **Maharashtra and Karnataka (2023-24)**, including clusters among pregnant women in Pune in 2024. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**Kyasanur Forest disease (KFD, 'monkey fever')** was discovered in **Shimoga, Karnataka in 1957** and has spread along the Western Ghats to **Kerala (Wayanad), Goa, Maharashtra (Sindhudurg) and Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris)**, with about 400-500 cases a year and a case fatality of 3-10%. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever (CCHF)** was first documented in India in **Gujarat in 2011** as a **nosocomial cluster**, with later cases in Gujarat and Rajasthan; case fatality is **10-40%**. [NCDC 2019]`,`**West Nile virus** circulates in south India; human encephalitis cases and deaths have been reported from **Kerala (Alappuzha, Malappuram, Kozhikode)** repeatedly since 2011, including 2024. [Kerala DHS 2024]`,`**Nipah virus** - a bat-borne paramyxovirus, not an arbovirus but in the same fever-encephalitis differential - caused outbreaks in **West Bengal (2001, 2007)** and **Kerala (2018, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2024, 2025)** with case fatality of **40-75%** (see the Emerging and epidemic infections topic). [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, vectors and risk factors`,points:[`**Chikungunya virus** is an **alphavirus (Togaviridae)**; vectors are **Aedes aegypti** (urban, indoor) and **Aedes albopictus** (peri-urban, outdoor), both **day-biting**, breeding in stored clean water. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zika virus** is a **flavivirus**; spread by Aedes, and also **sexually, vertically (transplacental), by transfusion and organ transplant** - the sexual route is unique among common arboviruses. [WHO 2025]`,`**KFD virus** is a tick-borne **flavivirus** spread by **Haemaphysalis spinigera** nymphs; **langurs and bonnet macaques** are amplifying hosts, and **monkey deaths in the forest herald human cases**. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`KFD risk groups are **forest workers, cashew and areca nut farmers, firewood and dry-leaf collectors and cattle grazers**; the season is **November to June**, peaking January-April. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF virus** is a **nairovirus (Bunyavirales)** spread by **Hyalomma ticks**, by **slaughtering viraemic livestock** and **person-to-person through blood and body fluids** - so health-care workers are at high risk. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile virus** is a flavivirus maintained in **birds and Culex mosquitoes**; humans and horses are dead-end hosts; risk is highest in the **elderly, diabetics and immunosuppressed**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah** spreads from **Pteropus fruit bats** through bat-contaminated fruit or raw **date-palm sap**, via pigs (Malaysia 1998), and **person to person** through respiratory secretions in hospitals and families. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chandipura virus** (a sandfly-borne vesiculovirus) caused a large outbreak of **acute encephalitis with high mortality in children in Gujarat in 2024** - remember it in paediatric AES. [WHO DON 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Chikungunya** replicates in **fibroblasts, synovial macrophages, muscle satellite cells and tendons**, causing very high viraemia (up to 10^9 copies/mL) and intense type I interferon and IL-6 responses - hence the abrupt fever and severe joint pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic chikungunya arthritis** results from **persistent viral RNA and antigen in synovial macrophages** driving a chronic inflammatory synovitis (IL-6, GM-CSF, Th1), which can become **erosive and RA-like**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zika** is **neurotropic** for **neural progenitor cells**: infection of the fetal brain causes apoptosis and failed cortical development - **microcephaly, subcortical calcifications and ventriculomegaly**; risk is highest with first-trimester infection. [WHO 2025]`,`**Zika-associated Guillain-Barre syndrome** is post-infectious and immune-mediated (often AMAN or AIDP pattern) and appears within days of the rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**KFD and CCHF** cause **endothelial and hepatocyte injury with platelet dysfunction and DIC**, producing a **viral haemorrhagic fever**; KFD then has an immune-mediated **second neurological phase** in some patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**West Nile virus** crosses the blood-brain barrier and infects **neurons of the brainstem, basal ganglia and anterior horn cells**, explaining encephalitis, tremor and **asymmetric acute flaccid paralysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah** causes **vasculitis with endothelial syncytia** in brain and lung, giving encephalitis and ARDS; relapsing and late-onset encephalitis can occur months later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why no NSAIDs early**: chikungunya and Zika co-circulate with **dengue**, where NSAIDs and aspirin increase bleeding; paracetamol is used until dengue is excluded. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**By syndrome**: fever-arthralgia-rash (**chikungunya, Zika, dengue**), viral haemorrhagic fever (**KFD, CCHF, dengue, yellow fever**), and encephalitis (**JE, West Nile, Chandipura, Nipah, KFD late phase**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By vector**: Aedes (dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever), Culex (JE, West Nile), ticks (KFD, CCHF), sandflies (Chandipura). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chikungunya phases**: **acute (up to 3 weeks)**, **post-acute (3 weeks to 3 months)** and **chronic (over 3 months)** - each with different treatment. [French SPILF 2014; NCVBDC 2016]`,`Chronic chikungunya is further split into **inflammatory arthritis** (RA-like, spondyloarthritis-like, undifferentiated polyarthritis) and **non-inflammatory musculoskeletal pain** (tendinitis, carpal tunnel, fibromyalgia-like). [French SPILF 2014]`,`**Atypical/severe chikungunya** (WHO): neurological (encephalitis, GBS), cardiac (myocarditis), renal, hepatic, bullous skin disease, and **neonatal chikungunya** - these patients need admission. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**Biosafety level**: CCHF and Nipah are **risk group 4 / BSL-3-4 pathogens** - samples go only to **NIV Pune** (or designated BSL-3 labs such as NIV Kerala unit) with triple packaging. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Chikungunya**: incubation **3-7 days**; abrupt high fever with **severe symmetrical polyarthralgia** of wrists, hands, ankles and feet - the patient is **bent and immobile**; over 90% of infected people are symptomatic. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chikungunya also gives **maculopapular rash (40-50%)**, headache, myalgia, and in some **aphthous ulcers and facial oedema**; fever lasts 3-5 days, but joint pain persists. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`**Zika**: about **80% are asymptomatic**; the rest have **low-grade fever, pruritic maculopapular rash, non-purulent conjunctivitis and arthralgia** for 2-7 days. [WHO 2025]`,`**KFD**: incubation 3-8 days, then **sudden fever, headache, severe myalgia and prostration**, with bleeding from gums, nose and gut; **10-20% get a second phase** after 1-2 weeks afebrile with meningoencephalitis. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF**: after a **tick bite (1-3 days) or blood exposure (5-6 days)**, abrupt fever, myalgia, dizziness, neck pain, photophobia and vomiting, followed on day 3-5 by **haemorrhage - large ecchymoses**, epistaxis, haematemesis. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile**: most are asymptomatic; about 20% get **West Nile fever**; under 1% get neuroinvasive disease - **encephalitis, meningitis or polio-like flaccid paralysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: fever, headache and drowsiness progressing over days to **encephalitis and coma**, often with **cough and breathlessness**; ask about **fruit bats, half-eaten fruit, date-palm sap and contact with a sick person**. [NCDC 2024]`,`In every arboviral history ask for **travel or residence** (Western Ghats forest - KFD; Gujarat/Rajasthan livestock - CCHF; Kerala - Nipah, West Nile), **pregnancy** and **sexual partners' travel** (Zika). [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Chikungunya: **swollen, tender small joints** with **tenosynovitis**; joint effusions are common; pain prevents a firm grip. [Harrison 22e]`,`**'Chik sign'**: **brownish hyperpigmentation of the nose and face** appearing in or after the acute illness is a useful bedside clue in India. [Indian J Dermatol 2016]`,`Zika: fine **pruritic maculopapular rash** spreading from face to limbs, with **conjunctival hyperaemia without discharge**; in a newborn look for **head circumference below the 3rd centile**. [WHO 2025]`,`KFD: **conjunctival suffusion, papulovesicular lesions on the soft palate, cervical lymphadenopathy**, bradycardia and hypotension; bleeding signs. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`CCHF: **petechiae progressing to large ecchymoses and haematomas** at injection sites, hepatomegaly; shock and bleeding indicate poor prognosis. [WHO 2023]`,`West Nile and Nipah: **altered sensorium, tremor, myoclonus, cranial nerve palsies**, flaccid weakness with **preserved sensation** (West Nile), or **segmental myoclonus, areflexia, hypertension and tachycardia** (Nipah brainstem involvement). [Harrison 22e]`,`Check every febrile patient for **pulse pressure, capillary refill and tourniquet test** - dengue shock must be excluded first. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Dengue**: retro-orbital pain, leucopenia, **rising haematocrit and thrombocytopenia**, critical phase at defervescence; joint pain is milder than chikungunya. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chikungunya**: arthritis dominates, **lymphopenia**, platelets usually normal or mildly low; bleeding and shock are rare. [WHO 2025]`,`**Zika**: mildest of the three, with **conjunctivitis and itch** - and the important consequence is in pregnancy. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chronic chikungunya arthritis versus rheumatoid arthritis**: history of an epidemic febrile onset, positive CHIKV IgG, and usually **RF and anti-CCP negative**. [French SPILF 2014]`,`**Other fever-with-rash**: measles, rubella, parvovirus B19, rickettsial spotted fever, drug rash and acute HIV (see the Approach to fever with rash topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemorrhagic fevers**: dengue, falciparum malaria, leptospirosis, meningococcaemia and severe scrub typhus are far more common than KFD or CCHF and must be tested for. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Encephalitis**: JE, HSV, bacterial meningitis, cerebral malaria, scrub typhus and Chandipura; **seasonal and geographic clustering** points to the virus. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`First line in all: **CBC (lymphopenia in chikungunya; thrombocytopenia and leucopenia in KFD/CCHF), dengue NS1/IgM, malaria RDT/smear**, LFT, creatinine. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Chikungunya RT-PCR** is positive in the **first 5-7 days** (high viraemia); **IgM ELISA (NIV kit)** from **day 5-7**, persisting 2-3 months; IgG confirms past infection in chronic arthritis. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`**Zika RT-PCR**: serum up to **7 days** and **urine up to 14 days or longer**; **IgM cross-reacts with dengue**, so PRNT is needed for serological confirmation. [WHO 2025]`,`Zika in pregnancy: **serial ultrasound every 3-4 weeks** for microcephaly, calcifications and ventriculomegaly; **amniotic fluid RT-PCR** can be considered after 15 weeks. [CDC 2024]`,`**KFD**: RT-PCR of blood in the first week, IgM ELISA after; samples to **NIV Pune or the Shimoga VRDL**. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF**: RT-PCR of blood in a **BSL-3/4 lab (NIV Pune)**; coagulation profile, platelets, AST/ALT; samples packed and transported under strict biosafety. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile**: **IgM in CSF** is diagnostic of neuroinvasive disease; CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis; MRI may show thalamic and basal ganglia lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: **RT-PCR of throat swab, urine, CSF and blood** at NIV Pune or a designated state BSL-3 lab (Kozhikode); CSF and MRI (multiple small hyperintensities) support. [NCDC 2024]`,`In chronic chikungunya, check **ESR/CRP, RF, anti-CCP, HLA-B27 where relevant, and X-rays** before DMARDs, and screen for **hepatitis B and C** before methotrexate. [French SPILF 2014]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`There is **no specific antiviral** for chikungunya, Zika, KFD or West Nile; care is supportive. [WHO 2025]`,`Acute chikungunya: rest, oral fluids, **paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly (max 4 g/day)**, cold compresses; **no NSAIDs or aspirin until dengue is excluded** (usually after day 5-7). [WHO 2025]`,`Once dengue is excluded, add an NSAID such as **naproxen 250-500 mg twice daily** or ibuprofen for 1-2 weeks, with a PPI in older adults; avoid in CKD and pregnancy after 20 weeks. [NCVBDC 2016]`,`**Avoid corticosteroids in the acute phase** - they give rebound arthritis and may worsen viraemia. [WHO 2025]`,`Post-acute (3 weeks to 3 months): NSAIDs; if inadequate, a short course of **prednisolone 10 mg/day for 5 days, tapered over about 10 days**; physiotherapy and joint exercises. [French SPILF 2014]`,`Chronic inflammatory arthritis (over 3 months): **methotrexate 7.5-25 mg once weekly with folic acid**, or **hydroxychloroquine 200-400 mg/day** and **sulfasalazine 1-3 g/day** in combination - manage as RA with a rheumatologist. [French SPILF 2014]`,`Zika: rest, fluids, paracetamol and antihistamine for itch; **counsel pregnant women and couples** and refer for antenatal ultrasound. [WHO 2025]`,`Give **mosquito-bite protection during the viraemic first week** (bed net, repellent, long sleeves) so the patient does not infect household Aedes. [NCVBDC 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit chikungunya** with **atypical or severe features** (encephalitis, myocarditis, bullous lesions, hepatitis, AKI), in **neonates, the elderly over 65 with comorbidity**, and with dehydration or unbearable pain. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**KFD**: supportive ICU care - fluids, **platelet and FFP replacement** for bleeding, management of shock and encephalitis; no antiviral is proven. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF**: isolate in a single room with **contact and droplet precautions plus airborne precautions for aerosol-generating procedures**; full PPE (gown, gloves, N95, eye shield); notify immediately. [WHO 2023]`,`CCHF supportive care: blood products, fluids, organ support; **ribavirin** (oral or IV, WHO regimen **30 mg/kg loading, then 15 mg/kg 6-hourly for 4 days, then 7.5 mg/kg 8-hourly for 6 days**) is used in India but evidence is weak and **favipiravir** is under study. [WHO 2023]`,`**Needlestick exposure to CCHF**: monitor temperature for 14 days; post-exposure oral ribavirin is used in some protocols. [WHO 2023]`,`**West Nile neuroinvasive disease**: supportive - airway, seizure control, raised ICP management, ventilation for flaccid respiratory paralysis; IVIG and interferon are not proven. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: isolate with **airborne, droplet and contact precautions**, supportive ICU care; the **m102.4 monoclonal antibody** and **remdesivir** are used only under ICMR compassionate or trial protocols. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy and Zika**: infection at any trimester can cause **congenital Zika syndrome** (microcephaly, calcifications, chorioretinal atrophy, arthrogryposis, hypertonia), highest risk in the **first trimester**; about **5-10% of infected pregnancies** have Zika-associated defects. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pregnancy and chikungunya**: no teratogenicity, but **intrapartum viraemia transmits to about half of neonates**, causing **neonatal chikungunya** (fever, poor feeding, rash, encephalopathy, myocarditis) in days 3-7 of life; **caesarean section does not prevent** transmission. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`Neonates of mothers febrile with chikungunya at delivery need **7 days of observation** in hospital. [WHO SEARO 2009]`,`**Breastfeeding** is advised to continue in chikungunya and Zika - benefits outweigh the theoretical risk. [WHO 2025]`,`**Elderly and comorbid**: higher risk of atypical chikungunya, decompensation of diabetes, heart failure and CKD, and of **chronic arthritis**; avoid NSAIDs in CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Methotrexate in women of child-bearing age** needs reliable contraception; stop 3 months before planned conception. [ACR 2020]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: syndromic diagnosis, paracetamol, early referral of bleeding or encephalopathy, and line-listing on **IHIP** - lab confirmation through the nearest **VRDL or sentinel surveillance hospital**. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Chronic chikungunya arthritis** affects about **30-40%** of patients at 3 months and a smaller proportion beyond a year; risk factors are **age over 45, severe acute arthritis, high viral load and pre-existing joint disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chikungunya mortality is low (about **0.1%**), mainly in the elderly and neonates; atypical forms include **encephalitis, GBS, myocarditis and bullous dermatosis**. [WHO 2025]`,`Zika complications: **congenital Zika syndrome** and **Guillain-Barre syndrome** (about 2-3 per 10,000 infections). [WHO 2025]`,`KFD case fatality is **3-10%**; survivors may have prolonged weakness; neurological phase adds morbidity. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`CCHF case fatality is **10-40%**, with death usually in the second week from **haemorrhage, DIC and shock**. [WHO 2023]`,`West Nile neuroinvasive disease has a **case fatality of about 10%** (higher in the elderly), and survivors of flaccid paralysis often have permanent weakness. [Harrison 22e]`,`Nipah has a case fatality of **40-75%**; about 20% of survivors have **residual neurological deficits** and relapse can occur. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Review chikungunya at **3-4 weeks** and at **3 months**: persistent inflammatory arthritis is referred to a **rheumatologist** for DMARDs. [French SPILF 2014]`,`On methotrexate: **CBC, LFT and creatinine every 4-8 weeks** at start, then every 3 months. [ACR 2020]`,`Zika in pregnancy: obstetric referral for **serial ultrasound**; newborns get **head circumference, eye examination, hearing screen and neurodevelopmental follow-up**. [WHO 2025]`,`KFD and CCHF contacts: **daily temperature check for 14 days** (CCHF contacts) and referral at first fever. [WHO 2023]`,`Nipah contacts are line-listed and **quarantined and monitored for 21 days** from last exposure (state protocols). [NCDC 2024]`,`**Notify** chikungunya, Zika, KFD, CCHF, West Nile, AES and Nipah immediately on **IHIP**; a single case of CCHF or Nipah triggers a rapid response team. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Aedes control** under **NCVBDC**: source reduction, **weekly 'dry day'**, covering water containers, larvivorous fish, temephos in containers and fogging only in outbreaks. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Personal protection**: repellents (DEET, picaridin), long sleeves, **bed nets for day sleepers (infants, the sick, pregnant women)**, screens. [WHO 2025]`,`**Zika sexual transmission**: use condoms or abstain for **3 months for men and 2 months for women** after symptoms or possible exposure; pregnant women should avoid travel to outbreak areas. [WHO 2022]`,`**KFD vaccine**: formalin-inactivated tissue-culture vaccine for people aged 7-65 in affected villages - **2 doses 1 month apart, booster at 6-9 months, then every 5 years**; efficacy has been suboptimal and a new vaccine is under development by ICMR. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`KFD protection: **DMP (dimethyl phthalate) oil repellent**, full clothing when entering forests, and reporting monkey deaths to health and forest officials. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`**CCHF prevention**: acaricide treatment of livestock, gloves during slaughter, and strict **standard and contact precautions** in hospitals. [WHO 2023]`,`**Nipah prevention**: avoid **half-eaten fruit and raw date-palm sap**, wash fruit, and strict infection-control in hospitals; contacts traced through the One Health approach. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Chikungunya vaccines**: live-attenuated **VLA1553 (Ixchiq)** and virus-like-particle **Vimkunya** have been licensed abroad for travellers; neither is in the Indian programme. [FDA 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases (dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever), July 2025** - the first unified guideline: paracetamol for pain and fever, **no NSAIDs until dengue is excluded**, **no corticosteroids in the acute phase**, and crystalloid-based fluid care. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chikungunya vaccines**: FDA approved **Ixchiq (VLA1553) in November 2023** and **Vimkunya (VLP) in February 2025**; after serious adverse events in older adults, the **FDA suspended the Ixchiq licence in August 2025** and regulators restricted its use in people aged 65 and over. [FDA 2025]`,`**Zika in India 2023-24**: clusters in Maharashtra and Karnataka prompted MoHFW advisories (2024) for **screening pregnant women in affected areas for Zika and fetal anomaly surveillance**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Nipah 2023-2025 in Kerala**: rapid point-of-care RT-PCR at the **Kozhikode state lab** and early monoclonal antibody use; outbreaks were contained with contact tracing and quarantine. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Chandipura AES outbreak, Gujarat 2024** - WHO Disease Outbreak News; sandfly control and early referral of encephalitic children. [WHO DON 2024]`,`**KFD**: ICMR began development and trials of a **new-generation KFD vaccine (2024)** to replace the old formalin-inactivated vaccine. [ICMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Chronic chikungunya arthritis'** - define the three phases (acute up to 3 weeks, post-acute to 3 months, chronic beyond 3 months) and the step-up therapy. [French SPILF 2014]`,`Viva: **'Why not ibuprofen for this arthralgic fever on day 2?'** - dengue has not been excluded; NSAIDs and aspirin raise the bleeding risk. [WHO 2025]`,`Viva: **'Which arbovirus spreads sexually?'** - **Zika**; counsel condoms for 3 months (men) and 2 months (women). [WHO 2022]`,`Viva: **'Monkey deaths in a Shimoga forest followed by fever with bleeding'** - **Kyasanur Forest disease**, Haemaphysalis tick. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`,`Viva: **'Which viral haemorrhagic fever in India spreads in hospitals?'** - **CCHF** (Gujarat 2011, nosocomial); Nipah also spreads person to person. [WHO 2023]`,`Common mistake: **starting steroids for acute chikungunya** joint pain - it causes rebound and is discouraged; steroids are for the post-acute phase only. [WHO 2025]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing Zika on **IgM alone** in a dengue-endemic area - cross-reactivity requires RT-PCR or PRNT. [WHO 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and vaccines in chikungunya and other arboviral fevers`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly, max 4 g/day; child 15 mg/kg`,`Acute phase, as needed`,`Hepatotoxicity; first choice until dengue excluded`],[`Naproxen (or ibuprofen)`,`250-500 mg oral BD (ibuprofen 400 mg TDS)`,`1-2 weeks after dengue excluded; post-acute phase`,`GI bleed, AKI; avoid in CKD, pregnancy after 20 weeks, suspected dengue`],[`Prednisolone (post-acute chikungunya)`,`10 mg oral OD`,`5 days, then taper over about 10 days`,`Rebound arthritis if used acutely; hyperglycaemia`],[`Methotrexate (chronic chikungunya arthritis)`,`7.5-25 mg oral once weekly + folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`At least 3-6 months, titrate to response`,`Hepatotoxicity, cytopenia, teratogenic; check CBC/LFT 4-8 weekly`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`200-400 mg oral OD (max 5 mg/kg/day)`,`3-6 months or longer, usually in combination`,`Retinopathy - baseline and annual eye check after 5 years`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`500 mg OD titrated to 1 g BD`,`3-6 months or longer`,`GI upset, rash, cytopenia; avoid in sulfa allergy`],[`Ribavirin (CCHF)`,`30 mg/kg IV/oral loading, then 15 mg/kg 6-hourly x 4 days, then 7.5 mg/kg 8-hourly x 6 days`,`10 days`,`Haemolytic anaemia, teratogenic; evidence weak`],[`KFD vaccine (inactivated)`,`Subcutaneous, 2 doses 1 month apart`,`Booster at 6-9 months, then 5-yearly`,`Local reactions; modest efficacy; ages 7-65 in endemic villages`]]},{heading:`Indian arboviral and allied viral fevers at a glance`,columns:[`Virus`,`Vector / reservoir`,`Indian distribution`,`Hallmark`,`Case fatality`],rows:[[`Chikungunya (alphavirus)`,`Aedes aegypti, A. albopictus`,`Nationwide, urban and peri-urban`,`Disabling polyarthritis, chronic in a third`,`About 0.1%`],[`Zika (flavivirus)`,`Aedes; sexual, vertical`,`Gujarat, Rajasthan, Kerala, Maharashtra, Karnataka`,`Mild rash, conjunctivitis; congenital Zika syndrome`,`Very low`],[`KFD (flavivirus)`,`Haemaphysalis tick; monkeys`,`Western Ghats - Karnataka, Kerala, Goa, Maharashtra, TN`,`Haemorrhagic fever then neurological phase`,`3-10%`],[`CCHF (nairovirus)`,`Hyalomma tick; livestock; nosocomial`,`Gujarat, Rajasthan`,`Ecchymoses, DIC`,`10-40%`],[`West Nile (flavivirus)`,`Culex; birds`,`Kerala, south India`,`Encephalitis, flaccid paralysis in elderly`,`About 10% of neuroinvasive`],[`Nipah (paramyxovirus)`,`Pteropus bats; person to person`,`Kerala, West Bengal`,`Encephalitis with respiratory illness`,`40-75%`],[`Chandipura (vesiculovirus)`,`Sandfly`,`Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh`,`Rapid encephalitis in children`,`High in children`]]},{heading:`Dengue versus chikungunya versus Zika`,columns:[`Feature`,`Dengue`,`Chikungunya`,`Zika`],rows:[[`Fever`,`High, 2-7 days`,`High, 3-5 days`,`Low-grade or none`],[`Joints`,`Myalgia, mild arthralgia`,`Severe polyarthritis, tenosynovitis`,`Mild arthralgia`],[`Rash`,`Flush, then convalescent 'islands of white'`,`Maculopapular, nasal hyperpigmentation`,`Pruritic maculopapular`],[`Eyes`,`Retro-orbital pain`,`Occasionally conjunctivitis`,`Non-purulent conjunctivitis`],[`Blood counts`,`Leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, rising haematocrit`,`Lymphopenia, platelets near normal`,`Usually normal`],[`Danger`,`Plasma leak and shock`,`Chronic arthritis, neonatal disease`,`Congenital Zika syndrome, GBS`]]},{heading:`Chikungunya phases and treatment`,columns:[`Phase`,`Duration`,`Clinical picture`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute`,`Up to 3 weeks`,`Fever, polyarthritis, rash`,`Rest, fluids, paracetamol; NSAID only after dengue excluded; no steroids`],[`Post-acute`,`3 weeks to 3 months`,`Persistent arthritis, tenosynovitis`,`NSAIDs; short prednisolone 10 mg taper; physiotherapy`],[`Chronic inflammatory`,`Over 3 months`,`RA-like or SpA-like arthritis`,`Methotrexate weekly; HCQ, sulfasalazine; rheumatology`],[`Chronic non-inflammatory`,`Over 3 months`,`Tendinitis, carpal tunnel, diffuse pain`,`Analgesics, physiotherapy, local injections`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic test windows`,columns:[`Virus`,`Early test (days 1-7)`,`Late test`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Chikungunya`,`RT-PCR serum`,`IgM ELISA from day 5-7`,`IgG only shows past infection`],[`Zika`,`RT-PCR serum (7 days), urine (14 days+)`,`IgM, PRNT`,`IgM cross-reacts with dengue`],[`KFD`,`RT-PCR blood`,`IgM ELISA`,`Only NIV/VRDL labs`],[`CCHF`,`RT-PCR blood (BSL-3/4)`,`IgM/IgG ELISA`,`Biohazard - strict packaging`],[`West Nile`,`RT-PCR (low yield)`,`IgM in CSF`,`Cross-reacts with JE`],[`Nipah`,`RT-PCR throat swab, urine, CSF, blood`,`IgM/IgG ELISA`,`BSL-3/4 only`]]}],redFlags:[`**Bleeding, ecchymoses or shock** in a febrile patient from a forest (KFD) or livestock area (CCHF)`,`**Altered sensorium, seizures or flaccid weakness** - encephalitis (West Nile, Nipah, Chandipura, JE)`,`**Fever with cough and encephalopathy in Kerala with a contact history** - suspect Nipah; isolate and notify`,`**Neonate born to a mother febrile with chikungunya** at delivery - observe 7 days`,`**Pregnant woman with rash and conjunctivitis** in a Zika-affected area`,`**Chikungunya with atypical features** - myocarditis, bullous skin, hepatitis, AKI or age over 65 with comorbidity`,`**Needlestick or blood exposure** to a suspected CCHF patient`],pearls:[`Chikungunya - **'that which bends up'**: the patient is stooped and stiff with symmetrical small-joint arthritis.`,`**Chik sign**: brown nasal hyperpigmentation after chikungunya.`,`**Paracetamol only, no NSAIDs, no steroids** until dengue is excluded.`,`Chikungunya phases: **acute 3 weeks, post-acute 3 months, chronic beyond 3 months**.`,`**Zika is the arbovirus that spreads sexually and causes microcephaly**.`,`**Monkey deaths in the forest herald KFD** - Haemaphysalis tick, Western Ghats.`,`**CCHF and Nipah spread in hospitals** - PPE and isolation are the treatment that saves staff.`,`Intrapartum chikungunya infects **about half the neonates**; caesarean does not prevent it.`,`West Nile: **flaccid paralysis with intact sensation** in an elderly patient with encephalitis.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old woman had an acute febrile illness with polyarthritis during a chikungunya outbreak 4 months ago. She still has symmetrical swelling of the wrists and MCP joints with morning stiffness of 1 hour. RF and anti-CCP are negative; CHIKV IgG is positive. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Oral acyclovir`,`Methotrexate once weekly with folic acid`,`Repeated intra-articular steroid injections only`,`Reassurance - it will resolve within days`],answer:1,explanation:`Arthritis persisting beyond 3 months after chikungunya is chronic inflammatory chikungunya arthritis, managed like RA with methotrexate once weekly with folic acid, alone or with hydroxychloroquine and sulfasalazine. Acyclovir has no activity against alphaviruses. Intra-articular injections can help a single joint but do not control polyarthritis, and reassurance ignores an established inflammatory arthritis that can become erosive.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q2`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman, 10 weeks pregnant, develops a pruritic maculopapular rash, low-grade fever and non-purulent conjunctivitis during a Zika outbreak in Pune. Which test is best to confirm current infection?`,options:[`Dengue NS1 antigen`,`Zika IgG ELISA`,`Zika RT-PCR on serum and urine`,`Widal test`],answer:2,explanation:`In the first week Zika RNA is detectable in serum (about 7 days) and for longer in urine (about 14 days), so RT-PCR on both is the confirmatory test. Zika IgG only indicates past infection and cross-reacts with dengue. Dengue NS1 detects dengue, not Zika, though dengue should also be excluded. The Widal test is for enteric fever and is irrelevant here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q3`,stem:`A forest worker from Shimoga presents in February with high fever, severe myalgia, gum bleeding and papulovesicular lesions on the soft palate. Villagers report dead langurs nearby. Which vector transmits the likely infection?`,options:[`Haemaphysalis spinigera tick`,`Aedes albopictus mosquito`,`Culex tritaeniorhynchus mosquito`,`Leptotrombidium chigger mite`],answer:0,explanation:`Fever with bleeding in a Western Ghats forest worker in the dry season, with monkey deaths, is Kyasanur Forest disease, transmitted by nymphs of the Haemaphysalis spinigera tick. Aedes albopictus carries chikungunya and dengue, Culex tritaeniorhynchus carries Japanese encephalitis, and the Leptotrombidium chigger transmits scrub typhus, not KFD.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q4`,stem:`On day 2 of an epidemic fever with severe joint pain in Delhi, a patient asks for a strong painkiller. Dengue has not yet been tested. What is the best analgesic advice?`,options:[`Ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily`,`Prednisolone 20 mg daily for 5 days`,`Aspirin 650 mg four times daily`,`Paracetamol, with NSAIDs only once dengue is excluded`],answer:3,explanation:`Chikungunya and dengue co-circulate, and NSAIDs or aspirin increase bleeding in dengue, so paracetamol is the analgesic until dengue is excluded (WHO 2025). Ibuprofen and aspirin are therefore premature on day 2. Corticosteroids are discouraged in the acute phase of chikungunya because of rebound arthritis and are reserved for the post-acute phase.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-q5`,stem:`A nurse in Ahmedabad develops fever, myalgia and ecchymoses 6 days after a needlestick while caring for a herdsman who died of a haemorrhagic fever. Which infection is most likely?`,options:[`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever`,`West Nile fever`,`Chikungunya`],answer:1,explanation:`CCHF, a Hyalomma tick-borne nairovirus endemic in Gujarat and Rajasthan livestock areas, spreads person to person through blood, causing nosocomial clusters such as Ahmedabad 2011; incubation after blood exposure is about 5-6 days. KFD has no person-to-person spread and occurs in the Western Ghats. West Nile and chikungunya are mosquito-borne and do not cause nosocomial haemorrhagic fever.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and management of chikungunya. Add a note on chronic chikungunya arthritis and on chikungunya in pregnancy.`,openingLines:[`Chikungunya is an Aedes-borne alphavirus infection causing abrupt fever with severe, often disabling, symmetrical polyarthralgia.`,`It is rarely fatal, but arthritis persists beyond 3 months in about a third, and intrapartum infection causes severe neonatal disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and virology`,points:[`Alphavirus (Togaviridae); Aedes aegypti and albopictus; E1-A226V mutation drove the 2005-06 Indian epidemic.`,`Reported nationally under NCVBDC; July-November with dengue.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 3-7 days; high fever, symmetrical polyarthralgia of small joints, tenosynovitis.`,`Maculopapular rash, nasal hyperpigmentation (chik sign), aphthous ulcers.`,`Atypical: encephalitis, GBS, myocarditis, bullous lesions, hepatitis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`CBC lymphopenia; exclude dengue (NS1/IgM) and malaria.`,`RT-PCR days 1-7; IgM ELISA from day 5-7.`]},{heading:`Management by phase`,points:[`Acute: rest, fluids, paracetamol; NSAIDs only after dengue excluded; no steroids.`,`Post-acute: NSAIDs, short prednisolone 10 mg taper, physiotherapy.`,`Chronic inflammatory: methotrexate weekly, HCQ, sulfasalazine; rheumatology.`]},{heading:`Chronic chikungunya arthritis`,points:[`Over 3 months; 30-40%; risk - age over 45, severe acute disease, prior joint disease.`,`Persistent viral antigen in synovial macrophages; RA-like, may be erosive; RF/anti-CCP usually negative.`]},{heading:`Pregnancy and neonate`,points:[`Not teratogenic; intrapartum viraemia transmits to about half of neonates.`,`Neonatal chikungunya day 3-7: fever, rash, encephalopathy; caesarean does not prevent; observe 7 days; continue breastfeeding.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Aedes source reduction, weekly dry day, repellents, bed nets; vaccines licensed abroad, not in Indian programme.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: acute, post-acute and chronic phases with treatment`,`Dengue versus chikungunya versus Zika comparison`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and virology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management by phase`,marks:2.5},{part:`Chronic arthritis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pregnancy and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`alphavirus`,`Aedes`,`polyarthralgia`,`chronic chikungunya arthritis`,`methotrexate`,`neonatal chikungunya`,`no NSAIDs before dengue excluded`]},{id:`infectious-fever-chikungunya-zika-arboviral-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on Kyasanur Forest disease.`,openingLines:[`Kyasanur Forest disease is a tick-borne flaviviral haemorrhagic fever endemic in the Western Ghats, first recognised in Shimoga, Karnataka in 1957.`,`It is transmitted by Haemaphysalis spinigera ticks, with monkeys as amplifying hosts; case fatality is 3-10%.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`Karnataka, Kerala (Wayanad), Goa, Maharashtra (Sindhudurg), Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris); November-June.`,`Forest workers, nut farmers, leaf collectors; monkey deaths herald outbreaks.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 3-8 days; fever, myalgia, prostration, bleeding, palatal papulovesicles.`,`Second neurological phase in 10-20% after 1-2 weeks.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and treatment`,points:[`RT-PCR in week 1, IgM ELISA later (NIV Pune, Shimoga VRDL); thrombocytopenia, leucopenia.`,`Supportive: fluids, blood products, ICU; no antiviral.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Inactivated vaccine: 2 doses 1 month apart, booster 6-9 months, then 5-yearly; new vaccine in development.`,`DMP oil repellent, full clothing, report monkey deaths.`]}],mustDraw:[`Transmission cycle: tick - monkeys and small mammals - humans (dead end)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis and treatment`,marks:1},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`KFD`,`monkey fever`,`Haemaphysalis spinigera`,`Shimoga`,`biphasic`,`KFD vaccine`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Arthropod-borne and rodent-borne viral infections`,`WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever, 2025`,`National Guidelines for Clinical Management of Chikungunya, NCVBDC, MoHFW, 2016`,`WHO SEARO. Guidelines for prevention and control of chikungunya fever, 2009`,`French Society for Infectious Diseases (SPILF). Recommendations for chronic chikungunya, 2014 (Simon F et al, Med Mal Infect 2015)`,`ICMR-NIV Pune. Kyasanur Forest disease and CCHF guidance, 2024; NCDC Nipah guidelines, 2024`,`WHO Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever fact sheet and clinical management, 2023`,`US FDA communications on chikungunya vaccines (Ixchiq, Vimkunya), 2023-2025`]}),r.push({id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis`,title:`Brucellosis, melioidosis and other neglected bacterial fevers`,oneLiner:`**Brucellosis** (an intracellular zoonosis from **raw milk and livestock**, causing undulant fever, **sacroiliitis/spondylitis** and orchitis, treated with **doxycycline for 6 weeks plus streptomycin or gentamicin, or plus rifampicin**), **melioidosis** (soil-borne **Burkholderia pseudomallei** sepsis with pneumonia and **multiple abscesses in diabetics**, treated with **ceftazidime or meropenem for at least 2 weeks then cotrimoxazole for 3-6 months**) and **Q fever** (Coxiella, culture-negative endocarditis) are under-diagnosed causes of prolonged or septic fever in India that need **culture, specific serology and long combination therapy**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`brucellosis`,`Malta fever`,`undulant fever`,`Mediterranean fever`,`Brucella melitensis`,`standard agglutination test`,`Rose Bengal plate test`,`melioidosis`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`Whitmore disease`,`safety-pin appearance`,`Ashdown medium`,`ceftazidime`,`meropenem`,`cotrimoxazole eradication`,`Q fever`,`Coxiella burnetii`,`culture-negative endocarditis`,`streptomycin`,`doxycycline rifampicin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Brucellosis** is a systemic zoonosis caused by facultative intracellular Gram-negative coccobacilli of the genus **Brucella**; it is the **commonest bacterial zoonosis worldwide**, with an estimated **1.6-2.1 million new human cases a year**. [Laine et al, Emerg Infect Dis 2023]`,`India has one of the largest livestock populations in the world and brucellosis is **endemic in cattle, buffalo, goats and sheep**; seroprevalence in Indian **veterinarians, dairy workers and shepherds** is several times that of the general population. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Melioidosis** is an infection by the environmental Gram-negative bacillus **Burkholderia pseudomallei**, estimated at **165,000 cases and 89,000 deaths a year worldwide**, with **India modelled to carry the largest burden** of any country. [Limmathurotsakul et al, Nat Microbiol 2016]`,`In India melioidosis is reported most from **coastal Karnataka (Udupi, Dakshina Kannada), Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, West Bengal and the North-East**, mostly in the **monsoon**, and remains grossly under-diagnosed because labs misidentify the organism. [Indian J Med Microbiol 2023]`,`**Q fever** is a zoonosis caused by the obligate intracellular **Coxiella burnetii**; in India it is documented in livestock and in **culture-negative endocarditis** and prolonged fever, but is rarely tested for. [Harrison 22e]`,`All three are **diseases of the farmer**: they cluster in rural, livestock-rearing, rice-growing and diabetic populations - precisely those served by family physicians. [WHO 2023]`,`These infections commonly present as **pyrexia of unknown origin** or **culture-negative sepsis**; think of them when the AUF panel is negative after 7-10 days (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Brucella species**: **B. melitensis** (goats, sheep - most virulent and commonest in humans), **B. abortus** (cattle, buffalo), **B. suis** (pigs) and **B. canis** (dogs). [Harrison 22e]`,`Brucella is acquired by **ingesting raw milk and unpasteurised dairy (paneer, khoa, cheese from raw milk)**, by **contact with placenta, aborted fetuses and vaginal discharge** of animals, and by **inhalation** - it is the **commonest laboratory-acquired bacterial infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Brucellosis risk groups: **veterinarians, livestock owners, shepherds, butchers, abattoir workers, dairy workers and microbiology staff**; person-to-person spread is very rare (breastfeeding, sexual, transplant). [WHO 2006]`,`**B. pseudomallei** lives in **wet soil and surface water** of rice paddies; infection is by **percutaneous inoculation**, **inhalation (especially during monsoon storms)** and ingestion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes mellitus** is the strongest risk factor for melioidosis - present in **over half of patients** - with **alcohol use, CKD, chronic lung disease, thalassaemia and corticosteroid use** next. [Wiersinga et al, NEJM 2012]`,`**Coxiella burnetii** is shed in huge numbers in **birth products of goats, sheep and cattle**, survives for months in dust and is inhaled; a **single organism can cause infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Valvular heart disease, prosthetic valves, vascular grafts, aneurysms, immunosuppression and pregnancy** predispose to persistent (chronic) Q fever. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Brucella is phagocytosed but **survives and multiplies inside macrophages** (it blocks phagosome-lysosome fusion), and spreads to the **reticuloendothelial system - liver, spleen, marrow, lymph nodes** and to bone and joints. [Harrison 22e]`,`Brucella **LPS is weakly endotoxic**, so patients look **surprisingly well for the fever** and septic shock is rare; granulomas form in liver, spleen and bone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why combination and long courses in brucellosis**: the intracellular niche protects the organism; drugs that penetrate cells (**doxycycline, rifampicin**) given with a bactericidal aminoglycoside for **at least 6 weeks** reduce the **relapse rate**, which is high with monotherapy. [Ioannina consensus 2007]`,`**B. pseudomallei** is also a **facultative intracellular** organism; it uses a **type III secretion system** to escape the phagosome, spreads cell-to-cell by actin tails forming **multinucleated giant cells**, and causes **abscesses in lung, liver, spleen, prostate and parotid**. [Wiersinga et al, Nat Rev Dis Primers 2018]`,`Diabetes impairs **neutrophil function and IL-12/IFN-gamma responses**, explaining the link with melioidosis; the organism can stay **latent for years and reactivate** ('Vietnam time bomb'). [Harrison 22e]`,`**B. pseudomallei is intrinsically resistant** to **aminoglycosides, polymyxins (colistin), penicillin, first- and second-generation cephalosporins and macrolides** - hence the empirical 'ceftriaxone plus gentamicin' for sepsis fails, and **ceftazidime or a carbapenem** is required. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why eradication therapy**: after the IV phase, organisms persist in tissue; **3-6 months of oral cotrimoxazole** cuts relapse from about 10-20% to under 5%. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Coxiella** lives in the **acidic phagolysosome**; **hydroxychloroquine alkalinises** the vacuole and makes **doxycycline bactericidal** - the reason for the doxycycline plus hydroxychloroquine regimen in persistent Q fever. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Brucellosis by duration**: **acute (under 8 weeks)**, **subacute (8-52 weeks)** and **chronic (over 1 year)**; plus **localised (focal)** disease and **relapse**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Focal brucellosis**: osteoarticular (**sacroiliitis, spondylitis, peripheral arthritis** - the commonest focus, in up to 40%), genitourinary (**epididymo-orchitis**), neurobrucellosis, endocarditis, hepatic, pulmonary. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis by course**: **acute (under 2 months of symptoms, 85%)**, **chronic (over 2 months)**, and **latent with reactivation**; and by focus - **pneumonia (about half)**, bacteraemia with or without focus, skin and soft tissue, visceral abscesses, genitourinary (prostatic), bone and joint, neurological. [Currie et al, Darwin cohort 2021]`,`**Severity in melioidosis**: localised infection without bacteraemia versus **bacteraemic or septic shock** (mortality up to 40-50%) - severity sets the IV duration and ICU need. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Q fever**: **acute (primary) infection** - flu-like illness, pneumonia, hepatitis - versus **persistent localised infection** (formerly 'chronic Q fever') - **endocarditis, vascular infection**, osteomyelitis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Brucellosis: incubation **2-4 weeks** (up to months); insidious **fever with drenching sweats** (classically malodorous), malaise, **back pain, arthralgia**, headache, weight loss and depression - fever may be **undulant** if untreated. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask every prolonged fever about **raw milk, home-made dairy from raw milk, goat or cattle rearing, animal abortions and assisting deliveries of animals**. [WHO 2006]`,`Brucella localising symptoms: **low back or buttock pain (sacroiliitis)**, **testicular pain and swelling**, headache with confusion (neurobrucellosis), breathlessness (endocarditis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis: in a **diabetic farmer in the monsoon**, sudden **fever with cough and breathlessness** (pneumonia), or **septic shock with no clear focus**, or a **subacute fever with weight loss** suggesting TB. [Wiersinga et al, Nat Rev Dis Primers 2018]`,`Melioidosis can also present as **skin ulcers or abscesses, urinary symptoms (prostatic abscess), joint pain, parotid swelling (children)** or **brainstem encephalitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chronic melioidosis **mimics tuberculosis** (cavitating upper-lobe pneumonia, weight loss) - ask about **failure of anti-TB therapy** and diabetes. [Indian J Med Microbiol 2023]`,`Q fever: abrupt fever, **severe headache**, myalgia, cough; persistent infection presents months to years later as **culture-negative endocarditis** in someone with a valve lesion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Brucellosis: patient looks **less ill than the fever suggests**; **hepatomegaly, splenomegaly** and lymphadenopathy in a third; **relative bradycardia** may be seen. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **sacroiliac tenderness** (FABER/Patrick test), **spinal tenderness with paraspinal spasm** (lumbar spondylitis), **swollen tender testis**, peripheral monoarthritis of knee or hip. [Harrison 22e]`,`Listen for a **new murmur** in brucellosis and Q fever - endocarditis (usually **aortic valve**) is the leading cause of death in brucellosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis: tachypnoea, crackles, **hypotension**; search the skin for **abscesses or ulcers**, palpate liver and spleen, examine the **prostate (per rectum)** and parotids, and do a neurological examination. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly in a diabetic with sepsis** in a coastal district should prompt an **ultrasound for liver and splenic abscesses** - melioidosis until proved otherwise. [Indian J Med Microbiol 2023]`,`Q fever: **hepatomegaly** with granulomatous hepatitis, pneumonia signs, and in persistent infection **clubbing, splenomegaly and purpura** of endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Tuberculosis**: the great mimic of both - **Brucella spondylitis** spares the disc longer, affects **lumbar spine with anterior-superior vertebral erosion (Pedro Pons sign)** and rarely causes psoas abscess or gibbus, unlike Pott spine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enteric fever**: prolonged fever with relative bradycardia and splenomegaly - blood culture separates them; a **raised Widal plus Brucella** can co-exist. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Kala-azar, lymphoma and HIV**: prolonged fever with splenomegaly - check rK39, LDH, HIV (see the PUO topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis versus **staphylococcal or Klebsiella sepsis**: multiple abscesses in liver and spleen with diabetes in Klebsiella too; culture and response to ceftriaxone separate them. [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis pneumonia versus **TB or community-acquired pneumonia**: rapidly progressive or cavitating pneumonia not responding to ceftriaxone-azithromycin in a diabetic. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Q fever pneumonia versus **Mycoplasma, Legionella, psittacosis**; Q fever endocarditis versus **Bartonella** and other culture-negative endocarditis (see the Infective endocarditis topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Brucellosis haemogram: **normal or low WBC with relative lymphocytosis**, mild anaemia, thrombocytopenia; **mildly raised transaminases**; raised ESR/CRP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood culture** (automated BACTEC, positive in 50-70% of acute brucellosis, usually within 7 days) - **warn the laboratory** because Brucella is a lab-acquired hazard; **bone marrow culture** is more sensitive (up to 90%). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serology**: **Rose Bengal plate test** for screening; **standard tube agglutination (SAT) titre of 1:160 or more** (1:320 in highly endemic areas), or a fourfold rise, supports active disease; **2-mercaptoethanol test** detects IgG (active/chronic); **Brucella ELISA (IgM, IgG)** and Coombs test for incomplete antibodies. [WHO 2006]`,`SAT pitfalls: **prozone** (false negative at low dilution - dilute serum beyond 1:320), **cross-reaction with Yersinia O:9, Francisella and V. cholerae**, and **B. canis is not detected** by standard antigen. [Harrison 22e]`,`Imaging in brucellosis: **MRI spine** for spondylitis, **MRI or bone scan for sacroiliitis**, scrotal ultrasound, echocardiography if a murmur or persistent bacteraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis: **culture is the gold standard** - blood, urine, sputum, throat swab and pus; **Ashdown selective medium** improves yield from non-sterile sites; Gram stain shows **bipolar 'safety-pin' Gram-negative bacilli**. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Melioidosis clue in the lab report: a **Gram-negative bacillus resistant to gentamicin and colistin but sensitive to amoxicillin-clavulanate** - it is often discarded as 'Pseudomonas' or a contaminant; automated systems may misidentify it, **MALDI-TOF** identifies it reliably. [Indian J Med Microbiol 2023]`,`In every melioidosis patient: **ultrasound abdomen** (liver, spleen and prostate abscesses), chest X-ray or CT, **HbA1c** and blood glucose, and CT/MRI brain if any neurological sign. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Q fever: **indirect immunofluorescence** - **phase II IgG and IgM** raised in acute infection (fourfold rise); **phase I IgG of 1:800 or more** suggests persistent infection; PCR of blood or valve tissue. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Uncomplicated brucellosis (adult) - preferred: **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 6 weeks plus streptomycin 1 g IM daily for 2-3 weeks**, or **gentamicin 5 mg/kg IV/IM once daily for 7 days**. [Ioannina consensus 2007]`,`Oral alternative: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily plus rifampicin 600-900 mg (15 mg/kg) once daily, both for 6 weeks** - slightly higher relapse than aminoglycoside regimens. [WHO 2006]`,`**Exclude active TB before rifampicin** (sputum, chest X-ray) - rifampicin monotherapy exposure risks resistance; many Indian physicians prefer the doxycycline-aminoglycoside regimen to preserve rifampicin. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Never use monotherapy** for brucellosis - relapse rates exceed 20%. [Harrison 22e]`,`Acute Q fever: **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 14 days**; screen for valve disease with echocardiography in those at risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis is not an OPD disease - **any suspected case with sepsis, pneumonia or deep abscess must be admitted**; localised skin melioidosis still needs the IV intensive phase. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Counsel: **boil or pasteurise milk**, stop raw-milk products, wear gloves when handling animal births; diabetics should **wear boots and gloves** in paddy fields. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Melioidosis intensive phase**: **ceftazidime 2 g IV 6-hourly** (50 mg/kg, max 2 g) or **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly** (2 g 8-hourly for CNS infection), for **at least 10-14 days** - longer (4-8 weeks) for deep abscesses, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis and CNS disease. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Meropenem is preferred** for septic shock, ICU patients and neuromelioidosis; switch to ceftazidime after improvement where carbapenems need to be conserved. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Add cotrimoxazole during the IV phase** for **CNS infection, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, skin and soft-tissue infection and prostatic abscess** (better tissue penetration). [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Eradication phase**: oral **cotrimoxazole twice daily by weight** - **under 40 kg: 160/800 mg (1 DS tablet); 40-60 kg: 240/1200 mg; over 60 kg: 320/1600 mg (2 DS tablets)** - with **folic acid 5 mg daily**, for **3 months** (6 months for CNS or osteomyelitis). [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Drain abscesses** larger than about 2-3 cm where feasible (liver, prostate, parotid, joint); multiple small hepatosplenic abscesses usually resolve with prolonged therapy. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Septic shock** in melioidosis: cultures, meropenem within 1 hour, **balanced crystalloid at least 30 mL/kg**, noradrenaline for MAP 65, glucose control; **G-CSF is not recommended**. [SSC 2026]`,`**Brucella spondylitis**: doxycycline plus rifampicin for **at least 12 weeks**, with streptomycin or gentamicin initially; surgery only for instability, cord compression or abscess not responding. [Ioannina consensus 2007]`,`**Neurobrucellosis and endocarditis**: triple therapy including **ceftriaxone** (CNS) or an aminoglycoside with doxycycline and rifampicin for **months (often 3-6 months or longer)**; **valve replacement** is usually needed in Brucella endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Persistent Q fever endocarditis**: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily plus hydroxychloroquine 200 mg three times daily for at least 18 months** (prosthetic valve 24 months), with serology and drug-level monitoring and surgery when indicated. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Brucellosis in pregnancy**: risk of **miscarriage and preterm delivery**; treat with **rifampicin 900 mg daily for 6 weeks**, with cotrimoxazole added in the second trimester (avoid near term - kernicterus); avoid doxycycline and streptomycin. [WHO 2006]`,`**Brucellosis in children under 8**: **cotrimoxazole plus rifampicin for 6 weeks**, with gentamicin for the first 5-7 days in severe disease; doxycycline is now acceptable in many guidelines for short courses but 6 weeks is long. [WHO 2006]`,`**Melioidosis in pregnancy and children**: intensive phase as adults (weight-based ceftazidime or meropenem); for eradication, **amoxicillin-clavulanate 20/5 mg/kg three times daily** is used when cotrimoxazole is unsuitable - relapse is higher. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Diabetes**: tight glycaemic control is part of melioidosis treatment and **lifelong diabetes control** lowers recurrence; check HbA1c in every patient. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**CKD**: reduce **ceftazidime, meropenem and cotrimoxazole** doses by creatinine clearance; monitor potassium (cotrimoxazole causes hyperkalaemia). [BNF 2025]`,`**Laboratory exposure to Brucella**: post-exposure prophylaxis with **doxycycline plus rifampicin for 3 weeks** and serological follow-up at 0, 2, 4 and 6 weeks. [CDC 2023]`,`**Q fever in pregnancy**: **cotrimoxazole for the rest of the pregnancy** reduces obstetric complications (miscarriage, fetal death); follow up for persistent infection after delivery. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Brucellosis relapse occurs in **5-15%**, usually **within 6 months**, from inadequate regimens or short courses - not resistance; retreat with the same regimen. [Harrison 22e]`,`Brucellosis mortality is **under 2%**, mostly from **endocarditis**; chronic fatigue and depression follow in some ('chronic brucellosis'). [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis mortality is **10-40% overall** and up to **50% with septic shock** even with appropriate therapy; most deaths occur in the first 48 hours. [Wiersinga et al, Nat Rev Dis Primers 2018]`,`Melioidosis **recurrence (relapse or reinfection) in 5-10%**, mostly from **poor adherence to eradication therapy**; diabetes control and adherence are the key modifiable factors. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Q fever: **persistent infection in up to 5%** of those with valve disease; endocarditis untreated is fatal; acute infection in pregnancy causes fetal loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`Cotrimoxazole eradication causes **rash, cytopenia, hyperkalaemia and rarely SJS** - check CBC and electrolytes at 2-4 weeks. [BNF 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer any **suspected melioidosis** to a hospital with a **microbiology lab and ICU** - mortality is front-loaded in the first 48 hours. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Refer brucellosis with **spondylitis, neurological signs, endocarditis, pregnancy or relapse**. [Harrison 22e]`,`During aminoglycoside therapy, check **creatinine and hearing** (streptomycin ototoxicity) weekly; during rifampicin check LFT and warn about **orange urine and drug interactions** (OCPs, warfarin). [BNF 2025]`,`Follow brucellosis **clinically at 3 and 6 months** after therapy for relapse; serology titres fall slowly and **should not be used alone to define cure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis follow-up: **adherence checks monthly** through eradication, repeat ultrasound for abscess resolution, CBC and potassium on cotrimoxazole, and **counsel on relapse symptoms** for years. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Q fever endocarditis: **phase I IgG every 3 months** during treatment; therapy continues until titres fall fourfold or below 1:800. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`Brucellosis control is **veterinary first**: the **National Animal Disease Control Programme (NADCP, 2019)** vaccinates **female calves aged 4-8 months with Brucella abortus S19 vaccine** across India. [DAHD 2024]`,`**Pasteurise or boil milk**, avoid raw-milk dairy, and use **gloves, masks and safe disposal of placenta and aborted fetuses**; there is **no human Brucella vaccine**. [WHO 2023]`,`Laboratories must handle suspected **Brucella, Burkholderia and Coxiella** cultures in a **biosafety cabinet (BSL-3 practices)**. [CDC 2023]`,`Melioidosis prevention: **boots and gloves in paddy fields**, cover wounds, avoid exposure to muddy water and storm aerosols - especially **diabetics, CKD and alcohol users**; there is **no vaccine**. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Good diabetes control** at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir is effectively melioidosis prevention in endemic coastal districts. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`These are classic **One Health** zoonoses: animal vaccination, milk hygiene and environmental surveillance are coordinated with the IDSP and state animal husbandry departments (see the One Health and zoonoses topic). [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Global brucellosis burden revised upward (2023)** to about **1.6-2.1 million cases a year**, with South Asia among the highest-incidence regions. [Laine et al, Emerg Infect Dis 2023]`,`The **revised Darwin melioidosis guideline (2020)** remains current: **meropenem preferred in shock and CNS disease**, longer IV phases for deep foci, weight-based cotrimoxazole for **3 months**; Indian centres follow it in the **ICMR antimicrobial treatment guidelines (2022)**. [Darwin guideline 2020; ICMR 2022]`,`**Indian melioidosis studies 2023-2024** confirm under-recognition, the dominance of diabetes as a risk factor, and the value of **MALDI-TOF identification and lab-clinician alerts** for gentamicin-resistant, amoxicillin-clavulanate-sensitive Gram-negative bacilli. [Indian J Med Microbiol 2023]`,`**SSC 2026** applies to septic melioidosis: NEWS2/MEWS screening, antibiotics within 1 hour, balanced crystalloid and MAP 65 mmHg. [SSC 2026]`,`The **Quadripartite One Health Joint Plan of Action (2022-2026)** lists brucellosis among priority endemic zoonoses; India's **NADCP** brucellosis vaccination continues to expand coverage. [FAO/UNEP/WHO/WOAH 2022; DAHD 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Why does ceftriaxone plus gentamicin fail in melioidosis?'** - **intrinsic resistance to aminoglycosides**; use ceftazidime or meropenem. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Safety-pin Gram-negative bacilli from a diabetic farmer with splenic abscesses'** - **B. pseudomallei**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Two phases of melioidosis treatment'** - **intensive IV (at least 2 weeks)** then **eradication oral cotrimoxazole (3-6 months)**. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Viva: **'Why 6 weeks of two drugs in brucellosis?'** - intracellular organism; monotherapy and short courses relapse. [Ioannina consensus 2007]`,`Viva: **'Brucella versus TB spine'** - Brucella: lumbar, anterior-superior erosion (Pedro Pons), disc preserved longer, less abscess and deformity. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: reporting **SAT 1:80 as positive** in an endemic area, or missing **prozone** in a strongly positive serum. [WHO 2006]`,`Common mistake: stopping cotrimoxazole when the patient feels well at 4 weeks - **eradication must complete 3 months**. [Darwin guideline 2020]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in brucellosis, melioidosis and Q fever`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (brucellosis)`,`100 mg oral BD`,`6 weeks (12 weeks in spondylitis)`,`Oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Streptomycin (brucellosis)`,`1 g IM OD (15 mg/kg)`,`2-3 weeks`,`Ototoxicity (vestibular), nephrotoxicity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Gentamicin (brucellosis)`,`5 mg/kg IV/IM OD`,`7 days`,`Nephro- and ototoxicity; monitor creatinine`],[`Rifampicin (brucellosis)`,`600-900 mg (15 mg/kg) oral OD`,`6 weeks`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions, enzyme induction; exclude TB first`],[`Ceftazidime (melioidosis)`,`2 g IV 6-hourly (50 mg/kg, max 2 g)`,`At least 10-14 days; longer for deep foci`,`Allergy, C. difficile; adjust for renal function`],[`Meropenem (melioidosis)`,`1 g IV 8-hourly; 2 g 8-hourly in CNS`,`At least 10-14 days; 4-8 weeks in CNS/bone`,`Seizures (high dose, renal failure); preferred in shock`],[`Cotrimoxazole (melioidosis eradication)`,`Under 40 kg 160/800 mg; 40-60 kg 240/1200 mg; over 60 kg 320/1600 mg, all BD + folic acid 5 mg OD`,`3 months (6 months for CNS/osteomyelitis)`,`Rash, SJS, cytopenia, hyperkalaemia; check CBC and K`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate (eradication alternative)`,`20/5 mg/kg oral TDS`,`3-6 months`,`Higher relapse; used in pregnancy or cotrimoxazole intolerance`],[`Doxycycline + hydroxychloroquine (persistent Q fever)`,`100 mg BD + HCQ 200 mg TDS`,`At least 18 months (24 for prosthetic valve)`,`Photosensitivity; HCQ retinopathy - eye checks`]]},{heading:`Brucellosis, melioidosis and Q fever compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Brucellosis`,`Melioidosis`,`Q fever`],rows:[[`Organism`,`Brucella spp (intracellular Gram-negative coccobacillus)`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei (Gram-negative bacillus, safety-pin)`,`Coxiella burnetii (obligate intracellular)`],[`Source`,`Raw milk, animal birth products, lab aerosol`,`Wet soil and water, monsoon aerosol`,`Inhaled dust from animal birth products`],[`Main host factor`,`Occupation, raw dairy`,`Diabetes, alcohol, CKD`,`Valve disease, pregnancy`],[`Typical picture`,`Undulant fever, sweats, sacroiliitis, orchitis`,`Pneumonia, sepsis, liver/spleen/prostate abscesses`,`Flu-like illness, pneumonia, hepatitis; later endocarditis`],[`Diagnosis`,`Blood/marrow culture, SAT 1:160 or more, 2-ME, ELISA`,`Culture (Ashdown), MALDI-TOF`,`Phase II/I IgG by IFA, PCR`],[`Treatment`,`Doxycycline 6 weeks + aminoglycoside or rifampicin`,`Ceftazidime/meropenem then cotrimoxazole 3-6 months`,`Doxycycline 14 days; HCQ + doxycycline 18 months if persistent`]]},{heading:`Melioidosis treatment duration by focus`,columns:[`Focus`,`Minimum IV intensive phase`,`Add cotrimoxazole in IV phase`,`Eradication`],rows:[[`Skin abscess, no bacteraemia`,`2 weeks`,`Yes`,`3 months`],[`Bacteraemia without focus or pneumonia`,`2 weeks (4 weeks if ICU or bilateral)`,`No`,`3 months`],[`Deep abscess (liver, spleen, prostate)`,`4 weeks (from drainage)`,`Yes for prostate`,`3 months`],[`Septic arthritis`,`4 weeks`,`Yes`,`3 months`],[`Osteomyelitis`,`6 weeks`,`Yes`,`6 months`],[`CNS infection`,`8 weeks (meropenem 2 g 8-hourly)`,`Yes`,`6 months`]]},{heading:`Brucella serology and culture - interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Positive result`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Rose Bengal plate test`,`Agglutination - screening`,`Low specificity; confirm with SAT`],[`Standard tube agglutination (SAT)`,`1:160 or more (1:320 highly endemic) or fourfold rise`,`Prozone; Yersinia O:9 cross-reaction; misses B. canis`],[`2-mercaptoethanol test`,`Persisting titre = IgG, active or chronic disease`,`Titres fall slowly after cure`],[`Brucella ELISA IgM/IgG`,`IgM acute, IgG chronic/relapse`,`Kit variability`],[`Blood culture (automated)`,`Growth in 50-70% acute cases`,`Warn lab - biohazard; hold bottles longer`],[`Bone marrow culture`,`Up to 90% sensitive`,`Invasive; useful in PUO`]]}],redFlags:[`**Diabetic with fever and hypotension or rapidly progressive pneumonia** in the monsoon - suspect melioidosis; meropenem and ICU`,`**Liver, spleen or prostate abscesses** on ultrasound in a febrile diabetic`,`**New murmur or heart failure** in brucellosis or prolonged fever with animal exposure - endocarditis`,`**Back pain with neurological deficit** - Brucella or TB spondylitis with cord compression`,`**Headache, confusion or cranial nerve palsy** - neurobrucellosis or neuromelioidosis`,`**Brucellosis or Q fever in pregnancy** - fetal loss risk; specialist care`,`**Gram-negative bacillus resistant to gentamicin and colistin** in a septic patient - treat as melioidosis now`],pearls:[`**Brucella = raw milk + sweats + sacroiliitis + orchitis**; the patient looks better than the chart.`,`Brucellosis: **two drugs, six weeks** - doxycycline plus streptomycin/gentamicin or rifampicin; never monotherapy.`,`**Exclude TB before rifampicin** for brucellosis.`,`SAT **1:160 or more** in endemic areas; beware **prozone** and Yersinia cross-reaction.`,`Melioidosis: **diabetic + monsoon + paddy + abscesses** - and 'Pseudomonas' resistant to gentamicin.`,`Melioidosis treatment is **two-phase**: ceftazidime/meropenem 2 weeks or more, then **cotrimoxazole 3 months**.`,`B. pseudomallei is **intrinsically resistant to aminoglycosides and colistin**.`,`**Pedro Pons sign** - anterosuperior vertebral erosion of Brucella spondylitis.`,`Persistent Q fever: **doxycycline + hydroxychloroquine for 18 months**.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-q1`,stem:`A 55-year-old diabetic paddy farmer from Udupi presents in August with fever, cough and hypotension. Ultrasound shows multiple small splenic abscesses. The lab reports a Gram-negative bacillus resistant to gentamicin and colistin but sensitive to amoxicillin-clavulanate. What is the best initial antibiotic?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone plus gentamicin`,`Colistin plus tigecycline`,`Meropenem`,`Oral cotrimoxazole alone`],answer:2,explanation:`The picture is septic melioidosis (diabetic farmer, monsoon, splenic abscesses, gentamicin- and colistin-resistant Gram-negative bacillus). B. pseudomallei needs ceftazidime or meropenem, and meropenem is preferred in septic shock. It is intrinsically resistant to aminoglycosides and colistin, so those combinations fail, and ceftriaxone is unreliable. Oral cotrimoxazole is the eradication phase given after at least 2 weeks of IV therapy, not initial treatment.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old goat herder has had fever with night sweats for 6 weeks, low back pain and a swollen tender right testis. Brucella SAT titre is 1:640. Which regimen is preferred?`,options:[`Doxycycline for 6 weeks plus streptomycin for the first 2-3 weeks`,`Ciprofloxacin for 14 days`,`Ceftriaxone for 7 days`,`Rifampicin alone for 6 weeks`],answer:0,explanation:`Brucellosis with orchitis and probable sacroiliitis needs two-drug therapy for at least 6 weeks; doxycycline plus streptomycin (or gentamicin for 7 days) has the lowest relapse rate. A 14-day ciprofloxacin course or 7 days of ceftriaxone is far too short and is not first line for this intracellular organism. Rifampicin alone is monotherapy, which relapses frequently, and also risks rifampicin resistance in undiagnosed TB.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-q3`,stem:`A patient with melioidosis pneumonia has finished 14 days of IV ceftazidime and is afebrile. He weighs 70 kg. What is the correct next step?`,options:[`Stop antibiotics - he is cured`,`Oral amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 1 week`,`Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 2 weeks`,`Oral cotrimoxazole 320/1600 mg twice daily with folic acid for 3 months`],answer:3,explanation:`After the intensive phase, eradication therapy with weight-based cotrimoxazole (over 60 kg: 320/1600 mg twice daily) plus folic acid for 3 months cuts relapse to under 5%. Stopping now leaves a high relapse risk. Plain amoxicillin has no activity, since only the clavulanate combination works, and a 2-week doxycycline course is too short and inferior to cotrimoxazole.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-q4`,stem:`A dairy worker with 3 months of fever has a negative Brucella SAT at a 1:80 dilution, yet blood culture later grows Brucella melitensis. What best explains the false-negative serology?`,options:[`Cross-reaction with Yersinia enterocolitica O:9`,`Prozone phenomenon from antibody excess at low dilutions`,`Infection with Brucella canis`,`Previous typhoid vaccination`],answer:1,explanation:`In the prozone phenomenon, very high antibody levels prevent lattice formation at low dilutions, so agglutination appears only when the serum is diluted further (beyond 1:320). Yersinia O:9 cross-reaction gives false positives, not false negatives. B. canis is missed by standard antigen, but the culture grew B. melitensis. Typhoid vaccination affects the Widal test, not Brucella serology.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-q5`,stem:`A man with a bicuspid aortic valve who rears goats has culture-negative endocarditis. Coxiella phase I IgG is 1:3200. What is the recommended treatment?`,options:[`Doxycycline for 14 days`,`Penicillin plus gentamicin for 4 weeks`,`Doxycycline plus hydroxychloroquine for at least 18 months`,`Azithromycin for 5 days`],answer:2,explanation:`A high phase I IgG with endocarditis means persistent Q fever. Hydroxychloroquine raises phagolysosomal pH and makes doxycycline bactericidal, and the combination is given for at least 18 months (24 for prosthetic valves). Fourteen days of doxycycline treats only acute Q fever. Penicillin-gentamicin is for streptococcal or enterococcal endocarditis and has no activity against intracellular Coxiella. A 5-day azithromycin course is inadequate.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the epidemiology, risk factors, clinical presentations, laboratory diagnosis and treatment of melioidosis in India.`,openingLines:[`Melioidosis is an infection by the soil- and water-dwelling Gram-negative bacillus Burkholderia pseudomallei, acquired by inoculation, inhalation or ingestion, mainly in diabetics during the monsoon.`,`India is modelled to carry the world's largest burden; it presents as pneumonia, sepsis or multiple abscesses, with mortality of 10-40%.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and risk factors`,points:[`Global 165,000 cases, 89,000 deaths/year; India - coastal Karnataka, Kerala, TN, Odisha, West Bengal, North-East.`,`Diabetes (over half), alcohol, CKD, thalassaemia, steroids; paddy farmers; monsoon.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Facultative intracellular; type III secretion; giant cells; abscesses; latency and reactivation.`,`Intrinsic resistance to aminoglycosides, colistin, early cephalosporins.`]},{heading:`Clinical presentations`,points:[`Pneumonia (about half), bacteraemia and septic shock, liver/spleen/prostate abscesses.`,`Skin, bone and joint, parotid (children), neuromelioidosis; chronic form mimics TB.`]},{heading:`Laboratory diagnosis`,points:[`Culture of blood, urine, sputum, throat, pus; Ashdown medium; safety-pin Gram stain.`,`Gentamicin- and colistin-resistant, amoxicillin-clavulanate-sensitive; MALDI-TOF; serology poor in endemic areas.`,`Ultrasound abdomen, chest imaging, HbA1c in all.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Intensive: ceftazidime 2 g 6-hourly or meropenem 1 g 8-hourly (2 g in CNS) for at least 10-14 days; longer for deep foci.`,`Add cotrimoxazole in IV phase for CNS, bone, joint, skin, prostate; drain abscesses.`,`Eradication: weight-based cotrimoxazole + folic acid for 3 months (6 for CNS/bone).`]},{heading:`Prognosis and prevention`,points:[`Mortality 10-40%, 50% with shock; relapse 5-10% with poor adherence.`,`Boots, gloves, diabetes control; no vaccine.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-phase treatment flow: intensive IV then oral eradication, with durations by focus`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and risk factors`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical presentations`,marks:2},{part:`Laboratory diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2.5},{part:`Prognosis and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`diabetes`,`ceftazidime`,`meropenem`,`cotrimoxazole eradication`,`Ashdown`,`intrinsic aminoglycoside resistance`]},{id:`infectious-fever-brucellosis-melioidosis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the diagnosis and treatment of brucellosis.`,openingLines:[`Brucellosis is a zoonosis caused by intracellular Brucella species, acquired from raw milk and contact with infected livestock, presenting as undulant fever with focal osteoarticular or genitourinary disease.`,`Diagnosis rests on culture and agglutination serology; treatment needs two drugs for at least 6 weeks.`],answer:[{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Blood culture (50-70%), bone marrow culture (up to 90%); warn the lab.`,`Rose Bengal screen; SAT 1:160 or more or fourfold rise; 2-ME for IgG; ELISA; prozone and cross-reaction pitfalls.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Doxycycline 100 mg BD 6 weeks + streptomycin 1 g IM 2-3 weeks or gentamicin 5 mg/kg 7 days.`,`Or doxycycline + rifampicin 600-900 mg 6 weeks (exclude TB first).`,`Spondylitis 12 weeks or more; neurobrucellosis add ceftriaxone; endocarditis - surgery.`,`Pregnancy: rifampicin (with cotrimoxazole); children under 8: cotrimoxazole + rifampicin.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Relapse 5-15% within 6 months; pasteurise milk; S19 calf vaccination under NADCP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of SAT, 2-ME, ELISA and culture with interpretation`],markSplit:[{part:`Introduction`,marks:.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Follow-up and prevention`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`Brucella`,`SAT`,`2-ME`,`prozone`,`doxycycline streptomycin`,`rifampicin`,`NADCP S19`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Brucellosis; Melioidosis and glanders; Q fever chapters`,`Ariza J et al. Perspectives for the treatment of brucellosis in the 21st century: the Ioannina recommendations. PLoS Med 2007`,`WHO/FAO/OIE. Brucellosis in humans and animals, 2006`,`Sullivan RP et al. 2020 review and revision of the 2015 Darwin melioidosis treatment guideline. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2020`,`Limmathurotsakul D et al. Predicted global distribution of Burkholderia pseudomallei and burden of melioidosis. Nat Microbiol 2016`,`Wiersinga WJ et al. Melioidosis. Nat Rev Dis Primers 2018; NEJM 2012`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2022`,`Laine CG et al. Global estimate of human brucellosis incidence. Emerg Infect Dis 2023`]}),r.push({id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail`,title:`Rickettsial infections beyond scrub typhus: Indian tick typhus, murine typhus, eschar hunting`,oneLiner:`**Rickettsioses** are **arthropod-borne infections of vascular endothelium** by obligate intracellular Rickettsia - in India mainly **spotted fever (Indian tick typhus, R. conorii, dog tick)** with a **centripetal rash involving palms and soles**, and **murine typhus (R. typhi, rat flea)** with a truncal rash sparing them; diagnosis is clinical (**search for an eschar**, rash pattern, exposure) with IgM ELISA, PCR or IFA - not the insensitive **Weil-Felix test** - and treatment is **doxycycline at every age**, with **azithromycin** in pregnancy and mild disease.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`rickettsial fever`,`spotted fever group`,`Indian tick typhus`,`Rickettsia conorii`,`Mediterranean spotted fever`,`murine typhus`,`endemic typhus`,`Rickettsia typhi`,`epidemic typhus`,`Rickettsia prowazekii`,`Brill-Zinsser disease`,`eschar`,`tache noire`,`Weil-Felix test`,`OX19 OX2 OXK`,`Rhipicephalus sanguineus`,`doxycycline in children`,`azithromycin`,`chloramphenicol`,`purpura fulminans`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Rickettsioses** are acute febrile infections by **obligate intracellular Gram-negative bacteria** of the order Rickettsiales, transmitted by **ticks, fleas, lice and mites**, whose shared lesion is a **disseminated endothelial vasculitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`They are divided into the **spotted fever group (SFG)** - R. conorii, R. rickettsii, R. africae, R. akari, R. felis - and the **typhus group (TG)** - R. typhi (murine) and R. prowazekii (epidemic); **Orientia tsutsugamushi** (scrub typhus) is now a separate genus (see the Scrub typhus topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, after scrub typhus, **spotted fever (Indian tick typhus)** is the commonest rickettsiosis, reported from **Maharashtra, Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Rajasthan, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu and Kashmir, Uttarakhand and the North-East**, often in **children**. [ICMR Rickettsial Guidelines 2015]`,`**Murine typhus** occurs sporadically worldwide in **rat-infested urban areas, markets and ports**; Indian reports come from the North-East, Kashmir, Kerala, Tamil Nadu and Karnataka, and it is under-diagnosed as 'viral fever'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus** is now rare in India but historically affected the **Himalayan and Kashmir regions** in cold weather, refugee camps and prisons, where body lice thrive. [Harrison 22e]`,`Rickettsial infections cause an estimated **10-20% of acute undifferentiated fever** in Indian studies when tested for, with peaks after the monsoon and in winter in the hills. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Untreated Rocky Mountain spotted fever** (the American prototype) kills **20-30%**; Indian tick typhus and murine typhus are milder but **severe and fatal cases with gangrene, myocarditis and AKI** are reported, especially with delayed doxycycline. [CDC MMWR 2016]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, vectors and risk factors`,points:[`**Indian tick typhus**: **Rickettsia conorii subspecies indica**, transmitted by the **brown dog tick, Rhipicephalus sanguineus**; risk from **pet or stray dogs, tick-infested homes and rural or peri-urban grass**. [ICMR 2015]`,`Ticks must feed for several hours to transmit; **removing an attached tick early** reduces the risk; the tick bite is usually **painless and unnoticed**. [CDC 2022]`,`**Murine typhus**: **R. typhi**, transmitted when **rat flea (Xenopsylla cheopis) faeces** are scratched into the bite or inhaled; reservoirs are **rats** (and opossums, cats in some regions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flea-borne spotted fever**: **R. felis** from the **cat flea (Ctenocephalides felis)** - increasingly found in India in PCR studies of febrile patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Epidemic typhus**: **R. prowazekii**, spread by **body-louse (Pediculus humanus corporis) faeces** rubbed into skin; flourishes with cold, crowding, war, famine and lack of bathing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Brill-Zinsser disease** is a **milder recrudescence of epidemic typhus years later** in a person previously infected, and can seed new louse-borne outbreaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors for severity: **delayed doxycycline**, use of **cotrimoxazole or a beta-lactam alone**, **G6PD deficiency**, extremes of age, alcoholism and diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Rickettsiae are inoculated into the skin, multiply at the site (sometimes forming an **eschar - focal necrosis from vasculitis**), and spread via lymphatics and blood to **infect endothelial cells** of small vessels throughout the body. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spotted fever group** rickettsiae move **cell to cell by actin-based motility** and spread along the endothelium; **typhus group** organisms multiply until the cell bursts - this difference partly explains the distinct rash patterns. [Harrison 22e]`,`Endothelial injury causes **increased vascular permeability** - oedema, hypovolaemia, hypoalbuminaemia, **non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema** and cerebral oedema - rather than true DIC in most cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **rash** is the visible vasculitis: maculopapular lesions become **petechial and purpuric** as vessel damage progresses; occlusive vasculitis gives **digital gangrene and purpura fulminans**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyponatraemia** (ADH release), **thrombocytopenia** (consumption), **raised transaminases** and **AKI** (pre-renal plus interstitial nephritis) follow from the vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why beta-lactams and aminoglycosides fail**: they do not reach effective intracellular levels and have no activity; **doxycycline** concentrates inside cells and inhibits the 30S ribosome; **chloramphenicol, azithromycin and rifampicin** are also intracellularly active. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sulfonamides (cotrimoxazole) worsen rickettsial disease** - they may promote rickettsial growth - so an inappropriate 'antibiotic for fever' can harm. [CDC MMWR 2016]`,`**Fluoroquinolones** are active in vitro but were associated with **worse outcomes** in Mediterranean spotted fever in children, so they are not recommended. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Spotted fever group**: R. conorii (Indian tick typhus, Mediterranean spotted fever), R. rickettsii (RMSF), R. africae (African tick-bite fever - travellers), R. akari (rickettsialpox - mite), R. felis (flea-borne spotted fever). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Typhus group**: R. typhi (murine/endemic typhus) and R. prowazekii (epidemic/louse-borne typhus and Brill-Zinsser disease). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus group**: Orientia tsutsugamushi (chigger mite) - the commonest Indian rickettsial infection, treated the same way but diagnosed with its own IgM ELISA. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Rash direction classification (exam favourite)**: spotted fever - **centripetal, from wrists and ankles inwards, involving palms and soles**; typhus group - **centrifugal, from trunk outwards, sparing palms, soles (and face)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity (ICMR 2015)**: **uncomplicated** (fever, rash, no organ failure) versus **complicated** (hypotension, ARDS, AKI, meningoencephalitis, myocarditis, hepatitis with jaundice, gangrene, DIC) - complicated disease needs admission and IV therapy. [ICMR 2015]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Incubation is **3-14 days** (median about 7) after a tick bite or flea exposure; the bite itself is usually not recalled. [Harrison 22e]`,`Abrupt **fever, severe headache, myalgia and malaise**; the rash appears around **day 3-5** - so a febrile child seen on day 2 may have no rash yet. [ICMR 2015]`,`Ask about **dogs at home, ticks on pets, picking ticks off children, rats in the house or shop, grassland and forest visits, travel to hills** and household cases. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Indian tick typhus in children** often presents with **fever, rash, periorbital and limb oedema**, abdominal pain and vomiting; severe cases have **purpura and gangrene of fingers and toes**. [IAP Rickettsial Guidelines 2017]`,`**Murine typhus**: fever, headache, myalgia, nausea, sometimes cough; rash appears in only about **half**, is faint and truncal - easily mislabelled as viral fever or enteric fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Epidemic typhus**: very high fever, **prostration, delirium ('typhos' = smoky, stuporous)**, centrifugal rash, cough; history of **lice, cold weather and crowding**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Symptoms of complications: **breathlessness** (pneumonitis/ARDS), **reduced urine**, **drowsiness or seizures**, **chest pain or palpitations**, black discoloured fingers. [ICMR 2015]`]},{heading:`Examination - eschar hunting and rash`,points:[`**Undress the patient completely** and search systematically for an **eschar**: **scalp, behind the ears, neck, axillae, under the breasts, waistband, umbilicus, groin, genitalia, perineum, gluteal cleft, popliteal fossae** - warm, moist, hidden folds where vectors feed. [ICMR 2015]`,`An **eschar (tache noire)** is a **painless, black, necrotic crust 5-20 mm across with an erythematous halo**, often with **tender regional lymphadenopathy**; it may have shed, leaving a **punched-out ulcer**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eschar prevalence differs**: common in **scrub typhus** in many Indian series, typical of Mediterranean spotted fever, African tick-bite fever and rickettsialpox, but **uncommon in Indian tick typhus** and **absent in murine and epidemic typhus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spotted fever rash**: erythematous macules on **wrists and ankles** spreading centrally to trunk, **involving palms and soles**, becoming maculopapular and then **petechial or purpuric**; a purpuric rash signals severe disease. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Typhus rash**: begins on the **trunk and axillae**, spreads to limbs, **spares palms, soles and usually the face**, maculopapular and rarely petechial in murine typhus. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **periorbital and peripheral oedema, conjunctival suffusion, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, relative bradycardia** and signs of meningism. [IAP 2017]`,`Check **peripheral pulses and digits** for impending gangrene, and record **BP, SpO2, urine output and GCS** - the markers of complicated disease. [ICMR 2015]`,`Repeat the examination daily: **rash and eschar may appear only on day 4-5** and are missed if the patient is examined clothed. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Meningococcaemia**: rapidly evolving purpura with shock and meningism - **give ceftriaxone immediately**; in doubt, treat both (ceftriaxone plus doxycycline). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dengue**: rash with leucopenia and rising haematocrit, convalescent 'islands of white in a sea of red', no eschar; palms and soles erythema may confuse. [WHO 2025]`,`**Measles, rubella, HFMD and drug rash**: prodrome, Koplik spots, vesicles on palms and soles (HFMD), new drug exposure (see the Approach to fever with rash topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus**: eschar more common, regional lymphadenopathy, ARDS; treatment is the same, but its IgM ELISA is different. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Secondary syphilis**: palms and soles rash without high fever - VDRL/TPHA; **Kawasaki disease** in children: fever 5 days or more with conjunctivitis, mucositis and extremity changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enteric fever and leptospirosis**: share relative bradycardia, hepatitis and thrombocytopenia - culture and serology separate them; doxycycline covers leptospirosis but not enteric fever. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Infective endocarditis and vasculitis** (palpable purpura, splinter haemorrhages) and **cutaneous anthrax** (painless black eschar with marked oedema) are rare eschar or purpura mimics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Haemogram: **normal or low WBC early**, later leucocytosis; **thrombocytopenia** in most severe cases; **hyponatraemia**, raised **AST/ALT**, hypoalbuminaemia and raised creatinine support rickettsial disease. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Weil-Felix test** (Proteus agglutination): **OX19 and OX2** positive in spotted fever, **OX19** strongly in typhus group, **OXK** in scrub typhus; a titre of **1:80 or more** is used as a cut-off in Indian guidelines, preferably with a **rising titre in paired sera**. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Weil-Felix limits**: low sensitivity (often under 50%), turns positive only **after day 7-10**, cross-reacts (Proteus UTI, leptospirosis, typhoid), and **cannot identify the species** - a negative test never excludes, and treatment must not wait for it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IgM ELISA** for spotted fever and typhus group antigens is more sensitive and specific, positive from **day 5-7**; scrub typhus has a separate IgM ELISA. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Indirect immunofluorescence assay (IFA)** is the **reference serological test**: a **fourfold rise in IgG between acute and convalescent sera (2-4 weeks apart)** confirms the diagnosis; cross-reaction within a group is common. [CDC 2022]`,`**PCR** of **eschar swab or biopsy** (highest yield), **skin biopsy of a rash lesion**, or **buffy coat/whole blood** in the first week confirms and speciates the infection; immunohistochemistry of skin biopsy also helps. [CDC 2022]`,`In complicated disease: **chest X-ray** (interstitial infiltrates, ARDS), **ECG and troponin** (myocarditis), **CSF** (lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose), coagulation profile, **ferritin and triglycerides if HLH suspected**. [ICMR 2015]`,`Always run the **AUF panel** in parallel - **malaria smear/RDT, dengue NS1/IgM, blood culture**, and leptospira IgM - because co-infections and look-alikes are common. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Treat on clinical suspicion** - do not wait for serology; mortality rises steeply when doxycycline starts **after day 5** of illness. [CDC MMWR 2016]`,`Adults: **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days**, or until **at least 3 days after defervescence**; expect the fever to settle within **24-48 hours**. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Children of all ages**: **doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg (maximum 100 mg) twice daily** for 5-7 days - short courses (21 days or less) **do not stain teeth**; AAP and CDC recommend it as first line regardless of age. [AAP Red Book 2024]`,`Alternative (pregnancy, doxycycline allergy, mild disease): **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days** (child 10 mg/kg/day); efficacy is established in scrub typhus and mild murine typhus and in children with Mediterranean spotted fever, but data are weaker in severe spotted fever. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Give doxycycline with a full glass of water, upright, not at bedtime** - pill oesophagitis is the commonest complaint; it may be taken with food. [BNF 2025]`,`Symptomatic: **paracetamol only**, oral fluids; **avoid NSAIDs** until dengue is excluded, and **avoid cotrimoxazole** (worsens rickettsioses). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Remove attached ticks** with fine tweezers close to the skin, steady pull, no crushing; **prophylactic antibiotics after a tick bite are not recommended** - advise to report fever within 2 weeks. [CDC 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** with hypotension, SpO2 under 94% or breathlessness, altered sensorium or seizures, oliguria or creatinine rise, jaundice, purpura or gangrene, platelets under 50,000, inability to take oral drugs, infancy or pregnancy. [ICMR 2015]`,`Severe disease: **doxycycline 100 mg IV 12-hourly** (200 mg loading dose often used), or oral/nasogastric doxycycline where the IV form is unavailable - absorption is excellent. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Combination IV doxycycline plus azithromycin** improved outcomes over either drug alone in **severe scrub typhus (INTREST trial)**, and is reasonable in severe undifferentiated rickettsial disease where scrub typhus is possible. [Varghese et al, NEJM 2023]`,`**Chloramphenicol 50-75 mg/kg/day IV in 4 divided doses** (max 4 g/day) for 7 days is the alternative when doxycycline cannot be used - it is **less effective** (higher mortality in RMSF) and causes marrow toxicity and **grey baby syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Where **meningococcaemia, leptospirosis or bacterial sepsis** cannot be excluded, give **ceftriaxone 2 g IV plus doxycycline** after blood cultures - ceftriaxone alone misses rickettsiae. [ICMR 2022]`,`Supportive care: **cautious fluids** (capillary leak - risk of pulmonary oedema), noradrenaline for MAP 65 mmHg, lung-protective ventilation for ARDS, correction of **hyponatraemia slowly**, dialysis for AKI, and anticonvulsants. [SSC 2026]`,`**Gangrene**: early vascular surgery or plastic surgery opinion; demarcate before amputation; heparin is not routinely indicated. [IAP 2017]`,`**Epidemic typhus**: doxycycline (a **single 200 mg dose is effective in outbreaks**), with **delousing** of patient, clothing and contacts. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations - children and pregnancy`,points:[`**Doxycycline in children**: the old fear of tooth staining came from **tetracycline**; doxycycline binds calcium less, and studies show **no visible staining after short courses** - withholding it costs lives. [AAP Red Book 2024; CDC MMWR 2016]`,`**Pregnancy - Indian practice**: **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5-7 days** is preferred for rickettsial and scrub fever (ICMR 2015, ICMR STW); avoid chloramphenicol in the third trimester (grey baby syndrome). [ICMR 2015]`,`**Pregnancy - CDC position**: for **life-threatening spotted fever, doxycycline should not be withheld** in pregnancy because the benefit outweighs the small risk; this differs from Indian guidance, which favours azithromycin - use doxycycline for severe disease when azithromycin fails or is unavailable. [CDC MMWR 2016]`,`**Breastfeeding**: short doxycycline courses and azithromycin are compatible with breastfeeding. [BNF 2025]`,`**G6PD deficiency**: risk of severe haemolysis and fulminant disease in RMSF; treat early and monitor haemoglobin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly and comorbid**: atypical presentation without rash, higher mortality; **CKD needs no doxycycline dose change**, and doxycycline is the tetracycline of choice in renal failure. [BNF 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: syndromic diagnosis (fever plus rash or eschar plus exposure), **oral doxycycline started at the PHC**, and referral of complicated cases - do not delay for Weil-Felix. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Pneumonitis and ARDS**, **myocarditis** (arrhythmia, heart failure), **meningoencephalitis**, **AKI**, **hepatitis**, **DIC** and **shock** are the major complications. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Digital gangrene and purpura fulminans** are characteristic of severe **Indian tick typhus in children** and may need amputation. [IAP 2017]`,`**Secondary HLH** (fever, cytopenias, ferritin above 500-10,000, high triglycerides) is a recognised complication of rickettsial infections (see the HLH topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`With early doxycycline, Indian tick typhus and murine typhus have **mortality under 1-5%**; delays of more than 5 days, purpura, and CNS involvement predict death. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Epidemic typhus** untreated carries **10-60% mortality**, highest in the elderly; survivors may have Brill-Zinsser relapse decades later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Failure to defervesce within 48-72 hours of doxycycline** should prompt rethinking: wrong diagnosis (enteric fever, malaria, dengue, TB), complication or co-infection. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Outpatients: **review at 48 hours** - persisting fever, new purpura, breathlessness or low urine output means admission. [ICMR 2015]`,`Inpatients: **daily CBC, sodium, creatinine and LFT**, SpO2 and urine output; ECG if tachycardic or arrhythmic. [ICMR 2015]`,`Refer to tertiary care for **ARDS needing ventilation, AKI needing dialysis, encephalopathy, gangrene, myocarditis or HLH**. [ICMR 2015]`,`Send **paired sera 2-4 weeks apart** for IFA or IgM/IgG when the diagnosis matters (surveillance, unusual course). [CDC 2022]`,`**Notify** rickettsial fevers (including scrub typhus) under **IDSP on the IHIP portal**; clusters prompt vector surveys. [IDSP 2024]`,`After discharge, check that **digits and peripheral pulses** recover, follow up abnormal LFT/creatinine at 2-4 weeks, and look for late neurological sequelae. [IAP 2017]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Tick control**: treat dogs with **acaricides and tick collars**, clean kennels and cracks in walls, check pets and children daily for ticks after outdoor play. [CDC 2022]`,`**Personal protection**: long sleeves and trousers tucked into socks, **DEET or picaridin repellents** on skin and **permethrin-treated clothing** for forest or field visits. [CDC 2022]`,`**Rat and flea control** for murine typhus: rodent-proof storage, flea control **before** rodent killing (fleas leave dead rats and bite humans). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Louse control** for epidemic typhus: bathing, washing clothes in hot water, **permethrin or ivermectin delousing** in camps and shelters. [Harrison 22e]`,`There is **no licensed vaccine** for any rickettsiosis in clinical use. [Harrison 22e]`,`Rickettsial diseases are part of the **IDSP syndromic surveillance** and ICMR-DHR rickettsial guidelines; the **VRDL network** offers IgM ELISA and PCR in many states. [ICMR VRDL 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**INTREST trial (NEJM 2023)**: in severe scrub typhus, **IV doxycycline plus azithromycin** reduced the composite of death, complications and persistent fever at day 7 versus monotherapy - now applied to severe undifferentiated rickettsial disease in many Indian ICUs. [Varghese et al, NEJM 2023]`,`**ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows (2022-23)** keep **empirical doxycycline** (azithromycin in pregnancy) for AUF after day 3-5 and **ceftriaxone plus doxycycline** for sepsis without focus - beta-lactams alone are explicitly discouraged. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**AAP Red Book 2024-2027** reaffirms **doxycycline for children of any age** for rickettsial disease, short courses carrying no tooth-staining risk. [AAP Red Book 2024]`,`**CDC** continues to recommend doxycycline even in pregnancy for life-threatening spotted fever (MMWR 2016, tick-borne diseases reference 2022) - an acknowledged difference from Indian azithromycin-first guidance. [CDC 2022]`,`**SSC 2026** sepsis care (NEWS2/MEWS screening, balanced crystalloids, MAP 65) applies to septic rickettsial disease, with extra caution about fluid overload because of capillary leak. [SSC 2026]`,`The **ICMR-DHR Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Rickettsial Diseases in India (2015)** remain the national reference; they discourage Weil-Felix as a sole diagnostic test where ELISA is available. [ICMR 2015]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Differentiate the rash of spotted fever from that of typhus'** - centripetal with palms and soles versus centrifugal sparing palms and soles. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Where will you look for an eschar?'** - scalp, ears, axilla, under breasts, waistband, groin, genitalia, perineum, gluteal cleft, popliteal fossa. [ICMR 2015]`,`Viva: **'Weil-Felix pattern in Indian tick typhus?'** - OX19 and OX2 positive, OXK negative; scrub typhus OXK only; typhus group OX19 strongly. [ICMR 2015]`,`Viva: **'Why is Weil-Felix unreliable?'** - heterophile, insensitive, positive late (after day 7-10), cross-reactive, cannot speciate. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Can you give doxycycline to a 4-year-old?'** - yes, **2.2 mg/kg twice daily**, short course; the staining risk belongs to tetracycline. [AAP Red Book 2024]`,`Common mistake: treating 'fever with rash' with **cotrimoxazole, amoxicillin or ceftriaxone alone** - none covers rickettsiae and cotrimoxazole may worsen them. [ICMR 2022]`,`Common mistake: **stopping doxycycline on day 2** when the fever breaks - continue for 7 days or 3 days after defervescence. [ICMR 2015]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for rickettsial infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (adult)`,`100 mg oral BD; severe 100 mg IV 12-hourly (200 mg load)`,`7 days or 3 days after afebrile`,`Pill oesophagitis, photosensitivity, nausea; first line`],[`Doxycycline (child, any age)`,`2.2 mg/kg oral/IV BD, max 100 mg/dose`,`5-7 days or 3 days after afebrile`,`No tooth staining with short courses`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg oral/IV OD; child 10 mg/kg OD`,`5 days (5-7 in pregnancy)`,`GI upset, QT prolongation; preferred in pregnancy in India`],[`Doxycycline + azithromycin (severe)`,`Doxycycline 100 mg IV BD + azithromycin 500 mg IV OD`,`7 days`,`Used in severe scrub/undifferentiated rickettsial disease (INTREST)`],[`Chloramphenicol`,`50-75 mg/kg/day IV/oral in 4 doses, max 4 g/day`,`7 days`,`Marrow suppression, aplastic anaemia, grey baby syndrome; less effective`],[`Doxycycline (epidemic typhus outbreak)`,`200 mg oral single dose`,`Single dose`,`Relapse possible; delouse contacts`]]},{heading:`Rickettsial diseases relevant to India`,columns:[`Disease`,`Organism`,`Vector / reservoir`,`Rash`,`Eschar`],rows:[[`Indian tick typhus`,`R. conorii indica`,`Brown dog tick (Rhipicephalus)`,`Centripetal, palms and soles, may be purpuric`,`Uncommon`],[`Flea-borne spotted fever`,`R. felis`,`Cat flea`,`Maculopapular`,`Occasional`],[`Murine (endemic) typhus`,`R. typhi`,`Rat flea faeces (Xenopsylla)`,`Truncal, centrifugal, spares palms/soles; in about half`,`Absent`],[`Epidemic typhus`,`R. prowazekii`,`Body louse faeces`,`Truncal, centrifugal, spares face, palms, soles`,`Absent`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Orientia tsutsugamushi`,`Larval trombiculid mite (chigger)`,`Truncal maculopapular, inconstant`,`Common`]]},{heading:`Fever with rash or eschar - differential`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`,`Treatment clue`],rows:[[`Spotted fever`,`Rash from wrists/ankles inward, palms and soles, oedema`,`SFG IgM ELISA, PCR`,`Doxycycline`],[`Murine typhus`,`Faint truncal rash, rat exposure`,`TG IgM ELISA, IFA`,`Doxycycline`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, regional nodes`,`Scrub IgM ELISA`,`Doxycycline or azithromycin`],[`Meningococcaemia`,`Rapid purpura, shock, meningism`,`Blood culture, CSF`,`Ceftriaxone immediately`],[`Dengue`,`Leucopenia, rising haematocrit, no eschar`,`NS1, IgM`,`Fluids; no antibiotic`],[`Secondary syphilis`,`Palms and soles rash, little fever`,`VDRL, TPHA`,`Benzathine penicillin`],[`Cutaneous anthrax`,`Painless black eschar with marked oedema`,`Gram stain, culture`,`Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests and their limits`,columns:[`Test`,`Positive result`,`Best timing`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Weil-Felix`,`1:80 or more, or rising titre; OX19+OX2 = SFG, OXK = scrub`,`After day 7-10`,`Insensitive, cross-reactive, cannot speciate`],[`IgM ELISA (SFG, TG, scrub)`,`Above kit cut-off`,`Day 5-7 onwards`,`Group-level only; local cut-offs vary`],[`IFA (IgG)`,`Fourfold rise in paired sera`,`Acute and 2-4 weeks later`,`Retrospective; reference labs`],[`PCR - eschar swab/biopsy`,`Species-specific DNA`,`Any time an eschar is present`,`Needs an eschar`],[`PCR - blood/buffy coat`,`DNA detected`,`First week, before doxycycline`,`Lower sensitivity than eschar`],[`Skin biopsy IHC`,`Rickettsial antigen in vessel walls`,`Early rash`,`Specialist labs`]]}],redFlags:[`**Purpuric rash or digital gangrene** - severe spotted fever or meningococcaemia; IV therapy now`,`**Hypotension or capillary refill over 3 seconds**`,`**Breathlessness, SpO2 under 94% or bilateral infiltrates** - rickettsial pneumonitis or ARDS`,`**Altered sensorium, seizures or neck stiffness** - meningoencephalitis`,`**Oliguria or rising creatinine; jaundice**`,`**No defervescence 48-72 hours after doxycycline** - rethink the diagnosis`,`**Pregnancy or infancy** with suspected rickettsial fever`],pearls:[`**Spotted fever spreads in, typhus spreads out**: centripetal with palms and soles versus centrifugal sparing them.`,`**Undress and hunt the eschar** - scalp to popliteal fossa, especially hidden folds.`,`Weil-Felix: **OX19 + OX2 = spotted fever; OX19 = typhus; OXK = scrub** - but never wait for it.`,`**Doxycycline at every age**: 2.2 mg/kg BD in children; staining is a tetracycline problem.`,`**Fever gone in 48 hours on doxycycline** is almost diagnostic of a rickettsiosis.`,`**Cotrimoxazole worsens** rickettsial disease; ceftriaxone misses it.`,`India: **azithromycin in pregnancy**; CDC: doxycycline even in pregnancy if life-threatening.`,`Severe disease: **IV doxycycline plus azithromycin** (INTREST 2023).`,`**Brill-Zinsser** = recrudescent epidemic typhus years later.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-q1`,stem:`A 6-year-old boy from Pune with a pet dog has fever for 5 days, periorbital oedema and a maculopapular rash that began on the wrists and ankles and now involves the palms, soles and trunk. No eschar is found. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg twice daily for 7 days`,`Amoxicillin 30 mg/kg three times daily`,`Cotrimoxazole twice daily for 7 days`,`Paracetamol and observation only`],answer:0,explanation:`A centripetal rash involving palms and soles with oedema in a child exposed to dogs suggests Indian tick typhus (R. conorii, brown dog tick). Doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg twice daily is first line at any age, and short courses do not stain teeth. Amoxicillin has no intracellular activity against rickettsiae. Cotrimoxazole may worsen rickettsial disease. Observing alone delays treatment, and mortality rises after day 5.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-q2`,stem:`Which statement about the Weil-Felix test is correct?`,options:[`It identifies the infecting Rickettsia species`,`It is usually positive in the first 3 days of illness`,`OX19 and OX2 agglutination with negative OXK suggests the spotted fever group`,`A negative test excludes rickettsial infection`],answer:2,explanation:`Weil-Felix detects heterophile antibodies against Proteus antigens: OX19 and OX2 in spotted fever, OX19 strongly in the typhus group, and OXK in scrub typhus. It cannot speciate. It becomes positive only after about day 7-10 and is insensitive, so a negative early test neither excludes infection nor justifies withholding doxycycline.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-q3`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman, 20 weeks pregnant, has fever, headache and a palpable eschar in the groin, with no organ dysfunction. Following Indian national guidance, which treatment is preferred?`,options:[`Chloramphenicol 50 mg/kg/day for 7 days`,`Azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5-7 days`,`Ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily for 7 days`],answer:1,explanation:`ICMR guidance prefers azithromycin for rickettsial and scrub typhus fevers in pregnancy, and it is effective in uncomplicated disease. Chloramphenicol causes marrow toxicity and grey baby syndrome and is less effective. Fluoroquinolones are linked to worse outcomes in spotted fever and are avoided in pregnancy. Ceftriaxone does not reach intracellular rickettsiae. CDC would accept doxycycline if the illness became life-threatening.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-q4`,stem:`A grain-market worker in Guwahati has 6 days of fever, headache and a faint maculopapular rash on the trunk and axillae that spares the palms and soles. Rats infest the market. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Indian tick typhus`,`Rickettsialpox`,`Secondary syphilis`,`Murine typhus`],answer:3,explanation:`A centrifugal truncal rash sparing palms and soles, with rat and flea exposure in a market, is typical of murine typhus (R. typhi, transmitted by rat flea faeces). Indian tick typhus gives a centripetal rash involving palms and soles. Rickettsialpox is mite-borne, with an eschar and a vesicular rash. Secondary syphilis classically involves the palms and soles, with little fever.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-q5`,stem:`A 45-year-old farmer with suspected rickettsial fever has SpO2 88%, bilateral infiltrates, BP 86/50 mmHg and creatinine 2.8 mg/dL on day 7. Scrub typhus cannot be excluded. What antimicrobial approach is best supported by recent trial evidence?`,options:[`Oral doxycycline alone, as an outpatient`,`IV ceftriaxone alone`,`IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin`,`IV chloramphenicol plus gentamicin`],answer:2,explanation:`In the INTREST trial (NEJM 2023), combination IV doxycycline plus azithromycin beat either drug alone in severe scrub typhus, and it is reasonable in severe undifferentiated rickettsial disease with ARDS, shock and AKI. Outpatient oral therapy is unsafe for this patient. Ceftriaxone alone does not cover rickettsiae or Orientia. Chloramphenicol is a less effective second-line drug, and gentamicin is nephrotoxic and has no rickettsial activity.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Classify rickettsial diseases. Discuss the clinical features, diagnosis and management of Indian tick typhus, with a note on the limitations of the Weil-Felix test.`,openingLines:[`Rickettsioses are acute febrile infections by obligate intracellular bacteria transmitted by ticks, fleas, lice and mites, whose common lesion is a disseminated endothelial vasculitis.`,`Indian tick typhus, caused by Rickettsia conorii indica and spread by the brown dog tick, is the commonest spotted fever in India and is treated with doxycycline at all ages.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Spotted fever group: R. conorii (ITT), R. rickettsii, R. africae, R. akari, R. felis.`,`Typhus group: R. typhi (murine), R. prowazekii (epidemic, Brill-Zinsser).`,`Scrub typhus group: Orientia tsutsugamushi.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Inoculation, local multiplication (eschar), endothelial infection, vasculitis, capillary leak.`,`Explains rash, oedema, pneumonitis, encephalitis, AKI, gangrene.`]},{heading:`Clinical features of ITT`,points:[`Incubation 3-14 days; fever, headache, myalgia; rash day 3-5 from wrists and ankles, palms and soles, petechial or purpuric.`,`Oedema in children; eschar uncommon; complications - ARDS, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis, AKI, gangrene, HLH.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`CBC, sodium, LFT, creatinine; AUF panel for malaria, dengue, lepto, enteric.`,`SFG IgM ELISA from day 5-7; IFA fourfold rise (reference); PCR of eschar, skin biopsy or blood.`]},{heading:`Weil-Felix limitations`,points:[`OX19 + OX2 in SFG; positive only after day 7-10; insensitive; cross-reacts; cannot speciate; never delay treatment.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Doxycycline 100 mg BD (child 2.2 mg/kg BD, any age) for 7 days or 3 days after afebrile.`,`Pregnancy: azithromycin (India); severe: IV doxycycline, add azithromycin; chloramphenicol second line.`,`Supportive: cautious fluids, ventilation, dialysis, gangrene care; avoid cotrimoxazole.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Tick control on dogs, repellents, protective clothing, early tick removal; notify on IHIP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Classification tree of rickettsial diseases with vectors`,`Body diagram of eschar sites and rash direction (centripetal versus centrifugal)`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis including Weil-Felix limits`,marks:2.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2.5},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`spotted fever group`,`Rickettsia conorii`,`Rhipicephalus`,`centripetal rash`,`Weil-Felix`,`doxycycline 2.2 mg/kg`,`azithromycin in pregnancy`]},{id:`infectious-fever-typhus-spotted-fever-detail-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between spotted fever, murine typhus and scrub typhus.`,openingLines:[`All three are rickettsial vasculitides treated with doxycycline, but they differ in organism, vector, rash pattern, eschar frequency and serology.`,`Spotted fever spreads centripetally to palms and soles; typhus spreads centrifugally sparing them; scrub typhus is marked by an eschar.`],answer:[{heading:`Organism and vector`,points:[`Spotted fever: R. conorii - brown dog tick.`,`Murine typhus: R. typhi - rat flea faeces.`,`Scrub typhus: Orientia tsutsugamushi - chigger mite.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Rash: centripetal with palms and soles / truncal centrifugal sparing palms and soles / inconstant truncal.`,`Eschar: uncommon / absent / common with regional nodes.`,`Severity: gangrene in children / usually mild / ARDS, myocarditis, AKI.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Weil-Felix OX19 + OX2 / OX19 / OXK; group-specific IgM ELISA; PCR of eschar.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Doxycycline for all; azithromycin in pregnancy; IV combination in severe disease.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Organism and vector`,marks:1},{part:`Rash and eschar`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`spotted fever`,`murine typhus`,`scrub typhus`,`eschar`,`Weil-Felix`,`doxycycline`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Rickettsial diseases chapter`,`Rahi M, Gupte MD, Bhargava A et al. DHR-ICMR Guidelines for diagnosis and management of rickettsial diseases in India. Indian J Med Res 2015`,`Rathi N et al. IAP Guidelines on rickettsial diseases in children. Indian Pediatrics 2017`,`Biggs HM et al. Diagnosis and management of tickborne rickettsial diseases: Rocky Mountain spotted fever and other spotted fever group rickettsioses, ehrlichioses and anaplasmosis - United States. CDC MMWR Recomm Rep 2016`,`Varghese GM et al. Intravenous doxycycline, azithromycin, or both for severe scrub typhus (INTREST). N Engl J Med 2023`,`American Academy of Pediatrics. Red Book: Report of the Committee on Infectious Diseases, 2024-2027`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India - Acute Undifferentiated Fever, 2022-2023`,`CDC. Tickborne Diseases of the United States: A Reference Manual for Healthcare Providers, 2022`]});var i=[];i.push({id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis`,title:`Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) and PKDL`,oneLiner:`**Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)** is a chronic systemic infection of the reticulo-endothelial system by **Leishmania donovani**, spread in India by the sandfly **Phlebotomus argentipes**, presenting as **fever over 2 weeks with massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia and weight loss** in a resident of the Bihar-Jharkhand-Bengal-UP belt; it is diagnosed by the **rK39 rapid test** and treated with **single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg**, and its sequel **post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL)** is the hidden reservoir the elimination programme must find.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`kala-azar`,`black fever`,`Dumdum fever`,`Leishmania donovani`,`Phlebotomus argentipes`,`sandfly`,`LD bodies`,`amastigote`,`rK39`,`liposomal amphotericin B`,`LAmB`,`miltefosine`,`paromomycin`,`sodium stibogluconate`,`PKDL`,`post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis`,`HIV-VL co-infection`,`KAMIS`,`indoor residual spraying`,`NCVBDC`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Visceral leishmaniasis (VL)** is a vector-borne protozoal infection of macrophages of the spleen, liver, bone marrow and lymph nodes; untreated, symptomatic VL is **fatal in over 95%** within about 2 years. [WHO 2023]`,`Globally VL occurs in about **80 countries**; WHO estimates **50,000-90,000 new cases a year**, and the burden has shifted from South Asia to **East Africa (Sudan, South Sudan, Ethiopia, Kenya)**, which now reports the majority. [WHO 2023]`,`In the **Indian subcontinent** VL is **anthroponotic** - humans are the only reservoir - so treating every case and every PKDL patient breaks transmission, unlike the zoonotic (dog) VL of the Mediterranean and Brazil. [Harrison 22e]`,`India's endemic focus is **Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and eastern Uttar Pradesh** - **54 districts, 633 blocks**; Bihar contributes the large majority of cases (Saran, Siwan, Vaishali, Muzaffarpur, Gopalganj belt). [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Indian cases fell from about **77,000 in 1992 and over 44,000 in 2007** to **under 1,000 a year since 2022** under the **Kala-azar Elimination Programme**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`The **elimination target** is **under 1 VL case per 10,000 population per year at block level**; India reached it in **all 633 endemic blocks in 2023** and must sustain it for **3 consecutive years** before WHO validation. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Bangladesh** became the **first country validated by WHO** for eliminating VL as a public-health problem in **October 2023**; Nepal and Bhutan are on the same pathway. [WHO 2023]`,`VL is a disease of the **rural poor**: mud-walled houses with cracks, damp floors, cattle sheds next to the house, poor nutrition and sleeping outdoors, mainly among **Musahar and other marginalised communities** in Bihar. [Park 27e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, vector and risk factors`,points:[`**Leishmania donovani** causes VL in India and East Africa; **L. infantum (L. chagasi)** causes zoonotic VL in the Mediterranean, Middle East, Central Asia and Brazil. [Harrison 22e]`,`The parasite has two forms: the flagellated **promastigote** in the sandfly gut and the non-flagellated intracellular **amastigote (Leishman-Donovan or LD body, 2-4 micrometres)** in human macrophages. [Harrison 22e]`,`The Indian vector is the female sandfly **Phlebotomus argentipes** - tiny (2-3 mm), hairy, a **weak hopping flier** with a range of under 50-100 m, active from dusk, resting low on walls (under 6 feet) and breeding in **moist organic soil and cracks in mud walls and cattle sheds**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Transmission is also possible by **blood transfusion, shared needles, congenital (transplacental)** and organ transplant routes. [Harrison 22e]`,`Incubation is usually **2-6 months** (range 10 days to several years); most infections are **asymptomatic** - only about 1 in 5-10 infected people develop disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors for disease: **malnutrition, HIV, immunosuppression (transplant, steroids, biologics), young age**, a household case in the last year and living within a cluster. [WHO 2023]`,`Seasonal peak of sandfly density is the **pre-monsoon and monsoon months (March-October)**, which is why indoor residual spraying is timed around **February-March and May-June**. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Inoculated promastigotes are taken up by **macrophages via complement receptors (CR3)**; the surface **lipophosphoglycan and gp63** inhibit the oxidative burst and phagolysosome fusion, letting amastigotes multiply inside. [Harrison 22e]`,`Control depends on a **Th1 response (IL-12, IFN-gamma activating macrophage nitric oxide)**; disease follows when **IL-10 and TGF-beta dominate** - so the skin-test (leishmanin) is **negative during active VL** and becomes positive after cure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Parasitised macrophages fill the spleen, liver and marrow, producing **massive splenomegaly** and **hypersplenism**, which with marrow infiltration explains **pancytopenia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Polyclonal B-cell activation** gives marked **hypergammaglobulinaemia (IgG)** with **reversed albumin-globulin ratio** - the basis of the obsolete **aldehyde (Napier) test** and of high ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemia** is multifactorial - hypersplenism, marrow suppression, haemolysis and blood loss; neutropenia and immune suppression make **secondary bacterial infection (pneumonia, TB, dysentery)** the commonest cause of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Grey-black skin hyperpigmentation**, giving the name kala-azar (black fever), is seen mainly in Indian patients; its mechanism is uncertain (proposed adrenal involvement or melanocyte stimulation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amphotericin B** binds **ergosterol-like sterols** in the Leishmania membrane, forming pores; the **liposomal form** is taken up by the same reticulo-endothelial macrophages that harbour the parasite, delivering drug to the target and sparing the kidney. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Miltefosine** (alkylphosphocholine) disrupts parasite membrane phospholipid and signalling and is the only **oral** drug; **paromomycin** is an aminoglycoside that blocks parasite ribosomes; **antimonials** inhibit trypanothione reductase and glycolysis - resistance in Bihar is linked to parasite thiol up-regulation. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Classification and case definitions`,points:[`**Clinical spectrum**: asymptomatic infection, **symptomatic VL**, **VL-HIV co-infection**, relapse VL, and **PKDL** as a dermal sequel. [WHO 2023]`,`**Suspected VL case (India)**: a person from an endemic area with **fever of more than 2 weeks and splenomegaly**, not responding to antimalarials and antibiotics. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Confirmed VL**: a suspected case with a **positive rK39 rapid test** or **LD bodies on splenic, marrow or lymph-node aspirate**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Primary unresponsiveness**: no initial cure at end of treatment; **relapse**: return of symptoms and parasites after initial cure, usually within **6-12 months**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Initial cure** is clinical improvement at end of treatment; **definitive (final) cure** is being **symptom-free at 6 months** after treatment. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**PKDL is classified clinically** as **macular (hypopigmented, the commonest in India), papular, nodular, or polymorphic** (mixed); WHO grades PKDL severity by extent - grade 1 face only, grade 2 face plus upper chest and arms, grade 3 most of the body. [WHO 2023]`,`**Splenic aspirate parasite density** is graded **0 to 6+** on a log scale (Chulay-Bryceson) - from fewer than 1 parasite per 1,000 fields to over 100 per field - and is used to judge response and relapse. [WHO 2010]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Prolonged fever** over 2 weeks, often irregular or **double-quotidian (two spikes in 24 hours)**, in a patient who looks surprisingly **well and ambulant** for the degree of fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`Progressive **abdominal fullness or left upper quadrant dragging pain** from splenomegaly, **weight loss and wasting** despite preserved appetite. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Darkening of the skin** of face, hands and abdomen, and thinning of hair, are noticed by the family. [Park 27e]`,`**Bleeding** - epistaxis, gum bleeding - and pallor with exertional breathlessness reflect thrombocytopenia and anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the **residence in an endemic block**, a **past VL episode** or treatment, **family or neighbour with VL or PKDL**, migration back from endemic Bihar, and **HIV risk**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Atypical presentations: **HIV-VL** with fever and cytopenia but small or absent spleen, gut, lung or skin involvement; **acute onset** mimicking malaria or typhoid; and VL first found during work-up of **pancytopenia or PUO** outside the endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PKDL history**: painless hypopigmented patches or nodules starting **around the mouth and chin**, appearing **months to years after treated VL** (in India median about 2-3 years; 15-20% give no VL history). [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Massive splenomegaly** - soft, non-tender, often crossing the umbilicus or reaching the right iliac fossa; splenomegaly of over 8 cm in a fever of weeks is kala-azar until proven otherwise in an endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Moderate hepatomegaly**, usually smaller than the spleen; jaundice and ascites are late signs suggesting advanced disease or co-infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pallor, wasting, oedema** (hypoalbuminaemia) and **dry, thin, brittle hair**; **grey-dark hyperpigmentation** of the skin. [Park 27e]`,`**Lymphadenopathy** is **uncommon in Indian VL** but common in **Sudanese/East African VL** - useful in a viva. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Petechiae, epistaxis, gum bleeding**; look for signs of secondary infection - crepitations, oral thrush, **cancrum oris** in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PKDL**: symmetric **hypopigmented macules** with preserved sensation (unlike leprosy), or papules and nodules on face, chin, lips, ears and trunk; **no nerve thickening**. [WHO 2023]`,`Record **spleen size in cm below the costal margin** at baseline - regression of the spleen and rise in Hb are the clinical markers of cure. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Chronic malaria and hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly** (tropical splenomegaly syndrome): very high IgM, high anti-malarial antibody, responds to prolonged antimalarials; rK39 negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haematological malignancy** - **CML** (leucocytosis, basophilia, BCR-ABL), **myelofibrosis** (tear-drop cells, dry tap), **lymphoma** (nodes, LDH): leucocytosis argues against VL, which causes leucopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enteric fever, tuberculosis (disseminated, splenic), brucellosis** and **infective endocarditis** - prolonged fever with splenomegaly but usually smaller spleen; cultures and imaging discriminate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cirrhosis with portal hypertension** - varices, ascites, stigmata of liver disease and no fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemolytic anaemias (thalassaemia)** - lifelong, family history, target cells and HbF/HbA2 on HPLC. [Harrison 22e]`,`For PKDL: **leprosy** (anaesthetic patches, thickened nerves, AFB on slit skin), **vitiligo** (depigmented, not hypopigmented), **pityriasis versicolor**, sarcoidosis and lupus vulgaris. [WHO 2023]`,`Outside the endemic belt, consider **cutaneous leishmaniasis (L. tropica) of Rajasthan and Himachal** - a skin ulcer, not a visceral disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**CBC**: pancytopenia - Hb often **under 8 g/dL**, WBC **under 3,000/microlitre** with neutropenia, platelets under 1 lakh; ESR very high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serum proteins**: albumin low, **globulin high with reversed A:G ratio**; LFT, creatinine and electrolytes before amphotericin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**rK39 immunochromatographic RDT** (recombinant kinesin antigen) is the **first-line diagnostic at PHC level**: sensitivity **about 95-99% and specificity about 90-98% in South Asia**; it uses a fingerprick sample and reads in **10-20 minutes**. [WHO 2023]`,`rK39 detects antibody, so it **stays positive for years after cure** - it cannot diagnose **relapse**, which needs parasitological confirmation; it is also **less sensitive in HIV-VL and in East Africa**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Splenic aspirate** has the highest sensitivity, **93-99%**, but carries a risk of fatal haemorrhage (about **1 in 1,000**); do it only with platelets over 40,000, INR normal and a palpable spleen over 3 cm. [WHO 2010]`,`**Bone marrow aspirate** is safer but less sensitive (**53-86%**); **lymph-node aspirate** 53-65%, more useful in East Africa; Giemsa stain shows **LD bodies (amastigotes with nucleus and rod-shaped kinetoplast)** in macrophages. [Harrison 22e]`,`Advanced: **PCR / qPCR** on blood or tissue (best in HIV-VL and PKDL; also for monitoring), **DAT (direct agglutination test)**, culture in **NNN medium**, and **rK28** RDT; **leishmanin skin test** is negative in active VL. [WHO 2023]`,`**Screen every VL patient for HIV and TB**; and in PKDL, rK39 positivity with typical lesions in an endemic area is enough for programme diagnosis, with **slit-skin smear or PCR** for confirmation. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Suspect, test with **rK39 at the PHC/CHC**, and **refer every confirmed case** to the designated kala-azar treatment centre - treatment is free under NCVBDC and is given **in a facility under supervision**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**First line in India: single-dose liposomal amphotericin B (LAmB) 10 mg/kg IV** in 5% dextrose over **2 hours** - cure rates **over 95%** with one hospital day. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Second-line combinations** (when LAmB is unsuitable or unavailable): **LAmB 5 mg/kg single dose + miltefosine for 7 days**; **LAmB 5 mg/kg + paromomycin for 10 days**; or **miltefosine + paromomycin for 10 days**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Miltefosine** dose is about **2.5 mg/kg/day orally**: adults over 25 kg **50 mg twice daily**, under 25 kg 50 mg once daily, children 2-11 years 2.5 mg/kg/day; take with food to reduce vomiting. [WHO 2010]`,`**Paromomycin** is **11 mg/kg base (15 mg/kg sulphate) IM once daily**; as monotherapy it is given for **21 days**. [WHO 2010]`,`**Amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg IV daily or on alternate days for 15 doses** (total 15 mg/kg) remains a backup where LAmB is unavailable - needs admission for renal and potassium monitoring. [WHO 2010]`,`**Miltefosine monotherapy (28 days) is no longer recommended** in India because relapse rose to **10-20%**, and **sodium stibogluconate is abandoned in Bihar** because over 60% are unresponsive. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Supportive care: **nutrition, iron and folate**, treat **intercurrent infections first**, transfuse if Hb under 5-6 g/dL or bleeding, and counsel on the **wage-loss compensation** paid to VL and PKDL patients under the programme. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Admit** for LAmB infusion; also admit for **severe anaemia (Hb under 5 g/dL)**, **bleeding, jaundice, oedema, severe malnutrition, age under 2 or over 45 years, HIV co-infection, suspected secondary infection** or relapse. [WHO 2023]`,`Before amphotericin: weight, **creatinine, potassium, magnesium**, CBC; **test dose** is not required for LAmB in programme practice but **watch the first 30 minutes** for infusion reactions. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Infusion reactions** (fever, rigors, back pain, flushing, hypoxia) are commoner with **deoxycholate**; slow the infusion, give paracetamol and antihistamine; hydrocortisone premedication for recurrent reactions. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`For **deoxycholate AmB**, prehydrate with **500 mL-1 L normal saline**, monitor creatinine and **potassium at least twice weekly**, and replace potassium and magnesium - nephrotoxicity and hypokalaemia are dose-limiting. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Treat bacterial infections empirically and aggressively** - pneumonia, sepsis and diarrhoea kill more VL patients than the parasite; screen for **TB** in prolonged illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relapse or unresponsive VL**: confirm parasitologically, then give **LAmB total 15-30 mg/kg** (for example 5 mg/kg on alternate days) or a combination; refer to a tertiary centre and test for HIV. [WHO 2023]`,`**Severe bleeding**: platelet transfusion and FFP; avoid splenic aspiration and IM injections while platelets are low. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HIV-VL co-infection** (about 2-6% of VL in Bihar): WHO recommends **LAmB 5 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11 (total 30 mg/kg) plus miltefosine 100 mg/day for 14 days** in South Asia (28 days in East Africa). [WHO 2022]`,`In HIV-VL start **ART** (dolutegravir-based) early, and give **secondary prophylaxis with LAmB 3-5 mg/kg every 3-4 weeks** while **CD4 is under 200 cells/microlitre**; relapse is common. [WHO 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: **LAmB is the drug of choice** (10 mg/kg single dose or 3 mg/kg daily to 21 mg/kg total); **miltefosine is teratogenic** and contraindicated, and antimonials cause abortion. [WHO 2023]`,`**Women of reproductive age on miltefosine** must have a pregnancy test first and use **effective contraception during treatment and for at least 3 months after** (current product labels advise up to 5 months). [WHO 2010]`,`**Children**: same single-dose LAmB 10 mg/kg; miltefosine 2.5 mg/kg/day (allometric dosing preferred); watch nutrition and secondary infection. [WHO 2023]`,`**CKD and elderly**: prefer **LAmB**, avoid deoxycholate and paromomycin (nephrotoxic, ototoxic); adjust supportive care. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Transplant and biologic users** develop VL with atypical, severe disease - think of it with unexplained cytopenia after travel or residence in an endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Secondary bacterial infections** - pneumonia, TB, sepsis, dysentery, **cancrum oris** - are the major cause of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe anaemia, bleeding** (epistaxis, GI), **hepatic dysfunction**, oedema and malnutrition; splenic rupture or infarction is rare. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug toxicity**: deoxycholate AmB - **nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, myocarditis**; antimonials - **QT prolongation, arrhythmia, pancreatitis**; paromomycin - **ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, raised transaminases, injection pain**; miltefosine - **vomiting, diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity, nephrotoxicity, teratogenicity, keratitis**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**PKDL** follows treated VL in **about 5-15% of Indian patients** (up to 50% in Sudan, where it appears within 6 months); in India it persists for years and **does not heal spontaneously**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Relapse** occurs in about **5% after single-dose LAmB** and much more often in HIV co-infection; most relapses appear within **6-12 months**. [WHO 2023]`,`With prompt treatment, mortality is **under 5%**; poor prognostic markers are **age under 2 or over 45, severe anaemia, jaundice, bleeding, oedema, HIV and malnutrition**. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`PKDL - the reservoir`,points:[`**Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis** is a dermal form with **parasites in the skin** after (or occasionally without) VL; nodular lesions contain abundant parasites and **infect sandflies**, so PKDL keeps transmission going after VL cases fall. [WHO 2023]`,`It is thought to reflect a **post-treatment Th1 response against persistent skin parasites** - IFN-gamma and IL-10 both high in the lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diagnosis**: typical lesions + endemic area + rK39 positive; **slit-skin smear** is often negative in macular PKDL, so **PCR of skin biopsy** confirms. [WHO 2023]`,`**Treatment (WHO 2022, South Asia)**: **miltefosine for 12 weeks with allometric dosing**, or **LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly for 3 weeks (total 30 mg/kg)** where miltefosine is unsuitable. [WHO 2022]`,`Stop miltefosine and refer to ophthalmology if **eye pain, redness or blurring** appear - **keratitis and ocular toxicity** were reported with 12-week courses in India. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Programme case search for PKDL is **house-to-house in villages with VL cases in the previous 3 years**, using ASHAs and photo cards. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer **all suspected cases** to the block/district kala-azar treatment centre; refer relapse, HIV-VL, pregnancy, severe disease and drug toxicity to a **tertiary centre (for example RMRIMS Patna)**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Follow up treated VL at **1, 3, 6 and 12 months** for fever, spleen size, Hb and weight; **definitive cure is declared at 6 months** if symptom-free. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Continue watching for **PKDL for at least 3 years** - teach the patient to report any new skin patch. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`During miltefosine: **monthly LFT and creatinine**, pregnancy test, contraception and eye symptoms; during paromomycin: creatinine, LFT and hearing. [WHO 2010]`,`Every case is entered into **KAMIS (Kala-azar Management Information System)** and the village is mapped for **active case detection and focal spraying**. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and the national elimination programme`,points:[`The programme runs under **NCVBDC** with four pillars: **early case detection and complete treatment, integrated vector management, community mobilisation, and surveillance with operational research**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Indoor residual spraying (IRS)** with the synthetic pyrethroid **alpha-cypermethrin** (replacing DDT, to which sandflies became resistant) in **two rounds a year**, walls and cattle sheds sprayed **up to 6 feet**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Active case detection** (camp approach and index-case-based search within **500 m**) plus incentives to **ASHAs** for referring a confirmed case and ensuring completion. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Pucca housing** under PMAY-G, **plastering of mud walls**, keeping cattle away from sleeping areas and **fine-mesh insecticide-treated nets** reduce sandfly contact. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`There is **no vaccine** for human VL; post-elimination surveillance must also screen **HIV patients from endemic districts** and migrants. [WHO 2023]`,`VL is a **notifiable disease** in endemic states; the **WHO NTD Road Map 2021-2030** targets elimination as a public-health problem in South-East Asia. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2022** guideline for **HIV-VL co-infection**: LAmB 30 mg/kg total plus **miltefosine** (14 days South Asia, 28 days East Africa) replaces LAmB monotherapy. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2022** guideline for **PKDL in South Asia**: miltefosine 12 weeks with allometric dosing, or LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly for 3 weeks. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2024** guideline for **East Africa**: **paromomycin + miltefosine for 14 days** replaces the 17-day SSG + paromomycin combination - an important contrast with India's single-dose LAmB. [WHO 2024]`,`**India reached the elimination target in all endemic blocks in 2023** and has been sustaining it through 2024-2025, entering the 3-year validation window; Bangladesh was validated in **October 2023**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**rK39 remains the programme diagnostic**, but PCR-based tools (qPCR, LAMP) are increasingly used for HIV-VL, PKDL and post-elimination surveillance. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Kala-azar - diagnosis and treatment'** or **'PKDL'**; always write the **case definition, rK39, single-dose LAmB 10 mg/kg** and the **elimination target under 1 per 10,000 at block level**.`,`Common mistake: **using rK39 to diagnose relapse** - it remains positive for years; relapse needs a tissue aspirate or PCR.`,`Common mistake: writing **SSG or miltefosine monotherapy** as first line in India - both are obsolete for Bihar.`,`Viva: **why is PKDL important?** - it is the **reservoir** in an anthroponotic disease, and nodular lesions infect sandflies.`,`Viva: **why is lymphadenopathy a clue to geography?** - common in Sudanese VL, uncommon in Indian VL.`,`Viva: **why is LAmB more effective and less toxic?** - liposomes are taken up by the **reticulo-endothelial macrophages** that harbour amastigotes and reach the kidney less.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for visceral leishmaniasis and PKDL`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Liposomal amphotericin B (first line, India)`,`10 mg/kg IV in 5% dextrose over 2 h`,`Single dose`,`Infusion reactions, back pain, mild hypokalaemia, rare nephrotoxicity; safe in pregnancy`],[`LAmB + miltefosine`,`LAmB 5 mg/kg IV once, then miltefosine about 2.5 mg/kg/day orally`,`Miltefosine 7 days`,`Miltefosine: vomiting, diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity, teratogenic`],[`LAmB + paromomycin`,`LAmB 5 mg/kg IV once, then paromomycin 11 mg/kg base IM OD`,`Paromomycin 10 days`,`Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, raised transaminases, injection pain`],[`Miltefosine + paromomycin`,`Miltefosine 2.5 mg/kg/day oral + paromomycin 11 mg/kg base IM OD`,`10 days (India); 14 days in East Africa (WHO 2024)`,`Contraception needed; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`1 mg/kg IV over 4-6 h, daily or alternate days`,`15 doses (total 15 mg/kg)`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, rigors, myocarditis, anaemia`],[`HIV-VL regimen`,`LAmB 5 mg/kg IV on days 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 + miltefosine 100 mg/day`,`Miltefosine 14 days (South Asia); then secondary prophylaxis LAmB every 3-4 weeks if CD4 under 200`,`As above; interactions minimal with dolutegravir ART`],[`PKDL - miltefosine`,`Allometric dose (adult about 50 mg BD)`,`12 weeks`,`Keratitis and eye toxicity - stop and refer; GI upset, hepatotoxicity`],[`PKDL - LAmB`,`5 mg/kg IV twice weekly`,`3 weeks (total 30 mg/kg)`,`Infusion reactions; hypokalaemia`],[`Sodium stibogluconate (obsolete in Bihar)`,`20 mg/kg/day IM or IV`,`30 days`,`QT prolongation, arrhythmia, pancreatitis, hepatitis; over 60% unresponsive in Bihar`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests for VL - what each is good for`,columns:[`Test`,`Sensitivity / specificity`,`Strength`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`rK39 RDT (fingerprick)`,`About 95-99% / 90-98% in South Asia`,`PHC-level, 10-20 min, programme test`,`Positive for years after cure - not for relapse; less sensitive in HIV and East Africa`],[`Splenic aspirate (Giemsa)`,`93-99%`,`Gold standard; grading 0-6+ for response`,`Fatal bleed about 1 in 1,000; needs platelets over 40,000 and normal INR`],[`Bone marrow aspirate`,`53-86%`,`Safer; also excludes leukaemia`,`Lower sensitivity`],[`Lymph-node aspirate`,`53-65%`,`Useful in East African VL`,`Nodes rarely enlarged in Indian VL`],[`PCR / qPCR (blood, tissue)`,`Over 95% in blood`,`HIV-VL, PKDL, relapse, monitoring`,`Tertiary labs; detects asymptomatic infection`],[`Direct agglutination test (DAT)`,`About 95% / 86-98%`,`Quantitative serology`,`Needs lab; remains positive after cure`],[`Leishmanin skin test`,`Negative in active VL`,`Marks past infection or cure`,`Not diagnostic of active disease`]]},{heading:`Fever with massive splenomegaly - differential`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Visceral leishmaniasis`,`Endemic Bihar belt; fever over 2 weeks; pancytopenia; dark skin`,`rK39 RDT; splenic aspirate LD bodies`],[`Hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly`,`Repeated malaria; high IgM`,`Malaria antibody, IgM; response to antimalarials`],[`Chronic myeloid leukaemia`,`Leucocytosis with left shift and basophilia; often afebrile`,`Peripheral smear, BCR-ABL`],[`Myelofibrosis`,`Older age, tear-drop cells, dry tap`,`Marrow biopsy, JAK2/CALR`],[`Lymphoma`,`Lymphadenopathy, B symptoms, high LDH`,`Node biopsy, CT`],[`Thalassaemia major/intermedia`,`Lifelong anaemia, facies, family history`,`HPLC`],[`Cirrhosis with portal hypertension`,`Varices, ascites, liver stigmata, no fever`,`USG Doppler, endoscopy`]]},{heading:`PKDL versus leprosy versus vitiligo`,columns:[`Feature`,`PKDL`,`Leprosy`,`Vitiligo`],rows:[[`Colour`,`Hypopigmented macules, papules, nodules`,`Hypopigmented or erythematous patches`,`Depigmented (milky white)`],[`Sensation`,`Normal`,`Reduced or absent`,`Normal`],[`Nerves`,`Not thickened`,`Thickened peripheral nerves`,`Normal`],[`Distribution`,`Starts around mouth and chin, spreads symmetrically`,`Asymmetric, any site`,`Periorificial, acral, segmental`],[`History`,`Treated VL months to years earlier`,`Contact with leprosy`,`Family history, autoimmunity`],[`Test`,`rK39 positive; skin PCR`,`Slit-skin smear AFB`,`Wood lamp accentuation`]]},{heading:`Classification of VL outcomes and timing`,columns:[`Term`,`Definition`,`When assessed`],rows:[[`Initial cure`,`Afebrile, spleen regressing, Hb rising at end of treatment`,`End of treatment`],[`Definitive cure`,`Symptom-free with no relapse`,`6 months after treatment`],[`Primary unresponsiveness`,`No initial cure`,`End of treatment`],[`Relapse`,`Return of symptoms with parasites after initial cure`,`Usually within 6-12 months`],[`PKDL`,`Dermal lesions with parasites after VL`,`Months to years (India); within 6 months (Sudan)`]]}],redFlags:[`Hb under 5 g/dL, breathlessness or heart failure from anaemia`,`Active bleeding - epistaxis, gum or GI bleeding - or platelets under 20,000`,`Fever with signs of pneumonia, sepsis or dysentery in a VL patient - secondary infection kills`,`Jaundice, ascites or generalised oedema`,`VL in a person with HIV, in pregnancy, or in a child under 2`,`Relapse after a completed course, or no response by end of treatment`,`Eye pain, redness or blurred vision during miltefosine (PKDL course)`],pearls:[`**Fever over 2 weeks + splenomegaly + endemic area = suspected kala-azar** - test with rK39.`,`**One dose, 10 mg/kg** - single-dose liposomal amphotericin B is first line in India.`,`**rK39 cannot diagnose relapse** - it stays positive for years.`,`Indian VL is **anthroponotic**, so **PKDL is the reservoir** that elimination must chase.`,`**Lymph nodes - Sudan; skin darkening - India.**`,`Elimination target: **under 1 per 10,000 population per year at block level** - achieved by India in 2023.`,`**Miltefosine** = oral, teratogenic, contraception during and for months after.`,`Spray **up to 6 feet** - the sandfly is a weak, low-flying hopper.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old man from Muzaffarpur, Bihar, has fever for 6 weeks, weight loss and a spleen palpable 12 cm below the costal margin. Hb is 7 g/dL, WBC 2,400/microlitre and platelets 80,000. The rK39 rapid test is positive. What is the recommended first-line treatment under the national programme?`,options:[`Sodium stibogluconate 20 mg/kg/day for 30 days`,`Miltefosine 50 mg twice daily for 28 days`,`Liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg IV as a single dose`,`Amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg on alternate days for 15 doses`],answer:2,explanation:`Single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg is the first-line regimen in India, curing over 95% with one hospital day. Sodium stibogluconate is abandoned in Bihar because over 60% are unresponsive, miltefosine monotherapy was withdrawn after relapse rose to 10-20%, and deoxycholate amphotericin is a nephrotoxic backup only where LAmB is unavailable.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q2`,stem:`A woman treated for kala-azar 8 months ago presents with fever and an enlarging spleen. Her rK39 test is positive. What is the most appropriate next step to confirm relapse?`,options:[`Repeat the rK39 test after 2 weeks`,`Splenic or bone marrow aspirate for LD bodies (or PCR)`,`Leishmanin skin test`,`Serum globulin and albumin-globulin ratio`],answer:1,explanation:`rK39 detects antibody that persists for years after cure, so a positive result cannot distinguish relapse from past infection; relapse needs parasitological confirmation by splenic or marrow aspirate, or PCR. A repeat rK39 adds nothing, the leishmanin test is negative in active disease and positive after cure, and globulin levels are non-specific.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q3`,stem:`Which feature best explains why post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis is a priority for the Indian elimination programme?`,options:[`It is a fatal condition if untreated`,`It is transmitted directly from person to person by contact`,`Its skin lesions harbour parasites that infect sandflies in an anthroponotic cycle`,`It always develops within 6 months of VL treatment`],answer:2,explanation:`Indian VL is anthroponotic: humans are the only reservoir, and PKDL nodules contain parasites that sandflies pick up, sustaining transmission after VL cases fall. PKDL itself is not fatal, is not spread by contact, and in India appears months to years (median 2-3 years) after VL - the within-6-months pattern is typical of Sudan.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old man with HIV (CD4 90 cells/microlitre) from Saran district is diagnosed with visceral leishmaniasis. Which regimen does WHO (2022) recommend in South Asia?`,options:[`Liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg single dose alone`,`Paromomycin IM for 21 days`,`Liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg on alternate days to a total of 30 mg/kg plus miltefosine 100 mg/day for 14 days`,`Sodium stibogluconate plus paromomycin for 17 days`],answer:2,explanation:`WHO 2022 recommends LAmB 30 mg/kg total (5 mg/kg on days 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11) combined with miltefosine for 14 days in South Asian HIV-VL, followed by ART and secondary prophylaxis while CD4 is under 200. Single-dose LAmB alone has high failure and relapse in HIV, paromomycin monotherapy is not used here, and SSG plus paromomycin was the old East African regimen.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-q5`,stem:`Indoor residual spraying against Phlebotomus argentipes is done only up to a height of about 6 feet on walls. What is the reason?`,options:[`The sandfly is a weak, hopping flier that rests low on walls`,`Insecticide above 6 feet is toxic to the household`,`The sandfly breeds only on roofs`,`Pyrethroids lose activity at higher levels`],answer:0,explanation:`Phlebotomus argentipes is a small, weak hopping flier that rests on the lower parts of walls and cattle sheds, so spraying up to 6 feet covers its resting sites efficiently. Toxicity is not the reason, the sandfly breeds in moist soil and wall cracks rather than on roofs, and pyrethroid activity does not depend on height.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the clinical features, diagnosis and management of visceral leishmaniasis. Add a note on the kala-azar elimination programme in India.`,openingLines:[`Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) is a chronic, potentially fatal infection of the reticulo-endothelial system by Leishmania donovani, transmitted in India by the sandfly Phlebotomus argentipes.`,`It presents as prolonged fever with massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia in residents of Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and eastern UP, and is treated with single-dose liposomal amphotericin B.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and agent`,points:[`L. donovani; anthroponotic in India; vector P. argentipes (weak, low flier).`,`54 districts, 633 blocks in 4 states; cases under 1,000 a year since 2022.`,`Incubation 2-6 months; most infections asymptomatic.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Amastigotes in macrophages; Th1 failure with high IL-10.`,`Hypersplenism and marrow infiltration: pancytopenia; polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fever over 2 weeks, double-quotidian; patient ambulant.`,`Massive soft splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, weight loss, dark skin, bleeding, pallor.`,`Lymphadenopathy uncommon in India.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Case definition: fever over 2 weeks + splenomegaly in an endemic area.`,`rK39 RDT (95-99% sensitive); splenic aspirate 93-99%; marrow 53-86%; PCR in HIV and PKDL.`,`CBC pancytopenia, reversed A:G ratio; screen HIV and TB.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`LAmB 10 mg/kg single dose IV (first line).`,`Combinations: LAmB + miltefosine 7 days; LAmB + paromomycin 10 days; miltefosine + paromomycin 10 days.`,`HIV-VL: LAmB 30 mg/kg total + miltefosine 14 days, ART, secondary prophylaxis.`,`Supportive: treat infections, nutrition, transfusion.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and PKDL`,points:[`Definitive cure at 6 months; watch for PKDL for 3 years.`,`PKDL treated with miltefosine 12 weeks or LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly x 3 weeks.`]},{heading:`Elimination programme`,points:[`Target under 1 case per 10,000 per year at block level - achieved 2023.`,`Early diagnosis and complete treatment, IRS with alpha-cypermethrin twice a year, active case detection, KAMIS surveillance, ASHA incentives, wage compensation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle of L. donovani (sandfly promastigote and human amastigote)`,`Diagnostic and treatment flow chart from suspected case to definitive cure`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment including HIV-VL and PKDL`,marks:3},{part:`Elimination programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`rK39`,`LAmB 10 mg/kg`,`PKDL`,`IRS`,`under 1 per 10,000`,`KAMIS`]},{id:`infectious-fever-visceral-leishmaniasis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL).`,openingLines:[`PKDL is a dermal form of leishmaniasis in which parasites persist in the skin, appearing months to years after apparent cure of visceral leishmaniasis.`,`In anthroponotic Indian VL it is the hidden reservoir that sustains transmission, so finding and treating PKDL is central to elimination.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and pathogenesis`,points:[`Follows 5-15% of treated VL in India (median 2-3 years); up to 50% in Sudan within 6 months.`,`Post-treatment immune response against persistent skin parasites.`]},{heading:`Clinical types`,points:[`Macular (commonest in India), papular, nodular, polymorphic.`,`Starts around mouth and chin; normal sensation; nerves not thickened.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Clinical + endemic area + rK39 positive; slit-skin smear or skin PCR to confirm.`,`Differentiate leprosy and vitiligo.`]},{heading:`Treatment and programme`,points:[`Miltefosine 12 weeks (allometric) or LAmB 5 mg/kg twice weekly x 3 weeks (WHO 2022).`,`Eye symptoms on miltefosine - stop and refer.`,`House-to-house PKDL search in villages with VL in last 3 years; wage compensation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: PKDL vs leprosy vs vitiligo`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and importance`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical types and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PKDL`,`macular`,`nodular`,`miltefosine 12 weeks`,`reservoir`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Leishmaniasis chapter`,`WHO. Guideline for the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis in HIV co-infected patients in East Africa and South-East Asia, 2022`,`WHO. Guideline for the treatment of PKDL in East Africa and South-East Asia, 2022`,`WHO. Control of the leishmaniases, Technical Report Series 949, 2010`,`WHO. Guideline for the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis in East Africa (paromomycin + miltefosine), 2024`,`National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC), MoHFW. Kala-azar elimination programme operational guidelines, 2024 update`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`,`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`]}),i.push({id:`infectious-fever-filariasis`,title:`Lymphatic filariasis and the elimination programme`,oneLiner:`**Lymphatic filariasis** is infection of the lymphatics by the nematodes **Wuchereria bancrofti** (over 99% of Indian cases, spread by **Culex quinquefasciatus**) and **Brugia malayi** (Mansonia), causing silent lymphatic damage, **acute dermatolymphangioadenitis**, **lymphoedema/elephantiasis and hydrocele**; it is diagnosed by **night blood smear** or the **filariasis test strip (FTS) antigen test**, treated with **DEC + albendazole (+ ivermectin)**, and is targeted for elimination in India by **2027** through **mass drug administration (IDA/DA)** and **morbidity management and disability prevention (MMDP)**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`elephantiasis`,`Wuchereria bancrofti`,`Brugia malayi`,`Culex quinquefasciatus`,`Mansonia`,`microfilaria`,`nocturnal periodicity`,`night blood smear`,`filariasis test strip`,`FTS`,`DEC`,`diethylcarbamazine`,`ivermectin`,`albendazole`,`IDA`,`MDA`,`Sarva Dawa Sevan`,`MMDP`,`hydrocele`,`ADLA`,`tropical pulmonary eosinophilia`,`chyluria`,`TAS`,`Wolbachia`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Lymphatic filariasis (LF)** is a mosquito-borne infection in which adult filarial worms live in the **lymphatic vessels and nodes**, releasing **microfilariae (mf)** into blood; it is a **leading cause of permanent disability** worldwide. [WHO 2024]`,`Globally, about **657 million people in 39 countries** still needed preventive chemotherapy in 2023; **21 countries** have been validated by WHO as having eliminated LF as a public-health problem (including Bangladesh, Maldives, Sri Lanka, Thailand, Lao PDR). [WHO 2024]`,`**India carries about 40% of the global burden**: LF is endemic in **345 districts of 20 states and UTs**, with the heaviest load in **Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Jharkhand, Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Maharashtra, West Bengal and Tamil Nadu**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`India has set an accelerated target of **eliminating LF by 2027**, three years ahead of the global **WHO NTD Road Map 2030** goal. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Over **6 lakh lymphoedema and over 1 lakh hydrocele cases** are on the national MMDP line-list; the true number is higher because of stigma and under-reporting. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`LF is an urban and peri-urban disease of **poor sanitation**: Culex breeds in **polluted water - open drains, soakage pits, septic tanks and cesspools**. [Park 27e]`,`Infection is acquired in childhood, but **disease (lymphoedema, hydrocele) appears in adulthood** after years of cumulative lymphatic damage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, vector and risk factors`,points:[`**Wuchereria bancrofti** causes **over 99% of Indian LF**; it is transmitted by **Culex quinquefasciatus** (urban, night-biting, breeds in dirty water). [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Brugia malayi** occurs in pockets of **Kerala, Odisha, Assam, West Bengal and Madhya Pradesh**, transmitted by **Mansonia** mosquitoes that attach to roots of aquatic plants such as **Pistia** (water lettuce). [Park 27e]`,`Indian strains show **nocturnal periodicity**: mf circulate in peripheral blood between **10 pm and 2 am**, matching the biting time of Culex; by day they sequester in lung capillaries. [Harrison 22e]`,`The mosquito is only a vector, not a host: mf develop into **L3 infective larvae** in the mosquito in **10-14 days**; there is **no multiplication** in the mosquito, so heavy repeated exposure is needed for infection. [Park 27e]`,`Adult worms live **5-7 years** (female 80-100 mm, male 40 mm) and release thousands of sheathed mf daily; the **prepatent period** is about **8-12 months**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors: **residence in endemic areas, open drains, outdoor sleeping, no bed nets, poverty**; secondary bacterial infection through skin cracks drives progression of lymphoedema. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Adult worms in the lymphatics cause **lymphangiectasia** (dilated, tortuous lymphatics) and **lymphatic dysfunction** even in apparently healthy, microfilaraemic people - seen as the **'filarial dance sign'** on ultrasound. [Harrison 22e]`,`Death of adult worms (natural or drug-induced) triggers **granulomatous inflammation**, producing **acute filarial lymphangitis (AFL)** that spreads **centrifugally (down the limb)** from a tender node. [Harrison 22e]`,`Damaged lymphatics allow **recurrent bacterial (streptococcal) entry through interdigital fungal cracks**, causing **acute dermatolymphangioadenitis (ADLA)**, which spreads like cellulitis and **is the main driver of progression to elephantiasis**. [WHO 2021]`,`Each ADLA episode leaves further **lymphatic fibrosis, dermal thickening and protein-rich oedema**, explaining why hygiene and infection prevention halt and even reverse early lymphoedema. [WHO 2021]`,`Most filariae carry the endosymbiont bacterium **Wolbachia**, needed for worm fertility and survival; it releases inflammatory molecules when worms die, and **doxycycline kills Wolbachia**, sterilising and slowly killing adult worms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (TPE)** is an **immune hyper-response** to mf trapped in lung capillaries - very high IgE and eosinophils, **no mf in blood (occult filariasis)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chyluria** occurs when obstructed retroperitoneal lymphatics rupture into the renal pelvis, leaking intestinal lymph (chyle) into urine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DEC** acts mainly on mf (and partly adults) by sensitising them to host immune attack; **ivermectin** opens glutamate-gated chloride channels, killing mf rapidly; **albendazole** blocks worm beta-tubulin and enhances the effect - together the triple drug clears mf for at least 2-3 years. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Clinical spectrum**: (1) **asymptomatic (subclinical) microfilaraemia**; (2) **acute** - ADLA, AFL, filarial fever; (3) **chronic** - lymphoedema/elephantiasis, hydrocele, chyluria; (4) **occult filariasis** - TPE, filarial arthritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dreyer (7-stage) lymphoedema staging** used in Indian MMDP: stage 1 swelling reversible overnight; 2 not reversible; 3 **shallow skin folds**; 4 **knobs (bumps)**; 5 **deep skin folds**; 6 **mossy lesions**; 7 unable to perform daily activities (dependent). [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO 3-grade system** (older): grade I pitting oedema reversible on elevation; grade II non-pitting, not reversible; grade III elephantiasis with skin thickening and folds. [WHO 2021]`,`**Hydrocele** is the commonest chronic manifestation in men with bancroftian filariasis; brugian filariasis **spares the genitalia** and lymphoedema stays **below the knee or elbow**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Programme units**: mf rate (percentage of night smears positive), antigen prevalence in children 6-7 years (TAS), and evaluation units of 1-2 million population. [WHO 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most infected people are **asymptomatic** for years despite microfilaraemia and lymphatic damage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ADLA**: sudden **fever with chills, a painful red swollen hot leg**, often with an interdigital crack or wound, lasting **4-7 days**, recurring several times a year, especially in the monsoon. [WHO 2021]`,`**AFL**: tender node with a red cord spreading **down the limb**, mild fever, little systemic toxicity; may follow DEC during MDA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Progressive limb swelling** (leg, arm, breast, vulva) - first pitting and reversible, then hard and permanent with heaviness, skin changes and disability. [WHO 2021]`,`**Scrotal swelling** - painless, slowly enlarging hydrocele; or **funiculitis / epididymo-orchitis** with acute pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Milky urine (chyluria)**, often worse after a fatty meal, with weight loss, clots and urinary retention. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TPE**: young adult from an endemic area with **nocturnal paroxysmal cough and wheeze**, low-grade fever and weight loss, misdiagnosed as asthma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Psychosocial history: **stigma, marriage difficulties, loss of work and depression** - part of the disability, ask and address. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Inspect the limb: **pitting vs non-pitting oedema**, skin folds, knobs, mossy lesions, **interdigital fungal infection ('entry lesions')**, wounds; stage by Dreyer. [WHO 2021]`,`**Stemmer sign** - inability to pinch the skin at the base of the second toe - indicates lymphoedema rather than venous or cardiac oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`In ADLA: warm, red, tender limb with **lymphangitis and tender inguinal nodes**, fever; distinguish from DVT. [WHO 2021]`,`Scrotum: **transilluminant, cystic swelling**, can get above it (unlike hernia), testis not felt separately in large hydrocele; thickened spermatic cord in funiculitis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Lungs in TPE: **bilateral wheeze and crackles**; chest X-ray shows diffuse reticulonodular mottling. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **lymph scrotum, breast and vulval lymphoedema** in women - often hidden by modesty. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Unilateral leg swelling**: DVT (acute, Doppler), **cellulitis** (no prior lymphoedema), **post-surgical or post-radiation lymphoedema** (cancer history), malignant lymphatic obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral leg oedema**: heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, **hypothyroid myxoedema**, chronic venous insufficiency, **lipoedema** (spares feet, symmetrical fat). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary lymphoedema** (Milroy disease congenital, **lymphoedema praecox** in adolescent girls) - no endemic exposure, family history. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrotal swelling**: inguinal hernia (cough impulse, cannot get above), epididymal cyst, varicocele, testicular tumour, TB epididymitis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**TPE versus asthma, ABPA, Loeffler syndrome (Ascaris), Strongyloides hyperinfection, EGPA and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia**; TPE responds dramatically to DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chyluria versus phosphaturia and pyuria** - chyle clears with ether and stains with Sudan III; urinary triglyceride is raised. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Night blood smear**: 60 microlitres of finger-prick blood collected **between 8:30 pm and 12:30 am**, thick smear stained with JSB or Giemsa - shows **sheathed mf**; W. bancrofti has **no nuclei in the tail tip**, B. malayi has **two terminal nuclei**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Concentration methods** (membrane filtration of 1 mL venous blood, Knott concentration) increase sensitivity; the **DEC provocation test** (100 mg DEC by day, smear after 30-60 min) is obsolete. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Filariasis Test Strip (FTS)** detects **circulating filarial antigen (CFA)** of adult W. bancrofti in **75-100 microlitres of finger-prick blood at any time of day**, read at **10 minutes**; it is the programme tool for **mapping and TAS** but stays positive for months to years after treatment. [WHO 2017]`,`**Brugia Rapid** is an IgG4 antibody RDT (BmR1 antigen) for brugian areas; **Og4C3 ELISA** measures CFA in the laboratory. [WHO 2017]`,`**Ultrasound of scrotum or breast** shows the **filarial dance sign** - live adult worms moving in dilated lymphatics; used to assess macrofilaricidal effect. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymphoscintigraphy** shows abnormal lymph flow and dermal backflow; reserved for tertiary evaluation or surgery planning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TPE**: eosinophils **over 3,000/microlitre**, serum **IgE over 1,000 IU/mL**, high antifilarial IgG, ESR raised, restrictive pattern with low DLCO on PFT, and **no mf in blood**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chyluria**: milky urine, ether-clearing, **urine triglyceride raised**; cystoscopy or lymphangiography localises the fistula. [Harrison 22e]`,`Note: in chronic lymphoedema and hydrocele **most patients are mf-negative** - absence of mf does not exclude filarial disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Individual antifilarial treatment** (mf or antigen positive): **DEC 6 mg/kg single dose plus albendazole 400 mg**, repeated yearly; the classical **DEC 6 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses for 12 days** is still used in clinic practice. [WHO 2021]`,`**Doxycycline 200 mg daily for 4-6 weeks** targets Wolbachia; it is macrofilaricidal and may improve **early lymphoedema (stages 1-3)**; avoid in pregnancy and children under 8. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymphoedema care - the WHO minimum package**: **wash the limb twice daily with soap and clean water, dry carefully between the toes**, apply antifungal cream to entry lesions, treat wounds, **elevate the limb** at night, exercise the limb, and wear proper footwear. [WHO 2021]`,`This hygiene package reduces **ADLA episodes by 50-75%** and halts progression; it is taught with an **MMDP kit** (soap, towel, tub, antifungal, antiseptic). [WHO 2021]`,`**ADLA treatment**: rest, **elevation, cold compresses, paracetamol**, and an antibiotic - oral **amoxicillin or penicillin V** (erythromycin or doxycycline if allergic) for about **7-10 days**; **do not give antifilarial drugs during the acute attack**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Recurrent ADLA** (several attacks a year): long-term antibiotic prophylaxis, for example **benzathine penicillin 1.2 million units IM every 3 weeks** or daily oral penicillin, together with foot hygiene. [WHO 2021]`,`Compression bandaging and stockings help **early (reversible) lymphoedema**; avoid in active infection. [WHO 2021]`,`**TPE**: **DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 21 days**; relapse in about 20%, which is re-treated; inhaled steroids may help residual airway symptoms. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and surgery`,points:[`**Admit severe ADLA**: high fever, rigors, spreading cellulitis, abscess, sepsis, diabetes or inability to walk - give **IV benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone** (add cloxacillin or clindamycin if abscess), elevation and drainage. [WHO 2021]`,`**Hydrocelectomy** is the definitive treatment: **excision of the sac** is preferred over eversion (Jaboulay) for filarial hydrocele with thick sac; done free at CHC/district hospital, often in camp mode. [WHO 2021]`,`**Antifilarial treatment before surgery** reduces recurrence and is given according to programme norms; hydrocele recurrence is commoner with eversion. [WHO 2021]`,`**Advanced lymphoedema (stage 5-7)**: hygiene still the core; surgical options - **debulking (Charles procedure), lymphovenous anastomosis, lymph node transfer** - are at specialised centres and have limited evidence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chyluria**: low-fat diet with **medium-chain triglycerides**, high fluid intake, DEC; **renal pelvic instillation sclerotherapy** (povidone-iodine or silver nitrate) or surgical lymphatic disconnection for refractory cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe MDA adverse events** (high fever, hypotension, seizures) are managed by the rapid response team; most systemic reactions are due to **dying mf** and settle in 24-48 hours. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **excluded from MDA** (DEC, ivermectin and albendazole are all withheld); treat after delivery; lactating mothers in the **first week after delivery** are excluded from ivermectin. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Children**: **no MDA under 2 years**; ivermectin not given **under 5 years or under 90 cm height (or 15 kg)**; DEC and albendazole doses are age-based. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Seriously ill and elderly bedridden** people are excluded from MDA; diabetes and hypertension are not exclusions. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Neurocysticercosis**: albendazole during MDA can unmask seizures in people with brain cysts - those with known epilepsy should be assessed (see the Tissue helminths topic). [WHO 2017]`,`**Loiasis and onchocerciasis areas** (Africa) cannot use DEC or IDA because of severe reactions - not an issue in India, a common viva point. [WHO 2017]`,`**Diabetes and lymphoedema**: higher risk of ADLA and foot sepsis; strict foot care and glucose control. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Recurrent ADLA** leads to progressive elephantiasis, **chronic wounds, lymphorrhoea, sepsis and cellulitis**. [WHO 2021]`,`Huge hydroceles cause **sexual dysfunction, difficulty walking and micturition**; complications include haematocele, infection and **chylocele**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Untreated **TPE** progresses to **chronic interstitial lung fibrosis** with restrictive lung disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chyluria** causes protein and fat loss - **hypoalbuminaemia, weight loss and lymphopenia**; clot retention. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mental health**: depression and anxiety are present in up to half of patients with lymphoedema or hydrocele; WHO includes mental-health care in MMDP. [WHO 2021]`,`Early lymphoedema (stages 1-3) can **regress** with hygiene and doxycycline; established elephantiasis is permanent but can be stabilised. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer **hydrocele for surgery**, **chyluria, TPE** and **advanced lymphoedema with wounds** to the district hospital or tertiary centre. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Enter every lymphoedema and hydrocele patient on the **MMDP line-list**, train the patient and family in hygiene, and review **every 3-6 months** for stage, ADLA count and wounds. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Count **ADLA episodes per year** as the key outcome of lymphoedema care; three or more a year calls for antibiotic prophylaxis. [WHO 2021]`,`After individual DEC treatment, repeat **night smear or FTS at 12 months**; antigen may remain positive for years and is not a test of cure. [WHO 2017]`,`**TPE**: repeat eosinophil count and spirometry at the end of DEC and at 3-6 months for relapse. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Prevention and the national elimination programme`,points:[`LF elimination runs under **NCVBDC** with two pillars: **interrupt transmission by MDA** and **alleviate suffering by MMDP**; vector control is supportive. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**MDA regimens**: **DA** - DEC 6 mg/kg + albendazole 400 mg once a year; **IDA** - **ivermectin 200 micrograms/kg + DEC 6 mg/kg + albendazole 400 mg** once a year, introduced in India in **2018** in selected districts and since expanded. [WHO 2017]`,`**India MDA doses**: DEC by age - **2-5 years 100 mg, 6-14 years 200 mg, 15 years and over 300 mg**; albendazole **400 mg chewed** for all over 2 years; ivermectin by **height** (90-119 cm 3 mg, 120-140 cm 6 mg, 141-158 cm 9 mg, over 158 cm 12 mg). [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Drugs are swallowed **in front of the drug administrator (directly observed)**, **never on an empty stomach**; at least **65% of the total population** must be covered in each round for effective MDA. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`India now runs **nationwide synchronised MDA rounds twice a year (around 10 February and 10 August)** in endemic districts, branded the **'Sarva Dawa Sevan'** campaign. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Stopping MDA**: after **5 effective DA rounds (or 2-3 IDA rounds)**, sentinel and spot-check sites must show **mf rate under 1% (or antigen under 2%)** - the pre-TAS - then the **Transmission Assessment Survey (TAS-1)** in children **6-7 years** using FTS; passing districts stop MDA and do **TAS-2 and TAS-3** at 2-3-year intervals. [WHO 2017]`,`**Vector control**: antilarval measures in drains (temephos, **expanded polystyrene beads in soakage pits and cesspools**), removal of Pistia for Mansonia, larvivorous fish, bed nets and repellents. [Park 27e]`,`**MMDP target**: every endemic district must provide the **minimum package of care** - lymphoedema hygiene, ADLA treatment, hydrocele surgery, antifilarial treatment - with services at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** level. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2017 guideline on alternative MDA**: **IDA** recommended in countries without onchocerciasis or loiasis where DA has not achieved elimination - the basis of India's IDA expansion. [WHO 2017]`,`**India's accelerated plan (2023-2024)** targets LF elimination by **2027**, with **biannual nationwide MDA campaigns (February and August)**, IDA expansion to more districts and strengthened MMDP line-listing. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**WHO 2021 MMDP guidance** (Lymphatic filariasis: managing morbidity and preventing disability, 2nd ed) adds **mental-health and psychosocial care** and integration with skin-NTD programmes. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO IDA monitoring guidance**: IDA districts move to impact assessment after **at least 2 effective rounds** with antigen and mf surveys, and **post-validation surveillance** is required after elimination. [WHO 2017]`,`**Countries validated recently**: Bangladesh and Lao PDR (2023) and Brazil (2024) - showing elimination is achievable in South-East Asia. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **'Filariasis - clinical features and management'**, **'Elimination of LF'**, **'MDA'**, **'TPE'**, and **'Lymphoedema management'**.`,`Viva: **why collect blood at night?** - nocturnal periodicity, mf peak 10 pm to 2 am; FTS works any time because it detects adult worm antigen.`,`Viva: **who is excluded from MDA?** - children under 2, pregnant women, seriously ill; plus ivermectin under 5 years or 90 cm and lactating mothers in the first week.`,`Common mistake: giving **DEC during an ADLA attack** - treat the bacterial infection first.`,`Common mistake: assuming a **negative night smear excludes filariasis** in elephantiasis - most chronic patients are amicrofilaraemic.`,`Viva: **why does doxycycline work?** - it kills the endosymbiont Wolbachia, which the worm needs for fertility and survival.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for lymphatic filariasis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`DEC (diethylcarbamazine)`,`6 mg/kg oral; MDA by age: 2-5 y 100 mg, 6-14 y 200 mg, 15 y+ 300 mg`,`Single dose yearly (MDA); 6 mg/kg/day x 12 days (individual); x 21 days (TPE)`,`Fever, headache, myalgia, AFL from dying worms; avoid in pregnancy, under 2 y, onchocerciasis/loiasis`],[`Albendazole`,`400 mg chewed`,`Single dose yearly with DEC`,`Mild GI upset; seizures if neurocysticercosis; avoid in first trimester`],[`Ivermectin (IDA)`,`200 micrograms/kg by height: 3, 6, 9, 12 mg`,`Single dose yearly`,`Dizziness, pruritus, fever; not under 5 y or 90 cm, pregnancy, lactation first week`],[`Doxycycline`,`200 mg oral daily`,`4-6 weeks`,`Photosensitivity, GI upset; not in pregnancy or under 8 y`],[`Amoxicillin (ADLA)`,`500 mg oral 8-hourly`,`7-10 days`,`Allergy; use erythromycin or doxycycline if penicillin-allergic`],[`Benzathine penicillin (recurrent ADLA)`,`1.2 million units deep IM`,`Every 3 weeks, long term`,`Anaphylaxis (rare), injection pain`],[`IV benzylpenicillin / ceftriaxone (severe ADLA)`,`Benzylpenicillin 2-4 MU IV 4-6-hourly or ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily`,`Until afebrile, then oral to complete 10-14 days`,`Allergy`]]},{heading:`Clinical spectrum and staging of LF`,columns:[`Category`,`Manifestation`,`Key feature`],rows:[[`Subclinical`,`Asymptomatic microfilaraemia`,`Lymphangiectasia on ultrasound; reservoir for transmission`],[`Acute`,`ADLA`,`Bacterial; red hot limb, entry lesion; drives progression`],[`Acute`,`Acute filarial lymphangitis`,`Adult worm death; centrifugal (descending) red cord`],[`Chronic`,`Lymphoedema (Dreyer 1-7)`,`1 reversible, 2 irreversible, 3 shallow folds, 4 knobs, 5 deep folds, 6 mossy, 7 dependent`],[`Chronic`,`Hydrocele, chylocele, lymph scrotum`,`Bancroftian only; commonest chronic sign in men`],[`Chronic`,`Chyluria`,`Milky urine; ether-clearing; raised urine triglyceride`],[`Occult`,`Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia`,`Nocturnal cough, eosinophils over 3,000, IgE over 1,000, no mf`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests in LF`,columns:[`Test`,`What it detects`,`When to use`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Night blood smear (60 microlitres, 8:30 pm-12:30 am)`,`Sheathed microfilariae`,`Mf survey, sentinel sites, individual diagnosis`,`Night collection; misses amicrofilaraemic disease`],[`Membrane filtration (1 mL)`,`Mf, concentrated`,`Low-density infection`,`Venous blood, lab needed`],[`Filariasis Test Strip (FTS)`,`Circulating filarial antigen (adult W. bancrofti)`,`Mapping, TAS in children 6-7 y; any time of day`,`Positive long after cure; not for Brugia`],[`Brugia Rapid`,`IgG4 antibody to BmR1`,`Brugian areas`,`Antibody persists`],[`Ultrasound`,`Filarial dance sign`,`Scrotal/breast worm nests; macrofilaricide trials`,`Operator dependent`],[`Lymphoscintigraphy`,`Lymph flow, dermal backflow`,`Tertiary assessment of lymphoedema`,`Cost, availability`]]},{heading:`Leg swelling in an endemic area - differential`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Test`],rows:[[`Filarial lymphoedema`,`Endemic area, recurrent ADLA, Stemmer sign positive, skin folds`,`FTS (often negative late), clinical`],[`Cellulitis without lymphoedema`,`Acute, single episode, normal limb before`,`Clinical, CBC`],[`Deep vein thrombosis`,`Acute, calf tenderness, risk factors`,`Compression Doppler, D-dimer`],[`Chronic venous insufficiency`,`Varicose veins, ankle pigmentation, ulcer at medial malleolus`,`Venous Doppler`],[`Cardiac / renal / hepatic oedema`,`Bilateral pitting, systemic signs`,`Echo, urine protein, LFT`],[`Lipoedema`,`Bilateral, symmetrical, spares feet, women`,`Clinical`],[`Post-surgical / malignant lymphoedema`,`Cancer surgery or radiotherapy, pelvic mass`,`Imaging`]]},{heading:`MDA regimens and eligibility`,columns:[`Item`,`DA`,`IDA`],rows:[[`Drugs`,`DEC + albendazole`,`Ivermectin + DEC + albendazole`],[`Rounds needed before TAS`,`At least 5 effective annual rounds`,`At least 2 effective annual rounds`],[`Minimum coverage`,`65% of total population`,`65% of total population`],[`Excluded`,`Under 2 y, pregnant, seriously ill`,`Also ivermectin under 5 y / under 90 cm, lactating first week`],[`Stopping criterion`,`Mf under 1% or antigen under 2%, then TAS-1 pass`,`Same, with IDA impact survey`]]}],redFlags:[`ADLA with high fever, rigors, spreading cellulitis, abscess or sepsis`,`ADLA in a patient with diabetes or chronic wounds`,`Rapidly increasing unilateral leg swelling with calf pain - exclude DVT`,`Hydrocele with pain, sudden enlargement or a hard testis - exclude torsion or tumour`,`Chyluria with clot retention, haematuria or weight loss`,`Seizure, hypotension or high fever within 48 hours of MDA drugs`,`Breathlessness with eosinophils over 3,000/microlitre - TPE needing DEC`],pearls:[`**W. bancrofti - Culex - dirty water; B. malayi - Mansonia - Pistia.**`,`**Night smear 8:30 pm to 12:30 am; FTS any time.**`,`**IDA = Ivermectin + DEC + Albendazole** - one dose a year.`,`**ADLA is bacterial** - antibiotic, not DEC, and hygiene prevents it.`,`**Wash, dry, elevate, exercise, footwear** - the five steps of lymphoedema care.`,`**TPE**: occult filariasis - eosinophils over 3,000, IgE over 1,000, no mf, DEC for 21 days.`,`**Brugia spares the scrotum** and keeps swelling below knee and elbow.`,`**Elimination by 2027** in India; TAS done in children aged 6-7.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-q1`,stem:`In a district doing a Transmission Assessment Survey for lymphatic filariasis, which test and age group are used?`,options:[`Night blood smear in adults over 20 years`,`Filariasis test strip antigen test in children aged 6-7 years`,`Brugia Rapid antibody test in pregnant women`,`Ultrasound filarial dance sign in men with hydrocele`],answer:1,explanation:`TAS uses a circulating filarial antigen test (FTS) in children aged 6-7 years, because antigen in children born after MDA began reflects recent transmission. Night smears in adults measure the old reservoir, Brugia Rapid is for brugian areas, and ultrasound is a research tool for adult worms rather than a survey test.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old man with stage 3 lymphoedema of the left leg has fever with chills and a hot, red, painful leg for 2 days. There is maceration between the toes. What is the best immediate management?`,options:[`DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 12 days`,`Ivermectin single dose and compression bandage`,`Rest, elevation, paracetamol and oral amoxicillin, with hygiene of the entry lesion`,`Immediate surgical debulking`],answer:2,explanation:`This is acute dermatolymphangioadenitis, a secondary bacterial infection through interdigital entry lesions, treated with rest, elevation, paracetamol, an antibiotic such as amoxicillin and antifungal care. Antifilarial drugs do not treat it and are avoided during the attack, compression is wrong while the limb is infected, and surgery has no role acutely.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-q3`,stem:`A 25-year-old man from coastal Odisha has nocturnal cough and wheeze for 3 months. Eosinophil count is 9,000/microlitre, IgE 4,500 IU/mL and night blood smear shows no microfilariae. What is the treatment of choice?`,options:[`Inhaled budesonide-formoterol alone`,`DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 21 days`,`Albendazole 400 mg single dose`,`Oral prednisolone for 6 weeks`],answer:1,explanation:`Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia is occult filariasis - an immune hyper-response to microfilariae trapped in the lungs, so blood smears are negative - and it responds dramatically to DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 21 days. Inhalers treat asthma but leave the cause, single-dose albendazole is not adequate, and steroids alone suppress without curing.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-q4`,stem:`Which person should NOT receive ivermectin during an IDA mass drug administration round in India?`,options:[`A 60-year-old man with controlled hypertension`,`A 4-year-old child 98 cm tall`,`A 30-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes`,`A 12-year-old child 135 cm tall`],answer:1,explanation:`Ivermectin is not given to children under 5 years or under 90 cm, so the 4-year-old is excluded from ivermectin (though DEC and albendazole can be given from age 2). Controlled hypertension and diabetes are not exclusions, and a 12-year-old 135 cm tall receives the 6 mg height-band dose.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-q5`,stem:`Doxycycline given for 4-6 weeks has a macrofilaricidal effect in lymphatic filariasis. What is its mechanism?`,options:[`Opening glutamate-gated chloride channels in worm muscle`,`Killing the endosymbiotic bacterium Wolbachia needed for worm fertility and survival`,`Inhibiting worm beta-tubulin polymerisation`,`Sensitising microfilariae to host phagocytes`],answer:1,explanation:`Doxycycline kills Wolbachia, the bacterial endosymbiont filariae depend on, which sterilises and slowly kills adult worms. Opening glutamate-gated chloride channels is how ivermectin works, beta-tubulin inhibition is the albendazole mechanism, and sensitising microfilariae to immune attack describes DEC.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the clinical manifestations and diagnosis of lymphatic filariasis. Outline the strategy for its elimination in India.`,openingLines:[`Lymphatic filariasis is a mosquito-borne infection of the lymphatics by Wuchereria bancrofti and Brugia malayi, causing lymphoedema, hydrocele and recurrent acute attacks.`,`India carries about 40% of the global burden in 345 districts and aims to eliminate it by 2027 through mass drug administration and morbidity management.`],answer:[{heading:`Agent, vector and burden`,points:[`W. bancrofti (over 99%) - Culex quinquefasciatus; B. malayi - Mansonia (Pistia).`,`Nocturnal periodicity 10 pm-2 am; adult worms live 5-7 years.`,`345 districts, 20 states/UTs.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Lymphangiectasia from adult worms; ADLA from bacteria through entry lesions; Wolbachia inflammation.`]},{heading:`Clinical manifestations`,points:[`Asymptomatic microfilaraemia.`,`Acute: ADLA, AFL, filarial fever.`,`Chronic: lymphoedema (Dreyer stages 1-7), hydrocele, chyluria.`,`Occult: TPE.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Night smear (sheathed mf), membrane filtration.`,`FTS antigen any time; Brugia Rapid.`,`Ultrasound filarial dance sign; eosinophils and IgE in TPE.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`DEC + albendazole; doxycycline 4-6 weeks; TPE DEC 21 days.`,`Lymphoedema hygiene; ADLA antibiotics; hydrocelectomy.`]},{heading:`Elimination strategy`,points:[`MDA: DA or IDA yearly, 65% coverage, directly observed; biannual nationwide rounds (Feb, Aug).`,`Exclusions: under 2, pregnancy, seriously ill; ivermectin under 5 y/90 cm.`,`Pre-TAS (mf under 1%), TAS-1, 2, 3 in children 6-7 y.`,`MMDP minimum package in every district; vector control; target 2027.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle of W. bancrofti (mosquito and human phases)`,`Flow chart: MDA rounds to pre-TAS to TAS-1/2/3 to validation`],markSplit:[{part:`Introduction, agent and vector`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical manifestations`,marks:3},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Elimination strategy (MDA, MMDP, TAS)`,marks:4}],keywords:[`W. bancrofti`,`IDA`,`MDA`,`FTS`,`TAS`,`MMDP`,`2027`]},{id:`infectious-fever-filariasis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on morbidity management and disability prevention (MMDP) in lymphatic filariasis.`,openingLines:[`MMDP is the second pillar of the Global Programme to Eliminate LF, aimed at relieving suffering in people who already have lymphoedema or hydrocele.`,`WHO defines a minimum package of care that every endemic district must provide for elimination to be validated.`],answer:[{heading:`Minimum package`,points:[`Lymphoedema management, ADLA treatment, hydrocele surgery, antifilarial treatment, mental-health support.`]},{heading:`Lymphoedema care`,points:[`Wash twice daily with soap and water, dry between toes, treat entry lesions, elevate, exercise, footwear.`,`Reduces ADLA by 50-75%; Dreyer staging to monitor.`]},{heading:`ADLA`,points:[`Rest, elevation, paracetamol, amoxicillin; prophylaxis with benzathine penicillin if recurrent; no DEC in attack.`]},{heading:`Hydrocele and programme`,points:[`Free hydrocelectomy (excision) at CHC/district hospital.`,`Line-list patients, MMDP kits, review every 3-6 months at Ayushman Arogya Mandir.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: Dreyer stages of lymphoedema with care at each stage`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and package`,marks:1},{part:`Lymphoedema and ADLA care`,marks:2.5},{part:`Hydrocele and programme delivery`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`MMDP`,`minimum package`,`ADLA`,`hydrocelectomy`,`Dreyer`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Filarial and related infections`,`WHO. Guideline: alternative mass drug administration regimens to eliminate lymphatic filariasis, 2017`,`WHO. Lymphatic filariasis: managing morbidity and preventing disability - an aide-memoire for national programme managers, 2nd edition, 2021`,`WHO. Global programme to eliminate lymphatic filariasis: progress report 2023 (Weekly Epidemiological Record), 2024`,`National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC), MoHFW. Guidelines on elimination of lymphatic filariasis and MDA (IDA/DA) operational guidelines, 2024`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`,`Bailey and Love's Short Practice of Surgery, 28th edition, 2023`,`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`]}),i.push({id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue`,title:`Tissue helminths: neurocysticercosis, hydatid, toxocara, trichinella`,oneLiner:`**Tissue helminths** are worms whose larvae invade organs rather than the gut lumen: **neurocysticercosis** (larval **Taenia solium** in the brain, the commonest preventable cause of epilepsy in India, treated with **albendazole plus corticosteroids** per IDSA 2017/WHO 2021 after staging), **cystic echinococcosis** (hydatid cyst of liver and lung, managed by the **WHO-IWGE stage** with albendazole, **PAIR** or surgery), and the larva migrans syndromes of **Toxocara** and **Trichinella**, which present with **eosinophilia** and organ-specific inflammation.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`neurocysticercosis`,`NCC`,`Taenia solium`,`solitary cysticercus granuloma`,`SCG`,`Del Brutto criteria`,`Escobar stages`,`albendazole`,`praziquantel`,`hydatid cyst`,`Echinococcus granulosus`,`cystic echinococcosis`,`WHO-IWGE classification`,`PAIR`,`water lily sign`,`Toxocara canis`,`visceral larva migrans`,`ocular larva migrans`,`Trichinella spiralis`,`trichinellosis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Tissue helminthiases** are infections in which helminth **larval stages** live in host tissues - brain, liver, lung, muscle, eye - so humans are usually **intermediate or dead-end hosts** and stool examination is unhelpful. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurocysticercosis (NCC)** is infection of the CNS by the larval stage (**cysticercus**) of the pork tapeworm **Taenia solium**; WHO estimates it causes **about 30% of epilepsy in endemic countries** and is the commonest preventable cause of epilepsy worldwide. [WHO 2021]`,`In **India** NCC is a leading cause of new-onset seizures in children and adults; community studies (Vellore) found NCC in **about a third of people with active epilepsy**, and the **solitary cysticercus granuloma (SCG)** is the commonest Indian form. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cystic echinococcosis (CE)** is caused by the larval cyst of the dog tapeworm **Echinococcus granulosus**; it is endemic in sheep-rearing areas and in India is reported most from **Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Punjab and Kashmir**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxocariasis** (Toxocara canis from dogs, T. cati from cats) is among the commonest zoonotic helminth infections; Indian seroprevalence studies show **5-30%** exposure, highest in children with pica and puppies at home. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinellosis** (Trichinella spiralis) follows eating undercooked pork or **wild boar** meat; sporadic outbreaks are reported from India, including the North-East and the hill tribes of the South. [Harrison 22e]`,`All four are diseases of **poor sanitation and close animal contact** - free-roaming pigs, open defecation, stray dogs fed offal, home slaughter - and are targeted under the **WHO NTD Road Map 2021-2030**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and life cycles`,points:[`**T. solium life cycle**: humans are the **definitive host** (adult tapeworm in gut - **taeniasis**, from eating undercooked measly pork); pigs are the intermediate host; humans become **accidental intermediate hosts** when they swallow **eggs** - this causes **cysticercosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`NCC is acquired by **faecal-oral ingestion of T. solium eggs** from a human tapeworm carrier (often a food handler or household member) - **not by eating pork** - so **vegetarians get NCC**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**E. granulosus**: the **dog is the definitive host**; sheep, goats, cattle and buffalo are intermediate hosts; humans swallow eggs from dog faeces (stroking dogs, contaminated vegetables), and larvae reach the **liver (about 70%) and lungs (about 20%)** via the portal circulation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**E. multilocularis** (alveolar echinococcosis, fox definitive host) grows like a tumour in the liver - rare in India, reported from the Himalayan belt. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxocara**: eggs passed by puppies and kittens embryonate in soil over **2-4 weeks**; children swallow them, larvae hatch and wander through liver, lungs, eye and brain **without maturing** (larva migrans). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinella**: encysted larvae in muscle of pig or wild boar are eaten; adults mature in the small intestine within days, and newborn larvae disseminate to **striated muscle**, where they encyst in **nurse cells**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Viable cysticerci actively **evade immunity** and cause little inflammation for years; symptoms begin when the cyst **degenerates** and the host mounts an inflammatory response - oedema, ring enhancement and **seizures**. [Harrison 22e]`,`This explains why **cysticidal drugs worsen symptoms initially** - killing the cyst provokes inflammation - and why **corticosteroids are started before or with albendazole**. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Calcified cysticerci** are dead, but intermittent **perilesional oedema** around calcifications causes recurrent seizures; antiparasitics have no role there. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Subarachnoid (racemose) and ventricular cysts** obstruct CSF flow and cause **hydrocephalus and arachnoiditis**; they need surgery, shunting or prolonged therapy. [IDSA 2017]`,`The **hydatid cyst** has three layers: host-derived **pericyst** (fibrous), parasite **laminated ectocyst**, and the germinal **endocyst** that buds **brood capsules, protoscolices (hydatid sand) and daughter cysts**; fluid is highly **antigenic**, so rupture causes **anaphylaxis** and seeding. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Albendazole** binds parasite **beta-tubulin**, blocking glucose uptake; absorption rises up to **5-fold with a fatty meal**, and **dexamethasone raises** albendazole sulfoxide levels while it **lowers praziquantel** levels. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Eosinophilia** marks tissue-invasive helminths: it is marked in **trichinellosis and visceral larva migrans**, variable in hydatid (under 25%), and usually **absent in NCC and ocular larva migrans**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In trichinellosis, larval invasion of muscle causes **myositis (raised CK)**, and an immune vasculitis gives **periorbital oedema and splinter haemorrhages**; invasion of myocardium or CNS causes the deaths. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**NCC by location**: **parenchymal** (commonest, seizures) and **extraparenchymal** - ventricular, subarachnoid (racemose), spinal and ocular; extraparenchymal disease has the worse prognosis. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Escobar stages of parenchymal cysts**: **vesicular** (viable, thin wall, eccentric scolex - 'hole with dot', no enhancement), **colloidal** (degenerating, ring enhancement with oedema), **granular-nodular** (nodular enhancement), **calcified** (dense dot). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Revised Del Brutto criteria (2017)** - **absolute**: histology, subretinal cyst on fundoscopy, or **scolex within a cyst** on imaging; **major neuroimaging**: cyst without scolex, enhancing lesion, multilobulated subarachnoid cysts, typical calcifications. [Del Brutto 2017]`,`Del Brutto **confirmative neuroimaging**: resolution after cysticidal drugs, **spontaneous resolution of a single small enhancing lesion**, or migration of ventricular cysts; **minor neuroimaging**: obstructive hydrocephalus or basal leptomeningeal enhancement. [Del Brutto 2017]`,`Del Brutto **clinical/exposure**: major - positive antibody (EITB) or antigen test, cysticercosis outside the CNS, household contact with T. solium; minor - suggestive clinical features, residence in an endemic area. [Del Brutto 2017]`,`**Definitive NCC**: one absolute criterion; or two major neuroimaging plus any clinical/exposure criterion; or one major plus one confirmative neuroimaging plus any clinical/exposure criterion; or one major neuroimaging plus two clinical/exposure criteria (one major), with other causes excluded. [Del Brutto 2017]`,`**Rajshekhar criteria for SCG** (Indian): seizures without raised ICP or progressive deficit, and a **single, round, enhancing lesion under 20 mm** with **no midline shift** - distinguishes SCG from tuberculoma. [Rajshekhar 2004]`,`**WHO-IWGE ultrasound classification of CE**: **CL** undifferentiated cyst; **CE1** unilocular with double wall and hydatid sand (active); **CE2** multivesicular, honeycomb (active); **CE3a** detached endocyst - **water lily sign** (transitional); **CE3b** daughter cysts in solid matrix (transitional); **CE4** heterogeneous, no daughter cysts - **ball of wool** (inactive); **CE5** calcified wall (inactive). [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Toxocariasis syndromes**: **visceral larva migrans** (young children), **ocular larva migrans** (older children, unilateral), and **covert/common toxocariasis** (non-specific symptoms with eosinophilia). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Parenchymal NCC**: **new-onset seizures** - focal with or without secondary generalisation - in a child or young adult, often a single seizure with normal examination; headache is common. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Extraparenchymal NCC**: **headache, vomiting, visual blurring** from hydrocephalus; **cysticercal encephalitis** (massive infection, young women and children) presents with altered sensorium and raised ICP. [IDSA 2017]`,`Ask about **pig rearing, open defecation, a household member passing tapeworm segments**, food handlers and travel - and remember vegetarians are not protected. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic hydatid** is often **asymptomatic**, found on ultrasound; symptoms are **right upper quadrant dragging pain**, mass, and complications (jaundice, cholangitis, anaphylaxis after trauma). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary hydatid**: cough, chest pain, haemoptysis, and **expectoration of salty fluid or 'grape-skin' membranes (hydatoptysis)** after rupture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Visceral larva migrans**: a child aged **2-7 years with pica** and a puppy at home - fever, cough, wheeze, hepatomegaly and very high eosinophils. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ocular larva migrans**: an older child with **unilateral visual loss, squint or white pupil (leukocoria)** - must be distinguished from **retinoblastoma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinellosis**: diarrhoea and abdominal pain in week 1 after a pork or wild-boar feast, then **fever, severe myalgia, periorbital oedema** in weeks 2-6, often in a cluster of people who shared the meal. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Fundoscopy before treating NCC**: papilloedema (raised ICP - antiparasitics are withheld) and **subretinal or intravitreal cysts** (antiparasitics can cause blindness - remove surgically first). [IDSA 2017]`,`Palpable **subcutaneous or muscle nodules** (painless, mobile) suggest disseminated cysticercosis - biopsy gives an absolute diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Focal deficit, meningism or a reduced GCS in a patient with seizures suggests **large cysts, encephalitis or an alternative diagnosis (tuberculoma, abscess, tumour)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid liver**: smooth, non-tender hepatomegaly or a cystic mass; the classic **'hydatid thrill'** on percussion is rarely elicited. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Trichinellosis**: **periorbital and facial oedema, subconjunctival and splinter haemorrhages, tender muscles** (masseter, diaphragm, limbs); tachycardia or arrhythmia suggests myocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxocara**: hepatomegaly and wheeze in VLM; in OLM, a **posterior pole or peripheral retinal granuloma** with vitreous traction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Single enhancing brain lesion in India**: **SCG vs tuberculoma** - tuberculoma is usually **over 20 mm, irregular, with marked oedema, midline shift and focal deficit**; MR spectroscopy shows a **lipid peak** in tuberculoma. [Rajshekhar 2004]`,`Other ring-enhancing lesions: **pyogenic abscess** (diffusion restriction), **toxoplasmosis** in HIV (multiple, basal ganglia), metastasis and glioma (older, irregular) - see the Brain abscess topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic cyst**: simple cyst (no wall layers, no septa), **amoebic or pyogenic abscess** (fever, tender, raised CRP), cystadenoma and cystic metastases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung hydatid** mimics a lung abscess, bronchogenic carcinoma or tuberculoma; the **water lily sign and meniscus (crescent) sign** on CXR or CT point to hydatid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eosinophilia with fever**: trichinellosis, VLM, **Strongyloides**, acute schistosomiasis (travel), **fascioliasis**, TPE, drug reaction (DRESS) and eosinophilic leukaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinellosis vs dermatomyositis, polymyositis and leptospirosis** - the meal history, cluster, periorbital oedema and eosinophilia discriminate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ocular toxocariasis vs retinoblastoma** - calcification on CT and a younger age favour retinoblastoma; serum and vitreous Toxocara ELISA help. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**NCC imaging**: **MRI is preferred** for cysts, scolex, ventricular and subarachnoid lesions; **CT is better for calcifications**; contrast imaging stages the lesion. [IDSA 2017]`,`**EITB (enzyme-linked immunoelectrotransfer blot)** with lentil-lectin glycoproteins: specificity close to **100%**, sensitivity over 90% with 2 or more viable cysts but **under 50% with a single lesion or calcifications only**. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Antigen ELISA (HP10/B158)** detects viable parasites and helps monitor subarachnoid disease; **serum ELISA** cross-reacts and is less reliable; **CSF** analysis in extraparenchymal NCC shows lymphocytic pleocytosis and eosinophils. [IDSA 2017]`,`Before steroids and antiparasitics, screen for **latent TB (IGRA or TST)** and **Strongyloides** (serology or empirical ivermectin), and **stool for Taenia eggs** in the patient and household. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Hydatid**: **ultrasound is the first-line test** and stages the cyst (WHO-IWGE); **CT** for complicated or lung cysts, and **MRCP** for biliary communication. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Hydatid serology** (ELISA, IHA, western blot): sensitivity **80-94% for liver**, only **50-60% for lung** cysts and low in calcified cysts - a negative test does not exclude CE; the **Casoni skin test is obsolete**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diagnostic aspiration of a suspected hydatid** is avoided outside a PAIR protocol (anaphylaxis, seeding); if fluid is obtained, protoscolices and hooklets confirm it. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Toxocara**: **Toxocara excretory-secretory (TES) antigen ELISA** with western blot confirmation; eosinophils often over 3,000 and high IgE in VLM; vitreous antibody in OLM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinellosis**: eosinophilia (often over 50% at peak), **raised CK and LDH**, serology turns positive after **3-5 weeks**, **muscle biopsy** (deltoid) shows encysted larvae; ECG and troponin for myocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - neurocysticercosis`,points:[`**Treat seizures first**: an antiseizure medicine (levetiracetam, carbamazepine or phenytoin; valproate is acceptable) is given to every patient with seizures. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Single enhancing lesion / 1-2 viable parenchymal cysts**: **albendazole 15 mg/kg/day in 2 divided doses (max 1,200 mg/day) for 10-14 days** (1-2 weeks for a single enhancing lesion) **plus corticosteroids**. [IDSA 2017]`,`**More than 2 viable parenchymal cysts**: **albendazole 15 mg/kg/day plus praziquantel 50 mg/kg/day for 10-14 days**, with corticosteroids. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Corticosteroids**: **prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day** or **dexamethasone 0.1 mg/kg/day**, started **before or with** antiparasitic drugs and tapered after the course. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Calcified lesions only**: **no antiparasitic drug**; antiseizure medicine alone; short steroid course for episodes with perilesional oedema. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Antiseizure medicine duration**: for SCG, commonly tapered after **6-12 months seizure-free** once the lesion has resolved without calcification; residual calcification or recurrent seizures predict relapse and favour longer treatment. [WHO 2021]`,`**Treat the household tapeworm carrier** with **praziquantel 5-10 mg/kg single dose or niclosamide 2 g single dose** to stop new egg exposure. [WHO 2021]`,`Repeat **MRI at 6 months** (and every 6 months until resolution) to confirm cyst resolution; retreat persistent viable cysts. [IDSA 2017]`]},{heading:`Management - hydatid, toxocara and trichinella`,points:[`**Albendazole for CE**: **400 mg twice daily (10-15 mg/kg/day) with a fatty meal**, given **continuously** (no monthly breaks) - for **3-6 months** as primary therapy, or from **1 day-1 week before to 1-3 months after** PAIR or surgery. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Stage-specific CE management**: **CE1 and CE3a under 5 cm** - albendazole alone; **5-10 cm** - **PAIR plus albendazole**; **over 10 cm** - catheterisation or surgery; **CE2 and CE3b** - non-PAIR percutaneous techniques or surgery with albendazole; **CE4 and CE5** - **watch and wait**. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**PAIR** = **Puncture, Aspiration, Injection of a scolicide (95% ethanol or 20% hypertonic saline) for about 15 minutes, Re-aspiration**, under ultrasound guidance with anaesthesia standby. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**PAIR is contraindicated** in CE2, CE3b, CE4, CE5, **lung cysts**, cysts **communicating with the biliary tree** (risk of sclerosing cholangitis) and inaccessible cysts. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Toxocara VLM**: **albendazole 400 mg twice daily for 5 days** (mebendazole 100-200 mg twice daily for 5 days is an alternative); add prednisolone for severe pulmonary, cardiac or CNS involvement. [CDC 2024]`,`**Ocular toxocariasis**: **corticosteroids (topical, periocular or oral)** to control inflammation plus albendazole; vitrectomy for traction or retinal detachment. [CDC 2024]`,`**Trichinellosis**: **albendazole 400 mg twice daily for 8-14 days** (or mebendazole 200-400 mg three times daily for 3 days then 400-500 mg three times daily for 10 days) plus **prednisolone 30-60 mg/day** for severe myalgia or allergic features, tapered over 10-14 days. [CDC 2024]`,`Antiparasitics are most effective in the **intestinal phase of trichinellosis**, so treat **everyone who shared the meal** promptly. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Raised ICP in NCC** (hydrocephalus, cysticercal encephalitis): **no antiparasitic drugs** until ICP is controlled - give **dexamethasone and mannitol**, and **VP shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy** for hydrocephalus. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Intraventricular cysts**: **neuroendoscopic removal** is preferred; **subarachnoid (racemose) NCC** needs **prolonged albendazole (often months) with high-dose steroids**, and steroid-sparing agents such as methotrexate. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Status epilepticus** from NCC: standard benzodiazepine-led protocol; image after stabilisation. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Ruptured hydatid cyst**: treat **anaphylaxis (IM adrenaline 0.5 mg)**, then surgery and albendazole to prevent secondary echinococcosis. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Intrabiliary rupture** with cholangitis or jaundice: ERCP with sphincterotomy and clearance of membranes, then definitive surgery. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Surgery for hydatid** (conservative pericystectomy, endocystectomy or radical resection) is indicated for **large CE2-CE3b cysts, superficial cysts at risk of rupture, infected cysts, biliary communication, compression** and **lung cysts**; protect the field with scolicide-soaked packs. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Trichinella myocarditis or encephalitis**: ICU care, high-dose steroids, antiparasitics, arrhythmia monitoring. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refer** extraparenchymal NCC, raised ICP, ocular cysts, multiple cysts or diagnostic doubt to neurology/neurosurgery, and every hydatid cyst to a surgical unit for staging-based treatment - never aspirate in primary care. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Follow-up imaging**: NCC **MRI at 6 months** until resolution; hydatid **ultrasound every 3-6 months** in the first year, then yearly; **LFT and CBC** on albendazole courses over 2 weeks. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: albendazole and mebendazole are avoided in the **first trimester**; praziquantel is considered safe; NCC seizures are managed with antiseizure medicine and treatment of cysts is usually deferred until after delivery. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Children**: SCG is very common in Indian children; the same albendazole 15 mg/kg/day regimen with steroids is used; hydatid in children is more often pulmonary. [IDSA 2017]`,`**HIV and immunosuppression**: NCC behaves similarly, but **toxoplasmosis and TB** must be excluded first for any ring-enhancing lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mass deworming with albendazole** in NCC-endemic communities can unmask seizures from occult cysts; follow programme precautions. [WHO 2021]`,`**Liver disease**: albendazole is hepatotoxic - monitor **LFT and CBC every 2 weeks in the first months** of long courses (raised transaminases, neutropenia, alopecia). [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Strongyloides hyperinfection** risk with steroids - empirical **ivermectin 200 micrograms/kg** before prolonged steroids in endemic settings. [IDSA 2017]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**SCG has an excellent prognosis**: most lesions resolve within **6 months** and seizure recurrence is about **10-20%**, higher when the lesion **calcifies**. [Rajshekhar 2004]`,`**Extraparenchymal NCC** causes **hydrocephalus, arachnoiditis, vasculitic stroke and shunt failure**; mortality can reach 20% in subarachnoid disease. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Hydatid complications**: rupture (anaphylaxis, **secondary echinococcosis**), **intrabiliary rupture** (cholangitis, jaundice), secondary infection (abscess), portal hypertension and Budd-Chiari from compression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrence after hydatid surgery** is about **2-25%**, reduced by peri-operative albendazole. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Ocular toxocariasis** may cause **permanent visual loss** through granuloma, retinal detachment or endophthalmitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinellosis** is usually self-limited, but heavy infections cause **myocarditis, encephalitis and thromboembolism**, the main causes of death. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Prevention and public-health link`,points:[`**T. solium control (One Health)**: **treat human tapeworm carriers**, end open defecation (**Swachh Bharat**), confine pigs, meat inspection, and **pig vaccination (TSOL18) plus oxfendazole** where available. [WHO 2021]`,`**Hand hygiene and washing vegetables** prevent NCC, hydatid and toxocariasis - all are acquired by swallowing eggs. [Park 27e]`,`**Hydatid control**: **deworm dogs with praziquantel**, prevent dogs eating offal, safe disposal at slaughterhouses. [WHO 2021]`,`**Toxocara**: deworm puppies and kittens, keep children's play areas free of dog faeces, discourage pica. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichinella**: cook pork and wild boar to a core temperature of **71 C**; freezing does not reliably kill sylvatic species. [CDC 2024]`,`**Mass albendazole deworming** (National Deworming Day, LF MDA) targets intestinal worms but may also affect cysticerci - with the seizure caveat. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**IDSA/ASTMH 2017 (published 2018) NCC guideline**: stage- and location-based treatment - albendazole monotherapy for 1-2 cysts, **albendazole plus praziquantel for more than 2 cysts**, steroids with all antiparasitic therapy, no antiparasitics for calcifications or untreated raised ICP. [IDSA 2017]`,`**WHO 2021 guidelines on Taenia solium NCC**: endorse albendazole (plus praziquantel for multiple cysts) with corticosteroids, antiseizure medicine for all with seizures, and imaging follow-up; recommend **individualised antiseizure medicine withdrawal**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Revised Del Brutto diagnostic criteria (2017)** replaced the 2001 criteria - simpler, with neuroimaging and clinical/exposure groups. [Del Brutto 2017]`,`**WHO-IWGE classification and expert consensus** remains the basis for CE: continuous albendazole without monthly breaks and a 'watch and wait' approach for inactive CE4/CE5 cysts. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**WHO NTD Road Map 2021-2030** sets intensified control targets for T. solium taeniasis/cysticercosis and echinococcosis in hyperendemic areas. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short notes: **'Neurocysticercosis - diagnosis and management'**, **'Hydatid cyst of liver'**, **'PAIR'**, **'Larva migrans'**.`,`Viva: **how does a vegetarian get NCC?** - by swallowing T. solium eggs from a human carrier; pork gives the intestinal tapeworm, not cysticercosis.`,`Viva: **why steroids with albendazole?** - dying cysts provoke inflammation and oedema; steroids blunt it (and raise albendazole levels).`,`Common mistake: **giving albendazole with papilloedema or untreated hydrocephalus** - it can precipitate herniation; control ICP first.`,`Common mistake: **treating calcified NCC with albendazole** - the parasite is dead; give antiseizure medicine only.`,`Viva: **water lily sign** = detached floating endocyst (CE3a) on ultrasound or CT; **ball of wool** = CE4.`,`Viva: **SCG vs tuberculoma** - size under 20 mm, round, no midline shift and a scolex favour SCG.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for tissue helminths`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Albendazole (NCC)`,`15 mg/kg/day oral in 2 doses (max 1,200 mg/day) with fatty meal`,`10-14 days (1-2 weeks for single enhancing lesion); repeat if viable cysts persist`,`Headache, seizures from inflammation, hepatotoxicity, neutropenia; avoid first trimester`],[`Praziquantel (NCC, more than 2 cysts)`,`50 mg/kg/day oral in 3 doses, with albendazole`,`10-14 days`,`Dizziness, headache; levels lowered by dexamethasone, phenytoin, carbamazepine`],[`Prednisolone / dexamethasone (NCC)`,`Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day or dexamethasone 0.1 mg/kg/day`,`Start before antiparasitic; taper after`,`Hyperglycaemia, Strongyloides hyperinfection, TB reactivation`],[`Albendazole (hydatid)`,`400 mg twice daily (10-15 mg/kg/day) with fatty meal`,`Continuous 3-6 months primary; 1 day-1 week before to 1-3 months after PAIR/surgery`,`Raised transaminases, neutropenia, alopecia - LFT and CBC every 2 weeks`],[`Albendazole (Toxocara VLM)`,`400 mg twice daily`,`5 days`,`As above; add steroids for severe or ocular disease`],[`Albendazole (trichinellosis)`,`400 mg twice daily`,`8-14 days`,`As above; add prednisolone 30-60 mg/day for severe symptoms`],[`Praziquantel / niclosamide (taeniasis carrier)`,`Praziquantel 5-10 mg/kg single dose; niclosamide 2 g single dose`,`Single dose`,`Praziquantel can provoke seizures if occult NCC - niclosamide safer`]]},{heading:`NCC: lesion type and treatment (IDSA 2017)`,columns:[`NCC type`,`Antiparasitic`,`Steroid`,`Other`],rows:[[`Single enhancing lesion (SCG)`,`Albendazole 1-2 weeks`,`Yes`,`Antiseizure medicine; MRI at 6 months`],[`1-2 viable parenchymal cysts`,`Albendazole 10-14 days`,`Yes`,`Antiseizure medicine`],[`More than 2 viable cysts`,`Albendazole + praziquantel 10-14 days`,`Yes`,`Antiseizure medicine`],[`Calcified lesions only`,`None`,`Only for perilesional oedema episodes`,`Antiseizure medicine`],[`Cysticercal encephalitis`,`None`,`High-dose; mannitol`,`ICU`],[`Intraventricular cyst`,`Usually none before surgery`,`Peri-operative`,`Neuroendoscopic removal; shunt if needed`],[`Subarachnoid (racemose)`,`Prolonged albendazole (often months) +/- praziquantel`,`High-dose, prolonged`,`Shunt for hydrocephalus; methotrexate as steroid-sparing`]]},{heading:`WHO-IWGE classification of cystic echinococcosis and management`,columns:[`Stage`,`Ultrasound appearance`,`Activity`,`Management`],rows:[[`CL`,`Unilocular cyst, no visible wall`,`Uncertain`,`Further tests; follow-up`],[`CE1`,`Unilocular, visible double wall, hydatid sand (snowflake sign)`,`Active`,`Under 5 cm albendazole; 5-10 cm PAIR + albendazole`],[`CE2`,`Multivesicular, septated, honeycomb or rosette`,`Active`,`Catheterisation or surgery + albendazole`],[`CE3a`,`Detached endocyst - water lily sign`,`Transitional`,`As CE1`],[`CE3b`,`Daughter cysts in a solid matrix`,`Transitional`,`Catheterisation or surgery + albendazole`],[`CE4`,`Heterogeneous, no daughter cysts - ball of wool`,`Inactive`,`Watch and wait`],[`CE5`,`Thick calcified wall - arch-like`,`Inactive`,`Watch and wait`]]},{heading:`Single enhancing brain lesion: SCG versus tuberculoma`,columns:[`Feature`,`Solitary cysticercus granuloma`,`Tuberculoma`],rows:[[`Size`,`Under 20 mm`,`Often over 20 mm`],[`Shape`,`Round, regular ring or disc`,`Irregular, lobulated, conglomerate`],[`Oedema / shift`,`Mild, no midline shift`,`Marked, may shift midline`],[`Scolex`,`Eccentric dot may be seen`,`Absent`],[`MR spectroscopy`,`Choline, succinate/alanine peaks`,`Prominent lipid peak`],[`Clinical`,`Seizure, normal examination`,`Focal deficit, fever, raised ICP, TB elsewhere`],[`Course`,`Resolves in 6 months`,`Needs 12-18 months ATT`]]},{heading:`Larva migrans and trichinellosis compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Visceral larva migrans`,`Ocular larva migrans`,`Trichinellosis`],rows:[[`Agent`,`Toxocara canis/cati`,`Toxocara canis/cati`,`Trichinella spiralis`],[`Source`,`Eggs in soil (pica, puppies)`,`Eggs in soil`,`Undercooked pork or wild boar`],[`Age`,`2-7 years`,`Older children`,`Any; outbreaks`],[`Key features`,`Fever, wheeze, hepatomegaly`,`Unilateral visual loss, leukocoria, squint`,`Myalgia, periorbital oedema, splinter haemorrhages`],[`Eosinophilia`,`Marked`,`Usually absent`,`Marked`],[`Test`,`TES-ELISA, western blot`,`Vitreous/serum ELISA, fundus`,`Serology after 3-5 weeks, CK, muscle biopsy`],[`Treatment`,`Albendazole 5 days`,`Steroids + albendazole, vitrectomy`,`Albendazole 8-14 days + prednisolone`]]}],redFlags:[`Seizures with headache, vomiting, papilloedema or reduced GCS - raised ICP from NCC`,`Status epilepticus or new focal deficit in a patient with NCC`,`Visual symptoms or a subretinal cyst before antiparasitic treatment`,`Hydatid cyst with sudden abdominal pain, urticaria or hypotension - rupture with anaphylaxis`,`Hydatid cyst with jaundice, fever and rigors - intrabiliary rupture with cholangitis`,`Trichinellosis with chest pain, arrhythmia, breathlessness or confusion - myocarditis or encephalitis`,`A child with leukocoria - exclude retinoblastoma urgently`],pearls:[`**Pork gives the tapeworm; eggs give cysticercosis** - vegetarians get NCC.`,`**Fundus first** - papilloedema or eye cyst means no albendazole yet.`,`**Steroids with every cysticidal course**; no antiparasitic for **calcified** lesions.`,`**More than 2 cysts: albendazole + praziquantel** (IDSA 2017).`,`**Escobar: vesicular, colloidal, granular-nodular, calcified** - 'hole with dot' is the scolex.`,`**CE1-CE2 active, CE3 transitional, CE4-CE5 inactive** - watch and wait for the inactive.`,`**PAIR**: Puncture, Aspirate, Inject scolicide, Re-aspirate - never for lung or biliary-communicating cysts.`,`**Trichinella triad**: myalgia, periorbital oedema, eosinophilia after a pork feast.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-q1`,stem:`A 22-year-old vegetarian woman has her first focal seizure. MRI shows a single 12 mm ring-enhancing lesion with an eccentric dot and mild oedema, no midline shift. Fundoscopy is normal. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Antitubercular therapy for 12 months`,`Antiseizure medicine plus albendazole for 1-2 weeks with corticosteroids`,`Antiseizure medicine plus albendazole without steroids`,`Immediate surgical excision of the lesion`],answer:1,explanation:`A single small round enhancing lesion with a scolex and no midline shift is a solitary cysticercus granuloma; IDSA 2017 recommends antiseizure medicine plus albendazole with corticosteroids to blunt the inflammation of the dying cyst. Tuberculoma is usually larger and irregular with shift, albendazole without steroids risks worsening oedema, and surgery is not needed for SCG.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-q2`,stem:`In which situation is albendazole contraindicated in neurocysticercosis until another problem is addressed?`,options:[`A single colloidal-stage cyst`,`Two viable parenchymal cysts`,`Obstructive hydrocephalus with papilloedema`,`Strongly positive EITB`],answer:2,explanation:`Killing cysts in a patient with untreated hydrocephalus or diffuse cerebral oedema can raise ICP further and cause herniation, so ICP is controlled first with steroids, mannitol and shunting. A single colloidal cyst and two viable cysts are standard indications for albendazole with steroids, and a positive EITB simply supports the diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-q3`,stem:`An ultrasound of the liver shows a 7 cm cyst with a detached, floating membrane inside (water lily sign). There is no biliary communication. Which WHO-IWGE stage is this and what is the preferred treatment?`,options:[`CE3a - PAIR with albendazole cover`,`CE4 - watch and wait`,`CE2 - albendazole alone`,`CE5 - radical surgery`],answer:0,explanation:`A detached endocyst (water lily sign) is CE3a, a transitional stage managed like CE1: a 5-10 cm cyst without biliary communication is suited to PAIR with peri-procedural albendazole. CE4 (ball of wool) and CE5 (calcified) are inactive and observed, while CE2 is multivesicular and needs catheterisation or surgery rather than albendazole alone or PAIR.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-q4`,stem:`Eight people from a village fall ill 2 weeks after sharing wild boar meat, with fever, severe muscle pain, swelling around the eyes and splinter haemorrhages. Eosinophils are 40%. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Leptospirosis`,`Trichinellosis`,`Dermatomyositis`,`Visceral larva migrans`],answer:1,explanation:`A cluster after eating undercooked wild boar with myalgia, periorbital oedema, splinter haemorrhages and marked eosinophilia is classic trichinellosis. Leptospirosis causes myalgia with conjunctival suffusion but neutrophilia rather than eosinophilia, dermatomyositis is not an outbreak illness, and visceral larva migrans affects young children with pica.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-q5`,stem:`A 6-year-old boy with a pet puppy presents with a unilateral squint and white pupillary reflex. Eosinophil count is normal. What is the key diagnosis to exclude before labelling this ocular toxocariasis?`,options:[`Retinoblastoma`,`Congenital cataract`,`Toxoplasma retinochoroiditis`,`Coats disease`],answer:0,explanation:`Ocular larva migrans presents with leukocoria and squint in older children, usually without eosinophilia, and its main mimic is retinoblastoma, a life-threatening tumour that must be excluded by fundus examination and imaging (calcification). Cataract, Coats disease and toxoplasma retinochoroiditis are differentials too, but missing retinoblastoma is the fatal error.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, diagnostic criteria and management of neurocysticercosis.`,openingLines:[`Neurocysticercosis is infection of the central nervous system by the larval stage of Taenia solium, acquired by swallowing eggs shed by a human tapeworm carrier.`,`It causes about 30% of epilepsy in endemic countries and is treated by stage and location with albendazole plus corticosteroids, after controlling seizures and raised intracranial pressure.`],answer:[{heading:`Life cycle and pathogenesis`,points:[`Pork - taeniasis; eggs (faecal-oral) - cysticercosis; vegetarians affected.`,`Viable cysts evade immunity; degeneration causes inflammation, oedema and seizures.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Parenchymal vs extraparenchymal (ventricular, subarachnoid, spinal, ocular).`,`Escobar stages: vesicular, colloidal, granular-nodular, calcified.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Seizures (commonest), headache, raised ICP, hydrocephalus, encephalitis, stroke.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Revised Del Brutto criteria 2017: absolute, neuroimaging (major, confirmative, minor), clinical/exposure.`,`MRI for cysts and scolex, CT for calcification; EITB; fundoscopy; screen TB and Strongyloides.`,`Rajshekhar criteria for SCG; differentiate tuberculoma.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Antiseizure medicine for all with seizures.`,`1-2 cysts: albendazole 15 mg/kg/day x 10-14 days; over 2: add praziquantel 50 mg/kg/day.`,`Steroids with all cysticidal therapy; none for calcified; ICP first.`,`Ventricular: endoscopic removal; subarachnoid: prolonged albendazole + steroids; shunt.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prevention`,points:[`MRI at 6 months; taper antiseizure medicine after seizure-free period if lesion resolves.`,`Treat household carriers; sanitation, pig confinement, meat inspection.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle of Taenia solium showing taeniasis and cysticercosis`,`Table of NCC lesion type versus treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiopathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic criteria and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Taenia solium`,`Del Brutto`,`Escobar`,`albendazole`,`praziquantel`,`steroids`,`SCG`]},{id:`infectious-fever-helminths-tissue-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the management of hydatid cyst of the liver, including PAIR.`,openingLines:[`Hydatid cyst is the larval stage of Echinococcus granulosus, for which the dog is the definitive host and man an accidental intermediate host.`,`Management is decided by the WHO-IWGE ultrasound stage and size: albendazole, PAIR, catheterisation, surgery, or watch and wait.`],answer:[{heading:`Diagnosis and staging`,points:[`Ultrasound with WHO-IWGE stage (CL, CE1-CE5); CT/MRCP; serology 80-94% sensitive for liver.`]},{heading:`Stage-specific treatment`,points:[`CE1/CE3a under 5 cm: albendazole; 5-10 cm: PAIR + albendazole; over 10 cm: catheterisation or surgery.`,`CE2/CE3b: catheterisation or surgery; CE4/CE5: watch and wait.`]},{heading:`PAIR`,points:[`Puncture, Aspiration, Injection of 95% ethanol or 20% hypertonic saline, Re-aspiration.`,`Contraindicated: CE2, CE3b, CE4, CE5, lung cysts, biliary communication.`]},{heading:`Albendazole and follow-up`,points:[`400 mg BD continuously with fatty meal; LFT and CBC every 2 weeks.`,`Ultrasound every 3-6 months in first year; prevent with dog deworming.`]}],mustDraw:[`WHO-IWGE stages CE1 to CE5 with ultrasound sketches`],markSplit:[{part:`Staging`,marks:1.5},{part:`Stage-wise management and PAIR`,marks:2.5},{part:`Albendazole and follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`WHO-IWGE`,`CE1-CE5`,`PAIR`,`albendazole`,`water lily sign`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Cestodes; Trichinellosis and other tissue nematodes`,`White AC Jr et al. Diagnosis and treatment of neurocysticercosis: 2017 clinical practice guidelines by IDSA and ASTMH. Clin Infect Dis 2018`,`WHO guidelines on management of Taenia solium neurocysticercosis, 2021`,`Del Brutto OH et al. Revised diagnostic criteria for neurocysticercosis. J Neurol Sci 2017`,`Brunetti E, Kern P, Vuitton DA; WHO-IWGE. Expert consensus for the diagnosis and treatment of cystic and alveolar echinococcosis in humans. Acta Trop 2010`,`Rajshekhar V, Chandy MJ. Validation of diagnostic criteria for solitary cerebral cysticercus granuloma, 2004`,`CDC. Clinical care of toxocariasis and trichinellosis (healthcare provider guidance), 2024`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`]}),i.push({id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other`,title:`Other protozoal infections: toxoplasmosis, amoebiasis extra-intestinal, babesiosis`,oneLiner:`Beyond malaria and leishmaniasis, three tissue protozoa matter to the family physician: **Toxoplasma gondii** (silent or lymphadenopathic in the healthy, but causing **retinochoroiditis, congenital toxoplasmosis** and **ring-enhancing brain lesions in HIV with CD4 under 100**, treated with **pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine or co-trimoxazole**), **extra-intestinal Entamoeba histolytica** (amoebic liver abscess and its pleuropulmonary, pericardial and cerebral extensions, treated with **metronidazole followed by a luminal agent**), and **Babesia** (tick- or transfusion-borne red-cell parasite that **mimics falciparum malaria** in asplenic patients, treated with **atovaquone plus azithromycin**).`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`toxoplasmosis`,`Toxoplasma gondii`,`toxoplasma encephalitis`,`congenital toxoplasmosis`,`ocular toxoplasmosis`,`retinochoroiditis`,`pyrimethamine`,`sulfadiazine`,`spiramycin`,`co-trimoxazole`,`IgG avidity`,`amoebic liver abscess`,`Entamoeba histolytica`,`anchovy sauce pus`,`metronidazole`,`tinidazole`,`paromomycin`,`diloxanide furoate`,`babesiosis`,`Maltese cross`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Toxoplasmosis** is infection by the obligate intracellular coccidian **Toxoplasma gondii**; about **one-third of the world's population** is seropositive, and Indian studies report seroprevalence of roughly **20-40%** in adults and pregnant women, varying by region and diet. [Harrison 22e]`,`Most toxoplasma infections in immunocompetent people are **asymptomatic (80-90%)**; disease is serious in **the fetus, the eye and the immunocompromised**, where it is a **reactivation** of latent tissue cysts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxoplasma encephalitis** is the commonest cause of **focal brain lesions in advanced HIV** (CD4 under 100); co-trimoxazole prophylaxis and ART have made it less common, but it is still seen in late presenters in India. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Amoebiasis** caused by **Entamoeba histolytica** infects an estimated 50 million people a year and causes up to **100,000 deaths**, mainly from colitis and **amoebic liver abscess (ALA)**; India is highly endemic. [Harrison 22e]`,`ALA is the commonest extra-intestinal form; it is **7-10 times commoner in men**, typically aged **20-50 years** with **alcohol use**, and is the commonest cause of liver abscess in India (the Liver abscess topic in gastroenterology covers drainage in detail). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis** is a tick-borne zoonosis of red cells by **Babesia microti** (USA), **B. divergens** (Europe) and other species; in India only sporadic human cases are reported, but it is a **classic exam mimic of falciparum malaria** and a transfusion-transmitted infection. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, transmission and risk factors`,points:[`**Toxoplasma life cycle**: the **cat is the definitive host** (sexual cycle in gut, oocysts in faeces); humans and other animals are intermediate hosts carrying **tissue cysts (bradyzoites)** in brain, muscle and retina. [Harrison 22e]`,`Humans are infected by **undercooked meat (lamb, pork, goat) with tissue cysts**, **oocysts in soil, water and unwashed vegetables**, **transplacental** transmission of tachyzoites, and **organ transplant** or transfusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Toxoplasma reactivation risk: **HIV with CD4 under 100**, **heart transplant** (seronegative recipient of a seropositive donor), haematopoietic stem cell transplant, lymphoma, high-dose steroids and **biologics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**E. histolytica** spreads by **faecal-oral ingestion of cysts** in water or food; only E. histolytica is pathogenic - the morphologically identical **E. dispar** and **E. moshkovskii** are usually harmless commensals. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors for invasive amoebiasis: **alcohol, male sex, malnutrition, pregnancy, corticosteroids, malignancy** and men who have sex with men; corticosteroids given for misdiagnosed 'ulcerative colitis' can cause fulminant amoebic colitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesia** is transmitted by **Ixodes ticks**, by **blood transfusion** (the commonest transfusion-transmitted parasite in the USA) and rarely transplacentally; severe disease occurs with **asplenia, age over 50, HIV, malignancy and rituximab**. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`**Tachyzoites** invade any nucleated cell and multiply rapidly in acute infection; **cell-mediated immunity (IFN-gamma, CD4 T cells)** converts them into dormant **bradyzoite cysts** that persist for life. [Harrison 22e]`,`When CD4 cells fall, bradyzoites revert to tachyzoites and cause **necrotising encephalitis**, typically in the **basal ganglia and grey-white junction** where cysts are densest - hence **multiple ring-enhancing lesions**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ocular toxoplasmosis** is necrotising retinochoroiditis from reactivation at the edge of an old scar - the **satellite lesion** - with overlying vitritis giving the **'headlight in the fog'** appearance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital infection** follows **primary maternal infection in pregnancy**; transmission **rises with gestational age** (about 15% in the first trimester to 60-70% in the third) while **fetal severity falls**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pyrimethamine** inhibits parasite **dihydrofolate reductase** and **sulfadiazine** inhibits dihydropteroate synthase - a sequential folate blockade; **folinic acid** (not folic acid) is given to protect human marrow without helping the parasite. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**E. histolytica** trophozoites adhere via the **Gal/GalNAc lectin**, kill host cells with **amoebapores and cysteine proteases**, form **flask-shaped colonic ulcers**, and reach the liver through the **portal vein** - so ALA is usually **single, in the right lobe**, with 'anchovy sauce' necrotic liquefied liver (not true pus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metronidazole and tinidazole** (nitroimidazoles) kill **tissue trophozoites** but are absorbed so well that luminal concentrations are low; **cysts persist in the gut in up to 40-60%**, so a **luminal agent** (paromomycin, diloxanide) must follow. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Babesia** multiplies inside red cells without pigment or synchronous schizogony; **intravascular haemolysis** causes anaemia, haemoglobinuria and jaundice, and asplenia removes the main clearing organ, allowing parasitaemia of over 10-80%. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification of clinical forms`,points:[`**Toxoplasmosis**: (1) acute infection in the immunocompetent - lymphadenopathy; (2) **ocular**; (3) **congenital**; (4) **in the immunocompromised** - encephalitis, pneumonitis, myocarditis, disseminated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital toxoplasmosis** classic **Sabin tetrad**: **retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcifications (diffuse/cortical) and convulsions**; most infected newborns are asymptomatic at birth but develop **eye lesions later**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amoebiasis (WHO)**: asymptomatic cyst passage, **intestinal** (dysentery, colitis, toxic megacolon, **amoeboma**) and **extra-intestinal** - liver abscess, pleuropulmonary, pericardial, peritoneal, cerebral, cutaneous and genitourinary. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complicated ALA** is defined by **rupture** (peritoneum, pleura, pericardium), **left-lobe abscess, size over 10 cm**, secondary bacterial infection or multiple abscesses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis severity**: asymptomatic, **mild-moderate** (flu-like, low parasitaemia) and **severe** - parasitaemia **10% or more**, haemolysis, ARDS, DIC, renal or hepatic failure. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Acute toxoplasmosis**: painless **cervical (often posterior) lymphadenopathy**, fatigue, low-grade fever, myalgia - a mononucleosis-like illness lasting weeks; ask about **cats, undercooked meat, gardening**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ocular toxoplasmosis**: **unilateral blurred vision and floaters** without much pain or redness - the commonest cause of **infectious posterior uveitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxoplasma encephalitis in HIV**: subacute **headache, fever, confusion, focal deficit (hemiparesis), seizures** over days to weeks in a person with **CD4 under 100** not on co-trimoxazole. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**ALA**: **fever and right upper quadrant pain** (often referred to the right shoulder) for under 2 weeks, anorexia and weight loss; **diarrhoea is present in fewer than a third** at presentation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleuropulmonary amoebiasis**: right-sided chest pain, cough, and expectoration of **anchovy-sauce sputum** (hepatobronchial fistula); **pericardial** rupture from a left-lobe abscess presents with pericarditis or tamponade. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral amoebiasis** is rare - sudden headache, seizures and altered sensorium in a patient with ALA - with high mortality. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis**: 1-4 weeks after a tick bite or **1-9 weeks after a transfusion**, gradual fever, chills, sweats, fatigue, dark urine; severe in a **splenectomised** patient. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Toxoplasma lymphadenopathy is **discrete, non-tender, rubbery, under 3 cm**, without suppuration; hepatosplenomegaly is mild. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fundus**: active creamy-white focal retinochoroiditis next to a **pigmented scar** with dense vitritis - 'headlight in the fog'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological**: focal signs, papilloedema, altered sensorium in toxoplasma encephalitis; also look for **oral thrush, wasting** and other markers of advanced HIV. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**ALA**: **tender hepatomegaly**, **intercostal tenderness** in the right lower intercostal spaces, decreased breath sounds at the right base; jaundice is uncommon and suggests large or multiple abscesses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Impending rupture**: bulging intercostal space, localised oedema, left-lobe tenderness in the epigastrium, pericardial rub. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Babesiosis**: fever, pallor, **jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly**; dark urine; no eschar or rash. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Lymphadenopathy with fever**: EBV and CMV mononucleosis, **TB lymphadenitis** (matted, caseating), lymphoma (progressive, B symptoms), HIV seroconversion - toxoplasma serology and FNAC discriminate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ring-enhancing brain lesions in HIV**: toxoplasmosis (multiple, basal ganglia, **IgG positive**, responds in 10-14 days) vs **primary CNS lymphoma** (single, periventricular, **EBV DNA in CSF**, thallium SPECT/PET hot) vs tuberculoma, cryptococcoma and PML (non-enhancing). [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Posterior uveitis**: toxoplasma vs **CMV retinitis** (CD4 under 50, haemorrhagic 'pizza pie', little vitritis), **TB**, syphilis and acute retinal necrosis (HSV/VZV). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver abscess**: amoebic (young man, single right lobe, serology positive, rapid response to metronidazole) vs **pyogenic** (older, diabetes, biliary disease, multiple, blood culture positive - Klebsiella) vs infected hydatid cyst and necrotic tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amoeboma** mimics **colon carcinoma, ileocaecal TB and Crohn disease**; serology and biopsy discriminate - avoid steroids until amoebiasis is excluded. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis vs falciparum malaria**: Babesia shows **tetrads ('Maltese cross')**, extracellular forms, **no pigment, no schizonts or gametocytes**, and a **negative malaria RDT**; travel or tick exposure and asplenia point to Babesia. [IDSA 2020]`,`**Free-living amoebae**: **Naegleria fowleri** causes fulminant primary amoebic meningoencephalitis after freshwater exposure (a cluster of cases from Kerala in 2024); Acanthamoeba causes keratitis in contact-lens users and granulomatous encephalitis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Toxoplasma serology**: **IgG** indicates infection at some time; **IgM** can persist for **over a year** and has false positives, so a positive IgM alone does **not** prove recent infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IgG avidity**: a **high-avidity IgG excludes infection within the previous 3-4 months** - decisive in the first trimester; low avidity is less informative because it can persist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prenatal diagnosis**: **PCR of amniotic fluid** at **18 weeks or later** and at least **4 weeks after** suspected maternal infection; fetal ultrasound for ventriculomegaly, calcifications and growth restriction. [ACOG 2015]`,`**Toxoplasma encephalitis**: **contrast MRI** (multiple ring-enhancing lesions in basal ganglia and grey-white junction, 'eccentric target sign') with **positive serum IgG**; CSF PCR is specific but insensitive; **brain biopsy** if no response to 2 weeks of treatment. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**ALA**: **ultrasound** - round, hypoechoic, homogeneous lesion abutting the liver capsule; leucocytosis without eosinophilia, raised ALP, elevated right hemidiaphragm on CXR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amoebic serology** (ELISA or IHA) is positive in **over 90% of ALA** but remains positive for years, so it is less useful in residents of endemic India; **stool microscopy finds cysts in fewer than a third** and cannot distinguish E. dispar. [Harrison 22e]`,`**E. histolytica-specific stool antigen or PCR** (and PCR of aspirate) differentiate it from E. dispar; aspirate is **odourless, reddish-brown 'anchovy sauce'**, sterile, with trophozoites seen only in the **abscess wall**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis**: **thin and thick Giemsa smear** (ring forms, tetrads), **Babesia PCR** (more sensitive, species-specific), IFA serology; check haemolysis markers, LFT, creatinine and parasitaemia percentage. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Acute toxoplasma lymphadenitis** in a healthy non-pregnant adult needs **no treatment**; treat only severe or persistent symptoms (over 4-6 weeks) with the standard regimen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ocular toxoplasmosis**: observation for small peripheral lesions in the immunocompetent; treat lesions **near the macula or optic disc, large lesions, severe vitritis, or any immunocompromised patient** - **co-trimoxazole 960 mg twice daily for 4-6 weeks** or pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine, adding **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg after 24-48 hours** of antimicrobials. [AAO 2024]`,`Intravitreal **clindamycin 1 mg plus dexamethasone 400 micrograms** is an effective alternative for sight-threatening lesions; **never give steroids alone** in ocular toxoplasmosis. [AAO 2024]`,`**Uncomplicated ALA**: **metronidazole 750 mg orally three times daily for 7-10 days** (children 35-50 mg/kg/day in 3 doses), or **tinidazole 2 g once daily for 3-5 days**; fever and pain settle within **72-96 hours**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Always follow with a luminal agent**: **paromomycin 25-35 mg/kg/day in 3 doses for 7 days** or **diloxanide furoate 500 mg three times daily for 10 days**; asymptomatic E. histolytica cyst passers also get a luminal agent alone. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel on metronidazole: **metallic taste, nausea, avoid alcohol (disulfiram-like reaction) during and for 72 hours after**, dark urine; prolonged courses cause **peripheral neuropathy and cerebellar toxicity**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Mild babesiosis**: **atovaquone 750 mg twice daily plus azithromycin 500 mg on day 1 then 250 mg daily for 7-10 days**; the alternative is **clindamycin 600 mg 8-hourly plus quinine 650 mg 8-hourly**, which is more toxic. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Toxoplasma encephalitis (HIV)**: **pyrimethamine 200 mg loading, then 50 mg (under 60 kg) or 75 mg (60 kg or more) daily + sulfadiazine 1 g (under 60 kg) or 1.5 g (60 kg or more) 6-hourly + folinic acid 10-25 mg daily for at least 6 weeks**. [NIH OI 2024]`,`Where pyrimethamine or sulfadiazine is unavailable (common in India), **co-trimoxazole (TMP 5 mg/kg + SMX 25 mg/kg) IV or orally twice daily** is an accepted, equally effective alternative; **pyrimethamine + clindamycin 600 mg 6-hourly** for sulfa allergy. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Dexamethasone** only for **mass effect or oedema**, briefly; **antiseizure medicine** only if seizures have occurred; start **ART within 2 weeks** of toxoplasma treatment. [NIH OI 2024]`,`Expect **clinical improvement by day 10-14** and radiological improvement by 2-3 weeks; no improvement means **biopsy to exclude lymphoma** - see the Opportunistic infections in HIV topic for prophylaxis details. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**ALA - aspiration or catheter drainage** for: **no response after 3-5 days** of metronidazole, **left-lobe abscess** (pericardial rupture risk), **large abscess (over 5-10 cm) or impending rupture**, and uncertain diagnosis (pyogenic or infected). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ruptured ALA**: IV metronidazole 500-750 mg 8-hourly with broad-spectrum cover for secondary infection, **percutaneous drainage** for pleural or peritoneal collections, and **urgent pericardiocentesis** for amoebic pericarditis with tamponade; laparotomy is rarely needed. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Severe babesiosis**: **IV azithromycin 500 mg daily + oral atovaquone 750 mg twice daily**; **exchange transfusion** for **parasitaemia over 10%**, severe haemolysis, or renal, hepatic or pulmonary compromise. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Toxoplasma in pregnancy - acute maternal infection before 18 weeks** (fetus not proven infected): **spiramycin 1 g (3 million units) 8-hourly** to reduce transmission until delivery or fetal PCR result. [ACOG 2015]`,`**Fetal infection confirmed or maternal infection after 18 weeks**: **pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid** (avoid pyrimethamine in the first trimester); infected infants are treated for **12 months**. [ACOG 2015]`,`**Routine toxoplasma screening in pregnancy is not recommended in India** (as in the USA and UK); test women with suggestive symptoms or fetal ultrasound abnormalities, and counsel all on prevention. [ACOG 2015]`,`**Primary prophylaxis in HIV**: **co-trimoxazole 960 mg once daily** when **CD4 is under 100** and toxoplasma IgG is positive (in India co-trimoxazole is given to all with CD4 under 350 or WHO stage 3-4); stop when CD4 stays **over 200 for over 3 months** on ART. [NACO 2021]`,`**Secondary prophylaxis** after toxoplasma encephalitis (lower-dose pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine or co-trimoxazole) continues until CD4 is **over 200 for over 6 months** on ART. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Amoebiasis in pregnancy**: metronidazole is safe for invasive disease in all trimesters (avoid high doses where possible in the first trimester); **paromomycin** is preferred as luminal agent because it is not absorbed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis in the immunocompromised or asplenic**: higher azithromycin doses (500-1,000 mg daily) and treatment for **at least 6 weeks, including 2 weeks after smears become negative**. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Congenital toxoplasmosis**: blindness, hydrocephalus, intellectual disability and epilepsy; **late retinochoroiditis** appears in most untreated infants by adolescence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ocular toxoplasmosis recurs** in up to half within 5 years; long-term co-trimoxazole prophylaxis (one tablet every 2-3 days) reduces recurrences in frequent relapsers. [AAO 2024]`,`**Toxoplasma encephalitis** mortality is high without treatment; survivors may have **residual hemiparesis or seizures**; relapse is common without secondary prophylaxis. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**ALA complications**: rupture into **pleura (commonest), peritoneum, pericardium (left lobe - highest mortality)**, bronchus; secondary bacterial infection; uncomplicated ALA mortality is **under 1-3%** with treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`Abscess cavities resolve slowly over **3-12 months** on ultrasound - a persisting cavity in a well patient is **not treatment failure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis** mortality is about **1-2%** overall but up to **20%** in severe, immunocompromised or transfusion-acquired cases; relapse occurs in the immunocompromised. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer **ocular toxoplasmosis** to ophthalmology for every suspected case; refer **suspected congenital toxoplasmosis** to fetal medicine and paediatrics. [AAO 2024]`,`On pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine: **CBC weekly** (marrow suppression), creatinine and urine (sulfadiazine **crystalluria** - keep fluids high), rash monitoring (SJS/TEN). [NIH OI 2024]`,`Repeat **MRI at 2-3 weeks** in toxoplasma encephalitis, and at 6 weeks before stepping down to maintenance. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**ALA**: clinical review at **72-96 hours**; repeat ultrasound only if not improving; confirm the luminal agent is completed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesiosis**: daily smear for parasitaemia in severe cases until under 5%, then weekly until negative; **PCR** to confirm clearance in the immunocompromised. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Toxoplasma prevention in pregnancy**: cook meat thoroughly (**core 63 C for whole cuts, 71 C for minced meat**), wash fruit and vegetables, **wear gloves for gardening**, avoid changing cat litter (or change it daily with gloves - oocysts need 1-5 days to sporulate), avoid unpasteurised goat milk. [ACOG 2015]`,`**Screen organ donors and recipients** for toxoplasma IgG; give **co-trimoxazole prophylaxis to D+/R- heart transplant recipients**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amoebiasis prevention**: **safe drinking water (boiling kills cysts; chlorination does not reliably)**, sanitation under **Swachh Bharat and Jal Jeevan Mission**, hand hygiene and food-handler screening. [Park 27e]`,`**Treat asymptomatic E. histolytica cyst passers** with a luminal agent to protect households and prevent later invasive disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Babesia prevention**: tick avoidance and repellents, prompt tick removal, and **blood-donor screening** in endemic countries; asplenic travellers should be warned. [IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**IDSA/ASTMH 2020 babesiosis guideline**: **atovaquone + azithromycin** preferred for **both mild and severe** disease (replacing clindamycin + quinine as first choice for severe), with exchange transfusion for high-grade parasitaemia or organ failure. [IDSA 2020]`,`**NIH/CDC/HIVMA-IDSA adult OI guidelines (Toxoplasma section, current update)**: **co-trimoxazole accepted as an alternative of equal efficacy** to pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine, important where pyrimethamine is scarce. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment 2021**: co-trimoxazole prophylaxis for CD4 under 350 or WHO stage 3-4, and **rapid ART initiation** including after OIs. [NACO 2021]`,`**Primary amoebic meningoencephalitis cluster in Kerala (2024)** prompted state guidance on early CSF testing after freshwater exposure and use of **miltefosine-containing combination therapy**, with survivors reported. [Kerala DHS 2024]`,`**IgG avidity and amniotic-fluid PCR** remain the cornerstone of prenatal diagnosis; routine universal screening is still **not recommended** by ACOG and is not part of Indian antenatal care. [ACOG 2015]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **'Toxoplasmosis in pregnancy'**, **'CNS toxoplasmosis in HIV'**, **'Amoebic liver abscess'**, **'Extra-intestinal amoebiasis'**.`,`Viva: **why folinic acid and not folic acid with pyrimethamine?** - folinic acid bypasses human DHFR without being usable by the parasite; folic acid could antagonise treatment.`,`Viva: **why a luminal agent after metronidazole?** - metronidazole is absorbed and leaves cysts in the colon in up to half, risking relapse and spread.`,`Common mistake: **treating a positive toxoplasma IgM in pregnancy as acute infection** without avidity testing - IgM persists for months.`,`Common mistake: **reporting Babesia as P. falciparum** - look for tetrads, no pigment, no gametocytes and a negative RDT.`,`Viva: **why drain a left-lobe ALA?** - risk of rupture into the pericardium, the most lethal complication.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for toxoplasmosis, extra-intestinal amoebiasis and babesiosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid (toxo encephalitis)`,`Pyrimethamine 200 mg load, then 50-75 mg/day; sulfadiazine 1-1.5 g 6-hourly; folinic acid 10-25 mg/day`,`At least 6 weeks, then maintenance until CD4 over 200 for 6 months`,`Marrow suppression, rash/SJS, crystalluria; pyrimethamine avoided in first trimester`],[`Co-trimoxazole (toxo treatment)`,`TMP 5 mg/kg + SMX 25 mg/kg IV or oral twice daily`,`At least 6 weeks`,`Rash, hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression, raised creatinine`],[`Co-trimoxazole (prophylaxis / ocular)`,`960 mg once daily (prophylaxis); 960 mg twice daily (ocular)`,`Prophylaxis until CD4 over 200 for 3 months; ocular 4-6 weeks`,`As above; G6PD caution`],[`Spiramycin (pregnancy, before 18 weeks)`,`1 g (3 MIU) oral 8-hourly`,`Until delivery or fetal infection confirmed`,`GI upset; reduces transmission, does not treat fetus`],[`Metronidazole (ALA)`,`750 mg oral 8-hourly (or 500-750 mg IV 8-hourly)`,`7-10 days`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram-like reaction, neuropathy, cerebellar toxicity`],[`Tinidazole (ALA)`,`2 g oral once daily`,`3-5 days`,`As metronidazole; better tolerated`],[`Paromomycin (luminal)`,`25-35 mg/kg/day oral in 3 doses`,`7 days`,`Diarrhoea, nausea; not absorbed - safe in pregnancy`],[`Diloxanide furoate (luminal)`,`500 mg oral 8-hourly`,`10 days`,`Flatulence; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Atovaquone + azithromycin (babesiosis)`,`Atovaquone 750 mg BD + azithromycin 500 mg day 1 then 250 mg/day (IV 500 mg/day if severe)`,`7-10 days; at least 6 weeks if immunocompromised`,`GI upset, rash, QT prolongation (azithromycin)`],[`Clindamycin + quinine (babesiosis alternative)`,`Clindamycin 600 mg 8-hourly + quinine 650 mg 8-hourly`,`7-10 days`,`Cinchonism, hypoglycaemia, C. difficile`]]},{heading:`Toxoplasma serology in pregnancy - interpretation`,columns:[`IgG`,`IgM`,`Avidity`,`Interpretation and action`],rows:[[`Negative`,`Negative`,`-`,`Susceptible - counsel on prevention`],[`Positive`,`Negative`,`-`,`Past infection (immune) - no risk to fetus unless immunocompromised`],[`Negative`,`Positive`,`-`,`Very early infection or false-positive IgM - repeat in 2-3 weeks`],[`Positive`,`Positive`,`High`,`Infection more than 3-4 months ago - in first trimester excludes infection in pregnancy`],[`Positive`,`Positive`,`Low`,`Possible recent infection - reference lab, spiramycin, amniotic PCR at 18 weeks or later`]]},{heading:`Ring-enhancing brain lesions in HIV`,columns:[`Feature`,`Toxoplasmosis`,`Primary CNS lymphoma`,`Tuberculoma`],rows:[[`CD4`,`Under 100`,`Under 50`,`Any`],[`Number`,`Multiple`,`Usually single`,`Single or multiple`],[`Site`,`Basal ganglia, grey-white junction`,`Periventricular, corpus callosum`,`Any, often infratentorial, with meningitis`],[`Serology / CSF`,`Toxoplasma IgG positive`,`EBV DNA PCR in CSF`,`CSF TB PCR, chest X-ray`],[`Functional imaging`,`Thallium SPECT cold`,`Thallium SPECT / PET hot`,`Variable`],[`Test of treatment`,`Improves in 10-14 days of anti-toxo therapy`,`No response - biopsy`,`Responds slowly to ATT`]]},{heading:`Amoebic versus pyogenic liver abscess`,columns:[`Feature`,`Amoebic`,`Pyogenic`],rows:[[`Patient`,`Young adult man, alcohol, endemic area`,`Older, diabetes, biliary disease, malignancy`],[`Number and site`,`Usually single, right lobe`,`Often multiple`],[`Organism`,`E. histolytica`,`Klebsiella, E. coli, streptococci, anaerobes`],[`Aspirate`,`Anchovy sauce, odourless, sterile`,`Foul pus, Gram stain and culture positive`],[`Serology / culture`,`Amoebic serology over 90% positive`,`Blood culture often positive`],[`Treatment`,`Metronidazole then luminal agent; drain selected cases`,`Drainage + antibiotics 4-6 weeks`]]},{heading:`Babesia versus Plasmodium falciparum on the smear`,columns:[`Feature`,`Babesia`,`P. falciparum`],rows:[[`Ring forms`,`Present, pleomorphic, multiple per cell`,`Present, delicate, appliqué forms`],[`Tetrads (Maltese cross)`,`Pathognomonic when seen`,`Absent`],[`Pigment (haemozoin)`,`Absent`,`Present in mature forms`],[`Schizonts / gametocytes`,`Absent`,`Banana-shaped gametocytes`],[`Extracellular forms`,`Common`,`Rare`],[`Malaria RDT`,`Negative`,`HRP-2 positive`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with focal deficit, seizures or confusion in a person with HIV - suspect toxoplasma encephalitis`,`Blurred vision or floaters with a retinal lesion near the macula or optic disc`,`Acute toxoplasma infection or seroconversion in pregnancy`,`Liver abscess in the left lobe, over 10 cm, or with bulging intercostal space - impending rupture`,`Liver abscess with breathlessness, pleural effusion, pericardial rub or shock`,`No response to metronidazole after 3-5 days`,`Ring forms with haemolysis in an asplenic patient and a negative malaria RDT - suspect babesiosis; exchange transfusion if parasitaemia over 10%`],pearls:[`**Cat, meat, mother, marrow** - the four routes and risks of Toxoplasma.`,`**IgM lingers; avidity decides** - high avidity excludes infection in the last 3-4 months.`,`**Spiramycin protects the fetus from infection; pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine treats the infected fetus.**`,`**Multiple ring lesions + CD4 under 100 + IgG positive = treat toxo first; no response in 2 weeks = biopsy.**`,`**Folinic, not folic**, with pyrimethamine.`,`**Metronidazole then luminal** - always finish amoebiasis with paromomycin or diloxanide.`,`**Anchovy sauce** is liquefied liver, sterile, with trophozoites in the wall.`,`**Babesia: Maltese cross, no pigment, asplenic patient** - atovaquone plus azithromycin.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman at 10 weeks of gestation has positive toxoplasma IgG and IgM. What is the most useful next test to decide whether the infection occurred during this pregnancy?`,options:[`Repeat IgM titre after 1 week`,`Toxoplasma IgG avidity test`,`Amniotic fluid PCR immediately`,`Fetal MRI`],answer:1,explanation:`IgM can persist for over a year, so a positive IgM does not prove recent infection; a high IgG avidity at 10 weeks means infection more than 3-4 months ago, i.e. before conception. Repeating IgM adds little, amniotic PCR is done only at 18 weeks or later and at least 4 weeks after maternal infection, and fetal MRI does not date maternal infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-q2`,stem:`A 34-year-old man with HIV (CD4 60 cells/microlitre), not on any prophylaxis, has fever, headache and right hemiparesis. MRI shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia. Toxoplasma IgG is positive. Pyrimethamine is unavailable. What is the best initial management?`,options:[`Brain biopsy before any treatment`,`Antitubercular therapy`,`High-dose co-trimoxazole for at least 6 weeks, with clinical and MRI review at 2 weeks`,`Dexamethasone alone and repeat MRI`],answer:2,explanation:`Multiple basal ganglia ring lesions with CD4 under 100 and positive IgG justify empirical toxoplasma treatment, and co-trimoxazole (TMP 5 mg/kg twice daily) is an accepted equivalent when pyrimethamine is unavailable; biopsy is reserved for failure to improve by 10-14 days. ATT is not first choice for this pattern, and steroids alone can shrink lymphoma and confuse the diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-q3`,stem:`A 38-year-old man who drinks alcohol daily has fever and right upper quadrant pain for 10 days. Ultrasound shows a single 6 cm hypoechoic lesion in the right lobe. Amoebic serology is positive. After treatment with metronidazole for 10 days, what should be given next?`,options:[`Paromomycin for 7 days`,`Repeat metronidazole for another 10 days`,`Albendazole for 3 days`,`No further treatment`],answer:0,explanation:`Metronidazole kills tissue trophozoites but is well absorbed, leaving cysts in the colon in up to half of patients, so a luminal agent such as paromomycin (or diloxanide) must follow to prevent relapse and transmission. Repeating metronidazole does not clear luminal cysts, albendazole has no action on amoebae, and stopping after metronidazole leaves the carrier state.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-q4`,stem:`Which amoebic liver abscess most clearly needs percutaneous aspiration or drainage in addition to metronidazole?`,options:[`A 4 cm right-lobe abscess with fever settling at 48 hours`,`A 5 cm left-lobe abscess with epigastric tenderness`,`A 3 cm right-lobe abscess in a pregnant woman`,`A residual cavity on ultrasound 2 months after cure`],answer:1,explanation:`Left-lobe abscesses risk rupture into the pericardium, the most lethal complication, so they are drained along with metronidazole. A small right-lobe abscess responding by 48 hours needs drugs alone, pregnancy is not by itself an indication to drain, and a residual cavity in a well patient is expected because cavities take 3-12 months to resolve.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-q5`,stem:`A splenectomised 60-year-old man has fever, haemolysis and dark urine after a trip abroad. The smear shows intra-erythrocytic ring forms and occasional tetrads, with no pigment or gametocytes. The malaria RDT is negative. What is the recommended treatment?`,options:[`Artesunate IV`,`Chloroquine and primaquine`,`Doxycycline for 7 days`,`Atovaquone plus azithromycin`],answer:3,explanation:`Tetrads (Maltese cross), absent pigment and gametocytes, and a negative RDT in an asplenic patient indicate babesiosis, which IDSA 2020 treats with atovaquone plus azithromycin, adding exchange transfusion if parasitaemia exceeds 10%. Artesunate and chloroquine-primaquine target Plasmodium, and doxycycline is the drug for rickettsial and scrub infections, not Babesia.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss toxoplasmosis with special reference to infection in pregnancy and in people living with HIV.`,openingLines:[`Toxoplasmosis is infection by the obligate intracellular protozoan Toxoplasma gondii, for which the cat is the definitive host; about a third of humans carry latent tissue cysts.`,`It is usually silent in the healthy, but primary infection in pregnancy causes congenital toxoplasmosis and reactivation in advanced HIV causes necrotising encephalitis.`],answer:[{heading:`Agent and transmission`,points:[`Cat definitive host; oocysts in soil, tissue cysts in meat, transplacental, transplant.`,`Tachyzoites in acute infection; bradyzoite cysts latent for life.`]},{heading:`Clinical forms`,points:[`Immunocompetent: lymphadenopathy, mono-like illness.`,`Ocular: retinochoroiditis, headlight in the fog.`,`Congenital: Sabin tetrad - retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, diffuse calcification, convulsions.`,`Immunocompromised: encephalitis, pneumonitis.`]},{heading:`Toxoplasmosis in pregnancy`,points:[`Transmission rises (15% to 60-70%) and severity falls with gestation.`,`IgG, IgM, avidity; amniotic PCR at 18 weeks or later; fetal ultrasound.`,`Spiramycin before 18 weeks; pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine-folinic acid if fetal infection or after 18 weeks.`,`No routine screening; prevention counselling.`]},{heading:`Toxoplasmosis in HIV`,points:[`CD4 under 100; multiple ring-enhancing basal ganglia lesions; IgG positive.`,`Pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine-folinic acid or co-trimoxazole for 6 weeks; ART within 2 weeks.`,`No response by 2 weeks - biopsy for lymphoma.`,`Primary prophylaxis co-trimoxazole; secondary until CD4 over 200 for 6 months.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Cook meat, wash vegetables, gloves for gardening, avoid cat litter; donor screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle of Toxoplasma gondii`,`Table of serology interpretation in pregnancy (IgG, IgM, avidity)`],markSplit:[{part:`Agent, life cycle and clinical forms`,marks:3},{part:`Pregnancy: diagnosis and treatment`,marks:3.5},{part:`HIV: diagnosis, treatment, prophylaxis`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`Toxoplasma gondii`,`IgG avidity`,`spiramycin`,`pyrimethamine`,`co-trimoxazole`,`CD4 under 100`]},{id:`infectious-fever-protozoa-other-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate amoebic liver abscess from pyogenic liver abscess, and outline the treatment of amoebic liver abscess.`,openingLines:[`Amoebic liver abscess is the commonest extra-intestinal form of Entamoeba histolytica infection and the commonest liver abscess in India.`,`It differs from pyogenic abscess in host, number, aspirate and response to metronidazole, and is treated with a nitroimidazole followed by a luminal agent.`],answer:[{heading:`Differences`,points:[`Host: young male, alcohol vs older, diabetes, biliary disease.`,`Single right-lobe vs multiple; anchovy sauce sterile vs foul culture-positive pus.`,`Amoebic serology over 90% vs blood culture positive (Klebsiella).`]},{heading:`Treatment of ALA`,points:[`Metronidazole 750 mg TDS x 7-10 days or tinidazole 2 g OD x 3-5 days.`,`Then paromomycin 25-35 mg/kg/day x 7 days or diloxanide 500 mg TDS x 10 days.`]},{heading:`Drainage indications`,points:[`No response in 3-5 days, left lobe, large or impending rupture, diagnostic doubt.`,`Complications: pleural, peritoneal, pericardial rupture.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: amoebic vs pyogenic liver abscess`],markSplit:[{part:`Differentiating table`,marks:2.5},{part:`Drug treatment`,marks:1.5},{part:`Drainage indications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`anchovy sauce`,`metronidazole`,`luminal agent`,`left lobe`,`Klebsiella`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Toxoplasma infections; Amebiasis and infection with free-living amebas; Babesiosis`,`Krause PJ et al. Clinical practice guidelines by IDSA and ASTMH: Diagnosis and management of babesiosis, 2020`,`NIH/CDC/HIVMA-IDSA. Guidelines for the prevention and treatment of opportunistic infections in adults and adolescents with HIV - Toxoplasma gondii encephalitis section, 2024 update`,`NACO, MoHFW. National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021`,`ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 151: Cytomegalovirus, parvovirus B19, varicella zoster, and toxoplasmosis in pregnancy, 2015 (reaffirmed)`,`American Academy of Ophthalmology. Basic and Clinical Science Course: Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation, 2024-2025`,`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`]});var a=[];a.push({id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic`,title:`Sexually transmitted infections: syndromic management and aetiological diagnosis`,oneLiner:`**Syndromic management** treats an STI by the **syndrome** the patient presents with (urethral discharge, vaginal discharge, genital ulcer, lower abdominal pain, inguinal bubo, scrotal swelling) using a flow chart that covers every common cause at the **first visit**, with India's **NACO colour-coded kits 1-7**, while **aetiological diagnosis** (Gram stain, NAAT, syphilis serology) confirms the organism where a laboratory exists - always completed by the **4 Cs: compliance, counselling, condoms and contact (partner) treatment**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`STI`,`RTI`,`syndromic case management`,`NACO kits`,`Suraksha clinic`,`DSRC`,`urethral discharge`,`genital ulcer disease`,`GUD`,`gonorrhoea`,`chlamydia`,`chancroid`,`LGV`,`donovanosis`,`herpes genitalis`,`benzathine penicillin`,`cefixime azithromycin`,`partner notification`,`expedited partner therapy`,`doxy-PEP`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Sexually transmitted infections (STIs)** are infections spread mainly by vaginal, anal or oral sex; **reproductive tract infections (RTIs)** is the wider Indian programme term that also includes endogenous infections (bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis) and iatrogenic infections after abortion, delivery or IUD insertion. [NACO 2014]`,`WHO estimates about **374 million new curable STIs** each year in people aged 15-49 - **trichomoniasis 156 million, chlamydia 129 million, gonorrhoea 82 million and syphilis 7.1 million** (2020 estimates); syphilis rose to about **8 million** new adult cases in 2022. [WHO 2024]`,`More than **1 million curable STIs are acquired every day** worldwide, and most are **asymptomatic**, especially chlamydia and gonorrhoea in women - which is why syndromic management alone misses a large share of infections. [WHO 2021]`,`In India, NACO estimates that about **6% of the adult population** has one or more STI/RTI at any time; the burden is highest in **key populations** - female sex workers, men who have sex with men, transgender people, injecting drug users and migrant workers. [NACO 2014]`,`STI services are delivered through about **1,100+ Designated STI/RTI Clinics (DSRCs) branded 'Suraksha Clinics'** in district hospitals and medical colleges, and through targeted interventions under **NACP Phase V (2021-2026)**. [NACP-V 2021]`,`**Herpes simplex type 2** has overtaken chancroid as the **commonest cause of genital ulcer disease** in India and worldwide; chancroid and donovanosis are now rare, while syphilis is resurging in many cities. [Harrison 22e]`,`STIs matter beyond the genitals: an ulcerative STI raises the risk of acquiring **HIV 2-4 fold**, untreated chlamydia and gonorrhoea cause **PID, ectopic pregnancy and tubal infertility**, and untreated syphilis in pregnancy causes **stillbirth and congenital syphilis**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Urethral discharge** in men: **Neisseria gonorrhoeae** and **Chlamydia trachomatis** (often together), then **Mycoplasma genitalium**, Trichomonas vaginalis, and rarely HSV or adenovirus. [WHO 2021]`,`**Vaginal discharge**: commonest causes are the endogenous **bacterial vaginosis** (Gardnerella and anaerobes) and **vulvovaginal candidiasis**, then the STI **Trichomonas vaginalis**; **cervicitis** from gonorrhoea or chlamydia is less common and often silent. [WHO 2021]`,`**Genital ulcer**: **HSV-2 (and HSV-1)**, **Treponema pallidum (syphilis)**, **Haemophilus ducreyi (chancroid)**, **Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1-L3 (LGV)** and **Klebsiella granulomatis (donovanosis)**; non-infective causes include fixed drug eruption, Behcet disease and trauma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower abdominal pain in women (PID)**: gonorrhoea, chlamydia, M. genitalium and mixed anaerobes ascending from the cervix. [CDC 2021]`,`**Acute scrotal swelling (epididymo-orchitis)**: under 35 years usually gonorrhoea or chlamydia; over 35 years or after instrumentation usually **enteric Gram-negative bacilli** - but **testicular torsion** must be excluded first. [CDC 2021]`,`Risk factors: **age 15-24 years**, a new partner or more than one partner in the last 3 months, a partner with an STI, inconsistent condom use, sex work, MSM, alcohol and drug use, previous STI and HIV infection. [WHO 2021]`,`Mother-to-child transmission occurs for **syphilis, HIV, hepatitis B, HSV, gonorrhoea and chlamydia** (ophthalmia neonatorum, neonatal pneumonia). [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the syndromes look the way they do`,points:[`**Gonococcus** adheres to columnar epithelium by **pili and Opa proteins**, invades, and triggers an intense **neutrophil response** - producing the profuse purulent discharge, the **Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** in neutrophils and the short incubation of **2-5 days**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chlamydia** is an **obligate intracellular** bacterium with an elementary body (infectious) and reticulate body (replicating) cycle; it causes a milder, mucoid discharge after **7-21 days** and chronic subclinical inflammation that scars the tubes - and beta-lactams do not reach it, hence **doxycycline or azithromycin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Treponema pallidum** enters through micro-abrasions and causes an **obliterative endarteritis** with plasma cells; the resulting ischaemic, **indurated, painless chancre** contrasts with the **painful, soft, ragged** ulcer of chancroid, where H. ducreyi toxin causes necrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV** replicates in epithelium to form vesicles that rupture into **multiple shallow painful erosions**, then travels up sensory nerves to establish **latency in sacral dorsal root ganglia** - which explains recurrences and asymptomatic shedding. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LGV serovars L1-L3** invade lymphatics and cause a **suppurative lymphadenitis**; nodes above and below the inguinal ligament produce the **groove sign**, and rectal LGV in MSM causes **proctocolitis** mimicking inflammatory bowel disease. [CDC 2021]`,`**Bacterial vaginosis** is not an infection in the classic sense but a **loss of hydrogen-peroxide-producing lactobacilli**, with overgrowth of anaerobes that release **amines (fishy odour)** and raise pH above **4.5**. [CDC 2021]`,`Inflamed or ulcerated genital mucosa recruits **CD4 target cells** and breaches the epithelial barrier - the mechanism by which STIs amplify **HIV acquisition and shedding**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification - the STI syndromes and the NACO kits`,points:[`NACO recognises **seven syndromes**: urethral discharge, vaginal discharge (vaginitis and cervicitis), genital ulcer (herpetic and non-herpetic), lower abdominal pain, inguinal bubo, scrotal swelling, and neonatal conjunctivitis, plus anorectal discharge and genital warts. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 1 - Grey**: urethral discharge, cervicitis, anorectal discharge, painful scrotal swelling - **azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg, both single oral doses**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 2 - Green**: vaginitis (BV, trichomonas, candida) - **secnidazole 2 g single dose + fluconazole 150 mg single dose**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 3 - White**: non-herpetic genital ulcer - **benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM single dose (with skin test where required by local policy) + azithromycin 1 g single dose**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 4 - Blue**: non-herpetic genital ulcer in penicillin allergy - **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 15 days + azithromycin 1 g single dose**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 5 - Red**: herpetic genital ulcer - **acyclovir 400 mg TDS for 7 days**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 6 - Yellow**: lower abdominal pain (PID) - **cefixime 400 mg single dose + metronidazole 400 mg BD for 14 days + doxycycline 100 mg BD for 14 days**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Kit 7 - Black**: inguinal bubo - **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 21 days + azithromycin 1 g single dose**; fluctuant nodes are aspirated through healthy skin, never incised. [NACO 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Take a **sexual history using the 5 Ps** - partners (number, gender), practices (vaginal, anal, oral), protection (condoms), past STIs, and pregnancy intention - in private, with a non-judgemental tone. [CDC 2021]`,`**Gonococcal urethritis**: sudden **purulent discharge and dysuria** 2-5 days after exposure; **chlamydial urethritis** is milder, mucoid and often noticed only as morning crusting; **up to 50% of chlamydia in men** and 70-80% in women is asymptomatic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vaginal discharge**: ask colour, odour and itch - **thin grey fishy** (BV, worse after sex), **frothy yellow-green with soreness** (trichomoniasis), **curdy white with intense itch** (candidiasis); post-coital bleeding or deep dyspareunia suggests cervicitis or PID. [WHO 2021]`,`**Genital ulcer**: ask pain, number and preceding vesicles - **painful grouped vesicles with prodromal tingling** (herpes), **single painless ulcer** (syphilis), **painful ragged ulcers with tender suppurative nodes** (chancroid), **painless beefy-red ulcer that bleeds** (donovanosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inguinal bubo**: a painful node mass weeks after a small transient ulcer that the patient may not recall suggests **LGV**; rectal pain, tenesmus and bloody discharge in MSM suggest **LGV proctitis**. [CDC 2021]`,`Ask about **extragenital symptoms**: sore throat after oral sex (pharyngeal gonorrhoea is usually silent), joint pain and skin pustules (**disseminated gonococcal infection**), rash on palms and soles (secondary syphilis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical presentations**: asymptomatic screening-detected infection, recurrent 'UTI' with sterile pyuria, infertility, reactive arthritis with conjunctivitis and urethritis after chlamydia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examine in good light with a chaperone: **milk the urethra** from base to meatus if no discharge is visible, retract the foreskin, and palpate the testes, epididymis and inguinal nodes. [NACO 2014]`,`In women, do a **speculum examination**: vaginal discharge on the walls suggests vaginitis, while **mucopurulent discharge from the os, friability and contact bleeding** suggest cervicitis; a **'strawberry cervix'** (punctate haemorrhages) suggests trichomoniasis. [CDC 2021]`,`A **bimanual examination** with **cervical motion tenderness, uterine or adnexal tenderness** in a sexually active woman with lower abdominal pain is enough to start PID treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`**Syphilitic chancre**: single, round, clean-based, **indurated (button-like)**, painless, with rubbery non-tender bilateral nodes; **chancroid**: multiple, soft, ragged undermined edges, purulent base, painful, with unilateral tender bubo that may rupture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LGV groove sign**: enlarged inguinal and femoral nodes separated by the inguinal ligament - seen in about 10-20%; **donovanosis pseudobubo**: subcutaneous granulation tissue, not a true node. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Warts (condylomata acuminata)** from HPV 6/11 are cauliflower-like; **condylomata lata** of secondary syphilis are flat, moist and highly infectious - do not confuse them. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **systemic clues**: oral ulcers, generalised lymphadenopathy, rash, jaundice (hepatitis B), oral candidiasis or weight loss (HIV). [NACO 2014]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Urethral discharge vs UTI**: urethritis has discharge and urethral dysuria with a normal urine culture; cystitis has frequency, urgency and suprapubic pain with bacteriuria. [CDC 2021]`,`**Vaginal discharge**: physiological discharge (clear, odourless, mid-cycle), foreign body (retained tampon), cervical ectropion and **cervical cancer** (blood-stained, foul) must be separated from vaginitis. [WHO 2021]`,`**Genital ulcer non-infective causes**: **fixed drug eruption** (recurs at the same site after NSAIDs, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole), **Behcet disease** (recurrent oral plus genital ulcers, pathergy), trauma, and **squamous cell carcinoma** in an older man with a non-healing ulcer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower abdominal pain**: **ectopic pregnancy** (always do a urine pregnancy test), appendicitis, ovarian torsion, endometriosis and UTI. [CDC 2021]`,`**Scrotal swelling**: **testicular torsion** (sudden, young, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex - surgical emergency), mumps orchitis, tuberculous epididymitis, hernia and tumour. [CDC 2021]`,`**Inguinal bubo**: pyogenic lymphadenitis from a leg wound, tuberculous adenitis, filarial lymphadenitis, plague (very rare) and lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Gram stain of urethral discharge**: **2 or more polymorphs per oil-immersion field** confirms urethritis; **Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** make gonorrhoea - sensitivity over 95% in symptomatic men, but poor in cervical samples. [CDC 2021]`,`**Vaginal discharge bedside tests**: **pH paper** (over 4.5 in BV and trichomoniasis, normal 3.8-4.5 in candidiasis), **whiff test** with 10% KOH, **saline wet mount** (motile trichomonads, clue cells) and **KOH mount** (pseudohyphae). [CDC 2021]`,`**Amsel criteria** for BV - **3 of 4**: thin homogeneous discharge, pH over 4.5, positive whiff test, **clue cells over 20%** of epithelial cells; the **Nugent score of 7-10** on Gram stain is the laboratory standard. [CDC 2021]`,`**NAAT** (nucleic acid amplification test) on **first-void urine in men** and **vaginal swab in women** is the test of choice for gonorrhoea and chlamydia (sensitivity over 90%); use rectal and pharyngeal swabs for extragenital sites in MSM. [WHO 2021]`,`**Gonococcal culture** on Thayer-Martin medium with **antimicrobial susceptibility testing** is needed in treatment failure, because ceftriaxone-resistant strains are rising; India is part of the WHO **Enhanced Gonococcal Antimicrobial Surveillance Programme**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Every STI patient** is offered **syphilis serology (RPR/VDRL with TPHA or a rapid treponemal test), HIV testing** and, where indicated, hepatitis B and C testing. [NACO 2014]`,`**Genital ulcer**: **HSV PCR** of the ulcer base is best for herpes (Tzanck smear is insensitive), **dark-field microscopy or treponemal PCR** for a chancre, and **tissue smear for Donovan bodies** (Giemsa) for donovanosis. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Treat on the **first visit** with the syndromic flow chart - the main advantage of syndromic management is that **no patient leaves untreated** while waiting for results. [WHO 2021]`,`**Gonorrhoea**: WHO 2024 recommends **ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose**; CDC 2021 uses **ceftriaxone 500 mg IM** (1 g if 150 kg or more); the NACO kit uses **cefixime 400 mg orally** - an acceptable option where injections are impractical, but cefixime is less reliable for pharyngeal infection. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chlamydia**: **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days** is now preferred over azithromycin 1 g single dose (better cure of rectal infection); azithromycin is used in pregnancy and when adherence is doubtful. [WHO 2024]`,`**Trichomoniasis**: **metronidazole 500 mg BD for 7 days** in women (better than the 2 g single dose); men may take 2 g single dose; avoid alcohol for 24 hours (48-72 hours after tinidazole or secnidazole). [CDC 2021]`,`**Bacterial vaginosis**: **metronidazole 400-500 mg BD for 7 days** or 0.75% gel; **candidiasis**: **fluconazole 150 mg single oral dose** or clotrimazole 500 mg vaginal tablet; partners of BV or candida patients are **not** treated routinely. [CDC 2021]`,`**First-episode genital herpes**: **acyclovir 400 mg TDS for 7-10 days** (or valacyclovir 1 g BD); recurrences: acyclovir 800 mg TDS for 2 days or episodic short courses; more than 6 recurrences a year: **suppressive acyclovir 400 mg BD**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Syphilis**: **benzathine penicillin G 2.4 MU IM single dose** (1.2 MU into each buttock) for primary, secondary and early latent syphilis - full detail in the Syphilis topic. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chancroid**: **azithromycin 1 g single dose** or ceftriaxone 250 mg IM; **donovanosis**: azithromycin 1 g weekly or 500 mg daily for **at least 3 weeks** and until healed; **LGV**: **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 21 days**. [CDC 2021]`,`Complete every visit with the **4 Cs** - **compliance** (take all doses), **counselling** (risk reduction, abstain for 7 days after treatment), **condoms** (demonstrate and provide), and **contact tracing** (partners). [NACO 2014]`]},{heading:`Partner management`,points:[`Notify and treat **all partners from the last 60 days** (or the last partner if none in 60 days) for urethral discharge, cervicitis and PID; for primary syphilis, the last **3 months plus duration of symptoms**, for secondary syphilis **6 months**, and for early latent **1 year**. [CDC 2021]`,`Partners are treated **epidemiologically** - for the same syndrome as the index patient - without waiting for their own test results. [NACO 2014]`,`Methods: **patient referral** (index patient informs, with a partner card), **provider referral** (the clinic contacts the partner), and **expedited partner therapy** (giving the drugs for the partner), which is legal and used in many countries but less common in India. [CDC 2021]`,`Both partners **abstain until 7 days after both** have completed treatment and symptoms resolve, to prevent the **ping-pong reinfection**. [CDC 2021]`,`Retest for chlamydia and gonorrhoea **3 months after treatment** because reinfection, not treatment failure, explains most positives. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`Admit **PID** if surgical emergency cannot be excluded, there is a **tubo-ovarian abscess**, pregnancy, high fever with vomiting, or failure of oral therapy at 72 hours; give **ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily + doxycycline 100 mg BD + metronidazole 500 mg BD**, switching to oral after 24-48 hours of improvement for 14 days total. [CDC 2021]`,`**Disseminated gonococcal infection** (arthritis-dermatitis syndrome, septic arthritis): **ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily**, for at least 7 days for arthritis; meningitis and endocarditis need 10-14 days and 4 weeks respectively. [CDC 2021]`,`**Severe primary herpes** with urinary retention, aseptic meningitis or disseminated disease: **acyclovir 5-10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** for 2-7 days then oral to complete 10 days; urinary retention may need a **suprapubic catheter**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Acute painful scrotal swelling** in a young man is **torsion until proven otherwise** - urgent surgical review and Doppler, do not delay exploration beyond 6 hours. [CDC 2021]`,`**Ophthalmia neonatorum** (purulent conjunctivitis in the first month): **ceftriaxone 25-50 mg/kg IV/IM single dose** (max 250 mg) for gonococcal; erythromycin 50 mg/kg/day for 14 days for chlamydial; treat both parents. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **doxycycline and tetracyclines are contraindicated** - use **azithromycin 1 g** for chlamydia, ceftriaxone for gonorrhoea, metronidazole is safe for BV and trichomonas, and syphilis is treated **only with penicillin** (desensitise if allergic). [CDC 2021]`,`**Pregnancy screening**: every pregnant woman has **syphilis and HIV testing at the first ANC visit**, ideally with a point-of-care **dual HIV/syphilis rapid test**, under India's push for **elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis (EVTHS)**. [WHO 2021]`,`**HIV co-infection**: the same regimens apply, but herpes is more severe and longer (acyclovir 400 mg TDS for 5-10 days, suppression often needed) and syphilis follow-up serology is more frequent. [CDC 2021]`,`**MSM**: test all exposed sites (urethra, rectum, pharynx) with NAAT; consider **rectal LGV**, and offer **HIV PrEP**, hepatitis A and B and HPV vaccination. [WHO 2021]`,`**Adolescents and children**: an STI in a prepubertal child is **sexual abuse until proven otherwise** - report under the **POCSO Act 2012**; consent and confidentiality rules apply to adolescents. [NACO 2014]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where no microscope exists, syndromic management is the standard; add a **speculum and risk score** for cervicitis because vaginal discharge alone predicts cervical infection poorly. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**Women**: PID leads to **tubal infertility (about 10-20% after one episode)**, **ectopic pregnancy** and **chronic pelvic pain**; gonorrhoea and chlamydia cause Fitz-Hugh-Curtis **perihepatitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Men**: epididymo-orchitis, urethral stricture after repeated gonorrhoea, and **reactive arthritis** after chlamydia (HLA-B27). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonates**: ophthalmia neonatorum (blindness), chlamydial pneumonia, congenital syphilis, neonatal herpes (high mortality when disseminated). [CDC 2021]`,`**LGV and donovanosis**: genital elephantiasis (esthiomene), rectal stricture, fistulae; **HPV**: cervical, anal and penile cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Treated bacterial STIs are cured in **over 95%**; the prognosis is limited by **reinfection**, rising **gonococcal resistance** and the lifelong nature of HSV and HIV. [WHO 2024]`,`Refer to the **Suraksha Clinic / DSRC** for laboratory diagnosis, partner services and confidential counselling; link to the **ICTC** for HIV testing. [NACP-V 2021]`,`Review urethral discharge at **7 days**: persistent symptoms mean **non-adherence, reinfection or resistant organisms** - test with culture/NAAT and treat **M. genitalium** (doxycycline 7 days then moxifloxacin 400 mg daily for 7 days) or trichomonas. [CDC 2021]`,`Review genital ulcers at **7 days**: a non-healing ulcer needs **biopsy, dark-field, HSV PCR and HIV testing** and referral. [NACO 2014]`,`Review PID at **72 hours**: no improvement means admission, ultrasound for tubo-ovarian abscess and IV therapy. [CDC 2021]`,`Repeat **syphilis serology at 6 and 12 months** after treatment and **HIV testing at 3 months** if the first test was in the window period. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`**Condoms** used correctly and consistently reduce transmission of gonorrhoea, chlamydia, trichomonas and HIV substantially, with lesser protection against herpes, HPV and syphilis on uncovered skin. [WHO 2021]`,`**HPV vaccination** (see the Adult vaccination topic) and **hepatitis B vaccination** prevent two viral STIs; **voluntary medical male circumcision** reduces female-to-male HIV acquisition by about 60%. [WHO 2021]`,`**Doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis (doxy-PEP)**: **doxycycline 200 mg within 72 hours** of condomless sex reduced syphilis and chlamydia by about two-thirds in MSM and transgender women; CDC recommends it (2024) for those with a bacterial STI in the last year - not part of NACO policy. [CDC 2024]`,`**NACP Phase V (2021-2026)** aims to eliminate **vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis** and to provide free syndromic kits and testing in all DSRCs, with STI services integrated into **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs**. [NACP-V 2021]`,`Report STIs through the **SIMS (Strategic Information Management System)** of NACO; maintain **confidentiality** under the HIV and AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017. [NACO 2017]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2021** - Guidelines for the management of symptomatic STIs: recommends **speculum examination plus point-of-care tests** where available and moves away from purely syndromic treatment of vaginal discharge for cervicitis. [WHO 2021]`,`**WHO 2024** - updated treatment recommendations: **ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose** for gonorrhoea, **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days** as preferred chlamydia treatment, and **benzathine penicillin** for syphilis with alternatives during shortages. [WHO 2024]`,`**CDC 2021** STI treatment guidelines: ceftriaxone **500 mg** (no routine co-treatment with azithromycin unless chlamydia is not excluded), metronidazole 7 days for trichomoniasis in women, and **metronidazole added to all PID regimens**. [CDC 2021]`,`**CDC 2024** - doxy-PEP recommended for MSM and transgender women with a recent bacterial STI; concern remains about **tetracycline resistance** in gonococci and commensals. [CDC 2024]`,`**Rising resistance**: ceftriaxone-resistant gonorrhoea (penA mosaic strains) is increasing in Asia - culture and susceptibility in every treatment failure; the NACO kit (2014) still uses oral **cefixime** - note the difference from WHO 2024. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Commonest viva: **'name the NACO kits and their colours'** - Grey, Green, White, Blue, Red, Yellow, Black for kits 1-7 (urethral, vaginal, non-herpetic ulcer, ulcer in penicillin allergy, herpetic, PID, bubo). [NACO 2014]`,`**Advantages of syndromic management**: same-day treatment, no laboratory needed, cheap, can be done by trained paramedics; **disadvantages**: overtreatment, misses asymptomatic infection, poor for cervicitis, promotes resistance. [WHO 2021]`,`Common mistake: treating **vaginal discharge as an STI** and notifying partners for BV or candidiasis - only trichomoniasis and cervicitis need partner treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`Common mistake: **incising a bubo** - aspirate through healthy skin with a wide-bore needle to prevent a sinus. [NACO 2014]`,`Always say **'offer HIV and syphilis testing and do partner management'** at the end of any STI answer. [NACO 2014]`]}],tables:[{heading:`NACO colour-coded STI/RTI kits`,columns:[`Kit (colour)`,`Syndrome`,`Contents`,`Key cautions`],rows:[[`Kit 1 (Grey)`,`Urethral discharge, cervicitis, anorectal discharge, scrotal swelling`,`Azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg, single oral doses`,`Cefixime weaker for pharyngeal gonorrhoea; exclude torsion`],[`Kit 2 (Green)`,`Vaginitis (BV, trichomonas, candida)`,`Secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg, single doses`,`No alcohol for 72 h; metronidazole 7 days preferred in pregnancy`],[`Kit 3 (White)`,`Non-herpetic genital ulcer`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM + azithromycin 1 g`,`Observe 30 min for anaphylaxis; keep adrenaline ready`],[`Kit 4 (Blue)`,`Non-herpetic ulcer, penicillin allergy`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD x 15 days + azithromycin 1 g`,`Doxycycline contraindicated in pregnancy - desensitise instead`],[`Kit 5 (Red)`,`Herpetic genital ulcer`,`Acyclovir 400 mg TDS x 7 days`,`Start within 72 h for best effect; suppression if 6+ recurrences/year`],[`Kit 6 (Yellow)`,`Lower abdominal pain (PID)`,`Cefixime 400 mg single + metronidazole 400 mg BD x 14 days + doxycycline 100 mg BD x 14 days`,`Pregnancy test first; admit if TOA, pregnancy or failure at 72 h`],[`Kit 7 (Black)`,`Inguinal bubo`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD x 21 days + azithromycin 1 g`,`Aspirate, never incise, fluctuant nodes`]]},{heading:`Drug table - aetiological treatment (WHO 2024 / CDC 2021)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone (gonorrhoea)`,`1 g IM (WHO 2024) or 500 mg IM (CDC 2021)`,`Single dose`,`Beta-lactam allergy; rising resistance in Asia`],[`Doxycycline (chlamydia, LGV)`,`100 mg orally BD`,`7 days chlamydia; 21 days LGV`,`Pregnancy, children under 8; photosensitivity, oesophagitis - take upright with water`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g orally`,`Single dose (chlamydia in pregnancy, chancroid); weekly x 3+ for donovanosis`,`GI upset; QT prolongation; M. genitalium macrolide resistance`],[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`2.4 MU deep IM (1.2 MU each buttock)`,`Single dose early syphilis; weekly x 3 late latent`,`Anaphylaxis (rare), Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, never IV`],[`Metronidazole`,`500 mg orally BD`,`7 days (trichomonas in women, BV)`,`Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; metallic taste`],[`Fluconazole`,`150 mg orally`,`Single dose; 3 doses 72 h apart in severe VVC`,`Avoid in first trimester (use topical clotrimazole); drug interactions`],[`Acyclovir`,`400 mg orally TDS`,`7-10 days first episode; 400 mg BD suppression`,`Reduce dose in CKD; IV form can crystallise in renal tubules - hydrate`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of genital ulcer disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Syphilis`,`Herpes`,`Chancroid`,`LGV`,`Donovanosis`],rows:[[`Organism`,`Treponema pallidum`,`HSV-2 / HSV-1`,`Haemophilus ducreyi`,`C. trachomatis L1-L3`,`Klebsiella granulomatis`],[`Incubation`,`9-90 days (mean 21)`,`2-7 days`,`3-10 days`,`3-30 days`,`1-4 weeks (up to 6 months)`],[`Ulcer`,`Single, indurated, clean base`,`Multiple grouped vesicles then shallow erosions`,`Multiple, soft, ragged, purulent`,`Small, transient, often unnoticed`,`Beefy red, bleeds on touch, painless`],[`Pain`,`Painless`,`Painful`,`Very painful`,`Painless`,`Painless`],[`Nodes`,`Bilateral, rubbery, non-tender`,`Bilateral, tender`,`Unilateral, tender, suppurative bubo`,`Large painful bubo, groove sign`,`Pseudobubo (subcutaneous granuloma)`],[`Test`,`Dark-field, RPR + TPHA`,`HSV PCR`,`Culture/PCR (rarely available)`,`Chlamydia NAAT with LGV genotyping`,`Donovan bodies on tissue smear`],[`Treatment`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM`,`Acyclovir 400 mg TDS 7-10 days`,`Azithromycin 1 g single`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD 21 days`,`Azithromycin 1 g weekly 3+ weeks`]]},{heading:`Bedside investigations of vaginal discharge`,columns:[`Test`,`Bacterial vaginosis`,`Trichomoniasis`,`Candidiasis`,`Normal`],rows:[[`Discharge`,`Thin, grey, homogeneous, fishy`,`Frothy, yellow-green, profuse`,`Thick, white, curdy`,`Clear or white, scant`],[`pH`,`Over 4.5`,`Over 4.5 (often 5-6)`,`4.0-4.5`,`3.8-4.5`],[`Whiff test (10% KOH)`,`Positive`,`Often positive`,`Negative`,`Negative`],[`Microscopy`,`Clue cells over 20%, few lactobacilli`,`Motile trichomonads (sensitivity 50-60%), many WBC`,`Budding yeast, pseudohyphae on KOH`,`Lactobacilli, few WBC`],[`Partner treatment`,`No`,`Yes`,`No (unless symptomatic balanitis)`,`Not applicable`]]},{heading:`Syndromic vs aetiological management`,columns:[`Aspect`,`Syndromic`,`Aetiological`],rows:[[`Basis`,`Symptoms and signs grouped into a syndrome`,`Identification of the organism`],[`Timing of treatment`,`First visit`,`After results (unless point-of-care)`],[`Cost and setting`,`Cheap, any PHC, paramedics can use`,`Needs laboratory, trained staff, NAAT expensive`],[`Accuracy`,`Good for urethral discharge and GUD; poor for cervicitis`,`High; detects asymptomatic infection`],[`Drawbacks`,`Overtreatment, resistance, misses asymptomatic cases`,`Delay, loss to follow-up, cost`],[`Best use`,`Primary care, where no lab exists`,`DSRC, medical college, treatment failure, surveillance`]]}],redFlags:[`Acute painful scrotal swelling in a young man - suspect testicular torsion, urgent surgical exploration`,`Lower abdominal pain with a positive pregnancy test or shock - ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise`,`PID with tubo-ovarian abscess, high fever, vomiting or no response to oral therapy in 72 hours`,`Fever with arthritis and pustular skin lesions - disseminated gonococcal infection needing IV ceftriaxone`,`Primary herpes with urinary retention, severe headache or neck stiffness`,`Purulent conjunctivitis in a neonate - ophthalmia neonatorum`,`STI in a prepubertal child - sexual abuse, report under POCSO`,`Non-healing genital ulcer beyond 2-3 weeks of treatment - biopsy for carcinoma and test for HIV`],pearls:[`NACO kits 1-7 colours in order: **Grey, Green, White, Blue, Red, Yellow, Black** - 'Good Girls Wear Blue, Red, Yellow, Black'.`,`Painful ulcer: herpes and chancroid; painless ulcer: syphilis, LGV and donovanosis.`,`The **groove sign** is LGV; the **pseudobubo** is donovanosis; the **button-like induration** is syphilis.`,`Gram stain with **2 or more PMN per oil-immersion field** confirms urethritis in a man.`,`Partners are treated for trichomoniasis and cervicitis, not for BV or candidiasis.`,`The **4 Cs** of STI care: compliance, counselling, condoms, contact tracing.`,`Aspirate a bubo through healthy skin - never incise.`,`Doxycycline is now the preferred chlamydia drug (WHO 2024); azithromycin in pregnancy.`,`Every STI patient gets an **HIV and syphilis test** - an STI is an HIV risk marker.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old truck driver has purulent urethral discharge for 3 days. At the PHC there is no laboratory. Which NACO kit should be given?`,options:[`Kit 2 (Green) - secnidazole and fluconazole`,`Kit 1 (Grey) - azithromycin 1 g and cefixime 400 mg`,`Kit 3 (White) - benzathine penicillin and azithromycin`,`Kit 7 (Black) - doxycycline for 21 days and azithromycin`],answer:1,explanation:`Urethral discharge is covered by the Grey kit, which treats both gonorrhoea (cefixime) and chlamydia (azithromycin) at the first visit. The Green kit is for vaginitis, the White kit is for non-herpetic genital ulcer, and the Black kit is for inguinal bubo.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q2`,stem:`A woman has thin grey vaginal discharge with a fishy odour, pH 5.0, and clue cells on wet mount. Which statement about her management is correct?`,options:[`Her partner must be treated to prevent recurrence`,`Fluconazole 150 mg single dose is the treatment of choice`,`Metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days is appropriate, and the partner need not be treated`,`She needs ceftriaxone for presumed cervicitis`],answer:2,explanation:`These are Amsel criteria for bacterial vaginosis, an endogenous loss of lactobacilli treated with metronidazole for 7 days; partner treatment does not reduce recurrence. Fluconazole treats candidiasis, which has a normal pH, and ceftriaxone is for gonococcal cervicitis, not suggested here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q3`,stem:`A man has multiple painful, soft, ragged genital ulcers with a purulent base and a tender, fluctuant unilateral inguinal node. The most likely organism is:`,options:[`Haemophilus ducreyi`,`Treponema pallidum`,`Klebsiella granulomatis`,`Chlamydia trachomatis L2`],answer:0,explanation:`Painful soft ragged ulcers with a suppurative unilateral bubo are classic chancroid caused by H. ducreyi. Syphilis gives a single painless indurated ulcer, donovanosis a painless beefy-red ulcer with a pseudobubo, and LGV a transient painless ulcer followed by a large bubo with the groove sign.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q4`,stem:`According to WHO 2024 STI treatment recommendations, the preferred treatment for uncomplicated urogenital chlamydia in a non-pregnant adult is:`,options:[`Azithromycin 1 g orally single dose`,`Amoxicillin 500 mg thrice daily for 7 days`,`Ceftriaxone 1 g IM single dose`,`Doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days`],answer:3,explanation:`WHO 2024 (and CDC 2021) prefer doxycycline for 7 days because it cures rectal chlamydia better than single-dose azithromycin. Azithromycin remains the choice in pregnancy, amoxicillin is a pregnancy alternative only, and ceftriaxone does not treat intracellular chlamydia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-q5`,stem:`A 19-year-old man has sudden severe left testicular pain for 2 hours with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex. He had unprotected sex last week. The next step is:`,options:[`Give the Grey kit and review in 7 days`,`Urgent surgical referral for possible testicular torsion`,`Ultrasound after 48 hours if pain persists`,`Doxycycline for 10 days for epididymo-orchitis`],answer:1,explanation:`Sudden pain, a high-riding testis and loss of the cremasteric reflex mean torsion, a surgical emergency where testicular salvage falls sharply after 6 hours. Treating it as STI-related epididymo-orchitis with the Grey kit or doxycycline, or delaying imaging, risks losing the testis.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the syndromic approach to sexually transmitted infections. Give the NACO colour-coded kits and outline the management of genital ulcer disease.`,openingLines:[`Syndromic management treats an STI on the first visit by the group of symptoms and signs (syndrome) using a flow chart that covers all common causes, without waiting for laboratory confirmation.`,`India delivers it through Suraksha Clinics (DSRCs) under NACP Phase V using seven colour-coded pre-packed kits, completed by the 4 Cs - compliance, counselling, condoms and contact treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale and burden`,points:[`About 374 million curable STIs a year globally; about 6% of Indian adults have an STI/RTI.`,`STIs raise HIV risk 2-4 fold and cause infertility, ectopic pregnancy and congenital syphilis.`,`Syndromic approach: same-day treatment, cheap, no laboratory; drawbacks - overtreatment, misses asymptomatic and cervical infection.`]},{heading:`The syndromes and NACO kits`,points:[`Kit 1 Grey - urethral discharge/cervicitis: azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg.`,`Kit 2 Green - vaginitis: secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg.`,`Kit 3 White - non-herpetic GUD: benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM + azithromycin 1 g; Kit 4 Blue - same with penicillin allergy: doxycycline 100 mg BD 15 days + azithromycin 1 g.`,`Kit 5 Red - herpetic GUD: acyclovir 400 mg TDS 7 days.`,`Kit 6 Yellow - PID: cefixime 400 mg + metronidazole 400 mg BD 14 days + doxycycline 100 mg BD 14 days; Kit 7 Black - bubo: doxycycline 100 mg BD 21 days + azithromycin 1 g.`]},{heading:`Genital ulcer - history and examination`,points:[`Sexual history with 5 Ps; pain, number, vesicles, recurrences, nodes.`,`Painful grouped vesicles - herpes; single painless indurated - syphilis; painful ragged with bubo - chancroid; beefy red - donovanosis; transient with groove sign - LGV.`]},{heading:`Genital ulcer - flow chart`,points:[`Vesicles or recurrent history: treat herpes (Kit 5).`,`No vesicles: treat syphilis and chancroid together (Kit 3, or Kit 4 if allergic and not pregnant).`,`Review at 7 days; non-healing ulcer - biopsy, dark-field, HSV PCR, HIV test, refer.`]},{heading:`Aetiological confirmation`,points:[`RPR/VDRL with TPHA, HSV PCR, Donovan bodies on tissue smear; HIV test for all.`]},{heading:`The 4 Cs and partner management`,points:[`Partners in last 3 months (primary syphilis) treated epidemiologically; abstain 7 days; condoms provided.`,`Follow-up syphilis serology at 6 and 12 months; HIV retest at 3 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of genital ulcer disease: ulcer, vesicles yes or no, Kit 5 vs Kit 3/4, review at 7 days`,`Table of NACO kits 1-7 with colours`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, rationale, advantages and drawbacks`,marks:2},{part:`NACO kits table`,marks:3},{part:`GUD flow chart and differential`,marks:3},{part:`4 Cs, partner management, follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`syndromic management`,`NACO kits`,`genital ulcer`,`benzathine penicillin`,`partner notification`]},{id:`infectious-fever-sti-syndromic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between the causes of vaginal discharge: bacterial vaginosis, trichomoniasis and candidiasis.`,openingLines:[`Vaginal discharge is the commonest RTI syndrome in women; most is due to endogenous vaginitis (bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis) rather than an STI.`,`Only trichomoniasis among the three is sexually transmitted and needs partner treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`BV: thin grey homogeneous fishy discharge, little itch; trichomoniasis: frothy yellow-green, soreness, strawberry cervix; candidiasis: thick curdy white, intense itch, normal pH.`]},{heading:`Bedside tests`,points:[`pH over 4.5 in BV and trichomoniasis; 4.0-4.5 in candida.`,`Whiff positive in BV; clue cells over 20% (Amsel 3 of 4; Nugent 7-10); motile trichomonads on saline mount; pseudohyphae on KOH.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`BV: metronidazole 500 mg BD 7 days; trichomoniasis: metronidazole 500 mg BD 7 days (women) plus partner; candida: fluconazole 150 mg single dose. Syndromic: Kit 2 Green.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table of BV, trichomoniasis and candidiasis`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Bedside tests`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and partner`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Amsel criteria`,`clue cells`,`whiff test`,`trichomonas`,`candida`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Sexually transmitted infections: overview and clinical approach`,`NACO. National Guidelines on Prevention, Management and Control of Reproductive Tract Infections including Sexually Transmitted Infections, 2014`,`NACO. National AIDS and STD Control Programme Phase V (2021-2026) strategy document`,`WHO. Guidelines for the management of symptomatic sexually transmitted infections, 2021`,`WHO. Recommendations for the treatment of Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis and Treponema pallidum (syphilis), 2024`,`CDC. Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021 (MMWR Recomm Rep 70(4))`,`CDC. Clinical guidelines on the use of doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis for bacterial STI prevention, 2024`]}),a.push({id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail`,title:`Syphilis: stages, serology interpretation and treatment`,oneLiner:`**Syphilis** is a chronic systemic infection by the spirochaete **Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum**, spread sexually and **vertically**, that passes through **primary (chancre), secondary (rash, condylomata lata), latent and tertiary (gumma, cardiovascular, neurosyphilis)** stages; it is diagnosed by pairing a **non-treponemal test (VDRL/RPR, titre follows activity)** with a **treponemal test (TPHA/TPPA/EIA, positive for life)** and cured with **benzathine penicillin G 2.4 MU IM** - once for early syphilis, weekly x 3 for late latent - while **neurosyphilis needs IV aqueous penicillin for 10-14 days**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`Treponema pallidum`,`lues`,`chancre`,`condylomata lata`,`VDRL`,`RPR`,`TPHA`,`TPPA`,`FTA-ABS`,`prozone phenomenon`,`biological false positive`,`serofast`,`neurosyphilis`,`tabes dorsalis`,`Argyll Robertson pupil`,`congenital syphilis`,`Hutchinson triad`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction`,`benzathine penicillin`,`penicillin desensitisation`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Syphilis** is caused by **Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum**, a thin, motile spirochaete that cannot be cultured on artificial media, divides slowly (every **30-33 hours**) and is killed by tiny, sustained penicillin levels - which is why a **long-acting depot penicillin** works. [Harrison 22e]`,`WHO estimated about **8 million new adult syphilis infections in 2022** worldwide, a rise of over 1 million since 2020, with congenital syphilis still a leading infectious cause of **stillbirth**. [WHO 2024]`,`In India, syphilis sero-positivity in antenatal women in national sentinel surveillance is **well under 1%**, but it is several-fold higher in **female sex workers, MSM, transgender people and injecting drug users**; NACO screens all ANC attendees and key populations. [NACO 2023]`,`Transmission is mainly **sexual** (about 30-60% of partners of a person with early syphilis become infected), **vertical** (transplacental at any stage of pregnancy) and rarely through **blood transfusion** (all donated blood is screened). [Harrison 22e]`,`Infectiousness is highest in the **first 1-2 years** (primary, secondary and early latent stages with mucocutaneous lesions); late latent syphilis is not sexually infectious but can still pass to the fetus. [CDC 2021]`,`Syphilis is an **HIV co-factor** - genital ulcers increase HIV acquisition, and HIV co-infection makes syphilis more aggressive, with earlier neurosyphilis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Organism**: T. pallidum has very few outer-membrane proteins (a 'stealth pathogen'), which allows immune evasion and decades of latency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Related treponemes** cause the non-venereal treponematoses - **yaws (T. pertenue)**, **bejel** and **pinta** - which give identical serology; India was declared **yaws-free by WHO in 2016**. [WHO 2016]`,`**Risk factors**: unprotected sex, multiple partners, sex work, MSM, transgender women, HIV infection, drug and alcohol use, and a partner with any STI. [CDC 2021]`,`**Congenital syphilis risk** rises with untreated maternal early syphilis (fetal infection in up to **70-100% of primary and secondary**), lack of ANC screening, and treatment less than **30 days before delivery**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Re-infection** is common: past treated syphilis gives no protective immunity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Treponemes penetrate intact mucosa or abraded skin and **disseminate within hours** through lymphatics and blood - syphilis is a systemic infection from the start, even while the chancre is the only sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`The basic lesion is an **obliterative endarteritis** with perivascular **plasma cells and lymphocytes**; ischaemia of the overlying tissue produces the **indurated, painless ulcer** of the chancre. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary syphilis** reflects haematogenous spread and immune-complex deposition - rash, mucous patches, lymphadenopathy, hepatitis, **membranous nephropathy** and early meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Latency** follows when cell-mediated immunity controls but does not eradicate the organism; secondary relapses occur mainly in the first year. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tertiary syphilis** is a slow destructive inflammation: **gummas** (granulomas with central necrosis) from delayed hypersensitivity; **aortitis** from endarteritis of the **vasa vasorum**, weakening the media of the **ascending aorta** (tree-bark intima, aneurysm, aortic regurgitation, coronary ostial stenosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurosyphilis** begins with CSF invasion in the first months in up to 40% of untreated people; it may clear, cause **early meningovascular** disease (stroke in a young adult), or progress decades later to **parenchymal** disease - general paresis (cortex) and **tabes dorsalis** (dorsal columns and dorsal roots). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-treponemal antibodies** (reagin) react to **cardiolipin-lecithin-cholesterol** released from damaged host cells and treponemes - so they track disease activity and also rise in other tissue-damaging illnesses (biological false positives). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - stages`,points:[`**Primary**: chancre at the inoculation site after **9-90 days (mean 21)**, with regional lymphadenopathy; heals in **3-6 weeks** even without treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`**Secondary**: **4-10 weeks after the chancre** - generalised rash, mucocutaneous lesions, lymphadenopathy and systemic features; resolves untreated in weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early latent**: positive serology without symptoms, infection acquired within **1 year (CDC) or 2 years (WHO)**; **late latent**: beyond that or of unknown duration - treated as late latent. [WHO 2024]`,`**Tertiary** (about one-third of untreated): gummatous (15%), cardiovascular (10%) and late neurosyphilis (general paresis, tabes) appearing **years to decades** later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurosyphilis, ocular syphilis and otosyphilis** can occur at **any stage**, so they are classified separately and treated with IV penicillin. [CDC 2021]`,`**Congenital**: **early** (signs before 2 years) and **late** (after 2 years), plus stigmata. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Primary**: a single painless genital sore (oral or anal in receptive sex) that the patient may not notice, especially on the cervix or in the rectum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary**: a non-itchy rash, sore throat, fever, malaise, headache, patchy hair loss, weight loss and joint pain - the great imitator, often seen first by a dermatologist or physician. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early neurosyphilis**: headache, neck stiffness, cranial nerve palsies (VII, VIII), **sudden hearing loss and tinnitus (otosyphilis)**, blurred vision or red eye (**ocular syphilis - uveitis**), and stroke in a young adult. [CDC 2021]`,`**General paresis**: personality change, poor memory, grandiosity, psychosis and dementia in middle age - a reversible cause of dementia that is worth testing for. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tabes dorsalis**: **lightning (lancinating) pains** in the legs, sensory ataxia worse in the dark, bladder dysfunction, impotence and visceral crises. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: dyspnoea and angina from aortic regurgitation or ostial stenosis, typically **15-30 years** after infection. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Chancre**: round, painless, clean-based ulcer with **button-like induration**, and **bilateral rubbery, non-tender inguinal nodes** ('bubon d'accompagnement'). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary rash**: symmetrical **macular, papular or papulosquamous** eruption, non-itchy, characteristically on the **palms and soles** (copper-coloured); also **condylomata lata** (moist flat papules in warm folds, teeming with treponemes), **mucous patches and snail-track ulcers**, and **moth-eaten alopecia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Generalised lymphadenopathy** including **epitrochlear nodes** is a useful bedside clue to secondary syphilis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Argyll Robertson pupil**: small, irregular pupils that **accommodate but do not react to light** (light-near dissociation) - classic of tabes and general paresis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tabes signs**: loss of vibration and position sense, **positive Romberg**, absent ankle reflexes, **Charcot neuropathic joints** (knee), and painless trophic ulcers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: early diastolic murmur of aortic regurgitation heard best at the **right sternal edge**, a 'tambour' A2, and an ascending aortic aneurysm on chest X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gumma**: painless nodule or punched-out ulcer of skin, bone (sabre tibia, palatal perforation), liver (hepar lobatum) or testis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Chancre**: herpes (painful, multiple), chancroid (painful, soft), donovanosis, fixed drug eruption, trauma and squamous cell carcinoma - see the STI syndromic topic for the full GUD table. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary rash**: pityriasis rosea (herald patch, spares palms and soles), drug eruption, guttate psoriasis, lichen planus, viral exanthem, acute HIV seroconversion and rickettsial fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palm and sole rash** list: secondary syphilis, rickettsial infections (Indian tick typhus), hand-foot-mouth disease, meningococcaemia, erythema multiforme and infective endocarditis (Janeway lesions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurosyphilis**: other chronic meningitis (TB, cryptococcal), stroke from vasculitis, other dementias, B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration mimics tabes) and diabetic pseudotabes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aortitis**: Takayasu arteritis, giant-cell arteritis, Marfan syndrome and bicuspid aortopathy. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - serology interpretation`,points:[`**Direct tests**: **dark-field microscopy** of chancre or condylomata serum (not oral lesions, which contain commensal treponemes) and **T. pallidum PCR** - useful before antibodies appear. [CDC 2021]`,`**Non-treponemal tests (VDRL, RPR)** are cheap, **quantitative** (titre 1:2, 1:4...), become positive **about 1-4 weeks after the chancre**, peak in secondary syphilis (nearly 100% positive), and fall with treatment - used to **diagnose activity and monitor cure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Treponemal tests (TPHA, TPPA, FTA-ABS, EIA/CLIA, rapid ICT)** detect specific antibody, turn positive slightly earlier, and stay **positive for life in about 85%** after treatment - they **confirm** but cannot judge activity or reinfection. [CDC 2021]`,`**A fourfold (two-dilution) change** in VDRL/RPR titre - e.g. 1:32 to 1:8 - is the significant change; VDRL and RPR titres cannot be compared with each other, so use the same test and laboratory. [CDC 2021]`,`**Prozone phenomenon**: very high antibody levels (secondary syphilis, pregnancy, HIV) block lattice formation and give a **false-negative undiluted VDRL** - ask the laboratory to **dilute the serum**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biological false-positive VDRL** (usually titre 1:8 or less, TPHA negative): **acute** (under 6 months) - viral infections, malaria, pregnancy, recent immunisation; **chronic** - SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome, leprosy, injecting drug use, old age. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reverse-sequence algorithm**: treponemal EIA first; if positive, RPR; if EIA positive and RPR negative, do a **second treponemal test (TPPA)** to separate past treated syphilis or early infection from a false-positive EIA. [CDC 2021]`,`**CSF examination** (lumbar puncture) indications: neurological, ocular or otic signs, tertiary disease, and **treatment failure**; CSF findings - **WBC over 5/microlitre**, raised protein, and **CSF-VDRL** (highly specific but only 30-70% sensitive); a negative **CSF FTA-ABS** largely excludes neurosyphilis. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - treatment by stage`,points:[`**Primary, secondary and early latent**: **benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM single dose** (1.2 MU into each buttock); the depot gives treponemicidal levels for about 2-3 weeks. [WHO 2024]`,`**Late latent, latent of unknown duration and tertiary (non-neuro)**: **benzathine penicillin G 2.4 MU IM weekly for 3 weeks** (7.2 MU total); a gap of more than 9 days between doses in pregnancy (10-14 days may be accepted outside pregnancy) means restarting the series. [CDC 2021]`,`**Penicillin allergy, not pregnant, early syphilis**: **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 14 days**; late latent: **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 28 days**; alternatives - **ceftriaxone 1 g IM/IV daily for 10-14 days**; azithromycin 2 g single dose is no longer advised because of macrolide resistance (A2058G mutation). [CDC 2021]`,`**When benzathine penicillin is short** (recurrent global shortages): WHO 2024 lists **procaine penicillin 1.2 MU IM daily for 10 days**, ceftriaxone 1 g IM daily for 10 days, or doxycycline 100 mg BD 14 days for early syphilis - reserve BPG for **pregnant women**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Before the injection**: explain the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, keep **adrenaline 1:1000 (0.5 mg IM)** ready, inject deep IM in the upper outer gluteal quadrant, and observe for **30 minutes**. [NACO 2014]`,`**Never give benzathine penicillin IV** - accidental intravascular injection causes cardiorespiratory arrest and limb ischaemia (Nicolau syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`Treat all partners (window: 90 days for any exposure, treat presumptively; longer windows by stage), test for **HIV**, and advise abstinence until lesions heal and 7 days after treatment. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - neurosyphilis and reactions`,points:[`**Neurosyphilis, ocular and otic syphilis**: **aqueous crystalline penicillin G 18-24 million units per day** given as **3-4 MU IV every 4 hours** (or continuous infusion) for **10-14 days**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Alternative for neurosyphilis** if adherence is assured: **procaine penicillin 2.4 MU IM daily plus probenecid 500 mg orally QID for 10-14 days**; ceftriaxone **2 g IV daily for 10-14 days** if penicillin allergic (or desensitise). [CDC 2021]`,`Many experts add **benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM weekly for up to 3 weeks** after IV therapy to match the duration of late syphilis treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`**Ocular syphilis** is managed with an ophthalmologist; topical or systemic **steroids** may be added for uveitis alongside, never instead of, IV penicillin. [CDC 2021]`,`**Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction**: fever, rigors, myalgia, headache, tachycardia and mild hypotension starting **2-8 hours** after the first dose and settling within **24 hours**; seen in up to **50% of primary and 90% of secondary** syphilis; caused by cytokine release from lysed treponemes. [Harrison 22e]`,`Manage J-H with **rest, fluids and paracetamol** - it is **not penicillin allergy**, so do not stop therapy; in pregnancy it may trigger **preterm contractions or fetal distress**, so monitor in the second half of pregnancy but never delay treatment. [CDC 2021]`,`**Cardiovascular syphilis**: treat as late syphilis (some use the neurosyphilis regimen after CSF review) and refer for **aortic valve replacement or aneurysm repair**; treatment does not reverse established aortic damage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations - pregnancy, congenital syphilis, HIV, allergy`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: screen at the **first ANC visit** (and again at 28-32 weeks and delivery in high-risk women); treat with **penicillin only** - no alternative reliably treats the fetus; a second BPG dose **1 week later** is advised by some for early syphilis in pregnancy. [CDC 2021]`,`**Penicillin-allergic pregnant woman**: **desensitise** (oral or IV graded doses in hospital) and then give BPG - doxycycline is contraindicated and macrolides do not cross the placenta reliably. [CDC 2021]`,`**Early congenital syphilis** (under 2 years): **snuffles** (rhinitis teeming with treponemes), bullous rash on palms and soles (**syphilitic pemphigus**), hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, anaemia, **osteochondritis with pseudoparalysis of Parrot**, and **Wimberger sign** (bilateral destruction of the medial proximal tibial metaphysis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Late congenital syphilis** (over 2 years): **Hutchinson triad - notched peg-shaped upper incisors, interstitial keratitis and VIII nerve deafness**; also saddle nose, **sabre shins**, frontal bossing, **mulberry molars**, rhagades and Clutton joints (painless knee effusions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infant treatment** (proven or highly probable congenital syphilis): **aqueous crystalline penicillin G 50,000 U/kg IV every 12 hours for the first 7 days, then every 8 hours, for a total of 10 days**. [CDC 2021]`,`**HIV**: same regimens as HIV-negative people, but serology may be atypical, CSF examination has a lower threshold (neurological symptoms, RPR 1:32 or more, CD4 under 350), and follow-up serology is at **3, 6, 9, 12 and 24 months**. [CDC 2021]`,`**Penicillin allergy labels**: about **10% of people** report penicillin allergy but **over 90% are not truly allergic**; assess risk and consider **skin testing or a direct oral amoxicillin challenge** for low-risk histories - details in the antibiotic allergy topic. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Without treatment about **one-third** of people develop tertiary disease; the rest stay latent or clear. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Untreated syphilis in pregnancy** causes fetal loss or stillbirth, neonatal death, prematurity or congenital infection in **up to 50-80%** of cases. [WHO 2024]`,`**Neurosyphilis** can leave permanent deafness, blindness, dementia or ataxia; outcomes are best when treated early (meningitis, uveitis). [CDC 2021]`,`**Serofast state**: titre falls less than fourfold yet remains low and stable (often 1:8 or less) despite adequate treatment - common in late or HIV-associated syphilis; needs CSF review if any doubt, but not repeated treatment if reinfection is excluded. [CDC 2021]`,`Early syphilis treated with penicillin has a cure rate of **over 95%**; penicillin resistance has **never** been documented in T. pallidum. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Early syphilis**: repeat quantitative RPR/VDRL at **6 and 12 months**; a **fourfold fall within 12 months** is the expected response. [CDC 2021]`,`**Latent syphilis**: repeat at **6, 12 and 24 months**; expect a fourfold fall within **12-24 months** if the initial titre was high. [CDC 2021]`,`**Treatment failure or reinfection**: a **fourfold rise** in titre, persistent or recurring signs - test for HIV, do a lumbar puncture, and retreat (BPG weekly x 3 if CSF normal). [CDC 2021]`,`**Neurosyphilis**: repeat CSF at 6 months if CSF cell count was raised and serology does not fall; normalisation of serum RPR is an acceptable marker in most patients on ART. [CDC 2021]`,`Refer **ocular, otic, neuro and cardiovascular** syphilis, pregnant women with allergy (desensitisation), and infants of reactive mothers to tertiary care. [CDC 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`**Universal ANC screening** with point-of-care treponemal or dual HIV/syphilis rapid tests, with **same-day treatment** of the mother and partner, is the key step to eliminating congenital syphilis. [WHO 2021]`,`India's **elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis (EVTHS)** under NACP Phase V aims for over 95% ANC syphilis screening and treatment; WHO validates elimination when congenital syphilis is **50 or fewer per 100,000 live births**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Blood safety**: every unit of donated blood is screened for syphilis along with HIV, HBV, HCV and malaria. [NACO 2023]`,`**Condoms, partner notification and periodic screening** (every 3-6 months for MSM and sex workers) reduce community transmission. [CDC 2021]`,`**Doxy-PEP** reduces new syphilis in high-risk MSM and transgender women (CDC 2024) but is not Indian programme policy. [CDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2024** syphilis treatment recommendations: benzathine penicillin remains first line; during shortages, **procaine penicillin, ceftriaxone or doxycycline** are alternatives for non-pregnant adults; early latent defined as under 2 years. [WHO 2024]`,`**CDC 2021** STI guideline: ocular and otic syphilis managed as neurosyphilis; **CSF examination not needed** before treatment in isolated ocular or otic symptoms with a normal neurological examination; azithromycin removed as an alternative. [CDC 2021]`,`**CDC 2023-2024**: benzathine penicillin (Bicillin L-A) shortage in the US - importation of **extencilline/lentocilin** allowed and doxycycline prioritised for non-pregnant patients. [CDC 2024]`,`**CDC 2024**: doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis guideline for STI prevention including syphilis in MSM and transgender women. [CDC 2024]`,`**WHO 2024 Global progress report**: rising syphilis in adults and congenital syphilis; renewed call for dual HIV/syphilis testing in ANC. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'VDRL is reactive 1:4 and TPHA is negative - interpret'** - a biological false positive; look for SLE, APS, pregnancy, malaria or leprosy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**'VDRL negative, TPHA positive'**: past treated syphilis, very early primary syphilis, late latent or tertiary syphilis (non-treponemal tests may wane), or the prozone effect - repeat with dilution and clinical correlation. [CDC 2021]`,`**Argyll Robertson pupil** = 'prostitute's pupil - accommodates but does not react'; **Hutchinson triad** = teeth, eyes (interstitial keratitis), ears (VIII nerve). [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: calling **J-H reaction an allergy** and switching to an inferior drug. [CDC 2021]`,`Common mistake: treating neurosyphilis with **benzathine penicillin** - it does not achieve treponemicidal CSF levels. [Harrison 22e]`,`Remember the **stage-dose rule**: early - BPG once; late latent - BPG weekly x 3; neuro - aqueous penicillin IV 10-14 days. [CDC 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - treatment of syphilis by stage`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Benzathine penicillin G (early: primary, secondary, early latent)`,`2.4 MU deep IM (1.2 MU each buttock)`,`Single dose`,`Anaphylaxis, J-H reaction; never IV; observe 30 min`],[`Benzathine penicillin G (late latent, unknown duration, tertiary non-neuro)`,`2.4 MU deep IM`,`Weekly x 3 weeks (7.2 MU)`,`Restart series if a dose is missed beyond 9-14 days`],[`Aqueous crystalline penicillin G (neuro, ocular, otic)`,`3-4 MU IV every 4 h (18-24 MU/day)`,`10-14 days`,`Hospital stay; sodium/potassium load; J-H reaction`],[`Procaine penicillin + probenecid (neurosyphilis alternative)`,`2.4 MU IM daily + probenecid 500 mg oral QID`,`10-14 days`,`Hoigne reaction (procaine), painful injections`],[`Doxycycline (allergy, not pregnant)`,`100 mg oral BD`,`14 days early; 28 days late latent`,`Contraindicated in pregnancy and children under 8`],[`Ceftriaxone (alternative)`,`1 g IM/IV daily (2 g IV for neuro)`,`10-14 days`,`Cross-reactivity small; less evidence`],[`Aqueous penicillin G (congenital)`,`50,000 U/kg IV 12-hourly x 7 days, then 8-hourly`,`10 days total`,`Restart if more than 1 day missed`]]},{heading:`Stages of syphilis`,columns:[`Stage`,`Timing`,`Key features`,`Infectious?`],rows:[[`Primary`,`9-90 days (mean 21) after exposure`,`Painless indurated chancre, bilateral rubbery nodes`,`Yes - highly`],[`Secondary`,`4-10 weeks after chancre`,`Palm-sole rash, condylomata lata, mucous patches, alopecia, lymphadenopathy`,`Yes - highly`],[`Early latent`,`Under 1 year (CDC) / 2 years (WHO)`,`Serology only; relapses possible`,`Yes (relapse)`],[`Late latent`,`Beyond early latent or unknown`,`Serology only`,`Not sexually; still vertically`],[`Tertiary`,`Years to decades`,`Gumma, aortitis, general paresis, tabes dorsalis`,`No`],[`Neurosyphilis (any stage)`,`Early or late`,`Meningitis, stroke, uveitis, deafness, dementia, tabes`,`Depends on stage`]]},{heading:`Interpreting syphilis serology`,columns:[`Non-treponemal (VDRL/RPR)`,`Treponemal (TPHA/TPPA/EIA)`,`Interpretation`,`Action`],rows:[[`Reactive (any titre)`,`Reactive`,`Active syphilis (untreated or reinfection) or recently treated`,`Stage clinically; treat unless documented adequate treatment with falling titre`],[`Reactive, low titre (1:8 or less)`,`Non-reactive`,`Biological false positive`,`Look for SLE, APS, pregnancy, malaria, leprosy; repeat`],[`Non-reactive`,`Reactive`,`Past treated, very early, late latent/tertiary, or prozone`,`Dilute serum; history of treatment; second treponemal test; treat if untreated`],[`Non-reactive`,`Non-reactive`,`No syphilis or very early incubation`,`Repeat in 2-4 weeks if recent exposure or chancre; dark-field/PCR`],[`Fourfold rise after treatment`,`Reactive`,`Treatment failure or reinfection`,`HIV test, CSF, retreat`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of secondary syphilis rash`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Palms and soles`,`Test`],rows:[[`Secondary syphilis`,`Non-itchy copper-coloured papules, mucous patches, nodes`,`Yes`,`RPR high titre + TPHA`],[`Pityriasis rosea`,`Herald patch, Christmas-tree pattern, mild itch`,`Usually spared`,`Clinical; RPR to exclude syphilis`],[`Drug eruption`,`New drug, itchy, eosinophilia`,`Variable`,`Drug history`],[`Guttate psoriasis`,`Silvery scale, after streptococcal sore throat`,`Rare`,`ASO, clinical`],[`Rickettsial fever`,`Acute febrile illness, eschar (scrub) or tick bite`,`Yes`,`Weil-Felix / IgM ELISA`],[`Acute HIV`,`Fever, pharyngitis, ulcers, recent exposure`,`Occasional`,`HIV p24 antigen / RNA`]]},{heading:`CSF findings in neurosyphilis`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Neurosyphilis finding`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Cell count`,`Over 5 WBC/microlitre (lymphocytic)`,`Active inflammation; first to fall after treatment`],[`Protein`,`Over 45 mg/dL`,`Non-specific`],[`CSF-VDRL`,`Reactive in 30-70%`,`Highly specific - confirms; negative does not exclude`],[`CSF FTA-ABS`,`Reactive`,`Sensitive - negative largely excludes`],[`HIV co-infection`,`Pleocytosis may be from HIV itself`,`Use a higher cut-off (over 20 cells) if on effective ART`]]}],redFlags:[`Visual blurring, red eye or uveitis in a patient with positive syphilis serology - ocular syphilis, IV penicillin`,`Sudden hearing loss or tinnitus with reactive serology - otosyphilis`,`Headache, neck stiffness, cranial nerve palsy or stroke in a young adult with syphilis`,`Reactive syphilis serology in pregnancy - treat the same day with penicillin and refer if allergic`,`Infant of a reactive mother with snuffles, rash, hepatosplenomegaly or pseudoparalysis`,`New aortic regurgitation murmur or ascending aortic aneurysm with reactive serology`,`Fourfold titre rise after treatment - failure or reinfection, needs HIV test and CSF`],pearls:[`Non-treponemal tests track **activity**; treponemal tests confirm **ever-infection** and stay positive for life.`,`Only a **fourfold (two-dilution) change** in VDRL/RPR titre is meaningful.`,`**Prozone**: false-negative VDRL with very high antibody - dilute the serum.`,`Early syphilis: **BPG 2.4 MU once**; late latent: **weekly x 3**; neurosyphilis: **IV aqueous penicillin 10-14 days**.`,`Pregnancy + penicillin allergy = **desensitise**; no substitute treats the fetus.`,`**J-H reaction** is cytokine release, not allergy - continue treatment.`,`Hutchinson triad: **teeth, interstitial keratitis, VIII nerve deafness**.`,`Argyll Robertson pupil: **accommodation reflex present, light reflex absent**.`,`Syphilitic aortitis hits the **ascending aorta** (vasa vasorum) - atherosclerosis hits the abdominal aorta.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old antenatal woman has VDRL reactive at 1:2 and TPHA non-reactive. She is asymptomatic. The most likely interpretation is:`,options:[`Late latent syphilis needing benzathine penicillin weekly for 3 weeks`,`Prozone phenomenon masking secondary syphilis`,`Biological false-positive non-treponemal test`,`Serofast state after previous treatment`],answer:2,explanation:`A low-titre reactive VDRL with a negative specific treponemal test is a biological false positive; pregnancy itself is a known cause, as are SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome. Late latent syphilis and the serofast state both require a reactive treponemal test, and the prozone effect causes a false-negative, not a false-positive, VDRL.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-q2`,stem:`A man with secondary syphilis develops fever, rigors and myalgia 4 hours after benzathine penicillin. Blood pressure is 110/70 mmHg and there is no wheeze or urticaria. The correct action is:`,options:[`Reassure, give paracetamol and continue the planned treatment`,`Label him penicillin allergic and switch to doxycycline`,`Give IM adrenaline and admit to ICU`,`Start IV ceftriaxone for presumed bacterial sepsis`],answer:0,explanation:`This is the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, cytokine release from lysed treponemes, common in secondary syphilis and self-limiting within 24 hours. It is not an allergy, so labelling him allergic or switching to doxycycline is wrong; adrenaline is for anaphylaxis with urticaria, wheeze or shock, which are absent here.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old man with reactive RPR (1:32) and TPHA has bilateral anterior uveitis and blurred vision. Normal neurological examination otherwise. Which treatment is recommended?`,options:[`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM single dose`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM weekly for 3 weeks`,`Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 28 days`,`Aqueous crystalline penicillin G 3-4 MU IV every 4 hours for 10-14 days`],answer:3,explanation:`Ocular syphilis is managed as neurosyphilis at any stage, needing IV aqueous penicillin 18-24 MU/day for 10-14 days. Benzathine penicillin, whether single or weekly, does not give treponemicidal levels in the eye or CSF, and doxycycline is only an allergy alternative for non-neurological syphilis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-q4`,stem:`A pregnant woman at 16 weeks with early latent syphilis gives a history of urticaria and wheeze after penicillin. The best management is:`,options:[`Erythromycin 500 mg four times daily for 14 days`,`Penicillin desensitisation followed by benzathine penicillin`,`Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 14 days`,`Defer treatment until after delivery`],answer:1,explanation:`Penicillin is the only drug proven to treat the mother and prevent congenital syphilis, so a truly allergic pregnant woman is desensitised and then given benzathine penicillin. Erythromycin does not reliably cross the placenta, doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy, and deferring treatment risks fetal infection or stillbirth.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-q5`,stem:`Bilateral symmetrical destruction of the medial proximal tibial metaphyses on an infant's X-ray is called:`,options:[`Hutchinson sign`,`Higoumenakis sign`,`Wimberger sign`,`Parrot nodes`],answer:2,explanation:`Wimberger sign is the bilateral medial proximal tibial metaphyseal erosion of early congenital syphilis. Hutchinson teeth and the Higoumenakis sign (thickening of the medial clavicle) belong to late congenital syphilis, and Parrot nodes are frontal bossing of the skull rather than a tibial lesion.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the stages of acquired syphilis. Discuss the interpretation of serological tests and outline the treatment of syphilis including neurosyphilis.`,openingLines:[`Syphilis is a chronic systemic infection by Treponema pallidum, transmitted sexually and vertically, progressing through primary, secondary, latent and tertiary stages, with neurosyphilis possible at any stage.`,`Diagnosis rests on pairing a non-treponemal test (VDRL/RPR) that tracks activity with a treponemal test (TPHA/TPPA) that confirms infection; benzathine penicillin cures early disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`About 8 million new adult infections in 2022 (WHO); high in key populations; India screens all ANC women under EVTHS.`]},{heading:`Stages`,points:[`Primary (9-90 days): painless indurated chancre, rubbery nodes.`,`Secondary (4-10 weeks later): palm-sole rash, condylomata lata, mucous patches, alopecia, lymphadenopathy.`,`Latent: early (under 1 year CDC, 2 years WHO) and late.`,`Tertiary: gumma, aortitis (ascending aorta, AR), general paresis, tabes dorsalis, Argyll Robertson pupil.`]},{heading:`Serology`,points:[`Non-treponemal: VDRL/RPR, quantitative, fourfold change significant, falls with treatment; prozone and biological false positives.`,`Treponemal: TPHA/TPPA/FTA-ABS/EIA, lifelong positivity, confirms.`,`Interpretation table of the four combinations; reverse algorithm.`,`CSF: WBC over 5, raised protein, CSF-VDRL specific, CSF FTA-ABS sensitive.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Early: BPG 2.4 MU IM once; late latent: weekly x 3.`,`Neuro, ocular, otic: aqueous penicillin G 18-24 MU/day IV for 10-14 days.`,`Allergy: doxycycline 14 or 28 days; pregnancy - desensitise.`,`J-H reaction: anticipate, paracetamol, continue treatment.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and partners`,points:[`RPR at 6 and 12 months (early), 6-12-24 months (latent); fourfold fall expected; HIV test; partner treatment.`]}],mustDraw:[`Timeline of the natural history of untreated syphilis`,`Serology interpretation 2 x 2 table (VDRL/RPR vs TPHA)`],markSplit:[{part:`Stages and clinical features`,marks:3},{part:`Serology interpretation`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment including neurosyphilis and pregnancy`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up and partner management`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Treponema pallidum`,`VDRL`,`TPHA`,`benzathine penicillin`,`neurosyphilis`]},{id:`infectious-fever-syphilis-detail-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on congenital syphilis.`,openingLines:[`Congenital syphilis is transplacental infection of the fetus by Treponema pallidum, possible at any stage of maternal syphilis but highest (70-100%) in untreated primary and secondary syphilis.`,`It is fully preventable by first-visit ANC screening and same-day benzathine penicillin given at least 30 days before delivery.`],answer:[{heading:`Early congenital (under 2 years)`,points:[`Snuffles, bullous palm-sole rash, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, anaemia, osteochondritis, pseudoparalysis of Parrot, Wimberger sign.`]},{heading:`Late congenital (over 2 years) and stigmata`,points:[`Hutchinson triad (notched incisors, interstitial keratitis, VIII nerve deafness), saddle nose, sabre shins, mulberry molars, rhagades, Clutton joints, frontal bossing.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Infant RPR fourfold higher than mother's, dark-field/PCR of lesions, long-bone X-rays, CSF.`]},{heading:`Treatment and prevention`,points:[`Aqueous penicillin G 50,000 U/kg IV 12-hourly x 7 days then 8-hourly to 10 days.`,`ANC screening (dual HIV/syphilis test), maternal BPG, partner treatment; EVTHS target 50 or fewer per 100,000 live births.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of early vs late congenital syphilis features`],markSplit:[{part:`Early and late features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Hutchinson triad`,`Wimberger sign`,`snuffles`,`EVTHS`,`aqueous penicillin`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Syphilis`,`CDC. Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021 - Syphilis`,`WHO. Recommendations for the treatment of Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis and Treponema pallidum (syphilis), 2024`,`WHO. Global guidance on criteria and processes for validation: elimination of mother-to-child transmission of HIV, syphilis and hepatitis B, 2021`,`WHO. Implementing the global health sector strategies on HIV, viral hepatitis and STIs - Global progress report, 2024`,`NACO. National Guidelines on Prevention, Management and Control of RTI including STI, 2014`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Diseases of the aorta`]}),a.push({id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult`,title:`Adult vaccination and vaccine-preventable infections`,oneLiner:`**Adult immunisation** extends protection beyond the childhood **UIP** with vaccines chosen by **age, risk condition, occupation, pregnancy and travel** - in India the core adult set is **Td/Tdap every 10 years, annual influenza, pneumococcal (PCV20, or PCV13/PCV15 followed by PPSV23), hepatitis B (0-1-6 months), recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV) at 50+, and HPV** - with live vaccines (MMR, varicella, yellow fever) **avoided in pregnancy and severe immunosuppression**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`adult immunisation`,`API adult vaccination`,`Td`,`Tdap`,`influenza vaccine`,`PCV13`,`PCV15`,`PCV20`,`PCV21`,`PPSV23`,`hepatitis B vaccine`,`anti-HBs`,`Shingrix`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`,`HPV vaccine`,`Cervavac`,`Gardasil 9`,`MMR`,`typhoid conjugate vaccine`,`RSV vaccine`,`healthcare worker vaccination`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Adult immunisation** is the planned use of vaccines in people aged **19 years and over** to prevent infections whose risk rises with age, chronic disease, pregnancy, occupation or travel - complementing the childhood **Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)**. [API 2022]`,`Vaccine-preventable diseases still cause a large adult burden: **influenza** causes about **290,000-650,000 respiratory deaths** a year globally, mostly in the elderly and chronically ill. [WHO 2023]`,`India carries about **1.24 lakh new cervical cancer cases and 77,000 deaths each year** (GLOBOCAN 2020) - almost all caused by **HPV 16 and 18**, which vaccination prevents. [GLOBOCAN 2020]`,`India has **intermediate hepatitis B endemicity (HBsAg about 2-4%, roughly 4 crore carriers)**; adults born before UIP added hepatitis B (2002-2011) are largely unvaccinated. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Herpes zoster** affects about **1 in 3 people** in their lifetime, with incidence rising sharply after 50; **post-herpetic neuralgia** occurs in 10-18%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adult vaccine coverage in India is very low** - influenza and pneumococcal uptake among the elderly and people with diabetes is in the low single digits in Indian surveys such as LASI; India has **no national adult programme** apart from Td in pregnancy, TT/Td for injuries and a few outbreak campaigns. [LASI 2020]`,`**Missed opportunities** are the main barrier: most unvaccinated adults saw a doctor in the past year; physician recommendation is the strongest predictor of uptake. [API 2022]`]},{heading:`Indications and risk groups`,points:[`**Age 50 and over**: RZV (zoster); pneumococcal vaccination is now recommended from **50 years** (ACIP 2024; earlier 65). [ACIP 2024]`,`**Age 65 and over (60 in many Indian recommendations)**: annual influenza (high-dose or adjuvanted preferred) and pneumococcal; RSV vaccine at **75+ and 50-74 at increased risk** (ACIP 2024-2025). [ACIP 2025]`,`**Chronic conditions** (diabetes, chronic heart, lung, liver or kidney disease, alcoholism, smoking): influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B, Tdap and zoster. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Immunocompromised** (HIV, transplant, chemotherapy, biologics, steroids): inactivated vaccines (pneumococcal, influenza, hepatitis B, RZV from 19 years, HPV up to 26), **no live vaccines** when severely immunosuppressed. [IDSA 2013]`,`**Asplenia or sickle cell disease**: pneumococcal, **meningococcal ACWY and B**, Hib and influenza - given **2 weeks before elective splenectomy** or 2 weeks after emergency splenectomy. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Healthcare workers**: hepatitis B (with anti-HBs check), annual influenza, MMR and varicella if non-immune, and a Tdap dose. [ACIP 2011]`,`**Pregnancy**: Td/Tdap and inactivated influenza; RSV (Abrysvo) at **32-36 weeks** where available. [ACIP 2023]`,`**Others**: travellers (see the Travel medicine topic), MSM (hepatitis A, B, HPV), people who inject drugs, lab and veterinary workers (rabies PrEP), and close contacts of hepatitis B carriers. [ACIP 2025]`]},{heading:`Immunology - why vaccines differ`,points:[`**Live attenuated vaccines** (MMR, varicella, yellow fever, oral typhoid Ty21a, live zoster) replicate and give strong, long-lasting cellular and humoral immunity from 1-2 doses - but can cause disease in the **immunosuppressed and the fetus**. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Inactivated, subunit and toxoid vaccines** (influenza, hepatitis B, Td, HPV, RZV) cannot replicate, are safe in pregnancy and immunosuppression, but need **boosters and adjuvants**. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Polysaccharide vaccines (PPSV23, ViPS typhoid)** stimulate B cells **without T-cell help** - no memory, no booster effect, poor response under 2 years, no effect on carriage, and **hyporesponsiveness** after repeated doses. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Conjugate vaccines (PCV13/15/20/21, TCV, MenACWY-conjugate)** link the polysaccharide to a protein carrier (CRM197, tetanus toxoid), recruiting **T-helper cells** for memory, affinity maturation, mucosal IgA and **herd immunity** through reduced carriage. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Adjuvants** (aluminium salts; **AS01B** in Shingrix - MPL plus QS-21) activate innate immunity and overcome **immunosenescence**, which is why RZV gives over 90% efficacy even at 70+ while the older live zoster vaccine fell to about 38% at 70+. [NEJM 2016]`,`**Immunosenescence** - thymic involution, fewer naive T cells and weaker germinal-centre reactions - explains poor influenza vaccine response in the elderly and the rationale for **high-dose (60 microgram HA per strain) or adjuvanted** influenza vaccine. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - vaccine types`,points:[`**Live attenuated**: MMR, varicella, yellow fever, live attenuated hepatitis A (Biovac-A, India), oral typhoid Ty21a and LAIV; the oral cholera vaccines used in India are **inactivated, not live**. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Inactivated whole/split**: influenza (IIV), inactivated hepatitis A, rabies cell-culture vaccine, IPV, JE (JENVAC). [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Subunit/recombinant**: hepatitis B (HBsAg), HPV (virus-like particles), **RZV (glycoprotein E + AS01B)**, recombinant influenza, RSV prefusion F. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Toxoid**: tetanus and diphtheria toxoids (Td), with acellular pertussis (Tdap). [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Polysaccharide**: PPSV23, Vi polysaccharide typhoid; **conjugate**: PCV13, PCV15, PCV20, PCV21, typhoid conjugate (Typbar-TCV), MenACWY conjugate. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**mRNA and viral vector**: COVID-19 vaccines (mRNA vaccines, and the adenovirus-vector Covishield; Covaxin is inactivated whole-virus) and a newer RSV mRNA vaccine. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Adult schedule - the core vaccines`,points:[`**Td/Tdap**: adults with completed primary series - **one dose of Tdap**, then Td or Tdap **every 10 years**; unvaccinated adults - 3 doses at **0, 4 weeks and 6-12 months** (one as Tdap). India's UIP replaced TT with **Td in 2018**. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Influenza**: **one dose every year** for all adults where feasible, prioritising 65+, chronic illness, pregnancy and HCWs; in most of India use the **WHO Southern Hemisphere** formulation **before the monsoon (April-June)**, while hill states with a winter peak may use the Northern Hemisphere vaccine. [API 2022]`,`**Pneumococcal (ACIP 2024)**: all adults **50 and over** and at-risk adults 19-49 - **a single dose of PCV20 or PCV21**, OR **PCV15 followed by PPSV23 after at least 1 year** (8 weeks if immunocompromised, CSF leak or cochlear implant). [ACIP 2024]`,`**Pneumococcal - Indian practice**: where only PCV13 and PPSV23 are available, give **PCV13 first, then PPSV23 after 8 weeks (high risk) or 1 year**; never give PPSV23 first if a conjugate is planned (blunts the response). [API 2022]`,`**Hepatitis B**: **20 microgram (1 mL) IM deltoid at 0, 1 and 6 months**; dialysis and immunosuppressed adults: **40 microgram at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months**; ACIP now recommends universal vaccination for adults **19-59**, and 60+ with risk factors. [ACIP 2022]`,`**Recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV, Shingrix)**: **2 doses of 0.5 mL IM, 2-6 months apart**, for all adults **50+** and immunocompromised adults **19+** (1-2 months apart if needed sooner); give even after previous zoster or live zoster vaccine (wait 8 weeks). Available in India since 2023. [ACIP 2022]`,`**HPV**: routine at **9-14 years** (India: **Cervavac** quadrivalent, Gardasil, Gardasil 9); catch-up to **26 years**; **27-45 years by shared decision**; 3 doses (0, 1-2, 6 months) if started at 15+; WHO 2022 allows a **single dose** up to 20 years. [WHO 2022]`,`**MMR**: adults born after vaccine introduction without evidence of immunity - 1 dose (2 doses 4 weeks apart for HCWs, students, travellers); **avoid pregnancy for 4 weeks** after. [ACIP 2013]`,`**Varicella**: seronegative adults with no history of chickenpox - **2 doses 4-8 weeks apart**; priority for HCWs, teachers and women planning pregnancy. [ACIP 2007]`,`**Typhoid conjugate vaccine**: **single 0.5 mL IM dose** for adults up to 45 years at risk (Indian food handlers, travellers); Vi polysaccharide needs revaccination every 3 years. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`History - the vaccination assessment`,points:[`Ask age, **records of childhood vaccines** (UIP card), previous adult doses and dates, and **previous chickenpox, zoster, hepatitis B** - documented history replaces vaccines for varicella and measles only when reliable. [API 2022]`,`Ask **chronic disease** (diabetes, CKD, COPD, heart failure, cirrhosis), **immunosuppression** (steroid dose and duration, biologics, chemotherapy, HIV with CD4), and splenectomy. [IDSA 2013]`,`Ask **pregnancy or plans for pregnancy** in women 15-49 - live vaccines are deferred and Tdap/influenza are offered during pregnancy. [ACIP 2025]`,`Ask **occupation** (HCW, lab worker, veterinarian, sewage worker, food handler) and **lifestyle** (MSM, injecting drugs, multiple partners). [ACIP 2025]`,`Ask **planned travel** (destination, duration, Hajj/Umrah) - see the Travel medicine topic. [WHO 2024]`,`Ask **previous reactions**: anaphylaxis to a vaccine or component (gelatin, neomycin, yeast for hepatitis B, latex), Guillain-Barre syndrome within 6 weeks of an influenza vaccine. [ACIP 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination and pre-vaccination assessment`,points:[`**Temperature**: a **moderate or severe acute illness** (with or without fever) is a precaution - defer; **minor illness** (common cold, low-grade fever) is **not** a reason to delay. [ACIP 2025]`,`Check the **deltoid site** (IM vaccines in the deltoid with a 25 mm needle; 38 mm for heavier adults) - **never the buttock** for hepatitis B or rabies, where fat reduces the response. [ACIP 2025]`,`Assess **bleeding risk**: on anticoagulants or with haemophilia, use a **23-gauge or finer needle** and firm pressure for 2 minutes; IM injection is still acceptable. [ACIP 2025]`,`Look for **signs of immunosuppression** (oral candida, wasting) before any live vaccine; screen CD4 in HIV (live vaccines like MMR/varicella only if **CD4 200 or more**). [IDSA 2013]`,`Observe for **15 minutes** after any vaccine (30 minutes if previous allergy or syncope risk) with adrenaline 1:1000 ready - anaphylaxis occurs at about **1 per million doses**. [ACIP 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - adverse events vs coincident illness`,points:[`**Local reactions** (pain, redness, swelling) are common - up to **80% after RZV**, with myalgia and fever in 10-20% for 1-3 days; warn patients that this is a sign of immune response, not illness. [NEJM 2016]`,`**Syncope** (vasovagal) after vaccination in adolescents and young adults must be distinguished from **anaphylaxis** - syncope has bradycardia, pallor and rapid recovery; anaphylaxis has urticaria, wheeze, hypotension with tachycardia. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Guillain-Barre syndrome** after influenza vaccine is very rare (about **1-2 extra cases per million doses**) - lower than the GBS risk from influenza itself. [CDC 2024]`,`**Shoulder injury related to vaccine administration (SIRVA)** follows injection too high into the subacromial bursa - inject into the thick central deltoid. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Vaccine-strain disease**: varicella-like rash after varicella vaccine, and **yellow fever vaccine-associated viscerotropic disease** in older adults and those with thymus disease. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Arthralgia** after rubella vaccine in adult women (up to 25%) is transient. [Plotkin 8e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - serology before and after vaccination`,points:[`**Anti-HBs 1-2 months after the 3rd dose** in HCWs, dialysis patients, HIV and sexual partners of carriers: **10 mIU/mL or more = protected**; non-responders get a **second full series** and retest; persistent non-responders are tested for **HBsAg** (chronic infection). [ACIP 2018]`,`**Pre-vaccination HBsAg and anti-HBc** screening is advised in high-prevalence groups so that carriers are identified and linked to care. [ACIP 2022]`,`**Varicella IgG** in adults with an uncertain history (most adults in India are immune); **measles/rubella IgG** in HCWs and women planning pregnancy without records. [ACIP 2013]`,`**Hepatitis A IgG** before vaccination in adults over 40 from endemic regions - most Indian adults are already immune. [API 2022]`,`**Dialysis patients**: check anti-HBs **every year** and give a booster if it falls **below 10 mIU/mL**. [ACIP 2001]`,`Serology is **not** needed before or after influenza, pneumococcal, RZV, Td or HPV vaccination. [ACIP 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - wound care and post-exposure`,points:[`**Clean minor wound**: give Td/Tdap if fewer than 3 doses, unknown history, or **last dose 10 or more years ago**; no TIG. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Dirty or major wound** (soil, faeces, puncture, burns, crush): Td/Tdap if fewer than 3 doses, unknown, or **last dose 5 or more years ago**; **TIG 250 IU IM** at a separate site if fewer than 3 doses or unknown. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Hepatitis B exposure (needle-stick)**: vaccinated responder - nothing; unvaccinated - **HBIG 0.06 mL/kg plus vaccine series** within 24 hours; known non-responder - HBIG x 2 a month apart. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Measles exposure in a non-immune adult**: MMR within **72 hours**, or immunoglobulin within 6 days if MMR is contraindicated (pregnancy, immunosuppressed). [ACIP 2013]`,`**Varicella exposure**: vaccine within **3-5 days** in non-immune healthy adults; **VZIG** within 10 days for pregnant or immunocompromised non-immune contacts. [ACIP 2013]`,`**Rabies PEP and PrEP** are covered in the Animal bite and rabies topic; WHO 2018 PrEP is **2 visits (day 0 and 7)**. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Rules of administration`,points:[`**Any two inactivated vaccines**, or an inactivated and a live vaccine, can be given **on the same day or at any interval**; use separate sites at least 2.5 cm apart. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Two live injectable vaccines** not given on the same day must be separated by **at least 4 weeks** (28 days). [ACIP 2025]`,`**Interrupted series**: never restart - just continue where it stopped (exception: oral typhoid). [ACIP 2025]`,`**Immunoglobulin or blood products** delay live MMR and varicella vaccines by **3-11 months** depending on the product; they do not affect inactivated vaccines. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Cold chain**: most vaccines at **+2 to +8 C**, never frozen (freezing destroys aluminium-adjuvanted vaccines - use the **shake test**); India's **eVIN** digitally tracks stock and temperature. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Record every dose (date, batch, site) - in India adult doses can be recorded on the **U-WIN** platform. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **Td** (2 doses 4 weeks apart in the first pregnancy, 1 booster if vaccinated within 3 years, per UIP) and **Tdap at 27-36 weeks** in each pregnancy (ACIP) to protect the newborn from pertussis; inactivated influenza in any trimester; hepatitis B if at risk; **no MMR, varicella, HPV or live vaccines**. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Elderly**: influenza (high-dose/adjuvanted preferred at 65+), pneumococcal, RZV, Tdap and RSV (75+); response is weaker but severe-disease prevention is greatest. [ACIP 2025]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: hepatitis B **40 microgram at 0, 1, 2, 6 months** (or 4 doses of double dose), pneumococcal, influenza, RZV - vaccinate **before dialysis** when response is better. [ACIP 2001]`,`**Diabetes**: hepatitis B for adults under 60 (and 60+ by assessment), influenza, pneumococcal, Tdap and RZV - people with diabetes have higher mortality from influenza and pneumococcal disease. [ADA 2025]`,`**Immunosuppression**: give live vaccines **at least 4 weeks before** starting immunosuppression and inactivated **at least 2 weeks before**; high-dose steroids (**prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more**) - wait **at least 1 month** after stopping before a live vaccine. [IDSA 2013]`,`**HIV**: all inactivated vaccines; MMR and varicella if **CD4 200 or more**; yellow fever only if unavoidable and CD4 200 or more. [IDSA 2013]`,`**Healthcare workers**: hepatitis B with documented anti-HBs, influenza yearly, MMR (2 doses), varicella (2 doses), Tdap - required by many Indian hospitals under their infection-control policies. [ACIP 2011]`]},{heading:`Complications of the diseases and vaccine safety`,points:[`**Pneumococcal disease**: community-acquired pneumonia, bacteraemia and meningitis with case fatality of **15-20% in invasive disease** in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Influenza**: pneumonia, myocarditis, exacerbation of COPD and heart failure, and a rise in **myocardial infarction in the week after infection**; influenza vaccination lowers major cardiovascular events in people with recent MI. [ESC 2023]`,`**Herpes zoster**: post-herpetic neuralgia, **herpes zoster ophthalmicus**, stroke risk in the following months; RZV prevents over 90% of zoster and about 90% of PHN. [NEJM 2016]`,`**Hepatitis B**: chronic infection, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma - the vaccine is the first **anti-cancer vaccine**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Contraindications** (true): anaphylaxis to a previous dose or component; for live vaccines - pregnancy and severe immunosuppression; **egg allergy is no longer a contraindication** to influenza vaccine. [ACIP 2025]`,`Report serious events (death, hospitalisation, anaphylaxis, GBS) as **AEFI** to the district immunisation officer under India's **AEFI surveillance system**. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Follow-up, referral and programme link`,points:[`Schedule the **next dose date** on the card: hepatitis B months 1 and 6; RZV at 2-6 months; HPV per schedule; PPSV23 after 1 year if PCV15/PCV13 was given. [ACIP 2024]`,`Recall annually for **influenza** (before the monsoon in most of India). [API 2022]`,`Check **anti-HBs** 1-2 months after the series in HCWs and dialysis patients, annually in dialysis. [ACIP 2018]`,`Refer **post-transplant, chemotherapy and HIV with low CD4** to the specialist for timed vaccination (often restarting schedules after stem-cell transplant). [IDSA 2013]`,`Refer suspected **serious AEFI** (anaphylaxis, GBS, encephalopathy) for investigation and reporting. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**UIP (2024)** gives adults only **Td in pregnancy and for injuries**; **adult BCG** vaccination of high-risk adults has been piloted in selected states under the TB elimination effort. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**HPV vaccination** for girls 9-14 years was announced for national roll-out in the **Interim Budget 2024**, using the indigenous **Cervavac** vaccine. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Hepatitis B vaccination** of high-risk adults and HCWs is part of the **National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP)**. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Opportunistic vaccination in **NP-NCD clinics and Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** - for every person with diabetes, CKD or COPD - is the most practical route in Indian primary care. [API 2022]`,`Build trust: address **vaccine hesitancy** with a clear recommendation, side-effect counselling and documentation. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ACIP October 2024**: pneumococcal vaccination age lowered from 65 to **50 years**; **PCV21 (Capvaxive)** added as an option alongside PCV20 or PCV15 plus PPSV23. [ACIP 2024]`,`**ACIP 2024-2025**: single-dose **RSV vaccine** for all **75+** and **60-74 at increased risk**, extended in 2025 to **50-59 at increased risk**; not repeated annually. [ACIP 2025]`,`**WHO/ACIP 2024**: influenza vaccines switched to **trivalent** from the 2024-25 season because B/Yamagata has not circulated since 2020. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2022** HPV position paper: **one-dose or two-dose schedule for 9-20 years**, two doses 21 and over, three doses in immunocompromised. [WHO 2022]`,`**ACIP 2022**: universal hepatitis B vaccination for adults 19-59; RZV for immunocompromised adults 19+. [ACIP 2022]`,`**ACIP 2023**: maternal RSV vaccine (Abrysvo) at **32-36 weeks** in the RSV season as an alternative to infant nirsevimab. [ACIP 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Why conjugate before polysaccharide?'** - conjugate primes T-cell memory; PPSV23 first causes hyporesponsiveness to later conjugate doses. [Plotkin 8e]`,`Classic viva: **'Tetanus prophylaxis for a dirty wound in a man last vaccinated 7 years ago'** - Td/Tdap booster, no TIG. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Live vaccines in adults**: MMR, varicella, yellow fever, Ty21a, live hepatitis A, LAIV - remember **'MMR, VZV, YF'** for the pregnancy and immunosuppression exclusion list. [ACIP 2025]`,`Common mistake: giving hepatitis B vaccine in the **gluteal muscle** - lower seroconversion. [ACIP 2025]`,`Common mistake: withholding RZV in someone who **already had zoster** - vaccinate once the acute episode has resolved. [ACIP 2022]`,`Common mistake: deferring vaccination for a **minor cold** - only moderate or severe illness is a reason to postpone. [ACIP 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - core adult vaccines`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Td / Tdap`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Tdap once, then Td/Tdap every 10 years; 3-dose primary if unvaccinated; Tdap 27-36 weeks each pregnancy`,`Local pain, Arthus reaction if too frequent; encephalopathy after pertussis vaccine (Tdap)`],[`Influenza (inactivated, trivalent)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Every year (pre-monsoon in most of India)`,`Local pain, low fever; GBS within 6 weeks of a prior dose is a precaution; egg allergy not a contraindication`],[`PCV20 / PCV21`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Single dose at 50+ or at-risk 19-49`,`Local pain, fatigue; anaphylaxis to diphtheria toxoid-containing vaccine`],[`PCV15 or PCV13 then PPSV23`,`0.5 mL IM each`,`PPSV23 at least 1 year later (8 weeks if high risk)`,`PPSV23: local reactions, more with revaccination`],[`Hepatitis B (recombinant)`,`20 microgram (1 mL) IM deltoid; 40 microgram in dialysis`,`0, 1, 6 months (dialysis 0, 1, 2, 6); check anti-HBs at 1-2 months`,`Yeast allergy; never gluteal`],[`Recombinant zoster (RZV)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`2 doses 2-6 months apart at 50+ (19+ if immunocompromised)`,`Reactogenic: pain, myalgia, fever for 1-3 days; not for active zoster`],[`HPV (Cervavac / Gardasil 9)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`9-14 y: 1-2 doses; 15-26 y: 3 doses at 0, 1-2, 6 months`,`Syncope - observe 15 min; avoid in pregnancy`],[`MMR (live)`,`0.5 mL SC/IM`,`1-2 doses 4 weeks apart`,`Pregnancy (avoid conception 4 weeks), severe immunosuppression, CD4 under 200`]]},{heading:`Classification of vaccines and what it means`,columns:[`Type`,`Examples`,`Memory and boosters`,`Pregnancy / immunosuppressed`],rows:[[`Live attenuated`,`MMR, varicella, yellow fever, Ty21a, live hepatitis A`,`Strong, long-lasting after 1-2 doses`,`Contraindicated`],[`Inactivated / subunit`,`Influenza, hepatitis B, HPV, RZV, rabies`,`Needs series and boosters`,`Safe`],[`Toxoid`,`Tetanus, diphtheria`,`Boosters every 10 years`,`Safe (Td/Tdap in pregnancy)`],[`Polysaccharide`,`PPSV23, Vi typhoid`,`No memory, hyporesponsiveness`,`Safe but weak`],[`Conjugate`,`PCV13/15/20/21, TCV, MenACWY`,`T-cell memory, reduces carriage`,`Safe`]]},{heading:`Tetanus prophylaxis in wound management`,columns:[`Previous tetanus doses`,`Clean minor wound: Td/Tdap`,`Clean minor wound: TIG`,`Dirty/major wound: Td/Tdap`,`Dirty/major wound: TIG`],rows:[[`Unknown or fewer than 3`,`Yes (start series)`,`No`,`Yes (start series)`,`Yes - 250 IU IM`],[`3 or more, last within 5 years`,`No`,`No`,`No`,`No`],[`3 or more, last 5-10 years ago`,`No`,`No`,`Yes`,`No`],[`3 or more, last 10 or more years ago`,`Yes`,`No`,`Yes`,`No`]]},{heading:`Vaccines by risk group`,columns:[`Group`,`Recommended`,`Avoid / caution`],rows:[[`Pregnancy`,`Td/Tdap 27-36 weeks, inactivated influenza, RSV 32-36 weeks (where available)`,`MMR, varicella, HPV, live vaccines`],[`Age 50-64`,`RZV x 2, pneumococcal (ACIP 2024), Td booster, influenza`,`None specific`],[`Age 65+`,`High-dose/adjuvanted influenza, pneumococcal, RZV, RSV at 75+`,`Yellow fever caution at 60+`],[`Diabetes / CKD / COPD / heart failure`,`Influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B, RZV`,`Double-dose hepatitis B in dialysis`],[`Asplenia`,`Pneumococcal, MenACWY + MenB, Hib, influenza`,`Time 2 weeks before elective splenectomy`],[`HIV / immunosuppressed`,`All inactivated, RZV from 19 y`,`Live vaccines if CD4 under 200 or on high-dose steroids`],[`Healthcare workers`,`Hepatitis B (anti-HBs), influenza, MMR, varicella, Tdap`,`Check immunity, document`]]},{heading:`Hepatitis B serology after vaccination`,columns:[`Anti-HBs (1-2 months after series)`,`Interpretation`,`Action`],rows:[[`10 mIU/mL or more`,`Responder - protected long term`,`No boosters (except dialysis: annual check)`],[`Under 10 mIU/mL after 1 series`,`Non-responder`,`Repeat a full 3-dose series and retest`],[`Under 10 after 2 series`,`Persistent non-responder`,`Test HBsAg; if negative, counsel and give HBIG after exposures`],[`HBsAg positive`,`Chronic infection - vaccine irrelevant`,`Refer for hepatitis B care; vaccinate contacts`]]}],redFlags:[`Anaphylaxis after vaccination - urticaria, wheeze, hypotension: adrenaline 0.5 mg IM and admit`,`Ascending weakness within 6 weeks of a vaccine - suspected Guillain-Barre syndrome`,`Dirty or crush wound in an unvaccinated adult - Td plus TIG today`,`Needle-stick from an HBsAg-positive source in an unvaccinated HCW - HBIG and vaccine within 24 hours`,`Live vaccine given inadvertently in pregnancy or severe immunosuppression - counsel and refer`,`Fever, jaundice or multi-organ failure within 10 days of yellow fever vaccine - viscerotropic disease`],pearls:[`Conjugate before polysaccharide - **PCV first, PPSV23 later**.`,`**PCV20 or PCV21 single dose** at 50+ completes pneumococcal vaccination (ACIP 2024).`,`**RZV** works in the elderly because of the **AS01B adjuvant** - over 90% efficacy at 70+.`,`Anti-HBs **10 mIU/mL or more** = protected; check 1-2 months after the third dose.`,`Two live vaccines: **same day or 4 weeks apart**.`,`Hepatitis B and rabies vaccine in the **deltoid, never the buttock**.`,`**Egg allergy is not a contraindication** to influenza vaccine.`,`Live vaccine list for adults: **MMR, varicella, yellow fever, Ty21a, live hepatitis A, LAIV**.`,`Tdap in **every pregnancy at 27-36 weeks** protects the newborn from pertussis.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-q1`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has never received a pneumococcal vaccine. According to ACIP 2024, which regimen completes his pneumococcal vaccination?`,options:[`PPSV23 now, followed by PCV13 after 1 year`,`PPSV23 every 5 years`,`A single dose of PCV20 or PCV21`,`No vaccine until he reaches 65 years`],answer:2,explanation:`ACIP lowered the pneumococcal age to 50 in 2024, and one dose of PCV20 or PCV21 is complete on its own. Giving PPSV23 first blunts the later conjugate response, repeated PPSV23 every 5 years is not recommended, and waiting until 65 reflects the old age threshold.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old farmer has a deep soil-contaminated foot wound. He completed his primary tetanus series and his last Td was 7 years ago. What prophylaxis is needed?`,options:[`Td booster and tetanus immunoglobulin 250 IU`,`Td booster only`,`Tetanus immunoglobulin only`,`Nothing - he is protected for 10 years`],answer:1,explanation:`For a dirty wound in someone with 3 or more doses, a booster is needed if the last dose was 5 or more years ago; TIG is only for those with fewer than 3 doses or an unknown history. The 10-year interval applies only to clean minor wounds, and TIG alone gives no lasting protection.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-q3`,stem:`A nurse completed three doses of hepatitis B vaccine. Anti-HBs 2 months later is 4 mIU/mL. What is the next step?`,options:[`Repeat a full 3-dose hepatitis B series and retest anti-HBs`,`She is protected; no further action`,`Give a single booster dose and stop`,`Give HBIG every 6 months`],answer:0,explanation:`An anti-HBs below 10 mIU/mL after one series means she is a non-responder, and a second full series makes about half of non-responders seroconvert, followed by a repeat anti-HBs. She is not protected, one booster is not enough, and routine HBIG is used only after an actual exposure.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-q4`,stem:`Which vaccine is contraindicated in a 28-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant?`,options:[`Tdap`,`Inactivated influenza vaccine`,`Hepatitis B vaccine`,`Varicella vaccine`],answer:3,explanation:`Varicella vaccine is live attenuated and carries a theoretical risk to the fetus, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy. Tdap is recommended at 27-36 weeks, inactivated influenza vaccine is safe in any trimester, and recombinant hepatitis B vaccine is safe for at-risk pregnant women.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-q5`,stem:`A 68-year-old woman had shingles 2 years ago. She asks about zoster vaccination. The most appropriate advice is:`,options:[`She is immune after shingles and needs no vaccine`,`Recombinant zoster vaccine, 2 doses 2-6 months apart`,`Live zoster vaccine single dose, as it is more effective in the elderly`,`Varicella vaccine 2 doses 4 weeks apart`],answer:1,explanation:`Zoster can recur, so RZV is recommended after the acute episode has resolved, as 2 doses 2-6 months apart, with over 90% efficacy even at 70+. Live zoster vaccine works poorly in the elderly and has been withdrawn in many countries, and varicella vaccine is meant for non-immune people, not to prevent zoster.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss adult immunisation in India. Describe the vaccines recommended for the elderly and for adults with chronic diseases, with schedules and contraindications.`,openingLines:[`Adult immunisation is the planned use of vaccines in people aged 19 and over to prevent infections whose risk rises with age, chronic disease, pregnancy, occupation or travel, complementing the childhood UIP.`,`India has no national adult programme beyond Td in pregnancy, so opportunistic vaccination in NCD clinics by the family physician is the key to closing very low coverage.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden and rationale`,points:[`Influenza 290,000-650,000 deaths/year globally; cervical cancer 1.24 lakh cases/year in India; HBsAg 2-4%; zoster 1 in 3 lifetime.`,`Immunosenescence and comorbidity increase severity; adult coverage in India is in single digits.`]},{heading:`Immunological principles`,points:[`Live vs inactivated; polysaccharide (no memory) vs conjugate (T-cell memory); adjuvants overcome immunosenescence (AS01B in RZV).`]},{heading:`Vaccines for the elderly`,points:[`Influenza yearly (high-dose/adjuvanted at 65+), pre-monsoon Southern Hemisphere formulation.`,`Pneumococcal from 50 (ACIP 2024): PCV20/PCV21 once or PCV15/PCV13 then PPSV23.`,`RZV 2 doses 2-6 months apart at 50+; Td/Tdap every 10 years; RSV at 75+.`]},{heading:`Chronic disease`,points:[`Diabetes: hepatitis B, influenza, pneumococcal, RZV.`,`CKD/dialysis: hepatitis B 40 microgram at 0, 1, 2, 6 months, annual anti-HBs.`,`Asplenia: pneumococcal, MenACWY + MenB, Hib, 2 weeks before splenectomy.`,`Immunosuppressed: inactivated only; live 4 weeks before immunosuppression.`]},{heading:`Contraindications and precautions`,points:[`Anaphylaxis to prior dose; live vaccines in pregnancy and severe immunosuppression; moderate-severe illness - defer; egg allergy is not a contraindication.`]},{heading:`Programme and practice`,points:[`UIP Td; HPV national roll-out (Cervavac); NVHCP hepatitis B for HCWs; opportunistic vaccination at NP-NCD clinics; AEFI reporting; U-WIN records.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of adult vaccines with dose, schedule and contraindications`,`Pneumococcal decision flow (PCV20/21 vs PCV15 then PPSV23)`],markSplit:[{part:`Burden and principles`,marks:2},{part:`Elderly schedule`,marks:3},{part:`Chronic disease groups`,marks:3},{part:`Contraindications and programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`adult immunisation`,`PCV20`,`RZV`,`hepatitis B`,`influenza`]},{id:`infectious-fever-vaccine-preventable-adult-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between polysaccharide and conjugate pneumococcal vaccines.`,openingLines:[`PPSV23 contains capsular polysaccharide from 23 serotypes and stimulates B cells without T-cell help, while conjugate vaccines (PCV13, 15, 20, 21) link fewer serotypes to a protein carrier to recruit T-cell help.`,`This difference explains memory, booster response, carriage reduction and the rule of conjugate first.`],answer:[{heading:`Immunology`,points:[`PPSV23: T-independent, IgM-dominant, no memory, hyporesponsiveness, poor under 2 years.`,`PCV: T-dependent, class switching, memory, mucosal IgA, effective in infants.`]},{heading:`Clinical effect`,points:[`PPSV23: covers more serotypes, prevents invasive disease modestly, no effect on carriage or herd immunity.`,`PCV: prevents pneumonia and invasive disease, reduces carriage, herd effect.`]},{heading:`Use in adults`,points:[`PCV20/PCV21 alone, or PCV15/PCV13 first then PPSV23 after 1 year (8 weeks high risk); never PPSV23 first.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table PPSV23 vs PCV`],markSplit:[{part:`Immunology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical effect`,marks:2},{part:`Schedule`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PPSV23`,`PCV20`,`T-dependent`,`hyporesponsiveness`,`herd immunity`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Immunization principles and vaccine use`,`Plotkin's Vaccines, 8th edition, 2023`,`CDC/ACIP. Adult Immunization Schedule by Age and Medical Condition, United States, 2025`,`Kobayashi M et al. Use of 21-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine among US adults: ACIP recommendations, MMWR 2024 (age lowered to 50, October 2024)`,`WHO. Human papillomavirus vaccines: WHO position paper, December 2022`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Adult immunisation (Association of Physicians of India)`,`MoHFW. Universal Immunization Programme - Td replacement of TT (2018) and National HPV vaccination announcement, 2024`,`Rubin LG et al. 2013 IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for Vaccination of the Immunocompromised Host`]}),a.push({id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine`,title:`Travel medicine and fever in the returning traveller`,oneLiner:`**Travel medicine** is the **pre-travel risk assessment** (destination, duration, activities, host factors) leading to **required vaccines (yellow fever, MenACWY for Hajj/Umrah, polio), recommended vaccines, malaria chemoprophylaxis (ABCD) and self-treatment advice**, plus the **approach to fever in the returning traveller**, where **malaria must be excluded first** (three smears/RDTs over 12-24 hours) and the **incubation period, geography and exposure** narrow the differential.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`pre-travel consultation`,`returning traveller`,`malaria chemoprophylaxis`,`atovaquone-proguanil`,`doxycycline prophylaxis`,`mefloquine`,`yellow fever certificate`,`ICVP`,`International Health Regulations`,`Hajj`,`MenACWY`,`travellers' diarrhoea`,`acute mountain sickness`,`acetazolamide`,`GeoSentinel`,`viral haemorrhagic fever`,`Katayama fever`,`typhoid conjugate vaccine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Travel medicine** prevents and manages health problems of international and long-distance domestic travellers; it covers **pre-travel advice, vaccines, chemoprophylaxis, and post-travel illness**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Of travellers from high-income to low- and middle-income countries, **22-64% report some illness** and about **8% seek medical care** during or after travel; **fever** is the main reason for post-travel hospital admission. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**GeoSentinel** surveillance shows **malaria** is the commonest specific cause of fever in travellers returning from **sub-Saharan Africa**, **dengue** from **South-East Asia and Latin America**, and **enteric fever** from **South Asia (India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal)**. [GeoSentinel 2023]`,`India is both a **source** (over 2.5 crore outbound departures a year, about **1.75 lakh Hajj pilgrims** annually and large migrant worker flows to the Gulf and Africa) and a **destination** (medical tourism, pilgrimage) - so Indian physicians see both pre-travel and returning-traveller problems. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**VFR travellers** (visiting friends and relatives) - such as Indians settled abroad returning home - have the **highest risk** of malaria and typhoid because they stay longer, live locally and rarely take prophylaxis. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`Most deaths in travellers are from **cardiovascular disease and injuries (road traffic accidents, drowning)**, not infection - pre-travel advice covers these too. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Risk factors and risk assessment`,points:[`**Destination** (country, rural vs urban, altitude), **season** (monsoon - dengue, leptospirosis; dry season - meningococcal belt), **duration** (over 1 month raises risk of rabies, JE, TB) and **accommodation**. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Activities**: trekking (altitude, rabies), freshwater swimming (**schistosomiasis**, leptospirosis), caving (histoplasmosis, rabies), animal contact, sexual contact, healthcare work, mass gatherings (Hajj, Kumbh Mela). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Host factors**: age, **pregnancy**, immunosuppression, asplenia, chronic disease (diabetes, heart, lung, kidney), previous vaccines, drug allergies and **G6PD status** (primaquine, tafenoquine). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Time before departure**: ideally **4-8 weeks** to complete vaccine series, but a last-minute traveller still benefits from accelerated schedules and advice. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Drug interactions**: antimalarials with warfarin, antiepileptics, ART and QT-prolonging drugs; travellers on insulin need a time-zone plan. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - incubation periods drive the differential`,points:[`Each infection has a **characteristic incubation period** set by its replication cycle - **short (under 10 days)** for arboviruses and bacteria with direct invasion, **intermediate (10-21 days)** for malaria (liver stage of about 7-30 days) and typhoid, and **long (over 21 days)** for parasites with tissue migration or latency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**P. falciparum** has a liver stage of about **7-14 days** and causes most severe malaria within **1 month** of return (over 90% within 2 months); **P. vivax and P. ovale** form **hypnozoites** that relapse months to over a year later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Altitude illness**: hypobaric hypoxia triggers cerebral vasodilatation and increased capillary permeability (**AMS, HACE**) and uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (**HAPE**); **acetazolamide** (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor) causes a bicarbonate diuresis and metabolic acidosis that drives ventilation and speeds acclimatisation. [WMS 2024]`,`**Travellers' diarrhoea**: mostly **enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC)**, enteroaggregative E. coli, Campylobacter (dominant in South and SE Asia, often fluoroquinolone-resistant), Shigella, Salmonella and norovirus. [IDSA/ISTM 2017]`,`**Schistosoma** cercariae penetrate skin in fresh water; migration and egg-laying 3-8 weeks later trigger an immune-complex illness (**Katayama fever**) with fever, urticaria and **high eosinophilia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Deep vein thrombosis** risk on flights over **4 hours** comes from immobility, cabin hypoxia and dehydration (Virchow triad). [ACCP 2012]`]},{heading:`Classification - pre-travel vaccines and the yellow fever certificate`,points:[`**Routine** - bring up to date: Td/Tdap, MMR, influenza, varicella, hepatitis B, pneumococcal and COVID-19 as per age (see the Adult vaccination topic). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Required** (by International Health Regulations or the destination country): **yellow fever** (ICVP) for endemic countries or for entry from them; **MenACWY** for Hajj and Umrah (Saudi Arabia); **polio** for residents of or long-stay visitors to polio-affected countries. [WHO IHR 2005]`,`**Recommended** by risk: **hepatitis A** (2 doses inactivated, or single live attenuated in India), **typhoid conjugate vaccine** (single dose), **rabies PrEP** (day 0 and 7), **Japanese encephalitis** (rural Asia, over 1 month in season), **cholera** (outbreak areas, aid workers), **dengue (Qdenga)** in some countries, and tick-borne encephalitis (Europe). [WHO 2024]`,`**Rabies PrEP** (WHO 2018): **2 visits on day 0 and day 7**, IM 1 vial or ID 0.1 mL at 2 sites; after PrEP, a bite needs only **2 booster doses (day 0 and 3)** and **no RIG**. [WHO 2018]`,`**Yellow fever vaccine (17D live attenuated)**: **0.5 mL SC/IM single dose**, gives **lifelong protection** - since **July 2016** the **International Certificate of Vaccination or Prophylaxis (ICVP)** is valid **for life**, starting **10 days after** vaccination. [WHO IHR 2016]`,`**India** has no yellow fever but has *Aedes aegypti*, so it **requires a valid YF certificate** from travellers arriving within **6 days** of leaving or transiting a YF-risk country (Africa, South America); travellers without one are **quarantined for up to 6 days**. [MoHFW 2023]`,`YF vaccine is given only at **designated Yellow Fever Vaccination Centres** (APHOs, PHOs and selected government hospitals and institutes), which issue the stamped ICVP. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Contraindications**: infants under **6 months**, **thymus disorder or thymectomy**, severe immunosuppression (HIV with CD4 under 200, chemotherapy, high-dose steroids), and anaphylaxis to egg or gelatin. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Precautions**: age **60 and over** (higher risk of **viscerotropic disease, YEL-AVD**, and neurotropic disease, **YEL-AND**), infants 6-8 months, pregnancy and breastfeeding (give only if travel to a high-risk area is unavoidable). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`When the vaccine is contraindicated, issue a **medical waiver (exemption) letter** on official letterhead and complete the exemption section of the ICVP; the traveller must accept the infection risk. [WHO IHR 2005]`]},{heading:`Hajj, Umrah and mass gatherings`,points:[`**Saudi Arabia requires MenACWY** for Hajj and Umrah pilgrims and seasonal workers, given **at least 10 days before arrival**; a **conjugate vaccine is valid for 5 years** (polysaccharide 3 years where still accepted) - check the current Saudi Ministry of Health requirements each season. [Saudi MoH 2025]`,`The requirement followed **meningococcal W135 outbreaks after Hajj in 2000-2001**, with international spread to pilgrims' contacts. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pilgrims from **polio-affected countries** need documented polio vaccination; Saudi Arabia also recommends **seasonal influenza** and COVID-19 vaccination, especially for elderly pilgrims. [Saudi MoH 2025]`,`Main Hajj health problems: **respiratory infections** (spread in crowds), **heat stroke and dehydration** (summer Hajj, temperatures over 45 C), diarrhoea, trauma from crowd crushes and **decompensated chronic disease**; MERS-CoV is a concern with camel contact. [WHO 2024]`,`Advise elderly pilgrims with diabetes or heart disease to carry medicines, a **medical summary**, foot care for long walks barefoot, and to drink and shelter from heat. [WHO 2024]`,`The **Hajj Committee of India** organises pre-departure vaccination (MenACWY, seasonal influenza) at state Hajj camps. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Malaria prevention - the ABCD`,points:[`**A - Awareness** of risk, **B - Bite avoidance**, **C - Chemoprophylaxis**, **D - prompt Diagnosis** and treatment of any fever during or up to a year after travel. [UKHSA 2024]`,`**Bite avoidance**: **DEET 20-50%** (safe in pregnancy and children over 2 months), picaridin or IR3535 repellents, permethrin-treated clothes, long sleeves at dusk and **long-lasting insecticidal nets**. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Atovaquone-proguanil** 250/100 mg one tablet daily, start **1-2 days before**, continue **7 days after** - best for short trips; avoid in severe CKD (CrCl under 30). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Doxycycline 100 mg daily**, start **1-2 days before**, continue **4 weeks after** - cheap, also protects against leptospirosis and rickettsia; not in pregnancy or under 8 years. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Mefloquine 250 mg weekly**, start **2-3 weeks before**, continue **4 weeks after** - for long stays and pregnancy (all trimesters); contraindicated with psychiatric illness, seizures or cardiac conduction disease. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Chloroquine 300 mg base (500 mg salt) weekly**, 1-2 weeks before and 4 weeks after - only for **chloroquine-sensitive areas** (Central America west of Panama canal, Haiti); not useful for Africa or most of Asia. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Primaquine 30 mg base daily** (1-2 days before, 7 days after) or **tafenoquine 200 mg weekly** (after a loading dose) are options in **G6PD-normal** travellers; primaquine 30 mg base daily for 14 days is used as **terminal prophylaxis** after heavy vivax exposure. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**India NCVBDC**: for travellers to high-risk areas within India - **doxycycline 100 mg daily for stays up to 6 weeks** (start 2 days before, continue 4 weeks after); **mefloquine 250 mg weekly for longer stays**. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Other pre-travel advice`,points:[`**Travellers' diarrhoea prevention**: 'boil it, cook it, peel it or forget it', bottled or treated water, hand hygiene; **antibiotic prophylaxis is not routine** (only for high-risk hosts such as IBD or immunosuppression). [IDSA/ISTM 2017]`,`**Self-treatment of travellers' diarrhoea**: ORS for all; **loperamide** for mild-moderate watery diarrhoea; for severe or dysentery - **azithromycin 1 g single dose or 500 mg daily for 3 days** (preferred in South and SE Asia); rifaximin 200 mg TDS for 3 days for non-invasive diarrhoea; avoid fluoroquinolones because of resistance and adverse effects. [IDSA/ISTM 2017]`,`**Altitude**: ascend gradually - above **3,000 m**, sleep no more than **500 m higher** each night and rest every 3-4 days; **acetazolamide 125 mg BD** from the day before ascent for moderate-high risk; **dexamethasone** is an alternative; **nifedipine** for HAPE prevention in those with prior HAPE. [WMS 2024]`,`**Flight-related DVT**: for flights over 4 hours, walk, flex calves and hydrate; **graduated compression stockings** (15-30 mmHg) for higher-risk travellers; LMWH only for very high risk. [ACCP 2012]`,`**Jet lag**: light exposure at the destination's daytime; **melatonin 0.5-5 mg** at local bedtime for flights across 5 or more time zones. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Sexual health, road safety, insurance and a travel kit** (ORS, antipyretic, antimalarials, prescription drugs in original packs, condoms, sunscreen). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Fever in the returning traveller - history`,points:[`**Exclude malaria first** in any febrile traveller from an endemic area within the last **3 months** (and up to a year for vivax) - **falciparum can kill within 24-48 hours**. [UKHSA 2024]`,`Take a **travel history**: exact itinerary with dates, rural or urban, **onset of fever relative to exposure**, prophylaxis (name, dose, adherence), vaccines and pre-travel advice. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Incubation under 10 days**: dengue, chikungunya, Zika, rickettsiae (African tick-bite fever), influenza, COVID-19, leptospirosis, traveller's diarrhoea pathogens, plague. [Harrison 22e]`,`**10-21 days**: **malaria**, **typhoid and paratyphoid**, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, acute HIV, brucellosis, African trypanosomiasis, viral haemorrhagic fevers (Lassa, Ebola up to 21 days). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Over 21 days**: **vivax/ovale malaria**, tuberculosis, hepatitis A, B and E, acute schistosomiasis (Katayama), amoebic liver abscess, **visceral leishmaniasis**, filariasis, rabies. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exposures**: fresh water (schistosomiasis, leptospirosis), insect and tick bites, animal bites, unpasteurised dairy (brucellosis), sexual contact (HIV, syphilis), raw food, healthcare contact (MDR organisms), sick contacts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viral haemorrhagic fever risk**: travel within **21 days** to an area with an active outbreak (Ebola, Marburg, Lassa, CCHF) plus contact with sick people, bodies or animals - **isolate first and call infection control** before taking samples. [UKHSA 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination in the returning traveller`,points:[`**Vital signs and sepsis screen**: hypotension, tachypnoea, confusion, jaundice and bleeding point to severe malaria, typhoid, leptospirosis, dengue, meningococcaemia or VHF. [UKHSA 2024]`,`**Rash**: maculopapular (dengue, chikungunya, Zika, acute HIV, rickettsiae, measles), petechial or purpuric (meningococcaemia, dengue, VHF), **rose spots** (typhoid), **eschar** (African tick-bite fever, scrub typhus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly**: malaria, typhoid, visceral leishmaniasis, brucellosis, acute schistosomiasis; **tender hepatomegaly**: amoebic liver abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Conjunctival suffusion and calf tenderness**: leptospirosis; **jaundice**: falciparum malaria, leptospirosis, hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neck stiffness or altered sensorium**: cerebral malaria, meningococcal meningitis, JE, rabies, African trypanosomiasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin lesions after travel**: **cutaneous larva migrans** (serpiginous itchy track from beach sand), myiasis, cutaneous leishmaniasis (painless ulcer), tungiasis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis by region`,points:[`**Sub-Saharan Africa**: falciparum malaria (top), African tick-bite fever, schistosomiasis, typhoid, Lassa/Ebola in outbreak areas, African trypanosomiasis (game parks). [GeoSentinel 2023]`,`**South Asia (including India)**: enteric fever, dengue, vivax and falciparum malaria, chikungunya, scrub typhus, hepatitis A and E, leptospirosis. [GeoSentinel 2023]`,`**South-East Asia**: dengue (top), chikungunya, malaria (border forests), leptospirosis, scrub typhus, **melioidosis**, JE. [GeoSentinel 2023]`,`**Latin America and Caribbean**: dengue, chikungunya, Zika, malaria (Amazon), leptospirosis, histoplasmosis (caves), yellow fever. [GeoSentinel 2023]`,`**Middle East**: brucellosis, MERS-CoV (camel exposure), meningococcal disease after Hajj, Q fever, CCHF. [GeoSentinel 2023]`,`**Non-travel causes** must still be considered: pneumonia, UTI, DVT/PE after a long flight, drug fever and influenza. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations in the returning traveller`,points:[`**Malaria smear (thick and thin) and RDT**: if negative, **repeat every 12-24 hours for a total of 3 sets** before excluding malaria; report parasitaemia percentage (**2% or more** in non-immunes raises concern; **over 10%** is severe in non-immunes per UK guidance). [UKHSA 2024]`,`**CBC**: thrombocytopenia (malaria, dengue, VHF, rickettsia), leucopenia (dengue, typhoid, malaria), **eosinophilia** (helminths - schistosomiasis, strongyloides, filariasis, Katayama), neutrophilia (bacterial sepsis, amoebic liver abscess, leptospirosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood cultures** (two sets) for enteric fever and bacteraemia; **LFT, RFT, glucose, lactate, urine**. [UKHSA 2024]`,`**Dengue NS1 (days 1-5) and IgM (from day 5)**, chikungunya and Zika PCR/IgM, leptospira IgM/PCR, scrub typhus IgM, **HIV test** (acute retroviral syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray** (pneumonia, TB), **ultrasound abdomen** (liver abscess, splenomegaly), stool microscopy and PCR, **schistosoma serology** (at least 6-12 weeks after exposure). [Harrison 22e]`,`**VHF suspected**: only **essential tests** in a designated lab with biosafety precautions; send samples to the **National Institute of Virology, Pune** (BSL-4) via the state surveillance unit. [ICMR-NIV 2024]`]},{heading:`Management - OPD and IPD`,points:[`**Uncomplicated falciparum malaria**: **artemether-lumefantrine** (20/120 mg, 4 tablets twice daily for 3 days in adults) or the Indian **ACT-SP** (artesunate 4 mg/kg daily for 3 days plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine on day 1) - NE India uses artemether-lumefantrine; details in the Malaria topic. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Severe malaria** (any WHO criterion, or parasitaemia over 2-5% in non-immune travellers): **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours then daily**, admit to HDU. [WHO 2025]`,`**Admit**: suspected falciparum malaria in a non-immune traveller (many centres admit all), sepsis, bleeding, altered sensorium, jaundice, respiratory distress, AKI, pregnancy, extremes of age, or **suspected VHF** (isolation room). [UKHSA 2024]`,`**Suspected enteric fever from South Asia**: blood culture first, then **azithromycin 500 mg-1 g daily for 7 days** (uncomplicated) or **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily** (severe); fluoroquinolones fail because of resistance; **XDR typhoid** from Pakistan needs **meropenem** (± azithromycin). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Empirical doxycycline 100 mg BD** when rickettsial infection (eschar, tick bite) or leptospirosis is likely. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Paracetamol only** until dengue is excluded; no NSAIDs or aspirin. [WHO 2025]`,`**Notify** malaria, dengue, VHF, cholera, yellow fever and other IHR diseases to the district surveillance officer through **IDSP-IHIP**; VHF and yellow fever are also notified to WHO under IHR. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: advise against travel to **falciparum-malaria** and **Zika** areas if possible; **mefloquine** is the prophylaxis of choice in all trimesters; no doxycycline, primaquine or tafenoquine; YF vaccine only if travel is unavoidable. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Children**: weight-based atovaquone-proguanil (paediatric tablets from 5 kg) or mefloquine; **doxycycline only from 8 years**; DEET is safe over 2 months; ORS sachets for diarrhoea. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Immunocompromised and HIV**: avoid live vaccines (YF, MMR, Ty21a) if severely immunosuppressed; use **TCV and inactivated** vaccines; higher risk of severe travellers' diarrhoea - consider standby antibiotics. [IDSA 2013]`,`**Elderly and chronic disease**: YF precaution at 60+, heat stroke risk on Hajj, drug interactions, adequate supply of medicines and a letter. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Asplenia**: high risk of **severe falciparum malaria** and overwhelming sepsis - strict prophylaxis and a standby antibiotic (amoxicillin-clavulanate). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Diabetes on insulin**: adjust doses across time zones (longer eastward days need less, westward more), carry insulin in hand luggage with a doctor's letter, monitor glucose more often. [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Delayed diagnosis of falciparum malaria** is the main preventable cause of death in returning travellers - most deaths follow missed or late diagnosis in non-immune travellers. [UKHSA 2024]`,`**Typhoid**: intestinal perforation and bleeding in week 3; **dengue**: shock and bleeding in the critical phase (day 3-7). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Altitude**: untreated **HACE and HAPE** can kill within hours - **descend** at least 300-1,000 m, oxygen, dexamethasone 8 mg then 4 mg 6-hourly (HACE), nifedipine (HAPE). [WMS 2024]`,`**Post-infectious IBS** follows travellers' diarrhoea in about 5-10%. [IDSA/ISTM 2017]`,`**Imported outbreaks**: travellers can import measles, VHF, mpox, cholera and MDR bacteria (e.g. carbapenemase producers) into hospitals. [WHO 2024]`,`Refer **suspected VHF** (isolate, use full PPE, inform the state surveillance unit), severe malaria, and complex eosinophilia to an infectious diseases centre. [UKHSA 2024]`,`**Post-travel screening** of asymptomatic long-stay travellers: CBC for eosinophilia, schistosoma serology after fresh-water exposure in Africa, strongyloides serology, TB testing (IGRA) after over 3 months in high-incidence settings with exposure. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`Advise travellers that **any fever up to 1 year after return** from a malarious area needs a malaria test and that they must **mention their travel**. [UKHSA 2024]`,`After treated vivax malaria, give **primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days** (G6PD-normal) for radical cure. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Check adherence to post-travel **chemoprophylaxis** (4 weeks for doxycycline/mefloquine, 7 days for atovaquone-proguanil). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**IHR amendments (WHA77, June 2024)** introduce a **'pandemic emergency'** category and stronger national IHR authorities; they entered into force in **September 2025**. [WHO 2024]`,`**WMS 2024** altitude illness guideline: **acetazolamide 125 mg BD** for prevention (higher doses unnecessary), dexamethasone alternative; ibuprofen is a second-line option for AMS prevention. [WMS 2024]`,`**WHO SAGE 2023**: **TAK-003 (Qdenga)** dengue vaccine recommended for ages 6-16 in high-transmission settings; not licensed in India as of 2026; WHO 2025 dengue clinical guideline for arboviral disease management. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chikungunya vaccine**: live **VLA1553 (Ixchiq)** licensed in 2023 had safety restrictions in older adults in 2025 after serious adverse events; a virus-like-particle vaccine was licensed in 2025. [FDA 2025]`,`**CDC Yellow Book 2024** (and 2026 edition): updated malaria prophylaxis maps, tafenoquine for prophylaxis, and rabies PrEP 2-dose schedule. [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`**Saudi MoH** pilgrim requirements (updated each season): MenACWY conjugate preferred; influenza and COVID-19 recommended. [Saudi MoH 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'A man returns from Nigeria with fever after 12 days - first test?'** - malaria smear/RDT, repeated 3 times over 12-24 hours if negative. [UKHSA 2024]`,`**YF certificate**: valid **for life** (IHR 2016), from **10 days** after vaccination; India quarantines travellers without one for up to 6 days. [WHO IHR 2016]`,`**Hajj**: MenACWY at least **10 days** before arrival is mandatory. [Saudi MoH 2025]`,`**Prophylaxis continuation**: atovaquone-proguanil **7 days**, doxycycline and mefloquine **4 weeks** after leaving - the 'causal vs suppressive' explanation (A-P kills liver stages). [CDC Yellow Book 2024]`,`Common mistake: excluding malaria on **one negative smear**. [UKHSA 2024]`,`Common mistake: giving **ciprofloxacin** for traveller's diarrhoea or typhoid from South Asia - azithromycin is preferred because of quinolone resistance. [IDSA/ISTM 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - malaria chemoprophylaxis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Atovaquone-proguanil`,`250/100 mg oral daily with food`,`Start 1-2 days before, continue 7 days after`,`GI upset; avoid if CrCl under 30; costly`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral daily`,`Start 1-2 days before, continue 4 weeks after (NCVBDC: stays up to 6 weeks)`,`Pregnancy, under 8 years; photosensitivity, oesophagitis, vaginal candidiasis`],[`Mefloquine`,`250 mg oral weekly`,`Start 2-3 weeks before, continue 4 weeks after (NCVBDC: stays over 6 weeks)`,`Neuropsychiatric effects; contraindicated in psychiatric illness, seizures, conduction defects; safe in pregnancy`],[`Chloroquine`,`300 mg base (500 mg salt) oral weekly`,`1-2 weeks before, 4 weeks after`,`Only chloroquine-sensitive areas; retinopathy with long use`],[`Primaquine`,`30 mg base oral daily`,`1-2 days before, 7 days after (14 days as terminal prophylaxis)`,`G6PD deficiency (haemolysis), pregnancy`],[`Tafenoquine`,`200 mg oral daily x 3 days, then weekly`,`Continue 1 week after return`,`G6PD test mandatory; psychiatric history; pregnancy, breastfeeding`]]},{heading:`Incubation periods in the febrile returning traveller`,columns:[`Incubation`,`Infections`,`Key clue`],rows:[[`Under 10 days`,`Dengue, chikungunya, Zika, rickettsiae, influenza, COVID-19, leptospirosis (often 7-12)`,`Rash, arthralgia, eschar, respiratory symptoms`],[`10-21 days`,`Falciparum malaria, typhoid, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, acute HIV, VHF (up to 21 days), brucellosis`,`Malaria must be excluded first`],[`Over 21 days`,`Vivax/ovale malaria, TB, hepatitis A/B/E, schistosomiasis, amoebic liver abscess, visceral leishmaniasis`,`Eosinophilia, hepatomegaly, weight loss`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever by travel region`,columns:[`Region`,`Most likely`,`Do not miss`,`Specific test`],rows:[[`Sub-Saharan Africa`,`Falciparum malaria`,`VHF (outbreaks), meningococcaemia, schistosomiasis`,`Smear/RDT x 3; schistosoma serology`],[`South Asia`,`Enteric fever, dengue`,`Malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis`,`Blood culture, NS1/IgM, scrub IgM`],[`South-East Asia`,`Dengue`,`Malaria, melioidosis, JE`,`NS1/IgM, smear, blood culture`],[`Latin America`,`Dengue, chikungunya, Zika`,`Malaria (Amazon), yellow fever`,`PCR/IgM, smear`],[`Middle East (Hajj)`,`Respiratory viral infection`,`Meningococcal disease, MERS-CoV, brucellosis`,`Blood culture, MERS PCR`]]},{heading:`Travel vaccines - required and recommended`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Schedule`,`Who needs it`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Yellow fever (17D, live)`,`0.5 mL single dose`,`Travel to endemic Africa/South America; required for entry to India from these`,`Certificate valid for life from day 10; precaution at 60+`],[`MenACWY conjugate`,`0.5 mL IM single dose`,`Hajj/Umrah (mandatory), meningitis belt`,`At least 10 days before arrival; valid 5 years`],[`Typhoid conjugate`,`0.5 mL IM single dose`,`South Asia, VFR travellers`,`Not 100% - food and water care still needed`],[`Hepatitis A`,`Inactivated 0, 6-12 months or live single dose`,`Most developing countries`,`Most Indian adults already immune`],[`Rabies PrEP`,`Day 0 and 7 (IM or ID)`,`Long stay, rural, trekkers, animal workers, children`,`After PrEP: 2 boosters, no RIG`],[`Japanese encephalitis`,`Live SA-14-14-2 single, or inactivated 2 doses 28 days apart`,`Rural Asia over 1 month in season`,`Indian UIP vaccine in endemic districts`],[`Polio (OPV/IPV)`,`Booster dose`,`Residents/long-stay visitors of polio-affected countries`,`Within 4 weeks-12 months before departure`]]},{heading:`Travellers' diarrhoea - severity and treatment (IDSA/ISTM 2017)`,columns:[`Severity`,`Definition`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Tolerable, does not interfere with activities`,`ORS, loperamide or bismuth; no antibiotic`],[`Moderate`,`Distressing, interferes with activities`,`Loperamide; consider azithromycin 1 g single dose or rifaximin`],[`Severe or dysentery`,`Incapacitating, or blood in stool, high fever`,`Azithromycin 1 g single or 500 mg daily x 3 days; avoid loperamide alone in dysentery`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever within 3 months of return from a malarious area with any sign of severity - severe falciparum malaria until proven otherwise`,`Fever with bleeding after travel to an area with an active Ebola, Marburg, Lassa or CCHF outbreak within 21 days - isolate now`,`Fever with petechial or purpuric rash after Hajj - meningococcaemia, give antibiotic immediately`,`Altered sensorium, jaundice, oliguria or respiratory distress in a returning traveller`,`Headache, ataxia or breathlessness at rest at altitude - HACE/HAPE, descend immediately`,`Bloody diarrhoea with high fever or dehydration in a traveller`,`Fever in a pregnant or asplenic returning traveller`],pearls:[`**Malaria first, malaria second, malaria third** - three negative smears over 12-24 hours before you exclude it.`,`Malaria prevention **ABCD**: Awareness, Bite avoidance, Chemoprophylaxis, Diagnosis.`,`Atovaquone-proguanil stops **7 days** after return (causal prophylaxis); doxycycline and mefloquine continue **4 weeks**.`,`YF certificate valid **for life**, from **10 days** after the dose.`,`**Hajj = MenACWY** at least 10 days before arrival.`,`Fever beginning **under 7 days** after first entering a malarious area is unlikely to be malaria - but test anyway; exposure dates are often wrong.`,`**VFR travellers** carry the highest risk of malaria and typhoid.`,`Eosinophilia after African fresh-water exposure = **schistosomiasis** until proven otherwise.`,`Azithromycin, not ciprofloxacin, for traveller's diarrhoea from South Asia.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-q1`,stem:`A 35-year-old engineer returned from Ghana 10 days ago and now has fever and headache. He took no prophylaxis. One malaria smear is negative. The most appropriate next step is:`,options:[`Exclude malaria and start treatment for typhoid`,`Send dengue IgM only`,`Discharge with paracetamol and review in a week`,`Repeat smear and RDT every 12-24 hours for a total of three sets`],answer:3,explanation:`A single negative smear cannot exclude falciparum malaria, which can kill within 48 hours; three sets over 12-24 hours are needed. Treating as typhoid, testing only for dengue, or discharging him all risk missing the commonest and deadliest cause of fever after travel to sub-Saharan Africa.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-q2`,stem:`A traveller arriving in India from Nigeria without a yellow fever vaccination certificate should be:`,options:[`Refused entry and deported`,`Quarantined for up to 6 days`,`Vaccinated at the airport and allowed to proceed`,`Allowed entry since the certificate is optional`],answer:1,explanation:`India has Aedes aegypti but no yellow fever, so arrivals from YF-risk countries without a valid certificate are quarantined for up to 6 days, the longest incubation period. Vaccination on arrival does not protect in time, and deportation or free entry is not the rule under India's IHR implementation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman, 14 weeks pregnant, must travel to rural Odisha for 3 months. Which malaria chemoprophylaxis is most appropriate?`,options:[`Mefloquine 250 mg weekly`,`Doxycycline 100 mg daily`,`Primaquine 30 mg base daily`,`Tafenoquine 200 mg weekly`],answer:0,explanation:`Mefloquine is safe in all trimesters of pregnancy and suits a stay longer than 6 weeks, matching NCVBDC advice. Doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy, and primaquine and tafenoquine can cause haemolysis in a G6PD-deficient fetus, so they are avoided in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-q4`,stem:`A man returned from a lake holiday in Malawi 5 weeks ago with fever, urticaria, dry cough and eosinophil count of 3,500/microlitre. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Falciparum malaria`,`Typhoid fever`,`Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever)`,`Dengue fever`],answer:2,explanation:`Fresh-water exposure in Africa followed 3-8 weeks later by fever, urticaria, cough and marked eosinophilia is Katayama fever from schistosome migration. Malaria, typhoid and dengue do not cause high eosinophilia; typhoid and dengue more often cause leucopenia, and dengue has a much shorter incubation period.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-q5`,stem:`An Indian pilgrim is preparing for Hajj. Which vaccination is mandatory for the Saudi visa?`,options:[`Typhoid conjugate vaccine`,`Hepatitis A vaccine`,`Yellow fever vaccine`,`Quadrivalent meningococcal (ACWY) vaccine`],answer:3,explanation:`Saudi Arabia requires MenACWY for Hajj and Umrah, given at least 10 days before arrival, after W135 outbreaks among pilgrims. Typhoid and hepatitis A vaccines are recommended but not required, and yellow fever is required only for travellers coming from YF-risk countries.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the approach to a patient with fever returning from international travel. Add a note on malaria chemoprophylaxis.`,openingLines:[`Fever in the returning traveller is a medical emergency until falciparum malaria is excluded, because it can kill within 24-48 hours of symptom onset in a non-immune person.`,`The diagnosis is narrowed by geography, incubation period, exposures and vaccination/prophylaxis history.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`GeoSentinel: malaria (Africa), dengue (SE Asia, Latin America), enteric fever (South Asia); VFR travellers at highest risk.`]},{heading:`History`,points:[`Itinerary, dates, rural/urban, prophylaxis adherence, vaccines, exposures (fresh water, insects, animals, sex, food), sick contacts, VHF risk within 21 days.`,`Incubation: under 10 days (dengue, rickettsia), 10-21 days (malaria, typhoid), over 21 days (vivax, TB, hepatitis, schistosomiasis, amoebic abscess, VL).`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Sepsis screen; rash, eschar, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, conjunctival suffusion, neck stiffness.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Malaria smear/RDT x 3 over 12-24 hours; CBC (eosinophilia), blood cultures, LFT, RFT, dengue NS1/IgM, HIV, CXR, ultrasound.`,`VHF suspected: isolate, essential tests only, NIV Pune.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Admit severe or falciparum; IV artesunate for severe malaria; azithromycin/ceftriaxone for enteric; doxycycline for rickettsia/lepto; paracetamol only; notify via IDSP-IHIP.`]},{heading:`Malaria chemoprophylaxis`,points:[`ABCD; atovaquone-proguanil daily (7 days after), doxycycline daily (4 weeks after), mefloquine weekly (pregnancy, long stay); NCVBDC: doxycycline up to 6 weeks, mefloquine longer.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: febrile returning traveller - VHF risk? - malaria smear x 3 - incubation-based differential`,`Table of incubation periods`],markSplit:[{part:`History and incubation periods`,marks:3},{part:`Examination and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management and notification`,marks:2},{part:`Chemoprophylaxis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`returning traveller`,`malaria`,`incubation period`,`chemoprophylaxis`,`GeoSentinel`]},{id:`infectious-fever-travel-medicine-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the yellow fever vaccine and the International Certificate of Vaccination.`,openingLines:[`Yellow fever vaccine is the live attenuated 17D strain, given as a single 0.5 mL dose that protects for life.`,`Under the International Health Regulations, the certificate (ICVP) is valid for life from 10 days after vaccination (since July 2016).`],answer:[{heading:`Vaccine`,points:[`17D live attenuated, 0.5 mL SC/IM single dose; seroconversion over 95% by day 10.`]},{heading:`Indian requirement`,points:[`Required from travellers arriving within 6 days from YF-risk countries; otherwise quarantine up to 6 days; given only at designated YF vaccination centres.`]},{heading:`Contraindications and precautions`,points:[`Under 6 months, thymus disease, severe immunosuppression, egg anaphylaxis; precautions at 60+, pregnancy, breastfeeding, infants 6-8 months.`,`Adverse events: YEL-AVD, YEL-AND; medical waiver letter if contraindicated.`]}],mustDraw:[`Box listing contraindications vs precautions`],markSplit:[{part:`Vaccine and certificate`,marks:2},{part:`Indian requirement`,marks:1},{part:`Contraindications and adverse events`,marks:2}],keywords:[`17D`,`ICVP`,`IHR`,`YEL-AVD`,`quarantine`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Health recommendations for international travel; Approach to the acutely ill infected febrile patient`,`CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2024 edition`,`UKHSA. Guidelines for malaria prevention in travellers from the UK, 2024; and UK malaria treatment guidelines`,`Riddle MS et al. Guidelines for the prevention and treatment of travelers' diarrhea (IDSA/ISTM), J Travel Med 2017`,`Luks AM et al. Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention, Diagnosis and Treatment of Acute Altitude Illness: 2024 Update`,`WHO. International Health Regulations (2005), third edition, and 2024 amendments`,`NCVBDC. National Drug Policy on Malaria and Guidelines for Diagnosis and Treatment of Malaria in India, 2023`]});var o=[];o.push({id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses`,title:`Herpes viruses: HSV, VZV, EBV and CMV`,oneLiner:`Human herpesviruses are **double-stranded DNA viruses that establish lifelong latency** after primary infection - **HSV and VZV in sensory ganglia**, **EBV in B cells**, **CMV in myeloid cells** - so disease is either a primary illness or a **reactivation when cell-mediated immunity falls**; the alpha-herpesviruses respond to **acyclovir/valacyclovir** (viral thymidine kinase), while CMV needs **ganciclovir/valganciclovir** or letermovir.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`HSV-1`,`HSV-2`,`herpes simplex`,`herpes labialis`,`genital herpes`,`HSV encephalitis`,`varicella`,`herpes zoster`,`shingles`,`Hutchinson sign`,`postherpetic neuralgia`,`Ramsay Hunt`,`infectious mononucleosis`,`Paul-Bunnell`,`Monospot`,`EBV VCA EBNA`,`cytomegalovirus`,`congenital CMV`,`acyclovir`,`valacyclovir`,`valganciclovir`,`letermovir`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`The **Herpesviridae** that infect humans are eight enveloped **double-stranded DNA viruses** (HHV-1 to HHV-8); the defining biological property is **latency with periodic reactivation** for the life of the host. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV-1** infects about **3.8 billion people under 50 (64%)** worldwide and **HSV-2** about **520 million aged 15-49 (13%)**; genital herpes from either type affects about **846 million** people aged 15-49. [WHO 2024]`,`In India, **HSV-2 is the commonest cause of genital ulcer disease** in STI clinics, having overtaken chancroid and syphilis; HSV-2 roughly **triples the risk of acquiring HIV**. [NACO STI/RTI 2024]`,`**Varicella** seroprevalence in Indian adults is lower than in temperate countries (about **70-85%** by early adulthood), so a substantial pool of **susceptible adults and pregnant women** develops severe adult chickenpox. [IAP 2023]`,`**Herpes zoster** has a lifetime risk of about **1 in 3**; incidence rises steeply after **50 years** (about 10 per 1000 person-years after 80) and is **15-25 times higher in HIV**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBV** seroprevalence exceeds **90% of adults**; in India most children are infected silently before 5 years, so classic **infectious mononucleosis (IM)** is seen mainly in adolescents and young adults of urban, higher-income families. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV** seroprevalence in Indian adults is **over 90%**, so most transplant recipients are R+; **congenital CMV** is the commonest congenital infection worldwide (about **0.5-1% of live births**) and the leading **non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HHV-6 and HHV-7** cause roseola infantum (exanthem subitum); **HHV-8** causes **Kaposi sarcoma**, primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease - mentioned here only to complete the family. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Virology, transmission and risk factors`,points:[`**Alpha-herpesvirinae** (HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV): short replication cycle, lytic in epithelium, **latency in sensory (dorsal root and cranial nerve) ganglia** - hence vesicles in a nerve distribution. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beta-herpesvirinae** (CMV, HHV-6, HHV-7): slow cycle, **cytomegaly with owl-eye inclusions**, latency in **monocytes, myeloid progenitors and endothelium** - hence transmission by blood, organs and marrow. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gamma-herpesvirinae** (EBV, HHV-8): **lymphotropic and oncogenic**; EBV enters B cells through **CD21 (complement receptor 2)** and stays latent in memory B cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transmission**: HSV by direct mucosal contact, often from **asymptomatic shedding** (most genital transmission); VZV by **airborne droplets and vesicle fluid** (secondary attack rate about 90% in household contacts); EBV by **saliva (kissing disease)**; CMV by saliva, urine, sex, breast milk, transplacental, blood and organs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zoster risk factors**: age over 50, **HIV**, haematological cancer, transplant, **high-dose steroids, JAK inhibitors** (tofacitinib, baricitinib), anti-TNF, diabetes and CKD. [ACIP 2022]`,`**CMV disease risk in transplant** is highest in **donor-positive/recipient-negative (D+/R-)** solid organ transplant and in **R+ allogeneic stem-cell transplant**; anti-thymocyte globulin and alemtuzumab raise risk further. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**HSV reactivation triggers**: fever (herpes febrilis), UV light, menstruation, stress, trauma, dental work and immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella incubation** is **10-21 days** (up to 28 days after VZIG); infectivity runs from **48 hours before the rash until all lesions are crusted** (usually 5-7 days). [Red Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and how the drugs work`,points:[`**Latency**: HSV and VZV travel retrograde along sensory axons to ganglia and express only **latency-associated transcripts (LAT)**; falling **T-cell immunity** allows reactivation and anterograde spread back to skin - explaining **dermatomal zoster** and recurrent lip or genital lesions at the same site. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zoster pain** comes from **haemorrhagic inflammation of the ganglion** and peripheral nerve; persistent central sensitisation and loss of large-fibre inhibition produce **postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)** with allodynia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ramsay Hunt syndrome** is VZV reactivation in the **geniculate ganglion**: lower motor neurone facial palsy, ear pain and vesicles in the external auditory canal, sometimes with vertigo and deafness (CN VIII). [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV encephalitis** (adults, nearly always HSV-1) is a **haemorrhagic necrotising encephalitis of the medial temporal and orbitofrontal lobes**, explaining olfactory hallucinations, personality change, aphasia and temporal-lobe seizures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IM is an immune disease**: EBV-infected B cells drive a massive **CD8 T-cell expansion** - the **atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells)**; polyclonal B-cell activation produces **heterophile antibodies** that agglutinate sheep and horse red cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acyclovir** is a guanosine analogue that is **monophosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase** (so it is active only in infected cells), then converted to the triphosphate, which **inhibits viral DNA polymerase and terminates the chain**; oral bioavailability is only **15-30%**, which is why **valacyclovir** (prodrug, about 55%) allows 2-3 doses a day. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ganciclovir** is phosphorylated by the CMV **UL97 kinase**; resistance arises from **UL97 or UL54 (polymerase)** mutations; **foscarnet** acts directly on the polymerase and needs no activation, so it works against TK-deficient HSV and UL97-mutant CMV. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Letermovir** blocks the **CMV terminase complex (UL56)** and is not myelotoxic but has no activity against HSV/VZV (add acyclovir); **maribavir** inhibits UL97 and is used for refractory or resistant CMV - it **antagonises ganciclovir**, so never co-prescribe. [ECIL 2019]`,`**Why CMV causes graft and marrow damage**: CMV is immunomodulatory - it raises risk of **acute rejection, opportunistic infections (Pneumocystis, fungi), EBV-PTLD** and graft loss (the **indirect effects**). [AST IDCOP 2019]`]},{heading:`Classification of clinical syndromes`,points:[`**HSV**: primary gingivostomatitis, herpes labialis, **genital herpes** (first-episode primary, first-episode non-primary, recurrent), herpetic whitlow, **herpes gladiatorum**, keratitis, **eczema herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption)**, encephalitis, meningitis (HSV-2, Mollaret), neonatal herpes and disseminated disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonatal HSV** is classified as **SEM (skin, eye, mouth)**, **CNS disease** and **disseminated disease** - the class decides the duration of IV acyclovir (14 versus 21 days). [Red Book 2024]`,`**VZV**: primary varicella, **herpes zoster** (dermatomal, multi-dermatomal, disseminated - over 20 vesicles outside the primary and adjacent dermatomes), **herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO)**, Ramsay Hunt, **zoster sine herpete**, and VZV vasculopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBV**: IM, chronic active EBV, **EBV-HLH**, oral hairy leukoplakia, and EBV-associated cancers - **Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, NK/T-cell lymphoma, PTLD**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV in transplant**: **CMV infection** (virus detected by PCR) versus **CMV disease** - either **CMV syndrome** (fever, malaise, cytopenia plus DNAemia) or **tissue-invasive disease** (colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, retinitis); this distinction decides pre-emptive versus treatment doses. [CMV Drug Development Forum 2017]`,`**Congenital CMV** is classified as **symptomatic** (moderate-severe: microcephaly, chorioretinitis, CNS calcification, hepatosplenomegaly, petechiae) versus **asymptomatic or isolated SNHL** - only moderate-severe disease gets valganciclovir. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - HSV`,points:[`**Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis** in a child: high fever, drooling, painful **vesicles and ulcers on gums, tongue and palate**, cervical nodes; **gingivitis** distinguishes it from herpangina (posterior pharynx only). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpes labialis**: prodrome of **tingling or burning for 6-24 hours**, then grouped vesicles at the vermilion border that crust in 7-10 days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**First-episode primary genital herpes**: multiple **painful grouped vesicles that ulcerate**, bilateral tender inguinal nodes, fever, dysuria (may cause **urinary retention** from sacral radiculitis) and aseptic meningitis; lasts **2-3 weeks** untreated. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Recurrent genital herpes** is milder, unilateral, shorter (**5-10 days**), with prodrome; HSV-2 recurs far more often genitally (about **4 per year**) than HSV-1 (under 1 per year). [CDC STI 2021]`,`**HSV keratitis**: red painful eye, photophobia, **dendritic ulcer staining with fluorescein**; repeated stromal episodes are a major cause of **corneal blindness**; topical steroids on an epithelial dendrite cause a **geographic ulcer**. [AAO 2023]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: acute fever, headache, **altered behaviour, aphasia, focal seizures** over days; untreated mortality is about **70%**, falling to about **20%** with early acyclovir. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eczema herpeticum**: monomorphic **punched-out erosions** on atopic or burned skin with fever - a dermatological emergency needing systemic acyclovir. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - VZV (varicella and zoster)`,points:[`**Varicella**: 1-2 days of mild prodrome (more in adults), then an intensely itchy rash that **starts on the trunk and spreads centrifugally**, evolving macule-papule-**dewdrop vesicle on a rose petal**-pustule-crust in crops, so **lesions of all stages coexist**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adult varicella** is more severe: **varicella pneumonia** in 1 in 400 adults (higher in **smokers and pregnancy**), presenting on days 3-5 with cough, breathlessness and hypoxia. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Herpes zoster**: **prodromal pain, burning or itch for 2-5 days** (mistaken for MI, renal colic or cholecystitis), then a **unilateral dermatomal vesicular band that does not cross the midline**; thoracic dermatomes are commonest (over 50%), then ophthalmic V1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO)**: V1 rash on forehead and upper eyelid; **Hutchinson sign** (vesicles on the **tip or side of the nose**, nasociliary branch) predicts eye involvement - conjunctivitis, keratitis, **anterior uveitis**, raised intraocular pressure, retinitis. [AAO 2023]`,`**Zoster in a young adult** or multi-dermatomal zoster is a **marker of HIV** - offer an HIV test. [NACO 2021]`,`**Disseminated zoster** or **visceral VZV** (pneumonitis, hepatitis, encephalitis) occurs in transplant, haematological cancer and advanced HIV; it may present as severe abdominal pain before any rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VZV vasculopathy**: stroke or TIA weeks to months after HZO (contralateral hemiparesis from carotid/MCA involvement); risk of stroke is raised for about **3 months after zoster**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - EBV and CMV`,points:[`**Infectious mononucleosis** (age 15-24): **fever, exudative pharyngitis, posterior and anterior cervical lymphadenopathy** (the triad), marked fatigue, **splenomegaly in about 50%**, hepatomegaly, **palatal petechiae** and **eyelid oedema (Hoagland sign)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`An **amoxicillin/ampicillin rash** - itchy maculopapular, from immune complexes, not true penicillin allergy - follows in about **30%** (older series up to 90%); it should **not be labelled as penicillin allergy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IM complications**: **splenic rupture (0.1-0.5%, usually in the first 3 weeks)**, airway obstruction from tonsillar hypertrophy, autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (cold, anti-i), thrombocytopenia, hepatitis, Guillain-Barre, encephalitis, and **EBV-HLH**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fatigue** after IM can last for months; IM is a recognised trigger of **post-infectious fatigue and ME/CFS** (see the Post-acute infection syndromes topic). [NICE NG206 2021]`,`**CMV mononucleosis** in the immunocompetent: prolonged fever (often 2-3 weeks), malaise, **mild hepatitis**, atypical lymphocytes, but **little pharyngitis or lymphadenopathy** and a **negative heterophile test**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV in transplant** peaks **1-6 months after transplant** (or after stopping prophylaxis - **late-onset CMV**): fever, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, raised transaminases, colitis with diarrhoea and bleeding, pneumonitis (worst in lung and stem-cell transplant). [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**CMV in advanced HIV (CD4 under 50)**: **retinitis** (floaters, field loss; fundus shows **haemorrhagic necrosis - 'pizza pie' or 'cottage cheese and ketchup'**), oesophagitis with large shallow ulcers, colitis, polyradiculopathy and adrenalitis. [NACO 2021]`,`**Congenital CMV** at birth: **SGA, microcephaly, 'blueberry muffin' purpura, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, chorioretinitis, periventricular calcification**; about **10-15% are symptomatic** and up to **10-15% of asymptomatic** babies develop later hearing loss. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base** in a dermatome = zoster; in the same site repeatedly = recurrent HSV (**zosteriform HSV** can mimic zoster but recurs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hutchinson sign** (nasal tip vesicles) means about a **2-4-fold higher risk of ocular involvement** - examine with fluorescein and refer to ophthalmology the same day. [AAO 2023]`,`**Polymorphic rash with lesions in all stages, centripetal (trunk-heavy) distribution, and involvement of scalp and mucosa** = varicella; contrast smallpox/mpox with **lesions in one stage, centrifugal, on palms and soles**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Posterior cervical nodes plus palatal petechiae plus splenomegaly** in a febrile young adult = IM until proved otherwise; **gently palpate the spleen** and avoid vigorous examination. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Facial palsy with vesicles in the ear canal or on the palate** = Ramsay Hunt; it has a **worse facial recovery than Bell palsy** (about 50-70% full recovery). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpetic whitlow** - painful vesicles on a fingertip - must **not be incised** (risk of spread and secondary infection). [Harrison 22e]`,`In an immunosuppressed patient with fever, **look at the fundus** (CMV retinitis), mouth (oral hairy leukoplakia - EBV, a white non-removable lateral tongue lesion) and whole skin (disseminated zoster). [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Genital ulcer**: herpes (multiple painful vesicles/shallow ulcers), **primary syphilis** (single painless indurated chancre), **chancroid** (painful ragged undermined ulcer, suppurative bubo), LGV and donovanosis - treat syndromically as per NACO (see the STI topic). [NACO STI/RTI 2024]`,`**Zoster before the rash** mimics angina, biliary or renal colic, appendicitis and radiculopathy - a burning unilateral dermatomal pain with hyperaesthesia in an older adult should prompt daily skin checks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella versus mpox**: mpox has **prominent lymphadenopathy**, deep-seated **monomorphic** lesions, palms and soles, and anogenital lesions; varicella is pleomorphic, itchy, without marked nodes. [WHO 2024]`,`**Varicella versus hand-foot-mouth disease**: HFMD has oval grey vesicles on palms, soles and buttocks with oral ulcers in a young child, without truncal crops. [IAP 2023]`,`**IM-like illness (heterophile-negative)**: **CMV, acute HIV seroconversion, toxoplasmosis**, HHV-6, adenovirus, streptococcal pharyngitis, and drug reactions (DRESS); **always consider acute HIV** - order HIV antigen/antibody test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exudative pharyngitis**: group A streptococcus (no splenomegaly, no posterior nodes, Centor score), **diphtheria** (grey adherent membrane that bleeds on removal, bull neck) - critical in unvaccinated Indian children. [IAP 2023]`,`**HSV encephalitis** versus Japanese encephalitis/AES, scrub typhus meningoencephalitis, autoimmune (anti-NMDAR) encephalitis - **start acyclovir empirically** in any encephalitis while CSF PCR is pending. [BIA/ABN 2012]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Most HSV and zoster is a **clinical diagnosis**; when needed, **swab the base of an unroofed vesicle for HSV/VZV PCR** - more sensitive than viral culture or a **Tzanck smear** (multinucleated giant cells, cannot tell HSV from VZV). [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Type-specific HSV serology** (gG-based IgG) helps only in recurrent undiagnosed genital symptoms or a partner of an HSV-2-positive person; **HSV IgM is unreliable** and should not be ordered. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: **CSF PCR for HSV** (sensitivity about 96%, specificity 99%; may be negative in the first 72 hours - repeat if suspicion persists); CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, raised protein, red cells; **MRI FLAIR temporal-lobe hyperintensity**; EEG periodic lateralised discharges. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**IM**: CBC with **lymphocytes 50% or more and atypical lymphocytes 10% or more** (Hoagland), raised ALT in 80-90%; **heterophile antibody (Monospot/Paul-Bunnell)** sensitivity about **85%** but **false negative in 25% in week 1** and in most children under 4. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBV-specific serology**: **VCA IgM positive with EBNA negative = acute infection**; VCA IgG plus EBNA IgG = past infection (EBNA appears **6-12 weeks** after onset); EBV PCR in blood is used for PTLD monitoring and EBV-HLH, not for routine IM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV in the immunocompromised**: quantitative **CMV DNA PCR in plasma** (reported in **IU/mL** to the WHO standard) for diagnosis, pre-emptive thresholds and response; tissue-invasive disease needs **biopsy with immunohistochemistry** (owl-eye inclusions) - colitis can occur with low or undetectable blood PCR. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**CMV in pregnancy**: **CMV IgM is not diagnostic** (persists months, false positives); use **IgG seroconversion** or **IgM positive with low IgG avidity** (under 30% suggests primary infection within 3 months); confirm fetal infection by **amniotic fluid PCR** after **20-21 weeks and 6-8 weeks after maternal infection**. [ECCI 2023]`,`**Congenital CMV**: **saliva or urine CMV PCR within the first 21 days** of life (after 21 days it cannot distinguish congenital from postnatal infection; dried blood spot PCR can be used retrospectively); a positive saliva test is confirmed on urine. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**First-episode genital herpes**: **acyclovir 400 mg oral three times daily** or **valacyclovir 1 g twice daily** for **7-10 days** (extend if not healed); saline bathing, analgesia, lidocaine gel, pass urine in a warm bath; screen for HIV and syphilis. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Recurrent genital herpes (episodic)**: start within 24 hours of prodrome - **valacyclovir 500 mg twice daily for 3 days** or **1 g once daily for 5 days**, or **acyclovir 800 mg twice daily for 5 days** or 800 mg three times daily for 2 days. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Suppressive therapy** for 6 or more recurrences a year (or to cut transmission): **valacyclovir 500 mg once daily** (1 g once daily if 10 or more recurrences) or **acyclovir 400 mg twice daily**; reduces recurrences by 70-80% and transmission by about 50%; review annually. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**Herpes labialis**: **valacyclovir 2 g twice daily for 1 day** at prodrome; topical acyclovir cream adds little. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herpes zoster**: within **72 hours of rash onset** (or later if new vesicles are still forming, eye involvement or immunosuppression) - **valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days**, **acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days** or famciclovir 500 mg three times daily for 7 days; antivirals speed healing and reduce acute pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zoster pain**: paracetamol plus NSAID, then tramadol or a short opioid for severe acute pain; adding **gabapentin or amitriptyline** early is reasonable; **oral steroids do not prevent PHN** and are not routine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella in a healthy child**: supportive - paracetamol (**never aspirin - Reye syndrome**; avoid NSAIDs - linked to invasive group A streptococcal infection), calamine, antihistamine, nails cut; **acyclovir 20 mg/kg (max 800 mg) four times daily for 5 days** for those over 12 years, adolescents, secondary household cases and chronic skin or lung disease. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Varicella in an adult**: **acyclovir 800 mg five times daily or valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days** if started **within 24 hours of rash**; exclude from work or school **until all lesions crust**. [Red Book 2024]`,`**IM**: rest, fluids, paracetamol, saline gargles; **no amoxicillin**; **avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for at least 3-4 weeks** (longer if spleen still palpable); **acyclovir and routine steroids are not indicated**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: suspected encephalitis, HZO with eye signs, disseminated or visceral zoster, varicella pneumonia, varicella or primary HSV in pregnancy with complications, any herpes infection in a neonate or immunocompromised host, IM with airway obstruction or suspected splenic rupture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly for 14-21 days** (use ideal body weight in obesity), started **within 6 hours of arrival** without waiting for MRI or PCR; repeat CSF PCR at end of therapy in complicated cases. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**Acyclovir safety**: infuse over **1 hour** with good **hydration** (crystal nephropathy); **reduce dose for CrCl under 50 mL/min**; watch for neurotoxicity (confusion, tremor, myoclonus) in elderly and CKD patients - easily mistaken for worsening encephalitis. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Severe varicella or disseminated zoster** (pneumonia, encephalitis, hepatitis, immunocompromised): **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** for 7-10 days, switch to oral when afebrile and no new lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HZO**: oral valacyclovir/acyclovir for **7-10 days** plus **same-day ophthalmology**; topical corticosteroids for stromal keratitis or uveitis **only under ophthalmologist** with antiviral cover; cycloplegics for pain. [AAO 2023]`,`**IM with impending airway obstruction**: **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day (or dexamethasone)** tapered over 1-2 weeks, ENT review; steroids also for severe haemolytic anaemia or thrombocytopenia. **Splenic rupture** (left upper quadrant pain, shoulder tip pain, shock) - resuscitate and call surgery; non-operative management in stable cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV disease treatment**: **ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly** or **valganciclovir 900 mg oral twice daily** (non-severe disease) for **at least 2 weeks and until 1-2 negative PCRs a week apart**, then consider secondary prophylaxis; renal dose adjustment is mandatory; monitor CBC twice weekly (**neutropenia**). [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Resistant or refractory CMV** (rising viral load after 2 weeks of adequate therapy): send **UL97/UL54 genotyping**, reduce immunosuppression, switch to **foscarnet 90 mg/kg IV 12-hourly** (nephrotoxic, Ca/Mg/K loss, genital ulcers) or **maribavir 400 mg twice daily**. [AST IDCOP 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy - genital herpes**: primary infection in the **third trimester** carries a neonatal transmission risk of **30-50%** versus **under 1-3%** for recurrent disease; give acyclovir for the episode, **suppressive acyclovir 400 mg three times daily from 36 weeks**, and offer **caesarean section** if primary lesions or prodrome are present at labour (or first episode within 6 weeks of delivery). [BASHH/RCOG 2014]`,`**Pregnancy - varicella**: give **oral acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days** if presenting **within 24 hours of rash** (from 20 weeks; consider before 20 weeks); admit if chest symptoms, haemorrhagic rash or neurological signs; **congenital varicella syndrome** risk is about **0.4% before 13 weeks and 2% at 13-20 weeks**. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Neonatal varicella**: maternal rash **from 5 days before to 2 days after delivery** exposes the baby without protective antibody (mortality up to 30% untreated) - give **VZIG** to the baby and IV acyclovir if lesions appear. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Varicella exposure in a susceptible pregnant woman or immunocompromised person**: check VZV IgG; if negative, give **VZIG within 10 days** of exposure, or **oral acyclovir/valacyclovir from day 7 to day 14 after exposure** (preferred by UKHSA since 2019; guidance reissued 2023). [UKHSA Green Book 2023]`,`**Congenital CMV**: **oral valganciclovir 16 mg/kg twice daily for 6 months**, started within the first month, for **moderate to severe symptomatic disease**; improves hearing and neurodevelopmental outcome; monitor neutrophils weekly for 6 weeks then monthly. [ECCI 2023]`,`**Primary maternal CMV in the periconception period or first trimester**: **valacyclovir 8 g/day (2 g four times daily)** until amniocentesis reduces vertical transmission (Shahar-Nissan RCT 2020); **CMV hyperimmune globulin is not recommended** routinely (negative RCTs). [ECCI 2023]`,`**Transplant**: **antiviral prophylaxis** (valganciclovir 900 mg once daily for 3-6 months; **6 months in kidney D+/R-**, 6-12 months in lung) or **pre-emptive therapy** (weekly CMV PCR for 12 weeks, treat above a centre threshold); **letermovir 480 mg daily** to day 100 (extendable to day 200) in **CMV R+ allogeneic HSCT** and an alternative to valganciclovir in **kidney D+/R-** (no myelotoxicity). [AST IDCOP 2019; FDA 2023]`,`**CKD**: acyclovir, valacyclovir, ganciclovir and valganciclovir are renally cleared - **dose by creatinine clearance**; valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for zoster becomes 1 g twice daily at CrCl 30-49 and 1 g daily at 10-29 mL/min. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**HIV**: zoster is treated as usual but for **7-10 days or until crusted**; CMV retinitis is treated with valganciclovir induction plus **early ART** (within 2 weeks), watching for immune recovery uveitis; acyclovir-resistant HSV needs foscarnet. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)** - pain persisting **90 days or more after rash onset** - affects about **10-18% of zoster patients**, rising to over 30% after 80; risk factors are **age over 50, severe acute pain, severe rash, prodromal pain and HZO**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PHN treatment ladder**: **5% lidocaine plaster** (up to 3 plasters, 12 hours on/12 off) for focal allodynia; **pregabalin 75 mg twice daily** (titrate to 150-300 mg twice daily) or **gabapentin 300 mg at night** titrated to 1800-3600 mg/day; **amitriptyline 10 mg at night** titrated to 25-75 mg (avoid in elderly with cardiac disease; nortriptyline preferred); **capsaicin 8% patch**; tramadol as add-on. [NICE CG173 2020]`,`**Varicella complications**: **secondary bacterial skin infection** (staphylococcal, invasive group A streptococcus, necrotising fasciitis), pneumonia, **acute cerebellar ataxia** (1 in 4000 children, benign), encephalitis, Reye syndrome (aspirin), purpura fulminans and hepatitis. [Red Book 2024]`,`**HZO** leads to chronic ocular disease in up to **50%** without antivirals - stromal keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy, glaucoma, cataract; acute retinal necrosis threatens sight in days. [AAO 2023]`,`**HSV encephalitis** survivors often have **memory loss, behaviour change and epilepsy**; about **25% develop anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis** within weeks (relapse with movement disorder) - test and treat with immunotherapy. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**EBV long-term**: **chronic active EBV**, **EBV-HLH** (high ferritin, cytopenias - see the HLH topic), PTLD in transplant, and a 4-fold higher risk of **Hodgkin lymphoma**; EBV infection is now considered a **near-necessary cause of multiple sclerosis** (32-fold risk after EBV; Bjornevik 2022). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital CMV outcome**: symptomatic infants have about **40-60%** long-term sequelae (SNHL, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability, epilepsy, visual loss); **hearing loss can be late-onset and progressive**, so audiology continues to 5-6 years. [ECCI 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer the same day**: suspected encephalitis, HZO with eye pain or red eye or Hutchinson sign, Ramsay Hunt (ENT), zoster or varicella in pregnancy or immunosuppression, eczema herpeticum, neonatal vesicles, IM with stridor or left upper quadrant pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Follow-up**: zoster - review at 1 week for healing and pain, and at **4-6 weeks** to detect early PHN; IM - repeat CBC and LFT in 2-4 weeks, clear for sport only when spleen is impalpable and at least 3-4 weeks have passed; HSV encephalitis - neuropsychology and seizure follow-up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV, Shingrix)**: two **0.5 mL IM doses 2-6 months apart** for **all adults 50 years and over** and adults **18 and over who are immunocompromised**; efficacy about **97% (50-69 years)** and **90% (70 and over)** against zoster and about 90% against PHN; available in India since **2023**. [ACIP 2022]`,`RZV can be given **after an episode of zoster** (once the rash has resolved) and to those who previously had live zoster vaccine; it is non-live and safe in immunosuppression; expect injection-site pain and 1-3 days of systemic reactogenicity. [ACIP 2022]`,`**Varicella vaccine** (live attenuated, Oka strain) is not in India's UIP; IAP recommends **2 doses at 15 months and 3-6 months later (or 4-6 years)**; susceptible adults, healthcare workers and women planning pregnancy get **2 doses 4-8 weeks apart**, avoiding pregnancy for 1 month after; **post-exposure vaccination within 3-5 days** prevents or modifies disease. [IAP 2023]`,`**HSV prevention**: condoms reduce HSV-2 transmission by about 30%, **abstain during lesions or prodrome**, daily suppressive valacyclovir in discordant couples; disclose to partners; **no licensed HSV vaccine** exists. [CDC STI 2021]`,`**CMV in pregnancy**: counsel all pregnant women on **hygiene** - hand-washing after nappy changes and contact with a toddler's saliva, not sharing cups, cutlery or kissing on the mouth - reduces seroconversion; routine universal CMV serology screening is not recommended in India. [ECCI 2023]`,`**Healthcare infection control**: varicella and disseminated zoster need **airborne plus contact precautions** (negative-pressure room if available); localised zoster in an immunocompetent patient needs **lesions covered and contact precautions**; only immune staff should care for them. [CDC 2007]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACIP 2022 (in force 2023-26)**: RZV extended to **immunocompromised adults aged 18 and over**; India licensed RZV in 2023 and API/ICMR adult-immunisation advice now lists it for adults 50 and over and those on immunosuppression. [ACIP 2022]`,`**ECCI 2023** (European Congenital CMV Initiative consensus): **valacyclovir 8 g/day** for primary maternal infection in the periconception period/first trimester; IgG avidity-based diagnosis; valganciclovir for 6 months for moderate-severe congenital CMV; hygiene counselling for all pregnant women. [ECCI 2023]`,`**FDA 2023**: **letermovir** approved for CMV prophylaxis in **adult kidney transplant D+/R-** (up to 200 days), after an RCT showed non-inferiority to valganciclovir with much less leucopenia; HSCT use extended to **day 200** in high-risk patients. [FDA 2023]`,`**WHO 2024 HSV estimates**: about **846 million** aged 15-49 live with genital herpes; WHO prioritises HSV vaccine development and lists HSV in the **Global Health Sector Strategy on STIs 2022-2030**. [WHO 2024]`,`**ZEDS (Zoster Eye Disease Study, 2024)**: suppressive **valacyclovir 1 g daily for 1 year** in HZO eye disease reduced flares of new or worsening disease over 18 months, though the 12-month primary end-point was not significant - use is now an ophthalmologist decision. [ZEDS 2024]`,`**UKHSA varicella PEP guidance (2023)**: confirms the switch from VZIG to **oral antivirals given days 7-14 after exposure** for most susceptible pregnant and immunosuppressed contacts; VZIG reserved for neonates and those who cannot take antivirals. [UKHSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic long question**: 'Herpes zoster - clinical features, complications and management including PHN and prevention' - write pathogenesis of latency, dermatome diagram, HZO with Hutchinson sign, antiviral doses, PHN ladder, RZV. [DNB pattern]`,`**Classic short notes**: Hutchinson sign; Ramsay Hunt syndrome; Monospot and EBV serology interpretation; congenital CMV; acyclovir mechanism and resistance; HSV encephalitis; varicella in pregnancy. [DNB pattern]`,`**Common mistake 1**: prescribing **acyclovir 400 mg** for zoster - VZV is about **10 times less sensitive** than HSV, so the zoster dose is **800 mg five times a day**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Common mistake 2**: treating a sore throat with amoxicillin in a young adult with posterior nodes - causes the **IM rash** and a false penicillin-allergy label for life. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake 3**: waiting for MRI or CSF PCR before starting **IV acyclovir** in encephalitis, and ordering **HSV IgM or CMV IgM alone** as diagnostic tests. [BIA/ABN 2012]`,`**Viva**: Why is acyclovir non-toxic to host cells? (needs viral TK for first phosphorylation). Why is letermovir not enough alone? (no HSV/VZV activity). Which CMV patient is highest risk? (**D+/R- SOT**). When is the heterophile test negative? (**week 1, children under 4, CMV/HIV/toxoplasma**). [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antiviral drugs for herpesviruses`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Acyclovir (HSV)`,`Genital first episode 400 mg oral TDS; recurrence 800 mg BD; suppression 400 mg BD`,`7-10 days; 5 days; continuous with annual review`,`Headache, nausea; crystal nephropathy with IV; reduce in CrCl under 50`],[`Acyclovir (VZV)`,`800 mg oral five times daily; child varicella 20 mg/kg (max 800 mg) QID`,`7 days (zoster, adult varicella); 5 days (child)`,`Poor oral bioavailability (15-30%) - adherence problem`],[`Acyclovir IV`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly over 1 h (ideal body weight); neonate 20 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`HSV encephalitis 14-21 days; neonatal 14 days SEM, 21 days CNS/disseminated`,`Hydrate; neurotoxicity in elderly/CKD; phlebitis`],[`Valacyclovir`,`Zoster 1 g TDS; first genital 1 g BD; recurrence 500 mg BD; suppression 500 mg-1 g OD; labialis 2 g BD`,`7 days; 7-10 days; 3 days; continuous; 1 day`,`Renal dose; TTP/HUS reported at 8 g/day in advanced HIV`],[`Famciclovir`,`Zoster 500 mg TDS; recurrent genital 1 g BD`,`7 days; 1 day`,`Headache; renal dose adjustment`],[`Ganciclovir`,`5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly (induction); 5 mg/kg OD (maintenance)`,`At least 2 weeks and until PCR negative`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia; teratogenic; renal dosing`],[`Valganciclovir`,`Treatment 900 mg oral BD; prophylaxis 900 mg OD; congenital CMV 16 mg/kg BD`,`2-3 weeks; 3-6 months (D+/R- kidney 6 months); 6 months`,`Leucopenia; take with food; renal dosing`],[`Letermovir`,`480 mg oral/IV OD (240 mg with ciclosporin)`,`To day 100 post-HSCT (up to 200); up to 200 days kidney D+/R-`,`No HSV/VZV cover - add acyclovir; raises tacrolimus, ciclosporin levels`],[`Foscarnet`,`90 mg/kg IV 12-hourly (induction)`,`Until resolution; for resistant HSV/CMV`,`Nephrotoxicity, low Ca/Mg/K/PO4, seizures, genital ulcers`],[`Maribavir`,`400 mg oral BD`,`8 weeks for refractory/resistant CMV`,`Taste disturbance; antagonises ganciclovir; not for CMV retinitis or CNS`]]},{heading:`Classification of human herpesviruses`,columns:[`Virus`,`Subfamily`,`Site of latency`,`Primary infection`,`Reactivation / key disease`],rows:[[`HSV-1 (HHV-1)`,`Alpha`,`Trigeminal ganglion`,`Gingivostomatitis`,`Herpes labialis, keratitis, encephalitis`],[`HSV-2 (HHV-2)`,`Alpha`,`Sacral ganglia`,`Primary genital herpes`,`Recurrent genital herpes, meningitis, neonatal herpes`],[`VZV (HHV-3)`,`Alpha`,`Dorsal root and cranial ganglia`,`Varicella`,`Herpes zoster, HZO, Ramsay Hunt, PHN`],[`EBV (HHV-4)`,`Gamma`,`Memory B cells`,`Infectious mononucleosis`,`Burkitt, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin, PTLD, hairy leukoplakia`],[`CMV (HHV-5)`,`Beta`,`Monocytes, myeloid progenitors`,`CMV mononucleosis, congenital CMV`,`Transplant CMV disease, HIV retinitis and colitis`],[`HHV-6 and HHV-7`,`Beta`,`T cells, monocytes`,`Roseola infantum`,`Encephalitis after HSCT, drug reaction (DRESS)`],[`HHV-8`,`Gamma`,`B cells, endothelium`,`Often silent`,`Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, Castleman`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of vesicular and mononucleosis-like illness`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Confirmatory test`],rows:[[`Varicella`,`Itchy pleomorphic crops, trunk-heavy, scalp involved`,`Clinical; vesicle VZV PCR`],[`Herpes zoster`,`Unilateral dermatomal band, prodromal pain, older adult`,`Clinical; VZV PCR if atypical`],[`Mpox`,`Prominent nodes, monomorphic deep lesions, palms/soles, anogenital`,`Lesion swab MPXV PCR`],[`Hand-foot-mouth disease`,`Young child, oral ulcers, oval vesicles on palms, soles, buttocks`,`Clinical; enterovirus PCR`],[`Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)`,`Exudative pharyngitis, posterior nodes, splenomegaly, atypical lymphocytes`,`Monospot; VCA IgM with EBNA negative`],[`CMV mononucleosis`,`Prolonged fever, mild hepatitis, little pharyngitis`,`CMV IgM with low IgG avidity; heterophile negative`],[`Acute HIV`,`Fever, rash, mucosal ulcers, risk exposure`,`HIV p24 antigen/antibody (4th generation) or viral load`],[`Streptococcal pharyngitis`,`Anterior nodes only, no splenomegaly, Centor 3-4`,`Rapid antigen test or throat culture`]]},{heading:`Key investigations and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Use`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`Vesicle swab HSV/VZV PCR`,`Atypical vesicles, genital ulcer, immunocompromised`,`Most sensitive; types HSV-1/2 and VZV; Tzanck cannot type`],[`CSF HSV PCR`,`Encephalitis`,`Sensitivity about 96%; may be negative in first 72 h - repeat`],[`Heterophile (Monospot)`,`Suspected IM`,`Sensitivity about 85%; negative in 25% in week 1 and in children under 4`],[`EBV VCA IgM / VCA IgG / EBNA IgG`,`Heterophile-negative IM`,`VCA IgM+ EBNA- acute; VCA IgG+ EBNA+ past`],[`CBC (Hoagland)`,`IM`,`Lymphocytes 50% or more with atypical lymphocytes 10% or more`],[`CMV IgG avidity`,`Pregnancy with positive IgM`,`Low avidity (under 30%) suggests primary infection in last 3 months`],[`Plasma CMV DNA PCR (IU/mL)`,`Transplant, HIV, treatment response`,`Trend matters; centre-specific pre-emptive threshold`],[`Neonatal saliva/urine CMV PCR`,`Suspected congenital CMV, failed hearing screen`,`Must be within first 21 days of life`]]},{heading:`Postherpetic neuralgia - drug ladder`,columns:[`Step`,`Drug and dose`,`Note`],rows:[[`Prevent`,`Antiviral within 72 h; RZV vaccine 50 years and over`,`Steroids do not prevent PHN`],[`Focal allodynia`,`Lidocaine 5% plaster, up to 3 for 12 h on/12 h off`,`Best tolerated in elderly`],[`First-line systemic`,`Pregabalin 75 mg BD to 300 mg BD, or gabapentin to 1800-3600 mg/day`,`Dizziness, sedation; renal dose`],[`First-line alternative`,`Amitriptyline or nortriptyline 10 mg at night to 25-75 mg`,`Anticholinergic; ECG in cardiac disease`],[`Second-line`,`Capsaicin 8% patch (specialist); tramadol add-on`,`Tramadol - serotonin syndrome with TCA/SSRI`],[`Refractory`,`Pain clinic: nerve blocks, combination therapy`,`Screen for depression and sleep loss`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with confusion, behaviour change, aphasia or seizures - start IV acyclovir for HSV encephalitis and admit`,`Vesicles on the tip of the nose, red painful eye or reduced vision in facial zoster - same-day ophthalmology`,`Varicella with breathlessness, cough or SpO2 under 94%, especially in an adult, smoker or pregnant woman`,`Any vesicular rash in a neonate, or maternal varicella from 5 days before to 2 days after delivery`,`Zoster or varicella that is disseminated, haemorrhagic or in an immunocompromised patient`,`Sore throat with stridor, drooling or inability to swallow in infectious mononucleosis`,`Left upper quadrant or shoulder-tip pain with hypotension in a patient with IM - splenic rupture`,`Fever, cytopenia and rising transaminases 1-6 months after a transplant - suspect CMV disease`],pearls:[`**Zoster dose is double the HSV dose**: acyclovir 800 mg five times daily, valacyclovir 1 g three times daily, for 7 days.`,`**Hutchinson sign** - nasal tip vesicles mean the nasociliary nerve and therefore the eye are involved.`,`**Varicella is centripetal and pleomorphic; smallpox and mpox are centrifugal and monomorphic.**`,`**Amoxicillin rash in IM** is not penicillin allergy - do not label the patient.`,`**Monospot is negative in week 1 and in children under 4** - repeat or send EBV VCA IgM/EBNA.`,`**CMV D+/R-** is the highest-risk transplant pairing; letermovir has no HSV/VZV cover - add acyclovir.`,`**Congenital CMV** must be diagnosed on saliva or urine PCR in the first 21 days - after that it cannot be called congenital.`,`**HSV encephalitis - start acyclovir first, image later**; 25% go on to anti-NMDAR encephalitis.`,`**PHN = pain 90 days after rash**; lidocaine plaster, pregabalin/gabapentin or a TCA; steroids do not prevent it.`,`**Zoster in a young adult - test for HIV.**`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old man has had burning pain over the right forehead for 3 days and now has vesicles on the forehead, upper eyelid and the tip of the nose. Visual acuity is normal. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Topical acyclovir cream and review in a week`,`Oral prednisolone alone to prevent postherpetic neuralgia`,`Oral valacyclovir 1 g three times daily for 7 days and same-day ophthalmology review`,`Oral acyclovir 400 mg three times daily for 5 days`],answer:2,explanation:`Vesicles on the nasal tip (Hutchinson sign) mark nasociliary involvement and a high risk of eye disease, so herpes zoster ophthalmicus needs systemic antiviral at zoster doses (valacyclovir 1 g TDS for 7-10 days) and a same-day slit-lamp examination. Topical acyclovir does not treat ganglionic infection, steroids alone are harmful and do not prevent PHN, and 400 mg acyclovir is the HSV dose - VZV needs 800 mg five times a day.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q2`,stem:`A 19-year-old student has fever, exudative tonsillitis, posterior cervical nodes and a palpable spleen. On day 4 of illness the heterophile (Monospot) test is negative. CBC shows 58% lymphocytes with 14% atypical lymphocytes. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Send EBV VCA IgM and EBNA IgG, and an HIV antigen/antibody test`,`Start amoxicillin for streptococcal pharyngitis`,`Start oral acyclovir for EBV infection`,`Arrange a bone marrow examination for lymphoma`],answer:0,explanation:`The heterophile test is falsely negative in about a quarter of IM cases in the first week, and Hoagland criteria are met, so EBV-specific serology (VCA IgM positive, EBNA negative = acute) confirms it; acute HIV is a key heterophile-negative mimic and should be tested. Amoxicillin causes the IM rash, acyclovir has no clinical benefit in IM, and marrow examination is unwarranted with a typical self-limiting picture.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q3`,stem:`A CMV-seronegative man receives a kidney from a CMV-seropositive deceased donor. Which prophylaxis strategy is recommended?`,options:[`No prophylaxis; test CMV IgM monthly`,`Acyclovir 400 mg twice daily for 1 month`,`Valganciclovir 900 mg once daily for 6 months, or letermovir as an alternative`,`Ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV twice daily for 2 weeks only`],answer:2,explanation:`D+/R- kidney recipients are at highest risk of primary CMV disease, so guidelines recommend 6 months of valganciclovir prophylaxis; since 2023 letermovir is an approved alternative with less leucopenia. IgM is useless for monitoring in transplant (quantitative PCR is used), acyclovir is too weak against CMV, and a 2-week IV course is a treatment regimen, not prophylaxis, leaving late-onset disease uncovered.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q4`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman at 28 weeks of gestation develops a varicella rash 12 hours ago. She has no chest symptoms. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Varicella vaccine immediately`,`VZIG intramuscularly only`,`Reassurance; antivirals are contraindicated in pregnancy`,`Oral acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days with advice to report chest symptoms`],answer:3,explanation:`RCOG advises oral acyclovir for pregnant women from 20 weeks who present within 24 hours of the rash, because it shortens fever and symptoms and may reduce varicella pneumonia; she must report any breathlessness for admission. Live varicella vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy and useless once disease has started, VZIG is post-exposure prophylaxis with no role after the rash appears, and acyclovir is not contraindicated in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-q5`,stem:`A 72-year-old man with CKD (creatinine clearance 25 mL/min) is admitted with HSV encephalitis and started on IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly. On day 3 he becomes more confused with myoclonus, although his fever has settled and MRI is unchanged. What is the most likely explanation?`,options:[`Acyclovir neurotoxicity from failure to adjust the dose for renal function`,`Development of acyclovir-resistant HSV`,`Progression of encephalitis needing a switch to ganciclovir`,`Post-HSV anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis`],answer:0,explanation:`Acyclovir is renally cleared, so at CrCl 25 mL/min the dosing interval must be extended (10 mg/kg 12-hourly); accumulation causes confusion, tremor and myoclonus that mimic worsening encephalitis while fever and imaging improve. Acyclovir resistance is rare in immunocompetent patients and takes longer, ganciclovir offers nothing extra for HSV, and anti-NMDAR encephalitis typically appears 2-6 weeks after HSV encephalitis, not on day 3.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, clinical features, complications and management of herpes zoster. Add a note on postherpetic neuralgia and its prevention.`,openingLines:[`Herpes zoster is the reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus (an alpha-herpesvirus) from a dorsal root or cranial nerve ganglion, producing a painful unilateral dermatomal vesicular eruption.`,`Lifetime risk is about 1 in 3, rising steeply after 50 years and in immunosuppression; its main burden is postherpetic neuralgia and herpes zoster ophthalmicus.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Primary varicella seeds sensory ganglia; virus stays latent, controlled by VZV-specific T cells.`,`Age, HIV, cancer, transplant, steroids and JAK inhibitors reduce T-cell immunity; virus reactivates and travels down the nerve.`,`Ganglionitis and neuritis cause prodromal pain; central sensitisation explains PHN.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Prodrome of burning pain 2-5 days; then grouped vesicles on erythematous base in one dermatome, not crossing the midline.`,`Thoracic dermatomes commonest; V1 (HZO) second; Hutchinson sign predicts ocular disease.`,`Variants: Ramsay Hunt (geniculate), zoster sine herpete, disseminated zoster (over 20 vesicles outside dermatome).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Clinical; vesicle-base VZV PCR if atypical or immunocompromised; Tzanck shows giant cells but cannot type.`,`Test for HIV in a young patient or multi-dermatomal disease.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Valacyclovir 1 g TDS or acyclovir 800 mg five times daily or famciclovir 500 mg TDS for 7 days, within 72 h.`,`IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly for disseminated disease or immunocompromise.`,`HZO: same-day ophthalmology; Ramsay Hunt: add steroid, ENT review.`,`Analgesia: paracetamol, NSAID, early gabapentinoid or TCA; renal dose adjustment.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`PHN, HZO keratitis and uveitis, acute retinal necrosis, facial palsy, motor zoster, VZV vasculopathy and stroke, secondary bacterial infection.`]},{heading:`Postherpetic neuralgia and prevention`,points:[`PHN is pain 90 days or more after rash onset in 10-18%; risk rises with age, severe acute pain and HZO.`,`Treat with lidocaine 5% plaster, pregabalin or gabapentin, amitriptyline or nortriptyline; capsaicin 8%; pain clinic.`,`Prevent with recombinant zoster vaccine - 2 doses 2-6 months apart from 50 years, or 18 years if immunocompromised (about 90% efficacy against PHN).`]}],mustDraw:[`Dermatome map showing a thoracic band and V1 with Hutchinson sign`,`PHN treatment ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and variants`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and management`,marks:3},{part:`PHN and prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`VZV latency`,`Hutchinson sign`,`valacyclovir`,`PHN`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`,`Ramsay Hunt`]},{id:`infectious-fever-herpes-viruses-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on congenital cytomegalovirus infection.`,openingLines:[`Congenital CMV is intrauterine infection with cytomegalovirus (HHV-5), the commonest congenital infection (about 0.5-1% of live births) and the leading non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss.`,`Transmission is 30-40% after primary maternal infection and 1-2% after reactivation or reinfection, with the worst outcome after first-trimester infection.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`10-15% symptomatic: SGA, microcephaly, periventricular calcification, chorioretinitis, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, blueberry muffin purpura.`,`Asymptomatic infants still risk late, progressive SNHL.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Maternal: IgG seroconversion or IgM with low avidity; amniotic fluid PCR after 20-21 weeks.`,`Neonate: saliva or urine CMV PCR within 21 days; cranial ultrasound/MRI, fundus, audiology.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Valganciclovir 16 mg/kg BD for 6 months, started in the first month, for moderate-severe symptomatic disease; monitor neutrophils.`,`Primary maternal infection in first trimester: valacyclovir 8 g/day to reduce transmission (ECCI 2023).`]},{heading:`Prevention and follow-up`,points:[`Hygiene counselling: hand-washing after nappies, no sharing utensils with toddlers.`,`Audiology and development follow-up to 5-6 years.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: maternal infection to fetal diagnosis to neonatal testing within 21 days`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`congenital CMV`,`SNHL`,`valganciclovir`,`IgG avidity`,`21 days`,`valacyclovir 8 g`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on HSV, VZV, EBV and CMV`,`CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021 (MMWR) - genital herpes`,`Razonable RR, Humar A. Cytomegalovirus in solid organ transplant recipients - AST Infectious Diseases Community of Practice guidelines, Clinical Transplantation, 2019`,`Leruez-Ville M, et al. Consensus recommendation for prenatal, neonatal and postnatal management of congenital CMV infection from the European Congenital Infection Initiative (ECCI), Lancet Regional Health Europe, 2024`,`ACIP. Use of recombinant zoster vaccine in immunocompromised adults aged 19 years and older, MMWR 2022`,`RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 13: Chickenpox in Pregnancy (2015, current) and BASHH/RCOG Management of Genital Herpes in Pregnancy, 2014`,`NACO National Technical Guidelines on STI/RTI, 2024 and NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021`,`IAP Guidebook on Immunization 2023 (Advisory Committee on Vaccines and Immunization Practices)`]}),o.push({id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult`,title:`Exanthematous fevers in adults and children: measles, rubella, mumps, varicella, HFMD, mpox`,oneLiner:`**Exanthematous fevers** are systemic viral infections announced by a **rash whose timing, distribution and morphology identify the virus** - **measles** (day-4 rash after the 3 Cs and **Koplik spots**), **rubella** (mild rash with postauricular nodes, dangerous only in pregnancy - **congenital rubella syndrome**), **mumps** (parotitis, orchitis), **varicella** (pleomorphic crops), **HFMD** and **mpox** (monomorphic deep pustules with lymphadenopathy); most are vaccine-preventable and **notifiable**, and India targets **measles-rubella elimination by 2026**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`measles`,`rubeola`,`Koplik spots`,`vitamin A`,`SSPE`,`rubella`,`German measles`,`congenital rubella syndrome`,`mumps`,`parotitis`,`orchitis`,`chickenpox`,`hand foot mouth disease`,`coxsackie`,`enterovirus A71`,`mpox`,`monkeypox`,`clade Ib`,`MR vaccine`,`MMR`,`fever with rash`,`exanthem`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`An **exanthem** is a widespread rash accompanying a systemic (usually viral) infection; an **enanthem** is the mucosal counterpart (Koplik spots, Forchheimer spots, oral ulcers of HFMD). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measles** is the most contagious human infection - **basic reproduction number 12-18**, secondary attack rate over 90% in susceptible contacts - so **95% two-dose coverage** is needed for herd immunity. [WHO 2024]`,`Globally measles caused an estimated **10.3 million cases and about 107,500 deaths in 2023**, mostly in unvaccinated children under 5, with a resurgence after pandemic-era immunisation gaps. [WHO/CDC 2024]`,`In India, first-dose measles-containing vaccine coverage was about **88% in NFHS-5 (2019-21)**; outbreaks (for example **Mumbai 2022**) cluster in pockets of unvaccinated and malnourished children. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`India adopted a goal of **measles and rubella elimination by 2026** (after missing the 2023 SEARO target) and received the **Measles and Rubella Champion award (2024)** for progress; elimination means no endemic transmission for 12 months or more with high-quality surveillance. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Mumps** is not in India's UIP (which gives MR, not MMR), so outbreaks continue - Kerala reported over **11,000 cases in early 2024** - mainly in school children and young adults. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Hand-foot-mouth disease (HFMD)** causes large summer-monsoon outbreaks in children under 5 across Asia; the 2022 **'tomato flu'** cluster in Kerala was HFMD due to **coxsackievirus A16**. [Lancet Resp Med 2022]`,`**Mpox** caused a WHO **Public Health Emergency of International Concern (PHEIC)** twice: **clade IIb (July 2022-May 2023)** in sexual networks worldwide, and **clade I, especially the new clade Ib (14 August 2024 - 5 September 2025)** spreading from the DRC; India confirmed its first **clade Ib** case in Kerala in **September 2024**. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, transmission and risk factors`,points:[`**Measles**: Morbillivirus (Paramyxoviridae), single serotype, **airborne** - virus stays infectious in room air for **up to 2 hours**; infectious from **4 days before to 4 days after rash onset**. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Rubella**: Rubivirus (Matonaviridae), droplet spread; infectious from **7 days before to 7 days after rash**; infants with **CRS shed virus for up to 1 year**. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Mumps**: Orthorubulavirus (Paramyxoviridae), droplets and saliva; infectious from **2 days before to 5 days after parotid swelling**. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Varicella**: VZV (alpha-herpesvirus), airborne and contact; infectious from **2 days before the rash until all lesions crust** (details in the Herpes viruses topic). [Red Book 2024]`,`**HFMD**: enteroviruses - **coxsackievirus A16**, **enterovirus A71** (neurological complications) and **coxsackievirus A6** (atypical widespread rash, nail shedding); faeco-oral and respiratory spread, with faecal shedding for weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox**: Orthopoxvirus; **clade I** (Ia endemic zoonotic in Central Africa; **Ib** with sustained human-to-human including sexual and household spread) and **clade II** (IIb caused the 2022 global outbreak); spread by **close skin-to-skin, sexual, respiratory droplet and fomite** contact, and from rodents and primates. [WHO 2024]`,`**Risk factors for severe disease**: infants, **malnutrition and vitamin A deficiency** (measles), pregnancy (varicella, measles), adults (varicella, mumps orchitis), **immunosuppression and uncontrolled HIV** (measles giant-cell pneumonia, necrotising mpox), unvaccinated status. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Measles** enters via **SLAM (CD150)** on alveolar macrophages, dendritic cells and lymphocytes, spreads to lymphoid tissue, then exits through **nectin-4** on respiratory epithelium - which is why it is so transmissible from the airway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measles immune amnesia**: the virus destroys memory B and T cells, erasing **11-73% of pre-existing antibody repertoire** and leaving the child susceptible to other infections for **2-3 years** - a major reason measles kills through pneumonia and diarrhoea. [Mina, Science 2019]`,`**The measles rash** is a T-cell response to virus-infected endothelium, so it appears as antibodies rise; **Koplik spots** are necrotic foci in the buccal mucosa; in severe T-cell deficiency the rash may be absent with **giant-cell pneumonia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin A** stores fall sharply in measles; deficiency damages corneal and respiratory epithelium - giving **xerophthalmia, keratomalacia and blindness** - and supplementation reduces mortality by about **50%** in hospitalised children. [WHO 2017]`,`**SSPE** is a persistent defective measles infection of neurons (lacking M-protein), appearing **7-10 years** after measles acquired usually **before 2 years of age**, with very high CSF anti-measles antibody. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella in early pregnancy** infects the placenta and fetal endothelium, causing **cell-cycle arrest and vasculopathy** of developing organs - risk of defects is up to **90% before 11 weeks**, falling to near zero after 20 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps** replicates in glandular epithelium (parotid, testis, pancreas, ovary, breast) and meninges; **orchitis** causes oedema within a non-distensible tunica albuginea, producing pressure necrosis and **testicular atrophy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox lesions** evolve synchronously in one stage because the virus disseminates in a single viraemic wave to skin; lymphadenopathy reflects early lymphoid replication - a feature smallpox lacked. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification: rash timeline and morphology`,points:[`**Day of fever on which the rash appears** - mnemonic **'Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets'**: Varicella day 1, Scarlet fever day 2, Pox (smallpox) day 3, Measles day 4, Typhus day 5, Dengue day 6, Typhoid (rose spots) day 7. [Park 28e]`,`**Maculopapular, centrifugal (head to feet)**: measles (confluent, starts behind ears), rubella (discrete pink, fades in 3 days), dengue (with **'islands of white in a sea of red'** in recovery), drug rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vesicular**: varicella (**pleomorphic, centripetal**), HFMD (oval grey vesicles on palms, soles, buttocks), mpox (deep, umbilicated, **monomorphic**, palms and soles), disseminated HSV/zoster. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Petechial/purpuric**: meningococcaemia, dengue, rickettsial spotted fever, leptospirosis, ITP - **a non-blanching rash with fever is meningococcaemia until proven otherwise**. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Erythrodermic/desquamating**: scarlet fever (sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue, Pastia lines), toxic shock syndrome, **Kawasaki disease** (5 days fever plus 4 of 5 criteria), DRESS and SJS/TEN. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox severity classification** (WHO): mild (under 25 lesions), moderate (25-99), severe (100-250), very severe (over 250), plus complications (eye, proctitis, sepsis, encephalitis) that make any case severe. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Measles**: incubation **10-14 days**; 2-4 days of high fever with the **3 Cs - cough, coryza, conjunctivitis** (photophobia), then the rash on **day 4** from behind the ears and hairline to face, trunk and limbs over 3 days; fever peaks with the rash and **should settle within 2-3 days of rash onset** - persisting fever means a complication. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella**: incubation **14-21 days**; low-grade fever, **tender postauricular and suboccipital nodes**, a pink discrete rash spreading from face downwards and **fading by day 3**; up to **70% of adult women develop arthralgia** of fingers, wrists and knees; 25-50% are subclinical. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps**: incubation **16-18 days** (12-25); fever, malaise, earache then **parotid swelling** (bilateral in about 70%), pain on chewing and with sour foods; about **30% subclinical**. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Mumps orchitis**: affects **15-30% of post-pubertal males**, 4-10 days after parotitis (may occur without it), with high fever, severe testicular pain and swelling; bilateral in 10-30%; sterility is rare but **subfertility** occurs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HFMD**: a child under 5 with low fever, sore mouth, refusal to eat, then vesicles on palms, soles, knees and buttocks; **CV-A6** gives widespread vesicles and **nail shedding (onychomadesis) 3-8 weeks later**; adults may be infected from their children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox**: incubation **3-21 days** (usually 6-13); prodrome of fever, headache, back pain, myalgia and **lymphadenopathy** for 1-4 days, then a rash evolving over 2-4 weeks; in the 2022 clade IIb outbreak lesions were often **few, anogenital or oral**, with **proctitis** and no prodrome; clade Ib affects households and children as well as sexual networks. [WHO 2024]`,`**History that matters for every fever with rash**: vaccination (MR/MMR, varicella), contact with a rash case 1-3 weeks earlier, travel, sexual history (mpox, secondary syphilis, acute HIV), new drugs in the last 8 weeks (DRESS), and **pregnancy status** of the patient and household contacts. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Koplik spots** - grey-white grains on a red base opposite the lower molars ('salt on a red background') - appear **1-2 days before the rash** and are pathognomonic of measles; they fade as the rash appears. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measles rash** becomes confluent and blotchy, then fades over 7 days in the order it appeared, leaving **brownish staining and fine desquamation** (not on palms and soles). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Forchheimer spots** (petechiae on the soft palate) and **suboccipital/postauricular nodes** suggest rubella; also look for **arthritis** in adult women. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps parotitis** lifts the **ear lobe upwards and outwards** and obliterates the angle of the mandible; the **opening of Stensen duct** (opposite the upper second molar) is red and swollen; submandibular glands may also swell. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox lesions** are firm, deep-seated, **umbilicated pustules of the same stage**, on face, palms, soles, genitals and mouth, with **tender cervical, axillary or inguinal lymphadenopathy**; check eyes (conjunctivitis, keratitis) and for proctitis. [WHO 2024]`,`**Bedside severity in measles**: count respiratory rate and SpO2 (pneumonia), look for stridor (croup), dehydration (diarrhoea), corneal clouding or Bitot spots (vitamin A deficiency), and **MUAC under 11.5 cm** (severe acute malnutrition). [WHO 2017]`,`**HFMD with EV-A71**: myoclonic jerks, ataxia, persistent vomiting, tachycardia, hypertension and mottled skin signal **brainstem encephalitis and impending pulmonary oedema**. [WHO WPRO 2011]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Measles versus rubella**: measles has a 3-4 day prodrome with the 3 Cs, high fever, Koplik spots and an ill child; rubella has minimal prodrome, **postauricular nodes** and a well child. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measles versus dengue**: dengue has retro-orbital pain, myalgia, leucopenia, **thrombocytopenia** and a flushing or petechial rash in recovery; measles has prominent catarrh and conjunctivitis. [WHO 2025]`,`**Measles versus Kawasaki disease**: Kawasaki has **non-exudative conjunctivitis sparing the limbus**, red cracked lips, extremity oedema and peeling, cervical node over 1.5 cm and no cough or Koplik spots - and needs **IVIG** to prevent coronary aneurysms. [AHA 2024]`,`**Mumps versus other parotid swelling**: bacterial (suppurative) parotitis (unilateral, pus from Stensen duct, dehydrated elderly), **parotid stone**, HIV-related lymphoepithelial cysts, Sjogren syndrome, and cervical lymphadenitis (the ear lobe is not lifted). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox versus varicella**: varicella - itchy pleomorphic crops, trunk more than limbs, no marked nodes; mpox - painful monomorphic deep lesions, palms and soles, **lymphadenopathy**, sexual exposure. [WHO 2024]`,`**Mpox versus secondary syphilis and herpes**: syphilis - painless copper-coloured papules on palms and soles with mucous patches (positive RPR/TPHA); herpes - small grouped painful vesicles; **co-infection is common**, so test for HIV and syphilis in all mpox. [CDC 2024]`,`**Drug eruption**: rash appearing **4-21 days after a new drug** (anticonvulsants, allopurinol, beta-lactams, sulfonamides); **DRESS** adds fever, facial oedema, eosinophilia and hepatitis 2-8 weeks after the drug. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Measles**: **serum measles IgM ELISA** collected at first contact (positive from rash onset to 28 days; may be negative in the first 72 hours) plus **throat/nasopharyngeal swab or urine for RT-PCR within 7 days of rash** for genotyping - samples go to the **WHO-accredited measles-rubella laboratory network**. [MoHFW MR surveillance 2023]`,`**India's case definition** for surveillance is now **'fever and rash'** (any fever with maculopapular rash, or any case a clinician suspects as measles) - every suspected case is investigated with a blood sample (**case-based surveillance**). [MoHFW MR surveillance 2023]`,`**Rubella**: rubella IgM (best 5 days or more after rash); in pregnancy, IgM must be confirmed with **IgG avidity** (low avidity = recent) because false-positive IgM is common; CRS confirmed by IgM in infant serum or RT-PCR of throat/urine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps**: buccal swab (massage the parotid for 30 seconds, then swab Stensen duct) for **RT-PCR within 3 days of parotitis**, plus serum IgM; **serum amylase** is raised in parotitis and pancreatitis (lipase discriminates pancreatitis). [CDC 2024]`,`**Mpox**: **vigorous dry swab of the lesion surface or crust for MPXV PCR** (two lesions, separate sites), with clade typing at **ICMR-NIV Pune** or a designated VRDL; blood PCR has lower sensitivity; do HIV and syphilis serology. [ICMR 2024]`,`**HFMD**: clinical; enterovirus PCR of throat swab, vesicle fluid or stool in outbreaks or neurological disease; **CSF pleocytosis and MRI brainstem lesions** in EV-A71 encephalitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**General work-up of fever with rash** in hospital: CBC (leucopenia in measles; thrombocytopenia - dengue), CRP, LFT, blood culture if toxic, dengue NS1/IgM, chest X-ray for measles pneumonia, and **CSF for any altered sensorium**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Measles - supportive care**: paracetamol, fluids and ORS for diarrhoea, continued breastfeeding and feeding, eye toilet, saline nose drops, and **isolation until 4 days after rash onset**. [WHO 2017]`,`**Vitamin A for every measles case** (WHO): **2 doses 24 hours apart** - **50,000 IU under 6 months**, **100,000 IU at 6-11 months**, **200,000 IU at 12 months and over**; give a **third dose 2-6 weeks later** if there are eye signs of vitamin A deficiency. [WHO 2017]`,`**Antibiotics in measles** only for a bacterial complication - pneumonia (amoxicillin per IMNCI), otitis media, or **corneal ulcer**; never routinely. [IMNCI 2023]`,`**Rubella**: symptomatic treatment, NSAID for arthritis, exclusion for **7 days after rash**, and **stay away from pregnant women**; any pregnant contact needs serology. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Mumps**: paracetamol or ibuprofen, soft diet, avoid sour foods, warm or cold compresses, exclusion for **5 days after parotid swelling**; **orchitis** - bed rest, scrotal support, ice packs, NSAIDs (steroids have no proven benefit). [Red Book 2024]`,`**HFMD**: paracetamol, cold fluids and soft food, topical oral analgesic gel; watch for dehydration; keep the child home **until fever settles and blisters are dry**; hand-washing after nappy changes. [WHO WPRO 2011]`,`**Mpox (mild)**: home isolation in a separate room until **all crusts have fallen off and new skin has formed** (usually 2-4 weeks); cover lesions; analgesia, antihistamines, saline sitz baths for anogenital lesions, stool softeners for proctitis; no sex during illness and **condoms for 12 weeks** after recovery. [MoHFW mpox 2024]`,`**Varicella in OPD** is covered in detail in the Herpes viruses topic - acyclovir within 24 hours for adults and adolescents, paracetamol not aspirin. [Red Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: measles with pneumonia, croup, dehydration, encephalitis, corneal involvement or severe malnutrition; mumps with meningitis, encephalitis or pancreatitis; HFMD with neurological signs; mpox that is severe, extensive, ocular, in pregnancy or uncontrolled HIV, or when home isolation is impossible. [WHO 2024]`,`**Isolation in hospital**: measles and varicella need **airborne precautions** (negative-pressure or single room, **N95 respirators**, only immune staff); rubella and mumps need **droplet precautions**; mpox needs **contact plus droplet** precautions with N95 for aerosol-generating procedures. [CDC 2024]`,`**Measles pneumonia**: oxygen to SpO2 94% or more, antibiotics for secondary bacterial infection (ceftriaxone, plus cloxacillin if staphylococcal features), vitamin A; consider **ribavirin** only in immunocompromised patients (off-label, weak evidence). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measles encephalitis** (1 per 1000 cases, days 2-10 after rash): supportive care, seizure control, ICU; it carries **10-15% mortality** and 25% neurological sequelae. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EV-A71 HFMD with brainstem involvement**: ICU, careful fluids, **milrinone** for cardiopulmonary failure, **IVIG** in severe cases per Asia-Pacific practice; hypertension and tachycardia precede pulmonary oedema. [WHO WPRO 2011]`,`**Severe mpox**: **tecovirimat** is no longer routine - the **PALM007 (clade I) and STOMP (clade II) RCTs in 2024 showed no faster lesion resolution**; it may still be used on compassionate grounds or in trials for severe disease and advanced HIV; start or optimise ART in people with HIV. [WHO 2025]`,`**Mpox complications to treat actively**: secondary bacterial skin infection, **ocular disease** (ophthalmology), proctitis with severe pain, urethritis with retention, pharyngeal swelling, and sepsis. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy - rubella**: maternal infection **before 12 weeks** carries up to **80-90% risk of CRS**, 13-16 weeks about 10-20%, rare after 20 weeks; counsel with confirmed serology - termination may be offered in the first trimester. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital rubella syndrome**: **Gregg triad - cataract, cardiac defects (PDA, pulmonary artery stenosis), sensorineural deafness** (commonest), plus microcephaly, 'blueberry muffin' purpura, hepatosplenomegaly, and late diabetes and thyroid disease; notifiable through India's **CRS sentinel surveillance**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy - measles**: causes miscarriage, preterm birth and severe maternal pneumonia but **no defined malformation syndrome**; susceptible exposed pregnant women get **normal human immunoglobulin within 6 days** (MR/MMR vaccine is contraindicated). [Red Book 2024]`,`**Pregnancy - mpox**: risk of miscarriage, stillbirth and congenital mpox; admit and involve obstetrics; **MVA-BN may be used in pregnancy** when the benefit outweighs risk. [WHO 2024]`,`**Post-exposure prophylaxis for measles**: **MR/MMR vaccine within 72 hours** of exposure in eligible contacts (from 6 months in an outbreak), or **immunoglobulin within 6 days** for infants under 6 months, pregnant women and the immunocompromised. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Immunocompromised and HIV**: measles may have no rash and present as giant-cell pneumonia or **measles inclusion-body encephalitis**; mpox in advanced HIV (CD4 under 200) causes necrotising lesions with mortality up to 15-27% in series - start ART. [WHO 2024]`,`**Adults** have more severe varicella (pneumonia), more mumps orchitis and more rubella arthritis; adult health workers without documented immunity should receive **2 doses of MMR** and varicella vaccine. [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Measles complications**: diarrhoea (commonest), otitis media (7-9%), **pneumonia (commonest cause of death)**, croup, keratitis and blindness, encephalitis (1 in 1000), **SSPE (about 1 in 1700-4000 in infants infected under 1 year; 4-11 per 100,000 overall)**, malnutrition and immune amnesia; case fatality is **3-6% in low-income settings**, up to 30% in camps. [WHO 2024]`,`**SSPE**: progressive personality change, myoclonic jerks, dementia and death within 1-3 years; **EEG shows periodic complexes (Radermecker)** and CSF has raised anti-measles IgG; it is still seen in India because of past low coverage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella complications**: arthritis in adult women, **thrombocytopenia (1 in 3000)**, encephalitis (1 in 6000) and progressive rubella panencephalitis (very rare); the main burden is CRS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps complications**: **aseptic meningitis (up to 10%)**, encephalitis, orchitis, oophoritis, mastitis, **pancreatitis**, unilateral **sensorineural deafness (1 in 20,000)** and myocarditis; first-trimester mumps may cause miscarriage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HFMD**: usually self-limited in 7-10 days; **EV-A71** causes aseptic meningitis, brainstem encephalitis, acute flaccid myelitis and neurogenic pulmonary oedema with significant mortality in outbreaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox prognosis**: CFR **under 0.2% for clade IIb**; historically up to **10% for clade Ia** in endemic DRC, lower (about **under 1%**) for clade Ib where care is available; scarring, ocular damage and stigma are long-term problems. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently**: fever with **non-blanching rash**, measles with breathing difficulty, stridor, altered sensorium, eye pain or corneal clouding; mumps with neck stiffness or testicular swelling; HFMD with jerks or lethargy; any rash illness in pregnancy. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Notify every suspected case** of measles, rubella, CRS, mumps outbreaks and mpox on the **IHIP (Integrated Health Information Platform)** of IDSP, and collect samples - not only lab-confirmed cases. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Measles follow-up**: check weight and nutrition at 2-4 weeks, complete vitamin A, catch up missed vaccines, and watch for **post-measles infections for 2-3 years** (immune amnesia). [WHO 2017]`,`**Mpox follow-up**: daily symptom monitoring of **contacts for 21 days** from last exposure; cases are released from isolation only when **all lesions have healed**; test and treat HIV and STIs. [MoHFW mpox 2024]`,`**CRS follow-up**: hearing, eye, cardiac and developmental assessment, and isolation from susceptible pregnant women until urine and throat PCR are negative (up to 1 year). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Outbreak response** for measles: line-list, sample the first 5 cases of each cluster, vitamin A to all cases, **ring vaccination of contacts aged 6 months to 15 years**, and selective MR catch-up in the area. [MoHFW MR surveillance 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme link`,points:[`**UIP schedule**: **MR vaccine at 9-12 months and 16-24 months** (subcutaneous, 0.5 mL), with vitamin A 1 lakh IU at 9 months alongside MR-1; India ran **nationwide MR campaigns (2017-2019)** for children 9 months-15 years and **intensified MR drives (2023-24)**. [MoHFW UIP 2024]`,`**IAP 2023** recommends **MMR at 9 months, 15 months and 4-6 years** in private practice (mumps protection), and **varicella vaccine at 15 months and 3-6 months later** or at 4-6 years. [IAP 2023]`,`**Women of reproductive age**: check rubella immunity or give **MR/MMR before pregnancy**, and avoid pregnancy for **1 month** after; rubella vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy but **inadvertent vaccination is not an indication for termination**. [ACIP 2024]`,`**MR/MMR contraindications**: pregnancy, severe immunosuppression (CD4 under 15% or under 200), anaphylaxis to a previous dose or neomycin; **egg allergy is not a contraindication**; HIV-positive children who are not severely immunosuppressed should be vaccinated. [IAP 2023]`,`**Mpox vaccine**: **MVA-BN** (non-replicating, **2 doses 4 weeks apart**, subcutaneous) was **WHO-prequalified in September 2024** (adolescents added 2024) and **LC16m8** received WHO emergency use listing in November 2024; used for high-risk groups and contacts within **4 days (up to 14 days) of exposure**; not part of India's routine programme. [WHO 2024]`,`**HFMD prevention**: hand hygiene, disinfecting toys and surfaces, excluding ill children from creche; **inactivated EV-A71 vaccines** are licensed only in China. [WHO WPRO 2011]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO mpox PHEIC (14 August 2024 to 5 September 2025)** over clade Ib; WHO standing recommendations continue for surveillance, contact tracing and vaccination of high-risk groups after the emergency ended. [WHO 2025]`,`**WHO 2024**: **MVA-BN prequalified** (September 2024) and later extended to adolescents 12-17 years; **LC16m8** given EUL; dose-sparing intradermal MVA-BN accepted in supply shortages. [WHO 2024]`,`**PALM007 (NEJM 2025) and STOMP (2024-25)** RCTs showed **tecovirimat did not shorten lesion resolution** in clade I or clade II mpox; WHO 2025 clinical guidance advises against routine tecovirimat outside trials or compassionate use. [WHO 2025]`,`**MoHFW measles-rubella elimination goal 2026**, with **'fever and rash' case-based surveillance** and intensified MR campaigns in 2023-24; India received the Measles and Rubella Champion award in 2024. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**WHO 2024 measles position**: the global measles resurgence (10.3 million cases in 2023) prompted renewed calls for **two-dose coverage of 95% or more** and outbreak-response immunisation from 6 months of age. [WHO/CDC 2024]`,`**NICE NG240 (2024)** on suspected meningitis and meningococcal disease reinforces **immediate parenteral antibiotic in primary care** for a febrile child with a non-blanching rash and signs of illness. [NICE NG240 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: 'Measles - clinical features, complications and role of vitamin A'; 'Congenital rubella syndrome'; 'Mumps orchitis'; 'Approach to fever with rash'; 'Mpox - clinical features and public health response'. [DNB pattern]`,`**Write the timeline**: measles incubation 10-14 days, Koplik spots day 2-3, rash day 4, infectious 4 days either side of rash; rubella 14-21 days, 7 days either side; mumps 16-18 days, 2 days before to 5 days after swelling. [Red Book 2024]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting **vitamin A** for every measles case, or giving a single dose instead of **two doses 24 hours apart**. [WHO 2017]`,`**Common mistake**: ordering rubella IgM in a pregnant woman without **IgG avidity**, leading to unnecessary termination after a false-positive IgM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: Why does measles kill? (pneumonia, diarrhoea, malnutrition, **immune amnesia**). Why does mpox differ from varicella? (**lymphadenopathy, monomorphic, palms and soles**). Which clade caused the 2024 PHEIC? (**clade Ib**). [WHO 2024]`,`**Viva**: SSPE - EEG periodic complexes, high CSF measles antibody, onset 7-10 years after measles in infancy; prevention is vaccination. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and prophylaxis in exanthematous fevers`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Vitamin A (measles)`,`Oral: under 6 months 50,000 IU; 6-11 months 100,000 IU; 12 months and over 200,000 IU`,`Day 1 and day 2; third dose 2-6 weeks later if eye signs`,`Transient bulging fontanelle, vomiting; avoid high doses in pregnancy`],[`Paracetamol`,`15 mg/kg oral 6-hourly (adult 500-1000 mg, max 4 g/day)`,`While febrile`,`Hepatotoxicity in overdose; preferred over aspirin (Reye)`],[`Normal human immunoglobulin (measles PEP)`,`IM 0.5 mL/kg (max 15 mL) or IV 400 mg/kg in pregnancy/immunocompromised`,`Single dose within 6 days of exposure`,`Delay MR/MMR vaccine for 6-8 months afterwards`],[`MR/MMR vaccine (PEP and routine)`,`0.5 mL subcutaneous`,`Within 72 h of exposure; UIP at 9-12 and 16-24 months`,`Contraindicated in pregnancy and severe immunosuppression`],[`Acyclovir (varicella)`,`800 mg oral five times daily (child 20 mg/kg QID, max 800 mg)`,`5-7 days, within 24 h of rash`,`Renal dosing; see Herpes viruses topic`],[`Tecovirimat (mpox)`,`600 mg oral 12-hourly with food (weight-based in children)`,`14 days; only trials or compassionate severe cases`,`No benefit on lesion resolution in PALM007/STOMP; headache, nausea`],[`MVA-BN vaccine (mpox)`,`0.5 mL subcutaneous, 2 doses 4 weeks apart`,`Pre-exposure or within 4 days (up to 14) of exposure`,`Injection-site reaction; non-replicating, usable in HIV and pregnancy`],[`Ibuprofen (mumps orchitis, rubella arthritis)`,`400 mg oral 8-hourly with food`,`3-7 days`,`Avoid in dengue, varicella (invasive GAS), CKD, peptic ulcer`]]},{heading:`Rash timeline and identifying features`,columns:[`Disease`,`Incubation`,`Rash day / pattern`,`Pathognomonic or key sign`,`Infectious period`],rows:[[`Measles`,`10-14 days`,`Day 4; maculopapular, behind ears to feet, confluent`,`Koplik spots; 3 Cs`,`4 days before to 4 days after rash`],[`Rubella`,`14-21 days`,`Day 1-2; pink discrete, face down, gone by day 3`,`Postauricular/suboccipital nodes; Forchheimer spots`,`7 days before to 7 days after rash`],[`Mumps`,`16-18 days`,`No rash`,`Parotitis lifting the ear lobe; red Stensen duct`,`2 days before to 5 days after swelling`],[`Varicella`,`10-21 days`,`Day 1; pleomorphic vesicles, centripetal`,`Lesions of all stages together`,`2 days before rash until crusted`],[`HFMD`,`3-6 days`,`Day 1-2; oval vesicles on palms, soles, buttocks`,`Oral ulcers; nail shedding later (CV-A6)`,`While symptomatic; stool for weeks`],[`Mpox`,`3-21 days`,`Day 1-4 after prodrome; deep monomorphic, palms and soles`,`Lymphadenopathy; anogenital lesions`,`Until all crusts fall and skin heals`],[`Scarlet fever`,`2-5 days`,`Day 2; sandpaper, flexural (Pastia lines)`,`Strawberry tongue, circumoral pallor`,`Until 12-24 h of antibiotics`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever with rash`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Measles`,`Ill child, 3 Cs, Koplik spots, rash day 4`,`Measles IgM; throat/urine RT-PCR`],[`Rubella`,`Well, postauricular nodes, 3-day rash, arthralgia`,`Rubella IgM (with avidity in pregnancy)`],[`Dengue`,`Retro-orbital pain, myalgia, thrombocytopenia, recovery rash`,`NS1 (day 1-5), IgM (day 5+)`],[`Meningococcaemia`,`Non-blanching purpura, rapidly ill, shock`,`Blood culture, PCR; treat first`],[`Kawasaki disease`,`Fever 5 days, limbus-sparing conjunctivitis, red lips, oedema`,`Clinical criteria; echo`],[`Rickettsial spotted fever`,`Rash on palms and soles, eschar, rural exposure`,`Weil-Felix limited; IgM ELISA, PCR`],[`Mpox`,`Monomorphic deep pustules, lymphadenopathy`,`Lesion swab MPXV PCR`],[`Drug rash / DRESS`,`New drug 4 days-8 weeks ago, eosinophilia, hepatitis`,`Eosinophil count, LFT, RegiSCAR score`]]},{heading:`Investigations and timing`,columns:[`Infection`,`Sample`,`Best timing`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Measles`,`Serum IgM ELISA + throat swab/urine RT-PCR`,`IgM from rash day 3 to 28; PCR within 7 days`,`IgM may be negative before 72 h - repeat`],[`Rubella`,`Serum IgM; IgG avidity in pregnancy`,`5 days or more after rash`,`Low avidity confirms recent infection`],[`Mumps`,`Buccal (Stensen duct) swab RT-PCR + IgM`,`Within 3 days of parotitis`,`IgM may be negative in vaccinated people`],[`Mpox`,`Dry vigorous lesion swab, 2 sites; HIV and syphilis serology`,`Any time lesions present`,`PCR positive confirms; clade typing at NIV Pune`],[`HFMD (neuro)`,`Throat, vesicle, stool enterovirus PCR; CSF; MRI`,`Early`,`EV-A71 identifies neurological risk`],[`Congenital rubella`,`Infant IgM; throat/urine PCR`,`Under 6 months`,`Infant IgG persisting beyond 6-12 months also supports CRS`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with a non-blanching petechial or purpuric rash - give parenteral antibiotic and transfer`,`Measles with fast breathing, chest indrawing, SpO2 under 94% or stridor`,`Measles with corneal clouding, ulcer or Bitot spots - vitamin A and ophthalmology`,`Altered sensorium, seizures or neck stiffness in any exanthematous fever`,`HFMD with myoclonic jerks, lethargy, persistent vomiting, tachycardia or hypertension`,`Rash illness in a pregnant woman, or a pregnant woman exposed to rubella, measles or varicella`,`Mpox with eye involvement, over 100 lesions, severe proctitis, sepsis, pregnancy or advanced HIV`,`Fever with rash plus severe malnutrition (MUAC under 11.5 cm) or dehydration`],pearls:[`**'Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets'** - rash day 1 to 7: varicella, scarlet, pox, measles, typhus, dengue, typhoid.`,`**Koplik spots appear 1-2 days before the measles rash** and fade as it appears.`,`**Vitamin A, two doses 24 hours apart, for every measles case** - halves mortality.`,`**Measles immune amnesia** - risk of other infections stays high for 2-3 years.`,`**Gregg triad of CRS**: cataract, PDA/pulmonary stenosis, sensorineural deafness.`,`**Rubella IgM in pregnancy needs IgG avidity** before any decision.`,`**Mumps lifts the ear lobe**; cervical nodes do not.`,`**Mpox = monomorphic, deep, palms and soles, with lymphadenopathy**; varicella is pleomorphic without big nodes.`,`**Clade Ib** caused the 2024-25 mpox PHEIC; tecovirimat failed in PALM007 and STOMP.`,`**India's MR elimination target is 2026**; surveillance definition is **'fever and rash'**.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-q1`,stem:`A 2-year-old unvaccinated child has had fever, cough, runny nose and red eyes for 3 days. Today a maculopapular rash has started behind the ears. Which additional management step most reduces mortality?`,options:[`Oral amoxicillin for 5 days`,`Vitamin A 200,000 IU orally on two consecutive days`,`Oral ribavirin for 7 days`,`MR vaccine today to abort the illness`],answer:1,explanation:`This is measles; WHO recommends vitamin A for every case - 200,000 IU at 12 months or older, two doses 24 hours apart - which roughly halves mortality and prevents corneal damage. Antibiotics are only for bacterial complications, ribavirin has no established role in immunocompetent children, and vaccination is useful within 72 hours of exposure, not once the rash has appeared.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-q2`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman at 10 weeks of gestation has a positive rubella IgM done as part of a 'TORCH panel'. She has had no rash or fever. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Advise termination of pregnancy because of congenital rubella syndrome risk`,`Give rubella vaccine now`,`Repeat serology with rubella IgG avidity testing`,`Reassure her that IgM is irrelevant in pregnancy`],answer:2,explanation:`Rubella IgM is frequently false-positive or persistent, especially from unrequested TORCH panels in asymptomatic women; low IgG avidity confirms a recent primary infection, while high avidity indicates past infection or immunity. Termination on IgM alone is a classic error, live rubella vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy, and dismissing the IgM entirely would miss a true primary infection with high CRS risk at 10 weeks.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old man presents with fever and tender inguinal lymphadenopathy followed 2 days later by firm, umbilicated pustules of the same stage on the genitals, palms and face. Which test confirms the diagnosis?`,options:[`Tzanck smear of a vesicle`,`Serum VDRL alone`,`Varicella IgM antibody`,`PCR of a dry swab taken vigorously from a lesion`],answer:3,explanation:`Lymphadenopathy, a prodrome and synchronous deep umbilicated lesions on palms and genitals are typical of mpox, which is confirmed by MPXV PCR of lesion material, with clade typing at NIV Pune. A Tzanck smear shows giant cells in herpes and cannot diagnose mpox, VDRL alone screens for syphilis (which should be tested as a co-infection, not as the diagnosis), and varicella IgM is not used to diagnose this monomorphic illness.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-q4`,stem:`A 12-year-old boy develops progressive decline in school performance, behavioural change and periodic myoclonic jerks. EEG shows periodic high-voltage complexes and CSF has markedly raised anti-measles IgG. Which history is most likely?`,options:[`Measles infection before 2 years of age`,`Mumps orchitis 1 year earlier`,`MR vaccination at 9 months`,`Congenital rubella syndrome`],answer:0,explanation:`This is subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, a persistent defective measles infection presenting 7-10 years after wild measles acquired usually under 2 years of age, with Radermecker periodic complexes on EEG and high CSF measles antibody. Measles vaccination prevents SSPE rather than causing it, mumps does not cause this syndrome, and CRS causes progressive rubella panencephalitis only rarely and with rubella, not measles, antibody.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-q5`,stem:`A 3-year-old child with hand-foot-mouth disease during an outbreak develops myoclonic jerks during sleep, persistent vomiting, heart rate 180/min and blood pressure 130/85 mmHg. What is the best interpretation and action?`,options:[`Febrile reaction; continue home care with paracetamol`,`Probable enterovirus A71 brainstem encephalitis; admit to an ICU-capable unit`,`Coxsackie A6 infection that will cause nail shedding; reassure`,`Secondary bacterial infection; start oral amoxicillin`],answer:1,explanation:`Myoclonic jerks, vomiting, tachycardia and hypertension in HFMD are warning signs of EV-A71 brainstem encephalitis with impending neurogenic pulmonary oedema, which needs urgent ICU-level care (careful fluids, milrinone, IVIG in severe cases). Treating it as a simple febrile reaction delays care, CV-A6 causes onychomadesis but not this neurological picture, and oral antibiotics do nothing for an enteroviral brainstem process.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, complications, diagnosis and management of measles. Add a note on the measles-rubella elimination strategy in India.`,openingLines:[`Measles is an acute, highly contagious (R0 12-18) airborne infection caused by a Morbillivirus, characterised by a prodrome of fever with cough, coryza and conjunctivitis, Koplik spots and a maculopapular rash on day 4.`,`It kills through pneumonia, diarrhoea and malnutrition aggravated by vitamin A deficiency and immune amnesia, and India aims to eliminate measles and rubella by 2026.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and pathogenesis`,points:[`Single serotype, airborne; infectious 4 days before to 4 days after rash; incubation 10-14 days.`,`Enters through SLAM (CD150), exits through nectin-4; destroys memory lymphocytes (immune amnesia).`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Prodrome: high fever, 3 Cs, photophobia; Koplik spots 1-2 days before rash.`,`Rash day 4 behind ears, spreads downwards over 3 days, confluent, fades with brown staining and fine desquamation.`,`Fever settles 2-3 days after rash onset; persisting fever means a complication.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Diarrhoea, otitis media, pneumonia (commonest killer), croup, keratitis and blindness.`,`Encephalitis 1 in 1000; SSPE years later; malnutrition; immune amnesia for 2-3 years.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Clinical plus 'fever and rash' surveillance; serum IgM ELISA and throat swab/urine RT-PCR within 7 days.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Supportive: fluids, ORS, feeding, eye care, paracetamol, isolation for 4 days after rash.`,`Vitamin A two doses 24 h apart (50,000/100,000/200,000 IU by age); third dose if eye signs.`,`Antibiotics only for complications; admit pneumonia, croup, encephalitis, malnutrition.`,`PEP: MR vaccine within 72 h; immunoglobulin within 6 days for infants, pregnant, immunocompromised.`]},{heading:`MR elimination in India`,points:[`UIP MR at 9-12 and 16-24 months with vitamin A; MR campaigns 2017-19 and 2023-24 drives.`,`Case-based 'fever and rash' surveillance on IHIP with lab confirmation; CRS sentinel surveillance; outbreak response immunisation.`,`Target: elimination by 2026, needing 95% two-dose coverage.`]}],mustDraw:[`Measles timeline: incubation, prodrome, Koplik spots, rash day 4, infectious window`,`Fever-chart showing fever peaking with rash`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, epidemiology, pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Complications`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and management incl. vitamin A`,marks:2.5},{part:`MR elimination strategy`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Koplik spots`,`vitamin A`,`SSPE`,`immune amnesia`,`MR elimination 2026`,`fever and rash surveillance`]},{id:`infectious-fever-measles-mumps-rubella-adult-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between varicella and mpox.`,openingLines:[`Varicella (VZV, an alpha-herpesvirus) and mpox (an orthopoxvirus) both cause vesiculopustular rashes, but they differ in the evolution, distribution and associated lymphadenopathy.`,`The distinction matters because mpox needs PCR confirmation, contact tracing for 21 days and IHIP notification.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and transmission`,points:[`Varicella: airborne, children, secondary attack 90%; mpox: close skin, sexual, household contact; clade Ib PHEIC 2024-25.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Prodrome: varicella mild; mpox fever, myalgia and lymphadenopathy 1-4 days.`,`Lesions: varicella pleomorphic, superficial, itchy, centripetal; mpox monomorphic, deep, umbilicated, painful, centrifugal, palms and soles, anogenital.`,`Lymphadenopathy: absent or mild in varicella; prominent in mpox.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and treatment`,points:[`Varicella: clinical, VZV PCR; acyclovir within 24 h in adults.`,`Mpox: lesion swab MPXV PCR; supportive care; tecovirimat not routine after PALM007/STOMP.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Varicella vaccine 2 doses, VZIG or antiviral PEP; mpox MVA-BN 2 doses, contact monitoring 21 days, isolation until crusts fall.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: varicella versus mpox`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and transmission`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and treatment`,marks:1},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`varicella`,`mpox`,`monomorphic`,`lymphadenopathy`,`clade Ib`,`MVA-BN`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - measles, rubella, mumps, enteroviruses and poxviruses`,`WHO. Measles vaccines: WHO position paper, 2017; and WHO/CDC Progress Toward Measles Elimination, MMWR November 2024`,`WHO. Clinical management and infection prevention and control for mpox: living guideline, 2025; WHO Director-General statements on the mpox PHEIC, August 2024 and September 2025`,`MoHFW, Government of India. Measles-Rubella Surveillance Guidelines (fever and rash), 2023, and Universal Immunization Programme schedule, 2024`,`MoHFW. Guidelines for Management of Mpox, updated 2024`,`American Academy of Pediatrics. Red Book: Report of the Committee on Infectious Diseases, 33rd edition, 2024-2027`,`IAP Guidebook on Immunization, 2023 (ACVIP)`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 28th edition, 2025`]}),o.push({id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections`,title:`Emerging and epidemic infections: Nipah, avian influenza, mpox, outbreak response`,oneLiner:`**Emerging infections** are those newly appearing in a population or rapidly increasing in incidence or range - **about 75% are zoonotic** - and in India the high-consequence ones are **Nipah** (fruit-bat henipavirus, encephalitis and ARDS, CFR 40-75%), **avian influenza A(H5N1)** and **mpox**; the family physician's job is to **suspect from a case definition, isolate, protect staff with the right PPE, sample safely, notify on IDSP-IHIP within 24 hours and support contact tracing**, within the **One Health** and **IHR (2005, amended 2024)** frameworks.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`Nipah virus`,`henipavirus`,`Pteropus`,`avian influenza`,`H5N1`,`bird flu`,`H9N2`,`mpox`,`clade Ib`,`IDSP`,`IHIP`,`rapid response team`,`case definition`,`contact tracing`,`PPE`,`donning doffing`,`One Health`,`IHR 2005`,`PHEIC`,`pandemic emergency`,`Chandipura`,`KFD`,`CCHF`,`outbreak investigation`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`An **emerging infection** is one that has newly appeared in a population, or existed before but is rapidly increasing in incidence or geographic range; a **re-emerging infection** is a known disease returning after decline (for example dengue, cholera, diphtheria, measles). [WHO 2024]`,`About **60% of all human pathogens and 75% of emerging infections are zoonotic**, most originating in **wildlife**; drivers are land-use change, deforestation, intensive livestock farming, wildlife trade, climate change, urbanisation and travel. [Jones, Nature 2008]`,`**Nipah** outbreaks in India: **Siliguri 2001** (66 cases, 45 deaths), Nadia 2007, and Kerala - **Kozhikode 2018** (18 confirmed, 17 deaths), Kochi 2019, Kozhikode 2021 and **2023**, Malappuram **2024** and further spillovers in **2025**; Bangladesh reports cases almost every winter. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Avian influenza A(H5N1)**: since 2003 about **990 human cases with a CFR near 48%** have been reported to WHO; the **clade 2.3.4.4b** panzootic since 2020 has spread to wild birds, poultry, marine mammals and, since March 2024, **US dairy cattle**, with about 70 mostly mild US human cases and the first US death in January 2025. [WHO 2025]`,`India reported its **first human H5N1 death in 2021** (a boy from Haryana), sporadic paediatric cases since (including Andhra Pradesh 2025), and an **H9N2** case in West Bengal in 2024; poultry outbreaks recur in Kerala, Maharashtra, Jharkhand and Andhra Pradesh. [NCDC 2025]`,`Other emerging threats in India: **Chandipura virus** encephalitis in children (Gujarat 2024 - WHO reported **245 AES cases with 82 deaths** nationally in June-August 2024), **Kyasanur Forest disease** (Western Ghats), **CCHF** (Gujarat, Rajasthan), **Zika** (Kerala 2021, Maharashtra 2024), and imported risks such as Ebola, Marburg and MERS. [WHO DON 2024]`,`The WHO has declared **eight PHEICs**: H1N1 (2009), polio (2014, ongoing), Ebola (2014, 2019), Zika (2016), COVID-19 (2020), and mpox (**2022** clade IIb and **2024** clade Ib). [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors (drivers of emergence)`,points:[`**Nipah reservoir**: **Pteropus fruit bats** (flying foxes); spillover via **raw date-palm sap** contaminated by bat saliva/urine (Bangladesh), **partly eaten fruit**, pigs as amplifying hosts (Malaysia 1998-99), and **human-to-human spread** through respiratory secretions to family caregivers and healthcare workers (India, Bangladesh). [WHO 2023]`,`**Avian influenza risk**: unprotected contact with **sick or dead poultry**, backyard slaughtering and defeathering, live-bird markets, wild birds, and now **raw milk and dairy cattle** (H5N1 2.3.4.4b); sustained human-to-human transmission has not occurred. [WHO 2025]`,`**Mpox risk**: close skin-to-skin and sexual contact (men who have sex with men in clade IIb; heterosexual networks, sex workers and households in clade Ib), bushmeat and rodent contact in Central Africa (clinical detail in the Exanthematous fevers topic). [WHO 2024]`,`**Healthcare amplification** is a recurring feature: Nipah in Siliguri 2001 (about 75% of cases hospital-acquired), SARS 2003, MERS in Korea 2015 and Ebola - **unrecognised index patients in emergency rooms** drive outbreaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Host factors for severe disease**: extremes of age, pregnancy, chronic lung, heart and kidney disease, diabetes, immunosuppression and uncontrolled HIV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**System factors** that let outbreaks grow: delayed recognition, weak laboratory networks, poor infection-prevention practice, mistrust and stigma that make people hide symptoms, and gaps between animal-health and human-health surveillance. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Nipah** (Henipavirus, Paramyxoviridae) binds **ephrin-B2 and ephrin-B3**, which are abundant on **endothelium, neurons and respiratory epithelium** - producing a **systemic vasculitis with thrombosis and microinfarcts**, explaining encephalitis, ARDS and multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah persists in neurons**, which explains **relapsed or late-onset encephalitis** months to years after recovery or after asymptomatic infection (in about 10% of survivors). [Harrison 22e]`,`**H5N1 virus** binds **alpha-2,3-linked sialic acid receptors**, which are found deep in the human **lower respiratory tract** rather than the upper airway - explaining severe primary viral pneumonia but **poor human-to-human transmission**; adaptation to alpha-2,6 receptors (upper airway) is the pandemic warning sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe H5N1** produces a **cytokine storm** (very high IL-6, IP-10, MCP-1), lymphopenia, diffuse alveolar damage, haemophagocytosis and multi-organ failure; viral replication is prolonged, which is why longer and higher-dose oseltamivir is used. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antigenic shift** - reassortment of avian and human influenza genes in a host co-infected with both (pigs, humans) - creates a novel subtype to which humans have no immunity, the mechanism of pandemics (1957, 1968, 2009). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chandipura virus** (Vesiculovirus, Rhabdoviridae, spread by **sandflies**) causes a rapidly progressive encephalitis in children under 15 with death often within 24-48 hours of admission. [NIV 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification: case definitions and alert levels`,points:[`**Case definitions** for outbreaks follow three tiers - **suspected** (clinical criteria plus epidemiological link), **probable** (suspected plus strong epidemiological link or death before testing) and **confirmed** (laboratory proof) - and include **time, place and person** limits. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Nipah suspected case (NCDC)**: a person from an affected area with **fever plus altered sensorium or seizures**, or **fever with severe headache**, or **fever with cough or breathlessness**; **confirmed** by RT-PCR (throat swab, blood, urine, CSF), IgM/IgG ELISA or virus isolation at a BSL-3/4 laboratory. [NCDC Nipah 2023]`,`**Nipah probable case**: a suspected case who lived in the same ward or village as confirmed cases during the outbreak and **died before complete samples could be collected**. [NCDC Nipah 2023]`,`**Avian influenza suspected case**: fever with cough or breathlessness **plus exposure within 10 days** to sick or dead poultry/wild birds, an affected farm, a confirmed human case, or laboratory work with H5 samples. [WHO 2024]`,`**Contact categories**: **high-risk** - household members, caregivers, people who touched body fluids, and healthcare workers exposed without PPE; **low-risk** - same room or vehicle without direct contact; high-risk contacts are quarantined, low-risk contacts self-monitor. [NCDC Nipah 2023]`,`**WHO R&D Blueprint priority pathogens (2024 update)** now group threats by **viral family**, listing prototype and priority pathogens plus **'Pathogen X'** (an unknown pathogen); Nipah, H5 influenza, filoviruses, coronaviruses, CCHF and Lassa remain priority. [WHO 2024]`,`**IHR alert levels**: event notified - **PHEIC** - and, from the 2024 amendments, a new highest level, **'pandemic emergency'** (a PHEIC with wide geographic spread that exceeds health-system capacity and causes substantial disruption). [WHO IHR 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features and examination`,points:[`**Nipah**: incubation **4-14 days** (reported up to 45); fever, headache, myalgia, vomiting, then **drowsiness, confusion, seizures and coma** within days; **respiratory illness (cough, ARDS)** in about 60% in India and Bangladesh, which drives human-to-human spread. [WHO 2023]`,`**Nipah examination clues**: **segmental myoclonus**, areflexia, hypotonia, **hypertension and tachycardia** (brainstem involvement), abnormal doll's eye reflex, pinpoint pupils, and rapid deterioration to coma within 24-48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**H5N1**: incubation **2-5 days (up to 10)**; high fever, cough, breathlessness and diarrhoea, progressing to **viral pneumonia and ARDS** in 4-6 days; clade 2.3.4.4b cases linked to dairy cattle in the US have been mostly **mild conjunctivitis** and influenza-like illness. [WHO 2025]`,`**Mpox**: prodrome with **lymphadenopathy**, then deep monomorphic pustules including palms, soles and anogenital area; full clinical detail is in the Exanthematous fevers topic. [WHO 2024]`,`**Viral haemorrhagic fevers** (CCHF, KFD, Ebola, Marburg): fever, myalgia, then bleeding from gums, injection sites and gut, shock; **KFD** adds a biphasic illness with meningoencephalitis in the second phase in Western Ghats forest workers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bedside triage**: record **SpO2, respiratory rate, GCS, BP and capillary refill**; look for bleeding, rash, eschar (to exclude scrub typhus), neck stiffness and focal deficits; ask exposure in the incubation window. [WHO 2024]`,`**The key history question** in every unusual fever is **'where have you been, what have you touched, and who else is ill?'** - travel, animal and bird contact, bat-bitten fruit or palm sap, hospital visits, and clusters among family or staff. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Nipah encephalitis versus Japanese encephalitis/AES**: JE is mosquito-borne, rarely spreads person-to-person, causes extrapyramidal signs and thalamic lesions; Nipah has **clusters in families and staff**, myoclonus and respiratory involvement. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Nipah versus scrub typhus meningoencephalitis, cerebral malaria and HSV encephalitis**: look for an eschar, do a malaria smear/RDT, and start **empirical doxycycline/azithromycin and acyclovir** while Nipah PCR is pending. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Avian influenza versus seasonal influenza, COVID-19 and community-acquired pneumonia**: only the **exposure history** distinguishes them clinically; subtyping PCR (H5) is needed. [WHO 2024]`,`**Mpox versus varicella, secondary syphilis, HSV and molluscum**: lymphadenopathy, monomorphic deep lesions and sexual exposure favour mpox; co-infections are common. [WHO 2024]`,`**VHF versus severe dengue, leptospirosis, falciparum malaria and meningococcaemia**: these are far commoner in India and treatable - test for them first, while applying VHF precautions if exposure fits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chandipura versus Reye syndrome, toxic encephalopathy (litchi-associated hypoglycaemic encephalopathy in Muzaffarpur)** and acute bacterial meningitis in children - check glucose, CSF and season. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations and safe sampling`,points:[`**Nipah samples**: **throat/nasal swab in VTM, blood (serum), urine and CSF** for **RT-PCR**; serum for IgM/IgG ELISA; packed in **triple packaging** with cold chain and sent to **ICMR-NIV Pune** (BSL-4) or approved BSL-3 labs (NIV Kerala Unit, Institute of Advanced Virology Thiruvananthapuram); point-of-care **TrueNat Nipah** testing was used in Kerala 2023. [ICMR 2023]`,`**Avian influenza**: **nasopharyngeal plus throat swab** (and lower respiratory sample if intubated; conjunctival swab if conjunctivitis) for **influenza A PCR with H5 subtyping**; an unsubtypable influenza A result in an exposed patient must be escalated to NIV or NCDC. [WHO 2024]`,`**Mpox**: vigorous **dry lesion swab from two sites** for MPXV PCR and clade typing. [ICMR 2024]`,`**Biosafety**: collect samples in a single room wearing **N95, eye protection, gown and gloves**; aerosol-generating procedures need an airborne-isolation room; label as high-risk and inform the lab before sending. [ICMR 2023]`,`**Supporting tests**: CBC (lymphopenia and thrombocytopenia in Nipah and H5N1), LFT, RFT, CRP, blood cultures, chest X-ray (multilobar consolidation in H5N1), MRI brain (multiple small **cortical and subcortical white-matter hyperintensities** in Nipah, unlike JE thalamic lesions), and exclusion tests for malaria, dengue and scrub typhus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genomic surveillance**: **INSACOG** (formed for SARS-CoV-2, now expanded) and NIV sequence outbreak isolates; Nipah from Kerala belongs to the **Indian genotype (NiV-I)**, close to the Bangladesh strain. [ICMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Management at first contact / primary care`,points:[`**Suspect** from a case definition plus exposure, and **separate the patient immediately** - mask the patient, move to a single well-ventilated room, and keep a 1-metre (preferably 2-metre) distance from others. [WHO 2024]`,`**Protect yourself before examining**: hand hygiene plus the PPE matching the route - **droplet and contact for Nipah and H5N1 with an N95 respirator** because of aerosol risk, contact plus droplet for mpox; minimise the number of staff in contact. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Notify the same day** - suspected Nipah, avian influenza, mpox, clusters of AES or unusual deaths go to the **District Surveillance Officer** and on **IHIP (P-form for presumptive cases)**; the district **Rapid Response Team** is activated. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Arrange safe transfer** in an ambulance with staff in PPE to the designated isolation facility, informing the receiving hospital in advance; do not send the patient by public transport. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Start empirical treatment that cannot wait**: oseltamivir for suspected avian influenza, doxycycline for possible scrub typhus, acyclovir for encephalitis, artesunate if malaria is positive - **do not delay treatable causes** while waiting for exotic tests. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Record all contacts** in the clinic - staff, other patients in the waiting area, accompanying relatives - for the contact-tracing team. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Isolation ward**: single rooms or a cohort ward with dedicated staff, equipment and toilets; **airborne infection isolation room** for aerosol-generating procedures (intubation, suction, nebulisation, bronchoscopy). [WHO 2024]`,`**Nipah treatment is supportive**: airway protection, ventilation for ARDS, seizure control, raised ICP measures, fluids and nutrition; **ribavirin** (oral or IV) has been used with uncertain benefit; the **monoclonal antibody m102.4** (procured by ICMR) and **remdesivir** (protective in primates) are used under compassionate protocols. [NCDC Nipah 2023]`,`**H5N1**: **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily** started as early as possible **regardless of the interval from symptom onset**, for at least 5 days and often **10 days in severe disease** (double dose has no proven extra benefit); add antibiotics for secondary bacterial pneumonia; **avoid routine corticosteroids**. [WHO 2024]`,`**H5N1 ventilation**: lung-protective ventilation (tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight, plateau pressure under 30 cmH2O), prone positioning and ECMO where available - as for other viral ARDS. [WHO 2024]`,`**Severe mpox**: supportive care, management of eye disease, proctitis, secondary infection and pain; tecovirimat only under trial or compassionate protocol. [WHO 2025]`,`**Safe management of the dead**: Nipah and VHF bodies are wrapped in leak-proof body bags by staff in full PPE, with **no washing or embalming**, minimal handling and burial or cremation under supervision, respecting family wishes. [NCDC Nipah 2023]`,`**Hospital outbreak control**: designate an infection-control nurse, keep a staff exposure register, monitor exposed staff daily for the incubation period, and restrict visitors. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Outbreak investigation and response (IDSP/IHIP)`,points:[`**IDSP** (launched **2004**, run by **NCDC**) does decentralised, laboratory-supported surveillance of epidemic-prone diseases through **District and State Surveillance Units** and a **Central Surveillance Unit**; it moved to the real-time **IHIP** platform nationwide in **2021**. [IDSP 2024]`,`**IHIP reporting forms**: **S (syndromic)** by health workers, **P (presumptive)** by clinicians, **L (laboratory-confirmed)** by labs - reported weekly and, for outbreak-prone diseases, **immediately**; automated alerts flag rising trends. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Steps of an outbreak investigation**: prepare (team, supplies) - **verify the diagnosis and confirm the outbreak** (cases above expected) - **define a case** - **find cases and line-list** - **descriptive epidemiology** (time: epidemic curve; place: spot map; person) - hypotheses - test with case-control or cohort study - **control measures** (start early, do not wait) - communicate and keep surveillance. [CDC Field Epidemiology 2019]`,`**The epidemic curve** tells the type: **point-source** (sharp peak within one incubation period - food-borne), **continuing common-source** (plateau - contaminated water), and **propagated** (successive peaks one incubation apart - person-to-person such as Nipah or measles). [Park 28e]`,`**Rapid Response Teams** (epidemiologist, clinician, microbiologist, entomologist/veterinarian as needed) reach the site within **24-48 hours**, set up a **control room**, collect samples, start active case search and give risk communication. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Nipah containment in Kerala**: **containment zones** with restricted movement, house-to-house fever surveillance, isolation of high-risk contacts for **21 days**, bat surveys with NIV, and an outbreak declared over after **42 days (twice the maximum incubation period)** with no new case. [Kerala Health 2023]`,`**Risk communication**: one official spokesperson, daily updates, clear messages (avoid fallen fruit and raw palm sap, report fever), and active **rumour and stigma management**, which keeps cases coming forward. [WHO 2024]`,`**PM-ABHIM (2021)** strengthens this system with **integrated public health laboratories in every district**, BSL-3 labs, new regional NIVs, the **National Institute for One Health (Nagpur)** and metropolitan surveillance units. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Contact tracing, quarantine and PPE`,points:[`**Contact tracing** = identify, list, inform and follow up every contact daily for the **maximum incubation period** since last exposure: **Nipah 21 days**, **mpox 21 days**, **avian influenza 10 days**, Ebola 21 days. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Quarantine** separates and restricts movement of **well people who were exposed**; **isolation** separates **people who are ill or infected** - examiners expect the distinction. [Park 28e]`,`**PPE by transmission route**: **standard precautions** for all; **contact** (gown, gloves); **droplet** (surgical mask within 1-2 m); **airborne** (**N95 or higher respirator**, fit-checked, negative-pressure room); high-consequence pathogens add coverall, hood and boot covers. [CDC 2007]`,`**Donning sequence**: hand hygiene - gown - **N95 respirator (seal check)** - goggles or face shield - gloves (over gown cuffs). [CDC 2024]`,`**Doffing sequence** (most contamination happens here): gloves - goggles/face shield - gown - **respirator last, outside the room** - hand hygiene, with hand hygiene between steps; use a trained **buddy or observer**. [CDC 2024]`,`**Post-exposure chemoprophylaxis** exists for **avian influenza** (oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for close contacts, continued for 5-10 days after last exposure) and **mpox vaccination within 4 days** of exposure (MVA-BN); there is **no licensed prophylaxis for Nipah**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Healthcare worker care**: daily temperature and symptom log for exposed staff, rest and psychological support, and **no blame** for reporting exposures - fear of blame hides exposures. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: H5N1 and seasonal influenza are more severe - **oseltamivir is safe and should be started at once**; mpox raises miscarriage and stillbirth risk; Nipah carries very high maternal mortality - joint obstetric care in isolation. [WHO 2024]`,`**Children**: Chandipura and EV-A71 kill rapidly through encephalitis; oseltamivir dose is weight-based (**3 mg/kg twice daily** under 1 year; 30-75 mg twice daily by weight thereafter). [WHO 2024]`,`**Immunocompromised and HIV**: prolonged viral shedding, severe necrotising mpox, and longer antiviral courses; screen people with mpox for HIV. [WHO 2024]`,`**CKD**: reduce oseltamivir to **30 mg twice daily at CrCl 30-60** and 30 mg once daily at CrCl 10-30 mL/min. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: when no isolation room exists, use the **end of a ward with screens and 2 m spacing**, natural ventilation (open windows, fans blowing out), dedicated staff and surgical masks for patients. [WHO 2024]`,`**Mass gatherings and travel** (Hajj, Kumbh Mela): surveillance for MERS, meningococcal disease, influenza and diarrhoeal outbreaks; Hajj pilgrims need meningococcal vaccination. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Nipah CFR is 40-75% globally** and over **70% in most Kerala and Bangladesh outbreaks**; respiratory involvement and age over 45 predict death. [WHO 2023]`,`**Nipah survivors**: about **20% have long-term neurological sequelae** (seizures, personality change, cognitive deficits), and some develop **relapsed encephalitis** months to years later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**H5N1**: ARDS, multi-organ failure, haemophagocytosis, Reye syndrome (if aspirin is given to children), and secondary bacterial pneumonia; historical **CFR about 48%**, although clade 2.3.4.4b US cases were mostly mild. [WHO 2025]`,`**Mpox**: secondary infection, scarring, eye damage, proctitis and stricture, encephalitis, and mental-health effects of stigma (see Exanthematous fevers topic). [WHO 2024]`,`**Societal consequences**: lockdown of containment zones, school closures, poultry culling (loss of income), and stigma toward affected families and health workers. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention: One Health and the International Health Regulations`,points:[`**One Health** is an integrated approach that balances and optimises the health of **people, animals and ecosystems**; the **Quadripartite (FAO, UNEP, WHO, WOAH) One Health Joint Plan of Action 2022-2026** guides countries. [Quadripartite 2022]`,`**India's One Health actions**: the **National One Health Mission**, the National Institute for One Health in Nagpur, joint human-animal surveillance with the Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying, and **NIHSAD Bhopal** for animal avian influenza testing. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Nipah prevention**: do not eat **fallen or bat-bitten fruit**, do not drink **raw date-palm sap or toddy**, wash fruit, avoid bat roosts, and use PPE when caring for patients; **no licensed vaccine** - candidates (ChAdOx1 NipahB, mRNA-1215) are in early trials. [WHO 2024]`,`**Avian influenza prevention**: culling within **1 km** of an infected poultry site and surveillance up to 10 km, with compensation (**Action Plan for Prevention, Control and Containment of Avian Influenza, DAHD 2021**); PPE and oseltamivir prophylaxis for cullers; avoid raw milk; seasonal flu vaccination of poultry workers to reduce reassortment. [DAHD 2021]`,`**IHR (2005)** is a legally binding instrument for 196 States Parties; each country has a **National IHR Focal Point** (in India, at **NCDC**) and must **assess events within 48 hours and notify WHO within 24 hours** of assessment using the **Annex 2 decision instrument**. [WHO IHR 2005]`,`**Annex 2**: **smallpox, wild poliovirus, human influenza from a new subtype and SARS are always notifiable**; for other events notify if **any 2 of 4** apply - serious public health impact, unusual or unexpected, significant risk of international spread, significant risk of travel or trade restrictions. [WHO IHR 2005]`,`**Core capacities** under IHR include surveillance, laboratories, risk communication and **points of entry** (airports, seaports, ground crossings) with screening and isolation arrangements - assessed through the annual **SPAR** report and voluntary **Joint External Evaluation**. [WHO IHR 2005]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**IHR amendments adopted by WHA77 on 1 June 2024**, in force from **19 September 2025**: new alert level **'pandemic emergency'**, a **National IHR Authority** in each country, a **Coordinating Financial Mechanism**, a States Parties Committee, and commitments on **equitable access to health products**. [WHO IHR 2024]`,`**WHO Pandemic Agreement adopted by WHA78 on 20 May 2025**, with the **Pathogen Access and Benefit-Sharing (PABS)** annex still under negotiation before it opens for signature. [WHO 2025]`,`**WHO mpox PHEIC for clade Ib (14 August 2024 - 5 September 2025)**; MVA-BN prequalified September 2024 and LC16m8 given EUL November 2024. [WHO 2025]`,`**WHO R&D Blueprint 2024**: pathogen-family approach with prototype pathogens and Pathogen X, replacing the short list of named diseases. [WHO 2024]`,`**H5N1 clade 2.3.4.4b in dairy cattle (2024-25)** led WHO and CDC to update guidance on **raw milk avoidance, PPE for farm workers and oseltamivir PEP twice daily**; WHO risk to the general public remains low. [WHO 2025]`,`**India 2024-25**: Nipah protocol updated after Kerala 2023-24 outbreaks (TrueNat point-of-care testing, mobile BSL-3 labs, m102.4 stock), and **Chandipura AES** prompted sandfly control and paediatric ICU readiness in Gujarat. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: 'Nipah virus infection - epidemiology, clinical features and control'; 'Steps of outbreak investigation'; 'IDSP/IHIP'; 'International Health Regulations'; 'One Health'; 'PPE donning and doffing'. [DNB pattern]`,`**Common mistake**: confusing **isolation (sick)** with **quarantine (exposed but well)**, or giving 14 days for Nipah contacts instead of **21 days**. [Park 28e]`,`**Common mistake**: delaying oseltamivir in suspected H5N1 because it is beyond 48 hours - in severe or novel influenza it is **given regardless of time since onset**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Common mistake**: removing the N95 inside the patient room or removing gloves last - **gloves first, respirator last, outside the room**. [CDC 2024]`,`**Viva**: Why does H5N1 rarely spread between humans? (**alpha-2,3 receptors deep in the lung**). What receptor does Nipah use? (**ephrin-B2/B3**). Which events are always notifiable under IHR? (**smallpox, wild polio, new influenza subtype, SARS**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: Draw an epidemic curve for a point-source versus propagated outbreak and say which fits Nipah (**propagated, with healthcare clusters**). [Park 28e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and prophylaxis for emerging infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oseltamivir (H5N1 treatment)`,`75 mg oral BD; child by weight (3 mg/kg BD under 1 year)`,`At least 5 days; 10 days in severe or immunocompromised`,`Nausea, vomiting; reduce to 30 mg BD at CrCl 30-60`],[`Oseltamivir (H5N1 post-exposure)`,`75 mg oral BD (treatment dose for novel influenza)`,`5-10 days after last exposure`,`Same; safe in pregnancy`],[`Baloxavir`,`40 mg (40-80 kg) or 80 mg (over 80 kg) oral single dose`,`Single dose; alternative for influenza`,`Resistance emergence; limited data in severe and pregnancy`],[`Ribavirin (Nipah, compassionate)`,`Oral or IV loading then maintenance per protocol`,`7-10 days`,`Haemolytic anaemia, teratogenic; benefit uncertain`],[`Monoclonal antibody m102.4 (Nipah)`,`IV, under ICMR compassionate protocol`,`Two doses early in illness`,`Experimental; supply through ICMR only`],[`Remdesivir (Nipah, experimental)`,`200 mg IV day 1 then 100 mg daily`,`Per protocol`,`Raised transaminases; animal data only`],[`MVA-BN vaccine (mpox)`,`0.5 mL subcutaneous, 2 doses 4 weeks apart`,`Pre-exposure or within 4 days (up to 14) of exposure`,`Local reactions; non-replicating`],[`Tecovirimat (mpox)`,`600 mg oral BD with food`,`14 days, trial or compassionate`,`No benefit on lesion resolution in RCTs`]]},{heading:`Key emerging infections in India`,columns:[`Infection`,`Agent and reservoir`,`Transmission`,`Incubation`,`Key feature / CFR`],rows:[[`Nipah`,`Henipavirus; Pteropus fruit bat`,`Bat-contaminated fruit or sap; human-to-human`,`4-14 days (up to 45)`,`Encephalitis and ARDS; CFR 40-75%`],[`Avian influenza H5N1`,`Influenza A; wild birds, poultry, dairy cattle`,`Contact with sick birds or animals`,`2-5 days (up to 10)`,`Viral pneumonia, ARDS; CFR about 48% historically`],[`Mpox`,`Orthopoxvirus; rodents`,`Skin, sexual, household contact`,`3-21 days`,`Lymphadenopathy, monomorphic rash; clade Ib PHEIC 2024`],[`Chandipura`,`Vesiculovirus; sandflies`,`Sandfly bite`,`Short (days)`,`Rapid encephalitis in children; high CFR`],[`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`Flavivirus; monkeys, ticks`,`Haemaphysalis tick bite`,`3-8 days`,`Biphasic haemorrhagic fever with encephalitis; vaccine exists`],[`CCHF`,`Nairovirus; livestock, Hyalomma ticks`,`Tick bite, animal blood, nosocomial`,`1-13 days`,`Haemorrhagic fever; nosocomial spread; ribavirin used`],[`Zika`,`Flavivirus; Aedes mosquito`,`Mosquito, sexual, vertical`,`3-14 days`,`Mild rash; microcephaly in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a suspected high-consequence infection`,columns:[`Presentation`,`Exotic possibility`,`Commoner treatable mimic to exclude first`,`Discriminator`],rows:[[`Fever with encephalitis cluster`,`Nipah, Chandipura`,`JE/AES, scrub typhus, HSV, cerebral malaria`,`Eschar, smear/RDT, CSF PCR, family/staff clusters`],[`Fever with severe pneumonia after bird contact`,`H5N1`,`Seasonal influenza, COVID-19, bacterial CAP`,`Exposure history; H5 subtyping PCR`],[`Fever with pustular rash`,`Mpox`,`Varicella, secondary syphilis, HSV`,`Lymphadenopathy, monomorphic; lesion PCR`],[`Fever with bleeding`,`CCHF, KFD, Ebola`,`Severe dengue, leptospirosis, falciparum, meningococcaemia`,`Travel/tick/forest exposure; NS1, smear, MAT`],[`Fever with paralysis in a child`,`EV-A71, polio`,`Guillain-Barre syndrome, cord compression`,`Stool samples for AFP surveillance`]]},{heading:`PPE and transmission-based precautions`,columns:[`Precaution`,`Used for`,`What to wear`,`Room`],rows:[[`Standard`,`All patients`,`Hand hygiene; gloves, apron, mask, eye protection if splash risk`,`Any`],[`Contact`,`Mpox, MRSA, C. difficile`,`Gown and gloves on entry`,`Single room or cohort`],[`Droplet`,`Influenza, mumps, rubella, meningococcus`,`Surgical mask within 1-2 m`,`Single room, 1-2 m spacing`],[`Airborne`,`TB, measles, varicella, aerosol procedures in H5N1/Nipah`,`N95 or higher respirator, fit-checked`,`Negative-pressure, 12 air changes/hour`],[`High-consequence (Nipah, VHF)`,`Nipah, Ebola, CCHF`,`N95, goggles/face shield, coverall or gown, double gloves, boot covers`,`Dedicated isolation unit; trained buddy for doffing`]]},{heading:`Outbreak investigation steps`,columns:[`Step`,`What it involves`],rows:[[`1. Prepare`,`Team, approvals, supplies, sample kits, PPE`],[`2. Confirm the outbreak`,`Compare with expected numbers from IDSP baseline`],[`3. Verify the diagnosis`,`Clinical review and laboratory confirmation`],[`4. Case definition`,`Suspected, probable, confirmed; time, place, person`],[`5. Find cases and line-list`,`Active search, hospital records, house-to-house`],[`6. Descriptive epidemiology`,`Epidemic curve, spot map, attack rates by age/sex`],[`7. Hypotheses and testing`,`Case-control or cohort study; environmental samples`],[`8. Control measures`,`Isolation, quarantine, vector or source control - start early`],[`9. Communicate`,`Report to state and NCDC; public risk communication`],[`10. Maintain surveillance`,`Declare over after twice the maximum incubation period`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with altered sensorium or seizures in a person from a Nipah-affected area, or in a family/staff cluster`,`Severe pneumonia or ARDS with contact with sick or dead poultry, wild birds or dairy cattle in the last 10 days`,`Any cluster of unexplained deaths or severe illness in a village, ward or hospital staff`,`Fever with bleeding after tick bite, forest exposure, livestock slaughter or travel to an outbreak area`,`Pustular rash with lymphadenopathy in a pregnant woman, child or person with advanced HIV`,`Unsubtypable influenza A result in a patient with animal exposure`,`A healthcare worker developing fever after caring for a suspected high-consequence case`],pearls:[`**Suspect - isolate - protect - sample - notify**: the five-word first-contact rule for any emerging infection.`,`**Isolation is for the sick; quarantine is for the exposed but well.**`,`**Nipah uses ephrin-B2/B3** - endothelium and neurons, so vasculitis, encephalitis and late relapse.`,`**H5N1 binds alpha-2,3 sialic acid deep in the lung** - severe pneumonia, poor human spread.`,`**Follow contacts for the maximum incubation period**: Nipah 21, mpox 21, avian flu 10 days.`,`**Declare an outbreak over after twice the maximum incubation period** without a new case (42 days for Nipah).`,`**Doffing: gloves first, respirator last, outside the room.**`,`**IHR Annex 2 always-notifiable four**: smallpox, wild polio, new influenza subtype, SARS.`,`**IHR 2024 amendments** created the 'pandemic emergency' level; in force since 19 September 2025.`,`**IHIP forms**: S by health workers, P by clinicians, L by labs.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-q1`,stem:`During a Nipah outbreak in Kozhikode, a 40-year-old nurse who cared for a confirmed case without an N95 respirator is well. What is the correct public health action?`,options:[`Isolate her in hospital and start ribavirin`,`No action unless she develops symptoms`,`Quarantine as a high-risk contact with daily symptom monitoring for 21 days from last exposure`,`Give oseltamivir prophylaxis for 10 days`],answer:2,explanation:`An unprotected healthcare worker exposed to a confirmed Nipah patient is a high-risk contact; she is well, so she needs quarantine (not isolation) with daily monitoring for 21 days from last exposure, the follow-up period used in India. Ribavirin is not proven as prophylaxis and isolation is for symptomatic people, waiting for symptoms risks onward spread, and oseltamivir is the prophylaxis for avian influenza, not Nipah.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-q2`,stem:`Why has avian influenza A(H5N1) caused severe pneumonia in humans but very limited human-to-human transmission?`,options:[`It binds alpha-2,3-linked sialic acid receptors that are found mainly in the lower respiratory tract`,`It cannot replicate at human body temperature`,`Humans have pre-existing neutralising antibodies to H5`,`It is inactivated by gastric acid after inhalation`],answer:0,explanation:`Avian influenza viruses prefer alpha-2,3 sialic acid receptors, which in humans lie deep in the alveoli and bronchioles, so infection causes severe pneumonia but little shedding from the upper airway; adaptation to alpha-2,6 receptors would signal pandemic potential. H5N1 replicates well at human core temperature, humans are largely immunologically naive to H5, and gastric acid has nothing to do with respiratory transmission.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-q3`,stem:`Under the International Health Regulations (2005), which event must always be notified to WHO regardless of the Annex 2 assessment?`,options:[`A cluster of cholera cases in an endemic district`,`A single case of human influenza caused by a new subtype`,`A dengue outbreak during the monsoon`,`An increase in scrub typhus cases after floods`],answer:1,explanation:`Annex 2 lists four diseases that are always notifiable - smallpox, wild poliovirus, human influenza caused by a new subtype, and SARS - because a single case may signal international spread. Cholera, dengue and scrub typhus are assessed with the four Annex 2 questions and are notified only if at least two criteria (serious impact, unusual, international spread risk, travel or trade risk) are met.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-q4`,stem:`An outbreak's epidemic curve shows successive peaks about 10-12 days apart, each larger than the last, with cases among family caregivers and hospital staff. What does this pattern indicate?`,options:[`Point-source outbreak from a single meal`,`Continuing common-source outbreak from contaminated water`,`Seasonal vector-borne increase`,`Propagated (person-to-person) outbreak`],answer:3,explanation:`Successive, enlarging peaks separated by roughly one incubation period with caregiver and staff clusters indicate a propagated outbreak, as seen in Nipah. A point-source outbreak has a single sharp peak within one incubation period, a continuing common source produces a plateau, and vector-borne seasonal rises do not cluster in caregivers and healthcare workers.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-q5`,stem:`Which change was introduced by the amendments to the International Health Regulations adopted at the World Health Assembly in June 2024?`,options:[`A new alert level called 'pandemic emergency' above a PHEIC`,`Abolition of the National IHR Focal Point`,`Mandatory travel bans during any PHEIC`,`Removal of SARS from the always-notifiable list`],answer:0,explanation:`The 2024 IHR amendments, in force since September 2025, created a 'pandemic emergency' level for PHEICs with wide spread exceeding health-system capacity, and added National IHR Authorities, a Coordinating Financial Mechanism and equity provisions. National Focal Points were retained, IHR continues to discourage unnecessary travel and trade restrictions, and SARS remains always notifiable.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the epidemiology, clinical features, diagnosis and management of Nipah virus infection. Outline the public health response to a Nipah outbreak in your district.`,openingLines:[`Nipah virus is a zoonotic henipavirus (Paramyxoviridae) whose reservoir is the Pteropus fruit bat; it causes acute encephalitis and respiratory illness with a case fatality of 40-75% and spreads person-to-person to caregivers and health workers.`,`India has had outbreaks in West Bengal (2001, 2007) and repeatedly in Kerala since 2018; there is no licensed vaccine or proven antiviral, so control depends on early detection, isolation, contact tracing and infection prevention.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`Reservoir Pteropus bats; spillover via bat-bitten fruit and raw date-palm sap; pigs amplified the 1998 Malaysian outbreak.`,`India: Siliguri 2001 (45 deaths), Nadia 2007, Kerala 2018, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2024, 2025.`,`Incubation 4-14 days (up to 45); human-to-human spread via respiratory secretions.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis and clinical features`,points:[`Ephrin-B2/B3 receptor - vasculitis, thrombosis, neuronal infection.`,`Fever, headache, vomiting, then drowsiness, seizures, coma; segmental myoclonus, areflexia, hypertension.`,`Respiratory illness and ARDS in about 60%; late or relapsed encephalitis in survivors.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`NCDC case definitions: suspected, probable, confirmed.`,`Throat swab, blood, urine and CSF RT-PCR; ELISA; triple packaging to NIV Pune (BSL-4) or Kerala BSL-3 labs.`,`MRI: multiple small cortical and subcortical white-matter lesions; exclude scrub, malaria, JE, HSV.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Isolation with droplet, contact and airborne precautions (N95).`,`Supportive ICU care: airway, ventilation, seizure control, ICP measures.`,`Ribavirin of uncertain benefit; m102.4 monoclonal antibody and remdesivir under compassionate protocols.`,`Safe dead-body management without washing.`]},{heading:`Public health response`,points:[`Notify immediately on IHIP; district RRT within 24-48 h; control room.`,`Containment zone, house-to-house fever survey, contact line-list; high-risk contacts quarantined 21 days.`,`Hospital IPC, staff monitoring, risk communication; bat surveys with NIV (One Health).`,`Declare over after 42 days without new cases.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Avoid fallen fruit and raw palm sap, wash fruit, PPE for caregivers; vaccine candidates in trials.`]}],mustDraw:[`Transmission cycle: bat - fruit/sap or pig - human - human`,`Flow chart of district outbreak response`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and transmission`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Public health response and prevention`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`Nipah`,`Pteropus`,`ephrin-B2`,`21 days`,`IHIP`,`containment zone`,`m102.4`]},{id:`infectious-fever-emerging-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the International Health Regulations (2005) and the 2024 amendments.`,openingLines:[`The International Health Regulations (2005) are a legally binding instrument adopted by 196 States Parties to prevent, detect and respond to the international spread of disease while avoiding unnecessary interference with travel and trade.`,`They were amended at the 77th World Health Assembly in June 2024, the amendments entering into force on 19 September 2025.`],answer:[{heading:`Key provisions`,points:[`National IHR Focal Point (NCDC in India); assess events within 48 h and notify WHO within 24 h of assessment.`,`Annex 2: smallpox, wild polio, new influenza subtype and SARS always notifiable; others if 2 of 4 criteria.`,`Core capacities: surveillance, labs, risk communication, points of entry; SPAR and JEE.`]},{heading:`PHEIC`,points:[`Declared by WHO Director-General on Emergency Committee advice; eight so far, latest mpox 2024.`]},{heading:`2024 amendments`,points:[`New 'pandemic emergency' alert level.`,`National IHR Authority; Coordinating Financial Mechanism; States Parties Committee.`,`Equitable access to health products and solidarity.`]},{heading:`Relevance to India`,points:[`IDSP-IHIP feeds the Focal Point; airport and port health organisations handle points of entry.`]}],mustDraw:[`Annex 2 decision instrument flow chart`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and purpose`,marks:1},{part:`Key provisions and Annex 2`,marks:2},{part:`2024 amendments`,marks:1.5},{part:`Indian relevance`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`IHR 2005`,`Annex 2`,`PHEIC`,`pandemic emergency`,`National IHR Focal Point`,`points of entry`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - zoonotic paramyxoviruses, influenza and emerging infections`,`WHO. International Health Regulations (2005), 3rd edition, and Amendments adopted by WHA77 (Resolution WHA77.17), June 2024`,`NCDC, MoHFW. Nipah Virus Disease: Guidelines for surveillance, diagnosis and management, updated 2023`,`WHO. Clinical practice guidelines for influenza, 2024; WHO avian influenza A(H5N1) risk assessments, 2025`,`NCDC. Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme and Integrated Health Information Platform operational guidelines, 2024`,`FAO, UNEP, WHO, WOAH. One Health Joint Plan of Action (2022-2026)`,`Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying. Action Plan for Prevention, Control and Containment of Avian Influenza (revised 2021)`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 28th edition, 2025`]});var s=[];s.push({id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections`,title:`Bone and joint infections: septic arthritis, osteomyelitis, prosthetic joint infection`,oneLiner:`**Septic arthritis** is bacterial infection of a joint space - an **orthopaedic emergency** in which cartilage is destroyed within days, so the hot swollen joint is **aspirated before antibiotics** and washed out; **osteomyelitis** is infection of bone (haematogenous, contiguous or with vascular insufficiency) that becomes chronic once **dead bone (sequestrum)** forms; **prosthetic joint infection** is a **biofilm** infection that needs surgery plus long, biofilm-active therapy.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`septic arthritis`,`pyogenic arthritis`,`hot swollen joint`,`osteomyelitis`,`acute haematogenous osteomyelitis`,`chronic osteomyelitis sequestrum involucrum`,`prosthetic joint infection PJI`,`DAIR two-stage revision`,`Kocher criteria`,`synovial fluid analysis`,`OVIVA oral step-down`,`diabetic foot osteomyelitis IWGDF`,`probe to bone`,`vertebral osteomyelitis spondylodiscitis`,`Pott spine spinal tuberculosis`,`Kingella kingae`,`Cierny-Mader`,`rifampicin biofilm`,`Brodie abscess`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Septic (bacterial) arthritis** is infection of the synovium and joint space, usually monoarticular (knee in about 50%, then hip, shoulder, ankle, wrist); incidence is **2-10 per 100 000 per year**, rising to 30-70 per 100 000 in rheumatoid arthritis and joint prostheses. [Harrison 22e]`,`Septic arthritis carries a **case fatality of 7-15%** (up to 30-50% when polyarticular) and leaves permanent joint damage in **25-50%** of survivors - the reason it is treated as an emergency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osteomyelitis** is infection of bone with inflammatory destruction; it is **acute** when it evolves over days to weeks and **chronic** when dead bone (**sequestrum**) and a sinus have formed, usually after 6 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute haematogenous osteomyelitis** is mainly a disease of **children** (metaphysis of long bones) and, in adults, of the **vertebrae**; contiguous osteomyelitis follows open fractures, surgery and ulcers. [IDSA/PIDS 2021]`,`**Prosthetic joint infection (PJI)** complicates about **1-2% of primary hip and knee arthroplasties** and is the commonest cause of early knee revision; India performs well over a lakh arthroplasties a year, so PJI is now an Indian problem. [ICM 2018]`,`**Diabetic foot osteomyelitis** is present in about **20% of moderate and 50-60% of severe diabetic foot infections**; with **101 million Indians with diabetes (ICMR-INDIAB 2023)**, it is the commonest osteomyelitis seen in Indian adult OPDs. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Skeletal tuberculosis** forms about **10% of extrapulmonary TB** and 1-3% of all TB; the **spine (Pott disease) accounts for about half** of bone and joint TB, and India carries about a quarter of the world's TB. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Staphylococcus aureus** causes **50-70%** of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis at every age; streptococci (groups A, B, C, G and pneumococcus) come next. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Age-specific organisms**: neonates - S. aureus, **group B streptococcus**, Gram-negative bacilli; children under 4 years - **Kingella kingae** (a fastidious organism, needs PCR); sexually active young adults - **Neisseria gonorrhoeae**. [IDSA/PIDS 2023]`,`**Host-specific organisms**: **sickle cell disease - Salmonella** osteomyelitis; injecting drug users - **Pseudomonas** and MRSA (sternoclavicular, sacroiliac joints); foot puncture through a rubber sole - **Pseudomonas**; animal bite - Pasteurella; raw milk - **Brucella** (sacroiliitis, spondylitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`In diabetic and alcoholic patients in South and East India (Odisha, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu), **Burkholderia pseudomallei (melioidosis)** is an under-recognised cause of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis that does not respond to standard cephalosporins. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Joint risk factors**: rheumatoid arthritis (and anti-TNF or JAK inhibitors), **prosthetic joint**, recent intra-articular steroid injection, gout, skin infection over the joint, and age over 80. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Systemic risk factors**: diabetes, CKD on dialysis (vascular access bacteraemia), cirrhosis, HIV, malignancy, steroids, and **S. aureus bacteraemia from any source**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PJI organisms**: early infection - S. aureus and Gram-negatives; delayed (3-24 months) - **coagulase-negative staphylococci and Cutibacterium acnes** (low-virulence, biofilm formers); late haematogenous - S. aureus and streptococci. [ICM 2018]`,`**Diabetic foot osteomyelitis** is polymicrobial: S. aureus dominates, with streptococci, Enterobacterales, **Pseudomonas** (macerated wounds, warm climates as in India) and anaerobes in ischaemic or necrotic wounds. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the joint and bone are destroyed`,points:[`**Synovium has no basement membrane** and a rich blood supply, so bacteraemic organisms seed it easily; S. aureus binds collagen and fibronectin through **adhesins (MSCRAMMs)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Neutrophils flood the joint and release **proteases, collagenases and cytokines (IL-1, TNF)**; together with bacterial toxins they degrade cartilage proteoglycan within **24-48 hours** and collagen within days - hence **aspirate and drain early**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Raised intra-articular pressure (especially the **hip, which is intracapsular**) compresses the epiphyseal vessels and causes **avascular necrosis** of the femoral head in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`In children, **metaphyseal capillary loops** turn sharply into slow-flowing sinusoids with few phagocytes - the site where haematogenous osteomyelitis starts; in infants under 18 months transphyseal vessels let it spread into the joint. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pus under pressure lifts the periosteum and cuts off cortical blood supply, producing a **sequestrum** (dead bone); new subperiosteal bone forms the **involucrum**, and holes in it through which pus drains are **cloacae**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Dead bone and implants are coated by **biofilm** - bacteria in a polysaccharide matrix, in a slow-growing state - which tolerates up to **1000 times the usual MIC**; this is why chronic osteomyelitis and PJI need **debridement or implant removal** and why **rifampicin** (biofilm-active against staphylococci) is added. [ICM 2018]`,`**Vertebral osteomyelitis** begins in the vascular **end-plate** of a vertebral body and crosses the disc to the adjacent body (pyogenic spondylodiscitis destroys the disc early); **TB spine** is paradiscal too but spares the disc until late and tracks under ligaments as a **cold abscess**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In diabetic feet, **neuropathy** (painless trauma), **ischaemia** (poor antibiotic delivery and healing) and immune dysfunction let an ulcer reach bone, usually under the metatarsal heads, toes or heel. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading that examiners ask`,points:[`**Waldvogel classification** of osteomyelitis by mechanism: (1) **haematogenous**, (2) **contiguous focus** (trauma, surgery, implants), (3) **vascular insufficiency** (diabetic and ischaemic foot); and by duration acute or chronic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cierny-Mader staging** for adult long-bone osteomyelitis: anatomic **type I medullary, II superficial, III localised (full-thickness cortical sequestrum, stable), IV diffuse (unstable)**, combined with host **A (normal), B (local or systemic compromise), C (treatment worse than disease)**. [Cierny-Mader]`,`**Kocher criteria** for septic hip in children: **non-weight-bearing, temperature over 38.5 C, ESR over 40 mm/h, WBC over 12 000/mm3**; predicted probability of septic arthritis is about **3% with one, 40% with two, 93% with three and 99% with four**; **CRP over 20 mg/L** (Caird) adds a fifth predictor. [Kocher 1999]`,`**PJI timing (Zimmerli)**: **early under 3 months** after surgery, **delayed 3-24 months** (low-grade, acquired at surgery), **late over 24 months** (usually haematogenous); the practical split for **DAIR** is implant under 30 days old or symptoms under 3 weeks. [ICM 2018]`,`**PJI definition (ICM/MSIS 2018)**: definite if a **sinus tract communicates with the prosthesis** or **two positive cultures of the same organism**; otherwise a weighted score of serum CRP, D-dimer, ESR, synovial WBC, neutrophil %, leukocyte esterase, **alpha-defensin** and histology; EBJIS 2021 offers a three-level (unlikely, likely, confirmed) alternative. [ICM 2018]`,`**IWGDF/IDSA 2023 diabetic foot infection grading**: 1 uninfected, 2 mild, 3 moderate, 4 severe (SIRS), with **(O) added when osteomyelitis is present** - for example grade **3(O)**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Pott paraplegia** is early-onset (active disease: abscess, granulation, sequestra) or late-onset (healed disease: bony ridge, internal gibbus, fibrosis); spinal TB on imaging is graded by vertebral involvement and kyphosis angle. [Tuli 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Septic arthritis**: acute (1-2 days) **painful, hot, swollen joint with marked restriction of active and passive movement**; fever is absent in up to 40-50% of adults, so its absence never excludes infection. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Gonococcal (disseminated) infection** gives a triad of **migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis (wrist, hand dorsum) and painless vesicopustular skin lesions**, or a purulent monoarthritis; ask about sexual history and menstruation or pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children with osteomyelitis** present with **limp, refusal to bear weight or pseudoparalysis** of a limb, fever and point tenderness; neonates may only feed poorly. [IDSA/PIDS 2021]`,`**Vertebral osteomyelitis**: **back pain in 90%**, often for weeks before diagnosis, worse at night and not relieved by rest; fever is present in only about half; ask for **bacteraemia, IV lines, dialysis, endocarditis, urinary procedures**. [IDSA 2015]`,`**TB spine**: insidious back pain, **night sweats, weight loss, evening fever**, stiffness, and later a **cold abscess** (psoas abscess presenting as groin swelling or hip flexion) or **paraplegia**; look for a TB contact. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Chronic osteomyelitis**: a **discharging sinus** with episodes of flare, bone pain and occasional extrusion of small bone fragments; a change in a long-standing sinus (bleeding, foul growth) suggests **Marjolin ulcer (squamous cell carcinoma)**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**PJI**: early - wound discharge, erythema, fever; delayed - **persistent pain since surgery** or early loosening without systemic signs; late - sudden painful joint after a bacteraemia (dental, urinary, skin). A painful prosthesis is PJI until proved otherwise. [ICM 2018]`,`**Diabetic foot**: a non-healing ulcer over 6 weeks, a **'sausage toe'** (red, swollen digit), or a previously healing ulcer that stalls; pain is often absent because of neuropathy. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Effusion with warmth and pain on the smallest passive movement** (the joint is held in the position of maximum capacity - hip in flexion, abduction and external rotation) means intra-articular pathology, unlike cellulitis or bursitis, where passive movement is relatively free. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Bursitis vs arthritis**: prepatellar or olecranon bursitis is a fluctuant swelling over the bony point with near-normal joint movement - do **not aspirate through infected skin into a joint**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Point tenderness over the metaphysis** with soft-tissue swelling suggests osteomyelitis in a child; tenderness over a spinous process with paraspinal spasm suggests vertebral infection. [IDSA/PIDS 2021]`,`**Probe-to-bone test**: touching hard, gritty bone with a sterile blunt metal probe through a diabetic foot ulcer has a **high positive predictive value in high-prevalence settings** and, with a plain X-ray, is enough to diagnose osteomyelitis. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`An ulcer **over 2 cm2 or deeper than 3 mm**, exposed bone, and a **sausage toe** each raise the likelihood of underlying osteomyelitis. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Gibbus** (sharp angular kyphosis) with tenderness in the thoracolumbar region, a fluctuant non-tender **cold abscess** and long-tract signs (spasticity, brisk reflexes, extensor plantars) are the bedside findings of Pott spine. [Tuli 2016]`,`Examine for the **source and spread**: skin lesions, IV drug use marks, a **new murmur (endocarditis)**, other involved joints and the gonococcal skin lesions; assess **peripheral pulses and ABPI** in every diabetic foot. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Crystal arthritis (gout, CPPD)** is the commonest mimic: also hot, red and with a high synovial WBC; **crystals do not exclude infection** - both coexist in up to 5% - so Gram stain and culture are always sent. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Transient synovitis of the hip** in a child aged 3-8 years: afebrile or low-grade, can bear weight, ESR and CRP near normal, Kocher score 0-1, settles in days - the key paediatric differential. [Kocher 1999]`,`**Reactive arthritis** (post-enteric or post-chlamydial) is usually an **oligoarthritis of the lower limbs** with enthesitis, conjunctivitis or urethritis, sterile fluid and onset 1-4 weeks after the infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cellulitis, bursitis and haemarthrosis** (anticoagulants, haemophilia, trauma) are told apart by preserved joint movement (cellulitis, bursitis) or blood on aspiration (haemarthrosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chikungunya** gives symmetric, severe small-joint polyarthritis with fever and rash - polyarticular and symmetric, unlike pyogenic arthritis; see the Chikungunya, Zika and other arboviral fevers topic. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Charcot neuro-osteoarthropathy** imitates diabetic foot osteomyelitis (warm, red, swollen foot with bone destruction); Charcot is usually **mid-foot with intact skin**, whereas osteomyelitis lies **under an ulcer** in the forefoot or heel. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Bone tumours** (Ewing sarcoma, osteosarcoma, metastasis, myeloma) and **brucellar or fungal spondylitis** mimic osteomyelitis and TB spine - which is why a **tissue diagnosis** (biopsy for culture and histology) is pursued in vertebral lesions. [IDSA 2015]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line to advanced, with cut-offs`,points:[`**Joint aspiration before antibiotics** is the key test: synovial fluid for appearance, **cell count with differential, Gram stain, culture (inoculate into blood culture bottles to raise yield) and polarised microscopy for crystals**. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Synovial WBC over 50 000/mm3 with over 90% neutrophils** strongly suggests bacterial arthritis, but up to a third of septic joints have lower counts (gonococcal, immunosuppressed, early) - **counts under 50 000 do not exclude infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gram stain** is positive in about **50%** and **synovial culture in 70-90%** of non-gonococcal septic arthritis; **blood cultures** are positive in about 50% - always take **two sets before antibiotics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gonococcal arthritis**: synovial culture positive in under 50%; send **NAAT of urethra, cervix, rectum and pharynx** - it is the most sensitive way to confirm DGI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CRP and ESR** support diagnosis and, more usefully, track response; **procalcitonin is not sensitive enough** to rule out septic arthritis. In diabetic foot, **ESR over 70 mm/h** supports osteomyelitis. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Plain X-ray** is normal for the first **10-14 days** of osteomyelitis (30-50% bone loss is needed); later it shows periosteal reaction, lytic areas and sequestrum - still the first image and the baseline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MRI** is the imaging test of choice for osteomyelitis and spondylodiscitis (sensitivity over 90%); **ultrasound** detects a hip effusion in children and guides aspiration; CT shows sequestra and guides biopsy; FDG-PET/CT or labelled-leucocyte scan when MRI is not possible or metal causes artefact. [IDSA 2015]`,`**Bone biopsy** (percutaneous through intact skin, or at surgery) for **culture and histology** is the reference standard for diabetic foot osteomyelitis and is needed in vertebral osteomyelitis when **blood cultures are negative**; **swabs of sinus tracts are unreliable**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**PJI work-up**: serum CRP and ESR, then **aspiration**: chronic PJI if synovial **WBC over 3000/uL or neutrophils over 80%**; acute postoperative PJI uses higher cut-offs (WBC over 10 000/uL, neutrophils over 90%); **leukocyte esterase ++** and **alpha-defensin** support; at revision send **3-5 tissue samples** and **sonicate the explant**. [ICM 2018]`,`**TB spine**: MRI (subligamentous spread, paradiscal involvement, large paraspinal abscess with thin enhancing wall), then **CT-guided biopsy for CBNAAT/Truenat (with rifampicin resistance), liquid culture and histology** - NTEP expects microbiological confirmation where feasible. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`A **hot swollen joint is never treated with oral antibiotics in the OPD**: aspirate if competent and stable, take blood cultures, then **refer the same day** for washout - give the first IV dose before transfer only if transfer is delayed and samples are taken. [BSR/BOAST]`,`A child who **will not bear weight with fever** needs same-day referral for ultrasound, Kocher assessment and aspiration; do not label a febrile limp 'transient synovitis' without blood tests. [IDSA/PIDS 2023]`,`**Diabetic foot**: offload, debride callus and slough, check pulses and ABPI, X-ray the foot, probe the ulcer; **do not treat an uninfected ulcer with antibiotics**; mild soft-tissue infection - oral **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 8-hourly** or cephalexin 500 mg 6-hourly for **1-2 weeks**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Optimise **glycaemic control** (insulin when HbA1c is very high or there is infection), stop smoking, treat anaemia and nutrition - each changes healing. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Oral step-down after IV start** is now standard (OVIVA): once afebrile, clinically improving and CRP falling, switch to a well-absorbed oral agent chosen by culture - see the drug table; native septic arthritis usually needs **2-4 weeks** in total after drainage, longer if bone is involved. [OVIVA NEJM 2019]`,`Follow outpatients on oral therapy weekly at first with **CRP, CBC, LFT and creatinine** (linezolid - platelets; co-trimoxazole - potassium and creatinine; rifampicin - LFT and interactions). [OVIVA NEJM 2019]`,`**TB spine** confirmed or strongly suspected: start NTEP treatment (**2HRZE + 4HRE**, extended in bone and joint TB as below), notify on **Ni-kshay**, enrol for **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** nutrition support, and refer for spinal surgical opinion. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Septic arthritis**: urgent **joint drainage** - repeated needle aspiration (knee), **arthroscopic lavage**, or **open arthrotomy** (hip in children and adults, shoulder, prosthetic joints and failure of aspiration); the hip is always surgically drained. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Empirical IV therapy after samples** (Indian practice): **cefazolin 2 g IV 8-hourly** or cloxacillin 2 g IV 6-hourly for MSSA and streptococci; add **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly** (trough or AUC guided) if MRSA risk; **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily** in elderly, immunocompromised or Gram-negative risk; **ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily** for gonococcal arthritis. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`Narrow therapy to the **Gram stain and culture**: MSSA - cefazolin or cloxacillin; MRSA - vancomycin, linezolid or teicoplanin; streptococci - penicillin or ceftriaxone; Pseudomonas - ceftazidime or piperacillin-tazobactam; **melioidosis - ceftazidime 2 g 6-8-hourly or meropenem** then co-trimoxazole eradication. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Children (PIDS/IDSA 2021-2023)**: short IV (**2-4 days**) of cefazolin 100-150 mg/kg/day in 3 doses (or clindamycin 40 mg/kg/day in 3-4 doses if MRSA common), then oral **cephalexin 100-150 mg/kg/day in 3-4 doses**; total **2-3 weeks for septic arthritis, 3-4 weeks for osteomyelitis** if CRP falls. [IDSA/PIDS 2023]`,`**Acute haematogenous osteomyelitis** usually responds to antibiotics alone; surgery for **subperiosteal abscess, failure at 48-72 hours, sequestrum, or adjacent septic joint**. [IDSA/PIDS 2021]`,`**Vertebral osteomyelitis (IDSA 2015)**: in a neurologically intact, stable patient **withhold antibiotics until blood culture or biopsy** is obtained; treat **6 weeks** (non-inferior to 12 weeks - Bernard, Lancet 2015); **surgery for neurological deficit, epidural abscess with cord compression, instability or failure**. [IDSA 2015]`,`**Chronic osteomyelitis** is a surgical disease: **radical debridement** of sequestrum and dead tissue, dead-space management (antibiotic-loaded beads or carriers), stabilisation and soft-tissue cover, then **6 weeks** of culture-directed therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PJI surgery**: **DAIR** (debridement, antibiotics, implant retention with exchange of modular parts) if implant under 30 days or symptoms under 3 weeks, stable implant and good soft tissue; otherwise **one-stage or two-stage exchange**; with retained hardware add **rifampicin 450 mg 12-hourly** once wounds are dry and bacteraemia cleared, with a fluoroquinolone for staphylococci; treat **12 weeks** (DATIPO 2021 showed 6 weeks inferior). [ICM 2018]`,`**Diabetic foot osteomyelitis**: moderate or severe infection is admitted for IV therapy (for example **amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV 8-hourly**, or **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly** if Pseudomonas or severe), urgent surgical drainage of abscess or gas, vascular imaging and revascularisation. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: Kingella is susceptible to beta-lactams but **resistant to vancomycin and clindamycin** - remember it when culture is negative in a child under 4 years; send PCR of synovial fluid or throat. [IDSA/PIDS 2023]`,`**Neonates**: septic arthritis of the hip may be silent; cover S. aureus, GBS and Gram-negatives (**cloxacillin or cefazolin plus cefotaxime or gentamicin**); risk of growth disturbance is high. [IDSA/PIDS 2021]`,`**Sickle cell disease**: osteomyelitis vs bone infarction crisis is hard clinically; cover **Salmonella** (ceftriaxone or a fluoroquinolone) and get MRI and blood cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: beta-lactams are safe; avoid fluoroquinolones where alternatives exist, avoid tetracyclines after 15 weeks and avoid co-trimoxazole near term; rifampicin can be used for TB. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: dialysis-access bacteraemia seeds the spine and joints; dose **vancomycin by levels**, give cefazolin after dialysis, and avoid prolonged unmonitored aminoglycosides. [IDSA 2015]`,`**Elderly and rheumatoid arthritis**: atypical presentation, polyarticular disease and higher mortality; an RA flare in a single joint that is out of step with other joints is infection until aspiration says otherwise. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: when MRI is unavailable, serial X-ray, ultrasound, ESR/CRP trends and CT-guided biopsy; for TB spine, **CBNAAT on pus** from an aspirated cold abscess is often achievable at a district hospital. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Septic arthritis**: cartilage loss and **secondary osteoarthritis**, ankylosis, **avascular necrosis of the femoral head**, dislocation, growth arrest and limb-length discrepancy in children, and sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osteomyelitis**: chronicity (5-25% after acute haematogenous disease), **pathological fracture**, septic arthritis of adjacent joint, deep vein thrombosis in children with MRSA, and **Marjolin squamous carcinoma** in long-standing sinuses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Brodie abscess** is a localised, walled-off subacute metaphyseal abscess with a sclerotic rim on X-ray - an exam picture distinct from acute osteomyelitis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Vertebral infection**: **epidural abscess**, cord compression and paraplegia, kyphotic deformity, psoas abscess, and late instability; neurological deficit at diagnosis predicts poor recovery. [IDSA 2015]`,`**PJI**: recurrent infection, need for repeated revision, bone loss, arthrodesis or amputation; **DAIR succeeds in about 70-80%** when the criteria are met but much less when they are not. [ICM 2018]`,`**Diabetic foot osteomyelitis**: remission in about two-thirds with antibiotics alone in selected forefoot cases, but recurrence and **minor or major amputation** remain common; mortality after major amputation approaches 50% at 5 years. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Poor prognostic factors in septic arthritis: **age over 65, delay in drainage, S. aureus, polyarticular disease, prosthetic joint, rheumatoid arthritis and immunosuppression**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Same-day orthopaedic referral** for every suspected septic joint, febrile non-weight-bearing child, painful prosthetic joint with fever, and back pain with new neurological signs. [BSR/BOAST]`,`Track **CRP every 2-3 days** in hospital and weekly on oral therapy; a falling CRP (often by half in 3-4 days) supports oral switch, a rising CRP suggests undrained pus or a wrong drug. [IDSA/PIDS 2021]`,`Review **joint movement and function** after drainage; early physiotherapy (once pain allows) prevents stiffness and contracture. [BSR/BOAST]`,`Monitor drug toxicity: **vancomycin levels and creatinine**, linezolid (CBC weekly, peripheral and optic neuropathy beyond 4 weeks, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs), rifampicin (LFT, interactions with warfarin, DOACs, contraceptives). [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Remission of diabetic foot osteomyelitis** is judged at least **6 months after stopping** antibiotics; keep the patient in a high-risk foot clinic with footwear and offloading. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**TB spine**: clinical, ESR and neurological review monthly; MRI changes lag clinical healing, so **do not extend treatment only because MRI shows residual change**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Before arthroplasty**: screen and treat **nasal S. aureus carriage** (mupirocin plus chlorhexidine), optimise HbA1c, stop smoking, treat dental and urinary foci; give **cefazolin within 60 minutes before incision**. [ICM 2018]`,`**Aseptic technique for intra-articular injections** (no injection through skin infection, single-use vials) prevents iatrogenic septic arthritis - a real cause in Indian practice where repeated steroid injections are common. [BSR/BOAST]`,`**Diabetic foot prevention**: annual foot screening for neuropathy and pulses at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** under **NP-NCD**, daily foot inspection, proper footwear, nail care and prompt attention to any break in the skin. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Open fractures** need antibiotics within **1 hour** and early debridement - the main preventable cause of contiguous osteomyelitis after road traffic injuries. [BOAST]`,`**TB spine**: case finding under **NTEP** - any back pain with constitutional symptoms is presumptive TB; contact screening and TB preventive treatment for household contacts. [NTEP 2025]`,`Counsel patients that bone infection needs **weeks, not days**, of antibiotics; non-adherence and early stopping are the commonest causes of relapse. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**OVIVA (NEJM 2019)**: oral antibiotics in the first 6 weeks were **non-inferior to IV** for complex bone and joint infection (1-year failure about 13% oral vs 15% IV) - the basis for early oral switch and fewer PICC lines. [OVIVA NEJM 2019]`,`**DATIPO (NEJM 2021)**: for PJI, **6 weeks of antibiotics was inferior to 12 weeks**, especially after DAIR - so 12 weeks is now the standard. [DATIPO NEJM 2021]`,`**IWGDF/IDSA 2023** diabetic foot infection guideline: added **(O)** to the grade, endorsed probe-to-bone plus X-ray and MRI, bone biopsy for culture, and durations of **6 weeks without resection, 3 weeks after minor amputation with positive bone margin**, and 1-2 weeks for soft tissue. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**PIDS/IDSA 2021 (osteomyelitis) and 2023 (septic arthritis) paediatric guidelines** endorse **short IV courses with early oral step-down** and shorter total courses (2-3 weeks arthritis, 3-4 weeks osteomyelitis) when the child improves and CRP falls. [IDSA/PIDS 2023]`,`Adult native septic arthritis: a randomised trial (Gjika, Ann Rheum Dis 2019) found **2 weeks non-inferior to 4 weeks** after surgical drainage, supporting shorter courses in uncomplicated cases. [Gjika 2019]`,`**ICM 2018 and EBJIS 2021** definitions of PJI rely on weighted scores including **alpha-defensin and leukocyte esterase**; serum procalcitonin is not recommended. [ICM 2018]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: 'Approach to an acute hot swollen knee' - answer **aspirate first** (cell count, Gram stain, culture, crystals), blood cultures, then IV antibiotics and drainage. [BSR/BOAST]`,`Viva: **Kocher criteria** with the four cut-offs and their probabilities; and why the septic hip in a child is an emergency (intracapsular pressure, AVN). [Kocher 1999]`,`Viva: define **sequestrum, involucrum and cloaca**, and Brodie abscess. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Common mistake: **starting antibiotics before aspiration or blood culture**, which sterilises the joint and leaves weeks of therapy without an organism. [BSR/BOAST]`,`Common mistake: believing a **synovial WBC under 50 000 or the presence of crystals rules out sepsis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: swabbing a diabetic foot sinus and treating the swab result; **bone biopsy or deep tissue** is what guides therapy. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Know the **rifampicin rule**: never as monotherapy, start after bacteraemia clears and wounds are dry, only when hardware is retained. [ICM 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - bone and joint infections (adult doses unless stated)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV 8-hourly; child 100-150 mg/kg/day in 3 doses`,`Until afebrile, improving, CRP falling; then oral`,`Allergy (low cross-reactivity); preferred for MSSA in India where IV cloxacillin is scarce`],[`Cloxacillin / flucloxacillin`,`2 g IV 6-hourly`,`As above`,`Phlebitis, cholestatic hepatitis (flucloxacillin), penicillin allergy`],[`Vancomycin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly; loading 25-30 mg/kg in severe infection`,`AUC 400-600 or trough 15-20 mg/L`,`Nephrotoxicity (worse with piperacillin-tazobactam), infusion reaction; inactive against Kingella`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV once daily (1 g for gonococcal arthritis)`,`Gonococcal 7 days total with oral switch; others by culture`,`Biliary sludge; no anti-pseudomonal or MRSA activity`],[`Linezolid`,`600 mg oral or IV 12-hourly`,`Oral step-down; monitor CBC weekly; caution beyond 4 weeks`,`Thrombocytopenia, peripheral/optic neuropathy, lactic acidosis, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Rifampicin (adjunct)`,`450 mg oral 12-hourly (or 600 mg daily)`,`With retained implants for staphylococci, with a partner drug; PJI 12 weeks`,`Never alone (resistance); hepatitis, orange secretions, many interactions (warfarin, DOACs, OCPs, ART)`],[`Levofloxacin / ciprofloxacin`,`Levofloxacin 750 mg OD; ciprofloxacin 750 mg 12-hourly`,`Oral step-down for Gram-negatives or with rifampicin for staphylococci`,`Tendinopathy, QT prolongation, C. difficile, aortic aneurysm risk; avoid in pregnancy and children if alternative`],[`Co-trimoxazole`,`2 DS tablets (320/1600 mg) 12-hourly for bone`,`Oral step-down MSSA/MRSA; melioidosis eradication 12 weeks`,`Hyperkalaemia, creatinine rise, marrow suppression, rash/SJS; add folic acid`],[`Clindamycin`,`600 mg IV 8-hourly or 450 mg oral 6-hourly; child 40 mg/kg/day in 3-4 doses`,`Oral step-down if susceptible (D-test)`,`C. difficile diarrhoea; inactive against Kingella and Gram-negatives`],[`Cephalexin (children)`,`100-150 mg/kg/day oral in 3-4 doses`,`Total 2-3 weeks (arthritis), 3-4 weeks (osteomyelitis)`,`GI upset; high doses needed for bone`]]},{heading:`Synovial fluid interpretation`,columns:[`Feature`,`Normal / non-inflammatory`,`Inflammatory (crystal, RA, reactive)`,`Septic`],rows:[[`Appearance`,`Clear, viscous, straw`,`Cloudy, less viscous`,`Turbid or purulent`],[`WBC per mm3`,`Under 2000`,`2000-50 000`,`Usually over 50 000 (can be lower)`],[`Neutrophils`,`Under 25%`,`Often over 50%`,`Over 90%`],[`Glucose (vs blood)`,`Near equal`,`Mildly low`,`Very low (under 50% of blood)`],[`Crystals`,`None`,`Urate (needle, negative birefringence) or CPPD (rhomboid, positive)`,`Usually none - but coexist in up to 5%`],[`Gram stain / culture`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Gram stain positive about 50%; culture 70-90%`]]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,columns:[`System`,`Use`,`Categories`],rows:[[`Waldvogel`,`Mechanism of osteomyelitis`,`Haematogenous; contiguous focus; vascular insufficiency - each acute or chronic`],[`Cierny-Mader`,`Adult long-bone osteomyelitis`,`Anatomic I medullary, II superficial, III localised, IV diffuse; host A, B, C`],[`Kocher criteria`,`Septic hip vs transient synovitis in children`,`Non-weight-bearing, T over 38.5 C, ESR over 40, WBC over 12 000; 1 = 3%, 2 = 40%, 3 = 93%, 4 = 99%`],[`Zimmerli / timing`,`PJI`,`Early under 3 months; delayed 3-24 months; late over 24 months (haematogenous)`],[`ICM/MSIS 2018`,`PJI definition`,`Major: sinus tract or 2 positive cultures; minor weighted score (CRP, D-dimer, synovial WBC/PMN, LE, alpha-defensin, histology)`],[`IWGDF/IDSA 2023`,`Diabetic foot infection`,`1 uninfected, 2 mild, 3 moderate, 4 severe; add (O) if osteomyelitis`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of the acute hot joint`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Septic arthritis`,`Single hot joint, pain on any movement, risk factors`,`Synovial WBC, Gram stain, culture`],[`Gout`,`Middle-aged man, 1st MTP, diuretics, alcohol`,`Negatively birefringent needle crystals`],[`CPPD (pseudogout)`,`Elderly, knee or wrist, chondrocalcinosis`,`Positively birefringent rhomboid crystals`],[`Reactive arthritis`,`Oligoarthritis lower limb, recent diarrhoea or urethritis`,`Sterile fluid, HLA-B27, enteric or chlamydia history`],[`Gonococcal arthritis`,`Young, sexually active, tenosynovitis, skin pustules`,`NAAT from genital, rectal, pharyngeal sites`],[`Transient synovitis (child)`,`Age 3-8, afebrile or mild, can bear weight`,`Kocher 0-1, normal CRP`],[`Haemarthrosis`,`Trauma, anticoagulant, haemophilia`,`Blood on aspiration`]]},{heading:`Antibiotic duration by syndrome`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`Usual total duration`,`Evidence / note`],rows:[[`Native septic arthritis, adult`,`2-4 weeks after drainage (4-6 weeks if bone involved)`,`Gjika 2019: 2 weeks non-inferior to 4 after surgical drainage`],[`Septic arthritis, child`,`2-3 weeks (IV 2-4 days then oral)`,`PIDS/IDSA 2023`],[`Acute osteomyelitis, child`,`3-4 weeks`,`PIDS/IDSA 2021`],[`Vertebral osteomyelitis`,`6 weeks`,`IDSA 2015; Bernard Lancet 2015`],[`Diabetic foot osteomyelitis`,`6 weeks without resection; 3 weeks after minor amputation with positive margin`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023`],[`Prosthetic joint infection`,`12 weeks (DAIR or one-stage)`,`DATIPO NEJM 2021`],[`Spinal TB`,`6 months NTEP, extended to 9-12 months on specialist judgment`,`NTEP 2025; INDEX-TB 2016`]]}],redFlags:[`Any acute hot swollen joint, especially with fever, prosthesis, rheumatoid arthritis or immunosuppression - same-day aspiration and orthopaedic review`,`Febrile child who refuses to bear weight or move a limb (pseudoparalysis)`,`Back pain with fever, IV drug use, recent bacteraemia or new weakness, numbness or sphincter disturbance (epidural abscess)`,`Painful or discharging prosthetic joint, or a sinus over an implant`,`Diabetic foot with spreading cellulitis, crepitus or gas, SIRS, or critical ischaemia`,`Septic arthritis with sepsis or shock, or polyarticular involvement`,`Change in a long-standing osteomyelitis sinus (bleeding, foul growth) - suspect Marjolin ulcer`],pearls:[`Aspirate before antibiotics - the joint will not wait, but the culture is lost once the first dose is given.`,`Crystals do not exclude sepsis; a synovial WBC under 50 000 does not exclude sepsis.`,`Kocher: NWB, fever over 38.5, ESR over 40, WBC over 12 000 - two criteria give 40%, four give 99%.`,`Sickle cell - Salmonella; rubber sole puncture - Pseudomonas; under 4 years - Kingella; IVDU - sternoclavicular joint.`,`Probe-to-bone plus X-ray diagnoses diabetic foot osteomyelitis; bone biopsy guides the drug.`,`OVIVA: oral is as good as IV for bone and joint infection; DATIPO: PJI needs 12 weeks, not 6.`,`Rifampicin only for retained hardware, never alone, after bacteraemia clears.`,`Pyogenic spondylodiscitis destroys the disc early; TB spares the disc and sends a cold abscess under the ligaments.`,`Kingella is resistant to vancomycin and clindamycin - give a beta-lactam.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-q1`,stem:`A 6-year-old boy has a painful right hip for 1 day. Temperature 38.9 C, he refuses to stand, ESR 52 mm/h and WBC 14 500/mm3. Ultrasound shows a hip effusion. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Oral ibuprofen and review in 48 hours as transient synovitis`,`Urgent aspiration and surgical drainage of the hip with IV antibiotics after samples`,`MRI of the pelvis under sedation before any intervention`,`Oral cephalexin for 3 weeks and outpatient review`],answer:1,explanation:`All four Kocher criteria are met (non-weight-bearing, fever over 38.5 C, ESR over 40, WBC over 12 000), predicting about a 99% probability of septic arthritis; the intracapsular hip needs urgent aspiration and surgical drainage with IV antibiotics after cultures to prevent avascular necrosis. Treating as transient synovitis or with oral antibiotics alone delays drainage, and MRI should not delay surgery.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-q2`,stem:`A 68-year-old man with gout presents with an acutely hot left knee. Aspirate is turbid with WBC 38 000/mm3, 92% neutrophils and negatively birefringent needle crystals. Gram stain is negative. What should be done?`,options:[`Diagnose acute gout and start colchicine only`,`Inject intra-articular methylprednisolone to settle the flare`,`Send culture and blood cultures and treat as possible septic arthritis until cultures are negative`,`Start allopurinol 300 mg daily and discharge`],answer:2,explanation:`Crystals do not exclude infection (the two coexist in a few per cent), a negative Gram stain misses about half of septic joints, and a WBC under 50 000 does not rule sepsis out. Culture results are needed before an intra-articular steroid, which would be dangerous in a septic joint; colchicine alone or starting allopurinol in an acute flare ignores the infection risk.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-q3`,stem:`A 55-year-old diabetic has a 3 cm plantar ulcer under the second metatarsal head for 2 months. A sterile metal probe touches gritty bone, and X-ray shows cortical erosion. What best guides the choice of antibiotic?`,options:[`Superficial swab of the ulcer base`,`Swab of the sinus discharge`,`Serum procalcitonin`,`Bone biopsy for culture and histology`],answer:3,explanation:`IWGDF/IDSA 2023 recommends bone sampling (percutaneous or at surgery) for culture and histology to guide therapy of diabetic foot osteomyelitis; superficial and sinus swabs grow colonisers and correlate poorly with bone pathogens. Probe-to-bone plus X-ray already makes the diagnosis; procalcitonin does not identify the organism or guide drug choice.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-q4`,stem:`A 72-year-old woman develops fever and a painful, discharging right total knee replacement 2 weeks after surgery. The implant is stable. Synovial culture grows MSSA. Which plan is most appropriate?`,options:[`Debridement, antibiotics and implant retention, with IV then oral therapy including rifampicin for 12 weeks`,`Oral cephalexin for 2 weeks without surgery`,`Rifampicin 600 mg daily alone for 6 weeks`,`Immediate above-knee amputation`],answer:0,explanation:`An early (under 30 days) PJI with a stable implant and good soft tissue is suited to DAIR; with retained hardware a biofilm-active rifampicin combination is added once wounds are dry, and DATIPO showed 12 weeks beats 6 weeks. Antibiotics without surgery fail against biofilm, rifampicin monotherapy breeds resistance within days, and amputation is a last resort.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-q5`,stem:`In the OVIVA trial of complex bone and joint infection, what was the main finding?`,options:[`IV therapy for 6 weeks was superior to oral therapy`,`Oral therapy in the first 6 weeks was non-inferior to IV therapy for treatment failure at 1 year`,`Rifampicin monotherapy was as effective as combination therapy`,`Antibiotics could be stopped after 2 weeks in prosthetic joint infection`],answer:1,explanation:`OVIVA (NEJM 2019) randomised over 1000 adults and showed oral antibiotics in the first 6 weeks were non-inferior to IV for definitive treatment failure at 1 year, with fewer line complications and shorter stays. It did not show IV superiority, did not test rifampicin monotherapy (never used alone), and DATIPO, not OVIVA, studied PJI duration and found 12 weeks better than 6.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, diagnosis and management of acute septic arthritis in adults. Add a note on prosthetic joint infection.`,openingLines:[`Septic arthritis is bacterial infection of the joint space - an orthopaedic emergency in which cartilage is destroyed within days; mortality is 7-15% and a quarter to half of survivors lose joint function.`,`The principle is: aspirate and culture before antibiotics, drain the joint, give IV then early oral culture-directed antibiotics.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`S. aureus 50-70%; streptococci; gonococcus in the young; Gram-negatives in elderly, immunocompromised, IVDU (Pseudomonas); Salmonella in sickle cell; melioidosis in Indian diabetics.`,`Risk: RA, prosthesis, intra-articular injection, diabetes, CKD, age over 80, skin infection, bacteraemia.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Synovium lacks a basement membrane; neutrophil proteases and cytokines destroy cartilage in 24-48 h; raised intracapsular pressure causes AVN at the hip.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Acute painful, hot, swollen joint with pain on any passive movement; fever absent in up to half; DGI triad - polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, pustules.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Aspirate before antibiotics: WBC usually over 50 000 with over 90% PMN, Gram stain (50%), culture (70-90%), crystals.`,`Two blood cultures, CBC, CRP, ESR, creatinine; NAAT for gonococcus; X-ray baseline; ultrasound or MRI as needed.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Drainage: repeated aspiration, arthroscopic lavage or arthrotomy (hip, shoulder, prosthesis).`,`Empirical IV cefazolin 2 g 8-hourly or cloxacillin 2 g 6-hourly; add vancomycin if MRSA risk; ceftriaxone 2 g if Gram-negative risk; 1 g for gonococcus.`,`Oral step-down once improving (OVIVA); total 2-4 weeks after drainage; physiotherapy.`]},{heading:`Prosthetic joint infection`,points:[`Early under 3 months, delayed 3-24, late over 24 months; ICM 2018 definition (sinus tract or 2 positive cultures, weighted minor criteria).`,`DAIR if implant under 30 days or symptoms under 3 weeks; otherwise one- or two-stage exchange; rifampicin combination for staphylococci; 12 weeks (DATIPO).`]},{heading:`Complications and prevention`,points:[`Secondary OA, ankylosis, AVN, sepsis; prevent with aseptic injections, decolonisation and cefazolin prophylaxis before arthroplasty.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: hot swollen joint - aspirate - cell count/Gram/culture/crystals - drain - IV then oral`,`Synovial fluid interpretation table (normal, inflammatory, septic)`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prosthetic joint infection`,marks:2}],keywords:[`aspirate before antibiotics`,`synovial WBC 50 000`,`S. aureus`,`arthroscopic lavage`,`OVIVA`,`DAIR`,`DATIPO 12 weeks`,`rifampicin`]},{id:`infectious-fever-bone-joint-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on diabetic foot osteomyelitis.`,openingLines:[`Diabetic foot osteomyelitis is contiguous infection of bone beneath a diabetic foot ulcer, present in about 20% of moderate and over half of severe diabetic foot infections.`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023 grades it by adding (O) to the infection grade and bases treatment on bone culture.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical clues`,points:[`Non-healing ulcer over 6 weeks, ulcer over 2 cm2 or deep, exposed bone, sausage toe, positive probe-to-bone.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`X-ray (baseline, repeat in 2-4 weeks), ESR over 70 supports, MRI if doubt; bone biopsy for culture and histology; ABPI and vascular imaging.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Offloading, debridement, glycaemic control, revascularisation.`,`Antibiotics by bone culture: 6 weeks without resection; 3 weeks after minor amputation with positive margin; days only if all infected bone removed.`,`Surgery for abscess, gas, necrosis, exposed joint or failure.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Remission judged 6 months after stopping; high-risk foot clinic, footwear, annual NP-NCD foot screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Algorithm: ulcer - probe-to-bone + X-ray - MRI if doubt - bone biopsy - medical vs surgical`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and clinical clues`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`probe-to-bone`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023`,`3(O)`,`bone biopsy`,`6 weeks`,`offloading`,`Charcot`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infectious arthritis; Osteomyelitis; Infections of prosthetic joints`,`Li HK et al. Oral versus intravenous antibiotics for bone and joint infection (OVIVA). N Engl J Med 2019`,`Bernard L et al. Antibiotic therapy for 6 or 12 weeks for prosthetic joint infection (DATIPO). N Engl J Med 2021`,`IWGDF/IDSA Guidelines on the diagnosis and treatment of diabetes-related foot infections, 2023`,`IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for native vertebral osteomyelitis in adults, 2015`,`PIDS/IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines: acute haematogenous osteomyelitis in children (2021) and acute bacterial arthritis in children (2023)`,`Second International Consensus Meeting (ICM) on Musculoskeletal Infection - PJI definition, 2018`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2022; ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows 2023; NTEP Guidelines 2025`]}),s.push({id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other`,title:`Brain abscess, encephalitis and chronic meningitis`,oneLiner:`**Encephalitis** is inflammation of brain parenchyma with **altered mental status for 24 hours or more** plus fever, seizures, focal signs, CSF pleocytosis or imaging/EEG change - **HSV encephalitis** is the commonest treatable sporadic cause, so **IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly** is started on suspicion; **chronic meningitis** is meningitis lasting **4 weeks or more**, in India most often **tuberculous** (staged by BMRC and treated with 12 months of ATT plus dexamethasone) or **cryptococcal**; a **brain abscess** is a focal suppurative collection that needs **aspiration plus 4-8 weeks of antibiotics**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`HSV encephalitis`,`herpes simplex encephalitis acyclovir`,`autoimmune encephalitis`,`anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis`,`LGI1 faciobrachial dystonic seizures`,`acute encephalitis syndrome AES`,`tuberculous meningitis TBM`,`BMRC staging`,`Lancet consensus TBM score`,`cryptococcal meningitis`,`CrAg lateral flow`,`AMBITION liposomal amphotericin`,`brain abscess`,`ring-enhancing lesion`,`Streptococcus anginosus milleri`,`chronic meningitis`,`dexamethasone TBM`,`therapeutic lumbar puncture`,`Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra CSF`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Encephalitis (International Encephalitis Consortium 2013)**: major criterion - **altered mental status for 24 hours or more** with no other cause; plus **2 minor criteria for possible, 3 or more for probable** - fever 38 C or more within 72 hours, new seizures, new focal signs, **CSF WBC 5/mm3 or more**, imaging change, or EEG change. [IEC 2013]`,`**HSV encephalitis** affects about **1 in 250 000-500 000 people a year**, at all ages and seasons, with peaks in the young and over 50; it is the commonest sporadic fatal encephalitis, with **70% mortality untreated** and about **10-20% with acyclovir**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, **acute encephalitis syndrome (AES)** causes large seasonal outbreaks - **JE, scrub typhus, dengue, HSV, Nipah, Chandipura** and others; the epidemic picture is covered in the Leptospirosis, chikungunya, JE/AES topic, while this topic covers the sporadic and treatable individual encephalitides. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Autoimmune encephalitis** is now as common as infectious encephalitis in many series; **anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis** is the commonest form, mostly in young women and children. [Graus 2016]`,`**Chronic meningitis** is meningeal inflammation with CSF pleocytosis persisting **4 weeks or more**; in India **tuberculosis** causes the majority, followed by **cryptococcus** (largely in advanced HIV), with carcinomatous, syphilitic and inflammatory causes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tuberculous meningitis (TBM)** is about 1% of all TB but the deadliest form - **mortality 20-50%** and disability in half of survivors; India's huge TB burden (about 27% of global cases, India TB Report 2024) makes it a daily ward problem. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis** causes about **15-20% of AIDS deaths** worldwide; it appears when **CD4 is under 100 cells/uL**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess** has an incidence of about **0.4-0.9 per 100 000 per year**; mortality has fallen to about **10-15%** with MRI, stereotactic aspiration and modern antibiotics. [ESCMID 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**HSV encephalitis**: **HSV-1 in over 90% of adults** (reactivation or primary infection), HSV-2 in neonates and the immunosuppressed; **TLR3-pathway defects** predispose children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other infective encephalitis**: VZV (vasculopathy, immunosuppressed elderly), enteroviruses, **JE**, dengue, **scrub typhus**, rabies, **Nipah (Kerala)**, CMV and toxoplasma in HIV, and Mycoplasma. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Autoimmune encephalitis**: antibodies to neuronal surface antigens - **NMDAR** (ovarian teratoma in a third of young women; also **after HSV encephalitis**), **LGI1** and **CASPR2** (older men), GABA-B and AMPA (often paraneoplastic, small-cell lung cancer). [Graus 2016]`,`**Chronic meningitis**: **TB**, **cryptococcus**, syphilis, brucellosis, Lyme, histoplasmosis; **non-infective** - carcinomatous (breast, lung, lymphoma), sarcoidosis, Behcet, Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada, IgG4 disease and drugs (NSAIDs, IVIG). [Harrison 22e]`,`**TBM risk**: young children, **HIV**, malnutrition, diabetes, alcohol, steroids and anti-TNF therapy, and contact with sputum-positive TB. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Brain abscess sources**: **contiguous** (25-50%) - otitis media or mastoiditis (temporal lobe, cerebellum), sinusitis or dental infection (frontal lobe); **haematogenous** (about 25%, often multiple) - **cyanotic congenital heart disease (Tetralogy of Fallot)**, endocarditis, lung abscess, bronchiectasis, pulmonary AVM (HHT); **trauma or neurosurgery**; cryptogenic in 15-30%. [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Brain abscess organisms**: **viridans streptococci, especially the S. anginosus (milleri) group**, anaerobes (Bacteroides, Prevotella, Fusobacterium), **S. aureus after trauma or surgery**, Enterobacterales from the ear; **Nocardia, Toxoplasma, Aspergillus, Mucorales and TB** in the immunocompromised. [ESCMID 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**HSV** reaches the brain along the **olfactory and trigeminal pathways**, producing a **haemorrhagic, necrotising encephalitis of the medial temporal lobes, insula and orbitofrontal cortex** - hence smell and taste hallucinations, personality change, aphasia and temporal-lobe seizures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acyclovir** is phosphorylated by **viral thymidine kinase** and then host kinases into acyclovir triphosphate, which inhibits viral DNA polymerase - selective for infected cells; it is excreted by the kidney and **crystallises in tubules** if the patient is dry. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Post-HSV autoimmune encephalitis**: neuronal destruction exposes antigens, and about **a quarter of patients develop anti-NMDAR (or other) antibodies** within 2-16 weeks, causing relapse with chorea (children) or psychiatric change (adults) - not viral relapse. [Armangue 2018]`,`**Anti-NMDAR antibodies** crosslink and internalise synaptic NMDA receptors, producing a reversible receptor hypofunction - psychosis, catatonia, orofacial dyskinesia, autonomic instability and central hypoventilation; reversibility explains why immunotherapy works even after months. [Dalmau 2019]`,`**TBM** starts with a **Rich focus** (subpial or subependymal tubercle) that ruptures into the subarachnoid space; a thick **basal gelatinous exudate** causes (1) **hydrocephalus** (blocks CSF flow), (2) **vasculitis and infarcts** of perforating arteries (basal ganglia, internal capsule), and (3) **cranial nerve palsies** (VI, III, VII). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dexamethasone in TBM** dampens the inflammatory exudate and cytokine-driven vasculitis, reducing death without increasing disability (Thwaites 2004); it does not sterilise the CSF. [Thwaites NEJM 2004]`,`**Cryptococcus neoformans** is inhaled, disseminates when T-cell immunity fails, and its **polysaccharide capsule** blocks CSF reabsorption at arachnoid villi, causing **very high intracranial pressure** with little inflammation - so **therapeutic LP**, not steroids or acetazolamide, lowers pressure. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess** evolves through **early cerebritis (days 1-3), late cerebritis (4-9), early capsule (10-13) and late capsule (14 onward)**; a capsule forms more slowly on the ventricular side, so abscesses tend to **rupture into the ventricle** (ventriculitis, high mortality). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging that examiners ask`,points:[`**BMRC staging of TBM**: **Stage I** - GCS 15 with no focal deficit; **Stage II** - GCS 11-14, or GCS 15 with focal deficit; **Stage III** - GCS 10 or less. Mortality rises from about 10-20% in stage I to over 50% in stage III. [BMRC; Thwaites 2004]`,`**Lancet consensus TBM diagnostic score (Marais 2010)**: clinical, CSF, imaging and extra-neural TB criteria; **definite** = AFB, culture or NAAT positive in CSF; **probable** = score 10 or more without imaging or 12 or more with imaging; **possible** = 6-9 without or 6-11 with imaging. [Marais 2010]`,`**Graus 2016 criteria for possible autoimmune encephalitis**: subacute onset (under 3 months) of working-memory deficit, altered mental status or psychiatric symptoms, plus one of new focal CNS signs, unexplained seizures, CSF pleocytosis, or MRI features of encephalitis, with reasonable exclusion of other causes. [Graus 2016]`,`**Brain abscess stages** on imaging: cerebritis (poorly defined enhancement) vs **capsule stage (thin smooth ring with DWI restriction)** - capsular abscesses are aspirated; small cerebritis may be treated medically. [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Chronic meningitis** is classified by CSF profile: **lymphocytic with low glucose** (TB, fungal, carcinomatous, syphilis rarely), **lymphocytic with normal glucose** (viral, syphilis, Lyme, sarcoid, Behcet), **neutrophilic** (Nocardia, Actinomyces, early TB, partially treated bacterial) and **eosinophilic** (Angiostrongylus, Gnathostoma, cysticercosis, coccidioidomycosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis**: raised ICP is defined as opening pressure **25 cm H2O or more**; markers of poor outcome are altered mentation, high fungal burden (India ink, CrAg titre) and low CSF WBC. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**HSV encephalitis**: fever and headache over a few days, then **confusion, personality or behaviour change, aphasia, olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, focal or generalised seizures** and falling consciousness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-NMDAR encephalitis**: a viral-like prodrome, then **psychiatric symptoms** (often first seen by a psychiatrist), insomnia, memory loss, **orofacial dyskinesias**, seizures, catatonia, autonomic swings and hypoventilation. [Dalmau 2019]`,`**LGI1 encephalitis**: older man with **faciobrachial dystonic seizures** (brief arm and face jerks many times a day), memory loss and **hyponatraemia**. [Graus 2016]`,`**TBM**: prodrome of **2-8 weeks** - low-grade fever, malaise, headache, vomiting, personality change - then neck stiffness, cranial nerve palsy (**VI nerve commonest**), hemiparesis, seizures and coma; children may present with poor feeding and irritability. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis**: indolent **headache over 2-4 weeks** with fever, blurred or double vision and confusion in a person with advanced HIV; neck stiffness is often absent because inflammation is minimal. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess**: **headache (about 70%)** is the commonest symptom; the classic **triad of fever, headache and focal deficit occurs in only about 20%**; seizures in a quarter; sudden worsening of headache and meningism suggests ventricular rupture. [ESCMID 2024]`,`Always ask about the **source**: ear discharge, sinusitis, dental work, congenital heart disease, endocarditis risk, head injury, neurosurgery, HIV risk, TB contact, travel, animal exposure and **new psychiatric symptoms in a young woman**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`Record **GCS** (it stages TBM and predicts outcome), **pupils, fundi (papilloedema)** and focal signs before any LP. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Neck stiffness and Kernig sign** suggest meningeal involvement (TBM, bacterial, crypto with less inflammation); their absence does not exclude chronic meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cranial nerve palsies** - VI (raised ICP, false localising), III and VII - with hemiparesis from basal ganglia infarcts are the signature of TBM's basal exudate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Choroid tubercles** on fundoscopy point to miliary TB with TBM; **papilloedema and visual loss** in crypto mean dangerously high ICP needing urgent therapeutic LP. [WHO 2022]`,`**Search for a source**: otoscopy (chronic suppurative otitis), sinus tenderness, dental caries, **cyanosis and clubbing (cyanotic heart disease)**, murmurs (endocarditis), chest signs (lung abscess, bronchiectasis). [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Skin and systemic clues**: oral thrush and wasting (advanced HIV), eschar (scrub typhus encephalitis), vesicular rash (VZV), lymphadenopathy, and molluscum-like umbilicated papules (disseminated cryptococcosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dyskinesias, catatonia and autonomic instability** in an encephalopathic young patient point to anti-NMDAR encephalitis; examine the pelvis with ultrasound for teratoma. [Dalmau 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Septic, metabolic and toxic encephalopathy** (hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, uraemia, hepatic, Wernicke, drugs) has a normal CSF and no focal MRI lesion - always check glucose, sodium and thiamine first. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral malaria** in a patient from an endemic area - rule out with smear or RDT in every febrile encephalopathy in India. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Autoimmune vs infectious encephalitis**: autoimmune has subacute onset over weeks, prominent psychiatric features, dyskinesias or faciobrachial seizures, mild CSF pleocytosis and negative PCR; antibody tests in **serum and CSF** settle it. [Graus 2016]`,`**Partially treated bacterial meningitis** mimics TBM (lymphocytic CSF, low glucose) - a short history, a prior antibiotic course and rapid response to ceftriaxone favour it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ring-enhancing lesions** in India: **tuberculoma, neurocysticercosis**, brain abscess, toxoplasmosis (HIV), primary CNS lymphoma, metastasis, glioblastoma - **DWI restriction (low ADC) in the cavity** favours abscess; a scolex favours cysticercosis (see the Tissue helminths topic). [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Carcinomatous meningitis** gives chronic lymphocytic meningitis with low glucose and multiple cranial neuropathies - diagnosed by **large-volume repeated CSF cytology** and MRI leptomeningeal enhancement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-convulsive status epilepticus** and primary psychiatric illness are excluded by **EEG** in any unexplained encephalopathy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line to advanced, with cut-offs`,points:[`**CT before LP** if immunocompromised, known CNS disease, new seizure, papilloedema, GCS under 10 (or falling) or focal deficit - but **never delay acyclovir or antibiotics** for imaging. [IDSA 2008]`,`**CSF in HSV encephalitis**: lymphocytic pleocytosis (10-500 cells), raised protein, normal glucose, red cells; **HSV PCR sensitivity about 96%, specificity 99%** - but it can be negative in the first 72 hours, so **repeat at 3-7 days** if suspicion persists. [ABN/BIA 2012]`,`**MRI with DWI and FLAIR** shows **asymmetric medial temporal, insular and cingulate hyperintensity** in HSV; **EEG** shows temporal slowing or **lateralised periodic discharges**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autoimmune encephalitis panel**: NMDAR, LGI1, CASPR2, GABA-B, AMPA in **serum and CSF** (CSF more sensitive for NMDAR); pelvic ultrasound or MRI for ovarian teratoma; CT chest for lung cancer in older patients. [Graus 2016]`,`**CSF in TBM**: opening pressure raised, **lymphocytic pleocytosis 100-500 cells**, **protein 1-5 g/L**, **CSF:blood glucose ratio under 0.5**; **ADA over 10 U/L** supports TBM but is not specific. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TBM microbiology**: send **at least 6 mL of CSF** for **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** (WHO-preferred initial test, sensitivity about 70% vs culture), liquid culture (MGIT) and AFB smear (sensitivity under 20%); a **negative Ultra does not exclude TBM**. [WHO 2024]`,`**TBM imaging**: **basal meningeal enhancement, hydrocephalus, basal ganglia infarcts and tuberculomas**; chest X-ray shows active or miliary TB in about half. [Marais 2010]`,`**Cryptococcal tests**: **CrAg lateral flow assay** on CSF or serum (sensitivity and specificity over 99%), India ink (sensitivity 70-90%), culture; always measure **opening pressure**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess**: **MRI with DWI** (bright DWI, low ADC in the cavity) distinguishes abscess from necrotic tumour; **LP is contraindicated** (herniation risk, low yield); blood cultures and **pus from aspiration** for Gram stain, aerobic and anaerobic culture, AFB, fungal culture and 16S PCR. [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Chronic meningitis work-up**: large-volume CSF (repeat 3 times), cytology and flow cytometry, VDRL, CrAg, Xpert Ultra, fungal and AFB culture, brucella serology, HIV, ACE, MRI with contrast, and **meningeal or brain biopsy** when unresolved; **metagenomic next-generation sequencing** where available. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Any fever with **confusion, behaviour change, new seizure or focal deficit** is referred as an emergency; check **capillary glucose** and treat hypoglycaemia before transfer. [Harrison 22e]`,`If transfer will be delayed and encephalitis is suspected, give the first dose of **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV** (and ceftriaxone 2 g IV if bacterial meningitis is possible) - early treatment is the main determinant of outcome. [ABN/BIA 2012]`,`Screen every patient with HIV and **CD4 under 100 (WHO) or under 200 (consider)** for **serum CrAg**; if positive without symptoms and CSF normal, give pre-emptive **fluconazole 800 mg daily for 2 weeks, then 400 mg for 8 weeks, then 200 mg** maintenance. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess prevention**: treat chronic suppurative otitis and dental sepsis, and give endocarditis care to children with cyanotic heart disease - a child with Tetralogy and headache plus fever is a brain abscess until imaged. [ESCMID 2024]`,`**After discharge on ATT for TBM**: monthly review of adherence (99DOTS or family DOT), weight, liver function symptoms, vision (ethambutol), hearing and neurological recovery; notify on **Ni-kshay**. [NTEP 2025]`,`Arrange **rehabilitation**: physiotherapy, speech therapy, neuropsychology and anti-seizure follow-up for survivors of HSV, TBM and abscess. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**ABCs, airway protection** if GCS 8 or less, seizure control (levetiracetam or phenytoin loading, benzodiazepine for status), treat raised ICP (head up 30 degrees, osmotherapy for herniation) and correct sodium. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** (infused over 1 hour, with good hydration, dose by ideal body weight and adjusted for creatinine clearance) for **14 days, 21 days if immunocompromised**; repeat CSF PCR at the end if still unwell and continue until negative. [ABN/BIA 2012]`,`**Neonatal HSV**: acyclovir **20 mg/kg IV 8-hourly for 21 days**; children 3 months-12 years **500 mg/m2 8-hourly**. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Autoimmune encephalitis**: first-line **methylprednisolone 1 g IV daily for 5 days**, **IVIG 2 g/kg over 5 days** or plasma exchange; second-line **rituximab** or cyclophosphamide if no response by 2 weeks; **remove the teratoma**. [Dalmau 2019]`,`**TBM**: start ATT on clinical suspicion - **NTEP: 2 months HRZE then 10 months HRE (12 months total)** at weight-band doses - plus **dexamethasone** (Thwaites): 0.4 mg/kg/day IV for stage II-III (0.3 mg/kg for stage I) tapered over **6-8 weeks**; obstructive hydrocephalus needs **VP shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy**. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis (WHO 2022)**: induction with a **single dose of liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** plus **14 days of flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day** (4 divided doses) and **fluconazole 1200 mg/day**; then consolidation fluconazole **800 mg/day for 8 weeks**, maintenance **200 mg/day**. [WHO 2022]`,`Where liposomal amphotericin is unavailable (common in Indian district hospitals): **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1 mg/kg/day plus flucytosine for 1 week**, then fluconazole 1200 mg/day for week 2; give saline preloading and potassium and magnesium supplements. [WHO 2022]`,`**Raised ICP in crypto**: if opening pressure is **25 cm H2O or more** or symptoms, do **therapeutic LPs** daily to reduce pressure by 50% or below 20 cm; **steroids, acetazolamide and mannitol are not used**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess**: **stereotactic aspiration or excision** for lesions over about 2.5 cm, mass effect or diagnostic need; empirical **ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly (or cefotaxime 2 g 4-6-hourly) plus metronidazole 500 mg IV 8-hourly**, adding **vancomycin** after trauma or neurosurgery and meropenem for ear-related Pseudomonas; total **6-8 weeks**, with oral step-down possible after 2 weeks of IV. [ESCMID 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HIV**: in TBM, start ART **4-8 weeks after ATT** (earlier risks fatal IRIS); in crypto, start ART **4-6 weeks after antifungal** therapy (COAT trial showed higher mortality with early ART). [WHO 2022]`,`**HIV and TBM steroids**: the ACT HIV trial (NEJM 2023) found **no survival benefit of dexamethasone** in HIV-positive TBM; many clinicians still use it in severe disease, but evidence is weaker than in HIV-negative patients. [ACT HIV 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: acyclovir is safe; first-line ATT (HRZE) is safe; amphotericin and fluconazole - avoid high-dose fluconazole in the first trimester where possible; flucytosine is teratogenic in animals and used only when benefit outweighs risk. [WHO 2022]`,`**CKD**: acyclovir needs dose reduction (for creatinine clearance 25-50 mL/min every 12 hours; 10-25 every 24 hours) to avoid neurotoxicity; amphotericin needs daily creatinine and electrolytes. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Children**: WHO 2022 allows a **6-month intensive regimen (high-dose H, R, Z and ethionamide)** for children and adolescents with drug-susceptible TBM as an alternative to 12 months; and brain abscess in Indian children is commonly due to **cyanotic heart disease or ear infection**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Elderly**: HSV encephalitis may mimic stroke or delirium; LGI1 encephalitis is mistaken for dementia or functional seizures. [Graus 2016]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: no MRI - CT with contrast; no PCR - start acyclovir and do not stop until an alternative diagnosis is made; no Xpert - treat TBM on clinical score (probable or possible) rather than wait. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**HSV encephalitis**: memory loss (Kluver-Bucy and Korsakoff-like syndromes), epilepsy, aphasia and personality change in most survivors; **delay in acyclovir over 48 hours** and age over 30 with low GCS predict poor outcome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acyclovir toxicity**: crystal nephropathy and AKI (up to 20%), neurotoxicity (confusion, tremor) in renal failure - prevent with hydration and dose adjustment. [IDSA 2008]`,`**TBM**: hydrocephalus (up to 75% in children), **infarcts and hemiplegia**, cranial nerve palsies, **hyponatraemia** (SIADH or cerebral salt wasting), optochiasmatic arachnoiditis, **paradoxical tuberculoma enlargement** during treatment, and spinal arachnoiditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcal**: blindness and death from untreated raised ICP, **IRIS** after ART, relapse with poor fluconazole adherence. [WHO 2022]`,`**Brain abscess**: **intraventricular rupture** (mortality up to 80%), herniation, epilepsy in up to a third of survivors, and recurrence if the source is not treated. [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Autoimmune encephalitis**: about **80% of anti-NMDAR patients recover substantially** with immunotherapy and tumour removal, but recovery takes months and relapses occur in about 12%. [Dalmau 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`All encephalitis, TBM, crypto and brain abscess cases are managed in hospital with **neurology, neurosurgery and ICU** access; refer from district level after the first doses. [Harrison 22e]`,`Monitor on acyclovir: **creatinine every 2-3 days**, urine output, fluid balance; on amphotericin: **creatinine, potassium and magnesium daily**, haemoglobin weekly; on flucytosine: CBC twice weekly. [WHO 2022]`,`Monitor TBM: daily GCS and sodium early, LFT at 2 weeks and monthly, visual acuity and colour vision (ethambutol), repeat imaging for deterioration (hydrocephalus, tuberculoma). [NTEP 2025]`,`**Brain abscess**: repeat MRI or CT **every 1-2 weeks** and at the end of therapy; enlargement or failure to shrink means re-aspiration. [ESCMID 2024]`,`**Crypto maintenance**: continue fluconazole 200 mg until on ART at least 1 year with **CD4 over 100 and suppressed viral load** (or CD4 over 200). [WHO 2022]`,`Long-term follow-up of survivors: **neuropsychology, anti-seizure medication review**, school or work rehabilitation; tumour surveillance for at least 2 years in seronegative-teratoma NMDAR encephalitis. [Dalmau 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**JE vaccination** under UIP in endemic districts, scrub typhus and malaria control, and AES surveillance under **NCVBDC and IDSP** reduce the burden of acute encephalitis. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**TBM prevention**: **BCG at birth** (protects young children against TBM and miliary TB), early case finding, and **TB preventive treatment** for household contacts and people with HIV under NTEP. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Advanced HIV disease package (NACO)**: CD4 testing, **CrAg screening**, TB-LAM, co-trimoxazole and rapid ART start prevent cryptococcal and TB meningitis deaths. [NACO 2021]`,`Prompt treatment of **chronic otitis media, sinusitis and dental infection**, and early surgery for cyanotic congenital heart disease, prevent brain abscess. [ESCMID 2024]`,`Notify **AES, JE, Nipah and TB** on IDSP-IHIP or Ni-kshay; counsel families about prolonged recovery, seizures and the need for adherence to 12 months of ATT. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2022 cryptococcal guideline** (based on **AMBITION-cm, NEJM 2022**): single high-dose liposomal amphotericin 10 mg/kg plus 14 days flucytosine and fluconazole is preferred induction - non-inferior to 7 days of amphotericin with fewer adverse events. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2022 TB consolidated guidelines (children)**: a **6-month intensive regimen (6HRZEto)** is an option for drug-susceptible TBM in children and adolescents. [WHO 2022]`,`**ACT HIV (NEJM 2023)**: adjunctive dexamethasone did not reduce mortality in HIV-positive TBM; LAST-ACT is testing dexamethasone in HIV-negative patients by LTA4H genotype. [ACT HIV 2023]`,`**WHO 2024 rapid diagnostics update** keeps **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** as the initial test on CSF for TBM; NTEP has rolled out Truenat and Xpert to district level. [WHO 2024]`,`**ESCMID 2024 brain abscess guideline**: neurosurgical aspiration or excision whenever feasible, ceftriaxone or cefotaxime plus metronidazole empirically, and **early oral step-down after about 2 weeks IV** in selected patients, with total 6-8 weeks. [ESCMID 2024]`,`Autoimmune encephalitis is now in every encephalitis algorithm: **send NMDAR antibodies when HSV PCR is negative** or when a post-HSV relapse occurs. [Graus 2016]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: 'Approach to fever with altered sensorium' - glucose, malaria smear, CT if indicated, LP, **acyclovir plus ceftriaxone empirically**, then targeted tests. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **why acyclovir is started before PCR** (mortality 70% untreated; delay over 48 hours worsens outcome; PCR can be negative early). [ABN/BIA 2012]`,`Viva: **BMRC TBM staging** and the dexamethasone dose by stage; CSF findings in TBM vs bacterial vs viral vs crypto (table). [Thwaites NEJM 2004]`,`Viva: **why steroids are not given in cryptococcal meningitis** (CryptoDex 2016: higher disability and slower fungal clearance) and why therapeutic LP is. [WHO 2022]`,`Common mistake: doing an **LP in a patient with a brain abscess** or with signs of herniation. [ESCMID 2024]`,`Common mistake: labelling a young woman with new psychosis, seizures and dyskinesia as **'conversion'** - think anti-NMDAR encephalitis. [Dalmau 2019]`,`Remember the **source map of brain abscess**: ear - temporal lobe and cerebellum; sinus and teeth - frontal; blood - multiple at the grey-white junction. [ESCMID 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - encephalitis, TBM, cryptococcal meningitis and brain abscess`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Acyclovir`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly over 1 h (neonate 20 mg/kg; child 500 mg/m2)`,`14 days; 21 days if immunocompromised or neonate; repeat PCR`,`Crystal nephropathy (hydrate), neurotoxicity in renal failure; adjust for CrCl`],[`HRZE then HRE (NTEP weight bands)`,`Isoniazid 5, rifampicin 10, pyrazinamide 25, ethambutol 15 mg/kg daily oral`,`2 months HRZE + 10 months HRE (12 months)`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy (add pyridoxine), optic neuritis, gout, drug interactions`],[`Dexamethasone (TBM)`,`0.4 mg/kg/day IV stage II-III; 0.3 mg/kg/day stage I`,`IV 4 weeks then oral taper over 4 weeks (6-8 weeks total)`,`Hyperglycaemia, GI bleed; benefit unproven in HIV-positive (ACT HIV 2023)`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`10 mg/kg IV single dose (induction)`,`Once, with flucytosine and fluconazole for 14 days`,`Infusion reactions, hypokalaemia, nephrotoxicity (less than deoxycholate)`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`1 mg/kg/day IV with 1 L saline preload`,`7 days with flucytosine (if liposomal unavailable)`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, anaemia, rigors`],[`Flucytosine`,`100 mg/kg/day oral in 4 divided doses`,`14 days (induction)`,`Marrow suppression, hepatitis; reduce in renal failure`],[`Fluconazole (crypto)`,`1200 mg/day induction; 800 mg/day consolidation; 200 mg/day maintenance`,`2 weeks; 8 weeks; until CD4 over 100 with suppressed VL for 1 year`,`QT prolongation, hepatitis, interactions; teratogenic at high dose`],[`Ceftriaxone + metronidazole (abscess)`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly + metronidazole 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,`6-8 weeks total; oral step-down after about 2 weeks in selected cases`,`Biliary sludge; metronidazole neuropathy, disulfiram reaction`],[`Methylprednisolone / IVIG (autoimmune)`,`Methylprednisolone 1 g IV daily x 5 days; IVIG 2 g/kg over 5 days`,`Second line rituximab if no response by 2 weeks`,`Steroid hyperglycaemia; IVIG thrombosis, renal failure, aseptic meningitis`]]},{heading:`CSF differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Cells per mm3 (type)`,`Protein`,`Glucose (CSF:blood)`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Normal`,`0-5 (lymphocytes)`,`0.15-0.45 g/L`,`Over 0.6`,`-`],[`Bacterial meningitis`,`Hundreds to thousands (neutrophils)`,`Over 1 g/L`,`Under 0.4`,`Gram stain, culture, antigen`],[`Viral / HSV encephalitis`,`10-500 (lymphocytes), red cells in HSV`,`Mildly raised`,`Normal`,`HSV-1/2, VZV, enterovirus PCR`],[`Tuberculous meningitis`,`100-500 (lymphocytes; neutrophils early)`,`1-5 g/L`,`Under 0.5`,`Xpert Ultra, MGIT culture, ADA over 10`],[`Cryptococcal meningitis`,`0-100 (lymphocytes), often very few in AIDS`,`Mildly raised`,`Low or normal`,`CrAg LFA, India ink; opening pressure high`],[`Autoimmune encephalitis`,`Normal or mild lymphocytosis`,`Normal or mild rise`,`Normal`,`NMDAR and other antibodies in CSF and serum`]]},{heading:`Staging and diagnostic criteria`,columns:[`System`,`Use`,`Content`],rows:[[`IEC 2013`,`Encephalitis definition`,`Altered mental status 24 h or more + 2 (possible) or 3 (probable) minor criteria`],[`BMRC`,`TBM severity`,`I: GCS 15, no focal deficit; II: GCS 11-14 or 15 with deficit; III: GCS 10 or less`],[`Lancet consensus (Marais 2010)`,`TBM diagnosis`,`Definite: CSF AFB/culture/NAAT; probable 10+ (12+ with imaging); possible 6-9 (6-11)`],[`Graus 2016`,`Possible autoimmune encephalitis`,`Subacute memory, mental or psychiatric change + focal signs, seizures, CSF pleocytosis or MRI change`],[`Abscess stages`,`Imaging and timing`,`Early cerebritis 1-3 d, late cerebritis 4-9 d, early capsule 10-13 d, late capsule 14 d onward`]]},{heading:`Brain abscess: source, site and organism`,columns:[`Source`,`Usual site`,`Likely organisms`,`Empirical add-on`],rows:[[`Otitis media / mastoiditis`,`Temporal lobe, cerebellum`,`Streptococci, anaerobes, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas`,`Consider ceftazidime or meropenem for Pseudomonas`],[`Sinusitis / dental`,`Frontal lobe`,`S. anginosus group, anaerobes, Haemophilus`,`Ceftriaxone + metronidazole`],[`Haematogenous (CHD, endocarditis, lung)`,`Multiple, grey-white junction, MCA territory`,`Streptococci, S. aureus, anaerobes`,`Add vancomycin if S. aureus suspected`],[`Trauma / neurosurgery`,`Near wound or operation site`,`S. aureus (MRSA), Gram-negatives`,`Vancomycin + meropenem or cefepime`],[`Immunocompromised (HIV, transplant)`,`Variable, often multiple`,`Toxoplasma, Nocardia, Aspergillus, Mucorales, TB`,`Pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine, co-trimoxazole, voriconazole or amphotericin by cause`]]},{heading:`Infectious vs autoimmune encephalitis`,columns:[`Feature`,`HSV encephalitis`,`Anti-NMDAR encephalitis`,`LGI1 encephalitis`],rows:[[`Patient`,`Any age`,`Young women, children`,`Older men`],[`Onset`,`Days`,`Weeks, psychiatric first`,`Weeks to months`],[`Hallmark`,`Fever, aphasia, temporal seizures`,`Psychosis, dyskinesia, autonomic instability`,`Faciobrachial dystonic seizures, hyponatraemia`],[`MRI`,`Asymmetric temporal, insular, haemorrhagic`,`Often normal`,`Medial temporal T2 change`],[`Test`,`CSF HSV PCR`,`CSF NMDAR antibody; look for teratoma`,`Serum LGI1 antibody`],[`Treatment`,`Acyclovir 14-21 days`,`Steroids, IVIG/PLEX, rituximab, tumour removal`,`Steroids, IVIG`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with confusion, behaviour change, new seizure or focal deficit - start acyclovir and refer`,`Falling GCS, unequal pupils or papilloedema (herniation or raised ICP)`,`Headache with fever in a child with cyanotic congenital heart disease or chronic ear discharge (brain abscess)`,`HIV with CD4 under 100 and headache or visual blurring (cryptococcal meningitis)`,`Subacute headache, vomiting and cranial nerve palsy over weeks (TBM)`,`Sudden worsening of headache with meningism in a known brain abscess (ventricular rupture)`,`Young patient with new psychosis plus seizures, dyskinesia or autonomic instability (autoimmune encephalitis)`],pearls:[`Suspect HSV - start acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly now; PCR can be negative in the first 72 hours.`,`HSV loves the temporal lobe: smell hallucinations, aphasia, temporal seizures, haemorrhagic MRI change.`,`A relapse 2-16 weeks after HSV encephalitis is usually anti-NMDAR autoimmunity, not viral relapse.`,`BMRC TBM: I = GCS 15 no deficit; II = 11-14 or deficit; III = 10 or less.`,`TBM triad of complications: hydrocephalus, vasculitic infarcts, cranial nerve palsies (VI commonest).`,`Crypto: CrAg LFA over 99% sensitive; lower pressure by LP, never by steroids.`,`AMBITION: one dose of liposomal amphotericin 10 mg/kg plus flucytosine and fluconazole.`,`Brain abscess: triad in only 20%; never LP; DWI bright means pus.`,`Delay ART 4-6 weeks in crypto and 4-8 weeks in TBM to avoid fatal IRIS.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man has 3 days of fever, then confusion, olfactory hallucinations and a focal seizure. CSF: 80 lymphocytes/mm3, protein 0.8 g/L, normal glucose, 200 red cells. What is the most appropriate immediate step?`,options:[`Wait for the CSF HSV PCR result before any antiviral`,`Start IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly immediately`,`Start oral valacyclovir 1 g three times daily`,`Give IV methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 5 days`],answer:1,explanation:`This is classic HSV encephalitis (temporal-lobe features, lymphocytic CSF with red cells). IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly must start on suspicion, because untreated mortality is about 70% and delay worsens outcome; PCR may be negative in the first 72 hours. Oral valacyclovir does not reach adequate CNS levels for initial therapy, and steroids are first-line for autoimmune, not viral, encephalitis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has 4 weeks of headache and vomiting, now drowsy with a right VI nerve palsy; GCS 13. CSF: 250 cells (80% lymphocytes), protein 2.4 g/L, glucose ratio 0.3. What is her BMRC stage?`,options:[`Stage I`,`Stage III`,`Stage II`,`Stage cannot be assigned without MRI`],answer:2,explanation:`The CSF picture is tuberculous meningitis. BMRC stage II is GCS 11-14, or GCS 15 with a focal deficit; her GCS of 13 places her in stage II. Stage I requires GCS 15 without focal deficit and stage III is GCS 10 or less. Staging is clinical and does not need imaging; she needs ATT plus dexamethasone 0.4 mg/kg/day.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old man with HIV (CD4 45 cells/uL) has cryptococcal meningitis with opening pressure 34 cm H2O. According to WHO 2022, which induction regimen is preferred?`,options:[`Fluconazole 400 mg daily alone for 8 weeks`,`Amphotericin B deoxycholate alone for 2 weeks plus dexamethasone`,`Voriconazole plus caspofungin for 14 days`,`Single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg with 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole 1200 mg/day`],answer:3,explanation:`WHO 2022, based on AMBITION-cm, prefers a single 10 mg/kg dose of liposomal amphotericin with 14 days of flucytosine and high-dose fluconazole, plus therapeutic LPs for the raised pressure. Fluconazole alone at low dose has high mortality, dexamethasone worsens outcome in cryptococcal meningitis (CryptoDex), and echinocandins have no activity against Cryptococcus.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-q4`,stem:`A 10-year-old boy with uncorrected Tetralogy of Fallot has fever, headache and left hemiparesis. CT shows a 3.5 cm ring-enhancing lesion in the right frontoparietal region. Which step is contraindicated?`,options:[`Lumbar puncture for CSF culture`,`Stereotactic aspiration of the lesion`,`IV ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`Blood cultures`],answer:0,explanation:`Cyanotic congenital heart disease with a ring-enhancing lesion is a brain abscess. Lumbar puncture is contraindicated because of herniation risk and adds little diagnostic yield. A lesion over 2.5 cm should be aspirated for culture and decompression, empirical ceftriaxone plus metronidazole covers streptococci and anaerobes, and blood cultures are always appropriate.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-q5`,stem:`Five weeks after successful treatment of HSV encephalitis, a 25-year-old woman develops new agitation, psychosis and orofacial dyskinesias. Repeat CSF HSV PCR is negative. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Acyclovir-resistant HSV relapse`,`Acyclovir neurotoxicity`,`Post-HSV anti-NMDA receptor autoimmune encephalitis`,`Primary psychotic disorder`],answer:2,explanation:`About a quarter of HSV encephalitis survivors develop neuronal-surface antibodies, usually anti-NMDAR, 2-16 weeks later, with psychiatric change and dyskinesias; the PCR is negative and it responds to immunotherapy. Viral relapse would show a positive PCR and new necrosis, acyclovir neurotoxicity occurs during therapy with renal failure, and dyskinesias argue against a primary psychosis.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the clinical features, diagnosis, staging and management of tuberculous meningitis. Add a note on its complications.`,openingLines:[`Tuberculous meningitis is the most lethal form of TB - a subacute basal meningitis with 20-50% mortality, caused by rupture of a Rich focus into the subarachnoid space.`,`Outcome depends on the stage at which treatment starts, so ATT plus dexamethasone is begun on clinical suspicion.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Rich focus ruptures; thick basal exudate causes hydrocephalus, vasculitis with infarcts, and cranial nerve palsies.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Prodrome 2-8 weeks of fever, headache, vomiting, personality change; then meningism, VI nerve palsy, hemiparesis, seizures, coma.`]},{heading:`BMRC staging`,points:[`I: GCS 15 no deficit; II: GCS 11-14 or 15 with deficit; III: GCS 10 or less.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CSF: lymphocytes 100-500, protein 1-5 g/L, glucose ratio under 0.5, ADA over 10; Xpert Ultra, MGIT culture.`,`MRI: basal enhancement, hydrocephalus, infarcts, tuberculomas; chest X-ray; HIV test; Lancet consensus score.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`NTEP 2HRZE + 10HRE (12 months); dexamethasone 0.4 mg/kg/day (0.3 in stage I) tapered over 6-8 weeks.`,`Hydrocephalus: VP shunt or ETV; manage sodium, seizures, nutrition; ART 4-8 weeks after ATT in HIV.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Hydrocephalus, stroke, cranial nerve palsy, hyponatraemia, paradoxical tuberculoma, optochiasmatic arachnoiditis, IRIS, drug hepatitis.`]}],mustDraw:[`CSF comparison table (bacterial, viral, TB, cryptococcal)`,`Diagram of basal exudate with its three consequences`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis and clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Staging and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Complications`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Rich focus`,`BMRC staging`,`basal exudate`,`CSF glucose ratio`,`Xpert Ultra`,`dexamethasone`,`12 months ATT`,`hydrocephalus`]},{id:`infectious-fever-cns-infections-other-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate HSV encephalitis from anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis.`,openingLines:[`HSV encephalitis is an acute necrotising viral encephalitis of the temporal lobes treated with acyclovir; anti-NMDAR encephalitis is an antibody-mediated, reversible encephalitis treated with immunotherapy.`,`The two are linked: HSV encephalitis triggers anti-NMDAR autoimmunity in about a quarter of survivors.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`HSV: any age, days, fever, aphasia, smell hallucinations, temporal seizures.`,`NMDAR: young women, weeks, psychiatric onset, dyskinesia, catatonia, autonomic instability, hypoventilation.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`HSV: CSF PCR positive, MRI asymmetric haemorrhagic temporal change, lateralised periodic discharges on EEG.`,`NMDAR: CSF antibody positive, MRI often normal, extreme delta brush on EEG, ovarian teratoma on imaging.`]},{heading:`Treatment and outcome`,points:[`HSV: acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly for 14-21 days; residual memory deficit common.`,`NMDAR: steroids, IVIG or PLEX, rituximab, tumour removal; about 80% recover substantially.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`temporal lobe`,`HSV PCR`,`acyclovir`,`NMDAR antibody`,`teratoma`,`dyskinesia`,`IVIG`,`rituximab`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Encephalitis; Chronic and recurrent meningitis; Brain abscess`,`Bodilsen J et al. ESCMID guideline on diagnosis and treatment of brain abscess, Clin Microbiol Infect 2024`,`WHO Guidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV, 2022`,`Solomon T et al. ABN/BIA national guidelines for management of suspected viral encephalitis in adults, 2012; IDSA encephalitis guideline 2008`,`Graus F et al. A clinical approach to diagnosis of autoimmune encephalitis. Lancet Neurol 2016`,`Thwaites GE et al. Dexamethasone for tuberculous meningitis. N Engl J Med 2004; Donovan J et al. ACT HIV trial, N Engl J Med 2023`,`Marais S et al. Tuberculous meningitis: a uniform case definition. Lancet Infect Dis 2010`,`NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB in India, 2025; WHO consolidated TB guidelines (Module 5 children, 2022; rapid diagnostics, 2024)`]}),s.push({id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria`,title:`Tetanus and diphtheria`,oneLiner:`**Tetanus** is a toxin-mediated disease in which **tetanospasmin** from Clostridium tetani blocks inhibitory (GABA and glycine) neurotransmission, causing **trismus, rigidity, reflex spasms and autonomic storm** - treated by wound debridement, **human tetanus immunoglobulin**, metronidazole, benzodiazepines, **magnesium** and airway care, and prevented by **Td and wound prophylaxis**; **diphtheria** is a toxin-mediated infection by toxigenic Corynebacterium diphtheriae causing an **adherent grey pseudomembrane, bull neck, myocarditis and neuropathy**, treated with **diphtheria antitoxin plus a macrolide or penicillin** - and resurging in India where booster coverage is low.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`tetanus`,`lockjaw trismus`,`risus sardonicus`,`opisthotonus`,`tetanospasmin`,`human tetanus immunoglobulin HTIG TIG`,`tetanus-prone wound`,`Td vaccine`,`Ablett classification`,`magnesium sulphate tetanus`,`spatula test`,`neonatal tetanus MNTE`,`diphtheria`,`pseudomembrane bull neck`,`diphtheria antitoxin DAT`,`Elek test Albert stain`,`diphtheritic myocarditis`,`Corynebacterium diphtheriae`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Tetanus** is an acute, often fatal disease caused by the exotoxin of **Clostridium tetani**, defined clinically by **hypertonia and painful muscle spasms** (usually starting with the jaw and neck) without another cause; there is no confirmatory laboratory test. [WHO 2024]`,`Global tetanus deaths fell from about 800 000 in the 1980s to **under 40 000 a year**, mostly in low-income countries; **India was validated for maternal and neonatal tetanus elimination (MNTE) in 2015** - fewer than 1 case per 1000 live births in every district. [WHO 2024]`,`**Adult tetanus persists in India** - farm workers, road injuries, chronic leg ulcers, intravenous and intramuscular injections, ear infections and unsafe abortion - because immunity wanes without boosters and disease confers **no immunity**. [MoHFW 2023]`,`With modern ICU care, tetanus mortality is about **10-20%**; without ventilation it is **up to 50%**, and neonatal tetanus without care exceeds 80%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria** is an acute infection of the upper respiratory tract (or skin) by **toxigenic Corynebacterium diphtheriae** (rarely C. ulcerans), defined by a **grey adherent membrane** with toxin-mediated complications. [WHO 2024]`,`**India reports the largest number of diphtheria cases in the world** - several thousand a year - with outbreaks in Kerala, Assam, Haryana, Delhi, Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh; cases have shifted to **older children, adolescents and adults** because boosters are missed. [WHO 2024]`,`Diphtheria case fatality is **5-10%**, up to 20% under 5 years and over 40 years, and higher when antitoxin is delayed. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**C. tetani** is an anaerobic, Gram-positive, spore-forming bacillus with a **terminal spore (drumstick)**; spores live in soil, dust and animal faeces and survive boiling and many disinfectants. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tetanus-prone wounds**: puncture wounds, wounds contaminated with soil or faeces, **devitalised tissue, compound fractures, burns, crush injuries, animal bites**, wounds over 6 hours old, and foreign bodies; also chronic ulcers, otitis media, dental infection, injections and unsafe delivery or abortion. [ACIP 2020]`,`In up to **a fifth of cases no wound is found** - the portal is trivial or healed by the time spasms begin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonatal tetanus** follows contamination of the **umbilical stump** (cutting with an unclean instrument, applying cow dung or ash) in a baby whose mother lacked protective antibodies. [WHO 2024]`,`**Diphtheria** spreads by **respiratory droplets** and contact with skin lesions; humans are the only reservoir, and **asymptomatic carriers** sustain transmission. [Harrison 22e]`,`Only strains carrying the **tox gene (from a lysogenic beta-corynephage)** make toxin; non-toxigenic strains cause sore throat or endocarditis without toxic complications. [Harrison 22e]`,`Diphtheria risk factors: **incomplete primary series or missed boosters**, crowding, poor hygiene, migrant and urban slum populations, and older adults whose immunity has waned. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - toxins explain the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Spores germinate in anaerobic tissue and release **tetanospasmin**, which binds peripheral nerve terminals and travels by **retrograde axonal transport** to the spinal cord and brainstem. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the CNS the toxin's light chain, a zinc protease, **cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP)** in inhibitory interneurons, blocking release of **GABA and glycine** - motor neurons fire unchecked, giving rigidity and spasms. [Harrison 22e]`,`Shortest nerves are affected first, so **trismus (masseter) appears early**; loss of inhibition of sympathetic neurons causes **autonomic instability** - labile hypertension, tachycardia, arrhythmia, sweating and hyperpyrexia - the main killer in ventilated patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`Bound toxin cannot be neutralised - **antitoxin only mops up unbound toxin**, so it is given early; recovery needs **new nerve terminals to sprout**, which is why illness lasts **4-6 weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Magnesium** blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction and is a physiological calcium antagonist that dampens catecholamine release - it reduces spasms and autonomic swings. [Thwaites Lancet 2006]`,`**Penicillin is a GABA-A antagonist** at high doses and may add to excitability - one reason **metronidazole** is preferred to eradicate C. tetani. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria toxin** (A-B toxin) enters cells and its A fragment **ADP-ribosylates elongation factor-2 (EF-2)**, stopping protein synthesis - one molecule can kill a cell; tissue necrosis plus fibrin and leucocytes form the **pseudomembrane**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Absorbed diphtheria toxin damages the **heart (myocarditis, conduction block) in 10-25%** and causes **demyelinating neuropathy** (palatal palsy first, then ocular and peripheral); toxin bound to cells is not reversed by antitoxin, so **DAT must be given on clinical suspicion**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading that examiners ask`,points:[`**Clinical forms of tetanus**: **generalised** (commonest, about 80%), **localised** (rigidity near the wound; may progress), **cephalic** (after head or face wounds or otitis, with **cranial nerve palsy, often VII**, and short incubation), and **neonatal**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ablett grading**: **I mild** - mild-moderate trismus, general spasticity, no spasms or respiratory difficulty; **II moderate** - moderate trismus, rigidity, short mild spasms, respiratory rate over 30, mild dysphagia; **III severe** - severe trismus, generalised rigidity, prolonged reflex spasms, respiratory rate over 40, apnoeic spells, severe dysphagia, pulse over 120; **IV very severe** - grade III plus violent autonomic instability. [Ablett 1967]`,`**Prognostic timing**: **incubation under 7 days** and **period of onset (first symptom to first spasm) under 48 hours** predict severe disease; the **Dakar and Phillips scores** quantify prognosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria by site**: **faucial (tonsillopharyngeal, commonest), laryngeal (stridor, airway obstruction), nasal (anterior, mild, serosanguinous discharge), and cutaneous** (punched-out ulcer); and by severity - localised vs **malignant (bull neck, extensive membrane, toxaemia)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria case definitions (WHO)**: **probable** - pharyngitis, tonsillitis or laryngitis with an **adherent membrane**; **confirmed** - toxigenic C. diphtheriae isolated or epidemiologically linked. [WHO 2024]`,`**Tetanus case definition (WHO)**: trismus or risus sardonicus, or generalised painful muscle contractions, with a history of injury or wound (not required); neonatal tetanus - normal suck and cry for the first 2 days, then inability to suck between day 3 and 28, with stiffness or spasms. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Incubation of tetanus** is usually **3-21 days** (median about 7-10), shorter with wounds near the head and heavy contamination. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trismus (lockjaw)** is the first symptom in about three-quarters; patients may present to a dentist or ENT clinic with jaw stiffness, or with **dysphagia, neck stiffness or back pain**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spasms** are triggered by noise, light, touch or swallowing; they are agonising because **consciousness is preserved**, and laryngeal spasm causes sudden apnoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonatal tetanus**: a baby who fed well for 2 days stops sucking between day 3 and 10, with stiffness, spasms and crying - ask about home delivery and cord care. [WHO 2024]`,`**Diphtheria** begins insidiously with **low-grade fever, sore throat, malaise** and dysphagia; a **membrane** appears over 2-3 days; the voice is hoarse and the cough brassy if the larynx is involved. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Late diphtheria symptoms**: **nasal regurgitation and nasal voice** (palatal palsy) in week 1-3, blurred near vision (ciliary paralysis), then limb weakness at 3-8 weeks; breathlessness or syncope suggests myocarditis in week 1-2. [Harrison 22e]`,`Always ask **vaccination history** (number of DPT/pentavalent doses, boosters, Td in pregnancy) and contact with a similar case, and note the **date of injury** - it decides prophylaxis. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Risus sardonicus** (fixed sneer from facial spasm), **opisthotonus** (arched back from extensor spasm), board-like abdominal rigidity, and clenched hands with flexed arms and extended legs during spasms are the signs of generalised tetanus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spatula test**: touching the posterior pharynx with a spatula causes **reflex masseter spasm and biting** in tetanus (instead of a gag) - sensitivity about 94%, specificity close to 100%. [Apte 1995]`,`**Autonomic signs**: swinging blood pressure, tachycardia over 120, sweating, fever and arrhythmia mark Ablett grade IV - monitor continuously. [Ablett 1967]`,`**Diphtheritic membrane**: **grey-white, firmly adherent, bleeds when lifted**, extends beyond the tonsils onto the uvula, soft palate or pharyngeal wall - unlike streptococcal exudate, which wipes off and stays on the tonsil. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bull neck**: massive cervical lymphadenopathy with **brawny soft-tissue oedema** of the neck - marks malignant diphtheria with high toxaemia and mortality. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myocarditis signs**: soft first heart sound, gallop rhythm, bradycardia or irregular pulse, hypotension, heart failure; check **ECG and troponin** daily in the first 2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuropathy signs**: palatal palsy (uvula does not move, nasal regurgitation), loss of accommodation with preserved light reflex, then symmetric peripheral weakness with areflexia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Trismus from local causes** - dental abscess, peritonsillar abscess, TMJ disease, mumps - has local pain and swelling but no generalised spasms or positive spatula test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced dystonia** (metoclopramide, haloperidol, prochlorperazine) causes oculogyric crisis and torticollis, not trismus with reflex spasms, and **reverses with IV promethazine or benztropine**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strychnine poisoning** mimics tetanus exactly (glycine antagonism) but the jaw is relaxed between spasms and onset is within minutes to hours; **hypocalcaemic tetany** gives carpopedal spasm with Chvostek and Trousseau signs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Meningitis, encephalitis and rabies** cause altered consciousness, which tetanus does not; **neonatal sepsis or hypoglycaemia** mimic neonatal tetanus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Streptococcal pharyngitis** has a non-adherent yellow exudate, higher fever and quick response to penicillin; **infectious mononucleosis** gives a white tonsillar membrane, splenomegaly, posterior nodes and atypical lymphocytes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vincent angina** (fusospirochaetal, painful unilateral ulcer, foul breath), **oral candidiasis** (wipes off, immunosuppressed), **agranulocytosis** (necrotic ulcers, neutropenia) and **peritonsillar abscess** are the other membrane mimics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Laryngeal diphtheria vs croup and epiglottitis**: diphtheria is slower, with membrane visible and toxaemia; croup is viral, barking and short; epiglottitis is abrupt with drooling and high fever. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line to advanced`,points:[`**Tetanus is a clinical diagnosis**: cultures of the wound grow C. tetani in only about a third and growth does not prove disease; **serum anti-tetanus antibody over 0.1 IU/mL** makes tetanus unlikely but must not delay treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`In tetanus, check **CBC, creatinine, electrolytes, CK** (raised from spasms - risk of rhabdomyolysis and AKI), **calcium and magnesium** (during magnesium therapy), ECG and arterial blood gas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria throat swab** is taken from beneath the edge of the membrane **before antibiotics** and sent on **Loeffler serum slope or tellurite (Tinsdale) medium**; **Albert stain** shows **metachromatic (volutin) granules** and Chinese-letter arrangement as a quick pointer. [WHO 2024]`,`**Elek immunoprecipitation test** (or **PCR for the tox gene**) confirms toxigenicity - culture alone does not distinguish toxigenic from non-toxigenic strains. [WHO 2024]`,`In diphtheria, do **ECG at baseline and daily** for 2 weeks (ST-T change, PR prolongation, heart block), **troponin**, CBC (leucocytosis, thrombocytopenia), creatinine; echocardiography if myocarditis is suspected. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take **serum for diphtheria antitoxin level before DAT** where possible; a level over 0.1 IU/mL indicates protection. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care - wound prophylaxis`,points:[`Clean the wound, **remove dead tissue and foreign bodies**, and decide on Td and TIG by **wound type and vaccination history** (table): clean minor wound with fewer than 3 doses or unknown - **Td only**; tetanus-prone wound with fewer than 3 doses or unknown - **Td plus TIG**. [ACIP 2020]`,`Fully immunised (3 or more doses): **clean minor wound - Td if last dose over 10 years ago; tetanus-prone wound - Td if last dose over 5 years ago**; TIG is not needed. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Prophylactic HTIG dose is 250 IU IM** (500 IU if the wound is over 24 hours old, heavily contaminated or a burn), given with a separate syringe at a **different site** from Td. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Td has replaced TT** in India's programme since 2018 so that every tetanus booster also boosts diphtheria immunity; an unimmunised adult needs **3 doses (0, 4 weeks, 6-12 months)**. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Do not give routine antibiotics** for tetanus prophylaxis; they do not replace debridement and immunisation. [WHO 2024]`,`**Suspected diphtheria at a PHC**: isolate, take a throat swab, give the first dose of antibiotic only after the swab, **notify immediately** and refer for antitoxin; never wait for culture to refer. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Tetanus**: admit to a **quiet, dark ICU room**, minimise handling; **secure the airway early** - in Ablett III-IV, an **early tracheostomy** is preferred because spasms may last 3-4 weeks and intubation can provoke laryngospasm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neutralise toxin**: **HTIG 3000-6000 IU IM** in divided sites (WHO notes **500 IU** is probably as effective); equine anti-tetanus serum 10 000 IU after a test dose if HTIG is unavailable; intrathecal TIG has mixed trial evidence and is not routine. [WHO 2024]`,`**Eradicate C. tetani**: **metronidazole 500 mg IV 6-8-hourly for 7-10 days** (preferred); alternatives - penicillin G or doxycycline; debride the wound after antitoxin. [WHO 2024]`,`**Control spasms**: **diazepam** (up to 0.5-1 mg/kg/day or more) or **midazolam infusion**; add non-depolarising neuromuscular blockade with ventilation if spasms persist; intrathecal **baclofen** in specialist units. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Magnesium sulphate**: **40 mg/kg IV over 30 minutes, then 2 g/h (1.5 g/h if 45 kg or less)**, titrated to spasm control, keeping **serum Mg 2-4 mmol/L** and patellar reflexes present - reduced the need for other drugs and helped autonomic control (Thwaites, Lancet 2006). [Thwaites Lancet 2006]`,`**Autonomic storm**: magnesium, morphine, deep sedation; short-acting **esmolol or labetalol** for hypertension; **avoid long-acting beta-blockers (propranolol) alone** - sudden cardiac death; treat hypotension with fluids and vasopressors. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria**: strict **droplet isolation**, airway assessment (laryngeal disease may need tracheostomy), and **diphtheria antitoxin (DAT) immediately on clinical suspicion** - do not wait for culture. [WHO 2024]`,`**DAT dose (equine, IV over 60 minutes)**: pharyngeal or laryngeal under 48 hours - **20 000-40 000 IU**; nasopharyngeal - **40 000-60 000 IU**; extensive disease over 3 days or **bull neck - 80 000-100 000 IU**; dose by severity, not weight; keep **adrenaline** ready and do not let sensitivity testing delay it. [WHO 2024]`,`**Diphtheria antibiotics**: **azithromycin** (10-12 mg/kg, max 500 mg daily) or **erythromycin 500 mg 6-hourly (child 40-50 mg/kg/day)**, or **procaine penicillin 600 000 U IM 12-hourly** (then oral penicillin V), usually **14 days**; WHO 2024 favours a macrolide. Antibiotics stop toxin production and spread but do not replace DAT. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: India gives **2 doses of Td** in the first pregnancy (4 weeks apart) and **1 booster** in a subsequent pregnancy within 3 years; this prevents neonatal tetanus by transplacental antibody. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Neonatal tetanus**: HTIG 500 IU IM, metronidazole, diazepam, nasogastric feeding, cord care, and **immunise the mother**; it is notifiable and each case triggers a field investigation. [WHO 2024]`,`**Elderly**: most adults over 60 lack protective tetanus antibodies; a fall or garden wound in an older person needs Td plus TIG if the history is uncertain. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Immunocompromised (HIV, chemotherapy)**: give **TIG for any tetanus-prone wound regardless of vaccination history**. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Diphtheria in vaccinated people** is usually milder; adults with waning immunity form a growing share of Indian cases - they need DAT on the same criteria. [WHO 2024]`,`**DAT shortage**: India relies on equine DAT from a few manufacturers and supply is intermittent; know the district or state cold-chain source before an outbreak, and give antibiotics and supportive care while sourcing it. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Tetanus complications**: laryngospasm and asphyxia, aspiration pneumonia, **ventilator-associated pneumonia**, fractures of vertebrae from spasms, rhabdomyolysis, AKI, DVT and pulmonary embolism, pressure sores and **autonomic sudden death**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Recovery from tetanus takes **4-6 weeks**; survivors may have prolonged stiffness, psychological trauma and must still be **vaccinated (3 doses of Td)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheritic myocarditis** (10-25%) appears in **week 1-2**, causing heart block, arrhythmia and heart failure - the commonest cause of death; it may need **temporary pacing**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheritic neuropathy** (up to 20%): palatal palsy in week 1-3, ocular (accommodation) palsy in week 3-5, and **peripheral polyneuropathy in week 3-8**, which may involve respiratory muscles - but recovery is usually complete. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Airway obstruction** from laryngeal membrane or a dislodged membrane can kill suddenly; **renal failure** and thrombocytopenia occur in severe toxaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognosis in diphtheria**: delayed DAT (mortality rises with each day), bull neck, extensive membrane, myocarditis, age under 5 or over 40. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer **every tetanus case** to a hospital with ICU and ventilator; **every suspected diphtheria** to a facility with isolation, antitoxin and airway expertise. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Monitor tetanus**: spasm frequency, oxygen saturation, BP and HR continuously, serum magnesium every 6 hours on infusion, patellar reflex, urine output, CK and creatinine. [Thwaites Lancet 2006]`,`**Monitor diphtheria**: daily ECG for 2 weeks and weekly until 6 weeks, cardiac rhythm, airway, palatal movement, limb power and swallowing; **bed rest for 2-3 weeks** in myocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**End isolation in diphtheria** after **two negative throat cultures taken 24 hours apart**, starting at least 24 hours after completing antibiotics. [WHO 2024]`,`**Vaccinate every survivor** of tetanus and diphtheria with a full course - natural disease does not give lasting immunity. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, contacts and national programme link`,points:[`**UIP schedule**: pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) at **6, 10 and 14 weeks**, **DPT boosters at 16-24 months and 5-6 years**, and **Td at 10 and 16 years**; Td in pregnancy as above. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Adult schedule**: a Td booster **every 10 years**, and at the time of a tetanus-prone injury if over 5 years since the last dose. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Diphtheria close contacts** (household, kissing, shared utensils, health workers exposed to secretions): throat swab for culture, **antibiotic prophylaxis** - single **benzathine penicillin IM (600 000 U if under 6 years, 1.2 million U if 6 years or older)** or erythromycin or azithromycin for 7-10 days - and daily surveillance for 7 days. [WHO 2024]`,`**Vaccinate contacts**: unimmunised contacts start a primary series; immunised contacts get a **booster if the last dose was over 5 years ago**; carriers are treated like contacts and re-cultured. [WHO 2024]`,`**Clean delivery practices** (clean hands, surface, blade, cord tie and nothing applied to the cord) and institutional delivery under **JSY/JSSK** maintain MNTE. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Surveillance**: diphtheria, pertussis and neonatal tetanus are under **case-based VPD surveillance** (WHO country office with MoHFW) and **IDSP-IHIP** - every case is notified within 24 hours and investigated. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO clinical management of diphtheria guideline (2024)**: give **DAT promptly** on clinical suspicion, prefer **macrolide antibiotics** (azithromycin, erythromycin, clarithromycin) over penicillin, and give contacts prophylaxis plus vaccination. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO tetanus position and management guidance (2017, updated surveillance standards 2024)**: 6 doses of tetanus-toxoid-containing vaccine (3 primary plus 3 boosters) through childhood and adolescence for lifelong protection; HTIG for treatment, metronidazole preferred. [WHO 2024]`,`**India switched from TT to Td (2018)** for pregnant women, adolescents and wound management - to close the adult diphtheria immunity gap behind the resurgence. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**ACIP 2020** allows **Td or Tdap** interchangeably for the 10-year booster and wound management; Tdap in every pregnancy (27-36 weeks) is US practice, while India uses Td. [ACIP 2020]`,`**Magnesium sulphate** remains the best-evidenced adjunct for spasm and autonomic control; large trials of intrathecal antitoxin have not established routine use. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Management of a tetanus-prone wound'** - draw the 2 x 2 prophylaxis table (clean vs tetanus-prone; under 3 vs 3 or more doses). [ACIP 2020]`,`Viva: **mechanism of tetanospasmin** (retrograde transport, cleaves synaptobrevin, blocks GABA and glycine) vs **botulinum toxin** (acts at the neuromuscular junction, cleaves SNAP-25 or synaptobrevin, blocks acetylcholine - flaccid paralysis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **Ablett grades**, the spatula test, and why **long-acting beta-blockers are dangerous** in tetanus. [Ablett 1967]`,`Viva: **diphtheria toxin inhibits EF-2**; complications by week - myocarditis week 1-2, palatal palsy week 1-3, peripheral neuropathy week 3-8. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **waiting for culture before giving DAT**, or giving antibiotics alone. [WHO 2024]`,`Common mistake: assuming a patient who recovered from tetanus or diphtheria is immune - **both need full vaccination**. [WHO 2024]`,`Remember **Td not TT**, and the India UIP booster ages: 16-24 months, 5-6 years, 10 years, 16 years. [MoHFW 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Tetanus wound prophylaxis`,columns:[`Vaccination history`,`Clean minor wound: Td`,`Clean minor wound: TIG`,`Tetanus-prone wound: Td`,`Tetanus-prone wound: TIG`],rows:[[`Unknown or fewer than 3 doses`,`Yes (complete series)`,`No`,`Yes (complete series)`,`Yes - HTIG 250 IU IM (500 IU if over 24 h, burns, heavy contamination)`],[`3 or more doses, last dose under 5 years`,`No`,`No`,`No`,`No`],[`3 or more doses, last dose 5-10 years`,`No`,`No`,`Yes`,`No`],[`3 or more doses, last dose over 10 years`,`Yes`,`No`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Immunocompromised (HIV, chemotherapy)`,`As above`,`No`,`Yes`,`Yes, regardless of history`]]},{heading:`Drug table - tetanus and diphtheria`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Human tetanus immunoglobulin (HTIG)`,`Prophylaxis 250 IU IM (500 IU high risk); treatment 3000-6000 IU IM (500 IU may suffice)`,`Single dose; different site from Td`,`Local pain; rare allergy; does not neutralise bound toxin`],[`Metronidazole`,`500 mg IV 6-8-hourly (child 7.5 mg/kg 8-hourly)`,`7-10 days`,`Metallic taste, neuropathy, disulfiram reaction`],[`Diazepam / midazolam`,`Diazepam 0.1-0.2 mg/kg IV 3-6-hourly, titrated; midazolam infusion`,`Taper over 2-3 weeks as spasms ease`,`Respiratory depression (ventilate), tolerance, withdrawal`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`40 mg/kg IV over 30 min, then 2 g/h (1.5 g/h if 45 kg or less)`,`Titrate to spasms; serum Mg 2-4 mmol/L; keep reflexes`,`Loss of reflexes, respiratory depression, hypocalcaemia; antidote calcium gluconate`],[`Esmolol / labetalol`,`Esmolol infusion titrated; labetalol 0.25-2 mg/min`,`For hypertensive surges`,`Hypotension, bradycardia; avoid propranolol alone`],[`Diphtheria antitoxin (equine)`,`20 000-100 000 IU IV over 60 min by extent and duration`,`Single dose, immediately on suspicion`,`Anaphylaxis (adrenaline ready), serum sickness at 7-14 days`],[`Azithromycin (diphtheria)`,`10-12 mg/kg (max 500 mg) oral or IV once daily`,`Per WHO 2024 (macrolide preferred); contacts 7-10 days`,`QT prolongation, GI upset`],[`Erythromycin (diphtheria)`,`500 mg 6-hourly (child 40-50 mg/kg/day in 4 doses)`,`14 days`,`GI upset, QT prolongation, interactions`],[`Procaine / benzathine penicillin`,`Procaine 600 000 U IM 12-hourly (treatment); benzathine 600 000 U (under 6 y) or 1.2 MU IM once (contacts)`,`Treatment 14 days total with oral switch; contacts single dose`,`Penicillin allergy; procaine reactions`]]},{heading:`Ablett grading of tetanus`,columns:[`Grade`,`Trismus and rigidity`,`Spasms`,`Respiration and swallowing`,`Autonomic`],rows:[[`I - Mild`,`Mild to moderate trismus, general spasticity`,`None`,`No respiratory embarrassment; little dysphagia`,`Normal`],[`II - Moderate`,`Moderate trismus, marked rigidity`,`Short, mild`,`RR over 30; mild dysphagia`,`Normal`],[`III - Severe`,`Severe trismus, generalised rigidity`,`Prolonged, reflex`,`RR over 40, apnoeic spells, severe dysphagia`,`Pulse over 120`],[`IV - Very severe`,`As grade III`,`As grade III`,`As grade III`,`Violent swings of BP and HR, arrhythmia, fever`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a throat membrane`,columns:[`Condition`,`Membrane`,`Other clues`,`Test`],rows:[[`Diphtheria`,`Grey, adherent, bleeds on removal, spreads to palate`,`Low fever, toxaemia, bull neck, unvaccinated`,`Albert stain, culture, Elek or tox PCR`],[`Streptococcal tonsillitis`,`Yellow-white exudate, wipes off, on tonsils`,`High fever, tender anterior nodes`,`Rapid antigen test, culture`],[`Infectious mononucleosis`,`White-grey on enlarged tonsils`,`Splenomegaly, posterior nodes, rash with amoxicillin`,`Atypical lymphocytes, heterophile or EBV VCA IgM`],[`Vincent angina`,`Unilateral ulcer with slough`,`Foul breath, poor oral hygiene`,`Fusiform bacilli and spirochaetes on smear`],[`Oral candidiasis`,`White plaques, wipe off leaving red base`,`Immunosuppression, inhaled steroids`,`KOH mount`],[`Agranulocytosis`,`Necrotic ulcers`,`Drug history (antithyroid, clozapine), sepsis`,`Neutrophils under 500/mm3`]]},{heading:`Tetanus vs diphtheria at a glance`,columns:[`Feature`,`Tetanus`,`Diphtheria`],rows:[[`Organism`,`C. tetani, anaerobic spore former in soil`,`Toxigenic C. diphtheriae, human reservoir`],[`Spread`,`Wound contamination; not person-to-person`,`Droplet and contact; carriers`],[`Toxin action`,`Cleaves synaptobrevin; blocks GABA and glycine release`,`ADP-ribosylates EF-2; stops protein synthesis`],[`Hallmark`,`Trismus, spasms, autonomic storm; conscious`,`Adherent membrane, bull neck, myocarditis, neuropathy`],[`Diagnosis`,`Clinical`,`Culture with Elek or tox PCR`],[`Antitoxin`,`HTIG`,`Equine DAT`],[`Antibiotic`,`Metronidazole`,`Macrolide or penicillin`],[`Isolation`,`Not needed`,`Droplet isolation until 2 negative cultures`]]}],redFlags:[`Trismus, neck stiffness or dysphagia after any wound - suspect tetanus and refer to ICU`,`Spasms with apnoea, cyanosis or laryngospasm`,`Swinging BP, tachycardia over 120, sweating or arrhythmia in tetanus (autonomic storm)`,`Neonate who stops sucking between day 3 and 28 with stiffness`,`Sore throat with grey adherent membrane, bull neck or stridor - suspect diphtheria, isolate and give antitoxin`,`Bradycardia, gallop, heart block or hypotension in diphtheria (myocarditis)`,`Nasal regurgitation or limb weakness weeks after a sore throat (diphtheritic neuropathy)`],pearls:[`Tetanus: conscious patient, clenched jaw, spasms on touch - and no lab test confirms it.`,`Spatula test: the patient bites instead of gagging - specificity close to 100%.`,`Antitoxin neutralises only unbound toxin - give HTIG or DAT early; recovery needs new nerve terminals.`,`Metronidazole over penicillin in tetanus - penicillin is a GABA antagonist.`,`Magnesium: 40 mg/kg then 2 g/h, keep reflexes and Mg 2-4 mmol/L.`,`Never propranolol alone in tetanus - sudden death; use esmolol or labetalol.`,`Diphtheria membrane bleeds when lifted; strep exudate wipes off.`,`Diphtheria timeline: myocarditis week 1-2, palate week 1-3, limbs week 3-8.`,`Give DAT on suspicion; azithromycin or erythromycin for the patient and contacts; Td for all.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-q1`,stem:`A 40-year-old farmer has a deep puncture wound from a rusty nail contaminated with soil. He received 3 doses of tetanus vaccine in childhood and his last booster was 7 years ago. What prophylaxis is indicated?`,options:[`Td booster and human tetanus immunoglobulin 250 IU`,`Human tetanus immunoglobulin only`,`No prophylaxis needed`,`Td booster only`],answer:3,explanation:`He is fully immunised (3 or more doses) with a tetanus-prone wound and his last dose was over 5 years ago, so a Td booster alone is needed. TIG is reserved for tetanus-prone wounds in people with fewer than 3 or unknown doses, or the immunocompromised. No prophylaxis would be correct only if the last dose was under 5 years ago.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-q2`,stem:`Tetanospasmin causes rigidity and spasms by which mechanism?`,options:[`Blocking acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction`,`Cleaving synaptobrevin in inhibitory interneurons, blocking GABA and glycine release`,`ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor-2`,`Antagonising glycine receptors on motor neurons directly`],answer:1,explanation:`Tetanospasmin travels by retrograde transport to the spinal cord, where its zinc-protease light chain cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP) in inhibitory interneurons and stops GABA and glycine release, so motor neurons fire unchecked. Blocking acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction is botulinum toxin (flaccid paralysis), EF-2 ADP-ribosylation is diphtheria toxin, and direct glycine receptor antagonism is strychnine.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-q3`,stem:`A ventilated patient with severe tetanus develops BP swinging between 200/110 and 80/50 mmHg with heart rate 150/min. Which drug is best avoided as a sole agent?`,options:[`Magnesium sulphate infusion`,`Morphine infusion`,`Propranolol`,`Esmolol infusion`],answer:2,explanation:`Long-acting non-selective beta-blockers such as propranolol, used alone, have been associated with sudden cardiac death and profound hypotension in tetanus autonomic storm. Magnesium sulphate and morphine dampen sympathetic surges and are recommended, while short-acting esmolol can be titrated and switched off quickly if pressure falls.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-q4`,stem:`A 12-year-old unvaccinated boy has sore throat, low-grade fever and a grey membrane over the tonsils and uvula that bleeds when touched, with marked neck swelling. What is the most important immediate treatment?`,options:[`Diphtheria antitoxin without waiting for culture, plus a macrolide`,`Oral amoxicillin and review after the throat culture`,`IV dexamethasone to reduce neck swelling`,`Td vaccine alone`],answer:0,explanation:`This is diphtheria with bull neck. Antitoxin neutralises only circulating toxin, so it must be given immediately on clinical suspicion, with a macrolide (WHO 2024) or penicillin to stop toxin production and spread. Waiting for culture lets toxin bind the heart and nerves, steroids do not neutralise toxin, and vaccination does not treat the current illness although it is given on recovery.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-q5`,stem:`Ten days after the onset of faucial diphtheria, a 9-year-old girl becomes dizzy with a pulse of 44/min. What is the most likely complication?`,options:[`Palatal palsy`,`Guillain-Barre syndrome`,`Serum sickness from antitoxin`,`Diphtheritic myocarditis with heart block`],answer:3,explanation:`Myocarditis affects 10-25% of diphtheria patients in week 1-2 and causes conduction block, arrhythmia and heart failure - it is the commonest cause of death, so daily ECG is required and temporary pacing may be needed. Palatal palsy causes nasal regurgitation, not bradycardia; serum sickness gives fever, rash and arthralgia at 7-14 days; Guillain-Barre is not the diphtheritic neuropathy pattern.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the pathogenesis, clinical features, grading and management of generalised tetanus. Add a note on prophylaxis of tetanus-prone wounds.`,openingLines:[`Tetanus is a toxin-mediated disease in which tetanospasmin from Clostridium tetani blocks inhibitory GABA and glycine release, producing trismus, rigidity, reflex spasms and autonomic instability in a conscious patient.`,`It is entirely preventable by Td vaccination; ICU care has cut mortality to 10-20%.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Spores germinate in anaerobic wounds; tetanospasmin travels by retrograde axonal transport; cleaves synaptobrevin; blocks GABA and glycine; sympathetic disinhibition causes autonomic storm.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 3-21 days; trismus first, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus, board-like abdomen, reflex spasms, dysphagia, laryngospasm; consciousness preserved; spatula test.`]},{heading:`Grading`,points:[`Ablett I-IV; poor prognosis with incubation under 7 days and period of onset under 48 hours.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Quiet ICU, early airway or tracheostomy; HTIG 3000-6000 IU IM (500 IU may suffice); metronidazole 500 mg 6-8-hourly 7-10 days; debride wound.`,`Spasms: diazepam or midazolam, magnesium 40 mg/kg then 2 g/h; neuromuscular blockade with ventilation if needed.`,`Autonomic: magnesium, morphine, esmolol or labetalol; avoid propranolol alone; nutrition, DVT prophylaxis, physiotherapy.`,`Start Td course - disease does not give immunity.`]},{heading:`Wound prophylaxis`,points:[`Fewer than 3 or unknown doses: Td for all wounds, plus HTIG 250 IU for tetanus-prone wounds.`,`3 or more doses: Td if over 10 years (clean) or over 5 years (tetanus-prone); no TIG.`]}],mustDraw:[`Wound prophylaxis 2 x 2 table`,`Ablett grading table`,`Diagram of tetanospasmin pathway from wound to spinal interneuron`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and grading`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:4},{part:`Wound prophylaxis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`tetanospasmin`,`synaptobrevin`,`trismus`,`Ablett`,`HTIG`,`metronidazole`,`magnesium sulphate`,`Td`,`tetanus-prone wound`]},{id:`infectious-fever-tetanus-diphtheria-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the management of diphtheria and its contacts.`,openingLines:[`Diphtheria is a toxin-mediated infection by toxigenic Corynebacterium diphtheriae; antitoxin given on clinical suspicion is the single most important life-saving step.`,`India reports the highest number of cases worldwide, largely in under-boosted older children and adults.`],answer:[{heading:`Patient`,points:[`Droplet isolation; airway care; throat swab before antibiotics (Albert stain, Loeffler culture, Elek or tox PCR).`,`DAT 20 000-100 000 IU IV by extent, adrenaline ready; azithromycin or erythromycin (WHO 2024) or penicillin for 14 days.`,`Daily ECG for 2 weeks; watch palate and limbs; isolation ends after 2 negative cultures 24 h apart.`]},{heading:`Contacts`,points:[`Throat swab, benzathine penicillin once or macrolide 7-10 days, surveillance 7 days.`,`Booster if last dose over 5 years; unimmunised start primary series.`]},{heading:`Public health`,points:[`Notify within 24 hours (IDSP, VPD surveillance); outbreak response with Td catch-up; vaccinate the patient on recovery.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected diphtheria - isolate, swab, DAT, antibiotic, ECG - contacts`],markSplit:[{part:`Diagnosis and antitoxin`,marks:2},{part:`Antibiotics and monitoring`,marks:1},{part:`Contacts and public health`,marks:2}],keywords:[`diphtheria antitoxin`,`pseudomembrane`,`Elek test`,`macrolide`,`myocarditis`,`contacts`,`benzathine penicillin`,`Td`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tetanus; Diphtheria and other corynebacterial infections`,`WHO. Clinical management of diphtheria: guideline, 2024`,`WHO. Tetanus vaccines: WHO position paper, 2017; WHO Vaccine-Preventable Diseases Surveillance Standards (tetanus, diphtheria), updated 2024`,`Thwaites CL et al. Magnesium sulphate for treatment of severe tetanus: a randomised controlled trial. Lancet 2006`,`CDC/ACIP. Use of Tdap and Td vaccines: updated recommendations, 2020 (wound management table)`,`MoHFW, Government of India. Universal Immunization Programme schedule and Td replacement of TT guidance, 2018; Immunization Handbook for Medical Officers, updated 2023`,`Ablett JJL. Analysis and main experiences in 82 patients treated in the Leeds Tetanus Unit, 1967`]});var c=[];c.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised`,title:`Fever in the immunocompromised: neutropenic fever, transplant, steroids and biologics`,oneLiner:`**Febrile neutropenia** is a single oral temperature of **38.3 C or more** (or 38.0 C sustained over 1 hour) with an **ANC under 500/mm3** (or expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours) - a **medical emergency** in which a broad-spectrum **antipseudomonal beta-lactam (piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime) is given within 60 minutes**, risk is stratified by the **MASCC score (21 or more = low risk)**, and every patient starting steroids, transplant immunosuppression or a biologic is first screened for **latent TB and hepatitis B**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`febrile neutropenia`,`neutropenic sepsis`,`MASCC score`,`CISNE`,`IDSA/ASCO 2018`,`piperacillin-tazobactam`,`cefepime`,`meropenem`,`empirical antifungal`,`galactomannan`,`transplant infection timeline`,`CMV`,`Pneumocystis jirovecii`,`anti-TNF`,`rituximab HBV reactivation`,`latent TB screening`,`IGRA`,`G-CSF`,`febrile neutropenia outpatient`,`immunosuppressed host`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Febrile neutropenia (FN)**: a single oral temperature of **38.3 C or more**, or **38.0 C or more sustained over 1 hour**, with an **absolute neutrophil count (ANC) under 500/mm3** or under 1000/mm3 and expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Profound neutropenia** is an ANC **under 100/mm3**; **prolonged neutropenia** lasts **more than 7 days** - both define the high-risk host for bacteraemia and invasive mould infection. [IDSA 2010]`,`FN complicates chemotherapy in **10-50% of solid tumour** patients and **over 80% of haematological malignancy** patients; a clinical or microbiological source is found in only **20-30%**, and bacteraemia in **10-25%**. [IDSA 2010]`,`Overall in-hospital mortality of FN in adults is about **5-10%**, rising to **over 20%** with Gram-negative bacteraemia, septic shock or each additional comorbidity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indian burden**: series from tertiary cancer centres (Tata Memorial, AIIMS, CMC Vellore) show **Gram-negative bacilli cause about 60-70%** of bacteraemias in FN (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas), unlike the Gram-positive predominance in Western centres. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Carbapenem resistance** in Klebsiella pneumoniae now exceeds half of isolates in ICMR AMRSN hospital data, which is why Indian guidance asks every unit to choose its first-line FN regimen from its own antibiogram. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`The wider **immunocompromised host** includes solid organ and stem cell transplant recipients, people on **prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more**, biologics (anti-TNF, rituximab, JAK inhibitors), advanced HIV, asplenia, and uncontrolled diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`Fever in these patients is often **blunted** (steroids, elderly, uraemia), so **any new deterioration, hypothermia or unexplained tachycardia** is treated as FN or sepsis until proved otherwise. [NICE NG151 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors - matching the defect to the organism`,points:[`**Neutropenia** (chemotherapy, aplastic anaemia, leukaemia): **Gram-negative bacilli** (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas), viridans streptococci, S. aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci, then **Candida and Aspergillus** after 7-10 days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cell-mediated (T-cell) defect** (steroids, calcineurin inhibitors, HIV, transplant, lymphoma): **intracellular pathogens** - Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Listeria, Nocardia, Salmonella, PJP, Cryptococcus, CMV, VZV, Toxoplasma, Strongyloides hyperinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Humoral defect and asplenia** (rituximab, myeloma, CLL, splenectomy): **encapsulated bacteria** - Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis - with fulminant sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-TNF agents** (infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept): **TB reactivation** (risk raised 4-20 times, often extrapulmonary or disseminated), histoplasmosis, Listeria, Legionella and HBV reactivation. [ACR 2022]`,`**Rituximab (anti-CD20)**: **hepatitis B reactivation** (risk over 10% in HBsAg-positive, can be fatal), hypogammaglobulinaemia, PML and prolonged poor vaccine response for 6 months. [AGA 2025]`,`**JAK inhibitors** (tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib): **herpes zoster** at 2-3 times the background rate, TB reactivation and opportunistic infections. [ACR 2022]`,`**Barrier breaks** add their own organisms: **mucositis** (viridans streptococci, anaerobes, Candida), **central venous catheters** (coagulase-negative staphylococci, S. aureus, Candida), and neutropenic enterocolitis (Clostridium septicum, Gram-negatives). [IDSA 2010]`,`Host risk factors for poor outcome: **age over 60**, haematological malignancy, **ANC under 100 for over 7 days**, hypotension, pneumonia, renal or hepatic dysfunction, uncontrolled cancer and prior fungal infection. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs vanish and why the drugs are chosen`,points:[`Neutrophils make **pus, infiltrates and inflammation**; without them, pneumonia may show **no sputum or consolidation**, UTI **no pyuria**, and cellulitis only faint erythema and pain - so **fever may be the only sign**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chemotherapy injures the **gut mucosa**, letting endogenous gut flora translocate; this is why **Gram-negative bacilli and Pseudomonas** must be covered empirically and why bacteraemia arises without a visible focus. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gram-negative bacteraemia in neutropenia can kill within **hours**; each hour of delay in the first antibiotic dose raises mortality, which is why IDSA/ASCO and NICE set a **door-to-needle time of 60 minutes**. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Moulds (Aspergillus, Mucorales)** are cleared by neutrophils and macrophages; risk rises steeply after **10-14 days of neutropenia**, explaining the rule to escalate to antifungal cover when fever persists beyond **4-7 days** of antibiotics. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Corticosteroids** redistribute lymphocytes, inhibit cytokine transcription (NF-kB), block neutrophil migration and lower the hypothalamic set-point - hence **masked fever, low CRP rise and T-cell opportunists** such as PJP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha** is essential for **granuloma maintenance**; blocking it dissolves granulomas that contain latent TB, which is why TB under anti-TNF is often disseminated and why screening precedes the first dose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**B-cell depletion** removes the antibody that controls HBV cccDNA in hepatocytes; HBV replication rebounds and the hepatitis flare comes on **immune reconstitution**, often weeks to months after the last rituximab dose. [AGA 2025]`,`Transplant immunosuppression follows a **predictable timeline** (net state of immunosuppression), so the month after transplant points to the likely pathogen. [Fishman, NEJM 2007]`]},{heading:`Classification - risk stratification that decides inpatient or outpatient care`,points:[`The **MASCC risk index** (Multinational Association for Supportive Care in Cancer) scores 7 items to a **maximum of 26**; a score of **21 or more = low risk** (serious complications under 5%), under 21 = high risk. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`MASCC items: burden of illness **none or mild 5**, moderate 3; **no hypotension 5**; **no COPD 4**; **solid tumour or haematological cancer with no previous fungal infection 4**; **no dehydration needing IV fluids 3**; **outpatient at onset 3**; **age under 60 years 2**. [MASCC 2000]`,`The **CISNE score** (Clinical Index of Stable Febrile Neutropenia) is used for **apparently stable solid tumour** patients: ECOG 2 or more, stress hyperglycaemia, COPD, cardiovascular disease, mucositis grade 2 or more, monocytes under 200 - **score 0 = low risk**. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`IDSA clinical high risk regardless of score: **expected neutropenia over 7 days, ANC 100 or less**, haemodynamic instability, new pneumonia, abdominal pain, neurological change, catheter infection, hepatic or renal dysfunction, or **AML/allogeneic HSCT**. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Transplant timeline**: **0-1 month** - nosocomial, donor-derived, surgical-site, C. difficile, HSV; **1-6 months** - opportunists (CMV, PJP, BK virus, Listeria, Nocardia, Toxoplasma, TB, Aspergillus); **over 6 months** - community infections, late CMV, EBV-driven PTLD. [Fishman, NEJM 2007]`,`**Steroid thresholds**: prednisolone **20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more** counts as immunosuppressive for live-vaccine purposes; **20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more** is the threshold for PJP prophylaxis in cancer patients. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Ask the **date and regimen of the last chemotherapy**: the ANC nadir is usually **7-14 days** after most cytotoxic cycles, so fever in that window is FN until the count is back. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Ask about **rigors after flushing a central line** (catheter-related bloodstream infection), mouth ulcers (mucositis), perianal pain, diarrhoea and abdominal pain (**neutropenic enterocolitis**, C. difficile). [IDSA 2010]`,`Ask about **dry cough and breathlessness with falling SpO2** - PJP under steroids or transplant drugs classically has **hypoxia out of proportion to the chest X-ray**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **headache or confusion** (Cryptococcus, Listeria, toxoplasma, PML), **visual symptoms** (CMV retinitis, candida endophthalmitis) and **sinus pain, black nasal discharge or facial swelling** (mucormycosis, aspergillosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the **transplant date, organ, induction agent, rejection treatment** (pulse steroids or ATG reset the clock to the high-risk window), CMV donor-recipient status and current prophylaxis. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`For biologics, ask the **drug, start date and screening done**: IGRA or TST, chest X-ray, HBsAg and anti-HBc, and whether **TB preventive treatment** was given. [ACR 2022]`,`Ask about **TB contact, previous TB, travel, soil and farm work** (Nocardia, histoplasma, melioidosis, Strongyloides) and pets or undercooked food (toxoplasma, Listeria, Salmonella). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and what they mean`,points:[`Record vitals with **NEWS2** and lactate: **SBP under 90, MAP under 65, RR 22 or more, SpO2 under 94%** or altered mentation mean neutropenic sepsis - start resuscitation and antibiotics at once. [SSC 2026]`,`**Do not perform a digital rectal examination** or insert rectal thermometers or suppositories in neutropenia - risk of bacteraemia and perianal abscess; inspect the perianal area instead. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Inspect **all catheter sites and tunnels**: erythema or pus over 2 cm along the tunnel is a **tunnel infection** that needs catheter removal. [IDSA 2009]`,`Examine the **mouth** (mucositis, thrush, HSV ulcers), **sinuses and palate** (black eschar = mucormycosis until proved otherwise) and **fundi** (candida endophthalmitis, CMV retinitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for skin lesions: **ecthyma gangrenosum** (painless necrotic ulcer with red halo) = **Pseudomonas** bacteraemia; tender nodules may be disseminated Candida, Fusarium or Nocardia; **dermatomal vesicles** = zoster. [Harrison 22e]`,`Right iliac fossa tenderness with fever and diarrhoea in neutropenia suggests **neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis)**; bowel wall over 4 mm on CT confirms. [IDSA 2010]`,`Absent or minimal signs are the rule; **repeat a full head-to-toe examination daily**, because foci declare themselves as the ANC recovers. [IDSA 2010]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever in the immunocompromised`,points:[`**Non-infectious fever** is common: **tumour fever**, transfusion reactions, drug fever (cytarabine, bleomycin, ATG, amphotericin), engraftment syndrome, GVHD and VTE - but these are diagnoses of exclusion after cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute rejection** in a transplant recipient can cause low-grade fever with graft dysfunction (raised creatinine, liver enzymes) - distinguished by biopsy, not by antibiotics. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS)** - worsening of a known infection as neutrophils or CD4 recover - mimics treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis** should be suspected when fever persists with cytopenias, ferritin over 10,000 and splenomegaly despite negative cultures (see the HLH topic). [HLH-2004]`,`**Endemic Indian infections** still occur in the immunosuppressed - malaria, dengue, scrub typhus, enteric fever - and must be tested for in parallel (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**PJP versus bacterial pneumonia**: PJP gives subacute dry cough, bilateral ground-glass change, high LDH and desaturation on exertion; bacterial pneumonia is lobar with sputum (if neutrophils exist). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`Before antibiotics: **two sets of blood cultures** - one from each lumen of any central line and one peripheral - but **never delay the first dose beyond 60 minutes** to obtain them. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Differential time to positivity**: a central-line culture turning positive **2 hours or more before** the peripheral one indicates catheter-related bloodstream infection. [IDSA 2009]`,`First-line panel: **CBC with differential** (ANC = WBC x (neutrophils + bands) %), creatinine, electrolytes, LFT, **lactate**, urine culture (even without pyuria), sputum, stool C. difficile if diarrhoea, and **chest X-ray** if respiratory signs. [IDSA 2010]`,`In India add a **malaria RDT and smear and dengue NS1** in season - endemic fevers are not suspended by chemotherapy. [ICMR 2022]`,`**High-resolution CT chest** is the investigation of choice for persistent fever beyond 4-7 days in high-risk neutropenia; nodules with a **halo sign** suggest invasive aspergillosis, the **reverse halo** suggests mucormycosis. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Serum galactomannan** (positive index 0.5 or more in serum, 1.0 in BAL) and **beta-D-glucan** support invasive fungal disease; galactomannan sensitivity falls on mould-active prophylaxis. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Transplant panel**: CMV quantitative PCR, BK virus PCR (kidney), EBV PCR, calcineurin inhibitor levels, and **BAL with PJP PCR or immunofluorescence** for infiltrates. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`Procalcitonin and CRP help less in neutropenia and are **not used to withhold antibiotics**; serial values may support de-escalation. [ECIL-4 2013]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - low-risk febrile neutropenia`,points:[`**First dose of antibiotic within 1 hour of triage in every patient**, before the risk assessment is complete; then decide outpatient or inpatient care with MASCC or CISNE. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Low-risk oral regimen**: **ciprofloxacin 750 mg orally twice daily plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg orally three times daily** (or 500/125 mg); levofloxacin or moxifloxacin 400 mg once daily are alternatives. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Do **not** use an oral fluoroquinolone regimen in patients who were already on **fluoroquinolone prophylaxis** - admit and give IV beta-lactam instead. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Outpatient care needs: **observation for at least 4 hours** after the first dose, tolerance of oral drugs, a caregiver, a telephone, transport and **reach of the hospital within 1 hour**, with review at 24-48 hours. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Penicillin allergy (non-severe): **ciprofloxacin plus clindamycin 300 mg orally 6-hourly** is the IDSA/ASCO alternative. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Re-admit if fever persists beyond **48-72 hours**, a new focus appears, cultures grow, the patient cannot take oral drugs or any red flag develops. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`In a patient on **steroids or biologics** with fever but a normal ANC, manage the focus as in a normal host but with a **lower threshold for admission, cultures and chest imaging**; hold the biologic during active infection. [ACR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency - high-risk febrile neutropenia`,points:[`**Empirical IV monotherapy with an antipseudomonal beta-lactam**: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly** or **cefepime 2 g IV 8-hourly**; **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly** where ESBL rates are high, in septic shock or with prior ESBL colonisation. [IDSA 2010; ICMR 2022]`,`Give piperacillin-tazobactam and meropenem as **extended or continuous infusions** in sepsis for better time above MIC. [SSC 2026]`,`Add **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly** (AUC 400-600) only for **haemodynamic instability, pneumonia, suspected catheter infection, skin or soft tissue infection, MRSA colonisation, severe mucositis on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis, or Gram-positive cocci in blood** - not routinely. [IDSA 2010]`,`Septic shock or known **CRE colonisation**: follow the local antibiogram - **ceftazidime-avibactam 2.5 g IV 8-hourly**, plus **aztreonam** for metallo-beta-lactamase (NDM) producers, which are common in India; add an aminoglycoside in shock. [IDSA AMR 2024; ICMR 2022]`,`**Persistent fever at 4-7 days** in high-risk patients with expected neutropenia over 7 days: start **empirical antifungal** - **caspofungin 70 mg IV day 1 then 50 mg daily** or **liposomal amphotericin B 3 mg/kg/day**; or a **pre-emptive** strategy guided by CT and galactomannan. [IDSA 2010; ECIL 2017]`,`Proven or probable **invasive aspergillosis**: **voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly for 2 doses then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly** (trough 1-5.5 mg/L) or isavuconazole; **mucormycosis**: liposomal amphotericin B 5-10 mg/kg/day plus surgery. [IDSA Aspergillus 2016; ECMM 2019]`,`**Duration**: in a stable patient with no source, **stop empirical antibiotics after 72 hours of apyrexia** irrespective of ANC (ECIL-4); IDSA 2010 continued until ANC over 500 - modern practice follows ECIL, supported by the How Long trial. [ECIL-4 2013]`,`**G-CSF** (filgrastim 5 mcg/kg/day SC) is **not routine** for established FN; consider it only in high-risk patients (sepsis, pneumonia, invasive fungal infection, expected long neutropenia). [ASCO 2015]`,`**Remove the central line** for S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Candida or mycobacterial bloodstream infection, tunnel or port-pocket infection, or persistent bacteraemia over 72 hours. [IDSA 2009]`]},{heading:`Special situations - transplant, steroids, biologics, pregnancy and CKD`,points:[`**Solid organ transplant fever**: cultures, CMV PCR, chest imaging and graft function; **never stop all immunosuppression** without the transplant team - reduce antimetabolite (mycophenolate) first in serious infection. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Drug interactions**: macrolides, azoles and rifampicin alter **tacrolimus and cyclosporine levels** (azoles raise, rifampicin lowers); check levels within 3-5 days of starting or stopping them. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**CMV prophylaxis** after SOT: **valganciclovir 900 mg orally once daily** (renally adjusted) for **3-6 months** in D+/R- or R+; in allogeneic HSCT **letermovir 480 mg daily** to day 100. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**PJP prophylaxis**: **cotrimoxazole one double-strength (160/800 mg) tablet daily or thrice weekly** for 6-12 months after SOT, during HSCT and with prednisolone **20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more**. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Before a biologic**: screen **IGRA or TST plus chest X-ray**, **HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs**, anti-HCV, HIV, and VZV serology; give TB preventive treatment for at least **1 month before** starting anti-TNF (complete the course). [ACR 2022; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**HBV before rituximab or chemotherapy**: HBsAg-positive - **entecavir 0.5 mg or tenofovir 300 mg daily** started before and continued **12-18 months** after the last dose; HBsAg-negative anti-HBc-positive on anti-CD20 - prophylaxis too (or HBV DNA monitoring every 1-3 months for lower-risk drugs). [AGA 2025]`,`**Pregnancy** with FN or on immunosuppression: piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime and meropenem are acceptable; avoid fluoroquinolones as first choice and voriconazole (teratogenic) - use liposomal amphotericin for fungal cover. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: reduce cefepime (neurotoxicity, non-convulsive status), piperacillin-tazobactam and meropenem by eGFR; monitor vancomycin by AUC or trough; amphotericin B worsens renal function - prefer the liposomal form. [Sanford 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Septic shock** develops in 5-10% of FN episodes and carries **mortality of 30-50%**, highest with Pseudomonas and carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis** mortality is 30-60% and **mucormycosis** 40-80%; early CT and galactomannan-guided therapy improve survival. [ECMM 2019]`,`**Neutropenic enterocolitis** can perforate; manage with bowel rest, broad cover including anaerobes (piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem) and surgery for perforation or uncontrolled bleeding. [IDSA 2010]`,`**CMV disease** after transplant causes pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis and graft rejection; **disseminated TB** under anti-TNF often presents as PUO with miliary or extrapulmonary disease. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**HBV reactivation** can cause fulminant hepatitis with mortality up to 20-30% in reported series - and it is almost entirely preventable by screening. [AGA 2025]`,`Delayed chemotherapy, dose reduction and longer hospital stay follow each FN episode - this is why **primary G-CSF prophylaxis** is given when the regimen's FN risk is **20% or more**. [ASCO 2015]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer the same day** to the oncology or transplant centre any patient with fever within 6 weeks of chemotherapy, any transplant recipient with fever, and anyone on a biologic with fever and hypotension or hypoxia. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`In-patient monitoring: **vitals 4-hourly**, daily CBC, creatinine and LFT, daily examination, blood cultures again if fever persists at 48-72 hours, and **drug levels** (vancomycin, voriconazole, tacrolimus). [IDSA 2010]`,`**De-escalate** once cultures are known and the patient is stable; switch to oral therapy in low-risk patients afebrile for 24-48 hours. [ECIL-4 2013]`,`On biologics: **annual IGRA or symptom screening** if TB exposure continues, ALT and HBV DNA every 1-3 months in anti-HBc-positive patients not on prophylaxis. [AGA 2025]`,`Teach the patient to **check the temperature at any chill** and to come at once at **38.0 C or more** - give a written neutropenic alert card. [NICE NG151 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Antibacterial prophylaxis** with **levofloxacin 500-750 mg once daily** is advised only for high-risk patients expected to have **ANC 100 or less for over 7 days** (AML, HSCT); not routinely in solid tumours. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Antifungal prophylaxis**: **posaconazole 300 mg delayed-release tablet twice daily on day 1 then 300 mg once daily** in AML/MDS induction and GVHD; fluconazole 400 mg daily in allogeneic HSCT pre-engraftment. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Antiviral prophylaxis**: **aciclovir 400-800 mg twice daily** for HSV-seropositive patients during HSCT or leukaemia induction. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`**Vaccinate before immunosuppression**: inactivated vaccines at least **2 weeks before**, live vaccines at least **4 weeks before**; avoid live vaccines (MMR, varicella, oral typhoid, yellow fever) during therapy. [IDSA 2013; ACR 2022]`,`Give **annual inactivated influenza**, **pneumococcal** (PCV20 alone, or PCV15/PCV13 then PPSV23), and **recombinant zoster vaccine** (2 doses) to adults 19 years or older who are immunocompromised. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Hand hygiene, a safe-food diet** (no unwashed raw produce, raw eggs, unpasteurised dairy), avoidance of construction dust and gardening during neutropenia; HEPA rooms for allogeneic HSCT. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Household contacts should receive **all routine inactivated vaccines and influenza vaccine**; they may receive MMR and varicella (avoid contact with a vaccine rash). [IDSA 2013]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** (adult guideline update): screen with **NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS rather than qSOFA alone**, give antibiotics **within 1 hour** in probable shock, use **balanced crystalloids** and **prolonged beta-lactam infusions** - applied to neutropenic sepsis. [SSC 2026]`,`**IDSA AMR guidance 2024**: for MBL-producing (NDM) Enterobacterales use **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam** or cefiderocol; for carbapenem-resistant Pseudomonas, ceftolozane-tazobactam or ceftazidime-avibactam - relevant to Indian FN units. [IDSA AMR 2024]`,`**AGA 2025 HBV reactivation guideline**: universal **HBsAg and anti-HBc** testing before immunosuppressive therapy, and antiviral prophylaxis (entecavir or tenofovir) for moderate and high-risk drug classes including **anti-CD20 agents**. [AGA 2025]`,`**CDC 2023** recommends **hepatitis B screening at least once in all adults** with the triple panel (HBsAg, anti-HBs, total anti-HBc). [CDC 2023]`,`**ACIP 2024**: PCV20 or PCV21 for adults with immunocompromise; recombinant zoster vaccine for immunocompromised adults from age 19. [ACIP 2024]`,`**WHO TPT 2024 (Consolidated guidelines Module 1)** keeps **3HP, 1HP, 6H and 4R** as options for latent TB before biologics; NTEP 2021 TPT guidance lists people starting anti-TNF, dialysis and transplant as eligible for testing and treatment. [WHO 2024; NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **define febrile neutropenia, outline MASCC and give empirical therapy** - write the definition, the score items with 21 cut-off, and the drug with dose and the 1-hour rule. [IDSA/ASCO 2018]`,`Common mistake: **adding vancomycin routinely** - it has no survival benefit unless one of the specific indications is present. [IDSA 2010]`,`Common mistake: **waiting for the ANC result** before giving the antibiotic; give the first dose on clinical suspicion. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Common mistake: **rectal examination or enema** in a neutropenic patient. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`Viva: why ceftriaxone is not empirical FN therapy - **no reliable antipseudomonal activity**. [IDSA 2010]`,`Viva: what to screen before infliximab - **IGRA/TST and chest X-ray, HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HCV, HIV**, vaccination status. [ACR 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`MASCC risk index (low risk = 21 or more of 26)`,columns:[`Characteristic`,`Points`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Burden of illness: none or mild symptoms`,`5`,`Clinician's global judgement`],[`Burden of illness: moderate symptoms`,`3`,`Severe symptoms or moribund score 0`],[`No hypotension (SBP 90 mmHg or more)`,`5`,`Hypotension excludes low risk in practice`],[`No chronic obstructive pulmonary disease`,`4`,`Active COPD needing treatment counts`],[`Solid tumour, or haematological cancer with no previous fungal infection`,`4`,`Prior invasive fungal infection removes these points`],[`No dehydration needing IV fluids`,`3`,`Inability to drink counts as dehydration`],[`Outpatient at onset of fever`,`3`,`Hospital-onset FN is higher risk`],[`Age under 60 years`,`2`,`Older age loses 2 points`]]},{heading:`Drugs in febrile neutropenia and the immunocompromised host`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly (extended 4-h infusion in sepsis)`,`Until 72 h afebrile and stable (ECIL-4) or per source`,`Hypersensitivity, hypokalaemia, AKI with vancomycin; reduce if eGFR under 40`],[`Cefepime`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`As above`,`Neurotoxicity and non-convulsive status in CKD - dose by eGFR`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly (2 g 8-hourly for CNS)`,`As above; de-escalate on cultures`,`Seizures (less than imipenem), C. difficile; valproate levels fall`],[`Ciprofloxacin + amoxicillin-clavulanate (low risk, oral)`,`Cipro 750 mg BD + amox-clav 625 mg TDS orally`,`Until afebrile 48 h and ANC recovering; usually 7 days`,`Not if on FQ prophylaxis; QT prolongation, tendinopathy`],[`Vancomycin (selected indications)`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly; AUC 400-600`,`Stop at 48-72 h if no Gram-positive grown`,`Nephrotoxicity, infusion reaction; monitor levels`],[`Caspofungin`,`70 mg IV day 1, then 50 mg IV daily`,`Until neutrophil recovery and fever resolves`,`Hepatotoxicity; few interactions`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`3 mg/kg/day IV (5-10 mg/kg for mucor)`,`Weeks; per response`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions`],[`Voriconazole`,`6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x 2, then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly; oral 200-300 mg BD`,`6-12 weeks for aspergillosis; trough 1-5.5 mg/L`,`Visual disturbance, hepatitis, QT; raises tacrolimus; teratogenic`],[`Cotrimoxazole (PJP prophylaxis)`,`1 DS tablet (160/800 mg) daily or thrice weekly`,`6-12 months post-SOT; while on 20 mg prednisolone 4 weeks or more`,`Rash, hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression, SJS`],[`Valganciclovir (CMV prophylaxis)`,`900 mg orally once daily (renal adjustment)`,`3-6 months post-SOT`,`Leucopenia, neutropenia; renal dosing`],[`Entecavir / tenofovir (HBV prophylaxis)`,`Entecavir 0.5 mg or TDF 300 mg orally daily`,`Start before therapy; continue 12-18 months after last dose`,`TDF - renal and bone toxicity; entecavir - lactic acidosis rare`]]},{heading:`Immune defect, typical drugs and the organisms to expect`,columns:[`Immune defect`,`Examples`,`Typical organisms`,`Empirical thinking`],rows:[[`Neutropenia`,`Chemotherapy, AML, aplastic anaemia`,`Gram-negative bacilli, Pseudomonas, viridans strep, Candida, Aspergillus`,`Antipseudomonal beta-lactam within 1 h; antifungal at 4-7 days`],[`T-cell (cell-mediated)`,`Steroids, tacrolimus, HIV, lymphoma`,`TB, Listeria, Nocardia, PJP, Cryptococcus, CMV, VZV, Toxoplasma`,`Sputum/BAL, CSF; cotrimoxazole for PJP`],[`B-cell / humoral, asplenia`,`Rituximab, myeloma, CLL, splenectomy`,`Pneumococcus, H. influenzae, meningococcus; HBV reactivation`,`Ceftriaxone early; vaccinate; HBV prophylaxis`],[`Anti-TNF blockade`,`Infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept`,`TB (often extrapulmonary), histoplasma, Listeria, Legionella`,`Screen and treat latent TB before; think disseminated TB`],[`JAK inhibition`,`Tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib`,`Herpes zoster, TB, opportunists`,`Recombinant zoster vaccine beforehand`],[`Barrier break`,`Central line, mucositis, surgery`,`CoNS, S. aureus, Candida, anaerobes`,`Vancomycin if line infection; remove line for S. aureus/Candida`]]},{heading:`Infection timeline after solid organ transplant`,columns:[`Period`,`Main infections`,`Why`,`Prophylaxis that changes it`],rows:[[`0-1 month`,`Surgical-site, line and urinary infection, C. difficile, donor-derived, HSV, Candida`,`Surgery, devices, hospital flora`,`Perioperative antibiotics, aciclovir`],[`1-6 months`,`CMV, PJP, BK virus, Listeria, Nocardia, Toxoplasma, TB reactivation, Aspergillus`,`Peak T-cell suppression`,`Valganciclovir, cotrimoxazole, TPT`],[`Over 6 months`,`Community pneumonia, UTI, late CMV, EBV-PTLD, VZV, endemic fungi`,`Maintenance immunosuppression lower`,`Vaccination, sun protection, surveillance`],[`After rejection treatment`,`Resets to the 1-6 month pattern`,`Pulse steroids or ATG`,`Restart CMV and PJP prophylaxis`]]},{heading:`Screening before steroids, biologics and transplant`,columns:[`Test`,`Positive result`,`Action before starting`],rows:[[`IGRA or TST (5 mm or more) + chest X-ray`,`Latent TB infection`,`TPT (3HP, 4R or 6H) at least 1 month before anti-TNF; rule out active TB first`],[`HBsAg`,`Chronic HBV`,`Entecavir or tenofovir before therapy, continue 12-18 months after`],[`Anti-HBc (HBsAg negative)`,`Resolved HBV`,`Prophylaxis with anti-CD20 or HSCT; otherwise HBV DNA every 1-3 months`],[`Anti-HCV, HIV`,`Chronic infection`,`Treat HCV with DAA; ART and OI prophylaxis`],[`VZV IgG`,`Seronegative`,`Varicella vaccine at least 4 weeks before; recombinant zoster vaccine if seropositive`],[`Strongyloides serology or empirical ivermectin`,`Exposure in endemic India`,`Ivermectin 200 mcg/kg for 2 days before high-dose steroids`]]}],redFlags:[`Temperature of 38.0 C or more within 6 weeks of chemotherapy - treat as neutropenic sepsis and give antibiotics within 1 hour`,`SBP under 90 mmHg, lactate 2 mmol/L or more, RR 22 or more or SpO2 under 94% in any immunocompromised patient`,`Black eschar on the palate or nasal turbinate, or periorbital swelling - suspected mucormycosis`,`New hypoxia with near-normal chest X-ray on steroids or after transplant - suspected PJP`,`Headache, confusion or seizure in a transplant recipient or on a biologic - CNS infection (Listeria, Cryptococcus, Toxoplasma)`,`Jaundice or ALT rise after rituximab or chemotherapy - HBV reactivation`,`Right iliac fossa pain with fever in neutropenia - neutropenic enterocolitis`,`Fever with tunnel infection or rigors on flushing a central line`],pearls:[`FN = 38.3 once or 38.0 for 1 hour, with ANC under 500 - and the antibiotic goes in within 60 minutes.`,`MASCC 21 or more of 26 = low risk; CISNE 0 = low risk for stable solid tumours.`,`Low-risk oral: cipro + amox-clav - but never if already on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis.`,`High risk: pip-taz 4.5 g 6-hourly or cefepime 2 g 8-hourly; meropenem if ESBL likely; ceftriaxone has no place.`,`Vancomycin only for the 'SPIRIT' indications: Shock, Pneumonia, Infection of line, Rash/skin, Identified Gram-positive, Tissue (mucositis) with FQ prophylaxis.`,`Fever at day 4-7 despite antibiotics in high-risk FN = think mould; CT chest and galactomannan.`,`Ecthyma gangrenosum = Pseudomonas bacteraemia.`,`Anti-TNF = TB; rituximab = HBV; JAK inhibitor = zoster; steroid = PJP.`,`No rectal examination in neutropenia.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q1`,stem:`A 48-year-old woman on adjuvant chemotherapy for breast cancer presents 10 days after her last cycle with a temperature of 38.6 C. She is alert, BP 118/76 mmHg, and her ANC is 300/mm3. Her MASCC score is 23. She was not on antibiotic prophylaxis. What is the most appropriate management after the first dose is given in the emergency room?`,options:[`Admit and start IV meropenem plus vancomycin`,`Observe for 4 hours, then discharge on oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate with review in 24 hours`,`Discharge on oral cefixime alone and repeat CBC in a week`,`Start G-CSF and withhold antibiotics until cultures return`],answer:1,explanation:`A MASCC score of 21 or more defines low-risk FN; IDSA/ASCO 2018 allows outpatient oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate after the first dose and a period of observation, if she has support and access. Meropenem with vancomycin is excessive for a stable low-risk patient, cefixime lacks antipseudomonal cover, and withholding antibiotics in FN is dangerous.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q2`,stem:`In high-risk febrile neutropenia, which of the following is an accepted indication to add vancomycin to the empirical beta-lactam?`,options:[`Persistent fever at 48 hours without any other change`,`ANC under 100/mm3`,`Suspected catheter-related infection with redness at the exit site`,`Age over 60 years`],answer:2,explanation:`IDSA lists suspected catheter-related infection, skin or soft-tissue infection, pneumonia, haemodynamic instability, MRSA colonisation and Gram-positive cocci in blood as reasons to add vancomycin. Persistent fever alone, profound neutropenia and older age are not indications - routine vancomycin adds toxicity without lowering mortality.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with Crohn disease is to start infliximab. His IGRA is positive and chest X-ray is normal; he has no symptoms. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start infliximab and repeat IGRA in 6 months`,`Start TB preventive treatment and begin infliximab after at least 1 month of it`,`Give full four-drug antitubercular treatment for 6 months before infliximab`,`Abandon infliximab permanently`],answer:1,explanation:`A positive IGRA with no active disease is latent TB infection; TB preventive treatment (for example 3HP or 4R) is started and anti-TNF therapy can begin after about 1 month while TPT is completed. Starting infliximab untreated risks disseminated TB, four-drug ATT is for active disease, and biologics need not be abandoned.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q4`,stem:`A kidney transplant recipient presents 3 months after transplant with fever, leucopenia and diarrhoea. He was CMV donor-positive, recipient-negative and stopped valganciclovir early due to cost. Which pathogen is most likely?`,options:[`Cytomegalovirus`,`Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus wound infection`,`EBV-driven post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease`,`Streptococcus pneumoniae`],answer:0,explanation:`The 1-6 month window is the period of opportunistic infection, and a D+/R- recipient off prophylaxis is at the highest risk of CMV disease, which classically gives fever, leucopenia and colitis. Wound MRSA belongs to the first month, PTLD usually appears later, and pneumococcus causes lobar pneumonia rather than leucopenia with colitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old man with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is HBsAg negative and anti-HBc positive. He will receive R-CHOP. According to current guidance, what is advised?`,options:[`No action, since HBsAg is negative`,`Hepatitis B vaccination only`,`Check ALT once after the course ends`,`Antiviral prophylaxis with entecavir or tenofovir during and for at least 12 months after rituximab`],answer:3,explanation:`Anti-CD20 therapy carries a high risk of HBV reactivation even in HBsAg-negative, anti-HBc-positive patients, so AGA 2025 recommends entecavir or tenofovir prophylaxis continued 12-18 months after the last dose. Doing nothing ignores occult HBV, vaccination does not suppress replication, and a single late ALT misses an early flare.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define febrile neutropenia. Describe its risk stratification and the management of a patient presenting to a district hospital with febrile neutropenia after chemotherapy.`,openingLines:[`Febrile neutropenia is a single oral temperature of 38.3 C or more, or 38.0 C sustained for 1 hour, with an ANC under 500/mm3 or expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours (IDSA).`,`It is an oncological emergency: Gram-negative bacteraemia can kill within hours, so the first antipseudomonal antibiotic dose is given within 60 minutes of arrival.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`Definition as above; profound neutropenia ANC under 100, prolonged over 7 days.`,`Occurs in 10-50% of solid tumour and over 80% of haematological malignancy patients; mortality 5-10%.`,`In India Gram-negative bacilli cause 60-70% of bacteraemias, with high ESBL and carbapenem resistance.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Absent neutrophils - no pus, minimal signs, fever often the only sign.`,`Mucositis and gut translocation of Gram-negatives; line infections with Gram-positives; fungi after 7-10 days.`]},{heading:`Initial assessment (first hour)`,points:[`Triage on suspicion; vitals, NEWS2, lactate; give the first dose of antibiotic within 60 minutes.`,`Two sets of blood cultures (line and peripheral), CBC, renal and liver tests, urine culture, chest X-ray; malaria and dengue tests in season.`,`Search mouth, lungs, skin, line sites and perianal area; no rectal examination.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`MASCC score: burden of illness 5/3, no hypotension 5, no COPD 4, solid tumour or no prior fungal infection 4, no dehydration 3, outpatient 3, age under 60 2; 21 or more = low risk.`,`CISNE for stable solid tumour; IDSA clinical high-risk features override the score.`]},{heading:`Treatment - low risk`,points:[`Oral ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg TDS after 4 h observation, with 24-48 h review; not if on FQ prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Treatment - high risk`,points:[`Piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly or cefepime 2 g IV 8-hourly; meropenem 1 g 8-hourly if ESBL likely or septic shock.`,`Add vancomycin only for specific indications; ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam for NDM CRE per antibiogram.`,`Fever at 4-7 days: CT chest, galactomannan, empirical caspofungin or liposomal amphotericin B.`,`Stop after 72 h apyrexia if stable and no source (ECIL-4); remove infected lines.`]},{heading:`Prevention and referral`,points:[`Levofloxacin prophylaxis in high-risk only; posaconazole in AML induction; primary G-CSF if regimen FN risk 20% or more.`,`Refer to the oncology centre after stabilisation and the first dose; neutropenic alert card and patient education.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: fever after chemotherapy - first dose in 60 min - MASCC - low-risk oral vs high-risk IV - reassess at 48-72 h - antifungal at 4-7 days`,`MASCC score table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Initial assessment and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Risk stratification (MASCC)`,marks:2},{part:`Empirical antibiotic management`,marks:3},{part:`Persistent fever, duration and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`febrile neutropenia`,`MASCC`,`piperacillin-tazobactam`,`cefepime`,`empirical antifungal`,`ECIL-4`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-immunocompromised-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on screening and prophylaxis before starting biologic therapy.`,openingLines:[`Biologics such as anti-TNF agents, rituximab and JAK inhibitors reactivate latent infections - chiefly tuberculosis, hepatitis B and herpes zoster.`,`Screening and prophylaxis before the first dose prevent most of these infections and is mandatory standard of care.`],answer:[{heading:`Tuberculosis`,points:[`Symptom screen, IGRA or TST and chest X-ray; exclude active TB.`,`Latent TB: TPT (3HP, 4R or 6H) at least 1 month before anti-TNF; complete the course.`]},{heading:`Hepatitis B and other viruses`,points:[`HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs; HBsAg-positive or anti-HBc-positive on anti-CD20 - entecavir or tenofovir until 12-18 months after therapy.`,`Anti-HCV and HIV testing; VZV serology.`]},{heading:`Vaccination`,points:[`Inactivated vaccines 2 weeks before, live vaccines 4 weeks before; influenza yearly, pneumococcal, recombinant zoster vaccine, hepatitis B if non-immune.`]},{heading:`Other prophylaxis and counselling`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole for PJP with prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more; ivermectin for Strongyloides risk before high-dose steroids.`,`Hold biologic during serious infection; report fever early.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: test - positive result - action before biologic`],markSplit:[{part:`TB screening and TPT`,marks:2},{part:`HBV and other viral screening`,marks:1.5},{part:`Vaccination and other prophylaxis`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`anti-TNF`,`IGRA`,`TPT`,`HBV reactivation`,`rituximab`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections in patients with cancer; Infections in transplant recipients`,`Freifeld AG et al. IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Use of Antimicrobial Agents in Neutropenic Patients with Cancer, 2010 update`,`Taplitz RA et al. Outpatient Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Adults Treated for Malignancy: ASCO/IDSA Guideline Update, 2018; and Antimicrobial Prophylaxis ASCO/IDSA, 2018`,`Averbuch D et al. ECIL-4 European guidelines for empirical antibacterial therapy for febrile neutropenic patients, 2013`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022; ICMR AMRSN Annual Report 2023`,`IDSA Guidance on the Treatment of Antimicrobial-Resistant Gram-Negative Infections, 2024`,`AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on Prevention and Treatment of Hepatitis B Virus Reactivation, 2025; ACR Guideline for Vaccinations in Rheumatic and Musculoskeletal Diseases, 2022`,`NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB Preventive Treatment in India, 2021; WHO Consolidated Guidelines on TB Module 1: Prevention, 2024`]}),c.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections`,title:`Infections in pregnancy: TORCH, malaria, UTI, GBS, antibiotics safety`,oneLiner:`Infections in pregnancy matter twice - **altered maternal immunity** makes malaria, influenza, listeriosis, hepatitis E and pyelonephritis more severe, and **vertical transmission** (the **TORCH** group, syphilis, HIV, HBV) harms the fetus; care rests on **targeted antenatal screening (HIV, syphilis, HBsAg, urine culture) rather than blanket TORCH IgM panels**, trimester-appropriate antimalarials, treating **asymptomatic bacteriuria**, intrapartum prophylaxis for **group B streptococcus** by risk factors in India, and choosing antibiotics from the **safe list (penicillins, cephalosporins, azithromycin, clindamycin)**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`TORCH`,`congenital rubella syndrome`,`congenital CMV`,`toxoplasmosis in pregnancy`,`spiramycin`,`parvovirus B19 hydrops`,`varicella in pregnancy`,`malaria in pregnancy`,`artemether-lumefantrine first trimester`,`asymptomatic bacteriuria`,`pyelonephritis pregnancy`,`group B streptococcus`,`intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis`,`listeriosis`,`antibiotics in pregnancy`,`IgG avidity`,`triple elimination`,`maternal sepsis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Infections in pregnancy** cover maternal infections that are more frequent or more severe in pregnancy and those transmitted **vertically** - transplacentally, during delivery or by breastfeeding - to the fetus or newborn. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**TORCH** is an acronym for **Toxoplasma, Others** (syphilis, varicella-zoster, parvovirus B19, HIV, HBV, Zika, Listeria), **Rubella, Cytomegalovirus and Herpes simplex** - infections that cross the placenta or infect at birth and cause congenital disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Maternal sepsis** causes about **11% of maternal deaths worldwide** and is the third commonest direct cause; most cases arise from genital tract infection, pyelonephritis and pneumonia. [WHO 2014; WHO 2017]`,`India's maternal mortality ratio is **93 per 100,000 live births** (SRS 2019-21); infection and sepsis remain an important preventable share, alongside haemorrhage and hypertensive disorders. [SRS 2019-21]`,`**CMV** is the commonest congenital infection worldwide (about **0.5-1% of live births**) and the leading **non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss** in children. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria** affects **2-10% of pregnant women**; untreated, **20-30%** progress to pyelonephritis, compared with about 1-2% after treatment. [USPSTF 2019]`,`**GBS rectovaginal colonisation** is about 10-30% in Western cohorts but lower in most Indian studies (roughly **2-12%**), which underlies the Indian preference for **risk-based** rather than universal-screening prophylaxis. [FOGSI 2021; RCOG GTG 36 2017]`,`**Malaria in pregnancy** raises the risk of maternal anaemia, severe malaria, miscarriage, stillbirth, preterm birth and low birth weight; falciparum is most dangerous in primigravidae and in low-transmission settings such as most of India. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Urinary tract**: E. coli causes **70-80%** of ASB and pyelonephritis; Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterococcus and GBS make up the rest. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Vector-borne fevers** in Indian pregnancy: malaria, dengue, scrub typhus and leptospirosis - season and region decide the likely cause (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Food-borne**: Listeria monocytogenes (unpasteurised dairy, soft cheese, ready-to-eat meats, unwashed raw produce), Toxoplasma (undercooked meat, cat faeces, soil) and hepatitis E (contaminated water). [CDC 2024]`,`**Primary maternal infection** carries the highest fetal risk for CMV, toxoplasma and HSV; **reactivation or reinfection** transmits far less often. [ECCI 2024]`,`Risk factors for CMV: **contact with young children's saliva and urine** (mothers of toddlers, nursery workers); for rubella: **non-immune women** - about 10-20% of Indian women of reproductive age in serosurveys. [ECCI 2024; Harrison 22e]`,`GBS early-onset disease risk factors: **preterm labour under 37 weeks**, **rupture of membranes 18 hours or more**, **intrapartum fever 38 C or more**, **GBS bacteriuria** in the current pregnancy and a **previous baby with GBS disease**. [ACOG 2020]`,`Host factors: diabetes, sickle cell disease, HIV, anaemia, urinary tract anomalies, previous UTI, poor sanitation and low socioeconomic status increase infection risk. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why pregnancy changes infection`,points:[`Pregnancy shifts immunity away from **cell-mediated (Th1) responses** towards tolerance of the fetal allograft, so **intracellular pathogens** - Listeria, malaria, influenza, varicella, hepatitis E - cause more severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Progesterone relaxes ureteric smooth muscle** and the gravid uterus compresses the ureters (right more than left), causing **hydroureter and urinary stasis** - this is why ASB progresses to pyelonephritis in pregnancy. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Placental malaria**: P. falciparum-infected red cells express **VAR2CSA**, which binds **chondroitin sulphate A** in the placenta; sequestration impairs nutrient transfer - hence low birth weight, and primigravidae (who lack anti-VAR2CSA antibody) suffer most. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transplacental transmission** of toxoplasma rises with gestation as the placenta grows and becomes more permeable, but **fetal damage is greatest early** when organogenesis is under way - transmission and severity move in opposite directions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella** infects the placenta and fetal endothelium, causing vasculitis, cell-cycle arrest and organ hypoplasia during organogenesis - eye, heart and cochlea are damaged most before **12 weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parvovirus B19** binds the **P antigen** on erythroid progenitors, halting fetal red cell production; with the short fetal red cell life-span this causes **severe anaemia, high-output failure and hydrops fetalis**, mostly before 20 weeks. [RCOG 2024]`,`**Listeria** has tropism for the placenta, crosses it via internalins binding E-cadherin, and causes chorioamnionitis, fetal loss and neonatal sepsis while the mother has only a flu-like illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Physiological changes** alter drug handling: **GFR rises about 50%** and volume of distribution increases, so renally cleared antibiotics (beta-lactams, aminoglycosides) need full doses. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Classification - TORCH organisms, timing and fetal risk`,points:[`**Rubella**: risk of congenital rubella syndrome is **up to 90% with infection before 11-12 weeks**, falls to about 10-20% at 13-16 weeks and is rare after **20 weeks**; the classic triad is **cataract, congenital heart disease (PDA, pulmonary artery stenosis) and sensorineural deafness**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV**: primary infection transmits in **30-40%**, recurrent in about 1%; severe sequelae follow mainly **first-trimester** infection - microcephaly, periventricular calcification, chorioretinitis, hearing loss. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Toxoplasma**: transmission about **15% at 13 weeks, 45% at 26 weeks and 70% at 36 weeks**; the classic triad is **chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus and diffuse intracranial calcification**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella**: **fetal varicella syndrome** (limb hypoplasia, dermatomal scarring, eye and brain defects) in about **0.4% before 13 weeks and 2% at 13-20 weeks**; maternal rash from 5 days before to 2 days after delivery causes severe **neonatal varicella**. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**HSV**: neonatal herpes risk is about **30-50% with primary genital infection near delivery** versus under 1-3% with recurrence - caesarean is advised for primary lesions in the third trimester. [RCOG 2024]`,`**Syphilis**: transmission up to **70-100% in untreated primary and secondary syphilis**; causes stillbirth, hydrops and early congenital syphilis (snuffles, rash, hepatosplenomegaly, osteochondritis). [WHO 2023]`,`**Hepatitis B**: transmission **70-90% when the mother is HBeAg-positive** without prophylaxis, versus 10-20% when HBeAg-negative; infected infants become chronic carriers in about 90%. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Record **gestational age**, trimester and LMP first - it decides fetal risk, which tests mean anything and which drugs are safe. [FOGSI 2021]`,`**Rubella** history: rash and lymphadenopathy (suboccipital, postauricular), arthralgia, a contact with rash illness, and whether **MR vaccine** was received. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV and toxoplasma** are usually silent or mononucleosis-like (fever, fatigue, lymphadenopathy); most are detected through **ultrasound abnormalities** - ventriculomegaly, calcification, echogenic bowel, growth restriction. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Pyelonephritis**: fever with rigors, loin pain, vomiting, dysuria; it is commonest in the **second and third trimesters** and on the right side. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Listeriosis** presents as a **febrile flu-like illness** with myalgia, backache and diarrhoea; ask about soft cheese, street food and unpasteurised milk; reduced fetal movements may follow. [CDC 2024]`,`**Malaria** may be atypical: low parasitaemia with heavy placental load, **hypoglycaemia**, severe anaemia, pulmonary oedema and preterm labour. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chorioamnionitis (intra-amniotic infection)**: intrapartum fever with **fetal tachycardia over 160**, uterine tenderness, foul-smelling liquor, maternal leucocytosis - usually after prolonged rupture of membranes. [ACOG 2017]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and what they mean`,points:[`Use an **obstetric early warning score (MEOWS)**; temperature **38 C or more**, HR over 100, RR over 20, SBP under 90 or SpO2 under 95% trigger sepsis evaluation. [RCOG GTG 64a 2012]`,`**Renal angle tenderness** with fever = pyelonephritis; **uterine tenderness and offensive discharge** = chorioamnionitis or endometritis. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Pallor, splenomegaly and jaundice** suggest malaria; **conjunctival suffusion and calf tenderness** suggest leptospirosis; an **eschar** suggests scrub typhus. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Vesicular rash** in crops with fever and cough = varicella - look for **varicella pneumonia** (tachypnoea, crepitations, hypoxia), which carries a high maternal risk. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Genital ulcers**: painless indurated chancre (syphilis) versus painful multiple vesicles (HSV) - both change delivery and neonatal plans. [WHO 2023]`,`**Fetal assessment**: fundal height (growth restriction), fetal heart rate (tachycardia in chorioamnionitis), and ultrasound for hydrops, ventriculomegaly or calcification. [FOGSI 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever in pregnancy`,points:[`**Pyelonephritis versus appendicitis**: loin pain, pyuria and positive culture favour pyelonephritis; the appendix is displaced upwards in late pregnancy, so right upper quadrant pain can be appendicitis. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP** mimic infective hepatitis or sepsis - hypoglycaemia, high uric acid and coagulopathy (AFLP) or hypertension with haemolysis and low platelets (HELLP) discriminate. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Hepatitis E in the third trimester** progresses to acute liver failure in up to **15-25%** - jaundice with fever in a pregnant woman needs IgM anti-HEV and urgent referral (see the Viral hepatitis topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dengue** in pregnancy causes thrombocytopenia that mimics HELLP; NS1 or IgM and the fever pattern separate them (see the Dengue topic). [WHO 2025]`,`**Chorioamnionitis versus epidural-related fever**: epidural fever is low-grade without fetal tachycardia or uterine tenderness; diagnosis of infection needs fever plus a second sign. [ACOG 2017]`,`**Puerperal fever** sources by day: genital tract (endometritis), breast (mastitis, abscess), urinary, wound, thrombophlebitis and VTE - the '6 Ws'. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - screening and diagnosis`,points:[`**Universal antenatal screening** at the first visit under MoHFW: **HIV, syphilis (RPR/VDRL or point-of-care rapid test) and HBsAg**, haemoglobin, blood group and urine; repeat syphilis and HIV in the third trimester where risk is high. [MoHFW 2023; NACO 2023]`,`**Urine culture at 12-16 weeks** (or at the first visit) screens for ASB: **10^5 CFU/mL or more of a single organism** in a midstream sample; any count of GBS is significant. [USPSTF 2019]`,`**Blanket TORCH IgM panels are not recommended** in asymptomatic women or after miscarriage: IgM is often **false-positive or persists for months**, causing needless termination; test only for a specific clinical or ultrasound indication. [FOGSI 2021; ACOG 2015]`,`**IgG avidity**: **high avidity** means infection over **3-4 months earlier** (before conception if tested in the first trimester); **low avidity** with IgM suggests recent primary infection - used for CMV and toxoplasma. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Amniotic fluid PCR** confirms fetal CMV or toxoplasma infection; do it **after 18-21 weeks and at least 6-8 weeks after maternal infection** to avoid false negatives. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Parvovirus**: maternal IgM and PCR; if positive, **weekly or fortnightly middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity Doppler** for 8-12 weeks - MCA-PSV over 1.5 MoM signals fetal anaemia. [RCOG 2024]`,`**Fever work-up**: CBC, smear and malaria RDT, dengue NS1/IgM, urine microscopy and culture, **two blood cultures**, LFT, renal function, lactate; chest X-ray with abdominal shielding when indicated. [ICMR 2022]`,`**GBS**: in a universal-screening system, **rectovaginal swab culture at 36+0 to 37+6 weeks** (ACOG); in India, culture is done when available, and risk factors guide prophylaxis. [ACOG 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**ASB and cystitis**: treat for **5-7 days** - **nitrofurantoin 100 mg MR orally twice daily** (avoid at term and in G6PD deficiency), **cephalexin 500 mg orally 8-12-hourly**, amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 8-hourly, or **fosfomycin 3 g single dose**; repeat culture 1-2 weeks later as **test of cure**. [ICMR 2022; ACOG 2023]`,`**Uncomplicated vivax malaria**: **chloroquine 25 mg base/kg over 3 days** (10, 10, 5 mg/kg) in any trimester; **primaquine is contraindicated in pregnancy** - give it after delivery and breastfeeding, with weekly chloroquine suppression meanwhile if relapse risk is high. [NCVBDC 2023; WHO 2024]`,`**Uncomplicated falciparum malaria** in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters: **ACT** - artesunate plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine (artemether-lumefantrine in the North-East) for 3 days; first trimester - see the table and the guideline note. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Syphilis**: **benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM single dose** for early syphilis, **weekly for 3 weeks** for late latent or unknown duration; only penicillin treats the fetus - **desensitise** if allergic; treat the partner. [WHO 2023; CDC 2021]`,`**Varicella contact** in a non-immune woman: **varicella-zoster immunoglobulin within 10 days** of exposure; with rash, **oral aciclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days** if seen within 24 hours of rash and at 20 weeks or more (consider earlier too). [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Toxoplasmosis** (recent maternal infection): **spiramycin 1 g (3 million IU) orally 8-hourly** until delivery if no fetal infection is shown; **pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine plus folinic acid** after 18 weeks if amniotic PCR is positive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus or rickettsial fever** in pregnancy: **azithromycin 500 mg orally once daily for 5 days** instead of doxycycline. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Influenza**: **oseltamivir 75 mg orally twice daily for 5 days**, started within 48 hours or at any time if severe - pregnant women are a high-risk group. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** pyelonephritis, severe or falciparum malaria in the first trimester, varicella pneumonia, listeriosis, chorioamnionitis, any sepsis, and dengue with warning signs; involve obstetrics and medicine jointly. [RCOG GTG 64a 2012]`,`**Pyelonephritis**: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily** until afebrile 24-48 hours, then oral cephalosporin to complete **10-14 days**; then **suppressive prophylaxis** (nitrofurantoin 50-100 mg or cephalexin 250-500 mg at night) for the rest of pregnancy after recurrence. [ACOG 2023; ICMR 2022]`,`**Severe malaria in any trimester**: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours then daily** until oral therapy can be taken, then a full ACT course; monitor **glucose 4-hourly** and avoid fluid overload. [WHO 2024; NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Maternal sepsis**: the **sepsis six within 1 hour** - oxygen, two blood cultures, IV broad-spectrum antibiotics (piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g 8-hourly or ceftriaxone plus metronidazole), fluids, lactate, urine output; left lateral tilt; deliver if the source is uterine. [RCOG GTG 64a 2012]`,`**Listeriosis**: **ampicillin 2 g IV 4-hourly** (plus gentamicin in severe cases) for **14 days** for bacteraemia; cephalosporins have **no activity against Listeria**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GBS intrapartum prophylaxis**: **benzylpenicillin 5 million units (3 g) IV, then 2.5-3 million units (1.5-1.8 g) 4-hourly** until delivery, or ampicillin 2 g then 1 g 4-hourly; started **4 hours or more before delivery** for adequacy. [ACOG 2020; RCOG GTG 36 2017]`,`GBS with penicillin allergy: **cefazolin 2 g IV then 1 g 8-hourly** if low risk of anaphylaxis; **clindamycin 900 mg IV 8-hourly** (if susceptible) or **vancomycin** if high risk. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Chorioamnionitis**: **ampicillin 2 g IV 6-hourly plus gentamicin 5 mg/kg once daily**, expedite delivery; add clindamycin or metronidazole after caesarean section. [ACOG 2017]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Malaria in the first trimester**: WHO (2022 onwards) recommends **artemether-lumefantrine** for uncomplicated falciparum malaria in the first trimester; older Indian guidance used **quinine 10 mg/kg 8-hourly plus clindamycin 10 mg/kg 12-hourly for 7 days** - follow the current NCVBDC circular. [WHO 2024; NCVBDC 2013]`,`**HIV**: all pregnant women with HIV start **lifelong TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir)** at once regardless of CD4; infant prophylaxis per risk (see the HIV special situations topic). [NACO 2021]`,`**HBsAg-positive mother**: **tenofovir disoproxil 300 mg daily from 28 weeks** to at least delivery if HBV DNA is **200,000 IU/mL or more** or HBeAg-positive (or for all HBsAg-positive women where DNA testing is unavailable); baby gets **HBIG plus birth-dose vaccine within 12-24 hours**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Breastfeeding**: penicillins, cephalosporins, macrolides, nitrofurantoin and metronidazole are compatible; avoid primaquine until the infant is tested for G6PD (or for the first 6 months). [WHO 2024]`,`**Postpartum**: endometritis after caesarean - **clindamycin 900 mg IV 8-hourly plus gentamicin 5 mg/kg daily**; mastitis - continue breastfeeding and give flucloxacillin or cloxacillin 500 mg 6-hourly or cephalexin. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: point-of-care dual HIV-syphilis tests at the first ANC visit with same-day benzathine penicillin; dipstick nitrite and leucocyte esterase are poor screening substitutes for culture (sensitivity about 50%). [WHO 2023; USPSTF 2019]`,`**Diabetes and sickle cell disease** raise the risk of ASB and pyelonephritis - screen urine each trimester. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Pyelonephritis** causes **ARDS in 1-2%**, septic shock, anaemia, preterm labour and low birth weight; recurrence occurs in 6-8% without suppression. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Congenital CMV**: about **10-15% of infected newborns are symptomatic** at birth; among them, most develop neurodevelopmental sequelae, while 10-15% of asymptomatic infants develop late hearing loss. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Untreated syphilis** causes stillbirth or neonatal death in up to **40%** of affected pregnancies; congenital syphilis is fully preventable with one penicillin injection before 28 weeks. [WHO 2023]`,`**Falciparum malaria in pregnancy** causes maternal mortality in severe disease, plus miscarriage, stillbirth and low birth weight from placental sequestration. [WHO 2024]`,`**Varicella pneumonia** develops in up to **10%** of pregnant women with chickenpox and is more severe in late pregnancy and in smokers. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Early-onset GBS disease** (within 7 days of birth) causes neonatal sepsis, pneumonia and meningitis; intrapartum prophylaxis reduces it by about **80%**. [ACOG 2020]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer to a **fetal medicine unit** for suspected fetal infection, abnormal ultrasound (hydrops, ventriculomegaly, calcification) or confirmed maternal primary CMV, toxoplasma, parvovirus or rubella. [ECCI 2024]`,`After treated ASB or UTI: **test-of-cure culture** in 1-2 weeks and then **monthly or each trimester** until delivery. [ACOG 2023]`,`After treated syphilis: **quantitative RPR at 3 and 6 months** and at delivery; a **fourfold rise** (two dilutions) means reinfection or failure. [CDC 2021]`,`**Neonatal follow-up**: hearing screen and CMV PCR in the first 3 weeks for suspected congenital CMV; RPR and examination for syphilis-exposed newborns; HBsAg and anti-HBs at 9-12 months for HBV-exposed infants. [ECCI 2024; WHO 2024]`,`**Malaria**: repeat smear on day 3, 7 and 28; give iron and folic acid for anaemia; monitor fetal growth. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Pre-conception**: check rubella immunity and give **MR vaccine**, then avoid pregnancy for **1 month**; varicella vaccine to non-immune women (2 doses, avoid pregnancy for 1 month); hepatitis B vaccine if non-immune. [MoHFW 2023; ACIP 2024]`,`**In pregnancy**: **Td** (two doses or one booster) under the Universal Immunization Programme, **inactivated influenza vaccine** in any trimester, and **Tdap at 27-36 weeks** where available; no live vaccines. [MoHFW UIP 2023; ACIP 2024]`,`**CMV hygiene counselling**: hand washing after nappy changes, not sharing cutlery or kissing young children on the mouth - reduces primary CMV infection. [ECCI 2024]`,`**Food safety**: avoid unpasteurised milk and soft cheese, undercooked meat, raw eggs and cat litter; wash produce - prevents listeriosis and toxoplasmosis. [CDC 2024]`,`**Malaria prevention**: long-lasting insecticidal nets given to pregnant women in high-endemic districts under NCVBDC; India does not use routine IPTp because transmission is low and unstable. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Programme links**: PMSMA (9th of every month free ANC), NACO PPTCT and the **elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis**, HBV birth dose under UIP, and NVHCP testing of pregnant women for HBsAg. [MoHFW 2023; NACO 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO malaria guidelines (2022 update, carried into 2024-2025 versions)**: **artemether-lumefantrine is recommended in the first trimester** for uncomplicated falciparum malaria, replacing quinine plus clindamycin as first choice. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO HBV guidelines 2024**: tenofovir prophylaxis from the second or third trimester for HBsAg-positive women with HBV DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more or HBeAg-positive - and for **all HBsAg-positive pregnant women where DNA or HBeAg testing is not available**. [WHO 2024]`,`**ECCI consensus 2024 on congenital CMV**: offer **valaciclovir 8 g/day (2 g four times daily)** after primary maternal infection in the periconception period or first trimester to reduce transmission; supports early serological screening where pathways exist. [ECCI 2024]`,`**ACIP 2023-2024**: maternal **RSV prefusion F vaccine at 32-36 weeks** in the RSV season to protect infants (not yet in the Indian programme). [ACIP 2024]`,`**WHO 2023 syphilis guidance** reaffirms benzathine penicillin as the only adequate treatment in pregnancy and promotes dual HIV-syphilis point-of-care testing at first ANC towards **triple elimination** (HIV, syphilis, HBV). [WHO 2023]`,`**ACOG Clinical Consensus on UTI in pregnancy (2023)**: screen ASB with culture early in pregnancy, treat for 5-7 days, avoid nitrofurantoin and sulfonamides near term only when alternatives exist. [ACOG 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'TORCH screening - useful or harmful?'** - write the limits of IgM, the role of avidity and amniotic PCR, and that targeted screening (HIV, syphilis, HBV) is the standard. [FOGSI 2021]`,`Classic question: **antimalarials by trimester** - chloroquine for vivax in all trimesters, ACT in 2nd and 3rd, AL or quinine plus clindamycin in the 1st, IV artesunate for severe malaria in all, **no primaquine**. [WHO 2024]`,`Common mistake: **giving doxycycline** for suspected scrub typhus in pregnancy - use azithromycin. [ICMR 2022]`,`Common mistake: **ceftriaxone for listeriosis** - Listeria is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins; use ampicillin. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **not treating asymptomatic bacteriuria** in pregnancy, while correctly not treating it in non-pregnant adults. [USPSTF 2019]`,`Viva: why risk-based GBS policy in India - **lower colonisation**, limited culture capacity and the RCOG risk-based model; ACOG uses universal screening at 36-37 weeks. [RCOG GTG 36 2017; ACOG 2020]`]}],tables:[{heading:`TORCH and related infections: timing, fetal effects and action`,columns:[`Infection`,`Highest fetal risk`,`Classic fetal/neonatal features`,`Key action`],rows:[[`Toxoplasma`,`Early infection severe; transmission higher late`,`Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, diffuse calcification`,`Spiramycin; pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine if fetal infection`],[`Rubella`,`Before 12 weeks (up to 90%)`,`Cataract, PDA/pulmonary stenosis, deafness, 'blueberry muffin' rash`,`MR vaccine before pregnancy; counselling`],[`CMV`,`Primary infection in first trimester`,`Microcephaly, periventricular calcification, hearing loss`,`Valaciclovir 8 g/day after first-trimester primary infection; neonatal valganciclovir`],[`HSV`,`Primary genital infection near term`,`Neonatal skin-eye-mouth, CNS or disseminated disease`,`Aciclovir from 36 weeks; caesarean for primary lesions`],[`Varicella`,`Before 20 weeks (FVS); peripartum rash (neonatal)`,`Limb hypoplasia, cicatricial skin, eye defects`,`VZIG within 10 days; aciclovir`],[`Parvovirus B19`,`Before 20 weeks`,`Fetal anaemia, hydrops`,`MCA-PSV Doppler; intrauterine transfusion`],[`Syphilis`,`Any stage, highest in early syphilis`,`Stillbirth, hydrops, snuffles, rash, bone lesions`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM`],[`Hepatitis B`,`HBeAg-positive or high HBV DNA`,`Chronic infection in about 90% of infected infants`,`Tenofovir from 28 weeks; HBIG + birth dose`]]},{heading:`Antibiotic safety in pregnancy`,columns:[`Category`,`Drugs`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Safe (first choice)`,`Penicillins, amoxicillin-clavulanate, cephalosporins, azithromycin, erythromycin base, clindamycin`,`Long safety record in all trimesters`],[`Safe with timing caution`,`Nitrofurantoin`,`Avoid at term (after 36-38 weeks) and in G6PD deficiency - neonatal haemolysis`],[`Safe with timing caution`,`Cotrimoxazole`,`Avoid in first trimester (folate antagonism) and near term (kernicterus)`],[`Acceptable when needed`,`Metronidazole, fosfomycin, carbapenems, vancomycin, gentamicin (short course)`,`Metronidazole safe in all trimesters; monitor aminoglycoside levels`],[`Avoid`,`Tetracyclines/doxycycline`,`Teeth discolouration and bone deposition after 15 weeks; use azithromycin`],[`Avoid`,`Fluoroquinolones`,`Cartilage toxicity in animals; use only if no alternative (e.g. MDR TB)`],[`Avoid`,`Streptomycin, kanamycin`,`Fetal ototoxicity (VIII nerve)`],[`Avoid`,`Chloramphenicol`,`Grey baby syndrome near term`],[`Contraindicated`,`Primaquine, tafenoquine`,`Fetal G6PD status unknown - haemolysis`]]},{heading:`Drugs for common infections in pregnancy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Nitrofurantoin MR`,`100 mg orally 12-hourly`,`5-7 days (ASB, cystitis)`,`Avoid at term, G6PD deficiency, eGFR under 45`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg orally 8-12-hourly`,`5-7 days; 250-500 mg nightly for suppression`,`Diarrhoea, allergy`],[`Fosfomycin`,`3 g orally single dose`,`Once`,`Diarrhoea; limited for pyelonephritis`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV once daily`,`Until afebrile 48 h, total 10-14 days for pyelonephritis`,`Biliary sludge; no Listeria cover`],[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`2.4 MU IM (1.2 MU each buttock)`,`Single (early) or weekly x 3 (late/unknown)`,`Anaphylaxis - desensitise if allergic; Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction can trigger labour`],[`Benzylpenicillin (GBS IAP)`,`5 MU IV, then 2.5-3 MU 4-hourly`,`Until delivery; 4 h or more before birth`,`Allergy - cefazolin or clindamycin`],[`Ampicillin (Listeria)`,`2 g IV 4-hourly`,`14 days (bacteraemia); 21 days if meningitis`,`Rash, allergy`],[`Chloroquine`,`25 mg base/kg over 3 days`,`3 days; weekly suppression until delivery if needed`,`Pruritus, nausea; safe in all trimesters`],[`Artemether-lumefantrine`,`80/480 mg (4 tablets) 12-hourly with fat`,`3 days (6 doses)`,`Take with food; WHO allows first trimester`],[`Artesunate IV`,`2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily`,`Until oral ACT tolerated`,`Delayed haemolysis; check glucose`],[`Spiramycin`,`1 g (3 MIU) orally 8-hourly`,`Until delivery if no fetal infection`,`GI upset; does not treat an infected fetus`],[`Aciclovir`,`800 mg orally 5 times daily (varicella); 400 mg 8-hourly from 36 weeks (HSV suppression)`,`7 days (varicella)`,`Renal dosing; IV 10 mg/kg 8-hourly for pneumonia`]]},{heading:`Antimalarials by trimester (uncomplicated malaria)`,columns:[`Situation`,`First trimester`,`Second and third trimesters`,`Note`],rows:[[`P. vivax / P. ovale`,`Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days`,`Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days`,`No primaquine until after delivery and breastfeeding`],[`P. falciparum (most of India)`,`Artemether-lumefantrine (WHO); quinine + clindamycin 7 days (older NCVBDC)`,`ACT: AS + SP (AL in North-East states)`,`Follow current NCVBDC circular`],[`Mixed infection`,`As for falciparum`,`ACT`,`Primaquine deferred`],[`Severe malaria (any species)`,`IV artesunate`,`IV artesunate`,`Life of mother takes priority in all trimesters`]]},{heading:`Serology interpretation for CMV and toxoplasma`,columns:[`IgG`,`IgM`,`IgG avidity`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Negative`,`Negative`,`Not applicable`,`Non-immune - counsel hygiene; repeat only if symptoms`],[`Positive`,`Negative`,`Not needed`,`Past infection - low fetal risk`],[`Positive`,`Positive`,`High`,`Infection over 3-4 months earlier - low risk if tested in first trimester`],[`Positive`,`Positive`,`Low`,`Recent primary infection - fetal medicine referral, amniocentesis after 18-21 weeks`],[`Negative`,`Positive`,`Not applicable`,`Very early infection or false-positive IgM - repeat in 2-3 weeks for seroconversion`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever with jaundice or low platelets in pregnancy`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clinical clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Falciparum malaria`,`Rigors, anaemia, hypoglycaemia`,`Smear or RDT`],[`Dengue`,`Retro-orbital pain, haemoconcentration`,`NS1 / IgM`],[`Hepatitis E`,`Third trimester, prodrome, very high ALT`,`IgM anti-HEV`],[`HELLP syndrome`,`Hypertension, proteinuria, epigastric pain`,`Haemolysis (LDH, smear), low platelets, raised AST`],[`Acute fatty liver of pregnancy`,`Third trimester, vomiting, hypoglycaemia`,`Swansea criteria, coagulopathy, high uric acid`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Flood exposure, calf tenderness, AKI`,`IgM ELISA / MAT`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with SBP under 90, HR over 120, RR over 25, SpO2 under 95% or altered sensorium - maternal sepsis`,`Fever with loin pain and vomiting - pyelonephritis needing admission and IV antibiotics`,`Falciparum malaria in pregnancy, or any malaria with hypoglycaemia, anaemia or breathlessness`,`Jaundice with fever in the third trimester - hepatitis E, AFLP, HELLP or severe malaria`,`Varicella with cough or breathlessness - varicella pneumonia`,`Intrapartum fever with fetal tachycardia or foul liquor - chorioamnionitis`,`Reduced fetal movements or hydrops on ultrasound after a febrile illness`],pearls:[`TORCH IgM screening of asymptomatic women causes more harm than good - test with a reason, interpret with avidity.`,`Rubella before 12 weeks: up to 90% fetal damage - cataract, PDA, deafness.`,`Toxoplasma: transmission rises, severity falls with gestation.`,`Chloroquine in every trimester for vivax; primaquine never in pregnancy.`,`IV artesunate for severe malaria in all trimesters - the mother comes first.`,`Treat ASB in pregnancy (culture 10^5 CFU/mL) and do a test of cure.`,`Listeria: ampicillin, not ceftriaxone.`,`Only penicillin treats syphilis in pregnancy - desensitise, do not substitute.`,`Scrub typhus in pregnancy: azithromycin, not doxycycline.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-q1`,stem:`A primigravida at 10 weeks has a 'TORCH panel' sent by another clinic that shows CMV IgM positive and IgG positive. She is well. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Advise termination of pregnancy`,`Start oral ganciclovir`,`Request CMV IgG avidity testing`,`Repeat the TORCH panel at 20 weeks and reassure`],answer:2,explanation:`CMV IgM is often false-positive or persists for months, so it cannot date infection on its own. IgG avidity separates recent primary infection (low avidity) from past infection (high avidity); if primary, she is referred to fetal medicine and valaciclovir may be offered. Termination on a single IgM is wrong, ganciclovir is not used in pregnancy, and waiting until 20 weeks loses the window.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-q2`,stem:`A woman at 24 weeks has smear-positive P. vivax malaria with no danger signs. Which treatment is correct?`,options:[`Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days with primaquine 0.25 mg/kg for 14 days`,`Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days, deferring primaquine until after delivery and breastfeeding`,`Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days`,`IV artesunate for 3 days`],answer:1,explanation:`Chloroquine is safe in all trimesters for vivax malaria; primaquine is contraindicated in pregnancy because fetal G6PD status cannot be known, so radical cure is deferred. Doxycycline is avoided in pregnancy and is not an antimalarial monotherapy, and IV artesunate is reserved for severe malaria.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-q3`,stem:`A pregnant woman at 32 weeks has fever, myalgia and diarrhoea after eating soft paneer from a street vendor. Blood culture grows Gram-positive rods with tumbling motility. Which antibiotic is the drug of choice?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone`,`Doxycycline`,`Ciprofloxacin`,`Ampicillin`],answer:3,explanation:`Gram-positive rods with tumbling motility after soft dairy in pregnancy indicate Listeria monocytogenes; ampicillin (with gentamicin if severe) is the treatment of choice. Listeria is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins such as ceftriaxone, while doxycycline and ciprofloxacin are avoided in pregnancy and are not reliable for listeriosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-q4`,stem:`Which finding on routine antenatal urine culture at 14 weeks in an asymptomatic woman requires treatment?`,options:[`10^5 CFU/mL of E. coli in a midstream sample`,`Mixed growth of three organisms at 10^4 CFU/mL`,`Lactobacilli at 10^5 CFU/mL`,`Sterile pyuria with no growth`],answer:0,explanation:`Asymptomatic bacteriuria is a single organism at 10^5 CFU/mL or more; in pregnancy it is treated because 20-30% would otherwise develop pyelonephritis. Mixed growth suggests contamination, lactobacilli are vaginal commensals, and sterile pyuria calls for a different work-up (for example TB or chlamydia), not empirical treatment for ASB.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-q5`,stem:`In an Indian labour room without routine GBS culture, which woman should receive intrapartum benzylpenicillin prophylaxis?`,options:[`A term primigravida with membranes ruptured for 6 hours and no fever`,`A woman in preterm labour at 34 weeks`,`A woman with a planned caesarean before labour with intact membranes`,`A woman whose previous baby was healthy and GBS-negative`],answer:1,explanation:`In a risk-based policy, preterm labour under 37 weeks is an indication for intrapartum GBS prophylaxis, as are rupture of membranes for 18 hours or more, intrapartum fever, GBS bacteriuria and a previous baby with GBS disease. Six hours of ruptured membranes at term is below the threshold, planned caesarean with intact membranes needs no prophylaxis, and a previous healthy baby is not a risk factor.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the effects of malaria on pregnancy and of pregnancy on malaria. Outline the diagnosis and management of malaria in pregnancy as per current Indian and WHO guidelines.`,openingLines:[`Malaria in pregnancy is more frequent and more severe because of altered cell-mediated immunity and placental sequestration of P. falciparum through VAR2CSA binding to chondroitin sulphate A.`,`It threatens the mother (severe anaemia, hypoglycaemia, pulmonary oedema, death) and the fetus (miscarriage, stillbirth, preterm birth, low birth weight).`],answer:[{heading:`Effect of pregnancy on malaria`,points:[`Higher parasitaemia and severity, especially in primigravidae and in low-transmission areas like India.`,`More hypoglycaemia (also quinine-induced), severe anaemia and pulmonary oedema.`]},{heading:`Effect of malaria on pregnancy`,points:[`Placental sequestration - low birth weight, intrauterine growth restriction.`,`Miscarriage, stillbirth, preterm labour; congenital malaria rarely.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Smear and RDT in every febrile pregnant woman in endemic areas; peripheral parasitaemia may underestimate placental load.`,`Assess severity: GCS, glucose, Hb, bilirubin, creatinine, lactate, SpO2; fetal well-being.`]},{heading:`Treatment - uncomplicated`,points:[`Vivax: chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days in any trimester; no primaquine until after delivery and breastfeeding; weekly chloroquine suppression if needed.`,`Falciparum 2nd and 3rd trimester: ACT (AS + SP; AL in North-East) for 3 days.`,`Falciparum 1st trimester: artemether-lumefantrine (WHO 2022 onwards); quinine + clindamycin 7 days per older NCVBDC guidance.`]},{heading:`Treatment - severe malaria`,points:[`IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily in all trimesters, then full oral ACT.`,`Glucose 4-hourly, careful fluids, transfusion for severe anaemia, treat seizures; obstetric care and fetal monitoring.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`LLINs for pregnant women in high-endemic districts; prompt diagnosis at ANC; iron-folic acid; IPTp not used in India.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: antimalarials by trimester and species`,`Flow chart: febrile pregnant woman - smear/RDT - severe or not - trimester-specific treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Pregnancy-malaria interactions`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and severity assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment by trimester and species`,marks:4},{part:`Prevention and programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`malaria in pregnancy`,`VAR2CSA`,`chloroquine`,`artemether-lumefantrine`,`IV artesunate`,`primaquine contraindicated`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-pregnancy-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on TORCH screening in pregnancy - its limitations and the rational approach.`,openingLines:[`TORCH stands for Toxoplasma, Others (syphilis, VZV, parvovirus, HIV, HBV), Rubella, CMV and HSV - infections causing congenital disease.`,`Blanket TORCH IgM panels in asymptomatic women are not recommended because IgM is often false-positive or persistent and leads to unnecessary termination.`],answer:[{heading:`Limitations of TORCH panels`,points:[`IgM false positives and persistence for months; cross-reactivity; no proof of fetal infection.`,`Even true maternal infection transmits only in a proportion (CMV primary 30-40%).`,`Testing after recurrent miscarriage has no proven value.`]},{heading:`What to screen universally`,points:[`HIV, syphilis and HBsAg at the first visit (triple elimination); rubella immunity pre-conception; urine culture for ASB.`]},{heading:`Targeted testing`,points:[`Only for a rash or mononucleosis-like illness, a known exposure, or ultrasound signs (ventriculomegaly, calcification, hydrops, growth restriction).`,`Use IgG avidity; confirm fetal infection by amniotic PCR after 18-21 weeks and 6-8 weeks after maternal infection.`]},{heading:`Management pointers`,points:[`Toxoplasma - spiramycin; CMV - valaciclovir 8 g/day in first-trimester primary infection; parvovirus - MCA Doppler; rubella - counselling.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: IgG / IgM / avidity combinations and interpretation`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and limitations`,marks:2},{part:`Rational screening approach`,marks:2},{part:`Management pointers`,marks:1}],keywords:[`TORCH`,`IgG avidity`,`amniocentesis PCR`,`triple elimination`,`valaciclovir`,`spiramycin`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Obstetrics, 26th edition, 2022`,`WHO Guidelines for Malaria, 2024 (incorporating the 2022 first-trimester artemether-lumefantrine recommendation); NCVBDC National Guidelines for Diagnosis and Treatment of Malaria in India`,`WHO Guidelines for the Prevention, Diagnosis, Care and Treatment for People with Chronic Hepatitis B Infection, 2024`,`Leruez-Ville M et al. European Congenital Infection Initiative (ECCI) consensus on congenital CMV, Lancet Regional Health Europe, 2024`,`ACOG Committee Opinion 797: Prevention of Group B Streptococcal Early-Onset Disease in Newborns, 2020; RCOG Green-top Guideline 36, 2017`,`RCOG Green-top Guideline 13: Chickenpox in Pregnancy; RCOG Green-top Guideline 64a: Bacterial Sepsis in Pregnancy`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2022; MoHFW/NACO guidance on elimination of vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis, 2023`]}),c.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections`,title:`Infections in older adults and long-term care`,oneLiner:`Infections in **older adults** present **atypically** - fever is absent or low in up to half, and **delirium, falls, anorexia or functional decline** may be the only sign - so the fever threshold is lowered (**oral 37.8 C once, or a rise of 1.1 C above baseline**), **asymptomatic bacteriuria is not treated**, antibiotics are chosen for **renal function, interactions and C. difficile risk**, and prevention rests on **vaccination (influenza, pneumococcal, zoster, RSV), hand hygiene and frailty-aware care**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`geriatric infection`,`long-term care facility`,`nursing home infection`,`atypical presentation`,`delirium`,`Confusion Assessment Method`,`asymptomatic bacteriuria`,`Loeb minimum criteria`,`McGeer criteria`,`Clostridioides difficile`,`fidaxomicin`,`aspiration pneumonia`,`frailty`,`Clinical Frailty Scale`,`Beers criteria`,`pneumococcal vaccine`,`recombinant zoster vaccine`,`RSV vaccine`,`NPHCE`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Older adults** are conventionally those aged **60 years or more** in India (65 or more in Western guidelines); **long-term care facilities (LTCF)** include nursing homes, old-age homes and residential care where residents need help with daily living. [Harrison 22e]`,`India had about **149 million people aged 60 or more (10.5%) in 2022**, projected to reach about **20% by 2050**; most care is family-based, with a small but growing formal LTCF sector. [UNFPA India Ageing Report 2023]`,`**LASI wave 1 (2017-19)** found that about **three-quarters of Indian older adults have at least one chronic disease** and a substantial share have functional limitation - the substrate for infection-related decline. [LASI 2020]`,`Infections are among the **leading causes of hospitalisation and death** in older adults; **pneumonia, urinary tract infection, skin and soft tissue infection** and gastroenteritis account for most LTCF infections. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pneumonia mortality rises steeply with age, and older adults bear **about 90% of influenza deaths** in high-income settings. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria** is present in **25-50% of women and 15-40% of men** in LTCF - far commoner than true UTI, and the main driver of inappropriate antibiotic use. [IDSA 2019]`,`LTCF residents act as a **reservoir of multidrug-resistant organisms** (ESBL Enterobacterales, MRSA, CRE) and C. difficile because of frequent antibiotics, devices and shared care. [SHEA/APIC 2008]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Immunosenescence**: thymic involution, fewer naive T cells, poor antibody responses to new antigens and chronic low-grade inflammation ('inflammageing') blunt both defence and vaccine response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspiration risk**: stroke, dementia, Parkinson disease, sedatives, poor oral hygiene and tube feeding - the main route to **pneumonia** in LTCF residents. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Urinary risk**: incomplete emptying (prostatic hypertrophy, neurogenic bladder), post-menopausal oestrogen loss, incontinence and **indwelling catheters** (bacteriuria develops at 3-7% per catheter-day). [IDSA 2009]`,`**Skin**: pressure injuries, venous ulcers, diabetic feet, intertrigo and **scabies outbreaks** (including crusted scabies) in institutions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**C. difficile risk**: age over 65, **recent antibiotics** (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins, co-amoxiclav), **proton pump inhibitors**, hospital or LTCF stay. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Reactivations**: herpes zoster (incidence rises sharply after 50), **tuberculosis** (often reactivation, atypical) and latent infections under steroids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Frailty, malnutrition (low albumin), diabetes, CKD and polypharmacy** independently raise infection risk and mortality. [BGS 2014]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the presentation is atypical`,points:[`Older adults have a **lower basal temperature** and a **blunted febrile response** - reduced pyrogen production and hypothalamic sensitivity - so **37.8 C may be as significant as 38.5 C** in a younger person. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Delirium** results from cytokine effects (IL-1, IL-6, TNF) on a brain with low cognitive reserve, cholinergic deficit and blood-brain barrier leak - infection is the commonest precipitant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reduced thirst, renal concentrating ability and sweating** make dehydration, AKI and hypernatraemia early consequences of infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Impaired cough reflex and mucociliary clearance**, with silent aspiration, explain pneumonia without cough or sputum. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Blunted leucocytosis** - total WBC may be normal; a **left shift (band forms over 6% or absolute band count over 1500/mm3)** is a better pointer to bacterial infection. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Age-related fall in GFR** (often hidden by low muscle mass and a 'normal' creatinine) changes antibiotic clearance - estimate creatinine clearance, not creatinine alone. [AGS Beers 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and criteria the examiner asks`,points:[`**Fever in LTCF residents** (IDSA 2008): single oral temperature **over 37.8 C (100 F)**, or repeated oral over 37.2 C or rectal over 37.5 C, or a rise of **over 1.1 C (2 F) above baseline**. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Loeb minimum criteria** for starting antibiotics for **UTI without a catheter**: acute dysuria alone, **or** fever plus at least one of new or worse urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain, gross haematuria, costovertebral tenderness or incontinence. [Loeb 2001]`,`Loeb criteria **with a catheter**: at least one of fever, new costovertebral tenderness, rigors, or new-onset delirium. [Loeb 2001]`,`**Revised McGeer criteria (2012)** are **surveillance** definitions for LTCF infections (UTI, respiratory, skin, GI) - used to count infections, not to decide treatment at the bedside. [Stone 2012]`,`**Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)**: **acute onset and fluctuating course plus inattention**, with either **disorganised thinking or altered level of consciousness** = delirium; the **4AT** is a quicker screen. [Inouye 1990; SIGN 157 2019]`,`**Frailty**: Fried phenotype (3 or more of weight loss, exhaustion, weak grip, slow gait, low activity) or the **Clinical Frailty Scale 1-9** (5 or more = frail) - higher scores predict infection mortality and guide escalation. [Rockwood 2005; BGS 2014]`,`**C. difficile severity** (IDSA/SHEA): **non-severe** WBC 15,000 or less and creatinine under 1.5 mg/dL; **severe** WBC over 15,000 or creatinine 1.5 or more; **fulminant** - hypotension, shock, ileus or megacolon. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Ask the carer **'what is different from usual?'** - new confusion, falls, reduced eating or drinking, new incontinence, sleepiness or loss of mobility are often the only signs of infection. [BGS 2014]`,`Fever is **absent in 30-50%** of older adults with serious bacterial infection, including bacteraemia, pneumonia and endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumonia** may present with delirium, tachypnoea and falls, without cough or sputum; ask about choking on food, change in voice and swallowing difficulty. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**UTI**: specific symptoms (dysuria, frequency, suprapubic or loin pain) matter; **cloudy or smelly urine alone is not a symptom of infection**. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Diarrhoea**: three or more unformed stools in 24 hours after recent antibiotics or PPI use suggests **C. difficile**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`Take a **medication review**: anticholinergics, sedatives, opioids and new drugs cause delirium; antibiotics in the last 3 months; PPIs; anticoagulants. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`Record **baseline cognition, function and advance care wishes** - goals of care decide investigation intensity and whether hospital transfer helps. [BGS 2014]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and what they mean`,points:[`**Respiratory rate over 25/min** is one of the most sensitive signs of pneumonia and sepsis in older adults - count it for a full minute. [IDSA 2008]`,`**NEWS2 5 or more** triggers urgent review; SBP thresholds mislead in hypertensive patients - compare with the usual BP. [RCP NEWS2 2017]`,`**Pulse oximetry under 90%** or a fall of 3% or more from baseline suggests lower respiratory infection. [IDSA 2008]`,`Look for **hidden foci**: pressure injuries over sacrum and heels, **feet**, catheter, cannula sites, oral cavity (abscess, candida), joints (septic arthritis, gout, pseudogout). [Harrison 22e]`,`Assess **hydration and delirium** (CAM or 4AT) at the first contact and every shift. [SIGN 157 2019]`,`**Faecal impaction and urinary retention** cause delirium and 'pseudo-infection' - palpate the bladder and do a bladder scan. [BGS 2014]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Delirium from non-infective causes**: drugs, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, urinary retention, constipation, pain, stroke and subdural haematoma - infection must not be the default label. [SIGN 157 2019]`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria with delirium**: a positive dipstick or culture in a confused older adult without urinary or systemic signs is usually incidental - look for another cause. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Crystal arthritis** (gout, CPPD) causes fever and a hot joint and mimics septic arthritis - aspirate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug fever**, VTE, malignancy and **polymyalgia rheumatica or giant cell arteritis** (ESR over 50, headache, jaw claudication) cause fever in older adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tuberculosis** in the elderly is often lower-lobe, non-cavitary and smear-negative, mimicking pneumonia or cancer - send CBNAAT if pneumonia fails to resolve. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Aspiration pneumonitis** (chemical, after witnessed vomiting) settles in 24-48 hours without antibiotics, unlike **aspiration pneumonia**. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`First line: **CBC with differential** (left shift), CRP, creatinine with **estimated creatinine clearance**, electrolytes (Na, glucose), LFT, and **two blood cultures** if fever, rigors or sepsis. [IDSA 2008]`,`**Urine culture only with urinary symptoms or sepsis without another source** - do not send urine from every febrile or confused patient. [IDSA 2019]`,`In catheterised patients, **replace the catheter** (if in for over 2 weeks) and send a sample from the new catheter before antibiotics. [IDSA 2009]`,`**Chest X-ray** for respiratory signs, SpO2 fall or unexplained delirium with tachypnoea; it may lag in dehydrated patients. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**C. difficile testing** only in **3 or more unformed stools in 24 hours** not explained by laxatives: two-step **GDH plus toxin EIA**, with NAAT for discordant results. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Procalcitonin** helps decide stopping antibiotics in lower respiratory infection; serum lactate 2 mmol/L or more marks sepsis. [SSC 2026]`,`Add **CBNAAT for TB**, influenza and SARS-CoV-2 testing in season or outbreaks, and skin scrapings for scabies in clusters. [NTEP 2025; MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Do not treat asymptomatic bacteriuria**, including in diabetes, dementia, falls or catheterised patients without systemic signs; exceptions are pregnancy and before urological procedures with mucosal breach. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Cystitis** in an older woman: **nitrofurantoin 100 mg MR twice daily for 5 days** (avoid if creatinine clearance under 30 mL/min) or **fosfomycin 3 g single dose**; cephalexin 500 mg 12-hourly for 5-7 days. [ICMR 2022; AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Community-acquired pneumonia** (CURB-65 0-1, well, able to swallow): **amoxicillin 1 g orally 8-hourly for 5 days**, adding **doxycycline or azithromycin** for atypicals; amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 8-hourly with aspiration risk. [ICMR 2022; ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Herpes zoster** within 72 hours: **valaciclovir 1 g orally 8-hourly for 7 days** (or aciclovir 800 mg five times daily); adequate analgesia to prevent delirium and post-herpetic neuralgia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cellulitis**: **cephalexin 500 mg 6-hourly** or **flucloxacillin/cloxacillin 500 mg 6-hourly for 5-7 days**; elevate the limb and treat tinea pedis (see the Skin and soft tissue infection topic). [IDSA 2014]`,`**Hydration, nutrition, mobilisation and delirium prevention** (orientation, glasses, hearing aids, sleep, avoid restraints and sedatives) are part of treating the infection. [SIGN 157 2019]`,`**Antibiotic doses**: adjust to creatinine clearance (Cockcroft-Gault), and check interactions - **clarithromycin with statins or calcium-channel blockers, cotrimoxazole with ACE inhibitors, spironolactone or warfarin**, fluoroquinolones with warfarin. [AGS Beers 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admission criteria**: sepsis (NEWS2 5 or more, lactate 2 or more), **CURB-65 2 or more**, hypoxia, inability to take oral fluids or drugs, AKI, new delirium without safe care, or failure of oral therapy at 48-72 hours. [ATS/IDSA 2019; BTS 2009]`,`**Severe pneumonia or sepsis**: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily plus azithromycin 500 mg daily**; **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g 6-8-hourly** for recent hospitalisation, LTCF residents with MDR risk or aspiration with severe illness. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fluids**: start **30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid** for sepsis-induced hypotension but **reassess after each 250-500 mL bolus** - older hearts and lungs tolerate overload poorly; MAP 60-65 is acceptable at 65 years or more. [SSC 2026]`,`**C. difficile, initial episode**: **fidaxomicin 200 mg orally twice daily for 10 days** (preferred) or **vancomycin 125 mg orally 6-hourly for 10 days**; **metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly** only for non-severe disease when neither is available. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Fulminant C. difficile**: **vancomycin 500 mg orally or by nasogastric tube 6-hourly plus IV metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly**, rectal vancomycin if ileus, and early **surgical review** (colectomy or loop ileostomy). [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Stop PPIs and unnecessary antibiotics** in C. difficile; use **contact precautions with soap-and-water hand washing** - alcohol rub does not kill spores - and chlorine-based cleaning. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Influenza in LTCF**: **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days** for cases and **75 mg once daily for at least 2 weeks (and 1 week after the last case)** as outbreak prophylaxis for all residents. [IDSA 2018]`,`Shorter courses work: **5 days for CAP** in a stable patient, **7 days for pyelonephritis or male UTI** with prompt response, **7 days for uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia**. [ATS/IDSA 2019; IDSA 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Catheter-associated UTI**: treat only when symptomatic; **remove or replace** the catheter; 7 days if prompt response, 10-14 days if delayed. [IDSA 2009]`,`**Diabetes**: higher risk of emphysematous pyelonephritis, foot infection and malignant otitis externa (see the Infections in diabetes topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: avoid nitrofurantoin under creatinine clearance 30, reduce beta-lactam and cotrimoxazole doses, avoid aminoglycosides where possible; watch for hyperkalaemia with trimethoprim. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Fluoroquinolones**: avoid as first line in older adults - **tendon rupture, aortic aneurysm or dissection, QT prolongation, hypoglycaemia and delirium**; FDA and EMA warnings. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Dementia and end of life**: in advanced dementia, recurrent pneumonia is a marker of the terminal phase; antibiotics may not prolong life - decide with the family according to goals of care. [BGS 2014]`,`**Resource-limited and home settings**: home-based care with oral antibiotics and carer training is reasonable for low-risk infection; ASHAs and Ayushman Arogya Mandir teams can monitor hydration and NEWS. [NPHCE 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Delirium** itself predicts longer stay, institutionalisation, dementia progression and death; it persists for weeks in a third of patients. [SIGN 157 2019]`,`**Functional decline**: up to a third of older inpatients lose independence in daily activities during an infective admission ('hospital-associated disability'). [BGS 2014]`,`**Pneumonia** in frail older adults carries 30-day mortality of **10-30%**; aspiration pneumonia and LTCF residence predict worse outcome. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**C. difficile** recurs in about **20-25%** after a first episode and in up to 40-60% after a recurrence; mortality is highest over 65 years. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Post-herpetic neuralgia** follows zoster in up to 30% of people over 80 - the main argument for zoster vaccination. [Harrison 22e]`,`Acute infection raises the short-term risk of **myocardial infarction and stroke** several-fold (pneumonia and influenza especially). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer urgently: sepsis, hypoxia, inability to swallow, fulminant C. difficile, suspected septic arthritis, necrotising infection or new focal neurological signs. [RCP NEWS2 2017]`,`**Antibiotic time-out at 48-72 hours**: review cultures, diagnosis, route, and duration; switch IV to oral when afebrile, haemodynamically stable and swallowing. [ICMR 2022]`,`Monitor **renal function** (aminoglycosides, vancomycin, beta-lactam dose adjustment), electrolytes (cotrimoxazole), **INR** if on warfarin, and delirium daily. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`After discharge, review in **1-2 weeks** for function, nutrition, falls and a comprehensive geriatric assessment where available. [BGS 2014]`,`Do **not** repeat urine culture after treatment ('test of cure') in non-pregnant older adults without symptoms. [IDSA 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, vaccination and programme links`,points:[`**Influenza vaccine every year** for all aged 60 or more (higher-dose or adjuvanted vaccines preferred at 65 or more where available). [ACIP 2024; API 2023]`,`**Pneumococcal vaccine**: a single dose of **PCV20 or PCV21**, or **PCV15 followed by PPSV23** a year later, for all adults aged **50 or more** (ACIP from October 2024) - Indian consensus advises it at 50-65 years and above. [ACIP 2024; API 2023]`,`**Recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV)**: 2 doses 2-6 months apart for adults **50 or more**, about **90% efficacy** sustained for years; available in India since 2023. [ACIP 2024]`,`**RSV vaccine**: single dose for all adults **75 or more** and for those **50-74 at increased risk** (chronic heart or lung disease, diabetes, LTCF residents). [ACIP 2025]`,`**Infection control in LTCF**: hand hygiene, oral care (reduces pneumonia), **minimise catheters**, swallow assessment, pressure-injury prevention, cohorting in outbreaks and an antibiotic stewardship programme. [SHEA/APIC 2008]`,`**NPHCE** (National Programme for Health Care of the Elderly) provides geriatric clinics at district hospitals and CHCs; **PM-JAY covers all citizens aged 70 or more** regardless of income since October 2024 (Ayushman Vay Vandana card). [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**AGS Beers Criteria 2023**: avoid nitrofurantoin when creatinine clearance is under 30 mL/min or for long-term suppression; flags fluoroquinolone, cotrimoxazole and macrolide interactions. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**ACIP October 2024**: pneumococcal conjugate vaccination age **lowered from 65 to 50**; **ACIP 2024-2025**: RSV vaccine for all 75 or more and **50-74 at increased risk**. [ACIP 2024; ACIP 2025]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026**: NEWS2/MEWS over qSOFA for screening, balanced crystalloids with dynamic reassessment, and **MAP 60-65 acceptable in adults 65 or older**. [SSC 2026]`,`**IDSA 2025 complicated UTI guideline**: shorter courses (about 7 days) when patients improve promptly, and oral step-down - relevant to older men and catheterised patients. [IDSA 2025]`,`**PM-JAY expansion (October 2024)**: health cover up to Rs 5 lakh per family per year for everyone aged 70 or more, regardless of income. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**IDSA/SHEA 2021 CDI update** (still current): **fidaxomicin preferred** for initial and recurrent episodes; FMT after the second recurrence; bezlotoxumab is no longer marketed (withdrawn in 2025). [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Atypical presentation of infection in the elderly'** - list delirium, falls, anorexia, incontinence, functional decline, absent fever, normal WBC, and the LTCF fever definition. [IDSA 2008]`,`Common mistake: **treating a positive urine dipstick in a confused older woman** - ASB is common and treatment causes C. difficile and resistance without benefit. [IDSA 2019]`,`Common mistake: **using alcohol hand rub alone** in a C. difficile outbreak - spores need soap and water and chlorine cleaning. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`Viva: which drug is preferred for a first episode of C. difficile - **fidaxomicin** (lower recurrence), else oral vancomycin; metronidazole only if neither available. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`Viva: why creatinine misleads in the elderly - **low muscle mass**; calculate creatinine clearance (Cockcroft-Gault) for drug dosing. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`Mnemonic for atypical presentation - **'the geriatric giants'**: immobility, instability (falls), incontinence and intellectual impairment (delirium). [BGS 2014]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Criteria used in long-term care`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Definition`,`Use`],rows:[[`Fever (IDSA 2008)`,`Oral over 37.8 C once; or repeated oral over 37.2 C / rectal over 37.5 C; or rise over 1.1 C above baseline`,`Trigger for evaluation`],[`Loeb UTI, no catheter`,`Acute dysuria alone, or fever plus 1 of urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain, haematuria, CVA tenderness, incontinence`,`Minimum to start antibiotics`],[`Loeb UTI, catheter`,`1 of fever, new CVA tenderness, rigors, new delirium`,`Minimum to start antibiotics`],[`Loeb pneumonia`,`Fever over 38.9 C plus RR over 25 or productive cough; or fever plus new cough plus SpO2 under 94% or RR over 25 or delirium`,`Minimum to start antibiotics`],[`Revised McGeer 2012`,`Surveillance definitions for UTI, respiratory, skin, GI infection`,`Counting infections, not treatment`],[`CAM (delirium)`,`Acute onset/fluctuation + inattention + (disorganised thinking or altered consciousness)`,`Diagnose delirium`],[`Clinical Frailty Scale`,`1 very fit to 9 terminally ill; 5 or more frail`,`Prognosis, goals of care`]]},{heading:`Drugs for common infections in older adults`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Nitrofurantoin MR`,`100 mg orally 12-hourly`,`5 days (cystitis)`,`Avoid if CrCl under 30 or long-term (Beers); pulmonary and hepatic toxicity`],[`Fosfomycin`,`3 g orally single dose`,`Once`,`Diarrhoea; cystitis only`],[`Amoxicillin`,`1 g orally 8-hourly`,`5 days (CAP)`,`Rash; reduce if CrCl under 30`],[`Ceftriaxone + azithromycin`,`1-2 g IV daily + 500 mg daily`,`5-7 days`,`Biliary sludge; azithromycin QT`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`Per source; 7 days typical`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI; renal dosing`],[`Fidaxomicin`,`200 mg orally 12-hourly`,`10 days`,`Costly; limited availability in India`],[`Vancomycin (oral, CDI)`,`125 mg orally 6-hourly (500 mg in fulminant)`,`10 days; taper-pulse for recurrence`,`Not absorbed - IV vancomycin does not treat CDI`],[`Valaciclovir`,`1 g orally 8-hourly`,`7 days (zoster)`,`Neurotoxicity if dose not reduced in CKD`],[`Oseltamivir`,`75 mg orally 12-hourly (treatment); 75 mg daily (prophylaxis)`,`5 days; prophylaxis 2 weeks or more`,`Nausea; halve dose if CrCl 30-60`]]},{heading:`Atypical presentations and what to look for`,columns:[`Presentation`,`Likely infection`,`First check`],rows:[[`New confusion or drowsiness`,`Pneumonia, UTI (only with urinary signs), sepsis, meningitis`,`RR, SpO2, CAM, glucose, Na, chest X-ray`],[`Fall or 'off legs'`,`Pneumonia, UTI, bacteraemia, septic arthritis`,`Vitals, NEWS2, joints, blood cultures`],[`Anorexia and reduced intake`,`Any infection, oral candida, C. difficile`,`Hydration, creatinine, mouth`],[`New incontinence`,`UTI, faecal impaction, delirium`,`Bladder scan, rectal check, urine if symptomatic`],[`Tachypnoea without cough`,`Pneumonia, sepsis, aspiration`,`SpO2, chest X-ray, lactate`],[`Diarrhoea after antibiotics`,`C. difficile`,`Stool GDH and toxin, WBC, creatinine`]]},{heading:`Vaccines for older adults`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Who`,`Schedule`,`Note`],rows:[[`Inactivated influenza`,`All 60 or more; LTCF staff`,`Annually before season`,`High-dose or adjuvanted at 65 or more where available`],[`Pneumococcal (PCV20/PCV21, or PCV15 + PPSV23)`,`All 50 or more (ACIP 2024)`,`Single dose; PPSV23 1 year after PCV15`,`Indian consensus: 50-65 and above`],[`Recombinant zoster (RZV)`,`50 or more; immunocompromised 19 or more`,`2 doses, 2-6 months apart`,`About 90% efficacy; even after past zoster`],[`RSV prefusion F`,`75 or more; 50-74 at increased risk`,`Single dose`,`Before RSV season`],[`Td / Tdap`,`All adults`,`Every 10 years`,`Tetanus-prone wounds`]]},{heading:`C. difficile: severity and treatment`,columns:[`Severity`,`Criteria`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Non-severe`,`WBC 15,000 or less and creatinine under 1.5 mg/dL`,`Fidaxomicin 200 mg BD x 10 days or vancomycin 125 mg 6-hourly x 10 days`],[`Severe`,`WBC over 15,000 or creatinine 1.5 mg/dL or more`,`Same drugs (fidaxomicin preferred)`],[`Fulminant`,`Hypotension, shock, ileus, megacolon`,`Vancomycin 500 mg 6-hourly oral/NG + IV metronidazole; surgery`],[`First recurrence`,`Within 8 weeks of a previous episode`,`Fidaxomicin, or vancomycin taper-pulse`],[`Second or later recurrence`,`Two or more recurrences`,`Faecal microbiota transplantation`]]}],redFlags:[`NEWS2 5 or more, RR over 25 or SpO2 under 90% in an older adult`,`New delirium with fever or hypotension`,`Inability to swallow or take oral fluids, or signs of AKI`,`C. difficile with WBC over 15,000, creatinine 1.5 or more, ileus or abdominal distension`,`Hot swollen joint with fever - septic arthritis until aspirated`,`Rapidly spreading cellulitis with pain out of proportion - necrotising infection`,`Fever with headache and neck stiffness or new focal deficit`],pearls:[`In the elderly, 37.8 C once or a rise of 1.1 C over baseline is fever.`,`Respiratory rate over 25 is the most useful single sign of pneumonia in the frail elderly.`,`Cloudy, smelly urine and a positive dipstick are not a UTI - do not treat ASB.`,`Delirium is a medical emergency - CAM: acute + inattention + (disorganised or drowsy).`,`C. difficile: fidaxomicin or oral vancomycin; soap and water, not alcohol rub.`,`Creatinine lies in the elderly - calculate creatinine clearance before dosing.`,`Avoid fluoroquinolones first line: tendons, aorta, QT, glucose, delirium.`,`Vaccinate: flu yearly, pneumococcal at 50, RZV at 50, RSV at 75 (or 50-74 at risk).`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-q1`,stem:`An 82-year-old woman in an old-age home has become more confused over 2 days. She is afebrile, BP 132/80, RR 16, SpO2 97%, with no urinary symptoms. Urine dipstick is positive for leucocyte esterase and nitrite. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Start oral nitrofurantoin for 5 days`,`Look for other causes of delirium and observe, without antibiotics`,`Start IV ceftriaxone`,`Send urine culture and treat whatever grows`],answer:1,explanation:`In a cognitively impaired older adult with bacteriuria and delirium but no urinary symptoms or systemic signs, IDSA 2019 advises looking for other causes (drugs, dehydration, retention, constipation, hyponatraemia) and observing rather than treating. Nitrofurantoin or ceftriaxone treats asymptomatic bacteriuria with harm and no benefit, and culture-directed treatment of whatever grows is the same mistake.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-q2`,stem:`According to IDSA criteria for long-term care residents, which of the following defines fever?`,options:[`A single oral temperature of 37.0 C`,`A single oral temperature of 38.3 C only`,`A rise in temperature of more than 1.1 C above the resident's baseline`,`A rectal temperature of 37.0 C`],answer:2,explanation:`IDSA 2008 defines fever in LTCF residents as a single oral temperature over 37.8 C, repeated oral over 37.2 C or rectal over 37.5 C, or a rise of over 1.1 C above baseline, because older adults mount a blunted febrile response. A threshold of 38.3 C would miss many infections, while 37.0 C oral or rectal values are normal.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-q3`,stem:`A 76-year-old man develops watery diarrhoea, 6 stools a day, after a course of cefuroxime. WBC is 18,000/mm3 and creatinine 1.7 mg/dL; there is no hypotension or ileus. Stool GDH and toxin are positive. What is the preferred treatment?`,options:[`Oral metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly for 10 days`,`IV vancomycin 1 g 12-hourly`,`Loperamide and oral rehydration`,`Oral fidaxomicin 200 mg twice daily for 10 days`],answer:3,explanation:`WBC over 15,000 or creatinine of 1.5 or more makes this severe C. difficile infection; IDSA/SHEA 2021 prefers fidaxomicin (oral vancomycin 125 mg 6-hourly is the alternative). Metronidazole is inferior and reserved for non-severe disease when others are unavailable, IV vancomycin does not reach the colon, and antimotility drugs risk toxic megacolon.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-q4`,stem:`Which antibiotic should generally be avoided for cystitis in an 85-year-old woman with a creatinine clearance of 22 mL/min, per the AGS Beers criteria?`,options:[`Nitrofurantoin`,`Fosfomycin`,`Cephalexin`,`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`],answer:0,explanation:`Beers 2023 advises avoiding nitrofurantoin when creatinine clearance is under 30 mL/min, because it is ineffective in poorly concentrated urine and accumulates, causing pulmonary, hepatic and neurological toxicity. Fosfomycin, cephalexin and amoxicillin-clavulanate can be used with dose adjustment where needed.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-q5`,stem:`An influenza A outbreak occurs in a nursing home: three residents are confirmed. What is the recommended measure for the unaffected residents, in addition to vaccination?`,options:[`No action unless symptoms appear`,`Oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days`,`Oseltamivir 75 mg once daily for at least 2 weeks and until 1 week after the last case`,`Azithromycin prophylaxis for 5 days`],answer:2,explanation:`IDSA 2018 recommends institutional outbreak chemoprophylaxis with oseltamivir 75 mg once daily for all residents for at least 2 weeks and continuing until 1 week after the last case, along with vaccination and isolation. Waiting for symptoms allows spread, twice-daily dosing for 5 days is the treatment regimen, and azithromycin has no activity against influenza.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe why infections present atypically in older adults. Discuss the approach to a suspected infection in a resident of a long-term care facility, including antibiotic stewardship.`,openingLines:[`Infections in older adults often present without fever or localising features - as delirium, falls, anorexia or functional decline - because of immunosenescence and a blunted febrile response.`,`Fever in LTCF residents is defined as an oral temperature over 37.8 C once, or a rise of over 1.1 C above baseline (IDSA 2008).`],answer:[{heading:`Why atypical`,points:[`Immunosenescence, lower basal temperature and blunted pyrogen response; low cognitive reserve - delirium.`,`Blunted leucocytosis (look for left shift), silent aspiration, reduced thirst and GFR.`]},{heading:`Common infections in LTCF`,points:[`Pneumonia (aspiration), UTI (often catheter-related), skin and pressure injuries, C. difficile, influenza and scabies outbreaks.`]},{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`Change from baseline per carer; vitals with RR and SpO2, NEWS2; CAM for delirium; hidden foci (sacrum, feet, joints, catheter).`,`CBC with differential, CRP, creatinine clearance, Na, glucose, blood cultures; urine culture only with urinary signs; chest X-ray.`]},{heading:`Minimum criteria for antibiotics`,points:[`Loeb criteria for UTI (with and without catheter), pneumonia and skin infection; ASB is not treated (IDSA 2019).`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Renal dosing, interactions, avoid fluoroquinolones first line; CAP amoxicillin 5 days; cystitis nitrofurantoin if CrCl 30 or more.`,`Admission criteria: sepsis, hypoxia, CURB-65 2 or more, not swallowing, AKI; careful fluids; goals of care.`]},{heading:`Stewardship and prevention`,points:[`48-72 h time-out, short courses, avoid treating ASB, C. difficile prevention.`,`Hand hygiene, oral care, catheter minimisation; vaccines - influenza, pneumococcal, RZV, RSV.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: atypical presentation - likely infection - first check`,`Box: Loeb minimum criteria for UTI`],markSplit:[{part:`Reasons for atypical presentation`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical assessment and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Antibiotic criteria, choice and stewardship`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention and vaccination`,marks:2}],keywords:[`atypical presentation`,`delirium`,`Loeb criteria`,`asymptomatic bacteriuria`,`stewardship`,`vaccination`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-elderly-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on Clostridioides difficile infection in older adults.`,openingLines:[`C. difficile is a spore-forming toxigenic anaerobe causing antibiotic-associated diarrhoea and colitis, commonest in older adults after antibiotics and PPIs.`,`Diagnosis needs 3 or more unformed stools in 24 hours and a positive two-step stool test; fidaxomicin or oral vancomycin is first-line (IDSA/SHEA 2021).`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors and pathogenesis`,points:[`Age over 65, antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins), PPIs, hospital or LTCF stay.`,`Antibiotics disrupt colonic flora; toxins A and B cause colitis and pseudomembranes.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and severity`,points:[`GDH plus toxin EIA, NAAT for discordance; severe if WBC over 15,000 or creatinine 1.5 or more; fulminant with shock or ileus.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Fidaxomicin 200 mg BD x 10 days or vancomycin 125 mg 6-hourly x 10 days; fulminant - vancomycin 500 mg + IV metronidazole, surgery.`,`Recurrence - fidaxomicin or vancomycin taper-pulse; FMT after second recurrence.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Stewardship, stop PPIs, contact precautions, soap-and-water hand washing, chlorine cleaning.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: severity criteria and treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors and pathogenesis`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis and severity`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`C. difficile`,`fidaxomicin`,`oral vancomycin`,`GDH`,`FMT`,`contact precautions`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections in the elderly`,`High KP et al. IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation of Fever and Infection in Older Adult Residents of Long-Term Care Facilities, 2008 update`,`Nicolle LE et al. IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Asymptomatic Bacteriuria, 2019`,`Johnson S et al. IDSA/SHEA Focused Update Guidelines on Management of Clostridioides difficile Infection in Adults, 2021`,`American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated AGS Beers Criteria for Potentially Inappropriate Medication Use in Older Adults`,`ACIP Adult Immunization Schedule 2024-2025 (pneumococcal, RSV, recombinant zoster); API Indian Consensus Guidelines on Adult Immunization, 2023`,`UNFPA India Ageing Report 2023; Longitudinal Ageing Study in India (LASI) Wave 1 Report, 2020; MoHFW National Programme for Health Care of the Elderly`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2022; Surviving Sepsis Campaign Adult Guidelines, 2026`]}),c.push({id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections`,title:`Infections that matter in diabetes: foot, emphysematous, malignant otitis, melioidosis`,oneLiner:`Diabetes raises both the frequency and the severity of infection through **hyperglycaemia-impaired neutrophil function, neuropathy and vasculopathy**, and produces a group of **diabetes-signature infections** - **diabetic foot infection and osteomyelitis**, **emphysematous pyelonephritis and cystitis**, **malignant otitis externa (Pseudomonas)**, **melioidosis**, rhino-orbital mucormycosis and Klebsiella liver abscess - each needing the right **imaging, antibiotic and early surgical source control**, with insulin to bring glucose to **140-180 mg/dL**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`diabetic foot infection`,`IWGDF/IDSA classification`,`Wagner grade`,`diabetic foot osteomyelitis`,`probe-to-bone test`,`emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`Huang-Tseng classification`,`emphysematous cystitis`,`malignant otitis externa`,`necrotising otitis externa`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`melioidosis`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`ceftazidime`,`Fournier gangrene`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Klebsiella liver abscess`,`TB-diabetes`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Diabetes-associated infections** are infections that are commoner, more severe or almost unique in people with diabetes; infection is a leading cause of hospital admission and a frequent precipitant of **DKA and hyperosmolar state**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB (2023)** estimated **101 million Indians with diabetes (11.4%)** and 136 million with prediabetes - so diabetes-related infection is a daily OPD problem in India. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`People with diabetes have about **1.5-2 times** the risk of infection-related hospitalisation and death, with the highest risk in poor glycaemic control (HbA1c over 9-10%). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetic foot ulcers** develop in **19-34%** of people with diabetes in their lifetime; about half become infected, and infection precedes most of the **lower-limb amputations** in diabetes. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: **over 90%** of patients have diabetes; women predominate; mortality has fallen to about **10-20%** with percutaneous drainage. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa** occurs mainly in **elderly people with diabetes** (over 90% of cases) and is caused by **Pseudomonas aeruginosa** in over 90%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: modelling estimates about **165,000 cases and 89,000 deaths a year** worldwide, with India predicted to carry the largest share; **diabetes is present in about half** of patients; coastal Karnataka, Odisha, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and the North-East report most Indian cases. [Limmathurotsakul 2016]`,`Diabetes **triples the risk of tuberculosis** and worsens TB outcomes; India runs **bidirectional TB-diabetes screening** under NTEP and NP-NCD. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Diabetic foot infection**: mild infections are mainly **Staphylococcus aureus and beta-haemolytic streptococci**; chronic, deep or previously treated wounds are **polymicrobial** with Gram-negatives (Pseudomonas, Enterobacterales) and anaerobes - Indian series show a **higher Gram-negative share** than Western ones. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: **E. coli (about 70%)** and Klebsiella pneumoniae; risk factors are poor glycaemic control, female sex and **urinary obstruction** (stones, papillary necrosis). [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: **Pseudomonas aeruginosa**; less often S. aureus, Aspergillus and Candida; triggers include ear syringing and minor trauma to the canal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: **Burkholderia pseudomallei**, an environmental Gram-negative bacillus in wet soil and surface water - acquired by **inoculation, inhalation or ingestion**, especially in paddy farmers during the monsoon. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`Other diabetes-linked infections: **rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis** (DKA, steroids - see the Mucormycosis topic), **hypervirulent Klebsiella liver abscess** with endophthalmitis, **emphysematous cholecystitis**, necrotising fasciitis and **Fournier gangrene**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** increase genital mycotic infection and, rarely, **Fournier gangrene** (FDA warning 2018) and euglycaemic DKA during infection. [ADA 2025]`,`Risk multipliers: **HbA1c over 9%, neuropathy (loss of protective sensation), peripheral arterial disease, CKD, previous ulcer or amputation, foot deformity, barefoot walking**. [IWGDF 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why diabetes breeds these infections`,points:[`**Hyperglycaemia (glucose over 200-250 mg/dL) impairs neutrophil chemotaxis, adhesion, phagocytosis and oxidative burst**, and glycation of complement and immunoglobulin reduces opsonisation - the common root of all diabetic infections. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sensory neuropathy** removes pain, so injuries go unnoticed; **motor neuropathy** causes clawing and high-pressure points; **autonomic neuropathy** dries and cracks the skin - the classic triad behind the neuropathic ulcer. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Peripheral arterial disease** limits oxygen, leucocyte and antibiotic delivery to the foot - why ischaemic wounds fail to heal and why revascularisation is part of infection treatment. [IWGDF 2023]`,`The foot's **compartments and tendon sheaths** let infection track proximally under the plantar fascia, so a small ulcer can hide a deep abscess. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Gas formation** in emphysematous infections comes from **mixed-acid fermentation of high tissue glucose** by E. coli and Klebsiella, producing CO2 and hydrogen, in tissue with poor perfusion that cannot clear it. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa** starts in the canal skin and spreads through the **fissures of Santorini** and the bony-cartilaginous junction to the **skull base**, causing **osteomyelitis** and cranial nerve palsies (VII first, then IX-XII). [Harrison 22e]`,`**B. pseudomallei** survives and replicates inside neutrophils and macrophages, spreading cell to cell; diabetic neutrophil dysfunction lets it disseminate to lungs, liver, spleen, prostate and bone, and causes **latency and relapse**. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`**Ketoacidosis** frees iron from transferrin and favours Mucorales (iron uptake, ketone reductase) - linking DKA with rhino-orbital mucormycosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - the ones examiners ask`,points:[`**IWGDF/IDSA classification of diabetic foot infection**: grade 1 uninfected; **grade 2 mild** - skin and subcutaneous tissue, erythema 0.5-2 cm around the wound; **grade 3 moderate** - erythema over 2 cm or deeper tissue (abscess, fasciitis, septic arthritis); **grade 4 severe** - with **2 or more SIRS criteria**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Add **'(O)'** to grade 3 or 4 when **osteomyelitis** is present - for example 3(O) - in the 2023 IWGDF/IDSA scheme. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Infection is clinical**: at least 2 of local swelling or induration, erythema over 0.5 cm, local tenderness or pain, warmth, or purulent discharge - a positive swab without these is colonisation. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Wagner grading** (ulcer depth): 0 at-risk foot, 1 superficial ulcer, 2 deep to tendon or capsule, 3 deep with abscess or osteomyelitis, 4 forefoot gangrene, 5 whole-foot gangrene - widely used in India though it does not grade infection. [Wagner 1981]`,`**Huang-Tseng CT classification of emphysematous pyelonephritis**: **class 1** gas in the collecting system only (emphysematous pyelitis); **class 2** gas in the renal parenchyma; **class 3A** extension to the perinephric space; **3B** to the pararenal space; **class 4** bilateral or a solitary kidney. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa** staging (Levenson / clinical): canal infection with granulation; skull-base osteomyelitis; then cranial nerve involvement and intracranial spread - **cranial nerve palsy marks advanced disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis** forms: acute bacteraemic (septic shock), **localised (pneumonia, liver or splenic abscess, prostatic abscess, parotid in children, osteomyelitis)**, chronic (over 2 months, TB-like) and latent with reactivation. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Foot: ask about **new pain in a previously numb foot** (deep infection), swelling, discharge, smell, fever, rising glucose and footwear, barefoot walking and home remedies. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: fever, loin pain, vomiting, confusion and **rapid deterioration to shock** in a patient with poorly controlled diabetes; pneumaturia occurs in emphysematous cystitis. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: **severe, persistent, nocturnal ear pain** out of proportion to findings, purulent otorrhoea, headache and failure of topical drops for weeks; fever is often absent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: fever for days to weeks with cough (pneumonia), abdominal pain (liver or splenic abscess), urinary symptoms (prostatic abscess), bone pain, after **monsoon exposure to mud or water** - often misdiagnosed as TB. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`Ask about **SGLT2 inhibitor use** with perineal pain and fever (Fournier gangrene) and with malaise and vomiting (euglycaemic DKA). [ADA 2025]`,`**Unexplained hyperglycaemia or new insulin requirement** may be the first sign of a hidden infection - always look for a focus. [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and what they mean`,points:[`**Probe-to-bone test**: a sterile metal probe touching bone through the ulcer has high positive predictive value for osteomyelitis in a high-risk foot; a negative test with low pretest risk helps exclude it. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**'Sausage toe'** (red, swollen digit) and an ulcer over a bony prominence **larger than 2 cm2 or deeper than 3 mm** suggest underlying osteomyelitis. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Assess perfusion: **pedal pulses, ankle-brachial index** (under 0.9 abnormal; may be falsely high over 1.3 from calcified arteries), toe pressure (under 30 mmHg poor healing). [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Crepitus, bullae, skin necrosis, pain beyond the erythema** and systemic toxicity point to necrotising soft-tissue infection - emergency surgery. [IDSA 2014]`,`**Granulation tissue on the floor of the external canal** at the bony-cartilaginous junction, with facial nerve palsy, = malignant otitis externa until proved otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly with multiple small abscesses**, pneumonia and a tender prostate in a farmer with diabetes suggests melioidosis. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`Check every diabetic with fever for **DKA (Kussmaul breathing, ketones)**, the feet, the perineum, the ears, the nose and palate (black eschar - mucormycosis). [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Charcot neuro-osteoarthropathy vs foot osteomyelitis**: Charcot - warm, swollen midfoot, **no ulcer** usually, temperature difference over 2 C, normal CRP; osteomyelitis - under an ulcer, forefoot, raised ESR. MRI and bone biopsy separate them. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Colonised ulcer vs infected ulcer**: decide clinically (IWGDF signs) - do not treat a positive swab from a clean ulcer. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis vs acute pyelonephritis or renal abscess**: gas on CT defines EPN; failure to improve within 48-72 hours mandates CT. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant vs simple otitis externa**: severe night pain, granulation tissue, cranial nerve palsy, high ESR and bone erosion on CT; also exclude **nasopharyngeal or ear canal carcinoma** by biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis vs tuberculosis**: both cause cavitating upper-lobe pneumonia and chronic abscesses; melioidosis is acute in the monsoon, culture-positive in days, and responds to ceftazidime, not ATT. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`**Melioidosis vs amoebic or pyogenic liver abscess**: multiple small 'honeycomb' or 'Swiss-cheese' abscesses in liver and spleen favour melioidosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`Every febrile diabetic: **capillary glucose, ketones, CBC, creatinine, electrolytes, HbA1c, urine routine and culture, blood cultures** and a chest X-ray if any respiratory signs. [ADA 2025]`,`**Foot**: culture **deep tissue obtained by curettage or biopsy after debridement**, not superficial swabs; **plain X-ray** first for osteomyelitis, gas and foreign body; **ESR over 70 mm/h** supports osteomyelitis. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**MRI** is the advanced imaging of choice for foot osteomyelitis; **bone biopsy** (percutaneous or surgical) gives culture and histology when diagnosis or organism is uncertain. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: plain X-ray or ultrasound may suggest gas, but **non-contrast or contrast CT** is diagnostic and gives the Huang-Tseng class; look for obstruction. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: ear swab culture before antibiotics, **CT temporal bone** (bone erosion), **MRI** (soft tissue and marrow extent), technetium-99m bone scan (sensitive early) and gallium or **ESR/CRP for follow-up**; biopsy to exclude malignancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: **blood, sputum, urine, pus and throat cultures** on Ashdown selective medium; alert the lab - **oxidase-positive Gram-negative bacilli with bipolar 'safety-pin' staining, resistant to gentamicin and colistin**; ultrasound or CT abdomen for liver, spleen and prostate abscesses. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`Melioidosis serology (indirect haemagglutination) is unreliable in endemic areas; **culture is the gold standard**, and automated identification systems may misidentify B. pseudomallei as B. cepacia. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - mild infections`,points:[`**Mild diabetic foot infection (grade 2)**: debride, offload and give oral agents active against **S. aureus and streptococci** - **cephalexin 500 mg 6-hourly**, **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 8-hourly**, or **clindamycin 300-450 mg 8-hourly** - for **1-2 weeks**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Add **MRSA cover** (cotrimoxazole, doxycycline or linezolid) only with prior MRSA, high local prevalence or failure; add **Pseudomonas cover** only with previous isolation, warm-climate macerated wounds or failure. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Do not give antibiotics for clinically uninfected ulcers**, to prevent infection or to speed healing. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Offloading** (total contact cast or removable knee-high walker), daily wound care, glycaemic control and smoking cessation are as important as the antibiotic. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Emphysematous cystitis** in a stable patient: catheter drainage plus antibiotics (ceftriaxone or culture-directed) for 7-14 days and glucose control; most do not need surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`Early **malignant otitis externa** without bone or nerve involvement may be treated with **ciprofloxacin 750 mg orally 12-hourly** plus aural toilet, under ENT supervision, for **6-8 weeks**; rising fluoroquinolone resistance makes culture essential. [Harrison 22e]`,`Switch to **insulin** when glucose stays over 250-300 mg/dL during infection, and hold **SGLT2 inhibitors and metformin** in severe infection, dehydration or AKI. [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency - source control`,points:[`**Moderate or severe foot infection**: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV 8-hourly** or **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly**; add vancomycin or linezolid for MRSA risk; meropenem for ESBL risk - adjust to deep-tissue culture. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023; ICMR 2022]`,`**Urgent surgery** for foot abscess, compartment syndrome, gas in tissues, necrotising fasciitis or wet gangrene - drain and debride within hours; **revascularisation** for ischaemia. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Diabetic foot osteomyelitis**: **6 weeks** of antibiotics without bone resection; **about 3 weeks** after minor amputation or resection when residual infected bone remains; **2-5 days** if all infected bone is removed. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: **IV carbapenem or piperacillin-tazobactam** (ESBL is common in India), relieve obstruction (stent or nephrostomy); classes 1-2 - antibiotics plus **percutaneous drainage**; classes 3-4 with fewer than 2 risk factors - drainage; **2 or more risk factors** (thrombocytopenia, AKI, altered sensorium, shock) or failed drainage - **nephrectomy**. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa (advanced)**: **IV antipseudomonal therapy** - **ceftazidime 2 g 8-hourly**, cefepime 2 g 8-hourly or piperacillin-tazobactam, with or without ciprofloxacin - for **6-8 weeks**, aural debridement, tight glucose control; surgery only for sequestrum or abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis intensive phase**: **ceftazidime 2 g IV 6-hourly** (or 40 mg/kg 8-hourly) or **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly** (2 g for CNS or septic shock) for **at least 10-14 days** (4-8 weeks for deep abscesses, bone or CNS); drain large abscesses. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Melioidosis eradication phase**: oral **cotrimoxazole by weight** (over 60 kg: 320/1600 mg 12-hourly; 40-60 kg: 240/1200 mg 12-hourly) with **folic acid** for **3 months** (6 months for bone or CNS disease) to prevent relapse. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Glucose in hospital**: basal-bolus or IV insulin to a target of **140-180 mg/dL**; manage DKA by protocol; avoid hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**CKD and dialysis**: renal dosing of ceftazidime, cotrimoxazole, piperacillin-tazobactam and vancomycin; avoid nephrotoxic combinations; cotrimoxazole raises potassium. [Sanford 2025]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation of all these infections; malignant otitis externa is almost confined to older diabetics (see the Infections in older adults topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy with diabetes**: ASB screening and treatment; avoid fluoroquinolones and cotrimoxazole near term - use beta-lactams (see the Infections in pregnancy topic). [ACOG 2023]`,`**Children**: melioidosis can present as **parotid abscess** (Southeast Asia) and suppurative lymphadenitis. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`**TB with diabetes**: screen every TB patient for diabetes and every diabetic with cough over 2 weeks for TB; diabetes raises TB treatment failure and relapse - control glucose during ATT (rifampicin lowers sulfonylurea levels). [NTEP 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: plain X-ray, probe-to-bone and ESR can diagnose most foot osteomyelitis; refer for MRI only if doubt; send deep tissue, not swabs, even at district level. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Diabetic foot infection leads to **amputation in up to 20%** of moderate-severe cases; 5-year mortality after major amputation in diabetes is about **50%**, worse than many cancers. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis** mortality is about **10-20%** with modern drainage-based care, higher with thrombocytopenia, shock, AKI or altered sensorium. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: skull-base osteomyelitis, cranial nerve palsies (VII, then IX-XII), sigmoid sinus thrombosis, meningitis; mortality has fallen to under 10-20% but **relapse occurs in up to 15-20%** within a year. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: septic shock mortality **40-50%** in many Indian series; overall mortality 10-40%; **relapse in about 5-10%** when eradication therapy is incomplete. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`Severe infection precipitates **DKA, hyperosmolar state, AKI and acute coronary events**. [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer urgently: moderate or severe foot infection, ischaemic foot, suspected necrotising infection, emphysematous infection, malignant otitis externa, melioidosis and any diabetic with sepsis or DKA. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Multidisciplinary foot care team** (physician, surgeon, vascular, podiatry, orthotics) reduces amputations; follow up every 1-3 months after an ulcer heals. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Remission of osteomyelitis** is judged after at least **6-12 months** off antibiotics with a healed wound and no recurrence. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Malignant otitis externa: follow **ESR/CRP** and symptoms monthly; repeat imaging (gallium or MRI) before stopping therapy in doubtful cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`Melioidosis: **monitor CBC and potassium** on cotrimoxazole, confirm adherence to the full eradication course and review for relapse for at least a year. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Annual foot examination** for every person with diabetes (monofilament, pulses, deformity), more often if at risk; **daily self-inspection**, never walk barefoot, proper footwear, nail care. [IWGDF 2023; ADA 2025]`,`**Glycaemic control** (HbA1c under 7% for most adults) reduces infection risk; avoid ear syringing in elderly diabetics; prefer suction cleaning. [ADA 2025]`,`**Melioidosis prevention**: boots and gloves for paddy and construction work, cover wounds, avoid exposure to floodwater and storms during the monsoon - especially for people with diabetes. [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`**Vaccination**: influenza yearly, pneumococcal, hepatitis B (adults under 60 with diabetes), recombinant zoster at 50, and COVID-19 as per national advice. [ADA 2025]`,`**NP-NCD** (National Programme for Prevention and Control of NCDs) screens everyone aged 30 or more for diabetes at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs; **NTEP-NP-NCD bidirectional screening** links TB and diabetes. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**IWGDF/IDSA 2023 guideline on diabetes-related foot infections**: adds the **'(O)' osteomyelitis modifier**, recommends **deep-tissue or bone samples**, MRI as advanced imaging, **6 weeks** for unresected osteomyelitis and **3 weeks after minor amputation** with residual bone, and **no antibiotics for uninfected ulcers**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023 also advises **against** topical antimicrobials, hyperbaric oxygen or adjunctive G-CSF to treat foot infection. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**ADA Standards of Care 2025-2026**: inpatient glucose target **140-180 mg/dL**; caution with SGLT2 inhibitors in acute illness (sick-day rules, euglycaemic DKA). [ADA 2025]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB 2023**: 101 million Indians with diabetes and 136 million with prediabetes - the largest diabetes population after China. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Darwin melioidosis guideline (2020 revision)** still underpins Indian practice: ceftazidime or meropenem intensive phase, weight-based cotrimoxazole eradication for 3-6 months. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**NTEP 2025** continues universal diabetes screening of all TB patients and TB screening in people with diabetes. [NTEP 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Classify diabetic foot infection and outline management'** - write the IWGDF/IDSA grades with '(O)', Wagner for depth, probe-to-bone, deep cultures, antibiotics by grade, offloading, revascularisation and surgery. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Classic viva: **organism of malignant otitis externa** - Pseudomonas aeruginosa; the first cranial nerve involved - **facial nerve (VII)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic viva: melioidosis - **'the great mimicker'** of TB; ceftazidime then cotrimoxazole; organism resistant to **gentamicin and colistin** (used in Ashdown medium). [Wiersinga, NEJM 2012]`,`Common mistake: **treating a superficial swab result** from an uninfected ulcer. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Common mistake: missing gas - **CT for any diabetic pyelonephritis not improving in 48-72 hours**. [Huang and Tseng 2000]`,`Mnemonic for diabetes-signature infections - **'FEMMM'**: Foot, Emphysematous (kidney, bladder, gallbladder), Malignant otitis externa, Melioidosis, Mucormycosis. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`IWGDF/IDSA 2023 classification of diabetic foot infection (with Wagner)`,columns:[`IWGDF/IDSA grade`,`Clinical description`,`Typical setting`,`Wagner equivalent (depth, approximate)`],rows:[[`1 - Uninfected`,`No local or systemic signs of infection`,`OPD - no antibiotic`,`0-2`],[`2 - Mild`,`Skin and subcutaneous only; erythema 0.5-2 cm around wound`,`Oral antibiotics, OPD`,`1-2`],[`3 - Moderate`,`Erythema over 2 cm or deeper structures (abscess, fasciitis, joint); no SIRS`,`Often admission; IV then oral`,`2-3`],[`4 - Severe`,`Any foot infection with 2 or more SIRS criteria`,`Admit; IV; urgent surgery`,`3-5`],[`Add (O)`,`Osteomyelitis present, e.g. 3(O) or 4(O)`,`MRI, bone biopsy, 6 weeks if unresected`,`3`]]},{heading:`Drugs for diabetes-associated infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cephalexin (mild foot)`,`500 mg orally 6-hourly`,`1-2 weeks`,`Allergy; no anaerobe or Pseudomonas cover`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg orally 8-hourly; 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`1-2 weeks soft tissue; 6 weeks bone`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic hepatitis`],[`Clindamycin`,`300-450 mg orally 8-hourly; 600 mg IV 8-hourly`,`1-2 weeks`,`C. difficile`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`Per source and culture`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI with vancomycin`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly (2 g for CNS melioidosis)`,`10-14 days or more`,`Seizures; reserve for ESBL or severe disease`],[`Ceftazidime`,`2 g IV 6-hourly (melioidosis) or 8-hourly (MOE)`,`Melioidosis 10-14 days or more; MOE 6-8 weeks`,`Renal dosing; C. difficile`],[`Ciprofloxacin (MOE, early)`,`750 mg orally 12-hourly`,`6-8 weeks`,`Tendinopathy, QT, hypoglycaemia; resistance rising`],[`Cotrimoxazole (melioidosis eradication)`,`Over 60 kg 320/1600 mg 12-hourly; 40-60 kg 240/1200 mg 12-hourly, with folic acid`,`3 months (6 for bone/CNS)`,`Hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression, rash`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA)`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly`,`Per culture`,`Nephrotoxicity; AUC monitoring`],[`Insulin`,`Basal-bolus or IV infusion`,`Target 140-180 mg/dL in hospital`,`Hypoglycaemia; hypokalaemia with IV insulin`]]},{heading:`Huang-Tseng classification of emphysematous pyelonephritis`,columns:[`Class`,`CT finding`,`Management`],rows:[[`1`,`Gas in collecting system only (emphysematous pyelitis)`,`Antibiotics, relieve obstruction`],[`2`,`Gas in renal parenchyma without extrarenal extension`,`Antibiotics + percutaneous drainage`],[`3A`,`Gas or abscess extending to perinephric space`,`Drainage if fewer than 2 risk factors; nephrectomy if 2 or more`],[`3B`,`Extension to pararenal space`,`As 3A`],[`4`,`Bilateral EPN or solitary kidney`,`Drainage plus antibiotics; avoid nephrectomy where possible`]]},{heading:`Diabetes-signature infections at a glance`,columns:[`Infection`,`Organism`,`Key clue`,`Imaging`,`Source control`],rows:[[`Diabetic foot osteomyelitis`,`S. aureus, polymicrobial`,`Probe-to-bone, ESR over 70, sausage toe`,`X-ray, then MRI`,`Debridement, resection, offloading`],[`Emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`E. coli, Klebsiella`,`Toxic pyelonephritis, gas`,`CT abdomen`,`PCD, stent; nephrectomy if 2 or more risk factors`],[`Malignant otitis externa`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Night ear pain, canal-floor granulation, VII palsy`,`CT temporal bone, MRI`,`Aural debridement; rarely surgery`],[`Melioidosis`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`Monsoon farmer, pneumonia, liver-spleen abscesses`,`CXR, USG/CT abdomen`,`Drain large abscesses`],[`Rhino-orbital mucormycosis`,`Rhizopus, Mucor`,`DKA, black eschar, proptosis`,`MRI/CT sinuses-orbit`,`Urgent debridement`],[`Klebsiella liver abscess`,`Hypervirulent K. pneumoniae K1/K2`,`Single abscess, endophthalmitis`,`USG/CT abdomen`,`Aspiration or drainage`],[`Fournier gangrene`,`Polymicrobial`,`Perineal pain, crepitus, SGLT2 inhibitor`,`CT pelvis (do not delay surgery)`,`Emergency debridement`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Use`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`Deep tissue or bone culture`,`Foot infection organism`,`Swabs of uninfected ulcers mislead`],[`ESR`,`Foot osteomyelitis; MOE follow-up`,`Over 70 mm/h supports osteomyelitis`],[`Ankle-brachial index`,`Perfusion`,`Under 0.9 PAD; over 1.3 incompressible - use toe pressure`],[`CT abdomen`,`Emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`Gas location gives Huang-Tseng class`],[`CT temporal bone + MRI`,`Malignant otitis externa`,`Bone erosion; marrow and soft tissue extent`],[`Culture on Ashdown medium`,`Melioidosis`,`Oxidase-positive GNB, gentamicin and colistin resistant, bipolar staining`],[`Ketones, glucose, venous gas`,`DKA in infection`,`Ketones 3 mmol/L or more with pH under 7.3 = DKA`]]}],redFlags:[`Diabetic foot with erythema over 2 cm, crepitus, gas on X-ray, necrosis or systemic signs (2 or more SIRS)`,`New pain in a previously painless neuropathic foot - deep abscess`,`Pyelonephritis in a diabetic not improving in 48-72 hours, or with shock, thrombocytopenia or confusion - emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`Severe night-time ear pain with granulation tissue or facial nerve palsy in an elderly diabetic`,`Fever with pneumonia and liver or splenic abscesses in a farmer after the monsoon - melioidosis`,`Black eschar on the palate or nose, proptosis or ophthalmoplegia - mucormycosis`,`Perineal pain and fever on an SGLT2 inhibitor - Fournier gangrene`,`Ketonuria with vomiting or Kussmaul breathing - DKA`],pearls:[`Infection of the diabetic foot is a clinical diagnosis - a swab cannot make it.`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023: grades 1-4 plus (O) for osteomyelitis.`,`Probe-to-bone positive + ESR over 70 = treat as osteomyelitis; MRI if in doubt.`,`Osteomyelitis: 6 weeks if bone left; about 3 weeks after minor amputation with residual bone; days if all removed.`,`Emphysematous pyelonephritis: CT, drain, and nephrectomy with 2 or more risk factors.`,`Malignant otitis externa: elderly diabetic, Pseudomonas, VII nerve, 6-8 weeks antipseudomonal therapy.`,`Melioidosis: ceftazidime or meropenem, then cotrimoxazole for 3 months - and tell the lab.`,`FEMMM: Foot, Emphysematous, Malignant otitis, Melioidosis, Mucor.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old man with diabetes has a plantar ulcer under the first metatarsal head. There is erythema of 3 cm around the ulcer, no fever, pulse 84/min and WBC 9,800/mm3. A sterile probe reaches bone. How is this classified under IWGDF/IDSA 2023?`,options:[`Grade 2 (mild)`,`Grade 4(O) (severe with osteomyelitis)`,`Grade 1 (uninfected)`,`Grade 3(O) (moderate with osteomyelitis)`],answer:3,explanation:`Erythema over 2 cm without SIRS makes the infection moderate (grade 3), and a positive probe-to-bone test in this setting indicates osteomyelitis, so the label is 3(O). Grade 2 requires erythema of 2 cm or less confined to skin, grade 4 needs 2 or more SIRS criteria, and an ulcer with these signs is not uninfected.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-q2`,stem:`A 72-year-old diabetic man has had severe left ear pain worse at night for 6 weeks despite ear drops, with purulent discharge, granulation tissue in the floor of the external canal and a new left facial palsy. Which organism is most likely?`,options:[`Staphylococcus aureus`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Streptococcus pneumoniae`,`Aspergillus niger`],answer:1,explanation:`This is malignant (necrotising) otitis externa with skull-base osteomyelitis and facial nerve involvement, caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa in over 90% of cases. S. aureus and Aspergillus are occasional causes, and pneumococcus causes acute otitis media rather than invasive external canal infection.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman with poorly controlled diabetes has pyelonephritis and remains febrile and hypotensive after 48 hours of antibiotics. CT shows gas in the renal parenchyma extending into the perinephric space; platelets are 60,000/mm3 and creatinine has risen to 3.2 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Continue IV antibiotics alone`,`Percutaneous drainage only`,`Change to oral ciprofloxacin`,`Resuscitation, IV carbapenem and emergency nephrectomy`],answer:3,explanation:`This is Huang-Tseng class 3A emphysematous pyelonephritis with two risk factors (thrombocytopenia and AKI) plus shock; such patients need resuscitation, broad IV cover and nephrectomy because drainage alone fails often. Antibiotics alone or drainage alone suit lower classes or fewer risk factors, and oral therapy is inappropriate in shock.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-q4`,stem:`A paddy farmer with diabetes from coastal Karnataka presents in August with fever, cough and multiple small hypoechoic lesions in the liver and spleen. Blood culture grows an oxidase-positive Gram-negative bacillus resistant to gentamicin and colistin. What is the recommended initial treatment?`,options:[`IV ceftazidime or meropenem for at least 10-14 days followed by oral cotrimoxazole`,`Four-drug antitubercular therapy`,`Oral doxycycline for 7 days`,`IV metronidazole followed by diloxanide`],answer:0,explanation:`The setting and the gentamicin- and colistin-resistant oxidase-positive bacillus identify Burkholderia pseudomallei (melioidosis). Treatment is an intensive phase of IV ceftazidime or meropenem followed by 3-6 months of cotrimoxazole eradication. Antitubercular therapy treats the mimic, not the disease; doxycycline alone is inadequate; and metronidazole with diloxanide treats amoebic liver abscess.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-q5`,stem:`A man with diabetic foot osteomyelitis of the second toe undergoes a toe amputation; the proximal bone margin culture is positive (residual infected bone). According to IWGDF/IDSA 2023, how long should antibiotics generally be continued after surgery?`,options:[`2-5 days`,`About 3 weeks`,`6 months`,`No antibiotics are needed after amputation`],answer:1,explanation:`IWGDF/IDSA 2023 suggests about 3 weeks of antibiotics after minor amputation when residual infected bone remains, 6 weeks when osteomyelitis is treated without bone resection, and only a few days when all infected bone has been removed. Six months is excessive, and stopping entirely ignores the positive margin.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, classification, evaluation and management of diabetic foot infection.`,openingLines:[`Diabetic foot infection is infection of soft tissue or bone below the malleoli in a person with diabetes, usually starting in a neuropathic or neuro-ischaemic ulcer.`,`It is diagnosed clinically and classified by the IWGDF/IDSA 2023 system (grades 1-4, with '(O)' for osteomyelitis); it precedes most diabetic amputations.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Neuropathy (sensory, motor, autonomic), peripheral arterial disease and hyperglycaemia-impaired neutrophil function.`,`Infection spreads along plantar compartments and tendon sheaths; S. aureus and streptococci in acute, polymicrobial in chronic wounds.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`IWGDF/IDSA: 1 uninfected, 2 mild, 3 moderate, 4 severe (SIRS), plus (O) for osteomyelitis.`,`Wagner 0-5 for ulcer depth and gangrene.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`Clinical signs of infection, depth, probe-to-bone, pulses, ABI or toe pressure, neuropathy (monofilament).`,`CBC, ESR (over 70 supports osteomyelitis), glucose, HbA1c, creatinine; deep tissue culture after debridement; X-ray, then MRI; bone biopsy when uncertain.`]},{heading:`Antibiotic therapy`,points:[`Mild: cephalexin, amoxicillin-clavulanate or clindamycin for 1-2 weeks.`,`Moderate/severe: IV amoxicillin-clavulanate or piperacillin-tazobactam; MRSA and Pseudomonas cover only with risk; adjust to culture.`,`Osteomyelitis: 6 weeks without resection; about 3 weeks after minor amputation with residual bone.`]},{heading:`Surgery, offloading and vascular care`,points:[`Urgent drainage and debridement for abscess, gas, necrotising infection; revascularisation for ischaemia.`,`Offloading with total contact cast or walker; wound care; insulin to 140-180 mg/dL in hospital.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Annual foot examination, daily self-inspection, footwear, no barefoot walking, multidisciplinary foot team; NP-NCD screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: IWGDF/IDSA grades with Wagner`,`Flow chart: diabetic foot ulcer - infected? - grade - investigations - antibiotic + surgery + offloading`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management (antibiotics, surgery, offloading)`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`diabetic foot infection`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023`,`Wagner`,`probe-to-bone`,`osteomyelitis`,`offloading`]},{id:`infectious-fever-diabetic-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate emphysematous pyelonephritis, malignant otitis externa and melioidosis in a patient with diabetes.`,openingLines:[`All three are diabetes-signature infections in which hyperglycaemia-impaired neutrophil function allows invasive, destructive disease.`,`They differ in organism, site, imaging and the role of surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Emphysematous pyelonephritis`,points:[`E. coli, Klebsiella; gas-forming necrotising renal infection; CT gives Huang-Tseng class 1-4.`,`Carbapenem or piperacillin-tazobactam, drainage; nephrectomy with 2 or more risk factors.`]},{heading:`Malignant otitis externa`,points:[`Pseudomonas aeruginosa; elderly; night ear pain, canal granulation, facial palsy; CT/MRI temporal bone.`,`Antipseudomonal therapy for 6-8 weeks; aural debridement; ESR for follow-up.`]},{heading:`Melioidosis`,points:[`Burkholderia pseudomallei from soil and water; pneumonia, liver-spleen and prostatic abscesses; mimics TB.`,`Ceftazidime or meropenem for 10-14 days or more, then cotrimoxazole for 3-6 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table: organism, clinical clue, imaging, treatment, surgery`],markSplit:[{part:`Emphysematous pyelonephritis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Malignant otitis externa`,marks:1.5},{part:`Melioidosis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`malignant otitis externa`,`melioidosis`,`Pseudomonas`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections in diabetes; Melioidosis; Otitis externa`,`Senneville E et al. IWGDF/IDSA Guidelines on the Diagnosis and Treatment of Diabetes-related Foot Infections, 2023`,`International Working Group on the Diabetic Foot (IWGDF) Practical Guidelines, 2023`,`Huang JJ, Tseng CC. Emphysematous pyelonephritis: clinicoradiological classification, management, prognosis and pathogenesis. Arch Intern Med, 2000`,`Wiersinga WJ et al. Melioidosis. N Engl J Med, 2012; Darwin Melioidosis Treatment Guideline, 2020 revision; Limmathurotsakul D et al. Nature Microbiology, 2016`,`American Diabetes Association Standards of Care in Diabetes, 2025`,`Anjana RM et al. ICMR-INDIAB national study, Lancet Diabetes and Endocrinology, 2023`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2022; NTEP Training Modules and TB-diabetes collaborative framework, 2025`]});var l=[];l.push({id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology`,title:`Microbiology for the clinician: cultures, serology, PCR and rapid diagnostic tests`,oneLiner:`Clinical microbiology is **diagnostic stewardship** - the **right specimen, taken the right way, on the right day of illness, before the first antibiotic** - read with its limits: **culture with MIC-based susceptibility** is the only test that gives the organism and the drug, **NAAT/PCR** gives speed but not full susceptibility, **IgM serology** turns positive only from about day 5, and **CRP and procalcitonin** support, never replace, clinical judgement.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`blood culture`,`culture and sensitivity`,`antimicrobial susceptibility testing`,`AST`,`MIC`,`minimum inhibitory concentration`,`breakpoint`,`CLSI M100`,`EUCAST`,`CBNAAT`,`Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra`,`Truenat`,`multiplex PCR`,`BioFire FilmArray`,`MALDI-TOF`,`procalcitonin`,`CRP`,`IgM ELISA`,`paired sera`,`rapid diagnostic test`,`diagnostic stewardship`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and the Indian laboratory landscape`,points:[`**Diagnostic stewardship** is the coordinated choice of **which test, on which patient, from which specimen, at what time** so that results change management - the laboratory twin of antimicrobial stewardship. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Microbiological tests are either **direct** (they find the organism or its parts - microscopy, culture, antigen, nucleic acid) or **indirect** (they find the host's antibody response - serology). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antimicrobial resistance** caused an estimated **1.27 million deaths directly and 4.95 million associated deaths worldwide in 2019**, with India among the countries carrying the largest absolute burden. [GRAM Lancet 2022]`,`The **ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance Network (AMRSN)** reports that **E. coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae** are the commonest isolates from Indian hospitals and that carbapenem susceptibility of K. pneumoniae has fallen to **under half** in recent annual reports. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`The **National Essential Diagnostics List (NEDL)** of ICMR lists the tests each tier should offer - smear microscopy, malaria RDT, Gram stain and simple serology at primary level, and culture with susceptibility at district hospitals. [ICMR NEDL 2019]`,`Most Indian microbiology laboratories interpret susceptibility using **CLSI M100** breakpoints, which are revised **every year** - a report must state the edition used. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`Blood culture is still underused in India: many patients with fever receive empirical antibiotics **before any culture**, which permanently lowers the yield of every later specimen. [ICMR 2022]`,`The family physician's job is to **order a test only when its result will change what is done**, collect it correctly, and read it in the light of **pre-test probability**. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Why tests mislead: pre-analytical errors and sources of false results`,points:[`Up to **70% of laboratory errors are pre-analytical** - wrong specimen, wrong container, wrong time, poor labelling or delay in transport - and no analyser can correct them. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Prior antibiotics** sharply cut culture yield: in septic patients, blood cultures drawn after antibiotics were positive in **19.4% versus 31.4%** when drawn before. [FABLED 2019]`,`**Low blood volume** is the commonest cause of a false-negative blood culture: yield rises by about **3% for every extra mL** of blood cultured in adults. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Skin contamination** from poor antisepsis creates false-positive cultures with coagulase-negative staphylococci, leading to unnecessary vancomycin, longer stay and extra cost. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**Colonisation is not infection**: Candida in sputum, organisms on a chronic ulcer swab, or bacteria in the urine of a catheterised patient without symptoms usually need no treatment. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Serology before seroconversion** gives a false negative (IgM sent on day 2), and **background seropositivity** in endemic areas gives false positives on a single titre. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Cross-reactivity** confounds serology - dengue IgM with Zika and Japanese encephalitis, Widal with other Gram-negative infections and prior vaccination, and IgM tests with malaria and polyclonal activation. [WHO 2025]`,`**Predictive value depends on prevalence**: a test with 95% specificity used in a low-prevalence population will give more false than true positives - test the patient, not the population. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`How the tests work: the science behind each method`,points:[`**Culture** grows viable organisms; it is slow (24-72 h for bacteria, weeks for mycobacteria) but is the only route to **phenotypic susceptibility** and typing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Automated blood culture systems** (BacT/ALERT, BACTEC) detect **CO2 produced by growing organisms** with a sensor at the base of the bottle and flag positives continuously; bottles are incubated for **5 days** as standard. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry** identifies an isolate within minutes from its **ribosomal protein fingerprint**, cutting a day or more off identification from a positive bottle or plate. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Antigen tests** (NS1, malaria HRP2/pLDH, cryptococcal antigen, urinary pneumococcal and Legionella antigen) detect microbial proteins or polysaccharides and are positive **early**, while the organism is present. [WHO 2025]`,`**Antibody tests** detect the host response: **IgM appears first (about days 5-7)** and **IgG later (about days 10-14)**, so serology lags behind the illness and diagnoses it best on **paired sera**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAAT/PCR)** amplify a target gene sequence millions of times; real-time PCR reads it as it amplifies, giving results in **1-3 hours** and, with a cycle threshold, a rough load. [Harrison 22e]`,`NAAT detects **DNA, not viability** - it can stay positive after cure (TB, bacterial DNA in a treated valve) and cannot be used as a **test of cure**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Genotypic resistance tests** look for known resistance genes (mecA, vanA, KPC, NDM, OXA-48, rpoB) - a detected gene predicts resistance well, but an undetected gene does not prove susceptibility. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification of diagnostic tests (the one examiners ask)`,points:[`**Microscopy**: Gram stain, Ziehl-Neelsen and fluorescent (auramine) staining for AFB, India ink, KOH mount, thick and thin malaria smears - cheap, rapid, operator-dependent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Culture**: aerobic and anaerobic bacterial, blood culture, mycobacterial (**solid LJ 4-8 weeks; liquid MGIT 960 usually 1-2 weeks, up to 6 weeks**), fungal and viral culture. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Antigen detection**: ELISA or immunochromatographic lateral flow - NS1, HBsAg, p24, malaria RDT, cryptococcal antigen, urinary LAM. [WHO 2025]`,`**Antibody detection**: ELISA, agglutination (Widal, Weil-Felix, MAT), immunofluorescence (IFA, gold standard for scrub typhus), immunoblot. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Molecular**: single-target PCR, real-time quantitative PCR (viral load), cartridge NAAT (**CBNAAT/Xpert, Truenat**), multiplex syndromic panels, line probe assay and sequencing (16S rRNA, targeted and metagenomic NGS). [WHO 2024]`,`**Host-response biomarkers**: CRP, procalcitonin, ESR, ferritin, lactate - they measure inflammation or illness severity, not the organism. [SSC 2021]`,`Each test is judged by **sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV and turnaround time** against a reference standard - a rule-out test needs high sensitivity, a rule-in test high specificity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Blood culture: indications and technique`,points:[`**Indications**: suspected sepsis or bacteraemia, fever with rigors, suspected **infective endocarditis**, enteric fever, febrile neutropenia, PUO, new fever in a hospitalised or catheterised patient, and severe pneumonia or pyelonephritis needing admission. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**One set = one aerobic plus one anaerobic bottle from one venepuncture**; take **2 sets (up to 3-4) from separate sites** before the first antibiotic - never a single set. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Volume**: **20 mL per set in adults (8-10 mL per bottle)**, so 40-60 mL in total; children by weight (about **1-3 mL in neonates**, never less than 1 mL), in paediatric bottles. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Detection rises with the number of sets: about **73% of bacteraemias with one set, 90% with two and 98% with three** - one set also cannot tell a contaminant from a pathogen. [Lee 2007]`,`**Skin antisepsis**: **2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol** (or tincture of iodine), applied with friction and allowed to **dry for 30 seconds**; wipe bottle tops with 70% alcohol; do not palpate the site after cleaning. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`Draw by **fresh peripheral venepuncture**; avoid drawing from an existing cannula except when **paired catheter and peripheral cultures** are taken for suspected catheter infection (see the Bloodstream infection topic). [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`In suspected **endocarditis** take **3 sets at least 30 minutes apart** before antibiotics in a stable patient; in sepsis, cultures must not delay antibiotics beyond **1 hour**. [ESC 2023]`,`Label each bottle with **site and time**, send at **room temperature** within 2 hours - never refrigerate - and tell the laboratory if brucella, fungi or a slow grower such as HACEK is suspected. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`In **suspected enteric fever** blood culture is positive in about **60-80% in week 1**, falling later; **bone marrow culture** stays positive in **80-95%** even after antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Reading a culture result: pathogen or contaminant`,points:[`**Always pathogens** in blood: **S. aureus, S. pneumoniae, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, beta-haemolytic streptococci, Candida** and Salmonella - never dismiss one positive bottle. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Usual contaminants**: **coagulase-negative staphylococci, Corynebacterium (diphtheroids), Bacillus (not anthracis), Cutibacterium (Propionibacterium) and Micrococcus** - especially if in only **one of two or more sets**. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`A skin organism becomes significant when it grows in **two or more sets drawn separately**, with a prosthetic valve, a long line or a cardiac device in place. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Time to positivity** helps: true pathogens usually flag within **12-24 hours**, while contaminants often take **more than 48 hours**; a short time to positivity suggests a high bacterial load. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`The **Gram stain of a positive bottle** is the first actionable result - Gram-positive cocci in clusters means staphylococcus until proved otherwise and justifies MRSA cover until speciation. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`The **blood culture contamination rate** is a quality marker: the long-standing benchmark is **under 3%**, and **1% or less** is now the target. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**Urine**: a clean-catch midstream sample processed within 2 hours (or refrigerated at 4 C for up to 24 hours); **10^5 CFU/mL** is the classic cut-off, lower counts count in symptomatic patients - details are in the UTI topic. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Sputum** is acceptable for culture only if it has **under 10 squamous epithelial cells and over 25 neutrophils per low-power field** - otherwise it is saliva. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Mixed growth of three or more organisms** in urine or a superficial swab usually means contamination - repeat the specimen rather than treat it. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Antimicrobial susceptibility testing, MIC and the sensitivity report`,points:[`The **minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC)** is the **lowest concentration of an antibiotic that inhibits visible growth** of an organism in vitro, read in two-fold dilutions (0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4 mg/L). [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Broth microdilution** is the reference method; **gradient strips (E-test)** and **automated systems (VITEK 2, BD Phoenix)** give MICs, while **Kirby-Bauer disc diffusion** on Mueller-Hinton agar gives zone diameters. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`A **clinical breakpoint** is the MIC (or zone) that separates likely success from likely failure at a standard dose; it is set from **PK/PD targets, MIC distributions and clinical outcomes**. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`CLSI categories: **S (susceptible), SDD (susceptible dose-dependent - needs a higher dose, e.g. cefepime), I (intermediate), R (resistant)** and NS (non-susceptible); EUCAST redefined **I as susceptible, increased exposure** in 2019. [EUCAST 2025]`,`**Never choose a drug by comparing MIC numbers across drugs** - each drug has its own breakpoint, so an MIC of 4 may be very susceptible for one agent and resistant for another. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Vancomycin for S. aureus must be tested by MIC, not disc**: S at 2 mg/L or less, VISA 4-8, VRSA 16 or more; **colistin is tested only by broth microdilution** and CLSI has no susceptible category (I at 2 mg/L or less). [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Salmonella** fluoroquinolone susceptibility is read by **ciprofloxacin MIC (S at 0.06 mg/L or less)** or pefloxacin disc - most Indian S. Typhi are non-susceptible, so ciprofloxacin is no longer empirical treatment. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Phenotypic resistance tests**: cefoxitin disc for **MRSA**, **D-test** for inducible clindamycin resistance, ESBL confirmation, and **mCIM with eCIM** to detect a carbapenemase and separate metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM) from serine enzymes (KPC, OXA-48). [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**Selective (cascade) reporting** releases broad-spectrum results only if narrow agents are resistant - it steers prescribers to the narrowest effective drug, as recommended by ICMR. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`A **cumulative antibiogram** (first isolate per patient, at least 30 isolates per organism, updated yearly) guides local empirical therapy. [CLSI M39 2022]`]},{heading:`Molecular diagnostics: CBNAAT, Truenat, multiplex PCR and sequencing`,points:[`**CBNAAT (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra)** is a cartridge real-time PCR that detects **M. tuberculosis and rpoB mutations (rifampicin resistance)** in about **2 hours**; Ultra is more sensitive and adds a **'trace' category**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Truenat (MTB, MTB Plus, MTB-RIF Dx)** is an Indian, battery-operated, chip-based micro-PCR deployable at **primary health centre level**; NTEP uses Xpert or Truenat as the **upfront first test for all presumptive TB**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Line probe assays** (first-line for isoniazid via katG and inhA and rifampicin; second-line for fluoroquinolones) and **Xpert MTB/XDR** detect resistance to isoniazid and fluoroquinolones within 1-2 days. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Multiplex syndromic panels** (e.g. BioFire FilmArray) test one specimen for many targets in about **1 hour**: blood culture identification with resistance genes (mecA, vanA/B, CTX-M, KPC, NDM, OXA-48, VIM, IMP), respiratory, gastrointestinal and **meningitis/encephalitis (14 targets)** panels. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Panels can **over-call** (colonisers and dead organisms in stool and respiratory panels) and **miss** organisms not on the list; the meningitis panel does not replace **CSF culture and cryptococcal antigen**. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Disease-specific PCR**: dengue RT-PCR in days 1-5; scrub typhus PCR (47-kDa or 56-kDa gene) on **eschar** or buffy coat; leptospira PCR on blood in week 1 and urine after it; HSV PCR on CSF. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Quantitative PCR (viral load)** monitors HIV, HBV, HCV and CMV treatment rather than diagnosing acute fever. [NACO 2021]`,`**16S rRNA PCR and sequencing** on culture-negative tissue (valve, bone, joint, abscess) after antibiotics, and **targeted next-generation sequencing** for drug-resistant TB, are tertiary tools. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Serology: IgM and IgG timing, paired sera and avidity`,points:[`**IgM** becomes detectable at about **days 5-7** of illness, peaks at 2-3 weeks and usually fades over **2-3 months** - but in scrub typhus and leptospirosis it can persist for many months. [ICMR 2022]`,`**IgG** rises from about **days 10-14** and persists for years; on its own it means **past infection** unless a **fourfold rise** is shown in paired sera. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paired sera** are taken in the acute phase and **10-14 days later**; a **fourfold or greater rise in titre** (or seroconversion) is the serological proof of acute infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **secondary dengue** IgG rises **early (by days 1-3)** and IgM may be low or absent, so a high IgG with low IgM early in illness suggests secondary infection rather than past exposure. [WHO 2025]`,`**IgG avidity** distinguishes recent from old infection: **low avidity means recent infection**, high avidity in toxoplasma excludes infection in roughly the previous **4 months** - used chiefly in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **Widal test** has poor sensitivity and specificity in endemic India; a single titre should not be used to diagnose enteric fever, and ICMR does not recommend it. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Weil-Felix** is obsolete for scrub typhus; use **IgM ELISA** (from day 5) with **IFA** as the reference, and the **MAT** remains the reference for leptospirosis (single titre of 1:400 or more, or a fourfold rise). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Window period**: fourth-generation HIV antigen-antibody tests detect infection from about **2-6 weeks** after exposure; a negative test inside the window needs repeating. [NACO 2021]`,`**Immunosuppressed patients** (advanced HIV, transplant, rituximab, hypogammaglobulinaemia) may never mount antibody - use antigen or NAAT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Rapid diagnostic tests at the point of care`,points:[`**Bivalent malaria RDT** detects **HRP2 (P. falciparum)** and **pan-pLDH (all species)**; HRP2 persists **2-4 weeks after cure**, so it cannot be used for test of cure, and **pfhrp2/3 gene deletions** cause false negatives. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`A negative malaria RDT in a strongly suspected case needs a **thick and thin smear**, repeated **12-hourly for up to 3 samples**; the smear also gives species and parasite density. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Dengue NS1 antigen** is positive in **days 1-5**; a combined NS1-IgM-IgG card helps across the illness, but ELISA (MAC-ELISA) is the confirmatory test under NCVBDC. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**rK39 RDT** diagnoses visceral leishmaniasis in a compatible clinical case in endemic districts; it stays positive for years, so it cannot diagnose relapse. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Cryptococcal antigen lateral flow** on serum in advanced HIV and **urine LF-LAM** in seriously ill people living with HIV are WHO-recommended bedside tests (see the HIV topics). [WHO 2022]`,`**Typhoid RDTs** (Typhidot, TUBEX) have inadequate accuracy and are **not recommended** for diagnosis; blood culture remains the standard. [WHO 2023]`,`**Urine dipstick**: **nitrite** (Enterobacterales convert nitrate) and **leucocyte esterase** together have a high negative predictive value for cystitis in women, but dipsticks are unreliable in older and catheterised patients. [IDSA 2019]`,`Antigen RDTs for **influenza, SARS-CoV-2 and group A streptococcus** have good specificity but modest sensitivity - a negative result in a high-probability patient needs PCR or culture. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`]},{heading:`Biomarkers: CRP and procalcitonin`,points:[`**C-reactive protein** is made by **hepatocytes under IL-6**; it rises from **4-6 hours**, doubles about every 8 hours, peaks at **36-50 hours** and has a half-life of about **19 hours**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Procalcitonin (PCT)** is the precursor of calcitonin; in bacterial infection many tissues release it under **IL-1, IL-6 and TNF**, while **interferon-gamma in viral infection suppresses it** - which is why PCT separates bacterial from viral better than CRP. [Harrison 22e]`,`PCT rises within **2-4 hours**, peaks at **6-24 hours** and has a half-life of about **24 hours**; normal is **under 0.05 ng/mL**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower respiratory tract infection algorithm**: PCT **under 0.1** strongly discourages antibiotics, **0.1-0.25** discourages, **0.25-0.5** encourages and **over 0.5 ng/mL** strongly encourages them. [ProHOSP 2009]`,`**Stopping rule in sepsis**: consider stopping antibiotics when PCT falls by **80% or more from its peak** or to **under 0.5 ng/mL** in a clinically improving patient. [SSC 2021]`,`PCT is **falsely raised** by major surgery, trauma, burns, cardiogenic shock, heat stroke, **medullary thyroid and small-cell lung cancer**, anti-thymocyte globulin, the first 48 hours of life and **falciparum malaria**; it is mildly higher in CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`PCT may be **falsely low** in early infection (first hours), localised infection (abscess, empyema), and atypical or intracellular organisms such as Mycoplasma and some rickettsial infections. [Harrison 22e]`,`In primary care, NICE uses **point-of-care CRP** in suspected lower respiratory infection: **under 20 mg/L no antibiotic, 20-100 mg/L consider a delayed prescription, over 100 mg/L offer an antibiotic**. [NICE CG191 2019]`,`Neither CRP nor PCT should be used to **withhold antibiotics from a patient who looks septic**; Surviving Sepsis advises against PCT to decide when to start. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Already on antibiotics**: still take blood cultures - draw just **before the next dose**; use PCR, antigen tests or bone marrow culture (enteric fever), which are less affected. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: screen for **asymptomatic bacteriuria by urine culture** at the first antenatal visit; use **IgG avidity** for toxoplasma and CMV IgG positives; serology is otherwise interpreted as in non-pregnant adults. [WHO ANC 2016]`,`**Neonates**: PCT rises physiologically in the **first 48 hours**, and CRP is slow - serial values and blood culture (at least 1 mL) are needed for early-onset sepsis. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Immunocompromised and neutropenic**: fever may be the only sign, antibody tests fail, and fungal antigens (galactomannan, beta-D-glucan), CMV PCR and repeated cultures are needed (see the Fever in the immunocompromised topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: baseline PCT is higher, so use trends rather than one value; culture any dialysis catheter and peritoneal fluid early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Older adults**: blunted fever and atypical presentations; **asymptomatic bacteriuria is common (up to 15-50% in long-term care)** and a positive urine culture alone should not be treated. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: rely on **Gram stain, smear microscopy, RDTs and NEDL tests**, and use the **NTEP sample transport** and district public health laboratory (IDSP) hub-and-spoke system for culture and NAAT. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Biosafety**: suspected TB, brucella, melioidosis or anthrax specimens must be flagged so the laboratory handles them in **biosafety level 2-3** conditions. [WHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications of poor testing and how to avoid them`,points:[`**Treating contaminants** (single CoNS bottle) exposes patients to vancomycin toxicity, longer stays and C. difficile; repeat cultures before treating a skin organism. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria** or a catheter urine culture without symptoms drives resistance and C. difficile, with no benefit except in pregnancy and before urological surgery. [IDSA 2019]`,`**Diagnosing typhoid by Widal** leads to overtreatment of viral fevers and missed dengue, malaria and scrub typhus. [ICMR 2022]`,`**False reassurance from early IgM** - a negative dengue or scrub IgM on day 2 means nothing; the test must be repeated after day 5. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Using PCR as test of cure** (persistent TB NAAT positivity) leads to wrongful labels of treatment failure; monitor TB response with smear and culture. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Ignoring a positive bottle** of S. aureus or Candida is a serious error - each carries high mortality and always needs treatment and source search. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Late cultures** after empirical broad-spectrum therapy prevent de-escalation and lock the patient onto carbapenems. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Communicating with the laboratory, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Write a **clinical summary on every request**: working diagnosis, day of illness, antibiotics already given, travel and exposure - it changes media, incubation and which tests are added. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`The laboratory should **phone critical results** - positive blood culture Gram stain, positive CSF, malaria parasites, carbapenemase producer - and the clinician must act the same day. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**Review every culture at 48-72 hours** and de-escalate or stop - this 'antibiotic time-out' is the key stewardship step. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**Repeat blood cultures** are needed for **S. aureus and Candida bloodstream infection** (until negative) and for endocarditis, but not routinely for Gram-negative bacteraemia that is responding. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Serial PCT** (day 0, then every 1-2 days) is useful only to **shorten** therapy; a single value is less useful than the trend. [ADAPT-Sepsis 2025]`,`Send isolates of public-health importance (cholera, diphtheria, carbapenemase producers, unusual resistance) to **reference or ICMR laboratories** and notify clusters on **IHIP** under IDSP. [IDSP 2024]`,`Viral fevers with outbreak potential (Nipah, Zika, Chandipura, avian influenza) are tested through the **ICMR-DHR Viral Research and Diagnostic Laboratories (VRDL)** network and NIV Pune. [ICMR 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and the national programme link`,points:[`Good specimen practice is itself **infection prevention**: correct antisepsis, closed blood culture systems and safe needle disposal protect both patient and staff. [WHO 2020]`,`**Laboratory waste**: microbiology cultures and stocks go into the **yellow category** and must be **pre-treated (autoclaved) on site** before disposal under the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`The **National Action Plan on AMR** and **NCDC's National AMR Surveillance Network (NARS-Net)** depend on quality culture and AST data from district and medical-college laboratories. [NCDC 2023]`,`The **ICMR AMR surveillance and stewardship programme** links microbiology reporting to hospital antimicrobial stewardship (AMSP) committees. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`**NTEP** provides free NAAT, culture and drug-susceptibility testing through a network of intermediate and national reference laboratories. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Free Diagnostics Service Initiative** of the National Health Mission makes essential tests free at public facilities, including at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** level. [NHM 2019]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**IDSA/ASM Guide to Utilization of the Microbiology Laboratory (2024 update)** sets the specimen, volume and test-choice standards for each syndrome, including 20 mL per adult blood culture set. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**CLSI M100** is revised yearly (**34th edition 2024, 35th edition 2025**); recent cycles **lowered piperacillin-tazobactam (2022) and aminoglycoside (2023) breakpoints** for Enterobacterales and added breakpoints for new agents such as sulbactam-durlobactam. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`**EUCAST breakpoint tables** (version 14.0 2024, 15.0 2025) keep the 'I = susceptible, increased exposure' definition; reports from labs using EUCAST and CLSI are not interchangeable. [EUCAST 2025]`,`**WHO TB diagnostics module (2024 update)** groups NAATs by complexity, endorses low-complexity automated NAATs (Xpert, Truenat) as initial tests and adds **targeted next-generation sequencing** for drug resistance. [WHO 2024]`,`**ADAPT-Sepsis (2025)**: daily **PCT-guided** stopping safely shortened antibiotic duration in sepsis, whereas a **CRP-guided** strategy did not. [ADAPT-Sepsis 2025]`,`**CLSI M47 (2022)** and CDC guidance (2024) set the **blood culture contamination target at 1% or less**, promoting initial-specimen diversion devices and dedicated phlebotomy teams. [CLSI M47 2022]`,`**NTEP** continues to scale **upfront NAAT for every presumptive TB patient**, replacing sputum smear as the first test wherever NAAT is available. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'How do you collect a blood culture?'** - two sets, separate sites, 20 mL per set, chlorhexidine-alcohol dried, before antibiotics, room temperature within 2 hours. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`Classic viva: **'What is MIC and breakpoint?'** - lowest concentration preventing visible growth; breakpoint separates S from R; never compare MICs between drugs. [CLSI M100 2024]`,`Classic viva: **'When is IgM ELISA useful?'** - from day 5 onwards; NS1 or PCR before that; paired sera for proof. [ICMR 2022]`,`Common mistake: **sending Widal** for fever of 3 days - it has no place; blood culture is the test. [ICMR 2022]`,`Common mistake: treating **CoNS in one bottle** as a pathogen, or **asymptomatic bacteriuria** in an older adult. [IDSA 2019]`,`Common mistake: using **PCT to withhold antibiotics in a septic patient** - PCT helps to stop, not to start. [SSC 2021]`,`Know why **colistin and vancomycin (for S. aureus)** cannot be reported by disc diffusion - large molecules diffuse poorly in agar. [CLSI M100 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Report-guided de-escalation: narrow drugs to switch to once the culture is back`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cefazolin (MSSA bacteraemia)`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`14 days uncomplicated, 4-6 weeks complicated (from first negative culture)`,`Rash, rare anaphylaxis; preferred over vancomycin for MSSA`],[`Cloxacillin (MSSA, alternative)`,`2 g IV 4-6-hourly`,`As for cefazolin`,`Phlebitis, hepatitis, interstitial nephritis; frequent dosing`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA)`,`Load 20-35 mg/kg IV, then 15-20 mg/kg 8-12-hourly`,`AUC/MIC target 400-600; trough monitoring if AUC not available`,`Nephrotoxicity (worse with pip-tazo), red man syndrome; not for MSSA`],[`Ceftriaxone (susceptible Enterobacterales)`,`1-2 g IV once daily`,`7 days for uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia with source control`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile; ineffective if ESBL`],[`Ertapenem (ESBL producer, stable)`,`1 g IV once daily`,`7-14 days by source`,`Seizures (rare), C. difficile; no Pseudomonas or Acinetobacter cover`],[`Nitrofurantoin (ESBL E. coli cystitis)`,`100 mg oral 12-hourly (modified release)`,`5 days`,`Avoid if eGFR under 30, in pyelonephritis and at term; pulmonary fibrosis with long use`],[`Ampicillin (Enterococcus faecalis, ampicillin-S)`,`2 g IV 4-hourly`,`7-14 days; 6 weeks in endocarditis`,`Rash, C. difficile; E. faecium is usually resistant`],[`Azithromycin (S. Typhi, azithromycin-S)`,`1 g oral once daily (child 20 mg/kg)`,`7 days`,`Diarrhoea, QT prolongation; preferred over ciprofloxacin in India`],[`Fluconazole (fluconazole-S Candida, step-down)`,`800 mg (12 mg/kg) load, then 400 mg (6 mg/kg) daily`,`14 days after first negative culture and symptom resolution`,`Hepatotoxicity, QT prolongation, interactions; C. krusei is intrinsically resistant`]]},{heading:`Classification of microbiological tests`,columns:[`Method`,`What it detects`,`Turnaround`,`Strength`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Microscopy (Gram, ZN, smear)`,`Organism morphology`,`Minutes-1 hour`,`Cheap, fast, guides empirical choice`,`Operator-dependent; low sensitivity (AFB smear)`],[`Culture and AST`,`Viable organism and its phenotypic susceptibility`,`24-72 h (TB 1-8 weeks)`,`Gold standard; gives MIC`,`Slow; killed by prior antibiotics`],[`Antigen tests (NS1, HRP2, CrAg)`,`Microbial protein or polysaccharide`,`15-60 min`,`Early, bedside`,`Antigen may persist after cure (HRP2)`],[`Antibody serology (IgM, IgG)`,`Host immune response`,`Hours`,`Useful after day 5 and for past exposure`,`Lags illness; cross-reacts; fails in immunosuppression`],[`NAAT/PCR (CBNAAT, Truenat)`,`Specific nucleic acid`,`1-3 h`,`Sensitive, rapid, some resistance genes`,`Detects dead organisms; limited targets`],[`Multiplex syndromic panels`,`Many organisms and resistance genes`,`About 1 h`,`Broad, fast`,`Costly; over-calls colonisers`],[`Biomarkers (CRP, PCT)`,`Host inflammation`,`1 h`,`Trend guides duration`,`Not organism-specific; false positives`]]},{heading:`Contaminant versus true bacteraemia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Favours contaminant`,`Favours true bacteraemia`],rows:[[`Organism`,`CoNS, diphtheroids, Bacillus, Cutibacterium, Micrococcus`,`S. aureus, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, streptococci, Candida`],[`Number of positive sets`,`One of two or more sets`,`Two or more separate sets`],[`Time to positivity`,`Over 48 hours`,`Under 24 hours`],[`Clinical picture`,`Well, no device, other cause for fever`,`Sepsis, prosthetic valve, long line, device`],[`Same organism at a site`,`No matching focus`,`Same organism from urine, pus or line tip`],[`Action`,`Repeat 2 sets before treating`,`Treat, search for source, repeat cultures if S. aureus or Candida`]]},{heading:`Investigations: timing and cut-offs that change decisions`,columns:[`Test`,`Best window`,`Cut-off or interpretation`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Blood culture`,`Before first antibiotic`,`Any growth of a true pathogen is significant`,`Low volume, single set, contamination`],[`Dengue NS1 / RT-PCR`,`Days 1-5`,`Positive confirms current dengue`,`Falls after day 5; negative does not exclude`],[`IgM ELISA (dengue, scrub, lepto)`,`From day 5`,`Positive suggests recent infection`,`False negative before day 5; persists months`],[`Paired IgG`,`Acute and 10-14 days later`,`Fourfold rise confirms acute infection`,`Retrospective only`],[`Leptospira MAT`,`Second week onwards`,`Single titre 1:400 or more, or fourfold rise`,`Reference labs only`],[`Procalcitonin`,`Serial, 0 and 24-48 h`,`Under 0.25 (LRTI) bacterial unlikely; stop at 80% fall or under 0.5 ng/mL`,`Raised by surgery, trauma, malaria`],[`CRP (primary care LRTI)`,`At presentation`,`Under 20 no antibiotic; 20-100 delayed; over 100 treat`,`Non-specific; slow to rise`],[`Urine culture`,`Clean catch, plated within 2 h`,`10^5 CFU/mL classic cut-off`,`Asymptomatic bacteriuria needs no treatment`]]},{heading:`Specimen collection at a glance`,columns:[`Specimen`,`How to collect`,`Transport`,`Common error`],rows:[[`Blood culture`,`2 sets, 20 mL per set, chlorhexidine-alcohol`,`Room temperature, within 2 h`,`Single set, under-filled bottles`],[`Urine`,`Midstream clean catch; catheter port, not bag`,`Within 2 h or 4 C up to 24 h`,`Culture of catheter bag urine`],[`Sputum`,`Deep cough, early morning, before antibiotic`,`Within 2 h`,`Saliva sent (over 10 squamous cells/LPF)`],[`CSF`,`Lumbar puncture, 3-4 tubes, 1-2 mL each`,`Immediately, never refrigerate for culture`,`Delay after antibiotics`],[`Pus / tissue`,`Aspirate or tissue in sterile container`,`Within 2 h; anaerobic transport if needed`,`Surface swab of an ulcer`],[`Stool`,`Fresh stool in sterile container`,`Within 2 h or Cary-Blair medium`,`Testing formed stool or after 3 days in hospital`]]}],redFlags:[`Gram-positive cocci in clusters, Gram-negative bacilli or yeast in a positive blood culture - start or adjust therapy the same day`,`Positive blood culture for S. aureus or Candida in any patient - always pathogenic, needs admission and source search`,`Carbapenemase producer (NDM, OXA-48, KPC) reported in a sick patient - urgent ID input and contact precautions`,`Positive CSF Gram stain, cryptococcal antigen or malaria parasites on smear - treat as an emergency`,`Rising PCT or lactate with clinical deterioration despite 'appropriate' antibiotics - look for an undrained source or resistant organism`,`Rifampicin resistance detected on CBNAAT or Truenat - refer to the NTEP DR-TB centre the same week`,`Suspected outbreak organism (cholera, diphtheria, Nipah) - notify on IHIP immediately`],pearls:[`**Two sets, 20 mL each, before the first antibiotic** - the volume, not the brand of bottle, decides the yield.`,`**One bottle of CoNS is a contaminant until a second set says otherwise.**`,`**S. aureus and Candida in blood are never contaminants.**`,`**IgM from day 5, NS1 and PCR before it** - the day of illness picks the test.`,`**MIC is drug-specific**: compare each MIC with its own breakpoint, never with another drug's MIC.`,`**PCT stops antibiotics; it does not start them** - stop at an 80% fall or under 0.5 ng/mL.`,`**NAAT detects DNA, not life** - never a test of cure for TB.`,`**Widal is not a test for enteric fever in India** - blood culture in week 1, bone marrow if already treated.`,`**Colonisation is not infection** - treat the patient, not the swab.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q1`,stem:`A 56-year-old man with fever and rigors is to start empirical antibiotics. What is the recommended blood culture practice before the first dose in an adult?`,options:[`One set of 5 mL drawn from the existing cannula`,`Two sets of about 20 mL each from separate venepunctures`,`One aerobic bottle of 10 mL from the antecubital fossa`,`Three sets drawn only after the fever spikes above 39 C`],answer:1,explanation:`Two sets of about 20 mL each (8-10 mL per aerobic and anaerobic bottle) from separate venepunctures detect about 90% of bacteraemias and let a contaminant be told from a pathogen. A single set or small volume misses cases, drawing from an old cannula raises contamination, and waiting for a fever spike only delays cultures and antibiotics.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman admitted with cellulitis has two blood culture sets taken. One aerobic bottle grows coagulase-negative staphylococci after 52 hours; the other set is sterile. She is improving on cefazolin and has no prosthetic device. What is the best interpretation?`,options:[`Probable contaminant; continue current therapy without adding vancomycin`,`Methicillin-resistant staphylococcal bacteraemia requiring vancomycin`,`Infective endocarditis until proved otherwise; start gentamicin`,`Catheter-related bloodstream infection requiring line removal`],answer:0,explanation:`A skin organism (coagulase-negative staphylococci) in only one of two sets, flagging after 48 hours, in an improving patient without a device is a classic contaminant. Adding vancomycin for presumed MRSA bacteraemia, labelling it endocarditis, or blaming a line she does not have would expose her to toxicity without benefit; repeat cultures only if she deteriorates.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q3`,stem:`A patient with fever of 2 days, myalgia and platelets of 1.1 lakh/microlitre has a negative dengue IgM ELISA. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Exclude dengue and start ceftriaxone for enteric fever`,`Send a Widal test to look for typhoid`,`Send dengue NS1 antigen now and repeat IgM after day 5 if needed`,`Send paired IgG on day 3 to look for a fourfold rise`],answer:2,explanation:`IgM usually appears only from about day 5, so a negative IgM on day 2 means nothing; NS1 antigen (or RT-PCR) is the test for days 1-5. Excluding dengue on this result and starting ceftriaxone is the classic error, the Widal test is not recommended in India, and paired IgG needs samples 10-14 days apart, not on day 3.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q4`,stem:`A Klebsiella pneumoniae isolate from blood has an MIC of 1 mg/L to drug X (susceptible breakpoint 1 mg/L or less) and an MIC of 4 mg/L to drug Y (susceptible breakpoint 16 mg/L or less). Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Drug X is more active because its MIC number is lower`,`Both drugs are resistant because the MICs are above 0.5 mg/L`,`MICs of different drugs can be compared directly once in the same units`,`Drug Y is further below its own breakpoint and is the more reliable choice on MIC grounds`],answer:3,explanation:`An MIC is meaningful only against that drug's own breakpoint: drug Y at 4 mg/L sits two dilutions below its breakpoint of 16, while drug X at 1 mg/L is right at its breakpoint, so Y has more margin. Choosing drug X because its number is lower, or comparing MICs across drugs in the same units, is the classic error; neither drug is resistant by its breakpoint.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-q5`,stem:`In which of the following is serum procalcitonin most likely to be raised in the absence of bacterial infection?`,options:[`Uncomplicated influenza A`,`Medullary carcinoma of the thyroid`,`Systemic lupus erythematosus flare`,`Infectious mononucleosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Procalcitonin is the calcitonin precursor, so medullary thyroid carcinoma (a C-cell tumour) raises it without infection, as do major surgery, trauma, burns and falciparum malaria. Viral infections such as influenza and infectious mononucleosis keep it low because interferon-gamma suppresses PCT, and lupus flares typically raise CRP-like markers little and PCT not at all.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the collection and interpretation of blood cultures. Explain the antimicrobial susceptibility report including MIC and breakpoints, and add a note on rapid molecular tests used in febrile illness.`,openingLines:[`Blood culture is the gold-standard test for bacteraemia and the only route to the organism and its phenotypic susceptibility (MIC), on which de-escalation depends.`,`Its yield is decided before the sample reaches the laboratory - by volume, number of sets, antisepsis and timing before the first antibiotic.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Sepsis, fever with rigors, suspected endocarditis, enteric fever, febrile neutropenia, PUO, hospital-onset fever.`,`Severe pneumonia, pyelonephritis or meningitis needing admission.`]},{heading:`Technique`,points:[`Two sets (aerobic plus anaerobic) from separate venepunctures; 20 mL per set in adults; 1-3 mL in neonates.`,`2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol, dry 30 seconds; bottle tops wiped; no palpation after cleaning.`,`Before antibiotics; within 1 hour in sepsis; 3 sets 30 minutes apart in suspected endocarditis.`,`Room temperature, to the laboratory within 2 hours; clinical details on the form.`]},{heading:`Processing`,points:[`Automated CO2-detecting systems, 5-day incubation; Gram stain of positive bottle phoned to clinician.`,`Identification by MALDI-TOF or biochemicals; AST by disc diffusion, gradient strip, VITEK or broth microdilution.`]},{heading:`Interpretation`,points:[`Always pathogens: S. aureus, Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, streptococci, Candida.`,`Likely contaminants: CoNS, diphtheroids, Bacillus, Cutibacterium in one set, time to positivity over 48 h.`,`Contamination benchmark under 3%, target 1% or less.`]},{heading:`Susceptibility report, MIC and breakpoints`,points:[`MIC = lowest concentration inhibiting visible growth; breakpoint separates S, SDD, I and R (CLSI M100 2024).`,`Never compare MICs across drugs; vancomycin (S. aureus) and colistin by MIC only.`,`Phenotypic tests: cefoxitin for MRSA, D-test, ESBL, mCIM/eCIM for carbapenemase.`,`Selective reporting and cumulative antibiogram guide therapy and stewardship.`]},{heading:`Rapid molecular tests`,points:[`CBNAAT/Truenat: TB and rifampicin resistance in 2 hours; NTEP upfront NAAT.`,`Multiplex panels: blood culture ID with resistance genes, meningitis/encephalitis, respiratory panels in 1 hour.`,`Disease PCR: dengue RT-PCR days 1-5, scrub eschar PCR, HSV CSF PCR.`,`Limitations: DNA not viability, limited targets, cost, over-calling colonisers.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: specimen collection to Gram stain to identification to AST to de-escalation`,`Table: contaminant versus true bacteraemia`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications and technique`,marks:3},{part:`Processing and interpretation`,marks:2},{part:`MIC, breakpoints and the report`,marks:3},{part:`Rapid molecular tests`,marks:2}],keywords:[`blood culture`,`MIC`,`breakpoint`,`CLSI`,`CBNAAT`,`multiplex PCR`,`contaminant`]},{id:`infectious-fever-lab-microbiology-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on procalcitonin and C-reactive protein in the management of infection.`,openingLines:[`CRP (an IL-6-driven hepatic acute-phase protein) and procalcitonin (the calcitonin precursor released by tissues in bacterial infection) are host-response biomarkers, not tests for an organism.`,`Their main value is in trends: PCT helps to shorten antibiotic courses, CRP helps primary-care decisions in respiratory infection.`],answer:[{heading:`Biology`,points:[`CRP: rises 4-6 h, peaks 36-50 h, half-life 19 h.`,`PCT: rises 2-4 h, peaks 6-24 h, half-life 24 h; suppressed by interferon-gamma in viral infection.`]},{heading:`Uses and cut-offs`,points:[`LRTI: PCT under 0.1 strongly against, 0.25-0.5 for, over 0.5 ng/mL strongly for antibiotics.`,`Sepsis: stop when PCT falls 80% from peak or under 0.5 ng/mL (SSC 2021; ADAPT-Sepsis 2025).`,`Primary care CRP: under 20 no antibiotic, 20-100 delayed, over 100 mg/L treat (NICE).`]},{heading:`Pitfalls`,points:[`PCT raised by surgery, trauma, burns, cardiogenic shock, medullary thyroid cancer, malaria, neonates.`,`Low in early or localised infection; do not withhold antibiotics in sepsis on a low value.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of PCT and CRP kinetics over 72 hours`],markSplit:[{part:`Biology and kinetics`,marks:2},{part:`Uses and cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Pitfalls`,marks:1}],keywords:[`procalcitonin`,`CRP`,`antibiotic stewardship`,`ADAPT-Sepsis`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Laboratory diagnosis of infectious diseases`,`Miller JM et al. A Guide to Utilization of the Microbiology Laboratory for Diagnosis of Infectious Diseases: 2024 Update by IDSA and ASM. Clin Infect Dis 2024`,`CLSI M100 Performance Standards for Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing, 34th edition, 2024`,`CLSI M47 Principles and Procedures for Blood Cultures, 2nd edition, 2022`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022, and ICMR AMRSN Annual Report 2023`,`WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis, Module 3: Diagnosis, 2024 update`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) Training Modules and Diagnostic Algorithms, MoHFW, 2024`,`Surviving Sepsis Campaign International Guidelines 2021; Dark P et al. ADAPT-Sepsis trial, JAMA 2025`]}),l.push({id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy`,title:`Antibiotic allergy: penicillin allergy delabelling and cross-reactivity`,oneLiner:`About **10% of patients carry a penicillin allergy label but over 90% are not allergic**; a **risk-stratified history (PEN-FAST)** followed by a **direct oral amoxicillin challenge in low-risk patients** removes the label safely, while true cross-reactivity with cephalosporins is about **1-2% and is driven by a shared R1 side chain**, and **SJS/TEN or DRESS** to any antibiotic (classically sulfonamides) is an absolute bar to rechallenge.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`penicillin allergy`,`beta-lactam allergy`,`delabelling`,`PEN-FAST`,`direct oral challenge`,`graded challenge`,`test dose`,`desensitisation`,`skin prick test`,`side chain`,`R1 side chain`,`cephalosporin cross-reactivity`,`aztreonam`,`sulfonamide allergy`,`cotrimoxazole`,`Stevens-Johnson syndrome`,`toxic epidermal necrolysis`,`DRESS`,`anaphylaxis`,`tryptase`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Drug allergy** is an **immunologically mediated** adverse drug reaction (IgE or T-cell); it is distinct from **intolerance** (nausea, diarrhoea, headache) and from **pseudo-allergy** caused by direct mast-cell activation. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`About **10% of patients** carry a penicillin allergy label, yet **over 90-95%** tolerate penicillin when properly tested. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`Around **80% of patients with true IgE-mediated penicillin allergy lose their sensitivity within 10 years**, so an old label is usually out of date. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`Penicillin **anaphylaxis is rare - roughly 1-5 per 10,000 courses**, and fatal anaphylaxis far rarer; beta-lactams remain the commonest cause of drug-induced anaphylaxis because they are so widely used. [Harrison 22e]`,`A penicillin label leads to **broader, costlier and more toxic antibiotics**: labelled patients had about a **69% higher risk of MRSA and 26% higher risk of C. difficile**. [BMJ 2018]`,`In India, data on labels are limited, but **cotrimoxazole, anticonvulsants and allopurinol** dominate case series of **Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN**, and reactions are reported to the **Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI)**. [PvPI 2023]`,`Delabelling is now an **antimicrobial stewardship priority**: removing an incorrect label restores first-line, narrow-spectrum therapy such as amoxicillin, benzylpenicillin and cefazolin. [BSACI 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Commonest culprits**: beta-lactams (IgE and T-cell reactions), **sulfonamide antibiotics** (cotrimoxazole - maculopapular rash, SJS/TEN, DRESS), fluoroquinolones, vancomycin and macrolides. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mislabelling** is common: viral exanthems during amoxicillin courses (especially **EBV** - rash in most patients given amoxicillin), side effects such as diarrhoea, and family history wrongly recorded as allergy. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**HIV infection** raises the risk of cotrimoxazole hypersensitivity many-fold, especially with low CD4 counts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HLA associations** predict severe T-cell reactions: **HLA-B*15:02** with carbamazepine SJS/TEN in South and East Asians, **HLA-B*58:01** with allopurinol, **HLA-A*32:01** with vancomycin DRESS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parenteral and repeated intermittent courses** sensitise more than continuous oral therapy; frequent courses in cystic fibrosis raise beta-lactam allergy rates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Female sex, older age and polypharmacy** increase the number of allergy labels, while a family history of drug allergy is **not** a risk factor and is no reason to avoid a drug. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Beta-blockers and ACE inhibitors** do not cause allergy but make anaphylaxis more severe and harder to treat. [WAO 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: haptens, side chains and mast cells`,points:[`Beta-lactams are small molecules that act as **haptens**: the beta-lactam ring opens and binds lysine on host proteins to form the **penicilloyl 'major determinant' (about 95%)**, with penicilloate and benzylpenicillin as **minor determinants**. [Harrison 22e]`,`On re-exposure, the hapten-protein cross-links **IgE on mast cells and basophils**, releasing **histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes and PAF** within minutes - urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm and shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cross-reactivity is driven mainly by the R1 side chain**, not the shared beta-lactam ring - so amoxicillin cross-reacts with cephalexin and cefadroxil (identical R1) but rarely with cefazolin (unique side chain). [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Aztreonam** (a monobactam) does not cross-react with penicillins but shares an identical side chain with **ceftazidime** (and cefiderocol). [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**T-cell (type IV) reactions** arise when the drug binds HLA or T-cell receptors directly (**p-i concept**) or via haptens, giving delayed rashes, DRESS and SJS/TEN days to weeks later. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **SJS/TEN**, drug-specific cytotoxic T cells and NK cells release **granulysin**, Fas ligand and perforin, causing keratinocyte apoptosis and full-thickness epidermal detachment. [BAD 2016]`,`**Pseudo-allergy**: vancomycin, fluoroquinolones and opioids activate the mast-cell receptor **MRGPRX2** directly - no prior sensitisation, rate-dependent, and not a contraindication to future use. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Sulfonamide antibiotics** carry an **N4 arylamine and an N1 heterocyclic ring** that generate reactive metabolites; non-antibiotic sulfonamides (furosemide, thiazides, sulfonylureas, celecoxib) lack these and do not meaningfully cross-react. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification: Gell and Coombs and by timing`,points:[`**Type I (IgE-mediated, immediate)**: onset usually **within 1 hour** (up to 6 hours) - urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, anaphylaxis. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Type II (cytotoxic antibody)**: drug-induced **haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia or neutropenia** - classically with high-dose penicillin, cephalosporins (ceftriaxone) and vancomycin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type III (immune complex)**: **serum sickness** 1-3 weeks after exposure - fever, rash, arthralgia; **cefaclor** and amoxicillin cause a serum-sickness-like reaction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type IV (T-cell, delayed)**: **IVa** contact dermatitis, **IVb DRESS**, **IVc SJS/TEN and maculopapular exanthem**, **IVd AGEP**; onset after **6 hours to weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`By timing, reactions are **immediate (within 1-6 hours)** or **delayed (after 6 hours, usually days)** - the single most useful history question. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SCARs)**: SJS, TEN, DRESS and AGEP - **never rechallenge or desensitise** after a SCAR. [BAD 2016]`,`**SJS** is epidermal detachment under **10% of body surface area**, **SJS-TEN overlap 10-30%** and **TEN over 30%**. [BAD 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: taking the allergy history`,points:[`Ask **which drug, which route, how long ago, how soon after the dose** the reaction began, and **what it looked like** (hives, swelling, breathing difficulty, blisters, mucosal ulcers, peeling). [BSACI 2022]`,`Ask **what treatment was needed** (adrenaline, admission, steroids) and whether the course was stopped; ask whether the patient has **since tolerated** the same or a related drug - many labelled patients already have. [BSACI 2022]`,`**Benign maculopapular rash** starting several days into a course, without mucosal involvement, fever or organ involvement, is the commonest 'allergy' and is low risk. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Anaphylaxis** history: rapid onset of skin or mucosal signs with **respiratory compromise, hypotension or gut symptoms** after exposure. [WAO 2020]`,`**SJS/TEN** prodrome: fever, sore eyes and throat for **1-3 days**, then painful dusky or atypical target lesions, **two or more mucosal sites** and skin detachment, **4-28 days** after starting the drug. [BAD 2016]`,`**DRESS** begins **2-8 weeks** after starting the drug with fever, extensive rash, **facial oedema**, lymphadenopathy, **eosinophilia** and organ involvement (liver commonest). [RegiSCAR 2007]`,`Record **intolerances** (nausea, diarrhoea, thrush, headache) separately - they are not allergies and should not appear on the allergy list. [NICE CG183 2014]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Urticaria** (raised, itchy, transient wheals, each lasting under 24 hours) and **angioedema** suggest an IgE-mediated or mast-cell reaction. [WAO 2020]`,`**Stridor, hoarseness, wheeze, hypotension or a falling SpO2** mean anaphylaxis - treat before examining further. [RCUK 2021]`,`**Nikolsky sign** (epidermis shears off with lateral pressure) and **haemorrhagic crusting of the lips** with conjunctivitis point to SJS/TEN. [BAD 2016]`,`**Facial oedema, generalised lymphadenopathy and hepatomegaly** with a morbilliform rash point to DRESS. [RegiSCAR 2007]`,`**Numerous small sterile pustules on erythema**, starting in flexures within 1-2 days, suggest AGEP - often beta-lactams or macrolides. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flushing and itching of the face, neck and upper trunk** during a rapid vancomycin infusion is vancomycin infusion reaction, not allergy. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Estimate body surface area detached** (palm of the patient is about 1%) to grade SJS versus TEN and calculate SCORTEN. [BAD 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Viral exanthem** (EBV, enteroviruses, dengue) during an antibiotic course - the commonest cause of a false penicillin label, especially in children. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Vancomycin infusion reaction (red man syndrome)** - rate-related flushing; slow the infusion to **over 1-2 hours** and pre-treat with an antihistamine rather than label allergy. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** - fever, rigors and myalgia within hours of treating syphilis, leptospirosis or relapsing fever; it is not penicillin allergy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome** - superficial (subcorneal) split, no mucosal involvement, mainly in children; separate from TEN on biopsy. [BAD 2016]`,`**Erythema multiforme** - typical target lesions acrally, usually after HSV or Mycoplasma, limited mucosal disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hoigne syndrome** - acute anxiety, hallucinations and paraesthesia after intramuscular procaine penicillin, from procaine microemboli, not allergy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vasovagal syncope** after an injection - bradycardia, pallor and sweating without urticaria or wheeze. [WAO 2020]`]},{heading:`Investigations: risk scores, tests and cut-offs`,points:[`**PEN-FAST** (for a reported penicillin allergy): **Five years or less** since the reaction = 2; **Anaphylaxis/angioedema or Severe cutaneous reaction** = 2; **Treatment required** = 1. [JAMA IM 2020]`,`**PEN-FAST under 3 is low risk** (positive allergy test under 5%) and **0 is very low risk (under 1%)**; 3 is moderate and **4-5 high risk**. [JAMA IM 2020]`,`**Direct oral challenge**: a single treatment dose of **amoxicillin (250-500 mg)** with observation for **30-60 minutes**, in a setting able to treat anaphylaxis - the gold standard to exclude allergy. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Skin testing** (prick then intradermal with penicilloyl-polylysine, benzylpenicillin and amoxicillin) has a **negative predictive value over 95%**, but standardised reagents are not routinely available in India. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Serum mast-cell tryptase** taken **30 minutes to 2 hours** after onset, with a baseline at **24 hours or later**, supports anaphylaxis if it exceeds **(1.2 x baseline) + 2 ng/mL**. [WAO 2020]`,`**Specific IgE (ImmunoCAP)** to penicilloyl or amoxicilloyl has **low sensitivity** and cannot exclude allergy; it does not replace challenge. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Skin biopsy** confirms SJS/TEN (full-thickness necrosis) and separates it from SSSS; DRESS is scored with the **RegiSCAR** criteria (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytes, organ involvement). [BAD 2016]`,`**SCORTEN** on day 1 for SJS/TEN: age over 40, malignancy, heart rate over 120, detachment over 10%, urea over 10 mmol/L, glucose over 14 mmol/L, bicarbonate under 20 mmol/L - mortality rises steeply with each point. [BAD 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: delabelling and choosing antibiotics`,points:[`**Low-risk history** (PEN-FAST under 3, benign rash, remote or vague reaction, intolerance only): offer a **direct oral amoxicillin challenge** - no skin test needed. [PALACE 2023]`,`**Pure intolerance or family history only**: remove the label after explaining, without any testing. [BSACI 2022]`,`**Moderate or high risk** (recent anaphylaxis, recent urticaria needing treatment): refer for **skin testing then challenge** by an allergist; meanwhile use a non-cross-reacting alternative. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Cephalosporins in penicillin-allergic patients**: with a non-severe, non-IgE history, **any cephalosporin** can be given; with a history of anaphylaxis, choose one with a **dissimilar R1 side chain** (e.g. cefazolin, ceftriaxone, cefuroxime after amoxicillin allergy). [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Carbapenems** can be given to penicillin-allergic patients **without testing** - cross-reactivity is under 1%. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Aztreonam** is safe in penicillin allergy but should be avoided in **ceftazidime allergy** (identical side chain). [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Two-step graded challenge (test dose)**: **10% of the dose, then the remaining 90%** after 30-60 minutes - used when the history is uncertain or moderate risk; it is not desensitisation and does not induce tolerance. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`The common Indian practice of an **intradermal 'sensitivity test' with the full drug** before injectable antibiotics is **not validated** - a negative result does not exclude anaphylaxis, and a positive non-standardised test produces false labels. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`After a negative challenge, **update the record, tell the patient in writing** and remove the label from all systems - otherwise the label returns. [BSACI 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in emergency and IPD: anaphylaxis, desensitisation and SCARs`,points:[`**Anaphylaxis**: **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM** (0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL, i.e. 1:1000) into the **anterolateral thigh** for adults (**0.01 mg/kg** in children, 0.15-0.3 mg), **repeat every 5 minutes** if no improvement. [RCUK 2021]`,`Lie the patient flat with legs raised (or sitting if breathless), give **high-flow oxygen** and an **IV crystalloid bolus of 500-1000 mL (20 mL/kg in children)** for hypotension. [RCUK 2021]`,`**Refractory anaphylaxis** (no response after two IM doses): start a **low-dose IV adrenaline infusion** under expert supervision; **glucagon 1-2 mg IV** for patients on beta-blockers. [RCUK 2021]`,`**Antihistamines and steroids are second-line** - they relieve skin symptoms but do not treat airway or circulatory failure, and must never delay adrenaline. [RCUK 2021]`,`Observe after anaphylaxis for at least **6-12 hours** (longer if severe or a biphasic risk), send **tryptase**, and discharge with an **allergy alert, written plan and allergist referral**. [RCUK 2021]`,`**Desensitisation** induces **temporary tolerance** in confirmed IgE allergy when the drug is essential (e.g. **benzathine penicillin for syphilis in pregnancy**, neurosyphilis): 12-16 escalating doses over 4-6 hours in a monitored bed; tolerance is lost if doses stop for over **24-48 hours**. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**SJS/TEN**: stop the culprit drug at once, admit to a **burns unit or ICU**, give fluids, wound care, eye care (ophthalmology within 24 hours), analgesia and nutrition; **ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day** for about 7 days is the most-used immunomodulator, with IVIG or etanercept as alternatives. [BAD 2016]`,`**DRESS**: stop the drug; **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day** tapered slowly over **6-12 weeks** for organ involvement; monitor liver, kidney, thyroid and HHV-6 reactivation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: penicillin-allergy evaluation (including direct oral challenge in low-risk women) is safe and enables **GBS prophylaxis and syphilis treatment** with penicillin; desensitise for syphilis if allergy is confirmed. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Benzathine penicillin** for syphilis and rheumatic fever prophylaxis should **not be withheld** for fear of anaphylaxis, which is very rare; give it where adrenaline and resuscitation are available. [WHO 2017]`,`**Children**: most rashes during amoxicillin are viral - a **direct oral challenge** without skin testing is recommended after a benign cutaneous reaction. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**HIV**: cotrimoxazole prophylaxis can often be continued through a mild rash or reintroduced by **graded desensitisation** - never after SJS/TEN or DRESS; dapsone is an alternative if G6PD is normal. [NACO 2021]`,`**Older adults** on beta-blockers have more severe, adrenaline-resistant anaphylaxis; keep glucagon available. [WAO 2020]`,`**Surgery**: penicillin-labelled patients should still receive **cefazolin** for surgical prophylaxis - cross-reactivity is negligible and alternatives such as vancomycin and clindamycin increase surgical-site infection. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: a careful history, PEN-FAST and an observed oral challenge need no special reagents - only adrenaline and a monitored chair. [BSACI 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Anaphylaxis** can kill within minutes from airway oedema or circulatory collapse; **biphasic reactions** occur in up to about 5% within 72 hours, usually within 12 hours. [WAO 2020]`,`**TEN mortality is about 25-35%** and SJS about 1-5%; SCORTEN of 5 or more predicts mortality over 90%. [BAD 2016]`,`**Long-term SJS/TEN sequelae**: dry eye, symblepharon, corneal scarring and blindness, nail loss, strictures (oesophageal, urethral, vaginal) and pigmentary change. [BAD 2016]`,`**DRESS** carries a mortality of up to about 10% (fulminant hepatitis, myocarditis) and can be followed months later by **autoimmune thyroiditis and type 1 diabetes**. [Harrison 22e]`,`An **inaccurate penicillin label** itself worsens outcomes: more MRSA, C. difficile, surgical-site infection, treatment failure and cost. [BMJ 2018]`,`**Cross-reactive drug exposure** after a SCAR (e.g. another aromatic anticonvulsant or another sulfonamide antibiotic) can trigger recurrence. [BAD 2016]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to an allergist**: anaphylaxis to an antibiotic, high-risk PEN-FAST (4-5), multiple drug allergies, a needed drug with no alternative, or a positive challenge. [BSACI 2022]`,`**Refer urgently to dermatology/burns**: mucosal ulceration, blisters, skin pain or detachment, or fever with a spreading rash and eosinophilia. [BAD 2016]`,`After delabelling, **review the electronic and paper records** at the next contact to confirm the label has been removed. [BSACI 2022]`,`After SJS/TEN or DRESS, give an **allergy card or alert bracelet** naming the drug and its cross-reactive relatives, and screen first-degree relatives for HLA risk where a test exists (e.g. HLA-B*15:02 before carbamazepine). [BAD 2016]`,`Follow **SJS/TEN survivors** with ophthalmology for at least a year and treat dry eye early. [BAD 2016]`,`Report every serious reaction to **PvPI** (ADR form, helpline 1800-180-3024 or the ADR PvPI app). [PvPI 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`Record allergies **precisely**: drug, date, reaction type, severity and treatment - 'allergic to antibiotics' is useless and harmful. [NICE CG183 2014]`,`**Do not label a viral rash** as allergy; tell parents that rash with amoxicillin in a febrile child is usually the infection. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**HLA-B*15:02 screening before carbamazepine** in people of South-East Asian (and some Indian) ancestry and **HLA-B*58:01** before allopurinol in high-risk groups prevent SJS/TEN. [CPIC 2023]`,`Avoid **unnecessary cotrimoxazole and nevirapine** exposure; start allopurinol at **100 mg/day** (lower in CKD) to reduce SCAR risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`Every facility giving injections, including **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** sub-centres, should keep **adrenaline 1 mg/mL ampoules** and an anaphylaxis kit. [MoHFW 2019]`,`Teach patients with a true allergy the names of **related drugs to avoid** and give an **adrenaline auto-injector** plan where anaphylaxis was severe and unavoidable exposure is possible. [WAO 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**AAAAI/ACAAI Drug Allergy Practice Parameter (2022)**: direct oral challenge without skin testing for low-risk penicillin histories; any cephalosporin without testing for non-anaphylactic penicillin allergy; carbapenems without testing. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`**BSACI guideline on penicillin allergy (2022)** gives a non-allergist pathway for delabelling with direct oral challenge in low-risk adults. [BSACI 2022]`,`**PALACE trial (2023)**: in adults with PEN-FAST under 3, **direct oral challenge was non-inferior to skin testing followed by challenge**, with no serious reactions. [PALACE 2023]`,`**Anaphylaxis practice parameter update (AAAAI/ACAAI 2023)** and **RCUK 2021**: IM adrenaline first, repeat at 5 minutes, early adrenaline infusion for refractory cases; antihistamines and steroids are not first-line. [RCUK 2021]`,`**WAO anaphylaxis guidance (2020)** broadened criteria to include isolated respiratory or circulatory compromise after a known allergen exposure. [WAO 2020]`,`**CPIC guidelines (2023 update)** reaffirm HLA-B*15:02 testing before carbamazepine and HLA-B*58:01 before allopurinol in high-risk populations. [CPIC 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Penicillin-allergic patient needs a cephalosporin - what do you do?'** - take the history, look at the side chain; non-severe reaction any cephalosporin, anaphylaxis a dissimilar side chain (cefazolin). [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`Classic viva: **'Name the PEN-FAST criteria'** - Five years, Anaphylaxis/Angioedema, Severe skin reaction, Treatment required; under 3 is low risk. [JAMA IM 2020]`,`Classic viva: **'Which drug cross-reacts with aztreonam?'** - ceftazidime (shared side chain), not penicillin. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`Common mistake: quoting the **old 10% penicillin-cephalosporin cross-reactivity** figure, which came from early cephalosporins contaminated with penicillin; the true figure is about 1-2%. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`Common mistake: avoiding **furosemide or thiazides** in a patient with cotrimoxazole allergy - non-antibiotic sulfonamides do not cross-react. [AAAAI/ACAAI 2022]`,`Common mistake: giving **hydrocortisone and chlorphenamine first** in anaphylaxis - IM adrenaline comes first. [RCUK 2021]`,`Common mistake: **desensitising or rechallenging after SJS/TEN** - absolutely contraindicated. [BAD 2016]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in antibiotic allergy and anaphylaxis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Adrenaline (1 mg/mL, 1:1000)`,`0.5 mg IM anterolateral thigh (child 0.01 mg/kg; 0.15 mg under 6 y, 0.3 mg 6-12 y)`,`Repeat every 5 min; IV infusion if refractory after 2 doses`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia, hypertension; no absolute contraindication in anaphylaxis`],[`Crystalloid (normal saline or Ringer lactate)`,`500-1000 mL IV bolus (child 20 mL/kg)`,`Repeat to restore BP`,`Fluid overload in heart failure`],[`Glucagon`,`1-2 mg IV over 5 min`,`Repeat or infusion 5-15 microgram/min`,`For beta-blocked patients; vomiting`],[`Salbutamol nebulised`,`5 mg nebulised`,`Repeat for persistent wheeze after adrenaline`,`Tremor, hypokalaemia`],[`Cetirizine / chlorphenamine`,`Cetirizine 10 mg oral or chlorphenamine 10 mg IV/IM`,`For skin symptoms after stabilisation`,`Sedation (chlorphenamine); not life-saving`],[`Amoxicillin (direct oral challenge)`,`250-500 mg oral single dose (or 10% then 90%)`,`Observe 30-60 min; some add a short course for delayed reactions`,`Only in low-risk histories; never after SCAR`],[`Ciclosporin (SJS/TEN)`,`3-5 mg/kg/day oral in divided doses`,`About 7 days, then taper`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension; check renal function`],[`Prednisolone (DRESS)`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day oral`,`Slow taper over 6-12 weeks`,`Flare on fast taper; hyperglycaemia, infection`]]},{heading:`Gell and Coombs classification of antibiotic reactions`,columns:[`Type`,`Mechanism`,`Onset`,`Clinical picture`,`Typical antibiotics`],rows:[[`I`,`IgE on mast cells`,`Minutes to 1 h (up to 6 h)`,`Urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, anaphylaxis`,`Penicillins, cephalosporins`],[`II`,`IgG/IgM cytotoxic`,`Days`,`Haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, neutropenia`,`Ceftriaxone, high-dose penicillin, vancomycin`],[`III`,`Immune complex`,`1-3 weeks`,`Serum sickness: fever, rash, arthralgia`,`Cefaclor, amoxicillin`],[`IVa-b`,`Th1 / Th2 with eosinophils`,`2-8 weeks`,`Contact dermatitis; DRESS`,`Vancomycin, sulfonamides, minocycline`],[`IVc`,`Cytotoxic T cells`,`4-28 days`,`Maculopapular rash; SJS/TEN`,`Cotrimoxazole, beta-lactams, fluoroquinolones`],[`IVd`,`Neutrophil (IL-8)`,`1-2 days`,`AGEP - sterile pustules`,`Beta-lactams, macrolides`]]},{heading:`PEN-FAST score and action`,columns:[`Item`,`Points`,`Action by total`],rows:[[`Five years or less since reaction`,`2`,`Total 0: very low risk (under 1%) - direct oral challenge or delabel`],[`Anaphylaxis or angioedema`,`2 (A or S, not both)`,`Total 1-2: low risk (under 5%) - direct oral challenge`],[`Severe cutaneous adverse reaction (SJS/TEN, DRESS)`,`2 (A or S, not both)`,`Total 3: moderate risk - skin test then challenge`],[`Treatment required for reaction`,`1`,`Total 4-5: high risk - allergist; avoid until tested; SCAR means never rechallenge`]]},{heading:`Beta-lactam side-chain cross-reactivity groups`,columns:[`Index drug`,`Shares R1 side chain with (avoid if anaphylaxis)`,`Safer alternatives`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin / ampicillin`,`Cephalexin, cefadroxil, cefaclor, cefprozil`,`Cefazolin, ceftriaxone, cefuroxime, carbapenem`],[`Benzylpenicillin`,`Cefoxitin, cephalothin (similar)`,`Cefazolin, ceftriaxone, carbapenem`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`Cefotaxime, cefepime, cefpodoxime`,`Cefazolin, ceftazidime, penicillins (test if severe)`],[`Ceftazidime`,`Aztreonam, cefiderocol`,`Ceftriaxone, carbapenem`],[`Cefazolin`,`None (unique side chains)`,`Most beta-lactams`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a reaction during antibiotic therapy`,columns:[`Reaction`,`Timing`,`Distinguishing features`,`Future use of drug`],rows:[[`IgE anaphylaxis`,`Minutes-1 h`,`Urticaria, wheeze, hypotension; raised tryptase`,`Avoid; allergist; desensitise only if essential`],[`Vancomycin infusion reaction`,`During rapid infusion`,`Flushing of face and upper trunk, no wheeze`,`Continue with slower infusion and antihistamine`],[`Benign maculopapular rash`,`Day 5-10`,`No mucosa, no fever, no organ involvement`,`Direct oral challenge later; often delabelled`],[`SJS/TEN`,`4-28 days`,`Mucosal ulcers, Nikolsky positive, detachment`,`Never again, nor close relatives`],[`DRESS`,`2-8 weeks`,`Facial oedema, eosinophilia, hepatitis, nodes`,`Never again`],[`Viral exanthem (EBV)`,`During infection`,`Pharyngitis, nodes, atypical lymphocytes`,`Drug safe; do not label`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor, wheeze, hypotension or collapse after an antibiotic dose - anaphylaxis: IM adrenaline now`,`Mucosal ulceration of two or more sites with painful skin or blisters - suspect SJS/TEN, stop drug, admit`,`Positive Nikolsky sign or skin detachment over 10% BSA - burns unit or ICU`,`Fever, facial oedema, spreading rash, eosinophilia and raised liver enzymes 2-8 weeks into a drug - DRESS`,`Anaphylaxis not responding to two doses of IM adrenaline - refractory, needs IV adrenaline infusion`,`Pregnant woman with syphilis and confirmed penicillin allergy - refer for desensitisation, not an alternative drug`],pearls:[`**Over 90% of penicillin labels are wrong** - and the label itself causes harm (MRSA, C. difficile).`,`**PEN-FAST under 3 means direct oral amoxicillin challenge** (PALACE 2023).`,`**Side chain, not ring**: amoxicillin cross-reacts with cephalexin, not cefazolin.`,`**Aztreonam pairs with ceftazidime**, not with penicillin.`,`**Carbapenems can be given in penicillin allergy without testing.**`,`**Sulfonamide antibiotic allergy does not forbid furosemide, thiazides or sulfonylureas.**`,`**Adrenaline IM 0.5 mg in the thigh, repeat in 5 minutes** - steroids and antihistamines are not first-line.`,`**Never rechallenge after SJS/TEN or DRESS.**`,`**Red man syndrome is a rate problem, not an allergy** - slow the vancomycin.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman needs treatment for streptococcal cellulitis. She reports a non-itchy rash on her trunk while taking amoxicillin 12 years ago; it resolved without treatment and there was no swelling or breathing difficulty. What is the best approach?`,options:[`Avoid all beta-lactams for life and give clindamycin`,`Refer for penicillin skin testing before any beta-lactam`,`Give an intradermal test dose of the full drug and proceed if negative`,`Offer a direct oral amoxicillin challenge and remove the label if tolerated`],answer:3,explanation:`Her PEN-FAST score is 0 (over 5 years, no anaphylaxis or severe skin reaction, no treatment), so the risk of true allergy is under 1% and a direct oral amoxicillin challenge without skin testing is recommended (PALACE 2023). Lifelong avoidance with clindamycin is worse care, skin testing is unnecessary at this risk level, and an intradermal 'test dose' of the full drug is not validated.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-q2`,stem:`A man had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin two years ago. He now needs a cephalosporin. Which cephalosporin is most likely to cross-react?`,options:[`Cephalexin`,`Cefazolin`,`Ceftriaxone`,`Cefuroxime`],answer:0,explanation:`Cephalexin (like cefadroxil and cefaclor) shares an identical R1 side chain with amoxicillin and ampicillin, so it carries the highest cross-reactivity risk. Cefazolin has unique side chains, and ceftriaxone and cefuroxime have dissimilar R1 groups, so they are the preferred choices after amoxicillin anaphylaxis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-q3`,stem:`A patient with a documented severe allergy to ceftazidime needs Gram-negative cover. Which drug should be avoided because of side-chain cross-reactivity?`,options:[`Meropenem`,`Aztreonam`,`Amikacin`,`Piperacillin-tazobactam`],answer:1,explanation:`Aztreonam does not cross-react with penicillins, but it shares an identical side chain with ceftazidime, so it should be avoided in ceftazidime allergy. Meropenem cross-reacts with other beta-lactams in under 1%, amikacin is not a beta-lactam, and piperacillin-tazobactam does not share ceftazidime's side chain.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old man living with HIV developed Stevens-Johnson syndrome on cotrimoxazole. He now has heart failure. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`He should be desensitised to cotrimoxazole because prophylaxis is essential`,`All sulfonamide-containing drugs, including furosemide, are contraindicated`,`Furosemide may be used, but cotrimoxazole must never be given again`,`A graded oral rechallenge with cotrimoxazole is safe after 6 months`],answer:2,explanation:`Non-antibiotic sulfonamides such as furosemide lack the N4 arylamine and N1 ring responsible for antibiotic sulfonamide reactions and do not meaningfully cross-react, so furosemide can be used. After SJS/TEN the culprit must never be rechallenged or desensitised, at any interval; desensitisation applies only to mild, non-severe cotrimoxazole reactions.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-q5`,stem:`Minutes after an IV ceftriaxone dose, a 60-year-old woman develops generalised urticaria, wheeze and a blood pressure of 78/40 mmHg. What is the first drug to give?`,options:[`Hydrocortisone 200 mg IV`,`Chlorphenamine 10 mg IV`,`Adrenaline 1 mg IV bolus`,`Adrenaline 0.5 mg IM into the anterolateral thigh`],answer:3,explanation:`This is anaphylaxis and the first treatment is IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL) into the anterolateral thigh, repeated after 5 minutes if needed, with fluids and oxygen. Hydrocortisone and chlorphenamine are second-line and do not reverse airway or circulatory failure, and a 1 mg IV adrenaline bolus is the cardiac-arrest dose that can cause fatal arrhythmias in a patient with a pulse.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the classification and evaluation of penicillin allergy. Describe how you would delabel a patient and choose antibiotics for a patient labelled penicillin-allergic, including cross-reactivity with cephalosporins.`,openingLines:[`Penicillin allergy is reported by about 10% of patients, but over 90% are not allergic on testing, and an incorrect label leads to broader, more toxic antibiotics with more MRSA and C. difficile.`,`Evaluation rests on a structured history, the PEN-FAST score and an observed oral challenge; cross-reactivity with cephalosporins is about 1-2% and is side-chain dependent.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Gell and Coombs types I-IV; immediate (within 1-6 h) versus delayed (after 6 h).`,`Severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN, DRESS, AGEP) - never rechallenge.`,`Intolerance and pseudo-allergy (vancomycin infusion reaction) are not allergy.`]},{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Hapten formation: penicilloyl major determinant (95%) and minor determinants.`,`IgE cross-linking on mast cells releases histamine and tryptase.`,`Cross-reactivity follows the R1 side chain.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History: drug, timing, features, treatment, later tolerance.`,`PEN-FAST: 5 years (2), anaphylaxis/angioedema or SCAR (2), treatment (1); under 3 low risk.`,`Skin tests (NPV over 95%); tryptase after anaphylaxis; specific IgE has low sensitivity.`]},{heading:`Delabelling pathway`,points:[`Intolerance only: delabel by history.`,`Low risk: direct oral amoxicillin 250-500 mg, observe 30-60 min (PALACE 2023).`,`Moderate or high risk: skin test then challenge; SCAR: avoid for life.`,`Update records and inform the patient in writing.`]},{heading:`Choosing antibiotics`,points:[`Non-severe history: any cephalosporin; anaphylaxis: dissimilar side chain (cefazolin).`,`Carbapenems without testing; aztreonam safe except ceftazidime allergy.`,`Graded challenge for uncertain history; desensitisation when penicillin is essential (syphilis in pregnancy).`]},{heading:`Anaphylaxis treatment`,points:[`Adrenaline 0.5 mg IM thigh, repeat every 5 min; oxygen, fluids; IV infusion if refractory.`,`Antihistamines and steroids second-line; observe and refer.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: penicillin label to history to PEN-FAST to delabel / challenge / skin test / avoid`,`Table: side-chain cross-reactivity groups`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation and PEN-FAST`,marks:3},{part:`Delabelling and choosing antibiotics`,marks:3},{part:`Anaphylaxis management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`penicillin allergy`,`PEN-FAST`,`delabelling`,`side chain`,`anaphylaxis`,`desensitisation`]},{id:`infectious-fever-antibiotic-allergy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis from DRESS syndrome.`,openingLines:[`SJS/TEN and DRESS are severe cutaneous adverse reactions (type IV, T-cell mediated); both demand immediate withdrawal of the culprit and lifelong avoidance.`,`They differ in latency, skin and mucosal findings, systemic involvement and treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`SJS/TEN`,points:[`Latency 4-28 days; cotrimoxazole, anticonvulsants, allopurinol, nevirapine.`,`Atypical targets, two or more mucosal sites, Nikolsky positive; SJS under 10%, TEN over 30% BSA.`,`Cytotoxic T cells and granulysin; mortality TEN 25-35%; SCORTEN.`,`Burns unit, supportive care, ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day.`]},{heading:`DRESS`,points:[`Latency 2-8 weeks; anticonvulsants, allopurinol, vancomycin, sulfonamides.`,`Morbilliform rash, facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia; mucosa mild or absent.`,`Organ involvement (liver, kidney, heart); RegiSCAR score; HHV-6 reactivation.`,`Prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day tapered over 6-12 weeks; later autoimmune thyroiditis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: latency, skin, mucosa, organs, blood, treatment, mortality`],markSplit:[{part:`SJS/TEN features`,marks:2},{part:`DRESS features`,marks:2},{part:`Common management principles`,marks:1}],keywords:[`SJS`,`TEN`,`DRESS`,`SCORTEN`,`RegiSCAR`,`SCAR`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Drug allergy and cutaneous drug reactions`,`Khan DA et al. Drug allergy: a 2022 practice parameter update (AAAAI/ACAAI). J Allergy Clin Immunol 2022`,`Savic L et al. BSACI guideline for the set-up of penicillin allergy delabelling services by non-allergists, 2022`,`Trubiano JA et al. PEN-FAST clinical decision rule. JAMA Intern Med 2020; Copaescu AM et al. PALACE randomized trial. JAMA Intern Med 2023`,`Resuscitation Council UK. Emergency treatment of anaphylaxis, 2021; World Allergy Organization Anaphylaxis Guidance 2020`,`Creamer D et al. UK guidelines for the management of Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis in adults (BAD), 2016`,`Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI), Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission, 2023`]}),l.push({id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral`,title:`IV to oral switch, OPAT and duration of antibiotic therapy`,oneLiner:`Most infections need **intravenous therapy only until the patient is clinically stable, afebrile for 24-48 hours and can absorb oral drugs**, after which a **highly bioavailable oral agent** is as effective (OVIVA, POET); **shorter fixed courses** (CAP 5 days, cellulitis 5-6 days, uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia 7 days, intra-abdominal infection 4 days after source control) are as good as long ones and cause less harm, and patients who still need parenteral drugs can receive them at home through **OPAT**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`IV to oral switch`,`IVOS`,`step-down therapy`,`sequential therapy`,`oral bioavailability`,`OPAT`,`outpatient parenteral antimicrobial therapy`,`home IV antibiotics`,`PICC`,`elastomeric pump`,`antibiotic duration`,`shorter is better`,`OVIVA`,`POET`,`BALANCE trial`,`Start Smart then Focus`,`antibiotic time-out`,`ceftriaxone once daily`,`ertapenem`,`dalbavancin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**IV-to-oral switch (IVOS)** is the change from parenteral to enteral antimicrobial therapy once defined clinical criteria are met - as **sequential** (same drug orally), **switch** (equivalent drug of another class) or **step-down** (narrower or lower-potency oral drug). [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Outpatient parenteral antimicrobial therapy (OPAT)** is the administration of IV (or IM) antimicrobials on at least two days **without an overnight hospital stay** - in a day-care unit, by a visiting nurse, or by the trained patient or carer at home. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`**Antibiotic duration** is now chosen as the **shortest course proven effective**; randomised trials across pneumonia, UTI, cellulitis, intra-abdominal infection and bacteraemia show short courses are non-inferior. [IDSA 2024]`,`In hospital point-prevalence surveys, **a large share of IV antibiotic days are unnecessary** because patients already met switch criteria; India's NCDC point-prevalence surveys found over half of inpatients on antibiotics, mostly parenteral. [NCDC 2021]`,`Each **extra day of antibiotic therapy** beyond what is needed raises the odds of an adverse event by about **5%** (rash, diarrhoea, C. difficile, organ toxicity). [Ann Intern Med 2019]`,`IV therapy carries its own harms: **phlebitis, cannula and PICC-related bloodstream infection, thrombosis, immobility and longer stay** - and costs far more in nurse time and consumables. [UKHSA 2023]`,`The **WHO AWaRe antibiotic book (2022)** and **ICMR treatment guidelines (2022)** both promote short courses and early oral therapy as core stewardship actions. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`]},{heading:`Why patients stay on IV too long: aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Habit and fear**: the belief that IV is 'stronger', a fixed '7 days IV' order, and lack of a **daily review prompt** keep patients on drips after they are well. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Genuine barriers to oral therapy**: vomiting, ileus, dysphagia, nil-by-mouth before surgery, short bowel, severe malabsorption and **shock with gut hypoperfusion**. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Infections that need high sustained concentrations** - meningitis, brain abscess, endophthalmitis, early S. aureus bacteraemia, febrile neutropenia, undrained abscess or empyema - require IV therapy at least initially. [IDSA 2024]`,`**No oral option** for the organism - e.g. MRSA without linezolid or cotrimoxazole susceptibility, carbapenem-resistant Gram-negatives, Pseudomonas resistant to fluoroquinolones. [IDSA 2024]`,`**Patient factors that favour OPAT over oral**: poor adherence risk, need for drugs only available IV (ceftriaxone, ertapenem, aminoglycosides, teicoplanin, daptomycin) and long courses (osteomyelitis, endocarditis). [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`**Financial incentives** in fee-for-service settings can reward IV therapy and longer stays; PM-JAY package rates do not by themselves drive IVOS. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: pharmacokinetics of oral therapy and the logic of short courses`,points:[`**Oral bioavailability (F)** is the fraction of an oral dose that reaches the systemic circulation unchanged; it depends on **dissolution, gut absorption (transporters, pH), first-pass hepatic metabolism and chelation**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Drugs with **F over 90%** (levofloxacin, linezolid, metronidazole, doxycycline, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole, voriconazole) give the **same serum levels orally as IV** - the IV route adds nothing once the gut works. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Oral beta-lactams** reach lower peak levels; because they are time-dependent (fT>MIC), they need **higher and more frequent doses** (amoxicillin 1 g three times daily) to match IV efficacy. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Divalent and trivalent cations** (calcium, iron, magnesium, zinc, aluminium antacids, enteral feeds) **chelate fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines** and can cut absorption by more than half. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Gastric acid** is needed for some drugs - PPIs reduce absorption of cefpodoxime, cefuroxime axetil, itraconazole capsules and posaconazole suspension. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`In **shock**, splanchnic vasoconstriction makes gut absorption unpredictable - so IV therapy is mandatory until perfusion is restored. [SSC 2021]`,`**Why shorter works**: bacterial density falls by several logs in the first days of effective therapy, and **host defences clear the residual** inoculum once the source is controlled. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why longer harms**: every day of exposure selects resistant organisms in the **gut microbiome** (ESBL, VRE, Candida) and raises toxicity, so excess duration damages both patient and community. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification: switch types, OPAT models and duration categories`,points:[`**Sequential therapy**: same drug IV then oral (e.g. levofloxacin IV to levofloxacin oral; metronidazole IV to oral). [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Switch therapy**: IV agent replaced by a different oral drug of equivalent spectrum (e.g. ceftriaxone IV to cefixime or amoxicillin-clavulanate oral). [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Step-down therapy**: oral drug of narrower spectrum guided by culture (e.g. piperacillin-tazobactam IV to amoxicillin oral for a susceptible E. coli). [IDSA 2024]`,`**OPAT models**: (1) **infusion centre / day-care** visits, (2) **visiting-nurse home therapy**, (3) **self- or carer-administered (S-OPAT)** after training, and (4) **continuous infusion via elastomeric pump**. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`,`**Vascular access by duration**: peripheral cannula for **5 days or fewer**, **midline for 6-14 days**, and **PICC or tunnelled line for 15 days or more**. [MAGIC 2015]`,`**Duration categories**: short fixed (3-7 days for most community infections), intermediate (10-14 days, e.g. uncomplicated S. aureus bacteraemia), and long (4-12 weeks for endocarditis, osteomyelitis, prosthetic joint infection, TB). [IDSA 2024]`,`**'Day 1'** is the first day of **effective** therapy - or, for S. aureus and Candida bloodstream infection, the **first day of negative blood cultures**. [IDSA 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment: the 48-72 hour review`,points:[`Review every IV antibiotic at **48-72 hours** ('antibiotic time-out'): **stop, switch to oral, change, continue IV, or start OPAT** - and document the decision and a **stop date**. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Ask: is the **diagnosis still infection**? Is the patient **clinically improving**? Is there a **culture result** that allows narrowing? Can the gut absorb? [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Swallowing and gut**: no vomiting, no ileus, tolerating fluids or diet, no severe diarrhoea, and no NG-feed interaction problem. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Check **specific indications for prolonged IV** (meningitis, endocarditis early phase, S. aureus bacteraemia, undrained abscess, febrile neutropenia, necrotising fasciitis) - if present, do not switch yet. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Ask about **adherence, home situation, phone access and distance** - they decide between oral therapy, OPAT and continued admission. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`Record **allergies and interactions** before choosing the oral agent (e.g. linezolid with SSRIs, clarithromycin with statins, ciprofloxacin with theophylline or tizanidine). [BNF 2025]`,`Plan the **total duration** at the first review, counting IV and oral days together. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`]},{heading:`Bedside markers of stability (switch criteria)`,points:[`**Temperature 36-38 C for at least 24 hours** (48 hours in some protocols) is the core criterion. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Haemodynamic stability**: heart rate **under 90-100/min**, systolic BP **90 mmHg or more** without vasopressors, and an **improving early-warning score (NEWS2)**. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Respiratory stability**: respiratory rate **24/min or less** and SpO2 **90% or more** on room air or stable oxygen (Halm criteria for CAP). [IDSA/ATS 2019]`,`**Normal mentation** and ability to **eat and drink** - also Halm stability criteria. [IDSA/ATS 2019]`,`**Falling inflammatory markers**: CRP or white cell count improving (CRP falling is supportive, not mandatory). [UKHSA 2023]`,`A **COMS** mnemonic summarises it: **C**linical improvement, **O**ral route available, **M**arkers improving, **S**pecific IV-only indication absent. [Start Smart Then Focus 2023]`,`Patients meeting criteria **within 3 days** in CAP can switch immediately; waiting longer adds no benefit. [IDSA/ATS 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of failure to improve`,points:[`**Undrained source**: empyema, abscess, obstructed urinary tract, infected collection - needs drainage, not more antibiotics. [IDSA 2024]`,`**Wrong diagnosis**: non-infective fever (drug fever, VTE, vasculitis, malignancy, gout) or a different infection (TB, scrub typhus, fungal). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resistant or uncovered organism**: ESBL or MRSA not covered, atypicals in CAP, rickettsiae with a beta-lactam. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Inadequate drug exposure**: wrong dose, poor oral absorption (vomiting, chelation, PPI), poor penetration (CNS, bone, abscess), or under-dosing in obesity or augmented renal clearance. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**New hospital complication**: cannula or PICC infection, C. difficile, hospital-acquired pneumonia or UTI. [IDSA 2024]`,`**Endovascular infection** (endocarditis, infected aneurysm) when bacteraemia persists despite therapy. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations that guide switch and duration`,points:[`**Culture and susceptibility** make step-down possible - always send cultures before antibiotics so that a narrow oral drug can be chosen later. [IDSA/ASM 2024]`,`**CRP trend**: a fall of about 50% by day 3-4 supports response; a rising CRP after day 3 prompts a search for an undrained focus. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Procalcitonin** serial values can shorten duration - stop when PCT falls **80% from peak or below 0.5 ng/mL**. [ADAPT-Sepsis 2025]`,`**Repeat blood cultures** at 48-72 hours in S. aureus and Candida bacteraemia; switch or OPAT only after documented clearance. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Echocardiography** in S. aureus bacteraemia before deciding a 14-day versus 4-6-week course. [ESC 2023]`,`**Imaging** (ultrasound, CT, MRI) to exclude collections when fever persists beyond 72 hours, and MRI for vertebral osteomyelitis before committing to a long course. [IDSA 2015]`,`**Baseline and weekly bloods during OPAT**: CBC, creatinine, LFT, CRP, plus **CK weekly for daptomycin** and **drug levels for vancomycin and aminoglycosides**. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and on the ward: oral step-down choices`,points:[`**CAP**: amoxicillin **1 g three times daily** (plus doxycycline 100 mg twice daily or azithromycin if atypical cover needed) for a **total of 5 days** once stable; levofloxacin **750 mg once daily** where a fluoroquinolone is justified and TB excluded. [IDSA/ATS 2019]`,`**Pyelonephritis / complicated UTI**: oral **ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days** or **cotrimoxazole 960 mg twice daily for 7-14 days** if susceptible; oral beta-lactams (cefixime, amoxicillin-clavulanate) need **10-14 days**. [IDSA 2024]`,`**Cellulitis**: oral **cephalexin 500 mg four times daily** or **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily** to complete **5-6 days**, extended only if not improving. [IDSA 2014]`,`**Intra-abdominal infection** with adequate source control: oral **amoxicillin-clavulanate** or **ciprofloxacin plus metronidazole 400 mg three times daily** to complete **4 days** after source control. [STOP-IT 2015]`,`**Uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia** from a controlled urinary or abdominal source: switch to a highly bioavailable oral agent (fluoroquinolone or cotrimoxazole) once stable, for a **total of 7 days**. [BALANCE 2025]`,`**Bone and joint infection**: oral therapy with high-bioavailability agents (fluoroquinolone plus rifampicin for staphylococci, cotrimoxazole, linezolid, clindamycin) can replace IV **within the first 7 days** (OVIVA). [OVIVA 2019]`,`**Left-sided endocarditis** (streptococci, E. faecalis, S. aureus, CoNS) that is stable after **at least 10 days IV** (7 after surgery) can be completed with **two oral drugs** under close follow-up (POET). [ESC 2023]`,`Give the oral dose **at the time the next IV dose is due** - no overlap or gap is needed. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Separate **fluoroquinolones and doxycycline** from calcium, iron, antacids and dairy - take them **2 hours before or 6 hours after**. [BNF 2025]`]},{heading:`OPAT: patient selection, set-up and drugs`,points:[`**Select patients** who are clinically stable, have a safe home, a working phone, a carer if needed, suitable venous access, and agree to weekly review; active injecting drug use is a relative contraindication. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`Every OPAT service needs an **ID physician or trained lead, a specialist nurse and a pharmacist**, with written protocols and **24-hour contact** for problems. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`,`**Once-daily agents** make OPAT practical: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily, ertapenem 1 g IV daily, teicoplanin** (loading 6-12 mg/kg 12-hourly for 3-5 doses, then daily), **daptomycin 6-10 mg/kg daily** and **amikacin 15-20 mg/kg daily**. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`,`**Long-acting lipoglycopeptides** (dalbavancin 1500 mg single dose or 1000 mg then 500 mg a week later) allow a full course with one or two infusions where available. [IDSA 2024]`,`**Elastomeric pumps** deliver 24-hour continuous infusion of time-dependent drugs such as **flucloxacillin or piperacillin-tazobactam** if drug stability in the device is assured. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`,`The **first dose of a new drug is given in hospital** under observation to detect anaphylaxis before home therapy. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`**IM ceftriaxone** (with lignocaine diluent) is a practical OPAT route in primary care and for short courses where no line is wanted. [ICMR 2022]`,`Typical OPAT indications: **cellulitis failing oral therapy, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, endocarditis completion, complicated UTI with resistant organisms, and liver abscess**. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: avoid **fluoroquinolones, tetracyclines (after 15 weeks) and cotrimoxazole near term**; amoxicillin, cephalexin, cefixime, azithromycin and nitrofurantoin (not at term) are the usual oral step-downs. [BNF 2025]`,`**Children**: early oral switch is standard - e.g. bone and joint infection after **2-4 days IV**, and oral amoxicillin for most pneumonia; doses are weight-based and liquid formulations taste matters for adherence. [IAP 2022]`,`**Older adults and dysphagia**: use liquids or dispersible tablets; crushing modified-release forms is unsafe; review interactions (warfarin with cotrimoxazole, metronidazole, fluoroquinolones). [BNF 2025]`,`**CKD**: adjust doses (levofloxacin, cotrimoxazole, cephalexin, amoxicillin at low eGFR); **avoid PICCs in CKD stage 3b-5** to preserve veins for fistulae. [MAGIC 2015]`,`**Enteral tube feeding**: ciprofloxacin and doxycycline bind feeds - **pause the feed** for 1-2 hours around the dose or use a non-chelating alternative. [BNF 2025]`,`**Diabetes and obesity**: higher weight-based doses for some agents (cefazolin 3 g if over 120 kg for prophylaxis), and diabetic foot infections often need longer courses tied to debridement. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: oral switch saves cannulae, nurse time and money; **once-daily IM or IV ceftriaxone** given at a nearby facility is a simple OPAT model. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Line complications** in OPAT: catheter-related bloodstream infection, occlusion, **upper-limb DVT** (PICCs), and phlebitis - the commonest reasons for unplanned return. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`**Drug toxicity** with prolonged courses: **beta-lactam neutropenia** (after about 2 weeks), interstitial nephritis, hepatitis, vancomycin nephrotoxicity, **daptomycin myopathy** and eosinophilic pneumonia. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`**Linezolid** beyond about **2 weeks** causes **thrombocytopenia, anaemia, peripheral and optic neuropathy and lactic acidosis**, and interacts with SSRIs (serotonin syndrome). [BNF 2025]`,`**C. difficile infection** rises with every extra day of broad-spectrum therapy, especially clindamycin, cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones and carbapenems. [IDSA 2021]`,`**Fluoroquinolone harms**: tendinopathy and rupture (older adults, steroids), QT prolongation, aortic aneurysm, dysglycaemia and neuropathy - avoid for minor infections. [MHRA 2024]`,`Well-run OPAT has **outcomes equivalent to inpatient therapy** with high patient satisfaction; unplanned readmission occurs in a minority, mostly for disease progression or line problems. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**OPAT review weekly** by the OPAT team: clinical response, line site, adverse effects, adherence and a decision on oral switch or stopping. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`**Weekly blood tests**: CBC with differential, creatinine, LFT, CRP; **CK weekly** with daptomycin; **vancomycin AUC or trough** and **aminoglycoside levels** once or twice weekly. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`,`Teach patients to return at once for **fever, rigors, line redness or swelling, arm swelling, rash, diarrhoea or reduced urine**. [BSAC OPAT 2019]`,`**Remove the PICC or midline promptly** when IV therapy ends - an unused line is only risk. [MAGIC 2015]`,`Arrange **end-of-treatment review** (and, for bone infection or endocarditis, follow-up at 1-3 months) to confirm cure and detect relapse. [ESC 2023]`,`Refer to an **ID physician** for OPAT set-up, oral therapy of endocarditis or bone infection, and any MDR organism without an oral option. [IDSA 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and the stewardship programme link`,points:[`**IVOS and short courses are core stewardship interventions** in the ICMR Antimicrobial Stewardship Programme and the National Action Plan on AMR. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`,`Use **automatic stop dates** and electronic prompts at 48-72 hours for every IV antibiotic order. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Prefer **Access-group (WHO AWaRe) oral agents** - amoxicillin, cephalexin, doxycycline, cotrimoxazole, nitrofurantoin - for step-down whenever susceptibility allows. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`Audit **IV-to-oral switch rates and days of therapy per 1000 patient-days** as stewardship quality indicators. [WHO 2019]`,`**Counsel patients** that finishing a short course is enough - the old 'complete a long course' message is not supported for most infections. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`Teach **line care and hand hygiene** to OPAT patients and carers to prevent line infections. [IDSA OPAT 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**BALANCE trial (NEJM 2025)**: in 3608 hospitalised patients with bloodstream infection (excluding S. aureus and complicated sources), **7 days was non-inferior to 14 days** for 90-day mortality. [BALANCE 2025]`,`**UKHSA national IV-to-oral switch criteria (2023)** and the updated **Start Smart Then Focus toolkit (2023)** give a standard daily switch checklist from 48 hours. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**ESC Endocarditis Guidelines (2023)** endorse **partial oral therapy** in stable left-sided endocarditis (class IIa), based on POET and its long-term follow-up. [ESC 2023]`,`**WHO AWaRe Antibiotic Book (2022)** sets short default durations - CAP 5 days, pyelonephritis 7 days, cellulitis 5 days - and prefers oral Access agents. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`**IWGDF/IDSA diabetic foot guideline (2023)** supports **3 weeks** of therapy after surgical debridement of osteomyelitis and 1-2 weeks for soft-tissue infection. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**ADAPT-Sepsis (2025)**: PCT-guided stopping shortened antibiotic duration safely in sepsis. [ADAPT-Sepsis 2025]`,`Exception to 'shorter is better': **DATIPO (2021)** showed 6 weeks was inferior to 12 weeks for prosthetic joint infection. [DATIPO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'When will you switch IV to oral?'** - afebrile 24-48 h, haemodynamically stable, improving, gut working, no IV-only indication; give a bioavailable drug. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Classic viva: **'Name drugs with near-100% oral bioavailability'** - linezolid, levofloxacin, metronidazole, doxycycline, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Classic viva: **'What are OVIVA and POET?'** - oral therapy non-inferior for bone and joint infection; partial oral therapy for stable left-sided endocarditis. [OVIVA 2019]`,`Common mistake: **oral vancomycin for systemic infection** - it is not absorbed and works only for C. difficile. [BNF 2025]`,`Common mistake: **cefuroxime axetil or cefixime as 'equivalent' to IV ceftriaxone** - bioavailability is about 40-50%, so they are weaker step-downs for bacteraemia. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Common mistake: **counting duration from the switch** rather than from the first effective dose, so courses become needlessly long. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`Know the exceptions where long courses still matter: **S. aureus bacteraemia, endocarditis, prosthetic joint infection, TB**. [IDSA 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Common oral step-down drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin`,`1 g oral 8-hourly (child 30 mg/kg 8-hourly)`,`CAP total 5 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea; penicillin allergy`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 8-hourly`,`Cellulitis 5-6 days; IAI 4 days after source control`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic hepatitis`],[`Cephalexin`,`500 mg-1 g oral 6-hourly`,`Cellulitis 5-6 days`,`Diarrhoea; avoid if amoxicillin anaphylaxis`],[`Levofloxacin`,`750 mg oral once daily`,`CAP 5 days; bone infection weeks`,`Tendinopathy, QT, dysglycaemia; exclude TB first; chelation`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`500-750 mg oral 12-hourly`,`Pyelonephritis 7 days`,`As levofloxacin; theophylline and tizanidine interactions`],[`Cotrimoxazole`,`960 mg oral 12-hourly (higher for bone/MRSA)`,`Pyelonephritis 7-14 days`,`Hyperkalaemia, rash/SJS, marrow suppression; warfarin interaction`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral 12-hourly`,`CAP atypical cover 5 days`,`Oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy after 15 weeks`],[`Linezolid`,`600 mg oral 12-hourly`,`Up to 2 weeks without close monitoring`,`Thrombocytopenia, neuropathy, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Metronidazole`,`400 mg oral 8-hourly`,`With cephalosporin or ciprofloxacin for IAI`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram reaction, neuropathy with long use`]]},{heading:`Oral bioavailability: which drugs can replace IV`,columns:[`Bioavailability`,`Drugs`,`Practical meaning`],rows:[[`Excellent (over 90%)`,`Levofloxacin, moxifloxacin, linezolid, metronidazole, doxycycline, minocycline, cotrimoxazole, clindamycin, rifampicin, fluconazole, voriconazole`,`Oral equals IV; switch as soon as gut works`],[`Good (70-90%)`,`Ciprofloxacin, amoxicillin, cephalexin, amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`Effective step-down at full doses`],[`Moderate (40-60%)`,`Cefuroxime axetil, cefixime, cefpodoxime, azithromycin, penicillin V`,`Fine for mild infection; weak for bacteraemia`],[`Poor (under 10%)`,`Vancomycin, aminoglycosides, colistin, most carbapenems`,`No systemic oral use (oral vancomycin for C. difficile only)`]]},{heading:`How long to treat: evidence-based durations`,columns:[`Infection`,`Recommended duration`,`Evidence / guideline`],rows:[[`Community-acquired pneumonia (stable)`,`5 days (3 days if stable by day 3)`,`IDSA/ATS 2019; PTC trial 2021`],[`Ventilator-associated pneumonia`,`7 days`,`IDSA/ATS 2016`],[`Cystitis in women`,`Nitrofurantoin 5 days; fosfomycin single dose`,`IDSA 2024`],[`Pyelonephritis`,`7 days fluoroquinolone; 10-14 days oral beta-lactam`,`IDSA 2024`],[`UTI in afebrile men`,`7 days`,`JAMA 2021`],[`Cellulitis`,`5-6 days`,`IDSA 2014`],[`Intra-abdominal infection with source control`,`4 days`,`STOP-IT 2015`],[`Uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia`,`7 days`,`BALANCE 2025`],[`Uncomplicated S. aureus bacteraemia`,`At least 14 days from first negative culture`,`IDSA 2011; ESC 2023`],[`Vertebral osteomyelitis`,`6 weeks`,`Lancet 2015`],[`Diabetic foot osteomyelitis after debridement`,`3 weeks`,`IWGDF/IDSA 2023`],[`Prosthetic joint infection (retained implant)`,`12 weeks`,`DATIPO 2021`]]},{heading:`IV-to-oral switch criteria (COMS)`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Threshold`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Clinical improvement`,`Temperature 36-38 C for 24-48 h; NEWS2 falling`,`Core requirement`],[`Haemodynamics`,`HR under 90-100, SBP 90 or more, no vasopressor`,`Shock means gut absorption is unreliable`],[`Oral route`,`Swallowing, no vomiting, ileus or malabsorption`,`Use liquids or tube-compatible drugs`],[`Markers`,`CRP or WCC falling`,`Supportive, not mandatory`],[`Specific IV-only indications absent`,`Meningitis, early endocarditis or S. aureus bacteraemia, undrained abscess, neutropenic fever, necrotising fasciitis`,`Re-assess daily`],[`Oral option exists`,`Susceptible organism, bioavailable drug`,`If not, consider OPAT`]]},{heading:`OPAT drugs and monitoring`,columns:[`Drug`,`Regimen`,`Monitoring`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV or IM once daily`,`Weekly CBC, LFT; biliary sludge`],[`Ertapenem`,`1 g IV once daily`,`Weekly CBC, creatinine; seizures rare`],[`Teicoplanin`,`Load 6-12 mg/kg 12-hourly x 3-5 doses, then daily or three times weekly`,`Trough levels; CBC, creatinine`],[`Daptomycin`,`6-10 mg/kg IV once daily`,`CK weekly; stop statin; eosinophilic pneumonia`],[`Amikacin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV once daily`,`Levels, creatinine, hearing and balance`],[`Vancomycin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 12-hourly or continuous pump`,`AUC or trough, creatinine twice weekly`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam (pump)`,`13.5-18 g over 24 h continuous infusion`,`CBC, creatinine; pump stability`]]}],redFlags:[`Persistent fever or rising CRP after 72 hours of appropriate therapy - look for undrained collection or wrong diagnosis; do not just switch drugs`,`Positive blood culture for S. aureus or Candida - no early oral switch; repeat cultures and echo`,`Hypotension, vomiting or ileus - keep IV; oral absorption unreliable`,`OPAT patient with fever, rigors, red or swollen line site - suspect line infection; admit and culture`,`Arm swelling with a PICC - suspect upper-limb DVT`,`Profuse diarrhoea on antibiotics - test for C. difficile and review the need for antibiotics`,`Falling platelets or visual symptoms on linezolid beyond 2 weeks - stop and review`],pearls:[`**COMS** - Clinical improvement, Oral route, Markers improving, Specific IV indication absent.`,`**Linezolid, levofloxacin, metronidazole, doxycycline, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole: oral equals IV.**`,`**Oral vancomycin is for C. difficile only.**`,`**OVIVA: oral for bone and joint; POET: partial oral for stable left-sided endocarditis.**`,`**7 days for uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia (BALANCE 2025); 4 days for abdominal infection after source control (STOP-IT).**`,`**Count duration from the first effective dose, not from the switch.**`,`**Each extra antibiotic day adds about 5% risk of an adverse event.**`,`**Pull the PICC when the drip stops.**`,`**Shorter is better - except S. aureus bacteraemia, endocarditis, PJI and TB.**`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-q1`,stem:`Which antibiotic achieves almost the same serum concentration when given orally as when given intravenously?`,options:[`Cefuroxime axetil`,`Vancomycin`,`Linezolid`,`Azithromycin`],answer:2,explanation:`Linezolid has close to 100% oral bioavailability, so oral and IV doses are interchangeable. Cefuroxime axetil (about 40-50%) and azithromycin (under 40%) are only partly absorbed, and vancomycin is essentially not absorbed, so oral vancomycin is used only for C. difficile in the gut.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-q2`,stem:`A 62-year-old woman with E. coli bacteraemia from pyelonephritis has been afebrile for 48 hours on IV ceftriaxone, is eating and has no obstruction on ultrasound. The isolate is ciprofloxacin-susceptible. What is the best plan?`,options:[`Switch to oral ciprofloxacin to complete a total of 7 days`,`Continue IV ceftriaxone for 14 days because she had bacteraemia`,`Switch to oral cefixime for 14 days`,`Continue IV therapy until the CRP is normal`],answer:0,explanation:`Uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia with a controlled urinary source can be completed with a highly bioavailable oral agent, and 7 days total is non-inferior to 14 (BALANCE 2025). Continuing IV ceftriaxone for 14 days adds harm without benefit, cefixime has only about 40-50% bioavailability and is a weaker step-down for bacteraemia, and a normal CRP is not a switch requirement.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-q3`,stem:`The OVIVA trial is best remembered for showing that:`,options:[`Partial oral therapy is non-inferior in left-sided infective endocarditis`,`Oral antibiotics in the first 6 weeks are non-inferior to IV for bone and joint infection`,`Six weeks of therapy is inferior to twelve weeks for prosthetic joint infection`,`Four days of therapy suffice for intra-abdominal infection after source control`],answer:1,explanation:`OVIVA (NEJM 2019) randomised bone and joint infections to oral or IV therapy during the first 6 weeks and found oral non-inferior for treatment failure at one year. Partial oral therapy for endocarditis is POET, 6 versus 12 weeks for prosthetic joint infection is DATIPO, and 4 days for intra-abdominal infection is STOP-IT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-q4`,stem:`A patient was switched from IV to oral ciprofloxacin for pyelonephritis but has not improved. He takes calcium carbonate with every meal and an iron tablet twice daily, all at the same time as the antibiotic. What is the most likely explanation?`,options:[`Ciprofloxacin-induced interstitial nephritis`,`Development of fluoroquinolone resistance within 48 hours`,`Reduced absorption of ciprofloxacin from chelation with calcium and iron`,`Drug fever caused by the iron preparation`],answer:2,explanation:`Divalent cations such as calcium and iron chelate fluoroquinolones in the gut and can reduce absorption by more than half; the dose should be taken 2 hours before or 6 hours after them. Interstitial nephritis and drug fever do not explain lack of response as well, and resistance emerging within 48 hours is far less likely than this common, avoidable interaction.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-q5`,stem:`Which patient is NOT yet suitable for an IV-to-oral switch on day 3?`,options:[`A man with community-acquired pneumonia, afebrile for 36 hours, HR 84, eating normally`,`A woman with cellulitis of the leg, afebrile for 48 hours with receding erythema`,`A man with pneumococcal meningitis who is afebrile and alert`,`A woman with pyelonephritis, afebrile for 24 hours, tolerating diet`],answer:2,explanation:`Bacterial meningitis needs high, sustained CSF drug concentrations and is a specific IV-only indication for the whole 10-14-day pneumococcal course, even when the patient looks well. The patients with pneumonia, cellulitis and pyelonephritis all meet the clinical, haemodynamic and oral-route criteria and should be switched to an oral agent.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the principles of intravenous-to-oral antibiotic switch. Describe the criteria, the role of oral bioavailability, and the evidence for shorter durations of antibiotic therapy.`,openingLines:[`IV-to-oral switch is the change to enteral therapy once the patient is clinically stable and can absorb oral drugs; with a highly bioavailable drug, oral therapy is as effective as IV.`,`Randomised trials show that shorter courses are non-inferior for most common infections and reduce adverse events, resistance and cost.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale`,points:[`IV harms: phlebitis, line infection, thrombosis, cost, longer stay.`,`Each extra antibiotic day raises adverse-event odds by about 5%.`]},{heading:`Switch criteria (COMS)`,points:[`Afebrile 24-48 h, HR under 90-100, SBP 90 or more, RR 24 or less, normal mentation.`,`Oral route and gut absorption available; markers falling.`,`No IV-only indication: meningitis, early endocarditis or S. aureus bacteraemia, undrained abscess, neutropenic fever.`]},{heading:`Bioavailability`,points:[`Over 90%: levofloxacin, linezolid, metronidazole, doxycycline, cotrimoxazole, fluconazole.`,`Moderate: oral cephalosporins, azithromycin; poor: vancomycin, aminoglycosides.`,`Chelation with calcium, iron, antacids; PPI effects; enteral feeds.`]},{heading:`Types of switch`,points:[`Sequential, switch and step-down therapy with examples.`]},{heading:`Evidence for short courses`,points:[`CAP 5 days (IDSA/ATS 2019; PTC 2021); cellulitis 5-6 days.`,`IAI 4 days after source control (STOP-IT); Gram-negative bacteraemia 7 days (BALANCE 2025).`,`Oral bone and joint therapy (OVIVA); partial oral endocarditis (POET; ESC 2023).`,`Exceptions: S. aureus bacteraemia, PJI 12 weeks (DATIPO), TB.`]},{heading:`Stewardship`,points:[`48-72 h antibiotic time-out, stop dates, audit of IV days; ICMR AMSP and WHO AWaRe.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: 48-72 h review - stop / switch to oral / continue IV / OPAT`,`Table: bioavailability classes with examples`],markSplit:[{part:`Rationale and switch criteria`,marks:3},{part:`Bioavailability and types of switch`,marks:3},{part:`Evidence for short durations`,marks:3},{part:`Stewardship link`,marks:1}],keywords:[`IV-to-oral switch`,`bioavailability`,`COMS`,`OVIVA`,`POET`,`BALANCE`,`short course`]},{id:`infectious-fever-opat-iv-to-oral-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`uncommon`,question:`Write a short note on outpatient parenteral antimicrobial therapy (OPAT).`,openingLines:[`OPAT is the delivery of parenteral antimicrobials on at least two days without an overnight hospital stay, at a day-care centre or at home.`,`It shortens admissions safely for stable patients who still need IV therapy, provided there is a structured team, monitoring and rapid access to care.`],answer:[{heading:`Models and selection`,points:[`Infusion centre, visiting nurse, self/carer-administered, elastomeric pump.`,`Stable patient, safe home, phone, carer, venous access, consent; first dose in hospital.`]},{heading:`Drugs and access`,points:[`Once-daily: ceftriaxone, ertapenem, teicoplanin, daptomycin, amikacin; dalbavancin where available.`,`Peripheral 5 days or fewer, midline 6-14 days, PICC 15 days or more.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and complications`,points:[`Weekly review and CBC, creatinine, LFT, CRP; CK for daptomycin; drug levels.`,`Line infection, thrombosis, neutropenia, C. difficile; remove line at end.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: OPAT patient pathway from selection to end-of-treatment review`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, models and selection`,marks:2},{part:`Drugs and vascular access`,marks:2},{part:`Monitoring and complications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`OPAT`,`PICC`,`ceftriaxone`,`elastomeric pump`,`monitoring`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Principles of antibacterial therapy`,`Norris AH et al. 2018 IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Outpatient Parenteral Antimicrobial Therapy. Clin Infect Dis 2019`,`Chapman ALN et al. Updated good practice recommendations for OPAT in adults and children in the UK (BSAC), JAC-AMR 2019`,`UKHSA. National antimicrobial intravenous-to-oral switch (IVOS) criteria for early switch, 2023, and Start Smart Then Focus toolkit, 2023`,`BALANCE Investigators. Seven versus fourteen days of antibiotic treatment for bloodstream infection. N Engl J Med 2025`,`Li HK et al. OVIVA trial, N Engl J Med 2019; Iversen K et al. POET trial, N Engl J Med 2019; 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`,`WHO AWaRe Antibiotic Book, 2022; ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2022`]}),l.push({id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary`,title:`Extrapulmonary tuberculosis: lymph node, pleural, pericardial, genitourinary, spinal`,oneLiner:`**Extrapulmonary TB (EPTB)** is TB of organs other than the lung parenchyma - in India mainly **lymph node, pleura, abdomen, CNS, spine and genitourinary tract** - diagnosed by **tissue or fluid sampling with CBNAAT/Truenat, culture and histology** (supported by **ADA over 40 U/L** in pleural and pericardial fluid), treated under **NTEP with 2HRZE + 4HRE for most sites but 12 months for CNS TB and 9-12 months for spinal and osteoarticular TB**, with **corticosteroids in TB meningitis** (and selectively in pericarditis) but not in pleural or nodal disease.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`EPTB`,`extrapulmonary tuberculosis`,`tuberculous lymphadenitis`,`scrofula`,`TB pleural effusion`,`adenosine deaminase`,`ADA`,`tuberculous pericarditis`,`constrictive pericarditis`,`genitourinary tuberculosis`,`sterile pyuria`,`female genital TB`,`Pott spine`,`spinal tuberculosis`,`tuberculous meningitis`,`CBNAAT`,`Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra`,`INDEX-TB`,`NTEP`,`paradoxical reaction`,`dexamethasone`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Extrapulmonary TB** is TB involving organs other than the lungs - lymph nodes, pleura, pericardium, abdomen, meninges, bones and joints, genitourinary tract, skin; **pleural and intrathoracic nodal TB without lung lesions are classed as extrapulmonary**. [WHO 2024]`,`A patient with **both pulmonary and extrapulmonary disease is classified as pulmonary TB** for notification. [NTEP 2024]`,`Globally EPTB makes up about **one in six notified TB cases**; it is commoner in **children, women, people with HIV** and in South Asia. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`India notifies over **25 lakh TB cases a year**, of which roughly **one-fifth to one-quarter are extrapulmonary** - the largest absolute EPTB burden in the world. [India TB Report 2024]`,`In Indian series the commonest site is **lymph node (about one-third of EPTB)**, followed by **pleural, abdominal, CNS, skeletal** and genitourinary TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`In **advanced HIV (CD4 under 200)** over half of TB is extrapulmonary or disseminated, and every EPTB patient must be offered HIV testing. [NACO 2021]`,`EPTB is **paucibacillary** - smear positivity is low, so diagnosis relies on **tissue or fluid sampling, NAAT, culture and histology**, and many patients are treated on clinical-radiological grounds. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Caused mainly by **Mycobacterium tuberculosis**; **M. bovis** (unpasteurised milk) adds to cervical and abdominal disease, and non-tuberculous mycobacteria cause a minority of childhood cervical lymphadenitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV** is the strongest risk factor for EPTB and disseminated TB, followed by **diabetes, malnutrition, CKD and dialysis, cirrhosis, alcohol and smoking**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Immunosuppressive drugs** - corticosteroids, **anti-TNF agents** (infliximab, adalimumab), JAK inhibitors and transplant drugs - cause extrapulmonary and disseminated reactivation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Undernutrition** is the leading population-attributable risk factor for TB in India; the **RATIONS trial (2023)** showed food support for household contacts cut TB incidence by about 40%. [RATIONS 2023]`,`**Young age** (under 5 years) favours lymph node and meningeal TB; **older adults** show more genitourinary and skeletal TB from late reactivation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Household contact** with pulmonary TB and residence in crowded urban slums raise infection risk. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why each site behaves as it does`,points:[`During primary infection bacilli reach **regional lymph nodes and then the blood** (silent bacillaemia), seeding organs with high oxygen tension or rich blood supply - **renal cortex, vertebral bodies, epiphyses, meninges, fallopian tubes** - where they lie dormant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reactivation** occurs when cell-mediated immunity (**CD4 Th1 cells, IFN-gamma, TNF-alpha**) wanes - which explains EPTB with HIV and anti-TNF therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Caseating granulomas** form from macrophages, epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells; caseous liquefaction produces **cold abscesses** - pus without the heat and redness of pyogenic infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural TB** is mainly a **delayed hypersensitivity reaction** to a few bacilli released from a subpleural focus - hence a lymphocytic exudate, **high ADA and IFN-gamma**, few organisms and frequent spontaneous resolution. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ADA** is an enzyme of purine metabolism produced by activated **T lymphocytes and macrophages**; its rise reflects the cell-mediated response, so it is also raised in lymphoma, empyema and rheumatoid effusions. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardial TB** progresses through **dry (fibrinous), effusive, absorptive and constrictive** stages; fibrin and granulation tissue thicken the pericardium and cause constriction in a substantial minority despite treatment. [ESC 2015]`,`**Spinal TB** begins in the **anterior paradiscal vertebral body**; mycobacteria lack proteolytic enzymes, so the **disc is relatively preserved early** and pus tracks under the anterior longitudinal ligament to form **paraspinal and psoas abscesses**; anterior collapse produces the **gibbus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB meningitis** follows rupture of a subependymal or subpial **Rich focus** into the subarachnoid space; thick basal exudate causes **hydrocephalus, cranial nerve palsies and vasculitic infarcts** - the targets of dexamethasone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal TB** starts in the cortex, ulcerates into calyces and seeds down the urinary tract, causing **sterile pyuria, ureteric strictures and a contracted 'thimble' bladder**; the end-stage calcified kidney is the **putty kidney**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging (the ones examiners ask)`,points:[`**By site (NTEP)**: lymph node, pleural, abdominal, CNS (meningitis, tuberculoma), skeletal (spine, joints), genitourinary, pericardial, cutaneous, ocular and disseminated/miliary. [NTEP 2024]`,`**By bacteriology**: **microbiologically confirmed** (NAAT, culture or smear positive from an EP specimen) versus **clinically diagnosed** (histology, imaging and response without bacteriological proof). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Jones and Campbell stages of TB lymphadenitis**: **I** enlarged firm mobile discrete nodes; **II** rubbery nodes fixed to surrounding tissue (periadenitis, matting); **III** central softening (cold abscess); **IV** **collar-stud abscess**; **V** sinus formation. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**BMRC grading of TB meningitis**: **grade I** GCS 15 with no focal deficit; **grade II** GCS 11-14, or 15 with focal deficit; **grade III** GCS 10 or less - grade predicts death and disability. [Thwaites 2004]`,`**Pericardial TB stages**: dry fibrinous, effusive, absorptive and constrictive; clinically **effusive, effusive-constrictive and constrictive** pericarditis. [ESC 2015]`,`**Pott paraplegia**: **early-onset** (active disease - abscess, granulation tissue, sequestra; good prognosis with treatment) versus **late-onset** (healed disease - bony ridge or severe kyphosis compressing the cord; poorer prognosis). [Tuli 2016]`,`**Genitourinary TB**: renal, ureteric, bladder; **male genital** (epididymis, prostate, seminal vesicles) and **female genital** (tubes in over 90%, endometrium, ovaries). [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history by site`,points:[`**Constitutional symptoms** - evening fever, night sweats, weight loss, anorexia - are present in only **about half** of EPTB patients; absence does not exclude TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Lymph node TB**: painless, slowly enlarging **cervical nodes** (posterior triangle and jugulodigastric commonest) over weeks to months, later softening and discharging. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural TB**: subacute fever, **pleuritic chest pain**, dry cough and breathlessness in a young adult; it can also present acutely and mimic pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pericardial TB**: fever, chest pain, breathlessness, **orthopnoea**, and in later stages ankle swelling, ascites and fatigue from constriction. [ESC 2015]`,`**Spinal TB**: insidious **back pain**, stiffness, local tenderness, then deformity, a lump in the groin or flank (psoas abscess) and **weakness or numbness of the legs** and bladder disturbance. [Tuli 2016]`,`**Genitourinary TB**: **frequency, dysuria, nocturia, haematuria and loin pain** with repeatedly 'sterile' urine cultures; men present with a **painless scrotal swelling** or infertility; women with **infertility**, menstrual disturbance or pelvic pain. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**TB meningitis**: headache, fever and vomiting over **1-3 weeks**, then confusion, **cranial nerve palsy (VI commonest)**, seizures and coma - subacute course distinguishes it from bacterial meningitis (see the Meningitis topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **TB contact, prior ATT and adherence, HIV risk, diabetes and immunosuppressive drugs** - previous treatment raises the possibility of drug resistance. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Matted, rubbery, non-tender cervical nodes**, a fluctuant **cold abscess** without warmth, or a **collar-stud abscess** with a sinus and bluish undermined skin (**scrofuloderma**) are typical of TB lymphadenitis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Pleural effusion signs**: reduced expansion, **stony dull** percussion, absent breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance on the affected side; tracheal shift away with large effusions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tamponade**: **Beck triad** (hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds), tachycardia and **pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg**. [ESC 2015]`,`**Constriction**: raised JVP with a **prominent y descent**, **Kussmaul sign** (JVP rises on inspiration), **pericardial knock**, hepatomegaly and ascites out of proportion to oedema. [ESC 2015]`,`**Spine**: localised **tenderness**, paraspinal spasm, **knuckle or angular gibbus**, cold abscess in the **groin or above the inguinal ligament (psoas)**, and signs of cord compression (spastic paraparesis, sensory level). [Tuli 2016]`,`**Genital TB**: **beaded vas deferens**, thickened craggy epididymis, a scrotal sinus posteriorly; in women adnexal masses may be found, but examination is often normal. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Meningeal TB**: neck stiffness, **VI nerve palsy**, papilloedema, hemiparesis; look in the fundi for **choroid tubercles** (miliary TB). [Harrison 22e]`,`Always examine the **chest, abdomen (ascites, doughy abdomen), spine and all node groups** - EPTB is multifocal in a significant minority. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Cervical lymphadenopathy**: reactive nodes, **lymphoma** (rubbery, painless, generalised, B symptoms), metastatic carcinoma (hard, older smoker), **NTM lymphadenitis** in young children, Kikuchi disease, sarcoidosis. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Lymphocytic exudative effusion**: **malignancy** (older age, blood-stained, ADA usually under 40, cytology positive), lymphoma, rheumatoid arthritis, post-CABG; neutrophilic **parapneumonic effusion/empyema** may also raise ADA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pericardial effusion**: viral or idiopathic (acute, self-limiting), malignancy, uraemia, hypothyroidism, purulent bacterial pericarditis; TB is the leading cause of large effusions and constriction in India. [ESC 2015]`,`**Spinal infection**: **pyogenic spondylodiscitis** (acute, early disc destruction, S. aureus), **brucellosis** (lumbar, preserved vertebral architecture, raw milk), and **metastasis** (disc spared, pedicle involved, older patient). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sterile pyuria**: partially treated UTI, chlamydia, interstitial nephritis, renal stones, **analgesic nephropathy** and urothelial cancer. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Female infertility with tubal block**: post-gonococcal or chlamydial PID, endometriosis - genital TB is **overdiagnosed** when PCR alone is used. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Chronic meningitis**: cryptococcal (HIV), partially treated bacterial, neurobrucellosis, carcinomatous meningitis and neurosyphilis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: first line and advanced`,points:[`**Get tissue or fluid for all EPTB**: send it for **CBNAAT (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra) or Truenat, liquid culture (MGIT) with DST, and histopathology** - and do a **chest X-ray** to look for concurrent pulmonary TB (sputum NAAT if abnormal). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Lymph node**: **FNAC** shows epithelioid granulomas with caseation and AFB; **Xpert on aspirate** has a sensitivity of roughly **80%** against culture; **excision biopsy** if FNAC is inconclusive or lymphoma is suspected. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural fluid**: **lymphocytic exudate** (protein over 3 g/dL, LDH high), **ADA over 40 U/L** (sensitivity and specificity about 90%), **mesothelial cells under 5%**; AFB smear is almost always negative and Xpert sensitivity is low (about 50%). [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural biopsy** (closed or **thoracoscopic**) shows granulomas in most cases and gives tissue for culture - indicated when ADA and the picture are discordant, e.g. an older smoker with ADA under 40. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardial**: echocardiography for effusion and tamponade; pericardial fluid **ADA over 40 U/L** and unstimulated IFN-gamma support TB; **CT/MRI** shows pericardial thickening (over 4 mm) and calcification in constriction. [ESC 2015]`,`**Spine**: **MRI is the investigation of choice** - paradiscal involvement, anterior subligamentous spread, **large paraspinal or psoas abscess with thin smooth walls**, skip lesions; **CT-guided biopsy** for Xpert, culture and histology before treatment. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Genitourinary**: **three to five early-morning urine samples** for culture and Xpert; **sterile pyuria** with haematuria; **CT urography** shows calyceal irregularity ('moth-eaten'), infundibular stenosis, ureteric strictures and a small bladder; endometrial biopsy (premenstrual) for female genital TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**CSF**: lymphocytic pleocytosis, **protein 1-5 g/L**, **CSF:plasma glucose under 0.5**; **Xpert Ultra on CSF** is the recommended initial test; MRI shows basal enhancement, hydrocephalus, tuberculomas and infarcts. [WHO 2024]`,`**TST and IGRA cannot diagnose active TB** in adults and are not used for EPTB diagnosis in India; serological antibody tests for TB are **banned** in India. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Universal drug-susceptibility testing**: every diagnosed patient, including EPTB, should have at least rifampicin resistance tested, plus isoniazid by LPA where possible. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: the NTEP regimen and supportive care`,points:[`**Drug-sensitive EPTB** at most sites is treated with **2HRZE (intensive) + 4HRE (continuation) daily**, using weight-band fixed-dose combinations. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Adult daily FDC (H75/R150/Z400/E275)**: **2 tablets for 25-34 kg, 3 for 35-49 kg, 4 for 50-64 kg, 5 for 65-75 kg, 6 over 75 kg**; doses correspond to **H 5, R 10, Z 25 and E 15 mg/kg**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**CNS TB (meningitis, tuberculoma)**: extend the continuation phase - **2HRZE + 10HRE (12 months total)**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Spinal and osteoarticular TB**: extend the continuation phase to give **9-12 months total**, guided by clinical and MRI response; the 6-month regimen is enough for lymph node, pleural, abdominal and genitourinary TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardial TB**: 6 months of standard ATT; adjunctive prednisolone may be considered in HIV-negative patients with large effusion or early constriction. [ESC 2015]`,`**Pyridoxine (10-25 mg daily)** for patients at risk of neuropathy (diabetes, HIV, alcohol, malnutrition, pregnancy, CKD). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Nutritional support**: enrol on **Ni-kshay**, ensure **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** direct benefit transfer (**Rs 1000 per month** since 2024) and link to a **Ni-kshay Mitra** for food baskets. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Adherence**: treatment supporter, **99DOTS or MERM** digital adherence technologies, and family counselling - EPTB is notifiable and treatment is free in public and private sectors. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Lymph node abscess**: **aspirate** a fluctuant node with a needle through healthy skin entering from above; avoid incision and drainage, which leaves a chronic sinus. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural TB**: therapeutic aspiration for large symptomatic effusions; **corticosteroids are not recommended** - they do not reduce pleural thickening or improve lung function. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency: steroids, drainage and surgery`,points:[`**TB meningitis - dexamethasone for all HIV-negative adults**: grade II-III **0.4 mg/kg/day IV in week 1, 0.3 in week 2, 0.2 in week 3, 0.1 in week 4**, then oral tapering over the next 4 weeks; grade I starts at **0.3 mg/kg/day** - about **6-8 weeks** in total. [Thwaites 2004]`,`**ACT HIV trial (NEJM 2023)**: adjunctive dexamethasone **did not improve survival in HIV-positive adults** with TB meningitis - steroids are no longer automatic in this group. [ACT HIV 2023]`,`**Hydrocephalus** in TB meningitis: communicating type may respond to acetazolamide and furosemide; **obstructive or worsening hydrocephalus needs VP shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Cardiac tamponade**: urgent **echocardiography-guided pericardiocentesis**; send fluid for ADA, Xpert, culture and cytology. [ESC 2015]`,`**Constrictive pericarditis**: give ATT; **pericardiectomy** if there is no improvement or deterioration after **4-8 weeks** of treatment - early surgery before calcification improves outcome. [ESC 2015]`,`**Pericardial steroids**: the **IMPI trial (2014)** found prednisolone (**120 mg/day tapering over 6 weeks**) did not reduce the composite of death, tamponade and constriction but **halved constriction** and raised cancer risk in HIV - consider only in HIV-negative patients. [IMPI 2014]`,`**Spinal TB surgery** ('middle path'): ATT first, with surgery for **neurological deficit not improving after 3-4 weeks or worsening**, instability, severe or progressive kyphosis, large abscess, diagnostic uncertainty or late-onset paraplegia. [Tuli 2016]`,`**Genitourinary TB**: relieve obstruction with **ureteric stenting or nephrostomy**; ureteric strictures can appear during treatment, so **repeat ultrasound at 6-8 weeks**; reconstructive surgery (augmentation cystoplasty) is deferred until after ATT. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Admit** patients with meningitis, tamponade, cord compression, disseminated TB, severe drug toxicity (hepatitis), or inability to take oral drugs. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HIV**: start ART **within 2 weeks** of ATT, but in **TB meningitis delay ART for 4-8 weeks** because early ART increases severe IRIS and death; give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis. [WHO 2021]`,`**Pregnancy**: **HRZE is safe** and must not be delayed; give pyridoxine; avoid streptomycin (ototoxic to the fetus); breastfeeding continues. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Children**: **WHO (2022)** recommends a **4-month regimen (2HRZ(E) + 2HR)** for non-severe TB including **peripheral lymph node TB**, and a **6-month intensive regimen (6HRZ-ethionamide)** as an alternative for TB meningitis. [WHO 2022]`,`**Diabetes**: screen all TB patients for diabetes; poor glycaemic control slows response - aim for tight control, often with insulin during the intensive phase. [NTEP 2024]`,`**CKD**: give **pyrazinamide and ethambutol three times a week** after dialysis (ethambutol 15-25 mg/kg, pyrazinamide 25-35 mg/kg) when eGFR is under 30; isoniazid and rifampicin need no adjustment. [WHO 2022]`,`**Liver disease**: if baseline transaminases are high, use regimens with fewer hepatotoxic drugs under specialist supervision; stop HRZ if **ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or 5 times without** (see the Pulmonary TB topic). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Drug-resistant EPTB**: if rifampicin resistance is detected on any EP specimen, refer to the **DR-TB centre** for the BPaLM or longer regimen (see the Drug-resistant TB topic). [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Paradoxical reaction** - nodes enlarge, new nodes or abscesses appear, or effusions increase after initial improvement - occurs in about **20-25% of TB lymphadenitis** in the first months; it is **not treatment failure**: continue ATT, aspirate pus, and give steroids if severe. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Residual nodes** at the end of 6 months are common and do not need extended therapy if the patient is otherwise well. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural TB** can leave **pleural thickening, trapped lung or empyema** (tuberculous empyema needs drainage and sometimes decortication). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constrictive pericarditis** develops in a substantial minority despite ATT; untreated TB pericarditis has high mortality. [ESC 2015]`,`**Spinal TB**: **paraplegia, progressive kyphosis**, chronic pain and sinuses; early-onset paraplegia often recovers with ATT, late-onset rarely without surgery. [Tuli 2016]`,`**Genitourinary TB**: **ureteric stricture, hydronephrosis, contracted bladder, renal failure**, and permanent infertility from tubal damage even after cure. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**TB meningitis** carries about **20-30% mortality** in HIV-negative adults (much higher in HIV and grade III), with disability in many survivors. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Monitoring EPTB response is clinical and radiological** - weight, symptoms, node size, effusion on X-ray or ultrasound, MRI for spine, echo for pericardium - because repeat bacteriology is rarely possible. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Review **monthly during treatment** on Ni-kshay: weight (adjust FDC dose band), adherence, adverse effects (hepatitis, rash, visual acuity and colour vision for ethambutol). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Baseline tests**: CBC, LFT, creatinine, blood glucose (HbA1c), HIV and **visual acuity**; repeat LFT if symptoms of hepatitis appear. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Post-treatment follow-up** at **6, 12, 18 and 24 months** after completion to detect relapse, as for all TB under NTEP. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Refer to tertiary care**: meningitis, tamponade or constriction, cord compression, obstructive uropathy, diagnostic uncertainty needing biopsy, and any drug-resistant EPTB. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Do not extend therapy** simply because a node remains palpable or an MRI shows residual change - look instead for adherence problems, resistance or an alternative diagnosis. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and the national programme link`,points:[`EPTB is **notifiable** - notify every case (public and private) on **Ni-kshay**; private providers receive an incentive for notification and outcome reporting. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Contact tracing**: screen household contacts of any patient with concurrent pulmonary TB and offer **TB preventive treatment** after excluding active TB (see the Latent TB topic). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Pasteurise or boil milk** to prevent M. bovis disease. [WHO 2024]`,`**BCG at birth** protects young children against TB meningitis and miliary TB but has little effect on adult EPTB. [WHO 2024]`,`**Screen for TB before anti-TNF and other biologics** and give preventive treatment where indicated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB Mukt Bharat**: India's elimination push (the **100-day intensified campaign** from December 2024) emphasises active case-finding, NAAT for all, nutrition support and Ni-kshay Mitra community support. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Counsel** that EPTB alone (without lung involvement) is **generally not infectious**, that the full course must be completed, and that free drugs and nutrition support are available. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**WHO consolidated TB guidelines, Module 3 diagnosis (2024)**: **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** on CSF, lymph node aspirate and other EP specimens as an initial test; targeted next-generation sequencing for resistance. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO Module 4 treatment (2022, consolidated 2025)**: 4-month regimen for non-severe childhood TB including peripheral node TB, and a 6-month intensive regimen for paediatric TB meningitis. [WHO 2022]`,`**ACT HIV (NEJM 2023)**: dexamethasone did not reduce mortality in HIV-associated TB meningitis. [ACT HIV 2023]`,`**NTEP (2024)** continues **upfront NAAT and universal DST** for all diagnosed TB, and **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** support was raised to **Rs 1000 per month** in 2024. [NTEP 2024]`,`**RATIONS (Lancet 2023)**: nutritional support for patients and household contacts in Jharkhand reduced TB incidence among contacts by about 40% and improved weight gain in patients. [RATIONS 2023]`,`**INDEX-TB (ICMR 2016)** remains the Indian reference for site-specific EPTB diagnosis and management, including the ADA cut-off of 40 U/L and the middle-path approach to spinal TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Young man with lymphocytic exudate and ADA 80 U/L'** - TB pleural effusion; start ATT in a high-prevalence setting; no steroids. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Classic viva: **'Stages of TB lymphadenitis'** - Jones and Campbell I to V, ending in collar-stud abscess and sinus. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Classic viva: **'In which EPTB do steroids help?'** - TB meningitis (proven, HIV-negative); pericarditis (reduce constriction, HIV-negative); not pleural or nodal TB. [ACT HIV 2023]`,`Classic viva: **'How do you differentiate TB from pyogenic spondylitis on MRI?'** - TB: paradiscal, relative disc preservation early, subligamentous spread, large thin-walled abscess, skip lesions. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Common mistake: **treating paradoxical node enlargement as failure** and switching drugs - continue ATT and aspirate. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Common mistake: **diagnosing female genital TB on PCR alone** in infertility clinics - INDEX-TB advises against it. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Common mistake: **starting ART in week 1-2 in TB meningitis** - delay 4-8 weeks. [WHO 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in extrapulmonary TB`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Isoniazid (H)`,`5 mg/kg oral daily (max 300 mg) in FDC`,`6 months (12 in CNS, 9-12 in spinal)`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy (give pyridoxine)`],[`Rifampicin (R)`,`10 mg/kg oral daily (max 600 mg) in FDC`,`As above`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions, many interactions (OCP, warfarin, ART)`],[`Pyrazinamide (Z)`,`25 mg/kg oral daily`,`Intensive phase 2 months`,`Hepatitis, hyperuricaemia, arthralgia`],[`Ethambutol (E)`,`15 mg/kg oral daily`,`Throughout (HRE continuation)`,`Optic neuritis - test acuity and colour vision; reduce in CKD`],[`Adult FDC (H75/R150/Z400/E275)`,`25-34 kg: 2; 35-49 kg: 3; 50-64 kg: 4; 65-75 kg: 5; over 75 kg: 6 tablets`,`2 months, then HRE FDC`,`Re-weigh monthly and change band`],[`Dexamethasone (TB meningitis)`,`0.4 mg/kg/day IV (grade II-III), 0.3 mg/kg/day (grade I)`,`Reduce weekly over 4 weeks IV, then oral taper; 6-8 weeks total`,`Hyperglycaemia, GI bleed; no survival benefit in HIV (ACT HIV)`],[`Prednisolone (pericarditis)`,`120 mg/day week 1, then 90, 60, 30, 15, 5 mg (IMPI)`,`6 weeks`,`Consider only in HIV-negative; cancer risk in HIV`],[`Pyridoxine`,`10-25 mg oral daily`,`Throughout ATT`,`For diabetes, HIV, alcohol, pregnancy, malnutrition, CKD`]]},{heading:`Classification and staging in EPTB`,columns:[`System`,`Stage / grade`,`Features`],rows:[[`Jones and Campbell (lymph node)`,`I-II`,`I: firm mobile discrete nodes; II: rubbery, matted (periadenitis)`],[`Jones and Campbell (lymph node)`,`III-V`,`III: cold abscess; IV: collar-stud abscess; V: sinus`],[`BMRC (TB meningitis)`,`I`,`GCS 15, no focal deficit`],[`BMRC (TB meningitis)`,`II`,`GCS 11-14, or 15 with focal deficit`],[`BMRC (TB meningitis)`,`III`,`GCS 10 or less`],[`Pericardial TB`,`Dry, effusive, absorptive, constrictive`,`Clinically effusive, effusive-constrictive, constrictive`],[`Pott paraplegia`,`Early-onset vs late-onset`,`Active disease (good prognosis) vs healed disease with bony compression (poorer)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis by site`,columns:[`Site`,`TB clue`,`Main mimic`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Cervical nodes`,`Matted, cold abscess, sinus, caseating granuloma`,`Lymphoma`,`Rubbery discrete nodes, B symptoms, Reed-Sternberg cells on biopsy`],[`Pleural effusion`,`Young, lymphocytic, ADA over 40, mesothelial cells under 5%`,`Malignant effusion`,`Older, blood-stained, ADA low, cytology positive`],[`Pericardium`,`Large effusion, ADA over 40, constriction`,`Viral/idiopathic pericarditis`,`Acute, self-limiting, small effusion, responds to NSAID/colchicine`],[`Spine`,`Paradiscal, disc preserved early, large thin-walled abscess`,`Pyogenic spondylodiscitis`,`Acute, early disc destruction, high CRP, S. aureus`],[`Spine`,`Contiguous vertebrae and disc`,`Metastasis`,`Disc spared, pedicle involved, known primary`],[`Urinary tract`,`Sterile pyuria, strictures, calcification`,`Chronic bacterial UTI or stones`,`Positive routine culture; stone on CT`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in EPTB`,columns:[`Specimen / test`,`Supports TB`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Pleural fluid ADA`,`Over 40 U/L`,`Sensitivity and specificity about 90%; also raised in empyema, lymphoma, RA`],[`Pericardial fluid ADA`,`Over 40 U/L`,`Unstimulated IFN-gamma adds specificity`],[`Ascitic fluid ADA`,`Over 39 U/L`,`Lower in cirrhosis with low protein ascites`],[`CSF`,`Lymphocytes, protein 1-5 g/L, CSF:plasma glucose under 0.5`,`Xpert Ultra initial test; culture slow`],[`Pleural fluid cytology`,`Lymphocytes over 50%, mesothelial cells under 5%`,`Many mesothelial cells argue against TB`],[`Xpert Ultra on EP specimens`,`MTB detected (rifampicin result)`,`High specificity; negative does not exclude (pleural sensitivity about 50%)`],[`Histopathology`,`Caseating epithelioid granulomas, AFB`,`Excision or core biopsy if FNAC inconclusive`],[`MRI spine`,`Paradiscal lesion, subligamentous spread, paraspinal abscess`,`Investigation of choice; biopsy for confirmation`]]},{heading:`Treatment duration by site (NTEP / INDEX-TB)`,columns:[`Site`,`Regimen`,`Adjuncts`],rows:[[`Lymph node`,`2HRZE + 4HRE (6 months)`,`Aspirate abscess; paradoxical reaction - continue ATT`],[`Pleural`,`2HRZE + 4HRE (6 months)`,`Therapeutic tap; no steroids`],[`Pericardial`,`2HRZE + 4HRE (6 months)`,`Pericardiocentesis for tamponade; consider prednisolone if HIV-negative; pericardiectomy if no response at 4-8 weeks`],[`Genitourinary`,`2HRZE + 4HRE (6 months)`,`Stent strictures; ultrasound at 6-8 weeks`],[`Spinal / osteoarticular`,`2HRZE + 7-10HRE (9-12 months)`,`Middle-path surgery for deficit, instability, deformity`],[`CNS (meningitis, tuberculoma)`,`2HRZE + 10HRE (12 months)`,`Dexamethasone (HIV-negative); delay ART 4-8 weeks; shunt for hydrocephalus`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with headache over 1-2 weeks, confusion, cranial nerve palsy or seizures - suspect TB meningitis; admit today`,`Breathlessness, hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds - tamponade; urgent echo and pericardiocentesis`,`Back pain with leg weakness, sensory level or bladder disturbance - cord compression from spinal TB; urgent MRI and spinal surgeon`,`Loin pain with falling urine output or rising creatinine on ATT - ureteric stricture and obstruction`,`Jaundice, vomiting or ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms on ATT - drug-induced hepatitis; stop HRZ`,`Rifampicin resistance on any EP specimen - refer to DR-TB centre`,`EPTB in a person with HIV and CD4 under 200 - disseminated TB likely; admit and start ART at the right time`],pearls:[`**Pleural and intrathoracic nodal TB without lung lesions are extrapulmonary; lung plus EP disease is notified as pulmonary.**`,`**Get tissue: NAAT, culture, histology - and a chest X-ray** in every EPTB.`,`**ADA over 40 U/L** in a lymphocytic exudate in a young Indian patient is TB until proved otherwise.`,`**Mesothelial cells over 5% argue against TB pleurisy.**`,`**6 months for most sites, 9-12 for spine, 12 for CNS.**`,`**Steroids: yes in TB meningitis (HIV-negative), maybe in pericarditis, no in pleural or nodal TB.**`,`**Paradoxical node enlargement is not failure** - continue ATT and aspirate.`,`**Aspirate a cold abscess from above through healthy skin - never incise.**`,`**Delay ART by 4-8 weeks in TB meningitis.**`,`**Sterile pyuria = think renal TB**: three early-morning urines for culture and Xpert.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-q1`,stem:`A 24-year-old man has a 3-week history of fever and right pleuritic pain. Pleural fluid is an exudate with 85% lymphocytes, ADA 78 U/L and mesothelial cells 1%. Xpert Ultra on the fluid is negative. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start ATT (2HRZE + 4HRE) without steroids`,`Repeat pleural fluid Xpert weekly until positive`,`Start prednisolone with ATT to prevent pleural thickening`,`Treat as parapneumonic effusion with ceftriaxone and review in 2 weeks`],answer:0,explanation:`A young patient with a lymphocytic exudate, ADA well over 40 U/L and few mesothelial cells in a high-prevalence country has TB pleurisy and should start standard ATT; Xpert on pleural fluid has only about 50% sensitivity, so a negative result does not exclude TB. Repeating Xpert only delays treatment, steroids do not reduce pleural thickening, and a parapneumonic effusion would be neutrophilic.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-q2`,stem:`Under NTEP, what is the total duration of treatment for drug-sensitive tuberculous meningitis in an adult?`,options:[`6 months`,`9 months`,`12 months`,`18 months`],answer:2,explanation:`CNS TB is treated with 2 months of HRZE followed by an extended continuation phase of 10 months of HRE, a total of 12 months. Six months is the standard for lymph node, pleural and most other EPTB, 9-12 months is used for spinal and osteoarticular TB, and 18 months is not a standard drug-sensitive regimen.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman on ATT for cervical TB lymphadenitis for 6 weeks has a new fluctuant node beside the original, which had initially shrunk. She is adherent, well and gaining weight. Xpert on the original aspirate showed rifampicin-sensitive TB. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Declare treatment failure and switch to a drug-resistant TB regimen`,`Incise and drain the node and stop ATT`,`Add a fluoroquinolone and extend treatment to 12 months`,`Continue the same ATT and aspirate the fluctuant node`],answer:3,explanation:`New or enlarging nodes after initial response in an adherent patient are a paradoxical reaction, seen in about 20-25% of TB lymphadenitis, and are not treatment failure; the same ATT continues and a fluctuant node is aspirated. Switching to a DR-TB regimen or adding a fluoroquinolone is unjustified without evidence of resistance, and incision leaves a chronic sinus.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-q4`,stem:`In which form of extrapulmonary tuberculosis has adjunctive corticosteroid therapy been shown to reduce mortality in HIV-negative adults?`,options:[`Tuberculous pleural effusion`,`Tuberculous meningitis`,`Tuberculous lymphadenitis`,`Genitourinary tuberculosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Dexamethasone reduced mortality in HIV-negative adults with tuberculous meningitis (Thwaites 2004), though the ACT HIV trial (2023) showed no benefit in HIV-positive patients. Steroids give no proven benefit in pleural effusion or lymphadenitis (except severe paradoxical reactions), and are not routinely used in genitourinary TB.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-q5`,stem:`A 35-year-old man has 4 months of back pain and weight loss. MRI shows involvement of the adjacent T10 and T11 vertebral bodies with relative preservation of the intervening disc, anterior subligamentous spread and a large thin-walled paraspinal abscess. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Pyogenic spondylodiscitis due to S. aureus`,`Metastatic carcinoma of the spine`,`Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott spine)`,`Brucella spondylitis`],answer:2,explanation:`A subacute course with paradiscal involvement, relative disc preservation early, subligamentous spread and a large thin-walled paraspinal abscess is classic for spinal TB, because mycobacteria lack proteolytic enzymes. Pyogenic spondylodiscitis destroys the disc early and is acute; metastases spare the disc and involve pedicles; brucellosis favours the lumbar spine with preserved architecture and small abscesses.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Classify extrapulmonary tuberculosis. Discuss the diagnosis and management of tuberculous lymphadenitis and tuberculous pleural effusion as per NTEP and INDEX-TB guidelines.`,openingLines:[`Extrapulmonary TB is TB of organs other than the lung parenchyma; in India it forms about one-fifth to one-quarter of notified TB, with lymph node and pleural TB the commonest sites.`,`Being paucibacillary, it is diagnosed by sampling tissue or fluid for NAAT, culture and histology, and most sites are treated with 2HRZE + 4HRE under NTEP.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By site: lymph node, pleural, abdominal, CNS, skeletal, genitourinary, pericardial, cutaneous, disseminated.`,`By bacteriology: microbiologically confirmed versus clinically diagnosed.`,`Lung plus EP disease is notified as pulmonary TB.`]},{heading:`TB lymphadenitis - diagnosis`,points:[`Painless matted cervical nodes; Jones and Campbell stages I-V.`,`FNAC with Xpert Ultra and culture; excision biopsy if inconclusive; chest X-ray, HIV test.`,`Differentials: lymphoma, metastasis, reactive, NTM.`]},{heading:`TB lymphadenitis - management`,points:[`2HRZE + 4HRE by weight-band FDC; pyridoxine if at risk.`,`Aspirate cold abscess from above; no incision.`,`Paradoxical reaction in 20-25%: continue ATT; residual nodes need no extension.`]},{heading:`TB pleural effusion - diagnosis`,points:[`Hypersensitivity reaction; lymphocytic exudate; ADA over 40 U/L; mesothelial cells under 5%.`,`Xpert on fluid about 50% sensitive; thoracoscopic biopsy when discordant.`,`Differentials: malignancy, parapneumonic effusion, lymphoma.`]},{heading:`TB pleural effusion - management`,points:[`2HRZE + 4HRE; therapeutic aspiration if large; no steroids.`,`Watch for empyema and trapped lung.`]},{heading:`Programme aspects`,points:[`Ni-kshay notification, universal DST, Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1000/month, adherence support, follow-up to 24 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of Jones and Campbell stages of TB lymphadenitis (collar-stud abscess)`,`Flow chart: pleural effusion - tap - lymphocytic exudate - ADA over 40 - ATT / biopsy`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`TB lymphadenitis`,marks:4},{part:`TB pleural effusion`,marks:3},{part:`Programme aspects`,marks:1}],keywords:[`EPTB`,`lymphadenitis`,`pleural effusion`,`ADA`,`CBNAAT`,`NTEP`,`INDEX-TB`,`paradoxical reaction`]},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-extrapulmonary-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the role of corticosteroids in extrapulmonary tuberculosis.`,openingLines:[`Corticosteroids are adjuncts to ATT that reduce inflammatory damage; their benefit is proven only in some forms of EPTB.`,`They are indicated in TB meningitis (HIV-negative), considered in pericarditis, and not recommended for pleural or nodal disease.`],answer:[{heading:`TB meningitis`,points:[`Dexamethasone 0.4 mg/kg/day (grade II-III) or 0.3 mg/kg/day (grade I), tapered over 6-8 weeks; reduces mortality (Thwaites 2004).`,`ACT HIV 2023: no survival benefit in HIV-positive adults.`]},{heading:`Pericarditis`,points:[`IMPI 2014: prednisolone 120 mg/day tapered over 6 weeks halved constriction, no mortality benefit; cancer risk in HIV.`,`Consider in HIV-negative patients with large effusion (ESC IIb).`]},{heading:`Other sites`,points:[`Pleural: no benefit on thickening or lung function.`,`Lymph node: only for severe paradoxical reactions; spinal: not routine.`,`Rifampicin induces steroid metabolism - doses are higher than usual.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: site, steroid, dose, evidence, verdict`],markSplit:[{part:`TB meningitis`,marks:2},{part:`Pericarditis`,marks:2},{part:`Other sites and cautions`,marks:1}],keywords:[`dexamethasone`,`prednisolone`,`TB meningitis`,`pericarditis`,`ACT HIV`,`IMPI`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis`,`ICMR. INDEX-TB Guidelines: Guidelines on Extra-pulmonary Tuberculosis for India, 2016`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP), MoHFW. Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB and Training Modules, 2021-2024; India TB Report 2024`,`WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis: Module 3 Diagnosis (2024 update) and Module 4 Treatment (2022; consolidated 2025)`,`Adler Y et al. 2015 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases`,`Thwaites GE et al. Dexamethasone for TB meningitis, N Engl J Med 2004; Donovan J et al. ACT HIV trial, N Engl J Med 2023; Mayosi BM et al. IMPI trial, N Engl J Med 2014`,`Bailey and Love's Short Practice of Surgery, 28th edition, 2023; Tuli SM. Tuberculosis of the Skeletal System, 5th edition, 2016`]});var u=[];u.push({id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb`,title:`Drug-resistant TB: MDR, pre-XDR and the BPaLM regimen`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Drug-resistant TB** is TB caused by M. tuberculosis resistant to one or more anti-TB drugs - **RR/MDR-TB** (rifampicin with or without isoniazid), **pre-XDR** (plus a fluoroquinolone) and **XDR** (plus bedaquiline or linezolid); under **NTEP** every diagnosed patient gets **universal DST by NAAT**, and most MDR/RR-TB and pre-XDR patients aged 14 or more are now treated with the all-oral **6-month BPaLM/BPaL regimen**.`,keywords:[`MDR-TB`,`RR-TB`,`pre-XDR`,`XDR-TB`,`BPaLM`,`BPaL`,`bedaquiline`,`pretomanid`,`linezolid`,`moxifloxacin`,`shorter oral regimen`,`line probe assay`,`CBNAAT`,`Truenat`,`universal DST`,`PMDT`,`Ni-kshay`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana`,`QTcF`,`Hr-TB`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Rifampicin-resistant TB (RR-TB)**: resistance to rifampicin detected by genotypic or phenotypic test, with or without resistance to other drugs; it is managed exactly like MDR-TB. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**MDR-TB**: resistance to at least **rifampicin and isoniazid**; **pre-XDR-TB**: MDR/RR-TB plus resistance to **any fluoroquinolone** (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin). [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**XDR-TB (2021 definition)**: MDR/RR-TB plus resistance to a fluoroquinolone **and at least one more Group A drug (bedaquiline or linezolid)**; the old definition based on second-line injectables was retired in 2021 because injectables are no longer core drugs. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**Hr-TB**: isoniazid-resistant, rifampicin-susceptible TB - a separate, commoner entity with its own 6-month regimen. [WHO 2025]`,`Globally about **4 lakh people developed MDR/RR-TB in 2023**, and only about two in five were diagnosed and started on treatment; India carries the largest single share, about **one quarter of the global MDR/RR-TB burden**. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`The **First National Anti-TB Drug Resistance Survey (2014-16)** found MDR-TB in **2.8% of new** and **11.6% of previously treated** patients, any isoniazid resistance in 11% of new patients, and **fluoroquinolone resistance in about 22% of MDR-TB** - the reason second-line LPA is mandatory. [NDRS India 2018]`,`India notified **over 60,000 MDR/RR-TB patients in 2023**, most through rapid molecular tests at peripheral NAAT sites. [India TB Report 2024]`,`Treatment success for MDR/RR-TB globally is about **68%** with older long regimens; the 6-month BPaLM regimen achieved favourable outcomes in about **89%** in TB-PRACTECAL. [WHO Global TB Report 2024; Nyang'wa NEJM 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Resistance arises by **spontaneous chromosomal mutation** (no plasmids) selected by inadequate therapy - monotherapy, erratic supply, poor adherence or adding a single drug to a failing regimen (**acquired resistance**). [Harrison 22e]`,`Most MDR-TB in India is now **primary (transmitted) resistance** - a new patient infected with a resistant strain - so absence of previous treatment does not exclude MDR-TB. [NDRS India 2018]`,`High-risk groups for testing priority: **previously treated** patients (relapse, treatment after loss to follow-up, failure), **contacts of DR-TB**, non-converters at end of intensive phase, PLHIV, and healthcare workers. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Private-sector irrational prescribing** - unsupervised fluoroquinolone courses for cough, non-standard regimens - drives fluoroquinolone resistance and pre-XDR. [India TB Report 2024]`,`**Diabetes, undernutrition, alcohol, tobacco and HIV** raise TB risk and worsen DR-TB outcomes; malabsorption (HIV, diabetes gastroparesis) lowers drug levels. [WHO 2025]`,`Congregate settings - **prisons, hostels, slums, hospitals without airborne infection control** - sustain transmission of resistant strains. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and drug mechanisms`,points:[`**Rifampicin resistance**: over **95% due to mutations in the 81-bp rifampicin-resistance-determining region (RRDR) of rpoB** - the exact target that Xpert/CBNAAT and Truenat probe, so a rapid test gives near-definitive rifampicin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isoniazid resistance**: **katG** mutations cause high-level resistance; **inhA promoter** mutations cause low-level resistance with **cross-resistance to ethionamide** - LPA reports which, and it decides whether high-dose isoniazid or ethionamide is still useful. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Fluoroquinolone resistance**: **gyrA/gyrB** mutations in DNA gyrase; second-line LPA detects them and defines pre-XDR. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Bedaquiline** (diarylquinoline) blocks the **c subunit of mycobacterial ATP synthase (atpE)**, killing both replicating and dormant bacilli; its very long terminal half-life (about 5 months) means the drug lingers after stopping. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Rv0678 (mmpR5) mutations** up-regulate the MmpS5-MmpL5 efflux pump and give **cross-resistance between bedaquiline and clofazimine** - a key emerging threat. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Pretomanid** (nitroimidazole prodrug, activated by the bacterial **Ddn nitroreductase**) inhibits mycolic-acid synthesis in replicating bacilli and releases **nitric oxide** that kills hypoxic, non-replicating bacilli - the sterilising partner in BPaL. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Linezolid** binds the **23S rRNA of the 50S subunit**; it also inhibits **human mitochondrial protein synthesis**, which explains time- and dose-dependent **myelosuppression, peripheral and optic neuropathy and lactic acidosis**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**QT prolongation** with bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, clofazimine and delamanid is due to **hERG potassium-channel blockade**; effects are additive, so ECG monitoring is built into every regimen. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification and drug grouping`,points:[`Classify by resistance pattern: **mono-resistant, poly-resistant, Hr-TB, RR-TB, MDR-TB, pre-XDR, XDR-TB** - examiners expect the 2021 WHO definitions. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`Classify by history: **new** (under 1 month of anti-TB treatment) versus **previously treated** (relapse, treatment after loss to follow-up, treatment failure, other). [NTEP 2024]`,`**WHO Group A** (use all three if possible): **levofloxacin or moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, linezolid**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Group B** (add one or both): **clofazimine, cycloserine or terizidone**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Group C** (to complete a regimen): **ethambutol, delamanid, pyrazinamide, imipenem-cilastatin or meropenem (with clavulanate), amikacin, ethionamide or prothionamide, p-aminosalicylic acid**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Kanamycin and capreomycin** are no longer recommended (worse outcomes); **amikacin** only if susceptible and audiometry is possible. [WHO 2025]`,`NTEP regimen families: **BPaLM/BPaL (6 months)**, **shorter oral bedaquiline-containing regimen (9-11 months)**, **longer oral M/XDR-TB regimen (18-20 months)**, and the **Hr-TB regimen (6 months)**. [NTEP PMDT 2021; NTEP BPaLM 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Symptoms are **identical to drug-sensitive TB** - cough over 2 weeks, fever, night sweats, weight loss, haemoptysis - resistance is a laboratory, not a clinical, diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspect DR-TB when a patient on first-line treatment **remains smear or culture positive at month 2-3**, or **deteriorates clinically or radiologically** despite adherence. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Ask a structured **previous-treatment history**: number of courses, drugs used, private versus programme, duration, adherence, and any **fluoroquinolone courses for cough** (pre-XDR risk). [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Ask about **contact with a known DR-TB patient** in the household or workplace - treat as DR-TB until DST returns if the patient is sick. [WHO 2025]`,`Screen for **comorbidity and drug-safety issues**: diabetes, HIV, alcohol, liver or kidney disease, psychiatric illness (cycloserine), pregnancy, palpitations or syncope (QT), and existing neuropathy or poor vision (linezolid). [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`Ask about **social barriers to adherence** - migration, daily-wage loss, alcohol, stigma, distance to the DR-TB centre - these predict loss to follow-up more than biology. [India TB Report 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Record **weight and BMI** at baseline - doses are weight-banded and a BMI under 18.5 qualifies for nutrition support; failure to gain weight is an early sign of failure. [NTEP 2024]`,`Look for **chronic lung damage**: clubbing, bronchial breathing, crepitations over upper zones, tracheal deviation from fibrosis, and signs of **cor pulmonale**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Examine for **extrapulmonary disease** - lymph nodes, effusion, spine tenderness, meningism - because CNS, osteoarticular or disseminated TB excludes BPaLM and needs a longer, individualised regimen. [WHO 2025]`,`Baseline **visual acuity (Snellen) and colour vision (Ishihara)** and a **peripheral neuropathy screen** (vibration, ankle jerks) are mandatory before linezolid. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`Check **pulse rhythm and BP**; palpitations or bradycardia point to QT risk; look for **jaundice and hepatomegaly** before pretomanid and pyrazinamide. [WHO 2025]`,`Look for signs of **HIV** (oral thrush, lymphadenopathy, wasting) and **diabetes** complications - both change drug choice and monitoring. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Non-adherent drug-sensitive TB**: persistent positivity due to missed doses - DST is sensitive and the fix is supervised treatment, not a new regimen. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM)**: smear positive but **NAAT negative for MTB**, or culture grows a non-MTB species; common in bronchiectasis and post-TB lungs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-TB lung disease**: fibrosis, bronchiectasis and cavities with chronic cough but **negative culture** - not active resistance. [WHO 2025]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis**: haemoptysis and cavity with fungal ball in a treated patient; **Aspergillus IgG** positive, TB tests negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung cancer** in an older smoker with a non-resolving mass or nodes - CT and biopsy before labelling treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paradoxical reaction or TB-IRIS** in PLHIV after ART: worsening nodes or infiltrates with falling bacillary load - do not change the TB regimen. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Universal DST**: every presumptive TB patient should get a **NAAT (CBNAAT/Xpert or Truenat)** as the first test, which reports MTB and **rifampicin resistance in about 2 hours**. [NTEP 2024]`,`On rifampicin resistance, send a **second sample** for **first-line LPA** (isoniazid: katG, inhA) and **second-line LPA** (fluoroquinolones: gyrA/gyrB; amikacin: rrs, eis) - the FQ result decides **BPaLM versus BPaL**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Do not wait** for LPA to start treatment in an RR-TB patient: start BPaLM (or the shorter regimen) and modify when the FQ result arrives. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`Send **liquid culture (MGIT) and phenotypic DST** for bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, delamanid and pretomanid in pre-XDR, failure or contacts of DR-TB - genotypic tests for these drugs are still limited. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**Targeted next-generation sequencing (tNGS)** detects resistance to many drugs at once, including bedaquiline and linezolid; WHO recommended it in 2024 and it is used at Indian reference laboratories. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`Baseline safety tests: **CBC, LFT, creatinine, K, Mg, Ca, TSH, blood glucose/HbA1c, HIV, pregnancy test, ECG with QTcF**, and audiometry if amikacin is planned. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**QTcF (Fridericia) over 500 ms** on any ECG means stop QT-prolonging drugs and correct electrolytes; **450-500 ms** means repeat ECG and closer monitoring. [WHO 2025]`,`Monitoring of response: **monthly sputum smear and culture**; **culture conversion** = two consecutive negative cultures taken at least 30 days apart. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Management: regimens and doses`,points:[`**BPaLM** for MDR/RR-TB aged **14 years or more**, FQ-susceptible or FQ status awaited: **bedaquiline + pretomanid + linezolid + moxifloxacin for 26 weeks (6 months)**. [WHO 2025; NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**BPaL** (moxifloxacin dropped) is used in **pre-XDR-TB** (FQ-resistant) for the same 26 weeks. [WHO 2025]`,`Doses: **bedaquiline 400 mg OD for 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times a week for 24 weeks**; **pretomanid 200 mg OD**; **linezolid 600 mg OD**; **moxifloxacin 400 mg OD** - all with food, for 26 weeks. [WHO 2025; NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Linezolid toxicity**: reduce to **300 mg OD** or interrupt for myelosuppression or neuropathy; ZeNix showed 600 mg for 26 weeks kept efficacy with less toxicity than 1200 mg. [Conradie NEJM 2022]`,`**BPaLM is not used** in pregnancy or breastfeeding, children under 14, **CNS, osteoarticular or disseminated TB**, or after more than 1 month of previous bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid or delamanid. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Shorter oral bedaquiline-containing regimen (9-11 months)**: 4-6 months of **bedaquiline (6 months), levofloxacin, clofazimine, pyrazinamide, ethambutol, high-dose isoniazid, ethionamide**, then 5 months of **levofloxacin, clofazimine, pyrazinamide, ethambutol** - for FQ-susceptible patients not eligible for BPaLM. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Longer oral M/XDR-TB regimen (18-20 months)**: all Group A plus Group B drugs (**bedaquiline, levofloxacin, linezolid, clofazimine, cycloserine**), individualised by DST - for XDR-TB, extensive EPTB, failure or intolerance. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Hr-TB**: **6 months of levofloxacin + rifampicin + ethambutol + pyrazinamide** once rifampicin susceptibility is confirmed. [WHO 2025; NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Adjuncts: **pyridoxine** with high-dose isoniazid, cycloserine or linezolid; **nutrition support**, smoking and alcohol cessation, and glycaemic control. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and DR-TB centres`,points:[`Treatment is initiated at a **Nodal/District DR-TB Centre** after pre-treatment evaluation; admission is for **sick patients, severe comorbidity, adverse events or where ambulatory care is not safe** - ambulatory care is the default. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Admit for **massive haemoptysis, respiratory failure, severe malnutrition, QTcF over 500 ms, hepatitis with jaundice, severe anaemia or neutropenia, psychosis, seizures** or pregnancy needing an individualised regimen. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Drug-induced hepatitis** (ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or over 5 times without, or bilirubin over 2 times): stop pretomanid, pyrazinamide and other hepatotoxic drugs, recheck LFT weekly, reintroduce under expert advice. [WHO 2025]`,`**QTcF over 500 ms**: stop bedaquiline, moxifloxacin and clofazimine, correct **K (keep over 4 mmol/L) and Mg**, repeat ECG, reintroduce one at a time once QTcF falls. [WHO 2025]`,`**Severe myelosuppression or optic neuritis** on linezolid: stop linezolid; optic neuritis means permanent discontinuation. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Airborne infection control** on the ward: separate DR-TB rooms with cross-ventilation, N95 respirators for staff, surgical masks for patients, cough hygiene. [NTEP Airborne Infection Control 2010]`,`**Massive haemoptysis** in cavitary DR-TB: lateral decubitus with bleeding side down, airway protection, tranexamic acid, and **bronchial artery embolisation**; consider **surgical resection** for localised disease with persistent positivity. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: BPaLM is avoided (no pretomanid safety data); use an individualised oral regimen with bedaquiline, linezolid, levofloxacin, clofazimine and cycloserine, avoiding **ethionamide (teratogenic)** and **aminoglycosides (fetal ototoxicity)**. [WHO 2025]`,`WHO 2025 added the 6-month **BDLLfxC** regimen (bedaquiline, delamanid, linezolid, levofloxacin, clofazimine) usable in **children and pregnant women** - adoption in India is under programme review. [WHO 2025]`,`**Children under 14**: shorter or longer oral regimens; **bedaquiline and delamanid are allowed at all ages**, with child-friendly formulations. [WHO 2025]`,`**PLHIV**: start **ART within 2-8 weeks** of DR-TB treatment; use **dolutegravir** - **efavirenz** induces CYP3A4 and lowers bedaquiline and pretomanid levels, and ritonavir raises bedaquiline levels. [WHO 2025]`,`Without rifampicin in the regimen, **dolutegravir is given at the standard 50 mg once daily** - no twice-daily dosing is needed. [WHO 2025]`,`**CKD**: adjust **levofloxacin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide and cycloserine** to creatinine clearance; bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin need no adjustment but monitor linezolid toxicity closely. [WHO 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: screen all; fluoroquinolones cause **dysglycaemia**, and linezolid neuropathy is commoner - aim for good glycaemic control from day 1. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Treatment outcomes: **cured, treatment completed, failed, died, lost to follow-up, not evaluated** - treatment success is cured plus completed. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`**Linezolid**: anaemia, thrombocytopenia, **peripheral neuropathy (often irreversible)**, **optic neuropathy**, lactic acidosis and serotonin syndrome with SSRIs. [WHO 2025]`,`**Bedaquiline**: QT prolongation, nausea, arthralgia, raised transaminases; **clofazimine**: brown-black skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, QT prolongation. [WHO 2025]`,`**Cycloserine**: depression, psychosis, suicidal ideation and seizures; **ethionamide**: GI upset, hypothyroidism; **amikacin**: irreversible hearing loss and nephrotoxicity. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pretomanid**: hepatotoxicity, and peripheral neuropathy when combined with linezolid; animal testicular toxicity has not been shown in humans. [WHO 2025]`,`Poor-prognosis features: **XDR-TB, HIV with low CD4, BMI under 16, extensive cavitation, diabetes, alcohol use and previous failure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relapse** after BPaLM is uncommon (under 5% in trials); most unfavourable outcomes are due to death or loss to follow-up rather than failure. [Nyang'wa NEJM 2022]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Sputum smear and culture monthly** throughout treatment; in BPaLM, culture positive at **month 4 or later** means evaluate for failure and repeat DST. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**ECG**: baseline, **week 2, then monthly** (more often if on several QT drugs or electrolyte loss); keep K and Mg normal. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**CBC** baseline, weeks 2 and 4, then monthly (linezolid); **LFT** monthly (pretomanid, pyrazinamide); **TSH** at 3 and 6 months with ethionamide or PAS. [WHO 2025]`,`**Visual acuity, colour vision and neuropathy screening monthly** on linezolid; refer to ophthalmology for any drop. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**Weight monthly** and dose re-banding; **post-treatment follow-up** with symptom check and sputum culture **every 6 months for 2 years**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Every visit, adverse event and result is entered in **Ni-kshay**; serious adverse events go through **aDSM (active drug-safety monitoring)** and PvPI. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, adherence and programme link`,points:[`**Prevent DR-TB** by treating drug-sensitive TB correctly - fixed-dose combinations, daily regimens, supervised adherence and **no fluoroquinolones for undiagnosed cough**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Contact tracing**: screen all household contacts of DR-TB with symptoms and chest X-ray; give **levofloxacin TPT for 6 months** to eligible contacts of MDR/RR-TB (FQ-susceptible) - see the latent TB topic. [WHO 2024 Module 1; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Adherence tools**: treatment supporter, **video-observed treatment**, MERM pill boxes, family DOT, Ni-kshay reminders; differentiated care for high-risk patients. [WHO 2025]`,`**Ni-kshay** is the notification and case-management portal; notification of every TB case, public or private, is **mandatory** (Gazette 2012, 2018). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana**: direct benefit transfer of **Rs 1000 per month** (raised from Rs 500 in November 2024) for the full duration of treatment; energy-dense nutrition supplements for underweight patients. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan**: Ni-kshay Mitras provide monthly food baskets; the **RATIONS trial** showed nutrition support reduced TB incidence in contacts by about 39-48%. [Bhargava Lancet 2023]`,`Free drugs, transport support, **DR-TB centres** with counselling, and **airborne infection control** in every facility. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2022**: 6-month **BPaLM/BPaL** recommended for MDR/RR-TB and pre-XDR aged 14 or more, replacing most 18-20 month regimens. [WHO 2022 Module 4]`,`**India 2024**: MoHFW approved **BPaLM** for NTEP in September 2024, with phased national roll-out from 2025 using domestically produced pretomanid. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`,`**WHO 2024-2025**: new 6-month options from endTB (for example **BLMZ, BLLfxCZ, BDLLfxZ**) and BEAT-Tuberculosis (**BDLLfxC**), with BDLLfxC usable in children and pregnancy. [WHO 2025 Module 4]`,`**WHO 2024 Module 3**: **targeted NGS** endorsed for detecting resistance to multiple drugs, including bedaquiline and linezolid. [WHO 2024 Module 3]`,`**WHO 2024 Module 1**: **6 months levofloxacin** TPT for contacts of MDR/RR-TB. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** increased to **Rs 1000 per month** in November 2024; Ni-kshay Mitra support extended to household contacts. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define MDR, pre-XDR and XDR **using the 2021 definitions** - quoting injectables in XDR is now a mark-losing error. [WHO 2021 definitions]`,`Write BPaLM doses exactly: **B 400 mg OD x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly; Pa 200 mg OD; L 600 mg OD; M 400 mg OD; 26 weeks**. [WHO 2025]`,`Classic viva: **why drop moxifloxacin in pre-XDR?** - the strain is FQ-resistant, so it adds only QT toxicity. [WHO 2025]`,`Classic viva: **how does Xpert detect rifampicin resistance?** - molecular beacons/probes over the 81-bp RRDR of rpoB. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **adding one drug to a failing regimen** - this is how resistance is amplified. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **baseline ECG, vision and pregnancy test** before BPaLM. [NTEP BPaLM 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in DR-TB`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Bedaquiline (B)`,`400 mg oral OD x 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times weekly; with food`,`24 weeks (BPaLM 26-week course)`,`QT prolongation, hepatitis, arthralgia; avoid with efavirenz`],[`Pretomanid (Pa)`,`200 mg oral OD with food`,`26 weeks`,`Hepatotoxicity, neuropathy with linezolid; not in pregnancy or under 14 years`],[`Linezolid (L)`,`600 mg oral OD (reduce to 300 mg for toxicity)`,`26 weeks in BPaLM`,`Myelosuppression, peripheral and optic neuropathy, lactic acidosis, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Moxifloxacin (M)`,`400 mg oral OD`,`26 weeks; dropped in pre-XDR (BPaL)`,`QT prolongation, dysglycaemia, tendinopathy`],[`Levofloxacin`,`750-1000 mg oral OD by weight`,`Shorter, longer and Hr-TB regimens; 6 months as TPT`,`Tendinopathy, QT, dysglycaemia; adjust in CKD`],[`Clofazimine`,`100 mg oral OD`,`Throughout shorter or longer regimen`,`Skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, QT prolongation`],[`Cycloserine`,`10-15 mg/kg/day oral (usually 500-750 mg)`,`Longer regimen`,`Depression, psychosis, seizures; give pyridoxine; avoid in epilepsy and severe CKD`],[`Delamanid`,`100 mg oral BD`,`24 weeks or longer`,`QT prolongation; allowed in children`]]},{heading:`Classification of drug-resistant TB (WHO 2021)`,columns:[`Type`,`Resistant to`,`NTEP regimen`],rows:[[`Hr-TB`,`Isoniazid, rifampicin susceptible`,`6 months levofloxacin + rifampicin + ethambutol + pyrazinamide`],[`RR-TB / MDR-TB`,`Rifampicin (with or without isoniazid)`,`BPaLM 6 months; shorter oral regimen if not eligible`],[`Pre-XDR-TB`,`MDR/RR plus any fluoroquinolone`,`BPaL 6 months; else longer oral regimen`],[`XDR-TB`,`MDR/RR plus fluoroquinolone plus bedaquiline or linezolid`,`Individualised longer regimen at expert centre`]]},{heading:`NTEP regimen comparison`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Drugs`,`Duration`,`Who`],rows:[[`BPaLM`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin`,`26 weeks`,`MDR/RR-TB, 14 years or more, not pregnant, no severe EPTB`],[`BPaL`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid`,`26 weeks`,`Pre-XDR-TB, same eligibility`],[`Shorter oral Bdq regimen`,`Bdq(6) Lfx Cfz Z E Hh Eto, then Lfx Cfz Z E`,`9-11 months`,`FQ-susceptible, not BPaLM eligible`],[`Longer oral M/XDR regimen`,`Bdq, Lfx, Lzd, Cfz, Cs, individualised`,`18-20 months`,`XDR, failure, extensive EPTB, intolerance`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests for drug resistance`,columns:[`Test`,`Detects`,`Turnaround`,`Use`],rows:[[`CBNAAT/Xpert, Truenat`,`MTB plus rifampicin resistance (rpoB RRDR)`,`About 2 hours`,`First test for every presumptive TB (universal DST)`],[`First-line LPA (MTBDRplus)`,`Rifampicin (rpoB), isoniazid (katG high-level, inhA low-level)`,`2-3 days`,`Hr-TB and isoniazid pattern`],[`Second-line LPA (MTBDRsl)`,`Fluoroquinolones (gyrA/B), amikacin (rrs, eis)`,`2-3 days`,`Every RR-TB; decides BPaLM versus BPaL`],[`Liquid culture DST (MGIT)`,`Bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, delamanid, pretomanid`,`4-6 weeks`,`Pre-XDR, failure, DR-TB contacts`],[`Targeted NGS`,`Many drugs including bedaquiline and linezolid`,`Days`,`Reference laboratories; complex resistance`]]},{heading:`Monitoring schedule on BPaLM`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Action threshold`],rows:[[`ECG with QTcF`,`Baseline, week 2, then monthly`,`Over 500 ms - stop QT drugs, correct K and Mg`],[`CBC`,`Baseline, weeks 2 and 4, then monthly`,`Hb under 8 g/dL or platelets under 50,000 - hold or reduce linezolid`],[`LFT`,`Baseline then monthly`,`ALT over 5 x ULN, or over 3 x with symptoms or jaundice - stop hepatotoxic drugs`],[`Vision and neuropathy screen`,`Monthly`,`Any drop in acuity or colour vision - stop linezolid`],[`Sputum smear and culture`,`Monthly`,`Positive at month 4 or later - evaluate for failure, repeat DST`]]}],redFlags:[`Massive haemoptysis (over 100-200 mL in 24 hours) or respiratory failure`,`QTcF over 500 ms, syncope or palpitations on bedaquiline, moxifloxacin or clofazimine`,`Jaundice or ALT over 5 times ULN on treatment`,`Sudden drop in vision or colour vision on linezolid`,`Haemoglobin under 8 g/dL, platelets under 50,000 or neutropenia on linezolid`,`Psychosis, suicidal thoughts or seizures on cycloserine`,`Culture still positive at month 4 or clinical worsening - suspect failure or XDR`,`Pregnancy, CNS TB or HIV with low CD4 - needs DR-TB centre and individualised regimen`],pearls:[`BPaLM = **B**edaquiline, **P**retom**a**nid, **L**inezolid, **M**oxifloxacin - 6 months, 14 years and above.`,`Pre-XDR: drop the **M** (BPaL) - the FQ no longer works.`,`XDR 2021 = MDR/RR + FQ + **bedaquiline or linezolid**; injectables are out of the definition.`,`Xpert reads the **81-bp RRDR of rpoB**; over 95% of rifampicin resistance lives there.`,`katG = high-level isoniazid resistance; **inhA = low-level plus ethionamide cross-resistance**.`,`Bedaquiline and clofazimine share resistance through **Rv0678** efflux.`,`QT drugs in DR-TB: **B, M, C, D** - bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, clofazimine, delamanid.`,`Never add a single drug to a failing regimen.`,`Ni-kshay Poshan is **Rs 1000 per month** since November 2024.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old man with sputum CBNAAT showing MTB detected, rifampicin resistance detected, has a second-line line probe assay that shows a gyrA mutation. Which regimen is preferred under current WHO and NTEP guidance?`,options:[`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin for 6 months`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid and linezolid for 6 months`,`Shorter oral bedaquiline regimen with levofloxacin for 9-11 months`,`Isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol with added levofloxacin`],answer:1,explanation:`A gyrA mutation means fluoroquinolone resistance, so this is pre-XDR-TB and BPaL (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid) for 26 weeks is used. BPaLM keeps moxifloxacin, which adds only QT toxicity against an FQ-resistant strain. The shorter regimen relies on levofloxacin and needs FQ susceptibility, and adding a drug to a first-line regimen amplifies resistance.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q2`,stem:`According to the 2021 WHO definitions, XDR-TB is MDR/RR-TB with resistance to a fluoroquinolone and to which additional drug?`,options:[`Amikacin, kanamycin or capreomycin`,`Ethionamide or cycloserine`,`Bedaquiline or linezolid`,`Pyrazinamide or ethambutol`],answer:2,explanation:`Since 2021 XDR-TB means MDR/RR-TB plus fluoroquinolone resistance plus resistance to at least one other Group A drug, which is bedaquiline or linezolid. The second-line injectables (amikacin, kanamycin, capreomycin) defined XDR in the old 2006 definition but are no longer core drugs. Ethionamide, cycloserine, pyrazinamide and ethambutol are Group B or C drugs and do not define XDR.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q3`,stem:`A woman on BPaLM for 8 weeks has an ECG showing QTcF 512 ms. Serum potassium is 3.1 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate immediate step?`,options:[`Stop bedaquiline and moxifloxacin, correct potassium and magnesium, and repeat the ECG`,`Continue all drugs and repeat the ECG in 1 month`,`Stop linezolid only and continue the rest`,`Switch moxifloxacin to levofloxacin and continue bedaquiline`],answer:0,explanation:`A QTcF over 500 ms is the threshold to stop QT-prolonging drugs (bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, clofazimine) and correct hypokalaemia and magnesium before cautious reintroduction. Continuing and waiting a month risks torsades. Linezolid does not prolong the QT interval. Levofloxacin also prolongs QT, so swapping fluoroquinolones while keeping bedaquiline does not remove the danger.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q4`,stem:`An isolate shows an inhA promoter mutation with no katG mutation. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`It causes high-level isoniazid resistance and isoniazid must never be used`,`It predicts bedaquiline cross-resistance`,`It indicates rifampicin resistance`,`It causes low-level isoniazid resistance with cross-resistance to ethionamide`],answer:3,explanation:`inhA promoter mutations cause low-level isoniazid resistance, so high-dose isoniazid may still work, but they also cause cross-resistance to ethionamide because both drugs act on InhA. High-level isoniazid resistance comes from katG mutations. Bedaquiline cross-resistance (with clofazimine) comes from Rv0678 efflux mutations, and rifampicin resistance from rpoB mutations.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-q5`,stem:`Which of the following patients with MDR-TB is NOT eligible for the BPaLM regimen under NTEP?`,options:[`A 16-year-old boy with pulmonary MDR-TB`,`A 40-year-old diabetic man with cavitary disease`,`A woman who is 14 weeks pregnant`,`A man living with HIV on dolutegravir-based ART`],answer:2,explanation:`BPaLM is not used in pregnancy or breastfeeding because pretomanid has no human safety data, so the pregnant woman needs an individualised oral regimen. Age 14 years or more is eligible, so the 16-year-old qualifies. Diabetes and cavitation are not exclusions. PLHIV are eligible when ART is dolutegravir-based, avoiding efavirenz, which lowers bedaquiline and pretomanid levels.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify drug-resistant tuberculosis. Describe the diagnosis and management of MDR/RR-TB under NTEP, including the BPaLM regimen and its monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Drug-resistant TB is disease caused by M. tuberculosis resistant to one or more anti-TB drugs; RR/MDR-TB involves rifampicin with or without isoniazid, pre-XDR adds fluoroquinolone resistance and XDR adds bedaquiline or linezolid resistance (WHO 2021).`,`India carries about a quarter of the global MDR/RR-TB burden, and NTEP now offers universal DST by NAAT and a 6-month all-oral BPaLM regimen (approved 2024).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By resistance: mono, poly, Hr-TB, RR-TB, MDR-TB, pre-XDR, XDR (2021 definitions).`,`By history: new versus previously treated (relapse, after loss to follow-up, failure).`,`WHO drug groups A (Lfx/Mfx, Bdq, Lzd), B (Cfz, Cs), C (E, Dlm, Z, carbapenem, Am, Eto, PAS).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Universal DST: CBNAAT/Truenat for every presumptive TB - MTB and rifampicin resistance in 2 hours.`,`RR detected: first-line and second-line LPA on a second sample; FQ result decides BPaLM versus BPaL.`,`Liquid culture DST for Bdq, Lzd, Cfz, Dlm in pre-XDR or failure; tNGS at reference laboratories.`,`Pre-treatment evaluation: CBC, LFT, RFT, K, Mg, TSH, glucose, HIV, pregnancy test, ECG QTcF, vision.`]},{heading:`BPaLM regimen`,points:[`Bedaquiline 400 mg OD x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly; pretomanid 200 mg OD; linezolid 600 mg OD; moxifloxacin 400 mg OD - 26 weeks.`,`Eligible: 14 years or more, not pregnant or breastfeeding, no CNS/osteoarticular/disseminated TB, under 1 month prior exposure to these drugs.`,`Pre-XDR: BPaL (drop moxifloxacin).`]},{heading:`Other regimens`,points:[`Shorter oral Bdq regimen 9-11 months for FQ-susceptible patients not eligible for BPaLM.`,`Longer oral 18-20 months individualised regimen for XDR, failure, extensive EPTB.`,`Hr-TB: 6 months Lfx + R + E + Z.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and adverse events`,points:[`Monthly smear and culture; culture conversion = 2 negatives 30 days apart.`,`ECG at baseline, week 2, monthly - stop QT drugs if QTcF over 500 ms.`,`CBC, vision, colour vision, neuropathy monthly for linezolid; LFT monthly for pretomanid.`,`Post-treatment follow-up 6-monthly for 2 years; aDSM reporting.`]},{heading:`Programme support`,points:[`Ni-kshay notification and tracking; Ni-kshay Poshan Rs 1000/month; Ni-kshay Mitra food baskets.`,`Adherence: treatment supporter, VOT, counselling; contact screening and 6 months levofloxacin TPT for eligible contacts.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagnostic algorithm: NAAT, then LPA, then BPaLM or BPaL`,`Table of BPaLM drugs, doses and monitoring`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm`,marks:2},{part:`BPaLM and other regimens with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Monitoring and adverse events`,marks:2},{part:`Programme support and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`MDR-TB`,`pre-XDR`,`BPaLM`,`LPA`,`QTcF`,`Ni-kshay`]},{id:`infectious-fever-drug-resistant-tb-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on bedaquiline and linezolid in drug-resistant TB: mechanism, dose and monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Bedaquiline (a diarylquinoline ATP-synthase inhibitor) and linezolid (an oxazolidinone) are WHO Group A drugs and the backbone of the 6-month BPaLM regimen.`,`Their main toxicities - QT prolongation and mitochondrial myelosuppression and neuropathy - set the monitoring schedule.`],answer:[{heading:`Bedaquiline`,points:[`Inhibits the c subunit of mycobacterial ATP synthase (atpE); kills dormant bacilli.`,`400 mg OD x 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times a week for 24 weeks, with food.`,`QT prolongation (hERG); ECG baseline, week 2, monthly; stop if QTcF over 500 ms.`,`Efavirenz lowers levels - use dolutegravir; Rv0678 mutations cause cross-resistance with clofazimine.`]},{heading:`Linezolid`,points:[`Binds 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit; also inhibits human mitochondrial protein synthesis.`,`600 mg OD for 26 weeks in BPaLM; reduce to 300 mg for toxicity.`,`Myelosuppression, peripheral and optic neuropathy, lactic acidosis, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs.`,`Monthly CBC, visual acuity, colour vision and neuropathy screen; stop for optic neuritis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: bedaquiline versus linezolid - mechanism, dose, toxicity, monitoring`],markSplit:[{part:`Bedaquiline`,marks:2.5},{part:`Linezolid`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`bedaquiline`,`linezolid`,`ATP synthase`,`QT`,`optic neuropathy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis chapter`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. Module 4: treatment and care, 2025`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. Module 3: diagnosis - rapid diagnostics for TB detection, 2024 update`,`Guidelines for Programmatic Management of Drug-Resistant Tuberculosis in India (PMDT), NTEP, MoHFW, 2021`,`NTEP Guidance on BPaLM regimen for MDR/RR-TB, Central TB Division, MoHFW, 2024`,`India TB Report 2024, Central TB Division, MoHFW`,`WHO Global Tuberculosis Report 2024`,`Nyang'wa BT et al. A 24-week, all-oral regimen for rifampin-resistant tuberculosis (TB-PRACTECAL). N Engl J Med 2022`]}),u.push({id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy`,title:`Latent TB infection and TB preventive treatment`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**TB infection (latent TB infection, LTBI)** is a state of persistent immune response to M. tuberculosis antigens **without clinical, radiological or microbiological evidence of active disease**; after active TB is **ruled out**, high-risk people (household contacts, PLHIV, immunosuppressed) receive **TB preventive treatment (TPT)** such as **6H, 3HP or 1HP** - or **6 months levofloxacin** for contacts of MDR-TB - under the NTEP TPT guidelines.`,keywords:[`LTBI`,`TB infection`,`TPT`,`IPT`,`IGRA`,`QuantiFERON`,`T-SPOT.TB`,`tuberculin skin test`,`Mantoux`,`Cy-TB`,`3HP`,`1HP`,`6H`,`4R`,`rifapentine`,`6Lfx`,`household contacts`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana`,`RATIONS trial`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**TB infection (TBI/LTBI)**: a positive immune response (IGRA, TST or TB antigen skin test) to M. tuberculosis antigens **with no active disease** - the person is **not infectious**. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`About **one quarter of the world's population** is estimated to carry TB infection; they are the reservoir from which most future TB cases arise. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`The **National TB Prevalence Survey of India (2019-21)** found TB infection (IGRA positive) in **31.3% of people aged 15 years or more** - roughly 35-40 crore Indians. [National TB Prevalence Survey 2019-21]`,`**Lifetime risk of progression** to active TB is about **5-10%**, half of it in the **first 2 years** after infection - so recent contacts gain most from TPT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PLHIV** are about **16 times more likely** to develop TB than HIV-negative people; TPT reduces TB and death in PLHIV independently of ART. [WHO Global TB Report 2024; TEMPRANO 2015]`,`TPT is a pillar of **India's NSP** and **TB Mukt Bharat**: the NTEP 2021 guideline expanded TPT beyond children under 5 and PLHIV to **all household contacts** of pulmonary TB. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`TB infection is now viewed as a **spectrum** - cleared infection, latent infection, **incipient TB** and **subclinical TB** - not a simple yes/no state. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Risk factors for progression - who needs TPT`,points:[`**Household contacts of bacteriologically confirmed pulmonary TB** - all ages; children under 5 have the highest risk of disseminated and meningeal TB. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**PLHIV** (adults, adolescents, children and infants exposed to TB), regardless of CD4 count, ART status or pregnancy. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Immunosuppression**: anti-TNF agents and other biologics, JAK inhibitors, long-term steroids (prednisolone 15 mg/day or more for 1 month or more), transplant candidates, chemotherapy. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Dialysis-dependent CKD, silicosis, diabetes (with other risk), undernutrition (BMI under 18.5), smoking and alcohol use** raise progression risk. [WHO 2024 Module 1; Harrison 22e]`,`**Silicosis** raises TB risk about 3-fold, and **CKD on dialysis** about 10-25-fold. [Harrison 22e]`,`Healthcare workers, prisoners, migrants from high-burden settings and people who use drugs are tested and treated where programmes allow. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Inhaled bacilli are engulfed by **alveolar macrophages**; in most people a **Th1 response (IFN-gamma, IL-12, TNF-alpha)** walls them off in a **granuloma**, where they persist in a slowly replicating or dormant state. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha** maintains granuloma integrity - which is why **anti-TNF drugs** (infliximab, adalimumab) cause reactivation, often extrapulmonary or disseminated, within months. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV** depletes CD4 T cells and impairs macrophage activation, so granulomas fail and infection progresses - risk rises even before CD4 falls. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IGRAs** measure IFN-gamma released by memory T cells stimulated by **ESAT-6 and CFP-10**, antigens encoded in the **RD1 region**, which is **absent from BCG and most NTM** - hence their specificity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TST** measures delayed-type (type IV) hypersensitivity to purified protein derivative, which shares antigens with **BCG and NTM** - hence false positives. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why TPT works**: the bacillary load in TBI is tiny, so **one or two drugs** for a short course sterilise it without selecting resistance - provided active TB (high load) is excluded first. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Rifapentine** has a long half-life, allowing **once-weekly (3HP)** or short daily (1HP) dosing; isoniazid kills replicating bacilli, rifamycins also kill semi-dormant bacilli. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Classification and TPT regimens`,points:[`**6H**: isoniazid daily for 6 months - the long-standing regimen, cheap, safe in pregnancy and with all ART. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**3HP**: **isoniazid + rifapentine once weekly for 12 weeks (12 doses)** - higher completion (82% versus 69% for 9H in PREVENT-TB) and less hepatotoxicity; age 2 years or more. [Sterling NEJM 2011; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**1HP**: **isoniazid + rifapentine daily for 28 days** - non-inferior to 9H in PLHIV (BRIEF-TB); age 13 years or more. [Swindells NEJM 2019; WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**3HR**: isoniazid + rifampicin daily for 3 months - useful in children (dispersible FDC). [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**4R**: rifampicin daily for 4 months - when isoniazid cannot be used (isoniazid-resistant index case, intolerance). [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**6Lfx**: levofloxacin daily for 6 months for contacts of **MDR/RR-TB** with FQ-susceptible index strain. [WHO 2024 Module 1; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`TBI is classified by **testing state**: tested positive, tested negative, untested - in India, untested high-risk contacts may still receive TPT once TB is excluded. [NTEP TPT 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical evaluation - history`,points:[`TB infection itself is **asymptomatic**; the clinical task is to **rule out active TB** before TPT. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Use the **four-symptom screen**: **current cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats** - any one positive means evaluate for active TB, not TPT. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`In children, ask about **poor weight gain, reduced playfulness, fever and cough**; any one means evaluate further. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Take the **index case history**: smear or NAAT status, rifampicin and fluoroquinolone resistance (decides 6Lfx), duration and closeness of contact. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Ask about **liver disease, alcohol, previous TPT or TB treatment, neuropathy, pregnancy** and concurrent drugs - **rifamycin interactions** with OCPs, warfarin, antiepileptics, methadone and protease inhibitors. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`In PLHIV ask **ART regimen** - 3HP is compatible with dolutegravir and efavirenz but not with protease inhibitors or nevirapine. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examination in TBI is **normal by definition**; look actively for signs that mean active TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`Check **weight, BMI and growth chart** (children) - weight loss or faltering growth needs active TB work-up. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Examine for **lymphadenopathy** (cervical TB), effusion, hepatosplenomegaly, spine tenderness and meningism. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **jaundice, hepatomegaly and signs of chronic liver disease** before isoniazid or rifamycins. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Check for **peripheral neuropathy** (isoniazid) in diabetes, alcohol use, HIV and malnutrition. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**BCG scar**: its presence or absence does not change interpretation of an IGRA; it matters only for TST. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Active pulmonary TB**: symptoms or abnormal chest X-ray - needs sputum NAAT and full treatment, never TPT (monotherapy breeds resistance). [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Subclinical TB**: no symptoms but **culture positive or CXR abnormal** - found in about half of prevalence-survey cases in India, which is why CXR is added to screening. [National TB Prevalence Survey 2019-21]`,`**BCG-related TST positivity**: positive TST with **negative IGRA**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NTM sensitisation**: TST positive, IGRA negative (except M. kansasii, M. marinum, M. szulgai, which contain ESAT-6/CFP-10). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Past treated TB**: tests stay positive for life - **IGRA and TST cannot measure cure or recent reinfection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**False-negative tests (anergy)**: advanced HIV, malnutrition, miliary TB, steroids, measles, young infants - a negative test never excludes infection in these groups. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,points:[`**Rule out active TB first**: symptom screen plus **chest X-ray** (WHO 2021 recommends CXR in contacts and PLHIV where available); any abnormality - sputum **NAAT**. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**TST (Mantoux)**: 0.1 mL (**2 TU PPD RT23 or 5 TU PPD-S**) intradermal on the forearm, read at **48-72 hours** as **induration (not erythema)**. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`TST positive cut-offs: **5 mm or more** in PLHIV, contacts, immunosuppressed and in NTEP practice; **10 mm or more** in other high-risk groups; **15 mm or more** in low-risk people. [CDC 2020; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**IGRA**: QuantiFERON-TB Gold Plus positive at **0.35 IU/mL or more** (TB1 or TB2 tube minus nil); T-SPOT.TB positive at **8 spots or more** (5-7 borderline). [Harrison 22e]`,`**IGRA advantages**: single visit, not affected by BCG, objective; disadvantages: cost, need for a laboratory, **indeterminate results** from low mitogen response in immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cy-TB** (Serum Institute of India): a **TB antigen-based skin test (TBST)** using ESAT-6 and CFP-10, read like a TST at 48-72 hours; positive at **5 mm or more** - IGRA specificity with TST convenience; introduced into NTEP in 2024. [WHO 2022; NTEP 2024]`,`**Neither IGRA nor TST diagnoses active TB** and neither should be used in adults with symptoms; they cannot distinguish TBI from disease. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Baseline **LFT** only in risk groups: alcohol use, liver disease, HIV, pregnancy and the 3 months postpartum, older age, hepatotoxic co-medication. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - regimens and doses`,points:[`**6H**: isoniazid **5 mg/kg (max 300 mg) daily** in adults, **10 mg/kg (7-15 mg/kg)** in children, for **6 months**, with **pyridoxine** (10-25 mg daily). [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**3HP (adults and 14 years or more, over 30 kg)**: **isoniazid 900 mg + rifapentine 900 mg once weekly x 12 doses**; children 2-14 years by weight band. [NTEP TPT 2021; WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**1HP (13 years or more)**: **isoniazid 300 mg + rifapentine 600 mg daily x 28 days**. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**3HR (children)**: isoniazid 10 mg/kg + rifampicin 15 mg/kg daily for **12 weeks**, using dispersible FDCs. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**4R**: rifampicin **10 mg/kg (max 600 mg) daily x 4 months** for adults (15 mg/kg in children). [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**6Lfx** for MDR/RR-TB contacts: levofloxacin **15-20 mg/kg daily** in children, **750-1000 mg daily by weight** in adults, for **6 months**. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Counsel** the patient that TPT is for an infection that may later become disease, that it is free under NTEP, and on symptoms of **hepatitis** (nausea, vomiting, dark urine, jaundice) and **3HP flu-like reaction**. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Record every TPT start and outcome in **Ni-kshay (TPT module)**; treatment completion = **80% or more doses within 133% of the planned time** (for example 6H within 9 months, 3HP 11 of 12 doses within 16 weeks). [NTEP TPT 2021]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and IPD issues`,points:[`**Hepatotoxicity**: commonest with isoniazid (risk rises with age, alcohol, liver disease); stop TPT if **ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms or over 5 x ULN without**. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**3HP systemic drug reaction**: flu-like illness, rash, hypotension or syncope in about **3-4%**, usually after the 3rd-4th dose - observe; stop if hypotension or syncope. [Sterling NEJM 2011]`,`**Isoniazid peripheral neuropathy** is prevented with pyridoxine; high-risk groups are diabetes, alcohol, HIV, pregnancy, malnutrition, CKD. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Rifamycin interactions**: halve the effectiveness of **oral contraceptives** (advise barrier method), lower warfarin, methadone, azoles and protease inhibitors. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Admission is rare: severe drug-induced hepatitis with jaundice or encephalopathy, or severe hypersensitivity. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Breakthrough active TB** during TPT: stop TPT, collect sputum for NAAT and DST, and start full treatment. [NTEP TPT 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **6H** is the preferred regimen; data for rifapentine are limited; in PLHIV, TB APPRISE showed more adverse pregnancy outcomes with antepartum isoniazid, so some defer TPT to postpartum when TB exposure is not recent. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**PLHIV**: give TPT regardless of CD4 and alongside ART; **3HP with dolutegravir** needs no dose change; avoid rifapentine with protease inhibitors and nevirapine. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Children under 5 in contact with TB**: TPT **without testing** once active TB is excluded; infants of mothers with TB get TPT and BCG is still given. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Before biologics** (anti-TNF, JAK inhibitors): test with IGRA and CXR; if positive, start TPT and ideally give **at least 1 month** before the biologic. [ACR 2022; BSR 2023]`,`**CKD on dialysis and transplant candidates**: test and treat; isoniazid needs no change; rifamycins interact with **tacrolimus and cyclosporine**. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**Elderly and liver disease**: monitor LFT monthly; prefer rifamycin-based short regimens with lower hepatotoxicity. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Efficacy: **6H reduces TB by about 60%** (up to 90% in those who complete); 3HP and 1HP are at least as effective with better completion. [Harrison 22e; WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`In PLHIV, TPT plus ART reduced **mortality by about 37%** in TEMPRANO, beyond ART alone. [TEMPRANO NEJM 2015]`,`Protection wanes with **ongoing exposure** in high-transmission settings - repeat screening, and in PLHIV WHO allows **36 months of isoniazid** in high-transmission settings. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Inadvertent TPT in **unrecognised active TB** risks **acquired isoniazid or rifamycin resistance** - the reason CXR and symptom screening come first. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Hepatotoxicity-related deaths are rare but occur, mainly with isoniazid in older patients and alcohol users. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Completion** is the main determinant of benefit; short regimens raise completion from about 50-60% (6H) to over 80%. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Review **monthly** (weekly or fortnightly for the first month in risk groups): adherence, symptoms of TB and of hepatitis, weight. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**LFT** monthly only in risk groups or if symptoms; routine LFT in all is not required. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Re-screen for active TB symptoms at **every visit**; any symptom - stop TPT and investigate. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Follow household contacts with **symptom screening for 2 years** after TPT, especially children and PLHIV. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Record outcomes in Ni-kshay: **completed, discontinued (toxicity), died, lost to follow-up, developed TB**. [NTEP TPT 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Contact investigation** is the entry point: every notified pulmonary TB case triggers a home visit to screen all household contacts and record them in **Ni-kshay**. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** pays **Rs 1000 per month** (from November 2024) to people **on TB treatment**, not to TPT recipients; nutrition reduces progression in contacts. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**RATIONS trial (Jharkhand)**: monthly food rations to household contacts cut incident TB by **about 39% (all forms) and 48% (microbiologically confirmed)**. [Bhargava Lancet 2023]`,`Under **PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan**, Ni-kshay Mitras give food baskets; support was extended to willing **household contacts** in 2024. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Adult BCG vaccination** of high-risk adults (elderly, contacts, past TB, diabetics, smokers, low BMI) was piloted in 2024 as part of TB Mukt Bharat. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Airborne infection control** at home - ventilation, cough hygiene, the index patient sleeping separately in the first weeks of treatment. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**NTEP 2021 TPT guideline**: TPT for **all household contacts** of pulmonary TB (not only under-5s), 3HP introduced, 6Lfx for MDR contacts. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**WHO 2022**: endorsed **TB antigen-based skin tests** (Cy-TB, C-Tb, Diaskintest) as alternatives to TST and IGRA. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2024 Module 1**: **6 months levofloxacin** recommended for contacts of MDR/RR-TB (VQUIN and TB-CHAMP trials); 1HP and 3HP endorsed across ages. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`**100-day TB Mukt Bharat campaign (December 2024-March 2025)**: intensified screening of vulnerable groups with CXR and NAAT, expanding TPT, nutrition and Cy-TB use. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** raised to **Rs 1000 per month** and Ni-kshay Mitra support extended to household contacts. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`First line of every answer: **rule out active TB before TPT** - symptom screen plus CXR. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Classic viva: **IGRA versus TST** - antigens, BCG effect, visits, cut-offs, cost. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic viva: **3HP dose** - isoniazid 900 mg + rifapentine 900 mg weekly x 12. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Classic viva: **TPT for contacts of MDR-TB** - 6 months levofloxacin. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Common mistake: using IGRA or TST to **diagnose active TB** in a symptomatic adult. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **rifamycin-OCP interaction** in women on 3HP or 1HP. [WHO 2024 Module 1]`]}],tables:[{heading:`TPT regimens`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`6H - isoniazid`,`5 mg/kg (max 300 mg) oral daily; child 10 mg/kg; with pyridoxine`,`6 months (180 doses)`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy; safe in pregnancy and with all ART`],[`3HP - isoniazid + rifapentine`,`H 900 mg + P 900 mg oral once weekly (adult over 30 kg); child by weight`,`12 weeks (12 doses)`,`Flu-like reaction, hypotension; not with PIs or nevirapine; OCP failure`],[`1HP - isoniazid + rifapentine`,`H 300 mg + P 600 mg oral daily`,`28 days`,`As 3HP; 13 years or more`],[`3HR - isoniazid + rifampicin`,`H 10 mg/kg + R 15 mg/kg oral daily`,`3 months`,`Hepatitis, orange urine; interactions`],[`4R - rifampicin`,`10 mg/kg (max 600 mg) oral daily; child 15 mg/kg`,`4 months`,`Interactions; for isoniazid-resistant contacts`],[`6Lfx - levofloxacin`,`Adult 750-1000 mg oral daily by weight; child 15-20 mg/kg`,`6 months`,`Tendinopathy, QT, GI upset; MDR/RR-TB contacts`]]},{heading:`IGRA versus TST versus Cy-TB`,columns:[`Feature`,`TST (Mantoux)`,`IGRA`,`Cy-TB (TBST)`],rows:[[`Antigen`,`PPD (many shared antigens)`,`ESAT-6, CFP-10 (RD1)`,`ESAT-6, CFP-10 (RD1)`],[`BCG effect`,`False positive`,`None`,`None`],[`Visits`,`Two (read at 48-72 h)`,`One (blood draw)`,`Two (read at 48-72 h)`],[`Positive cut-off`,`5, 10 or 15 mm by risk`,`QFT 0.35 IU/mL or more; T-SPOT 8 spots`,`5 mm induration or more`],[`Laboratory`,`None`,`Required`,`None`],[`Distinguishes active TB`,`No`,`No`,`No`]]},{heading:`Who gets TPT under NTEP`,columns:[`Group`,`Test before TPT`,`Regimen options`],rows:[[`Household contacts under 5 years`,`No test; rule out TB`,`6H or 3HR (3HP from 2 years)`],[`Household contacts 5 years or more`,`TBI test where available; TB ruled out`,`6H or 3HP`],[`PLHIV (all ages)`,`No test needed; rule out TB`,`6H, 3HP (with DTG or EFV), 1HP`],[`Contacts of MDR/RR-TB`,`Rule out TB; index FQ susceptible`,`6Lfx`],[`Anti-TNF, transplant, dialysis, silicosis`,`IGRA or TST, CXR`,`6H, 3HP or 4R`]]},{heading:`Ruling out active TB before TPT`,columns:[`Step`,`What is done`,`If abnormal`],rows:[[`Symptom screen`,`Cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats (children: poor growth)`,`Evaluate for active TB`],[`Chest X-ray`,`Any abnormality`,`Sputum NAAT`],[`Sputum NAAT`,`CBNAAT or Truenat`,`MTB detected - full treatment, no TPT`],[`All negative`,`Active TB excluded`,`Start TPT and record in Ni-kshay`]]}],redFlags:[`Any TB symptom (cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats) or abnormal CXR - evaluate for active TB, do not give TPT`,`Jaundice, vomiting or abdominal pain on TPT (ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms)`,`Hypotension, syncope or severe flu-like reaction after a 3HP dose`,`Child under 5 contact with poor weight gain, fever or lethargy - exclude TB meningitis and disseminated TB`,`PLHIV with low CD4 and any symptom - investigate urgently for active TB`,`Contact of MDR-TB index - needs DR-TB centre input and 6Lfx, not isoniazid`],pearls:[`**Rule out TB, then treat infection** - TPT given to active TB breeds resistance.`,`IGRA antigens **ESAT-6 and CFP-10** sit in **RD1**, missing from BCG.`,`IGRA and TST **cannot tell latent from active** TB.`,`**3HP** = H 900 + P 900 weekly x 12; **1HP** = H 300 + P 600 daily x 28.`,`MDR contact = **6 months levofloxacin**.`,`India: **31.3%** of adults have TB infection (Prevalence Survey 2019-21).`,`RATIONS: food baskets cut TB in contacts by about **40%**.`,`Rifapentine and rifampicin make **oral contraceptives fail** - advise barrier method.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman lives with her husband, who has sputum-positive drug-sensitive pulmonary TB. She is asymptomatic and her chest X-ray is normal. Under NTEP, which is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Start TB preventive treatment, such as 3HP, after ruling out active TB`,`Start four-drug anti-TB treatment for 6 months`,`No action unless she develops symptoms`,`Repeat chest X-ray every month for a year without treatment`],answer:0,explanation:`A household contact with no symptoms and a normal CXR has active TB excluded and should receive TPT (6H or 3HP) under the NTEP 2021 guideline. Full four-drug treatment is for active disease. Waiting for symptoms or serial X-rays wastes the 2-year window in which most progression occurs. She should be counselled that rifapentine reduces oral contraceptive efficacy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-q2`,stem:`Which property makes interferon-gamma release assays more specific than the tuberculin skin test in a BCG-vaccinated population?`,options:[`They measure antibody against lipoarabinomannan`,`They use ESAT-6 and CFP-10 antigens encoded in the RD1 region that BCG lacks`,`They detect mycobacterial DNA in blood`,`They are read at 48-72 hours as induration`],answer:1,explanation:`IGRAs stimulate T cells with ESAT-6 and CFP-10, encoded in the RD1 region deleted from all BCG strains and most NTM, so BCG does not cause false positives. They measure interferon-gamma from T cells, not antibody or DNA. Reading induration at 48-72 hours describes the TST and the Cy-TB skin test, not a blood assay.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-q3`,stem:`A 10-year-old child is a household contact of a patient with confirmed MDR-TB whose isolate is fluoroquinolone susceptible. Active TB has been excluded. Which TPT regimen is recommended?`,options:[`Isoniazid daily for 6 months`,`Isoniazid and rifapentine weekly for 12 weeks`,`Rifampicin daily for 4 months`,`Levofloxacin daily for 6 months`],answer:3,explanation:`WHO 2024 (based on VQUIN and TB-CHAMP) and NTEP recommend 6 months of levofloxacin for contacts of MDR/RR-TB with a fluoroquinolone-susceptible strain. Isoniazid and rifamycin regimens (6H, 3HP, 4R) are useless because the index strain is resistant to rifampicin and usually isoniazid. 4R is reserved for contacts of isoniazid-resistant, rifampicin-susceptible TB.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-q4`,stem:`A man on weekly 3HP develops fever, myalgia, dizziness and a BP of 84/50 mmHg two hours after his fourth dose. What is the most appropriate action?`,options:[`Continue 3HP and give paracetamol with the next dose`,`Halve the rifapentine dose and continue`,`Stop 3HP, manage hypotension, and switch to an alternative such as 6H`,`Add prednisolone and continue 3HP`],answer:2,explanation:`This is a 3HP systemic drug reaction; hypotension or syncope means stopping rifapentine permanently, supportive treatment and switching to an isoniazid-only regimen once recovered. A mild flu-like reaction without hypotension can be observed with paracetamol, but not this one. Dose halving and steroid cover are not recommended and risk recurrence.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-q5`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis is to start adalimumab. QuantiFERON-TB Gold Plus is 1.2 IU/mL and chest X-ray is normal. She has no symptoms. What is best?`,options:[`Start adalimumab now and recheck QuantiFERON in 6 months`,`Start TPT and begin adalimumab after at least 1 month of TPT`,`Start four-drug anti-TB treatment before adalimumab`,`Avoid all biologics for life`],answer:1,explanation:`A positive IGRA (0.35 IU/mL or more) with normal CXR and no symptoms means TB infection; anti-TNF agents disrupt granulomas, so TPT is started first and the biologic ideally after at least a month. Starting adalimumab untreated risks disseminated TB, repeat IGRA adds nothing, four-drug therapy is for active disease, and biologics are not barred once TPT is under way.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define latent TB infection. Discuss the diagnosis of TB infection and the TB preventive treatment options and target groups under NTEP.`,openingLines:[`TB infection is a state of persistent immune response to M. tuberculosis antigens without clinical, radiological or microbiological evidence of active disease; it affects a quarter of the world and 31.3% of Indian adults.`,`After excluding active TB, NTEP 2021 offers TPT (6H, 3HP, 6Lfx) to household contacts, PLHIV and other high-risk groups, a key pillar of TB Mukt Bharat.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`Spectrum: cleared, latent, incipient, subclinical TB; lifetime progression 5-10%, half in 2 years.`,`India: 31.3% IGRA positive (Prevalence Survey 2019-21); PLHIV 16 times higher risk.`]},{heading:`Target groups`,points:[`Household contacts of pulmonary TB - all ages; under 5 without testing.`,`PLHIV regardless of CD4, ART or pregnancy.`,`Anti-TNF, transplant, dialysis, silicosis, long-term steroids - test and treat.`]},{heading:`Rule out active TB`,points:[`Four-symptom screen; CXR; sputum NAAT if any abnormality.`,`Never give TPT to active TB.`]},{heading:`Tests for TB infection`,points:[`TST: 2 TU RT23, read 48-72 h; 5 mm in contacts/PLHIV, 10 mm, 15 mm.`,`IGRA: QFT-Plus 0.35 IU/mL or more; T-SPOT 8 spots; unaffected by BCG.`,`Cy-TB: ESAT-6/CFP-10 skin test, 5 mm or more; neither distinguishes active TB.`]},{heading:`Regimens`,points:[`6H 5 mg/kg (max 300) x 6 months + pyridoxine.`,`3HP: H 900 + P 900 weekly x 12; 1HP: H 300 + P 600 daily x 28 days.`,`4R, 3HR; 6Lfx for MDR/RR-TB contacts.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and programme`,points:[`Monthly review for adherence, TB symptoms and hepatitis; LFT in risk groups.`,`Stop if ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms or 5 x without; 3HP hypotension - stop.`,`Ni-kshay TPT module; nutrition (RATIONS); Ni-kshay Mitra support for contacts.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: contact identified, symptom screen and CXR, test, TPT or TB treatment`,`Table: TPT regimens with doses`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Target groups`,marks:2},{part:`Ruling out TB and tests`,marks:3},{part:`Regimens with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Monitoring and programme`,marks:1}],keywords:[`LTBI`,`TPT`,`IGRA`,`TST`,`3HP`,`6Lfx`]},{id:`infectious-fever-tb-preventive-therapy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between the tuberculin skin test and interferon-gamma release assays.`,openingLines:[`Both TST and IGRA detect cell-mediated immune memory to M. tuberculosis and are tests for TB infection, not for active disease.`,`They differ in antigen, effect of BCG, logistics and cut-offs.`],answer:[{heading:`Principle and antigen`,points:[`TST: type IV hypersensitivity to PPD, shared with BCG and NTM.`,`IGRA: IFN-gamma release from T cells to ESAT-6 and CFP-10 (RD1), absent in BCG.`]},{heading:`Logistics and reading`,points:[`TST: 2 visits, read induration at 48-72 h; cheap, no laboratory; reader variability, boosting.`,`IGRA: 1 visit, laboratory-based, objective; costly; indeterminate in immunosuppression.`]},{heading:`Cut-offs and use`,points:[`TST: 5, 10, 15 mm by risk; IGRA: QFT 0.35 IU/mL, T-SPOT 8 spots.`,`IGRA preferred in BCG-vaccinated adults and those unlikely to return; TST or Cy-TB in field settings.`,`Neither distinguishes latent from active TB or measures cure.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table TST versus IGRA`],markSplit:[{part:`Principle and antigen`,marks:2},{part:`Logistics`,marks:1.5},{part:`Cut-offs and use`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`TST`,`IGRA`,`ESAT-6`,`CFP-10`,`BCG`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis chapter`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis. Module 1: prevention - tuberculosis preventive treatment, 2024 update`,`Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB Preventive Treatment in India, NTEP, MoHFW, 2021`,`National TB Prevalence Survey in India 2019-2021, ICMR and MoHFW, 2022`,`India TB Report 2024, Central TB Division, MoHFW`,`Bhargava A et al. Nutritional support for adult patients with pulmonary tuberculosis and household contacts (RATIONS). Lancet 2023`,`Sterling TR et al. Three months of rifapentine and isoniazid for latent tuberculosis infection (PREVENT TB). N Engl J Med 2011`]}),u.push({id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions`,title:`Leprosy: classification, MDT and reactions (medicine view)`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Leprosy (Hansen disease)** is a chronic granulomatous infection by **Mycobacterium leprae** of skin and **peripheral nerves**, diagnosed by **one of three cardinal signs** (anaesthetic patch, thickened nerve with sensory or motor loss, or AFB on slit-skin smear), treated with **3-drug MDT (rifampicin, clofazimine, dapsone) - 6 months for PB and 12 months for MB** - and complicated by immune-mediated **type 1 (reversal) and type 2 (ENL) reactions** that cause most of the nerve damage.`,keywords:[`Hansen disease`,`Mycobacterium leprae`,`NLEP`,`MDT`,`paucibacillary`,`multibacillary`,`Ridley-Jopling`,`type 1 reaction`,`reversal reaction`,`erythema nodosum leprosum`,`ENL`,`Lucio phenomenon`,`thalidomide`,`clofazimine`,`dapsone`,`neuritis`,`SDR-PEP`,`slit-skin smear`,`Nikusth`,`grade 2 disability`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Leprosy** is a chronic infection by **M. leprae** (and rarely **M. lepromatosis**) affecting skin, peripheral nerves, upper respiratory mucosa and eyes; nerve damage causes the disability and stigma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Globally about **1.8 lakh new cases** are reported each year; **India reports over half (about 55-60%)** of them, followed by Brazil and Indonesia. [WHO Weekly Epidemiological Record 2024]`,`India achieved **elimination as a public health problem (prevalence under 1 per 10,000)** nationally in **December 2005**, but pockets of high endemicity persist in several states and districts. [NLEP 2023]`,`India still detects **over 1 lakh new cases a year**, with a high proportion of **MB cases, child cases and grade 2 disability** - markers of ongoing transmission and late detection. [NLEP 2023]`,`The **National Strategic Plan and Roadmap for Leprosy 2023-2027** targets **interruption of transmission (zero autochthonous cases) by 2027**, ahead of SDG 2030. [NLEP NSP 2023-27]`,`**Child cases** (under 15) indicate recent transmission; **grade 2 disability per million** indicates delayed diagnosis - both are key programme indicators. [WHO Global Leprosy Strategy 2021-2030]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and transmission`,points:[`**M. leprae** is an **obligate intracellular**, acid-fast (weakly, 5% sulphuric acid decolourisation) bacillus that **cannot be cultured in vitro**; grown in mouse footpad and nine-banded armadillo. [Harrison 22e]`,`It has the **longest doubling time of any bacterium (12-14 days)** and prefers **cooler body sites (27-30 C)** - ear lobes, nose, testes, superficial nerves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Incubation is long**: about **2-5 years for PB** and **5-10 years or more for MB**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Transmission is mainly by **nasal droplets from untreated MB patients** after prolonged close contact; **household contacts** of MB patients have 5-10 times higher risk. [WHO 2018]`,`Most exposed people do not develop disease - **over 95% have natural immunity**; genetic factors (PARK2, LRRK2, NOD2, HLA-DR) influence susceptibility and reaction risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors: **close contact, poverty, overcrowding, undernutrition**; HIV does not clearly increase leprosy incidence but causes **IRIS-type reactions** after ART. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`M. leprae enters **Schwann cells** by binding of its **phenolic glycolipid-1 (PGL-1)** to the **laminin-alpha2** of the Schwann-cell basal lamina, causing demyelination and nerve damage. [Harrison 22e]`,`The clinical spectrum mirrors **host cell-mediated immunity**: strong **Th1** response (IFN-gamma, IL-2) gives **tuberculoid** disease with few bacilli; weak Th1 and **Th2/regulatory** response (IL-4, IL-10) gives **lepromatous** disease with huge bacillary loads. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tuberculoid** lesions are well-formed epithelioid granulomas that **destroy nerves early** (focal, asymmetric); **lepromatous** lesions are foamy macrophages (**Virchow lepra cells**) full of bacilli and **globi**, with diffuse symmetrical nerve involvement later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 1 reaction** is a sudden **increase in cell-mediated immunity (type IV hypersensitivity)** against M. leprae antigens in **borderline** patients - inflammation within skin lesions and nerves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 2 reaction (ENL)** is an **immune-complex (type III) reaction** with neutrophilic vasculitis and high **TNF-alpha**, in **BL and LL** patients with high bacterial load - often after MDT kills bacilli and releases antigen. [Harrison 22e]`,`This explains the drugs: **steroids** suppress both reactions; **thalidomide** inhibits TNF-alpha in ENL; **clofazimine** has anti-inflammatory action useful in ENL. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nerve damage** is due to bacillary invasion, granulomatous inflammation and **oedema within a tight perineurium** at sites of entrapment (ulnar at elbow, common peroneal at fibular neck) - the rationale for urgent steroids in neuritis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Ridley-Jopling** (immunological): **TT, BT, BB, BL, LL**, plus **indeterminate**; borderline forms are immunologically unstable and prone to type 1 reactions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**WHO/NLEP operational**: **PB** = 1-5 skin lesions, no nerve or only one nerve involved, **slit-skin smear negative**; **MB** = 6 or more lesions, or more than one nerve involved, or **smear positive regardless of lesion count**. [NLEP 2023; WHO 2018]`,`**Pure neuritic leprosy**: nerve involvement without skin lesions - common in India (about 5-10% of cases); classified and treated as MB if more than one nerve is involved. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Histoid leprosy**: a variant of LL with shiny dome-shaped nodules on normal skin, very high bacterial load, often linked to dapsone monotherapy resistance. [IAL Textbook of Leprosy 2016]`,`**Bacteriological index (BI)**: Ridley logarithmic scale **0 to 6+** (6+ = over 1000 bacilli per oil-immersion field); **morphological index (MI)** = percentage of solidly staining (viable) bacilli. [Harrison 22e]`,`**WHO disability grading**: **grade 0** no impairment; **grade 1** loss of sensation without visible damage; **grade 2** visible deformity (claw hand, foot drop, ulcers, lagophthalmos, visual acuity under 6/60). [WHO 2018]`,`**Reactions**: type 1 (reversal), type 2 (ENL), and **Lucio phenomenon** (necrotising vasculitis in diffuse non-nodular LL). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Commonest presentation: a **hypopigmented or erythematous patch with reduced sensation**, not itchy, not sweating, slowly enlarging. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Neurological symptoms**: numbness or tingling of hands and feet, **painless burns or injuries**, weakness of grip, dropping slippers (foot drop), difficulty closing the eyes. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Lepromatous features**: nasal stuffiness, **epistaxis**, nodules on ear lobes and face, loss of eyebrows (**madarosis**), gynaecomastia and testicular atrophy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 1 reaction history**: existing patches suddenly become **red, swollen and tender**, new lesions appear, hand or foot swelling, and **new nerve pain or weakness** - often within the first 6-12 months of MDT or postpartum. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Type 2 reaction history**: crops of **painful red nodules**, fever, malaise, joint pains, **red painful eyes**, testicular pain - in a BL/LL patient during or after MDT; often **recurrent or chronic**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Silent neuropathy**: loss of nerve function **without pain or tenderness** - only found by routine sensory and motor testing. [NLEP 2023]`,`Ask about **household contacts**, previous treatment (dapsone monotherapy era), duration, and drug history before dapsone (sulfa allergy, **G6PD deficiency**). [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Cardinal signs (NLEP/WHO)** - any one confirms leprosy: (1) **definite loss of sensation** in a pale or reddish patch; (2) **thickened or enlarged peripheral nerve** with loss of sensation and/or weakness; (3) **AFB in slit-skin smear**. [NLEP 2023]`,`Test sensation in patches with a **wisp of cotton, ballpoint pen or monofilament**; **sweating and hair loss** in the patch support the diagnosis. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Palpate nerves**: **ulnar (commonest)** at the elbow, **greater auricular** in the neck, radial cutaneous at the wrist, median, **common peroneal** at the fibular neck, **posterior tibial** behind the medial malleolus; note tenderness. [IAL Textbook of Leprosy 2016]`,`**Voluntary muscle testing (VMT) and sensory testing (ST)**: little-finger abduction (ulnar), thumb abduction (median), wrist extension (radial), ankle dorsiflexion (peroneal), eye closure (facial), monofilament on palms and soles. [NLEP 2023]`,`Deformities: **claw hand** (ulnar plus median), **wrist drop** (radial), **foot drop** (common peroneal), **lagophthalmos** (facial), **trophic ulcers** on soles, resorption of digits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LL signs**: diffuse infiltration, **leonine facies**, ear-lobe nodules, saddle nose (nasal septum collapse), madarosis, ichthyosis, symmetric glove-and-stocking anaesthesia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eyes**: corneal anaesthesia, exposure keratitis, and **iridocyclitis** in ENL - check visual acuity and red eye at every visit. [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pityriasis versicolor**: fine scaly hypopigmented macules, **normal sensation**, KOH shows spaghetti-and-meatballs. [IADVL 2021]`,`**Vitiligo**: depigmented (milky white), not hypopigmented, **sensation normal**, no nerve thickening. [IADVL 2021]`,`**Pityriasis alba and post-inflammatory hypopigmentation**: ill-defined, children, normal sensation. [IADVL 2021]`,`**Tinea corporis, granuloma annulare, sarcoidosis, cutaneous TB (lupus vulgaris)**: annular plaques without anaesthesia; biopsy distinguishes. [IADVL 2021]`,`**Other neuropathies** (diabetic, hereditary, CTS, ulnar entrapment): no skin patches and **no nerve thickening** except in hereditary (CMT, amyloid). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ENL mimics**: erythema nodosum from TB, sarcoid or drugs (no LL features), **cellulitis, vasculitis, Sweet syndrome**; type 1 reaction mimics cellulitis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Diagnosis is **clinical** in most cases under NLEP; investigations support classification and difficult cases. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Slit-skin smear (SSS)**: from both ear lobes, forehead and an active lesion, stained by **modified Ziehl-Neelsen (5% H2SO4)**; report **BI and MI**; positive smear = MB. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Skin biopsy**: granulomas around nerves (TT), foamy macrophages with bacilli (LL), **Fite-Faraco stain**; nerve biopsy in pure neuritic leprosy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lepromin test (Mitsuda reaction at 3-4 weeks)**: positive in TT, negative in LL - **prognostic, not diagnostic**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PCR for M. leprae DNA** and **anti-PGL-1 serology** (high in MB) - research and reference laboratories; PCR also detects **rifampicin (rpoB), dapsone (folP1) and ofloxacin (gyrA) resistance**. [WHO 2018]`,`**Nerve conduction studies and high-resolution ultrasound** detect early and silent neuritis. [IAL Textbook of Leprosy 2016]`,`Before MDT and reaction treatment: **CBC, G6PD (before dapsone), LFT, RFT, blood glucose**; screen for **TB (CXR)** and **strongyloides** before long steroid courses; **pregnancy test** before thalidomide. [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Management - MDT`,points:[`**Adult MDT (both PB and MB)**: **rifampicin 600 mg + clofazimine 300 mg once monthly supervised**, plus **clofazimine 50 mg daily + dapsone 100 mg daily** self-administered. [WHO 2018; NLEP 2023]`,`**Duration**: **PB 6 months** (6 blister packs within 9 months); **MB 12 months** (12 packs within 18 months). [NLEP 2023]`,`**Uniform 3-drug regimen for PB**: WHO (2018) and NLEP now add **clofazimine to PB MDT** - simpler logistics and cover for MB patients misclassified as PB. [WHO 2018; NLEP 2023]`,`**Child 10-14 years**: rifampicin 450 mg + clofazimine 150 mg monthly; clofazimine 50 mg alternate days + dapsone 50 mg daily. **Under 10**: rifampicin 10 mg/kg, clofazimine 100 mg monthly + 50 mg twice weekly, dapsone 2 mg/kg daily. [NLEP 2023]`,`MDT is **free** under NLEP in calendar blister packs; patient is **Released From Treatment (RFT)** after completing the course - no need to wait for smear negativity. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Rifampicin-resistant leprosy**: at least **two second-line drugs** (clarithromycin, minocycline or a fluoroquinolone) **plus clofazimine for 6 months**, then clofazimine plus one of them for **18 months**. [WHO 2018]`,`**Self-care**: daily inspection of hands, feet and eyes, soaking and oiling dry skin, **MCR footwear**, protective gloves, eye protection and exercises. [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Management of reactions and neuritis`,points:[`**Continue MDT** throughout any reaction; reactions are immune events, not treatment failure. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Mild type 1 reaction** (skin only, no neuritis): **NSAIDs or aspirin** and rest; review closely. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Severe type 1 reaction or neuritis** (nerve pain, tenderness or new loss of function within 6 months): **prednisolone 40 mg/day (up to 1 mg/kg)** tapered over **12 weeks** - 40, 30, 20, 15, 10, 5 mg, each for 2 weeks; MB and nerve damage may need **20 weeks or longer**. [NLEP 2023; WHO 2018]`,`**Mild ENL**: NSAIDs; **severe ENL** (neuritis, iritis, orchitis, ulcerating nodules, high fever): **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day (1 mg/kg)** with rapid taper over about 12 weeks. [WHO 2018]`,`**Clofazimine for ENL**: **100 mg three times daily (300 mg/day) for up to 12 weeks**, then tapered - steroid-sparing; total high-dose use should not exceed 12 months. [WHO 2018]`,`**Thalidomide for chronic or recurrent ENL**: **100 mg four times daily (400 mg/day)**, reduced to **50-100 mg/day** maintenance; it is **absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy (phocomelia)** - two methods of contraception and negative pregnancy tests; causes sedation, constipation, neuropathy and DVT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other ENL options**: **pentoxifylline 400 mg three times daily**, and **methotrexate** for steroid-dependent ENL (MaTHEN trial evidence). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supportive**: splint the affected limb in neuritis, **atropine and topical steroid eye drops** for iridocyclitis, scrotal support for orchitis; **nerve decompression** for nerve abscess or neuritis not responding to steroids. [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: MDT is **safe** and must continue; **type 1 reactions peak postpartum** and ENL during pregnancy and lactation; prednisolone is safe; **thalidomide is contraindicated**. [WHO 2018]`,`**Children**: weight-based MDT; a child case means an **infectious adult in the household** - examine all contacts. [NLEP 2023]`,`**HIV**: MDT unchanged; leprosy may appear as **type 1 reaction-like IRIS** within 6 months of starting ART - treat with steroids, continue ART. [Harrison 22e]`,`**G6PD deficiency**: dapsone causes **haemolysis** - test where possible; monitor haemoglobin and stop if severe. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Diabetes and long steroids**: monitor glucose, screen for TB, give **albendazole** (strongyloides) and consider calcium-vitamin D and gastric protection. [WHO 2018]`,`**Elderly**: higher steroid toxicity; osteoporosis and diabetes risk - shortest effective course. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Disability** is the main complication: anaesthetic hands and feet, **plantar ulcers**, clawing, foot drop, digit resorption, blindness from lagophthalmos or iridocyclitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome**: fever, rash, hepatitis, lymphadenopathy 4-6 weeks after starting - linked to HLA-B 13:01; stop dapsone permanently, give steroids. [Zhang NEJM 2013]`,`**Dapsone**: haemolysis, **methaemoglobinaemia**, agranulocytosis; **clofazimine**: **reddish-brown to black skin pigmentation** (reversible over months), ichthyosis, and at high dose **crystal enteropathy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relapse** after MDT is rare (about 1% PB, under 1-2% MB over 10 years); distinguish from a late type 1 reaction (table). [WHO 2018]`,`**Chronic ENL** may last years, with steroid dependence, amyloidosis and glomerulonephritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lucio phenomenon**: haemorrhagic necrotic ulcers in untreated diffuse LL, high mortality from sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Bacteriologically the cure rate with MDT is **over 98%**; nerve function recovers in about two-thirds of neuritis cases if steroids start **within 6 months**. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Nerve function assessment (VMT/ST) monthly** during MDT and at every visit for reactions; any new loss within 6 months - start steroids. [NLEP 2023]`,`Monitor **CBC and LFT** periodically on dapsone; **glucose and BP** on steroids. [NLEP 2023]`,`After RFT, follow **PB for 2 years and MB for 5 years** (annual examination) or advise return for new symptoms. [NLEP 2023]`,`Refer to a **district nodal centre or tertiary hospital** for severe reactions, steroid failure, nerve abscess, eye involvement, thalidomide, suspected relapse or drug resistance. [NLEP 2023]`,`Refer for **reconstructive surgery (RCS)** for stable deformities (claw hand, foot drop, lagophthalmos) after 6-12 months of stability; NLEP pays a **welfare allowance of Rs 12,000** to patients undergoing RCS. [NLEP 2023]`,`Report every new case, reaction and RFT on the **Nikusth 2.0** portal. [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`**Early detection and complete MDT** break transmission - MB patients become non-infectious within days of the first rifampicin dose. [WHO 2018]`,`**Single-dose rifampicin post-exposure prophylaxis (SDR-PEP)** to eligible contacts after excluding leprosy and TB: **600 mg adults**, 450 mg at 10-14 years, 300 mg at 6-9 years (10-15 mg/kg), not under 2 years - reduces risk by about **57%** in the first 2 years. [WHO 2018; NLEP 2023]`,`NLEP activities: **Leprosy Case Detection Campaign (LCDC)** house-to-house, **SPARSH Leprosy Awareness Campaign** (from Anti-Leprosy Day, 30 January), ASHA-based surveillance and incentives. [NLEP 2023]`,`**BCG** gives partial protection against leprosy; **Mycobacterium indicus pranii (MIP) vaccine** was developed in India for contacts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DPMR (Disability Prevention and Medical Rehabilitation)**: free MCR footwear, splints, self-care kits, ulcer care, RCS. [NLEP 2023]`,`**Stigma**: the **Leprosy (repeal of discriminatory laws)** efforts and counselling - leprosy is curable and patients on MDT can live and work normally. [NLEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2018 guideline**: 3-drug MDT (with clofazimine) for **PB** as well as MB; SDR-PEP for contacts; regimen for rifampicin resistance. [WHO 2018]`,`**WHO Global Leprosy Strategy 2021-2030** (Towards zero leprosy): targets 70% fewer new cases and 90% less grade 2 disability by 2030. [WHO 2021]`,`**NLEP National Strategic Plan and Roadmap 2023-2027**: interrupt transmission by 2027; uniform 3-drug MDT for PB; SDR-PEP scale-up; Nikusth 2.0 reporting. [NLEP NSP 2023-27]`,`**Enhanced PEP (PEP++)** - rifampicin plus clarithromycin for contacts of MB patients - is under evaluation and not yet standard. [WHO 2023]`,`Growing evidence for **methotrexate** in chronic ENL and for **ultrasound** in early neuritis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **three cardinal signs** - any one is enough to diagnose leprosy. [NLEP 2023]`,`Classic table: **type 1 versus type 2 reaction** - immunology, type of leprosy, lesions, systemic features, treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic differentiate: **reaction versus relapse** - timing, onset, old or new lesions, BI, steroid response. [WHO 2018]`,`Common mistake: **stopping MDT during a reaction**. [NLEP 2023]`,`Common mistake: giving **thalidomide** to a woman of child-bearing age without contraception, or using it in type 1 reactions (it does not work there). [Harrison 22e]`,`Commonest nerve thickened: **ulnar**; commonest deformity: **claw hand**; most important disability for the eye: **lagophthalmos**. [IAL Textbook of Leprosy 2016]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in leprosy and reactions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Rifampicin`,`600 mg oral once monthly supervised (child 10-14 y: 450 mg)`,`PB 6 months; MB 12 months`,`Orange urine, hepatitis, flu-like syndrome, interactions`],[`Clofazimine (MDT)`,`300 mg monthly supervised + 50 mg daily (child: 150 mg monthly + 50 mg alternate days)`,`PB 6 months; MB 12 months`,`Red-brown skin pigmentation, ichthyosis, GI upset`],[`Dapsone`,`100 mg oral daily (child 50 mg or 2 mg/kg)`,`PB 6 months; MB 12 months`,`Haemolysis (G6PD), methaemoglobinaemia, DHS (HLA-B 13:01), agranulocytosis`],[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg/day (up to 1 mg/kg) oral`,`Taper over 12 weeks (longer for MB and nerve damage)`,`Diabetes, TB reactivation, strongyloides hyperinfection, osteoporosis`],[`Clofazimine (ENL)`,`100 mg three times daily`,`Up to 12 weeks, then taper; max 12 months high dose`,`Pigmentation, crystal enteropathy`],[`Thalidomide (ENL)`,`100 mg four times daily, taper to 50-100 mg/day`,`Weeks to months for chronic ENL`,`Phocomelia - absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy; sedation, neuropathy, DVT`],[`Rifampicin SDR-PEP`,`600 mg once (450 mg at 10-14 y; 300 mg at 6-9 y)`,`Single dose`,`Exclude leprosy and TB first; not under 2 years`]]},{heading:`Ridley-Jopling spectrum`,columns:[`Feature`,`TT`,`BT`,`BB`,`BL`,`LL`],rows:[[`Lesions`,`1-3, well defined, dry, anaesthetic`,`Few, satellite lesions`,`Punched-out, dimorphic`,`Many, poorly defined`,`Countless, symmetric, nodules, infiltration`],[`Sensation in lesion`,`Absent`,`Markedly reduced`,`Reduced`,`Slightly reduced`,`Normal early; glove-stocking late`],[`Nerves`,`One, thickened`,`Few, asymmetric`,`Several`,`Many`,`Symmetric, late`],[`Bacterial index`,`0`,`0-1+`,`2-3+`,`3-5+`,`5-6+ with globi`],[`Lepromin test`,`Strongly positive`,`Positive`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Negative`],[`Reaction risk`,`Rare`,`Type 1`,`Type 1`,`Type 1 and 2`,`Type 2`]]},{heading:`Type 1 versus type 2 reaction`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 1 (reversal)`,`Type 2 (ENL)`],rows:[[`Immunology`,`Type IV, rise in cell-mediated immunity`,`Type III immune complex, high TNF-alpha`],[`Leprosy type`,`Borderline (BT, BB, BL)`,`BL and LL`],[`Skin`,`Existing lesions red, swollen, tender; new lesions`,`Crops of new tender red nodules on normal skin`],[`Systemic`,`Usually mild; oedema of hands and feet`,`Fever, arthritis, iritis, orchitis, nephritis, lymphadenitis`],[`Nerves`,`Acute neuritis common - main cause of disability`,`Neuritis also occurs`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone 40 mg taper over 12-20 weeks`,`Prednisolone, high-dose clofazimine, thalidomide, pentoxifylline`]]},{heading:`Reaction versus relapse`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 1 reaction`,`Relapse`],rows:[[`Timing`,`During MDT or within 3 years of RFT`,`Usually over 3 years after RFT`],[`Onset`,`Sudden`,`Slow, insidious`],[`Lesions`,`Old lesions inflamed, shiny, tender`,`New lesions, not inflamed`],[`Nerves`,`Many, painful and tender`,`Few, slowly involved`],[`Smear (BI)`,`No change`,`Rise of 2+ or more over previous`],[`Response to steroids`,`Rapid improvement`,`No response`]]}],redFlags:[`New nerve pain, tenderness or loss of sensation or power within 6 months - start steroids urgently`,`Painful red eye, reduced vision or inability to close the eye (iridocyclitis, lagophthalmos)`,`Severe ENL with high fever, ulcerating nodules, orchitis or nephritis`,`Nerve abscess or neuritis not responding to steroids - surgical decompression`,`Fever, rash, jaundice and lymphadenopathy 4-6 weeks after starting dapsone (dapsone hypersensitivity)`,`Cyanosis or breathlessness on dapsone (methaemoglobinaemia or haemolysis)`,`Woman of child-bearing age needing thalidomide - specialist only, with strict contraception`],pearls:[`Three cardinal signs: **anaesthetic patch, thickened nerve with loss, positive smear** - any one diagnoses leprosy.`,`MDT now **3 drugs for both PB and MB**: PB 6 months, MB 12 months.`,`Monthly supervised dose: **rifampicin 600 + clofazimine 300**; daily: clofazimine 50 + dapsone 100.`,`Type 1 = **type IV**, borderline, old lesions flare; type 2 = **type III**, LL/BL, new nodules plus systemic.`,`**Never stop MDT** during a reaction.`,`Thalidomide works in **ENL only** and causes **phocomelia**.`,`Commonest nerve thickened: **ulnar**; commonest deformity: **claw hand**.`,`PGL-1 binds **laminin-alpha2** of Schwann cells - why leprosy is a nerve disease.`,`SDR-PEP: **rifampicin 600 mg once** for adult contacts, about 57% protection.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-q1`,stem:`A 30-year-old man has 3 hypopigmented anaesthetic patches, thickened right ulnar and left common peroneal nerves, and a negative slit-skin smear. How is he classified and treated under NLEP?`,options:[`Paucibacillary; rifampicin and dapsone for 6 months`,`Paucibacillary; 3-drug MDT for 6 months`,`Multibacillary; 3-drug MDT for 12 months`,`Multibacillary; 3-drug MDT for 24 months`],answer:2,explanation:`Involvement of more than one nerve makes this multibacillary even though there are only 3 lesions and the smear is negative, so he needs rifampicin, clofazimine and dapsone for 12 months. Classifying by lesion count alone gives the wrong PB label. The 2-drug PB regimen has been replaced by 3-drug MDT, and 24 months is an obsolete MB duration.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-q2`,stem:`A woman with borderline tuberculoid leprosy, 4 months into MDT, develops sudden swelling and redness of her existing patches with pain and weakness in the left hand. What is the best management?`,options:[`Stop MDT and start thalidomide`,`Continue MDT and start prednisolone 40 mg daily tapered over at least 12 weeks`,`Continue MDT and give only ibuprofen`,`Stop MDT and restart it as an MB course`],answer:1,explanation:`Inflamed old lesions with neuritis in a borderline patient is a severe type 1 (reversal) reaction; MDT continues and prednisolone 40 mg/day tapered over 12 weeks or longer is given to save the nerve. Thalidomide works only in ENL and is teratogenic. NSAIDs alone suit only mild reactions without neuritis, and a reaction is not treatment failure needing a new course.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-q3`,stem:`Which mechanism underlies erythema nodosum leprosum?`,options:[`Immune-complex deposition with neutrophilic vasculitis and high TNF-alpha`,`A sudden increase in Th1 cell-mediated immunity in borderline disease`,`IgE-mediated mast-cell degranulation`,`Direct cytopathic invasion of endothelium by M. leprae`],answer:0,explanation:`ENL is a type III (immune-complex) reaction in BL and LL patients with high bacillary load, with neutrophilic vasculitis and raised TNF-alpha - which is why thalidomide (an anti-TNF agent) helps. A rise in Th1 cell-mediated immunity in borderline disease describes the type 1 reversal reaction. IgE mechanisms and direct endothelial invasion (closer to the Lucio phenomenon) are not the basis of ENL.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-q4`,stem:`A patient released from MB MDT 6 years ago presents with slowly appearing new non-tender lesions. Slit-skin smear BI has risen by 3+ compared with RFT. A 4-week steroid trial gives no improvement. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Late type 1 reaction`,`Erythema nodosum leprosum`,`Dapsone hypersensitivity syndrome`,`Relapse`],answer:3,explanation:`Late onset over 3 years after RFT, slow appearance of new non-inflamed lesions, a rising BI and no response to steroids are the features of relapse, which needs re-treatment and resistance testing. A type 1 reaction is sudden, inflames old lesions and improves rapidly on steroids. ENL gives tender nodules with fever, and dapsone hypersensitivity occurs within weeks of starting dapsone.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-q5`,stem:`What is the recommended single-dose rifampicin post-exposure prophylaxis for an adult household contact of a newly diagnosed leprosy patient, after leprosy and TB are excluded?`,options:[`Rifampicin 300 mg once`,`Rifampicin 450 mg once`,`Rifampicin 600 mg once`,`Rifampicin 600 mg monthly for 6 months`],answer:2,explanation:`WHO 2018 and NLEP recommend a single 600 mg dose of rifampicin for adult contacts, reducing leprosy risk by about 57% over 2 years. 450 mg is the dose for children aged 10-14 and 300 mg for 6-9 years. Monthly rifampicin for 6 months is part of treatment, not prophylaxis, and is not used for contacts.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the classification of leprosy. Discuss the clinical features and management of lepra reactions, and outline the current NLEP MDT regimen.`,openingLines:[`Leprosy is a chronic granulomatous infection by Mycobacterium leprae of skin and peripheral nerves; India reports over half of the world's new cases.`,`Lepra reactions are immune-mediated episodes - type 1 (type IV, reversal) and type 2 (type III, ENL) - that cause most of the nerve damage and disability.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Ridley-Jopling: TT, BT, BB, BL, LL, indeterminate - spectrum of cell-mediated immunity.`,`NLEP/WHO: PB (1-5 lesions, 0-1 nerve, smear negative) and MB (6+ lesions, 2+ nerves, or smear positive).`,`WHO disability grades 0, 1, 2.`]},{heading:`Type 1 reaction`,points:[`Type IV hypersensitivity in borderline leprosy; first year of MDT, postpartum.`,`Old lesions red, swollen, tender; new lesions; oedema of hands and feet; acute neuritis.`,`Mild: NSAIDs; severe or neuritis: prednisolone 40 mg/day tapered over 12-20 weeks.`]},{heading:`Type 2 reaction (ENL)`,points:[`Type III immune complex, TNF-alpha; BL/LL with high BI.`,`Crops of tender nodules, fever, arthritis, iridocyclitis, orchitis, neuritis, nephritis.`,`Prednisolone 40-60 mg; clofazimine 100 mg TDS up to 12 weeks; thalidomide 400 mg/day tapered (not in pregnancy); pentoxifylline.`]},{heading:`Neuritis care`,points:[`Monthly VMT and ST; steroids for loss within 6 months; splint; decompression for nerve abscess.`]},{heading:`MDT (NLEP)`,points:[`Adult: rifampicin 600 + clofazimine 300 monthly supervised; clofazimine 50 + dapsone 100 daily.`,`PB 6 months, MB 12 months; continue MDT during reactions.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Contact screening, SDR-PEP 600 mg, LCDC, SPARSH, DPMR, Nikusth reporting; NSP 2023-27 targets zero transmission by 2027.`]}],mustDraw:[`Ridley-Jopling spectrum diagram with Th1/Th2 and BI`,`Table type 1 versus type 2 reaction`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Type 1 reaction`,marks:2.5},{part:`Type 2 reaction`,marks:2.5},{part:`MDT and neuritis care`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Ridley-Jopling`,`reversal reaction`,`ENL`,`MDT`,`thalidomide`,`neuritis`]},{id:`infectious-fever-leprosy-reactions-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between a type 1 lepra reaction and relapse of leprosy.`,openingLines:[`Both present with new or worsening lesions after MDT, but a reaction is an immune flare needing steroids while relapse is bacterial regrowth needing re-treatment.`,`Timing, onset, character of lesions, smear and steroid response separate them.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Timing: reaction during MDT or within 3 years of RFT; relapse usually over 3 years after RFT.`,`Onset: sudden versus slow; lesions: old ones inflamed versus new non-inflamed.`,`Nerves: many, painful versus few, slowly involved.`]},{heading:`Laboratory and response`,points:[`BI unchanged in reaction; rises by 2+ or more in relapse.`,`Rapid response to steroids in reaction; none in relapse (therapeutic test).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Reaction: prednisolone 40 mg taper, continue MDT.`,`Relapse: re-treat with MDT, test for rifampicin and dapsone resistance.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table reaction versus relapse`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2.5},{part:`Smear and steroid response`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1}],keywords:[`reaction`,`relapse`,`bacterial index`,`steroid trial`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Leprosy chapter`,`WHO Guidelines for the diagnosis, treatment and prevention of leprosy, 2018`,`WHO Towards zero leprosy: Global Leprosy (Hansen's disease) Strategy 2021-2030`,`National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP): National Strategic Plan and Roadmap for Leprosy 2023-2027, MoHFW`,`NLEP Operational Guidelines and Training Manual for Medical Officers, MoHFW, 2023`,`IAL Textbook of Leprosy, 2nd edition, 2016`,`WHO Weekly Epidemiological Record: Global leprosy update, 2024`]}),u.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach`,title:`Approach to fever with rash`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Fever with rash** is a syndromic presentation analysed by **rash morphology (maculopapular, petechial-purpuric, vesiculobullous, erythrodermic, nodular, eschar), distribution and day of appearance**; the first job is to pick out the killers - **meningococcaemia, dengue with warning signs, rickettsial disease, toxic shock, SJS/TEN and DRESS** - treat them empirically within the hour, and otherwise make a specific diagnosis (measles, rubella, varicella, chikungunya, drug rash, Kawasaki) with timed tests.`,keywords:[`exanthem`,`maculopapular rash`,`petechiae`,`purpura fulminans`,`meningococcaemia`,`dengue rash`,`measles`,`rubella`,`varicella`,`rickettsia`,`Indian tick typhus`,`DRESS`,`RegiSCAR`,`SJS/TEN`,`Kawasaki disease`,`toxic shock syndrome`,`scarlet fever`,`mpox`,`Koplik spots`,`glass test`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Fever with rash** is fever (38.0 C or more) accompanied by a skin eruption (exanthem) or mucosal eruption (enanthem); it is one of the syndromes under **IDSP** surveillance in India. [IDSP 2024]`,`Most cases are **benign viral exanthems**, but a small group - **meningococcaemia, rickettsial disease, dengue shock, toxic shock syndrome, SJS/TEN, DRESS** - can kill within hours to days, so triage comes first. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India the commonest causes are **dengue, chikungunya, measles, varicella, scrub typhus and spotted fever rickettsioses, HFMD and drug eruptions**, with marked post-monsoon seasonality (July-November). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Measles** remains a priority: India is under the **measles-rubella elimination** drive (target revised to 2026) with case-based surveillance of every fever with maculopapular rash. [MoHFW MR Elimination 2023]`,`**Drug eruptions** are the commonest cause of fever with rash in hospitalised adults; **SCAR** (SJS/TEN, DRESS, AGEP) carry mortality of about **10% (DRESS) to 30% or more (TEN)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox** (WHO PHEIC 2022-23 and again 2024-25, clade Ib) and outbreaks of **Kyasanur forest disease** and **CCHF** in India keep vesicular and haemorrhagic rashes on the differential. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - causes by rash type`,points:[`**Maculopapular, central**: measles, rubella, dengue, chikungunya, Zika, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, EBV (especially after amoxicillin), acute HIV, secondary syphilis, typhoid (rose spots), drug rash, Still disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Maculopapular, peripheral (palms and soles)**: **spotted fever rickettsioses (Indian tick typhus)**, secondary syphilis, HFMD, erythema multiforme, Kawasaki (palm and sole erythema), infective endocarditis (Janeway lesions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Petechial or purpuric**: **meningococcaemia**, dengue, rickettsia (late), gonococcaemia, infective endocarditis, viral haemorrhagic fevers (KFD, CCHF), leptospirosis, DIC, TTP, ITP, acute leukaemia, vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vesiculobullous**: varicella, disseminated zoster, HSV, **HFMD**, **mpox**, rickettsialpox, bullous impetigo, **SSSS**, SJS/TEN. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diffuse erythema with desquamation**: **scarlet fever, toxic shock syndrome (staphylococcal or streptococcal), Kawasaki disease, SSSS, DRESS, TEN**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nodular**: erythema nodosum (TB, streptococcus, sarcoid, drugs), **ENL**, disseminated fungal infection, Sweet syndrome; **urticarial**: viral, serum sickness, drugs, Still disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eschar**: **scrub typhus** (painless black crust in axilla, groin, waistband), tick typhus (tache noire), cutaneous anthrax, spider bite. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Rashes arise by **direct infection of skin** (varicella, HSV, mpox), **immune response to a systemic infection** (measles, rubella - rash appears as antibody rises), **vascular injury** (rickettsia, meningococcus) or **toxin action** (TSS, scarlet fever, SSSS). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rickettsiae** invade **endothelial cells** of small vessels, causing a disseminated vasculitis: maculopapular rash that becomes petechial, plus pneumonitis, myocarditis, encephalitis and AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Meningococcal endotoxin** triggers massive cytokine release, endothelial damage and DIC - petechiae evolve within hours into **purpura fulminans** with adrenal haemorrhage (**Waterhouse-Friderichsen**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Superantigens** (TSST-1, streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins) bind MHC II and T-cell receptors outside the antigen groove, activating up to 20% of T cells - **cytokine storm** with erythroderma, shock and multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dengue** rash is a capillary phenomenon: early **flushing**, and in recovery a confluent rash with **islands of white in a sea of red**; petechiae reflect thrombocytopenia and capillary fragility. [WHO 2025]`,`**DRESS** is a T-cell-mediated (type IVb) reaction with **eosinophil activation** and **reactivation of HHV-6/EBV/CMV**; **SJS/TEN** is cytotoxic T-cell and granulysin-mediated keratinocyte apoptosis (type IVc). [Harrison 22e]`,`**HLA associations** explain population risk: HLA-B 15:02 (carbamazepine SJS/TEN in South and East Asians), HLA-B 58:01 (allopurinol SCAR), HLA-B 13:01 (dapsone). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - rash morphology and timing`,points:[`Classify by **morphology first**: macular, papular, maculopapular, vesicular, pustular, bullous, petechial (under 3 mm, non-blanching), purpuric (over 3 mm), nodular, urticarial, erythrodermic. [Harrison 22e]`,`Then by **distribution**: central (trunk first - measles, dengue, drug), **peripheral (wrists and ankles first, palms and soles - rickettsia)**, **cephalocaudal spread** (measles, rubella), **centripetal** (varicella) versus **centrifugal** (mpox, smallpox). [Harrison 22e]`,`Then by **day of fever on which the rash appears** - mnemonic **Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets**: Varicella day 1, Scarlet fever day 2, Pox (smallpox) day 3, Measles day 4, Typhus day 5, Dengue day 6, Typhoid (Enteric) day 7. [Park 27e]`,`**Varicella**: lesions in **crops**, all stages present together (pleomorphic), **dewdrop on a rose petal**, centripetal, involves scalp and mucosa. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mpox**: lesions at the **same stage** (synchronous), deep-seated, umbilicated, involve palms and soles, with **prominent lymphadenopathy**; in clade IIb often genital or perianal. [WHO 2024]`,`**Measles**: **Koplik spots** on buccal mucosa 1-2 days before rash; rash starts behind the ears and hairline on **day 4**, spreads down, fades in the same order leaving **brown staining and fine desquamation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella**: mild, rash on day 1-2 spreading fast, with **post-auricular and suboccipital nodes** and **Forchheimer spots** on the soft palate; **chikungunya**: rash day 2-5 with severe polyarthralgia and post-inflammatory nose pigmentation (**chik sign**). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`History`,points:[`**Timeline**: when did fever start, when did rash appear (day of illness), where did it begin and how did it spread. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug history in the last 8 weeks** is mandatory: **SJS/TEN 4-28 days** after starting, **DRESS 2-8 weeks**, simple exanthem 7-14 days; anticonvulsants, allopurinol, sulfonamides, dapsone, beta-lactams, vancomycin, NSAIDs, anti-TB drugs. [RegiSCAR; Harrison 22e]`,`**Exposure**: sick contacts (measles, varicella, HFMD), **forest, grass or tick exposure** (scrub, tick typhus, KFD), travel, animal contact, **sexual history** (secondary syphilis, acute HIV, mpox), mosquito season. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Vaccination**: MR/MMR, varicella, meningococcal; **previous chickenpox or measles**; immunosuppression, asplenia (meningococcus), pregnancy (rubella, varicella, parvovirus risk to fetus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Associated symptoms**: headache and neck stiffness (meningococcus), **severe joint pains** (chikungunya), cough, coryza, conjunctivitis (measles - the **3 Cs**), sore throat (scarlet fever, EBV), mucosal pain or skin pain (SJS/TEN). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Itch versus pain**: itch suggests urticaria, varicella, drug exanthem; **skin pain or burning** is a warning sign of SJS/TEN or TSS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Child under 5 with fever over 5 days**: ask about red eyes, red lips, swollen hands and feet - think **Kawasaki disease**. [AHA 2017]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Vital signs first**: hypotension, tachycardia, low pulse pressure, SpO2, capillary refill, GCS - any abnormality means sepsis, dengue shock or TSS pathway. [SSC 2026]`,`**Glass (tumbler) test**: rash that **does not blanch** under a glass is petechial or purpuric - in a febrile patient treat as **meningococcaemia until proved otherwise**. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Undress fully** and look for an **eschar** (axilla, groin, waist, under breasts), and examine **palms and soles** (rickettsia, syphilis, HFMD, mpox, Kawasaki). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Mucosae**: Koplik spots, strawberry tongue (scarlet fever, Kawasaki, TSS), **erosions on 2 or more mucosal sites** (SJS/TEN), Forchheimer spots, oral ulcers in HFMD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nikolsky sign** (epidermis shears off with lateral pressure) and **target lesions** - atypical flat targets and dusky macules suggest SJS/TEN; **facial oedema** suggests DRESS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymph nodes, liver and spleen**: generalised nodes and hepatitis in DRESS, EBV, acute HIV, secondary syphilis; **unilateral cervical node 1.5 cm or more** in Kawasaki; post-auricular in rubella. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scarlet fever**: sandpaper rash, **Pastia lines** in skin folds, circumoral pallor; **endocarditis**: splinter haemorrhages, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, new murmur. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - key discriminators`,points:[`**Dengue versus chikungunya**: dengue - retro-orbital pain, leucopenia, **thrombocytopenia and rising haematocrit**; chikungunya - **disabling symmetric polyarthritis**, milder cytopenias, nose pigmentation. [WHO 2025]`,`**Measles versus drug rash**: measles - prodrome of **3 Cs plus Koplik spots** and cephalocaudal spread; drug rash - itchy, starts on trunk, recent new drug, eosinophilia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rickettsia versus dengue**: rickettsia - **rash from wrists and ankles involving palms and soles, eschar**, rash persists and becomes purpuric, responds to doxycycline in 48 hours. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Meningococcaemia versus dengue petechiae**: meningococcus - **rapidly spreading purpura in a toxic patient within hours**, neutrophil leucocytosis; dengue - petechiae in the critical phase with leucopenia. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Varicella versus mpox**: varicella - pleomorphic crops, centripetal, minimal nodes; mpox - synchronous, centrifugal, palms and soles, **marked lymphadenopathy**. [WHO 2024]`,`**DRESS versus SJS/TEN**: DRESS - latency 2-8 weeks, facial oedema, **eosinophilia, hepatitis, lymphadenopathy**, little mucosal involvement; SJS/TEN - 4-28 days, **painful skin, mucosal erosions, epidermal detachment**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kawasaki versus measles and scarlet fever**: Kawasaki - fever 5 days or more, **non-exudative bilateral conjunctival injection**, extremity changes, no Koplik spots, no response to antibiotics. [AHA 2017]`,`**Adult-onset Still disease**: evanescent **salmon-pink rash with evening fever spikes**, arthritis, neutrophilia and **ferritin often over 3000 ng/mL**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Do not delay antibiotics** for tests in suspected meningococcaemia or TSS - take blood cultures if it adds no delay. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`First line: **CBC with haematocrit and platelets** (leucopenia - dengue, measles; neutrophilia - bacterial; **eosinophilia over 1500/microL** - DRESS; atypical lymphocytes - EBV, DRESS), **LFT, creatinine, CRP**, urine. [Harrison 22e]`,`Timed tests by day of illness: **dengue NS1 (days 1-5) and IgM (day 5 onward)**, **chikungunya RT-PCR (days 1-5) or IgM (day 5 onward)**, **scrub typhus IgM ELISA** from day 5, malaria smear or RDT. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Measles/rubella**: serum **IgM ELISA** (day 4-28 after rash) plus throat swab or urine for RT-PCR, sent through the **MR surveillance laboratory network**. [MoHFW MR Elimination 2023]`,`**Vesicular rash**: lesion swab **PCR for VZV, HSV, mpox or enterovirus**; Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells (HSV, VZV) but cannot separate them. [WHO 2024]`,`**Bacterial**: blood cultures (2 sets), throat swab and **ASO** (scarlet fever), **VDRL/RPR with TPHA** (syphilis), **HIV 4th-generation antigen-antibody** test; CSF if meningism and safe to tap. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug reactions**: **RegiSCAR score** for DRESS (5 or more probable, over 5 definite), **SCORTEN** for TEN prognosis, **skin biopsy** (full-thickness epidermal necrosis in TEN). [RegiSCAR; Harrison 22e]`,`**Kawasaki**: CRP 3 mg/dL or more, ESR 40 mm/h or more, low albumin, raised ALT, platelets over 4.5 lakh after day 7, sterile pyuria, and **echocardiography** at diagnosis, 1-2 weeks and 4-6 weeks. [AHA 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Paracetamol only** for fever (15 mg/kg in children, 500-1000 mg in adults, max 4 g/day) until dengue is excluded - **no NSAIDs, aspirin or IM injections**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Suspected meningococcal disease in primary care**: give **benzylpenicillin 1.2 g IM/IV** (child 1-9 years 600 mg, under 1 year 300 mg) or **ceftriaxone** before urgent transfer, unless it delays transfer. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Suspected rickettsial disease** (palms-soles rash or eschar): **doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days** (child 2.2 mg/kg BD), azithromycin in pregnancy - see the Scrub typhus topic. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Measles**: supportive care plus **vitamin A** - 2 doses 24 hours apart: **2 lakh IU (12 months or more), 1 lakh IU (6-11 months), 50,000 IU (under 6 months)**; isolate for 4 days after rash onset; notify. [WHO 2024; MoHFW MR Elimination 2023]`,`**Varicella in adolescents and adults**: **acyclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days** (or valacyclovir 1 g TDS) if started within 24-72 hours of rash; children who are healthy need symptomatic care only. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scarlet fever / streptococcal pharyngitis**: **amoxicillin 50 mg/kg/day (max 1 g) for 10 days** or penicillin V; benzathine penicillin 1.2 MU IM once if adherence is uncertain. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Simple drug exanthem**: stop the suspect drug, antihistamine and topical steroid, and **record the allergy**; review for SCAR features in 24-48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`Give a **written safety-net**: return immediately for non-blanching spots, drowsiness, breathlessness, bleeding, skin peeling, mouth or eye sores, or reduced urine. [NICE NG240 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Meningococcaemia**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly** (child 80-100 mg/kg/day) within the hour, sepsis bundle with fluid boluses and noradrenaline, **droplet isolation for 24 hours** of therapy; 7 days of treatment. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Toxic shock syndrome**: source control (remove tampon or packing, drain abscess), **clindamycin 600-900 mg IV 8-hourly (antitoxin) plus a beta-lactam or vancomycin**, aggressive fluids; **IVIG** for streptococcal TSS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SJS/TEN**: **stop all suspect drugs**, admit to ICU or burns unit, fluids, wound care, ophthalmology within 24 hours, analgesia; **cyclosporine 3-5 mg/kg/day for 7-10 days** has the best evidence; steroids are debated. [BAD 2016; Harrison 22e]`,`**DRESS**: stop the drug, **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day** tapered **slowly over 6-8 weeks or more** (rapid taper causes relapse); cyclosporine for steroid-refractory disease; monitor liver, kidney, thyroid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kawasaki disease**: **IVIG 2 g/kg as a single infusion over 10-12 hours** within 10 days of fever onset, plus **aspirin 30-50 mg/kg/day (moderate) or 80-100 mg/kg/day** until afebrile, then **3-5 mg/kg/day** for 6-8 weeks. [AHA 2017]`,`**Dengue with warning signs or severe dengue**: admit, haematocrit-guided crystalloid as in the Dengue topic; no platelet transfusion for counts alone without bleeding. [WHO 2025]`,`**Isolation**: measles - **airborne** until 4 days after rash; varicella - **airborne and contact** until all lesions crust; meningococcus - **droplet** for 24 hours of therapy; mpox - contact and droplet until lesions heal. [CDC Isolation 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **rubella before 12 weeks** carries up to about 90% risk of congenital rubella syndrome; **varicella** can cause severe pneumonia in the mother and fetal varicella syndrome before 20 weeks; **parvovirus B19** causes fetal anaemia and hydrops. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella exposure in pregnancy** without immunity: **VZIG within 10 days** of exposure; established chickenpox: **oral acyclovir**, IV if pneumonia. [RCOG 2015]`,`**Neonates and infants**: any fever with rash under 3 months is admitted; **SSSS** in neonates and young children; maternal varicella 5 days before to 2 days after delivery needs VZIG for the baby. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immunocompromised and HIV**: disseminated varicella or zoster, atypical measles without rash, disseminated fungal nodules (cryptococcus, histoplasma, talaromyces in North-East India) - biopsy early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: drug eruptions dominate because of polypharmacy; **herpes zoster** and SCAR mortality are higher. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: allopurinol SCAR risk is higher - start low (50-100 mg) and consider HLA-B 58:01 screening where available. [ACR 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Meningococcaemia**: mortality **10-15%**, up to 40% with purpura fulminans; survivors may have amputations, skin grafts, deafness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measles**: pneumonia (commonest cause of death), diarrhoea, **encephalitis (1 in 1000)**, and **SSPE** 7-10 years later; vitamin A deficiency worsens outcomes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TEN**: mortality about **30%**, predicted by **SCORTEN** (age over 40, malignancy, HR over 120, BSA detached over 10%, urea over 10 mmol/L, bicarbonate under 20, glucose over 14 mmol/L); ocular scarring is the main long-term sequel. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DRESS**: mortality about **10%** (mainly fulminant hepatitis); late autoimmune thyroiditis and type 1 diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kawasaki**: **coronary artery aneurysms in about 25% untreated** versus about 4% with timely IVIG. [AHA 2017]`,`**Varicella in adults**: pneumonia, encephalitis, secondary bacterial skin infection (streptococcal necrotising fasciitis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer immediately**: non-blanching rash with fever, hypotension, altered sensorium, **mucosal involvement or skin detachment**, erythroderma, suspected Kawasaki, pregnant women with rubella or varicella, neonates. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Dengue**: daily CBC with haematocrit from day 3 until 48 hours after defervescence. [WHO 2025]`,`**DRESS**: weekly LFT, creatinine and CBC during steroid taper; **TSH and glucose** at 2-3 months and later for autoimmune sequelae. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kawasaki**: echocardiography at 1-2 weeks and 4-6 weeks; long-term cardiology follow-up if aneurysm. [AHA 2017]`,`**Allergy documentation**: record the culprit drug in the case sheet and give the patient an **allergy card**; avoid structurally related drugs (aromatic anticonvulsants cross-react). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Notify** suspected measles, rubella, dengue, chikungunya, meningococcal disease, mpox and clusters on the **IHIP (IDSP)** portal. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`**MR vaccine** under UIP at **9-12 months and 16-24 months**; MR catch-up campaigns support measles-rubella elimination. [MoHFW UIP 2024]`,`**Varicella vaccine** (not in UIP; IAP recommends 2 doses) and **meningococcal vaccine** for Hajj pilgrims, asplenia, complement deficiency and outbreaks. [IAP 2023]`,`**Meningococcal contacts**: chemoprophylaxis with **ciprofloxacin 500 mg once** (adult), **rifampicin 600 mg BD for 2 days**, or **ceftriaxone 250 mg IM once** (pregnancy) within 24 hours. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**Vector control**: Aedes source reduction, weekly dry day, repellents (dengue, chikungunya); avoiding scrub vegetation and ticks (rickettsia, KFD); KFD vaccine in endemic Karnataka districts. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Pharmacogenomic screening**: HLA-B 15:02 before carbamazepine and HLA-B 58:01 before allopurinol in high-risk groups where available. [CPIC 2018]`,`**IDSP syndromic surveillance**: fever with rash is reported weekly (S, P, L forms) to detect measles, dengue and chikungunya outbreaks early. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**NICE NG240 (2024)**: meningitis and meningococcal disease - pre-hospital benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone, ceftriaxone in hospital, recognition of non-blanching rash. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`**WHO 2025 arboviral guideline** (dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever): unified clinical management; paracetamol, avoid NSAIDs and steroids; dengue fluid protocols. [WHO 2025]`,`**Mpox**: WHO declared a PHEIC again in **August 2024** for clade Ib; **MVA-BN vaccine** prequalified by WHO in 2024; PHEIC ended in 2025. [WHO 2024-2025]`,`**Measles resurgence** in 2024-2025 worldwide; India revised its **MR elimination target to 2026**. [WHO 2025; MoHFW MR Elimination 2023]`,`**SSC 2026**: screen sepsis with NEWS2/MEWS/SIRS rather than qSOFA alone and give antibiotics within 1 hour in probable septic shock - applies to the febrile purpuric patient. [SSC 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva opener: **describe the rash** - morphology, distribution, spread, day of appearance, blanching, mucosa, palms and soles. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mnemonic for day of rash: **Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets**. [Park 27e]`,`Classic: **causes of rash on palms and soles** - rickettsia, secondary syphilis, HFMD, mpox, Kawasaki, erythema multiforme, endocarditis, drug rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic: **DRESS versus SJS/TEN** latency, eosinophilia, mucosa, steroid taper. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **waiting for investigations** before giving antibiotics in a febrile non-blanching rash. [NICE NG240 2024]`,`Common mistake: giving **NSAIDs** for fever with rash before excluding dengue, or **amoxicillin** for sore throat with EBV. [WHO 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in fever with rash`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone (meningococcaemia)`,`2 g IV 12-hourly; child 80-100 mg/kg/day`,`7 days`,`Biliary sludge; avoid calcium co-infusion in neonates`],[`Benzylpenicillin (pre-hospital)`,`1.2 g IM/IV once (child 1-9 y 600 mg; under 1 y 300 mg)`,`Single dose before transfer`,`Anaphylaxis in penicillin allergy`],[`Doxycycline (rickettsia)`,`100 mg oral BD; child 2.2 mg/kg BD`,`7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis, photosensitivity; azithromycin in pregnancy`],[`Acyclovir (varicella)`,`800 mg oral five times daily; 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly if severe`,`7 days`,`Renal impairment - adjust dose, hydrate`],[`Vitamin A (measles)`,`2 lakh IU oral (12 months or more), 1 lakh IU (6-11 months), 50,000 IU (under 6 months)`,`Two doses 24 hours apart`,`Bulging fontanelle, vomiting with overdose`],[`IVIG (Kawasaki)`,`2 g/kg IV over 10-12 hours`,`Single infusion, within 10 days`,`Delays live vaccines 11 months; haemolysis`],[`Clindamycin (TSS)`,`600-900 mg IV 8-hourly with a beta-lactam or vancomycin`,`Until shock resolves, then step down`,`C. difficile colitis`],[`Cyclosporine (SJS/TEN)`,`3-5 mg/kg/day oral in 2 doses`,`7-10 days`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension`]]},{heading:`Rash morphology and likely causes`,columns:[`Morphology`,`Key causes`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Maculopapular, central`,`Measles, rubella, dengue, chikungunya, drug, EBV-amoxicillin`,`Koplik spots; joint pain; new drug`],[`Maculopapular, palms and soles`,`Rickettsia, secondary syphilis, HFMD, Kawasaki`,`Eschar; genital ulcer history; oral ulcers`],[`Petechial or purpuric`,`Meningococcaemia, dengue, rickettsia, VHF, endocarditis`,`Non-blanching; toxic patient - treat now`],[`Vesiculobullous`,`Varicella, HSV, HFMD, mpox, SJS/TEN`,`Crops versus synchronous; mucosal erosions`],[`Diffuse erythema`,`Scarlet fever, TSS, Kawasaki, SSSS, DRESS`,`Hypotension; desquamation; facial oedema`],[`Eschar`,`Scrub typhus, tick typhus, anthrax`,`Axilla, groin, waistband`]]},{heading:`Day of rash and pattern of common exanthems`,columns:[`Disease`,`Day rash appears`,`Distribution and spread`,`Pathognomonic or key sign`],rows:[[`Varicella`,`Day 1`,`Centripetal, crops, scalp involved`,`Dewdrop on rose petal; pleomorphic`],[`Scarlet fever`,`Day 2`,`Neck and chest, then generalised`,`Sandpaper rash, Pastia lines, circumoral pallor`],[`Measles`,`Day 4`,`Behind ears, then face, trunk, limbs`,`Koplik spots; brown staining on fading`],[`Rubella`,`Day 1-2`,`Face to body within 24 hours`,`Post-auricular nodes, Forchheimer spots`],[`Rickettsia (tick typhus)`,`Day 3-5`,`Wrists and ankles to trunk, palms and soles`,`Eschar (tache noire), purpura later`],[`Dengue`,`Day 1-2 flush; day 3-6 convalescent`,`Generalised`,`Islands of white in a sea of red`],[`Typhoid`,`Day 7-10`,`Trunk`,`Rose spots, 2-4 mm, blanching, few`]]},{heading:`Severe cutaneous adverse reactions`,columns:[`Feature`,`DRESS`,`SJS/TEN`],rows:[[`Latency`,`2-8 weeks`,`4-28 days`],[`Skin`,`Morbilliform rash over 50% BSA, facial oedema`,`Painful dusky macules, atypical targets, detachment`],[`Mucosa`,`Mild or absent`,`2 or more sites, severe erosions`],[`Blood`,`Eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytes`,`Lymphopenia; no eosinophilia typically`],[`Organs`,`Hepatitis, nephritis, myocarditis, pneumonitis`,`Mainly skin, eyes, airway`],[`Score`,`RegiSCAR`,`SCORTEN`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg, slow 6-8 week taper`,`Burns care, cyclosporine 3-5 mg/kg/day`]]},{heading:`Investigations by syndrome`,columns:[`Suspected`,`Test`,`When / cut-off`],rows:[[`Dengue`,`NS1; IgM ELISA; CBC with haematocrit`,`NS1 days 1-5; IgM from day 5; rising haematocrit 20%`],[`Measles/rubella`,`Serum IgM; throat swab or urine RT-PCR`,`IgM day 4-28 after rash`],[`Rickettsia/scrub`,`IgM ELISA; eschar PCR`,`From day 5; Weil-Felix not recommended`],[`Meningococcaemia`,`Blood culture, PCR; CSF if safe`,`Never delay antibiotics`],[`DRESS`,`CBC eosinophils, LFT, RFT, HHV-6 PCR`,`Eosinophils over 1500/microL; RegiSCAR 5 or more probable`],[`Kawasaki`,`CRP, ESR, albumin, platelets, echo`,`CRP 3 mg/dL or more, ESR 40 mm/h or more`]]}],redFlags:[`Non-blanching petechial or purpuric rash with fever (meningococcaemia until proved otherwise)`,`Hypotension, cold peripheries or low pulse pressure (septic, dengue or toxic shock)`,`Altered sensorium, seizures or neck stiffness`,`Skin pain, blistering, positive Nikolsky sign or erosions on 2 or more mucosal sites (SJS/TEN)`,`Fever, facial oedema, eosinophilia and raised ALT 2-8 weeks after a new drug (DRESS)`,`Child with fever 5 days or more plus red eyes, red lips, swollen hands (Kawasaki)`,`Pregnant woman with rash illness or rash exposure (rubella, varicella, parvovirus)`,`Bleeding or rash with fever after forest or tick exposure in KFD or CCHF areas`],pearls:[`Day of rash: **Very Sick Person Must Take Double Tablets** - varicella 1, scarlet 2, pox 3, measles 4, typhus 5, dengue 6, typhoid 7.`,`**Non-blanching + fever = antibiotics now**; benzylpenicillin 1.2 g before transfer.`,`Palms and soles: **rickettsia, syphilis, HFMD, mpox, Kawasaki, EM, endocarditis**.`,`Varicella **pleomorphic and centripetal**; mpox **synchronous and centrifugal** with big nodes.`,`Measles: **3 Cs + Koplik spots**, rash day 4, give **vitamin A**.`,`Dengue recovery rash: **islands of white in a sea of red**.`,`DRESS **2-8 weeks**, eosinophils, slow steroid taper; SJS/TEN **4-28 days**, mucosa, cyclosporine.`,`Kawasaki: **IVIG 2 g/kg within 10 days** cuts aneurysms from about 25% to 4%.`,`Paracetamol only until dengue is excluded.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-q1`,stem:`A 19-year-old student has fever for 12 hours, headache and a rapidly spreading non-blanching purpuric rash on the legs. BP is 88/50 mmHg. He is at a primary health centre 2 hours from the district hospital. What should be done first?`,options:[`Send blood for culture and dengue NS1 and wait for results`,`Give oral doxycycline and review in 24 hours`,`Give IV fluids and refer without antibiotics so cultures are not spoilt`,`Give benzylpenicillin 1.2 g IM or IV (or ceftriaxone) immediately and transfer urgently`],answer:3,explanation:`A febrile, hypotensive patient with spreading non-blanching purpura has meningococcaemia until proved otherwise; NICE NG240 advises parenteral benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone at once in primary care and urgent transfer, because mortality rises by the hour. Waiting for tests or withholding antibiotics to protect cultures is dangerous. Oral doxycycline is too slow and the wrong drug for meningococcus.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man started on carbamazepine 5 weeks ago presents with fever, a morbilliform rash over 60% of his body, facial oedema, cervical lymphadenopathy, eosinophils 2800/microL and ALT 410 U/L. Oral mucosa is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Stevens-Johnson syndrome`,`Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)`,`Measles`,`Toxic shock syndrome`],answer:1,explanation:`A latency of 2-8 weeks, widespread morbilliform rash, facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia and hepatitis fit DRESS, which needs drug withdrawal and a slow steroid taper. SJS appears within 4-28 days with painful skin and mucosal erosions, which are absent. Measles has a coryzal prodrome with Koplik spots, and toxic shock causes hypotension with diffuse erythroderma, not eosinophilia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-q3`,stem:`A 3-year-old child has fever for 6 days, bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection, cracked red lips, a polymorphous rash, and swelling of the hands and feet. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`IVIG 2 g/kg as a single infusion with aspirin`,`Oral amoxicillin for 10 days`,`Vitamin A 2 lakh IU for 2 days`,`Oral prednisolone alone for 5 days`],answer:0,explanation:`Fever of 5 days or more with 4 of the 5 principal features is Kawasaki disease; IVIG 2 g/kg over 10-12 hours within 10 days of fever, with aspirin, reduces coronary aneurysm risk from about 25% to about 4% (AHA 2017). Amoxicillin treats scarlet fever, which has exudative pharyngitis and sandpaper rash. Vitamin A is for measles, and steroids alone are not first-line therapy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-q4`,stem:`A farmer from Maharashtra has fever for 5 days and a maculopapular rash that began on the wrists and ankles and now involves the palms and soles. A small black crust is present at the groin. Which treatment is most appropriate?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily`,`Amoxicillin-clavulanate oral`,`Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days`,`Acyclovir 800 mg five times daily`],answer:2,explanation:`A peripheral rash spreading to palms and soles with an eschar after field exposure indicates rickettsial disease (scrub typhus or spotted fever), and doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days is the drug of choice, with defervescence in about 48 hours. Ceftriaxone and amoxicillin-clavulanate do not act on intracellular rickettsiae. Acyclovir treats varicella or herpes, which cause vesicles, not an eschar.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-q5`,stem:`Which feature best distinguishes mpox from varicella in an adult with fever and vesiculopustular rash?`,options:[`Presence of fever before the rash`,`Lesions all at the same stage with prominent lymphadenopathy`,`Itching of the lesions`,`Involvement of the trunk`],answer:1,explanation:`Mpox lesions evolve synchronously (all at the same stage), are deep-seated and centrifugal including palms and soles, and come with marked lymphadenopathy. Varicella lesions appear in crops at different stages, are centripetal and nodes are not prominent. A fever prodrome, itching and truncal involvement can occur in both and do not discriminate.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the approach to an adult presenting with fever and rash in the outpatient department. Outline the management of the life-threatening causes.`,openingLines:[`Fever with rash is a syndromic presentation analysed by rash morphology, distribution, day of appearance and associated features, against a background of exposure and drug history.`,`Most cases are self-limiting viral exanthems, but meningococcaemia, rickettsial disease, severe dengue, toxic shock syndrome, SJS/TEN and DRESS must be recognised and treated within hours.`],answer:[{heading:`Triage first`,points:[`Vitals, GCS, SpO2, capillary refill; glass test for non-blanching rash.`,`Red flags: purpura, shock, altered sensorium, mucosal erosions, skin detachment, erythroderma.`]},{heading:`History`,points:[`Day of fever when rash appeared; site of onset and spread; itch or pain.`,`Drug history 8 weeks (SJS 4-28 days, DRESS 2-8 weeks); contacts, travel, forest or tick exposure, sexual history, vaccination, pregnancy.`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Morphology: maculopapular, petechial/purpuric, vesicular, erythrodermic, nodular, eschar.`,`Distribution: central, peripheral with palms-soles, centripetal versus centrifugal.`,`Mucosae, Koplik spots, Nikolsky sign, nodes, liver, spleen, eschar search.`]},{heading:`Differential by morphology`,points:[`Maculopapular: measles, rubella, dengue, chikungunya, drug, EBV, acute HIV, syphilis.`,`Purpuric: meningococcaemia, dengue, rickettsia, VHF, endocarditis, DIC.`,`Vesicular: varicella, HSV, HFMD, mpox; erythroderma: TSS, scarlet fever, Kawasaki, DRESS, TEN.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC with haematocrit, eosinophils, LFT, RFT, CRP, blood culture.`,`Timed: NS1 days 1-5, IgM from day 5; measles IgM; lesion PCR; RegiSCAR and SCORTEN.`]},{heading:`Management of killers`,points:[`Meningococcaemia: benzylpenicillin 1.2 g pre-hospital; ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly; sepsis bundle; contact prophylaxis.`,`Rickettsia: doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days; TSS: clindamycin plus beta-lactam, source control, IVIG.`,`SJS/TEN: stop drug, burns unit, cyclosporine; DRESS: prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg slow taper.`,`Dengue: paracetamol only, haematocrit-guided fluids; notify on IHIP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: fever with rash - triage, morphology branches, action`,`Table: day of rash appearance in common exanthems`],markSplit:[{part:`Triage and red flags`,marks:1.5},{part:`History and examination`,marks:2.5},{part:`Differential by morphology`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management of life-threatening causes`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`fever with rash`,`meningococcaemia`,`rickettsia`,`DRESS`,`SJS/TEN`,`glass test`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-rash-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between DRESS and Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis.`,openingLines:[`DRESS and SJS/TEN are severe cutaneous adverse drug reactions (SCAR) mediated by drug-specific T cells, with different latency, organ involvement and treatment.`,`Recognising which one is present decides whether the patient needs a slow steroid taper or burns-unit care with cyclosporine.`],answer:[{heading:`Timing and drugs`,points:[`DRESS: 2-8 weeks; anticonvulsants, allopurinol, dapsone, sulfonamides, vancomycin.`,`SJS/TEN: 4-28 days; carbamazepine (HLA-B 15:02), allopurinol, sulfonamides, nevirapine, lamotrigine, NSAIDs.`]},{heading:`Clinical and laboratory`,points:[`DRESS: morbilliform rash over 50% BSA, facial oedema, nodes, eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytes, hepatitis; mucosa mild.`,`SJS/TEN: painful dusky macules, detachment (SJS under 10%, overlap 10-30%, TEN over 30%), erosions of 2 or more mucosae, Nikolsky positive.`]},{heading:`Scores, treatment, prognosis`,points:[`DRESS: RegiSCAR; prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg tapered over 6-8 weeks; mortality about 10%; late autoimmunity.`,`SJS/TEN: SCORTEN; burns unit, eye care, cyclosporine 3-5 mg/kg/day; mortality up to 30% in TEN.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table DRESS versus SJS/TEN`],markSplit:[{part:`Timing and drugs`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical and laboratory features`,marks:2},{part:`Scores, treatment and prognosis`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`DRESS`,`SJS`,`TEN`,`RegiSCAR`,`SCORTEN`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Fever and rash chapter and atlas`,`NICE guideline NG240: Meningitis (bacterial) and meningococcal disease - recognition, diagnosis and management, 2024`,`WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever, 2025`,`McCrindle BW et al. Diagnosis, treatment and long-term management of Kawasaki disease: AHA scientific statement, Circulation 2017`,`ICMR Guidelines for diagnosis and management of acute febrile illness / rickettsial diseases, 2022`,`MoHFW National Strategic Plan for Measles and Rubella Elimination 2023-2026`,`WHO Clinical management and infection prevention and control for mpox, 2024`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`]});var d=[];d.push({id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia`,title:`Approach to fever with thrombocytopenia and fever with jaundice`,oneLiner:`**Fever with thrombocytopenia** (platelets under **150,000/uL**) and **fever with jaundice** are syndromic labels for a short, treatable list in India - **dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis, enteric fever and sepsis/DIC** - with **HLH and TTP** as the mimics that kill if missed; the **fever-jaundice-AKI triad** means **leptospirosis or severe falciparum malaria** until proved otherwise, and the first hour needs a **smear plus RDT, a blood culture, and empirical doxycycline (plus ceftriaxone if toxic)**, never a platelet count alone.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`fever with thrombocytopenia`,`fever with jaundice`,`acute febrile illness`,`Weil disease`,`fever jaundice AKI`,`tropical fever`,`DIC`,`ISTH DIC score`,`TTP`,`PLASMIC score`,`thrombotic microangiopathy`,`pseudothrombocytopenia`,`artesunate`,`doxycycline`,`platelet transfusion`,`hepatitis E`,`ascending cholangitis`,`HLH`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Thrombocytopenia** is a platelet count **below 150,000/uL**; it is mild at 100,000-150,000, moderate at 50,000-100,000 and severe **below 50,000**, and spontaneous bleeding is unusual until counts fall **below 10,000-20,000** unless platelet function is also impaired. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with thrombocytopenia** is one of the syndromes in which the ICMR asks the clinician to classify **acute febrile illness - fever plus what** - because each syndrome carries a short, treatable differential. [ICMR 2022]`,`In Indian hospital series, **dengue is the commonest cause** of fever with thrombocytopenia, followed by **malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis and enteric fever**; together these explain most of the cases in which a cause is found. [API 2019]`,`**Fever with jaundice** (bilirubin over **2-3 mg/dL** with visible icterus) in India is led by **acute viral hepatitis (A and E), leptospirosis, falciparum malaria and scrub typhus**, with **ascending cholangitis, liver abscess and sepsis** as the surgical and bacterial causes. [API 2019]`,`The **fever-jaundice-AKI triad** (with or without bleeding) is the classic presentation of **Weil disease (severe leptospirosis)**, but in India it is equally often **severe falciparum malaria, scrub typhus or Gram-negative sepsis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The burden is **seasonal - July to November** after the monsoon - with regional hot-spots: leptospirosis in Kerala, coastal Karnataka, Gujarat and Maharashtra; scrub typhus in the sub-Himalayan belt, Tamil Nadu and the North-East; malaria in Odisha, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and the North-East. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Dengue cases reported to NCVBDC have exceeded **2 lakh in peak years (2022-2024)**, and India reports the largest number of malaria cases in the WHO South-East Asia Region despite a steep fall since 2015. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`The case-fatality of these syndromes is driven by **delay** - missed falciparum malaria, missed leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage and untreated scrub typhus each carry mortality above **10-30%** once multi-organ failure sets in. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infective causes of fever with thrombocytopenia**: dengue, malaria (P. falciparum and P. vivax), scrub typhus and other rickettsioses, leptospirosis, enteric fever, sepsis with DIC, acute HIV, EBV and CMV, hantavirus, Kyasanur Forest disease and severe COVID-19. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-infective and mimics**: **haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)**, **thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)**, haemolytic uraemic syndrome, acute leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, SLE, drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia and **heparin-induced thrombocytopenia** in hospitalised patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Causes of fever with jaundice**: viral hepatitis A, E, B (and D), leptospirosis, falciparum malaria (and severe vivax), scrub typhus, enteric hepatitis, dengue hepatitis, **ascending cholangitis**, pyogenic or amoebic liver abscess, sepsis-associated cholestasis, haemolysis (G6PD deficiency with infection), and drug-induced liver injury (anti-TB drugs, co-amoxiclav). [API 2019]`,`**Exposure clues**: wading in flood water, paddy work, sewer work and rat exposure (leptospirosis); grass, farm and forest exposure (scrub typhus); travel or residence in a malarious district; day-biting mosquitoes and stored water (dengue); unsafe water or street food (hepatitis A and E, enteric fever). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Host factors that change the list**: pregnancy (hepatitis E, HELLP, acute fatty liver, severe malaria), **alcohol and cirrhosis**, diabetes (melioidosis, liver abscess), splenectomy (overwhelming sepsis, severe malaria), and HIV or immunosuppression (disseminated infection, HLH). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug history** matters in both syndromes: **heparin, linezolid, vancomycin, beta-lactams, valproate and quinine** cause thrombocytopenia, and **NSAIDs or aspirin** taken for fever convert dengue thrombocytopenia into bleeding. [API 2019]`,`**Kyasanur Forest disease (KFD)** - a tick-borne flavivirus of the Western Ghats - presents in the dry season (November-June) with fever, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia and bleeding in forest workers; see the One Health and zoonoses topic. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the platelets fall and the bilirubin rises`,points:[`**Dengue**: the virus suppresses megakaryopoiesis early, then **anti-NS1 and anti-platelet antibodies** and complement drive peripheral destruction; the nadir comes at **defervescence (day 3-7)**, the same time as plasma leak. [WHO 2025]`,`**Malaria**: thrombocytopenia comes from **splenic sequestration, immune clearance of platelets coated with parasite antigen, and platelet activation** in cytoadherent microvessels; it is almost universal in falciparum and common in vivax but is **not itself a WHO severity criterion**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Rickettsial infection and leptospirosis** cause **endothelial vasculitis**, so platelets are consumed on injured endothelium; in leptospirosis the same vasculitis produces **pulmonary haemorrhage, conjunctival suffusion and AKI**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis-induced coagulopathy and DIC**: tissue factor on monocytes and endothelium triggers thrombin generation, natural anticoagulants (antithrombin, protein C) fall and fibrinolysis is suppressed (**PAI-1**), giving microvascular thrombosis, consumption of platelets and factors, and organ failure. [ISTH 2019]`,`**TTP**: severe deficiency of **ADAMTS13 (under 10%)**, usually from an autoantibody, leaves ultra-large von Willebrand multimers that build platelet-rich microthrombi - so platelets fall, red cells fragment (**schistocytes**) and the brain, heart and kidney become ischaemic while **coagulation tests stay normal**. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Jaundice in malaria** is mixed: **haemolysis** raises unconjugated bilirubin, while hepatocyte dysfunction and cholestasis raise conjugated bilirubin; transaminases are usually **under 200-300 U/L**, which separates it from viral hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice in leptospirosis** is **cholestatic with bilirubin out of proportion** to modestly raised transaminases (usually under 5 times normal) and a raised **CK** from myositis; the kidney lesion is tubulo-interstitial with **non-oliguric, hypokalaemic AKI** from proximal tubular sodium and potassium wasting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viral hepatitis** is hepatocellular: **ALT in the thousands**, ALT usually above AST, and the prodromal fever typically **settles as jaundice appears** - persistence of fever after jaundice should prompt a search for another cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cholangitis** is bacterial infection above an obstructed bile duct; raised biliary pressure drives bacteria and endotoxin into the blood, producing **Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain)** and septic shock. [TG18]`]},{heading:`Classification - the syndromic frame examiners ask for`,points:[`**Fever with thrombocytopenia alone** (no organ dysfunction): dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, enteric fever and viral fevers - usually OPD or ward care with daily counts. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fever with thrombocytopenia and bleeding**: severe dengue, DIC in sepsis, leptospirosis, KFD, meningococcaemia, acute leukaemia and, rarely, viral haemorrhagic fever - always an admission. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with thrombocytopenia and anaemia**: malaria, **TTP/HUS (with schistocytes)**, HLH, leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, kala-azar and infective endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with jaundice - three patterns**: **prehepatic** (haemolysis: malaria, G6PD, HUS), **hepatocellular** (viral hepatitis, dengue, scrub typhus, drug injury, enteric) and **cholestatic** (leptospirosis, cholangitis, sepsis, liver abscess). [API 2019]`,`**Fever-jaundice-AKI (plus or minus bleeding or ARDS)** is the most dangerous combination - **leptospirosis, severe falciparum malaria, scrub typhus, Gram-negative sepsis, hantavirus and HUS** - and should be managed in HDU or ICU from the first hour. [API 2019]`,`**Bicytopenia or pancytopenia with fever, splenomegaly and very high ferritin** is HLH until shown otherwise - apply the **HScore** (see the HLH topic). [HLH-2004]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Fix the **day of illness**: dengue platelets fall from **day 3-7**, scrub typhus and leptospirosis complications appear at **day 5-10**, and viral hepatitis jaundice comes after a **3-7 day prodrome** in which fever settles. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Fever pattern**: abrupt fever with retro-orbital pain and myalgia (dengue); rigors and sweats (malaria, cholangitis, liver abscess); severe **calf myalgia, headache and red eyes** (leptospirosis); headache, cough and a hidden eschar (scrub typhus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding history**: gum bleeding, epistaxis, petechiae and menorrhagia (thrombocytopenia), **haemoptysis** (leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage), melaena (dengue, DIC) and oozing from venepuncture sites (DIC). [WHO 2025]`,`**Jaundice history**: dark urine and pale stools (cholestasis), **cola-coloured urine** (haemoglobinuria from malaria or G6PD haemolysis - historically 'blackwater fever'), RUQ pain with rigors (cholangitis, liver abscess) and itching. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine output and breathlessness**: reduced urine or oliguria suggests AKI; breathlessness on day 4-7 suggests leptospiral or malarial ARDS, dengue plasma leak with effusion, or scrub typhus pneumonitis. [API 2019]`,`**Neurological symptoms**: confusion, seizures or coma point to **cerebral malaria**, scrub meningoencephalitis, leptospiral aseptic meningitis, hepatic encephalopathy or **TTP (fluctuating focal signs)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **drugs and toxins**: self-medicated NSAIDs, antibiotics before cultures, anti-TB drugs, alcohol, herbal remedies and paracetamol overdose. [API 2019]`,`Ask the **LMP in every woman aged 15-49**: pregnancy brings in hepatitis E, HELLP, acute fatty liver of pregnancy and severe malaria. [FOGSI 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Vitals first**: pulse, BP, **pulse pressure (20 mmHg or less = compensated dengue shock)**, respiratory rate, SpO2, temperature, capillary refill and urine output; score with **NEWS2** rather than qSOFA alone. [SSC 2026]`,`**Eschar** - a painless black scab with a red rim in the axilla, groin, waistband or under the breasts - is diagnostic of scrub typhus in the right setting; undress fully to find it. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Conjunctival suffusion** (redness without discharge), calf tenderness and subconjunctival haemorrhage point to **leptospirosis**; a flushed face, petechiae and a positive **tourniquet test** point to dengue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** suggests malaria, enteric fever, kala-azar, HLH, leukaemia, infective endocarditis or brucellosis; **tender hepatomegaly** suggests dengue, viral hepatitis or liver abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Purpura fulminans** or a rapidly spreading purpuric rash with shock suggests **meningococcaemia or DIC** - give the first antibiotic within the hour. [SSC 2026]`,`**Murphy sign, RUQ tenderness and a palpable gall bladder** suggest cholangitis or cholecystitis; **intercostal tenderness** over the liver suggests liver abscess. [TG18]`,`**Pallor with jaundice and no tender liver** suggests haemolysis (malaria, G6PD, TTP); **lymphadenopathy** suggests scrub typhus, EBV, HIV, TB or lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding signs**: petechiae over legs, gum oozing, venepuncture bleeding and fundal haemorrhages; check the fundus in TTP and severe thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - the discriminators`,points:[`**Dengue vs malaria**: dengue has **leucopenia with rising haematocrit** and a falling platelet count at defervescence; malaria has anaemia, splenomegaly, rigors and a positive smear or RDT. [WHO 2025]`,`**Leptospirosis vs viral hepatitis**: leptospirosis has **neutrophilia, raised CK, AKI and bilirubin out of proportion to modest transaminases**; viral hepatitis has ALT in the thousands, normal creatinine and fever that settles once jaundice appears. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus vs leptospirosis**: both cause fever, myalgia, AKI and jaundice; **eschar, lymphadenopathy and pneumonitis** favour scrub, while **conjunctival suffusion, calf pain and pulmonary haemorrhage** favour leptospirosis - doxycycline covers both. [ICMR 2022]`,`**DIC vs TTP**: DIC has a **prolonged PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen and very high D-dimer**; TTP has **normal coagulation, schistocytes over 1%, LDH very high and ADAMTS13 under 10%**. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Severe malaria vs sepsis**: both cause shock, AKI and jaundice; malaria shows parasites or a positive RDT, while sepsis has a focus or positive culture - in a malarious area, **treat both until one is excluded**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Cholangitis vs hepatitis**: cholangitis has **rigors, RUQ pain, raised ALP and GGT** and a dilated duct on ultrasound; hepatitis has hepatocellular enzymes and a normal duct. [TG18]`,`**HLH vs severe sepsis**: HLH has **ferritin often over 3000-10,000 ng/mL, falling ESR, low fibrinogen, raised triglycerides and splenomegaly** with persistent fever despite antibiotics. [HLH-2004]`,`**Pseudothrombocytopenia** (EDTA-induced platelet clumping) gives a falsely low count in a well patient with no bleeding; confirm on a **peripheral smear or a citrate tube** before acting. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line in OPD or district hospital`,points:[`**CBC with haematocrit and a peripheral smear** read by a person: platelet clumps, parasites, schistocytes, toxic granulation, atypical lymphocytes and blasts each change the diagnosis. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Malaria RDT plus thick and thin smear** in every febrile patient in an endemic area; repeat the smear **every 12-24 hours for 3 sets** if negative but suspicion is high. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Dengue NS1 antigen (day 1-5)** and **IgM MAC-ELISA (from day 5)**; scrub typhus and leptospira **IgM ELISA from day 5**; an early negative IgM is falsely reassuring. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Liver tests** (bilirubin fractions, AST, ALT, ALP, GGT, albumin) and **renal function with electrolytes** - hypokalaemia with AKI suggests leptospirosis; **AST or ALT over 1000 U/L** in dengue is severe organ involvement. [WHO 2025]`,`**Two sets of blood cultures before the first antibiotic** in any toxic or prolonged fever; culture is the only way to find enteric fever and melioidosis. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Urine**: haemoglobinuria (dipstick blood with no red cells) suggests intravascular haemolysis; proteinuria and casts suggest leptospiral or malarial nephropathy. [API 2019]`,`**Ultrasound abdomen**: gall-bladder wall oedema, ascites and pleural effusion (dengue plasma leak), dilated bile ducts (cholangitis), liver abscess, splenomegaly and renal size. [TG18]`,`**Bedside glucose and lactate**: hypoglycaemia in malaria and sepsis; **lactate 2 mmol/L or more** is abnormal and 4 or more is high risk. [SSC 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and interpretation cut-offs`,points:[`**Coagulation screen** - PT/INR, aPTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer - and the **ISTH overt DIC score**: platelets, D-dimer rise, PT prolongation and fibrinogen; a score of **5 or more** is overt DIC. [ISTH 2019]`,`**Sepsis-induced coagulopathy (SIC) score** uses platelets, INR and SOFA; a score of **4 or more** identifies early coagulopathy before overt DIC. [ISTH 2019]`,`**Haemolysis screen**: LDH, reticulocytes, haptoglobin, indirect bilirubin and **direct antiglobulin test**; schistocytes **over 1%** on a smear support a thrombotic microangiopathy. [ICSH 2021]`,`**PLASMIC score** (7 items: platelets under 30,000, haemolysis, no active cancer, no transplant, MCV under 90 fL, INR under 1.5, creatinine under 2.0 mg/dL); a score of **6-7 means high probability of severe ADAMTS13 deficiency** - start plasma exchange before the level returns. [ISTH 2020]`,`**HLH screen**: ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, soluble CD25 and bone-marrow examination; ferritin **over 10,000 ng/mL** is highly suggestive in adults. [HLH-2004]`,`**Leptospira microscopic agglutination test (MAT)** is the reference test: a single titre of **1:400 or more** in an endemic area, or a **fourfold rise** in paired sera, is diagnostic; **PCR** on blood in the first week is positive before antibodies. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Scrub typhus IgM ELISA** uses a locally validated optical density cut-off; **PCR on eschar or blood** confirms early disease; Weil-Felix (OX-K) is insensitive and should not be used to exclude it. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Viral hepatitis markers**: IgM anti-HAV, **IgM anti-HEV**, HBsAg with IgM anti-HBc, and anti-HCV; **CT or MRCP** for suspected biliary obstruction, and **ERCP** for drainage. [TG18]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Give **paracetamol only** - 500-1000 mg orally every 6 hours, maximum 3-4 g a day (lower in liver disease and alcohol use) - until dengue is excluded; **no NSAIDs, aspirin or intramuscular injections**. [WHO 2025]`,`Advise **oral fluids 2.5-3 L a day** (ORS, soups, coconut water) and passing urine at least every 6 hours; record intake and output on a home chart. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Uncomplicated P. vivax**: chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days (10, 10, 5 mg/kg) plus **primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days** after G6PD testing; **uncomplicated P. falciparum**: artesunate plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine for 3 days (artemether-lumefantrine in the North-East) plus single-dose primaquine 0.75 mg/kg. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Suspected scrub typhus, leptospirosis or rickettsia** after day 3-5 with negative malaria and dengue tests: **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days**; **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days** in pregnancy or doxycycline intolerance. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Suspected acute viral hepatitis A or E** with normal INR, no encephalopathy and tolerating oral intake: rest, oral fluids, avoid alcohol and hepatotoxic drugs, and review LFT and INR in **1 week**. [API 2019]`,`**Daily review** in the critical phase: CBC and haematocrit, warning signs, urine output and bleeding; **refer the same day** if any red flag appears. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Do not treat a platelet count** - in a stable dengue patient, platelets of 20,000 without bleeding need monitoring, not a transfusion. [WHO 2025]`,`**Notify** dengue, malaria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, hepatitis A/E and clusters of fever on the **IHIP (IDSP) portal**. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** any fever with thrombocytopenia or jaundice plus bleeding, warning signs, shock, SpO2 under 94%, oliguria, encephalopathy, INR over 1.5, pregnancy or social isolation. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Severe AUF with thrombocytopenia or jaundice and no diagnosis yet**: take cultures, then give **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily plus doxycycline 100 mg (oral or IV) twice daily** within the first hour; add **IV artesunate** if malaria cannot be excluded. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Severe malaria**: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then once daily** (3 mg/kg in children under 20 kg) until oral intake, then a full 3-day oral ACT; check glucose 4-hourly. [WHO 2025]`,`**Severe leptospirosis**: **IV benzylpenicillin 1.5 million units 6-hourly** or **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily for 7 days**; doxycycline is the oral alternative; pulmonary haemorrhage needs early ventilation and dialysis for oligo-anuric AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe scrub typhus**: **IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin combination** is now preferred over either alone for severe disease (INTREST trial, 2025); see the scrub typhus topic for doses. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Dengue with warning signs or shock**: crystalloid by the haematocrit-guided ladder (see the Dengue topic); **platelet transfusion only for significant bleeding**, not for a number. [WHO 2025]`,`**DIC**: treat the cause; transfuse **platelets to keep over 50,000** in active bleeding, **FFP 15-30 mL/kg** for prolonged PT/aPTT with bleeding, and **cryoprecipitate** for fibrinogen under 1.5 g/L. [ISTH 2019]`,`**Ascending cholangitis**: blood cultures, IV antibiotics (piperacillin-tazobactam or ceftriaxone plus metronidazole), and **biliary drainage (ERCP) within 24 hours** for moderate and immediately for severe (Tokyo grade III) disease. [TG18]`,`**Suspected TTP**: urgent **plasma exchange**, methylprednisolone and rituximab, with caplacizumab where available; **avoid platelet transfusion** unless life-threatening bleeding - refer to a haematology centre. [ISTH 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy with fever and jaundice**: think **hepatitis E (maternal mortality up to 20-25% in the third trimester)**, HELLP syndrome, acute fatty liver of pregnancy and severe malaria; IV artesunate is used in all trimesters for severe malaria. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: doxycycline is avoided - use **azithromycin** for scrub typhus and leptospirosis; primaquine is withheld until after delivery and breastfeeding of a G6PD-unknown infant. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Elderly**: fever may be absent and delirium the only sign; hidden sepsis, cholangitis from stones and drug-induced thrombocytopenia are commoner. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: leptospirosis and malaria AKI need early dialysis; doxycycline and ceftriaxone need **no renal dose change**, but penicillin G and many cephalosporins do. [Sanford 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: think **melioidosis** (fever, hepatosplenic abscesses, thrombocytopenia) and **pyogenic liver abscess (Klebsiella)** - blood culture and ultrasound before closing the case. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Children**: dengue shock appears at defervescence with a narrow pulse pressure, and enteric hepatitis is commoner; doxycycline is safe for short courses in children of all ages (**2.2 mg/kg twice daily**). [AAP 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if tests are unavailable and the patient is toxic, give **artesunate (or an ACT) plus doxycycline plus ceftriaxone** and refer; do not wait for results to start treatment. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Bleeding**: GI bleeding in dengue and DIC, **pulmonary haemorrhage in leptospirosis (mortality often over 50%)**, intracranial haemorrhage in TTP and severe thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AKI**: leptospirosis (non-oliguric, hypokalaemic), falciparum malaria (acute tubular necrosis, haemoglobinuria), scrub typhus and sepsis; most recover if dialysed in time. [API 2019]`,`**ARDS** in falciparum malaria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus and dengue; it can appear after parasite clearance in malaria. [WHO 2025]`,`**Acute liver failure** (INR 1.5 or more with encephalopathy) from hepatitis A, E or B, dengue and drugs - list early for transplant evaluation using King's College criteria. [EASL 2017]`,`**Secondary HLH** after dengue, EBV, TB, scrub typhus or leishmaniasis presents as fever that persists despite treatment with worsening cytopenias. [HLH-2004]`,`**Prognosis**: with early treatment, mortality is under 1% for dengue and uncomplicated malaria, but severe malaria, Weil disease and severe scrub typhus carry **10-30% mortality**, highest with multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer same day** to a hospital with ICU, dialysis and blood bank for shock, bleeding, oliguria, altered sensorium, respiratory distress, INR over 1.5 or rapidly falling platelets with rising haematocrit. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Monitor in the ward**: vitals and urine output 2-4 hourly, haematocrit and platelets **12-24 hourly** in the critical phase, electrolytes, LFT, creatinine and glucose daily. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Malaria follow-up**: a smear on **day 3** (parasitaemia should fall by over 75%) and on day 28 for treatment failure; vivax patients complete 14 days of primaquine. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Discharge** a dengue patient when afebrile for 48 hours without antipyretics, appetite and urine output are normal, haematocrit is stable and **platelets are over 50,000** and rising. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Viral hepatitis follow-up**: LFT and INR at 1-2 weeks until normal; **HBsAg at 6 months** to define chronic infection. [API 2019]`,`**Persistent fever with cytopenias** after 5-7 days of appropriate treatment needs **ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen and bone-marrow examination** for HLH, leukaemia, kala-azar and TB. [HLH-2004]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**Vector control** under **NCVBDC**: source reduction of Aedes breeding (weekly 'dry day'), insecticide-treated nets and indoor residual spray for malaria. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Leptospirosis prophylaxis**: **doxycycline 200 mg orally once a week** during exposure in flood-hit or high-risk groups (farmers, sanitation workers), as used by Kerala and Maharashtra health departments. [NPPCL 2023]`,`**Hepatitis A and E** are prevented by safe water and sanitation under **Jal Jeevan Mission and Swachh Bharat**; hepatitis B by vaccination under **UIP and NVHCP**. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Counsel** every dengue-risk patient to avoid NSAIDs, keep fluids up, and return on warning signs; counsel forest and farm workers on protective clothing and repellents against mites and ticks. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Notify** through **IDSP/IHIP**: clusters of fever with thrombocytopenia or jaundice trigger a rapid response team; see the National communicable disease programmes topic. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Guidelines for clinical management of arboviral diseases (2025)** cover dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever together and recommend **against prophylactic platelet transfusion** in dengue without bleeding. [WHO 2025]`,`The older **Indian national dengue guideline (NVBDCP 2014)** allowed prophylactic platelets below 10,000; current WHO and ICMR practice reserves transfusion for **significant bleeding** - follow the newer advice and document the reason if you transfuse. [WHO 2025]`,`**INTREST trial (NEJM 2025)**: in severe scrub typhus, **combined IV doxycycline and azithromycin** reduced the composite of death, persistent complications and fever compared with either drug alone. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** recommends against **qSOFA as a single screening tool**, favouring NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS, and keeps antibiotics within 1 hour when shock is likely. [SSC 2026]`,`**NCVBDC National Drug Policy on Malaria** and the **National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027** keep artesunate-based ACT and target zero indigenous cases by 2027 and elimination by 2030. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**WHO Guidelines for malaria (2025 update)** keep IV artesunate for severe malaria in all age groups and trimesters, and single low-dose primaquine for falciparum transmission blocking. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic long question**: 'Approach to a patient with fever and thrombocytopenia' - open with the syndromic frame, list five tropical causes and three mimics (HLH, TTP, DIC), then day-of-illness tests and the empirical first hour. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Classic short note**: 'Fever with jaundice and AKI' - Weil disease, severe falciparum malaria, scrub typhus, sepsis, hantavirus; give the lab discriminators. [API 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: transfusing platelets in stable dengue to a number - it does not prevent bleeding and can cause volume overload. [WHO 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: giving ceftriaxone alone to a patient with fever and thrombocytopenia - it misses scrub typhus and rickettsia; **add doxycycline**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Viva**: 'Why is the platelet count low but PT normal?' - points to dengue, malaria, ITP or TTP rather than DIC; then look for schistocytes. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Viva**: 'In viral hepatitis, when does fever settle?' - usually when jaundice appears; fever persisting after jaundice suggests leptospirosis, malaria, cholangitis or abscess. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Fever with thrombocytopenia - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clinical clue`,`Laboratory clue`,`Confirmatory test`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Dengue`,`Retro-orbital pain, myalgia, rash, leak at defervescence`,`Leucopenia, rising haematocrit, AST over ALT`,`NS1 (day 1-5), IgM MAC-ELISA (day 5+)`,`Paracetamol, fluids by haematocrit`],[`Malaria`,`Rigors, sweats, splenomegaly, travel`,`Anaemia, raised indirect bilirubin, low platelets`,`Smear plus RDT`,`ACT; IV artesunate if severe`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, lymphadenopathy, cough`,`Raised transaminases, variable WBC`,`IgM ELISA (day 5+), PCR on eschar`,`Doxycycline or azithromycin`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Conjunctival suffusion, calf pain, flood exposure`,`Neutrophilia, raised CK, hypokalaemic AKI`,`IgM ELISA, MAT 1:400 or more, PCR`,`Doxycycline; IV penicillin or ceftriaxone`],[`Enteric fever`,`Step-ladder fever, relative bradycardia, constipation`,`Leucopenia, eosinopenia, mild LFT rise`,`Blood culture`,`Azithromycin or ceftriaxone`],[`Sepsis with DIC`,`Focus, shock, ooze from lines`,`Long PT/aPTT, low fibrinogen, high D-dimer`,`Cultures, ISTH DIC score 5+`,`Antibiotics within 1 h, source control`],[`TTP`,`Fluctuating neurology, little fever`,`Schistocytes over 1%, LDH high, normal PT`,`ADAMTS13 under 10%, PLASMIC 6-7`,`Plasma exchange, steroid, rituximab`],[`HLH`,`Persistent fever, splenomegaly, cytopenias`,`Ferritin very high, low fibrinogen, high TG`,`HLH-2004 criteria, HScore, marrow`,`Treat trigger; dexamethasone, refer`]]},{heading:`Fever with jaundice - pattern and discriminators`,columns:[`Cause`,`Bilirubin and enzymes`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Viral hepatitis A or E`,`ALT in thousands, ALT over AST`,`Fever settles as jaundice appears; normal creatinine`,`IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HEV`],[`Leptospirosis (Weil)`,`High bilirubin, ALT under 5x normal, high CK`,`AKI with hypokalaemia, suffusion, haemoptysis`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, PCR`],[`Falciparum malaria`,`Mixed bilirubin, ALT usually under 300`,`Anaemia, splenomegaly, haemoglobinuria`,`Smear, RDT`],[`Scrub typhus`,`ALT and AST 100-500, mild bilirubin`,`Eschar, lymph nodes, pneumonitis`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`],[`Ascending cholangitis`,`Raised ALP and GGT, conjugated bilirubin`,`Charcot triad, dilated CBD`,`Ultrasound, MRCP`],[`Liver abscess`,`ALP raised, mild bilirubin`,`Tender liver, intercostal tenderness`,`Ultrasound, amoebic serology`],[`Haemolysis (G6PD, TTP)`,`Unconjugated bilirubin, normal ALT`,`Pallor, dark urine, LDH high`,`Smear, retics, G6PD, DAT`],[`Sepsis cholestasis`,`Conjugated bilirubin, modest enzymes`,`Shock with a non-hepatic focus`,`Cultures, lactate`]]},{heading:`Drug table - empirical and specific therapy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg orally 6-hourly; child 15 mg/kg`,`While febrile; max 3-4 g/day (2 g in liver disease)`,`Hepatotoxic in overdose; no NSAID or aspirin in dengue`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg orally or IV twice daily; child 2.2 mg/kg`,`7 days`,`Oesophagitis (take upright with water); avoid in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg orally or IV once daily`,`5 days (scrub); 7 days at 1 g (enteric)`,`QT prolongation; safe in pregnancy`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV once daily (1 g for leptospirosis)`,`7 days or by culture`,`Biliary sludge; no Orientia/Rickettsia cover`],[`Benzylpenicillin`,`1.5 million units IV 6-hourly`,`7 days (severe leptospirosis)`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction; renal dose change`],[`Artesunate IV`,`2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily (3 mg/kg under 20 kg)`,`Until oral intake (minimum 24 h), then 3-day ACT`,`Delayed haemolysis at 1-3 weeks; check Hb`],[`Artesunate + SP`,`AS 4 mg/kg daily + SP (25/1.25 mg/kg) day 1`,`3 days + primaquine 0.75 mg/kg once`,`SP: sulfa allergy, Stevens-Johnson syndrome`],[`Chloroquine + primaquine (vivax)`,`CQ 10, 10, 5 mg/kg; PQ 0.25 mg/kg daily`,`3 days CQ; 14 days PQ`,`PQ haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; not in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Platelet count`,`Under 150,000 (severe under 50,000; bleeding risk under 10,000-20,000)`,`Grade of thrombocytopenia; confirm on smear`],[`Haematocrit`,`Rise of 20% over baseline`,`Dengue plasma leak`],[`AST/ALT in dengue`,`Over 1000 U/L`,`Severe dengue organ involvement`],[`ISTH overt DIC score`,`5 or more`,`Overt DIC; repeat daily`],[`SIC score`,`4 or more`,`Sepsis-induced coagulopathy`],[`Schistocytes on smear`,`Over 1%`,`Thrombotic microangiopathy`],[`PLASMIC score`,`6-7`,`High risk of severe ADAMTS13 deficiency (TTP)`],[`Leptospira MAT`,`1:400 single titre or fourfold rise`,`Confirmed leptospirosis`],[`Ferritin`,`Over 10,000 ng/mL`,`Strongly suggests HLH in adults`],[`Lactate`,`2 or more abnormal; 4 or more high risk`,`Hypoperfusion in sepsis or severe malaria`]]},{heading:`DIC vs TTP vs HLH - the three mimics`,columns:[`Feature`,`DIC`,`TTP`,`HLH`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Tissue factor-driven thrombin, consumption`,`ADAMTS13 deficiency, VWF microthrombi`,`Uncontrolled T-cell and macrophage activation`],[`PT and aPTT`,`Prolonged`,`Normal`,`Often prolonged`],[`Fibrinogen`,`Low`,`Normal`,`Low (under 1.5 g/L)`],[`Schistocytes`,`Few`,`Many (over 1%)`,`Few`],[`Ferritin`,`Raised`,`Mildly raised`,`Very high (often over 3000-10,000)`],[`Splenomegaly`,`No`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Key treatment`,`Treat cause, replace products`,`Plasma exchange, steroids, rituximab`,`Treat trigger, dexamethasone, etoposide`]]}],redFlags:[`Bleeding (haemoptysis, melaena, haematemesis, venepuncture ooze) with fever and thrombocytopenia`,`Shock or pulse pressure 20 mmHg or less, cold peripheries, capillary refill over 2 seconds`,`Fever with jaundice and oliguria or rising creatinine (fever-jaundice-AKI triad)`,`Breathlessness, SpO2 under 94% or haemoptysis (leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage, ARDS)`,`Altered sensorium, seizures or focal neurology (cerebral malaria, TTP, hepatic encephalopathy)`,`INR over 1.5 with jaundice, or hypoglycaemia`,`Schistocytes on the smear or a rapidly falling haemoglobin with low platelets`,`Fever persisting beyond 5-7 days of treatment with worsening cytopenias and very high ferritin`],pearls:[`**Fever plus what** - classify acute fever by its companion: thrombocytopenia, jaundice, AKI, rash, ARDS or altered sensorium.`,`**Low platelets with a normal PT** is not DIC - look for dengue, malaria, ITP or TTP.`,`**Bilirubin high, ALT modest, CK raised, potassium low, creatinine rising** - leptospirosis.`,`In viral hepatitis **fever goes when jaundice comes**; persistent fever with jaundice means look again.`,`**Treat the patient, not the platelet count** - no prophylactic platelets in stable dengue.`,`**Ceftriaxone alone misses scrub** - add doxycycline in severe undifferentiated fever.`,`Mnemonic for fever-jaundice-AKI: **'LMS-SH'** - Leptospirosis, Malaria, Scrub, Sepsis, Hantavirus/HUS.`,`**Check the smear before trusting a low count** - EDTA pseudothrombocytopenia is common.`,`**Pregnant, febrile and jaundiced** - hepatitis E, HELLP, AFLP and malaria; deliver the right test the same day.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old paddy farmer from coastal Karnataka presents on day 6 of fever with calf pain, red eyes, jaundice and reduced urine. Bilirubin is 14 mg/dL, ALT 110 U/L, CK 2400 U/L, creatinine 3.8 mg/dL and potassium 3.0 mmol/L. Platelets are 60,000/uL. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Acute hepatitis E`,`Severe leptospirosis (Weil disease)`,`Dengue with hepatitis`,`Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura`],answer:1,explanation:`Weil disease fits every clue: flood or paddy exposure, conjunctival suffusion, calf myalgia with a raised CK, bilirubin out of proportion to modestly raised ALT, and non-oliguric hypokalaemic AKI. Hepatitis E would show ALT in the thousands and normal creatinine; dengue hepatitis has AST over ALT without marked cholestasis; TTP has little fever, schistocytes and normal transaminases.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q2`,stem:`A patient with fever and confusion has platelets of 18,000/uL, haemoglobin 7.8 g/dL, LDH 1900 U/L, schistocytes 4% on the smear, PT and aPTT normal, and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL. Malaria RDT and smear are negative. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Transfuse four units of random-donor platelets`,`Start IV ceftriaxone and await blood culture`,`Start urgent plasma exchange with corticosteroids and send ADAMTS13`,`Give FFP and cryoprecipitate for DIC`],answer:2,explanation:`Thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic haemolysis with schistocytes over 1%, neurological signs and normal coagulation give a high PLASMIC score, so TTP is likely and plasma exchange must start before the ADAMTS13 result. Platelet transfusion can worsen microthrombosis; ceftriaxone alone treats nothing here; DIC is unlikely when PT and aPTT are normal.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q3`,stem:`A 22-year-old with confirmed dengue on day 5 is haemodynamically stable, eating, passing urine and has no bleeding. Platelets are 14,000/uL. According to current WHO guidance, what is the correct approach?`,options:[`Close monitoring with oral fluids and paracetamol; no prophylactic platelet transfusion`,`Prophylactic platelet transfusion because the count is under 20,000`,`Start oral prednisolone to raise the platelet count`,`Give IV immunoglobulin`],answer:0,explanation:`WHO arboviral guidance (2025) recommends against prophylactic platelet transfusion in stable dengue without bleeding; it does not prevent bleeding and risks fluid overload. Steroids and IV immunoglobulin have no proven benefit in dengue thrombocytopenia. The older Indian 2014 threshold of 10,000 is superseded and would not apply at 14,000 anyway.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q4`,stem:`Which finding best separates disseminated intravascular coagulation from thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura in a febrile patient with low platelets?`,options:[`Raised LDH`,`Presence of fever`,`Raised serum creatinine`,`Prolonged PT with low fibrinogen`],answer:3,explanation:`DIC consumes clotting factors, so PT and aPTT are prolonged and fibrinogen falls; in TTP the coagulation screen is characteristically normal because the lesion is VWF-platelet microthrombi from ADAMTS13 deficiency. Raised LDH, fever and a raised creatinine occur in both and do not discriminate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-q5`,stem:`A 40-year-old from a malaria-endemic district arrives on day 7 of fever with jaundice, hypotension and platelets of 40,000/uL. The malaria RDT is unavailable at night. Blood cultures have been drawn. Which empirical regimen is most appropriate in the first hour?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone alone`,`IV artesunate plus ceftriaxone plus doxycycline`,`Chloroquine plus primaquine`,`Meropenem plus vancomycin`],answer:1,explanation:`Severe undifferentiated fever with jaundice in an endemic area must cover severe malaria (IV artesunate), Gram-negative sepsis and leptospirosis (ceftriaxone) and scrub typhus or rickettsia (doxycycline) until tests narrow it. Ceftriaxone alone misses malaria and scrub; chloroquine has no role in severe malaria; meropenem with vancomycin is broad yet misses malaria and intracellular organisms.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, approach to diagnosis and management of a patient presenting with fever and thrombocytopenia.`,openingLines:[`Fever with thrombocytopenia - fever with a platelet count below 150,000/uL - is a syndromic presentation of acute febrile illness in India, dominated by dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis and enteric fever.`,`The approach rests on the day of illness, the companion syndrome (bleeding, jaundice, AKI, anaemia), a smear-led first panel and an empirical first hour that covers what kills, while excluding the mimics DIC, TTP and HLH.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`Thrombocytopenia under 150,000/uL; severe under 50,000; bleeding risk under 10,000-20,000.`,`Seasonal post-monsoon burden; dengue the commonest cause in Indian series.`]},{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Infective: dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis, enteric fever, sepsis/DIC, viral (HIV, EBV, hantavirus, KFD).`,`Non-infective mimics: TTP/HUS, HLH, leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, drug-induced, pseudothrombocytopenia.`]},{heading:`Mechanisms`,points:[`Marrow suppression (dengue, viral), immune destruction (dengue, malaria), sequestration (malaria), consumption (DIC, vasculitis), microthrombi (TTP).`]},{heading:`Clinical approach`,points:[`Day of illness, exposure, bleeding, jaundice, urine output, drugs, pregnancy.`,`Examination: vitals and pulse pressure, eschar, conjunctival suffusion, spleen, liver, purpura, fundus.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`First line: CBC with haematocrit and smear, malaria RDT and smear, NS1/IgM, LFT, RFT with potassium, blood cultures, urine, ultrasound.`,`Second line: PT/aPTT/fibrinogen/D-dimer (ISTH DIC score), LDH, retics, schistocytes, PLASMIC, ferritin, MAT, scrub PCR, marrow.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Paracetamol only; oral fluids; no NSAID or IM injection; daily counts in critical phase.`,`Specific: ACT or IV artesunate; doxycycline or azithromycin for scrub and lepto; ceftriaxone or penicillin for severe lepto; azithromycin or ceftriaxone for enteric.`,`Severe undifferentiated: cultures then ceftriaxone plus doxycycline within 1 h, plus artesunate if malaria not excluded.`,`Platelets only for significant bleeding (dengue) or to keep over 50,000 in bleeding DIC; avoid in TTP.`]},{heading:`Red flags, prevention and notification`,points:[`Admit for bleeding, shock, oliguria, ARDS, encephalopathy, jaundice with INR over 1.5.`,`Vector control (NCVBDC), leptospirosis doxycycline prophylaxis, IDSP/IHIP notification.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: fever with thrombocytopenia - stable vs unstable, first panel, empirical first hour, then DIC/TTP/HLH branch`,`Table: dengue vs malaria vs scrub vs leptospirosis vs enteric - clue, lab, test, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical approach`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Management including empirical therapy`,marks:3},{part:`Red flags and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`fever with thrombocytopenia`,`dengue`,`malaria`,`scrub typhus`,`leptospirosis`,`DIC`,`TTP`,`HLH`,`doxycycline`,`artesunate`]},{id:`infectious-fever-fever-with-thrombocytopenia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate leptospirosis, falciparum malaria and acute viral hepatitis in a patient with fever and jaundice.`,openingLines:[`Fever with jaundice in India is led by acute viral hepatitis, leptospirosis and falciparum malaria; each has a distinct biochemical signature.`,`The discriminators are the timing of fever relative to jaundice, the transaminase-to-bilirubin ratio, the kidney and the blood film.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Leptospirosis: flood/paddy exposure, calf myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, haemoptysis.`,`Malaria: rigors, anaemia, splenomegaly, dark urine.`,`Viral hepatitis: prodrome then jaundice; fever settles as jaundice appears; tender liver.`]},{heading:`Laboratory`,points:[`Leptospirosis: neutrophilia, high bilirubin with ALT under 5x normal, CK raised, hypokalaemic non-oliguric AKI.`,`Malaria: anaemia, mixed bilirubin, ALT under 300, parasites on smear or RDT positive.`,`Viral hepatitis: ALT in thousands, normal creatinine, IgM anti-HAV or anti-HEV.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Leptospirosis: doxycycline; IV penicillin or ceftriaxone if severe; dialysis.`,`Malaria: IV artesunate for severe; ACT for uncomplicated.`,`Viral hepatitis: supportive; watch INR and encephalopathy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table: leptospirosis vs falciparum malaria vs viral hepatitis`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Laboratory differences`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`fever with jaundice`,`Weil disease`,`falciparum malaria`,`hepatitis E`,`CK`,`MAT`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022 (Acute undifferentiated febrile illness)`,`WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever, 2025`,`WHO Guidelines for malaria, 2025 update; NCVBDC National Drug Policy on Malaria and National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027`,`ISTH guidance on sepsis-induced coagulopathy and DIC, 2019; ISTH guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of TTP, 2020`,`Tokyo Guidelines 2018 (TG18) for acute cholangitis`,`Varghese GM et al. Intravenous treatment for scrub typhus (INTREST). N Engl J Med 2025`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022; API Expert Consensus on tropical fevers`]}),d.push({id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation`,title:`Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and infection-triggered hyperinflammation`,oneLiner:`**Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)** is a life-threatening **hyperinflammatory syndrome of uncontrolled cytotoxic T-cell, NK-cell and macrophage activation** - primary (familial perforin-pathway defects) or secondary to **infection (EBV, dengue, TB, leishmania, scrub typhus), malignancy or autoimmunity (macrophage activation syndrome)** - recognised by **persistent fever, splenomegaly, cytopenias, very high ferritin, high triglycerides and low fibrinogen**, diagnosed by the **HLH-2004 criteria (5 of 8)** or an **HScore of 169 or more**, and treated by controlling the trigger plus **dexamethasone, with etoposide** at a specialist centre.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`HLH`,`haemophagocytic syndrome`,`macrophage activation syndrome`,`MAS`,`HLH-2004 criteria`,`HScore`,`ferritin`,`soluble CD25`,`sIL-2R`,`familial HLH`,`perforin`,`EBV-HLH`,`dengue HLH`,`etoposide`,`dexamethasone`,`anakinra`,`emapalumab`,`ruxolitinib`,`cytokine storm`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**HLH** is a syndrome of **excessive, ineffective immune activation** in which cytotoxic lymphocytes fail to clear an antigen, so activated T cells and macrophages flood the body with cytokines and cause fever, cytopenias, hepatitis, coagulopathy and multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary (familial) HLH** is an autosomal recessive or X-linked disease of infancy and childhood, with an estimated incidence of about **1 in 50,000 live births**; **secondary HLH** is far commoner in adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Macrophage activation syndrome (MAS)** is the name for HLH arising in rheumatic disease - classically **systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis and adult-onset Still disease**, and also SLE. [EULAR/ACR 2023]`,`In adults worldwide, triggers are roughly **infection about half, malignancy (mainly T/NK-cell lymphoma) about a third, and autoimmune disease about a tenth**, with some cases having more than one. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`In **Indian adult series** from tertiary centres, **infection is the leading trigger** - especially **tuberculosis, dengue, EBV, scrub typhus, visceral leishmaniasis and enteric fever** - followed by lymphoma and autoimmune disease. [API 2022]`,`HLH is **under-diagnosed** because it masquerades as sepsis, severe dengue or liver failure; in ICU cohorts with refractory 'sepsis' and hyperferritinaemia, a proportion meet HLH criteria. [Crit Care Med 2022]`,`Overall adult mortality is about **40%**, worst with **malignancy-associated HLH** and best with treatable infections such as dengue and leishmaniasis. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and triggers`,points:[`**Familial HLH genes** impair perforin-granule killing: **PRF1 (FHL2), UNC13D (FHL3), STX11 (FHL4), STXBP2 (FHL5)**; syndromic forms include **Griscelli type 2 (RAB27A), Chediak-Higashi (LYST)** and **X-linked lymphoproliferative disease (SH2D1A, XIAP)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viral triggers**: **EBV (commonest worldwide)**, CMV, HSV, HIV (acute infection and IRIS), influenza, **dengue** and severe COVID-19. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Bacterial and intracellular triggers important in India**: **tuberculosis**, scrub typhus, rickettsiae, enteric fever, brucellosis and leptospirosis. [API 2022]`,`**Parasitic and fungal triggers**: **visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)**, malaria, histoplasmosis and other disseminated fungi. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignancy**: **T-cell and NK-cell lymphomas** (including EBV-driven extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma), Hodgkin lymphoma, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma and leukaemias; HLH can precede the lymphoma diagnosis by weeks. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Autoimmune**: adult-onset Still disease, systemic JIA, SLE, Kawasaki disease and vasculitis (MAS). [EULAR/ACR 2023]`,`**Iatrogenic**: **CAR-T cell therapy**, bispecific T-cell engagers, **immune checkpoint inhibitors**, allogeneic stem-cell transplant and severe drug reactions (DRESS). [ASTCT 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`Normally **CD8 T cells and NK cells kill infected cells and antigen-presenting cells** with perforin and granzymes, which ends the immune response; when killing fails, antigen persists and stimulation continues. [Harrison 22e]`,`Persisting stimulation drives massive **interferon-gamma** release from CD8 T cells, which activates macrophages to secrete **IL-1, IL-6, IL-18 and TNF-alpha** - a self-amplifying cytokine storm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ferritin** is secreted by activated macrophages and rises steeply; **soluble CD25 (sIL-2R alpha)** is shed by activated T cells - both are activity markers of the syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha inhibits lipoprotein lipase**, raising **triglycerides**; activated macrophages release **plasminogen activator**, which lowers **fibrinogen** and makes the ESR fall paradoxically. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cytopenias** come from cytokine (IFN-gamma, TNF) suppression of haematopoiesis plus **haemophagocytosis** by activated macrophages in marrow, spleen and liver; haemophagocytosis is a late and inconsistent finding. [Harrison 22e]`,`Infiltration of liver, spleen and nodes by activated lymphocytes and histiocytes gives **hepatosplenomegaly, transaminitis, cholestasis and coagulopathy**; CNS infiltration causes seizures and encephalopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs follow the mechanism**: **dexamethasone** damps cytokine transcription and crosses into CSF; **etoposide** selectively deletes activated T cells; **anakinra** blocks IL-1; **emapalumab** neutralises IFN-gamma; **ruxolitinib** blocks JAK1/2 signalling of multiple cytokines. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**Classification**: **primary (familial or syndromic genetic)** vs **secondary (acquired)** - infection-associated, malignancy-associated, rheumatic (MAS) and iatrogenic (CAR-T, checkpoint inhibitors); adults may carry hypomorphic gene variants that blur the line. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**HLH-2004 criteria**: a molecular diagnosis, **or 5 of 8**: fever 38.5 C or more; splenomegaly; cytopenia in 2 or more lineages (Hb under 9 g/dL, platelets under 100,000/uL, neutrophils under 1000/uL); triglycerides 265 mg/dL or more and/or fibrinogen 150 mg/dL or less; haemophagocytosis in marrow, spleen, node or liver; low or absent NK-cell activity; **ferritin 500 ng/mL or more**; **soluble CD25 2400 U/mL or more**. [HLH-2004]`,`HLH-2004 was **derived in children**; the ferritin threshold of 500 ng/mL is **non-specific in adults**, and NK activity and sCD25 are rarely available in district hospitals. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**HScore** (9 weighted variables: known immunosuppression, temperature, organomegaly, number of cytopenias, ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, AST, marrow haemophagocytosis); a score of **169 or more** gives about **93% sensitivity and 86% specificity** for reactive HLH in adults. [Fardet 2014]`,`HScore weights the highest bands heavily: **ferritin over 6000 ng/mL scores 50**, triglycerides over 4 mmol/L score 64, temperature over 39.4 C scores 49 and three-lineage cytopenia scores 34. [Fardet 2014]`,`**MAS in systemic JIA (2016 EULAR/ACR/PRINTO)**: a febrile patient with known or suspected sJIA and **ferritin over 684 ng/mL** plus any 2 of: platelets 181,000/uL or less, AST over 48 U/L, triglycerides over 156 mg/dL, fibrinogen 360 mg/dL or less. [EULAR/ACR 2016]`,`**Optimised HLH inflammatory (OHI) index** - **sCD25 over 3900 U/mL with ferritin over 1000 ng/mL** - identifies adults with HLH and high mortality risk, validated chiefly in malignancy-associated HLH. [Blood 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Persistent high fever** that does not respond to antibiotics, or that **returns or worsens after the expected recovery** of an infection (for example after day 7-10 of dengue), is the cardinal symptom. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Malaise, anorexia, weight loss and night sweats** suggest an underlying lymphoma or TB as the trigger. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding** (petechiae, gum bleeding, GI bleeding) reflects thrombocytopenia plus hypofibrinogenaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological symptoms** - confusion, seizures, ataxia, irritability in children - occur in up to a third and signal CNS HLH. [HLH-2004]`,`**Jaundice, abdominal distension and oedema** reflect hepatitis, capillary leak and hypoalbuminaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clues to the trigger**: a recent dengue or EBV-like illness, TB contact or cough, residence in a kala-azar district (Bihar, Jharkhand), grass exposure (scrub), arthritis and evanescent rash (Still disease), recent CAR-T or checkpoint inhibitor. [API 2022]`,`In **infants**, consanguinity, a sibling death from 'sepsis' and partial albinism (Griscelli, Chediak-Higashi) point to familial HLH. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Splenomegaly** in about 70-90% and hepatomegaly in most; massive splenomegaly suggests leishmaniasis or lymphoma as the trigger. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Lymphadenopathy** points to EBV, TB, HIV or lymphoma; biopsy the most accessible abnormal node. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: non-specific maculopapular rash, petechiae and purpura, an **eschar** (scrub typhus) or a salmon-pink evanescent rash (Still disease). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice, ascites, pleural effusion and oedema** reflect liver involvement and capillary leak. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological signs**: altered sensorium, meningism, cranial nerve palsy or seizures - indicate CSF examination once platelets and fibrinogen allow. [HLH-2004]`,`**Haemodynamic instability** - tachycardia, hypotension and hypoxia - mimics septic shock and often needs ICU care. [Crit Care Med 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Sepsis with DIC**: both cause fever, cytopenias and coagulopathy; sepsis rarely gives ferritin over 10,000 ng/mL, splenomegaly and high triglycerides together, and responds to source control. [Crit Care Med 2022]`,`**Severe dengue**: platelets fall at day 3-7 and recover by day 8-10 with leak; **HLH is suspected when fever persists beyond day 7** with worsening cytopenias, rising ferritin and organomegaly. [WHO 2025]`,`**Acute liver failure**: ferritin and transaminases are both very high; HLH has splenomegaly, cytopenias and high triglycerides. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TTP and DIC**: TTP has schistocytes and normal fibrinogen; DIC lacks splenomegaly and extreme ferritin - see the fever with thrombocytopenia topic. [ISTH 2020]`,`**Lymphoma, leukaemia and kala-azar** may present exactly like HLH and may themselves be the trigger - marrow, node biopsy, PET-CT and rK39 are needed. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**CAR-T cytokine release syndrome** occurs within days of infusion with fever and hypotension; **IEC-HS** (immune effector cell-associated HLH-like syndrome) is the later, HLH-pattern variant. [ASTCT 2023]`,`**Ferritin over 10,000 ng/mL** is not unique to HLH - it also occurs in liver failure, renal failure, haematological malignancy, transfusional iron overload and disseminated infection in HIV. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line`,points:[`**CBC with smear**: bicytopenia or pancytopenia, atypical lymphocytes (EBV), blasts, parasites (malaria) and absence of schistocytes. [HLH-2004]`,`**Serum ferritin** - the best screening test: **over 500 ng/mL** meets HLH-2004, over 3000 should prompt full evaluation, and **over 10,000 ng/mL** is highly suggestive (sensitivity about 90%, specificity about 96% in children). [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Fasting triglycerides** (265 mg/dL or 3 mmol/L or more) and **fibrinogen** (150 mg/dL or 1.5 g/L or less), with PT, aPTT and D-dimer. [HLH-2004]`,`**Liver function** (AST, ALT, bilirubin, albumin), **LDH** (usually very high), creatinine, electrolytes and sodium (hyponatraemia from SIADH). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ESR is often low or falling** despite a very high CRP, because fibrinogen is consumed - a useful bedside clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ultrasound abdomen** for hepatosplenomegaly, nodes and ascites; **chest X-ray** for TB and effusions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calculate the HScore** at the bedside from these first-line results; a score of 169 or more, or rising ferritin with falling counts, means urgent referral. [Fardet 2014]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and trigger work-up`,points:[`**Soluble CD25 (sIL-2R)** 2400 U/mL or more, **NK-cell activity**, **CD107a degranulation**, and flow cytometry for **perforin, SAP and XIAP** expression - available at Indian tertiary immunology laboratories. [HLH-2004]`,`**Bone-marrow aspirate and biopsy**: haemophagocytosis, lymphoma infiltration, granulomas (TB), **Leishman-Donovan bodies**, and culture for TB and fungi; haemophagocytosis is **neither necessary nor sufficient** for diagnosis. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Infection work-up**: quantitative **EBV DNA PCR** in blood, CMV PCR, **HIV**, dengue NS1/IgM, scrub IgM or PCR, **rK39** for kala-azar, blood and marrow cultures, CBNAAT on samples, and histoplasma antigen where relevant. [API 2022]`,`**Malignancy work-up**: **PET-CT** to find a biopsy target, excision node biopsy, peripheral flow cytometry, and EBV in situ hybridisation (EBER) on tissue. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Autoimmune screen**: ANA, anti-dsDNA, complement and the clinical criteria for Still disease or sJIA. [EULAR/ACR 2023]`,`**CSF** (cells, protein, cytology) and **MRI brain** when there are neurological signs or in all children, once coagulopathy is corrected. [HLH-2004]`,`**Genetic testing** (familial HLH panel) in children, in adults with recurrent HLH or no trigger, and in all candidates for stem-cell transplant. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`HLH is **not an OPD disease**; the family physician's job is **early suspicion** - persistent fever with cytopenias, organomegaly and a ferritin over 3000 ng/mL - and **same-day referral** to a centre with haematology and ICU. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`Before referral, send **CBC, ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, LFT, LDH and the trigger screen** (dengue, malaria, scrub, EBV, HIV, rK39) so the next centre starts without delay. [API 2022]`,`**Treat a likely infectious trigger** while arranging transfer: doxycycline for suspected scrub typhus, ACT or artesunate for malaria, and do not withhold ATT in proven TB. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Do not start empirical steroids** before marrow and node biopsy if lymphoma is possible, unless the patient is deteriorating - steroids can mask lymphoma histology. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Avoid NSAIDs and IM injections**; transfuse only to safe thresholds for transfer. [WHO 2025]`,`**After discharge**, the family physician monitors CBC, ferritin and LFT, steroid adverse effects, glucose, BP, infection and adherence to co-trimoxazole and antifungal prophylaxis. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - specialist treatment`,points:[`**Principles**: support organs, **treat the trigger**, and **suppress hyperinflammation** - the intensity matched to severity and to the trigger. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Mild, infection-triggered HLH** (dengue, scrub typhus, leishmaniasis) often settles with **specific anti-infective therapy plus corticosteroids** (dexamethasone, or methylprednisolone pulses) with or without **IVIG 1-1.6 g/kg over 2-3 days**. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**HLH-94 backbone**: **dexamethasone 10 mg/m2/day for 2 weeks, 5 mg/m2 for 2 weeks, 2.5 mg/m2 for 2 weeks, 1.25 mg/m2 for 1 week, then taper over week 8**, with **etoposide 150 mg/m2 IV twice weekly for 2 weeks, then weekly**. [HLH-94]`,`**Adult modification**: etoposide **50-100 mg/m2 once weekly**, reduced further in renal or hepatic impairment; HLH-2004 added upfront ciclosporin but showed no survival benefit over HLH-94. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**EBV-HLH**: **early etoposide (within 4 weeks)** improves survival; add **rituximab 375 mg/m2 weekly** when EBV is in B cells; persistent EBV viraemia warrants stem-cell transplant. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**MAS (Still disease, sJIA)**: high-dose methylprednisolone plus **anakinra** (IL-1 blockade; doses up to 2-10 mg/kg/day IV or SC in severe cases) and ciclosporin; etoposide is reserved for refractory disease. [EULAR/ACR 2023]`,`**Refractory HLH**: **emapalumab** (anti-IFN-gamma), **ruxolitinib**, alemtuzumab or the DEP regimen (liposomal doxorubicin, etoposide, methylprednisolone) as a bridge to **allogeneic stem-cell transplant**. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**CNS HLH**: intrathecal **methotrexate with hydrocortisone** in addition to systemic therapy. [HLH-2004]`,`**Supportive care**: blood products (platelets over 20,000-50,000 if bleeding, cryoprecipitate for fibrinogen under 1.5 g/L), **co-trimoxazole PJP prophylaxis**, antifungal and antiviral prophylaxis, PPI, glucose control and early ICU transfer. [Crit Care Med 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Dengue-associated HLH**: suspect when fever lasts beyond 7 days or recurs, with worsening cytopenias and ferritin rising (often over 10,000); most respond to **steroids with or without IVIG**, without etoposide. [WHO 2025]`,`**TB-associated HLH**: start **ATT promptly** (with monitoring of drug-induced liver injury); rifampicin speeds dexamethasone metabolism, so steroid doses may need increasing. [NTEP 2025]`,`**Kala-azar HLH**: treat with **liposomal amphotericin B**; HLH usually resolves with antileishmanial therapy alone, and etoposide is avoided. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: HLH is rare but carries high maternal and fetal mortality; corticosteroids, IVIG and ciclosporin are preferred, with etoposide reserved for refractory cases after multidisciplinary review. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**HIV**: HLH occurs at diagnosis, with opportunistic infections (TB, histoplasmosis, Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus) or as IRIS after ART; treat the infection and continue ART. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly and renal impairment**: reduce etoposide dose and watch for marrow failure and infection; malignancy is a commoner trigger in older adults. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Children**: a first episode in infancy or recurrent HLH means familial disease until proved otherwise - genetic testing and early transplant referral. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Multi-organ failure** - shock, ARDS, AKI and liver failure - is the commonest cause of early death. [Crit Care Med 2022]`,`**Bleeding** from thrombocytopenia and hypofibrinogenaemia, including intracranial haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opportunistic infections** from neutropenia and steroids: invasive aspergillosis, candidaemia, PJP and CMV reactivation. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**CNS sequelae** - seizures, developmental delay and cognitive deficits - in children with CNS HLH. [HLH-2004]`,`**Relapse** occurs in up to a fifth of secondary HLH, especially with persistent EBV or untreated lymphoma. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Prognosis**: untreated familial HLH is almost uniformly fatal; with HLH-94 and transplant, 5-year survival in children is about **54-60%**; adult mortality is about 40%, highest in **lymphoma-associated HLH**. [HLH-94]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer immediately** any patient with an HScore of 169 or more, HLH-2004 criteria nearly met, or ferritin rising with falling counts and persistent fever. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Response markers**: fever, spleen size, blood counts, **ferritin (a fall of over 50% by 2 weeks is a good response)**, sCD25, fibrinogen and LFT, checked daily to twice weekly in the first weeks. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Rising ferritin or sCD25 during treatment** signals relapse or an untreated trigger such as lymphoma - re-biopsy. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Steroid monitoring**: glucose, BP, mood, gastric protection, bone protection with calcium and vitamin D, and a taper plan. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**After recovery**: CBC and ferritin monthly for 6 months, EBV PCR where relevant, and genetic counselling for familial disease. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Prevent the triggers**: early treatment of dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, TB (NTEP) and kala-azar (NCVBDC) reduces infection-associated HLH. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Screen before biologic or CAR-T therapy** and monitor ferritin after immune-effector therapy to catch IEC-HS early. [ASTCT 2023]`,`**Counsel families** of a child with familial HLH about recessive inheritance (25% risk in each pregnancy) and offer **prenatal diagnosis** once the variant is known. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Counsel adults** that HLH may relapse; any new fever needs same-day CBC and ferritin and contact with the treating centre. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**PM-JAY (Ayushman Bharat)** packages cover inpatient haematology care and bone-marrow transplant at empanelled hospitals, which matters for etoposide-based protocols and transplant. [NHA 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026) and exam angle`,points:[`**2022 EULAR/ACR points to consider for MAS/HLH (published 2023)**: screen early with ferritin and serial labs, treat promptly with high-dose glucocorticoids plus **anakinra, ciclosporin or emapalumab**, and involve a multidisciplinary team. [EULAR/ACR 2023]`,`**Emapalumab** (anti-IFN-gamma), approved for refractory primary HLH since 2018, received **US FDA approval for MAS in Still disease in 2025**; availability in India is limited. [FDA 2025]`,`**ASTCT 2023** defined **IEC-HS** (immune effector cell-associated HLH-like syndrome) as a distinct, later complication of CAR-T therapy, separate from cytokine release syndrome. [ASTCT 2023]`,`**Histiocyte Society adult recommendations (2019)** remain the reference: use HScore and HLH-2004 together, reduce etoposide dose in adults, and search hard for lymphoma. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Viva**: 'Name the HLH-2004 criteria' - fever, spleen, 2-lineage cytopenia, TG or fibrinogen, haemophagocytosis, NK activity, ferritin 500, sCD25 2400; **5 of 8**. [HLH-2004]`,`**Common mistake**: waiting for haemophagocytosis on marrow before treating - it is absent early in many cases and present in some septic patients. [Histiocyte Society 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: treating dengue with persistent fever beyond day 7 as 'resistant dengue' or adding antibiotics - check ferritin and think HLH. [WHO 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`HLH-2004 diagnostic criteria (molecular diagnosis or 5 of 8)`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Threshold`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Fever`,`38.5 C or more`,`Persistent, antibiotic-unresponsive`],[`Splenomegaly`,`Clinical or imaging`,`Massive spleen suggests kala-azar or lymphoma`],[`Cytopenias (2 or more lineages)`,`Hb under 9 g/dL; platelets under 100,000/uL; ANC under 1000/uL`,`Hb under 10 g/dL in infants under 4 weeks`],[`Hypertriglyceridaemia and/or hypofibrinogenaemia`,`Fasting TG 265 mg/dL (3 mmol/L) or more; fibrinogen 150 mg/dL (1.5 g/L) or less`,`TNF inhibits lipoprotein lipase; macrophage plasminogen activator`],[`Haemophagocytosis`,`In marrow, spleen, node or liver`,`Neither necessary nor sufficient`],[`NK-cell activity`,`Low or absent`,`Specialist laboratory`],[`Ferritin`,`500 ng/mL or more`,`Over 10,000 highly suggestive`],[`Soluble CD25 (sIL-2R)`,`2400 U/mL or more`,`Marker of T-cell activation`]]},{heading:`HScore - variables and points (cut-off 169)`,columns:[`Variable`,`Bands`,`Points`],rows:[[`Known immunosuppression`,`No / Yes`,`0 / 18`],[`Temperature`,`Under 38.4 C / 38.4-39.4 C / over 39.4 C`,`0 / 33 / 49`],[`Organomegaly`,`None / liver or spleen / both`,`0 / 23 / 38`],[`Cytopenias`,`1 lineage / 2 lineages / 3 lineages`,`0 / 24 / 34`],[`Ferritin (ng/mL)`,`Under 2000 / 2000-6000 / over 6000`,`0 / 35 / 50`],[`Triglycerides (mmol/L)`,`Under 1.5 / 1.5-4 / over 4`,`0 / 44 / 64`],[`Fibrinogen (g/L)`,`Over 2.5 / 2.5 or less`,`0 / 30`],[`AST (U/L)`,`Under 30 / 30 or more`,`0 / 19`],[`Marrow haemophagocytosis`,`No / Yes`,`0 / 35`]]},{heading:`Drug table - HLH and MAS`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dexamethasone`,`10 mg/m2/day IV or oral`,`2 weeks, then 5, 2.5, 1.25 mg/m2 in 2-week steps; taper by week 8`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection, psychosis, GI bleed; masks lymphoma histology`],[`Etoposide`,`150 mg/m2 IV twice weekly (children); 50-100 mg/m2 weekly (adults)`,`Twice weekly x 2 weeks, then weekly to week 8`,`Myelosuppression, mucositis, secondary AML; reduce in renal/hepatic failure`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`1 g IV daily (or 30 mg/kg in children)`,`3-5 days, then oral taper`,`As for steroids; used in MAS and dengue HLH`],[`IV immunoglobulin`,`1-1.6 g/kg total IV`,`Over 2-3 days`,`Fluid overload, thrombosis, renal injury, aseptic meningitis`],[`Anakinra`,`100 mg SC up to 2-10 mg/kg/day IV or SC in severe MAS`,`Until remission, then taper`,`Injection reaction, neutropenia, infection`],[`Ciclosporin`,`3-5 mg/kg/day orally in 2 doses`,`Trough-guided, months`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension, PRES, hypomagnesaemia`],[`Rituximab (EBV-HLH)`,`375 mg/m2 IV weekly`,`1-4 doses`,`Infusion reaction, HBV reactivation (screen HBsAg)`],[`Emapalumab`,`1 mg/kg IV twice weekly, titrated`,`Until stem-cell transplant or remission`,`Infection (TB, herpes, fungal); screen latent TB`]]},{heading:`HLH vs its mimics`,columns:[`Feature`,`HLH`,`Sepsis with DIC`,`Severe dengue`,`Acute liver failure`],rows:[[`Fever course`,`Persistent, unresponsive`,`Responds to source control`,`Settles by day 5-7`,`Variable`],[`Ferritin`,`Often over 10,000`,`Usually under 3000`,`Raised, falls with recovery`,`Very high`],[`Spleen`,`Enlarged`,`Usually normal`,`Mild or normal`,`Normal`],[`Triglycerides`,`High`,`Normal or mild rise`,`Mild rise`,`Variable`],[`Fibrinogen`,`Low`,`Low`,`Normal or low`,`Low`],[`Blood counts`,`2-3 lineage fall, worsening`,`Thrombocytopenia`,`Platelets recover by day 8-10`,`Platelets mildly low`]]},{heading:`Triggers and trigger-directed treatment (Indian focus)`,columns:[`Trigger`,`Clue`,`Test`,`Trigger treatment`],rows:[[`EBV`,`Young adult, nodes, atypical lymphocytes`,`Quantitative EBV PCR`,`Early etoposide, rituximab`],[`Dengue`,`Fever persisting beyond day 7`,`NS1, IgM`,`Steroids with or without IVIG`],[`Tuberculosis`,`Night sweats, nodes, marrow granuloma`,`CBNAAT, marrow culture, biopsy`,`ATT; higher steroid dose with rifampicin`],[`Visceral leishmaniasis`,`Massive spleen, Bihar/Jharkhand`,`rK39, marrow LD bodies`,`Liposomal amphotericin B`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, grass exposure`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`,`Doxycycline or azithromycin`],[`Lymphoma`,`B symptoms, nodes, high LDH`,`PET-CT, node biopsy`,`Chemotherapy (haematology)`],[`Still disease/sJIA (MAS)`,`Arthritis, evanescent rash`,`Clinical criteria, ferritin`,`Steroids, anakinra, ciclosporin`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever persisting beyond 5-7 days of appropriate therapy with worsening bi- or pancytopenia`,`Ferritin over 3000 ng/mL and rising, or over 10,000 ng/mL`,`Splenomegaly with cytopenias and low fibrinogen (under 1.5 g/L)`,`HScore of 169 or more`,`Seizures, encephalopathy or focal signs in a patient with cytopenias and high ferritin`,`Shock, hypoxia or rising creatinine with persistent fever (multi-organ failure)`,`Dengue with fever beyond day 7 and falling counts`],pearls:[`**HLH-2004 - 5 of 8**: 'FEVER, SPLEEN, CYTO, TG/FIB, HAEMO, NK, FERRITIN, CD25'.`,`**HScore 169 or more** = probable reactive HLH (93% sensitive, 86% specific).`,`**High CRP, low ESR** - fibrinogen consumption; think HLH.`,`**Ferritin over 10,000 ng/mL** - HLH until proved otherwise in a febrile patient with cytopenias.`,`**Haemophagocytosis is neither necessary nor sufficient** - do not wait for the marrow.`,`**Dengue fever beyond day 7** is not resistant dengue - check ferritin.`,`**Adults: find the lymphoma** - biopsy before steroids if the patient allows.`,`**EBV-HLH: etoposide within 4 weeks** saves lives.`,`**Kala-azar HLH** responds to liposomal amphotericin B alone.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old man with confirmed dengue is still febrile on day 10. He has hepatosplenomegaly, haemoglobin 8.2 g/dL, platelets 32,000/uL, neutrophils 800/uL, ferritin 18,500 ng/mL, triglycerides 320 mg/dL and fibrinogen 1.1 g/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Dengue shock syndrome`,`Secondary bacterial sepsis`,`Dengue-associated haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis`,`Immune thrombocytopenic purpura`],answer:2,explanation:`Persistent fever beyond day 7 of dengue with splenomegaly, three-lineage cytopenia, ferritin over 10,000, triglycerides over 265 mg/dL and fibrinogen under 1.5 g/L meets at least five HLH-2004 criteria. Dengue shock occurs at defervescence with leak, not late fever; sepsis rarely gives this ferritin with high triglycerides; ITP affects only platelets.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-q2`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT one of the eight HLH-2004 diagnostic criteria?`,options:[`Ferritin of 500 ng/mL or more`,`Soluble CD25 of 2400 U/mL or more`,`Low or absent NK-cell activity`,`Raised aspartate aminotransferase`],answer:3,explanation:`AST is a variable in the HScore, not in HLH-2004. The HLH-2004 eight are fever, splenomegaly, two-lineage cytopenia, high triglycerides or low fibrinogen, haemophagocytosis, low NK activity, ferritin 500 or more and sCD25 2400 or more - so the ferritin, sCD25 and NK-activity choices are all genuine criteria.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-q3`,stem:`In HLH, which mechanism best explains the hypertriglyceridaemia?`,options:[`Inhibition of lipoprotein lipase by TNF-alpha`,`Increased hepatic VLDL synthesis from steroid therapy`,`Haemophagocytosis releasing red-cell membrane lipids`,`Biliary obstruction from hepatosplenomegaly`],answer:0,explanation:`Cytokines, chiefly TNF-alpha, suppress lipoprotein lipase, so triglyceride-rich lipoproteins are not cleared. Raised TG is present before any steroid is given, so steroid-driven VLDL is not the explanation; haemophagocytosis and cholestasis do not raise triglycerides to the levels seen.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-q4`,stem:`A 19-year-old with EBV infection develops HLH and fails to improve after 5 days of dexamethasone. What is the most appropriate next step according to Histiocyte Society recommendations?`,options:[`Add oral acyclovir and continue dexamethasone alone`,`Add etoposide early, with rituximab if EBV is in B cells`,`Stop steroids and give IVIG only`,`Proceed directly to splenectomy`],answer:1,explanation:`In EBV-HLH, starting etoposide early (within about 4 weeks of diagnosis) improves survival, and rituximab clears EBV-infected B cells. Acyclovir has no useful effect on latent EBV; IVIG alone is too weak for steroid-refractory EBV-HLH; splenectomy is not a standard treatment.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-q5`,stem:`A 45-year-old from Bihar has 2 months of fever, massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia, ferritin 9000 ng/mL and low fibrinogen. Marrow aspirate shows haemophagocytosis and Leishman-Donovan bodies. What is the best treatment?`,options:[`HLH-94 protocol with etoposide and dexamethasone`,`Rituximab and ciclosporin`,`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`Anakinra`],answer:2,explanation:`Visceral leishmaniasis-triggered HLH usually resolves with antileishmanial therapy alone, and liposomal amphotericin B is the NCVBDC drug of choice. Etoposide would add marrow toxicity without treating the parasite; rituximab, ciclosporin and anakinra are for EBV-HLH or MAS, not for parasitic triggers.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis. Describe its pathogenesis, diagnostic criteria, infectious triggers in India and management.`,openingLines:[`Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis is a life-threatening hyperinflammatory syndrome of uncontrolled activation of cytotoxic lymphocytes and macrophages, either familial or secondary to infection, malignancy or autoimmunity.`,`It presents as persistent fever, splenomegaly, cytopenias, very high ferritin, high triglycerides and low fibrinogen, and is diagnosed by the HLH-2004 criteria or the HScore.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`Primary (familial: PRF1, UNC13D, STX11, STXBP2; syndromic: Griscelli, Chediak-Higashi, XLP).`,`Secondary: infection, malignancy, rheumatic (MAS), iatrogenic (CAR-T, checkpoint inhibitors).`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Failed perforin-granule killing; persistent antigen; IFN-gamma from CD8 T cells; macrophage activation; IL-1, IL-6, IL-18, TNF storm.`,`Ferritin from macrophages, sCD25 from T cells, TG up (lipoprotein lipase inhibited), fibrinogen down, cytopenias from cytokines and haemophagocytosis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`HLH-2004: molecular diagnosis or 5 of 8 criteria (list with thresholds).`,`HScore 169 or more in adults; MAS criteria in sJIA (ferritin over 684 plus 2 of 4).`]},{heading:`Infectious triggers in India`,points:[`EBV, dengue, tuberculosis, scrub typhus, visceral leishmaniasis, enteric fever, malaria, HIV, CMV.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, ferritin, TG, fibrinogen, LFT, LDH; sCD25, NK activity, marrow; trigger screen (EBV PCR, NS1, rK39, scrub IgM, CBNAAT, HIV); PET-CT and biopsy for lymphoma.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Support organs, treat trigger, suppress inflammation.`,`HLH-94: dexamethasone 10 mg/m2 tapering over 8 weeks plus etoposide 150 mg/m2 (adults 50-100 mg/m2 weekly).`,`Infection-triggered: anti-infective plus steroids with or without IVIG; EBV: early etoposide and rituximab; MAS: anakinra, ciclosporin.`,`Refractory: emapalumab, ruxolitinib; HSCT for familial, relapsed and refractory disease.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Adult mortality about 40%, worst with lymphoma; dengue and kala-azar HLH do better.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pathogenesis cycle: failed cytotoxicity - persistent antigen - IFN-gamma - macrophage activation - cytokine storm - organ damage`,`Table: HLH-2004 criteria with thresholds`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:1},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Indian triggers and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`HLH`,`HLH-2004`,`HScore`,`ferritin`,`etoposide`,`dexamethasone`,`EBV`,`dengue`,`tuberculosis`,`kala-azar`]},{id:`infectious-fever-hlh-macrophage-activation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the HScore and macrophage activation syndrome.`,openingLines:[`The HScore is a weighted 9-variable score that estimates the probability of reactive HLH in adults; 169 or more gives about 93% sensitivity and 86% specificity.`,`Macrophage activation syndrome is HLH arising in rheumatic disease, classically systemic JIA and adult-onset Still disease.`],answer:[{heading:`HScore`,points:[`Variables: immunosuppression, temperature, organomegaly, cytopenias, ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, AST, marrow haemophagocytosis.`,`Highest weights: TG over 4 mmol/L (64), ferritin over 6000 (50), temperature over 39.4 C (49).`,`Cut-off 169; probability over 99% at 250 or more.`]},{heading:`Macrophage activation syndrome`,points:[`Triggers: flare of sJIA, Still disease, SLE, infection, drug change.`,`2016 criteria in sJIA: ferritin over 684 ng/mL plus 2 of platelets 181,000 or less, AST over 48, TG over 156 mg/dL, fibrinogen 360 mg/dL or less.`,`Treatment: methylprednisolone pulses, anakinra, ciclosporin; emapalumab (FDA 2025) in refractory Still MAS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of HScore variables with points`],markSplit:[{part:`HScore variables and cut-off`,marks:2.5},{part:`MAS definition, criteria and treatment`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`HScore`,`MAS`,`Still disease`,`sJIA`,`anakinra`,`ferritin 684`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Histiocytic disorders and HLH`,`Henter JI et al. HLH-2004: Diagnostic and therapeutic guidelines for hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis. Pediatr Blood Cancer 2007`,`La Rosee P et al. Recommendations for the management of hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis in adults (Histiocyte Society). Blood 2019`,`Fardet L et al. Development and validation of the HScore. Arthritis Rheumatol 2014`,`Shakoory B et al. 2022 EULAR/ACR points to consider for the diagnosis and management of MAS/HLH. Ann Rheum Dis 2023`,`Hines MR et al. Consensus-based guidelines for the recognition, diagnosis and management of HLH in critically ill children and adults. Crit Care Med 2022`,`Hines MR et al. Immune effector cell-associated HLH-like syndrome (IEC-HS): ASTCT consensus. Transplant Cell Ther 2023`,`WHO Guidelines for the clinical management of arboviral diseases, 2025`]}),d.push({id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection`,title:`National communicable disease programmes: NCVBDC, NTEP, NACP, NVHCP, IDSP`,oneLiner:`India's communicable disease programmes run under the **National Health Mission** - **NCVBDC** (malaria, dengue, chikungunya, JE/AES, lymphatic filariasis, kala-azar), **NTEP** (TB, with **Ni-kshay** notification and **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana**), **NACP phase V** (HIV/STI, **95-95-95**), **NVHCP** (free hepatitis B and C care, **HCV elimination by 2030**) and **IDSP/IHIP** (weekly S-P-L surveillance and outbreak response) - and every family physician must know their **case definitions, notification rules, free drugs and incentives**, and the **elimination targets: malaria 2030, lymphatic filariasis 2027, kala-azar sustained, TB ahead of the SDG 2030**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`NCVBDC`,`NVBDCP`,`NTEP`,`RNTCP`,`Ni-kshay`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana`,`Ni-kshay Mitra`,`TB Mukt Bharat`,`NACP phase V`,`NACO`,`NVHCP`,`IDSP`,`IHIP`,`S P L forms`,`malaria elimination`,`kala-azar elimination`,`lymphatic filariasis MDA`,`annual parasite incidence`,`95-95-95`,`notifiable diseases`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, framework and why they matter`,points:[`A **national health programme** is a centrally sponsored, time-bound scheme with defined targets, free diagnostics and drugs, case definitions, reporting formats and incentives, implemented by states through the **National Health Mission (NHM)**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`The communicable disease programmes sit under the **NHM 'Communicable Diseases' pool**: **NCVBDC, NTEP, NLEP, IDSP, NVHCP and the National Rabies Control Programme**, while **NACP** is run by **NACO** with its own budget line. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Control** reduces incidence to a locally acceptable level; **elimination** reduces incidence to zero (or below a set threshold) in a defined area with continued measures; **eradication** is permanent global reduction to zero - achieved only for smallpox. [Park 27e]`,`Primary care delivery runs through **Ayushman Arogya Mandir (formerly Health and Wellness Centres)**, where Community Health Officers, ANMs and ASHAs screen, test (RDT, sputum collection, HIV and HBV screening) and supervise treatment. [MoHFW 2024]`,`The **Global Burden of Disease** and programme data show infections still account for a large share of Indian DALYs; TB alone is India's leading infectious killer and India carries about a quarter of the world's TB. [WHO Global TB Report 2025]`,`**Free services under all programmes** - tests, drugs and follow-up in public facilities - are the basis for referral from private practice; most programmes also reimburse **private providers** for notification and outcomes. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Epidemiology and current burden`,points:[`**TB**: India's estimated incidence fell from **237 per lakh in 2015 to 187 per lakh in 2024 (about 21%)**, and TB deaths fell by about a quarter, per the WHO Global TB Report 2025. [WHO Global TB Report 2025]`,`**TB notification** reached about **25-26 lakh cases a year in 2023-2024**, the highest ever, with about a third from the private sector. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Malaria**: cases and deaths fell by about **80% between 2015 and 2023**, and India **exited the WHO High Burden High Impact group in 2024**; residual transmission is concentrated in tribal and forest districts of Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Meghalaya and Mizoram. [WHO World Malaria Report 2024]`,`**HIV**: adult prevalence is about **0.2%**, with about **25 lakh people living with HIV** and about 66,000 new infections a year (India HIV Estimates 2023); prevalence is highest in the North-East (Mizoram, Nagaland, Manipur). [NACO 2024]`,`**Hepatitis**: an estimated **4 crore people live with chronic hepatitis B and 60 lakh to 1.2 crore with hepatitis C** in India; hepatitis A and E remain the commonest causes of acute viral hepatitis. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Kala-azar**: India reached the elimination target of **under 1 case per 10,000 population in every block in 2023** and sustained it in 2024; cases are now confined to a few districts of Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and Uttar Pradesh. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Lymphatic filariasis**: about **340 districts** were endemic; mass drug administration is ongoing in the remaining high-burden districts of Bihar, Jharkhand, Uttar Pradesh, Odisha and others. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Structure and governance`,points:[`**NCDC (National Centre for Disease Control)** in Delhi houses the **Central Surveillance Unit of IDSP**, the National Viral Hepatitis programme cell and the National Rabies Control Programme. [NCDC 2024]`,`**NCVBDC** (National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control, formerly the Directorate of NVBDCP) runs six diseases: **malaria, dengue, chikungunya, Japanese encephalitis/AES, lymphatic filariasis and kala-azar**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**NTEP** (renamed from RNTCP in **2020**) is run by the **Central TB Division**, with state TB cells, district TB centres, TB units and designated microscopy centres, and the **Ni-kshay** digital case-based platform. [NTEP 2024]`,`**NACO** runs **NACP phase V (2021-2026)** through State AIDS Control Societies, **ICTCs** (testing), **ART centres**, link ART centres, targeted interventions for key populations and **DSRC/STI clinics**. [NACO 2024]`,`**NVHCP** (launched **28 July 2018**, World Hepatitis Day) works through **Model Treatment Centres** in medical colleges and **Treatment Centres** in district hospitals. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**District level**: the District Surveillance Officer (IDSP), District TB Officer, District Malaria Officer and district Rapid Response Team, all under the Chief Medical Officer. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification and indicators examiners ask`,points:[`**Malaria indicators**: **Annual Parasite Incidence (API)** = confirmed cases in a year per 1000 population; **ABER** (annual blood examination rate) = blood smears or RDTs examined per 100 population a year - **target over 10%**; **SPR** = slide positivity rate; **SfR** = slide falciparum rate. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Malaria elimination categories (NFME 2016)**: **Category 0** - no indigenous case for 3 years (prevent re-establishment); **Category 1** - API under 1 in all districts (elimination); **Category 2** - state API under 1 but some districts 1 or more (pre-elimination); **Category 3** - state API 1 or more (intensified control). [NFME 2016]`,`**TB classification under NTEP**: by site (pulmonary, extrapulmonary), by bacteriology (**bacteriologically confirmed** vs **clinically diagnosed**), by history (new vs previously treated) and by drug resistance (drug-sensitive, **H mono-resistant, RR/MDR, pre-XDR, XDR**). [NTEP 2024]`,`**TB treatment outcomes**: cured, treatment completed, treatment failed, died, **lost to follow-up (interruption of 1 month or more)**, not evaluated, and treatment regimen changed; 'successful' = cured plus completed. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Kala-azar elimination** is defined as **under 1 case per 10,000 population per year at block (PHC) level**, sustained for 3 years before WHO validation. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Lymphatic filariasis elimination** is assessed by **Transmission Assessment Surveys (TAS)** in children aged 6-7 years after at least 5 effective MDA rounds with **microfilaria rate under 1%** in sentinel sites. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**HIV cascade (UNAIDS 95-95-95)**: 95% of PLHIV know their status, 95% of those diagnosed are on ART, and 95% of those on ART are virally suppressed (**viral load under 1000 copies/mL**). [NACO 2024]`]},{heading:`Case definitions used in the programmes`,points:[`**Presumptive TB (adult)**: any one of **cough for 2 weeks or more, fever for 2 weeks or more, significant weight loss, haemoptysis or night sweats**, or any abnormality on chest X-ray; in **PLHIV, contacts and other vulnerable groups, cough of any duration** counts. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Microbiologically confirmed TB**: positive smear, **NAAT (CBNAAT or Truenat)**, culture or LPA; **clinically diagnosed TB**: started on treatment by a doctor on clinical, radiological or histological grounds without microbiological confirmation. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Malaria case**: fever (or history of fever) with a **positive RDT or microscopy**; every suspected case must be tested before treatment - presumptive treatment without a test is no longer policy. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Probable dengue**: fever with 2 or more of headache, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, arthralgia, rash, bleeding or leucopenia; **confirmed dengue**: positive **NS1 ELISA, IgM MAC-ELISA**, RT-PCR or virus isolation at a **sentinel surveillance hospital** (RDT positives are 'probable'). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Kala-azar case**: fever for **more than 2 weeks** with splenomegaly in a resident of or visitor to an endemic area, confirmed by a positive **rK39 RDT** or parasites in splenic or marrow aspirate. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Acute Encephalitis Syndrome (AES)**: acute onset of fever with a change in mental status (confusion, disorientation, coma) and/or new-onset seizures (excluding simple febrile seizures) in a person of any age, at any time of year. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**HIV diagnosis** in adults requires **three reactive tests on different antigens or principles (strategy III)** at an ICTC; infants are diagnosed by **DNA PCR** (early infant diagnosis) from 6 weeks. [NACO 2024]`]},{heading:`Notification and reporting - what the clinician must do`,points:[`**TB is a notifiable disease** in India since **May 2012**; private doctors, laboratories and chemists must notify every case on **Ni-kshay**, and failure to notify is punishable under sections 269-270 of the old IPC (now the corresponding sections of the **Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023**). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Chemists** must maintain a record (Schedule H1) of anti-TB drug sales, which feeds private-sector notification. [NTEP 2024]`,`**IDSP forms**: **S (syndromic)** by health workers, **P (presumptive)** by doctors in clinics and hospitals, **L (laboratory-confirmed)** by laboratories - reported **weekly (Monday to Sunday)** on the **IHIP** portal. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Immediate (outbreak) reporting**: any cluster of fever, jaundice, acute diarrhoea, AES, measles-like rash or unusual deaths must be reported at once to the District Surveillance Unit, without waiting for the weekly cycle. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Notifiable diseases are declared by states** under public health acts and the **Epidemic Diseases Act 1897 (amended 2020)**; most states notify malaria, dengue, chikungunya, JE, kala-azar, cholera, hepatitis, leptospirosis and scrub typhus. [IDSP 2024]`,`**HIV results are confidential** under the **HIV and AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017**; reporting is through NACO's facility system, not public notification, and informed consent is mandatory for testing. [NACO 2024]`]},{heading:`NCVBDC - malaria`,points:[`Goals: **National Framework for Malaria Elimination 2016-2030** and the **National Strategic Plan 2023-2027**: zero indigenous cases by 2027 and **malaria-free India by 2030**. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Test every fever**: bivalent **RDT** by ASHA or CHO plus microscopy at PHC; ASHAs receive an incentive for testing and for ensuring complete treatment. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**P. vivax**: **chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days plus primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days** (not in pregnancy, infants or G6PD deficiency). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**P. falciparum**: **ACT-SP (artesunate 4 mg/kg daily for 3 days plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine on day 1)**, but **ACT-AL (artemether-lumefantrine)** in the **North-Eastern states** because of SP resistance; plus **single-dose primaquine 0.75 mg/kg** on day 2. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Severe malaria**: **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then daily**; pre-referral rectal artesunate or IM artemether where IV is impossible. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Vector control**: **long-lasting insecticidal nets (LLIN)**, indoor residual spraying, larvivorous fish (Gambusia, guppy) and source reduction; integrated vector management. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Surveillance**: every case is investigated, classified as **indigenous or imported**, and responded to with focal screening in elimination districts. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`NCVBDC - dengue, chikungunya, JE/AES, lymphatic filariasis and kala-azar`,points:[`**Dengue and chikungunya**: no vaccine in the programme; strategy is **sentinel surveillance hospitals (ELISA-based)**, case management per national guidelines, Aedes source reduction, **'dry day' once a week** and community mobilisation. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**JE**: **live attenuated SA-14-14-2 or inactivated JE vaccine** under UIP in endemic districts - **2 doses at 9-12 months and 16-24 months** - plus adult campaigns in high-burden districts; AES is reported syndromically. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Lymphatic filariasis**: target **elimination by 2027** (ahead of the global 2030 goal) via biannual-to-annual **mass drug administration** - **IDA (ivermectin, DEC and albendazole)** or **DA (DEC and albendazole)** - plus **MMDP** (morbidity management: limb care, hydrocele surgery). [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**MDA doses**: DEC 6 mg/kg (adult 300 mg), albendazole 400 mg, and ivermectin 200 micrograms/kg by height; not given to children under 2, pregnant women or the seriously ill, and drugs are swallowed **in front of the drug administrator**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Kala-azar**: **single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg IV** is first line; alternatives are miltefosine plus paromomycin; **PKDL** is treated with miltefosine for 12 weeks; vector control with indoor residual spray of synthetic pyrethroids. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Kala-azar incentives**: wage-loss compensation for VL and PKDL patients and ASHA incentives for case detection and treatment completion (amounts vary, with additional state support in Bihar). [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`NTEP - TB elimination`,points:[`Goals: **National Strategic Plan 2017-2025** ('Detect - Treat - Prevent - Build') aimed at **ending TB ahead of the SDG 2030 target** - an **80% fall in incidence and 90% fall in mortality** from 2015 levels; **PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan** (2022) and **TB Mukt Panchayat** add community support. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Universal drug susceptibility testing**: every diagnosed patient gets a **NAAT (CBNAAT or Truenat)** for rifampicin resistance, and further LPA or liquid culture for fluoroquinolone and other drugs. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Drug-sensitive TB**: **2HRZE + 4HRE** daily as **weight-band fixed-dose combinations** (25-34, 35-49, 50-64, 65-75, over 75 kg) with a treatment supporter and **99DOTS or MERM** adherence technology. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Drug-resistant TB**: the **6-month BPaLM regimen** (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin) for MDR/RR-TB from 2024, with shorter and longer oral regimens for those ineligible. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana**: direct benefit transfer for nutrition, raised from **Rs 500 to Rs 1000 per month** for the full treatment duration from **November 2024**, with **energy-dense nutritional supplements** for underweight patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Incentives**: **Rs 500 to a private provider for notification and Rs 500 for reporting outcome**, **Rs 500 informant incentive**, and an honorarium for treatment supporters (**Rs 1000 for DS-TB, Rs 5000 for DR-TB**); **Rs 750** transport support for tribal patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Mitra**: individuals, companies and NGOs adopt TB patients and provide monthly food baskets for at least 6 months. [NTEP 2024]`,`**TB preventive treatment (TPT)**: household contacts, PLHIV and other risk groups receive **6H, 3HP (weekly isoniazid-rifapentine for 12 weeks) or 1HP** after ruling out active TB - see the Latent TB topic. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`NACP phase V - HIV and STI`,points:[`**NACP phase V (2021-2026)** aims to reduce new infections and AIDS deaths by **80% from 2010**, reach **95-95-95**, and **eliminate vertical transmission of HIV and syphilis** (EVTHS). [NACO 2024]`,`**Test and Treat** (since 2017): every person diagnosed with HIV starts **ART irrespective of CD4 count**, ideally the same day or within 7 days; first-line is **TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir)** once daily. [NACO 2024]`,`**Viral load monitoring** at 6 months, at 12 months and then annually; suppression is viral load under 1000 copies/mL, and 'undetectable = untransmittable'. [NACO 2024]`,`**Prevention**: **targeted interventions** for female sex workers, MSM, transgender people and people who inject drugs (with **opioid substitution therapy**), condom promotion, **PEP**, and **PrEP** at selected sites. [NACO 2024]`,`**STI/RTI**: syndromic management with **colour-coded kits (kits 1-7)** at Designated STI/RTI Clinics (Suraksha clinics), and syphilis screening of every pregnant woman. [NACO 2024]`,`**Legal protection**: the **HIV and AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017** prohibits discrimination, requires informed consent and confidentiality, and appoints an ombudsman in each state; helpline **1097**. [NACO 2024]`]},{heading:`NVHCP - viral hepatitis`,points:[`**Goals**: **eliminate hepatitis C by 2030**, significantly reduce infected population, morbidity and mortality from **hepatitis B and C** (cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma), and reduce the risk and burden of **hepatitis A and E**. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Free testing and treatment**: HBsAg and anti-HCV screening, **HCV RNA** confirmation, and treatment at Model Treatment Centres and Treatment Centres. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Hepatitis C treatment**: pangenotypic direct-acting antivirals - **sofosbuvir 400 mg plus daclatasvir 60 mg** or **sofosbuvir 400 mg plus velpatasvir 100 mg** daily for **12 weeks** (24 weeks or with ribavirin in decompensated cirrhosis); **SVR12** (undetectable RNA 12 weeks after therapy) defines cure. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Hepatitis B treatment**: **tenofovir disoproxil fumarate 300 mg daily** (or entecavir) for those meeting treatment criteria, usually long-term. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Prevention of perinatal hepatitis B**: HBsAg screening of pregnant women, **hepatitis B birth dose within 24 hours** for all newborns, and **HBIG within 12-24 hours** for babies of HBsAg-positive mothers. [NVHCP 2024]`,`**Hepatitis B vaccination of health-care workers** and high-risk groups, safe injection practice, blood safety and safe water and sanitation for hepatitis A and E. [NVHCP 2024]`]},{heading:`IDSP and IHIP - surveillance and outbreak response`,points:[`**IDSP** began in **2004** as a World Bank-assisted project and continues as the **Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme** under NHM, with surveillance units at central (NCDC), state and district levels. [IDSP 2024]`,`**IHIP (Integrated Health Information Platform)**, rolled out nationwide in **2021**, replaced paper and aggregate reporting with **near real-time, case-based, geo-tagged digital reporting** of the S, P and L forms. [IDSP 2024]`,`About **33 or more diseases and syndromes** are under weekly surveillance, including AFI, acute diarrhoea, cholera, typhoid, jaundice, malaria, dengue, chikungunya, AES, measles, diphtheria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus and SARI/ILI. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Early warning signal**: an unusual rise or clustering of cases in time or place, or a single case of a disease of outbreak potential (for example cholera, AES, measles, Nipah), triggers verification within 24-48 hours. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Rapid Response Teams (RRT)** at district and state level - epidemiologist, clinician, microbiologist and entomologist - investigate, collect samples, institute control measures and report; outbreaks are published in the **weekly outbreak report**. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Laboratory network**: district public health laboratories, state referral laboratories, VRDLs (DHR-ICMR) and **Integrated Public Health Laboratories** being built under **PM-ABHIM**. [PM-ABHIM 2024]`]},{heading:`What the family physician does - OPD and IPD`,points:[`**Screen** every OPD patient with fever, cough over 2 weeks, weight loss, jaundice or genital symptoms against the programme case definitions, and **test with free programme diagnostics** (RDT, NAAT, HIV and HBV/HCV screens). [NTEP 2024]`,`**Notify** TB on Ni-kshay and outbreak-prone fevers on IHIP the same day, and **link** HIV-positive patients to ART centres and HBV/HCV patients to NVHCP treatment centres. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Use programme regimens**: weight-band FDCs for TB, ACT-SP (or ACT-AL in the North-East) for falciparum, chloroquine plus 14-day primaquine for vivax, TLD for HIV - private prescriptions that differ fuel resistance. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**In IPD**: admitted severe malaria, dengue, AES and DR-TB patients are reported, and **contact tracing and TPT** are started for household contacts of pulmonary TB. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Counsel and enrol** for benefits: open a bank account linked to Ni-kshay for Poshan Yojana, register for **PM-JAY** for inpatient care, and connect patients with Ni-kshay Mitras. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Teach and supervise** ASHAs and CHOs at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir on RDT use, sputum collection and adherence support. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: TB treated with first-line drugs (streptomycin avoided); falciparum malaria in the **first trimester** is treated with artemether-lumefantrine per WHO (2022 onwards), whereas older NCVBDC policy used quinine - follow the current state drug policy; primaquine is withheld; HIV-positive women continue TLD; syphilis and HBsAg screening are universal. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Children**: paediatric TB FDCs in dispersible form by weight band; malaria doses by age and weight; MDA not given under 2 years. [NTEP 2024]`,`**PLHIV**: screened at every visit for TB with the **4-symptom screen**; those negative get TPT; TB-HIV patients get co-trimoxazole prophylaxis and ART within 2 weeks of ATT (later in TB meningitis). [NACO 2024]`,`**Diabetes and TB**: bidirectional screening - every TB patient is tested for diabetes and HIV, and diabetics are screened for TB symptoms. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Migrant and tribal populations**: special incentives, mobile teams and cross-border coordination, as most residual malaria and TB loss to follow-up occurs here. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Private-sector patients**: free drugs are available for privately diagnosed TB, and **Patient Provider Support Agencies (PPSA)** link private doctors with NTEP. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline and programme updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027** targets zero indigenous cases by 2027; India **exited the WHO High Burden High Impact group in 2024**. [WHO World Malaria Report 2024]`,`**NTEP 2024**: **BPaLM 6-month regimen** for MDR/RR-TB; **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana raised to Rs 1000 per month** and energy-dense supplements added (November 2024). [NTEP 2024]`,`**100-day intensified TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan** (launched 7 December 2024, later extended nationwide) used **handheld chest X-ray and NAAT** to screen vulnerable groups - elderly, diabetics, smokers, malnourished, PLHIV and past TB patients. [NTEP 2025]`,`**WHO Global TB Report 2025**: India's incidence fell about 21% since 2015 - nearly double the global decline - but remains short of the 80% End TB target. [WHO Global TB Report 2025]`,`**Kala-azar**: the block-level elimination threshold was met in **2023 and sustained in 2024**; India now works toward WHO validation. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Lymphatic filariasis**: nationwide biannual MDA campaigns with IDA in 2024-2025 to meet the **2027 elimination target**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**IHIP** is now the single digital platform for IDSP in all states, enabling case-based, geo-tagged outbreak detection. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic short note**: 'Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana' - DBT of **Rs 1000 per month** (since November 2024) for nutrition throughout treatment. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Classic short note**: 'IDSP' - launched 2004, S-P-L forms, weekly reporting on IHIP, RRT and early warning signals. [IDSP 2024]`,`**Viva**: 'What is API and ABER?' - API is confirmed cases per 1000 population per year; ABER is tests per 100 population per year, target over 10%. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Viva**: 'Which ACT is used in the North-East and why?' - **artemether-lumefantrine**, because of sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine resistance. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Viva**: 'What is the kala-azar elimination target?' - under 1 case per 10,000 population at block level. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Common mistake**: quoting the old RNTCP regimen (thrice-weekly, category I/II) - NTEP uses **daily FDCs** and has abolished the category II retreatment regimen. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Common mistake**: saying TB notification is optional for private doctors - it is **mandatory since 2012**. [NTEP 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Programmes at a glance`,columns:[`Programme`,`Diseases`,`Key goal/target`,`Core strategy`,`Digital platform / incentive`],rows:[[`NCVBDC`,`Malaria, dengue, chikungunya, JE/AES, LF, kala-azar`,`Malaria-free by 2030; LF elimination 2027; KA under 1/10,000 per block`,`Test every fever, ACT, LLIN, IRS, MDA, sentinel hospitals`,`ASHA incentives; wage-loss compensation for KA/PKDL`],[`NTEP`,`Tuberculosis`,`80% fall in incidence, 90% in mortality from 2015`,`NAAT for all, daily FDC, BPaLM, TPT, nutrition`,`Ni-kshay; Poshan Yojana Rs 1000/month; notification Rs 500 + 500`],[`NACP V`,`HIV, STI`,`95-95-95; 80% fall in new infections and deaths from 2010; EVTHS`,`Test and treat, TLD, viral load, targeted interventions, PrEP`,`SOCH/PALS information systems; helpline 1097`],[`NVHCP`,`Hepatitis A, B, C, E`,`HCV elimination by 2030`,`Free screening, DAA for HCV, tenofovir for HBV, birth dose`,`NVHCP MIS`],[`IDSP`,`33+ epidemic-prone diseases and syndromes`,`Early detection and response to outbreaks`,`S-P-L weekly reporting, RRT`,`IHIP`]]},{heading:`Programme drug regimens`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`HRZE FDC (DS-TB)`,`Weight-band daily FDC (H 5, R 10, Z 25, E 15 mg/kg)`,`2 months HRZE, then 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis, optic neuritis (E), gout (Z), orange urine (R)`],[`BPaLM (MDR/RR-TB)`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid 600 mg, moxifloxacin daily (oral)`,`6 months`,`QT prolongation, neuropathy, myelosuppression (linezolid)`],[`ACT-SP (falciparum)`,`Artesunate 4 mg/kg daily + SP 25/1.25 mg/kg on day 1`,`3 days + primaquine 0.75 mg/kg once`,`Sulfa allergy, SJS; ACT-AL in North-East`],[`Chloroquine + primaquine (vivax)`,`CQ 10, 10, 5 mg/kg; PQ 0.25 mg/kg daily`,`3 days CQ; 14 days PQ`,`PQ haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; not in pregnancy or infants`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`10 mg/kg IV single dose`,`Single infusion`,`Infusion reaction, hypokalaemia, renal injury`],[`IDA (MDA for LF)`,`Ivermectin 200 micrograms/kg + DEC 6 mg/kg + albendazole 400 mg`,`Single dose per round`,`Fever, myalgia from dying microfilariae; not under 2 y or pregnancy`],[`TLD (HIV)`,`Tenofovir 300 + lamivudine 300 + dolutegravir 50 mg once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Weight gain, insomnia; double DTG with rifampicin`],[`Sofosbuvir + daclatasvir/velpatasvir (HCV)`,`400 mg + 60 mg (or velpatasvir 100 mg) daily`,`12 weeks (24 in some cirrhosis)`,`Bradycardia with amiodarone; HBV reactivation`]]},{heading:`Case definitions`,columns:[`Condition`,`Suspected / probable`,`Confirmed`],rows:[[`TB (adult)`,`Cough or fever 2 weeks or more, weight loss, haemoptysis, night sweats, abnormal CXR`,`Smear, NAAT, culture or LPA positive`],[`Malaria`,`Fever in endemic area`,`RDT or microscopy positive`],[`Dengue`,`Fever plus 2 of headache, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, arthralgia, rash, bleeding, leucopenia`,`NS1 ELISA, IgM MAC-ELISA, RT-PCR`],[`Kala-azar`,`Fever over 2 weeks with splenomegaly in endemic area`,`rK39 RDT positive or LD bodies`],[`AES`,`Fever with altered mental status or new seizures`,`Aetiology by JE IgM, CSF PCR`],[`HIV`,`Risk behaviour or indicator illness`,`Three reactive tests (strategy III); DNA PCR in infants`]]},{heading:`Indicators and elimination thresholds`,columns:[`Indicator`,`Definition`,`Target / threshold`],rows:[[`Annual Parasite Incidence (API)`,`Confirmed malaria cases per 1000 population per year`,`Under 1 for elimination categories`],[`ABER`,`Blood smears/RDTs examined per 100 population per year`,`Over 10%`],[`Kala-azar elimination`,`VL cases per 10,000 population per year, block level`,`Under 1, sustained 3 years`],[`LF elimination`,`Mf rate in sentinel sites; TAS in 6-7-year-olds`,`Mf under 1%; TAS passed`],[`TB End TB milestones`,`Incidence and deaths vs 2015`,`80% and 90% reduction`],[`HIV cascade`,`Diagnosed, on ART, suppressed`,`95-95-95; VL under 1000 copies/mL`],[`HCV cure`,`SVR12`,`Undetectable RNA 12 weeks after therapy`]]},{heading:`IDSP reporting formats`,columns:[`Form`,`Filled by`,`Level of diagnosis`,`Frequency`],rows:[[`S (syndromic)`,`ASHA, ANM, MPW`,`Syndromes: fever, cough, diarrhoea, jaundice, AFP, unusual deaths`,`Weekly, plus immediate for clusters`],[`P (presumptive)`,`Doctors in PHC, CHC, hospitals, private clinics`,`Clinical diagnosis by standard case definition`,`Weekly, plus immediate for outbreak-prone`],[`L (laboratory)`,`Laboratories`,`Laboratory-confirmed cases`,`Weekly`]]}],redFlags:[`Cluster of fever, jaundice, diarrhoea, AES or rash in one village or ward - report immediately to the District Surveillance Unit`,`Severe malaria (altered sensorium, jaundice, AKI, shock) - IV artesunate and refer`,`Presumptive TB with haemoptysis, breathlessness or in a child or PLHIV - same-day NAAT and referral`,`Rifampicin resistance on NAAT - refer to the nodal DR-TB centre`,`New HIV diagnosis with cough, fever or CD4 under 200 - screen for TB and opportunistic infection before ART`,`Decompensated cirrhosis in HBV or HCV - refer to Model Treatment Centre`],pearls:[`**NCVBDC six**: 'Mad Dogs Can Jump Fences Kindly' - Malaria, Dengue, Chikungunya, JE, Filariasis, Kala-azar.`,`**TB notifiable since 2012**; notify on Ni-kshay - Rs 500 for notification plus Rs 500 for outcome.`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana = Rs 1000/month** from November 2024.`,`**ACT-AL in the North-East**, ACT-SP elsewhere.`,`**IDSP forms S-P-L**: Syndromic (health worker), Presumptive (doctor), Laboratory.`,`**Kala-azar elimination = under 1 per 10,000 per block**; single-dose L-AmB 10 mg/kg.`,`**LF elimination target 2027**; MDA with IDA - ivermectin, DEC, albendazole.`,`**NVHCP launched 28 July 2018**; HCV elimination by 2030.`,`**NACP V = 2021-2026**; test and treat with TLD.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-q1`,stem:`Under the Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana, what monthly direct benefit transfer is a TB patient entitled to from November 2024?`,options:[`Rs 500`,`Rs 750`,`Rs 2000`,`Rs 1000`],answer:3,explanation:`The nutritional DBT was doubled from Rs 500 to Rs 1000 per month for the entire treatment duration in November 2024. Rs 500 was the earlier amount and is also the private provider notification incentive; Rs 750 is the one-time transport support for tribal patients; Rs 2000 is not a Poshan Yojana amount.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-q2`,stem:`Which artemisinin-based combination is the first-line treatment for uncomplicated falciparum malaria in the North-Eastern states under NCVBDC?`,options:[`Artesunate plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine`,`Artemether-lumefantrine`,`Chloroquine plus primaquine`,`Arterolane-piperaquine`],answer:1,explanation:`Because of sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine resistance in the North-East, NCVBDC uses artemether-lumefantrine (ACT-AL) there, while ACT-SP is used in the rest of India. Chloroquine with primaquine is for vivax malaria; arterolane-piperaquine is marketed privately but is not the programme drug.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-q3`,stem:`A doctor in a private clinic diagnoses a case of typhoid on clinical grounds using the standard case definition. Which IDSP form should be used to report it?`,options:[`S form`,`L form`,`P form`,`Ni-kshay notification form`],answer:2,explanation:`The P (presumptive) form is filled by doctors who make a clinical diagnosis using standard case definitions. The S form is for syndromes reported by health workers, the L form is for laboratory-confirmed cases, and Ni-kshay is the TB notification platform, not the IDSP route for typhoid.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-q4`,stem:`A state has an overall annual parasite incidence below 1 per 1000, but two of its districts have an API of 1 or more. Under the National Framework for Malaria Elimination, this state is in which category?`,options:[`Category 0`,`Category 1`,`Category 3`,`Category 2`],answer:3,explanation:`Category 2 (pre-elimination) is a state API under 1 with some districts at 1 or more. Category 1 requires API under 1 in every district; Category 3 is a state API of 1 or more needing intensified control; Category 0 means no indigenous case for 3 years.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-q5`,stem:`What is the elimination threshold for visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) in India?`,options:[`Under 1 case per 10,000 population per year at block level`,`Under 1 case per 1000 population per year at district level`,`Zero cases for 3 consecutive years in the state`,`Under 1 case per 1,00,000 population nationally`],answer:0,explanation:`Kala-azar elimination as a public health problem is defined as fewer than 1 case per 10,000 population per year at block (PHC) level, which India met in 2023 and sustained in 2024. The district-level 1 per 1000 figure confuses it with malaria API; zero cases for 3 years describes malaria Category 0; a national rate would hide endemic blocks.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) with reference to case definitions, diagnostic algorithm, treatment regimens, patient support schemes and the role of the private sector.`,openingLines:[`The National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP), renamed from RNTCP in 2020, aims to end TB in India ahead of the SDG 2030 target - an 80% fall in incidence and 90% fall in mortality from 2015.`,`Its pillars are Detect, Treat, Prevent and Build, delivered through universal NAAT-based testing, daily FDC regimens, Ni-kshay digital notification and patient support schemes.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden and goals`,points:[`India about a quarter of global TB; incidence 237 (2015) to 187 per lakh (2024).`,`NSP 2017-2025; PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan 2022; TB Mukt Panchayat; 100-day campaign December 2024.`]},{heading:`Case definitions`,points:[`Presumptive TB: cough or fever 2 weeks or more, weight loss, haemoptysis, night sweats, abnormal CXR; any cough in PLHIV and contacts.`,`Microbiologically confirmed vs clinically diagnosed; new vs previously treated; DS vs Hr, RR/MDR, pre-XDR, XDR.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic algorithm`,points:[`Upfront NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat) for all presumptive TB; CXR as screening tool.`,`Rifampicin resistant - LPA and liquid culture for FQ and second-line drugs; universal DST.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`DS-TB: 2HRZE/4HRE daily weight-band FDC; treatment supporter, 99DOTS/MERM.`,`MDR/RR-TB: BPaLM 6 months; shorter and longer oral regimens.`,`TPT: 6H, 3HP or 1HP for contacts and PLHIV.`]},{heading:`Patient support`,points:[`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1000/month (November 2024); energy-dense supplements for underweight.`,`Ni-kshay Mitra food baskets; Rs 750 tribal transport support; treatment supporter honorarium Rs 1000 (DS) / Rs 5000 (DR).`]},{heading:`Private sector`,points:[`Mandatory notification since 2012; Rs 500 notification + Rs 500 outcome incentive; Schedule H1 for chemists.`,`PPSA links private providers; free drugs and NAAT for private patients.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and outcomes`,points:[`Outcomes: cured, completed, failed, died, lost to follow-up, not evaluated; bidirectional TB-diabetes and TB-HIV screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`NTEP diagnostic algorithm: presumptive TB - NAAT - rifampicin sensitive (DS-TB regimen) vs resistant (LPA, culture, BPaLM)`,`Table of patient support incentives`],markSplit:[{part:`Goals and case definitions`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment regimens`,marks:2},{part:`Patient support and incentives`,marks:2},{part:`Private sector engagement`,marks:2}],keywords:[`NTEP`,`Ni-kshay`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana`,`NAAT`,`BPaLM`,`TB Mukt Bharat`,`notification`]},{id:`infectious-fever-national-programmes-infection-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP).`,openingLines:[`IDSP, started in 2004 and now run under NHM by NCDC, is India's decentralised, laboratory-supported surveillance system for epidemic-prone diseases.`,`Its objective is early detection of outbreaks through weekly S, P and L reporting on the IHIP digital platform and rapid response by trained teams.`],answer:[{heading:`Structure`,points:[`Central Surveillance Unit at NCDC; state and district surveillance units; District Surveillance Officer.`,`Laboratory network: district labs, state referral labs, VRDLs, IPHLs under PM-ABHIM.`]},{heading:`Reporting`,points:[`S form (health worker, syndromic), P form (doctor, presumptive), L form (laboratory, confirmed).`,`Weekly Monday-Sunday reporting; immediate reporting of clusters; IHIP since 2021.`]},{heading:`Response`,points:[`Early warning signal - verify within 24-48 hours - Rapid Response Team investigates, samples, controls, reports.`,`Weekly outbreak report; feedback to districts.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: S/P/L forms - IHIP - district unit - trigger - RRT - control and feedback`],markSplit:[{part:`Objectives and structure`,marks:1.5},{part:`Forms and reporting`,marks:2},{part:`Outbreak response`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`IDSP`,`IHIP`,`S form`,`P form`,`L form`,`RRT`,`NCDC`]}],references:[`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`,`NTEP: Training Modules for Programme Managers and Medical Officers, Central TB Division, MoHFW, 2023-2024; India TB Report 2024`,`WHO Global Tuberculosis Report 2025`,`NCVBDC: National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027 and National Drug Policy on Malaria; National Framework for Malaria Elimination 2016-2030`,`WHO World Malaria Report 2024`,`NACO: National AIDS and STD Control Programme Phase V (2021-2026) and India HIV Estimates 2023`,`NVHCP Operational Guidelines and National Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Viral Hepatitis, NCDC, MoHFW`,`IDSP/IHIP Operational Guidelines, National Centre for Disease Control, MoHFW`]}),d.push({id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses`,title:`One Health and zoonoses: rabies elimination, scrub, KFD, AMR in animals`,oneLiner:`**One Health** is an integrated approach that **balances and optimises the health of people, animals and ecosystems together** (Quadripartite FAO-UNEP-WHO-WOAH definition, 2021); it matters because about **60% of human infections and 75% of emerging infections are zoonotic** - in India the flagship targets are **dog-mediated rabies elimination by 2030 (NAPRE)**, control of vector-borne zoonoses such as **scrub typhus and Kyasanur Forest disease**, curbing **antimicrobial use in food animals** (colistin ban 2019, NAP-AMR) and **food safety**, coordinated through the **National One Health Mission** and NCDC's zoonoses programme.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`One Health`,`zoonoses`,`Quadripartite`,`OHHLEP`,`National One Health Mission`,`NOHM`,`NOHP-PCZ`,`NAPRE`,`Zero by 30`,`rabies elimination`,`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`monkey fever`,`scrub typhus`,`chiggers`,`spillover`,`AMR in animals`,`colistin ban`,`FSSAI`,`food safety`,`Nipah`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**One Health** (One Health High-Level Expert Panel, adopted by the Quadripartite in 2021) is 'an integrated, unifying approach that aims to sustainably balance and optimise the health of people, animals and ecosystems', recognising that they are closely linked and interdependent. [OHHLEP 2021]`,`The **Quadripartite** - **FAO, UNEP, WHO and WOAH** (World Organisation for Animal Health, formerly OIE) - runs the **One Health Joint Plan of Action 2022-2026**, with six action tracks including zoonoses, endemic neglected diseases, food safety and AMR. [Quadripartite 2022]`,`A **zoonosis** is an infection naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and humans; about **60% of known human pathogens and about 75% of emerging infectious diseases** are zoonotic, most from wildlife. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India** is a hot-spot for spillover because of high human and livestock density (the world's largest livestock population - about 536 million in the 20th Livestock Census 2019), close human-animal contact, forest fringe agriculture and rapid land-use change. [DAHD 2019]`,`**Rabies**: India accounts for roughly a third of global rabies deaths by older WHO estimates (about 18,000-20,000 a year); a recent ICMR national community survey estimated **about 5,700 human rabies deaths a year**, with several million animal bites annually, mostly by dogs. [ICMR 2025]`,`**Scrub typhus** is now among the commonest causes of acute febrile illness in India, and **Kyasanur Forest disease** has spread from Shivamogga in Karnataka to Kerala, Goa, Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu along the Western Ghats. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`Recurrent **Nipah outbreaks in Kerala (2018, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2024, 2025)**, avian influenza H5N1 in poultry with sporadic human cases, and CCHF in Gujarat and Rajasthan show India's continuing spillover risk. [NCDC 2025]`]},{heading:`Drivers of zoonotic spillover - aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Land-use change and deforestation** bring people and livestock into contact with wildlife reservoirs - for example KFD after forest clearance for cashew and farms, and Nipah from fruit bats roosting near homes. [Quadripartite 2022]`,`**Intensive livestock and poultry farming** amplifies pathogens (avian influenza, swine influenza, Streptococcus suis) and drives antimicrobial use. [FAO 2023]`,`**Climate change** shifts the range and season of vectors - Aedes, ticks and chiggers - and of rodent populations after floods. [WHO 2023]`,`**Urbanisation with poor waste management** sustains free-roaming dogs (rabies), rats (leptospirosis, plague) and synanthropic monkeys. [NAPRE 2021]`,`**Wildlife trade, bushmeat and live-animal markets** provide multi-species mixing; SARS-CoV and mpox spilled over through such contacts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Occupational exposure**: farmers, dairy workers, butchers, veterinarians, forest workers, sanitation workers and laboratory staff carry the highest risk. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Unsafe food and water** - raw milk, undercooked meat, contaminated produce - transmit brucellosis, salmonellosis, taeniasis, toxoplasmosis and hepatitis E. [FSSAI 2024]`]},{heading:`Mechanism - how a pathogen crosses species`,points:[`**Spillover** requires a chain: pathogen in a **reservoir host**, release (saliva, urine, faeces, vector), survival in the environment, human exposure, and successful infection of human cells - each step is a barrier that One Health interventions can break. [Plowright 2017]`,`**Reservoir, amplifier and bridge hosts**: in JE, ardeid birds are reservoirs and **pigs are amplifiers**; in KFD, small mammals maintain the virus while **monkeys amplify** it and signal outbreaks by dying; in Nipah, **Pteropus fruit bats** are reservoirs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Receptor compatibility** decides host range - Nipah uses **ephrin-B2/B3**, conserved across mammals, and avian H5 influenza binds alpha-2,3 sialic acid receptors found in the human lower respiratory tract. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dead-end hosts**: most zoonoses (rabies, JE, KFD, leptospirosis, brucellosis) do not spread person to person; a few (Nipah, plague, some influenza, mpox) acquire **human-to-human transmission**, which creates epidemic potential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rabies virus** travels by retrograde axonal transport from the bite to the CNS, so wound washing and immediate vaccine plus immunoglobulin prevent infection before the virus enters nerves - the logic of PEP. [WHO 2018]`,`**AMR spread across sectors**: antimicrobials in animals select resistant bacteria and **mobile genes (for example mcr-1 for colistin resistance, ESBL and carbapenemase genes)** that reach humans through food, water, manure and direct contact. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification of zoonoses (exam favourite)`,points:[`**By direction of transmission**: **anthropozoonoses** (animal to human - rabies, plague), **zooanthroponoses** (human to animal - human TB in cattle) and **amphixenoses** (both ways - staphylococci, salmonella). [Park 27e]`,`**By life cycle**: **direct zoonoses** (one vertebrate host - rabies, brucellosis, anthrax), **cyclozoonoses** (more than one vertebrate host - hydatid, taeniasis), **metazoonoses** (vertebrate plus invertebrate vector - JE, KFD, plague, scrub typhus) and **saprozoonoses** (vertebrate plus non-animal site such as soil - histoplasmosis, larva migrans). [Park 27e]`,`**By agent**: viral (rabies, JE, KFD, Nipah, H5N1, CCHF), bacterial (brucellosis, leptospirosis, anthrax, plague, bovine TB, salmonellosis), rickettsial (scrub typhus, Indian tick typhus, Q fever), parasitic (hydatid, cysticercosis, toxoplasmosis, leishmaniasis) and fungal (dermatophytes, sporotrichosis). [Park 27e]`,`**WHO rabies exposure categories**: **I** - touching or feeding, licks on intact skin (no PEP); **II** - nibbling of uncovered skin, minor scratches without bleeding (vaccine); **III** - transdermal bites or scratches, licks on broken skin or mucosa, bat contact (vaccine plus RIG) - details in the Animal bite and rabies PEP topic. [WHO 2018]`,`**Priority zoonoses for India** (NCDC list): rabies, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, brucellosis, Japanese encephalitis, KFD, Nipah, avian influenza, CCHF, anthrax, plague and taeniasis/cysticercosis. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical approach - history in the OPD`,points:[`**Ask the One Health questions** in every febrile patient: occupation, contact with dogs, cattle, goats, pigs, poultry, monkeys or rodents, forest or field work, animal bites, and consumption of **raw milk or undercooked meat**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Animal deaths nearby** are a strong clue: dead monkeys in the forest (KFD), dead crows or poultry (avian influenza), sudden livestock deaths (anthrax), dead rats (plague). [NCDC 2024]`,`**Season and place**: scrub typhus after the monsoon in grassy fields; KFD in the dry season (**November to June, peak January to April**) in Western Ghats forests; leptospirosis after floods; Nipah between December and May in Kerala and West Bengal. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Brucellosis clues**: undulant fever, sweats, back pain, sacroiliitis and orchitis in dairy farmers, veterinarians or raw-milk drinkers - see the Brucellosis topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rabies clues**: a dog or other mammal bite weeks to months earlier, paraesthesia at the bite site, hydrophobia and aerophobia, or ascending paralysis (paralytic rabies). [WHO 2018]`,`**Cluster history**: illness in family members, neighbours or animals in the same village suggests a common source and must be reported under IDSP. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination clues`,points:[`**Eschar** (black, painless scab with a red rim) in axilla, groin or waistband - scrub typhus. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Conjunctival suffusion and calf tenderness** - leptospirosis; **conjunctival suffusion with bleeding and lymphadenopathy in a forest worker** - KFD. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Painless black eschar with marked surrounding oedema** on exposed skin of a butcher or farmer - cutaneous anthrax. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydrophobic spasms, aerophobia, hypersalivation and agitation** with lucid intervals - furious rabies. [WHO 2018]`,`**Encephalitis with myoclonus or respiratory involvement** after bat or sick-patient contact - Nipah. [NCDC 2025]`,`**Tender suppurative lymph node (bubo)** with fever after rodent or flea exposure - bubonic plague. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - priority zoonoses in India`,points:[`**Scrub typhus vs leptospirosis vs KFD**: all cause fever, myalgia and thrombocytopenia; eschar points to scrub, flood exposure and AKI to leptospirosis, and forest exposure with monkey deaths, bleeding and a biphasic course to KFD. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**KFD vs dengue**: both give fever, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia and bleeding; KFD has forest exposure, the dry season, a second encephalitic phase and a negative dengue NS1/IgM. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Nipah vs JE vs other AES**: Nipah has bat or patient contact, respiratory symptoms and high mortality; JE affects children in rice-pig areas during the monsoon with basal ganglia changes on MRI. [NCDC 2025]`,`**Brucellosis vs TB vs enteric fever**: prolonged fever with sweats and spondylitis in a dairy worker suggests brucellosis; serology (SAT) and blood culture separate them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rabies vs tetanus vs GBS**: hydrophobia and aerophobia with altered sensorium point to rabies; tetanus has trismus and clear sensorium; GBS has areflexia without hydrophobia. [WHO 2018]`,`**CCHF vs severe dengue**: CCHF follows a tick bite or slaughter of livestock, causes severe haemorrhage early and spreads to health-care workers through blood. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line in a district hospital**: CBC (leucopenia, thrombocytopenia), LFT, RFT, urine, malaria RDT and dengue NS1 to exclude the commoner causes. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Scrub typhus**: IgM ELISA from day 5 and **PCR on eschar or blood**; leptospirosis: IgM ELISA, MAT and PCR. [ICMR 2022]`,`**KFD**: **real-time RT-PCR** on blood in the first week and IgM ELISA later, at **ICMR-NIV Pune**, the Shivamogga field unit or VRDLs; samples go with a case form. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Nipah, avian influenza and CCHF**: samples (throat swab, serum, CSF, urine) collected with full PPE and sent in triple packaging to **NIV Pune** (BSL-3/BSL-4) or designated VRDLs. [NCDC 2025]`,`**Rabies ante-mortem**: RT-PCR on saliva, skin biopsy from the nape (nuchal skin) and CSF; **post-mortem**: direct fluorescent antibody test on brain tissue; no test excludes rabies in an exposed person, so PEP is never delayed for tests. [WHO 2018]`,`**Animal side**: brain testing of the biting animal, monkey carcass testing in KFD areas, poultry testing for H5N1 at **ICAR-NIHSAD Bhopal**, and livestock serology for brucellosis. [DAHD 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Animal bite**: wash the wound with **soap and running water for 15 minutes**, apply povidone-iodine, categorise the exposure, and start rabies vaccine (plus RIG or rabies monoclonal antibody for category III) - see the Animal bite and rabies PEP topic. [NRCP 2024]`,`**Rabies pre-exposure prophylaxis** for veterinarians, dog handlers, laboratory staff and high-risk children: **2 visits on days 0 and 7** (IM 1 dose or ID 0.1 mL at 2 sites). [WHO 2018]`,`**Suspected scrub typhus or leptospirosis**: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days** (azithromycin 500 mg daily for 5 days in pregnancy). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Suspected KFD**: no specific antiviral; give **paracetamol, fluids and close monitoring**, avoid NSAIDs and IM injections, and refer if bleeding or neurological signs appear. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Counsel exposed workers**: protective clothing (long sleeves, trousers tucked into socks), **DEET or DMP repellents**, gloves and boots, and avoiding sitting on grass in scrub areas. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Notify** suspected zoonoses on IHIP and inform the block veterinary officer when animal involvement is suspected - joint investigation is the essence of One Health. [IDSP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Severe KFD**: admit for monitoring of platelets, bleeding and sensorium; supportive care with fluids, blood products and management of encephalitis; mortality is **about 3-5%**. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**Severe scrub typhus**: IV doxycycline with IV azithromycin (INTREST 2025) and organ support - see the Scrub typhus topic. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Clinical rabies**: palliative care in a quiet, dimly lit room with sedation and analgesia; staff use standard precautions and those with mucosal exposure to saliva receive PEP. [WHO 2018]`,`**Nipah**: isolation in a single room with **airborne, droplet and contact precautions**, contact tracing for 21 days, and supportive care; the **m102.4 monoclonal antibody** and remdesivir are used under research protocols. [NCDC 2025]`,`**Avian influenza H5N1**: isolation and **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days (longer if severe)** started as early as possible, plus chemoprophylaxis for close contacts. [WHO 2024]`,`**CCHF**: strict barrier nursing (health-care-associated transmission is well described), supportive care with blood products and **ribavirin** in some protocols, though trial evidence of benefit is weak. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Rabies elimination - NAPRE and Zero by 30`,points:[`**Zero by 30**: the global strategic plan (WHO, FAO, WOAH and GARC, 2018) to end **human deaths from dog-mediated rabies by 2030**. [WHO 2018]`,`**NAPRE** - the **National Action Plan for dog-mediated Rabies Elimination from India by 2030** - was launched on **World Rabies Day, 28 September 2021**, jointly by MoHFW and the Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying. [NAPRE 2021]`,`**Health-sector pillars**: free **anti-rabies vaccine and RIG** in all public facilities (both are on the **NLEM** and the free drugs list), **intradermal PEP** to save vaccine, model anti-rabies clinics, trained staff, and making **human rabies a notifiable disease** in states. [NRCP 2024]`,`**Veterinary pillars**: **mass dog vaccination reaching 70% or more of the dog population** for herd immunity, dog population management under the **Animal Birth Control (ABC) Rules 2023**, and laboratory surveillance of animal rabies. [NAPRE 2021]`,`**Community pillars**: awareness on bite prevention and wound washing, responsible pet ownership, garbage management to reduce street-dog carrying capacity, and inter-sectoral district rabies committees. [NAPRE 2021]`,`**Indicators**: human rabies deaths, PEP completion, dog vaccination coverage, and proportion of bite victims receiving RIG for category III exposures. [NRCP 2024]`]},{heading:`Scrub typhus and KFD through a One Health lens`,points:[`**Scrub typhus** is caused by **Orientia tsutsugamushi**, transmitted by the bite of **larval trombiculid mites (chiggers, chiefly Leptotrombidium deliense)** that live on rodents in 'mite islands' of scrub vegetation, field edges and grassland. [Harrison 22e]`,`The mite is both **vector and reservoir** (transovarial transmission), so control relies on **personal protection, clearing vegetation around homes, rodent control** and early doxycycline rather than eradication. [ICMR 2022]`,`**KFD virus** is a **tick-borne flavivirus**, discovered in **1957 in Shimoga (Shivamogga) district, Karnataka**, after deaths of **black-faced langurs and bonnet macaques** - hence 'monkey fever'; the main vector is the hard tick **Haemaphysalis spinigera**. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**KFD clinical course**: abrupt fever, headache, severe myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, bleeding, leucopenia and thrombocytopenia; after a 1-2 week afebrile period, **about 10-20% develop a second phase** with meningoencephalitis. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**KFD sentinel surveillance**: every **monkey death in the forest** is reported, the carcass tested and the area within about 50 m treated with **acaricide**; villagers are vaccinated and advised to use **DMP oil** repellent. [ICMR-NIV 2023]`,`**KFD vaccine**: a formalin-inactivated tissue-culture vaccine (2 doses a month apart with boosters) had modest effectiveness and its supply stopped in 2022 over quality concerns; **ICMR is developing a new KFD vaccine** with an industry partner. [ICMR 2024]`]},{heading:`AMR in animals and food safety`,points:[`**Antimicrobial use in food animals** exceeds human use globally, and India was projected to be among the countries with the largest increases, driven by poultry and aquaculture. [PNAS 2015]`,`**Colistin ban**: in **July 2019** MoHFW prohibited manufacture, sale and distribution of colistin and its formulations for **food-producing animals, poultry, aquaculture and animal feed supplements**, after mcr-1 colistin resistance spread globally. [MoHFW 2019]`,`**Chloramphenicol and nitrofurans** are prohibited in food-producing animals, and **FSSAI** sets **maximum residue limits** for antibiotics in meat, milk, eggs, fish and honey under the Food Safety and Standards (Contaminants, Toxins and Residues) Regulations. [FSSAI 2024]`,`**WHO Medically Important Antimicrobials (MIA) list, 2024** (replacing the CIA list) tells veterinary and agricultural sectors which drugs (for example colistin, third-generation cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones, carbapenems) must be preserved for human medicine. [WHO 2024]`,`**INFAAR** (ICAR's Indian Network for Fishery and Animal Antimicrobial Resistance) and ICMR's AMRSN provide integrated surveillance across animal and human isolates. [ICAR 2024]`,`**Food safety basics** - the **WHO Five Keys to Safer Food**: keep clean, separate raw and cooked, cook thoroughly (core 70 C or more), keep food at safe temperatures (under 5 C or over 60 C) and use safe water and raw materials. [WHO 2023]`,`**Milk pasteurisation** (HTST 72 C for 15 seconds, or 63 C for 30 minutes) prevents brucellosis, bovine TB, Q fever and salmonellosis - counsel against raw milk. [FSSAI 2024]`]},{heading:`Institutional framework in India`,points:[`**National One Health Mission (NOHM)**, approved through the **Prime Minister's Science, Technology and Innovation Advisory Council (PM-STIAC)**, brings together health (ICMR, NCDC), animal husbandry (DAHD, ICAR), environment, wildlife and other ministries for integrated surveillance, outbreak response and research. [NOHM 2024]`,`**National Institute of One Health, Nagpur** (ICMR) is being built as a BSL-3 hub for zoonotic research and outbreak response. [ICMR 2024]`,`**NCDC's National One Health Programme for Prevention and Control of Zoonoses** funds state and district zoonosis committees, joint training and inter-sectoral coordination. [NCDC 2024]`,`**National Animal Disease Control Programme (NADCP, 2019)** vaccinates cattle and buffalo against **foot-and-mouth disease** and **female calves aged 4-8 months against brucellosis** (strain 19), aiming at control and eventual eradication. [DAHD 2024]`,`**Pandemic Fund grant (2024)** supports DAHD's **animal health security** project to strengthen veterinary surveillance, laboratories and early warning. [DAHD 2024]`,`**NAP-AMR**: the first National Action Plan on AMR (2017-2021) was explicitly One Health; its successor **NAP-AMR 2.0 (2025-2029)** extends the approach to animal, food and environmental sectors. [MoHFW 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations and the family physician's role`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: avoid raw milk, undercooked meat and cat litter (toxoplasmosis, listeriosis, brucellosis); rabies PEP is **safe and must not be withheld** in pregnancy; azithromycin replaces doxycycline. [WHO 2018]`,`**Children** are the commonest victims of dog bites, often on the face and head (category III); teach bite prevention in schools and Anganwadis. [NAPRE 2021]`,`**Immunocompromised** people (HIV, transplant, steroids) need **full RIG and a complete vaccine schedule** after exposure and should avoid high-risk animal contact. [WHO 2018]`,`**Occupational groups**: offer rabies PrEP to veterinarians and animal handlers, and hepatitis and tetanus vaccination where relevant; screen dairy workers for brucellosis when febrile. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Family physician as One Health sentinel**: take the animal history, notify early, link with the veterinary officer, counsel on food and water safety, and practise **antimicrobial stewardship** - see the AMR and stewardship topic. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest updates (2023-2026) and exam angle`,points:[`**WHO Pandemic Agreement** adopted by the **World Health Assembly in May 2025** includes a One Health article on preventing spillover; its pathogen access and benefit-sharing annex is still being negotiated. [WHA 2025]`,`**Amended International Health Regulations** (adopted 2024, in force from September 2025) add a **'pandemic emergency'** alert level and strengthen national IHR authorities. [WHO 2025]`,`**UN General Assembly political declaration on AMR (September 2024)** set a target to **cut AMR-associated deaths by 10% by 2030** and to meaningfully reduce antimicrobials used in agri-food systems. [UNGA 2024]`,`**ABC Rules 2023** replaced the 2001 rules, making local bodies responsible for sterilisation and anti-rabies vaccination of community dogs. [ABC Rules 2023]`,`**Viva**: 'Define One Health and name the Quadripartite' - FAO, UNEP, WHO and WOAH. [OHHLEP 2021]`,`**Viva**: 'What is a metazoonosis?' - needs a vertebrate host and an invertebrate vector, for example KFD, JE, plague and scrub typhus. [Park 27e]`,`**Common mistake**: delaying rabies PEP while waiting to observe the dog - start PEP at once; it can be stopped only if a dog or cat remains healthy for **10 days**. [WHO 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Priority zoonoses in India - differential and control`,columns:[`Zoonosis`,`Reservoir / vector`,`Key clinical clue`,`Test`,`Human treatment / prevention`],rows:[[`Rabies`,`Dogs (over 95%), other mammals`,`Bite, hydrophobia, aerophobia`,`Saliva/skin RT-PCR; brain DFA`,`Wound wash, vaccine, RIG; dog vaccination 70%+`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Chigger mites on rodents`,`Eschar, lymphadenopathy, pneumonitis`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`,`Doxycycline or azithromycin`],[`KFD`,`Haemaphysalis ticks; monkeys amplify`,`Forest worker, bleeding, biphasic encephalitis`,`RT-PCR, IgM ELISA (NIV)`,`Supportive; repellent, acaricide, vaccine (new one awaited)`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Rats, cattle, dogs (urine)`,`Flood exposure, suffusion, AKI, jaundice`,`IgM ELISA, MAT, PCR`,`Doxycycline; penicillin or ceftriaxone if severe`],[`Brucellosis`,`Cattle, goats, sheep; raw milk`,`Undulant fever, sacroiliitis, orchitis`,`SAT, blood culture`,`Doxycycline plus rifampicin or streptomycin`],[`Japanese encephalitis`,`Ardeid birds; pigs amplify; Culex`,`Child with AES in rice-pig areas`,`JE IgM in CSF/serum`,`Supportive; JE vaccine under UIP`],[`Nipah`,`Pteropus fruit bats; pigs`,`Encephalitis with respiratory illness, contact`,`RT-PCR (NIV)`,`Isolation, supportive; contact tracing 21 days`],[`Avian influenza H5N1`,`Wild birds, poultry`,`Severe pneumonia after poultry contact`,`RT-PCR`,`Oseltamivir; culling; PPE`],[`CCHF`,`Hyalomma ticks; livestock`,`Early severe haemorrhage, nosocomial spread`,`RT-PCR`,`Barrier nursing, supportive, ribavirin (weak evidence)`]]},{heading:`Classification of zoonoses (Park)`,columns:[`Type`,`Definition`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Anthropozoonoses`,`Animal to human`,`Rabies, plague, leptospirosis`],[`Zooanthroponoses`,`Human to animal`,`Human TB in cattle`],[`Amphixenoses`,`Both directions`,`Staphylococci, salmonella`],[`Direct zoonoses`,`One vertebrate host`,`Rabies, brucellosis, anthrax`],[`Cyclozoonoses`,`More than one vertebrate host`,`Hydatid, taeniasis`],[`Metazoonoses`,`Vertebrate plus invertebrate vector`,`JE, KFD, plague, scrub typhus`],[`Saprozoonoses`,`Vertebrate plus non-animal site (soil, plants)`,`Histoplasmosis, larva migrans`]]},{heading:`Drug table - zoonoses in primary and secondary care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Rabies vaccine (PEP, ID)`,`0.1 mL ID at 2 sites`,`Days 0, 3, 7 (updated Thai Red Cross)`,`Local reactions; no contraindication after exposure`],[`Rabies vaccine (PEP, IM)`,`1 dose IM deltoid`,`Days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 (Essen) or 2-1-1 Zagreb`,`Never in gluteal region`],[`Rabies immunoglobulin / monoclonal`,`ERIG 40 IU/kg or HRIG 20 IU/kg infiltrated into wound; mAb per label`,`Once, day 0 (up to day 7)`,`Serum sickness with ERIG (rare); maximum dose by weight`],[`Rabies PrEP`,`1 dose IM or 0.1 mL ID at 2 sites`,`Days 0 and 7`,`Booster only after exposure`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg orally twice daily`,`7 days (scrub, lepto); 6 weeks with rifampicin (brucellosis)`,`Oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg orally once daily`,`5 days (scrub typhus in pregnancy)`,`QT prolongation`],[`Oseltamivir`,`75 mg orally twice daily (renal adjustment)`,`5 days, longer if severe; 10 days once daily for prophylaxis`,`Nausea, neuropsychiatric effects (rare)`],[`Paracetamol (KFD, viral)`,`500-1000 mg orally 6-hourly`,`While febrile; max 3-4 g/day`,`No NSAIDs or aspirin in haemorrhagic fevers`]]},{heading:`NAPRE - pillars and actions`,columns:[`Sector`,`Key actions`,`Target / indicator`],rows:[[`Human health`,`Free vaccine and RIG, ID PEP, model anti-rabies clinics, rabies notifiable`,`Zero human deaths from dog rabies by 2030`],[`Animal health`,`Mass dog vaccination, ABC Rules 2023, animal rabies surveillance`,`70% or more dog vaccination coverage`],[`Community`,`Bite prevention, wound washing, waste management, pet ownership`,`Wound washing and PEP completion rates`],[`Governance`,`Inter-sectoral rabies committees at state and district level`,`Joint investigation of every human death`]]},{heading:`KFD vs scrub typhus vs dengue`,columns:[`Feature`,`KFD`,`Scrub typhus`,`Dengue`],rows:[[`Vector`,`Haemaphysalis tick`,`Chigger mite`,`Aedes mosquito`],[`Season`,`November to June (dry)`,`Post-monsoon`,`Monsoon and post-monsoon`],[`Setting`,`Western Ghats forest`,`Grassland, fields, scrub`,`Urban and peri-urban`],[`Hallmark`,`Monkey deaths, biphasic encephalitis`,`Eschar`,`Leak at defervescence`],[`Test`,`RT-PCR, IgM (NIV)`,`IgM ELISA, PCR`,`NS1, IgM`],[`Treatment`,`Supportive`,`Doxycycline`,`Fluids, paracetamol`]]}],redFlags:[`Category III animal bite (bleeding bite, bite on face or hands, bat contact) - same-day vaccine plus RIG`,`Hydrophobia, aerophobia or agitation after an animal bite - clinical rabies, admit for palliative care`,`Fever with encephalitis after bat, pig or sick-patient contact in a Nipah area - isolate and notify immediately`,`Fever with bleeding in a Western Ghats forest worker during the dry season - suspected KFD`,`Severe pneumonia after contact with sick or dead poultry - suspected avian influenza`,`Cluster of fever or deaths in people together with animal deaths - joint outbreak investigation`,`Early severe haemorrhage after a tick bite or livestock slaughter - suspected CCHF, barrier nursing`],pearls:[`**One Health = people + animals + ecosystems**; Quadripartite = **FAO, UNEP, WHO, WOAH**.`,`**60% of human pathogens, 75% of emerging infections** are zoonotic.`,`**NAPRE launched 28 September 2021**; target **zero dog-mediated rabies deaths by 2030**.`,`**70% dog vaccination coverage** breaks rabies transmission.`,`**KFD: 1957, Shimoga, monkeys die first**, Haemaphysalis ticks, biphasic illness.`,`**Metazoonosis = vertebrate + vector**: KFD, JE, plague, scrub typhus.`,`**Colistin banned in food animals since July 2019**.`,`**Never wait to observe the dog** - start PEP; stop only if the animal is healthy at 10 days.`,`**Pigs amplify JE and Nipah; monkeys amplify KFD; bats reserve Nipah.**`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old cashew plantation worker from Shivamogga presents in February with high fever, severe myalgia, conjunctival suffusion and gum bleeding. Villagers have reported dead langurs in the nearby forest. Platelets are 70,000/uL and WBC 2,800/uL. Dengue NS1 and IgM are negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Scrub typhus`,`Leptospirosis`,`Kyasanur Forest disease`,`Chikungunya`],answer:2,explanation:`Forest exposure in the Western Ghats during the dry season, monkey deaths, haemorrhage, leucopenia and thrombocytopenia are the hallmarks of KFD, a Haemaphysalis tick-borne flavivirus. Scrub typhus peaks after the monsoon and has an eschar; leptospirosis follows flood exposure with neutrophilia and AKI; chikungunya causes severe arthralgia rather than bleeding.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-q2`,stem:`Which group of organisations makes up the Quadripartite that leads the global One Health Joint Plan of Action (2022-2026)?`,options:[`WHO, UNICEF, World Bank and FAO`,`FAO, UNEP, WHO and WOAH`,`WHO, WOAH, CDC and Gavi`,`UNEP, UNICEF, WOAH and WHO`],answer:1,explanation:`The Quadripartite is FAO (food and agriculture), UNEP (environment), WHO (human health) and WOAH (animal health, formerly OIE); UNEP joined the earlier Tripartite in 2022. UNICEF, the World Bank, CDC and Gavi are partners in global health but are not members of the Quadripartite.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-q3`,stem:`Under India's National Action Plan for dog-mediated Rabies Elimination (NAPRE), what minimum dog vaccination coverage is targeted to interrupt transmission?`,options:[`40%`,`50%`,`90%`,`70%`],answer:3,explanation:`Sustained vaccination of at least 70% of the dog population produces herd immunity that interrupts dog-to-dog rabies transmission, the basis of NAPRE and Zero by 30. Coverage of 40-50% leaves enough susceptible dogs for transmission to continue, while 90% is not required and is rarely achievable in free-roaming dog populations.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-q4`,stem:`Japanese encephalitis, Kyasanur Forest disease and plague are all examples of which category of zoonoses?`,options:[`Metazoonoses`,`Cyclozoonoses`,`Saprozoonoses`,`Direct zoonoses`],answer:0,explanation:`Metazoonoses need both a vertebrate host and an invertebrate vector - mosquitoes for JE, ticks for KFD and fleas for plague. Cyclozoonoses need more than one vertebrate host (hydatid, taeniasis); saprozoonoses need a non-animal site such as soil (histoplasmosis); direct zoonoses pass with one vertebrate host and no vector (rabies, brucellosis).`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-q5`,stem:`In July 2019 the Government of India prohibited the use of which antimicrobial in food-producing animals, poultry, aquaculture and animal feed supplements, because of the spread of plasmid-mediated resistance?`,options:[`Tetracycline`,`Colistin`,`Enrofloxacin`,`Amoxicillin`],answer:1,explanation:`Colistin was banned for food animals after the plasmid-mediated mcr-1 gene showed that animal use threatens a last-resort drug for carbapenem-resistant Gram-negative infections in humans. Tetracyclines, enrofloxacin and amoxicillin remain permitted for veterinary therapeutic use with withdrawal periods and residue limits, though their misuse also drives AMR.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define One Health. Discuss the drivers of zoonotic spillover and describe India's strategies for rabies elimination and control of antimicrobial use in animals.`,openingLines:[`One Health is an integrated, unifying approach that aims to sustainably balance and optimise the health of people, animals and ecosystems (OHHLEP, 2021), led globally by the Quadripartite - FAO, UNEP, WHO and WOAH.`,`About 60% of human pathogens and 75% of emerging infections are zoonotic, so rabies, KFD, scrub typhus, Nipah and AMR can only be controlled by joint human, veterinary and environmental action.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`OHHLEP definition; Quadripartite One Health Joint Plan of Action 2022-2026.`,`India: largest livestock population, rabies deaths (about 5,700/year, ICMR 2025), KFD, scrub typhus, Nipah, H5N1.`]},{heading:`Drivers of spillover`,points:[`Land-use change, deforestation, intensive farming, climate change, urbanisation with waste, wildlife trade, occupational and food exposure.`,`Reservoir, amplifier and bridge hosts; receptor compatibility; dead-end vs human-to-human spread.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Direct, cyclo-, meta- and saprozoonoses; anthropozoonoses, zooanthroponoses, amphixenoses.`]},{heading:`Rabies elimination (NAPRE 2030)`,points:[`Launched 28 September 2021; Zero by 30.`,`Human: free vaccine and RIG, ID PEP, notifiability; animal: 70% dog vaccination, ABC Rules 2023; community: wound washing, bite prevention, waste management.`]},{heading:`AMR in animals`,points:[`Colistin ban for food animals (July 2019); chloramphenicol and nitrofurans prohibited; FSSAI residue limits.`,`WHO MIA list 2024; INFAAR surveillance; NAP-AMR (2017) and NAP-AMR 2.0 (2025-2029); UNGA 2024 targets.`]},{heading:`Institutions and the family physician`,points:[`NOHM, National Institute of One Health Nagpur, NCDC zoonoses programme, NADCP.`,`Animal history, early notification, joint investigation, food safety counselling, stewardship.`]}],mustDraw:[`Venn diagram of human, animal and environmental health with One Health at the centre`,`Spillover chain: reservoir - release - environment - exposure - human infection, with intervention points`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1.5},{part:`Drivers of spillover`,marks:2.5},{part:`Rabies elimination strategy`,marks:3},{part:`AMR in animals`,marks:2},{part:`Institutions`,marks:1}],keywords:[`One Health`,`Quadripartite`,`spillover`,`NAPRE`,`rabies`,`colistin`,`AMR`,`NOHM`]},{id:`infectious-fever-one-health-zoonoses-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on Kyasanur Forest disease.`,openingLines:[`Kyasanur Forest disease ('monkey fever') is a tick-borne flaviviral haemorrhagic fever first identified in 1957 in Shimoga district, Karnataka, after deaths of langurs and bonnet macaques.`,`It is transmitted by Haemaphysalis spinigera ticks, occurs in the dry season along the Western Ghats, and is a classic One Health disease with monkey deaths as the sentinel.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`Karnataka, Kerala, Goa, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu; November to June, peak January to April.`,`Forest workers, cashew and arecanut workers, firewood collectors; CFR about 3-5%.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`Fever, myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, bleeding, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia; 10-20% second encephalitic phase.`,`RT-PCR in first week, IgM ELISA later (NIV Pune, VRDLs).`]},{heading:`Management and prevention`,points:[`Supportive: paracetamol, fluids, blood products; no NSAIDs.`,`Monkey death surveillance, acaricide around carcass, DMP repellent, protective clothing; inactivated vaccine (supply halted 2022), new ICMR vaccine in development.`]}],mustDraw:[`Transmission cycle: small mammals and monkeys - Haemaphysalis tick - humans (dead-end)`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and transmission`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`KFD`,`monkey fever`,`Haemaphysalis`,`Shimoga`,`flavivirus`,`DMP oil`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Zoonoses, rabies and rickettsial diseases`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023 - Zoonoses`,`One Health High-Level Expert Panel (OHHLEP) definition, 2021; Quadripartite One Health Joint Plan of Action 2022-2026`,`National Action Plan for dog-mediated Rabies Elimination from India by 2030 (NAPRE), MoHFW and DAHD, 2021; National Guidelines for Rabies Prophylaxis, NCDC`,`WHO Expert Consultation on Rabies, third report (TRS 1012), 2018`,`ICMR-NIV: Kyasanur Forest disease - guidelines for diagnosis, management and control, 2023`,`WHO List of Medically Important Antimicrobials, 2024; MoHFW notification prohibiting colistin in food-producing animals, July 2019`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`]});var f=[],p=[];p.push({id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections`,title:`Streptococcal infections: pharyngitis, invasive GAS, post-streptococcal sequelae`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)** causes **pharyngitis, scarlet fever, impetigo, cellulitis and invasive disease (necrotising fasciitis, streptococcal toxic shock)**, and through **molecular mimicry** the non-suppurative sequelae **acute rheumatic fever and post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis**; it is **universally penicillin-sensitive**, so in India - a moderate-to-high RHD-risk country - a **Centor/McIsaac-guided 10-day course of penicillin or amoxicillin** for sore throat and **3-weekly benzathine penicillin** after rheumatic fever are the two prescriptions that prevent rheumatic heart disease.`,keywords:[`GAS`,`Streptococcus pyogenes`,`strep throat`,`Centor score`,`McIsaac score`,`FeverPAIN`,`rapid antigen detection test`,`scarlet fever`,`streptococcal toxic shock syndrome`,`STSS`,`necrotising fasciitis`,`acute rheumatic fever`,`rheumatic heart disease`,`revised Jones criteria 2015`,`post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis`,`ASO titre`,`anti-DNase B`,`benzathine penicillin G`,`Lancefield grouping`,`Sydenham chorea`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Streptococci** are Gram-positive cocci in chains, catalase-negative, classified by **haemolysis** on blood agar and by **Lancefield carbohydrate antigen**; **group A (S. pyogenes)** is the major human pathogen of the genus apart from the pneumococcus. [Harrison 22e]`,`GAS causes **5-15% of sore throats in adults** and **20-30% in children aged 5-15 years**; the rest are mostly viral, which is why most sore throats need no antibiotic. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Asymptomatic pharyngeal carriage** of GAS is found in **5-20% of school-age children**, so a positive swab in a child with a viral picture may be a carrier, not the cause. [IDSA 2012]`,`Globally GAS causes an estimated **over 500,000 deaths a year**, most from **rheumatic heart disease (RHD)** and invasive disease; GBD estimates put RHD prevalence at about **40 million people** with about **300,000 deaths a year**. [GBD 2019]`,`In India, clinical school surveys (**ICMR Jai Vigyan RF/RHD project**) found RHD in about **0.5-1 per 1000 school children**, while **echocardiographic screening** finds **about 20 per 1000** - most of it clinically silent (**latent RHD**). [ICMR 2010; Saxena 2011]`,`India carries one of the **largest absolute RHD burdens in the world**; RHD remains a leading cause of **mitral stenosis, heart failure, stroke and maternal cardiac death** in young Indian adults. [CSI 2023]`,`**Invasive GAS (iGAS)** - GAS from a normally sterile site - has an incidence of about **2-4 per 100,000** in high-income countries, with a sharp **post-pandemic surge in 2022-2023** in Europe and the UK driven by the **emm1 (M1UK) lineage**. [UKHSA 2023]`,`Peaks of GAS pharyngitis are in **winter and early spring** in temperate regions; in India **pyoderma (impetigo)** is the commoner GAS infection in the humid tropics and the commoner trigger of **PSGN**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Group A (S. pyogenes)**: pharyngitis, scarlet fever, impetigo, erysipelas, cellulitis, necrotising fasciitis, STSS, puerperal sepsis, and the sequelae ARF and PSGN. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Group B (S. agalactiae)**: neonatal sepsis and meningitis, peripartum infection, and in adults **bacteraemia, cellulitis and osteomyelitis in elderly diabetics** (see the Infections in pregnancy topic for intrapartum prophylaxis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Groups C and G (S. dysgalactiae subsp. equisimilis)**: pharyngitis, cellulitis and bacteraemia in the elderly; they can cause PSGN and reactive arthritis but **ARF is not established**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viridans streptococci** (alpha-haemolytic, no Lancefield group): dental caries and **native-valve infective endocarditis**; the **S. anginosus (milleri) group** forms **abscesses** in brain, liver and lung (see Infective endocarditis topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**S. gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis biotype I)** bacteraemia or endocarditis mandates **colonoscopy** because of its association with **colorectal neoplasia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk factors for GAS pharyngitis and ARF are **overcrowding, poverty, poor housing, school-age (5-15 years)** and limited access to primary care - the social determinants that explain the RHD map of India. [WHO 2024]`,`Risk factors for **invasive GAS** are **varicella (chickenpox) in children**, skin breaks and burns, **injection drug use, diabetes, age over 65, alcohol, malignancy, the postpartum state** and recent **NSAID use** (which may mask early signs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Streptococcus pneumoniae** is alpha-haemolytic and optochin-sensitive; it is covered in the Invasive pneumococcal disease topic and not here. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and virulence`,points:[`**M protein** - a fibrillar surface protein encoded by **emm** (over 250 emm types) - resists phagocytosis by blocking complement deposition; type-specific anti-M antibody is protective, which is why reinfection with a new emm type is possible. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **hyaluronic acid capsule** mimics host connective tissue and is antiphagocytic; heavily encapsulated **mucoid strains** are linked to ARF outbreaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Streptolysin O** (oxygen-labile, immunogenic - basis of the **ASO titre**) and **streptolysin S** (oxygen-stable, non-immunogenic, causes beta-haemolysis on the plate surface) lyse cells; **DNase B** gives the **anti-DNase B** test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins (SpeA, SpeC)** are **superantigens** that bind MHC class II and the T-cell receptor V-beta outside the antigen groove, activating up to **20% of T cells** and causing the cytokine storm of **STSS** and the rash of **scarlet fever**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SpeB (cysteine protease), streptokinase, hyaluronidase and C5a peptidase** help spread through tissue planes - the basis of rapid fascial spread in **necrotising fasciitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ARF is autoimmune via molecular mimicry**: antibodies and T cells against **M protein and group A carbohydrate (N-acetylglucosamine)** cross-react with **cardiac myosin, tropomyosin, laminin and valvular endothelium**, giving a **pancarditis** that scars mainly the **mitral then aortic valves**. [Harrison 22e]`,`ARF follows **pharyngitis, not skin infection** (classic teaching), after a **latent period of about 3 weeks (1-5 weeks)**; **Sydenham chorea** comes later, after **1-6 months**, from antibodies to basal ganglia neurons (lysoganglioside, dopamine receptors). [Harrison 22e]`,`**PSGN** is an **immune-complex nephritis** caused by **nephritogenic strains** (antigens SpeB and NAPlr deposit in the glomerulus), with **low C3 and normal C4** (alternative pathway); it follows **pharyngitis by 1-2 weeks** or **skin infection by 3-6 weeks**, and penicillin does **not** prevent it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aschoff bodies** (perivascular granulomas with Anitschkow 'caterpillar' cells) are the pathognomonic myocardial lesion of rheumatic carditis. [Robbins 10e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**By haemolysis**: **alpha** (partial, green - viridans, pneumococcus), **beta** (complete, clear - groups A, B, C, G), **gamma** (none - most enterococci, formerly group D). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By Lancefield group** (cell-wall carbohydrate): A, B, C, D (now mostly Enterococcus and S. gallolyticus), F, G; beta-haemolytic large-colony A, C and G are pyogenic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GAS disease is classified as**: **suppurative non-invasive** (pharyngitis, impetigo, otitis, sinusitis), **suppurative invasive** (bacteraemia, necrotising fasciitis, STSS, pneumonia, puerperal sepsis) and **non-suppurative sequelae** (ARF, PSGN, post-streptococcal reactive arthritis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Revised Jones criteria (AHA 2015)** separate **low-risk** populations (ARF incidence 2 or less per 100,000 school children a year or RHD prevalence 1 or less per 1000) from **moderate-to-high risk** populations - **India is moderate-to-high risk**, so the more sensitive criteria apply. [AHA 2015]`,`**Initial ARF** = evidence of preceding GAS infection plus **2 major, or 1 major + 2 minor** criteria; **recurrent ARF** = 2 major, 1 major + 2 minor, or **3 minor** criteria. [AHA 2015]`,`**Major criteria (high-risk)**: **carditis (clinical or subclinical on echo)**, **arthritis - polyarthritis, monoarthritis or polyarthralgia**, **chorea**, **erythema marginatum**, **subcutaneous nodules**. [AHA 2015]`,`**Minor criteria (high-risk)**: **monoarthralgia**, **fever 38 C or more**, **ESR 30 mm/h or more and/or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more**, and **prolonged PR interval** for age (unless carditis is a major criterion). [AHA 2015]`,`**Exceptions**: **Sydenham chorea** alone or **indolent carditis** presenting months later can establish ARF without other criteria or proof of recent GAS infection. [AHA 2015]`,`**STSS (CDC 2010 case definition)**: GAS isolated plus **hypotension (SBP 90 mmHg or less)** and **2 or more** of: renal impairment, coagulopathy, liver involvement, ARDS, generalised desquamating erythematous rash, soft-tissue necrosis. [CDC 2010]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**GAS pharyngitis**: **sudden sore throat, painful swallowing and fever**, often with headache, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting in children; **cough, coryza, hoarseness, conjunctivitis and diarrhoea point to a virus**. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Scarlet fever** follows GAS pharyngitis (rarely a wound) in children aged 5-15, with a **sandpaper rash starting on day 1-2** on the neck and trunk, spreading to limbs and sparing palms and soles, then **desquamating at 7-10 days**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Impetigo**: honey-crusted lesions on the face and limbs of young children in hot humid months; bullous impetigo is usually **S. aureus** (see Skin and soft tissue infection topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Necrotising fasciitis**: **pain out of proportion to the visible findings**, rapidly spreading swelling, fever and toxicity, often after minor trauma, surgery, varicella or childbirth. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**ARF history**: sore throat 2-4 weeks earlier (recalled in only about **two-thirds** - many are subclinical), then **migratory painful large-joint arthritis**, fever, breathlessness or palpitations; a dramatic response of arthritis to aspirin within 48 hours is typical. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sydenham chorea**: girls more than boys, **clumsiness, dropping things, deteriorating handwriting and emotional lability** months after a sore throat - often the only feature. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PSGN**: **cola-coloured urine, facial puffiness and oliguria** 1-2 weeks after a sore throat or 3-6 weeks after skin sores, usually in children aged 5-12. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis (PSRA)**: non-migratory, persistent arthritis starting **within 10 days** of pharyngitis, responding poorly to aspirin, without carditis. [AHA 2009]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and eponymous signs`,points:[`**Pharyngitis**: **tonsillopharyngeal erythema with exudate**, **tender enlarged anterior cervical nodes**, **palatal petechiae** and a beefy red swollen uvula; vesicles or ulcers suggest coxsackie or HSV. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Scarlet fever**: **sandpaper (goose-flesh) rash**, **Pastia lines** (linear petechiae in skin folds), **circumoral pallor** and **strawberry tongue** (white then red). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Necrotising fasciitis signs**: **tense oedema beyond the erythema, bullae, skin anaesthesia, dusky or violaceous skin and crepitus** (crepitus suggests mixed or clostridial infection); **LRINEC 6 or more** raises suspicion but a low score does not exclude it. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Rheumatic carditis**: new **apical pansystolic murmur of mitral regurgitation**, **Carey Coombs murmur** (mid-diastolic, from valvulitis), early diastolic aortic murmur, **tachycardia out of proportion to fever**, pericardial rub, cardiomegaly and heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Erythema marginatum**: evanescent pink rings with pale centres on the trunk and proximal limbs, never on the face, worse with heat; seen in under **6%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subcutaneous nodules**: painless, firm, 0.5-2 cm, over **extensor surfaces, olecranon, knuckles, occiput and spine**, usually with severe carditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chorea signs**: **milkmaid grip**, darting (**jack-in-the-box**) tongue, **pronator sign**, spooning of outstretched hands, and explosive speech. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PSGN signs**: **hypertension, periorbital oedema**, and signs of fluid overload - pulmonary oedema or hypertensive encephalopathy in severe cases. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Viral pharyngitis** (adenovirus, rhinovirus, influenza, coxsackie): cough, coryza, conjunctivitis; **no antibiotic** needed. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)**: exudative tonsillitis with **posterior cervical nodes, splenomegaly and atypical lymphocytes**; amoxicillin causes a rash in most (see Infectious mononucleosis syndromes topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diphtheria**: **grey adherent pseudomembrane** that bleeds on removal, bull neck, low-grade fever, unvaccinated child - still seen in India (see Tetanus and diphtheria topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute HIV seroconversion, gonococcal pharyngitis** (oral sex) and **Lemierre syndrome** (Fusobacterium; neck pain, septic emboli) are the dangerous lookalikes in adolescents and young adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peritonsillar abscess (quinsy)**: trismus, **hot-potato voice, uvular deviation** and unilateral bulge - needs drainage, not just antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ARF arthritis versus**: septic arthritis (single hot joint, no migration), **juvenile idiopathic arthritis** (no aspirin response, longer course), reactive arthritis, **sickle cell crisis**, viral arthritis (chikungunya), **SLE**, and leukaemia in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scarlet fever versus Kawasaki disease** (younger, no exudate, conjunctival injection, coronary aneurysm), **staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome**, measles and drug rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PSGN versus IgA nephropathy**: IgA nephropathy gives **synpharyngitic haematuria (1-2 days)** with **normal C3**; PSGN has a latent period and **low C3 that normalises within 8 weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation`,points:[`**Rapid antigen detection test (RADT)**: specificity **over 95%**, sensitivity **70-90%**; a **negative RADT in a child should be backed up by throat culture**, but this is not needed in adults (low GAS prevalence and ARF risk). [IDSA 2012]`,`**Throat culture** on sheep blood agar (swab both tonsils and the posterior pharynx without touching the tongue) is the **reference standard** - sensitivity 90-95%; **molecular (NAAT) point-of-care tests** have sensitivity over 95% and need no back-up. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Do not test children under 3 years** routinely (ARF rare) and **do not test** when features are clearly viral; **do not do test-of-cure** in asymptomatic patients after treatment. [IDSA 2012]`,`**ASO titre** rises from **1 week, peaks at 3-6 weeks** after infection; use the **age- and region-specific upper limit of normal** (higher in Indian school children) or a **rise between paired sera 2-4 weeks apart**. [AHA 2015]`,`**Anti-DNase B** rises later (**peaks 6-8 weeks**) and stays up longer; it is the better test after **skin infection** (ASO response to pyoderma is weak because skin lipids bind streptolysin O). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ASO has no role in diagnosing acute pharyngitis** - it reflects infection weeks ago; it supports ARF or PSGN only. [IDSA 2012]`,`**For suspected ARF**: ESR, CRP, **ECG (PR interval)**, chest X-ray, and **echocardiography in every case** - subclinical carditis on Doppler counts as a major criterion in the 2015 Jones criteria. [AHA 2015]`,`**For iGAS and STSS**: **blood cultures** (positive in about 60% of STSS), tissue culture at debridement, CK, lactate, creatinine, LFT, coagulation; **MRI or CT** can support but must **never delay surgical exploration** of suspected necrotising fasciitis. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**For PSGN**: urine for **RBC casts and dysmorphic red cells**, proteinuria, creatinine, **C3 (low) and C4 (normal)**, ASO and anti-DNase B; biopsy only if C3 stays low beyond 8 weeks or the course is atypical. [KDIGO 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - pharyngitis and skin`,points:[`**Score first**: Centor (exudate, tender anterior nodes, fever, no cough) **plus McIsaac age modifier** (+1 for 3-14 years, 0 for 15-44, -1 for 45 or more); **score 0-1 - no test, no antibiotic**; **2-3 - RADT or culture, treat if positive**; **4-5 - test, or treat empirically where tests are unavailable**. [McIsaac 1998; IDSA 2012]`,`In Indian primary care without RADT, **treat a child or adolescent with a high score (4 or more) empirically** because of the moderate-high ARF risk; reassure and give symptomatic care for low scores. [ICMR TG 2022]`,`**Phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V)**: children **250 mg twice or three times daily**, adolescents and adults **250 mg four times daily or 500 mg twice daily**, orally for **10 days**. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Amoxicillin**: **50 mg/kg once daily (max 1000 mg)** or **25 mg/kg twice daily (max 500 mg per dose)** orally for **10 days** - better taste and once-daily adherence make it the usual Indian choice. [IDSA 2012; ICMR TG 2022]`,`**Benzathine penicillin G** single IM dose - **600,000 U if under 27 kg, 1.2 million U if 27 kg or more** - when adherence to 10 days is doubtful; it is the **ARF primary prevention gold standard**. [AHA 2009]`,`**Penicillin allergy (non-anaphylactic)**: **cephalexin 20 mg/kg twice daily (max 500 mg)** or **cefadroxil 30 mg/kg once daily (max 1 g)** for **10 days**; **anaphylactic**: **clindamycin 7 mg/kg three times daily (max 300 mg) for 10 days** or **azithromycin 12 mg/kg once daily (max 500 mg) for 5 days**. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Avoid** tetracyclines, sulfonamides/co-trimoxazole and fluoroquinolones for GAS pharyngitis (resistance or poor eradication); **macrolide resistance is variable and rising**, so macrolides are for true allergy only. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Symptomatic care**: paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain and fever, warm saline gargles, fluids; **no steroids routinely**; the child can return to school **24 hours after starting antibiotics**. [NICE NG84 2018]`,`**Impetigo**: localised - topical **mupirocin 2% or fusidic acid** three times daily for 5 days; extensive - oral **cephalexin or amoxicillin-clavulanate** (to cover S. aureus) for 7 days. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Recurrent tonsillitis**: **tonsillectomy** is considered for **7 or more episodes in 1 year, 5 or more a year for 2 years, or 3 or more a year for 3 years** (Paradise criteria). [AAO-HNS 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency - invasive GAS and ARF`,points:[`**Necrotising fasciitis is a surgical emergency**: **urgent debridement within hours**, repeated every 24-36 hours until no necrotic tissue remains; delay is the main determinant of death (see Necrotising soft-tissue infection topic). [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Antibiotics for confirmed iGAS/STSS**: **benzylpenicillin 4 million U (2.4 g) IV every 4 hours** plus **clindamycin 900 mg IV every 8 hours**; clindamycin **suppresses toxin synthesis** and is unaffected by the **Eagle (inoculum) effect** that blunts penicillin in large, slow-growing bacterial loads. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Empirical cover before identification** in necrotising infection: **vancomycin or linezolid plus piperacillin-tazobactam or a carbapenem**, with **clindamycin** added for toxin suppression. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Linezolid 600 mg IV twice daily** is an alternative anti-toxin agent where **clindamycin resistance** is present (inducible MLSB resistance is reported in India). [Harrison 22e]`,`**IVIG** (for example **1 g/kg on day 1 then 0.5 g/kg on days 2 and 3**) may be **considered in STSS** to neutralise superantigens; evidence comes from small trials and observational data. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supportive STSS care**: early **30 mL/kg crystalloid**, noradrenaline for MAP 65 mmHg, source control, renal replacement if needed - per the Sepsis and septic shock topic. [SSC 2021]`,`**ARF inpatient care**: admit to confirm diagnosis and echo; **aspirin 50-60 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses (up to 80-100 mg/kg/day)** for arthritis for 2-4 weeks, then taper; **naproxen 10-20 mg/kg/day** is an alternative. [AHA 2015; WHO 2024]`,`**Severe carditis with heart failure**: **prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day (max 80 mg)** for 2-3 weeks then taper, plus diuretics and ACE inhibitor; steroids relieve symptoms but **have not been shown to reduce long-term valve damage**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Give the first eradication dose of benzathine penicillin** (1.2 million U IM) at diagnosis of ARF, regardless of throat culture, and **start secondary prophylaxis** at once. [WHO 2024]`,`**Sydenham chorea**: quiet environment; **valproate 15-20 mg/kg/day** or **carbamazepine** for disabling chorea (haloperidol is a second choice due to extrapyramidal effects). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: penicillin and amoxicillin are safe; benzathine penicillin prophylaxis **must continue in pregnancy** - pregnant women with RHD, especially **mitral stenosis**, decompensate in the second trimester and need cardiology-obstetric co-management. [ESC 2018 Pregnancy]`,`**Children under 3 years**: GAS pharyngitis and ARF are rare; test only with a GAS-positive sibling or ARF in the family. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Elderly and diabetics**: **group B, C and G streptococcal cellulitis and bacteraemia** are commoner; recurrent cellulitis with lymphoedema may need **penicillin prophylaxis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varicella**: a child with chickenpox who develops **new fever after day 3-4, spreading erythema or severe pain** has iGAS until proved otherwise. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Postpartum (puerperal sepsis)**: GAS is the classic cause of rapidly fatal puerperal sepsis; treat with penicillin plus clindamycin and inform infection control. [RCOG 2012]`,`**Penicillin allergy and benzathine prophylaxis**: anaphylaxis to BPG is rare (about **0.2%**); a true allergy should be confirmed (see Antibiotic allergy topic), and **oral erythromycin 250 mg twice daily** is the alternative for prophylaxis. [AHA 2009]`,`**Severe valve disease and BPG**: deaths after BPG injection in patients with severe MS or ventricular dysfunction are thought to be **cardiovascular (vasovagal) collapse** rather than anaphylaxis; inject with the patient lying down and observe for 15-30 minutes. [WHO 2024]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: **BPG shortages** occur in India; procure through state drug lists and keep cold-chain-free storage per label; never substitute procaine penicillin for long-term prophylaxis. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Suppurative complications of pharyngitis**: peritonsillar and retropharyngeal abscess, otitis media, sinusitis, cervical lymphadenitis, mastoiditis, bacteraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ARF risk after untreated GAS pharyngitis** is about **0.3% in endemic settings and up to 3% in epidemics**; antibiotics started **within 9 days** of symptom onset prevent ARF. [AHA 2009]`,`**Recurrence of ARF** occurs in up to half of patients without prophylaxis; each recurrence adds valve damage - this is why secondary prophylaxis is the single most cost-effective RHD intervention. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chronic RHD**: **mitral regurgitation** is the commonest lesion in children; **mitral stenosis** develops over 10-20 years and predominates in adults; atrial fibrillation, stroke, infective endocarditis and heart failure follow. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chorea** resolves within **6 months** in most, but recurs in about a third and can unmask psychiatric symptoms (OCD); it may coexist with silent carditis, so echo every case. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PSGN prognosis**: excellent in children - over **95% recover** with C3 normalising by 8 weeks; adults and those with nephrotic-range proteinuria have a higher risk of chronic kidney disease. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**STSS mortality is 30-60%** and necrotising fasciitis mortality **20-30%** even with treatment; survivors may need amputation and reconstruction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer the same day**: suspected **necrotising fasciitis or STSS**, quinsy, stridor or drooling, inability to swallow, ARF with heart failure, PSGN with hypertension, oliguria or encephalopathy. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Refer for echo** any child with a **new murmur, ARF, chorea or recurrent sore throats with joint pains**; echo detects carditis missed by auscultation. [WHO 2024]`,`**RHD register**: every patient on secondary prophylaxis should be entered in a **local register** with the due date of each injection; register-based programmes raise adherence above 80%. [WHO 2024]`,`**Follow-up on prophylaxis**: injection every **21 days (3 weeks)**, clinical review and **echo every 1-2 years** (more often with moderate or severe disease), dental review every 6 months. [WHO 2024; CSI 2023]`,`**PSGN follow-up**: blood pressure and urine at 2, 6 and 12 weeks; **C3 should normalise by 8 weeks** and microscopic haematuria may persist up to a year; persistent low C3 means **C3 glomerulopathy or MPGN** - refer for biopsy. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**After iGAS**: report to public health; **household contacts at high risk** (age 75 or more, pregnancy from 37 weeks or within 28 days of delivery, neonates and mother-baby pairs) should be offered chemoprophylaxis. [UKHSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Primary prevention of ARF** = prompt **10-day penicillin or amoxicillin** (or single BPG) for GAS pharyngitis in children and adolescents. [WHO 2024]`,`**Secondary prophylaxis**: **benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million U IM every 3 weeks** in India (every 4 weeks in low-risk settings); alternatives are **penicillin V 250 mg twice daily** or **erythromycin 250 mg twice daily** orally, which are less effective. [AHA 2009; WHO 2024]`,`**Duration (AHA)**: ARF without carditis - **5 years or until age 21**, whichever is longer; carditis without residual valve disease - **10 years or until 21**; carditis with residual valve disease - **10 years or until 40, sometimes lifelong**. [AHA 2009]`,`**Indian Working Group / IAP** recommends **no carditis - 5 years or until 18**, **mild carditis - 10 years or until 25**, and **moderate-severe RHD or after valve surgery - lifelong**. [IAP 2008]`,`**Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK)** screens children 0-18 years for **4Ds**, which include **rheumatic heart disease**, with referral to District Early Intervention Centres. [MoHFW RBSK]`,`**Social measures**: less overcrowding, better housing, school health checks and hygiene reduce GAS transmission; there is **no licensed GAS vaccine** yet (M-protein and conserved-antigen candidates are in trials). [WHO 2024]`,`**Counselling**: explain why the full 10 days is needed (eradication, not symptom relief), that BPG injections are painful but life-saving, and that **lidocaine 1% can be mixed** with BPG to reduce pain. [WHO 2024]`,`**Stewardship**: most sore throats are viral - scoring rules plus **delayed (back-up) prescriptions** cut antibiotic use without increasing complications (see AMR and stewardship topic). [NICE NG84 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Guideline on the prevention and diagnosis of rheumatic fever and RHD (2024)**: endorses the **2015 revised Jones criteria**, use of **echocardiography (including handheld) for diagnosis**, **penicillin for GAS pharyngitis** in children in high-burden settings, and **BPG secondary prophylaxis** including for **echo-detected latent RHD**. [WHO 2024]`,`**GOAL trial (NEJM 2022)**: in Ugandan children with **latent (echo-detected) RHD**, 4-weekly BPG reduced progression at 2 years to **0.8% versus 8.2%** without prophylaxis - the basis for prophylaxis in screen-detected disease. [GOAL 2022]`,`**World Heart Federation 2023 echo criteria** update the 2012 criteria and introduce **staging of RHD (stages A to D)** to guide prophylaxis and follow-up after screening. [WHF 2023]`,`**UKHSA iGAS guidance (2023)** after the 2022-2023 surge lowered the threshold for **antibiotic treatment of scarlet fever and sore throat in children** during outbreaks and refined **contact chemoprophylaxis**. [UKHSA 2023]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign (2021)** and IDSA SSTI guidance continue to recommend **clindamycin with penicillin** for STSS; linezolid is accepted when clindamycin resistance is present. [SSC 2021]`,`**NICE NG84** (sore throat) uses **FeverPAIN or Centor** with **5-10 days of penicillin V** (5 days in low-risk UK practice); in India, with higher ARF risk, the **10-day course** remains standard - a deliberate difference. [NICE NG84 2018; ICMR TG 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: revised Jones criteria (2015) with low- versus high-risk differences; secondary prophylaxis schedules; Centor/McIsaac; STSS criteria; PSGN versus IgA nephropathy. [AHA 2015]`,`**Why clindamycin in STSS?** - it inhibits protein (toxin) synthesis, is unaffected by the **Eagle effect**, and suppresses M-protein production. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why does penicillin prevent ARF but not PSGN?** - ARF needs ongoing pharyngeal antigen exposure over weeks (eradication within 9 days prevents it), while nephritogenic immune complexes form early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: ordering **ASO for an acute sore throat** - it reflects past infection and does not guide pharyngitis treatment. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping prophylaxis at 5 years in a patient with **residual mitral regurgitation**; the duration is driven by **carditis and residual disease**, not just time. [AHA 2009]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing **amoxicillin to a teenager with exudative tonsillitis and splenomegaly** - EBV, with a near-certain rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spot diagnosis**: strawberry tongue, Pastia lines and circumoral pallor = **scarlet fever**; pain out of proportion = **necrotising fasciitis**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for streptococcal infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin`,`50 mg/kg once daily (max 1 g) or 25 mg/kg twice daily (max 500 mg) oral`,`10 days for pharyngitis`,`Rash (avoid in EBV), diarrhoea; penicillin allergy`],[`Phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V)`,`Child 250 mg BD-TDS; adult 250 mg QID or 500 mg BD oral`,`10 days (pharyngitis); 250 mg BD long term (prophylaxis)`,`Poor adherence QID; allergy`],[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`600,000 U IM (under 27 kg); 1.2 million U IM (27 kg or more)`,`Single dose (treatment); every 3 weeks (secondary prophylaxis)`,`Painful injection; anaphylaxis about 0.2%; vasovagal collapse in severe valve disease; never IV`],[`Cephalexin / cefadroxil`,`Cephalexin 20 mg/kg BD (max 500 mg); cefadroxil 30 mg/kg OD (max 1 g) oral`,`10 days`,`Avoid if immediate-type (anaphylactic) penicillin allergy`],[`Clindamycin`,`Oral 7 mg/kg TDS (max 300 mg); IV 900 mg every 8 h in STSS`,`10 days oral; IV until stable in STSS`,`C. difficile colitis; inducible resistance - D-test`],[`Azithromycin`,`12 mg/kg once daily (max 500 mg) oral`,`5 days`,`QT prolongation; variable GAS macrolide resistance`],[`Benzylpenicillin (penicillin G)`,`4 million U (2.4 g) IV every 4 h`,`Per source control, usually 10-14 days for iGAS`,`Seizures at very high doses in renal failure; hypersensitivity`],[`Aspirin (ARF)`,`50-60 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses, up to 80-100 mg/kg/day oral`,`2-4 weeks then taper over 2-4 weeks`,`Tinnitus, gastritis, Reye syndrome with influenza or varicella`],[`Prednisolone (severe carditis)`,`1-2 mg/kg/day oral (max 80 mg)`,`2-3 weeks then taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, fluid retention; no proven long-term valve benefit`]]},{heading:`Revised Jones criteria (AHA 2015) - low-risk versus moderate/high-risk populations`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Low-risk population`,`Moderate/high-risk population (India)`],rows:[[`Carditis (major)`,`Clinical and/or subclinical (echo)`,`Clinical and/or subclinical (echo)`],[`Arthritis (major)`,`Polyarthritis only`,`Monoarthritis, polyarthritis or polyarthralgia`],[`Chorea, erythema marginatum, nodules (major)`,`Major`,`Major`],[`Joint symptoms (minor)`,`Polyarthralgia`,`Monoarthralgia`],[`Fever (minor)`,`38.5 C or more`,`38.0 C or more`],[`Acute phase reactants (minor)`,`ESR 60 mm/h or more and/or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more`,`ESR 30 mm/h or more and/or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more`],[`ECG (minor)`,`Prolonged PR for age (unless carditis counted)`,`Prolonged PR for age (unless carditis counted)`],[`Diagnosis`,`Initial: 2 major or 1 major + 2 minor; recurrent: same`,`Initial: 2 major or 1 major + 2 minor; recurrent: also 3 minor`]]},{heading:`Centor/McIsaac score for sore throat`,columns:[`Feature or score`,`Points`,`Action`],rows:[[`Tonsillar exudate or swelling`,`+1`,`-`],[`Tender or swollen anterior cervical nodes`,`+1`,`-`],[`Temperature over 38 C`,`+1`,`-`],[`Absence of cough`,`+1`,`-`],[`Age 3-14 / 15-44 / 45 or more`,`+1 / 0 / -1`,`-`],[`Total 0-1`,`GAS risk about 1-10%`,`No test, no antibiotic; symptomatic care`],[`Total 2-3`,`GAS risk about 11-35%`,`RADT or culture; treat if positive`],[`Total 4-5`,`GAS risk about 51-53%`,`Test; treat empirically if tests unavailable (India)`]]},{heading:`Sore throat - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating features`,`Key test`,`Management point`],rows:[[`GAS pharyngitis`,`Exudate, tender anterior nodes, fever, no cough, age 5-15`,`RADT or culture`,`10 days penicillin/amoxicillin`],[`Viral pharyngitis`,`Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, hoarseness`,`None`,`Symptomatic only`],[`Infectious mononucleosis`,`Posterior nodes, splenomegaly, fatigue, atypical lymphocytes`,`Heterophile/EBV VCA IgM`,`Avoid amoxicillin; avoid contact sport`],[`Diphtheria`,`Grey adherent membrane, bull neck, unvaccinated`,`Throat swab culture, toxin test`,`Antitoxin plus penicillin/erythromycin; notify`],[`Peritonsillar abscess`,`Trismus, hot-potato voice, uvula deviated`,`Clinical; USG/CT`,`Needle aspiration or incision plus antibiotics`],[`Lemierre syndrome`,`Neck pain and swelling, rigors, septic lung emboli`,`Blood culture (Fusobacterium), CT neck`,`IV beta-lactam plus metronidazole`],[`Acute HIV / gonococcal pharyngitis`,`Rash, ulcers, sexual history`,`HIV antigen-antibody test, NAAT`,`Link to ART or ceftriaxone`]]},{heading:`Post-streptococcal sequelae compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Acute rheumatic fever`,`PSGN`,`Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis`],rows:[[`Preceding infection`,`Pharyngitis only`,`Pharyngitis or skin`,`Pharyngitis`],[`Latent period`,`About 3 weeks (chorea 1-6 months)`,`1-2 weeks (throat); 3-6 weeks (skin)`,`Under 10 days`],[`Mechanism`,`Molecular mimicry (M protein - myosin)`,`Immune complexes, nephritogenic strains`,`Immune-mediated, unclear`],[`Key labs`,`ESR/CRP, ASO, echo`,`Low C3, normal C4, RBC casts`,`Raised ASO, normal echo`],[`Antibiotic prevents it?`,`Yes, if started within 9 days`,`No`,`Uncertain`],[`Prophylaxis`,`BPG every 3 weeks, years to lifelong`,`Not needed`,`Up to 1 year (AHA), echo follow-up`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`When to use`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`RADT`,`Centor/McIsaac 2 or more`,`Specificity over 95%, sensitivity 70-90%; negative in child - culture`],[`Throat culture`,`Reference standard; back-up to negative RADT in children`,`Sensitivity 90-95%; carriers 5-20% of children`],[`ASO titre`,`Suspected ARF or PSGN`,`Rise from 1 week, peak 3-6 weeks; age/region ULN or paired rise`],[`Anti-DNase B`,`Post-skin infection; ARF with normal ASO`,`Peak 6-8 weeks; stays raised for months`],[`Echocardiography`,`Every suspected ARF; chorea; screening`,`Pathological MR/AR counts as carditis (WHF 2023 criteria)`],[`C3 / C4`,`Suspected PSGN`,`Low C3, normal C4; C3 normal by 8 weeks`],[`Blood culture, CK, lactate`,`iGAS / necrotising fasciitis / STSS`,`Positive in about 60% of STSS; raised CK suggests deep tissue`]]}],redFlags:[`Pain out of proportion to skin findings, bullae, anaesthetic or dusky skin - necrotising fasciitis`,`Hypotension with rash, rising creatinine or coagulopathy after a sore throat, wound, varicella or delivery - STSS`,`Trismus, drooling, stridor, inability to swallow or neck swelling - deep neck abscess or Lemierre syndrome`,`New murmur, tachycardia out of proportion to fever or heart failure in a child with joint pain - rheumatic carditis`,`Cola-coloured urine with hypertension, oliguria or headache and seizures - PSGN with encephalopathy`,`Chickenpox with new high fever after day 3-4 or spreading painful erythema - invasive GAS`,`Pregnant woman with known RHD and breathlessness - decompensated mitral stenosis`],pearls:[`**GAS is universally penicillin-sensitive** - no clinically significant penicillin resistance has ever been documented.`,`**10 days, not 5**: the full course is for **eradication to prevent ARF**, not for symptom relief.`,`Antibiotics started **within 9 days** of a GAS sore throat prevent ARF but **do not prevent PSGN**.`,`**ARF licks the joints and bites the heart** (Lasegue) - arthritis heals completely, carditis scars.`,`Jones major criteria mnemonic **J-O-N-E-S**: Joints, O (heart shape - carditis), Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea.`,`**India = moderate-high risk**: monoarthritis and polyarthralgia count as major, fever 38 C and ESR 30 count as minor.`,`**Chorea alone is enough** for ARF diagnosis - and still needs echo and prophylaxis.`,`**Clindamycin plus penicillin** in STSS - the Eagle effect and toxin suppression.`,`**PSGN**: low C3, normal C4, normalises within **8 weeks**; IgA nephropathy is synpharyngitic with normal C3.`,`**BPG every 3 weeks** in India; skip the skin test, inject lying down, observe 15-30 minutes.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-q1`,stem:`A 9-year-old girl in Lucknow has sudden sore throat and fever of 38.6 C. She has tonsillar exudate and tender anterior cervical nodes, and no cough. No rapid test is available. What is the best management?`,options:[`Symptomatic treatment only, since most sore throats are viral`,`Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg once daily for 10 days`,`Azithromycin 12 mg/kg once daily for 5 days as first choice`,`Send ASO titre and treat if raised`],answer:1,explanation:`Her Centor score is 4 plus the McIsaac age point (5): GAS is likely, and in a moderate-high ARF-risk country without a rapid test empirical penicillin or amoxicillin for 10 days is right. Symptomatic care alone fits low scores; azithromycin is reserved for true penicillin allergy because of rising macrolide resistance; ASO reflects infection weeks ago and has no role in acute pharyngitis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-q2`,stem:`Under the 2015 revised Jones criteria applied to a moderate-to-high risk population such as India, which of the following counts as a MAJOR criterion?`,options:[`Prolonged PR interval on ECG`,`Fever of 38.0 C`,`ESR of 35 mm/h`,`Polyarthralgia`],answer:3,explanation:`In moderate-high risk populations the 2015 criteria widen the arthritis major criterion to include monoarthritis and polyarthralgia (in low-risk settings only polyarthritis is major and polyarthralgia is minor). A prolonged PR, fever of 38 C or more and ESR of 30 mm/h or more are minor criteria in high-risk settings, not major ones.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman develops severe thigh pain, hypotension and rising creatinine 2 days after a minor abrasion. Blood cultures grow beta-haemolytic group A streptococci. Why is clindamycin added to high-dose penicillin?`,options:[`It suppresses toxin synthesis and is not affected by the inoculum (Eagle) effect`,`Group A streptococci are frequently resistant to penicillin`,`It provides synergistic cell-wall killing with penicillin`,`It prevents post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis`],answer:0,explanation:`In streptococcal toxic shock and necrotising fasciitis, clindamycin inhibits ribosomal synthesis of superantigen toxins and M protein, and works on large stationary-phase inocula where penicillin loses activity (Eagle effect). GAS has never shown clinical penicillin resistance; clindamycin is bacteriostatic and not a cell-wall synergist; nothing reliably prevents PSGN.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-q4`,stem:`A 7-year-old boy presents with cola-coloured urine and periorbital puffiness. He had impetigo 4 weeks ago. Blood pressure is 130/90 mmHg. Which laboratory pattern is expected?`,options:[`Normal C3, normal C4, raised serum IgA`,`Low C3, low C4, positive anti-dsDNA`,`Low C3, normal C4, raised anti-DNase B`,`Normal C3, low C4, raised ASO`],answer:2,explanation:`Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis after skin infection activates the alternative pathway, so C3 is low with normal C4, and anti-DNase B is the better antibody after pyoderma because skin lipids blunt the ASO response. Normal complements with IgA fits IgA nephropathy; low C3 and C4 with anti-dsDNA fits lupus nephritis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-q5`,stem:`A 14-year-old had acute rheumatic fever with carditis; echo now shows moderate mitral regurgitation. Using AHA recommendations, how long should benzathine penicillin prophylaxis continue?`,options:[`5 years or until age 21, whichever is longer`,`10 years or until age 21, whichever is longer`,`Only until the ESR normalises`,`10 years or until age 40, whichever is longer, sometimes lifelong`],answer:3,explanation:`Carditis with residual valve disease needs prophylaxis for 10 years after the last attack or until age 40, whichever is longer, and sometimes lifelong. Five years or until 21 applies to ARF without carditis; 10 years or until 21 applies to carditis without residual disease; acute phase reactants do not decide prophylaxis duration.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever using the revised Jones criteria. Outline its management and the primary and secondary prevention of rheumatic heart disease in India.`,openingLines:[`Acute rheumatic fever is a non-suppurative, immune-mediated sequel of group A streptococcal pharyngitis, occurring about 3 weeks after infection through molecular mimicry between streptococcal M protein and cardiac antigens.`,`It is diagnosed by the 2015 AHA revised Jones criteria - India being a moderate-to-high risk population - and its importance lies in rheumatic heart disease, which is preventable by penicillin.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis in brief`,points:[`GAS pharyngitis, latent period 1-5 weeks; molecular mimicry of M protein and N-acetylglucosamine with myosin, laminin, valve.`,`Pancarditis, Aschoff bodies; mitral then aortic valve.`]},{heading:`Revised Jones criteria 2015`,points:[`Low- versus moderate/high-risk population definitions.`,`Major and minor criteria table; evidence of preceding GAS (ASO, anti-DNase B, culture, RADT).`,`Initial versus recurrent ARF; chorea and indolent carditis exceptions; subclinical carditis on echo.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ESR, CRP, ASO/anti-DNase B, throat culture, ECG for PR, chest X-ray, echocardiography in every case.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Bed rest during active carditis; eradication dose BPG 1.2 million U IM.`,`Aspirin 50-60 mg/kg/day (up to 80-100) or naproxen for arthritis; prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg for severe carditis with failure.`,`Heart failure treatment; chorea - valproate or carbamazepine.`]},{heading:`Primary prevention`,points:[`Centor/McIsaac scoring; 10-day amoxicillin or penicillin V, or single BPG; within 9 days of sore throat.`]},{heading:`Secondary prevention`,points:[`BPG 1.2 million U IM every 3 weeks; oral alternatives.`,`Duration table (AHA versus Indian Working Group); RHD register; WHO 2024 and GOAL trial for latent RHD.`,`RBSK school screening; housing and access to care.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of revised Jones criteria (low-risk versus high-risk columns)`,`Flow chart: sore throat - Centor/McIsaac - test/treat - ARF - secondary prophylaxis`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:1},{part:`Jones criteria with table`,marks:3},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Primary and secondary prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Jones criteria 2015`,`molecular mimicry`,`benzathine penicillin`,`secondary prophylaxis`,`RBSK`,`GOAL trial`]},{id:`infectious-fever-streptococcal-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on streptococcal toxic shock syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome is shock with multi-organ failure caused by invasive group A streptococcal infection, mediated by superantigen exotoxins (SpeA, SpeC).`,`It is defined (CDC 2010) by isolation of GAS with hypotension and two or more organ dysfunctions, and carries 30-60% mortality.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Superantigens bind MHC II and TCR V-beta outside the groove, polyclonal T-cell activation, cytokine storm, capillary leak.`]},{heading:`Clinical and criteria`,points:[`Portal: skin, necrotising fasciitis, varicella, postpartum; pain out of proportion.`,`CDC criteria: hypotension plus 2 of renal, coagulopathy, liver, ARDS, desquamating rash, soft-tissue necrosis.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Resuscitation per sepsis bundle; urgent surgical debridement.`,`Benzylpenicillin 4 MU IV 4-hourly plus clindamycin 900 mg IV 8-hourly; linezolid if clindamycin-resistant; IVIG may be considered.`,`Contact chemoprophylaxis for high-risk household contacts; notify.`]}],mustDraw:[`Box of CDC STSS criteria`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Criteria and clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`superantigen`,`Eagle effect`,`clindamycin`,`IVIG`,`necrotising fasciitis`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Streptococcal infections; Acute rheumatic fever`,`Shulman ST et al. Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis: IDSA, 2012 update`,`Gewitz MH et al. Revision of the Jones Criteria for the Diagnosis of Acute Rheumatic Fever in the Era of Doppler Echocardiography: AHA Scientific Statement, 2015`,`WHO Guideline on the prevention and diagnosis of rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease, 2024`,`Rwebembera J et al. 2023 World Heart Federation guidelines for the echocardiographic diagnosis of rheumatic heart disease, 2023`,`Stevens DL et al. Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Skin and Soft Tissue Infections: IDSA, 2014`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`UK Health Security Agency. Guidelines for the public health management of invasive group A streptococcal infections, 2023`]});var m=[],h=[];h.push({id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal`,title:`Seasonal and avian influenza: oseltamivir, vaccination, H1N1 in India`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Influenza** is an acute febrile respiratory illness caused by the segmented RNA **influenza A and B viruses**, spreading in **seasonal epidemics through antigenic drift** and in **pandemics through antigenic shift**; in India it is managed by the **MoHFW Category A/B/C triage**, in which **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days** is started without waiting for a test in Category B (high-risk) and C (severe) patients, and prevented by **annual vaccination with the Southern Hemisphere formulation before the monsoon**.`,keywords:[`flu`,`swine flu`,`H1N1pdm09`,`H3N2`,`influenza B Victoria`,`avian influenza`,`bird flu`,`H5N1`,`H7N9`,`H9N2`,`oseltamivir`,`Tamiflu`,`zanamivir`,`peramivir`,`baloxavir marboxil`,`MoHFW categorisation A B C`,`influenza-like illness ILI`,`SARI`,`antigenic drift and shift`,`trivalent influenza vaccine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Influenza** is an acute respiratory infection by **influenza A, B (and rarely C)** orthomyxoviruses; types A and B cause seasonal epidemics, only **type A causes pandemics** because it has an animal reservoir. [Harrison 22e]`,`**WHO estimate**: about **1 billion cases a year**, **3-5 million severe cases** and **290,000-650,000 respiratory deaths** worldwide, most in adults over 65 and in low-income countries. [WHO 2025]`,`**Influenza-like illness (ILI)**, WHO surveillance definition: acute respiratory infection with **measured fever of 38 C or more and cough, onset within the last 10 days**; **SARI** is the same with the need for hospitalisation. [WHO 2014]`,`**India has two seasonal patterns**: north India peaks in **winter (January-March)** and again in the monsoon, while south and coastal India peak in the **monsoon (June-September)** - so a single winter-only campaign does not fit. [NCDC 2023]`,`The **2009 H1N1 pandemic** virus (A/H1N1pdm09) is now a **seasonal strain**; India's worst post-pandemic year was **2015, with about 42,600 laboratory-confirmed cases and nearly 3,000 deaths**, and 2019 recorded about 28,800 cases and 1,200 deaths. [IDSP/NCDC]`,`**2023 H3N2 surge**: India saw a large **A/H3N2-dominated wave in February-March 2023** with prolonged cough, prompting an ICMR advisory against indiscriminate antibiotic use (azithromycin, amoxiclav). [ICMR 2023]`,`Influenza surveillance in India runs through **IDSP/IHIP** (ILI and SARI reporting) and the **ICMR VRDL network with NIV Pune** as the WHO National Influenza Centre feeding the **WHO GISRS**. [NCDC 2023]`,`**Avian influenza H5N1**: since 2003 WHO has recorded **about 990 human cases with a case-fatality near 48%**; India reported its **first human H5N1 death in 2021** (a 12-year-old from Haryana) and a further child death in Andhra Pradesh in 2025. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, virology and risk factors`,points:[`Influenza A is subtyped by surface **haemagglutinin (H1-H18) and neuraminidase (N1-N11)**; current human seasonal strains are **A/H1N1pdm09, A/H3N2 and B/Victoria** - **B/Yamagata has not been detected since March 2020**. [WHO 2024]`,`The genome has **8 RNA segments**, which allows **reassortment** when two viruses infect one cell (pig as the 'mixing vessel') - the basis of antigenic shift. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antigenic drift**: point mutations in HA/NA from error-prone RNA polymerase - needs **annual vaccine update**; **antigenic shift**: a new HA (with or without NA) entering humans - the cause of the **1918, 1957, 1968 and 2009 pandemics**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transmission** is by droplets, aerosols and fomites; **incubation 1-4 days (mean 2)**; adults shed virus from **1 day before to 5-7 days after onset**, children and the immunocompromised for longer. [CDC 2024]`,`**High-risk groups (MoHFW)**: **children under 5, adults 65 and over, pregnant women**, and people with chronic lung, heart, liver, kidney, blood, neurological or metabolic disease (diabetes), cancer, HIV/AIDS or long-term steroids. [MoHFW]`,`Other high-risk groups in IDSA/CDC lists: **morbid obesity (BMI 40 or more)**, residents of long-term care, children on long-term aspirin (Reye risk), up to **2 weeks postpartum**, and indigenous populations. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Avian influenza exposure risk**: handling or culling sick or dead poultry, live-bird markets, backyard slaughter, raw poultry products, and since 2024 **raw milk and dairy cattle** (H5N1 clade 2.3.4.4b in US herds). [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**HA binds sialic acid** on respiratory epithelium: human viruses prefer **alpha-2,6 linkages (upper airway)**, avian viruses **alpha-2,3 linkages (alveoli, lower airway)** - so H5N1 causes deep pneumonia but transmits poorly between people. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid** to release new virions from the cell and through mucus - **oseltamivir, zanamivir and peramivir block this**, trapping virus on the cell surface. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Baloxavir** blocks the **cap-dependent endonuclease (PA subunit)** that 'snatches' host mRNA caps, stopping viral transcription - viral load falls faster than with oseltamivir. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **M2 ion channel** allows uncoating; **amantadine and rimantadine** block it, but nearly all circulating A strains are resistant and B lacks M2 - **adamantanes are not used**. [CDC 2024]`,`**Cytokine release** (IL-6, TNF, interferons) from infected epithelium and macrophages explains the abrupt **fever, myalgia and prostration**; H5N1 provokes an exaggerated **'cytokine storm'** with ARDS and multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ciliated epithelial necrosis** and loss of mucociliary clearance, plus increased bacterial adherence, set up **secondary bacterial pneumonia** by **S. pneumoniae, S. aureus (including MRSA) and group A streptococcus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Systemic **inflammation and platelet activation** explain a **6-fold rise in acute MI in the first week** after influenza, and the cardiovascular benefit of vaccination. [NEJM 2018]`]},{heading:`Classification: MoHFW categorisation and severity`,points:[`**Category A**: mild fever plus cough or sore throat, with or without body ache, headache, vomiting or diarrhoea - **no oseltamivir, no test**; home isolation, symptomatic care and **review in 24-48 hours**. [MoHFW]`,`**Category B(i)**: Category A plus **high-grade fever and severe sore throat** - may need **oseltamivir at home** on clinical judgement; no test needed. [MoHFW]`,`**Category B(ii)**: Category A plus **one or more high-risk conditions** (under 5, 65 and over, pregnancy, chronic lung/heart/liver/kidney/blood/neurological disease, diabetes, cancer, HIV, long-term steroids) - **start oseltamivir without testing**; home isolation. [MoHFW]`,`**Category C**: Category A or B plus **breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, fall in blood pressure, blood-stained sputum or cyanosis**, or **worsening of an underlying chronic illness** - **test, admit and start oseltamivir immediately**. [MoHFW]`,`**Category C red flags in children**: somnolence, high and persistent fever, **inability to feed well**, convulsions, shortness of breath or difficulty breathing. [MoHFW]`,`**WHO 2024 severity tiers** used in its guideline: **non-severe**, **severe** (needs hospital care - hypoxia, pneumonia) and **critical** (ARDS, sepsis, ventilation) - antiviral advice differs by tier and by risk group. [WHO 2024]`,`Testing in Category C, cluster investigations and SARI surveillance only - **testing every Category A case wastes VRDL capacity and delays nothing useful**. [MoHFW]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Abrupt onset** of fever (38-40 C), chills, headache, **severe myalgia**, malaise and dry cough - patients can often name the hour it started. [Harrison 22e]`,`Fever lasts **about 3 days (range 1-5)**; respiratory symptoms (cough, sore throat, rhinorrhoea) become prominent as fever settles; **cough and malaise can persist for 2 weeks or more**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children** have higher fever, more GI symptoms (vomiting, diarrhoea), otitis media, croup and **febrile seizures**; infants may present as **sepsis-like illness** or apnoea. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Older adults** may have **no fever**, presenting with confusion, falls, anorexia or decompensated heart failure or COPD. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Influenza B myositis**: in school-age children, **bilateral calf pain and refusal to walk** as the fever settles, with high CK; it resolves in days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biphasic course** - improvement then return of fever, productive cough and breathlessness at day 4-7 - suggests **secondary bacterial pneumonia**. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Avian H5N1** history: poultry or cattle exposure within **7-10 days**, high fever, early lower respiratory symptoms, **diarrhoea, vomiting and abdominal pain** are common; conjunctivitis dominates the US dairy-worker H5N1 cases. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Flushed face, hot moist skin**, injected conjunctivae, mild cervical lymphadenopathy and a hyperaemic pharynx without exudate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory rate and SpO2** are the vital signs that triage: **RR over 24/min or SpO2 under 94% in room air** means Category C. [WHO 2024]`,`**Chest examination is usually clear** in uncomplicated influenza; **bilateral crackles** with hypoxia suggest primary viral pneumonia, **focal consolidation** suggests secondary bacterial pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative bradycardia is not a feature** - tachycardia proportional to fever; a disproportionate tachycardia or hypotension prompts a search for **myocarditis or sepsis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calf tenderness and dark urine** point to myositis or **rhabdomyolysis**; check CK and urine myoglobin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Altered sensorium** in a child or young adult should prompt consideration of **influenza-associated encephalopathy** (acute necrotising encephalopathy in children). [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**COVID-19**: indistinguishable clinically; anosmia and longer incubation (about 3-5 days) favour SARS-CoV-2 - **multiplex PCR** settles it (see the COVID-19 in 2025-2026 topic). [IDSA 2024]`,`**RSV and hMPV** in adults: more wheeze and rhinorrhoea, slower onset, older or cardiopulmonary patients (see the RSV and other respiratory viruses topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dengue**: fever, myalgia and headache with **retro-orbital pain, leucopenia and falling platelets** but little cough - test NS1 in the first 5 days (see the Dengue topic). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Leptospirosis**: myalgia with **calf tenderness, conjunctival suffusion**, jaundice, oliguria and haemoptysis after flood exposure (see the Leptospirosis topic). [ICMR STW]`,`**Malaria and scrub typhus**: rule out with RDT/smear and an eschar hunt in the monsoon - the same season as south Indian influenza. [ICMR STW]`,`**Streptococcal pharyngitis**: exudate, tender anterior nodes and **no cough** (Centor criteria) - antibiotics for GAS, not for influenza. [IDSA 2012]`,`**Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia**: productive purulent sputum, focal signs, **high procalcitonin and neutrophilia** - may coexist with influenza. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**No test is needed for Category A and B** - the diagnosis is clinical during the season and treatment must not wait for a report. [MoHFW]`,`**RT-PCR on a nasopharyngeal plus throat swab** in viral transport medium, kept at **2-8 C and reaching the lab within 48 hours**, is the reference test; lower respiratory samples (endotracheal aspirate, BAL) are better in ventilated patients. [ICMR/NIV]`,`**Rapid antigen detection tests** have sensitivity of only **50-70%** (specificity over 90%) - a negative result does not exclude influenza; **rapid molecular assays** (sensitivity over 90%) are preferred where available. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Multiplex PCR panels** (influenza A/B, SARS-CoV-2, RSV, hMPV, adenovirus) help in admitted patients, the immunocompromised and outbreak work. [IDSA 2018]`,`**CBC**: normal or low WBC with **lymphopenia**; **neutrophilia with left shift** suggests bacterial superinfection; mild thrombocytopenia occurs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray** for all Category C: bilateral interstitial or patchy infiltrates (viral) versus lobar consolidation (bacterial); **ABG, creatinine, LFT, CK and troponin** in severe disease. [WHO 2024]`,`**Procalcitonin under 0.25 ng/mL** argues against bacterial co-infection and supports withholding or stopping antibiotics; send **blood and sputum cultures** before antibiotics when pneumonia is present. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Suspected avian influenza**: notify the **district surveillance officer (IDSP)** and send samples to **NIV Pune or a designated VRDL** with full biosafety - subtyping for H5/H7/H9 is done only there. [NCDC]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Category A**: rest, oral fluids, **paracetamol 500-650 mg every 6 hours** (maximum 3-4 g/day), home isolation for **7 days or 24 hours after fever ends**; no antiviral, no antibiotic. [MoHFW]`,`**Never give aspirin to children and adolescents** with influenza - risk of **Reye syndrome** (hepatic failure with encephalopathy). [IAP 2023]`,`**Oseltamivir adult dose: 75 mg orally twice daily for 5 days**; benefit is greatest within **48 hours of onset**, but in high-risk, pregnant and hospitalised patients start it **whenever they present**. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Paediatric oseltamivir (1 year and over)**: **15 kg or less 30 mg BD; over 15-23 kg 45 mg BD; over 23-40 kg 60 mg BD; over 40 kg 75 mg BD** for 5 days; **infants under 1 year 3 mg/kg BD**. [CDC 2024]`,`**Renal adjustment of oseltamivir**: CrCl over 30-60 mL/min **30 mg BD**; CrCl over 10-30 **30 mg once daily**; on haemodialysis **30 mg after each session**; on CAPD 30 mg once a week. [CDC 2024]`,`Oseltamivir shortens illness by **about 1 day in healthy adults**, and observational data show lower mortality in hospitalised patients; nausea and vomiting are reduced by **taking it with food**. [Cochrane 2014; IDSA 2018]`,`**Zanamivir 10 mg (two 5 mg inhalations) twice daily for 5 days** is the alternative for age 7 and over - **avoid in asthma and COPD** (bronchospasm). [CDC 2024]`,`**Baloxavir marboxil**: single oral dose **40 mg (40-79 kg) or 80 mg (80 kg or more)** within 48 hours, from age 5; WHO 2024 prefers it for **non-severe disease in high-risk patients**, but it is not widely available in India and not advised in pregnancy. [WHO 2024]`,`**Antibiotics are not indicated** for uncomplicated influenza; prescribe them only for evidence of **otitis, sinusitis or pneumonia** - give amoxicillin or amoxiclav with cover for S. aureus when post-influenza pneumonia is suspected. [ICMR 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit Category C**: breathlessness, **SpO2 under 94%**, RR over 30, hypotension, altered sensorium, haemoptysis, or decompensated comorbidity; use a **CURB-65 or qSOFA** score when pneumonia is present. [MoHFW; WHO 2024]`,`**Isolate** in a single room or cohort with **droplet precautions** (surgical mask, gloves, gown); use an **N95 respirator for aerosol-generating procedures** (intubation, suction, nebulisation, NIV). [MoHFW]`,`**Oseltamivir 75 mg BD as early as possible**, by nasogastric tube if needed; continue **beyond 5 days (often 10)** in critical illness or immunosuppression with ongoing shedding. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Double-dose oseltamivir (150 mg BD) is not recommended** - RCTs showed no added benefit; **IV peramivir 600 mg once** (single dose; used daily in severe disease) or IV zanamivir is used where enteral absorption fails. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Oxygen** to keep **SpO2 92-96%** (88-92% in hypercapnic COPD); **HFNC or NIV** with caution; lung-protective ventilation (**tidal volume 6 mL/kg PBW, plateau under 30 cm H2O**) and **prone positioning** for ARDS; ECMO at tertiary centres. [WHO 2024]`,`**Do not give corticosteroids for influenza pneumonia** - observational data show higher mortality and prolonged shedding; use them only for another indication (COPD or asthma exacerbation, refractory septic shock). [WHO 2024; IDSA 2018]`,`**Empirical antibiotics for pneumonia** (ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus azithromycin 500 mg daily), adding **vancomycin or linezolid for MRSA** in necrotising or cavitating pneumonia after influenza. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Conservative fluid strategy** after initial resuscitation, VTE prophylaxis, and monitoring of **creatinine, CK, troponin and glucose**. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: higher risk of severe disease (especially third trimester) and preterm birth; **treat with oseltamivir 75 mg BD at once** whatever the trimester or time since onset - it is the preferred antiviral. [ACOG 2024; MoHFW]`,`**Pregnancy vaccination**: inactivated vaccine is **safe in any trimester**, and maternal antibody protects the infant for the **first 6 months** when no infant vaccine can be given. [WHO 2022]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, fever may be absent; outbreaks in old-age homes need **oseltamivir prophylaxis 75 mg once daily for all residents** for at least **2 weeks and 7 days after the last case**. [IDSA 2018]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: dose-adjust oseltamivir (see above); the vaccine is strongly recommended and these patients may respond poorly. [CDC 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: higher hospitalisation and DKA risk; monitor glucose closely during illness ('sick day rules') and vaccinate yearly. [ADA 2025]`,`**Immunocompromised** (transplant, chemotherapy, HIV with low CD4): prolonged shedding, **resistance emergence (H275Y)**, longer courses and repeat PCR to guide stopping. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Children under 5** (especially under 2): treat with weight-based oseltamivir; **IAP recommends annual influenza vaccination from 6 months to 5 years**, and beyond for high-risk children. [IAP 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Primary influenza viral pneumonia**: rapidly progressive bilateral pneumonia with hypoxia within days of onset - the leading cause of H1N1pdm09 and H5N1 deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary bacterial pneumonia** (S. pneumoniae, **S. aureus including MRSA**, H. influenzae, GAS) - the classic killer of the elderly; **staphylococcal necrotising pneumonia** may kill young adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac**: myocarditis, pericarditis, **acute MI (6-fold risk in week 1)** and decompensated heart failure. [NEJM 2018]`,`**Neurological**: febrile seizures, encephalopathy, **acute necrotising encephalopathy**, transverse myelitis and **Guillain-Barre syndrome** (risk much higher after infection than after vaccine). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Musculoskeletal and renal**: myositis, **rhabdomyolysis with AKI**; **Reye syndrome** with aspirin use (now rare). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exacerbation of chronic disease**: COPD and asthma flares, heart failure, diabetic decompensation - often the actual cause of death in the elderly. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Prognosis**: uncomplicated influenza resolves in about a week; H1N1pdm09 mortality in hospitalised Indian patients was high (10-20% in tertiary series); **H5N1 case-fatality near 50%**. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Review Category A and B at 24-48 hours** (in person or by phone); any Category C feature means **same-day referral** to a facility with oxygen and isolation beds. [MoHFW]`,`Teach the family the **danger signs**: breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, blood in sputum, bluish lips, and in children inability to feed or convulsions. [MoHFW]`,`Monitor admitted patients with **SpO2 and RR every 2-4 hours**, daily CBC, creatinine and CXR as indicated, and watch for **biphasic worsening** (bacterial superinfection). [WHO 2024]`,`**Persistent fever beyond 5 days on oseltamivir** - look for bacterial pneumonia, empyema, alternative diagnoses (dengue, scrub, leptospirosis, TB) or antiviral resistance in the immunocompromised. [IDSA 2018]`,`**Notify** clusters of ILI/SARI and every suspected avian influenza case to IDSP on the **IHIP portal**; laboratory-confirmed H1N1 is reported weekly. [NCDC]`,`**After recovery**, update vaccination (influenza next season, pneumococcal for 65 and over or high-risk) and reassess cardiac symptoms in elderly patients. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, vaccination and avian influenza control`,points:[`**Inactivated influenza vaccine 0.5 mL IM annually** (deltoid; anterolateral thigh in infants); children **6 months to 8 years receiving it for the first time need 2 doses 4 weeks apart**. [WHO 2022; IAP 2023]`,`**India uses the WHO Southern Hemisphere formulation**, given **April-June before the monsoon peak**; NCDC and IAP endorse this timing, though north India also has a winter peak. [NCDC; IAP 2023]`,`**Priority groups**: health-care workers, pregnant women, adults 65 and over, children 6 months-5 years, people with chronic disease, and residents of care homes; India has **no universal programme vaccine** - it is given to HCWs by some states and is privately purchased. [MoHFW; API 2024]`,`**Vaccine effectiveness** is typically **40-60%** when well matched to circulating strains, lower against H3N2 and in the elderly; **high-dose or adjuvanted vaccines** are preferred for 65 and over in the US. [CDC/ACIP 2024]`,`**Contraindications**: severe allergic reaction to a previous dose; **egg allergy is no longer a contraindication** for any age-appropriate vaccine; GBS within 6 weeks of a previous dose is a precaution. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Live attenuated intranasal vaccine** (age 2-49) is contraindicated in pregnancy, immunosuppression and in children on aspirin; not in routine Indian use. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Post-exposure chemoprophylaxis**: **oseltamivir 75 mg once daily for 10 days** (MoHFW; 7 days after last exposure per CDC) for **high-risk unvaccinated contacts** and HCWs exposed without PPE; not for healthy low-risk contacts. [MoHFW; WHO 2024]`,`**Non-drug measures**: respiratory hygiene, hand washing, masks for symptomatic people, staying home while febrile, and ventilation of rooms. [WHO 2024]`,`**Avian influenza control** (DAHD Action Plan 2021): **culling of poultry within 1 km of an outbreak**, surveillance in a 10 km zone, PPE and **oseltamivir prophylaxis for cullers**, and human surveillance for 10 days after the last exposure. [DAHD 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Clinical Practice Guidelines for Influenza (2024)**: oseltamivir for **severe** influenza as soon as possible; **baloxavir suggested for non-severe disease in high-risk patients**; no antiviral for low-risk non-severe disease; **against corticosteroids** for severe influenza. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2024 chemoprophylaxis advice**: antivirals after exposure only for people at **extremely high risk** of severe disease (or exposure to a novel strain); not for the general population. [WHO 2024]`,`**Switch to trivalent vaccines**: WHO advised dropping **B/Yamagata** (not seen since 2020) and from the **2024-25 season** most vaccines are **trivalent** (H1N1pdm09, H3N2, B/Victoria). [WHO 2023-24]`,`**Baloxavir and transmission**: the **CENTERSTONE trial (2025)** showed baloxavir given to index cases **reduced household transmission by about 30%**; baloxavir also gained approval for **post-exposure prophylaxis** in age 5 and over. [NEJM 2025]`,`**H5N1 clade 2.3.4.4b panzootic**: since 2024 it has spread to **US dairy cattle** with about 70 human cases (mostly mild conjunctivitis) and the first US death in January 2025 - raw milk is now a recognised exposure. [CDC 2025]`,`**ACIP 2024-25** reaffirmed annual vaccination for everyone 6 months and over, with **high-dose, adjuvanted or recombinant vaccine preferred for 65 and over** and a self-administered intranasal LAIV approved in the US in 2024. [ACIP 2024]`,`**India 2023 ICMR advisory** during the H3N2 surge: symptomatic treatment, **no empirical azithromycin or amoxiclav**, test and treat high-risk patients. [ICMR 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic short note**: 'MoHFW categorisation of H1N1 and its management' - draw the **A/B/C table** with who gets oseltamivir, who gets tested, who gets admitted.`,`**Viva**: 'Why does H5N1 cause severe pneumonia but little human spread?' - **alpha-2,3 receptors in the alveoli**, not the alpha-2,6 receptors of the upper airway.`,`**Common mistake**: withholding oseltamivir from a pregnant woman or a hospitalised patient because **48 hours have passed** - start it at any time in these groups.`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing **steroids** for influenza pneumonia or **antibiotics** for uncomplicated flu.`,`**Drift versus shift** is a favourite - drift gives epidemics and annual vaccine change, shift gives pandemics (1918 H1N1, 1957 H2N2, 1968 H3N2, 2009 H1N1pdm09).`,`**Know the paediatric dose bands** (30/45/60/75 mg at 15/23/40 kg) and the renal doses - these are frequent MCQs.`,`**Mechanism MCQ**: oseltamivir = neuraminidase inhibitor; baloxavir = cap-dependent endonuclease inhibitor; amantadine = M2 blocker (obsolete).`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antiviral drugs for influenza`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oseltamivir (neuraminidase inhibitor)`,`75 mg orally BD (adult); children by weight 30/45/60/75 mg BD; infants 3 mg/kg BD`,`5 days (longer in critical or immunocompromised); prophylaxis 75 mg OD for 10 days`,`Nausea, vomiting (take with food); rare neuropsychiatric events in children; reduce dose if CrCl 60 or less`],[`Zanamivir (inhaled neuraminidase inhibitor)`,`10 mg (2 inhalations) BD; age 7 and over`,`5 days; prophylaxis 10 mg OD`,`Bronchospasm - avoid in asthma and COPD; not for ventilated patients via nebuliser of the powder`],[`Peramivir (IV neuraminidase inhibitor)`,`600 mg IV single infusion (adult)`,`Single dose for uncomplicated; daily in severe use (off-label)`,`Diarrhoea, neutropenia, rare skin reactions; adjust for renal function`],[`Baloxavir marboxil (endonuclease inhibitor)`,`40 mg orally once (40-79 kg); 80 mg once (80 kg or more); age 5 and over`,`Single dose; also as post-exposure prophylaxis`,`Avoid with dairy, antacids, iron, calcium (chelation); resistance (I38T) emerges; not in pregnancy; limited availability in India`],[`Amantadine / rimantadine (M2 blockers)`,`Not recommended`,`Not used`,`Near-universal resistance in influenza A; no activity against B; CNS toxicity`]]},{heading:`MoHFW categorisation of influenza-like illness`,columns:[`Category`,`Features`,`Oseltamivir`,`Testing`,`Place of care`],rows:[[`A`,`Mild fever, cough or sore throat, with or without body ache, headache, vomiting, diarrhoea`,`Not needed`,`Not needed`,`Home; review in 24-48 h`],[`B(i)`,`Category A plus high-grade fever and severe sore throat`,`May be given on clinical judgement`,`Not needed`,`Home isolation`],[`B(ii)`,`Category A plus a high-risk condition (under 5, 65 and over, pregnancy, chronic disease, cancer, HIV, steroids)`,`Start at once`,`Not needed`,`Home isolation`],[`C`,`Breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, hypotension, blood-stained sputum, cyanosis, worsening chronic disease; child unable to feed, convulsions`,`Start immediately`,`Yes (RT-PCR)`,`Hospital admission`]]},{heading:`Seasonal versus avian influenza`,columns:[`Feature`,`Seasonal influenza (H1N1pdm09, H3N2, B)`,`Avian influenza (H5N1, H7N9)`],rows:[[`Source`,`Human to human`,`Infected poultry, wild birds, dairy cattle; very limited human-to-human spread`],[`Receptor preference`,`Alpha-2,6 sialic acid (upper airway)`,`Alpha-2,3 sialic acid (alveoli, conjunctiva)`],[`Incubation`,`1-4 days`,`2-8 days (up to 10)`],[`Clinical picture`,`Abrupt fever, myalgia, cough; mostly self-limiting`,`Early pneumonia, ARDS, diarrhoea; conjunctivitis in US dairy cases`],[`Case-fatality`,`Under 0.1% overall`,`About 48% for H5N1 (WHO cumulative); about 39% for H7N9`],[`Treatment`,`Oseltamivir 5 days in high-risk and severe`,`Oseltamivir at once, any time from onset, often 10 days`],[`Public health`,`IDSP ILI/SARI reporting, annual vaccine`,`Immediate notification, culling, contact prophylaxis, 10-day surveillance`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of acute febrile respiratory illness`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Influenza`,`Abrupt onset, prominent myalgia, epidemic season`,`RT-PCR (multiplex) on NP swab`],[`COVID-19`,`Anosmia, longer incubation, household clusters`,`SARS-CoV-2 RT-PCR or antigen test`],[`RSV / hMPV`,`Wheeze, rhinorrhoea, elderly with heart or lung disease`,`Multiplex PCR`],[`Dengue`,`Retro-orbital pain, rash, falling platelets, little cough`,`NS1 antigen (day 1-5), IgM after day 5`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Calf tenderness, conjunctival suffusion, jaundice, haemoptysis`,`IgM ELISA, MAT`],[`Bacterial CAP`,`Purulent sputum, pleuritic pain, focal consolidation`,`CXR, procalcitonin, sputum and blood culture`],[`Streptococcal pharyngitis`,`Exudate, tender anterior nodes, no cough`,`Rapid antigen or throat culture`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`RT-PCR NP and throat swab (VTM, 2-8 C)`,`Category C, outbreaks, avian suspicion`,`Reference test; subtypes H1/H3; H5/H7 subtyping at NIV/VRDL`],[`Rapid antigen test`,`Point of care if PCR unavailable`,`Sensitivity 50-70%: a negative test does not exclude influenza`],[`CBC`,`All admitted patients`,`Leucopenia and lymphopenia typical; neutrophilia suggests bacterial superinfection`],[`Chest X-ray`,`Category C`,`Bilateral interstitial shadows (viral) versus lobar consolidation (bacterial)`],[`Procalcitonin`,`Pneumonia on admission`,`Under 0.25 ng/mL argues against bacterial co-infection`],[`CK, creatinine, troponin`,`Myalgia, dark urine, chest pain, severe disease`,`Myositis or rhabdomyolysis; myocarditis or MI`]]}],redFlags:[`Breathlessness, RR over 30/min or SpO2 under 94% on room air`,`Chest pain, haemoptysis or blood-stained sputum`,`Drowsiness, confusion or seizures`,`Hypotension (systolic under 90 mmHg) or cyanosis`,`Child unable to feed, persistently high fever, convulsions or chest indrawing`,`Worsening of underlying heart, lung, kidney or diabetic disease`,`Return of fever with purulent sputum after initial improvement (secondary bacterial pneumonia)`,`Febrile illness after contact with sick or dead poultry (suspected avian influenza)`],pearls:[`**A - no drug, no test; B - drug, no test; C - drug, test, admit**: the MoHFW categorisation in one line.`,`Start oseltamivir **without waiting for a test** and **beyond 48 hours** in pregnant, high-risk and hospitalised patients.`,`**Oseltamivir child bands: 15-23-40 kg for 30-45-60-75 mg BD.**`,`**Steroids harm** in influenza pneumonia - give them only for another indication.`,`**Drift = epidemics, shift = pandemics**; only influenza A shifts.`,`India uses the **Southern Hemisphere vaccine before the monsoon** (April-June).`,`**Egg allergy no longer contraindicates** influenza vaccination.`,`Post-influenza necrotising pneumonia in a young adult - think **MRSA**, add vancomycin or linezolid.`,`**H5N1 likes alpha-2,3 receptors deep in the lung** - severe pneumonia, poor human spread.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman at 30 weeks of pregnancy presents on day 4 of fever, cough and myalgia during an H1N1 season. She is not breathless, SpO2 is 98% and chest is clear. What is the most appropriate action under MoHFW guidance?`,options:[`Symptomatic treatment only, because more than 48 hours have passed since onset`,`Send an RT-PCR swab and start oseltamivir only if the test is positive`,`Start oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days without waiting for testing`,`Admit and start IV peramivir with methylprednisolone`],answer:2,explanation:`Pregnancy is a high-risk condition, which makes her Category B(ii): oseltamivir is started at once without testing, and in high-risk patients the 48-hour window does not apply. Symptomatic care alone is for Category A. Waiting for PCR delays treatment and testing is not required in Category B. She has no Category C features, so admission is not needed, and corticosteroids are harmful in influenza.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-q2`,stem:`Which mechanism explains why avian H5N1 influenza causes severe pneumonia in humans yet rarely spreads from person to person?`,options:[`Preferential binding of haemagglutinin to alpha-2,3 sialic acid receptors in the lower respiratory tract`,`Absence of neuraminidase on the viral surface`,`Resistance to all neuraminidase inhibitors`,`Replication restricted to the nasal mucosa at 33 C`],answer:0,explanation:`Avian HA binds alpha-2,3-linked sialic acid, which in humans is found mainly on alveolar and lower airway cells, so infection is deep (pneumonia) but little virus is shed from the upper airway to transmit. H5N1 does carry neuraminidase (N1), and most strains remain sensitive to oseltamivir. Replication restricted to the cooler nasal mucosa is the property of live attenuated vaccine strains, not H5N1.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-q3`,stem:`A 70-year-old man on haemodialysis has confirmed influenza A. What is the appropriate oseltamivir regimen?`,options:[`75 mg twice daily for 5 days`,`150 mg twice daily for 10 days`,`30 mg once daily for 5 days`,`30 mg after each haemodialysis session`],answer:3,explanation:`Oseltamivir carboxylate is renally cleared and dialysable, so patients on haemodialysis receive 30 mg after each session for the treatment course. The full 75 mg BD dose would accumulate; 30 mg once daily is the dose for CrCl over 10-30 mL/min, not for dialysis. Double-dose 150 mg BD has no proven benefit even in patients with normal kidneys.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-q4`,stem:`A 35-year-old man is admitted with influenza A pneumonia, SpO2 88% on room air, bilateral infiltrates and no other comorbidity. Which intervention should be avoided?`,options:[`Oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily via nasogastric tube if needed`,`Routine systemic corticosteroids for the viral pneumonia`,`Oxygen titrated to SpO2 92-96%`,`Droplet precautions with N95 respirators during aerosol-generating procedures`],answer:1,explanation:`WHO 2024 and IDSA advise against corticosteroids for influenza pneumonia because they are associated with higher mortality, secondary infection and prolonged viral shedding; unlike COVID-19, there is no dexamethasone benefit. Oseltamivir should be started immediately in any hospitalised patient, oxygen to a 92-96% target is standard, and droplet plus airborne precautions for aerosol-generating procedures protect staff.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-q5`,stem:`Regarding influenza vaccination in India, which statement is correct?`,options:[`The Northern Hemisphere vaccine given in October is recommended for the whole country`,`Egg allergy is an absolute contraindication to inactivated influenza vaccine`,`The WHO Southern Hemisphere formulation is recommended, given before the monsoon`,`Live attenuated vaccine is preferred in pregnancy`],answer:2,explanation:`Because much of India peaks in the monsoon, NCDC and IAP recommend the WHO Southern Hemisphere formulation given around April-June. A Northern Hemisphere October campaign misses the monsoon peak in much of the country. Egg allergy is no longer a contraindication for any age-appropriate vaccine (ACIP), and live attenuated vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy - the inactivated vaccine is used in any trimester.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the virology, clinical features, MoHFW categorisation and management of seasonal influenza (H1N1). Add a note on prevention.`,openingLines:[`Influenza is an acute febrile respiratory illness caused by segmented RNA influenza A and B viruses, which cause seasonal epidemics through antigenic drift and pandemics (type A only) through antigenic shift.`,`In India H1N1pdm09, H3N2 and B/Victoria circulate with winter and monsoon peaks; MoHFW triages patients into Categories A, B and C to decide who gets oseltamivir, testing and admission.`],answer:[{heading:`Virology and pathogenesis`,points:[`Orthomyxovirus with 8 RNA segments; HA (binds sialic acid) and NA (releases virions) define subtypes.`,`Drift (point mutations) - seasonal epidemics; shift (reassortment) - pandemics.`,`Human strains bind alpha-2,6 receptors (upper airway); avian alpha-2,3 (alveoli).`,`Epithelial necrosis predisposes to secondary S. aureus and pneumococcal pneumonia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Incubation 1-4 days; abrupt fever, myalgia, headache, dry cough; fever 3 days, cough up to 2 weeks.`,`Atypical: no fever in elderly; GI symptoms and seizures in children; influenza B calf myositis.`,`Complications: viral and bacterial pneumonia, ARDS, myocarditis, MI, encephalopathy, GBS, Reye syndrome.`]},{heading:`MoHFW categorisation`,points:[`A: mild - no oseltamivir, no test, review 24-48 h.`,`B(i): high fever and severe sore throat - oseltamivir on judgement; B(ii): high-risk groups - oseltamivir without testing.`,`C: breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, hypotension, haemoptysis, cyanosis - test, admit, treat at once.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`RT-PCR NP and throat swab in VTM for Category C; rapid antigen tests miss 30-50%.`,`CBC, CXR, procalcitonin, ABG, creatinine, CK in admitted patients.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Oseltamivir 75 mg BD x 5 days; child bands 30/45/60/75 mg; renal dose reduction.`,`Start beyond 48 h in pregnant, high-risk and hospitalised patients.`,`Supportive: paracetamol, fluids, oxygen to 92-96%, lung-protective ventilation; no steroids; antibiotics only for bacterial pneumonia.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Annual inactivated vaccine 0.5 mL IM - Southern Hemisphere formulation, April-June; 2 doses in children under 9 vaccinated for the first time.`,`Priority: HCWs, pregnancy, 65 and over, 6 months-5 years, chronic disease.`,`Chemoprophylaxis oseltamivir 75 mg OD x 10 days for high-risk contacts; respiratory hygiene, isolation.`]}],mustDraw:[`MoHFW Category A/B/C table`,`Structure of influenza virus with HA, NA and M2 and the drug targets`],markSplit:[{part:`Virology and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and complications`,marks:2},{part:`MoHFW categorisation`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and treatment`,marks:2.5},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`H1N1`,`antigenic drift`,`Category B`,`oseltamivir`,`Southern Hemisphere vaccine`,`Reye syndrome`]},{id:`infectious-fever-influenza-seasonal-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on avian influenza (H5N1): epidemiology, clinical features and containment.`,openingLines:[`Avian influenza H5N1 is a zoonotic influenza A infection acquired from infected poultry (and since 2024 dairy cattle), with a WHO cumulative case-fatality near 48% but very limited human-to-human transmission.`,`India reported its first human H5N1 death in 2021; containment follows the DAHD Action Plan with culling, surveillance and oseltamivir prophylaxis.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology`,points:[`About 990 human cases since 2003, CFR about 48%; clade 2.3.4.4b panzootic in birds, mammals and US dairy cattle since 2024.`,`Exposure: sick or dead poultry, live-bird markets, raw milk; incubation 2-8 days.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`High fever, early pneumonia, ARDS, diarrhoea; lymphopenia and thrombocytopenia; conjunctivitis in dairy workers.`,`RT-PCR with H5 subtyping at NIV Pune or a designated VRDL; notify IDSP immediately.`]},{heading:`Treatment and containment`,points:[`Oseltamivir 75 mg BD at once regardless of day of illness, often 10 days; ICU support; isolation with airborne precautions.`,`Culling within 1 km, surveillance in 10 km, PPE and oseltamivir 75 mg OD prophylaxis for cullers, 10-day follow-up of contacts.`]}],mustDraw:[`Seasonal versus avian influenza comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment and containment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`H5N1`,`alpha-2,3 receptor`,`culling`,`NIV Pune`,`clade 2.3.4.4b`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on influenza`,`WHO Clinical Practice Guidelines for Influenza, 2024`,`IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines on Seasonal Influenza (Uyeki et al., Clin Infect Dis), 2018`,`MoHFW / NCDC Seasonal Influenza (H1N1): Guidelines on categorisation of patients, treatment and vaccination`,`CDC Influenza Antiviral Medications: Summary for Clinicians, 2024-25 season`,`ACIP Prevention and Control of Seasonal Influenza with Vaccines, 2024-25 (MMWR 2024)`,`IAP Advisory Committee on Vaccines and Immunization Practices (ACVIP) recommendations, 2023`,`DAHD Action Plan for Preparedness, Control and Containment of Avian Influenza, revised 2021`]});var g=[];g.push({id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs`,title:`Mumps, rubella and congenital rubella syndrome`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Mumps** is a vaccine-preventable **paramyxovirus** infection whose hallmark is **parotitis** and whose adult cost is **orchitis, meningitis and deafness**; **rubella** is a mild **matonavirus** exanthem whose real burden is **congenital rubella syndrome (CRS)** - the **cataract, heart disease, deafness** triad after first-trimester infection - which India tracks through **sentinel CRS surveillance** on the road to **measles-rubella elimination**.`,keywords:[`mumps`,`epidemic parotitis`,`mumps orchitis`,`oophoritis`,`mumps meningitis`,`rubella`,`German measles`,`congenital rubella syndrome`,`CRS`,`congenital rubella infection`,`Gregg triad`,`Forchheimer spots`,`rubella IgG avidity`,`MR vaccine`,`MMR vaccine`,`Jeryl Lynn`,`Leningrad-Zagreb`,`RA 27/3`,`measles-rubella elimination`,`fever-rash surveillance`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Mumps** is an acute systemic infection by **mumps virus (genus Orthorubulavirus, family Paramyxoviridae)** with a single serotype; genotype **G** dominates recent outbreaks worldwide. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella** is caused by **rubella virus (genus Rubivirus, now family Matonaviridae, formerly Togaviridae)**; humans are the only reservoir, which is why elimination is possible. [WHO 2020]`,`**Congenital rubella syndrome (CRS)** is the pattern of defects in an infant after **maternal rubella in pregnancy**; **congenital rubella infection (CRI)** is laboratory evidence of infection without the clinical defects. [WHO CRS surveillance standards 2018]`,`Before vaccination India was estimated to have **the largest number of CRS births of any country** - tens of thousands a year - and seroprevalence studies showed **10-30% of women of child-bearing age were rubella-susceptible**. [WHO SEARO 2019]`,`The **MR catch-up campaign (2017-2019)** vaccinated over **300 million children aged 9 months-15 years**, and MR replaced measles vaccine in the **Universal Immunisation Programme (UIP)** at 9-12 and 16-24 months. [MoHFW 2019]`,`**Mumps vaccine is not in the UIP**, so mumps remains endemic in India with seasonal outbreaks (late winter-spring), such as the large **2024 surge in Kerala and other states** with thousands of cases in schoolchildren. [IDSP 2024]`,`India (and WHO South-East Asia Region) now targets **measles and rubella elimination by 2026**, supported by case-based **fever-rash surveillance** with IgM testing of every suspected case. [MoHFW 2025]`]},{heading:`Transmission and risk factors`,points:[`**Mumps** spreads by **respiratory droplets and saliva**; incubation is **16-18 days (range 12-25)**, and patients are infectious from about **2 days before to 5 days after parotid swelling**. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Rubella** spreads by droplets; incubation is **about 14 days (12-23)**, and patients are infectious from **7 days before to 7 days after rash onset**; 25-50% of infections are subclinical yet still transmissible. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Infants with CRS shed virus** in nasopharyngeal secretions and urine for **up to 1 year**, so they are a hidden source of nosocomial outbreaks. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`Risk factors for mumps: **unvaccinated or single-dose vaccinated**, crowded hostels, schools and military camps; outbreaks now occur even in **two-dose vaccinated** college populations through **waning immunity**. [ACIP 2018]`,`Risk factors for CRS: **rubella-susceptible women** of child-bearing age, especially those who missed MR (born before 2008-2010 catch-up cohorts), migrant and urban-slum women, and adolescent girls not reached by campaigns. [WHO SEARO 2019]`,`Partial vaccine coverage (under 80%) can **raise CRS** by shifting infection to older ages - the classic **paradoxical effect**, which is why rubella vaccine is introduced only with high routine coverage. [WHO position paper 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Mumps** replicates in the upper respiratory epithelium and regional nodes, then a **viraemia** seeds glandular and neural tissue - **salivary glands, testis, ovary, pancreas, breast, thyroid and the CNS** - explaining why mumps is a systemic illness, not just parotitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the parotid, interstitial oedema and lymphocytic infiltration of ducts cause **painful swelling** and release of **salivary amylase**, which is why serum amylase rises without pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Orchitis** results from viral replication in the testis inside an **unyielding tunica albuginea**; the rise in pressure causes **ischaemic pressure necrosis and seminiferous tubule atrophy**, explaining severe pain and later atrophy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mumps virus is **neurotropic**: CSF pleocytosis occurs in about half of cases, usually silent, and the virus can damage the **cochlea and organ of Corti**, causing sudden sensorineural deafness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella** replicates in nasopharynx and nodes (hence **post-auricular and suboccipital lymphadenopathy a week before the rash**); the rash and arthritis are largely **immune-complex mediated** and coincide with antibody appearance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CRS mechanism**: placental infection leads to chronic fetal infection that **inhibits mitosis, causes vascular endothelial damage and apoptosis** during organogenesis - so timing decides the defect: eye and heart (weeks 2-10), ear (up to week 16-18). [Harrison 22e]`,`The fetus has **no effective immune response before 20 weeks**, so infection persists throughout pregnancy and infancy, giving **ongoing organ damage, virus shedding and late manifestations** such as diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Mumps** clinical spectrum: **asymptomatic or non-specific respiratory (about 30%)**, **classic parotitis (most symptomatic cases)**, and **extra-salivary disease** - orchitis, oophoritis, mastitis, pancreatitis, meningitis, encephalitis, deafness. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Rubella** in practice is classified as **postnatal (acquired) rubella** - mild exanthem - and **congenital rubella** (CRS or CRI). [WHO 2018]`,`**Fetal risk by gestation at maternal infection**: **under 11-12 weeks up to 90% CRS**; **12-16 weeks about 10-20%, mostly deafness**; **16-20 weeks minimal risk (deafness only)**; **after 20 weeks rare**. [RCOG 2016; Harrison 22e]`,`**WHO CRS clinical groups**: **Group A** - cataract, congenital glaucoma, congenital heart disease (PDA or peripheral pulmonary artery stenosis), hearing impairment, pigmentary retinopathy; **Group B** - purpura, splenomegaly, microcephaly, developmental delay, meningoencephalitis, radiolucent bone disease, jaundice within 24 hours of birth. [WHO 2018]`,`**Clinically confirmed CRS** = a suspected case with **2 Group A findings, or 1 Group A plus 1 Group B**; **laboratory-confirmed CRS** = suspected case with positive rubella IgM, RT-PCR, or sustained IgG beyond the age when maternal antibody wanes; **CRI** = lab-positive without Group A findings. [WHO 2018]`,`**Suspected CRS** (surveillance trigger) = any infant under 1 year with heart disease, suspected deafness, or an eye sign (white pupil, poor vision, pendular nystagmus, squint, microphthalmos, glaucoma), or a mother with suspected or confirmed rubella in pregnancy. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Mumps** prodrome of fever, malaise, headache and anorexia for 1-2 days, then **earache and pain on chewing or with sour food** as the parotid swells; swelling is **bilateral in 70-75%**, often sequential, and settles in **7-10 days**. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Mumps orchitis** affects **15-30% of unvaccinated post-pubertal males** (far fewer if vaccinated), usually **4-10 days after parotitis** (it can precede it or occur alone), with high fever, rigors and severe testicular pain; it is **bilateral in 10-30%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oophoritis** (about 5% of post-pubertal females) presents as lower abdominal pain mimicking appendicitis; **mastitis** is reported in up to 30% of women over 15 years in outbreaks. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Mumps meningitis** - headache, vomiting and neck stiffness - is clinically evident in about **1-10%** and is usually benign; encephalitis is rare (under 1 in 1000) but causes most deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella** in children is often a **1-3 day pink maculopapular rash** spreading from face to trunk with mild fever; adolescents and adults have a **1-5 day prodrome** of low fever, sore throat, conjunctivitis and tender nodes. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Rubella arthralgia or arthritis** of the fingers, wrists and knees occurs in **up to 70% of adult women**, lasting up to a month; it is rare in children and men. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`In every woman with fever and rash, ask the **LMP, pregnancy status, contact with a rash illness, and MR/MMR vaccination** - the answer decides whether this is a fetal emergency. [RCOG 2016]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Parotid swelling** fills the space between the posterior ramus of the mandible and the mastoid, **obliterates the angle of the jaw** and lifts the ear lobe upward and outward. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stensen duct orifice** (opposite the upper second molar) is red and swollen, but **no pus** can be expressed - pus suggests bacterial (suppurative) parotitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps orchitis**: swollen, warm, exquisitely tender testis with scrotal oedema; **epididymitis coexists in up to 85%**; the cremasteric reflex is preserved, unlike torsion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella**: **post-auricular, suboccipital and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy** (the most characteristic sign), discrete pink macules that do not coalesce as in measles, and **Forchheimer spots** (petechiae on the soft palate) in about 20%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CRS newborn**: low birth weight, **cataract (white pupil)**, microphthalmia, **salt-and-pepper retinopathy**, continuous murmur of **PDA**, hepatosplenomegaly, **blueberry-muffin rash** (dermal erythropoiesis) and microcephaly. [Harrison 22e]`,`Always examine **hearing (distraction test or OAE/BERA)**, red reflex and heart in any infant of a mother with rash in pregnancy - deafness is the **commonest single CRS defect** and is often missed at birth. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Parotid swelling** differentials: **acute suppurative parotitis** (S. aureus; elderly, dehydrated, post-operative; pus from the duct, unilateral), **other viruses** (influenza, parainfluenza, EBV, coxsackie, HIV), **salivary stone** (meal-related swelling) and **cervical lymphadenitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chronic or recurrent bilateral parotid enlargement suggests **HIV (diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis)**, **Sjogren syndrome**, sarcoidosis, **IgG4-related disease**, alcohol, malnutrition or drugs - not mumps. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute scrotum**: orchitis versus **testicular torsion** (sudden onset, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex, absent flow on Doppler) and bacterial epididymo-orchitis (urinary symptoms, older men, STI risk). [EAU 2024]`,`**Rubella rash** is confused with **measles** (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots, confluent rash, sicker child), **parvovirus B19** (slapped cheek, lacy rash, arthritis in women), dengue, HHV-6 (roseola), enteroviruses and drug rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`Because rubella and parvovirus both cause rash with arthralgia in pregnant women and both harm the fetus, **test for both** in a pregnant woman with rash. [RCOG 2016]`,`**CRS mimics** in the newborn: other **TORCH** infections - CMV (periventricular calcification, more microcephaly), toxoplasma (hydrocephalus, diffuse calcification, chorioretinitis), syphilis (snuffles, bone lesions), HSV and Zika. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Mumps** is usually clinical; in outbreaks or atypical cases, the best test is **RT-PCR on a buccal swab** after massaging the parotid for 30 seconds, ideally **within 3 days (up to 8 days) of parotitis**. [CDC 2024]`,`**Mumps IgM** may be negative or transient in **vaccinated people**, so a negative IgM does not exclude mumps; a fourfold IgG rise in paired sera also confirms. [CDC 2024]`,`**Serum amylase** is raised from salivary isoenzyme in parotitis; a raised **lipase** with abdominal pain points to true pancreatitis. **CSF** in mumps meningitis shows **lymphocytic pleocytosis (often under 500 cells), low glucose in up to 30%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Use **scrotal colour Doppler ultrasound** whenever torsion cannot be excluded; orchitis shows **increased flow**. [EAU 2024]`,`**Rubella**: serum **IgM ELISA** (best from day 5 of rash; positive in about 50% on rash day 1) and/or **RT-PCR of throat swab or urine** within 5-7 days of rash - these are the specimens sent under **MR case-based surveillance**. [WHO 2018]`,`**False-positive rubella IgM** occurs with parvovirus B19, EBV, CMV and rheumatoid factor, so in pregnancy confirm with **IgG avidity**: **low avidity indicates infection within the past 2-3 months**; high avidity largely excludes recent primary infection. [RCOG 2016]`,`**CRS work-up of infant**: rubella **IgM in serum (positive in nearly all under 6 months)** and/or RT-PCR of throat swab or urine, plus **echocardiography, slit-lamp and fundus examination, and hearing assessment (OAE then BERA)**. [WHO 2018]`,`**Prenatal diagnosis**: RT-PCR on **amniotic fluid** after **18-20 weeks** and at least **6 weeks after maternal infection**; done in fetal medicine units. [RCOG 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Mumps**: supportive - **paracetamol 500-1000 mg 6-hourly (child 15 mg/kg)** or ibuprofen, warm or cold compresses, soft diet, avoid sour foods, and good hydration; there is **no antiviral**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mumps orchitis**: bed rest, **scrotal support and ice packs**, NSAIDs for pain; **corticosteroids reduce pain but do not prevent atrophy or subfertility** and are not routinely recommended. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exclude from school or work for 5 days after the onset of parotitis** (droplet precautions); notify clusters under **IDSP**. [CDC 2024]`,`**Rubella**: paracetamol and rest; NSAIDs for arthritis; **exclude for 7 days after rash onset**, and keep away from pregnant women. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`Notify every **fever-rash case** as suspected measles-rubella and collect **serum (and throat swab)** for the MR surveillance laboratory network. [MoHFW 2025]`,`**Pregnant woman exposed to rubella**: check **rubella IgG urgently** (a stored booking sample helps); if immune and asymptomatic, reassure; if non-immune, repeat IgG/IgM at 3-4 weeks and refer to fetal medicine. [RCOG 2016]`,`**Immunoglobulin** after rubella exposure does **not prevent fetal infection** and is considered only when termination would not be acceptable to a non-immune exposed woman. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`Admit mumps with **encephalitis, severe meningitis, pancreatitis, inability to drink or severe bilateral orchitis**; manage under **droplet precautions**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute scrotum** is surgical until proven otherwise: urology review and Doppler; **do not delay exploration** when torsion is possible, even in a mumps epidemic. [EAU 2024]`,`**Sudden hearing loss** with mumps needs **audiometry and ENT referral**; mumps deafness is usually unilateral and often permanent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rubella encephalitis (about 1 in 6000)** and **thrombocytopenic purpura (about 1 in 3000)** need admission; ITP usually recovers but IVIG or steroids may be used for bleeding. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Neonate with suspected CRS**: **contact isolation** until 1 year of age (or two negative cultures after 3 months), only rubella-immune staff to handle, and early **cardiac, ophthalmic and audiology** referral. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Confirmed first-trimester maternal rubella**: counselling in a fetal medicine unit about the **up to 90% risk of CRS**, with the option of termination under the **MTP (Amendment) Act 2021**, which allows termination up to 24 weeks for specified categories and beyond with a medical board for substantial fetal anomalies. [MTP Act 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy - mumps**: no congenital syndrome; first-trimester mumps may slightly increase spontaneous abortion, and **MMR is contraindicated** in pregnancy. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Pregnancy - rubella vaccine**: avoid pregnancy for **28 days (1 month) after MR/MMR**; **inadvertent vaccination is not an indication for termination** - no CRS has been documented in registries of such pregnancies. [CDC Pink Book 2024; WHO 2020]`,`**Postpartum**: give MR/MMR to **rubella-non-immune women after delivery** (breastfeeding is not a contraindication); this is the classic postpartum missed opportunity in India. [WHO 2020]`,`**Adults and adolescents** get more complications: orchitis, oophoritis and arthritis in mumps and rubella almost never occur before puberty. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immunocompromised and HIV with CD4 under 200 (or under 15% in children)**: live MMR/MR is **contraindicated**; protect by vaccinating household contacts. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Healthcare workers** in obstetric, neonatal and paediatric units should have **documented 2 doses of MMR or rubella IgG positive**; non-immune staff are excluded from day 5 to day 21 after exposure. [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Mumps orchitis leads to **testicular atrophy in up to half of affected testes**, but **sterility is rare** even after bilateral orchitis; subfertility (oligospermia) occurs in up to 13%. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other mumps complications: **pancreatitis (about 4%)**, **sensorineural deafness (about 1 in 20,000 permanent)**, myocarditis, thyroiditis, nephritis, and a small increase in miscarriage in early pregnancy. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`,`**Urabe-strain mumps vaccine** caused aseptic meningitis and was withdrawn in many countries; **Jeryl Lynn** and **Leningrad-Zagreb** strains are used now. [WHO 2007; Harrison 22e]`,`Rubella complications: **arthritis** (women), **thrombocytopenia**, **encephalitis** and the very rare **progressive rubella panencephalitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CRS late manifestations**: **type 1 diabetes (about 20% by adulthood)**, thyroid disease, glaucoma, keratoconus, behavioural disorders, autism-spectrum features and progressive hearing loss - so CRS is a lifelong disability. [Harrison 22e]`,`CRS mortality in infancy is high when there is **complex heart disease, thrombocytopenia or meningoencephalitis**. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer every **acute scrotum** urgently, and every **mumps patient with hearing loss** to ENT for audiometry. [EAU 2024]`,`Offer a **semen analysis at about 3 months** to men with bilateral orchitis who are concerned about fertility. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CRS child**: paediatric cardiology (PDA closure), ophthalmology (early cataract surgery, glaucoma watch), **hearing aids or cochlear implant under RBSK/ADIP**, early intervention for development, and screening for diabetes and thyroid disease over life. [RBSK 2023]`,`Report every suspected CRS from a sentinel site using the **CRS case investigation form**, with infant serum, and follow lab-confirmed cases until virus shedding stops. [MoHFW CRS guidelines 2018]`,`Follow the pregnant woman exposed to rubella with **repeat serology at 3-4 weeks** (and 6 weeks if needed) before final reassurance. [RCOG 2016]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme link`,points:[`**UIP schedule**: **MR vaccine 0.5 mL subcutaneously at 9-12 months and 16-24 months**; MR also given in **catch-up campaigns** and outbreak response. [MoHFW UIP 2024]`,`**IAP ACVIP** recommends **MMR at 9 months, 15 months and 4-6 years** in private practice, giving the mumps protection that MR lacks. [IAP 2023]`,`One dose of mumps vaccine is about **78% effective** and two doses about **88%**; ACIP recommends a **third MMR dose for people at increased risk in a mumps outbreak**. [ACIP 2018]`,`One dose of rubella-containing vaccine gives **over 95% long-lasting protection**; WHO recommends combining rubella with measles vaccination and pairing introduction with **vaccinating women of child-bearing age**. [WHO position paper 2020]`,`**Screen and vaccinate** non-immune women at **pre-conception** and **postpartum**; verify MR status in adolescent girls at school health visits. [WHO 2020]`,`**Surveillance**: case-based **measles-rubella fever-rash surveillance** with IgM testing in the accredited MR laboratory network, plus **sentinel CRS surveillance** in tertiary hospitals that started in 2016 - CRS cases measure how well rubella elimination is going. [WHO SEARO 2023]`,`MR is a **live vaccine**: contraindicated in pregnancy, severe immunosuppression and previous anaphylaxis to a component; mild fever and rash at 7-12 days and transient arthralgia in adult women are expected. [CDC Pink Book 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**India measles-rubella elimination target 2026** (MoHFW, with WHO SEARO), with intensified MR campaigns and case-based surveillance after the earlier 2023 target was missed. [MoHFW 2025]`,`**WHO rubella vaccine position paper (2020)**: a single dose of rubella-containing vaccine is sufficient for protection, but it is delivered as MR/MMR in a two-dose measles schedule; introduce only when measles coverage is at least 80%. [WHO 2020]`,`**WHO Immunization Agenda 2030** lists rubella elimination in at least 5 of 6 WHO regions; India received the **Measles and Rubella Champion Award (2024)** for progress. [WHO 2024]`,`**ACIP third-dose MMR in outbreaks (2018)** remains current guidance for groups at increased risk during mumps outbreaks. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Mumps diagnostics** (CDC 2024): buccal swab RT-PCR preferred over IgM, especially in vaccinated people where IgM is often negative. [CDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **Gregg triad** of CRS - **cataract, PDA, sensorineural deafness**; deafness is the commonest single defect, PDA and peripheral pulmonary stenosis the commonest heart lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: why does **mumps orchitis cause atrophy**? The **inelastic tunica albuginea** - oedema raises pressure and causes ischaemic necrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: the most characteristic sign of rubella - **post-auricular and suboccipital lymphadenopathy**, appearing before the rash. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing mumps in **every parotid swelling** - pus from Stensen duct, unilateral swelling in an elderly dehydrated patient means **bacterial parotitis** needing anti-staphylococcal antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **terminating a pregnancy for inadvertent MR vaccination** - no CRS has been documented; counsel and continue. [WHO 2020]`,`Common mistake: relying on **rubella IgM alone** in pregnancy - false positives are common, so confirm with **IgG avidity** and repeat serology before counselling termination. [RCOG 2016]`,`Know the **WHO CRS case definitions (Group A and B)** and the timing table of fetal risk - these are the commonest short-note questions. [WHO 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and vaccines used`,columns:[`Drug / vaccine`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / schedule`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`500-1000 mg oral 6-hourly (child 15 mg/kg)`,`Symptomatic, max 4 g/day`,`Hepatotoxicity in overdose or chronic alcohol use`],[`Ibuprofen (orchitis, arthritis)`,`400 mg oral 8-hourly with food`,`3-7 days`,`Gastritis, AKI; avoid in pregnancy after 20 weeks and if dengue possible`],[`Prednisolone (severe orchitis pain, ITP)`,`Orchitis: limited evidence; ITP 1 mg/kg/day oral`,`Short course with taper`,`Does not prevent testicular atrophy; hyperglycaemia`],[`IVIG (rubella ITP with bleeding)`,`1 g/kg IV once (may repeat)`,`1-2 days`,`Headache, aseptic meningitis, thrombosis, volume load`],[`MR vaccine (UIP)`,`0.5 mL subcutaneous, right upper arm`,`9-12 months and 16-24 months; campaigns`,`Live: avoid in pregnancy and severe immunosuppression; fever, rash at 7-12 days`],[`MMR vaccine (IAP)`,`0.5 mL subcutaneous or IM`,`9 months, 15 months, 4-6 years; 3rd dose in outbreaks`,`Febrile seizure risk 1 in 3000 doses; transient arthralgia in adult women; avoid pregnancy for 28 days`]]},{heading:`Fetal risk after maternal rubella by gestation`,columns:[`Gestation at maternal infection`,`Risk of fetal infection / defect`,`Typical defects`,`Counselling`],rows:[[`Under 11-12 weeks`,`Up to 90% CRS`,`Cataract, heart disease, deafness, microcephaly`,`Fetal medicine referral; termination is a legitimate option`],[`12-16 weeks`,`About 10-20%`,`Mainly deafness, some retinopathy`,`Individual counselling; amniocentesis after 18-20 weeks`],[`16-20 weeks`,`Minimal`,`Deafness only, rare`,`Reassure; hearing screen at birth`],[`After 20 weeks`,`Fetal infection possible, defects rare`,`Usually none`,`Reassure; test infant for CRI`],[`Pre-conception rash (before LMP)`,`Very low`,`No increased defect risk`,`Reassure`]]},{heading:`WHO CRS surveillance case classification`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Findings that count`,`Action`],rows:[[`Suspected CRS`,`Infant under 1 year with heart disease, suspected deafness or an eye sign, or maternal rubella in pregnancy`,`White pupil, nystagmus, squint, microphthalmos, glaucoma, murmur`,`Collect serum IgM; full clinical work-up`],[`Clinically confirmed CRS`,`Suspected case with 2 Group A, or 1 Group A plus 1 Group B findings, no adequate specimen`,`Group A: cataract, glaucoma, CHD, hearing loss, pigmentary retinopathy; Group B: purpura, splenomegaly, microcephaly, delay, meningoencephalitis, radiolucent bones, early jaundice`,`Report; treat as infectious`],[`Laboratory-confirmed CRS`,`Suspected case meeting clinical criteria plus positive lab test`,`Rubella IgM, RT-PCR, or sustained IgG beyond maternal antibody`,`Report; contact isolation till 1 year`],[`Congenital rubella infection (CRI)`,`Lab-positive infant without Group A findings`,`IgM or PCR positive`,`Follow up hearing and development; isolate`],[`Discarded`,`Suspected case with adequate negative specimen`,`Rubella IgM negative`,`Look for another cause (CMV, toxoplasma)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of parotid swelling and of fever with rash`,columns:[`Condition`,`Key clue`,`Investigation`,`Treatment point`],rows:[[`Mumps parotitis`,`Bilateral, angle of jaw obliterated, no pus, outbreak, unvaccinated`,`Buccal swab RT-PCR`,`Supportive; isolate 5 days`],[`Suppurative parotitis`,`Unilateral, elderly or post-op, dehydration, pus from Stensen duct`,`Pus culture, ultrasound for abscess`,`Anti-staphylococcal antibiotic, hydration, sialogogues`],[`Sialolithiasis`,`Swelling with meals, submandibular more often`,`Ultrasound, sialography`,`Stone removal`],[`HIV parotid enlargement`,`Chronic bilateral, painless, lymphoepithelial cysts`,`HIV test`,`ART`],[`Rubella`,`Mild illness, post-auricular nodes, rash 3 days, arthralgia in women`,`IgM ELISA, throat swab PCR`,`Supportive; pregnancy contact tracing`],[`Measles`,`Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots, confluent rash`,`Measles IgM`,`Vitamin A; see Measles topic`],[`Parvovirus B19`,`Slapped cheek, lacy rash, symmetrical arthritis in women`,`B19 IgM, PCR`,`Fetal anaemia watch in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`When to send`,`Interpretation`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Buccal swab RT-PCR (mumps)`,`Within 3 days, up to 8 days of parotitis`,`Positive confirms mumps`,`Massage parotid 30 s first`],[`Mumps IgM`,`From day 3-5`,`Positive supports acute mumps`,`Often negative in vaccinated`],[`Rubella IgM ELISA`,`Day 5 or later after rash`,`Positive suggests recent infection`,`False positives: parvovirus, EBV, RF`],[`Rubella IgG avidity`,`When IgM positive in pregnancy`,`Low avidity: infection within 2-3 months`,`Needs reference laboratory`],[`Amniotic fluid RT-PCR`,`After 18-20 weeks and 6 weeks post-infection`,`Positive: fetal infection`,`Does not grade severity`],[`Infant serum rubella IgM`,`Any suspected CRS under 1 year`,`Positive confirms CRS or CRI`,`Lower sensitivity after 6 months; then repeat IgG`]]}],redFlags:[`Acute scrotal pain where torsion cannot be excluded clinically or by Doppler`,`Mumps with severe headache, drowsiness, seizures or focal signs (encephalitis)`,`Sudden hearing loss during or after mumps`,`Mumps with severe epigastric pain and raised lipase (pancreatitis)`,`Rubella with purpura, mucosal bleeding or platelets under 20,000`,`Fever with rash, or rash contact, in a pregnant woman in the first 20 weeks`,`Newborn with cataract, PDA, purpura or failed hearing screen after maternal rash in pregnancy`],pearls:[`Mumps orchitis: inelastic tunica albuginea causes pressure necrosis - atrophy common, sterility rare.`,`Mumps exclusion is 5 days after parotitis; rubella exclusion is 7 days after rash.`,`Rubella's signature sign is post-auricular and suboccipital lymphadenopathy before the rash.`,`Gregg triad of CRS: cataract, PDA, sensorineural deafness; deafness is the commonest single defect.`,`CRS risk falls with gestation: about 90% before 12 weeks, 10-20% at 12-16 weeks, rare after 20 weeks.`,`Inadvertent MR in pregnancy is not an indication for termination.`,`Rubella IgM positive in pregnancy is confirmed with IgG avidity before any decision.`,`UIP gives MR (no mumps); IAP gives MMR - India still has mumps outbreaks.`,`CRS infants shed virus for up to a year - isolate and let only immune staff handle them.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-q1`,stem:`A 19-year-old hostel student develops bilateral parotid swelling followed 6 days later by fever and a painful swollen right testis. Which statement about this complication is correct?`,options:[`It commonly causes permanent sterility even when unilateral`,`Corticosteroids prevent testicular atrophy and should be given to all`,`Testicular atrophy is common but sterility is rare`,`It occurs mainly in pre-pubertal boys`],answer:2,explanation:`Mumps orchitis affects 15-30% of unvaccinated post-pubertal males; pressure necrosis under the tunica albuginea causes atrophy in up to half of affected testes, but sterility is rare even when bilateral. It is rare before puberty, and steroids relieve pain without preventing atrophy or subfertility.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-q2`,stem:`A woman at 9 weeks of gestation has rash and positive rubella IgM. What is the next best step before counselling about termination?`,options:[`Rubella IgG avidity testing with repeat serology`,`Immediate termination as IgM confirms infection`,`Give rubella immunoglobulin to prevent fetal infection`,`Give MR vaccine to boost immunity`],answer:0,explanation:`Rubella IgM has frequent false positives (parvovirus, EBV, rheumatoid factor), so low IgG avidity and serological change must confirm recent primary infection before counselling. Immunoglobulin does not prevent fetal infection, and MR vaccine is a live vaccine contraindicated in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-q3`,stem:`Under WHO CRS surveillance standards, which combination makes a suspected case 'clinically confirmed CRS'?`,options:[`Purpura and splenomegaly`,`Microcephaly and jaundice within 24 hours`,`Developmental delay alone`,`Cataract and a patent ductus arteriosus`],answer:3,explanation:`Clinically confirmed CRS needs two Group A findings, or one Group A plus one Group B. Cataract and congenital heart disease are both Group A. Purpura, splenomegaly, microcephaly, early jaundice and developmental delay are all Group B, so two of them, or one alone, do not qualify.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-q4`,stem:`In a vaccinated adolescent with suspected mumps during an outbreak, which test is preferred for confirmation?`,options:[`Serum amylase`,`Buccal swab RT-PCR after parotid massage`,`Mumps IgM on day 1`,`Throat culture on blood agar`],answer:1,explanation:`CDC recommends a buccal swab collected after massaging the parotid, tested by RT-PCR within a few days of parotitis. IgM is often negative or transient in vaccinated people, amylase rises in any parotitis, and blood agar culture grows bacteria, not mumps virus.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-q5`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman received MR vaccine and learns 2 weeks later that she was 4 weeks pregnant at the time. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Recommend termination because of high CRS risk`,`Give immunoglobulin and repeat vaccine after delivery`,`Reassure; inadvertent vaccination is not an indication for termination`,`Perform amniocentesis at 12 weeks to exclude CRS`],answer:2,explanation:`Registries of women vaccinated in pregnancy have documented no CRS, so WHO and CDC advise reassurance and continuation. Termination is not indicated, immunoglobulin has no role, and amniotic fluid testing is done after 18-20 weeks only for confirmed wild-type maternal infection, not after vaccination.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the clinical features and diagnosis of rubella. Discuss congenital rubella syndrome and its surveillance and prevention in India.`,openingLines:[`Rubella is a mild, droplet-spread exanthem caused by rubella virus (Matonaviridae), whose public health importance lies in congenital rubella syndrome after infection in early pregnancy.`,`Maternal infection before 12 weeks carries up to a 90% risk of CRS - cataract, heart disease and deafness - which India tracks by sentinel surveillance under the measles-rubella elimination goal.`],answer:[{heading:`Virology and transmission`,points:[`Rubella virus, humans only reservoir`,`Droplets; incubation 14 days; infectious 7 days before to 7 days after rash`,`CRS infants shed virus up to 1 year`]},{heading:`Clinical features of postnatal rubella`,points:[`Prodrome in adults; mild in children`,`Post-auricular and suboccipital nodes`,`Pink maculopapular rash face to trunk for 3 days; Forchheimer spots`,`Arthralgia in up to 70% of adult women; ITP, encephalitis rare`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`IgM ELISA from day 5; throat swab or urine RT-PCR`,`False-positive IgM; IgG avidity in pregnancy`,`Amniotic fluid PCR after 18-20 weeks`]},{heading:`Congenital rubella syndrome`,points:[`Risk by gestation table`,`Gregg triad: cataract, PDA, deafness`,`Others: microcephaly, retinopathy, blueberry muffin rash, radiolucent bones, hepatosplenomegaly`,`Late: diabetes, thyroid disease, progressive deafness`]},{heading:`WHO case classification and surveillance`,points:[`Suspected, clinically confirmed (Group A and B), lab-confirmed, CRI`,`Sentinel CRS surveillance in India since 2016`,`MR case-based fever-rash surveillance with IgM in network labs`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`MR in UIP at 9-12 and 16-24 months`,`MR campaign 2017-2019 for 9 months-15 years`,`Pre-conception and postpartum vaccination of non-immune women`,`Avoid pregnancy 28 days after vaccination; inadvertent vaccination not an indication for MTP`,`Elimination target 2026`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of fetal risk by gestation`,`WHO CRS Group A and Group B criteria box`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis of rubella`,marks:3},{part:`CRS features and risk by gestation`,marks:3},{part:`Surveillance case definitions`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention and national programme`,marks:2}],keywords:[`rubella`,`CRS`,`Gregg triad`,`IgG avidity`,`MR vaccine`,`sentinel surveillance`,`elimination 2026`]},{id:`infectious-fever-mumps-rubella-crs-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on complications of mumps.`,openingLines:[`Mumps is a systemic paramyxovirus infection; viraemia seeds glands and the CNS, so complications are commoner after puberty.`,`The important ones are orchitis, oophoritis, meningitis, pancreatitis and sensorineural deafness.`],answer:[{heading:`Glandular`,points:[`Orchitis in 15-30% of post-pubertal unvaccinated males; bilateral 10-30%; atrophy up to half, sterility rare`,`Oophoritis about 5%; mastitis in women`,`Pancreatitis about 4%; thyroiditis`]},{heading:`Neurological`,points:[`CSF pleocytosis in half; symptomatic meningitis 1-10%`,`Encephalitis rare but causes most deaths`,`Sensorineural deafness, usually unilateral, permanent`]},{heading:`Others`,points:[`Myocarditis, nephritis, arthritis`,`First-trimester miscarriage`]},{heading:`Management and prevention`,points:[`Supportive; scrotal support, ice, NSAIDs; steroids do not prevent atrophy`,`Exclude torsion with Doppler`,`MMR two doses; third dose in outbreaks (ACIP 2018)`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the viraemic spread from parotid to testis, pancreas, CNS and cochlea`],markSplit:[{part:`Glandular complications`,marks:2},{part:`Neurological complications`,marks:2},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`orchitis`,`tunica albuginea`,`aseptic meningitis`,`deafness`,`pancreatitis`,`MMR`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on mumps and rubella`,`WHO. Rubella vaccines: WHO position paper, Weekly Epidemiological Record 2020`,`WHO. Surveillance Standards for Vaccine-Preventable Diseases: Rubella and Congenital Rubella Syndrome, 2018`,`CDC. Epidemiology and Prevention of Vaccine-Preventable Diseases (Pink Book), mumps and rubella chapters, 2024`,`ACIP. Recommendation for a third dose of MMR vaccine during mumps outbreaks, MMWR 2018`,`RCOG. Rubella in pregnancy guidance (2016 update) and UKHSA viral rash in pregnancy guidance`,`MoHFW Government of India. Universal Immunisation Programme operational guidelines and Measles-Rubella Elimination strategy, 2024-2025`,`IAP Advisory Committee on Vaccines and Immunization Practices (ACVIP) schedule, 2023`]});var _=[];_.push({id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep`,title:`Hepatitis B vaccination and post-exposure protocols`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Hepatitis B vaccine** is a **recombinant HBsAg** vaccine given as **3 doses at 0, 1 and 6 months** (20 mcg IM deltoid in adults); a post-vaccination **anti-HBs of 10 mIU/mL or more** defines a lifelong responder, and **post-exposure prophylaxis** is decided by the exposed person's vaccine response and the source's HBsAg - **HBIG 0.06 mL/kg plus vaccine within 24 hours** for the unprotected.`,keywords:[`HBV vaccine`,`recombinant HBsAg vaccine`,`anti-HBs titre`,`10 mIU/mL`,`non-responder`,`HBIG`,`hepatitis B immunoglobulin`,`needlestick injury`,`occupational exposure`,`post-exposure prophylaxis`,`PEP`,`birth dose`,`Heplisav-B`,`CpG 1018`,`dialysis vaccine schedule`,`Engerix-B`,`NVHCP`,`healthcare worker vaccination`,`post-vaccination serology`,`escape mutant`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hepatitis B vaccine** is a **subunit vaccine of recombinant HBsAg** made in yeast (Saccharomyces or Hansenula) and adsorbed on aluminium hydroxide; it contains no live virus and cannot cause infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`WHO estimates **254 million people** were living with chronic HBV in 2022, with about **1.1 million deaths** a year from cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. [WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024]`,`**India** is a country of **intermediate endemicity** (HBsAg about **2-4%** in the general population, higher in tribal groups of Arunachal, Andaman and Nicobar and parts of the North-East), with an estimated **about 40 million** chronic carriers. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Most Indian chronic infection is acquired **perinatally or in early childhood**, which is why the **birth dose within 24 hours** is the single most effective intervention in the programme. [MoHFW UIP]`,`**Healthcare workers (HCWs)** have a needlestick risk several times higher than the public; Indian surveys repeatedly find that **only about half** of HCWs, and fewer nurses, housekeeping and lab staff, are fully vaccinated with documented anti-HBs. [Indian HCW surveys]`,`Risk of transmission after a **percutaneous injury** from an HBsAg-positive source is **up to 30%** if the source is **HBeAg-positive** and about **1-6% clinical hepatitis** (higher serological infection) if HBeAg-negative - far above HCV (about 0.2-1.8%) and HIV (about 0.3%). [CDC 2013]`,`HBV survives on environmental surfaces for **at least 7 days** and is infectious in tiny volumes of blood, which explains outbreaks from shared glucometers, dialysis machines and multi-dose vials. [CDC 2013]`,`The vaccine is **95% effective** in preventing chronic infection and was the **first anti-cancer vaccine**, reducing hepatocellular carcinoma in Taiwan after universal infant vaccination. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Who must be vaccinated and risk factors for exposure`,points:[`**Universal infant vaccination**: birth dose (monovalent, 0.5 mL, 10 mcg) within 24 hours, then **pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib) at 6, 10 and 14 weeks** under India's Universal Immunisation Programme. [MoHFW UIP]`,`**Healthcare and laboratory workers, students** of medicine, nursing and dentistry, and **sanitation and biomedical waste handlers** must complete vaccination and documented anti-HBs testing before clinical exposure. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Patients on haemodialysis and with CKD**, people who **inject drugs**, **men who have sex with men**, sex workers, people with **multiple partners or an STI**, and **household and sexual contacts** of HBsAg-positive people are high priority. [ACIP 2022]`,`People with **HIV, HCV, chronic liver disease, diabetes**, those in prisons and institutions, recipients of **repeated transfusion** (thalassaemia, haemophilia) and travellers to endemic areas should be vaccinated. [ACIP 2022]`,`**ACIP 2022** moved the US to **universal vaccination of all adults aged 19-59 years** (and adults 60 or older with risk factors or who request it), because risk-based programmes missed most infections. [ACIP 2022]`,`**Exposure risk is highest** with **hollow-bore needles** (phlebotomy, IV cannula), deep injury, visible blood on the device and a source with **high HBV DNA or HBeAg**. [CDC 2013]`,`**Host factors for poor vaccine response**: age over 40, **male sex, obesity, smoking**, diabetes, **CKD and dialysis**, HIV, immunosuppressive drugs, chronic liver disease, certain HLA types (DRB1*03, DQB1*02) and **gluteal injection**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Immunology - why the schedule and titre work`,points:[`Vaccine HBsAg is taken up by dendritic cells and presented to **CD4 T-helper cells**, which drive **B cells to make anti-HBs** against the **a determinant** of the S protein - shared by all HBV genotypes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-HBs neutralises virions** before they enter hepatocytes through the **NTCP receptor**; an anti-HBs of **10 mIU/mL or more** after the primary series correlates with protection. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **first two doses prime** the response and the **third dose at 6 months boosts** affinity maturation and memory, which is why a longer gap before the last dose gives higher titres. [Plotkin 8e]`,`**Immune memory outlasts antibody**: anti-HBs falls below 10 mIU/mL in many responders after 10-15 years, but **anamnestic memory protects against chronic infection for at least 30 years**, so routine boosters are not needed. [WHO position paper 2017]`,`**Incubation is long (45-180 days)**, so active vaccine given promptly after exposure can generate antibody before the virus establishes infection; **HBIG** fills the gap with passive antibody in the first weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HBIG** is pooled human plasma with high anti-HBs; its half-life is about **3-4 weeks**, so a non-responder needs **two doses one month apart** to cover the incubation window. [CDC 2013]`,`**Vaccine escape mutants** (classically **G145R** in the a determinant) can infect vaccinated people and evade some HBsAg assays, but they have not undermined population control. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dialysis and CKD** patients have **uraemic T-cell dysfunction**, so they need **higher antigen dose (40 mcg) and more doses**, and their antibody wanes faster - hence annual anti-HBs. [CDC dialysis guidance]`]},{heading:`Classification - exposure type and vaccine response status`,points:[`**Exposure types**: **percutaneous** (needlestick, sharp), **mucosal** (splash to eye, mouth), **non-intact skin**, **sexual**, and **perinatal**; intact skin contact with blood is **not** an exposure. [CDC 2013]`,`**Responder**: anti-HBs **10 mIU/mL or more** at 1-2 months after a complete series - protected for life, needs **no further testing, boosters or PEP** whatever the source. [CDC 2013]`,`**Non-responder after one series**: anti-HBs under 10 mIU/mL after 3 doses - give a **second complete series** and retest; about **30-50%** respond to the second series. [CDC 2013]`,`**Confirmed non-responder**: anti-HBs under 10 mIU/mL after **two complete series (6 doses)**; first check **HBsAg and anti-HBc** to exclude chronic infection as the reason. [CDC 2013]`,`**Response unknown**: vaccinated but never tested - the common Indian HCW situation; test anti-HBs at the time of exposure and act on the result. [CDC 2013]`,`**Unvaccinated or incompletely vaccinated**: no protection assumed; needs HBIG plus vaccine if the source is positive or unknown. [CDC 2013]`,`**Source status**: **HBsAg-positive**, **HBsAg-negative**, or **unknown or untestable** (discarded needle); a high-risk unknown source is managed as positive. [CDC 2013]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - what the exposed person reports`,points:[`Take the **exact time**, mechanism, device (hollow or solid), **depth**, visible blood and whether gloves were worn - these grade the risk and decide urgency. [NACO PEP 2021]`,`Ask the **vaccination history with dates** and any recorded **anti-HBs** value; a vaccine card without a titre counts as **response unknown**. [CDC 2013]`,`Record the **source**: known diagnosis, HBsAg, HIV and HCV status, ART, HBV DNA, injecting drug use; obtain **informed consent** for source testing. [NACO PEP 2021]`,`Ask about **pregnancy, breastfeeding**, prior allergy to yeast or vaccine, and current immunosuppression - none of these contraindicates HBV vaccine or HBIG. [ACIP 2018]`,`Acute hepatitis B after exposure presents after **1-6 months** with anorexia, nausea, **dark urine, jaundice** and right upper quadrant pain; **about 70% of adults are anicteric**. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **serum-sickness-like prodrome** (urticaria, arthralgia, fever) from immune complexes may precede jaundice by 1-2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`Vaccine side effects reported: **sore arm (3-29%)**, low-grade fever (1-6%); anaphylaxis is about **1 per 1.1 million doses**; there is **no link with multiple sclerosis or Guillain-Barre syndrome**. [WHO position paper 2017]`]},{heading:`Examination of the exposed person`,points:[`Inspect the **wound**: depth, bleeding, foreign body; a deep puncture with visible blood is the highest-risk injury. [CDC 2013]`,`Look for **pre-existing liver disease** in the HCW (jaundice, stigmata) - an occult HBsAg-positive HCW explains non-response and needs care, not vaccine. [CDC 2013]`,`Record **BMI and smoking**, which predict poor response and favour a more immunogenic vaccine for revaccination. [ACIP 2018]`,`At follow-up, look for **icterus, tender hepatomegaly** and flapping tremor; asterixis or confusion means **acute liver failure** and immediate referral. [Harrison 22e]`,`Injection technique matters: give the vaccine **IM in the deltoid** (anterolateral thigh in infants) with a 25 mm needle; **gluteal injection into fat lowers response**. [ACIP 2018]`]},{heading:`Differential and interpretation pitfalls`,points:[`**Low anti-HBs years after vaccination** does not mean non-response; it is waning antibody with intact memory - one **challenge (booster) dose** and retest at 1-2 months separates the two. [CDC 2013]`,`**Isolated anti-HBs from HBIG** (passive) can mimic vaccine response for 4-6 months, so anti-HBs should be tested **4-6 months after HBIG** to judge the vaccine. [CDC 2013]`,`**Anti-HBs with anti-HBc** means immunity from **past natural infection**, not vaccination; anti-HBs alone means vaccine immunity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HBsAg transiently positive for up to 2-3 weeks after a vaccine dose** (especially in dialysis patients and infants) is antigen, not infection - repeat after a month. [CDC dialysis guidance]`,`**Isolated anti-HBc** may be a false positive, a window period or occult HBV; check HBV DNA in the immunosuppressed and vaccinate the rest. [AASLD 2018]`,`Post-exposure jaundice is also caused by **HCV, HAV, HEV, drug-induced liver injury** (ART PEP regimens, antituberculars) and **leptospirosis** - test, do not assume HBV. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Exposed HCW at baseline**: **anti-HBs** (if response unknown), and **HBsAg and total anti-HBc** when unvaccinated or a non-responder, plus **anti-HIV and anti-HCV** at baseline. [CDC 2013]`,`**Source**: **HBsAg**, anti-HCV (with HCV RNA if positive or high risk) and **rapid anti-HIV** with consent; a rapid HBsAg card result is acceptable for an immediate decision. [NACO PEP 2021]`,`**Post-vaccination serology** (anti-HBs) for HCWs, dialysis, HIV, immunocompromised, sexual partners of carriers and infants of HBsAg-positive mothers - test **1-2 months after the last dose**. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Protective cut-off**: anti-HBs **10 mIU/mL or more**; quantitative ELISA or CLIA is required - a qualitative card test is not enough to document responder status. [CDC 2013]`,`**Follow-up after an exposure** from an HBsAg-positive source in an unprotected person: **HBsAg and total anti-HBc at 6 months**; check ALT if symptomatic. [CDC 2013]`,`**Infants of HBsAg-positive mothers**: test **HBsAg and anti-HBs at 9-12 months** (1-2 months after the last dose); HBsAg-negative with anti-HBs under 10 needs a repeat series. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Dialysis patients**: anti-HBs **annually**, and HBsAg every month for susceptible patients (monthly surveillance in dialysis units). [CDC dialysis guidance]`,`**HCV after exposure** (cross-reference): baseline anti-HCV, then **HCV RNA at 3-6 weeks** and anti-HCV at 4-6 months; there is no HCV PEP. [CDC 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - vaccination schedules`,points:[`**Standard adult schedule**: **20 mcg (1 mL) IM deltoid at 0, 1 and 6 months**; children and adolescents up to 19 years get **10 mcg (0.5 mL)** at the same intervals. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Interrupted series**: never restart - **continue where it stopped**; minimum intervals are 4 weeks between doses 1 and 2, 8 weeks between 2 and 3, and **16 weeks between 1 and 3**. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Accelerated schedule** (urgent travel, new HCW starting duty): **0, 7 and 21 days** plus a **booster at 12 months**, or **0, 1, 2 and 12 months**. [Engerix-B prescribing information]`,`**CpG-adjuvanted Heplisav-B** (20 mcg with CpG 1018) for adults 18 years or older: **2 doses one month apart**, with higher seroprotection (90-95%) in older, obese, diabetic and CKD patients; not routinely marketed in India. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Haemodialysis and CKD stage 4-5**: **40 mcg at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months** (two 20 mcg doses in one sitting, or a 40 mcg dialysis formulation), with annual anti-HBs and a **booster when it falls under 10**. [CDC dialysis guidance]`,`**HIV**: give the standard or double dose (40 mcg) series; response is best when **CD4 is above 350** and viral load is suppressed - but do not delay vaccination waiting for CD4 to rise. [NIH OI guidelines 2024]`,`**Pre-vaccination screening** with HBsAg, anti-HBc and anti-HBs is worthwhile in high-prevalence groups (household contacts, PWID, HIV); for HCWs, screen HBsAg and vaccinate in the same visit. [ACIP 2023]`,`**Non-responder after one series**: give a **second full series** (or a double-dose or Heplisav-B series) and retest; after **6 doses** with no response, stop vaccinating and counsel the person as susceptible. [CDC 2013]`]},{heading:`Post-exposure management (occupational and non-occupational)`,points:[`**First aid within minutes**: wash the wound with **soap and running water**, flush mucosa or eyes with water or saline for several minutes; **do not squeeze, scrub or apply bleach**. [NACO PEP 2021]`,`**Documented responder**: **no HBIG or vaccine needed**, whatever the source status. [CDC 2013]`,`**Unvaccinated or incompletely vaccinated, source HBsAg-positive or unknown**: **HBIG 0.06 mL/kg IM** (about 1000-2000 IU in adults) **plus the first vaccine dose at a different site**, then complete the series. [CDC 2013]`,`**Response unknown**: test anti-HBs now; **10 or more** - nothing further; **under 10** with a positive or unknown source - **HBIG once plus a vaccine booster**, then retest anti-HBs 1-2 months later. [CDC 2013]`,`**Non-responder after one series**: **HBIG once plus start a second series**; **confirmed non-responder after 6 doses**: **HBIG twice, one month apart**. [CDC 2013]`,`**Source HBsAg-negative**: no HBIG; complete or start vaccination in the unvaccinated, booster in the unknown-response HCW with low anti-HBs. [CDC 2013]`,`**Timing**: HBIG is most effective **within 24 hours**, and is of uncertain value after **7 days** for percutaneous and **14 days for sexual exposure**. [CDC 2013]`,`**Sexual and household exposure** to an HBsAg-positive person: same algorithm; **sexual assault** survivors get vaccine (and HBIG if the assailant is known positive) with HIV PEP and STI cover - see the HIV special situations topic. [CDC STI 2021]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and institutional protocols`,points:[`Every hospital needs a **24-hour sharps injury protocol**: report to the casualty medical officer, register in the **needlestick injury register**, test source and HCW, and give PEP within hours. [NACO PEP 2021]`,`**Hospital infection control committee** must keep a **vaccination and anti-HBs register** for all staff, including contract housekeeping and waste handlers. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Dialysis units**: isolate **HBsAg-positive patients in a separate room with dedicated machines** and staff, monthly HBsAg for susceptible patients, and vaccinate all new patients. [CDC dialysis guidance]`,`**Admit** the exposed person only for complications: acute hepatitis with **INR over 1.5, encephalopathy** or bilirubin rising - suspect acute liver failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonate of HBsAg-positive mother** in the labour room: **vaccine (10 mcg) plus HBIG 0.5 mL (100 IU) IM within 12 hours** at separate thighs; breastfeeding is allowed. [MoHFW NVHCP 2018]`,`HBIG is stored at **2-8 C** and supplied free at many government hospitals for newborns under **NVHCP**; ensure stock in the labour room and casualty. [NVHCP 2018]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy and breastfeeding**: vaccine and HBIG are **safe**; an exposed pregnant HCW gets PEP exactly as a non-pregnant one. [ACIP 2018]`,`**HBsAg-positive pregnant woman**: check HBV DNA or HBeAg; **tenofovir disoproxil 300 mg daily from 24-28 weeks** when HBV DNA is 200,000 IU/mL or more (or HBeAg-positive); **WHO 2024 allows TDF for all HBsAg-positive women** where DNA testing is unavailable. [WHO HBV 2024]`,`**Preterm under 2000 g** born to an HBsAg-positive or unknown mother: vaccine plus HBIG within 12 hours, and the birth dose **does not count** - give **3 more doses**. [ACIP 2018]`,`**CKD before dialysis**: vaccinate **early (eGFR above 30)**, when response is much better than on dialysis. [KDIGO]`,`**Elderly, obese, diabetic and smokers**: expect lower titres; check anti-HBs after the series in those at continuing risk and consider an adjuvanted vaccine for revaccination. [ACIP 2018]`,`**Immunosuppressed (biologics, chemotherapy, transplant)**: vaccinate **before** immunosuppression; HBsAg and anti-HBc screening before rituximab or chemotherapy is mandatory for **HBV reactivation** prophylaxis. [AASLD 2018]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if anti-HBs testing is unavailable, an exposed HCW of unknown status with a positive or unknown source should get **HBIG plus a vaccine dose** rather than wait. [CDC 2013]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Without prophylaxis, **perinatal infection becomes chronic in about 90%**, childhood infection in 25-50%, and **adult infection in under 5%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Birth dose plus HBIG** cuts transmission from HBeAg-positive mothers from **70-90% to about 5-10%**, and adding **antenatal tenofovir** brings it to **under 1-2%**. [WHO HBV 2024]`,`**Acute liver failure** complicates under 1% of adult acute hepatitis B but has high mortality without liver transplantation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic HBV** carries a lifetime risk of **cirrhosis (up to 40%)** and **hepatocellular carcinoma**; this is what vaccination prevents. [EASL 2025]`,`Vaccine adverse events are trivial; the only absolute contraindication is **anaphylaxis to a previous dose or to yeast**. [WHO position paper 2017]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Repeat **anti-HBs 1-2 months** after a completed series or after any booster given for low titre. [CDC 2013]`,`After HBIG, test anti-HBs **4-6 months later** (after passive antibody has cleared) to judge the vaccine response. [CDC 2013]`,`**Six-month HBsAg and anti-HBc** in an exposed susceptible person; counsel **safe sex and no blood donation** until infection is excluded. [CDC 2013]`,`Refer to hepatology an exposed HCW who becomes **HBsAg-positive**, has **ALT over 10 times normal**, rising INR or jaundice. [EASL 2025]`,`**HCW who is HBsAg-positive** should be evaluated by an expert panel; with **HBV DNA under 1000 IU/mL** (on treatment if needed) most can continue practice, including exposure-prone procedures in many policies. [SHEA 2010]`,`Confirmed non-responders must be **counselled that they are susceptible** and need HBIG after every significant exposure. [CDC 2013]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme link`,points:[`**National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP, 2018)** aims to **eliminate HCV and reduce HBV** burden, with free testing, tenofovir, **HCW vaccination** and **HBIG for newborns of positive mothers**. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**UIP** covers the birth dose and three pentavalent doses; NFHS-5 (2019-21) shows **HepB birth dose coverage still well below pentavalent coverage**, mainly from home and late deliveries. [NFHS-5]`,`**Antenatal HBsAg screening** of every pregnant woman is the entry point to tenofovir and newborn HBIG; WHO's triple elimination initiative covers **HIV, syphilis and HBV**. [WHO HBV 2024]`,`**Standard precautions**: gloves, **no recapping**, puncture-proof sharps containers at the point of use, **safety-engineered devices** and auto-disable syringes. [BMW Rules 2016]`,`**Safe injection, safe blood**: NAT-screened blood where available, single-use glucometer lancets and dialysis-unit hygiene prevent nosocomial outbreaks. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Counsel **household contacts and sexual partners** of HBsAg-positive patients to test and get vaccinated; **condoms until the partner's anti-HBs is 10 or more**. [ACIP 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ACIP 2022**: universal hepatitis B vaccination for **all adults 19-59 years**, and for those 60 or older with risk factors or on request. [ACIP 2022]`,`**CDC 2023 screening**: **once-in-a-lifetime triple-panel test (HBsAg, anti-HBs, total anti-HBc)** for every adult 18 years or older - it identifies infection, immunity and the need for vaccine together. [CDC 2023]`,`**WHO 2024 hepatitis B guideline**: simplified treatment criteria, and **tenofovir prophylaxis for pregnant women** with HBV DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more or HBeAg-positive - or for all HBsAg-positive women where testing is unavailable. [WHO HBV 2024]`,`**EASL 2025 HBV guideline**: broader treatment indications and reinforces vaccination of all at-risk adults and screening before immunosuppression. [EASL 2025]`,`**ACIP December 2025** voted to drop the universal US birth dose for infants of **HBsAg-negative mothers** in favour of individual decision-making; **WHO, AAP and India's UIP retain the universal birth dose within 24 hours** - write the WHO/India position in the exam. [ACIP 2025]`,`**NACO PEP guidance (2021)** keeps HBV assessment inside the integrated occupational exposure protocol with HIV PEP - see the HIV special situations topic. [NACO PEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **needlestick injury in a resident** - write first aid, risk grading, source testing, the HBV table by vaccine status, HIV PEP and HCV follow-up in one flow. [CDC 2013]`,`Commonest mistake: giving **HBIG to a documented responder** - a responder needs nothing, ever. [CDC 2013]`,`Second mistake: **restarting an interrupted series** - doses are never repeated, only completed. [ACIP 2018]`,`Know the numbers: **10 mIU/mL** protective, test at **1-2 months**, HBIG **0.06 mL/kg**, newborn HBIG **0.5 mL within 12 hours**, non-responder after **6 doses**. [CDC 2013]`,`Viva: why test anti-HBc in a non-responder? - because **chronic HBV** is a hidden cause of non-response. [CDC 2013]`,`Viva: why two HBIG doses in a confirmed non-responder? - HBIG's **half-life is 3-4 weeks** and the incubation period is up to 6 months. [CDC 2013]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Post-exposure management of HBV by the exposed person's status (CDC 2013)`,columns:[`Exposed person`,`Source HBsAg-positive or unknown`,`Source HBsAg-negative`,`Follow-up`],rows:[[`Documented responder (anti-HBs 10 or more)`,`No action`,`No action`,`None`],[`Unvaccinated or incomplete series`,`HBIG 0.06 mL/kg once plus start or complete vaccine series`,`Start or complete vaccine series`,`Anti-HBs 1-2 months after series; HBsAg and anti-HBc at 6 months if source positive`],[`Vaccinated, response unknown - anti-HBs now 10 or more`,`No action`,`No action`,`None`],[`Vaccinated, response unknown - anti-HBs now under 10`,`HBIG once plus one vaccine booster`,`One vaccine booster`,`Anti-HBs 1-2 months after booster (4-6 months if HBIG given); complete second series if still under 10`],[`Non-responder after one series (3 doses)`,`HBIG once plus start second series`,`Start second series`,`Anti-HBs 1-2 months after second series`],[`Non-responder after two series (6 doses)`,`HBIG twice, 1 month apart`,`No action`,`HBsAg and anti-HBc at 6 months if source positive`]]},{heading:`Drugs and vaccines for HBV prevention`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Recombinant HBV vaccine (adult)`,`20 mcg (1 mL) IM deltoid`,`0, 1, 6 months; accelerated 0, 7, 21 days plus 12-month booster`,`Sore arm, low fever; anaphylaxis to previous dose or yeast is the only contraindication`],[`Recombinant HBV vaccine (child to 19 y)`,`10 mcg (0.5 mL) IM thigh (infant) or deltoid`,`Birth dose within 24 h, then pentavalent 6, 10, 14 weeks`,`Same; safe in preterm and pregnancy`],[`Dialysis / CKD 4-5 schedule`,`40 mcg IM (2 x 20 mcg or dialysis formulation)`,`0, 1, 2, 6 months; annual anti-HBs, booster if under 10`,`Lower and shorter-lived response; vaccinate before dialysis`],[`CpG 1018-adjuvanted HBsAg (Heplisav-B)`,`20 mcg IM`,`2 doses at 0 and 1 month (adults 18 or older)`,`Not routinely available in India; data in pregnancy limited`],[`Hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG) - adult PEP`,`0.06 mL/kg IM (about 1000-2000 IU)`,`Single dose within 24 h (up to 7 days); repeat at 1 month in confirmed non-responders`,`Local pain; rare hypersensitivity; give vaccine at a different site`],[`HBIG - newborn of HBsAg-positive mother`,`0.5 mL (100 IU) IM anterolateral thigh`,`Once within 12 h of birth, with birth-dose vaccine`,`Breastfeeding allowed; test infant at 9-12 months`],[`Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (antenatal)`,`300 mg orally once daily`,`From 24-28 weeks until delivery or 1-3 months postpartum`,`Renal tubular toxicity, bone loss; check creatinine; ALT flare after stopping`]]},{heading:`Interpreting hepatitis B serology`,columns:[`HBsAg`,`Anti-HBc (total)`,`Anti-HBs`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Negative`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Susceptible - vaccinate`],[`Negative`,`Negative`,`10 mIU/mL or more`,`Immune from vaccination`],[`Negative`,`Positive`,`Positive`,`Immune from past natural infection`],[`Positive`,`Positive (IgM)`,`Negative`,`Acute HBV infection`],[`Positive`,`Positive (IgG)`,`Negative`,`Chronic HBV infection`],[`Negative`,`Positive`,`Negative`,`Isolated anti-HBc: false positive, window, or occult HBV - check HBV DNA if immunosuppressed`],[`Negative`,`Negative`,`Positive within 4-6 months of HBIG`,`Passive antibody - cannot judge vaccine response yet`]]},{heading:`Why a person fails to reach anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL - and what to do`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Action`],rows:[[`Waning antibody in a past responder`,`Completed series years ago, never tested`,`One booster, retest at 1-2 months`],[`Host factors (age over 40, obesity, smoking, diabetes)`,`Low titre after a correct series`,`Second series, double dose or adjuvanted vaccine`],[`CKD or dialysis`,`Raised creatinine, on dialysis`,`40 mcg schedule at 0, 1, 2, 6 months; annual titre`],[`HIV or immunosuppression`,`Low CD4, biologics, steroids`,`Double dose; revaccinate after immune recovery`],[`Chronic HBV infection`,`HBsAg or anti-HBc positive`,`Stop vaccinating; refer for HBV care`],[`Poor technique`,`Gluteal injection, broken cold chain`,`Repeat series IM deltoid with proper storage`]]}],redFlags:[`Needlestick from an HBsAg-positive or unknown source in an unvaccinated or non-responder HCW - HBIG and vaccine within 24 hours, do not wait for reports.`,`Exposed person with jaundice, INR over 1.5 or confusion - acute liver failure; refer to a liver unit.`,`Newborn of an HBsAg-positive mother without HBIG and vaccine by 12 hours - give both now.`,`HBsAg-positive pregnant woman with high HBV DNA or HBeAg not on tenofovir by 28 weeks.`,`HBsAg or anti-HBc positive patient about to start rituximab, chemotherapy or high-dose steroids - needs antiviral prophylaxis first.`,`Combined exposure with HIV risk - HIV PEP must start within 72 hours, ideally 2 hours.`,`HBV case in a dialysis unit - outbreak investigation and isolation.`],pearls:[`**Responder once, protected forever** - no boosters, no PEP, no repeat titres in immunocompetent HCWs.`,`**10-1-2**: protective titre 10 mIU/mL, tested 1-2 months after the last dose.`,`**Never restart** an interrupted series - just complete it.`,`**HBIG 0.06 mL/kg within 24 hours**; newborn HBIG 0.5 mL within 12 hours with the birth dose.`,`**Non-responder = 6 doses** with anti-HBs under 10 and HBsAg negative; gets two HBIG doses after exposure.`,`**Rule of 3s for needlestick risk**: HBV 30%, HCV 3%, HIV 0.3% (classical teaching; current HCV figure is lower).`,`**Anti-HBs alone = vaccine; anti-HBs plus anti-HBc = past infection.**`,`**Dialysis needs 40 mcg x 4 doses** and annual anti-HBs.`,`Check **HBsAg and anti-HBc** in every non-responder - a hidden carrier does not make antibody.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-q1`,stem:`A nursing officer had anti-HBs of 120 mIU/mL one month after her third hepatitis B vaccine dose 8 years ago. She now sustains a deep needlestick from an HBsAg-positive patient. What is the correct HBV management?`,options:[`HBIG 0.06 mL/kg plus one vaccine booster`,`Test anti-HBs now and give HBIG if under 10 mIU/mL`,`No HBIG or vaccine is needed`,`A full repeat 3-dose vaccine series`],answer:2,explanation:`A documented responder (anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL or more after a complete series) has immune memory that protects against chronic infection for decades, so no HBIG, booster or retesting is needed whatever the source. Testing anti-HBs now is for people whose response was never documented, HBIG with booster is for unknown-response people with a low titre, and repeating the series is for non-responders.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-q2`,stem:`A laboratory technician has anti-HBs under 10 mIU/mL after two complete hepatitis B vaccine series, and HBsAg and anti-HBc are negative. After a needlestick from an HBsAg-positive source, what is recommended?`,options:[`HBIG once plus a third vaccine series`,`HBIG two doses, one month apart`,`Tenofovir for 4 weeks`,`No prophylaxis because he is a known non-responder`],answer:1,explanation:`After six doses without response the person is a confirmed non-responder; further vaccine is futile, so passive protection is given as two HBIG doses a month apart to cover the long incubation (HBIG half-life is 3-4 weeks). HBIG once with a new series is for non-responders after only one series; tenofovir is not an HBV PEP standard; doing nothing leaves him unprotected.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-q3`,stem:`A healthy 25-year-old received hepatitis B vaccine doses in January and February and then missed the third dose. He presents in December of the same year. What should be done?`,options:[`Give the third dose now`,`Restart the series from dose one`,`Check anti-HBs and give the third dose only if under 10`,`Give two doses one month apart`],answer:0,explanation:`An interrupted hepatitis B series is never restarted; the missed dose is simply given, because longer intervals before the last dose actually improve the final titre. Restarting wastes doses, checking anti-HBs mid-series is not meaningful for deciding the third dose, and two extra doses are not required for a healthy adult.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-q4`,stem:`A baby is born to a mother who is HBsAg-positive and HBeAg-positive. Which is the correct newborn prophylaxis?`,options:[`Hepatitis B vaccine at 6 weeks with pentavalent`,`HBIG alone within 72 hours`,`Hepatitis B vaccine and HBIG within 12 hours at separate sites`,`Vaccine within 24 hours and avoid breastfeeding`],answer:2,explanation:`Infants of HBsAg-positive mothers need both active and passive immunisation - birth-dose vaccine plus HBIG 0.5 mL (100 IU) within 12 hours, at different thighs - which reduces transmission to about 5-10%, further lowered by antenatal tenofovir. Waiting until 6 weeks or giving HBIG alone loses the perinatal window, and breastfeeding is not contraindicated.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-q5`,stem:`Which vaccine schedule is recommended for an adult starting maintenance haemodialysis who is susceptible to hepatitis B?`,options:[`20 mcg at 0, 1 and 6 months`,`40 mcg at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months with annual anti-HBs`,`10 mcg intradermal monthly for 6 months`,`Single 40 mcg dose with a booster every 5 years`],answer:1,explanation:`Uraemic immune dysfunction blunts and shortens the response, so dialysis patients receive higher-dose 40 mcg vaccine at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months, with annual anti-HBs and a booster when it drops below 10 mIU/mL. The standard 20 mcg schedule gives poor response in dialysis; intradermal regimens are an off-label rescue option, and a single dose with fixed 5-yearly boosters is not a recommended schedule.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`A postgraduate resident sustains a needlestick injury while drawing blood from a patient with chronic hepatitis B. Describe the immediate management, the post-exposure protocol for hepatitis B based on vaccination status, and the follow-up. Add a note on hepatitis B vaccination of healthcare workers.`,openingLines:[`Percutaneous exposure to an HBsAg-positive source carries a transmission risk of up to 30% (HBeAg-positive) and is managed by first aid, source testing, and post-exposure prophylaxis decided by the exposed person's anti-HBs status.`,`A documented responder (anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL or more after a complete series) needs nothing; the unprotected need HBIG 0.06 mL/kg plus vaccine within 24 hours (CDC 2013).`],answer:[{heading:`Immediate first aid and reporting`,points:[`Wash with soap and running water; do not squeeze or apply antiseptic bleach; flush mucosa with water.`,`Report to the casualty medical officer, register the injury, grade the exposure (hollow needle, depth, blood).`]},{heading:`Risk assessment and testing`,points:[`Source: HBsAg, anti-HCV, rapid HIV with consent.`,`Exposed: anti-HBs if response unknown; HBsAg and anti-HBc if unvaccinated or non-responder; baseline HIV and HCV.`]},{heading:`HBV PEP by vaccination status`,points:[`Responder - no action.`,`Unvaccinated - HBIG 0.06 mL/kg plus vaccine series.`,`Response unknown - anti-HBs now; under 10: HBIG plus booster.`,`Non-responder after 3 doses - HBIG plus second series; after 6 doses - HBIG twice, 1 month apart.`,`HBIG within 24 h, not beyond 7 days.`]},{heading:`HIV and HCV in the same exposure`,points:[`HIV PEP (TDF-3TC-DTG for 28 days) within 72 h if source positive or unknown high risk.`,`HCV: no PEP; HCV RNA at 3-6 weeks, anti-HCV at 4-6 months.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Anti-HBs 1-2 months after series (4-6 months if HBIG given).`,`HBsAg and anti-HBc at 6 months; counsel no blood donation and safe sex meanwhile.`]},{heading:`Vaccination of HCWs`,points:[`20 mcg IM deltoid 0, 1, 6 months before clinical exposure; anti-HBs at 1-2 months.`,`Under 10: second series; non-responder after 6 doses - check HBsAg/anti-HBc, counsel.`,`No boosters for responders; NVHCP mandates HCW vaccination; prevention by standard precautions.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of HBV PEP by vaccine status of the exposed HCW`,`Table of HBV serology interpretation`],markSplit:[{part:`First aid and risk assessment`,marks:2},{part:`HBV PEP algorithm`,marks:4},{part:`HIV/HCV and follow-up`,marks:2},{part:`HCW vaccination`,marks:2}],keywords:[`needlestick`,`HBIG`,`anti-HBs`,`non-responder`,`PEP`,`occupational exposure`]},{id:`infectious-fever-hepatitis-b-vaccine-pep-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on non-responders to hepatitis B vaccine.`,openingLines:[`A non-responder is a person whose anti-HBs stays under 10 mIU/mL 1-2 months after two complete hepatitis B vaccine series (6 doses), with HBsAg and anti-HBc negative.`,`About 5-10% of healthy adults fail a first series; about half of them respond to a second.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Age over 40, male, obesity, smoking, diabetes, CKD, HIV, immunosuppression, HLA types, gluteal injection.`,`Hidden chronic HBV - always test HBsAg and anti-HBc.`]},{heading:`Approach`,points:[`Distinguish waning antibody: one booster and retest at 1-2 months.`,`Second series - standard, double dose 40 mcg, or CpG-adjuvanted vaccine (2 doses).`,`Retest; after 6 doses stop vaccinating.`]},{heading:`Management after exposure`,points:[`HBIG 0.06 mL/kg twice, one month apart, if source HBsAg-positive or unknown.`,`Counsel that they remain susceptible; record status in the HCW register.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: anti-HBs under 10 - booster - second series - non-responder`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Revaccination strategy`,marks:2},{part:`Post-exposure management`,marks:1}],keywords:[`non-responder`,`anti-HBs`,`revaccination`,`HBIG`,`Heplisav-B`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Acute viral hepatitis and immunisation chapters`,`CDC. Guidance for evaluating health-care personnel for hepatitis B virus protection and for administering postexposure management. MMWR 2013`,`Schillie S et al. Prevention of hepatitis B virus infection in the United States: recommendations of ACIP. MMWR 2018`,`Weng MK et al. Universal hepatitis B vaccination in adults aged 19-59 years: updated ACIP recommendations. MMWR 2022`,`Conners EE et al. Screening and testing for hepatitis B virus infection: CDC recommendations, MMWR 2023`,`WHO Guidelines for the prevention, diagnosis, care and treatment for people with chronic hepatitis B infection, 2024`,`National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP) operational and technical guidelines, MoHFW, 2018`,`NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment (post-exposure prophylaxis), 2021`]});var v=[],y=[],b=[];b.push({id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides`,title:`Strongyloidiasis and hyperinfection`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Strongyloidiasis** is infection with the soil-transmitted nematode **Strongyloides stercoralis**, which alone among human helminths completes its life cycle inside the host by **autoinfection** and so persists for decades; when cell-mediated immunity falls - above all with **corticosteroids** or **HTLV-1** - it explodes into **hyperinfection or disseminated disease with Gram-negative sepsis** (mortality up to 60-85%), which is why anyone from an endemic area should be **screened or given ivermectin before steroids**.`,keywords:[`Strongyloides stercoralis`,`strongyloidiasis`,`hyperinfection syndrome`,`disseminated strongyloidiasis`,`autoinfection`,`larva currens`,`filariform larva`,`rhabditiform larva`,`ivermectin`,`albendazole`,`HTLV-1`,`corticosteroid-induced hyperinfection`,`agar plate culture`,`Baermann technique`,`Strongyloides serology`,`Gram-negative meningitis`,`eosinophilia`,`pre-emptive ivermectin`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Strongyloidiasis** is infection by the intestinal nematode **Strongyloides stercoralis** (and rarely **S. fuelleborni** in Africa and Papua New Guinea); it is classed by WHO as a **soil-transmitted helminth** and a neglected tropical disease. [WHO 2020]`,`The global burden is estimated at about **600 million infections** (Buonfrate 2020 modelling: 614 million), concentrated in South-East Asia, sub-Saharan Africa and the Western Pacific - far more than older estimates of 30-100 million. [Buonfrate 2020]`,`**South-East Asia including India** carries the largest share of the global burden; Indian stool surveys report low single-digit prevalence, but these use **direct microscopy**, which misses most light infections, so the true burden is underestimated. [Harrison 22e]`,`Higher rates are reported from **rural, humid and agricultural settings** - the North-East, tea-garden workers of Assam, Kerala, coastal Odisha and West Bengal - and in institutionalised people with poor sanitation. [Harrison 22e]`,`The infection is **lifelong without treatment** because of autoinfection - cases have presented with hyperinfection **more than 40-60 years** after leaving an endemic area (e.g. Second World War prisoners of war in South-East Asia). [Harrison 22e]`,`Most chronic infections (about **50-70%**) are **asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic**, so the first presentation may be fatal hyperinfection after an innocent steroid course. [Mandell 9e]`,`The **COVID-19 pandemic** produced a cluster of reports of hyperinfection after **dexamethasone and tocilizumab**, renewing the advice to screen or treat empirically before immunosuppression in endemic regions. [Stauffer 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, life cycle and risk factors`,points:[`**Free-living cycle**: rhabditiform larvae passed in stool develop in warm moist soil into free-living adults or directly into infective **filariform (L3) larvae**, which **penetrate intact skin** (bare feet, hands in soil). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parasitic cycle**: larvae travel by blood to the lungs, ascend the airways, are swallowed and mature into **parthenogenetic females** embedded in the **duodenal and jejunal mucosa**; there are no parasitic males. [Harrison 22e]`,`Eggs hatch **inside the mucosa**, so the stool contains **rhabditiform larvae, not eggs** - the single most tested diagnostic fact (S. fuelleborni is the exception and passes eggs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autoinfection**: some rhabditiform larvae moult into filariform larvae **within the gut** and re-penetrate the **colonic mucosa (internal) or perianal skin (external)**, so the worm burden is maintained without new exposure. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Corticosteroids at any dose and duration** are the single most important trigger of hyperinfection - reported after prednisolone 20 mg or more for as little as **6-17 days**, after pulse methylprednisolone and after dexamethasone for COVID-19 or cerebral oedema. [Mandell 9e]`,`**HTLV-1 coinfection** skews immunity towards Th1 with high interferon-gamma, suppressing the Th2/IgE/eosinophil response that contains the worm; it causes recurrent and refractory infection and treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other risk factors: **solid-organ and stem-cell transplantation**, haematological malignancy, chemotherapy, **biologics (anti-TNF, tocilizumab)**, alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, hypogammaglobulinaemia, diabetes and CKD. [Mandell 9e]`,`**HIV** is surprisingly **not a major risk factor** for hyperinfection in most series, though cases occur, particularly with **IRIS** after ART. [Harrison 22e]`,`Transmission may also occur through a **transplanted organ** from an infected donor, and rarely person-to-person in institutions and by sexual (oro-anal) contact. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why steroids are dangerous`,points:[`Control of autoinfection depends on a **Th2 response** - IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, **eosinophils, mast cells and IgE** - that kills migrating larvae in the mucosa; anything that blunts this lets autoinfection accelerate. [Harrison 22e]`,`Steroids cause **eosinopenia** and lymphocyte apoptosis, and are thought to act like **ecdysteroids (moulting hormones)**, directly speeding the change of rhabditiform to filariform larvae - so larval numbers rise exponentially. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Hyperinfection** is accelerated autoinfection with a massive larval load confined to the **normal migratory route (gut, peritoneum, lungs)**; **dissemination** means larvae in organs outside it - CNS, liver, kidney, heart, skin. [Mandell 9e]`,`Penetrating filariform larvae **carry enteric bacteria** on their surface and through ulcerated mucosa, producing **Gram-negative bacteraemia, E. coli or Klebsiella meningitis and polymicrobial sepsis** - the usual cause of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`Massive mucosal invasion gives **oedema, ulceration and malabsorption**, protein-losing enteropathy and **paralytic ileus or small-bowel obstruction**; in the lung, larval migration causes **alveolar haemorrhage, pneumonitis and ARDS**. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Eosinophilia is usually absent in hyperinfection** (steroids, sepsis), so a normal eosinophil count never excludes it; persistent eosinophilia in a treated patient suggests ongoing infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Larva currens** is an urticarial hypersensitivity track produced by filariform larvae migrating through the dermis during external autoinfection; it moves **5-10 cm per hour**, far faster than hookworm larva migrans. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - clinical stages`,points:[`**Acute infection**: local pruritus at the entry site, then **Loeffler-like pneumonitis** (cough, wheeze, eosinophilia) at about 1-2 weeks and GI symptoms; larvae appear in stool after about **3-4 weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic infection**: asymptomatic or intermittent epigastric pain, diarrhoea, bloating, urticaria and larva currens with fluctuating **eosinophilia**; persists for decades. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyperinfection syndrome**: increased larval load with **worsening GI and pulmonary disease** in an immunosuppressed host, with larvae in **sputum, BAL or stool in large numbers**. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Disseminated strongyloidiasis**: larvae in **organs outside the gut-lung axis** (CNS, liver, kidney, skin); in practice it overlaps with hyperinfection and both carry high mortality. [Mandell 9e]`,`Mortality of hyperinfection/dissemination is reported at **about 60% and up to 85%**, higher with Gram-negative bacteraemia, shock or delayed diagnosis. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Ask about **barefoot walking, farm or plantation work and childhood in a rural endemic area** - exposure may have been decades ago. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chronic symptoms: **intermittent epigastric pain** mimicking peptic ulcer, diarrhoea alternating with constipation, bloating, weight loss, and recurrent **pruritic rash of buttocks and thighs**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrent urticaria or wheeze** with unexplained eosinophilia, or asthma that **worsens when steroids are given**, is a classic clue. [Mandell 9e]`,`Hyperinfection presents **days to weeks after starting steroids**: fever, abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhoea or ileus, **GI bleeding**, cough, haemoptysis, breathlessness. [Mandell 9e]`,`Then **Gram-negative sepsis or meningitis** - fever, headache, neck stiffness, confusion - in a steroid-treated patient from an endemic area; recurrent enteric bacteraemia with no source should prompt the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Atypical presentations: **SIADH-like hyponatraemia**, protein-losing enteropathy, unexplained **petechial or purpuric periumbilical rash**, and acute abdomen. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Larva currens**: a serpiginous, raised, intensely itchy urticarial line on the **buttocks, perineum, groins, thighs or lower trunk** that advances 5-10 cm per hour and fades in hours to days - essentially pathognomonic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Periumbilical 'thumbprint' purpura** (petechiae spreading from the umbilicus) is a sign of **disseminated** disease from dermal larval migration. [Mandell 9e]`,`Look for **wheeze, crepitations and hypoxia** (larval pneumonitis, alveolar haemorrhage, ARDS). [Mandell 9e]`,`Abdominal **distension with absent bowel sounds** suggests paralytic ileus; tenderness with guarding may indicate perforation or peritonitis. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Meningism and altered sensorium** in a steroid-treated patient suggest Gram-negative meningitis secondary to hyperinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for features of the underlying immunosuppression - **Cushingoid habitus**, transplant scar, lymphadenopathy - and of HTLV-1 (**spastic paraparesis**, adult T-cell leukaemia skin lesions). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Hookworm**: eggs, not larvae, in fresh stool; skin entry causes ground itch, and **cutaneous larva migrans (animal hookworm)** moves only **1-2 cm per day**, usually on the feet. [Harrison 22e]`,`In stool left standing, hookworm eggs hatch into rhabditiform larvae: Strongyloides larvae have a **short buccal canal and prominent genital primordium**; hookworm larvae have a **long buccal canal**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia** (filarial): nocturnal wheeze, eosinophils often over 3000/microL, **very high IgE** and high filarial antibody, with response to DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ascaris Loeffler syndrome**: transient pneumonitis with eosinophilia; eggs appear in stool 6-8 weeks later; no autoinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ABPA, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, drug reactions and hypereosinophilic syndrome** - exclude Strongyloides before steroids for any of these. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hyperinfection with Gram-negative sepsis mimics **intra-abdominal sepsis, typhoid, Klebsiella liver abscess or nosocomial sepsis** - the clue is steroids plus pulmonary infiltrates plus enteric bacteraemia. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**CBC**: eosinophilia (over 500/microL) in about **50-80% of chronic infections**, but often absent in hyperinfection; IgE may be raised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stool microscopy** for rhabditiform larvae: a single direct smear detects only about **30% or less** of chronic infections because larval output is low and intermittent; examining **3 or more samples** improves yield. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Agar plate culture (Koga)**: larvae crawl on the agar carrying bacteria, leaving **visible tracks** in 2-3 days - more sensitive than smear; **Baermann concentration** and formol-ether concentration also improve yield. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Serology (IgG ELISA)** is the best screening test, sensitivity about **80-95%**; it cross-reacts with filariasis and other nematodes and may be falsely negative in severe immunosuppression and HTLV-1. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Stool PCR** (real-time) is highly specific but its sensitivity in light infection is similar to or below serology; it is available in tertiary centres. [Mandell 9e]`,`In suspected hyperinfection, examine **sputum, BAL, gastric aspirate and duodenal aspirate** - larvae are abundant; also send **blood cultures** and **CSF** (Gram-negative meningitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Duodenal or colonic biopsy may show larvae and adult females in crypts; **chest X-ray** may show migratory infiltrates or diffuse alveolar opacities. [Mandell 9e]`,`Check **HTLV-1 serology** in recurrent, refractory or severe disease, and screen for the underlying immunosuppression (HIV, haematological malignancy). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Treat every infection, even if asymptomatic**, because autoinfection makes it lifelong and steroids may be given later. [CDC 2024]`,`Drug of choice: **ivermectin 200 microgram/kg orally once daily for 1-2 days** (CDC: 2 days); take on an empty stomach with water; tablets 3, 6 and 12 mg are available in India. [CDC 2024]`,`**Ivermectin is superior to albendazole**: Cochrane review showed higher parasitological cure with ivermectin; albendazole **400 mg twice daily for 7 days** is the second-line option when ivermectin is unavailable. [Cochrane 2016]`,`**Single-dose albendazole 400 mg** (as used on National Deworming Day) is **inadequate** for Strongyloides. [Cochrane 2016]`,`Some experts repeat ivermectin after **2 weeks** (one autoinfection cycle) in immunocompromised patients and those with HTLV-1. [Mandell 9e]`,`Follow-up: eosinophil count and serology fall over **6-12 months**; persistent eosinophilia or positive stool culture after treatment means retreatment and HTLV-1 testing. [Mandell 9e]`,`Ivermectin is well tolerated; mild dizziness, nausea and pruritus occur; in travellers from **Central/West Africa**, exclude **Loa loa** microfilaraemia first because ivermectin can cause encephalopathy. [CDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Pre-emptive strategy before steroids and immunosuppression`,points:[`**Anyone who has lived in an endemic area (all of India counts)** due to receive high-dose or prolonged steroids, a biologic, chemotherapy or a transplant should be **screened or treated empirically**. [IDSA 2023]`,`**Screen-and-treat**: serology with or without stool agar culture, and treat if positive - suitable for elective immunosuppression such as transplant work-up. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Empirical ivermectin 200 microgram/kg once daily for 1-2 days** is reasonable when steroids are needed urgently or testing is unavailable, as it is cheap and safe. [Stauffer 2020]`,`For **COVID-19 or other short urgent steroid courses** in endemic regions, empirical ivermectin was recommended by expert groups to prevent hyperinfection - an anti-helminthic use, not an antiviral one. [Stauffer 2020]`,`For **transplant candidates**, screen donors from endemic areas and treat recipients before transplant; positive recipients may receive ivermectin again post-transplant. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`Do **not delay life-saving steroids** while waiting for results - give ivermectin alongside and watch for hyperinfection. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Management of hyperinfection and dissemination (IPD / ICU)`,points:[`**Admit to ICU-level care**; involve infectious diseases early; **reduce or stop immunosuppression** as far as possible, including steroids. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg orally or by nasogastric tube once daily**, continued until stool and sputum are **negative for at least 2 weeks** (one autoinfection cycle), typically 2-3 weeks or longer. [CDC 2024]`,`Some experts **add albendazole 400 mg twice daily** in severe disease, though benefit is unproven. [Mandell 9e]`,`If **ileus or malabsorption** prevents oral absorption, **subcutaneous veterinary ivermectin** (200 microgram/kg) or rectal ivermectin has been used on a compassionate basis with case-report evidence. [Mandell 9e]`,`Give **broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric Gram-negatives** (e.g. ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly for meningitis, or meropenem where ESBL is likely) after blood and CSF cultures; anaerobic and enterococcal cover as needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`Supportive care: **ventilatory support for ARDS or alveolar haemorrhage**, nasogastric decompression for ileus, nutrition, correction of hyponatraemia and protein loss. [Mandell 9e]`,`Monitor **daily sputum and stool for larvae**; contact precautions for stool and secretions, as hyperinfection material contains infective filariform larvae. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: ivermectin safety data are limited; defer treatment of uncomplicated infection until after delivery or at least the first trimester, but **treat hyperinfection immediately** as benefit outweighs risk. [CDC 2024]`,`**Lactation**: ivermectin passes into milk in small amounts; treatment is usually deferred in uncomplicated disease. [CDC 2024]`,`**Children**: ivermectin is licensed for children **15 kg or more**; albendazole is used in smaller children with uncomplicated infection. [CDC 2024]`,`**Transplant recipients**: hyperinfection often occurs in the **first 3 months** after transplant; donor-derived infection is described. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**HTLV-1**: expect relapse; use repeated or prolonged ivermectin and long follow-up with stool culture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly and institutionalised**: chronic infection may surface decades later with steroids for COPD, polymyalgia or rheumatoid arthritis - ask about early-life exposure. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Gram-negative bacteraemia and meningitis** (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, Streptococcus bovis) - the leading cause of death in hyperinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary**: ARDS, diffuse alveolar haemorrhage, secondary pneumonia, respiratory failure. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Gastrointestinal**: paralytic ileus, small-bowel obstruction, massive GI bleeding, perforation, protein-losing enteropathy and malabsorption. [Mandell 9e]`,`Others: **SIADH**, eosinophilic or Gram-negative pericarditis, hepatic granulomas. [Mandell 9e]`,`Chronic uncomplicated infection has an **excellent prognosis** with ivermectin (cure 80-95%); hyperinfection mortality remains **about 60-85%**, emphasising prevention. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer** any steroid-treated patient with **fever, pulmonary infiltrates, ileus or Gram-negative bacteraemia** - hyperinfection is an emergency. [Mandell 9e]`,`Follow treated chronic infection with **eosinophil count at 3-6 months** and serology at 6-12 months; stool agar culture if available. [Mandell 9e]`,`Prevention: **footwear, sanitary latrines** (Swachh Bharat Mission) and hand hygiene interrupt soil transmission. [WHO 2020]`,`**National Deworming Day** albendazole does not control Strongyloides; WHO now advocates **ivermectin-based** control in school-age children where prevalence is high. [WHO 2020]`,`Counsel patients who have been treated to **tell any doctor before steroids** that they had Strongyloides. [Mandell 9e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO NTD Road Map 2021-2030** added strongyloidiasis as a target for control, with **ivermectin preventive chemotherapy** in school-age children where prevalence is 10% or more. [WHO 2020]`,`**Cochrane 2016 review** (Henriquez-Camacho): ivermectin gives higher cure than albendazole, and equivalent cure to thiabendazole with fewer adverse effects - the basis for ivermectin first-line. [Cochrane 2016]`,`**COVID-era recommendations (2020-2022)**: empirical ivermectin before dexamethasone in people from endemic regions to prevent hyperinfection, endorsed by several national societies. [Stauffer 2020]`,`**IDSA/AST and ESCMID transplant infection guidance** reinforce screening of transplant candidates and donors from endemic areas. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Moxidectin**, a long-acting macrocyclic lactone, is under trial as a single-dose alternative to ivermectin; it is not yet a standard of care. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NLEM 2022** lists ivermectin, improving access in public facilities in India. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Why does Strongyloides persist for decades?'** - autoinfection; no other common human helminth does it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stool shows larvae, not eggs** (eggs hatch in the mucosa) - a favourite MCQ. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Larva currens moves 5-10 cm/hour**; cutaneous larva migrans moves 1-2 cm/day. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Absent eosinophilia does not exclude hyperinfection** - steroids suppress it. [Mandell 9e]`,`**Hyperinfection plus Gram-negative meningitis** in a steroid-treated patient - the bugs ride on the larvae. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: treating with **single-dose albendazole** and assuming cure; or giving steroids to an 'asthmatic' with eosinophilia without excluding Strongyloides. [Mandell 9e]`,`**HTLV-1** is the viral co-factor; HIV is not a major one. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug treatment of strongyloidiasis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ivermectin (first line)`,`200 microgram/kg orally on empty stomach`,`Once daily for 1-2 days (chronic); repeat at 2 weeks if immunocompromised`,`Dizziness, nausea, pruritus; avoid in Loa loa microfilaraemia; limited data in pregnancy and children under 15 kg`],[`Ivermectin (hyperinfection)`,`200 microgram/kg orally or by NG tube`,`Daily until stool and sputum negative for at least 2 weeks`,`Poor absorption in ileus - consider subcutaneous veterinary preparation (compassionate use)`],[`Albendazole (second line)`,`400 mg orally twice daily with fatty food`,`7 days; added to ivermectin in some severe cases`,`Lower cure than ivermectin; hepatotoxicity, neutropenia with prolonged use; avoid in first trimester`],[`Empirical pre-steroid ivermectin`,`200 microgram/kg orally`,`Single dose or 2 daily doses before or with steroids`,`Cheap and safe - do not delay life-saving steroids`],[`Antibiotics for associated sepsis`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly (meningitis) or meropenem 1-2 g IV 8-hourly`,`Per culture and site, typically 10-21 days`,`Cover enteric Gram-negatives; adjust for ESBL/CRE`]]},{heading:`Clinical stages of strongyloidiasis`,columns:[`Stage`,`Host`,`Features`,`Larval load / location`,`Prognosis`],rows:[[`Acute`,`Any`,`Entry-site itch, Loeffler-like cough and wheeze, GI upset`,`Migrating larvae; stool positive after 3-4 weeks`,`Good`],[`Chronic`,`Immunocompetent`,`Asymptomatic or epigastric pain, diarrhoea, urticaria, larva currens, eosinophilia`,`Low, intermittent; gut`,`Excellent with ivermectin`],[`Hyperinfection`,`Steroids, HTLV-1, transplant`,`Fever, ileus, GI bleed, pneumonitis, ARDS, Gram-negative sepsis`,`Massive; gut and lungs (normal route)`,`Mortality about 60%`],[`Disseminated`,`Severe immunosuppression`,`As hyperinfection plus CNS, liver, kidney, purpuric rash`,`Organs outside gut-lung axis`,`Mortality up to 85%`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of helminth-related eosinophilia with skin or lung signs`,columns:[`Condition`,`Skin sign`,`Lung picture`,`Stool / blood clue`,`Discriminator`],rows:[[`Strongyloidiasis`,`Larva currens, buttocks, 5-10 cm/hour`,`Loeffler-like; ARDS in hyperinfection`,`Rhabditiform larvae, agar plate tracks`,`Autoinfection; worsens with steroids`],[`Hookworm / cutaneous larva migrans`,`Ground itch; CLM on feet, 1-2 cm/day`,`Mild Loeffler`,`Eggs in fresh stool; iron-deficiency anaemia`,`No autoinfection`],[`Ascariasis`,`None`,`Transient Loeffler syndrome`,`Eggs 6-8 weeks after lung phase`,`Self-limiting pneumonitis`],[`Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia`,`None`,`Nocturnal wheeze, miliary mottling`,`Eosinophils over 3000/microL, IgE very high, filarial antibody`,`Responds to DEC`],[`ABPA`,`None`,`Central bronchiectasis, fleeting infiltrates`,`Aspergillus-specific IgE, total IgE over 500 IU/mL`,`Asthma or cystic fibrosis background`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their performance`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`Sensitivity / cut-off`,`Use`],rows:[[`Eosinophil count`,`Over 500/microL`,`Present in 50-80% chronic; often absent in hyperinfection`,`Clue only`],[`Direct stool microscopy (1 sample)`,`Rhabditiform larvae`,`30% or less`,`Cheap; repeat 3 or more samples`],[`Agar plate culture / Baermann`,`Larval tracks on agar / concentrated larvae`,`Higher than smear`,`Best stool method`],[`IgG ELISA serology`,`Antibody`,`About 80-95%; cross-reacts with filaria`,`Screening before immunosuppression`],[`Stool real-time PCR`,`Parasite DNA`,`High specificity; variable sensitivity`,`Tertiary confirmation`],[`Sputum / BAL / duodenal aspirate`,`Filariform larvae in large numbers`,`High in hyperinfection`,`Diagnosis of hyperinfection`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever, abdominal pain or ileus in a patient on steroids who grew up in a rural endemic area`,`Gram-negative bacteraemia or meningitis (E. coli, Klebsiella) with no obvious source in an immunosuppressed patient`,`New pulmonary infiltrates, haemoptysis or ARDS with larvae in sputum`,`Periumbilical purpuric (thumbprint) rash`,`Upper GI bleeding or small-bowel obstruction in a transplant or chemotherapy patient`,`Known HTLV-1 infection with recurrent strongyloidiasis`,`Worsening 'asthma' with eosinophilia after steroids are started`],pearls:[`Strongyloides is the only common human helminth that multiplies inside the host - **autoinfection** explains lifelong infection and hyperinfection.`,`**Larvae, not eggs**, in stool.`,`**Larva currens: 5-10 cm per hour** on buttocks and thighs; larva migrans: 1-2 cm per day on feet.`,`**Ivermectin before steroids** in anyone from an endemic area - 'worm before wean (immunosuppression)'.`,`Hyperinfection = **steroids + lungs + gut + Gram-negative sepsis**; eosinophils are often normal.`,`**HTLV-1, not HIV**, is the classic viral co-factor.`,`Treat hyperinfection with daily ivermectin **until 2 weeks of negative samples** - one full autoinfection cycle.`,`Serology is the **screening** test; agar plate culture is the best stool test.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old farmer from Assam with COPD is given prednisolone 40 mg daily for an exacerbation. Ten days later he develops fever, abdominal distension, haemoptysis and E. coli meningitis. Which feature of the causative parasite best explains this presentation?`,options:[`Autoinfection allowing larval numbers to multiply within the host`,`Haematogenous spread of eggs from the portal venous system`,`Release of a cardiotoxic exotoxin by adult worms`,`Migration of adult worms from bowel to lung`],answer:0,explanation:`Strongyloides stercoralis completes its life cycle within the host by autoinfection; steroids accelerate this into hyperinfection, and penetrating larvae carry enteric Gram-negatives into blood and CSF. Egg embolisation to the portal system is the mechanism of schistosomiasis, not Strongyloides; there is no exotoxin; and it is larvae, not adult worms, that migrate to the lung.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-q2`,stem:`In a fresh stool sample from a patient with chronic strongyloidiasis, which form of the parasite is typically seen?`,options:[`Embryonated eggs`,`Adult males and females`,`Rhabditiform larvae`,`Proglottids`],answer:2,explanation:`Strongyloides eggs hatch within the intestinal mucosa, so fresh stool contains rhabditiform larvae rather than eggs. Embryonated eggs are seen in hookworm and other nematodes, adult males do not exist in the parasitic cycle, and proglottids are segments of tapeworms such as Taenia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-q3`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman from rural Tamil Nadu is to start rituximab and high-dose prednisolone for vasculitis next week. Strongyloides serology is not available. What is the most appropriate step?`,options:[`Single dose albendazole 400 mg`,`Defer immunosuppression until three stool samples are negative`,`No action unless eosinophilia is present`,`Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg once daily for 2 days before starting immunosuppression`],answer:3,explanation:`Empirical ivermectin is cheap, safe and prevents hyperinfection in people from endemic areas who cannot be screened. Single-dose albendazole has poor efficacy against Strongyloides; stool microscopy is insensitive and delaying immunosuppression is unnecessary; and the absence of eosinophilia does not exclude infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-q4`,stem:`Which viral infection is most strongly associated with refractory and recurrent Strongyloides hyperinfection?`,options:[`HIV-1`,`HTLV-1`,`Hepatitis C virus`,`Epstein-Barr virus`],answer:1,explanation:`HTLV-1 shifts immunity towards Th1 with high interferon-gamma, suppressing the Th2/IgE/eosinophil response that contains Strongyloides, causing hyperinfection and treatment failure. HIV, surprisingly, is not a major risk factor except during IRIS; hepatitis C and EBV have no specific association.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-q5`,stem:`A renal transplant recipient with Strongyloides hyperinfection is started on ivermectin. For how long should ivermectin be continued?`,options:[`Two doses 24 hours apart`,`Until stool and sputum have been negative for at least 2 weeks`,`A single dose repeated after 3 months`,`Seven days, then switch to albendazole`],answer:1,explanation:`In hyperinfection ivermectin is given daily until stool and sputum are negative for at least 2 weeks, covering one autoinfection cycle. The 2-day course is for uncomplicated chronic infection, a 3-month repeat leaves the patient exposed during the most dangerous period, and switching to albendazole replaces the better drug with a less effective one.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the life cycle of Strongyloides stercoralis. Discuss the pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of Strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome, and outline its prevention in patients receiving corticosteroids.`,openingLines:[`Strongyloidiasis is infection with the soil-transmitted nematode Strongyloides stercoralis, which uniquely completes its life cycle within the host by autoinfection and so persists for decades.`,`In the immunosuppressed - especially with corticosteroids or HTLV-1 - autoinfection accelerates into hyperinfection or dissemination with Gram-negative sepsis, carrying mortality of 60-85%.`],answer:[{heading:`Life cycle`,points:[`Free-living cycle in soil; filariform larvae penetrate skin, travel to lungs, are swallowed.`,`Parthenogenetic females in duodenal mucosa; eggs hatch in mucosa - larvae, not eggs, in stool.`,`Autoinfection: internal (colonic mucosa) and external (perianal skin) - draw the cycle.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis of hyperinfection`,points:[`Loss of Th2/eosinophil/IgE control; steroids cause eosinopenia and accelerate moulting.`,`Massive larval load in gut and lungs; dissemination to CNS, liver, kidney.`,`Larvae carry enteric Gram-negatives - bacteraemia and meningitis.`]},{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Corticosteroids (any dose), HTLV-1, transplant, haematological malignancy, biologics, alcohol, malnutrition.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`GI: pain, vomiting, ileus, bleeding, protein-losing enteropathy.`,`Pulmonary: cough, haemoptysis, infiltrates, ARDS.`,`Sepsis, meningitis, periumbilical purpura, SIADH; eosinophilia often absent.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Larvae in stool, sputum, BAL, duodenal aspirate; agar plate culture.`,`Blood and CSF cultures; serology for screening; HTLV-1 testing.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`ICU care, reduce immunosuppression.`,`Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg daily until samples negative 2 weeks; consider albendazole addition; subcutaneous ivermectin in ileus.`,`Broad-spectrum Gram-negative antibiotics; ventilatory and nutritional support.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Screen (serology) or give empirical ivermectin before steroids, biologics, chemotherapy or transplant in anyone from an endemic area.`,`Footwear, sanitation; WHO 2030 road map ivermectin-based control.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle showing the free-living, parasitic and autoinfection loops`,`Table of acute, chronic, hyperinfection and disseminated stages`],markSplit:[{part:`Life cycle with diagram`,marks:2},{part:`Pathogenesis and risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention before steroids`,marks:2}],keywords:[`autoinfection`,`hyperinfection`,`ivermectin`,`HTLV-1`,`Gram-negative sepsis`,`larva currens`]},{id:`infectious-fever-strongyloides-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate larva currens from cutaneous larva migrans.`,openingLines:[`Larva currens is the rapidly migrating urticarial track of Strongyloides filariform larvae during external autoinfection.`,`Cutaneous larva migrans is the slowly advancing serpiginous track of animal hookworm larvae (Ancylostoma braziliense) that cannot complete their cycle in humans.`],answer:[{heading:`Agent and mechanism`,points:[`Larva currens: S. stercoralis, human parasite, autoinfection - systemic infection.`,`CLM: dog/cat hookworm, dead-end host - infection confined to skin.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Speed: 5-10 cm per hour vs 1-2 cm per day.`,`Site: buttocks, perianal, thighs, trunk vs feet, hands, buttocks after beach exposure.`,`Duration: fades in hours, recurs vs persists weeks, self-limiting over months.`]},{heading:`Investigation and treatment`,points:[`Larva currens: eosinophilia, serology, stool culture; ivermectin 200 microgram/kg x 2 days and pre-steroid caution.`,`CLM: clinical diagnosis; ivermectin single dose or albendazole 400 mg for 3-7 days; topical thiabendazole.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table of agent, speed, site, duration and treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Agent and mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`larva currens`,`cutaneous larva migrans`,`Ancylostoma braziliense`,`ivermectin`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Intestinal nematode infections`,`Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases, 9th edition, 2020 - Strongyloides`,`CDC. Strongyloidiasis - Clinical care and treatment guidance, updated 2024`,`Henriquez-Camacho C et al. Ivermectin versus albendazole or thiabendazole for Strongyloides stercoralis infection. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2016`,`Buonfrate D et al. The global prevalence of Strongyloides stercoralis infection. Pathogens 2020`,`World Health Organization. Ending the neglect to attain the SDGs: NTD Road Map 2021-2030, 2020`,`Stauffer WM et al. Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and Strongyloides: deworming before corticosteroids. Open Forum Infect Dis 2020`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare. National List of Essential Medicines 2022`]});var x=[];x.push({id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children`,title:`Malaria in pregnancy and children`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Malaria in pregnancy and childhood** is malaria in the two groups with the least immunity and the highest risk of **severe disease, hypoglycaemia, severe anaemia and death**; in India it is treated by **trimester and weight band** - chloroquine for vivax in any trimester, an **ACT in the second and third trimesters** (quinine or, per WHO 2022, artemether-lumefantrine in the first), **IV artesunate for severe malaria at any stage**, and **no primaquine in pregnancy, in infants or without knowing G6PD status** for the 14-day course; **IPTp is not used in India**.`,keywords:[`malaria in pregnancy`,`placental malaria`,`VAR2CSA`,`congenital malaria`,`paediatric malaria`,`cerebral malaria in children`,`Blantyre coma scale`,`rectal artesunate`,`IPTp-SP`,`artemether-lumefantrine`,`ACT-SP`,`quinine and clindamycin`,`chloroquine prophylaxis in pregnancy`,`primaquine contraindications`,`G6PD deficiency`,`fluorescent spot test`,`low birth weight`,`severe malarial anaemia`,`RTS,S`,`R21/Matrix-M`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Malaria in pregnancy (MiP)** is infection with Plasmodium at any time from conception to delivery, including **placental (sequestered) infection** that may be smear-negative in peripheral blood. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Globally the **World Malaria Report 2024** counted about **263 million cases and 597,000 deaths in 2023**; around **three-quarters of deaths are children under 5** in sub-Saharan Africa. [WMR 2024]`,`In Africa an estimated **12 million pregnancies a year** are exposed to malaria, causing roughly **1 in 6 low-birth-weight** babies in moderate-high transmission areas. [WMR 2024]`,`**India** reports a few lakh confirmed cases a year (NCVBDC), concentrated in **Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Tripura and tribal-forest districts**; India exited the WHO **High Burden High Impact** group in 2024. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`India is a **low and unstable transmission** country, so pregnant women and children have **little acquired immunity** - infection is symptomatic and progresses to severe malaria more often than in Africa. [Harrison 22e]`,`In low-transmission settings pregnant women are about **3 times more likely to develop severe malaria** than non-pregnant adults, with maternal mortality of **up to 50%** in severe falciparum malaria in the second and third trimesters. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Vivax** accounts for a large share of Indian cases and is not benign in pregnancy: it causes **anaemia, low birth weight and miscarriage**, and can turn severe. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Children in India most at risk are **under-5s in tribal and forest areas**, where falciparum predominates and access to care is delayed. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**P. falciparum and P. vivax** cause almost all Indian malaria; P. malariae and P. ovale are rare and **P. knowlesi** has been reported from the Andaman and Nicobar Islands. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Main Indian vectors: **An. culicifacies** (rural plains), **An. fluviatilis** (hills and forest), **An. minimus and An. baimaii** (North-East), **An. stephensi** (urban) and **An. sundaicus** (coast). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Pregnancy risk is highest in **primigravidae and secundigravidae**, adolescents, **HIV-infected women** and in the **second trimester** when parasite density peaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pregnancy-specific factors: **raised cortisol, Th2 shift**, increased attractiveness to mosquitoes (higher exhaled CO2 and skin temperature) and the placenta as a new site for sequestration. [Harrison 22e]`,`Child-specific factors: age **6 months to 5 years** (maternal antibody waning, no acquired immunity), malnutrition, sickle cell disease (protective trait but severe in disease), and delayed treatment. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Congenital malaria** follows transplacental or intrapartum transfer; it presents at **10-30 days** of life and is commoner with vivax in non-immune mothers. [Nelson 22e]`,`**G6PD deficiency** (X-linked) affects about **7-8% of Indians on average**, ranging from under 2% to over 20% in some tribal groups; it does not cause malaria but decides who can safely take primaquine. [ICMR 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why pregnancy and childhood are different`,points:[`Falciparum-infected red cells express **PfEMP1**; in pregnancy a variant, **VAR2CSA**, binds **chondroitin sulphate A** on the syncytiotrophoblast, so parasites **sequester in the placenta**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Women in their first pregnancy lack **anti-VAR2CSA antibodies**, which is why primigravidae suffer most; antibody builds with each pregnancy in high-transmission areas. [Harrison 22e]`,`Placental sequestration causes **intervillositis, fibrin deposition and impaired nutrient transfer**, giving **intrauterine growth restriction, preterm birth, low birth weight and stillbirth** even when the mother feels well. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** is common in pregnancy because of fetal glucose use, parasite consumption and **quinine-induced hyperinsulinaemia**; it can be recurrent and asymptomatic. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Pulmonary oedema** is commonest immediately after delivery, when **autotransfusion of placental blood** and loss of placental sequestration overload the leaky pulmonary capillaries. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`In children, **severe anaemia** results from haemolysis of infected and uninfected cells, **splenic clearance** and **dyserythropoiesis** due to haemozoin and TNF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory distress in children** is usually **deep acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing** from lactic acidosis, not pneumonia - the strongest predictor of death with coma. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Paediatric **cerebral malaria** reflects sequestration in brain microvessels plus **brain swelling**; seizures are frequent (up to 60%) and retinopathy (whitening, vessel changes, haemorrhages) is specific. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why primaquine is restricted**: its oxidant metabolites generate hydrogen peroxide; **G6PD-deficient red cells** cannot regenerate NADPH and glutathione, so they **haemolyse** - and fetal or infant G6PD status cannot be known. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Uncomplicated malaria**: symptomatic, parasite-positive, with **no WHO criterion of severity** - treated with oral drugs. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Severe malaria** (WHO): impaired consciousness, prostration, multiple convulsions (2 or more in 24 h), acidosis, **hypoglycaemia under 2.2 mmol/L (40 mg/dL)**, severe anaemia, renal impairment, jaundice, pulmonary oedema, bleeding, shock or **hyperparasitaemia over 10%**. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Severe anaemia** cut-offs: **haemoglobin under 5 g/dL or haematocrit under 15% in children under 12**, and under 7 g/dL or 20% in adults, with parasitaemia over 10,000/microL. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Pregnancy treatment depends on **trimester**: first trimester (up to 13 weeks) versus second and third - this alone decides the ACT question. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`In children, the **Blantyre coma scale** (eye movement 0-1, best motor 0-2, best verbal 0-2; total 0-5) is used under 5 years; **score under 3** defines cerebral malaria. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Indian ACT and primaquine doses are given in **age bands** (under 1, 1-4, 5-8, 9-14, 15 years and over) with pre-packed blister packs; WHO doses artemether-lumefantrine by **weight bands**. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**G6PD status**: deficient (under 30% activity), intermediate (30-70%, mostly heterozygous females) and normal (70% or more) on quantitative testing. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Pregnant women present with **fever, headache, body ache, vomiting** - easily mistaken for **hyperemesis, UTI, pyelonephritis or viral fever**; any fever in pregnancy in an endemic area needs an RDT or smear. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Pregnancy-specific complaints: **reduced fetal movements, preterm labour pains, bleeding** from miscarriage; malaria is a recognised cause of **abortion in the first trimester**. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`In children the picture is **non-specific**: fever, irritability, poor feeding, vomiting, diarrhoea, cough - overlapping with pneumonia, sepsis and gastroenteritis (IMNCI). [IMNCI 2024]`,`Ask about **convulsions, drowsiness, inability to drink or breastfeed, fast or deep breathing, dark urine, pallor** - each is an IMNCI general danger sign or a severity feature. [IMNCI 2024]`,`**Congenital malaria** in a neonate of 10-30 days: fever, irritability, poor feeding, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly and anaemia - often misdiagnosed as neonatal sepsis. [Nelson 22e]`,`Record **residence and travel** (forest, mine, tribal block, North-East), **previous malaria**, prior antimalarials and the **G6PD history** (past haemolysis, neonatal jaundice, family history). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Recurrent vivax episodes in the same child or woman 3-8 weeks after treatment suggest **relapse** (incomplete primaquine) rather than a new infection. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pallor** is the most consistent sign in pregnancy and childhood; marked pallor with tachycardia means **check haemoglobin now** - severe anaemia is a severity criterion. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Splenomegaly** is common in children; a tender, rapidly enlarging spleen warns of **splenic rupture** (more with vivax). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Deep (Kussmaul) breathing** without chest signs in a child means **metabolic acidosis** - severe malaria, not pneumonia. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Sweating, tremor, altered behaviour or seizures in a woman on quinine suggest **hypoglycaemia** - check capillary glucose at once, not after the next dose. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Jaundice** with pallor suggests haemolysis plus hepatic dysfunction; in pregnancy distinguish from **HELLP, acute fatty liver and viral hepatitis**. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`Obstetric examination: **fundal height** (growth restriction), fetal heart rate (fetal tachycardia or distress with maternal fever), uterine contractions and cervical change. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`In a comatose child, perform **fundoscopy for malarial retinopathy**; its presence supports cerebral malaria over other encephalopathies. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pyelonephritis in pregnancy**: loin pain, dysuria, pyuria and bacteriuria - but malaria can coexist, so test both. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Dengue**: retro-orbital pain, rash, falling platelets with rising haematocrit and **leucopenia**; NS1 positive, malaria RDT negative. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Enteric fever**: step-ladder fever, relative bradycardia, coated tongue, abdominal pain - blood culture positive, smear negative. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**HELLP syndrome and pre-eclampsia**: hypertension, proteinuria, haemolysis and low platelets without fever or parasites - the key mimic of severe falciparum in the third trimester. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`In children: **bacterial meningitis** (stiff neck, CSF pleocytosis), **pneumonia** (chest indrawing, crackles), **septicaemia** - treat both if in doubt, because parasitaemia may be incidental. [IMNCI 2024]`,`**Neonatal sepsis** versus congenital malaria: a peripheral smear from every febrile neonate of an infected mother settles it. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus and leptospirosis**: eschar, conjunctival suffusion, myalgia, AKI with jaundice - seasonal post-monsoon mimics (see the Acute undifferentiated fever topic). [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bivalent RDT** (HRP2 for falciparum, pan-pLDH for others) at the point of care by ASHA or ANM; result in 15-20 minutes. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Microscopy (thick and thin smear)** remains the gold standard for species and **parasite count**; repeat 12-hourly for 3 days if the first is negative and suspicion persists. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`HRP2 stays positive for **2-4 weeks** after cure, so a positive RDT in a recently treated child does not mean failure - use microscopy to judge response. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Placental sequestration means peripheral smear may be **negative in pregnant women**; an RDT detecting HRP2 picks up placental antigen and is useful here. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Baseline: **haemoglobin, platelets, blood glucose** (repeat 4-hourly in severe malaria or on quinine), creatinine, bilirubin, and in pregnancy **urine protein and blood pressure**. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**G6PD test before 14-day primaquine**: qualitative fluorescent spot test or RDT detects under 30% activity; **quantitative point-of-care biosensor** identifies intermediate females and is required before tafenoquine. [WHO 2022]`,`Obstetric ultrasound for **fetal growth and wellbeing** after an episode, and lactate, blood gas and blood culture in severe malaria. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`In children with coma do a **lumbar puncture when safe** (no signs of raised pressure) because meningitis coexists in a small proportion. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - uncomplicated malaria in pregnancy`,points:[`**Vivax in any trimester**: **chloroquine 25 mg/kg base over 3 days** (10 mg/kg day 1, 10 mg/kg day 2, 5 mg/kg day 3; adult 600-600-300 mg base) - safe throughout pregnancy. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**No primaquine in pregnancy**: after a vivax episode give **chloroquine 300 mg base (500 mg salt) weekly** as chemoprophylaxis until delivery and breastfeeding is over (or infant G6PD known), then radical cure. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Falciparum, first trimester** (India national guideline): **quinine 10 mg/kg salt 8-hourly for 7 days** (with clindamycin 10 mg/kg twice daily for 7 days where available). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 change**: **artemether-lumefantrine (AL) is now preferred in the first trimester** because pooled data showed no excess miscarriage or anomaly and quinine is less tolerated - follow the current state circular if it differs. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2022]`,`**Falciparum, second and third trimesters**: ACT as for non-pregnant adults - **artesunate 4 mg/kg daily for 3 days plus SP (25/1.25 mg/kg) on day 1**, or **AL** in the North-East states where SP resistance is high. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Primaquine single dose for falciparum** (gametocidal) is also **contraindicated in pregnancy**; it is withheld, not delayed. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Give the **first dose under observation**, with food (fat improves lumefantrine absorption), repeat the dose if vomited within 30 minutes, and give **paracetamol** for fever. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Also treat **anaemia** (IFA as per Anaemia Mukt Bharat, IV iron sucrose or FCM if moderate-severe after 20 weeks) and refer every pregnant woman with falciparum to a **PHC/FRU with a medical officer**. [AMB 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - uncomplicated malaria in children`,points:[`**Vivax**: chloroquine 10-10-5 mg/kg over 3 days by age band, then **primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days** (age 1-4 y 2.5 mg, 5-8 y 5 mg, 9-14 y 10 mg, 15 y and over 15 mg) - **not under 1 year** in the Indian schedule. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Falciparum (non-North-East)**: **ACT-SP age-band blister packs** - artesunate 25 mg (under 1 y), 50 mg (1-4 y), 100 mg (5-8 y), 150 mg (9-14 y), 200 mg (15 y and over) daily for 3 days with SP on day 1. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Falciparum (North-East states)**: **AL twice daily for 3 days** by weight band of 20/120 mg tablets - 5-14 kg 1 tablet, 15-24 kg 2, 25-34 kg 3, 35 kg and over 4 tablets per dose. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Infants under 5 kg** with falciparum: treat with an ACT at the **same mg/kg dose as for a 5 kg child** (WHO); avoid SP in the first weeks of life (kernicterus risk). [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Single-dose primaquine** for falciparum in India: **0.75 mg/kg on day 2** (age bands 1-4 y 7.5 mg, 5-8 y 15 mg, 9-14 y 30 mg, adult 45 mg); WHO advises **0.25 mg/kg without G6PD testing** - see Guideline updates. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Counsel mothers to give doses with **milk or food**, watch for **danger signs** and return on **day 3 and day 7**; a child unable to swallow or vomiting everything needs referral. [IMNCI 2024]`,`Control fever with **paracetamol 15 mg/kg 6-hourly** and tepid sponging; avoid aspirin (Reye) and NSAIDs until dengue is excluded. [IAP 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency - severe malaria`,points:[`**IV artesunate is first line in all trimesters, including the first**, because the risk to mother and fetus from severe malaria outweighs any theoretical drug risk. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Artesunate dose**: **2.4 mg/kg IV or IM at 0, 12 and 24 h, then daily**; children under 20 kg get **3 mg/kg** per dose; continue for at least 24 h and until oral intake possible, then a full 3-day ACT. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`If artesunate is unavailable: **artemether IM** or **quinine 20 mg/kg salt loading over 4 h, then 10 mg/kg 8-hourly** (infusion in 5% dextrose), switching to oral when able. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Pre-referral**: give a single IM artesunate dose; for children **under 6 years** where injections are not possible, **rectal artesunate 10 mg/kg**, and complete the referral (WHO 2023 cautions that RAS without onward care increases deaths). [WHO 2023]`,`Check **glucose 4-hourly** (hourly in coma); treat glucose under 3 mmol/L with **10% dextrose 2-5 mL/kg (children) or 25% dextrose 50-100 mL (adults)** and run maintenance dextrose. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Fluids cautiously** in pregnancy and children - no aggressive boluses (FEAST trial harm in febrile African children without shock); nurse propped up after delivery to prevent pulmonary oedema. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Transfuse** a child with Hb under 5 g/dL (or under 7 with distress), and a pregnant woman with Hb under 7 g/dL; pack cells 10 mL/kg (or whole blood 20 mL/kg) over 3-4 h. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Treat seizures with **IV/rectal diazepam or IV lorazepam**, then levetiracetam or phenytoin; **no prophylactic phenobarbital** (increased deaths). [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Obstetric care: **treat the mother first**; induction or caesarean is for obstetric indications, and fetal distress often resolves as fever and parasitaemia fall. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Watch for **post-artesunate delayed haemolysis (PADH)** 7-21 days later; check haemoglobin on day 7, 14 and 28 in children and in hyperparasitaemic patients. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Breastfeeding**: chloroquine, quinine, ACTs and SP are compatible; primaquine is given only once the **infant is 6 months old or its G6PD status is normal** (WHO). [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Mefloquine, doxycycline and tetracycline** are avoided in pregnancy and children under 8 for treatment; **doxycycline is contraindicated** for prophylaxis in pregnancy. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**HIV in pregnancy**: women on **co-trimoxazole prophylaxis must not receive SP** (additive antifolate toxicity, sulfa reactions); efavirenz lowers lumefantrine levels. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Severe malnutrition**: ACTs may underperform due to poor absorption; monitor response closely and manage hypoglycaemia and hypothermia. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Sickle cell disease**: children are at high risk of severe malaria and crisis; give **monthly SP or proguanil** in endemic areas per paediatric haematology advice. [IAP 2024]`,`**Travel**: pregnant women and young children should **avoid travel to high-falciparum areas**; if unavoidable, **mefloquine** prophylaxis is acceptable in the second and third trimesters (CDC), plus nets and repellents (DEET safe in pregnancy). [CDC Yellow Book 2026]`,`**Resource-limited (sub-centre or Ayushman Arogya Mandir)**: RDT, first dose of ACT or chloroquine, pre-referral artesunate and **referral with the RDT result and dose card**. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Maternal: **severe anaemia, hypoglycaemia, cerebral malaria, pulmonary oedema/ARDS, AKI, postpartum haemorrhage** and death. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Fetal: **miscarriage, stillbirth, preterm birth, IUGR, low birth weight** and congenital malaria; LBW is the main route by which MiP kills infants. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Childhood cerebral malaria has **15-20% mortality** even with treatment, and **10-20% of survivors** have neurological sequelae (epilepsy, cognitive and behavioural deficits). [Harrison 22e]`,`Strongest predictors of death in children: **coma, acidosis (deep breathing), hypoglycaemia and severe anaemia with respiratory distress**. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`**Primaquine-induced haemolysis** in G6PD deficiency causes dark urine, falling Hb and jaundice at day 2-4; stop the drug, hydrate and transfuse if severe. [ICMR 2023]`,`With prompt ACT, uncomplicated malaria in both groups has an excellent outcome; **delay beyond 24 h** of symptom onset is the main modifiable risk. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Follow-up on day 3, 7, 14 and 28**: clinical review and smear (day 3 positive with falling count is acceptable; positive on day 7-28 is **treatment failure** - switch ACT). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Recheck **haemoglobin** at 2-4 weeks and continue iron and folic acid; deworm children over 1 year with albendazole. [AMB 2023]`,`After delivery, give **vivax radical cure** (primaquine 14 days after G6PD test) once breastfeeding conditions are met, or keep **weekly chloroquine** until then. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Every pregnant woman in endemic areas gets an **LLIN at the first ANC visit** and is tested for malaria at each febrile episode; children under 5 should sleep under nets. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**IPTp is not recommended in India** - with low and unstable transmission, the national strategy is **case detection (RDT at every fever) and prompt treatment** plus vector control, not intermittent preventive SP. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`WHO recommends **IPTp-SP monthly from 13 weeks** and perennial or seasonal chemoprevention for children only in **moderate-high transmission Africa**; these do not apply in India. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`**Malaria vaccines RTS,S/AS01 (2021) and R21/Matrix-M (2023)** are WHO-recommended for African children from 5 months; they are **not part of India's programme**. [WHO 2023]`,`Notify every confirmed case for **case-based surveillance** (1-3-7 approach) - see the Malaria elimination topic. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO Guidelines for Malaria (2022 update, consolidated through 2025)**: artemether-lumefantrine recommended for falciparum in the **first trimester**; previously quinine plus clindamycin. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2022]`,`**WHO 2022-2024**: primaquine can be given to **breastfeeding women** when the infant is G6PD-normal or over 6 months, and to infants from 6 months; earlier advice excluded all breastfeeding women and infants. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2024]`,`**WHO 2023 rectal artesunate information note**: use only as pre-referral with assured onward care and follow-on injectable artesunate. [WHO 2023]`,`**WHO prequalification 2023 of R21/Matrix-M** and its rollout in Africa from 2024 - relevant for exam context, not Indian practice. [WHO 2023]`,`**NCVBDC National Strategic Plan 2023-2027** keeps ACT-SP (AL in North-East), chloroquine plus 14-day primaquine, and IV artesunate as the Indian standards, and stresses G6PD testing before radical cure. [NSP 2023-27]`,`**Guideline conflict - Pf gametocidal primaquine**: WHO uses **0.25 mg/kg single low dose without G6PD test**; India's treatment guideline has used **0.75 mg/kg on day 2** - quote both and follow the current state directive. [WHO 2025; NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **"Treatment of malaria in pregnancy"** - draw a trimester table (vivax: chloroquine any trimester; falciparum: first trimester quinine or AL, second/third ACT; severe: IV artesunate any trimester; no primaquine). [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Viva: **"Why no primaquine in pregnancy?"** - fetal G6PD status is unknown and the fetus is exposed to oxidant haemolysis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **"Why is IPTp not used in India?"** - low, unstable transmission makes test-and-treat cheaper and safer than presumptive SP. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Common mistake: giving **IV fluids liberally** to a pregnant woman with severe malaria - pulmonary oedema follows, especially after delivery. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Common mistake: **missing hypoglycaemia** in a child or pregnant woman on quinine - check glucose before blaming cerebral malaria for drowsiness. [WHO Severe Malaria 2014]`,`Common mistake: prescribing **doxycycline** with quinine in pregnancy or a child under 8 - use **clindamycin**. [WHO Malaria Guidelines 2025]`,`Remember the age bands **under 1, 1-4, 5-8, 9-14, 15+** for Indian blister packs and primaquine. [NCVBDC 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antimalarial choice by group (India, NCVBDC, with WHO differences)`,columns:[`Situation`,`Vivax`,`Falciparum (uncomplicated)`,`Severe malaria`,`Primaquine`],rows:[[`Pregnancy - 1st trimester`,`Chloroquine 3 days`,`Quinine 7 days (+ clindamycin); WHO 2022: AL preferred`,`IV artesunate`,`Contraindicated; weekly chloroquine until delivery`],[`Pregnancy - 2nd and 3rd trimester`,`Chloroquine 3 days`,`ACT-SP (AL in North-East)`,`IV artesunate`,`Contraindicated`],[`Breastfeeding`,`Chloroquine 3 days`,`ACT as for adults`,`IV artesunate`,`Only if infant G6PD normal or over 6 months (WHO)`],[`Infant under 1 year`,`Chloroquine by age band`,`ACT by age/weight band; avoid SP in early weeks`,`IV/IM artesunate 3 mg/kg`,`Not given (India); WHO from 6 months`],[`Child 1-14 years`,`Chloroquine + PQ 0.25 mg/kg x 14 d`,`ACT-SP or AL by band + single PQ`,`Artesunate 3 mg/kg if under 20 kg`,`After G6PD test for 14-day course`]]},{heading:`Drug table - pregnancy and children`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Chloroquine`,`10 mg/kg base day 1, 10 mg/kg day 2, 5 mg/kg day 3 orally (adult 600-600-300 mg)`,`3 days; prophylaxis 300 mg base weekly in pregnancy`,`Pruritus, vomiting; fatal in overdose in children - store safely`],[`Artesunate + SP (ACT-SP)`,`Artesunate 4 mg/kg daily; SP 25/1.25 mg/kg single dose day 1`,`3 days`,`SP: Stevens-Johnson, avoid with co-trimoxazole, sulfa allergy, early neonate`],[`Artemether-lumefantrine`,`20/120 mg tabs: 1-4 tabs per dose by weight, at 0, 8, 24, 36, 48, 60 h`,`3 days (6 doses) with fatty food`,`Headache, dizziness; QT caution; efavirenz and rifampicin reduce levels`],[`Quinine`,`10 mg/kg salt 8-hourly oral; IV 20 mg/kg loading over 4 h then 10 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`7 days total`,`Hypoglycaemia, cinchonism, QT prolongation, arrhythmia with fast infusion`],[`Clindamycin`,`10 mg/kg twice daily orally`,`7 days with quinine`,`Diarrhoea, C. difficile colitis`],[`IV/IM artesunate`,`2.4 mg/kg (3 mg/kg if under 20 kg) at 0, 12, 24 h then daily`,`At least 24 h, then full oral ACT`,`Delayed haemolysis day 7-21; safe in all trimesters`],[`Rectal artesunate`,`10 mg/kg single dose (children under 6 y)`,`Pre-referral only`,`Must be followed by referral and injectable treatment`],[`Primaquine`,`0.25 mg/kg daily (vivax) or 0.75 mg/kg weekly x 8 weeks if G6PD deficient`,`14 days`,`Haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; contraindicated in pregnancy and infants`]]},{heading:`Primaquine contraindications and cautions`,columns:[`Group`,`Primaquine 14-day (vivax)`,`Single dose (falciparum)`,`Reason`],rows:[[`Pregnant women`,`Contraindicated`,`Contraindicated`,`Fetal G6PD unknown; fetal haemolysis`],[`Infants`,`Not under 1 y (India); WHO from 6 months`,`Not under 1 y (India); WHO from 6 months`,`G6PD status unknown, immature methaemoglobin reductase`],[`Breastfeeding woman`,`Only if infant over 6 months or G6PD normal`,`Allowed (WHO low dose)`,`Drug enters milk in small amounts`],[`G6PD deficient (under 30%)`,`Not daily; 0.75 mg/kg weekly for 8 weeks under supervision`,`0.25 mg/kg allowed (WHO)`,`Oxidant haemolysis is dose-related`],[`Intermediate (30-70%) females`,`Daily 0.25 mg/kg with Hb monitoring on day 3, 8`,`Allowed`,`Partial deficiency - tafenoquine not allowed`],[`On quinacrine or other haemolytic drugs`,`Avoid`,`Avoid`,`Additive toxicity`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever with severe features in pregnancy`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating features`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Severe falciparum malaria`,`Fever, anaemia, hypoglycaemia, jaundice, coma`,`Smear/RDT positive`],[`HELLP / severe pre-eclampsia`,`Hypertension, proteinuria, epigastric pain, no fever`,`BP, urine protein, LDH, smear negative`],[`Acute fatty liver of pregnancy`,`Third trimester, hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, encephalopathy`,`Swansea criteria, ammonia, smear negative`],[`Pyelonephritis / urosepsis`,`Loin pain, dysuria, rigors`,`Urine culture, pyuria`],[`Dengue`,`Rash, leucopenia, rising haematocrit`,`NS1/IgM`],[`Viral hepatitis E`,`Prodrome then jaundice, very high ALT`,`Anti-HEV IgM`]]},{heading:`Severity criteria that differ in children and pregnancy`,columns:[`Feature`,`Children`,`Pregnant women`,`Action`],rows:[[`Coma`,`Blantyre score under 3`,`GCS under 11`,`IV artesunate, glucose, airway`],[`Severe anaemia`,`Hb under 5 g/dL`,`Hb under 7 g/dL`,`Transfuse`],[`Hypoglycaemia`,`Glucose under 2.2 mmol/L (40 mg/dL)`,`Glucose under 2.2 mmol/L, often recurrent on quinine`,`10% dextrose; maintenance infusion`],[`Respiratory distress`,`Deep acidotic breathing`,`Pulmonary oedema, worst after delivery`,`Correct acidosis; avoid fluid overload`],[`Seizures`,`2 or more in 24 h`,`Exclude eclampsia (MgSO4 if eclampsia)`,`Benzodiazepine; no prophylactic phenobarbital`]]}],redFlags:[`Any **falciparum malaria in a pregnant woman** - refer to a facility with a medical officer and obstetric care`,`**Drowsiness, convulsions or inability to drink/breastfeed** in a child with fever (IMNCI danger signs)`,`**Deep or fast breathing** in a febrile child (acidosis)`,`**Haemoglobin under 7 g/dL** in pregnancy or **under 5 g/dL** in a child`,`**Blood glucose under 2.2 mmol/L** or recurrent hypoglycaemia on quinine`,`**Breathlessness after delivery** in a woman treated for malaria (pulmonary oedema)`,`**Dark urine, jaundice or falling Hb** after primaquine (G6PD haemolysis)`,`Febrile **neonate of a mother with malaria** (congenital malaria)`],pearls:[`**IV artesunate in all trimesters** for severe malaria - the first trimester is no exception.`,`**No primaquine in pregnancy or infancy** - weekly chloroquine bridges vivax pregnancies until radical cure is possible.`,`**IPTp is not used in India**; test every fever and treat.`,`**VAR2CSA binding to chondroitin sulphate A** explains placental sequestration and why primigravidae suffer most.`,`**Pulmonary oedema strikes after delivery** - autotransfusion from the contracting uterus.`,`**Quinine causes hypoglycaemia** by releasing insulin - check glucose 4-hourly.`,`In children, **deep breathing without chest signs = acidosis**, not pneumonia.`,`Indian age bands: **under 1, 1-4, 5-8, 9-14, 15 and above**.`,`**SP never with co-trimoxazole** - an HIV-positive pregnant woman gets AL.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-q1`,stem:`A 24-year-old primigravida at 10 weeks' gestation from a tribal block of Odisha has fever for 2 days; RDT is positive for P. falciparum. She is alert, taking orally, Hb 10.5 g/dL, glucose normal. Which treatment does the Indian national guideline use?`,options:[`Artesunate 3 days plus SP on day 1`,`Oral quinine 10 mg/kg 8-hourly for 7 days (with clindamycin)`,`Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days with primaquine`,`Doxycycline plus quinine for 7 days`],answer:1,explanation:`In the first trimester, the Indian NCVBDC guideline treats uncomplicated falciparum with oral quinine for 7 days, with clindamycin where available. WHO since 2022 prefers artemether-lumefantrine here. ACT-SP is used in the second and third trimesters. Chloroquine does not work against Indian falciparum, and primaquine is contraindicated in pregnancy. Doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-q2`,stem:`A woman at 30 weeks' gestation is treated for P. vivax malaria with chloroquine. What is the correct plan to prevent relapse?`,options:[`Primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days after a normal G6PD test`,`Single-dose tafenoquine 300 mg`,`Weekly chloroquine prophylaxis until delivery and breastfeeding allow radical cure`,`Primaquine 0.75 mg/kg weekly for 8 weeks`],answer:2,explanation:`Primaquine and tafenoquine are contraindicated in pregnancy because the fetus's G6PD status is unknown and oxidant haemolysis can occur, even if the mother's own G6PD is normal. Relapses are suppressed with weekly chloroquine 300 mg base until delivery. Radical cure follows once the infant is G6PD normal or over 6 months. The weekly 0.75 mg/kg regimen is for G6PD-deficient non-pregnant adults.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-q3`,stem:`A 3-year-old child with falciparum malaria has a Blantyre coma score of 2 and deep, rapid breathing with clear lungs. Which is the most appropriate immediate management?`,options:[`Oral artemether-lumefantrine and observe`,`IV ceftriaxone alone pending blood culture`,`Rapid 20 mL/kg saline boluses repeated until breathing settles`,`IV artesunate 3 mg/kg, bedside glucose, and treat acidosis-related causes`],answer:3,explanation:`Blantyre score under 3 defines cerebral malaria and deep breathing signals metabolic acidosis - both severe malaria. Children under 20 kg receive artesunate 3 mg/kg IV/IM, with immediate glucose check. Oral ACT is inadequate for severe disease. Antibiotics can be added but cannot replace antimalarial. Aggressive boluses increased mortality in the FEAST trial in children without shock.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-q4`,stem:`Why is intermittent preventive treatment in pregnancy (IPTp-SP) not part of India's malaria programme?`,options:[`India has low and unstable transmission, where testing every fever and treating is preferred`,`SP is teratogenic in all trimesters`,`Indian falciparum is fully resistant to artemisinin`,`Pregnant women in India are immune to malaria`],answer:0,explanation:`WHO recommends IPTp-SP for moderate-to-high transmission areas, mainly in Africa, where many infected women have no symptoms. India's transmission is low and focal, so the programme uses an LLIN at the first ANC visit plus testing and treating every fever. SP is used in the second and third trimesters as part of ACT, so it is not teratogenic there. Indian women have little immunity, so they are not protected from malaria.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-q5`,stem:`Placental sequestration of P. falciparum in primigravidae is mediated by binding of which parasite ligand to which host receptor?`,options:[`PfEMP1 variant VAR2CSA to chondroitin sulphate A`,`Duffy-binding protein to the Duffy antigen`,`PfEMP1 to ICAM-1 on cerebral endothelium`,`Circumsporozoite protein to hepatocyte HSPG`],answer:0,explanation:`VAR2CSA, a PfEMP1 variant, binds chondroitin sulphate A on the syncytiotrophoblast. Primigravidae lack antibodies to it, so they have the most placental malaria and low birth weight. ICAM-1 binding drives cerebral sequestration, not placental. Duffy-binding protein is how P. vivax enters red cells. Circumsporozoite protein acts at the liver stage and is the basis of the RTS,S vaccine.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the effects of malaria on pregnancy and outline the management of uncomplicated and severe malaria in a pregnant woman in India.`,openingLines:[`Malaria in pregnancy is Plasmodium infection from conception to delivery; in low-transmission India, non-immune women develop symptomatic and often severe disease, with placental sequestration causing fetal growth restriction.`,`Treatment is chosen by species and trimester - chloroquine for vivax, quinine or AL in the first and ACT in later trimesters for falciparum, IV artesunate for severe disease at any stage, and no primaquine.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`VAR2CSA binds chondroitin sulphate A, so parasites sequester in the placenta; this is worst in primigravidae.`,`Immune changes of pregnancy and higher attraction to mosquitoes add to the risk.`]},{heading:`Effects on mother and fetus`,points:[`Mother: anaemia, hypoglycaemia, cerebral malaria, pulmonary oedema (especially after delivery), AKI, death.`,`Fetus: miscarriage, stillbirth, preterm birth, IUGR, low birth weight, congenital malaria.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`RDT or smear at every fever; peripheral smear may be negative with placental infection.`,`Also check Hb, glucose, platelets, creatinine, bilirubin, BP and urine protein, and do an obstetric ultrasound.`]},{heading:`Uncomplicated malaria`,points:[`Vivax: chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days, then weekly chloroquine 300 mg base until delivery.`,`Falciparum first trimester: quinine 10 mg/kg 8-hourly + clindamycin for 7 days (WHO 2022: AL).`,`Falciparum second/third trimester: AS + SP (AL in North-East).`]},{heading:`Severe malaria`,points:[`IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily in every trimester; then a full ACT.`,`Glucose 4-hourly, cautious fluids, transfuse if Hb under 7, prop up after delivery, treat seizures; deliver only for obstetric indications.`]},{heading:`Prevention and follow-up`,points:[`Give an LLIN at the first ANC visit; IPTp is not used in India; manage anaemia.`,`Follow up with a smear on days 3, 7, 14 and 28, and give radical cure after delivery once breastfeeding conditions are met.`]}],mustDraw:[`Trimester-wise treatment table (vivax / falciparum / severe)`,`Flow chart of fever in pregnancy in an endemic area`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathophysiology and effects`,marks:3},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Uncomplicated treatment by trimester`,marks:3},{part:`Severe malaria management`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`VAR2CSA`,`quinine first trimester`,`IV artesunate`,`no primaquine`,`LLIN`,`IPTp not in India`]},{id:`infectious-fever-malaria-pregnancy-children-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on primaquine: indications, dosing and contraindications, including G6PD testing.`,openingLines:[`Primaquine is an 8-aminoquinoline that kills vivax hypnozoites (radical cure) and falciparum gametocytes (transmission blocking).`,`Its oxidant metabolites cause dose-related haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, so it is withheld in pregnancy and infancy and given after a G6PD test.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications and dose`,points:[`Vivax/ovale radical cure: 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days (adult 15 mg); WHO option 0.5 mg/kg for 7 days.`,`Falciparum gametocidal: single dose 0.75 mg/kg in India, or 0.25 mg/kg per WHO.`]},{heading:`G6PD testing`,points:[`Qualitative fluorescent spot test or RDT picks up under 30% activity; a quantitative biosensor separates intermediate females.`,`If G6PD deficient: 0.75 mg/kg once weekly for 8 weeks under supervision.`]},{heading:`Contraindications`,points:[`Pregnancy, infants (under 1 year in India, under 6 months in WHO guidance), and breastfeeding of an infant with unknown G6PD status.`,`Severe G6PD deficiency (for daily dosing), concurrent haemolytic drugs, and acute illness that suppresses the marrow.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`Counsel about dark urine, pallor and jaundice; check Hb on day 3 and day 8 in intermediate patients.`,`Poor CYP2D6 metabolisers may relapse despite full adherence.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: groups vs primaquine 14-day vs single dose`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and indications`,marks:1},{part:`Doses`,marks:1.5},{part:`G6PD testing`,marks:1},{part:`Contraindications and monitoring`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`hypnozoite`,`radical cure`,`G6PD`,`0.25 mg/kg 14 days`,`0.75 mg/kg weekly`,`pregnancy contraindicated`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Malaria chapter`,`WHO Guidelines for Malaria, consolidated update 2025 (incorporating 2022 first-trimester AL recommendation)`,`WHO Severe Malaria, Tropical Medicine and International Health supplement, 2014`,`National Centre for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC), National Guidelines for Diagnosis and Treatment of Malaria in India, and National Strategic Plan for Malaria Elimination 2023-2027`,`World Malaria Report 2024, WHO`,`Williams Obstetrics, 26th edition, 2022`,`Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd edition, 2024`,`CDC Yellow Book 2026: Health Information for International Travel`]});var S=[];S.push({id:`infectious-fever-je-aes`,title:`Japanese encephalitis and acute encephalitis syndrome`,oneLiner:`**Acute encephalitis syndrome (AES)** is **acute fever with altered mental status and/or new seizures** in a person of any age at any time of year; in India its leading treatable causes are **scrub typhus, Japanese encephalitis (JE, a Culex-borne flavivirus with pigs as amplifiers), HSV, dengue, malaria and litchi-associated hypoglycaemic encephalopathy** - so every AES child gets a **bedside glucose, empirical doxycycline or azithromycin and acyclovir** while JE, which has no antiviral, is prevented by **JE vaccine under UIP**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`JE`,`AES`,`Japanese B encephalitis`,`chamki bukhar`,`litchi encephalopathy`,`hypoglycin A`,`MCPG`,`Muzaffarpur`,`Gorakhpur`,`Culex tritaeniorhynchus`,`SA 14-14-2`,`JENVAC`,`JEEV`,`Chandipura virus`,`scrub typhus encephalitis`,`NPPCJA`,`MAC-ELISA`,`thalamic lesions`,`acyclovir`,`Dastak campaign`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**AES (WHO/NCVBDC syndromic case definition)**: a person of **any age, at any time of year**, with **acute onset of fever** and a **change in mental status** (confusion, disorientation, coma, inability to talk) **and/or new-onset seizures**, excluding simple febrile seizures. [WHO JE surveillance standard 2018]`,`**Laboratory-confirmed JE** is an AES case with **JE-specific IgM in CSF (preferred) or serum** by MAC-ELISA, or JE RNA or antigen, in a person **not recently vaccinated** against JE. [WHO JE surveillance standard 2018]`,`**Japanese encephalitis** is the commonest vaccine-preventable cause of viral encephalitis in Asia: about **68,000 clinical cases a year** in **24 countries** of the WHO South-East Asia and Western Pacific regions, with over **3 billion people at risk**. [WHO JE fact sheet 2024]`,`Only about **1 in 250 infections** is symptomatic, but of those with encephalitis the **case fatality is 20-30%** and **30-50% of survivors** have lasting neurological, cognitive or psychiatric sequelae. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India**: JE was first recognised in **Vellore (1955)**; the large **Bankura, West Bengal epidemic (1973)** and the **Gorakhpur, Uttar Pradesh epidemic (2005, over 6000 cases and 1500 deaths)** made it a national priority. [NCVBDC]`,`AES and JE are reported from most states, but the burden is concentrated in **Uttar Pradesh (Gorakhpur division), Bihar, Assam, West Bengal and Tamil Nadu**, with a **monsoon and post-monsoon peak (June-November)**; Assam peaks earlier in June-July. [NCVBDC]`,`JE explains only a **minority (roughly 5-20%) of AES** in India today, because vaccination has reduced it while **scrub typhus, dengue, HSV, enteroviruses and non-infectious causes** fill the rest - so AES is a syndrome to work up, not a synonym for JE. [ICMR]`,`**Muzaffarpur, Bihar** has a separate seasonal AES (**May-July**, litchi harvest) in young undernourished children, shown to be a **non-inflammatory hypoglycaemic encephalopathy** linked to litchi toxins; over **150 child deaths** occurred in the **2019** season. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**JE virus** is a single-stranded positive-sense RNA **flavivirus** (same family as dengue, West Nile and Zika); **genotype III** historically dominated India, with **genotype I** now co-circulating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vector**: mainly **Culex tritaeniorhynchus** (also C. vishnui and C. gelidus), which breeds in **flooded rice paddies and irrigation ditches** and bites outdoors at **dusk and night**. [NCVBDC]`,`**Enzootic cycle**: **ardeid birds (herons, egrets)** are maintenance hosts and **pigs are amplifying hosts** with high viraemia; **humans and horses are dead-end hosts** whose viraemia is too low to infect mosquitoes, so there is no person-to-person spread. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Risk factors for JE**: rural residence near **paddy fields and pigsties**, age **under 15 years** in endemic areas (adults are immune by exposure), outdoor sleeping, monsoon season and **lack of JE vaccination**; in newly affected areas (Assam) adults are also hit. [WHO 2024]`,`**Other infectious causes of AES in India**: **scrub typhus**, dengue, **HSV-1**, enteroviruses, measles, mumps, VZV, **Chandipura virus** (sandfly-borne rhabdovirus), **Nipah**, West Nile, rabies, **cerebral malaria**, tuberculous and bacterial meningitis. [ICMR]`,`**Non-infectious AES**: **litchi-associated hypoglycaemic encephalopathy**, autoimmune encephalitis (**anti-NMDAR**), ADEM, toxic and metabolic encephalopathy (pesticides, Reye syndrome, hepatic or uraemic), heat stroke and sepsis-associated encephalopathy. [ICMR]`,`**Risk factors for litchi encephalopathy**: child aged **1-10 years**, **undernutrition**, spending the day in **litchi orchards eating fruit (matched OR 9.6)** and **skipping the evening meal (OR 2.2)**; onset is typically **in the early morning**. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`After the bite, JE virus replicates in **dermal and lymphoid tissue**, produces a brief low viraemia, and crosses the **blood-brain barrier** through endothelium; most infections are cleared by **neutralising antibody** before neuroinvasion - hence the 1:250 ratio. [Harrison 22e]`,`JE virus has a **tropism for the thalamus, basal ganglia, substantia nigra, brainstem and anterior horn cells** - which explains the **parkinsonian features, dystonia, coma, breathing irregularity and poliomyelitis-like flaccid paralysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Neuronal death is driven by direct viral cytopathy plus **microglial activation and cytokine storm (TNF-alpha, IL-6)**; the result is **cerebral oedema, raised intracranial pressure and seizures**, the usual immediate causes of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIADH** and cerebral salt wasting are common in encephalitis and produce **hyponatraemia**, which lowers the seizure threshold and worsens oedema - so sodium is checked on admission and daily. [IAP]`,`**Litchi toxins**: unripe litchi contains **hypoglycin A** and **methylenecyclopropylglycine (MCPG)**, whose metabolites block **fatty-acid beta-oxidation** and **gluconeogenesis**; a child with little glycogen who misses dinner cannot raise glucose overnight and develops **hypoglycaemia with a toxic encephalopathy** by dawn. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`Because litchi encephalopathy is **metabolic, not inflammatory**, the **CSF is normal (under 5 cells)** and the child may stay encephalopathic even after glucose is corrected, as accumulated acyl-carnitines are neurotoxic - the same mechanism as **Jamaican vomiting sickness (ackee)**. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`**Scrub typhus AES**: Orientia tsutsugamushi causes an **endothelial vasculitis** of the brain, producing meningoencephalitis with **lymphocytic CSF and low or normal glucose**; it responds within **48 hours to doxycycline or azithromycin** and does not respond to beta-lactams. [ICMR]`,`**HSV-1 encephalitis** is a necrotising haemorrhagic encephalitis of the **medial temporal and orbitofrontal lobes**; untreated mortality is about **70%**, falling to under 20% with **early acyclovir** - the reason acyclovir is started before the PCR result. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**AES surveillance categories (NCVBDC)**: **suspected AES** (clinical case definition), **laboratory-confirmed JE**, **AES due to other agents** (scrub, dengue, HSV, etc.) and **AES of unknown cause** - every case is reported through **IDSP-IHIP**. [NCVBDC]`,`**International Encephalitis Consortium (2013) criteria**: the major criterion is **altered mental status for 24 hours or more** with no other cause; **2 minor criteria = possible**, **3 or more = probable/confirmed** encephalitis. [IEC 2013]`,`**Minor criteria (IEC 2013)**: **fever 38 C or more within 72 hours**, generalised or partial **seizures** not due to a known seizure disorder, **new focal neurological signs**, **CSF white cells 5/mm3 or more**, **neuroimaging** suggesting encephalitis, and an **EEG** abnormality consistent with encephalitis. [IEC 2013]`,`**Clinical stages of JE**: **prodrome** (2-4 days of fever, headache, vomiting), **acute encephalitic stage** (altered sensorium, seizures, focal deficits, movement disorder), **late stage and sequelae** (recovery or persistent deficits). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity at admission** is graded by the **Glasgow Coma Scale** (paediatric GCS in children): **GCS 8 or less**, status epilepticus, signs of herniation or shock define severe AES needing PICU or ICU. [IAP]`,`**Litchi encephalopathy (operational)**: AES in a child from a litchi area in the season with **blood glucose under 70 mg/dL**, **normal CSF** and no other cause - used to trigger glucose protocols rather than as a formal criterion. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Incubation of JE is 5-15 days**; the illness starts with **abrupt high fever, headache, vomiting and malaise**, often mistaken for an ordinary viral fever for 1-4 days. [Harrison 22e]`,`The encephalitic stage brings **altered sensorium (drowsiness to coma), irritability, abnormal behaviour and seizures** - **seizures occur in most children** and fewer adults, and may be subtle (twitching, eye deviation). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **season, residence near paddy and pigs, JE vaccination**, recent forest or grass exposure (**scrub**), sandfly-infested mud houses (**Chandipura**), bat-bitten fruit or a sick contact (**Nipah**) and travel. [NCVBDC]`,`In a Muzaffarpur-type case ask: **litchi eaten the previous day, missed evening meal**, onset **in the early morning (4-7 am)** with sudden seizures and unconsciousness, usually **without a prodromal fever**. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`**Clues for HSV**: **behaviour change, olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, aphasia, focal seizures** and a temporal-lobe picture; **clues for scrub**: fever over 5 days before encephalopathy, **eschar**, rash, breathlessness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clues for autoimmune (anti-NMDAR) encephalitis**: young woman or child with **psychiatric symptoms, orofacial dyskinesia, autonomic instability and reduced speech** over days to weeks, often without high fever; ask about **ovarian teratoma**. [Graus criteria 2016]`,`Ask for **drugs and toxins** (organophosphates, salicylates in Reye syndrome, alcohol), **diabetes and insulin use**, and **dog bite** (rabies) before labelling an infectious encephalitis. [ICMR]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Start with **ABC, GCS, pupils and bedside capillary glucose** in the first 5 minutes - **hypoglycaemia is the one cause of coma that kills or maims within hours and is cured within minutes**. [IAP]`,`**Signs of raised ICP**: **Cushing triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing)**, unequal or sluggish pupils, decerebrate or decorticate posturing, papilloedema (often absent early) - each demands immediate ICP measures. [IAP]`,`**JE**: **mask-like facies, tremor, cogwheel rigidity, choreoathetosis and dystonia** (thalamus and basal ganglia), opsoclonus, cranial nerve palsies, and **acute flaccid paralysis of a limb** (anterior horn cells). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neck stiffness** is present in many viral encephalitides but is more marked in **bacterial and tuberculous meningitis**; a purpuric rash points to **meningococcaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hunt for an eschar** in the axilla, groin, waistband and genitals, plus **lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly** - an eschar makes scrub typhus the working diagnosis. [ICMR]`,`Look for **hepatomegaly with hypoglycaemia** (Reye-like), **jaundice and pallor** (cerebral malaria, leptospirosis), **parotid swelling** (mumps), a **vesicular rash** (VZV) and **hydrophobia or aerophobia** (rabies). [Harrison 22e]`,`In litchi encephalopathy the child is **afebrile or mildly febrile**, with **sudden convulsions, coma and often decerebrate posturing**, but **no meningeal signs**; liver may be enlarged. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Bacterial meningitis**: abrupt fever, **neck stiffness, purpura**, CSF **neutrophils with glucose under 40 mg/dL** (or CSF:blood under 0.4) and high protein - give ceftriaxone without waiting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tuberculous meningitis**: subacute over **more than 5 days**, cranial nerve palsies, **CSF lymphocytes with low glucose and protein over 100 mg/dL**, basal exudates and hydrocephalus on imaging. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral malaria**: fever with coma in a malarious area, **positive smear or RDT**, hypoglycaemia, anaemia and retinopathy - give **IV artesunate** immediately. [WHO 2025]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: focal temporal signs, **CSF red cells**, **temporal lobe T2/FLAIR hyperintensity** on MRI and **periodic lateralised epileptiform discharges** on EEG. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus meningoencephalitis**: fever for 5-7 days before encephalopathy, eschar, **thrombocytopenia, transaminitis, ARDS** and a positive **scrub IgM ELISA**. [ICMR]`,`**Litchi hypoglycaemic encephalopathy**: early-morning onset in summer, **low glucose, normal CSF** and no fever - treated by dextrose, not antimicrobials. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`**Chandipura**: rapid-onset fever, vomiting, seizures and coma in children **under 15** with death often **within 24-48 hours**, sandfly exposure in rural mud houses (Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh). [WHO DON 2024]`,`**Autoimmune encephalitis and ADEM**: subacute psychiatric or multifocal picture, **post-infectious or post-vaccinal** timing (ADEM), MRI showing large white-matter lesions (ADEM) and positive serum or CSF neuronal antibodies. [Graus criteria 2016]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Immediately**: capillary and venous **glucose**, **malaria RDT plus smear**, CBC, **sodium, potassium, calcium**, creatinine, LFT, blood culture, and arterial or venous blood gas if breathing is abnormal. [NCVBDC]`,`**Lumbar puncture** after excluding raised ICP clinically (or by CT): JE and viral AES give **lymphocytic pleocytosis 10-500 cells/mm3, protein 50-150 mg/dL, normal glucose**; defer LP in **GCS 8 or less, papilloedema, focal deficit, shock or bleeding** and start empirical treatment. [IAP]`,`**JE IgM capture ELISA (MAC-ELISA)** on **CSF** (most specific) and **serum**; IgM appears by **day 3-7** of illness, so a **negative early serum** is repeated after day 7-10; **cross-reactivity with dengue** and recent vaccination can confuse serum results. [WHO JE surveillance standard 2018]`,`Send in parallel **scrub typhus IgM ELISA**, **dengue NS1/IgM**, **leptospira IgM**, and CSF **HSV-1/2 PCR** - CSF HSV PCR is over **95% sensitive and specific**, but may be negative in the first 72 hours, so repeat if suspicion is high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuroimaging**: **MRI** is preferred - JE shows **bilateral thalamic T2/FLAIR hyperintensity (often haemorrhagic)** with basal ganglia, substantia nigra and brainstem involvement; HSV shows **medial temporal and insular** lesions; CT is used first if MRI is unavailable or to exclude a mass before LP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EEG** helps in non-convulsive status (common in comatose encephalitis) and shows **PLEDs** in HSV; anti-NMDAR encephalitis may show an **extreme delta brush** pattern. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Referral labs**: AES samples go to **sentinel sites and apex labs (ICMR-NIV Pune and its Gorakhpur unit, VRDL network)** for JE, enterovirus, Chandipura and Nipah testing; **autoimmune panels** (anti-NMDAR in CSF) when infection is negative. [NCVBDC]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Any child or adult with **fever plus altered sensorium or new seizures** is an **AES emergency**: stabilise and refer to the nearest **AES treatment centre (designated PHC/CHC, district hospital or medical college)** - the first hour decides outcome. [NCVBDC]`,`**Check glucose before transfer**; if under **70 mg/dL** (or unmeasurable and the child is comatose), give **10% dextrose 5 mL/kg IV** in children (adults 25% dextrose 100 mL or 50% dextrose 50 mL), and continue a **dextrose-containing infusion** during transfer. [Bihar AES SOP]`,`**Stop a seizure before transfer**: **midazolam 0.2 mg/kg intranasal or buccal (max 10 mg)** or **IV lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg (max 4 mg)** / IV diazepam 0.2-0.3 mg/kg; place in **recovery position**, give oxygen. [IAP]`,`**Give the first dose of empirical treatment if transfer will take over 1 hour**: **ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg (max 2 g) IV/IM** plus **doxycycline** or **azithromycin 10 mg/kg** for scrub typhus; give **artesunate** if the RDT is positive. [ICMR]`,`Treat **fever with paracetamol 15 mg/kg** and tepid sponging; **avoid aspirin** (Reye syndrome) and NSAIDs until dengue is excluded. [IAP]`,`Community health workers (ASHA) are trained to recognise **chamki bukhar**, feed a sugar solution to a conscious child, and arrange **free transport (108/102 ambulance)** to the nearest centre - the Bihar and Uttar Pradesh model. [Bihar AES SOP]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Airway and breathing**: intubate for **GCS 8 or less**, loss of airway reflexes, status epilepticus or respiratory failure; keep **SpO2 94-98%** and **PaCO2 35-40 mmHg** (avoid hyperventilation except for impending herniation). [IAP]`,`**Circulation and fluids**: treat shock with isotonic crystalloid boluses; otherwise give **normal-saline-based maintenance with 5-10% dextrose at about two-thirds of maintenance** only if SIADH is present, and keep **sodium 135-145 mmol/L**. [IAP]`,`**Raised ICP**: head end **15-30 degrees**, neutral neck, normothermia, normoglycaemia; **mannitol 20% 0.5-1 g/kg IV over 20 minutes** 6-8 hourly or **3% saline 5-10 mL/kg** then 0.1-1 mL/kg/hour, targeting serum sodium 145-155 mmol/L. [IAP]`,`**Seizures and status epilepticus**: benzodiazepine, then **levetiracetam 40-60 mg/kg IV (max 4.5 g)**, **fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg** or **valproate 40 mg/kg**; refractory status needs midazolam infusion and EEG. [ESETT 2019]`,`**Empirical antimicrobials until results**: **acyclovir 10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** (20 mg/kg 8-hourly from 3 months to 12 years) for **14-21 days if HSV confirmed**; **ceftriaxone 100 mg/kg/day** until bacterial meningitis is excluded; and **doxycycline or azithromycin** for scrub typhus in all AES in endemic belts. [ICMR]`,`**Scrub typhus AES with severity**: the **INTREST trial** found **IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin** better than either alone for **severe scrub typhus**, so combination therapy is reasonable in scrub meningoencephalitis. [NEJM 2023]`,`**JE itself has no proven antiviral**: trials of **ribavirin, interferon alfa-2a, IV immunoglobulin, minocycline and dexamethasone** showed **no mortality benefit**; care is supportive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supportive care**: 2-hourly position change, eye care, NG feeding by 24-48 hours, bladder care, physiotherapy from day 1, and screening for **aspiration pneumonia** - nursing quality is a major determinant of survival. [NCVBDC]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children under 15** carry most JE and almost all litchi AES; drug doses are by weight, and **doxycycline short courses are safe at any age** (no dental staining at 2.2 mg/kg 12-hourly for under 21 days). [AAP Red Book 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: JE in the first and second trimesters can cause **miscarriage and intrauterine infection**; use **azithromycin rather than doxycycline** for suspected scrub, acyclovir is safe, and **live SA 14-14-2 vaccine is contraindicated** (inactivated vaccine may be used if risk is high). [WHO JE position paper 2015]`,`**Adults in newly endemic areas** (Assam, parts of West Bengal and Uttar Pradesh) have high JE attack rates and higher mortality, the reason for **adult JE vaccination campaigns (15-65 years)** in those districts. [NCVBDC]`,`**Elderly and immunocompromised**: encephalitis may present with **confusion and little fever**; widen the search to **Listeria (add ampicillin), cryptococcus, CMV, VZV and TB**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where LP, MRI or PCR are unavailable, treat syndromically - **glucose, artesunate if RDT positive, ceftriaxone, doxycycline/azithromycin and acyclovir** - and refer; never delay dextrose or antibiotics for a test. [ICMR]`,`**Travellers** to rural Asia for **over a month in the season**, or shorter trips with heavy rural exposure, should receive **inactivated Vero-cell JE vaccine** before travel. [CDC Yellow Book 2026]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Acute complications**: status epilepticus, **raised ICP with herniation**, aspiration pneumonia, **hyponatraemia**, upper GI bleed (stress ulcer), pressure sores and nosocomial sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Case fatality** of JE encephalitis is **20-30%**, higher in infants and the elderly; **poor prognostic markers** are deep coma (GCS under 8), status epilepticus, **raised ICP**, respiratory failure and high CSF viral load. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sequelae in 30-50% of JE survivors**: **intellectual disability, behavioural change, epilepsy, parkinsonism and dystonia, motor deficits, speech and hearing loss** - a lifelong burden for families. [WHO 2024]`,`**HSV encephalitis**: even with acyclovir, many survivors have **memory and behavioural deficits**, and about a quarter develop **post-HSV anti-NMDAR encephalitis** within weeks - a treatable cause of relapse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Litchi encephalopathy** has a high case fatality when glucose correction is delayed beyond **4 hours**; early dextrose markedly improves survival. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`**Chandipura** carries a case fatality of **up to 50-75%** in outbreaks, often within 48 hours of onset. [WHO DON 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer immediately** every AES case to a centre with **paediatric or adult ICU** if GCS falls, seizures recur, shock or respiratory failure develops. [NCVBDC]`,`**Monitor in hospital**: **GCS, pupils and vitals 1-2 hourly**, glucose **4-6 hourly** (hourly in litchi encephalopathy), sodium 12-24 hourly, input-output, and seizures charted. [IAP]`,`**After discharge**: review at **2 weeks, 3 months and 6 months** for development, cognition, behaviour, hearing, vision, epilepsy and movement disorder; plan **anti-seizure drug withdrawal** after 1-2 seizure-free years. [IAP]`,`Link survivors to **physical medicine and rehabilitation (PMR) departments and District Early Intervention Centres (DEIC under RBSK)** - rehabilitation is a named component of the NPPCJA. [NPPCJA]`,`Report every case to **IDSP-IHIP within 24 hours** and send CSF and serum for JE IgM; a **cluster of AES** triggers a rapid response team investigation. [IDSP]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and the national programme`,points:[`**JE vaccine in UIP**: live attenuated **SA 14-14-2** in endemic districts, **2 doses - at 9-12 months (with MR) and 16-24 months (with DPT booster)**; the campaign began in **2006** and routine use followed. [UIP / MoHFW]`,`**Indian inactivated vaccines**: **JENVAC** (Bharat Biotech; Indian Kolar strain, Vero cell) and **JEEV** (Biological E; SA 14-14-2, Vero cell, the same product as IXIARO) - preferred in pregnancy and immunocompromise. [MoHFW]`,`**Adult JE vaccination (15-65 years)** has been carried out in high-burden districts of **Assam, Uttar Pradesh and West Bengal** where adult cases were frequent. [NCVBDC]`,`**NPPCJA (National Programme for Prevention and Control of JE/AES, 2014)** is a **multi-ministry convergence plan** covering vaccination, safe drinking water and sanitation, nutrition, vector control, early case management and **rehabilitation** in high-burden districts. [MoHFW 2014]`,`**Vector control**: larvivorous fish, intermittent irrigation of paddies, **pigsties away from homes**, fogging in outbreaks, and personal protection (repellents, full sleeves, nets) - bednets help less because Culex bites outdoors at dusk. [NCVBDC]`,`**Litchi AES prevention**: ensure children **eat an evening meal**, limit litchi eating (especially unripe fruit) in the season, and **give glucose to any child found unwell in the morning** - with this, Muzaffarpur deaths fell sharply. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`**Uttar Pradesh Dastak campaign (from 2018)**: door-to-door awareness, early referral, JE vaccination, sanitation and **empirical doxycycline or azithromycin** at peripheral AES centres - AES mortality in Gorakhpur division fell steeply. [UP NHM]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**INTREST trial (NEJM 2023)**: for **severe scrub typhus, IV doxycycline plus azithromycin** reduced the composite of death, complications and persisting illness at day 7 compared with either drug alone - relevant to scrub meningoencephalitis. [NEJM 2023]`,`**WHO Chandipura disease outbreak news (2024)**: Gujarat reported the **largest Chandipura outbreak in 20 years** (about 245 AES cases with 82 deaths, June-August 2024), reinforcing **sandfly control** and Chandipura testing in AES. [WHO DON 2024]`,`**WHO encephalitis technical brief (2024)**: calls encephalitis a neglected global syndrome, stresses **early acyclovir**, access to **MRI, EEG and CSF PCR**, vaccination (JE, measles) and **rehabilitation** for survivors. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO JE fact sheet (2024)** keeps the **inactivated Vero-cell, live attenuated and live recombinant** vaccines as acceptable, with a **single dose of live attenuated or recombinant** vaccine sufficient for long-term protection. [WHO 2024]`,`**Bihar AES SOP (updated seasonally since 2019)**: mandatory **glucometer check and 10% dextrose** at every PHC, night-time ASHA visits during litchi season and free ambulance transfer. [Bihar AES SOP]`,`**CDC Yellow Book (2026)** continues to recommend IXIARO for travellers to endemic Asia with prolonged or rural exposure, with the **accelerated 0, 7-day schedule for adults 18-65**. [CDC Yellow Book 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define **AES precisely** (fever plus altered sensorium and/or new seizures, any age, any season) - examiners penalise defining AES as JE. [WHO JE surveillance standard 2018]`,`Name the **transmission cycle**: **Culex tritaeniorhynchus - pigs (amplifier) - ardeid birds (reservoir) - humans (dead end)**; a common error is to call humans the reservoir or Aedes the vector. [Harrison 22e]`,`The classic MRI is **bilateral thalamic** involvement in JE versus **temporal lobe** involvement in HSV. [Harrison 22e]`,`The single most important bedside test in AES is a **glucometer reading** - litchi encephalopathy is hypoglycaemia until proven otherwise. [Lancet Glob Health 2017]`,`**Scrub typhus is the commonest treatable cause** of AES in eastern Uttar Pradesh; give **doxycycline or azithromycin** to every AES case in that belt. [ICMR]`,`Common mistakes: giving **steroids** for JE, giving **aspirin** for fever, waiting for **HSV PCR before acyclovir**, and doing **LP in a deeply comatose child** with signs of herniation. [IAP]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in acute encephalitis syndrome`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dextrose 10%`,`Child 5 mL/kg IV bolus; adult 25% dextrose 100 mL or 50% dextrose 50 mL IV`,`Then 10% dextrose-containing infusion; recheck glucose at 15-30 min and hourly`,`Hyperglycaemia; thrombophlebitis with 25-50% solutions in small veins`],[`Acyclovir`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly over 1 hour (20 mg/kg 8-hourly from 3 months to 12 years)`,`14-21 days if HSV confirmed; stop if CSF PCR negative and another cause found`,`Crystal nephropathy - hydrate, adjust for eGFR; phlebitis; neurotoxicity in CKD`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`100 mg/kg/day IV in 1-2 doses (max 4 g/day); adult 2 g IV 12-hourly`,`Until bacterial meningitis excluded; 7-14 days if confirmed`,`Biliary sludge; avoid with IV calcium in neonates; allergy`],[`Doxycycline`,`Adult 100 mg 12-hourly oral/IV; child 2.2 mg/kg 12-hourly (max 100 mg)`,`7 days (or 3 days after fever settles)`,`Oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin`,`Adult 500 mg IV/oral daily; child 10 mg/kg daily`,`5 days (with doxycycline in severe scrub per INTREST)`,`QT prolongation, GI upset; safe in pregnancy`],[`Mannitol 20%`,`0.5-1 g/kg (2.5-5 mL/kg) IV over 20 minutes`,`6-8 hourly for 24-72 hours; keep serum osmolality under 320 mOsm/kg`,`Hypovolaemia, AKI, rebound oedema`],[`Hypertonic saline 3%`,`5-10 mL/kg IV bolus then 0.1-1 mL/kg/hour`,`Target sodium 145-155 mmol/L`,`Hypernatraemia, osmotic demyelination if sodium was chronically low`],[`Levetiracetam`,`40-60 mg/kg IV over 10-15 minutes (max 4.5 g); maintenance 10-30 mg/kg 12-hourly`,`Second-line for status; continue during acute phase`,`Behavioural change; reduce dose in CKD`]]},{heading:`Causes of AES in India - clues and first test`,columns:[`Cause`,`Epidemiological clue`,`Clinical clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Japanese encephalitis`,`Monsoon, paddy fields, pigs, unvaccinated child`,`Parkinsonism, dystonia, flaccid limb`,`JE IgM MAC-ELISA in CSF; MRI thalamic lesions`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Grass, farm or forest exposure, monsoon to winter`,`Fever over 5 days, eschar, ARDS, thrombocytopenia`,`Scrub IgM ELISA; response to doxycycline in 48 h`],[`Litchi hypoglycaemic encephalopathy`,`Muzaffarpur, May-July, missed dinner`,`Early-morning seizures, little fever`,`Glucose under 70 mg/dL; normal CSF`],[`HSV encephalitis`,`Any season, any age`,`Behaviour change, aphasia, focal seizures`,`CSF HSV PCR; MRI temporal lobe`],[`Cerebral malaria`,`Malarious district, travel`,`Coma, hypoglycaemia, anaemia, jaundice`,`Smear and RDT`],[`Chandipura virus`,`Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh; sandflies in mud houses`,`Child under 15, death in 24-48 h`,`RT-PCR at NIV`],[`Bacterial meningitis`,`Any season; CSF leak, otitis`,`Neck stiffness, purpura`,`CSF neutrophils, low glucose, Gram stain, culture`],[`Autoimmune (anti-NMDAR)`,`Young female, post-HSV`,`Psychiatric onset, dyskinesia, dysautonomia`,`CSF anti-NMDAR antibody; pelvic imaging`]]},{heading:`CSF patterns in the comatose febrile patient`,columns:[`Condition`,`Cells (per mm3)`,`Protein (mg/dL)`,`Glucose`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Under 5 lymphocytes`,`15-45`,`Over 60% of blood glucose`],[`Viral encephalitis (JE, HSV)`,`10-500, lymphocytes (red cells in HSV)`,`50-150`,`Normal`],[`Bacterial meningitis`,`Over 1000, neutrophils`,`Over 200`,`Low (CSF:blood under 0.4)`],[`Tuberculous meningitis`,`50-500, lymphocytes`,`100-500`,`Low`],[`Scrub typhus meningoencephalitis`,`10-300, lymphocytes`,`Mildly raised`,`Normal or mildly low`],[`Litchi encephalopathy / metabolic`,`Under 5`,`Normal`,`Normal (blood glucose low)`]]},{heading:`JE vaccines available in India`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Type`,`Schedule`,`Notes`],rows:[[`SA 14-14-2 (Chengdu)`,`Live attenuated, cell culture`,`UIP: 0.5 mL SC at 9-12 months and 16-24 months`,`Contraindicated in pregnancy and immunosuppression; one dose gives long protection`],[`JENVAC (Bharat Biotech)`,`Inactivated Vero cell, Indian Kolar strain`,`0.5 mL IM, 2 doses 4 weeks apart from 1 year`,`Indigenous; usable in pregnancy if risk high`],[`JEEV (Biological E)`,`Inactivated Vero cell, SA 14-14-2 strain`,`1-3 years 0.25 mL IM, over 3 years 0.5 mL IM; days 0 and 28`,`Same product as IXIARO used for travellers`],[`IXIARO (travellers)`,`Inactivated Vero cell`,`Days 0 and 28 (adults 18-65 may use 0 and 7); booster at 1 year if continued risk`,`Complete at least 1 week before travel`],[`Mouse-brain derived (Nakayama)`,`Inactivated`,`Discontinued`,`Withdrawn for ADEM and hypersensitivity`]]},{heading:`JE versus HSV versus litchi encephalopathy`,columns:[`Feature`,`Japanese encephalitis`,`HSV encephalitis`,`Litchi encephalopathy`],rows:[[`Season and place`,`Monsoon, paddy belts`,`Any season, anywhere`,`May-July, Muzaffarpur litchi belt`],[`Age`,`Children under 15 in endemic areas`,`Any age, bimodal`,`1-10 years, undernourished`],[`Onset`,`Prodrome 2-4 days then coma`,`Days of behaviour change`,`Sudden at dawn, no prodrome`],[`CSF`,`Lymphocytic pleocytosis`,`Lymphocytes plus red cells`,`Normal`],[`MRI`,`Bilateral thalami, basal ganglia`,`Medial temporal, insula`,`Diffuse oedema or normal`],[`Specific treatment`,`None - supportive`,`Acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`10% dextrose`],[`Prevention`,`JE vaccine, vector control`,`None`,`Evening meal, limit litchi`]]}],redFlags:[`Blood glucose under 70 mg/dL in a drowsy or convulsing child - give dextrose now`,`GCS 8 or less, or a fall of 2 or more points`,`Seizure lasting over 5 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery`,`Unequal pupils, abnormal posturing or Cushing triad - impending herniation`,`Shock, irregular breathing or SpO2 under 94%`,`Purpuric rash or neck stiffness with high fever - bacterial meningitis`,`Cluster of AES cases in a village or a sick contact with Nipah-like illness - notify at once`],pearls:[`AES is a syndrome; JE is one cause - and today scrub typhus is often the commoner one in eastern Uttar Pradesh.`,`Culex tritaeniorhynchus, pigs amplify, herons maintain, humans are dead ends - no person-to-person spread of JE.`,`Thalamus means JE; temporal lobe means HSV.`,`In AES, check sugar first: Glucose, Artesunate if RDT positive, Ceftriaxone, Doxy/azithro, Acyclovir - GACDA.`,`Litchi AES: hypoglycin A and MCPG block beta-oxidation - dawn onset, low sugar, normal CSF.`,`JE has no antiviral; ribavirin, interferon, IVIG, minocycline and steroids all failed trials.`,`UIP JE vaccine: SA 14-14-2 at 9-12 months and 16-24 months in endemic districts.`,`One in 250 JE infections is symptomatic, but a quarter of those die and up to half of survivors are disabled.`,`Never give aspirin to a febrile child with encephalopathy - Reye syndrome mimics AES.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-q1`,stem:`In the transmission cycle of Japanese encephalitis virus, which host acts as the principal amplifying host?`,options:[`Pigs`,`Humans`,`Fruit bats`,`Rodents`],answer:0,explanation:`Pigs develop high-titre viraemia and amplify JE virus for Culex tritaeniorhynchus, which then bites humans; ardeid birds are maintenance hosts. Humans are dead-end hosts with low viraemia and do not transmit, fruit bats are the reservoir of Nipah not JE, and rodents are linked to leptospirosis and scrub typhus.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-q2`,stem:`A 4-year-old undernourished child from Muzaffarpur is brought in June at 6 am with sudden seizures and unconsciousness after spending the previous day in an orchard and skipping dinner. Temperature is 37.8 C. What is the most important immediate step?`,options:[`Lumbar puncture for JE IgM`,`Intravenous acyclovir`,`Capillary glucose and 10% dextrose if low`,`CT scan of the brain`],answer:2,explanation:`This is the classic litchi-associated hypoglycaemic encephalopathy: hypoglycin A and MCPG block gluconeogenesis and beta-oxidation, so a fasting child becomes hypoglycaemic overnight. Checking glucose and giving 10% dextrose 5 mL/kg saves the brain within minutes. Lumbar puncture, CT and acyclovir all have a place in AES work-up but none corrects the lethal hypoglycaemia, and the CSF is usually normal.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-q3`,stem:`Which MRI finding is most characteristic of Japanese encephalitis?`,options:[`Unilateral medial temporal lobe hyperintensity`,`Ring-enhancing lesions at the grey-white junction`,`Periventricular ovoid lesions perpendicular to the ventricles`,`Bilateral thalamic T2/FLAIR hyperintensity, often haemorrhagic`],answer:3,explanation:`JE virus has a tropism for the thalamus, basal ganglia, substantia nigra and brainstem, so bilateral thalamic hyperintensity is its hallmark and explains the parkinsonian features. Medial temporal lobe involvement suggests HSV encephalitis, ring-enhancing lesions suggest neurocysticercosis, tuberculoma or abscess, and periventricular ovoid lesions suggest multiple sclerosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-q4`,stem:`A 9-year-old from Gorakhpur has had fever for 7 days followed by drowsiness and a seizure. Platelets are 60,000/mm3, ALT is 180 U/L and there is a small black scab in the groin. CSF shows 80 lymphocytes with normal glucose. Which empirical drug most directly treats the likely cause?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone`,`Doxycycline or azithromycin`,`Ribavirin`,`Dexamethasone`],answer:1,explanation:`Fever before encephalopathy, eschar, thrombocytopenia and transaminitis point to scrub typhus meningoencephalitis, the commonest treatable AES cause in eastern Uttar Pradesh; Orientia is intracellular and responds to doxycycline or azithromycin within 48 hours. Ceftriaxone has no activity against Orientia, ribavirin has no proven benefit in JE or scrub, and dexamethasone does not help viral or rickettsial encephalitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-q5`,stem:`Regarding JE vaccination under India's Universal Immunization Programme, which statement is correct?`,options:[`A single dose of inactivated vaccine is given at birth`,`It is given at 6, 10 and 14 weeks with pentavalent vaccine`,`Live SA 14-14-2 vaccine is given at 9-12 months and 16-24 months in endemic districts`,`The live vaccine is preferred in pregnant women in endemic districts`],answer:2,explanation:`UIP gives live attenuated SA 14-14-2 vaccine in two doses - at 9-12 months with MR and at 16-24 months with the DPT booster - in JE-endemic districts, with adult campaigns in high-burden districts. It is not given at birth or with the primary pentavalent series, and live vaccines are contraindicated in pregnancy, when an inactivated Vero-cell vaccine such as JENVAC or JEEV is used if risk is high.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define acute encephalitis syndrome. Discuss the aetiology, clinical approach and management of AES in India, and add a note on the prevention of Japanese encephalitis.`,openingLines:[`Acute encephalitis syndrome is a clinical case definition: a person of any age, at any time of year, with acute fever and altered mental status and/or new-onset seizures (excluding simple febrile seizures).`,`In India its main causes are scrub typhus, Japanese encephalitis, dengue, HSV, malaria and litchi-associated hypoglycaemic encephalopathy, and it is managed syndromically while specific tests are awaited.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`WHO/NCVBDC AES definition; laboratory-confirmed JE by IgM in CSF or serum.`,`JE: 68,000 cases a year in Asia; CFR 20-30%; sequelae in 30-50%.`,`India: UP, Bihar, Assam, West Bengal, Tamil Nadu; monsoon peak; Muzaffarpur litchi season May-July.`]},{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Viral: JE, HSV, dengue, enterovirus, Chandipura, Nipah, measles, mumps, rabies.`,`Bacterial/rickettsial: scrub typhus, pyogenic and TB meningitis; parasitic: cerebral malaria.`,`Non-infectious: litchi hypoglycaemia, autoimmune encephalitis, ADEM, toxic-metabolic.`]},{heading:`Clinical approach`,points:[`History: season, exposure, vaccination, prodrome length, litchi and missed meal, eschar, behaviour change.`,`Examination: GCS, pupils, ICP signs, eschar, movement disorder, meningism, rash, spleen.`,`Investigations: glucose first, malaria RDT, CBC, sodium, LP (if safe), JE and scrub IgM, HSV PCR, MRI, EEG.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`ABC, glucose (10% dextrose 5 mL/kg if under 70 mg/dL), stop seizures, treat raised ICP (mannitol or 3% saline).`,`Empirical acyclovir, ceftriaxone, doxycycline or azithromycin; artesunate if malaria.`,`Supportive care, sodium control, nutrition, physiotherapy; no antiviral for JE.`]},{heading:`Prevention of JE`,points:[`UIP SA 14-14-2 at 9-12 and 16-24 months; inactivated JENVAC, JEEV; adult campaigns.`,`Vector control, pigsties away from homes, personal protection; NPPCJA convergence; rehabilitation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Transmission cycle: Culex - pigs/ardeid birds - humans (dead end)`,`Flow chart of AES management in the first hour`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical approach and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`AES`,`JE`,`scrub typhus`,`litchi`,`acyclovir`,`SA 14-14-2`,`NPPCJA`]},{id:`infectious-fever-je-aes-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on litchi-associated acute encephalitis syndrome in Muzaffarpur.`,openingLines:[`Litchi-associated AES is a non-inflammatory hypoglycaemic encephalopathy of young undernourished children in the Muzaffarpur litchi belt during May-July.`,`It is caused by hypoglycin A and MCPG in litchi fruit, which block gluconeogenesis and fatty-acid oxidation when the evening meal is skipped.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and cause`,points:[`Children 1-10 years, undernourished, orchard exposure; litchi eating (OR 9.6) and missed dinner (OR 2.2).`,`Toxins hypoglycin A and MCPG; similar to ackee poisoning.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`Sudden early-morning seizures and coma, little fever, no meningism.`,`Blood glucose under 70 mg/dL, normal CSF, toxin metabolites in urine.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Glucometer at every PHC; 10% dextrose 5 mL/kg then infusion; seizure control; ICU care.`,`Empirical antimicrobials until infection excluded.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Evening meal for every child, limit litchi, ASHA visits, free ambulance - Bihar AES SOP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Mechanism: MCPG and hypoglycin A blocking beta-oxidation and gluconeogenesis`],markSplit:[{part:`Cause and epidemiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`litchi`,`hypoglycin A`,`MCPG`,`hypoglycaemia`,`Muzaffarpur`,`chamki bukhar`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Arthropod-borne and rodent-borne virus infections; Encephalitis`,`WHO Japanese encephalitis fact sheet, 2024; WHO Vaccine-Preventable Diseases Surveillance Standards: Japanese encephalitis, 2018`,`WHO position paper on Japanese encephalitis vaccines, Weekly Epidemiological Record, 2015`,`NCVBDC (MoHFW) guidelines for prevention, control and clinical management of AES/JE; National Programme for Prevention and Control of JE/AES, 2014`,`Shrivastava A et al. Association of acute toxic encephalopathy with litchi consumption in an outbreak in Muzaffarpur, India, 2014. Lancet Global Health, 2017`,`Varghese GM et al. Doxycycline, azithromycin, or both for severe scrub typhus (INTREST). New England Journal of Medicine, 2023`,`Venkatesan A et al. International Encephalitis Consortium case definitions and diagnostic algorithms, Clinical Infectious Diseases, 2013`,`WHO Disease Outbreak News: Chandipura virus - India, 2024`]});var C=[],w=[],T=[],E=[];E.push({id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas`,title:`Cellulitis, erysipelas and abscess: antibiotic choice`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Cellulitis** is an acute spreading infection of the **deep dermis and subcutaneous fat**, and **erysipelas** its superficial, sharply demarcated form involving the **upper dermis and lymphatics**; the treatment hinges on one bedside split - **non-purulent** (beta-haemolytic streptococci: a **beta-lactam such as cefalexin or cloxacillin for 5 days**) versus **purulent** (Staphylococcus aureus: **incision and drainage**, with an anti-MRSA oral agent for larger or complicated abscesses) - after first excluding the **mimics** that make up about a third of referred 'cellulitis'.`,keywords:[`cellulitis`,`erysipelas`,`St Anthony fire`,`skin abscess`,`furuncle`,`carbuncle`,`purulent cellulitis`,`non-purulent cellulitis`,`Eron classification`,`ALT-70 score`,`pseudocellulitis`,`Milian ear sign`,`MRSA`,`cefalexin`,`cloxacillin`,`flucloxacillin`,`co-trimoxazole`,`PATCH trial`,`benzathine penicillin prophylaxis`,`acute dermatolymphangioadenitis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Cellulitis** is an acute, non-necrotising bacterial infection of the **deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue**, presenting as a poorly demarcated, warm, tender, spreading erythema without a palpable edge. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Erysipelas** involves the **upper dermis and superficial lymphatics**, producing a **raised, shiny, sharply demarcated** plaque; many European texts treat it as a superficial cellulitis, and treatment is the same. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **skin abscess** is a collection of pus within the dermis or subcutis; a **furuncle** is an abscess of a single hair follicle and a **carbuncle** is a coalescence of several furuncles with multiple draining sinuses, typically on the nape or back. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Cellulitis is one of the commonest reasons for antibiotic prescribing and admission worldwide, with an incidence of roughly **25 per 1000 person-years** in a large US cohort, rising with age; the **lower limb is involved in over 70%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrence is the rule, not the exception**: about **one in three patients has another episode within 3 years**, mostly at the same site, driven by persisting lymphoedema, venous disease and toe-web intertrigo. [PATCH I, NEJM 2013]`,`In India, cellulitis is driven by **barefoot walking, tinea pedis, diabetes and filarial lymphoedema**; in endemic districts recurrent **acute dermatolymphangioadenitis (ADLA)** attacks are a form of streptococcal cellulitis on a lymphoedematous limb - see the Lymphatic filariasis topic. [NCVBDC MMDP 2023]`,`**MRSA** makes up roughly **40% of S. aureus isolates** in Indian tertiary-care surveillance, so purulent hospital-acquired infections need an anti-MRSA plan, while community non-purulent cellulitis still does not. [INSAR / ICMR AMRSN]`,`About **30% of patients admitted as 'cellulitis' have a mimic** (pseudocellulitis), most often venous stasis dermatitis, and over 90% of these receive unnecessary antibiotics. [Weng, JAMA Dermatol 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Non-purulent cellulitis and erysipelas** are caused mainly by **beta-haemolytic streptococci** - group A (S. pyogenes) and, increasingly, **groups C and G (S. dysgalactiae subsp. equisimilis)**; S. aureus is a minor contributor. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Purulent cellulitis, abscesses, furuncles and carbuncles** are caused mainly by **Staphylococcus aureus**, including community-acquired MRSA carrying the **Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL)** toxin, which causes recurrent boils and necrotising lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Local risk factors** with the strongest association are **lymphoedema (OR about 70)**, a **breach in the skin barrier** such as toe-web intertrigo, ulcer or wound (OR about 24), venous insufficiency and leg oedema. [Dupuy, BMJ 1999]`,`**Systemic risk factors** are **obesity, diabetes, previous cellulitis**, chronic kidney and liver disease, immunosuppression, and injecting drug use (abscesses at injection sites). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Saphenous vein harvest, axillary node clearance and pelvic node dissection or radiotherapy** leave a lymph-damaged limb prone to recurrent cellulitis years later (post-mastectomy arm, post-hysterectomy leg). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exposure clues change the organism**: freshwater injury - **Aeromonas**; seawater or raw seafood in cirrhosis - **Vibrio vulnificus**; cat or dog bite - **Pasteurella multocida**; fish or meat handling - **Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae** (erysipeloid); puncture through footwear - **Pseudomonas**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **severe immunosuppression or neutropenia**, cellulitis may be caused by Gram-negative bacilli, **Cryptococcus**, or moulds, and a biopsy with culture is needed rather than empirical beta-lactam alone. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Organisms enter through a **portal** - fissured toe webs, a scratch, ulcer, insect bite or surgical wound - often invisible by the time the patient presents; the toe web is the reservoir in recurrent leg cellulitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Streptococci spread through tissue planes using **hyaluronidase, streptokinase and DNases**, which is why streptococcal cellulitis spreads fast with little pus, while **S. aureus coagulase and fibrin** wall off infection into an **abscess**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Much of the redness and swelling in streptococcal cellulitis is **host inflammation to few organisms and toxins**, so tissue cultures are usually negative and inflammation may **worsen for 24-48 hours after a correct antibiotic** as bacteria lyse. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Lymphatic damage** impairs clearance of bacteria and immune cells; each episode further scars the lymphatics, explaining the **vicious cycle of lymphoedema and recurrent cellulitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **erysipelas**, the infection sits in the superficial dermis and lymphatics, so the oedema produces the **raised, sharp edge** and lymphatic streaking, and the **Milian ear sign** (ear involvement) because the ear has no deep dermis to support cellulitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abscess** pus is avascular, so antibiotics penetrate poorly and the acidic, protein-rich environment inactivates aminoglycosides - this is why **drainage, not antibiotics, cures an abscess**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Beta-lactams act well on streptococci (no penicillin resistance described in GAS); **clindamycin and macrolide resistance** in GAS is rising, so these are second choices, not first. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification and severity grading`,points:[`The IDSA 2014 classification first separates **purulent** (abscess, furuncle, carbuncle, purulent cellulitis with drainage or exudate) from **non-purulent** (cellulitis, erysipelas, necrotising infection) infection. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Each arm is graded **mild** (no systemic signs), **moderate** (systemic signs of infection) or **severe** (failed I&D plus oral antibiotics, SIRS with hypotension, immunocompromise, or signs of deeper infection). [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**SIRS criteria** used in grading: temperature over 38 C or under 36 C, heart rate over 90, respiratory rate over 24, or WBC over 12,000 or under 400/mm3. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`The **Eron (CREST) classification**: Class I no systemic toxicity or comorbidity (oral, OPD); Class II systemically unwell or comorbidity that may delay recovery; Class III significant systemic upset or limb-threatening infection; Class IV sepsis or life-threatening infection such as necrotising fasciitis. [CREST 2005]`,`Classes I and II are usually managed in OPD or with OPAT; Classes III and IV need **admission and IV therapy**, and Class IV needs **surgical review** for a necrotising process. [CREST 2005]`,`The **WSES/SIS-E 2022** SSTI classification separates **non-necrotising superficial**, **necrotising**, and **surgical-site** infections, emphasising early surgical source control for the last two. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Typical history: **unilateral** red, hot, painful, swollen leg evolving over **1-3 days**, often with **fever, chills and malaise** that may precede the skin change by hours (especially erysipelas). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about the **portal**: itching between toes, cracked heels, insect bite, trauma, recent surgery, injection or a previous ulcer. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`Ask about **previous episodes** at the same site, **chronic leg swelling**, varicose veins, diabetes, and previous node dissection or radiotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **exposures**: animal bite, freshwater or seawater, fish or meat handling, gardening (sporotrichosis is a slower mimic), injecting drugs, and travel. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pain out of proportion** to the visible skin change, rapid progression over hours, numbness, or a patient who looks toxic suggest **necrotising soft tissue infection** - see the Necrotising soft-tissue infection topic. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Bilateral 'cellulitis'** is almost never infection; it points to **venous stasis dermatitis**, lipodermatosclerosis, contact dermatitis or bilateral oedema. [Weng, JAMA Dermatol 2017]`,`A **painful, fluctuant, pointing** lump with a central punctum suggests an abscess or furuncle; repeated boils in a household suggest **PVL-positive S. aureus** colonisation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Cellulitis: **ill-defined erythema, warmth, oedema and tenderness**, sometimes with lymphangitic streaks and tender regional nodes; **peau d'orange** and bullae may appear. [Harrison 22e]`,`Erysipelas: **bright red, indurated, raised plaque with a sharp, palpable edge**; face (butterfly) and legs are the classic sites; the **Milian ear sign** separates it from deeper cellulitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mark the edge of erythema with a skin pen** and note date and time - this is the simplest objective measure of progression and response. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`**Always examine the toe webs** for maceration, scaling and fissures - treating tinea pedis is the single most neglected step in preventing recurrence. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`Look for **fluctuance** or use **point-of-care ultrasound**: a hypoechoic collection means abscess (drain it); **cobblestoning** of subcutaneous fat means oedema of cellulitis. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`,`Signs of **deeper infection**: woody induration beyond the erythema, **crepitus**, **bullae with haemorrhagic fluid**, skin necrosis, anaesthesia over the area, and **hypotension**. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Around the eye: **periorbital (preseptal) cellulitis** spares eye movement and vision; **orbital cellulitis** causes **proptosis, painful or restricted eye movement, chemosis and reduced acuity** and needs CT and IV antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - the mimics`,points:[`**Venous stasis dermatitis** is the commonest mimic: bilateral, itchy rather than painful, brown haemosiderin staining, varicose veins and no fever. [Weng, JAMA Dermatol 2017]`,`**Lipodermatosclerosis** causes a painful, indurated, inverted-champagne-bottle lower leg in chronic venous disease; it is chronic and afebrile. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Deep vein thrombosis** gives calf swelling and tenderness with less erythema; use the **Wells score and compression ultrasound** when in doubt (the two can coexist). [NICE NG158 2020]`,`**Acute gout** and septic arthritis give a hot, red, swollen joint with peri-articular erythema; the joint itself is maximally tender and aspiration decides. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contact dermatitis** is itchy with vesicles and a geometric edge matching the allergen (dressing, topical neomycin); **ruptured Baker cyst** gives calf swelling and a crescent bruise at the ankle. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other mimics: **erythema nodosum, Charcot foot** in diabetes (warm, red, minimal pain, normal CRP), **eosinophilic (Wells) cellulitis**, fixed drug eruption, radiation dermatitis, and **herpes zoster** before the vesicles appear. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **ALT-70 score** helps in lower limb cellulitis: **A**symmetry 3, **L**eucocytosis 10,000 or more 1, **T**achycardia 90 or more 1, age **70** or more 2; a score of **0-2 makes cellulitis unlikely** and **5 or more likely**. [Raff, J Am Acad Dermatol 2017]`,`**Dermatology consultation** in suspected cellulitis reduces misdiagnosis and unnecessary antibiotics; consider it when the picture is atypical or not responding. [Li, JAMA Dermatol 2018]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Mild uncomplicated cellulitis needs no investigation**; diagnosis is clinical. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Blood cultures** are positive in under 5% and are recommended only for **systemic toxicity, immunosuppression, malignancy on chemotherapy, neutropenia, animal bites and immersion injuries**. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Gram stain and culture of pus** from an abscess or purulent cellulitis is recommended (it guides MRSA cover), but treatment of a typical case need not wait for it. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Swabs of intact cellulitis skin and **needle aspirates** have low yield; **punch biopsy culture** is reserved for immunocompromised patients and unusual exposures. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`In moderate-severe disease check **CBC, CRP, creatinine, glucose and HbA1c** (new diabetes is often found), and **lactate** if septic. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`,`**Point-of-care ultrasound** detects occult abscesses that change management; **X-ray** for gas or foreign body; **MRI** if osteomyelitis or a deep collection is suspected. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`,`Do not use **ASO titre** for acute management; it rises late and only supports a streptococcal cause retrospectively. [Harrison 22e]`,`If **necrotising infection** is suspected, imaging must not delay **surgical exploration** - see the Necrotising soft-tissue infection topic. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - non-purulent cellulitis and erysipelas`,points:[`**Elevate the limb** above heart level, mark the edge, encourage fluids, give paracetamol for pain, and treat the portal (**topical terbinafine 1% or clotrimazole 1%** for tinea pedis). [NICE NG141 2019]`,`First line oral: **cefalexin 500 mg four times daily** or **cloxacillin/flucloxacillin 500 mg-1 g four times daily**; amoxicillin or phenoxymethylpenicillin covers erysipelas, which is almost always streptococcal. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`**Amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily** is used for facial cellulitis (near the eye or nose) and bites, where mixed flora is likely. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`**Penicillin allergy (non-severe)**: cefalexin is usually safe; for **severe (anaphylaxis) allergy** use **clindamycin 300-450 mg three to four times daily**, or doxycycline 100 mg twice daily with some loss of streptococcal reliability - see the Antibiotic allergy topic. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`**Duration: 5 days**, extended only if the infection has **not improved** by then - 5 days is as effective as 10 days in uncomplicated cellulitis. [IDSA SSTI 2014; Hepburn 2004]`,`**MRSA cover is not needed** for non-purulent cellulitis unless there is penetrating trauma, injecting drug use, known MRSA colonisation, purulent drainage or SIRS. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Warn that redness may **spread a little in the first 24-48 hours**; review at **48-72 hours**, and switch or admit only if fever persists, the patient worsens, or the marked edge advances after 48 hours. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`Short **systemic steroids (prednisolone 40 mg for 7 days)** speed resolution in non-diabetic adults but are optional and not routine practice in India. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`]},{heading:`Management of abscess and purulent infection`,points:[`**Incision and drainage (I&D) is the treatment** of a skin abscess, furuncle or carbuncle; small furuncles respond to warm compresses. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Adjunct antibiotics** after drainage improve cure and reduce recurrence for abscesses, especially **2 cm or more**: in two RCTs, **co-trimoxazole** or **clindamycin** raised cure by about 7-10 percentage points over placebo. [Talan, NEJM 2016; Daum, NEJM 2017]`,`Always give antibiotics after I&D for **SIRS, extensive cellulitis, multiple sites, rapid progression, extremes of age, immunosuppression, face, hand or genital site**, or a failed previous drainage. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Oral anti-MRSA choices: **co-trimoxazole 1-2 DS tablets (160/800 mg) twice daily**, **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily**, or **clindamycin 300-450 mg three times daily** (check local inducible resistance); for MSSA, cefalexin or cloxacillin. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Duration 5-7 days** for purulent infection after drainage, guided by response. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Packing** of small abscesses after drainage adds pain without benefit; cover with a dry dressing. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`,`**Recurrent boils**: drain and culture each lesion; for household clusters, **decolonise** with **mupirocin 2% nasal ointment twice daily for 5 days** plus **chlorhexidine body washes** daily, and launder towels and bedding. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`In recurrent boils, **screen for diabetes and HIV**; recurrent infection in early childhood raises the question of neutrophil disorders such as chronic granulomatous disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for Eron Class III-IV, SIRS or sepsis, hypotension, altered sensorium, rapid spread, suspected necrotising infection, **orbital cellulitis**, severe immunosuppression, inability to take oral drugs, or failure of oral therapy at 48-72 hours. [CREST 2005]`,`IV non-purulent: **cefazolin 1-2 g IV 8-hourly**, **cloxacillin 1-2 g IV 6-hourly** or **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily**; penicillin allergy: **clindamycin 600 mg IV 8-hourly**. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`IV MRSA cover (purulent severe, healthcare-associated, known MRSA, injection drug use): **vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly** (AUC 400-600 target), **linezolid 600 mg twice daily** IV or oral, or **teicoplanin**; daptomycin is a reserve drug. [IDSA SSTI 2014; ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Severe infection with systemic toxicity or suspicion of necrotising infection**: urgent surgical review plus **vancomycin or linezolid plus piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly** (or a carbapenem), with **clindamycin** for toxin suppression. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Switch IV to oral** once afebrile for 24-48 hours, erythema regressing and able to swallow; total course usually **5-10 days** for inpatients depending on response. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`,`Monitor **temperature, pulse, BP, the marked edge, pain and CRP** daily; check **vancomycin trough or AUC** and creatinine every 2-3 days. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`Give **DVT prophylaxis** to immobile inpatients with leg cellulitis, and manage glucose tightly in diabetes. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes**: a foot cellulitis is a **diabetic foot infection** with different organisms, grading (IWGDF/IDSA) and need for probe-to-bone and X-ray - see the Infections that matter in diabetes topic. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: cefalexin, cloxacillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate and clindamycin are safe; **avoid doxycycline**, and avoid co-trimoxazole in the first trimester and near term. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`**Children**: cefalexin **25-50 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses**; periorbital cellulitis in a child under 1 year or with any orbital sign needs admission. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`**CKD**: reduce cefalexin and cefazolin intervals when eGFR under 30; avoid co-trimoxazole in severe CKD (hyperkalaemia, rising creatinine); linezolid needs no renal adjustment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly and immobile**: cellulitis is often confused with stasis dermatitis and pressure injury; delirium may be the presenting feature - see the Infections in older adults topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Filarial lymphoedema**: acute attacks (ADLA) are treated with **amoxicillin or penicillin**, rest and elevation; daily **limb hygiene** and benzathine penicillin prophylaxis cut attack frequency. [NCVBDC MMDP 2023]`,`**Animal bites**: amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily for 3-5 days (prophylaxis) or 7-10 days (infection), plus rabies and tetanus assessment - see the Animal bite topic. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Most uncomplicated cellulitis settles in **7-10 days**; residual swelling and skin desquamation can last weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`Local complications: **abscess, necrotising fasciitis, bullae with skin necrosis, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis and thrombophlebitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Systemic complications: **bacteraemia, sepsis, streptococcal toxic shock**, infective endocarditis (S. aureus) and **post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis** after skin streptococcal infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Facial cellulitis can spread to the **cavernous sinus** (dangerous triangle of the face); orbital cellulitis can cause vision loss, subperiosteal abscess and meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrence** causes progressive lymphoedema (**elephantiasis nostras verrucosa**), which in turn invites more cellulitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mortality of community cellulitis is low (under 1%) but rises steeply with sepsis, necrotising infection, age and comorbidity. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`]},{heading:`Recurrent cellulitis - prevention and prophylaxis`,points:[`First treat the **predisposing factors**: tinea pedis and toe-web maceration, **lymphoedema (compression)**, venous eczema, obesity, and poor foot hygiene. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`**Compression therapy** in chronic leg oedema reduced recurrence from **40% to 15%** over follow-up in an RCT; it is the non-drug intervention with the best evidence. [Webb, NEJM 2020]`,`Consider **antibiotic prophylaxis** for **3-4 or more episodes a year** despite treating predisposing factors (IDSA), or after **2 or more episodes** of leg cellulitis (PATCH trial population). [IDSA SSTI 2014; PATCH I 2013]`,`**Phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V) 250 mg twice daily for 12 months** roughly **halved recurrences** (hazard ratio 0.55), but protection faded once the drug was stopped. [PATCH I, NEJM 2013]`,`Alternatives: **benzathine penicillin 1.2 million units IM every 2-4 weeks** (useful where adherence or oral penicillin V availability is a problem in India), or **erythromycin 250 mg twice daily** in penicillin allergy. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Review prophylaxis every **6-12 months**; patients with BMI over 33 or lymphoedema respond less well and need aggressive treatment of the oedema. [PATCH I, NEJM 2013]`,`Teach **daily foot care**: wash and dry between toes, emollient for cracked heels, footwear, prompt treatment of cuts - the core of the NCVBDC **MMDP** home-care package for lymphoedema. [NCVBDC MMDP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO AWaRe Antibiotic Book (2022)** lists **cefalexin or cloxacillin** (Access antibiotics) as first choice for mild cellulitis, amoxicillin-clavulanate as an alternative, co-trimoxazole or doxycycline if MRSA is likely, with a **5-day course**. [WHO AWaRe 2022]`,`**WSES/SIS-E 2022** guideline on SSTIs reinforces early source control, POCUS for occult abscess, and short courses with early IV-to-oral switch. [WSES/SIS-E 2022]`,`**ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes (2019, revised 2022)** recommend cefazolin or cloxacillin for cellulitis and reserve anti-MRSA drugs for purulent or healthcare-associated infection. [ICMR 2022]`,`**NICE NG141** (cellulitis and erysipelas antimicrobial prescribing) recommends a **5-7 day** course with review, and flucloxacillin first line; the IDSA 2014 SSTI guideline (5 days) remains the US reference while an update is awaited. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`**IWGDF/IDSA 2023** diabetic foot infection guideline separates diabetic foot cellulitis from ordinary cellulitis and recommends **1-2 weeks** for soft-tissue infection. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Antimicrobial stewardship focus (ICMR AMSP, NAP-AMR 2.0 2025 draft): **avoid double-cover** (for example a cephalosporin plus clindamycin plus metronidazole) for simple cellulitis, a common Indian prescribing error. [ICMR AMSP 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Differentiate cellulitis and erysipelas'** - depth (deep dermis plus fat versus upper dermis plus lymphatics), edge (ill-defined versus raised sharp), organism (streptococci plus S. aureus versus mostly streptococci), Milian ear sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'What is the first-line antibiotic for non-purulent cellulitis?' - a **beta-lactam active against streptococci** (cefalexin, cloxacillin, penicillin), **not ciprofloxacin or metronidazole**. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Viva: 'How long?' - **5 days**, extend if not improved; the edge may advance slightly in the first 24-48 hours. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Common mistake: **treating bilateral red legs as cellulitis** - bilateral cellulitis is rare; think stasis dermatitis. [Weng, JAMA Dermatol 2017]`,`Common mistake: giving antibiotics **without draining an abscess** - drainage is the cure. [IDSA SSTI 2014]`,`Common mistake: **forgetting the toe webs** in recurrent leg cellulitis. [NICE NG141 2019]`,`Short note favourite: **Eron classification**, **ALT-70 score**, and the **PATCH trial** on penicillin prophylaxis. [CREST 2005; PATCH I 2013]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antibiotics for cellulitis, erysipelas and abscess`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cefalexin`,`500 mg oral 6-hourly; child 25-50 mg/kg/day in 4 doses`,`5 days, extend if not improved`,`Diarrhoea, rash; usually safe in non-severe penicillin allergy`],[`Cloxacillin / flucloxacillin`,`500 mg-1 g oral 6-hourly on an empty stomach; 1-2 g IV 6-hourly`,`5 days oral; IV until improving`,`Cholestatic jaundice (more in elderly), phlebitis; avoid in penicillin anaphylaxis`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 8-hourly; 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days (facial, bite-related)`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic hepatitis; penicillin allergy`],[`Cefazolin`,`1-2 g IV 8-hourly`,`Until switch to oral (usually 2-4 days)`,`Rash; dose reduction if eGFR under 35`],[`Clindamycin`,`300-450 mg oral 6-8-hourly; 600 mg IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`C. difficile colitis; rising GAS and MRSA resistance (D-test)`],[`Co-trimoxazole`,`1-2 DS tablets (160/800 mg) oral 12-hourly`,`5-7 days after I&D`,`Rash and SJS, hyperkalaemia, raised creatinine; avoid first trimester and near term; unreliable for streptococci alone`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral 12-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Pill oesophagitis, photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Vancomycin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly; loading 20-35 mg/kg in severe`,`AUC 400-600 guided`,`Nephrotoxicity, infusion reaction (vancomycin flushing); monitor levels`],[`Linezolid`,`600 mg oral or IV 12-hourly`,`Up to 10-14 days`,`Myelosuppression after 2 weeks, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, lactic acidosis`],[`Penicillin V (prophylaxis)`,`250 mg oral 12-hourly`,`6-12 months, review`,`Rash; protection lost on stopping`],[`Benzathine penicillin (prophylaxis)`,`1.2 million units deep IM every 2-4 weeks`,`6-12 months, review`,`Painful injection, rare anaphylaxis - give where resuscitation is available`]]},{heading:`IDSA 2014 severity grid for skin and soft-tissue infection`,columns:[`Severity`,`Purulent (abscess, furuncle, carbuncle)`,`Non-purulent (cellulitis, erysipelas)`],rows:[[`Mild - no systemic signs`,`Incision and drainage alone (antibiotics if 2 cm or more or risk factors)`,`Oral beta-lactam: cefalexin, cloxacillin or penicillin V, 5 days`],[`Moderate - systemic signs`,`I&D plus oral co-trimoxazole or doxycycline; culture pus`,`IV cefazolin, ceftriaxone or penicillin; clindamycin if allergic`],[`Severe - failed therapy, SIRS with hypotension, immunocompromise or deeper infection`,`I&D plus IV vancomycin, linezolid or teicoplanin; de-escalate to MSSA drug if sensitive`,`Emergency surgical inspection to exclude necrotising infection; vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam`]]},{heading:`Cellulitis versus erysipelas versus abscess`,columns:[`Feature`,`Cellulitis`,`Erysipelas`,`Abscess`],rows:[[`Tissue level`,`Deep dermis and subcutaneous fat`,`Upper dermis and superficial lymphatics`,`Localised pus collection in dermis or subcutis`],[`Edge`,`Ill-defined, flat`,`Raised, sharply demarcated, shiny`,`Fluctuant swelling with surrounding erythema`],[`Usual organism`,`Beta-haemolytic streptococci, less often S. aureus`,`Streptococci (group A, C, G)`,`S. aureus including MRSA`],[`Typical site`,`Lower leg`,`Face (butterfly), leg; ear involved (Milian sign)`,`Buttock, axilla, limbs, injection sites`],[`Onset`,`Over 1-3 days`,`Abrupt, fever often precedes rash`,`Days, pointing then discharge`],[`Key treatment`,`Oral beta-lactam 5 days`,`Penicillin or amoxicillin 5 days`,`Incision and drainage, plus antibiotic if large or complicated`]]},{heading:`Mimics of lower-limb cellulitis (pseudocellulitis)`,columns:[`Mimic`,`Clue that discriminates`,`Confirm with`],rows:[[`Venous stasis dermatitis`,`Bilateral, itchy, brown staining, varicose veins, afebrile`,`Clinical; venous Doppler`],[`Lipodermatosclerosis`,`Chronic woody induration, inverted champagne bottle leg`,`Clinical`],[`Deep vein thrombosis`,`Calf swelling and tenderness, less erythema, risk factors`,`Wells score, D-dimer, compression ultrasound`],[`Gout or septic arthritis`,`Joint maximally tender, restricted movement`,`Joint aspiration, crystals and culture`],[`Contact dermatitis`,`Itch, vesicles, geometric edge, no fever`,`History of topical agent or dressing`],[`Ruptured Baker cyst`,`Sudden calf pain, crescent bruise below malleolus`,`Ultrasound popliteal fossa`],[`Charcot foot`,`Warm red swollen foot, little pain, neuropathy, normal CRP`,`X-ray, MRI; temperature gradient over 2 C`],[`Erythema nodosum`,`Multiple tender nodules on shins, bilateral`,`Look for TB, sarcoid, streptococcal or drug cause`]]},{heading:`Eron (CREST) classification of cellulitis`,columns:[`Class`,`Definition`,`Setting and route`],rows:[[`I`,`No systemic toxicity and no uncontrolled comorbidity`,`OPD, oral antibiotics`],[`II`,`Systemically unwell, or well with comorbidity (PVD, obesity, lymphoedema) that may delay recovery`,`Oral or OPAT/IV short course`],[`III`,`Significant systemic upset (confusion, tachycardia, hypotension) or limb-threatening infection from vascular compromise`,`Admit, IV antibiotics`],[`IV`,`Sepsis syndrome or life-threatening infection such as necrotising fasciitis`,`Admit, IV antibiotics, urgent surgery, ICU`]]}],redFlags:[`**Pain out of proportion** to the skin findings, crepitus, haemorrhagic bullae, skin necrosis or anaesthesia - suspect necrotising fasciitis`,`**Hypotension, confusion or lactate over 2 mmol/L** - sepsis; start IV antibiotics within 1 hour`,`**Orbital signs**: proptosis, painful or restricted eye movement, reduced vision`,`**Rapid spread** of the marked edge over hours, or progression after 48 hours of correct antibiotics`,`Cellulitis in **neutropenia, transplant or advanced HIV**`,`**Diabetic foot** with cellulitis beyond 2 cm, deep ulcer or probe-to-bone positive`,`**Facial cellulitis** in the dangerous triangle with headache or cranial nerve signs (cavernous sinus thrombosis)`],pearls:[`**Pus means Staph, no pus means Strep** - choose the drug by that single split.`,`**Five days is enough** for uncomplicated cellulitis; extend only if not improved.`,`**Bilateral red legs are rarely cellulitis** - think venous stasis first.`,`**Drain the abscess** - I&D cures; antibiotics are an adjunct for 2 cm or more or risk factors.`,`**Milian ear sign** = erysipelas, because the ear has no deep dermis for cellulitis.`,`**Mark the edge** with a pen and date it; redness may advance slightly in the first 24-48 hours.`,`**Treat the toe webs** - tinea pedis is the hidden portal in recurrent leg cellulitis.`,`**ALT-70**: Asymmetry 3, Leucocytosis 1, Tachycardia 1, age 70 two points; 0-2 cellulitis unlikely.`,`**PATCH**: penicillin V 250 mg BD for 12 months halves recurrences, but the effect stops when the drug stops.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old farmer has a 2-day history of a red, hot, tender, ill-defined swelling of the right shin with temperature 38.2 C, no pus, no systemic toxicity and macerated toe webs. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?`,options:[`Oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days`,`Oral cefalexin 500 mg four times daily for 5 days with elevation and topical antifungal for the toe webs`,`Oral co-trimoxazole DS twice daily plus metronidazole for 7 days`,`IV vancomycin 15 mg/kg 12-hourly until afebrile`],answer:1,explanation:`Mild non-purulent cellulitis is streptococcal, so an oral beta-lactam such as cefalexin (or cloxacillin) for 5 days is first line, with elevation and treatment of the tinea pedis portal. Ciprofloxacin has poor streptococcal activity, co-trimoxazole alone is unreliable for streptococci and metronidazole adds nothing, and IV vancomycin is reserved for severe or MRSA-suspected infection.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-q2`,stem:`Which feature most reliably distinguishes erysipelas from cellulitis at the bedside?`,options:[`Presence of fever and chills`,`Involvement of the lower limb`,`A raised, sharply demarcated edge with involvement of the ear`,`Regional lymphadenopathy`],answer:2,explanation:`Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and lymphatics, so it produces a raised, sharply demarcated, shiny plaque and can involve the ear (Milian ear sign), which has no deep dermis for cellulitis. Fever, leg involvement and regional nodes occur in both conditions and do not discriminate.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old man has a 4 cm fluctuant abscess on the thigh with 2 cm of surrounding erythema; he is afebrile and otherwise well. After incision and drainage, what does current evidence support?`,options:[`Adding oral co-trimoxazole or clindamycin for 5-7 days improves cure and reduces recurrence`,`Packing the cavity daily for 7 days is essential for healing`,`IV ceftriaxone for 5 days is needed because the abscess exceeds 2 cm`,`No culture should be sent because results never change management`],answer:0,explanation:`Two large RCTs (Talan 2016, Daum 2017) showed that co-trimoxazole or clindamycin after drainage improves cure of uncomplicated abscesses, particularly those 2 cm or more, and IDSA recommends pus culture. Packing adds pain without benefit, and IV ceftriaxone does not cover MRSA and is unnecessary in a well patient.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-q4`,stem:`A 62-year-old woman has had four episodes of right leg cellulitis in 18 months. She has chronic leg oedema and toe-web tinea. Besides treating the tinea, which intervention has RCT evidence for reducing recurrence?`,options:[`Monthly oral ciprofloxacin for one week`,`Long-term topical mupirocin to the leg`,`Oral prednisolone 10 mg daily for 6 months`,`Compression therapy for the oedema plus penicillin V 250 mg twice daily for 12 months`],answer:3,explanation:`Compression therapy reduced recurrence from 40% to 15% in an RCT (Webb 2020) and penicillin V 250 mg twice daily for 12 months roughly halved recurrences in PATCH I. Pulsed ciprofloxacin and topical mupirocin on the leg have no evidence and poor streptococcal logic, and long-term steroids increase infection risk.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-q5`,stem:`In the ALT-70 score for suspected lower-limb cellulitis, which combination of findings gives the highest score?`,options:[`Leucocytosis and tachycardia in a 50-year-old with bilateral leg redness`,`Unilateral involvement in a 72-year-old with heart rate 96`,`Bilateral involvement in a 75-year-old with normal WBC`,`Unilateral involvement in a 40-year-old with normal pulse and WBC`],answer:1,explanation:`ALT-70 gives 3 points for asymmetry, 2 for age 70 or more, and 1 each for leucocytosis and tachycardia of 90 or more, so unilateral disease at 72 with heart rate 96 scores 6 (cellulitis likely). Bilateral redness with leucocytosis and tachycardia at 50 scores 2, the bilateral 75-year-old scores 2, and the unilateral 40-year-old scores only 3.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define cellulitis. Discuss its aetiology, clinical features, differential diagnosis and management, including the prevention of recurrent cellulitis.`,openingLines:[`Cellulitis is an acute, non-necrotising spreading bacterial infection of the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue, caused mainly by beta-haemolytic streptococci when non-purulent and by Staphylococcus aureus when purulent (IDSA 2014).`,`It is treated by the purulent versus non-purulent split, with a 5-day beta-lactam course for non-purulent disease and drainage for abscess, after excluding mimics that account for about a third of referrals.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and aetiology`,points:[`Cellulitis versus erysipelas versus abscess (depth, edge, organism).`,`Streptococci (groups A, C, G) in non-purulent; S. aureus including MRSA in purulent; exposure-specific organisms (Pasteurella, Vibrio, Aeromonas, Erysipelothrix).`,`Risk factors: lymphoedema, toe-web intertrigo, venous disease, obesity, diabetes, filarial lymphoedema in India.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and classification`,points:[`Unilateral warm, red, tender, ill-defined swelling over 1-3 days with fever.`,`IDSA mild-moderate-severe grid and Eron Class I-IV.`,`Red flags for necrotising infection and orbital cellulitis.`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`Venous stasis dermatitis, lipodermatosclerosis, DVT, gout, contact dermatitis, Baker cyst, Charcot foot.`,`ALT-70 score; bilateral redness is rarely cellulitis.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`None in mild disease; blood cultures only in toxic, immunocompromised, bite or immersion cases.`,`Pus culture in purulent infection; POCUS for occult abscess; CBC, CRP, glucose, creatinine in moderate-severe.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Elevation, mark the edge, treat portal.`,`Mild: cefalexin or cloxacillin 500 mg QID for 5 days; clindamycin if allergic.`,`Moderate: IV cefazolin or ceftriaxone; severe: surgical review plus vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam.`,`Abscess: I&D plus co-trimoxazole, doxycycline or clindamycin for 2 cm or more or risk factors.`]},{heading:`Recurrence and prevention`,points:[`Treat tinea pedis, compression for oedema (40% to 15%), weight loss, foot care.`,`Penicillin V 250 mg BD for 12 months (PATCH I, HR 0.55) or benzathine penicillin 1.2 MU every 2-4 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`IDSA purulent versus non-purulent severity grid`,`Table: cellulitis versus erysipelas versus abscess`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Differential diagnosis and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention of recurrence`,marks:1}],keywords:[`cellulitis`,`erysipelas`,`IDSA 2014`,`Eron`,`ALT-70`,`PATCH`,`cefalexin`,`5 days`]},{id:`infectious-fever-cellulitis-erysipelas-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between cellulitis, erysipelas and skin abscess, and give the antibiotic choice for each.`,openingLines:[`Cellulitis, erysipelas and abscess are graded depths of the same skin and soft tissue infection spectrum, separated by tissue level, edge and the presence of pus.`,`The presence of pus predicts S. aureus and the need for drainage; its absence predicts streptococci and a beta-lactam.`],answer:[{heading:`Comparison`,points:[`Tissue level: deep dermis and fat versus upper dermis and lymphatics versus a walled-off pus collection.`,`Edge: ill-defined versus raised and sharp versus fluctuant.`,`Organism: streptococci more than S. aureus versus streptococci versus S. aureus.`,`Signs: Milian ear sign in erysipelas; fluctuance or POCUS collection in abscess.`]},{heading:`Antibiotic choice`,points:[`Cellulitis: cefalexin or cloxacillin 500 mg QID for 5 days.`,`Erysipelas: penicillin V or amoxicillin 500 mg TDS-QID for 5 days.`,`Abscess: incision and drainage; co-trimoxazole DS BD, doxycycline or clindamycin for 5-7 days if 2 cm or more, systemic signs or risk factors.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Comparison table`,marks:3},{part:`Antibiotic choice`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Milian ear sign`,`purulent`,`non-purulent`,`incision and drainage`,`co-trimoxazole`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections of the skin, muscles and soft tissues`,`Stevens DL et al. Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Skin and Soft Tissue Infections: 2014 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America. Clin Infect Dis 2014`,`Sartelli M et al. WSES/GAIS/WSIS/SIS-E/AAST global clinical pathways for patients with skin and soft tissue infections, World J Emerg Surg 2022`,`NICE guideline NG141: Cellulitis and erysipelas - antimicrobial prescribing, 2019`,`WHO AWaRe (Access, Watch, Reserve) Antibiotic Book, 2022`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2019 (revised 2022)`,`Thomas KS et al. Penicillin to prevent recurrent leg cellulitis (PATCH I). N Engl J Med 2013; Webb E et al. Compression therapy to prevent recurrent cellulitis of the leg. N Engl J Med 2020`,`NCVBDC Morbidity Management and Disability Prevention (MMDP) guidelines for lymphatic filariasis, 2023`]});var D=[];D.push({id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd`,title:`Fever after chemotherapy in primary care`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Febrile neutropenia (FN)** is a single oral temperature of **38.3 C or more, or 38.0 C or more sustained for 1 hour**, in a patient whose **absolute neutrophil count (ANC) is under 500/microL or expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours**; any fever **7-14 days after chemotherapy** is treated as FN until proved otherwise - the first dose of a **broad-spectrum anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam within 60 minutes** saves lives, and only **MASCC-low-risk (score 21 or more)** patients are eligible for **oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate** at home.`,keywords:[`febrile neutropenia`,`neutropenic fever`,`neutropenic sepsis`,`FN`,`ANC`,`chemotherapy nadir`,`MASCC score`,`CISNE score`,`door-to-needle time`,`piperacillin-tazobactam`,`cefepime`,`meropenem`,`ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`oral step-down`,`G-CSF`,`filgrastim`,`pegfilgrastim`,`levofloxacin prophylaxis`,`neutropenic enterocolitis`,`typhlitis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Febrile neutropenia** is an oral temperature of **38.3 C or more once, or 38.0 C or more sustained over 1 hour**, with an **ANC under 500/microL** or under 1000/microL expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours. [IDSA 2010; ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**Neutropenia grades**: mild ANC 1000-1500, moderate 500-1000, **severe under 500**, and **profound under 100/microL** - infection risk rises steeply below 500 and with each day the count stays low. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NICE** uses a simpler trigger, **neutropenic sepsis**: ANC 500/microL or lower with temperature over 38 C **or any sign of sepsis** - because a neutropenic patient may be septic with no fever at all. [NICE CG151]`,`FN complicates about **10-50% of solid-tumour regimens and over 80% of haematological malignancy regimens** during at least one cycle. [IDSA 2010]`,`Mortality of adult FN is about **5% in solid tumours and up to 11% in haematological cancers**; it exceeds **20-50% once septic shock or Gram-negative bacteraemia** is present. [ASCO/IDSA 2018; Harrison 22e]`,`**India** has an estimated **14.6 lakh new cancers a year** (2022), most treated on day-care chemotherapy and living far from the cancer centre - so the first contact for fever is often the family physician or district hospital. [ICMR-NCRP 2022]`,`Indian series show **Gram-negative organisms in about 60-70% of bacteraemias** in FN (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter) and high **ESBL and carbapenem resistance**, unlike the Gram-positive shift seen in Western centres. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Timing: the **neutrophil nadir** usually falls **7-14 days after a chemotherapy cycle** (day 10-14 for most solid-tumour regimens), with recovery by day 21-28. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High-myelotoxicity regimens** (FN risk 20% or more) include **docetaxel-based (TAC, TC), dose-dense AC-T, BEACOPP, induction for acute leukaemia** and autologous transplant conditioning. [NCCN Myeloid Growth Factors 2024]`,`Patient risk factors: **age 65 or older**, advanced disease, prior FN, marrow infiltration, poor performance status, **open wounds or mucositis**, renal or liver dysfunction, diabetes and malnutrition. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Organisms - bacteria first**: Gram-negative bacilli (**E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas aeruginosa**) and Gram-positive cocci (**coagulase-negative staphylococci, S. aureus, viridans streptococci**) from gut and skin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fungi come later**: Candida after 7 days and **Aspergillus or Mucorales after 10-14 days** of profound neutropenia, especially on broad-spectrum antibiotics or steroids. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Viruses**: HSV reactivation causing mucositis, VZV, respiratory viruses (influenza, RSV, COVID-19) and, after transplant, CMV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Device and barrier breaches**: central lines, PICC and chemoport infections, mucositis (viridans streptococci with ARDS after cytarabine) and perianal fissures. [IDSA 2010]`,`Only about **20-30% of FN episodes have a documented infection**; most remain clinically unexplained fever, but empirical treatment is still mandatory. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why neutropenic fever is different`,points:[`Neutrophils make **pus, erythema and consolidation**; without them a pneumonia may show **no sputum and a clear early X-ray**, and a UTI may show **no pyuria** - fever may be the only sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`Cytotoxic drugs kill rapidly dividing **gut and oral mucosa**, opening a portal for gut flora to translocate into blood - explaining the dominance of **enteric Gram-negative bacteraemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gram-negative bacteraemia, especially **Pseudomonas**, can progress to septic shock **within hours**, which is why the guideline target is antibiotics **within 60 minutes of arrival**. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**Steroids, paracetamol and NSAIDs** blunt fever; an afebrile but unwell patient with hypotension, confusion or rigors after chemotherapy is treated as **neutropenic sepsis**. [NICE CG151]`,`Duration matters: neutropenia **over 7 days or ANC under 100** removes the patient from the low-risk group and opens the window for invasive moulds. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Anti-pseudomonal beta-lactams** are chosen because Pseudomonas carries the highest early mortality; vancomycin is not routine because Gram-positive bacteraemia is slower and less lethal in the first 48 hours. [IDSA 2010]`]},{heading:`Risk stratification - MASCC and CISNE`,points:[`**MASCC score** (Multinational Association for Supportive Care in Cancer) totals 26; a score of **21 or more is low risk** with serious complications under 5%. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`MASCC items: **burden of illness none or mild 5 (moderate 3)**, no hypotension (SBP 90 or more) 5, no COPD 4, solid tumour or no prior fungal infection 4, no dehydration needing IV fluids 3, **outpatient onset 3**, age under 60 years 2. [MASCC 2000]`,`**CISNE score** (Clinical Index of Stable Febrile Neutropenia) is for **solid-tumour patients who look stable**: ECOG 2 or more 2, stress hyperglycaemia 2, COPD 1, chronic cardiovascular disease 1, mucositis grade 2 or more 1, monocytes under 200/microL 1. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`CISNE **0 = low, 1-2 = intermediate, 3 or more = high risk**; ASCO/IDSA 2018 advises CISNE for apparently stable solid-tumour patients, because MASCC overcalls low risk in that group. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**Clinical high-risk features override any score**: expected neutropenia over 7 days, ANC under 100, haemodynamic instability, new pneumonia, abdominal pain, neurological change, catheter infection, **hepatic or renal impairment**, uncontrolled cancer or acute leukaemia. [IDSA 2010]`,`Low risk also needs **social criteria**: lives within about 1 hour of a hospital, a caregiver at home, a telephone, can take oral drugs, and no fluoroquinolone prophylaxis. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`]},{heading:`History - the phone call and the OPD visit`,points:[`Ask **date and name of the last chemotherapy** - fever between **day 5 and day 21** of a cycle is FN until the ANC proves otherwise. [ESMO 2016]`,`Ask the **highest temperature and how it was measured**; oral or axillary only - **never rectal** in neutropenia because of mucosal tear and bacteraemia. [NICE CG151]`,`Ask about **rigors after flushing a central line** - classic for catheter-related bloodstream infection. [IDSA 2010]`,`Ask **focus symptoms**: sore mouth or painful swallowing (mucositis, candida, HSV), cough, dysuria, diarrhoea (C. difficile, neutropenic enterocolitis), **right lower abdominal pain (typhlitis)**, perianal pain, skin lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **G-CSF, antibiotic or antifungal prophylaxis, steroids** and recent hospital stay - prophylaxis with a fluoroquinolone **rules out the oral fluoroquinolone outpatient regimen**. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`Ask about **recent antibiotics and known colonisation** (ESBL, CRE, MRSA from previous cultures) - this decides the empirical drug. [ICMR 2019]`,`Telephone triage: any fever after chemotherapy, or feeling unwell with shivers even without fever, means **come to the hospital now** - not an OPD appointment tomorrow. [UKONS 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination - look hard because signs are muted`,points:[`Record **temperature, pulse, BP, respiratory rate, SpO2, capillary refill and mental state**; calculate **NEWS2** - a score of 5 or more, or any single 3, demands urgent senior review. [RCP NEWS2]`,`Inspect the **mouth** for mucositis, candida and HSV ulcers, and the **pharynx and sinuses** for tenderness (moulds in prolonged neutropenia). [IDSA 2010]`,`Examine the **central line or port site and tunnel**: erythema, tenderness or pus over 2 cm along the tunnel means tunnel infection and line removal. [IDSA 2009]`,`Examine the **skin** for cellulitis, ecthyma gangrenosum (a necrotic ulcer from **Pseudomonas**) and nodules (Candida, Fusarium). [Harrison 22e]`,`Palpate the abdomen for **right iliac fossa tenderness** (neutropenic enterocolitis) and inspect - do not digitally examine - the **perianal area** for abscess. [IDSA 2010]`,`Auscultate the chest - crackles may be few, so a **normal examination does not exclude pneumonia**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Non-neutropenic fever after chemotherapy** (fever in week 1 before the nadir) - still investigate, but outpatient treatment is easier once the ANC is known to be adequate. [ESMO 2016]`,`**Drug fever and infusion reactions** - bleomycin, cytarabine, gemcitabine, rituximab and G-CSF cause fever within hours of dosing; a diagnosis of exclusion in neutropenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tumour fever** - lymphoma, renal cell carcinoma and liver metastases; the **naproxen test** helps only after infection is excluded and the count has recovered. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transfusion reaction** - fever within 4 hours of blood or platelets; stop the transfusion and send a reaction work-up. [NACO/NBTC 2022]`,`**Venous thromboembolism** - cancer, chemotherapy and central lines raise DVT and PE risk, which can cause low-grade fever (see the Postoperative and post-procedural fever topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical fevers** - dengue, malaria and scrub typhus still occur in chemotherapy patients in India; send smear or RDT and NS1 as part of the panel in season. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Send **two sets of blood cultures before the first dose** - one peripheral and one from each lumen of any central line - but **never delay antibiotics** for cultures or for the ANC result. [IDSA 2010]`,`A **differential time to positivity** of the line culture **2 hours or more earlier** than the peripheral culture indicates catheter-related bloodstream infection. [IDSA 2009]`,`**CBC with differential** (ANC, monocytes), **creatinine, electrolytes, liver function, lactate**, urine routine and culture, and site cultures (sputum, stool for C. difficile if diarrhoea). [ESMO 2016]`,`**Chest X-ray** if respiratory symptoms; **CT chest** if fever persists beyond 3-4 days in high-risk patients, looking for nodules or the **halo sign** of invasive aspergillosis. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Serum galactomannan** (index 0.5 or more on serum) and **beta-D-glucan** support invasive fungal infection in prolonged neutropenia. [ECIL-6 2017]`,`**CRP and procalcitonin** may be useful trends, but a normal value **does not exclude infection** and must never withhold antibiotics in FN. [ESMO 2016]`,`**Point-of-care ANC** is ideal; in a PHC without a counter, the decision to treat is made on the **clinical picture and the chemotherapy date**, not on the count. [NICE CG151]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**First rule**: give the **first antibiotic dose within 60 minutes** of arrival - in a PHC or district hospital, start it, then refer; do not refer first. [ASCO/IDSA 2018; NICE CG151]`,`If the cancer centre is over an hour away, give **ceftriaxone is not enough** - use an anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam if stocked (**piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV**, or **cefepime 2 g IV**); if only ceftriaxone is available, give it with **amikacin 15 mg/kg IV** and transfer. [ICMR 2019]`,`**Low-risk oral regimen** (MASCC 21 or more, or CISNE 0, after the first IV dose and 4 hours of observation): **ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily** (or 875/125 mg twice daily). [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**Penicillin allergy**: ciprofloxacin plus **clindamycin 300 mg four times daily**; **levofloxacin 750 mg once daily** or moxifloxacin 400 mg once daily monotherapy are accepted alternatives. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**Not eligible for the oral regimen** if already on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis, unable to swallow, vomiting, or without a caregiver and phone - these patients are admitted. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`Outpatient patients need **daily contact for 3 days**, a review in 24-48 hours, and immediate return for **persistent fever beyond 2-3 days, new symptoms or intolerance**. [ESMO 2016]`,`Use **paracetamol 650 mg 6-hourly** for comfort only after antibiotics are given; **avoid NSAIDs** because of thrombocytopenia and renal risk. [NICE CG151]`,`**Do not hold the next chemotherapy cycle yourself** - inform the oncologist, who decides on dose reduction or G-CSF for the next cycle. [NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`Admit **all high-risk patients** (MASCC under 21, CISNE 3 or more, clinical high-risk feature) for IV **anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam monotherapy**: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly, cefepime 2 g IV 8-hourly or meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly**. [IDSA 2010; ESMO 2016]`,`In Indian centres with high ESBL rates, **meropenem** is first choice for septic shock, known ESBL colonisation or hospital-acquired FN; **cefoperazone-sulbactam** is used in some ICMR-network centres. [ICMR 2019]`,`**Add vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12 hourly** only for haemodynamic instability, suspected catheter infection, skin or soft-tissue infection, pneumonia, **MRSA colonisation** or severe mucositis on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis; stop at 48-72 hours if cultures do not grow a Gram-positive. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Add amikacin 15 mg/kg once daily** for septic shock or suspected resistant Pseudomonas; **colistin or ceftazidime-avibactam with aztreonam** only when CRE colonisation is known (see the MDR Gram-negative topic). [ICMR 2019]`,`**Septic shock**: balanced crystalloid **30 mL/kg** within 3 hours, noradrenaline for **MAP 65 mmHg**, lactate repeat and ICU (see the Sepsis and septic shock topic). [SSC 2026]`,`**Persistent fever at 4-7 days** of broad-spectrum antibiotics in high-risk neutropenia: CT chest and galactomannan, then **empirical or pre-emptive antifungal** (liposomal amphotericin B 3 mg/kg/day, caspofungin or voriconazole). [IDSA 2010; ECIL-6 2017]`,`**Do not change antibiotics just for persistent fever** in a stable patient; change for a new site, a culture result or clinical deterioration. [IDSA 2010]`,`**G-CSF is not routine treatment** of established FN; consider it for high-risk patients - septic shock, pneumonia, invasive fungal infection, **age over 65** or expected prolonged neutropenia. [ASCO G-CSF 2015]`]},{heading:`Oral step-down and duration`,points:[`**IV-to-oral step-down** is allowed after **48-72 hours** if the patient is afebrile or improving, haemodynamically stable, eating and drinking, with negative cultures and no high-risk focus. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`Step-down drugs are the same **ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg TDS** combination; tailor to culture sensitivity if an organism grew. [ESMO 2016]`,`**Unexplained fever**: ECIL-4 allows stopping empirical antibiotics after **72 hours of IV therapy once afebrile for 48 hours and stable, regardless of ANC**; IDSA 2010 traditionally continued until ANC is 500 or more. [ECIL-4 2013; IDSA 2010]`,`**Documented infection**: treat for the duration the infection needs - for example **7-14 days for Gram-negative bacteraemia** or uncomplicated pneumonia - and at least until the ANC recovers. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Catheter-related S. aureus, Pseudomonas, fungal or mycobacterial infection** needs **line removal** plus at least 14 days of therapy. [IDSA 2009]`,`If the patient was discharged on oral therapy, recheck **CBC at 48-72 hours** and stop when afebrile 48 hours and ANC is rising, per the oncology unit protocol. [ESMO 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: use the **paediatric FN guideline (Lehrnbecher 2023)** - risk-adapted outpatient or oral step-down in low-risk children, piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime monotherapy for high risk. [International Paediatric FN Guideline 2023]`,`**Elderly over 65**: higher mortality and frequent atypical (afebrile) sepsis; most guidelines recommend **primary G-CSF prophylaxis** when regimen risk is 10-20% in this group. [NCCN 2024]`,`**CKD**: renally adjust **piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime (neurotoxicity), meropenem and ciprofloxacin**; avoid aminoglycosides where possible (see the Antimicrobial pharmacology topic). [Sanford Guide 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: hyperglycaemia is a CISNE item, raises risk of mucormycosis and Candida; check glucose and ketones. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**Pregnancy** (cancer in pregnancy): beta-lactams are safe, **fluoroquinolones are avoided** where an alternative exists - such women are always admitted. [ESMO 2016]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if no blood culture bottles, give the antibiotic anyway and refer; a written **neutropenia alert card** carried by the patient speeds triage. [UKONS 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Septic shock and multi-organ failure** - the leading cause of death; each hour of delay in antibiotics raises mortality in septic shock. [SSC 2026]`,`**Neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis)**: fever, right lower quadrant pain and diarrhoea; **CT bowel wall thickening over 4 mm**; treat with bowel rest and anaerobic-active cover (piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem), surgery for perforation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis** after prolonged neutropenia - nodules, halo sign, black nasal eschar (see the Systemic fungal infections and Mucormycosis topics). [ECIL-6 2017]`,`**Clostridioides difficile infection** from broad-spectrum antibiotics - test any diarrhoea of 3 or more loose stools in 24 hours. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Chemotherapy delay or dose reduction** after FN lowers relative dose intensity and can reduce cure in curable cancers (lymphoma, breast, germ cell). [NCCN 2024]`,`Low-risk patients managed as outpatients have **serious complications in under 5%** and fewer hospital-acquired infections. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`]},{heading:`Prevention, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Primary G-CSF prophylaxis** when regimen FN risk is **20% or more** (or 10-20% with patient risk factors): **filgrastim 5 microgram/kg/day SC** from 24-72 hours after chemotherapy until ANC recovery, or **pegfilgrastim 6 mg SC once per cycle**. [NCCN 2024; ASCO 2015]`,`**Levofloxacin 500 mg once daily** prophylaxis is advised only for **high-risk patients expecting ANC under 100 for over 7 days** (leukaemia induction, transplant). [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`Counsel every patient to **check temperature twice daily** in the nadir window, keep a thermometer, and carry the **oncology unit's 24-hour phone number**. [UKONS 2023]`,`Advise **hand hygiene, safe cooked food, boiled water**, oral care with soft brush and saline rinses, and avoiding crowds during the nadir; strict neutropenic diet has no proven benefit. [ESMO 2016]`,`**Vaccinate household contacts** with inactivated influenza; give the patient **inactivated influenza and COVID-19 vaccine**, avoid live vaccines during chemotherapy (see the Vaccination in special groups topic). [IDSA 2013]`,`Cancer care in India is linked to **NP-NCD** screening and treatment under **PM-JAY**; day-care chemotherapy centres in district hospitals widen access but need FN pathways. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ASCO/IDSA 2018 outpatient guideline** (current) - antibiotics within 1 hour, CISNE for stable solid-tumour patients, 4-hour observation before discharge, oral fluoroquinolone plus amoxicillin-clavulanate. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`**NCCN Prevention and Treatment of Cancer-Related Infections (2024-2025 versions)** - keeps anti-pseudomonal monotherapy, risk-stratified outpatient care and reserves vancomycin for specific indications. [NCCN 2024]`,`**International Paediatric Fever and Neutropenia Guideline 2023** - supports oral step-down and stopping antibiotics in low-risk children with negative cultures afebrile 24 hours, regardless of count. [Lehrnbecher 2023]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2026** - screen with NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS rather than qSOFA alone; balanced crystalloid, MAP 65 and prolonged beta-lactam infusion. [SSC 2026]`,`**ICMR AMRSN 2023 report** shows rising **carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella** (over half of isolates in many centres) - local antibiograms must decide when meropenem is first line. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**WHO AWaRe 2023**: piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime and meropenem are **Watch** antibiotics - de-escalate at 48-72 hours. [WHO AWaRe 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: define FN, give MASCC, and write the **first-hour bundle** - cultures, anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam, fluids, risk score. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`,`Common mistake: **waiting for the ANC report** before the first dose - treat on the chemotherapy date and clinical picture. [NICE CG151]`,`Common mistake: giving **ceftriaxone alone** - it has no anti-pseudomonal activity. [IDSA 2010]`,`Common mistake: **rectal temperature or digital rectal examination** in neutropenia. [NICE CG151]`,`Viva: why vancomycin is not routine - Gram-positive bacteraemia is less lethal early and routine use adds toxicity and VRE without benefit. [IDSA 2010]`,`Viva: oral regimen - ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate; contraindicated if on fluoroquinolone prophylaxis. [ASCO/IDSA 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`MASCC risk index (low risk = 21 or more of 26)`,columns:[`Characteristic`,`Points`],rows:[[`Burden of illness: no or mild symptoms`,`5`],[`Burden of illness: moderate symptoms`,`3`],[`No hypotension (SBP 90 mmHg or more)`,`5`],[`No chronic obstructive pulmonary disease`,`4`],[`Solid tumour, or haematological cancer with no previous fungal infection`,`4`],[`No dehydration needing IV fluids`,`3`],[`Outpatient at onset of fever`,`3`],[`Age under 60 years`,`2`]]},{heading:`Antibiotics in febrile neutropenia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ciprofloxacin + amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`750 mg oral BD + 625 mg oral TDS`,`Low risk; after first IV dose; until afebrile 48 h and ANC rising`,`Not if on FQ prophylaxis; tendinopathy, QT, C. difficile`],[`Levofloxacin (alternative)`,`750 mg oral OD`,`As above`,`QT prolongation, tendon rupture, dysglycaemia`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly (extended infusion)`,`Empirical, review at 48-72 h`,`Hypersensitivity; adjust in CrCl under 40`],[`Cefepime`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`Empirical, review at 48-72 h`,`Encephalopathy and seizures in CKD; poor ESBL cover`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly (2 g in CNS infection)`,`Septic shock, ESBL, hospital-onset`,`Seizures (lower than imipenem); selects CRE; reduces valproate`],[`Vancomycin (add-on)`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12 hourly; trough or AUC-guided`,`Stop at 48-72 h if no Gram-positive`,`Nephrotoxicity, infusion reaction`],[`Amikacin (add-on)`,`15 mg/kg IV once daily`,`Short course for shock or resistant GNB`,`Nephro- and ototoxicity`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`3 mg/kg/day IV`,`Persistent fever 4-7 days or proven mould`,`Hypokalaemia, renal impairment (less than conventional)`],[`Filgrastim (prophylaxis)`,`5 microgram/kg/day SC`,`From 24-72 h after chemo to ANC recovery`,`Bone pain, splenic rupture (rare)`]]},{heading:`Low-risk versus high-risk febrile neutropenia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Low risk - outpatient/oral`,`High risk - admit/IV`],rows:[[`Score`,`MASCC 21 or more; CISNE 0`,`MASCC under 21; CISNE 3 or more`],[`Expected neutropenia`,`7 days or less`,`Over 7 days or ANC under 100`],[`Cancer type`,`Solid tumour, controlled`,`Acute leukaemia, transplant, uncontrolled cancer`],[`Haemodynamics`,`Stable`,`Hypotension, NEWS2 5 or more`],[`Focus`,`None or simple`,`Pneumonia, catheter, abdominal pain, mucositis grade 3-4`],[`Organ function`,`Normal`,`Creatinine or transaminases raised`],[`Social`,`Caregiver, phone, within 1 h of hospital`,`Lives alone, far, cannot take oral drugs`],[`Antibiotic`,`Cipro + amox-clav oral`,`Piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime or meropenem IV`]]},{heading:`Investigations in fever after chemotherapy`,columns:[`Test`,`Purpose`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Blood cultures x 2 (peripheral + each lumen)`,`Bacteraemia, CRBSI`,`Line positive 2 h or more earlier = CRBSI`],[`CBC with differential`,`ANC, monocytes`,`ANC under 500 = FN; under 100 = profound`],[`Creatinine, LFT, electrolytes`,`Organ function, drug dosing`,`Raised = high risk`],[`Lactate`,`Hypoperfusion`,`2 mmol/L or more abnormal; 4 or more high risk`],[`Urine routine and culture`,`UTI`,`Pyuria may be absent`],[`Chest X-ray / CT chest`,`Pneumonia, mould`,`Halo sign suggests invasive aspergillosis`],[`Galactomannan, beta-D-glucan`,`Invasive fungal infection`,`Serum GM index 0.5 or more positive`],[`Stool C. difficile toxin/PCR`,`Antibiotic diarrhoea`,`3 or more loose stools in 24 h`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever after chemotherapy`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Action`],rows:[[`Bacterial FN`,`Day 7-14 of cycle, ANC under 500`,`Antibiotic within 60 min`],[`Catheter infection`,`Rigors on flushing, exit-site pus`,`Paired cultures, vancomycin, consider removal`],[`Neutropenic enterocolitis`,`Right lower quadrant pain, diarrhoea`,`CT, bowel rest, anaerobic cover`],[`Invasive mould`,`Fever beyond day 4-7 on antibiotics, nodules`,`CT, galactomannan, antifungal`],[`Drug or infusion fever`,`Within hours of bleomycin, cytarabine, rituximab`,`Exclusion only`],[`Tumour fever`,`Lymphoma, RCC, liver metastases`,`Naproxen test after infection excluded`],[`Tropical fever`,`Monsoon, thrombocytopenia, eschar`,`Smear/RDT, NS1, scrub IgM`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever or feeling unwell within 21 days of chemotherapy with SBP under 90 mmHg, NEWS2 5 or more or lactate 2 mmol/L or more`,`Rigors after flushing a central line or pus at the line exit site`,`Breathlessness, SpO2 under 94% or new infiltrate`,`Severe abdominal pain, especially right lower quadrant (neutropenic enterocolitis)`,`Confusion or drowsiness - treat as neutropenic sepsis even without fever`,`Severe mucositis preventing oral intake, persistent vomiting`,`Fever persisting 48-72 hours on oral outpatient regimen`,`Acute leukaemia, stem-cell transplant or expected ANC under 100 for over 7 days`],pearls:[`**Door-to-needle under 60 minutes** - treat on the chemotherapy date, not the ANC report.`,`**Ceftriaxone is not an FN drug** - no anti-pseudomonal cover.`,`**MASCC 21 or more = low risk**; CISNE 0 = low risk in stable solid tumours.`,`Oral regimen: **cipro 750 BD + amox-clav** - not if already on FQ prophylaxis.`,`**No rectal thermometer, no PR examination** in neutropenia.`,`Vancomycin is an **add-on for 5 indications** (shock, line, skin, pneumonia, MRSA), stopped at 48-72 h if not needed.`,`Persistent fever at day 4-7 means **look for fungus** - CT chest and galactomannan.`,`Ecthyma gangrenosum = **Pseudomonas** until proved otherwise.`,`**G-CSF prophylaxis at FN risk 20% or more**; not routine treatment of established FN.`],mcqs:[{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old woman on docetaxel-cyclophosphamide for breast cancer presents on day 9 of cycle 2 with a temperature of 38.6 C. BP 118/76, alert, no focus, MASCC 23, not on any prophylaxis. ANC is 300/microL. After cultures and a first IV dose, which outpatient regimen is recommended?`,options:[`Oral cefixime 200 mg twice daily`,`Oral ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily plus amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`Oral azithromycin 500 mg once daily`,`No antibiotic, paracetamol and review in 48 hours`],answer:1,explanation:`Low-risk FN (MASCC 21 or more, stable, caregiver available) can be treated as an outpatient with ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate, which together cover Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas and streptococci. Cefixime and azithromycin lack anti-pseudomonal cover, and withholding antibiotics in FN is never acceptable.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-q2`,stem:`Which one of the following scores 5 points in the MASCC risk index?`,options:[`Age under 60 years`,`Outpatient onset of fever`,`Absence of hypotension (SBP 90 mmHg or more)`,`No dehydration needing IV fluids`],answer:2,explanation:`In MASCC, absence of hypotension and no or mild burden of illness each score 5 points. Outpatient onset and no dehydration each score 3, and age under 60 scores only 2. A total of 21 or more out of 26 defines low risk.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-q3`,stem:`A man with acute myeloid leukaemia on induction chemotherapy has fever on day 12, BP 84/50 and rigors after flushing his PICC line. Which empirical regimen is most appropriate in an Indian tertiary centre with high ESBL prevalence?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`Oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`Cefepime alone`,`Meropenem plus vancomycin`],answer:3,explanation:`Septic shock with suspected catheter infection is a high-risk FN with two vancomycin indications (haemodynamic instability and line infection), and high ESBL prevalence favours meropenem over cefepime. Ceftriaxone has no Pseudomonas cover and the oral regimen is only for low-risk stable patients.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-q4`,stem:`A neutropenic patient has fever persisting on day 5 of piperacillin-tazobactam despite negative cultures. He is stable. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`CT chest and serum galactomannan, then consider an antifungal`,`Add vancomycin because fever persists`,`Stop all antibiotics and observe`,`Switch to oral ciprofloxacin for discharge`],answer:0,explanation:`Persistent fever at 4-7 days of broad-spectrum antibiotics in neutropenia should prompt a search for invasive fungal infection with CT chest and galactomannan, followed by empirical or pre-emptive antifungal therapy. Adding vancomycin for persistent fever alone gives no benefit, and stopping antibiotics or discharging on oral therapy while febrile and neutropenic is unsafe.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-q5`,stem:`A patient on chemotherapy calls the PHC at night with a temperature of 38.4 C. The nearest cancer centre is 3 hours away. What should the medical officer do first?`,options:[`Advise paracetamol and an OPD visit next morning`,`Wait for a complete blood count before deciding`,`Take blood cultures if available and give the first broad-spectrum IV antibiotic dose, then transfer`,`Refer immediately without any treatment`],answer:2,explanation:`The first antibiotic dose must be given within 60 minutes of arrival, so the medical officer cultures if possible, gives an anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam (or the best available combination) and then transfers. Waiting for the count or delaying to the next morning raises mortality, and referral without the first dose wastes the critical hour.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define febrile neutropenia. Describe the risk stratification and the initial management of a patient presenting with fever after chemotherapy. Add a note on outpatient oral therapy and step-down.`,openingLines:[`Febrile neutropenia is an oral temperature of 38.3 C or more once, or 38.0 C or more for over 1 hour, with an ANC under 500/microL or expected to fall below 500 within 48 hours.`,`It is an oncological emergency: the first dose of an anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam must be given within 60 minutes, and only low-risk patients are treated with oral antibiotics at home.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`IDSA/ASCO definition with ANC grades (under 500 severe, under 100 profound).`,`Nadir 7-14 days after chemotherapy; mortality 5% solid tumours, up to 11% haematological, 20-50% with shock.`,`Indian flora: Gram-negatives 60-70% of bacteraemias, high ESBL and CRE.`]},{heading:`Initial assessment`,points:[`History: chemo date, line, focus symptoms, prophylaxis, prior resistant organisms.`,`Examination: vitals and NEWS2, mouth, line site, skin, abdomen, perianal inspection (no PR).`,`Investigations: 2 blood cultures (peripheral and each lumen), CBC, renal and liver function, lactate, urine, chest X-ray.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`MASCC items and points; 21 or more low risk.`,`CISNE for stable solid-tumour patients; 0 low, 3 or more high.`,`Clinical high-risk features override scores.`]},{heading:`Empirical therapy`,points:[`Within 60 minutes: piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g 6-hourly, cefepime 2 g 8-hourly or meropenem 1 g 8-hourly.`,`Vancomycin add-on indications; amikacin in shock.`,`Septic shock bundle: 30 mL/kg crystalloid, noradrenaline for MAP 65.`]},{heading:`Outpatient oral therapy and step-down`,points:[`Low risk after 4 h observation: ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD plus amoxicillin-clavulanate; clindamycin if penicillin-allergic.`,`Not if on FQ prophylaxis, vomiting, no caregiver or far from hospital.`,`IV to oral at 48-72 h if afebrile and stable; stop per ECIL-4 or at ANC recovery.`]},{heading:`Persistent fever and prevention`,points:[`Day 4-7: CT chest, galactomannan, antifungal.`,`G-CSF prophylaxis at FN risk 20% or more; levofloxacin prophylaxis for expected ANC under 100 for over 7 days.`,`Patient education: thermometer, 24-hour number, alert card.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: fever after chemotherapy - cultures and first dose within 60 minutes - MASCC/CISNE - low-risk oral vs high-risk IV - 48-72 h review - step-down or antifungal`,`MASCC score table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Assessment and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Risk stratification`,marks:2},{part:`Empirical IV therapy`,marks:2},{part:`Outpatient oral and step-down`,marks:2},{part:`Persistent fever and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`febrile neutropenia`,`MASCC`,`CISNE`,`anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam`,`oral step-down`,`G-CSF`]},{id:`infectious-fever-febrile-neutropenia-opd-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the MASCC score and outpatient management of low-risk febrile neutropenia.`,openingLines:[`The MASCC risk index scores 7 clinical features to a maximum of 26; a score of 21 or more identifies low-risk febrile neutropenia with under 5% serious complications.`,`Low-risk patients may be treated with oral ciprofloxacin plus amoxicillin-clavulanate after a first IV dose and 4 hours of observation.`],answer:[{heading:`MASCC items`,points:[`Burden of illness none/mild 5 or moderate 3; no hypotension 5; no COPD 4.`,`Solid tumour or no prior fungal infection 4; no dehydration 3; outpatient 3; age under 60 years 2.`]},{heading:`Eligibility for outpatient care`,points:[`MASCC 21 or more (CISNE 0 in stable solid tumours), stable, can take oral drugs.`,`Caregiver, phone, within 1 hour of hospital, not on FQ prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Regimen and follow-up`,points:[`Ciprofloxacin 750 mg BD plus amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg TDS; levofloxacin 750 mg OD or clindamycin combination if allergic.`,`Daily phone contact, review at 24-48 h, return if fever persists beyond 48-72 h or new symptoms.`]}],mustDraw:[`MASCC table with points`],markSplit:[{part:`MASCC items and cut-off`,marks:2},{part:`Eligibility`,marks:1.5},{part:`Regimen and follow-up`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`MASCC`,`low-risk febrile neutropenia`,`ciprofloxacin`,`amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`outpatient`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections in patients with cancer`,`Freifeld AG et al. Clinical Practice Guideline for the Use of Antimicrobial Agents in Neutropenic Patients with Cancer: 2010 Update by the IDSA. Clin Infect Dis 2011`,`Taplitz RA et al. Outpatient Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Adults Treated for Malignancy: ASCO and IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline Update, J Clin Oncol 2018`,`Klastersky J et al. Management of febrile neutropaenia: ESMO Clinical Practice Guidelines, Ann Oncol 2016`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Prevention and Treatment of Cancer-Related Infections, 2024; Hematopoietic Growth Factors, 2024`,`NICE CG151 Neutropenic sepsis: prevention and management in people with cancer`,`Lehrnbecher T et al. Guideline for the Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Pediatric Patients With Cancer and Hematopoietic Cell Transplantation Recipients: 2023 Update, J Clin Oncol 2023`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2019 (febrile neutropenia) and ICMR AMRSN Annual Report 2023`]});var O=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d,...f,...p,...m,...h,...g,..._,...v,...y,...b,...x,...S,...C,...w,...T,...E,...D];export{O as default};